(comment hasRelationFoundInLocationAtTime-WithSource "(#$hasRelationFoundInLocationAtTime-WithSource GROUP MEMBER LOCATION TIME SOURCE PRED) holds ...")
(comment TimeIntervalInclusiveFn "A binary function that takes two instances of #$TemporalThing as arguments and returns an instance of #$ContinuousTimeInterval.  <code>(#$TimeIntervalInclusiveFn START END)</code> is the continuous time interval between, and including, <code>START</code> and <code>END</code>. Note that <code>START</code> must end no later than <code>END</code> ends, and <code>END</code> must start no earlier than <code>START</code> starts; in the limiting case, <code>START</code> and <code>END</code> may be #$cotemporal (q.v.).  
<p>
See also the similar #$TimeIntervalBetweenFn, which returns an interval that excludes the temporal extents of its arguments.")
(comment TVSketchComedy "A collection of #$ComedyTVShows, those in which actors play out live skits, e.g.             'Saturday Night Live'.")
(comment formulator "Agent(s) who formulated this #$AspatialInformationStore.")
(comment SpiderShaped "#$SpiderShaped is the #$AnimalBodyShapeType defined by the typical shape of a spider.")
(comment UnitedStatesPerson "An instance of #$PersonTypeByNationality.  Each instance of #$UnitedStatesPerson is a person who is currently among that country's citizens (see the predicate #$citizens), or is a participant in the #$NationalCulture of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica (see #$UnitedStatesCulture).")
(comment changeOfSymptomIntensity "(#$changeOfSymptomIntensity TIME COND ORG QCA)
indicates whether ORG is experiencing more or less discomfort
as a result of COND over the time period TIME.  If QCA is #$PositiveChange it
means that ORG is experiencing more discomfort from COND as TIME goes
by.  #$NegativeChange indicates decreased discomfort from COND and #$NoChange
indicates no change.  Note that this predicate can be taken to discuss the change in an entire physiological condition over a time period, not just an isolated symptom of it.")
(comment relationAllExistsSame "(#$relationAllExistsSame PRED COLL1 COLL2) holds just in case every instance
of COLL1 is related by PRED to the same instance of COLL2.  For example, if 
HoustonTexansCollection is the collection of all the group members of the #$HoustonTexans,
then (#$relationAllExistsSame #$hasCoach HoustonTexansCollection #$SportsCoach).  This means
that every instance of the collection of players on the Houston Texans football team has
the same coach.  Another example: 
(#$relationAllExistsSame #$uniformColorOfObject #$Ruby-Gem #$Color) means every ruby
is the same color.  This predicate is useful if you want to state that a collection of things
all share some feature, but can't be specific about what feature they share.")
(comment PreparingForSituation "The collection of #$PurposefulActions in which an agent prepares for a situation (#$Situation). The action can be entirely mental, as in #$MakingAPlan, or can involve #$CompositePhysicalAndMentalEvents, for example, buying food as a preparation for cooking a meal. The action may make the situation prepared for more likely to happen or it may have no causal bearing on that situation. For example, preparing for college may make it more likely that the agent will attend college. However, preparing for a power outage by buying a flashlight doesn't make it more likely that the power outage will occur. See also #$PreparingForUndesirableSituation. To relate #$PreparingForSituations to the #$Situation types that they prepare for, use the function #$PreparingForSitTypeFn. To relate #$PreparingForUndesirableSituations to the #$Situations that they prepare for, use the function #$PreparednessFn. See also #$PreventFromHappening and #$PreventionFn.")
(comment CityTourFn "(#$CityTourFn CITY) returns the collection of all
instances of #$TouringACity in which the city toured is CITY.")
(disjointWith HomogeneousStructure Organization)
(comment levelOfEfficiencyWithRespectTo "(#$levelOfEfficiencyWithRespectTo SYSTEM LEVEL EVTTYPE)
 says that SYSTEM
has a LEVEL level of efficiency with respect to 
EVTTYPE, a kind of activity.  For example,
 (#$levelOfEfficiency MySprinklerSystem 
 (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$Efficiency) WateringALawn) says that my
sprinkler system is inefficient with respect to
 lawn-watering activities.  Note, however, that
MySprinklerSystem might be fairly efficient with 
respect to other activities, such as spraying 
pedestrians from a distance.")
(comment creditor "The predicate that identifies an #$IntelligentAgent who transfers a right to use some quantity of money temporarily, subject to some #$LoanAgreement.  (#$creditor BORROW AGENT) means that after the event BORROW, AGENT loses some user right over the #$objectOfPossessionTransfer, in this case a quantity of money.  Also see #$borrower.")
(comment DistributedAssignment "A specialization of #$Assignment-Specification (q.v.). Each instance of #$DistributedAssignment is an #$Assignment-Specification that bears the #$assignees relation to several instances of #$IntelligentAgent.  Further, the obligation associated with such an assignment requires that each assignee is responsible for an individual action to satisfy the assignment.  Thus, if N agents are assigned to  #$DistributedAssignment ASSIGN, then it requires N actions to satisfy ASSIGN (with 1 action per assignee).  This collection should be contrasted with #$TeamAssignment.")
(comment Capoeira-StyleOfDance "The collection of instances of #$Dancing-Human that are in the
capoeira style.                            ")
(comment HijackerControllingATransporter "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$ControllingATransportationDevice. Each instance of #$HijackerControllingATransporter is a #$subEvents of a #$Hijacking, and an event in which a #$perpetrator in the #$Hijacking is controlling the #$Hijacking's #$transporterHijacked.")
(comment PaxilProduct "An #$Antidepressant drug product manufactuered by SmithKline Beecham.")
(comment Paroxetine "A #$PharmaceuticalType which is a subcategory of #$Antidepressant.")
(comment OneDimensionalQuantity "A specialization of #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$OneDimensionalQuantity is a measurable quantity that is <i>not</i> defined in terms of (i.e. as the product or ratio of) other quantities.  For example, (#$Mile 65) and (#$HoursDuration 1) are both instances of #$OneDimensionalQuantity; but (#$MilesPerHour 65), derived by dividing the former quantity by the latter, is not (cf. #$MultiDimensionalQuantity).
<p>
Specializations of #$OneDimensionalQuantity include #$Distance, #$Mass, and #$Time-Quantity.
<p>
Each one-dimensional quantity is equal to the result of applying some #$OneDimensionalUnitOfMeasure (q.v.) to some number or other #$NumericInterval (or pair of such intervals).   If a #$OneDimensionalQuantity is multiplied or divided by a numeric-interval, the result is still a one-dimensional quantity; if two one-dimensional quantities that are #$numericallyComparable (q.v.) are added or subtracted, the result is still a one-dimensional quantity.  Conversely, whenever one #$OneDimensionalQuantity is multiplied or divided by another #$OneDimensionalQuantity, the result is a #$TwoDimensionalQuantity.  Two quantities that are not numerically-comparable cannot be added to or substracted from each other.  And when a #$OneDimensionalQuantity is multiplied or divided by (or divides) a #$TwoDimensionalQuantity, the result is in many cases a #$ThreeDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).
<p>
See also #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and #$FundamentalUnitType.")
(comment EducationalTVShowForChildren "A collection of TV shows (#$TVShow-CW) with educational content suitable for young children.")
(comment hasInterAgentStatusWith "(#$hasInterAgentStatusWith AGENT1 AGENT2 STATUS) means that AGENT2 has STATUS with respect to AGENT1. For example, it is believed that, at least until the US invasion of #$Afghanistan, (#$hasInterAgentStatusWith #$AlQaida #$Afghanistan #$SupportOfSponsorshipOfTerrorism).  This predicate is useful for talking about people and political entities like countries. Countries (and their political leaders) change and obviously so does the status they attribute to people so uses of this predicate should be temporally qualified. ")
(comment WormShaped "#$WormShaped is the instance of #$AnimalBodyShapeType defined by the typical shape of an earthworm.")
(comment sightseeingsIn "#$sightseeingsIn relates an instance of #$GeographicalThing to the collection of #$Sightseeings which occur there.")
(comment Album-CW "The collection of all named, recorded audio #$ConceptualWorks regardless of the means of recording (instantiations of an individual #$Album-CW may be instances of #$CompactDisc-Audio, #$AudioCassette, #$PhonographRecord-LP, ...).  Typically used for music recordings. Not used for things like books on tape or recorded speeches.")
(comment Flammability "A specialization of #$DispositionalQuantity. Each instance of #$Flammability represents a specific readiness with which some tangible substance burns. Different degrees of #$Flammability are represented using #$GenericValueFunctions. Flammability of an object is indicated with the predicate #$flammabilityOfObject.")
(comment SinglePartyRepublic "A #$GeopliticalEntityTypeBySystemOfGovernment in which the headOfState is not a Monarch-HeadOfState and is usually a President-HeadOfState, but there's only one political party")
(comment fragilityOfObject "(#$fragilityOfObject OBJ DEGREE) means that the tangible object OBJ has the #$Fragility DEGREE.  The higher the fragility, the more easily the object can be broken.")
(comment AdoptionTypeOfNormByAgentFn "<code>(#$AdoptionTypeOfNormByAgentFn NORM AGENT)</code> denotes the collection of events in which <code>AGENT</code> adopts the norm <code>NORM</code>.")
(comment HasBeenReviewedInRolePredicateSweep "A documentation constant.  Constants denoting instances of #$BinaryRolePredicate have been explicitly tagged as members of this collection just in case they have been reviewed and no other relevant tag has been asserted of the predicate.  That is, none of the following tags have been deemed appropriate for the predicate.
<p>
#$RolePredicateAgnosticTowardsPresentTenseTimeIndex
#$PresentTenseVersionHoldsSomeTimeInDuration
#$PresentTenseVersionHoldsThroughoutDuration
<p>
In order to keep track of which predicates have been reviewed, the three collections above are asserted to be specializations of this collection.
<p>
Also, we forwardly derive membership in this collection if the term appears in arg1 of any of the following predicates:
<p>
#$roleWithEventTypeEntailsTimeIndex
#$roleWithEventTypeEntailsSomeTimeInTimeIndex
#$roleWithEventTypeAgnosticWRTTimeIndex
<p>
.")
(comment sourceOfTypeForProject "(#$sourceOfTypeForProject SOURCE CW-TYPE SOURCE-TYPE PROJECT) means that SOURCE is, or is likely to be, a source of CW-TYPE, which is of SOURCE-TYPE, of facts or knowledge entered into the CycKB on behalf of PROJECT.")
(comment strategyConfidenceCeiling "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that relates an instance of #$ProblemSolvingStrategy to a real number between 0 and 1 that represents the highest confidence that any result of applying that strategy to a problem can have.")
(comment corroboratingSourcesForProposition "<code>(#$corroboratingSourcesForProposition  SOURCE SENTENCE)</code> means that the instance of #$InformationStore <code>SOURCE</code> is a non-trivial source for the proposition expressed by the instance of #$CycLSentence <code>SENTENCE</code>. 
<p>
This predicate is meant to take temporal qualification into account.  Thus, if SENTENCE is temporally qualified, a SOURCE corroborates SENTENCE iff an assertion corresponding to SENTENCE (with temporal qualification) is provable  in the context that corresponds to SOURCE.")
(comment inverseBinaryPredicateOf "A symmetric binary #$MetaPredicate that is a specialization of #$genlInverse (q.v.).  <code>(#$inverseBinaryPredicateOf BINPRED INVPRED)</code> means that <code>BINPRED</code> and <code>INVPRED</code>
are inverses of each other.  That is, <code>(BINPRED ARG1 ARG2)</code> holds if and only if <code>(INVPRED ARG2 ARG1)</code> holds.
<p>
Furthermore, we stipulate that the respective argument-type constraints on <code>BINPRED</code> and <code>INVPRED</code> are such that a given thing is an #$admittedArgument (q.v.) to <code>BINPRED</code>'s first (second) argument-place if and only if it is an #$admittedArgument to <code>INVPRED</code>'s second (first) argument-place.
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$InverseBinaryPredicateFn.")
(comment DavidsonianToNonDavidsonianInferenceTest "A specialization of #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified.  Instances are inference tests that test our ability to deduce appropriate non-Davidsonian output from a Davidsonian representation (such as what is typically produced by a NL-to-CycL parser.")
(comment authorityFor "(#$authorityFor SOURCE ASSERTION) means that ASSERTION has been entered into the KB on the authority of #$InformationStore.  Use the more specific (#$containsInformation IBT INFO) to state that an #$InformationBearingThing, IBT, has INFO as part of its information content.  See also the related predicate, #$mtSource.")
(comment sourceOfAssertion-NonTrivial "<code>(#$sourceOfAssertion-NonTrivial SOURCE ASSERTION)</code> means that the instance of #$InformationStore <code>SOURCE</code> is a non-trivial source for the instance of #$CycLAssertion <code>ASSERTION</code>. 
<p>
A <i>non-trivial source</i> is a source such as a dictionary, a webpage or a periodicals that contains the information expressed by <code>ASSERTION</code>, and which was used to as a justification for representing <code>ASSERTION</code>. A <i>trivial source</i>, in contrast, is the mechanism used to assert <code>ASSERTION</code> (such as the work of a particular cyclist -- see #$assertionAssertBy).
<p>
This predicate allows the system to distinguish source information that should be
generated to a user from source information that should not. For example, the assertion '(#$literacyRateForGroupInRegion #$Estonia #$Person (#$Percent 100))' was asserted into the KB by Kathy Panton, and she got this information from the CIA 1997 World Factbook. #$sourceOfAssertion-NonTrivial is defined to indicate that the CIA 1997 World Factbook, not Kathy, is the non-trivial source.")
(comment authorityClaims-Term "(#$authorityClaims-Term INFO-STORE CLAIM) means that INFO-STORE, an instance of #$InformationStore, has claimed the information
expressed in CLAIM is true.")
(comment DefiningTimeUnitForCalendarCoveringTypeFn "(#$DefiningTimeUnitForCalendarCoveringTypeFn DATE-TYPE) denotes the unit of time that is the #$definingTimeUnit for DATE-TYPE (the unit of time that defines DATE-TYPE, an instance of #$CalendarCoveringType.  For example, (#$DefiningTimeUnitForCalendarCoveringTypeFn #$CalendarWeek) denotes a function that takes as its argument one or two real numbers MIN and MAX and returns the (properly interval-like) #$Time-Quantity of being at least MIN weeks and at most MAX weeks in duration.")
(comment residesInDwelling "A #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate and specialization of #$usualLocationOfObject that relates a person or other sort of animal to a constructed shelter in which s/he/it lives or resides. 
<p>
<code>(#$residesInDwelling RESIDENT SHELTER)</code> means that <code>RESIDENT</code> resides in <code>SHELTER</code>.  That is, <code>SHELTER</code> is the place where <code>RESIDENT</code> usually sleeps, spends much of its time, lays its eggs; a place it cleans and maintains and repairs; a place it defends against intrusion., <i>etc.</i>  
<p>
A person or animal might reside in more than one dwelling at a given time.  For example, a person might #$residesInDwelling some tent during a camping trip, and at the same time it would still be true that she #$residesInDwelling her apartment or house.  Note that the dwelling in which a person #$residesInDwelling is not necessarily her legal domicile, though of course it often will be.  Note also that #$residesInDwelling does not subsume parasitical or symbiotic relationships, in which one animal lives in or on another.  The more general predicate #$objectFoundInLocation would be the appropriate one, for example, for a case of a flea living on a dog. See also #$occupants and #$residesInRegion.")
(comment FETIntermediateYesOrNoAnswer "A collection of two
#$CycLConstants: #$FETYesValue and #$FETNoValue. Instances of
#$FETIntermediateYesOrNoAnswer are used in the fact entry tool to
provide users with sensible choices to yes/no questions. Templates
that ask such questions use specialized predicates in their
formulae. These predicates, which take instances
#$FETIntermediateYesOrNoAnswer as arguments, are then reformulated
into useful CycL assertions.")
(comment formulaTemplateHasTopic "(#$formulaTemplateHasTopic TEMPLATE TOPIC) means that TEMPLATE is one of the templates included in TEMPLATE-TYPE.")
(comment FTETemplate "A spec of #$FormulaTemplate. The collection of formula templates for the formula template editor.")
(comment KnowledgeRepresentation-CW "The collection of conceptual works consisting of formally represented knowledge.")
(comment KRWork-UpwardlyClosed "The collection of formally expressed works of  knowledge representation in which every term referenced in the work is also defined in the work.")
(comment KnowledgePackage "The collection of CycL-based #$KnowledgeRepresentation-CWs. Each KnowledgePackage is either published (in which case it should be treated as read-only) or unpublished (in which case users of the package should take into account that the contents may change over time). Instances of KnowledgePackage are used as dimension values in the knowledge package dimension. Knowledge packages assist with knowledge sharing among KBs, facilitate knowledge collection, and help identify causes of KB content test regression.")
(comment KnowledgePackage-Published "The collection of Knowledge Packages each of which is not intended to change, usually because it has been provided to another agent (person or program) or incorporated into another knowledge package (via genlMt) that may depend on the stability of the content of the published knowledge package.")
(comment KnowledgePackage-Unpublished "The collection of Knowledge Packages whose contents may change over time and, therefore, should not be relied upon by any agent or microtheory where stability of content is a desired trait.")
(comment embarrassed "(#$embarrassed AGT PROP) means that that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT is embarrassed that the proposition (#$CycLSentence-Assertible) PROP is true. For example, (#$holdsIn (#$YearFn 1998) (#$embarrassed #$BillClinton (#$sexualPartners #$BillClinton #$MonicaLewinsky))) means that it holds throughout 1998 that Bill Clinton is embarrassed that he and Monica Lewinsky were sexual partners.")
(comment hasEpistemicEmotion "(#$hasEpistemicEmotion AGT PROP) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT has an epistemic emotion whose propositional content is expressed by the #$CycLSentence-Assertible PROP. #$hasEpistemicEmotion is a generalization of more specific predicates that relate agents to specific epistemic emotions. These relationships are expressed in English using expressions such as 'S fears that p', 'S is worried that p', 's hopes that p'. See  #$hopes, #$fears. For the theory behind the classification of emotions as factive/epistemic, see #$SharedNoteOnFactiveVersusEpistemicEmotion.")
(comment hasFactiveEmotion "(#$hasFactiveEmotion AGT PROP) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT has a factive emotion whose propositional content is expressed by the #$CycLSentence-Assertible PROP. #$hasFactiveEmotion is a generalization of more specific predicates that relate agents to specific factive emotions. These relationships are expressed in English using expressions such as 'S is proud that p', 'S resents that p', 'S is thankful that p', 'S regrets that p' and 'S is embarrassed that p' (see #$embarrassed). For the theory behind the classification of emotions as factive/epistemic see #$SharedNoteOnFactiveVersusEpistemicEmotion.")
(comment CloudlikeObject "A cloud is a kind of #$Suspension that has air as the suspending fluid.  Things like clouds of locusts are metaphorical, and are not treated as clouds in the kb.  See #$Suspension for the structure of the material out of which clouds are made.")
(disjointWith CommonSenseTest-OpenSentenceQuery CommonSenseTest-ClosedSentenceQuery)
(disjointWith CommonSenseTestTypeByQueryForm CommonSenseTestTypeByReasoningTypeUsed)
(comment obtainsDuring "#$obtainsDuring is a binary predicate relating a situation to a time interval during which it obtains.  <code>(#$obtainsDuring SITUATION TIMEINTERVAL)</code> means that the instance of #$Situation <code>SITUATION</code> obtains entirely within the the instance  <code>TIMEINTERVAL</code> of #$TimeInterval.  Thus, <code>(#$obtainsDuring SITUATION TIMEINTERVAL)</code> implies that <code>(#$temporallySubsumes TIMEINTERVAL EVENT)</code>. Where SITUATION is an instance of #$Event, use the more specific #$occursDuring.")
(comment residentialRegion-Role "An instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate.  (#$residentialRegion-Role RESIDING REGION) identifies REGION as the geographical region (see #$GeographicalRegion) such that throughout the temporal extent of the instance RESIDING of #$ResidingSomewhere, (#$residesInRegion ANIMAL REGION) will be true for each instance ANIMAL of #$Animal that is a resident (plays the #$residents-Role) in RESIDING.   Note that #$residentialRegion-Role is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$situationLocation, which identifies a region that subsumes the spatial extent of a situation.  One can meaningfully reside in a region while outside of it, e.g., one can take a visit Leicester while residing in London.")
(comment residents-Role "An instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate.  (#$residents-Role RESIDING ANIMAL) identifies ANIMAL as one of the residents in the instance of #$ResidingSomewhere, RESIDING.")
(comment ResidingSomewhere "The collection of instances of #$Situation in which an animal resides, or makes its \"home\" in some place, permanently or semi-permanently.")
(comment intangibleComponent-Maximal "<code>(#$intangibleComponent-Maximal WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>PART</code> is the entire intangible part of the #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject <code>WHOLE</code>.")
(comment intangibleParts "<code>(#$intangibleParts WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>PART</code> is one of the intangible parts of <code>WHOLE</code>.  To relate something with both tangible and intangible parts to its entire intangible extent, use the specialization #$intangibleComponent-Maximal.")
(comment containsInformationAbout "An #$ObjectPredicate and specialization of #$topicOfIndividual that relates a given #$InformationStore (q.v.) to the thing(s) it contains information about.  <code>(#$containsInformationAbout INFOSTORE THING)</code> means  <code>INFOSTORE</code> is at least in part \"about\" <code>THING</code>.  <code>THING</code> might be any sort  of thing at all.  For instance, <code>THING</code> may be an individual, as in   (#$containsInformationAbout #$TheTorontoStar-TheNewspaper  #$CityOfTorontoOntario); or <code>THING</code> might be a collection, as in   (#$containsInformationAbout #$PokemonWorld-WWWSite #$PokemonCharacter).
<p>
This is a very general notion of aboutness, which does not require that  <code>THING</code> be explicitly referred to in <code>INFOSTORE</code>, or that it be the main  topic of <code>INFOSTORE</code>.  (For cases where <code>THING</code> is the main topic, use the  more specific #$containsInformationAbout-Focally.)  On the other hand, note that an information store's containing information about a given  collection or set is generally <i>not</i> a sufficient condition for its  containing information about any particular elements of that collection  or set.
<p>
See also #$containsInformation, which relates  #$InformationBearingThings to the information (in the form of  #$AspatialInformationStores) they contain.")
(comment exampleSentence-Negative "An expression of the form (#$exampleSentence-Negative TERM SENTENCE) expresses that the #$CycLTerm TERM is used in the #$CycLSentence SENTENCE and that SENTENCE has some significant semantic flaw. For example, the TERM could be the predicate in SENTENCE and its sematnic constraints are violated by the terms it takes as values.")
(comment exampleSentence-Positive "An expression of the form (#$exampleSentence-Negative TERM SENTENCE) expresses that the #$CycLTerm TERM is used in the #$CycLSentence-Assertible SENTENCE and that SENTENCE has no significant semantic flaw, although the sentence could be false.")
(comment Temple "The collection of all religious temples: structures designed for worship, prayer, or contemplation.  Specializations include #$Church-Building, #$Synagogue-Building, and #$HinduTemple.")
(comment ReligiousBuilding "A specialization of #$Building.  Each instance of #$ReligiousBuilding is a building designed and primarily used for religious purposes.  Specializations include #$Temple and #$Convent-Residence.")
(comment Chapel "A specializationof #$Temple.  Each instance of #$Chapel is a comparatively small chamber of worship, built as an adjunct to a larger #$Church-Building for purposes of private worship and private ceremonies.")
(comment Ash "An #$ExistingStuffType and specialization of (#$MobFn (#$ParticleFn #$SolidTangibleThing)).  Each instance of #$Ash is the powdery #$Grayish residue of a #$CombustionProcess.")
(comment Daylight "The light of day. In #$WeatherMt #$Daylight is the light of daytime, even in the shade.")
(comment evidenceForNLTranslation "(#$evidenceForNLTranslation TREE CYCL SCORE) means that CYCL is an appropriate CycL translation for the parse tree TREE, with a confidence score of SCORE.")
(comment ParseMtForSourceFn "(ParseMtForSourceFn SOURCE) creates a microtheory which contains parse tree-CycL interpretation mappings for NL phrases derived from that source.")
(comment claimsResponsibilityFor "<code>(#$claimsResponsibilityFor AGENT EVENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> has communicated that <code>EVENT</code> was #$performedBy <code>AGENT</code>. For example, (#$claimsResponsibilityFor <code>Person001</code> #$SinkingOfTheLusitania-MilitaryAttack) means that <code>Person001</code> has claimed responsibility for the sinking of the Lusitania. To 'claim responsibility' in this context means that <code>AGENT</code> performs a communication act with the propositional content <code>(#$deliberateActors AGENT EVENT)</code>. See also #$deniesResponsibilityFor.")
(comment deniesResponsibilityFor "<code>(#$deniesResponsibilityFor AGENT EVENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> has communicated that <code>EVENT</code> was not #$performedBy <code>AGENT</code>. For example, (#$deniesResponsibilityFor <code>Person001</code> #$SinkingOfTheLusitania-MilitaryAttack) means that <code>Person001</code> has denied responsibility for the sinking of the Lusitania. To 'deny responsibility' in this context means that <code>AGENT</code> performs a communication act with the propositional content <code>(#$not (#$performedBy AGENT EVENT))</code>. This must be distinguished from the case of <code>(#$not (#$claimsResponsibilityFor AGENT EVENT))</code>. #$deniesResponsibilityFor is a positive action undertaken by some agent.")
(comment mtSource "<code>(#$mtSource MT SOURCE)</code> means that the information in <code>MT</code> originated with, or is authenticated by the #$TemporalThing, <code>SOURCE</code>.")
(comment Channel-BodyOfWaterLink "A specialization of #$Channel-WaterPath.  Each instance of #$Channel-BodyOfWaterLink is a relatively narrow body of water, such as a navigable canal (see #$NavigationCanal) or a #$Strait, that connects two larger and wider bodies of water.
<p>
See also the disjoint collection #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart.  Note that a #$Channel-BodyOfWaterLink (e.g. a #$Strait) might have an instance of #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart as a subregion.")
(comment Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart "A specialization of #$Channel-WaterPath.  Each instance of #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart is a subregion of a body of water that is intended to allow the passage of boats or ships which would otherwise run aground or encounter hazardous rocks or wrecks.  They are often deeper than their surrounding bodies of water.  Whole channels extend from the surface to the bottom of the body of water and are long and thin.  Unlike a #$Channel-BodyOfWaterLink (cf.), a #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart does not have land banks above the water's surface as borders.  Often, #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayParts are created or maintained by human dredging (and are thus #$ConstructionArtifacts).  Less often, they are naturally deep places.  They are usually marked with navigational bouys and appear on nautical charts.
<p>
Instances of #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart include the channel that allows ships safe passage up the East River off Manhattan, through Hell's Gate and into
Long Island Sound.  Conversely, the #$EnglishChannel and the #$StraitOfHormuz (e.g.) are <i>not</i> instances of this collection, but are instead instances of #$Channel-BodyOfWaterLink.  But note that an instance of the latter (especially a #$Strait) might have a #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart as a subregion.")
(comment AnimalPen "A specialization of #$AnimalContainer whose instances are fenced-in outdoor areas for non-human animals.")
(comment Estuary "A subcollection of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$Estuary an arm of the sea at the lower end of a river, where tide meets outflow, usually associated with a widening of the river channel.
<p>
Compare with #$Delta.  Also see #$riverEndTopography.")
(disjointWith Indoors-IsolatedFromOutside GeographicalRegion)
(comment Levee "A subcollection of both #$Embankment and #$FlowControlDevice.  Each instance of #$Levee is an embankment built alongside a river to restrain high water from flooding bordering land.")
(comment Canal "A specialization of both #$BodyOfWater and #$PathArtifact.  Each instance of #$Canal is an artificial waterway designed to serve either as a path for watercraft (see #$NavigationCanal), or as a device for irrigating or draining land (#$IrrigationCanal, #$DrainageCanal).  Instances of #$Canal include the #$SuezCanal and the #$PhuCamCanal.")
(comment Glacier "A specialization of #$TopographicalFeature and #$InanimateObject-Natural.  Each instance of #$Glacier is a very slowly moving (practically stationary) thick sheet of ice which expands down a slope or out from the center (see #$Icecap).")
(comment Icecap "A specialization of #$Glacier. Each instance of #$Icecap is a glacier that slowly expands out from its center.")
(comment Causeway "A specialization of #$Bridge.  Each instance of #$Causeway is a raised roadway across wet ground or shallow water.  Causeways tend to be longer than the average bridge.")
(comment SuspendedPathArtifact "#$SuspendedPathArtifact is the collection of agent-made paths that are suspended or elevated above ground or water surface.")
(comment Bridge "A specialzation of #$SuspendedPathArtifact and #$TransportFacility.  #$Bridges are elevated roadways, usually over water or some other pathway artifact.")
(comment Island "A specialization of both #$LandBody and #$IslandRegion.   Each instance of #$Island is a body of land surrounded by water.   #$Islands are typically much smaller in area than (instances of  the similarly-defined collection of) #$TrueContinents (q.v.), though it would be rather arbitrary to try to distinguish these  types on the basis of size alone.  (And note that  #$ContinentOfAustralia is both an #$IslandRegion and a #$TrueContinent.)  For groups of #$Islands that form a geographical cluster, see #$Archipelago.")
(comment IslandRegionTypeByPlurality "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$IslandRegionTypeByPlurality is a specialization of #$IslandRegion whose instances all consist of the same \"plurality\" of whole #$Islands, where that might be either <i>none</i>, <i>one<i>, or <i>more-than-one</i>.")
(comment IslandRegion "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$IslandRegion is either an island, a group of islands (such as an #$Archipelago), or an area that includes part but not all of an island (see #$IslandSubregion).  
<p>
Note that the instances of #$IslandRegion include certain groups of scattered islands that do not form a natural geographical area, but which are geopolitical areas and/or were historically significant. For example, #$FrenchPolynesia, #$Cyclades, #$OuterHebrides-Archipelago, and #$Grenadines-Islands.")
(comment IslandSubregion "A subcollection of #$IslandRegion. Each instance of #$IslandSubregion is an area (typically a #$GeopoliticalRegion and/or a region of historic importance) that is a proper subregion of an #$Island.  Examples include the geographic territory of #$England and the territory of the #$DominicanRepublic.")
(comment Archipelago "A specialization of #$IslandRegion.  Each instance of #$Archipelago is a group of many islands that form a geographical cluster.  Examples include #$Ionia, #$InnerHebrides-Archipelago, #$SocietyIslands-FrenchPolynesia, and #$ChannelIslands.")
(comment genls-GenlDenotesSpecInstances "A instance of #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections (q.v.) and a specialization of #$generalizations. #$genls-GenlDenotesSpecInstances relates a given #$SubLExpressionType collection (see #$Collection) to those collections whose instances it denotes. More precisely, (#$genls-GenlDenotesSpecInstances SUBCOL SUPERCOL) means that SUBCOL is a quoted subcollection of SUPERCOL: anything that is an instance of (see #$quotedIsa) SUBCOL is a quoted instance (see #$isa) of SUPERCOL.")
(comment Mosque-Building "A specialization of #$Temple that is associated with the practice of #$Islam.  Each instance of #$Mosque-Building is a religious building where Islamic services and activities are held.")
(comment Synagogue-Building "A specialization of #$Temple that is associated with the practice of #$Judaism.  Each instance of #$Synagogue-Building is a religious building where Jewish services and activities are held.")
(comment Church-Building "A specialization of #$Temple that is associated with the practice of #$Christianity.  Each instance of #$Church-Building is a religious building where Christian services and activities are held.")
(comment predicateGAFGatheringMethod "(#$predicateGAFGatheringMethod PRED (#$SubLQuoteFn :METHOD)) means that METHOD represents the GAF-gathering method that should be used to complete a partially bound GAF that has PRED as its arg0.")
(comment assertionNthArgBindingDerivedFromString "An expression of the form (#$assertionNthArgBindingDerivedFromString ASSERT N STRING) relates an instance of #$CycLAssertion to an instance of #$SubLString. The #$SubLString was instrumental in providing the binding value for the Nth argument in the #$CycLAssertion.")
(comment Peninsula "A specialization of #$Promontory.  Each instance of #$Peninsula is a protuberance of a geographic land region into water region. The length of a peninsula exceeds the width of its connecting neck.")
(comment MobFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  When applied to any collection <code>COL</code> that is both an instance of #$ObjectType and a specialization of #$TemporalThing, #$MobFn returns a subcollection of #$Mob, namely the collection of those mobs whose #$groupMembers all are instances of <code>COL</code>.  
<p>
For example, a clump of hair on a person's head is an instance of (#$MobFn #$Hair-Strand); the collection #$Galaxy is a specialization of (#$MobFn #$Star); and the collection #$Applauding-Clapping (i.e. all applause events) could be identified with (#$MobFn \"TwoHandsClappingEvent\").")
(comment RockFormation "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion whose instances consist of large rocks or voluminous groups of rocks in close proximity.  An instance of #$RockFormation can be either a naturally-occuring rock outcropping or a human-made rock formation.")
(comment SolidTangibleArtifact "A specialization of both #$Artifact and #$SolidTangibleThing .  Each instance of #$SolidTangibleArtifact is a partially tangible artifact that is also a solid.  Instances include both solid objects (for example, instances of the collection #$Automobile) as well as portions of stuff in solid form (for example, instances of the collection #$Cloth).")
(comment Pillar-Topographical "An instance of #$LandTopographicalFeature and a specialization of both #$RockFormation and #$StoneObject-Natural.  Each instance of  #$Pillar-Topographical is a vertical, standing, often spire-shaped, natural rock formation, consisting of a single large rock.")
(disjointWith Hat Helmet)
(disjointWith Helmet Cap)
(disjointWith Cap CowboyHat)
(disjointWith Hat Skullcap)
(disjointWith Sombrero Cap)
(disjointWith Hat SwimmingCap)
(disjointWith Turban Hat)
(disjointWith Cap WitchHat)
(comment Badlands-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.   Each instance of #$Badlands-Topographical is a region characterized by a maze of very closely-spaced, deep, narrow, steep-sided ravines; sharp crests; and pinnacles.")
(disjointWith Flat-Topographical Badlands-Topographical)
(comment Cairn "A specialization of #$StationaryArtifact and #$HumanAccessibleIBO.  Each instance of #$Cairn is a mound of stones piled up as a memorial (and thus is a #$SymbolicObject) or to mark a boundary (see #$BoundaryMarker) or path.")
(comment CycLVariable "The collection of all variables in the CycL language.
A #$CycLVariable is a character string consisting of a question mark '?' (as its initial character) followed by one or more characters, where each of these latter characters is either an (upper- or lower-case) Roman letter, an Arabic numeral (i.e. '0' through '9'), a hyphen ('-'), an underscore ('_'), or another question mark.  (The letters occurring in a CycL variable used in an actual assertion will typically be all uppercase; but this convention is not enforced in any formal way.)  Examples: '?WHAT', '?OBJ-TYPE', and '?var0'.")
(comment thereExistExactly "An #$ExistentialQuantifier (q.v.) and a specialization of both #$thereExistAtLeast and  #$thereExistAtMost (qq.v.).  Compared to the standard existential quantifier #$thereExists, #$thereExistExactly has an extra argument-place for specifying exactly how many things satisfy a given condition (specified by an #$CycLSentence-Assertible, in which the also-specified #$CycLVariable will typically occur free).  A closed sentence of the form <code>(#$thereExistExactly NUM VAR SENT)</code> means that there are exactly<code> NUM</code> distinct things that satisfy <code>SENT</code>, i.e. that render <code>SENT</code> true when taken as the value of <code>VAR</code>.  For example, the sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$thereExistExactly 7 ?X (#$isa ?X #$Sea))
</pre>
<p>
means that there are exactly seven seas.
<p>
In the degenerate case where <code>VAR</code> does not appear free in <code>SENT</code>, then <code>(#$thereExistExactly NUM VAR SENT)</code> is equivalent to <code>SENT</code>.")
(comment adoptedChildren "(#$adoptedChildren PERSON CHILD) means that PERSON is CHILD's parent, but not a biological one.  In many societies, e.g. modern western ones, PERSON and CHILD must have gone through the official legal process of #$Adoption in order to enter into the #$adoptedChildren relationship.")
(comment aunts "(#$aunts PERSON AUNT) means that AUNT is one of PERSON's aunts, i.e., either a sister of one of PERSON's parents, or the wife of a sibling of one of PERSON's parents.")
(comment uncles "(#$uncles PERSON UNCLE) means that UNCLE is one of PERSON's uncles, i.e., either a brother of one of PERSON's parents, or the husband of a sibling of one of PERSON's parents.")
(comment siblings "(#$siblings ANIMAL-1 ANIMAL-2) means that the #$Animal ANIMAL-1 and the #$Animal ANIMAL-2 are siblings; that is, either they share one or more biological parents (which is always the case if ANIMAL-1 and ANIMAL-2 are #$NonHumanAnimals), or they are siblings by adoption, marriage, or some other social arrangement.  Human siblings are generally (but not always) reasonably close in age (within 15 years or so), and generally (but not always) have grown up together in the same family.")
(comment twins-Identical "(#$twins-Identical ANIMAL1 ANIMAL2) means that ANIMAL1 and ANIMAL2 are identical twins, meaning the are twins that arose from the same zygote.  As a consequence, they are very nearly genetically identical, and thus, apart from differences induced by the environment, will have the same appearance, will sound the same when speaking or vocalizing, will smell the same, etc.")
(comment twins "(#$twins ANIMAL1 ANIMAL2) means that ANIMAL1 and ANIMAL2 are twins, meaning two animals who shared their mother's womb with no third #$Embryo.  Twins are #$siblings, and are very close to the same age. Identical twins arose from the same zygote, while fraternal twins came from different eggs.")
(comment twins-Fraternal "(#$twins-Fraternal ANIMAL1 ANIMAL2) means that ANIMAL1 and ANIMAL2 are fraternal twins, meaning they are twins that came from different eggs and different sperm.  Fraternal twins always always share the same mother, and probably typically share the same father, but it is possible for fraternal twins to have different fathers.  This happens more often with #$NonHumanAnimals.")
(comment stepchildren "(#$stepchildren PARENT STEP-CHILD) holds just in case PARENT is married to a biological or adoptive parent of STEP-CHILD, and the marriage began after STEP-CHILD was born or was adopted.")
(comment Shack "A specialization of #$Building.  Each instance of #$Shack is a very small shelter from the elements, which might or might not be a residence.  Shacks are simple constructions, typically having just one room.")
(disjointWith Shack House-Country)
(disjointWith Tower Shack)
(disjointWith Villa Shack)
(disjointWith Gazebo Shack)
(comment AbandonedCamp "A specialization of #$Campsite and #$Abandoned-ExistentialStatus whose instances are abandoned encampments.")
(comment Yard-BuildingGrounds "A specialization of #$OutdoorLocation and #$Ground.  Each instance of #$Yard-BuildingGrounds is an (at least mostly) unpaved outdoor space around a building, owned by the owner(s) of that building.  For example, the #$LotOfLand on which a house sits is a #$Yard-BuildingGrounds; and certain parts of the lot -- e.g. house's #$Backyard -- might also be instances of #$Yard-BuildingGrounds.")
(comment ArgTypeBinaryPredicate "Those instances of both #$BinaryPredicate and #$ArgTypePredicate used to specify the required #$isa or #$genls or #$quotedIsa of an argument of a #$Relation; the arg1 is the #$Relation; the arg2 is the required type, a #$Collection.")
(comment ArgTypeTernaryPredicate "Those instances of both #$TernaryPredicate and #$ArgTypePredicate used to specify the required #$isa or #$genls or #$quotedIsa of an argument of a #$Relation; the arg1 is the Relation; the arg2 is the argument constrained; the arg3 is the required type: a #$Collection.")
(comment adoptiveFather "(#$adoptiveFather CHILD MAN) says that MAN is the #$father of CHILD, yet is not CHILD's #$biologicalFather. Depending on the society to which they belong, MAN and CHILD may have gone through an official legal #$Adoption process to enter this relationship.")
(comment adoptiveMother "(#$adoptiveMother CHILD WOMAN) says that WOMAN is the   #$mother of CHILD, yet is not the #$biologicalMother of CHILD.  Depending on the society to which they belong, WOMAN and CHILD may have been through an official legal process of #$Adoption to enter this relationship.")
(comment Family-Human "Each instance of #$Family-Human is a loose #$Organization consiting of human beings who are related to each other by birth, marriage,  adoption or other social arrangements.  Whether someone related in a particulary way is considered ''part of the family'' likely varies depending on the society in which the family exists, and may have a very fuzzy edge.  Traditional families center around a couple, consisting of a man and a woman, and their children. (Secular Western cultures (and probably others, too) admit families which are headed only by a father, or only by a mother.  They may admit families headed by a same-gender couple.)  Relatives who are direct ancestors or descendents are usually considered part of the core family: (great) grandparents, grandchildren.  Other people related by blood or marriage to these core family members are typically also considered to be part of a family, at least in modern Western culture: #$aunts, #$uncles, #$cousins, and so on.  The greater the number of blood/marriage/adoption relation steps one individual is away from another, the more distantly related those individuals are and the less likely they would consider the other to be part of their family.  Societies differ in how closely related individuals need to be to be considered part of the same family.  Human families typically have a functional role in society; core members often live together and pool resources.  The family to which one belongs can, in many societies, dictate what opportunities a person has during their life.")
(comment familyHasMember "(#$familyHasMember FAM MEM) means that MEM is a member of the family, FAM.  In the case of instances of #$Family-Human, family membership is by consensus of the involved people and is affected by birth, marriage, adoption, and possibly by the establishment of other social relationships. In the case of non-human animals, family membership is assessed by human observers (usually biologists) on the basis of blood relationship only.")
(comment RailroadGun "A form of super-heavy artillery mounted on railroads.")
(comment Dora-RailroadGun "A massive German #$RailroadGun nicknamed 'Dora'.  One was
used by the Germans during the siege of Sebastopol in June of 1942.")
(comment Darla-RailroadGun "A fictional #$RailroadGun similar to #$Dora-RailroadGun.")
(comment ANSPS-49-Radar "The AN/SPS-49 Very Long-Range Air Surveillance Radar is a radar used to provide long-range
target position location information to a surface ship, such as an AEGIS Cruiser")
(comment grandchildren "An instance of #$FamilyRelationSlot.  (#$grandchildren OLDER YOUNGER) means that YOUNGER is a child (see the predicate #$children) of a child of OLDER. Among #$NonHumanAnimals, only biological descent determines this relationship.  However, among humans, because the #$children relationship can be established by other social arrangements (#$Adoption, fostering,...), there may not exist a direct line of descent from a person to their #$grandchildren.")
(comment granddaughters "(#$granddaughters OLDER YOUNGER) means YOUNGER is the granddaughter (female child of one of the children) of OLDER.  Among #$NonHumanAnimals, this relationship is established strictly on the basis of biological descent and gender.   However, among humans, because the #$children relationship can be established through other social arrangements (#$Adoption, fostering, marriage), there may not exist a direct line of descent from a person to their #$granddaughters.")
(comment grandsons "(#$grandsons OLDER YOUNGER) means YOUNGER is the grandson (male child of one of the children) of OLDER.  Among #$NonHumanAnimals, this relationship is established strictly on the basis of biological descent and gender. However, among humans the #$children relationship can be established though other social arrangements (e.g., #$Adoption, fostering, marriage), so there may not exist a strict line of descent from a person to their #$grandsons.")
(comment TalentScout "A #$PersonTypeByActivity and #$PersonTypeByPositionInOrg.  Each instance of #$TalentScout is a #$Person who seeks out talented potential recruits for some #$Organization, e.g. a #$SportsTeam or #$Business.")
(comment Ditch "A specialization of #$Excavation-Hole.  Each instance of #$Ditch is a long, narrow channel dug into the soil.")
(comment DrainageCanal "A specialization of both #$Canal and #$FlowPath.  Each instance of #$DrainageCanal is an artificial waterway carrying water away from a wetland or from drainage ditches.")
(comment NavigationCanal "A specialization of both #$Canal and #$Channel-BodyOfWaterLink.  Each instance of #$NavigationCanal is a watercourse constructed for navigation of vessels.  It is disjoint with both #$IrrigationCanal and #$DrainageCanal.")
(comment DrainageDitch "A specialization of #$Ditch whose instances are intended as channels for draining water.  If a drainage ditch actually has water running through it, said water constitutes an instance of #$DrainageCanal (q.v.).")
(comment IrrigationDitch "A specialization of #$Ditch whose instances are intended as channels for #$Irrigation.  If a irrigation ditch actually has water running through it, that water constitutes an instance of #$IrrigationCanal (q.v.).")
(comment GovernmentRelatedEntity "This is a very general collection of things that are, in some sense, \"government related\".  Positive examples include actual governments, government agencies, government contractors, etc.  Negative examples include Bob whose brother works for the government and anything that is not, in some sense, an agent.")
(comment spouse "(#$spouse PERSON1 PERSON2) means the two #$Persons PERSON1 and PERSON2 are married and committed to being permanent, lifelong partners.  Cultures vary in whether such a couple must be of opposite genders, or may possibly belong to the same gender.  Cultures also vary in whether a person is restricted to having only one cotemporal spouse.")
(comment relativesByMarriage "If (#$relativesByMarriage PER1 PER2), then PER1 and PER2 are part of the same family due to marriage.  Either PER1 is married to a biological or adoptive relative of PER2, or vice versa (in the case of #$inLaws), or close family members of PER1 and PER2 are married (in the case of step-siblings), or PER1 is married to PER2.")
(comment biologicalRelatives "<code>(#$biologicalRelatives ORG1 ORG2)</code> means that the #$Organism-Whole <code>ORG1</code> and the #$Organism-Whole <code>ORG2</code> are biological relatives, related by a #$BiologicalReproductionEvent; i.e., either one of them is descended from the other, or they share some parent, grandparent, or earlier ancenstor.")
(comment cousins "(#$cousins PERSON1 PERSON2) means that PERSON2 is a cousin of PERSON1.  I.e., PERSON2 is the child (cf. #$children) of a #$sibling of one of PERSON1's parents (#$mother or #$father).")
(comment grandmothers "A #$FamilyRelationSlot relating an #$Animal to his or her grandmothers.  If (#$grandmothers ANIM GM), then GM is the female parent (#$mother) of one of the parents of ANIM.  In the case of #$NonHumanAnimals, there is a line of direct biological descent from GM to ANIM.  However, in the case of humans, because the #$children relationship can also arise due to a variety of social arrangements in addition to birth (e.g., #$Adoption, marriage, fostering), a person and their grandmothers may not be biologically related.")
(comment uncles-aunts "(#$uncles-aunts PERSON UNCLE-AUNT) means that UNCLE-AUNT is one of PERSON's uncles or aunts, i.e. either a brother or sister of one of PERSON's parents, or the spouse of one PERSON's parents' silblings.")
(comment nephews-nieces "(#$nephews-nieces PERSON NEPHEW-NIECE) holds just in case NEPHEW-NIECE is one of PERSON's nephews or nieces.  I.e., NEPHEW-NIECE is the child of one of PERSON's siblings, or the child of a sibling of PERSON's spouse.")
(comment brotherInLaw "If (#$brotherInLaw PERS BRO), then either PERS is married to someone who has BRO as one of his or her #$brothers, or BRO is married to one of PERS's #$siblings.")
(comment sisterInLaw "If (#$sisterInLaw PERS SIS), then either PERS is married to someone who has SIS as one of his or her #$sisters, or SIS is married to one of PERS's #$siblings.")
(comment daughterInLaw "If (#$daughterInLaw PARENT DAUGHT), then DAUGHT is married to one of the #$sons of PARENT.")
(comment sonInLaw "If (#$sonInLaw PARENT SON), then SON is married to one of the #$daughters of PARENT.")
(comment fatherInLaw "If (#$fatherInLaw ?PERS ?DAD), then ?DAD is the #$father  of ?PERS's spouse.")
(comment motherInLaw "If (#$motherInLaw ?PERS ?MOM), then ?MOM is the #$mother  of ?PERS's spouse.")
(comment motivesForAction "(#$motivesForAction ACTION PRED AGENT THING) means one of the motives behind AGENT's performance of ACTION is AGENT's relationship PRED to THING during some period leading up to ACTION.  For example, to express that AGENT's motive for committing a particular roberry is (his or her feelings of) greed, one can write (#$motivesForAction BankRobbery001 AGENT #$feelsEmotion (#$PositiveAmountFn Greed)).  Where THING is an instance of #$CycLSentence-Assertible and PRED is an instance of #$CognitivePredicate, use the more specific #$reasonsForAction.")
(comment resultGenlPreds "A #$MetaFunction and #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a #$PredicateDenotingFunction to a #$Predicate.  (#$resultGenlPreds FUNC PRED) means that the result of applying FUNC to any legitimate series of arguments is a specialization of PRED.  #$resultGenlPreds is to #$Predicates as #$resultGenl is to #$Collections.")
(comment TextualEntailmentProblem "These are tests which require a determination of whether or not some assertion is true on the basis of some particular background text. The predicates #$backgroundTextForTEP and #$queryTextForTEP can be used to specify the background text and query text, respectively.")
(comment taskTypeForTEP "(#$taskTypeForTEP TEP TASK) means that TYPE is the task type for the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment idNumberForTEP "(#$idNumberForTEP TEP NUM) means that NUM is the the id number for the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment expectedAnswerForTEP "(#$expectedAnswerForTEP TEP VAL) means that VAL is the expected answer for the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment QueryContentForTEPFn "(#$QueryContentForTEPFn TEP) is an evaluatable function which, for a given #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP, returns the #$CycLSentence-Askable which reflects (the disjunction of possible) cyclifier outputs for query string associated with TEP (also see #$queryTextForTEP).")
(comment queryTextForTEP "(#$queryTextForTEP TEP TEXT) means that TEXT is the text which should be taken as specifying the content to be queried when answering the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment backgroundTextForTEP "(#$backgroundTextForTEP TEP TEXT) means that TEXT is the text which should be taken as background material when answering the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment TextualEntailmentProblemSolvingContext "Instances of this collection are problem solving contexts for #$TextualEntailmentProblems.")
(comment TEPQueryContentFromStringFn "(#$TEPQueryContentFromStringFn STRING) is an evaluatable function which, for a given sentence STRING, returns the #$CycLSentence-Askable which reflects (the disjunction of possible) cyclifier outputs for STRING.")
(comment TEPPremiseContextFromStringFn "(QueryContextForTEPFn STRING) is an evaluatable function which, for a given sentence STRING representing the premise text for some textual entailment problem (c.f. #$TextualEntailmentProblem),returns a #$TextualEntailmentProblemSolvingContext which has been updated with the possible cyclified contents of STRING.")
(comment QueryContextForTEPFn "(#$QueryContextForTEPFn TEP) is an evaluatable function which, for a given #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP, will return a #$TextualEntailmentProblemSolvingContext for that problem (cf. #$queryingContextForTEP).")
(comment queryingContextForTEP "(#$queryingContextForTEP TEP MT) means that MT, a #$TextualEntailmentProblemSolvingContext, is the context in which background content should be asserted and queries should be asked for the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment Lighthouse "A specialization of #$NavigationBeacon and #$Tower.  Each instance of #$Lighthouse is a tall structure topped by a powerful light used as a beacon or signal to aid marine navigation.")
(comment actionMotives "(#$actionMotives ACTION MOTIVE) identifies MOTIVE as one of the instances of #$IntentionalMentalState that led the performer of ACTION to carry ACTION out.")
(comment Ridge-Hill "A specialization of both #$Ridge and #$Hill.  Each instance of #$Ridge-Hill is a hill with a linearly extended crest and two slopes to either side.  The top of a #$Ridge-Hill, i.e. the vertex of the two slopes, is an instance of #$RidgeLine-Topographical.")
(comment Ridge "This is the collection of all ridges that stick out from other objects.  More precisely, each instance of #$Ridge is a long and thin, bilaterally symmetrical, external part of some larger tangible object.  A swatch of corduroy, the edge of a dime, and a Ruffles brand potato chip all have ridges as parts.
<p>
For those ridges that are #$TopographicalFeatures, see the specialization #$Ridge-Hill.  And for the top of such a ridge, see #$RidgeLine-Topographical.")
(comment RidgeLine-Topographical "A specialization of #$TopographicalFeature and #$EdgeOnObject.  Each instance of #$RidgeLine-Topographical is the top edge of a #$Ridge-Hill (q.v.).  A ridgeline is a vertex of two or more adjoining slopes of terrain from which (if the ridge on on dry land) water begins to flow in opposite direction.")
(comment Driveway "A specialization of #$PathForWheeledVehicles.  A #$Driveway is a relatively short path that typically connects a #$Roadway to some point on a #$LotOfLand near a building (such as a house or apartment building), where vehicles may be parked.")
(comment Farmhouse "A #$Farmhouse is a #$HumanResidence building located on a farm.  It is where the farmers live.")
(comment UnderwaterLocation "A specialization of #$Underwater and #$Place-NonAgent.  #$UnderwaterLocation is the collection of all underwater spaces.")
(comment Mountain "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$Mountain is a topographical feature of significantly higher elevation than its surrounding area.  Mountains may occur individually or as part of a chain (see #$MountainRange).  Instances of #$Mountain include #$MountEverest, #$MountLogan, and #$Kebnekaise-Sweden.
<p>
See also #$MountainRange.")
(comment LandTopographicalFeature "A collection of geographical regions  and a specialization of #$TopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$LandTopographicalFeature is a region of land distinguishable  from surrounding regions by salient physical differences in surface texture, three-dimensional shape, or elevation.  Note that this collection <i>includes</i> features that are covered by bodies of water, snow, or ice.  
<p>
Specializations of  #$LandTopographicalFeature include #$Mountain, #$Crater, and #$Continent. Instances include the #$ArabianPeninsula, #$MountZion, the #$SanAndreasFault,  the #$GreatSmokyMountains, and the #$JavaTrench.")
(comment TopographicalFeature "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion.  Each instance of #$TopographicalFeature is a three-dimensional feature of a planet's surface, typically with boundaries defined by formations of rock, dirt, water, etc., or by significant changes in elevation.  
<p>
Some important specializations of #$TopographicalFeature include #$Mountain, #$MountainRange, #$Peninsula, #$Harbor, and #$Shoreline. Instances include #$KyushuIsland-Japan, #$SaintThomas-Island, #$LakeErie, #$VictoriaFalls, and #$MalayPeninsula.
<p>
Note that collections of regions defined by the presence of human artifacts (e.g. #$CitySkyline) or ecological characteristics (e.g. #$Forest) are <i>not</i> specializations of #$TopographicalFeature.")
(comment Gorge "A specialization of #$Crevice-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Gorge is a deep, narrow, steep-walled passage.  Also known as a <i>ravine</i>.")
(disjointWith LandBody Valley)
(comment Valley "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature, #$CavityWithWalls, and #$InanimateObject-Natural. Each instance of #$Valley is of significantly lower altitude than the area surrounding it. A #$Valley is distinguished from a #$Basin by having sharper sides, and typically has #$Mountains (or smaller elevations) surrounding it.")
(comment Downtown "A specialization of #$UrbanArea.  Instances of #$Downtown are the centers, most densely urbanized areas, or central business districts of a city or town.  In large cities downtowns tend to have large buildings and even skyscrapers.  In the downtowns of small towns, there are fewer and smaller buildings.")
(comment Town "A subcollection of #$City whose instances have populations larger than those of #$Villages (q.v.) but smaller than those typically referred to as \"cities\".")
(comment Beach "A specialization of #$Shoreline.  Each instance of #$Beach is a sandy or pebbly region along an edge of a large body of water such as a #$River, #$Lake, or #$Sea.  See the specialization #$SandyBeach.")
(comment Shoreline "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature. Each instance of #$Shoreline is a region along an edge of a #$BodyOfWater.  
<p>
Specializations of #$Shoreline include #$Beach, #$Seacoast, and #$Riverbank.")
(comment Seacoast "A specialization of #$Shoreline.  Each instance of #$Seacoast is the edge of a land mass where it adjoins a sea or ocean.  A seacoast might consist of a #$Beach, tidal flats, rocks, or glaciers.")
(disjointWith Seacoast Riverbank)
(comment Promontory "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$Protrusion.  Each instance of #$Promontory is a protrusion of #$LandStuff that is elevated above and juts out into a #$BodyOfWater or #$Flat-Topographical.  A notable specialization is #$Peninsula.")
(disjointWith Seacoast Streambank)
(comment Streambank "A specialization of both #$Shoreline and #$Bank-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Streambank is a region alongside the edge of a #$Stream.")
(comment Riverbank "A specialization of #$Streambank (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Riverbank is a bank at the edge of a river.")
(comment Bank-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$Bank-Topographical is an elevation, either at the edge of a stream (see #$Streambank) or underwater (see #$Shoal).")
(comment MilitarySite-Historical "A specialization of #$HistoricalSite.  A #$MilitarySite-Historical is a facility or region formerly used for various aspects of or relating to military activity, and which is currently considered to be of historical interest.  Examples include historical battlefields, decomissioned military bases, and shipyards.")
(comment taxonomicCousinCollections "An assertion of the form (#$taxonomicCousinCollections COLL1 COLL2) states that the instances of #$Collection COLL1 and COLL2 are such that (1) they share a common generalization that is a sub-collection of #$Thing, (2) they are not disjoint, and  (3) neither bears the relation #$genls to the other.  This is a more general relationship than #$taxonomicSiblingCollections.  This predicate is useful for determining whether two collections can share common instances.")
(comment Crater "A specialization of #$Basin-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Crater is a bowl-shaped depression on the surface of a planet, either (i) at the mouth of a volcano (see e.g. #$Caldera) or geyser or (ii) caused by the impact of a body such as a meteroid (see #$ImpactCrater).")
(comment ImpactCrater "A specialization of #$Crater.  Each instance of #$ImpactCrater is a crater on a planetary body formed by the impact of another body.  Typically, the other body is a meteor or asteroid; but it could be an artifact such as a bomb.")
(disjointWith ImpactCrater Caldera)
(comment Creek "A specialization of #$Stream.  Each instance of #$Creek is a natural stream of water with a comparatively smaller amount of flow than a #$River.  (Note that the distinction between a creek and a river is difficult to specify with any precision.)")
(comment Rapids "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$Rapids is a part of a stream where the water flows unusually fast and (typically) is broken by obstructions such as large rocks.")
(comment TidalCreek "A specialization of #$Stream.  Each instance of #$TidalCreek is a meandering channel in a coastal wetland subject to
bi-directional tidal currents. Cf. #$Creek.")
(comment Channel-WaterPath "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater and #$Path-Spatial.  Each instance of #$Channel-WaterPath is a long and narrow watery region that either (i) is itself a #$BodyOfWater that connects two larger bodies of water (see #$Channel-BodyOfWaterLink) or (ii) is a deeper, navigable part of a body of water that is not navigable all-over (see #$Channel-DeeperWaterwayPart).")
(comment Strait "A specialization of #$Channel-BodyOfWaterLink.  Each instance of #$Strait is a naturally-occurring narrow channel that connects two larger bodies of water.")
(disjointWith ClearWeatherArea StormyArea)
(comment CompletelyCloudCoveredArea "A instance of #$DegreeOfCloudiness and a specialization of #$CloudyArea. Each instance of #$CompletelyCloudCoveredArea is an #$OutdoorLocation that is so clouded-over with a uniform layer of grey or white clouds that direct sunlight is blocked out.  There are no shadows, and the shape or exact location of the sun or moon is impossible to identify.")
(comment BalingStation "A specialization of #$AgriculturalImplement and #$FixedStructure.  each instance of #$BalingStation is a facility for baling agricultural products.")
(comment ApartmentBuilding "A specialization of #$ModernHumanResidence.  Each instance of #$ApartmentBuilding is a building that consists of a number of #$ApartmentUnits.")
(comment Foundry-Building "A specialization of both #$Building and #$Workplace.  Each instance of #$Foundry-Building is a building where metal
casting is done.")
(comment Field-Land "A specialization of #$FlatRegion and #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$Field-Land is a plot of ground covered by low vegetation, either naturally-occurring or cultivated (see #$Field-Agricultural).")
(comment Field-Agricultural "A specialization of #$Field-Land and #$ArableLand.  Each instance of #$Field-Agricultural is a field in which crops are grown.")
(comment Pasture "A specialization of #$Field-Land and #$Grassland.  Each instance of #$Pasture is a field where animals are allowed to graze.")
(disjointWith Plain-Topographical Swamp)
(disjointWith Beach Plain-Topographical)
(disjointWith Field-Land Forest)
(comment Gravesite "A specialization of #$FixedStructure and #$StorageConstruct.  Each instance of #$Gravesite is a place which has been used or is intended to be used as a place to store the remains of one or more dead humans.  Specializations of #$Gravesite include #$Graveyard and #$Tomb.")
(comment Tomb "A specialization of #$Gravesite.  The collection of all tombs: structures in which (or under which) the dead bodies or other remains of persons are deposited.  A #$Tomb acts as a #$MemorialMarker of the dead within.  Note: A #$MemorialMarker or #$Monument in which (or under which) the honored person was never buried or deposited is not a #$Tomb. See also #$TombOfFn.")
(comment Grave "A specialization of #$Gravesite.  Each instance of #$Grave is a site where a dead human is buried.  Some graves are located in #$Graveyards.")
(comment Graveyard "A specialization of #$Gravesite.  Each instance of #$Graveyard is a plot of land containing a number of #$Graves of people.")
(comment geographicalSuperRegionTypes "(#$geographicalSuperRegionTypes SUPERREGION-TYPE REGION-TYPE) means that every geographical region, or feature that is an instance of the collection REGION-TYPE, is a subregion of at least one geographical region (via #$geographicalSubRegions) which is an instance of the collection SUPERREGION-TYPE.  For example, ''Every forest clearing is part of some forest'' may be expressed as (#$geographicalSuperRegionTypes #$Forest #$ForestClearing).")
(comment Caldera "A specialization of #$Depression-Topographical.  A #$Caldera is a large depression (typically measuring several kilometers across) formed by the
collapse of a volcanic mountain or by a volcanic explosion.")
(comment HotelBuilding "Each instance of #$HotelBuilding is a #$Building that consists primarily of a large number of #$HotelRoomUnits (q.v.), which are rented out on a short-term (e.g. nightly) basis.")
(comment State-UnitedStates "A specialization of #$State-Geopolitical.  Each instance of #$State-UnitedStates is one of the states of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica.  Instances include #$Alabama-State, #$Alaska-State, #$Arizona-State, and #$Arkansas-State.")
(comment State-Geopolitical "A specialization of #$CountrySubsidiary.  Each instance of #$State-Geopolitical is a geopolitical entity whose government is at the very next administrative level down from the national level.  In different countries, instances of #$State-Geopolitical may be called \"regions\", \"states\",  \"provinces\", \"prefectures\", \"departments\", \"parishes\", or something else.  In the U.S.A., instances are called \"states\" (see #$State-UnitedStates).  See also #$Province.")
(comment UnionTerritory-India "A specialization of #$CountrySubsidiary.  This is the collection of all the union territories of the nation of #$India. A union territory is an administrative subdivision controlled directly by the national government, as opposed to states (see #$State-India), which have their own local governments.")
(comment Fill-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$ManMadeThing.  Each instance of #$Fill-Topographical is a built-up piece of land.")
(comment AircraftHangar "A specialization #$Building and #$VehicleStorageConstruct.  Each instance of #$AircraftHangar is a building designed to contain (or partially contain) aircraft, in order to protect them from the elements.")
(comment AmusementPark "A specialization of #$OutdoorRecreationArea.  This is the collection of all amusement parks.  An amusement park can be as elaborate and permanent as #$Disneyworld-TouristAttraction or as makeshift and transitory as amusement parks whose facilities are moved from location to location during the summer.")
(comment GolfCourse "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea.  Each instance of #$GolfCourse is an grass-covered outdoor area used for playing #$Golf.")
(comment Campground "A specialization of #$Campsite and #$OutdoorRecreationArea.  Each instance of #$Campground is an outdoor area intended for #$Camping and designed to accommodate multiple parties.")
(disjointWith Province City)
(comment predicateFormulaTemplates "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a #$Predicate to a #$FormulaTemplate.  (#$predicateFormulaTemplates PRED TEMPLATE) means that PRED occurs in the formula for TEMPLATE (see #$formulaForFormulaTemplate).")
(comment MumpsVirus "The specialization of #$Virus that causes #$Mumps in #$HomoSapiens.")
(comment stepRelatives "If (#$stepRelatives PER1 PER2), then PER2 is PER1's #$stepmother or #$stepfather, one of PER1's #$stepchildren or #$stepsiblings, or vice-versa. A #$stepRelatives relationship is established when someone who is a parent marries a person different from the parent of their existing child or children.")
(comment stepbrother "If (#$stepbrother PER1 PER2), then one of PER2's parents and one of PER1's parents are married, their marriage began after at least one of PER1 and PER2 were already born, PER1 and PER2 do not share any biological parents, and PER2 is a #$MaleHuman.")
(comment stepsiblings "If (#$stepsiblings PER1 PER2), then one of PER2's parents and one of PER1's parents are married, their marriage began after at least one of PER1 and PER2 were already born to, or adopted by, their non-step parent, and PER1 and PER2 do not share any biological parents.")
(comment stepsister "If (#$stepsister PER1 PER2), then one of PER2's parents and one of PER1's parents are married, their marriage began after at least one of PER1 and PER2 were already born, PER1 and PER2 do not share any biological parents, and PER2 is a #$FemaleHuman.")
(comment stepdaughters "(#$stepdaughters PER GIRL) means that GIRL is one of the #$stepchildren of PER, and is female.  This relationship was established when PER married one of GIRL's parents.")
(comment stepsons "(#$stepsons PER BOY) means that BOY is one of the #$stepchildren of PER, and is male.  This relationship was established when PER married one of the parents of BOY.")
(comment agentsActionHasMotive "An instance of #$TernaryPredicate that relates an agent and one of his or her actions to one of the motives behind that action.  (#$agentsActionHasMotives AGENT ACTION MOTIVE) identifies MOTIVE as one of the instances of #$IntentionalMentalState that motivated AGENT to perform (c.f., #$performedBy) the action (c.f., #$Action) ACTION.  See also #$motivesForAction.")
(comment stepfather "(#$stepfather CHILD FATHER) holds just in case FATHER is the step father of CHILD.  That is, just in case:
  <pre>   
    (#$and
     (#$stepchildren FATHER CHILD)
     (#$isa FATHER #$AdultMaleHuman))
  </pre>
This relationship was established when one of the parents of CHILD married FATHER, after CHILD had already been born or adopted.")
(comment stepmother "(#$stepmother CHILD MOTHER) holds just in case MOTHER is the step mother of CHILD.  That is, just in case:
  <pre>
    (#$and
     (#$stepchildren MOTHER CHILD)
     (#$isa MOTHER #$AdultFemaleHuman))
  </pre>
This relationship was established when one of the parents of CHILD married MOTHER,  after CHILD was already born or adopted by his or her parent.")
(comment Cirque "A specialization of #$Basin-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Cirque is a bowl-like hollow partially surrounded by cliffs or steep slopes at the head of a glaciated valley.")
(comment greaterOrEqualUnit "A symmetric #$OrderingPredicate and specialization of #$interconvertibleUnits (q.v.). <code>(#$greaterOrEqualUnit UNIT1 UNIT2)</code> means that the units of measure (see #$UnitOfMeasure) <code>UNIT1</code> and <code>UNIT2</code> are interconvertible units such that <code>UNIT1</code> is at least as large as <code>UNIT2</code>.  This implies that there is some #$Real1-Infinity <code>N</code> such that <code>(#$unitMultiplicationFactor UNIT1 UNIT2 N)</code> holds.
<p>
For example, (#$greaterOrEqualUnit #$Meter #$Inch) and (#$greaterOrEqualUnit #$CubicCentimeter (#$Milli #$Liter)) both hold, but (#$greaterOrEqualUnit #$Inch #$Meter) does not.
<p>
For a similar predicate that compares the sizes of measurable quantities directly, see #$greaterThanOrEqualTo.  See also #$greaterUnit, a specialization of #$greaterOrEqualUnit.")
(comment Overpass "A specialization of #$PathForWheeledVehicles and #$Bridge.  When two wheeled-vehicle paths cross in such a way that they do not join or intersect each other, but instead one path passes over the other, the portion of the former path that passes directly over the latter is an instance of #$Overpass.  The corresponding portion of the latter path is an instance of #$Underpass.")
(comment Underpass "A specialization of #$PathForWheeledVehicles.  When two wheeled-vehicle paths cross in such a way that they do not join or intersect each other, but instead one path passes under the other, the portion of the former path that passes directly under the latter is an instance of #$Underpass.  The corresponding portion of the latter path is an instance of #$Overpass, which is a type of #$Bridge.")
(comment Cutoff "A specialization of #$Channel-WaterPath.  Each instance of #$Cutoff is a relatively new channel that cuts across and joins two points of a meandering stream (e.g. across the neck of an oxbow).")
(comment RollingGrassland "A specialization of #$Grassland.  Each instance of #$RollingGrassland is a geographical region that is grassy and slightly hilly.")
(comment OilWell "A specialization #$Well.  This is the collection of all oil wells. Each instance of #$OilWell mainly consists of a hole in the ground that is used for extracting #$Petroleum-CrudeOil from some underground reservoir (cf. #$OilField). Most (if not all) #$OilWells are created by drilling (see #$DrillingSomething).  Many #$OilWells are equiped with a #$Pump-Mechanical because the underground reservoir is not pressurized enough to push up the oil.  And even though an oil field may have enough pressure at first, artificial lift devices are needed in later stages of production. ")
(comment Well "A specialization of #$FixedStructure and #$PhysicalDevice.  Each instance of #$Well is a device for extracting liquids, usually water or petroleum, out from a reservoir under the ground.  Generally, a well is a hole created by drilling or digging. Artificial lift devices (like #$Pump-Mechanical or #$Buckets) are also typically part of a well.")
(comment GasWell "A specialization of #$Well.  Each instance of #$GasWell is a well from which #$NaturalGas is extracted.")
(comment HinduTemple "The collection of all #$Temples dedicated to the religious practices of #$Hinduism.")
(comment BuddhistTemple "The collection of all #$Temples dedicated to the religious practices of #$Buddhism.")
(comment Bay "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$Bay is an inlet of a sea or lake, and is usually smaller than a #$Gulf.")
(comment FordInStream "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater and #$BidirectionalCustomaryPath.  Each instance of #$FordInStream is a shallow part of a stream, which can be crossed on foot or by land vehicle.")
(comment ConfluenceOfStreams "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater and #$JunctionOfPaths.  Each instance of #$ConfluenceOfStreams is a region where two or more #$Streams flow together.")
(comment FreshWaterLake "A specialization of #$Lake.  Each instance of #$FreshWaterLake is a freshwater land-locked body of water.  Examples include #$LakeMaracaibo, #$LakeWinnipeg, #$LakeTanganyika, #$LochNess-Lake, #$LakeErie, and #$LakeTahoe.  Note that inland saltwater lakes, commonly referred to as `seas', belong to the collection #$InlandSea (q.v.), rather than to #$FreshWaterLake.")
(comment InlandSea "A specialization of #$Lake.  Each instance of #$InlandSea is a large body of salt water (of second rank in relation to an #$Ocean) that is entirely, or almost entirely, land-locked.")
(comment Waterfall "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater and #$FlowPath.  each instance of #$Waterfall is a part of a stream where the water falls off over an edge.")
(comment Lagoon "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$Lagoon is a shallow body of water that is connected to a larger body of water, but separated from it (e.g. by sandbars or coral reefs).")
(comment ShippingLane "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater and #$WaterSurfacePath.  Each instance of #$ShippingLane is a customary path for shipping traffic, either internationally (see #$ImportExportEvent) or intranationally.  A shipping lane might or might not be contained within the territorial waters (see #$TerritorialSeaFn) of some instance of some #$GeopoliticalEntity.")
(comment Cut-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$InertArtifact.  Each instance of #$Cut-Topographical is a man-made feature resulting from cutting through raised ground, usually to form a level bed for a road or railroad track.")
(comment intentionalMentalSituationRelation "An intentional mental situation (c.f., #$IntentionalMentalSituation) is a situation in which an agent (c.f., #$Agent-Generic) experiences a mental state that is directed towards, or is \"about\" some object.  This \"aboutness\" is characterized through a relation between the subject and object, such as a belief relation (c.f., #$beliefs) or some other binary relation that defines propositional attitudes.  #$intentionalMentalSituationRelation is a predicate that relates intentional mental situations to the relation (or relations) that hold between the subject and object.  (#$intentionalMentalSituationRelation SITUATION RELATION) means that where AGENT is the subject and OBJECT is the object of SITUATION, SITUATION is such that (RELATION AGENT OBJECT) obtains.  For example, (#$intentionalMentalSituationRelation BobsHopeThatHeWillPassHisExam001 #$hopes) means that in the situation where Bob hopes that he will pass his exam, the #$hopes relation obtains between Bob and the proposition that Bob will pass.  See also #$situationIsSuchThat.")
(comment Basin-Topographical "A specialization of #$Depression-Topographical and #$Basin.  Each instance of #$Basin-Topographical is a bowl-shaped depression in the surface of the land or the ocean floor.")
(comment Depression-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$Depression-Topographical is a depression in a planet's surface, either on land or underwater. For bowl-shaped depressions, see the specialization #$Basin-Topographical.")
(comment Dune "A specialization of #$Hill and #$SandMob.  Each instance of #$Dune is a hill composed of #$Regolith sand grains.")
(disjointWith Dune Butte)
(disjointWith Mesa Dune)
(comment Foothill "A specialization of #$Hill.  Each instance of #$Foothill is a hill near the base of a mountain or mountain range.")
(comment Mesa "A specialization of #$Hill.  Each instance of #$Mesa is a hill with steep sides and a broad, flat top (see #$Plateau).  Mesas are typically found in arid climates.  See also #$Butte.")
(comment Summit "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$Protrusion.  Each instance of #$Summit is a prominent elevation above the surrounding level of the Earth's surface.  A summit is usually the top of a #$Mountain.")
(comment Plateau "A subcollection of #$Flat-Topographical. Each instance of #$Plateau is an elevated, level or nearly level portion of the earth's surface, larger in area than the #$Summit of a #$Mountain, and bounded on at least one side by steep slopes.  For example, the top surface of a #$Mesa is a plateau.  Plateaus are found both on dry land and on the ocean floor.")
(disjointWith Playa Plateau)
(comment Bench-Topographical "A specialization of #$Flat-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Bench-Topographical is an area of relatively level land on the flank of an elevation such as a hill, ridge, or mountain, such that the slope of the
land rises on one side and descends on the opposite side.  For example, a mountain terrace is a bench.")
(comment Playa "A specialization of #$Flat-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Playa is the floor of a desert basin with limited drainage.  A playa might become the bed of a shallow lake during the rainy season.")
(comment Butte "A specialization of #$Hill.  Each instance of #$Butte is a small, isolated, usually flat-topped hill with steep sides  See also #$Mesa.")
(comment SOEQuery "The collection of all instances of #$CycLQuerySpecification that are used to drive or test the development of the SOE ontology grapher.")
(comment orderForFormulaTemplateType "This predicate informs the fact entry tool how to order the individual #$FormulaTemplates within a single #$FormulaTemplateType. (#$orderForFormulaTemplateType TYPE (#$TheList TEMPLATE-1 TEMPLATE-2 ... TEMPLATE-N)) means that within TYPE, TEMPLATE-1 should appear first, TEMPLATE-2 second, and so on. It is critical that each instance of #$FormulaTemplate appearing in the list have a corresponding assertion of the form (#$formulaTemplateHasType TEMPLATE TYPE).")
(comment SomeExamplePredicateOfTypeFn "An #$IndeterminateTermDenotingFunction, #$PredicateDenotingFunction, and specialization (via #$genlFunctions) of #$SomeExampleFn.  #$SomeExamplePredicateOfTypeFn takes a #$PredicateType and returns an indeterminate instance of that type.  For example, (#$SomeExamplePredicateOfTypeFn #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate) denotes an indeterminate transitive binary predicate.")
(disjointWith Valley Pit-Topographical)
(comment Pit-Topographical "A specialization of #$Depression-Topographical and #$CavityWithWalls.  Each instance of #$Pit-Topographical is a significantly-sized cavity or hole in the ground with steep sides.")
(comment WaterTower "A specialization of #$Tower.  Each instance of  #$WaterTower is a tower for the storage of water.  Typically, a water tower consists of an elevated tank that serves as a reservoir and helps maintain equal pressure in a water system.")
(comment PhysicalCompositionPredicate "A specialization of both #$PartPredicate and #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate (qq.v.).  This is a collection of binary predicates that relate partially tangible things to their partially tangible parts or components, where those parts/components might be either spatially continuous or discrete.  As such, each physical-composition has both its argument-places constrained (see #$argIsa) to instances of #$PartiallyTangible or some subcollection thereof.  Each instance of #$PhysicalCompositionPredicate is a specialization (either via #$genlPreds or #$genlInverse) of the predicate #$physicalDecompositions (q.v.).")
(comment physicalParts-Separated "A proper #$PhysicalPartPredicate.  <code>(#$physicalParts-Separated WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>PART</code> is a detached part of the physical object <code>WHOLE</code>.  That is, <code>PART</code> does not touch any part of <code>WHOLE</code> other than (parts of) itself.  
<p>
For example, <code>(#$physicalParts-Separated #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Alaska-State)</code> holds.")
(comment Terrace-Topographical "A specialization of #$Bench-Topographical and #$Artifact.  Each instance of #$Terrace-Topographical is a human-made flat portion of the ground that is elevated somewhat.  It adjoins and is between two slopes: one descending from the terrace and the other ascending from it.  Terraces are often carved into the side of a hill or mountain, and used for planting crops.")
(comment Geyser "A specialization of #$HotSpring.  Each instance of #$Geyser is a spring that periodically emits steam and hot water.  The water has been heated to boiling underground by lava, and is then forced up through the hole in the ground.")
(comment TollRoad "A specialization of #$Roadway.  Each instance of #$TollRoad is a roadway that (most) users must pay a standard toll or fee to cross.  Some users, e.g. on-duty #$LawEnforcementOfficers, may be exempt.")
(comment Railway "A specialization of #$TransportFacility and #$PathForWheeledVehicles.  Each instance of #$Railway is a pathway made of railroad tracks and used for train transportation.  It includes main lines and sidings.")
(comment SeparatedPath "#$SeparatedPath is the collection of roads that have divided roadbeds and the distance between the centerlines of those roadbeds is at least 75 meters.")
(comment Trail "A specialization of #$PathArtifact.  Each instance of #$Trail is a relatively narrow, outdoor pathway, usually unpaved.  Trails are frequently found in wilderness or recreational areas, and are often intended for pedestrians, bicycles, and/or horseback riding.")
(comment Tunnel "A specialization of #$PathArtifact and #$Underground.  Each instance of #$Tunnel is a human-made pathway that goes underground.")
(comment Stairway "A specialization of #$PathArtifact and #$FixedStructure.  Each instance of #$Stairway is a series of steps or flights of steps for passing from one level to another.  A stairway has at least one step, and usually more.")
(comment LandBody "A specialization of #$LandMass.  Each instance of #$LandBody is a cohesive chunk of land surrounded by one or more bordering bodies of water.  Types of #$LandBody include the specializations #$Island and #$TrueContinent.  Instances include #$ContinentOfAustralia, #$Trinidad-Island, and #$Crete-Island-Greece.  Areas of land defined by human occupation or political boundaries, for example, #$NorthernCalifornia or #$NorthernIreland, are instances of #$GeographicalRegion, but not of #$LandBody.")
(comment PoliceBarrack "A specialization of #$PoliceFacility and #$Dormitory.  Each instance of #$PoliceBarrack is a building for lodging police personnel.")
(comment MilitaryBarrack "A specializaion of #$MilitaryFacility and #$Dormitory.  Each instance of #$MilitaryBarrack is a building for lodging military personnel.")
(comment ArchaeologicalSite "A specialization of #$Place and #$CulturalThing.  each instance of #$ArchaeologicalSite is a place where #$ArchaeologicalArtifacts (q.v.) are found.")
(comment PostalCodeRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion.  Each instance of #$PostalCodeRegion is the area corresponding to a particular #$PostalCode.")
(comment BoundaryMarker "A specialization of #$StationaryArtifact.  Each instance of #$BoundaryMarker is a fixture marking a point along a #$Border.")
(comment Oasis "A specialization of #$EcologicalRegion.  Each instance of #$Oasis is a small fertile region surrounded by a more arid #$Desert region.")
(comment Apron-Topographical "A specializaion of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Apron-Topographical is a gentle slope, with a generally smooth surface, found around groups of islands and seamounts.")
(comment Gap-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$Crevice.  Each instance of #$Gap-Topographical is a low point or opening between hills or mountains, or in a ridge, mountain range, cliff, or other topographical feature.")
(comment Gut-Topographical "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$Gut-Topographical is a relatively small coastal waterway connecting larger bodies of water or other waterways.")
(disjointWith BodyOfWater-Large LittoralRegion)
(disjointWith InternationalWaters LittoralRegion)
(comment LittoralRegion "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$LittoralRegion is a relatively narrow body of water that immediately borders on a #$Shoreline.  Littoral regions are often tangential (i.e. \"outer\") proper subregions of larger bodies of water.")
(comment SubwayStation "A specialization of #$FixedStructure and #$TransportFacility.  Each instance of #$SubwayStation is located at least partly underground, on subway tracks, and its primary purpose is to be a place where subway trains discharge and receive passengers.")
(comment StrictlyFunctionalSlot "A specialization of both #$FunctionalSlot and #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate (qq.v.) each instance of which is a binary  predicate that is strictly functional in its second argument position (see #$strictlyFunctionalInArgs).  
<p>
More precisely, a binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot if, for any legal occupant <code>THING1</code> of <code>PRED</code>'s first (or \"arg1\") argument position, there is at most one legal occupant <code>THING2</code> of <code>PRED</code>'s second (or \"arg2\") argument position such that <code>(PRED THING1 THING2)</code> holds.  #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot is thus the subcollection of the \"intersection\" of #$FunctionalSlot and #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate containing those predicates whose second argument (at least) is strictly functional.  
<p>
Examples of strictly functional slots include #$countryOfAddress and #$femaleParentActor.")
(comment bodyPartsUsed "An instance of #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$actorPartsInvolved and #$instrument-Generic.  (#$bodyPartsUsed ACTION BODYPART) means that the #$AnimalBodyPart BODYPART, an anatomical part of one of the doers of ACTION, is used (i.e. plays an instrumental role) in the #$PurposefulPhysicalAction ACTION.  For example, (#$bodyPartsUsed Kicking001 BobsRightFoot).")
(comment Stadium "A specialization of #$FixedStructure and #$HumanHighOccupancyConstruct.  Each instance of #$Stadium is a large structure that can hold thousands of people, and is typically used for sports events or music concerts.  Traditionally, stadiums are open-air structures; but some modern stadiums have roofs.")
(comment BusStation "A specialization of #$TransportFacility and #$HumanOccupationConstruct.  Each instance of #$BusStation is a structure, usually located in some city or town, whose primary purpose is to be a place where buses discharge and receive passengers.")
(comment orderForFormulaTemplateType "This predicate informs the fact entry tool how to order the individual #$FormulaTemplates withing a single #$FormulaTemplateType. (#$orderForFormulaTemplateType TYPE (#$TheList TEMPLATE-1 TEMPLATE-2 ... TEMPLATE-N)) means that within TYPE, TEMPLATE-1 should appear first, TEMPLATE-2 second, and so on. It is critical that each instance of #$FormulaTemplate appearing in the list have a corresponding assertion of the form (#$formulaTemplateHasType TEMPLATE TYPE).")
(comment UIListEditorInput "Instances of #$IUListEditorInput are specialized editors within a user interface that allow the user to view, create, or edit a #$List-Extensional.")
(comment PyramidMonument "A specialization of #$Monument.  Each instance of #$PyramidMonument is an (at least roughly) pyramid-shaped monument.  Examples include the pyramid of Teotihaucan in Mexico and the #$PyramidOfCheops in Egypt. 
<p>
Note that #$PyramidMonument is <i>not</i> a specialization of the shape-type #$Pyramid, as some instances of the former have shapes that differ too much from that of a geometrical pyramid to be instances of the latter.  #$EgyptianPyramid, however, <i>is</i> a specialization of #$Pyramid.")
(disjointWith OngoingStatus InterruptedStatus)
(comment Crevasse "A specialization of #$Crevice-Topographical. Each instance of #$Crevasse is a large crack in a stone object or a glacier.  Crevasses are at least as long as a person, and are a hazard for mountain climbers.")
(comment CraterRange "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$CraterRange is a group of craters in close proximity on the surface of some planet.")
(comment NationalPark "A specialization of #$WildernessPark.  Each instance of #$NationalPark is a park in the wilderness that is managed by the national government of the country in which it is located.")
(comment TownSquare "A specialization of #$Park.  Each instance of #$TownSquare is a park (usually one that is roughly square-shaped) located near the commercial center of a town or city.")
(comment Park "A specialization of #$OutdoorRecreationArea and #$PublicProperty.  Each instance of #$Park is an area of land set aside for outdoor recreational use; it is typically managed by a government.  Specializations include #$Park-City and #$WildernessPark.")
(comment Park-City "A specialization of #$Park.  Each instance of #$Park-City is a park managed and maintained by the city government of the city in which it is located.")
(comment Playground "A specialization of #$OutdoorRecreationArea.  Each instance of #$Playground is an area for children to play in.  Playgrounds often contain apparati to play on, such as swings, slides, see-saws, and jungle-gyms.  Playground are often located inside city #$Parks or on school grounds.")
(comment PredicateType "A collection of collections, and a specialization of #$RelationshipType.  Each instance of #$PredicateType is a collection of #$Predicates.  The predicates in a given predicate category represented in the Knowledge Base are typically those sharing some common feature(s) considered significant enough that the collection of all such predicates is useful to reify.  Although what constitutes a \"significant common feature\" is difficult to specify precisely, there are many clear-cut cases. Instances of #$PredicateType include #$TemporalPartPredicate, #$QuantitySlot, #$FunctionalSlot, #$InterActorSlot, #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate, and #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot.  See also the comment on #$Predicate.")
(comment BaseballField "a specialization of #$AthleticField.  Each instance of #$BaseballField is a area intended for playing baseball, and consists of a #$BaseballDiamond and an outfield.")
(comment AthleticField "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea and #$OpenArea.  Each instance of #$AthleticField is a large, open area intended for field sports events.")
(disjointWith IceRink AthleticField)
(disjointWith AthleticField WrestlingRing)
(disjointWith HandballCourt AthleticField)
(disjointWith SkiSlope AthleticField)
(disjointWith AthleticField GoalScoringRegion)
(disjointWith BoxingRing AthleticField)
(disjointWith GymnasiumRoom AthleticField)
(disjointWith AthleticField SandTrap)
(disjointWith AthleticField MiniatureGolfCourse)
(comment BoulderField "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature, #$Ground, and #$OpenArea.  Each instance of #$BoulderField is a flat area bare of vegetation and covered with #$Boulders.  Boulder fields are typically found in high altitude and high latitude regions.")
(disjointWith AthleticField NaturalRegion)
(comment WildernessArea "A specialization of #$NaturalRegion.  Each instance of #$WildernessArea is a undeveloped region that is virtually unpopulated by humans.")
(comment FootballField "A specialization of #$AthleticField whose instances are intended for playing #$Football-American.")
(comment Meadow "A specialization of #$Grassland and #$NaturalRegion. Each instance of #$Meadow is a wild grassy area.")
(comment FactoryBuilding "A specialization of #$ModernShelterConstruction and #$Workplace. Each instance of #$FactoryBuilding is a large building in which products are manufactured or processed and packaged. A typical factory building contains heavy machinery and assembly lines.")
(comment SchoolBuilding "A specialization of #$Building and #$ModernShelterConstruction.  Each instance of #$SchoolBuilding is a building where school classes meet and/or school adminstrative work is done.  The physical quarters of a given school might include many school buildings.")
(comment PowerPlantBuilding "A specialization of #$Workplace and #$ModernShelterConstruction.  Each instance of #$PowerPlantBuilding is a building whose primary purpose is to house one or more power generators.")
(comment HospitalBuilding "A specialization of #$MedicalFacility and #$ModernShelterConstruction.  Each instance of #$HospitalBuilding is a building that serves as (all or part of) the physical quarters of a given #$Hospital (q.v.).  A hospital building typically contains rooms for patients, examination rooms, operating rooms, doctors' offices, and nurses' stations.")
(comment nonDisplayableLinkInSystem "<code>(#$nonDisplayableLinkInSystem LINK SYSTEM)</code> tells the Graphic Format Library code not to display the link <code>LINK</code> in the path system <code>SYSTEM</code>.")
(comment affectedAgent "The predicate #$affectedAgent relates a particular event to those agents who are affected by it.  (#$affectedAgent EVT AGT) means that AGT is harmed, benefitted, or otherwise acted upon in or as a result of EVT.  #$affectedAgent is not an instance of #$ActorSlot because it is not a specialization of #$actors -- if an individual is affected in or as a result of an event,  that individual need not participate in the event; indeed, that individual need not temporally intersect the event.")
(comment expresses "A #$MetaLanguagePredicate (q.v.) and a specialization of #$means (q.v.) that relates a CycL sentence to the #$Proposition (if any) it expresses with respect to the intended model of the CycL language.  <code>(#$expresses SENTENCE PROPOSITION)</code> means that <code>SENTENCE</code> expresses, or has as its <i>intensional</i> meaning, <code>PROPOSITION</code>.
In order to express a proposition (see #$CycLPropositionalSentence), a CycL sentence must at the very least be closed and obey the arity of its main operator.
<p>
For example, the sentence
<pre>
  (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Murray)
</pre>
expresses the proposition that Muffet likes Murray, and we can represent that fact as follows:
<pre>
  (#$expresses
    (#$Quote (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Murray))
    (#$ThatFn (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Murray)))
</pre>
Now the above sentence is of course nearly trivial, given the intimate connection that exists between the function #$ThatFn and the #$expresses relation.  But, as we shall see, not all <code>#$expresses</code> sentences are trivial.
<p>
As the example illustrates, the first argument-term in an <code>#$expresses</code> statement might consist of a particular CycL sentence wrapped with <code>#$Quote</code>.  But note that this need not be the case.  Suppose the above <code>#$likesAsFriend</code> sentence happened to be the first CycL assertion made by Muffet.  We might then reify a constant named <i>MuffetsFirstCycLAssertion</i> to denote that sentence.  Then we could say:
<pre>
  (#$expresses
    MuffetsFirstCycLAssertion
    (#$ThatFn (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Murray)))
</pre> 
As the example also illustrates, the second argument-term in an <code>#$expresses</code> statement might consist of a non-atomic term formed by applying the functor <code>#$ThatFn</code> to a particular CycL sentence.  But this also need not be the case.  Suppose the proposition that Muffet likes Murray happened to be Murray's favorite proposition.  We might then reify a constant named <i>MurraysFavoriteProposition</i> to denote that proposition.  Then we could say:
<pre>
  (#$expresses
    MuffetsFirstCycLAssertion
    MurraysFavoriteProposition)
</pre> 
See also #$denotes.")
(disjointWith Proposition CycLExpression)
(disjointWith Proposition GraphicalStructure)
(comment CycLPropositionalSentence "The collection of CycL sentences that express propositions (see #$Proposition).  Two necessary conditions for a CycL sentence's expressing a proposition is that it be closed (see #$CycLClosedSentence) and not violate any applicable arity constraints (see #$CycLSentence-Askable).")
(comment ThatFn "A unary function that takes a #$CycLPropositionalSentence (q.v.) to the #$Proposition it #$expresses with respect to the intended interpretation of the CycL language.  <code>(#$ThatFn SENTENCE)</code> is the proposition that <code>SENTENCE</code> is true.  For example, the non-atomic term
<pre>
  (#$ThatFn (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Murray))
</pre>
denotes the proposition that Muffet like Murray.
<p>
Note that the argument-place of #$ThatFn is \"implicitly quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument).  Thus the above non-atomic term was constructed by applying the functor <code>#$ThatFn</code> to the <code>#$likesAsFriend</code> sentence directly; there was no need to wrap the sentence in <code>#$Quote</code>.  Note further that, because of this quoted argment-place, #$ThatFn is <i>not</i> related to the predicate #$expresses (which has no quoted argument-place) via #$functionCorrespondingPredicate.  But for any #$CycLPropositionalSentence <code>SENTENCE</code>, the following holds:
<pre>
  (#$expresses (#$Quote SENTENCE) (#$ThatFn SENTENCE)) .")
(comment RefineTypesForTermByStringFn "(#$RefineTypesForTermByStringFn TERM STRING COL) returns a list of possible #$isa assertions for term based on (parses of the headword of) STRING which satisfy (i.e. involvie specializations of) the constraining collection COL.")
(comment facets-Generic "A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections and a specialization (via #$genlInverse) of #$typeGenls (q.v.). This is the most general of the \"faceting\" predicates. <code>(#$facets-Generic COL COLTYPE)</code> means that <code>COLTYPE</code> corresponds to one way of dividing <code>COL</code> into subcollections. The instances of <code>COLTYPE</code> are all \"the same sort\" of subcollection of <code>COL</code>. For example, #$MovieTypeByGenre facets #$Movie-CW according to movie-genre; the former's instances thus include such collections as #$WesternMovie and #$HorrorMovie. And #$FunctionTypeByRangeType facets #$Function-Denotational according to the type of thing a given function returns as values; the former's instances thus include such collections as #$IndividualDenotingFunction and #$CollectionDenotingFunction.
<p>
Note that generic faceting does not entail that the instances of <code>COLTYPE</code> are mutually disjoint. Thus, a given film might be an instance of <i>both</i> #$ChildrensMovie and #$ActionMovie.
<p>
Note also that generic faceting does not entail that every instance of <code>COL</code> falls under some instance of <code>COLTYPE</code>. Thus, consider the universal identity function #$IdentityFn. Its range of values is so broad that it does not fall under any instance of #$FunctionTypeByRangeType.
<p>
Specializations of #$facets-Generic include #$facets-Covering, #$facets-Strict, and #$facets-Partition, as well as #$facets-NearCovering, #$facets-NearStrict, and #$facets-NearPartition.  Whenever possible, one of these more specific predicates should be used.")
(comment OpeningADoor "The collection of events in which a door is opened.  The #$Portal is treated as an #$objectOfStateChange in such an event, and the #$DoorInABuilding at that #$Portal is an #$objectActedOn.  The room or building to which the portal provides access (which is  a #$ContainerArtifact) is also an #$objectOfStateChange.")
(comment cacheTransitiveClosureForArgs "(#$cacheTransitiveClosureForArgs PRED ARGNUM) means that the transitive closure of PRED values for any term as its arg ARGNUM should be precached, for efficient retrieval. For example, if we have (#$cacheTransitiveClosureForArgs #$eventOccursAt 2), then for any valid arg2 of #$eventOccursAt -- a location -- we will cache the arg1 values -- event -- related to it via #$eventOccursAt given its transitivity properties.")
(comment TextualEntailmentKBContentTest "These are KBCTs which test whether Cyc can prove that a particular text answers a particular question.")
(comment SaturnTheCompany "The maker of instances of #$SaturnCar.")
(comment GeospatialReasoningTest-Mappable "Each instance of #$GeospatialReasoningTest-Mappable is a specification for a CycL test query that produces results which are commonly visualized as features on a map.")
(comment NGAContentTest "#$NGAContentTest is a collection of #$SKSIContentTests which test the content of the #$NGA-KS database.")
(comment unspecifiedSOELink "An instance of #$SOEDefaultGraphablePredicate.  <code>(#$unspecifiedSOELink THING-1 THING-2)</code> means that a user of the SOE has said that <code>THING-1</code> and <code>THING-2</code> are connected in some unspecified way.  Generally, a user will strengthen this connection by indicating what specific relation <code>THING-1</code> bears to <code>THING-2</code>.")
(comment KBContentRegressionTest "A subcollection of #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified which are all expected to succeed, and for which it is considered an error if they begin to fail.")
(comment SecondsSince1970ToDateFn "#$SecondsSince1970ToDateFn is an evaluatable function that takes a positive integer and returns a date.  The date returned is the date that one gets by adding the number of seconds specified by the first argument of the function to the start of 1970.  For instance, (#$SecondsSince1970ToDateFn 1260) = (#$MinuteFn 21 (#$DayFn 1 (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 1970)))).")
(comment ContextOfAgentOnTheRecordFn "A Binary function that returns an instance of #$Microtheory when given an instance of #$IntelligentAgent and a time interval as an argument. <code>(#$ContextOfAgentOnTheRecordFn AGENT TIME)</code> denotes the microtheory containing all of the propositions (and their closure) that an agent, at TIME, goes on the record claiming.")
(disjointWith TemporalExistencePredicate HistoricalPredicate)
(comment Bend-Topographical "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$Bend-Topographical is a curved section in the course of a #$Stream.")
(comment Isthmus "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$Isthmus is a narrow strip of land that has bodies of water on either side, and which connects two larger bodies of land.")
(comment equalSymbols "A binary #$MetaPredicate and a specialization of #$equals, both of whose arguments are #$quotedArguments (q.v.), that can be used to make identity assertions about symbols.  Stated loosely,  <code>(#$equalSymbols X Y)</code> means that <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> are one and the same symbol.  Given that the arguments are \"quoted\", however, it is more accurate to say: A closed atomic formula consisting of the expression <code>#$equalSymbols</code> followed by CycL expressions <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> (all enclosed within a pair of parentheses) is true if and only if <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> are (two occurrences of) the same expression.
<p>
Note that this is stronger than merely saying that <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> denote the same thing.  As an example (using English expressions for convenience), the name 'Bertrand Russell' and the description 'the author of \"On Denoting\"' are two different expressions, even though they both denote the same person.  Two symbols can even share the same intensional meaning and still be different symbols; e.g. 'bachelor' and 'unmarried male'.  The relation #$equalSymbols <i>does</i> hold, however, between 'bachelor' and 'bachelor' (at least if we pretend that 'bachelor' is a CycL expression), as it does between the CycL constant <code>#$BertrandRussell</code> and the CycL constant <code>#$BertrandRussell</code>.
<p>
See also #$differentSymbols.")
(comment HistoricalPredicate "A #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation (qq.v.).  A  predicate <code>PRED</code> is a #$HistoricalPredicate if and only if it has the following property: if any given sentence of the form <code>(PRED ARG1 ... ARGN)</code> is true at time index <code>T</code> (see #$mtTimeIndex) it necessarily remains so for the rest of time.  Nothing can begin its existence with an #$HistoricalPredicate holding of it.
<p>
For example, #$hasAlumni is a #$HistoricalPredicate.  Once a sentence of the form <code>(#$hasAlumni SCHOOL AGENT)</code> becomes true, it remains true for the rest of time.  Further, for nothing is it the case that #$hasAlumni holds of it at the begining of its existence.")
(comment TemporalExistencePredicate "A #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation.  #$TemporalExistencePredicate is the collection of predicates that entail the existence of at least some of their arguments throughout the entire time in which the relation holds of those arguments.
<p>
More precisely, <code>PRED</code> is a #$TemporalExistencePredicate if and only if it is \"contemporary\" in at least one of its argument-places (see #$contemporaryInArg).  That is, there is at least one integer <code>I</code> (1 <= <code>I</code> <= <code>N</code>) such that, if <code>(PRED ARG1 ... ARGN)</code> holds at a time-index <code>TIME</code> (see #$mtTimeIndex), then the temporal extent of <code>ARGI</code> subsumes <code>TIME</code> (see #$temporallySubsumes).
<p>
Examples of #$TemporalExistencePredicates are #$lengthOfObject and #$thinksAbout.  A specialization is #$CotemporalPredicate.")
(comment Locomotion-Air "The collection of all instances of #$LocomotionProcess in which the locomotor is #$in-ImmersedFully and supported by some #$GaseousTangibleThing. Includes both the movement of an airplane in flight, and the movement of birds and bats while flying.")
(comment spatialThingTypeFoundInLocation "(#$spatialThingTypeFoundInLocation TYPE LOC)  means that objects or stuff of type TYPE are/is found at LOC.--Davis, June 16, 1998")
(comment foundsOrganization "An instance of #$ComplexActionPredicate.  This is the relation that holds definitionally between the fillers of the #$performedBy and #$outputsCreated roles in any instance of #$FoundingAnOrganization.  Note that this predicate is not an instance of #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate.  This predicate holds of its args from the time of the founding until the end of time.")
(comment actorRoles-EPFT "A simple event-predicate template.  (#$actorRoles-EPFT EVT-TYPE (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn ACTOR1 ROLE1) (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn ACTOR2 ROLE2) ...) means that an instance of EVT-TYPE occurs which is also an instance of (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn ACTOR1 ROLE1), and  (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn ACTOR2 ROLE2), and so on.  In other words, it means an instance of EVT-TYPE occurs in which ACTOR1 is engaged by the relation expressed by ROLE1, and in which ACTOR2 is engaged by the relation expressed by ROLE2, and so on.  For example, (#$actorRoles-EPFT #$ImposingSanctions (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn #$UNSecurityCouncil #$performedBy) (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn #$Iraq #$maleficiary)) is true iff the UN Security Council imposes (is imposing) sanctions on Iraq.")
(comment doFuelDevice "(#$doFuelDevice DEVICE) means that steps are taken to fuel DEVICE.")
(comment RadiologicalExplosiveWeapon "The collection of instances #$ExplosiveDevice and #$WeaponOfMassDestruction whose use results in the scattering of #$RadioactiveStuff.")
(comment dispersionOrigin "A binary predicate relating an event in which things are ditributed to the source from which the distribution takes place. For example, in an event of spraying an aerosol from a can, the can is the source of the dispersion: (#$dispersionSource Spraying001 Can001). Similarly, if the explosion of a bomb, Bomb001, causes the distribution-of-shrapnel event, Dist001, then (#$dispersionSource Dist001 Bomb001) is true. Despite the lack of rules to enforce this, not every pair of an instance of PhysicalDistributionEvent and the instance of PartiallyTangible where the distribution intuitively originates will satisfy #$dispersionSource. For example, although Lake Itasca is the headwaters of the Missippi, and therefore in a sense the source of the (very long duration) event in which the Mississippi flows into the Gulf of Mexico, it is not true that (dispersionSource FlowOfTheMississippi Itasca), and not just because there are tributaries of the Mississippi that themselves have source this is not the right kind of distribution event to have a dispersionSource. Finally, note that #$dispersionSource is interesting primarily not for its own sake, but rather for the sake of enabling interesting restrictions to be put on kinds of instances of PhysicalDistributionEvent.")
(comment BioDataPredicate "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$BioDataPredicate is a #$Predicate that, when applied to an admissible tuple of arguments, expresses biographical data about one of those arguments.  For example, #$birthDate is a #$BioDataPredicate since a sentence of the form (#$birthDate PERSON DATE) expresses biographical data about PERSON.")
(comment CollectionDifferenceFn "A binary #$SubcollectionFunction that takes two collections to the collection that is their \"difference\".  <code>(#$CollectionDifferenceFn INCLUDECOL EXCLUDECOL)</code> is the collection <code>DIFFCOL</code> such that a given <code>THING</code> is an instance of <code>DIFFCOL</code> if and only if <code>THING</code> is an instance of <code>INCLUDECOL</code> but <i>not</i> an instance of <code>EXCLUDECOL</code>. 
<p>
For example,  (#$CollectionDifferenceFn #$MailOrderCompany #$Pharmacy) is the collection of mail order companies that are not pharmacies.
<p>
Note that if <code>(#$disjointWith INCLUDECOL EXCLUDECOL)</code> holds, then <code>DIFFCOL</code> is just <code>INCLUDECOL</code> itself.  If <code>(#$genls INCLUDECOL EXCLUDECOL)</code> holds, then <code>DIFFCOL</code> is empty (i.e.   
<code>(#$extent DIFFCOL #$TheEmptySet)</code> holds).")
(disjointWith Point (CollectionDifferenceFn HomogeneousSpaceRegion Point))
(comment Nothing "An instance of #$EmptyCollection (q.v.).  #$Nothing is in effect the collection-theoretical complement of #$Thing (q.v.): the necessarily empty collection that is by definition equal to (see #$rewriteOf) (#$CollectionDifferenceFn #$Thing #$Thing).")
(comment TermSummaryPredicate "A #$PredicateType and specialization of #$SummaryPredicate.  Each instance of #$TermSummaryPredicate is a #$BinaryPredicate that relates a term to a (quoted instance of) #$CycLSentence-Assertible that expresses a piece of information about that term's denotation.")
(comment CoordinatedMultipleAttackTerrorOperation "The collection of terrorist operations that consist of multiple terrorist attacks timed to occur near-simulaneoulsy, often (but not necessarily) directed at targets of a similar type.  Such operations are a hallmark of #$AlQaida.")
(comment CoordinatedMultipleAttackMilitaryOperation "The collection of military operations that consist of multiple attacks timed to occur near-simulaneoulsy, often with the intent of generating confusion or panic on the part of the enemy.")
(comment EventDateAndLocationPredicate "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$EventDateAndLocationPredicate is a #$Predicate that, when applied to an admissible tuple of arguments, expresses a fact about the date and/or location of one of those arguments which is an #$Event.  For example, #$eventOccursAt is an #$EventDateAndLocationPredicate since a sentence of the form (#$eventOccursAt EVENT LOCATION) expresses data about the location of EVENT.")
(comment eventDateAndLocationDataSentence "A #$TermSummaryPredicate and specialization of #$interestingSentence that relates an #$Event to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  (#$eventDateAndLocationDataSentence EVENT SENTENCE) means that SENTENCE expresses a fact about the date and/or location of EVENT.  See also #$EventDateAndLocationPredicate.")
(comment eventDevicesUsedDataSentence "A binary #$SummaryPredicate and specialization of #$interestingSentence that relates an #$Event to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  (#$eventDevicesUsedDataSentence EVENT SENTENCE) means that SENTENCE expresses a fact about what devices (or types of devices) are used in EVENT.")
(comment EventPerformerPredicate "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$EventPerformerPredicate is a #$Predicate that, when applied to an admissible tuple of arguments, expresses a fact about who performed (or played a similar role, e.g. planner or directing agent, in) one of those arguments.")
(comment eventPerformerDataSentence "A #$TermSummaryPredicate and specialization of #$interestingSentence that relates an #$Event to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  (#$eventPerformerDataSentence EVENT SENTENCE) means that SENTENCE expresses a fact about who performed EVENT, or who played a similar role, e.g. planner or directing agent, in EVENT.  See also #$EventPerformerPredicate.")
(comment Fiberglass "The collection of all pieces of #$Fiberglass, the building material.  Basically, fiberglass consists of glass fibers held together by a binding agent, such as resin or epoxy.")
(comment TypeCoercionPredicate "A collection of predicates used represent possible type coerecerions which may be needed to render NL input as WFF.")
(comment intendedReferent "This is a specialization of #$referents which relates a #$LinguisticExpressionPeg to its most \"deeply embedded\" referent (cf. #$DiscourseReferentPeg) within the discourse context. Typically, for a given group of #$coReferring expressions, the most \"embedded\" referent will be the #$naiveReferent of whichever of these expresssions is intruduced first (least recently) within the  discourse. Thus (#$intendedReferent LINGPEG REFPEG) entails that REFPEG is the #$naiveReferent of the least recent #$LinguisticExpressionPeg (not nec. distinct from LINGPEG) which is #$coReferring with LINGPEG.")
(comment ConnectedSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion and #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (qq.v.).  This is the collection of self-connected space regions.  A homogeneous extended space region is an instance of #$ConnectedSpaceRegion if and only if it is \"connected\" in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory (q.v.).   A connected region <code>REGION</code> is \"all in one piece\": it cannot be split in two mutually-disconnected homogeneous extended space regions whose sum is <code>REGION</code>.
<p>
For example, the geographical location of Switzerland is a connected region; but the geographical location of the Italian-speaking part of Switzerland is a disconnected, 3-piece region.
<p>
Cf. #$DisconnectedSpaceRegion, with which this collection makes up a partition of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion.")
(comment (ConveyFn Person) "This is the collection of events in which people (see #$Person) are moved from one place to another by some instrument (see #$conveyor-Generic).  Like #$Conveying-Generic, it is agnostic as to whether the instrument moves with the people, or whether it remains stationary.  Thus, although an event such as 'taking a patient to the hospital in an ambulance' meets the membership criteria of (#$ConveyFn #$Person), #$TransportingPeople (a specialization of #$TransportationEvent) is more appropriate since it entails that the instrument (the ambulance would be playing the role of #$transporter), moves with the patient.  An event such as  '5 skiers riding on a 100 chair chairlift' would also be an element of (#$ConveyFn #$Person).  However, the entire chairlift would not play the role #$transporter since it does not translate up the hill as it moves the skiers.  Rather, it remains stationary as a whole.  Thus, '5 skiers riding on a 100 chair chairlift' would be a element of #$Conveying-Stationary as well as an element of (#$ConveyFn #$Person).")
(disjointWith EmptyCollection NonEmptyCollection)
(comment NonEmptySetOrCollection "A collection of #$SetOrCollections. A set or collection is an instance of #$NonEmptySetOrCollection if and only if it has at least one element. Cf. #$EmptySetOrCollection.")
(comment containsCavityWithWalls "<code>(#$containsCavityWithWalls OBJECT CAVITY)</code> means that the #$PartiallyTangible <code>OBJECT</code> contains the #$CavityWithWalls <code>CAVITY</code> either somewhere within <code>OBJECT</code>, or on <code>OBJECT</code>'s surface.")
(comment FreelyAccessibleWWWDatabase "The collection of databases published and freely accessbile on the world wide web.")
(comment OpenCycExampleTransportationPlanningDomainMt "A microtheory containing data for demonstrating the use of rules in #$TransportationPlanningMt for OpenCyc.")
(disjointWith InfiniteSetOrCollection FiniteSetOrCollection)
(disjointWith DenumerableSetOrCollection NonDenumerableSetOrCollection)
(comment NonDenumerableSetOrCollection "A specialization of #$InfiniteSetOrCollection.  A set or collection is an instance of #$NonDenumerableSetOrCollection if and only if its #$cardinality is <i>greater</i> than aleph-0 (i.e. #$PlusInfinity).  
<p>
Cf. #$DenumerableSetOrCollection.")
(comment DenumerableSetOrCollection "A specialization of #$InfiniteSetOrCollection.  A set or collection is an instance of #$DenumerableSetOrCollection if and only if its #$cardinality is aleph-0 (i.e. #$PlusInfinity).
<p>
Cf. #$NonDenumerableSetOrCollection.")
(comment FiniteSetOrCollection "A collection of #$SetOrCollections. A set or collection <code>CLASS</code> is an instance of #$FiniteSetOrCollection if and only if it has a finite number of elements (possibly zero).  That is, the #$cardinality of <code>CLASS</code> is some #$NonNegativeInteger.  
<p>
Cf. #$InfiniteSetOrCollection.")
(comment unassertForNonTKBBuild "A unary #$MetaKnowledgePredicate.  (#$unassertForOpenCycBuild ASSERTION) means that ASSERTION should be unasserted before compiling a ResearchCyc build that should not include TKB data.")
(comment EmptyCollection "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$EmptySetOrCollection (q.v.).  A collection is an instance of #$EmptyCollection if and only if it has no instances.  
<p>
Some collections are by their very nature necessarily empty; e.g. #$Nothing (q.v.).  Conversely, some collections are empty in some contexts and non-empty in others; e.g. #$Unicorn might be empty in the context of the actual world but non-empty in certain mythological contexts.  Similarly, a #$TimeDependentCollection might be empty at certain times and non-empty at certain other times; e.g. the collection of female US presidents is currently empty but will hopefully become non-empty some time in the near future.  This last example demonstrates that #$EmptyCollection is itself a time-dependent collection.
<p>
Note that each instance of #$EmptyCollection is #$disjointWith (q.v.) every collection -- including itself.
<p>
Cf. #$NonEmptyCollection.")
(comment NonEmptyCollection "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$NonEmptySetOrCollection.  A collection is an instance of #$NonEmptyCollection if and only if it has at least one instance.  
<p>
Some collections, aguably, are by their very nature necessarily non-empty; e.g. #$Set-Mathematical.  Conversely, some collections are non-empty in some contexts and empty in others; e.g. #$Horse is non-empty in the context of the present-day actual world but empty in certain counterfactual contexts in which horses never existed.  Similarly, a #$TimeDependentCollection might be empty at certain times and non-empty at certain other times; e.g. the collection of female US presidents is currently empty but will hopefully become non-empty some time in the near future.  This last example demonstrates that #$NonEmptyCollection is itself a time-dependent collection.
<p>
Note that each instance of #$NonEmptyCollection #$intersectsWith (q.v.) #$Thing.
<p>
Cf. #$EmptyCollection.")
(comment SetOrCollectionTypeByCardinality "A collection of collections of sets or collections.  Each instance of #$SetOrCollectionTypeByCardinality is the collection of all set-or-collections with a particular cardinality, ranging from 0 (see #$EmptySetOrCollection) to aleph-0 or #$PlusInfinity (see #$DenumerableSetOrCollection).  For cardinalities not explicitly represented as reified collections, use #$CardinalityTypeFn.")
(comment CardinalityTypeFn "A unary #$CollectionDenotingFunction that takes a #$Cardinal-Mathematical to the corresponding #$SetOrCollectionTypeByCardinality (q.v.).  <code>(#$CardinalityTypeFn NUM)</code> is the collection of all of and only those set-or-collections whose #$cardinality is <code>NUM</code>.
<p>
For example, #$PersonTypeByEuropeanZodiacSign is an instance of (#$CardinalityTypeFn 12).")
(disjointWith ProperSpaceRegion StrictlySemiProperSpaceRegion)
(disjointWith ProperSpaceRegion (CollectionDifferenceFn SemiProperSpaceRegion ProperSpaceRegion))
(comment hasIsaPredicateForm "This is the corresponding predicate of the function #$IsaPredFn.")
(comment hasGenlsPredicateForm "This is the corresponding predicate of the function #$hasGenlsPredicateForm.")
(comment genlsLinkedArgIsas "An expression of the form (#$genlsLinkedArgIsas  PRED1 ARG1 PRED2 ARG2 COLLECTION) means that either (1)  the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th argument is a sub-collection of the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED2's ARG2th value or (2) that the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED2's ARG2th argument is a sub-collection of the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th value.  Further, the COLLECTION is one of those arguments' constraining collection and, so, is a common sub-collection of both argument's constraining collections.")
(comment genlsLinkedArgGenls "An expression of the form (#$genlsLinkedArgGenls  PRED1 ARG1 PRED2 ARG2 COLLECTION) means that either (1) the collection that is the #$argGenl value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th argument is a sub-collection of the collection  is the #$argGenl value for the #$Predicate PRED2's ARG2th value or (2) the collection that is the #$argGenl value for the #$Predicate PRED2's ARG2th argument is a sub-collection ofthe collection  is the #$argGenl value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th value. Further, the COLLECTION is one of those arguments' constraining collection and, so, is a common sub-collection of both argument's constraining collections.")
(comment incompatibleArgIsas "An expression of the form (#$incompatibleArgIsas  PRED1 ARG1 PRED2 ARG2) means that the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th argument is disjoint with the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED2's ARG2th value.")
(comment incompatibleArgGenls "An expression of the form (#$disJointArgGenls  PRED1 ARG1 PRED2 ARG2) means that the collection that is the value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th argument is a type of collection, such that it is incompatible with the values for PRED2's ARG2th argument. This predicate <b>does not</b> assume that PRED2's  ARG2th argument also takes a collection as a value.")
(comment predicateOutputModesForDetermination "An assertion of the form (#$predicateOutputModesForDetermination PRED N TYPE DET BIN-PRED) means that relative to the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination DET, the Nth argument of the #$Predicate PRED should  bear the relationship BIN-PRED to TYPE.")
(comment ReleasingAnimalsFromConfinementToGraze "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ReleasingFromConfinement. In each #$ReleasingAnimalsFromConfinementToGraze, someone sets animals loose to graze.")
(comment SubcontractingSomething-EmployeeHiring "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EmployeeHiring. In each #$SubcontractingSomething-EmployeeHiring, someone arranges something for contracted work to be done by others.")
(comment OfferingSomething-Worshipping "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Worshipping. In each #$OfferingSomething-Worshipping, someone presents something as an act of worship.")
(comment BreakingAContinuity "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$BreakingAContinuity, something varies or interrupts a uniformity or continuity.")
(comment ServingSomeone-HelpingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HelpingAnAgent. In each #$ServingSomeone-HelpingAnAgent, someone works for or is a servant to someone.")
(comment InvertibleFunction "A specialization of #$OneToOneFunction and #$UnaryFunction (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all functions that have well-defined inverses (see #$inverseFunctions).  A unary function is an instance of #$InvertibleFunction if and only if it is one-to-one: it never takes distinct arguments to the same value.")
(comment OneToOneFunction "A specialization of #$Function-Denotational each instance of which is a function that always maps distinct argument-sequences to distinct values; i.e. it never maps two different elements of its domain to the same element  of its range.  Such a function is sometimes called an <i>injection</i>, and if it is also a mapping onto a given set, (see #$functionOntoSet) it is called a <i>bijection</i> with  respect to that set.  (Thus a one-to-one function is a  bijection with respect to its own range.)
<p>
An important specialization of this collection is #$InvertibleFunction.
<p> 
See also  #$DomainOfFunctionFn, #$RangeOfFunctionFn, and  #$valueOfFunctionForArgs.")
(comment STREEMQuerySpecification "Each instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification gives a description of how a particular query should be run by the #$CycInferenceEngine.  At minimum, this description includes the CycL formula that expresses the query, but can also include a number of other parameters that control inference, such as what microtheory to do the query in, how much time to allow, etc.")
(comment WordNetSynsetImportStatus-Skip "The collection of WordNet-Version2_0 synsets that have been reviewed and skipped for the time being")
(comment behaviorCapable "The predicate #$behaviorCapable is used to indicate that an object (an instance of #$SomethingExisting) can play a role (an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate) in a type of situation (a specialization of #$Situation).  <code>(#$behaviorCapable OBJ SIT-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is able to play <code>ROLE</code> in a situation of type <code>SIT-TYPE</code>.  Note that <code>OBJ</code> may or may not have been designed to function in that way (see the specializations of #$behaviorCapable, #$primaryFunction and #$intendedBehaviorCapable).  Moreover, unlike #$capableOf (q.v.), #$behaviorCapable does not imply that <code>OBJ</code> can unquestionably act in that way in every such situation, since extrinsic factors may prevent it from doing so; for example, if <code>OBJ</code> is a tool, it may be in the wrong location or operated by a person lacking the requisite skills.  Examples: (intended capability) a hammer is #$behaviorCapable of being the #$deviceUsed in instances of #$HammeringANail; (unintended capability) an inner tube is capable of being the #$deviceUsed in instances of people #$FloatingInLiquid.")
(comment ProprietaryConstant "Instances of #$ProprietaryConstant and any assertions involving them will be omitted from all (fully) non-proprietary KBs and will not displayed in non-proprietary applications.  One should create a spec of #$ProprietaryConstant for each includable/excludable proprietary KB module.  #$ProprietaryConstant should have no direct instances.")
(comment ConveyingOfIndividualFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  When applied to an instance OBJ of #$PartiallyTangible, #$ConveyFn returns the collection of all events in which OBJ is conveyed from one location to another (so the collection in question is a specialization of #$ConveyingMaterials).  For example, (#$ConveyFn #$GeorgeWashington) is the collection of all events in which George Washington is conveyed from one location to another.")
(comment conveyingTypeOfMaterialType "A predicate which relates a type of material to the collection of #$ConveyingMaterials events in which that type of thing is conveyed. <code>(#$conveyingTypeOfMaterialType MATERIAL-TYPE EVENT-COL)</code> means that all events in <code>EVENT-COL</code> involve the transportation of materials of type <code>MATERIAL-TYPE</code>. See also #$ConveyFn.")
(comment conveyingTypeOfIndividual "A predicate which relates an instance of #$PartiallyTangible to the collection of #$ConveyingMaterials events in which that individual is conveyed. (#$conveyingTypeOfIndividual PARTIALLY-TANGIBLE EVENT-COL) means that all events in EVENT-COL involve the transportation of PARTIALLY-TANGIBLE. See also #$ConveyingOfIndividualFn.")
(comment doMoveTo "(#$doMoveTo AGENT OBJ LOCALIZED-THING) means that steps are taken by AGENT to move OBJ to the location of LOCALIZED-THING.")
(comment FaceToFaceConversation "A specialization of #$FaceToFacePresenceCommunicating, #$SociabilityBasedAction, and #$Conversation.  In each instance of #$FaceToFaceConversation, two or more parties coverse with each other in each other's presence.")
(comment Reservation-Feeling "Feeling of reservation; related to #$Doubt.  When someone is feeling reservation, then they waiting to pass judgement, express one's feelings, or share thoughts or intentions.")
(comment SittingPosture-Slouching "The attribute that describes, most generically, a #$Person who is sitting and slouching on a chair or couch.")
(comment LegsCrossedOnFurniture "The posture of sitting with one's legs crossed at some point on some furniture - like a table or desk.")
(comment KeepingHandsInPocket "The human action of keeping one or both hands in the pockets of one's clothing.")
(comment ExtendingHandToShake "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$NonVerbalCommunicating.  Each instance of #$ExtendingHandToShake involves one #$SocialBeing extending his hand to another person to shake hands.  If the other person reciprocates the action, then you have a #$ShakingHands event.   The person who initiates this action usually is attempting to convey to #$Friendliness and #$GoodWill.  By convention, this action is typically performed with the right hand (see (#$RightObjectOfPairFn #$Hand)).")
(comment IMDB-PersonFn "(#$IMDB-PersonFn NAME ID) denotes the person identified in
the Internet Movie database as having the string NAME as their name
and the integer ID as their unique IMDb identifier.  For example,
(#$IMDB-PersonFn 'Tom Hanks' 158) denotes the actor Tom Hanks.")
(comment IMDB-MovieFn "(#$IMDB-MovieFn NAME ID) denotes the movie identified in
the Internet Movie database as having the canonical title string NAME
as its title and the integer ID as its unique IMDb identifier.  For
example, (#$IMDB-PersonFn 'Star Wars (1977)' 76759) denotes the movie
'Star Wars' that was released in 1977.")
(comment externalTermStrings "(#$externalTermStrings TERM STRING) states that some
external system considers TERM as a possible interpretation for the
string STRING.")
(comment undefined "A unary predicate, whose argument-place is \"quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument), that is used to state that a given CycL non-atomic term does not denote anything.  Where <code>NAT</code> has the form <code>(FUNC ARG1..ARGN)</code>, <code>(#$undefined NAT)</code> means that <code>NAT</code> has no denotatum because <code>FUNC</code> (the function denoted by the main functor in <code>NAT</code>) returns no value for the argument sequence <code>ARG1..ARGN</code>.   
<p>
Typically, <code>NAT</code> will be semantically well-formed (see #$CycLNonAtomicTerm-Assertible) but will fail to denote because <code>FUNC</code> is a #$PartialFunction (q.v.) that is not defined for <code>ARG1...ARGN</code>.  For example, <code>(#$undefined (#$QuotientFn 5 0))</code> holds because division is undefined for divisor zero.  Similarly, <code>(#$undefined (#$BorderBetweenFn #$Canada  #$Mexico))</code> holds because Canada does not border Mexico, and so there is no such border as that which the term <code>(#$BorderBetweenFn #$Canada #$Mexico)</code> purports to describe.")
(comment sufficientFor-Props "A binary predicate that relates instances of #$CycLSentence-Assertible to instances of #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  #$sufficientFor-Props is used to state that one formula expresses a sufficient condition for the truth of another. <code>(#$sufficientFor-Props COND PROP)</code> means that <code>COND</code>'s being satisfied is sufficient for <code>PROP</code>'s being satisfied. See also #$preconditionFor-Props.")
(comment preconditionFor-Props "A binary predicate that relates instances of #$CycLSentence-Assertible to instances of #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  #$preconditionFor-Props is used to state that one formula expresses a necessary precondition for the truth of another. <code>(#$preconditionFor-Props COND PROP)</code> means that <code>PROP</code> isn't true unless <code>COND</code> is true.  Preconditions of various types are distinguished by the more specialized predicates #$legalPreconditionFor and #$causalPreconditionFor. See also #$sufficientFor-Props.")
(comment implies "A binary #$LogicalConnective (q.v.) that corresponds to the material implication operator of propositional calculus.  The #$implies relation takes two #$CycLSentence-Assertibles as its arguments; and the syntactic result of applying the term '#$implies' to two sentences is itself a sentence, called a \"conditional\" or \"implication\".  A sentence of the form (#$implies ANTECEDENT CONSEQUENT) is true if and only if ANTECEDENT is false or CONSEQUENT is true (or both).  Equivalently: it is not the case that ANTECEDENT is true and CONSEQUENT is false.  For example,
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$knows #$Muffet #$Patches-Cat)
    (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Patches-Cat))
</pre>
<p>
means that if Muffet knows Patches then she likes him.
<p>
Cf. the predicate #$sentenceImplies, which is not a logical connective, and is primarily used in rules involving quantification over CycL sentences.")
(comment effectOfAction-Props "<code>(#$effectOfAction-Props ACTION-SENT SENT)</code> means that an effect of the state of affairs described by <code>ACTION-SENT</code> is the state of affairs described by <code>SENT</code>, where <code>ACTION-SENT</code> is a sentence of the form <code>(PRED . ARGS)</code>, <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$ActionPredicate, and <code>SENT</code> is any sentence that describes the effect(s) of <code>ACTION-SENT</code>. Thus, #$effectOfAction-Props should be used only when the first argument is a sentence in which an instance of #$ActionPredicate occurs in the arg0 place.")
(comment referencedEditorOfPublication "(#$referencedEditorOfPublication PERSON LITERARYWORK N) means that PERSON is one of the editors of LITERARYWORK, and is the Nth editor credited in references to LITERARYWORK.")
(comment pageNumbersOfSubwork "(#$pageNumbersOfSubwork ARTICLE N M) means there is some WORK such that (#$subWorks WORK ARTICLE) holds, and ARTICLE appears from page N to page M in WORK.")
(comment pageNumberOfSubwork-Beginning "(#$pageNumberOfSubwork-Beginning ARTICLE N) means there is some WORK such that (#$subWorks WORK ARTICLE) holds, and ARTICLE begins on page N of WORK.")
(comment articlePresentedAtConference "(#$articlePresentedAtConference ARTICLE CONFERENCE) means that ARTICLE was presented at CONFERENCE. More precisely, there was a subevent of CONFERENCE in which the content of ARTICLE was presented to some audience.")
(comment volumeOfPeriodicalByVolumeNumber "A #$TernaryPredicate. (#$editionOfPeriodicalByVolume SERIES VOLUME ISSUE) means that ISSUE is the #$EditionOfCWSeries-Periodic of the #$PeriodicalSeries SERIES denoted by volume number VOLUME. See also #$EditionByVolumeFn.")
(comment VolumeOfPeriodicalByVolumeNumberFn "(EditionByVolumeFn PERIODICAL NUMBER) denotes the #$EditionOfCWSeries-Periodic of PERIODICAL that is released with volume number NUMBER. For instance, (#$EditionByVolumeFn #$HarpersMagazine-TheMagazine 20) denotes volume 20 of Harper's Magazine. Note that the #$EditionOfCWSeries-Periodic returned may be an instance of #$PeriodicalIssue or some subcollection of PERIODICAL. For example, (#$EditionByVolumeFn #$ComputationalLinguistics-Journal 19) denotes volume 19 of 'Computational Linguistics,' which happens to be the four issues published in 1993.")
(comment EditionByIssueNumberFn "(#$EditionByIssueNumberFn PERIODICAL NUMBER) denotes the #$EditionOfCWSeries-Periodic of PERIODICAL that is released with issue number NUMBER. For instance, (#$EditionByIssueNumberFn #$HarpersMagazine-TheMagazine 20) denotes issue 20 of Harper's Magazine. Note that the #$EditionOfCWSeries-Periodic returned may be an instance of #$PeriodicalIssue or some subcollection of PERIODICAL. For example, (#$EditionByIssueNumberFn ComputationalLinguistics-Journal 1) denotes all the first-quarter issues of 'Computational Linguistics.'")
(comment concatenateStringTokens "This predicate relates serial character-string tokens (see #$CharacterStringToken-Serial) to sequences of subseries into which they can be divided.  <code>(#$concatenateStringTokens STRINGTOKEN SUBSERIES1 ... SUBSERIESN)</code> means that <code>SUBSERIES1</code> ... <code>SUBSERIES</code> are adjacent segments of <code>STRINGTOKEN</code> (occurring in the order indicated by their respective argument positions) which jointly constitute the whole of <code>STRINGTOKEN</code>.  This n-member sequence might or might not exist as part of a longer series (i.e. a superseries of <code>STRINGTOKEN</code> that starts before <code>SUBSERIES1</code> and/or ends after <code>SUBSERIESN</code>).  See also   #$subStringTokens.")
(comment CycLFunctor "A #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of #$CycLOperator (q.v.).  Characterized synatactically, this is the collection of all and only those CycL terms that can appear as the main operator (i.e. in the \"arg0\" position) of a #$CycLClosedNonAtomicTerm. Characterized semantically, this is also the collection of all and only those CycL terms that denote #$Function-Denotationals (q.v.).  Instances of #$CycLFunctor are <i>not</i> themselves functions, but rather are expressions of the CycL language that are used to <i>denote</i> functions.
<p>
Examples of #$CycLFunctors include these four expressions:
<pre>
  #$PlusFn
  #$Lambda
  #$BorderBetweenFn
  (#$FunctionToArg 2 #$biologicalFather)
</pre>  
These examples reflect the fact that while most CycL functors encountered in practice are #$CycLConstants, a functor might be a #$CycLNonAtomicTerm.
<p>
See also #$CycLPredicator.")
(comment spatiallyEquidimensional "An #$EquivalenceRelation that relates #$SpatialThings.  <code>(#$spatiallyEquidimensional OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> both occupy the same number of spatial dimensions (see #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions).
<p>
Note that there are a number of #$SpatialPredicates that are conceived as only relating objects of the same dimensionality; see for example the instances of 
#$RCCBinaryPredicate.")
(comment CompositeSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of \"composite regions\" in the sense used in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory (q.v.).  A homogeneous extended space region is an instance of #$CompositeSpaceRegion if and only if it has another such region as a proper part (see #$properPartOfSpaceRegion).  
<p>
For example, the space occupied by a full bottle of wine is a composite space region, one of its proper parts being the space occupied by the wine itself.
<p>
Is space <i>infinitely divisible</i> into smaller and smaller proper parts?   On this question #$RCCMt is agnostic, #$RCCContinuousSpaceMt assumes the answer is <i>No</i>, and #$RCCWithAtomicRegionsMt assumes the answer is <i>Yes</i>.")
(comment AtomicSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion (q.v.) and (in #$RCCWithAtomicRegionsVocabularyMt) #$ConvexSpaceRegion.  This is the collection of atomic regions of pure space.  A homogeneous extended space region is an instance of #$AtomicSpaceRegion if and only if it has no proper parts in the sense of #$partOfSpaceRegion.  An atomic region has no parts other than itself.
<p>
Note that in #$RCCWithAtomicRegionsVocabularyMt every extended space region is assumed to be decomposable, ultimately, into atomic regions.  Conversely, in #$RCCContinuousSpaceMt #$AtomicSpaceRegion is assumed to be an #$EmptyCollection.
<p>
Cf. #$CompositeSpaceRegion; and see also #$ScatteredSpaceRegion.")
(disjointWith TwoDimensionalThing ThreeDimensionalThing)
(comment NonFluidlikeTangibleThing "The subcollection of #$PartiallyTangible whose instances are not like fluids.  #$NonFluidlikeTangibleThings do not flow and have an intrinsic shape.  #$NonFluidlikeTangibleThing is primarily a collection union of #$SolidTangibleThing and #$SemiSolidTangibleThing.  It is useful as a genl collection for certain collections whose members can be both #$SolidTangibleThings and #$SemiSolidTangibleThings, like #$Deformable.")
(disjointWith PolyDimensionalThing OneDimensionalThing)
(comment contradictoryPreds "A #$MetaPredicate and a specialization of #$negationPreds.  #$contradictoryPreds holds between two predicates  just in case they are direct \"contradictories\" of each other in the following sense.  <code>(#$contradictoryPreds PRED1 PRED2)</code> means that <code>PRED1</code> and <code>PRED2</code> are defined for exactly the same sequences of arguments, and that either one of these predicates holds of a given such sequence if and only  if the other predicate does not hold of that sequence.  That is, both of these are true:
<pre>
  (#$equiv (PRED1 . ARGS) (#$not (PRED2 . ARGS)))

  (#$equiv (PRED2 . ARGS) (#$not (PRED1 . ARGS)))
</pre>
This  is stronger than the corresponding #$negationPreds form, whose meaning would be given by replacing <code>#$equiv</code> with <code>#$implies</code> in the above sentences.
<p>
For example, (#$contradictoryPreds #$knownSentence #$unknownSentence) holds.  
<p>
To say that \"<code>PRED1 </code>and <code>PRED2 </code>are defined for exactly the same  sequences of arguments\" implies that a closed atomic sentence formed with a CycL term that denotes <code>PRED1</code> followed by a sequence of argument-terms is semantically well-formed (see #$CycLSentence-Assertible) just in case  the similar sentence formed instead with a term for <code>PRED2</code> is semantically  well-formed.  Thus, any argument-type constraint (see #$ArgTypePredicate) on a given argument-place (say the Nth) of <code>PRED1</code> must also apply to the Nth argument-place of <code>PRED2</code>, and vice versa.  Otherwise, it could happen that a sentence of the form <code>(PRED1 . ARGS)</code> was true while the corresponding <code>(PRED2 . ARGS)</code> was semantically malformed, and thus <code>(#$not (PRED2 . ARGS))</code> would not be true.")
(comment enables-SitProp "<code>(#$enables-SitProp SIT SENT)</code> means that <code>SIT</code> enables the proposition <code>PROP</code> expressed by <code>SENT</code> to become or remain true.  This relation is weaker then causation, but the above formula entails that <code>SIT</code>'s obtaining is at least a salient contributing cause of <code>PROP</code>'s becoming or remaining true.")
(comment enables-ThingProp "<code>(#$enables-ThingProp THING SENT)</code> means that <code>THING</code> enables the proposition <code>PROP</code> expressed by <code>SENT</code> to become or remain true.  This relation is weaker than causation, but the above formula entails (partly depending on the kind of thing <code>THING</code> is) that either <code>THING</code>'s existing, obtaining, occurring, playing a certain role in an event, or performing a certain action is at least a salient contributing cause of <code>PROP</code>'s becoming or remaining true.  For example, my car enabled -- but did not cause -- it to be true that I arrived at work today.")
(comment CKBTestQuery "A sub-collection of #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified.
#$CKBTestQuery is the collection of KB content tests designed to test
knowledge about cultural practices acquired as part of the
#$CulturalKBProject.")
(comment hasDenotatum "A unary #$MetaLanguagePredicate (q.v.) that holds of instances of #$CycLTerm that denote or refer to something in the intended model of the CycL language.  <code>(#$hasDenotatum TERM)</code> means that there is something <code>THING</code> such that <code>TERM</code> denotes (see #$denotes) <code>THING</code>.  Note that #$hasDenotatum's argument-place is <i>not</i> implicitly quoted (see #$quotedArgument); instead, <code>TERM</code> will often be an explicitly quoted term -- i.e. something wrapped in the syncategorematic quotation symbol '#$Quote' (see #$Quote).  For example, the sentence '(#$hasDenotatum (#$Quote #$Plato))' means that the CycL term '#$Plato' denotes something, which is true because that term denotes the man Plato.  But note that the argument to #$hasDenotatum need not be quoted.  Suppose (e.g.) that the term '#$Plato' were the CycL constant most beloved of #$Cyclists, and that a new constant 'CyclistsFavoriteConstant' were reified and defined so as to reflect this fact.  Then 'CyclistsFavoriteConstant' would refer to the CycL constant '#$Plato', which in turn denotes the man Plato; and so the sentence '(#$hasDenotatum CyclistsFavoriteConstant)' would be true.  Note finally that the sentence '(#$hasDenotatum #$Plato)' is <i>not</i> true, as it means that the man Plato is a CycL term that denotes something, which is a patent falsehood since no man is a CycL term and no man denotes anything.  See also #$EscapeQuote, #$quotedIsa, and the shared #$NoteAboutQuotingInCycL.")
(comment specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration "A unary #$MetaLanguagePredicate (q.v.) that holds of a certain kind of #$CycLTerm that, via its internal structure, specifies its denotatum -- which will either be a set or a list (ordered n-tuple) -- by enumerating the items that constitute it.
<p>
<code>(#$specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration NAT)</code> means that there is some <code>SETORLIST</code> such that <code>NAT</code> denotes (see #$denotes) <code>SETORLIST</code>, and does so in virtue of having constituent terms that serve to enumerate the items that belong to <code>SETORLIST</code>.  These constituent terms supply the arguments to a function (denoted by <code>NAT</code>'s arg0 operator) that returns the value <code>SETORLIST</code> (a particular #$Set-Mathematical or #$List) for those arguments.  The function in question might be #$TheSet, #$TheList, #$TheCovering, or #$ThePartition.
<p>
For example, the sentence
<p>
(#$specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration (#$Quote (#$ThePartition #$BaleenWhale #$ToothedWhale)))
<p>
is true because the CycL term \"(#$ThePartition #$BaleenWhale #$ToothedWhale)\" specifies, by enumeration, the set whose members are the two collections #$BaleenWhale and #$ToothedWhale.
<p>
Note that the predicate's argument-place is <i>not</i> a #$quotedArgument (q.v.), so the arg1 position of a #$specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration sentence will typically be filled by an <i>explicitly</i> quoted expression.  Thus the use of #$Quote (q.v.) in the above example is crucial; without it the arg1 would be a partition-set instead of a CycL term, in violation of the predicate's argument-constraint.")
(comment TemplateAuthoringTemplate "A spec of #$FormulaTemplate. The collection of formula templates used for creating formula templates.")
(comment Lifetime "The collection of all instances of #$ContinuousTimeInterval that are cotemporal (c.f., #$cotemporal) with the life of (see #$lifeOf) some organism.")
(comment LifetimeFn "A function from individuals who at some point in time are living organisms to the instance of #$ContinuousTimeInterval that is that individual's lifetime.")
(comment lifetimeOf "(#$lifetimeOf LIFETIME INDIVIDUAL) identifies LIFETIME as the instance of #$ContinuousTimeInterval that is co-temporal (see #$cotemporal) with the life of (c.f., #$lifeOf) INDIVIDUAL.")
(comment spatiallySeparated "An instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$spatiallyDisjoint.  <code>(#$spatiallySeparated REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> do <i>not</i> share a spatial part (and thus are disjoint) and do <i>not</i> touch (see #$touches).")
(comment CycLQuotedTerm "A #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerm (q.v.).  This is the collection of CycL terms formed by wrapping some CycL expression in the syncategorematic symbol <code>#$Quote</code> (q.v.).  Where <code>EXPR</code> is any #$CycLExpression, the quoted term <code>(#$Quote EXPR)</code> -- the expression formed by writing <code>#$Quote</code> followed by <code>EXPR</code> and enclosing the result in parentheses -- is a closed term that denotes <code>EXPR</code>.  Thus, CycL quoted terms cannot fail to denote: they always denote CycL expressions.  
<p>
For example, the term <code>(#$Quote #$Plato)</code> denotes the constant <code>#$Plato</code>, the term <code>(#$Quote <b>?X</b>)</code> denotes the variable <code><b>?X</b></code>, and the term <code>(#$Quote (#$isa #$Plato <b>?X</b>))</code> denotes the open sentence <code>(#$isa #$Plato <b>?X</b>)</code>.")
(comment CycLExpressionTypeByDenotationType "A collection of collections.  Each instance EXPRESSION-TYPE of #$CycLExpressionTypeByDenotationType is a type of CycL expression (see #$CycLExpression) for which there is a collection COLL such that by definition, every instance of EXPRESSION-TYPE denotes (see #$denotes) an instance of COLL, and any CycL expression that denotes an instance of COLL will, by virtue of that denotation, be an instance of EXPRESSION-TYPE.  For example, #$CycLOperator is the collection of CycL expressions that denote an instance of #$Relation, making #$CycLOperator an instance of #$CycLExpressionTypeByDenotationType.  This denotation relationship is expressed in CycL using the predicate, #$denotes-TypeType.")
(comment DiscontinuingAnActivity "A #$TemporalObjectType and specialization of #$PurposefulAction.  Each instance of #$DiscontinuingAnActivity is an event in which an agent discontinues an activity.  This can involve deliberately bringing the performance of a particular #$Action to an end (as in \"She quit running after twenty minutes\"), or deliberately stopping the performance of an activity (type) that was previously performed often or regularly (as in, \"The airline discontinued its service to Austin\").  Note that an instance of #$DiscontinuingAnActivity must be performed deliberately; thus, e.g., a person's quitting snoring at some point during his sleep would not count as an instance of #$DiscontinuingAnActivity.")
(comment meatTypeOfAnimalType "A functional #$BinaryPredicate that relates a specialization of #$Meat to a specialization of #$Animal.  (#$meatTypeOfAnimalType MEAT-TYPE ANIMAL-TYPE) means that MEAT-TYPE is the collection of all instances of #$Meat that come from an instance of ANIMAL-TYPE.  This is the predicate corresponding to #$MeatFn (q.v.).")
(comment meatTypeIsMadeFromAnimalType "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a specialization of #$Meat to a specialization of #$Animal.  (#$meatTypeIsMadeFromAnimalType MEAT-TYPE ANIMAL-TYPE) means that every instance of MEAT-TYPE comes from an instance of ANIMAL-TYPE.  For example, (#$meatTypeIsMadeFromAnimalType #$Bacon #$Pig) means that bacon is made from pigs.  See also the more specialized #$meatTypeOfAnimalType.")
(comment Information-Content "A specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore.  Each instance of #$Information-Content is an abstract object that can be the content, or meaning, of some token in some language.  A pair of tokens of linguistic objects mean the same thing just in case each has the same content as the other. Instance of this collection can be used to represent the content of an instance of #$ConceptualWork.")
(comment IsaPredFn "An expression of the form (#$IsaPredFn COLL) is used to denote a #$UnaryPredicate.  An assertion of the form ((#$IsaPredFn COLL) THING) is equivalent to the expression (#$isa THING COLL).  So, this function provides a means of representing collections as predicates.")
(comment GenlsPredFn "An  expression of the form (#$GenlsPredFn COLL) is used to denote a #$UnaryPredicate.  An assertion of the form ((#$GenlsPredFn COLL1) COLL2) is equivalent to the expression (#$genls COLL2 COLL1).  Compare with #$IsaPredFn.")
(comment Bust-Sculpture "A specialization of #$Sculpture.  Each instance of #$Bust-Sculpture is a sculpture that depicts a human head.")
(comment actionConnotesFeeling "(#$actionConnotesFeeling ACTION FEELING) means that
ACTION, by virtue of its type or circumstances, suggests an expression
of FEELING.  ACTION may or may not be genuinely motivated by FEELING
(see #$actionExpressesFeeling), but it in some way
suggests this.   For example, some types of actions commonly express  certain 
feelings (see
#$actionTypeExpressesFeelingType), and so their instances 
may suggest such a motivation regardless of their real cause.

 E.g., one might cover one's face with
one's hands out of a genuine feeling of #$Shame, or merely to feign
such a feeling.  Similarly, there are various (often
culturally-specific) ways of signaling certain feelings within certain
social settings, e.g., culturally-defined ways of expressing #$Respect
or #$Friendliness.  Such actions are often engaged in to conform to a
cultural norm, and aren't always motivated by possession of the
associated feeling.")
(comment actionConnotesFeelingToward "(#$actionConnotesFeelingToward ACTION FEELING THING) means
that ACTION suggests or gives the appearance of expressing FEELING (#$Friendliness,
#$Disapproval, etc.) towards THING. ACTION may or may not be genuinely
motivated by FEELING (see #$actionExpressesFeeling), but it in some
way suggests this.  For further discussion of this type of link
between action and feeling, see #$actionConnotesFeeling.")
(comment GraphicalUserInterfaceProgramRunning "The collection of all events in which a #$ComputerProcessRunning occurs and the #$programUsed is an instance of #$GraphicalUserInterfaceProgram and there is a display on a computer monitor that conveys information to the user and 'receives' inputs from the user.")
(comment UsingTheFactEntryTool "The collection of all events in which the #$CycBrowserFactEntryToolModule is used.")
(comment RemovingACycLTermFromATool "The collection of all events which involve the use of some tool for viewing or interacting with #$TheCycOntology and removing some #$CycLExpression from the tool.  For instance, after a search within a tool returns a number of results and only a few are satisfactory, if the other instances of #$CycLExpression are removed, then that event would be an instance of this collection.")
(comment SearchingForACycLTerm "The collection of all events in which the some tool is used to look for some (quoted and, possibly, indeterminate) #$CycLExpression.  For instance, entering 'perform*' into the search window of the #$CycBrowser to look for '#$performedBy' would be an instance of this collection.")
(comment CompletingAnAssignment "The collection of events which result in the content specified by an instance of #$Assignment-Specification being realized.")
(comment PostponingAnEvent "A first-order collection and a sub-collection of #$PurposefulAction.  #$PostponingAnEvent is the collection of actions in which some agent makes a decision not to try to have some type of #$Situation occur. Most typically, this will involve deciding to delay an attempt to perform a #$PurposefulAction.  This refers to the decision making action, not the possible action that is the subject of the decision-making process.")
(comment postponedSitType "A #$BinaryPredicate, #$postponedSitType is used to relate an instance of #$PostponingAnEvent to a sub-collection of #$Situation.  An expression of the form (#$postponedSitType POST SIT-TYPE) means that SIT-TYPE is the type of the situation that someone decided to  postpone in POST.")
(comment PostponingAnAssignment "A first-order collection and a sub-collection of #$PostponingAnEvent.  #$PostponingAnAssignment is the collection of actions in which some agent makes a decision to perform work on some instance of #$Assignment-Specification at some later date.")
(comment WebSearchForACycConcept "A first-order collection and a sub-collection of #$InternetSearching.  This involves a search process for information about the concept that is denoted by a (quoted) #$CycLExpression.")
(comment AffirmingASentence "A sub-collection of #$Informing.  The instances of this collection involve a judgment by some #$Agent-Generic that a particular sentence, expressed in some language, is true.")
(comment AffirmingACycLSentence "A sub-collection of #$AffirmingASentence.  The instances of this collection involve a judgment by some #$Agent-Generic that a particular sentence, which is represented in the language #$CycL, is true.")
(disjointWith AffirmingASentence DenyingASentence)
(comment DenyingASentence "A sub-collection of #$Informing.  The instances of this collection involve a judgment by some #$Agent-Generic that a particular sentence, expressed in some language, is false.")
(comment DenyingACycLSentence "A sub-collection of #$DenyingASentence.  The instances of this collection involve a judgment by some #$Agent-Generic that a particular sentence, which is represented in the language #$CycL, is false.")
(comment cycLActors "An expression of the form (#$cycLActors SIT EXPRESSION) means that the quoted #$CycLExpression EXPRESSION plays some role in the #$Situation SIT.  This is the most general predicate for relating instances of #$Situation to quoted instances of #$CycLExpression.  It is generic enough to subsume the relationship between a quoted instance of  #$CycLSentence-Askable and an instance of #$CycQueryRunning in which it is asked, or an instance of #$CycLConstant that is created during an instance of #$CreatingNewConceptInCyc. It specializations are preferrable in all of these cases.")
(comment instrumentalCycL "An expression of the form (#$instrumentalCycL EVENT CYCL) means that the #$CycLExpression CYCL was somehow used during the #$Action EVT.")
(comment focalDisplayedCycL "An expression of the form (#$focalDisplayedCycL DIS EXPRESSION) means that during the #$VisualInformationGeneratingAct DIS includes a reprvesentation of of the (quoted)  CycLExpression EXPRESSION during the event.  Further, there is some characteristic of the representation (e.g., highlighting) that makes the representation focal to the displaying.")
(comment cycLActedUpon "An expression of the form (#$cycLActedUpon  PA EXPRESSION) means that the quoted #$CycLExpression EXPRESSION is the thing that is acted upon during the instance of #$PurposefulAction SEL.")
(comment cycLRemoved "An expression of the form (#$cycLRemoved DE EXPRESSION) means that the quoted #$CycLExpression EXPRESSION is removed from an instance of #$CycKB of #$EditingACycKB ECKB.")
(comment ibtActors "A generic predicate for establishing a relationship between an instance of #$Situation and an instance of #$InformationBearingThing.")
(comment stringActors "A generic predicate for establishing a relationship between an instance of #$Situation and an instance of #$CharacterString that is somehow an inovolved in that situation.  More specific versions of this predicate include #$editingTextActedOn.")
(comment DisplayingACycQueryAnswer "A sub-collection of #$VisualInformationGeneratingAct, instances of #$DisplayingACycQueryAnswer are those events in which some running program  displays some answer that was generated by an instance of #$CycQueryRunning.")
(comment DisplayingACycQueryJustification "A sub-collection of #$VisualInformationGeneratingAct, instances of #$DisplayingACycQueryJustification are those events in which some running program displays the justification to an answer that was generated by an instance of  #$CycQueryRunning.")
(comment AddingACycLTermToATool "The collection of all events which involve the use of some tool for viewing or interacting with #$TheCycOntology and adding some #$CycLExpression to the tool.  For instance, after a search within a tool returns a number of results and only a few are satisfactory, if one of the instances of #$CycLExpression is selected for further work, then that event would be an instance of this collection.")
(comment CycLOperator "A #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerm (q.v.).  Characterized syntactically, this is the collection of all and only those CycL terms that can appear as the main operator (i.e. in the \"arg0\" position) of a #$CycLClosedFormula. Characterized semantically, this is also the collection of all and only those CycL terms that denote #$Relations (q.v.) -- i.e. that denote #$Function-Denotationals, #$Predicates, #$LogicalConnectives, #$Quantifiers, or other #$ScopingRelations.  
<p>
Examples of #$CycLOperators include these four CycL expressions:
<pre>
  #$genls
  #$and
  #$PlusFn 
  (#$FunctionToArg 2 #$biologicalFather)  
</pre>
These examples reflect the fact that while most #$CycLOperators encountered in practice are #$CycLConstants, an operator might be a #$CycLNonAtomicTerm.  
<p>
It is important to distinguish a given CycL operator from the relation it denotes.  The former is a specific expression of the CycL language, while the latter is an abstract relation.  To refer to a particular CycL operator <i>with 
a CycL term</i>, one can <i>quote</i> the operator by wrapping it with the syncategorematic expression <code>#$Quote</code> (q.v.).  Thus the CycL sentence
<pre>
  (#$denotes (#$Quote #$genls) #$genls)
</pre>
means that the CycL operator <code>#$genls</code> denotes the #$genls (or collection-subsumption) relation.
<p>
Specializations of #$CycLOperator include #$CycLFunctor and #$CycLPredicator.")
(comment CycLFormulaicSentence "A subcollection of syntactically well-formed sentences of the CycL language. Each instance of #$CycLFormulaicSentence consists of a CycL expression denoting an instance of #$TruthFunction (e.g., an instance of #$Predicate or #$SententialRelation) followed by one or more CycL terms (see #$CycLTerm), with the entire sequence enclosed in parentheses. For example, (#$isa #$Collection #$Thing) and (#$genls ?FOO #$SpatialThing) are both formulaic CycL sentences. The only two CycL sentences that are not formulaic are #$True and #$False. Note that #$CycLFormulaicSentences need not obey arity constraints (see #$arity) or other semantic constraints (such as argument type constraints ; see #$ArgTypePredicate). #$CycLFormulaicSentences are also called \"logical formulas\", and are to be distinguished from \"denotational formulas\" (which are also known as \"NAT\"s; see #$CycLNonAtomicTerms). Note that this notion of a formulaic CycL sentence is broader than the notion of \"sentence\" standardly used in formal logic, where a sentence is defined as a _closed_ well-formed formula: formulaic CycL sentences may be _open_ (i.e., contain free variables; see #$CycLOpenSentence).")
(disjointWith CycLTruthValueSentence CycLFormulaicSentence)
(comment operatorFormulas "A non-assertible #$MetaLanguagePredicate and specialization of #$termFormulas (q.v.) that relates CycL operators to CycL formulas in which they occur as the main operator.  <code>(#$operatorFormulas OPERATOR FORMULA)</code> means that <code>OPERATOR</code> occurs as the main operator (i.e. in the \"arg0\" position) of <code>FORMULA</code>.  
<p>
Note that both argument-places of #$operatorFormulas are implicitly \"quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument). So if the above #$operatorFormulas sentence is closed (see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence), the two CycL expressions it mentions, <code>OPERATOR</code> and <code>FORMULA</code>, actually <i>appear in</i> that sentence -- as names of themselves.  Thus, the sentence 

<pre>  (#$operatorFormulas #$bordersOn (#$bordersOn #$France #$Germany))</pre> 

is true, due to the manifest fact that the <i>term</i> <code>#$bordersOn</code> occurs as the main operator in the sentence <code>(#$bordersOn #$France #$Germany)</code>. 
<p>
Note that <code>FORMULA</code> in the above need not be true (if it's a sentence) or even semantically well-formed.")
(comment DedicationCeremony "The collection of instances of #$OpeningCeremony in which a focal object, typically a building, a monument, or historical marker, is officially \"introduced\" and made available to the public.")
(comment openingCeremonyFor "(#$openingCeremonyFor CEREMONY FOCAL-OBJECT) means that CEREMONY is the instance of #$OpeningCeremony that \"introduces\" or \"kicks off\" the instance of #$Individual, FOCAL-OBJECT.  Where FOCAL-OBJECT is an event, such as a conference or a sport season, CEREMONY will typically be the first sub-event (see #$firstSubEvents) or start shortly before FOCAL-OBJECT.  Where FOCAL-OBJECT is an instance of #$Artifact, so that CEREMONY is an instance of #$DedicationCeremony, the temporal extent of FOCAL-OBJECT will typically start prior to CEREMONY.")
(comment ceremonyDedicatesArtifact "(#$ceremonyDedicatesArtifact CEREMONY ARTIFACT) means that ARTIFACT is the instance of #$Artifact -- typically, a building, monument, park, or other strucutre -- that is officially dedicated, or presented to the public, in the instance of #$DedicationCeremony, CEREMONY.")
(comment MetaphoricalSpeech "Word senses of words or phrases which may be considered metaphorical. Metaphor is a figure of speech in which a comparison is made between two seemingly unlike objects. See also #$Simile-FigureOfSpeech.")
(comment Simile-FigureOfSpeech "Phrases or sentences which may be considered similes. A simile is an explicit comparison between two things, often using a connectice such as 'like'. See also #$MetaphoricalSpeech.")
(comment FigureOfSpeech "The collection of figures of speech, or rhetorical devices.")
(comment EuphemisticSpeech "The collection of words or phrases that can be considered euphemistic. Euphemism involves substitution of a less offensive word or phrase for the original term.")
(comment rhetoricalDeviceOfPhrase "(#$rhetoricalDeviceOfPhrase ASRTN FIG) means that the word or phrase whose meaning is given by the #$CycLNLSemanticAssertion ASRTN is being used as the type of figure of speech indicated by FIG.")
(comment DisconnectedSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$CompositeSpaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of non-self-connected space regions.  A homogeneous extended space region is an instance of #$DisconnectedSpaceRegion if and only if it is the sum (see #$spaceRegionSum) of two (non necessarily self-connected) parts that are disconnected from each other (see #$disconnectedSpaceRegions). 
<p>
For example, the geographical space of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica 
is a disconnected region; the space of #$Alaska-State (e.g.) is a part that is disconnected from the rest of the US-space.  Conversely, the space of #$ContiguousUnitedStates is itself <i>not</i> a disconnected region, but a connected one (cf. #$ConnectedSpaceRegion).
<p>
For regions that are <i>totally disconnected</i>, see the specialization #$ScatteredSpaceRegion.")
(comment diseaseTypeSymptomTypes-NecessaryAndSufficient "A specialization of #$diseaseTypeSymptomTypes-Necessary (q.v.) that relates #$DiseaseTypes to #$PhysiologicalConditionTypes that constitute their necessary and sufficient symptoms.  <code>(#$diseaseTypeSymptomTypes-NecessaryAndSufficient DISTYPE SYMPTYPE)</code> means that both (i) each case of <code>DISTYPE</code> involves (as #$properSubSituations) one or more occurrences of <code>SYMPTYPE</code> and (ii) no occurrence of <code>SYMPTYPE</code> is a proper sub-situation of a case of any other type of disease than <code>DISTYPE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$diseaseTypeSymptomTypes-NecessaryAndSufficient #$Asthma #$AsthmaAttack)</code> holds.
<p>
Occurrences <code>SYMPTYPE</code> are typically more readily identifiable than the occurrences of <code>DISTYPE</code> that subsume them; hence their usefulfulness in diagnosis and in gauging treatment effectiveness.  But note that this is <i>not</i> part of the definition of the predicate #$diseaseTypeSymptomTypes-NecessaryAndSufficient.")
(comment cycLFound "An expression of the form (#$cycLFound SEARCH CYCL) means that during the instance of #$SearchingAnInformationStore SEARCH< the quoted #$CycLExpression CYCL was found.  For instance, if SEARCH involved using the string 'person' to probe a #$CycKB, the constant '#$Person' were discovered, then '#$Person' would play the role #$cycLFound in SEARCH.")
(comment topicCycL "A very general predicate for relating instances of #$Situation to quoted instances of #$CycLExpression in the sense that the #$Situation is about that #$CycLExpression.  For instance, the reading of this comment has '#$topicCycL' in the role #$topicCycL.")
(comment cycLOutputs "An expression of the form (#$cycLOutputs CREATE CYCL) means that during the instance of #$IBOCreation CREATE, the quoted instance of #$CycLExpression CYCL was formulated. For example, the instance of #$CycLSentence-Askable that is formulated during an instance of #$ComposingACycQuery would play this role in that event.")
(comment UsingTheQueryLibraryTool "The collection of all events in which the #$CycBrowserQueryLibraryToolModule  is used.")
(comment queryVariableConstraints "(#$queryVariableConstraints QUERYFORMULA VARIABLE MEANINGSENTENCE) means that VARIABLE is an open or existentially bound
#$CycLVariable in the formula QUERYFORMULA, and MEANINGSENTENCE expresses the constraints on VARIABLE.")
(comment cultureAdheresToCode "(#$cultureAdheresToCode CULTURE CODE) means that CODE is a system of norms or an individual norm that is generally followed 
within CULTURE.  CODE may regard such things as morality, etiquette, honor, or religious observance -- see #$CodeOfConduct and its specializations.  Adherence to CODE helps determine the range of
acceptable behaviors within CULTURE, and so helps determine some of its
characteristic practices (see comment on #$HumanCulture). Note that a
non-conformist sub-culture of CULTURE (cf. #$subCultures-Unfriendly)
may repudiate some or all elements of COC, and in fact, doing so may
help define that sub-culture.")
(comment Quote "The symbol '#$Quote' is used in CycL as a device for referring to particular CycL expressions, in much the same way that quotation marks are often used in English in order to \"mention\" (rather than simply \"use\") an English word or phrase.  (And in much the same way that single-quotes are used in the preceding sentence in order to refer to a particular CycL symbol.)  If <code>EXPR</code> is a CycL expression, the term <code>(#$Quote EXPR)</code> -- formed by writing '#$Quote' followed by <code>EXPR</code> and enclosing the result in parentheses -- is interpreted as denoting <code>EXPR</code> itself, as opposed to whatever it is (if anything) that <code>EXPR</code> ordinarily denotes (i.e. what it denotes when not inside the scope of <code>#$Quote</code> or in a #$quotedArgument position).  For example, while the CycL constant <code>#$Plato</code> denotes a certain Greek philosopher, the compound expression <code>(#$Quote #$Plato)</code> denotes the term <code>#$Plato</code> itself.  Similarly, the expression <code>(#$Quote <b>?X</b>)</code> denotes the variable <code><b>?X</b></code>, and the expression <code>(#$Quote (#$isa #$Plato #$Philosopher))</code> denotes the sentence <code>(#$isa #$Plato #$Philosopher)</code>.
<p>
<code>#$Quote</code> is also used in conjunction with the symbol <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> (q.v.), as a device for making <i>general</i> statements about CycL expressions.  Let <code>EXPR = (E<sub>1</sub>...E<sub>n</sub>)</code> be a well-formed CycL expression whose <b>n</b> immediate constituents are the CycL expressions <code>E<sub>1</sub></code>, ..., and <code>E<sub>n</sub></code> (where <b>n</b> >= 1).  The expression 
<pre>
  (#$Quote EXPR) = (#$Quote (E<sub>1</sub>...E<sub>n</sub>))
</pre> 
-- consisting of the symbols <code>#$Quote</code>, <code>EXPR</code>, and a surrounding pair of parentheses -- is a term that denotes <code>EXPR</code>.  The expression (call it <i><b>EQ</b></i>) 
<pre>
  (#$Quote (E<sub>1</sub>...(#$EscapeQuote E<sub>i</sub>) ...E<sub>n</sub>)
</pre> 
(where 1 <= <b>i <= n</b>) is a term whose meaning is similar to <code>(#$Quote EXPR)</code>, except that any free occurrence of a variable in <code>E<sub>i</sub></code> remains free with respect to <b>EQ</b> as a whole.  If <code>E<sub>i</sub></code> contains no free variables, then <b>EQ</b> is a closed term (see #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerm) that denotes <code>EXPR</code>, and is equivalent to <code>(#$Quote EXPR)</code>.  If <code>E<sub>i</sub></code> does contain free variables, then <b>EQ</b> is an open term (see #$CycLOpenDenotationalTerm) in which exactly the same variables occur free; and any closed instantiation of <b>EQ</b> denotes the corresponding instantiation of <code>EXPR</code>.  (A <i>closed instantiation</i> of an open expression is any <i>closed</i> expression that results from simultaneously replacing each free occurrence of a variable in the open expression with some closed term, replacing any two occurrences of the same variable by the same closed term.)
<p>
For a simple example, the variable <code><b>?X</b></code> occurs free in the term
<pre>
  (#$Quote (#$EscapeQuote (#$MotherFn <b>?X</b>)) ;
</pre>
and one instantiation of this is the closed term
<pre>
  (#$Quote (#$EscapeQuote (#$MotherFn #$Plato)) ,
</pre>
which denotes the non-atomic term <code>(#$MotherFn #$Plato)</code>.  
<p>
For a more interesting example, consider a case where <i>some but not all</i> of the free variables in a term are quoted.  In the expression
<pre>
  (#$Quote (#$loves (#$EscapeQuote <b>?X</b>) <b>?Y</b>))
</pre>
only <code><b>?X</b></code> occurs free -- the <code><b>?Y</b></code> is quoted. Thus this instantiation of the above:
<pre>
  (#$Quote #$loves (#$EscapeQuote #$Plato) <b>?Y</b>)
</pre>
is a closed term that denotes the <i>open</i> formula <code>(#$loves #$Plato <b>?Y</b>)</code>.  
<p>
Note that the sort of quotation done in the first example above can also be done -- and done more elegantly -- using just #$QuasiQuote instead of #$Quote and #$EscapeQuote.  But the sort of quotation done in the second example cannot be accomplished with #$QuasiQuote.
<p>
See also #$QuasiQuote, #$EscapeQuote, #$denotes, #$quotedIsa, #$quotedArgument, #$NoteAboutQuotingInCycL, and #$NoteAboutSyncategorematicSymbols.")
(comment forbidUserEntryOnPosition "(#$forbidUserEntryOnPosition TEMPLATE POS) means that, for TEMPLATE, the user is unable to type anything into replaceable position POS. This has the effect of restricting the user's actions to making choices from the drop-down menu, which is useful in many situations. Note, using this predicate on a position in conjunction with #$templateReplacementsInvisibleForPosition creates a replaceable position that can take no input at all.")
(comment investigationFocusSentence "(#$investigationFocusSentence INVESTIGATION SENT) means that determining the truth value of SENT -- or, if SENT is an open sentence, what #$Tuples satisfy SENT -- is the primary focus of INVESTIGATION.
<p>
For example, if Investigation01 is an investigation to determine who committed some #$CriminalAct Crime01, the following would be true:
<p>
(#$investigationFocusSentence Investigation01 (#$perpetrator Crime01 ?WHO)).")
(comment objectOfInvestigation "A #$BinaryRolePredicate that relates an #$Investigation INV to the #$Thing(s) that are being investigated in INV.")
(comment ProperSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$CompositeSpaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of \"proper regions\" in the sense used in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory (q.v.).  A homogeneous extended spatial region is an instance of #$ProperSpaceRegion if and only if it has another such region as a non-tangential part (see #$nontangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion).
<p>
Note that #$RCCContinuousSpaceMt assumes that this collection is coextensional with (the elsewhere more general collection) #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion.")
(comment IntermittentCollection "A specialization of both #$TimeDependentCollection and #$FirstOrderCollection (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all collections of which something can be an instance during (and only during) several non-overlapping periods.  
<p>
For example, #$FamousHuman is an intermittant collection, as a person might be famous as a child, fade into obscurity as a young adult, and then become famous again as an older adult.")
(comment UsingNooescape "A specialization of #$ComputerActivity.  Each instance of
#$UsingNooescape is an event in which a computer user logs into a
running copy of #$Nooescape-TheProgram and uses it in the ordinary way.  Because
Nooescape is an analytic environment designed to support typical
activity types of intelligence analysts, the pattern of Nooescape use
closely follows the pattern of analysis behaviors (see
#$IntelligenceAnalysisProcess).")
(comment displayedCycL "An expression of the form (#$displayedCycL DIS EXPRESSION) means that during the #$VisualInformationGeneratingAct DIS includes a visual representation (e.g., in the form of some #$NaturalLanguage) of the (quoted)  #$CycLExpression EXPRESSION during the event.")
(comment displaysCycLAsString "An expression of the form (#$displaysCycLAsString EVT CYCL STRING) means that during the #$VisualInformationGeneratingAct EVT, the (quoted) #$CycLExpression CYCL is represented in the form of the #$CharacterString STRING.")
(comment parsesStringToCycL "An expression of the form (#$parsesStringToCycL EVT STRING CYCL) means that during the #$IBTTranslating EVT, the #$CharacterString STRING is parsed to the (quoted) #$CycLExpression CYCL.")
(comment selectedCycL "An expression of the form (#$selectedCycL SEL EXPRESSION) means that the quoted #$CycLExpression EXPRESSION is represented via some representation or actual instrument that a user interacts with during the instance of #$SelectingSomething SEL.  For example, if a person clicks on an aspect of a GUI display that represents an NL translation of EXPRESSION, although the user may be ignorant of the existence or nature of EXPRESSION, the selection of EXPRESSION does describe their behavior.  See")
(comment aisActors "A generic predicate for establishing a relationship between an instance of #$Situation and an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure.")
(comment ComposingACycLQuerySentence "A FirstOrderCollection and a sub-collection of #$CreationEvent. #$ComposingACycLQuerySentence is the set of all events in which some representation of a #$CycLSentence-Askable is formulated.")
(comment ComposingACycQuery "A #$FirstOrderCollection and a sub-collection of #$IBTGeneration.  #$ComposingACycQuery is the set of all events in which some representation of a #$CycLQuerySpecification is formulated in some representational medium.")
(comment SwagingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShapingSomething. In each #$SwagingSomething, someone forms metals with a swage.")
(comment BlurringSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$BlurringSomething, something makes something else dim or indistinct.")
(comment AffectingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$AffectingSomething, something has an effect upon something else.")
(comment ContaminatingSomethingWithRadioactivity "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ContaminationEvent. In each #$ContaminatingSomethingWithRadioactivity, something makes something else radioactive by adding radioactive material.")
(comment Chucking-AbandoningSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AbandoningSomething. In each #$Chucking-AbandoningSomething, someone throws away something.")
(comment CatchingSomething-Hearing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Hearing. In each #$CatchingSomething-Hearing, someone perceives something by hearing.")
(comment Dunking-EatingEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EatingEvent. In each #$Dunking-EatingEvent, something dips something else into a liquid while eating.")
(comment LiftingAnObject "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion. In each #$LiftingAnObject, something takes hold of something and moves it to a different location.")
(comment Commanding-CommunicationAct "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Ordering-CommunicationAct. In each #$Commanding-CommunicationAct, something makes someone do something.")
(comment CuttingSomething-CreationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CreationEvent. In each #$CuttingSomething-CreationEvent, something forms something else by probing, penetrating, or digging.")
(comment Acing-ScoringPoints "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ScoringPoints. In each #$Acing-ScoringPoints, someone scores an ace against someone or scores very well in a competition.")
(comment CommercializingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CommercialActivity. In each #$CommercializingSomething, someone makes something commercial.")
(comment ShootingAndHittingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShootingAProjectileWeapon. In each #$ShootingAndHittingSomething, someone hits something with a missile from a weapon.")
(comment RidingSomething-SexualCopulation "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SexualCopulation. In each #$RidingSomething-SexualCopulation, some animal copulates with another animal by  mounting it.")
(comment Sounding-AnnouncingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AnnouncingSomething. In each #$Sounding-AnnouncingSomething, someone announces something by means of a sound.")
(comment WritingOutSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Writing. In each #$WritingOutSomething, someone makes something out and issues it.")
(comment ChasteningSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$ChasteningSomeone, someone restrains or tempers someone else.")
(comment ProjectingSomething-Drawing-Depicting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Drawing-Depicting. In each #$ProjectingSomething-Drawing-Depicting, someone draws a projection of something.")
(comment TouchingSomethingBriefly "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TouchingEvent. In each #$TouchingSomethingBriefly, someone causes something to be in brief contact with something something else.")
(comment TippingSomething-MarkingOnASurface "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MarkingOnASurface. In each #$TippingSomething-MarkingOnASurface, something marks something else with a tip.")
(comment TyingMusicalNotesTogether "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ConnectingTogether. In each #$TyingMusicalNotesTogether, someone unites musical notes by a tie.")
(comment SpendingExtravagantly "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SpendingMoney. In each #$SpendingExtravagantly, someone spends extravagantly.")
(comment ImpoundingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TakingSomething. In each #$ImpoundingSomething, someone takes temporary possession of as a security, by legal authority.")
(comment DefendingSomethingAgainstAttack "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ProtectingSomething. In each #$DefendingSomethingAgainstAttack, someone protects something against a challenge or attack.")
(comment DraftingSomething-WritingAComposition "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WritingAComposition. In each #$DraftingSomething-WritingAComposition, someone draws up an outline or sketch for something.")
(comment CuttingSomething-HittingAnObject "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HittingAnObject. In each #$CuttingSomething-HittingAnObject, someone hits (a ball) with a spin so that it turns in the opposite direction.")
(comment ConfusingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$ConfusingSomething, something makes something else unclear, indistinct, or blurred.")
(comment Declaring-Evaluating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Evaluating. In each #$Declaring-Evaluating, something declares something else to be or some proposition to hold.")
(comment Stamping-ShapingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShapingSomething. In each #$Stamping-ShapingSomething, someone forms or cuts out something else with a mold, form, or die.")
(comment Nodding-Gesture "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingAGesture. In each #$NoddingOnesHead, someone expresses or signifies something by nodding.")
(comment Bestowing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GivingSomething. In each #$Bestowing, someone gives something else as a gift.")
(comment Piling-PuttingSomethingSomewhere "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingSomethingSomewhere. In each #$Piling-PuttingSomethingSomewhere, someone places or lays something as if in a pile.")
(comment Hitting-CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion. In each #$Hitting-CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion, something drives something else violently into a location.")
(comment TransmittingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion. In each #$TransmittingSomething, something sends something else from one person or place to another.")
(comment ShieldingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ProtectingSomething. In each #$ShieldingSomething, something protects, hides, or conceals something else from danger or harm.")
(comment Waltz "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Dancing-Human. In each #$Waltz, someone dances a waltz.")
(comment Wishing-GreetingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GreetingSomeone. In each #$Wishing-GreetingSomeone, someone invokes something upon someone else while greeting.")
(comment WorkingSomething-ProcessingEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ProcessingEvent. In each #$WorkingSomething-ProcessingEvent, someone shapes, forms, or improves a material.")
(comment ScavengingSomething-Cleaning "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Cleaning. In each #$ScavengingSomething-Cleaning, something removes unwanted substances from something else.")
(comment CallingUpSomething-RequestingPresence "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RequestingPresence. In each #$CallingUpSomething-RequestingPresence, someone calls something to arms; especially military personnel.")
(comment PuttingSomethingIntoACertainState "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$PuttingSomethingIntoACertainState, something causes something else to be in a certain state; causes something else to be in a certain relation.")
(comment SeizingAnObjective "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MilitaryAttack. In each #$SeizingAnObjective, someone takes or captures something by force.")
(comment StretchingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GrowthEvent. In each #$StretchingSomething, something becomes longer by being stretched and pulled.")
(comment ElasticColliding "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Colliding. In each #$ElasticColliding, something springs back; springs away from an impact.")
(comment Testifying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Informing. In each #$Testifying, something provides evidence for something else.")
(comment ObscuringSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HidingAnObject. In each #$ObscuringSomething, something makes something else less visible or unclear.")
(comment GraspingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HoldingAnObject. In each #$GraspingSomething, something holds something else firmly.")
(comment ExtendingSomething-MakingSomethingAvailable "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$ExtendingSomething-MakingSomethingAvailable, someone makes something available; provides something.")
(comment HonkingSomething-EmittingSound "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EmittingSound. In each #$HonkingSomething-EmittingSound, something makes a loud noise.")
(comment Jolt-Physical "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PhysicalEvent. In each #$Jolt-Physical, someone moves or cause to move with a sudden jerky motion.")
(comment RecallingSomeone-RequestingPresence "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RequestingPresence. In each #$RecallingSomeone-RequestingPresence, someone summons someone else to return.")
(comment VoiceBreaking "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$VoiceBreaking, a male's voice in puberty permanently changes to a deeper tone.")
(comment FracturingABone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IncurringAnInjury. In each #$FracturingABone, someone fractures a bone.")
(comment Lending "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TransferringPossession. In each #$Lending, someone gives something temporarily; someone lets someone else have something for a limited time.")
(comment Traumatizing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IncurringAnInjury. In each #$Traumatizing, something inflicts a trauma upon something.")
(comment HittingSomethingAsIfByWhipping "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HittingAnObject. In each #$HittingSomethingAsIfByWhipping, something strikes something else as if by whipping.")
(comment BreakingTheCompletenessOfSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$BreakingTheCompletenessOfSomething, someone destroys the completeness of a set of related items.")
(comment Clearing-RemovingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RemovingSomething. In each #$Clearing-RemovingSomething, someone removes something.")
(comment Kissing-TouchingLightly "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TouchingEvent. In each #$Kissing-TouchingLightly, something touches something else lightly or gently.")
(comment SuggestedSentenceEvaluationsMtFn "(#$SuggestedSentenceEvaluationsMtFn MT) creates a microtheory in which evaluation assertions for suggested sentences can be added.  MT is the microtheory from which the suggested sentences are drawn.  Typically, the evaluation assertions are added by the Suggested Sentence Review tool in Cyc.")
(comment suggestedSentenceEvaluationsMt "This is the corresponding predicate of the function #$SuggestedSentenceEvaluationsMtFn.")
(disjointWith HighReliefSculpture BasReliefSculpture)
(comment Relief-Sculpture "The collection of sculptures whose figures are attached to a background.")
(disjointWith Relief-Sculpture SculptureInTheRound)
(comment HighReliefSculpture "The collection of instances of #$Relief-Sculpture in which the a significant portion of the sculpted figures \"emerges\" from the background in a way that allows for a much greater range of viewability from different positions than can be achieved in a low-relief (c.f., #$BasReliefSculpture).")
(comment BasReliefSculpture "The collection of bas-relief sculptures.  Bas-reliefs, or low-reliefs are instances of #$Relief-Sculpture in which the portion of the sculpted figures that \"emerges\" from the background is very limited, resulting in very low variation in the range of positions from which the figures can be viewed.  Contrast with #$HighReliefSculpture.")
(comment ReliefBust "The collection of instances of #$Bust-Sculpture that are also instances of #$Relief-Sculpture, such as found on almost any modern US coin.")
(comment SpillingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FluidFlow-Translation. In each #$SpillingSomething, something flows, runs or falls out and becomes lost.")
(comment HyperventilatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MedicalTreatmentEvent. In each #$HyperventilatingSomeone, someone produces hyperventilation in someone.")
(comment Aspirating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Inhaling. In each #$Aspirating, someone sucks in air.")
(comment InsufflatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BlowingAir. In each #$InsufflatingSomething, someone blows or breathes hard on or into something.")
(comment Batting-Blinking "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Blinking. In each #$Batting-Blinking, someone winks briefly.")
(comment ReawakeningSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WakingUpFromSleep. In each #$ReawakeningSomeone, someone awakens once again.")
(comment EntrancingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HypnotizingSomeone. In each #$EntrancingSomeone, someone puts someone else into a trance.")
(comment EtherizingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Anesthesia. In each #$EtherizingSomeone, someone anesthetizes someone else with ether.")
(comment CocainizingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Anesthesia. In each #$CocainizingSomeone, someone anesthetizes someone else with cocaine.")
(comment ChloroformingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Anesthesia. In each #$ChloroformingSomeone, someone anesthetizes someone else with chloroform.")
(comment FreezingSomeone-Anesthesia "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Anesthesia. In each #$FreezingSomeone-Anesthesia, someone anesthetizes someone else by cold.")
(comment SomeoneZonkingOut-BodilyFunctionEvent-Involuntary "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BodilyFunctionEvent-Involuntary. In each #$SomeoneZonkingOut-BodilyFunctionEvent-Involuntary, someone loses consciousness due to a sudden trauma, for example.")
(comment RefreshingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$RefreshingSomething, something is made fresh again.")
(comment ExsertingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingAGesture. In each #$ExsertingSomething, some animal thrusts or extends out something as a gesture.")
(comment Shrugging-Gesture "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingAGesture. In each #$Shrugging-Gesture, someone raises their shoulders to indicate indifference or resignation.")
(comment ScrewingUp-Grimacing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Grimacing. In each #$ScrewingUp-Grimacing, someone twists their face into a strained configuration.")
(comment FomentingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TakingABath. In each #$FomentingSomething, someone bathes something with warm water or medicated lotions.")
(comment EpilatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PersonalGrooming. In each #$EpilatingSomething, someone removes body hair.")
(comment ShavingSomethingWithARazor "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShavingBody. In each #$ShavingSomethingWithARazor, someone shaves something with a razor.")
(comment TonsuringSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShavingBody. In each #$TonsuringSomeone, someone shaves the head of a newly inducted monk.")
(comment ManicuringSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CuttingSomething. In each #$ManicuringSomething, someone trims something carefully and neatly.")
(comment GainingBodyWeight "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$GainingBodyWeight, someone increases (their body weight).")
(comment BundlingUpSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$BundlingUpSomeone, someone dresses warmly.")
(comment WearingSomething-PuttingOnAClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$WearingSomething-PuttingOnAClothingItem, someone puts clothing on their body.")
(comment Dandify-PuttingOnAClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$Dandify-PuttingOnAClothingItem, someone dresses like a dandy.")
(comment InseminatingAFemaleAnimal "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ReproductiveFertilizationEvent. In each #$InseminatingAFemaleAnimal, someone introduces semen into (a female).")
(comment ConvexSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of convex regions as defined in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory (q.v.).  A homogeneous extended space region <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$ConvexSpaceRegion if and only if any two parts of (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) <code>REGION</code> contain the respective endpoints of a straight line-segment that is itself wholly contained in <code>REGION</code>.
<p>
For example, the space occupied by a rubber ball is a convex region, but that occupied by a fork or a can of soda are not.
<p>
Because the notion of the region delineated by the hull of a space
region (see #$ConvexSpaceRegionFn) is a primitive notion in RCC, we axiomatize convex regions as those #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegions that are equal to their own convex region; i.e. (#$convexSpaceRegionOf <code>REGION REGION</code>).")
(disjointWith NaturalFeatureType ArtifactualFeatureType)
(comment TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical "An instance of #$UniversalSpaceRegion-3D and #$SpaceChunk-Empirical (qq.v.).  This is the intangible universal three-dimensional space region in which #$ThePhysicalUniverse (q.v.) is exactly located. It spatially subsumes every #$SpaceRegion-Empirical and has every #$SpaceChunk-Empirical as a space-region-part (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).")
(comment UniversalSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$ConvexSpaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of spatial universes. There is at most one known instance of #$UniversalSpaceRegion with respect to any context in which some #$SpatialThings are known to have locations, either absolutely or relatively (see #$LocationalPredicate and its immediate specializations).   
<p>
Each #$UniversalSpaceRegion is extended in some particular number of dimensions (see #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions).  See the specializations #$UniversalSpaceRegion-1D, #$UniversalSpaceRegion-2D, #$UniversalSpaceRegion-3D.
<p>
The spatial universe <code>UNI</code> of a given context #$spatiallySubsumes every known spatial thing of the same or lower dimensionality as <code>UNI</code>, and every known #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion of the same dimensionality as <code>UNI</code> is a #$partOfSpaceRegion <code>UNI</code>.")
(disjointWith UniversalSpaceRegion HomogeneousExtendedNonUniversalSpaceRegion)
(comment HomogeneousExtendedNonUniversalSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of homogeneous extended space regions other than #$UniversalSpaceRegions.")
(comment iGPertinentPredForTypeInArg "(#$iGPertinentPredForTypeInArg PRED COL N) means that when an #$InformationGathering IG bears #$infoGatheredAbout to an instance INST of COL, the sentence resulting from placing INST in arg N of PRED (and filling all other argument positions with distinct free variables) is likely to be pertinent to IG.")
(comment keRelevantInverse "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate (q.v.) that is (along with #$keRelevantPreds) the most general of those that relate a collection to a binary predicate.  (#$keRelevantInverse COL BINPRED) means that for any reified instance INST of COL, if there is anything THING such that BINPRED holds between THING and INST, asserting (BINPRED THING INST) in the Cyc Knowledge Base is worth considering and perhaps advisable.  But note: the specializations of #$keRelevantInverse are #$keWeakSuggestionInverse, #$keStrongSuggestionInverse, #$keConsiderationInverse, and #$keStrongConsiderationInverse (qq.v.); and one should use one of these instead of #$keRelevantInverse itself whenever possible.  In fact, #$keRelevantInverse was created mainly for use in situations where one wants to say something general about all of these specializations.")
(comment ConceivingSomething-BiologicalReproductionEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent. In each #$ConceivingSomething-BiologicalReproductionEvent, someone becomes pregnant; undergoes conception.")
(comment SomePlantFructifying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent. In each #$SomePlantFructifying, some plant bears fruit.")
(comment SomeBirdBroodingEggs "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent. In each #$SomeBirdBroodingEggs, some bird sits on eggs.")
(comment WritingPen "A specialization of #$WritingImplement.  Each instance is a stick with a point at one end which can transmit ink to a writing surface.")
(comment selectedCycLViaStringRep "An expression of the form (#$selectedCycLViaStringRep EVT CYCL STRING) means that during the #$SelectingSomething EVT, the #$CycLExpression CYCL was indicated (perhaps without their knowing) via some representation of the #$CharacterString STRING.  For example, if a graphical user interface displays the string 'dog' as the generation for the #$CycLConstant #$Dog this relation would hold between that event, the string that is represented in the GUI and #$Dog.")
(comment BeginningToInjureSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActionOnObject. In each #$BeginningToInjureSomething, something begins to injure something else.")
(comment SedatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActionOnObject. In each #$SedatingSomething, someone causes some animimal to be calm or quiet as by administering a sedative.")
(comment StimulatingSomeAnimal "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AnimalActivity. In each #$StimulatingSomeAnimal, something causes some animal to be alert and energetic.")
(comment HyperextendingAJoint "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExsertingSomething. In each #$HyperextendingAJoint, someone extends a joint beyond its normal range.")
(comment Smirking "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Smiling. In each #$Smirking, someone smiles affectedly or derisively.")
(comment SweatingOffWeight "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WeightLosingProcess. In each #$SweatingOffWeight, someone loses weight by sweating.")
(comment TryingOnAClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WearingSomething-PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$TryingOnAClothingItem, someone puts on a garment in order to see whether it fits and looks nice.")
(comment Covering-PuttingOnAClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$Covering-PuttingOnAClothingItem, someone puts on clothes, as if for protection from the elements.")
(comment PuttingOnAJacket "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnAJacket, someone puts a jacket on someone.")
(comment PuttingOnAFrock "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnAFrock, someone puts a frock on someone.")
(comment PuttingOnAShirt "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnAShirt, someone puts a shirt on someone.")
(comment PuttingOnAHabit "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnAHabit, someone puts a habit on someone.")
(comment CoveringWithACloak "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$CoveringWithACloak, someone provides or covers someone with a cloak.")
(comment OverdressingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$OverdressingSomeone, someone dresses excessively.")
(comment PuttingOnACorset "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnACorset, someone dresses with a corset.")
(comment PeelingOff-TakingOffAClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TakingOffAClothingItem. In each #$PeelingOff-TakingOffAClothingItem, someone takes off clothing, as with some difficulty.")
(comment WrappingWithAScarf "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WearingSomething-PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$WrappingWithAScarf, someone wraps something in a scarf or adorns something with a scarf.")
(comment SlippingOnSomeClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WearingSomething-PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$SlippingOnSomeClothingItem, someone puts some clothing item on with ease or speed.")
(comment SlippingOffSomeClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TakingOffAClothingItem. In each #$SlippingOffSomeClothingItem, someone takes some clothing item off with ease or speed.")
(comment PuttingOnACoatClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnACoatClothingItem, someone covers or provides someone with a coat.")
(comment PuttingOnACostume "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnACostume, someone dresses someone in a costume.")
(comment PuttingOnAFormalRobe "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnAFormalRobe, someone clothes formally; especially someone in ecclesiastical robes.")
(comment PuttingOnAnEcclesiasticalGarment "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnAnEcclesiasticalGarment, someone clothes someone in ecclesiastical garments.")
(comment StratifyingSeeds-ReproductiveFertilizationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ReproductiveFertilizationEvent. In each #$StratifyingSeeds-ReproductiveFertilizationEvent, someone renders seeds fertile and preserves them by placing between layers of earth or sand.")
(comment ImpregnatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InseminatingAFemaleAnimal. In each #$ImpregnatingSomeone, someone male animal makes some female animal pregnant.")
(comment ImpregnatingAnEgg "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InseminatingAFemaleAnimal. In each #$ImpregnatingAnEgg, someone fertilizes an egg and causes it to grow.")
(comment CrossFertilizingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ReproductiveFertilizationEvent. In each #$CrossFertilizingSomething, someone causes some living thing to undergo cross-fertilization.")
(comment PollinatingSomePlant "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ReproductiveFertilizationEvent. In each #$PollinatingSomePlant, something fertilizes some plant by transfering pollen.")
(comment SomeAnimalBreeding "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent. In each #$SomeAnimalBreeding, something has young (animals).")
(comment removedCycLViaStringRep "An expression of the form (#$removedCycLViaStringRep EVT CYCL STRING) means that during the #$RemovingACycLTermFromATool EVT, the #$CycLExpression CYCL was removed from a tool (perhaps without their knowing) via the removal or deletion of representation of the #$CharacterString STRING.  For example, if a graphical user interface displays the string 'fact' as the generation for the #$CycLExpression  (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn #$Thing #$ist #$Microtheory)   this relation would hold between that event, the string that is represented in the GUI and (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn #$Thing #$ist #$Microtheory).")
(comment infoGatheredAbout-Secondary "A #$BinaryPredicate and specialization of #$infoGatheredAbout that relates an instance of #$InformationGathering to a #$Thing.  (#$infoGatheredAbout-Secondary IG THING) means that IG involves gathering information about THING, but only because THING is relevant to something else that is the primary focus of the information gathering.  Contrast this with #$infoGatheredAbout-Primary.")
(comment JapaneseAffix "The set of lexical items which may be concatenated with other morphemes in the inflectional or derivational morphology of Japanese.")
(comment LinguisticConceptExtraction "A #$FirstOrderCollection and a sub-collection of #$LinguisticTranslationProcess.  #$LinguisticConceptExtraction is the collection of all events in which the elements of some representation using some #$Language are converted into representations in a different #$Language, with the end result not being made up of units that are smaller than complete sentences, even if the source representations do contain complete sentences.  For instance, a process that took the English term 'bulldog' from the phrase 'the pugnacious bulldog'  and mapped it to #$CycLConstant #$Bulldog would be an instance of the collection. This is collection does not assume that the events are successful.")
(comment LinguisticTranslationProcess "A sub-collection of #$PurposefulAction in which the content of an expression in one #$Language (either natural or artificial) is converted into an expression of a different #$Language. This is a generic collection. It is (1)  neutral on the completion of the process and its semantic accuracy and (2) neutral on the categories of the linguistic objects are the inputs and outputs of the process (i.e., words versus phrases versus sentences).")
(comment LanguageProcessing "The collection of all events in which representations of one #$Language undergo some form of processing to extract some information from representations of some #$Language and putting that extracted information into some new representational form. The range of possible output representations is not limited to linguistic representations.")
(comment SemanticParsing "A #$FirstOrderCollection and a sub-collection of #$LinguisticTranslationProcess.  Instances of this collection involve taking representations of sentences in one #$Language and converting them into representations of sentences in another #$Language.  This process relies on some mapping to the underlying semantics of the terms in the language in order to complete this process.")
(comment SyntacticParsing "A #$FirstOrderCollection and a sub-collection of #$LanguageProcessing.  Instances of this collection involve taking some representation of a sentence in one #$Language and developing a formal representation of that sentence's syntactic structure.")
(comment CathectingSomeAnimal "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$StimulatingSomeAnimal. In each #$CathectingSomeAnimal, something injects some animal with libidinal energy.")
(comment AnimatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$StimulatingSomeAnimal. In each #$AnimatingSomething, someone gives new life or energy to some animal.")
(comment UnbendingSomething-Resting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Resting-Relaxing. In each #$UnbendingSomething-Resting, someone releases something from mental strain, tension, or formality.")
(comment InvigoratingSomeAnimal "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$StimulatingSomeAnimal. In each #$InvigoratingSomeAnimal, something imparts vigor, strength, or vitality to some animal.")
(comment InoculatingSomething-VaccinationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$VaccinationEvent. In each #$InoculatingSomething-VaccinationEvent, someone impregnates some living thing with the virus or germ of a disease in order to render it immune.")
(comment Pullulating-SomeAnimalBreeding "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SomeAnimalBreeding. In each #$Pullulating-SomeAnimalBreeding, some animal breeds freely and abundantly.")
(comment IndisposedCondition "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$PhysiologicalCondition. In each #$IndisposedCondition, something causes someone to feel unwell.")
(comment SomeAnimalSuperfetating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ConceivingSomething-BiologicalReproductionEvent. In each #$SomeAnimalSuperfetating, some animal conceives when a fetus is already present in the uterus.")
(comment ExudingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Excreting. In each #$ExudingSomething, something releases (a liquid) in drops or small quantities.")
