(comment GoverningBehavior "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$GoverningBehavior, something directs or strongly influences the behavior of something.")
(comment RegimentingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$RegimentingSomething, someone subjects something to rigid discipline, order, and systematization.")
(comment MonopolizingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$MonopolizingSomething, someone has and control fully and exclusively.")
(comment Harnessing-ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$Harnessing-ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething, someone controls and directs with or as if by reins.")
(comment Cornering-ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$Cornering-ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething, someone gains control over something.")
(comment BuildingSomething-Supervising "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Supervising. In each #$BuildingSomething-Supervising, someone orders, supervises, or finances the construction of something.")
(comment FoundingAnOrgranizationByChoosingUpSides "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FoundingAnOrganization. In each #$FoundingAnOrgranizationByChoosingUpSides, someone forms sides, as for a game.")
(comment DrawingUp-FoundingAnOrganization "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FoundingAnOrganization. In each #$DrawingUp-FoundingAnOrganization, something forms or arranges members in order or formation, as of a body of soldiers.")
(comment Regimenting-FoundingAnOrganization "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FoundingAnOrganization. In each #$Regimenting-FoundingAnOrganization, someone forms (military personnel) into a regiment.")
(comment Syndicating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FoundingAnOrganization. In each #$Syndicating, someone organizes entities into a syndicate or forms a syndicate.")
(comment OrganizationsSyndicatingTogether "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ConnectingTogether. In each #$OrganizationsSyndicatingTogether, two or more organizations join together into a syndicate.")
(comment PrivilegingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GrantingPermission. In each #$PrivilegingSomeone, someone bestows a privilege upon someone.")
(comment HousingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShelteringSomething. In each #$HousingSomething, someone provides housing for something.")
(comment KennelingSomeDomesticAnimal "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShelteringSomething. In each #$KennelingSomeDomesticAnimal, someone shelters some animal in a kennel.")
(comment StablingSomeHorse "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShelteringSomething. In each #$StablingSomeHorse, someone shelters some horse in a stable.")
(comment ShelterSomeHorseInAStall "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShelteringSomething. In each #$ShelterSomeHorseInAStall, someone shelters a horse in a stall.")
(comment TenantingSomething "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$InhabitingSomething. In each #$TenantingSomething, someone occupies something as a tenant.")
(comment CastingAVoteByWriteIn "A sub-collection of #$Voting.  Each instance is a vote cast by writing in the name of a candidate who is not listed on the ballot.")
(comment DefeatingSomethingByAMajorityOfVotes "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Voting. In each #$DefeatingSomethingByAMajorityOfVotes, something defeats something else by a majority of votes.")
(comment Dismissing-ExpellingFromMembership "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExpellingFromMembership. In each #$Dismissing-ExpellingFromMembership, someone is stopped from associating with some organization.")
(comment Shopping-ShowingSupportForSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShowingSupportForSomeone. In each #$Shopping-ShowingSupportForSomeone, someone does their shopping at something; does business with something; is a customer or client of something.")
(comment Liberalizing-PoliticalAction "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PoliticalAction. In each #$Liberalizing-PoliticalAction, someone makes some political entity liberal or more liberal with respect to laws and rules.")
(comment PartitioningSomething "A sub-collection of #$SeparationEvent.  Each instance is the act of dividing or partitioning something; separation by the creation of a boundary that divides or keeps apart.")
(comment SectionalizingIntoRegions "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PartitioningSomething. In each #$SectionalizingIntoRegions, someone divides something into sections, especially into geographic sections.")
(comment Triangulating-SeparationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SeparationEvent. In each #$Triangulating-SeparationEvent, someone divides something into triangles or gives a triangular form to something.")
(comment UnitizingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SeparationEvent. In each #$UnitizingSomething, someone divides (bulk material) and processes it as units.")
(comment DividingIntoLots "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SeparationEvent. In each #$DividingIntoLots, someone divides something into lots, as of land, for example.")
(comment Parcellation "A sub-collection of #$Separation-Complete.  Each instance is the division of something into parcels.")
(comment Splintering-SeparationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SeparationEvent. In each #$Splintering-SeparationEvent, something divides something else into slivers or splinters.")
(comment DividingIntoCantons "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SeparationEvent. In each #$DividingIntoCantons, someone divides a country into cantons.")
(comment BalkanizingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SeparationEvent. In each #$BalkanizingSomething, someone divides a territory into small, hostile states.")
(comment ActingInConcert "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$OrganizationalTransferIn. In each #$ActingInConcert, someone acts  in concert or unites in a common purpose or belief with someone else.")
(comment Registering-IBOCreation "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IBOCreation. In each #$Registering-IBOCreation, something records something else in writing; enters something into a book of names or events or transactions.")
(comment Canceling-Declaring-Evaluating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Declaring-Evaluating. In each #$Canceling-Declaring-Evaluating, someone declares something null and void; makes it ineffective.")
(comment LegalizingSomethingPreviouslyIllegal "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GrantingPermission. In each #$LegalizingSomethingPreviouslyIllegal, someone makes something legal that was previously illegal.")
(comment Trusting-GrantingPermission "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GrantingPermission. In each #$Trusting-GrantingPermission, someone allows something else without fear.")
(comment BurkingSomeone-Murder "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Murder. In each #$BurkingSomeone-Murder, someone murders someone else without leaving a trace on the body.")
(comment ExecutionByDrawingAndQuartering "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Torture. In each #$ExecutionByDrawingAndQuartering, someone pulls (a person) apart with four horses tied to his extremities, so as to execute him.")
(comment PilloryingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Punishing. In each #$PilloryingSomeone, someone punishes someone else by putting them in a pillory.")
(comment Pillorying-DisplayingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DisplayingSomething. In each #$Pillorying-DisplayingSomething, someone exposes someone else to ridicule or public scorn.")
(comment ExecutionStyledMurder "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Murder. In each #$ExecutionStyledMurder, someone murders someone else execution-style.")
(comment LynchingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$KillingByOrganism. In each #$LynchingSomeone, someone kills someone else without legal sanction.")
(comment ShootingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShootingAndHittingSomething. In each #$ShootingSomeone, someone kills someone else by firing a missile.")
(comment Electrocution-Execution "A sub-collection of #$Execution-Judicial.  Each instance is an execution by electricity.")
(comment Hanging-Execution-Judicial "A sub-collection of #$Execution-Judicial.  Each instance is a form of capital punishment; the victim is suspended by the neck from a gallows or gibbet until dead.")
(comment DoubleDating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GoingOnADate. In each #$DoubleDating, someone goes  out on a date with a partner and another couple.")
(comment Seeing-VisitingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$VisitingSomeone. In each #$Seeing-VisitingSomeone, someone goes to see someone else for professional or business reasons.")
(comment SeeingSocially-VisitingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$VisitingSomeone. In each #$SeeingSocially-VisitingSomeone, someone goes to see someone else for a social visit.")
(comment VisitingSomeoneInformally "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$VisitingSomeone. In each #$VisitingSomeoneInformally, someone visits informally and spontaneously.")
(comment Entertainment-RecreationalActivity "A sub-collection of #$RecreationalActivity.  Each instance is a diversion that holds the attention.")
(comment AmusingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HumanActivity. In each #$AmusingSomeone, someone occupies someone else in an agreeable, entertaining or pleasant fashion.")
(comment BailingOutSomeoneFromConfinement "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ReleasingFromConfinement. In each #$BailingOutSomeoneFromConfinement, someone frees someone else from confinement on bail.")
(comment SearchingWithoutWarning "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AttackOnTangible. In each #$SearchingWithoutWarning, someone searches something without warning, makes a sudden surprise attack on something.")
(comment SubjugatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DominatingSomething. In each #$SubjugatingSomeone, someone makes someone else subservient; forces someone to submit or subdue.")
(comment EmancipatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PoliticalAction. In each #$EmancipatingSomeone, someone gives equal rights to women and minorities.")
(comment BookingSomeone-MakingACriminalCharge "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingACriminalCharge. In each #$BookingSomeone-MakingACriminalCharge, someone records a charge in a police register.")
(comment CourtMartial "A sub-collection of #$Trial.  Each instance is a trial by court martial.")
(comment CastigatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Punishing. In each #$CastigatingSomeone, someone inflicts severe punishment on someone.")
(comment AmercingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Punishing. In each #$AmercingSomeone, someone punishes someone else with an arbitrary penalty.")
(comment VictimizingSomeone-Punishing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Punishing. In each #$VictimizingSomeone-Punishing, someone punishes someone else unjustly.")
(comment ScourgingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Punishing. In each #$ScourgingSomeone, someone punishes severely; excoriates.")
(comment ForcingSomeoneToLeave "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion. In each #$ForcingSomeoneToLeave, someone forces someone else to leave or move out.")
(comment RepatriatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PermittingEntrance. In each #$RepatriatingSomeone, someone admits someone else back into the country.")
(comment Readmiting-PermittingEntrance "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PermittingEntrance. In each #$Readmiting-PermittingEntrance, someone admits someone else anew into a place.")
(comment RepatriatingSomeone-Extradition "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Extradition. In each #$RepatriatingSomeone-Extradition, someone sends someone back to his homeland against his will, as of refugees.")
(comment GreatlyCoercingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CoercingAnAgent. In each #$GreatlyCoercingAnAgent, someone applies great or increased pressure to someone else.")
(comment Drive-PhysiologicalCondition "A sub-collection of #$PhysiologicalCondition.  Each instance is a physiological state corresponding to a strong need or desire.")
(comment RelentlesslyCoercingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CoercingAnAgent. In each #$RelentlesslyCoercingAnAgent, someone compels or forces or urges relentlessly or exerts coercive pressure on, or motivates strongly someone else.")
(comment CrudelyCoercingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CoercingAnAgent. In each #$CrudelyCoercingAnAgent, someone overcomes someone else or coerces them as if by using a heavy club.")
(comment OverwhelminglyCoercingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CoercingAnAgent. In each #$OverwhelminglyCoercingAnAgent, someone brings something to a specified state by overwhelming force or pressure.")
(comment CoercingAnAgentForSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CoercingAnAgent. In each #$CoercingAnAgentForSomething, someone coerces someone else for money, information, etc..")
(comment TerrorizingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CoercingAnAgent. In each #$TerrorizingSomeone, someone coerces someone else by violence or with threats.")
(comment Overcorrecting-MakingReparationsForSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingReparationsForSomething. In each #$Overcorrecting-MakingReparationsForSomething, someone makes excessive corrections for fear of making an error.")
(comment MakingAmendsForSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingReparationsForSomething. In each #$MakingAmendsForSomething, someone makes amends for something.")
(comment CoOptingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$NeutralizingSomething-PoliticalAction. In each #$CoOptingSomeone, someone neutralizes or wins over someone else through assimilation into an established group.")
(comment Manipulating-InfluencingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InfluencingAnAgent. In each #$Manipulating-InfluencingAnAgent, someone influences or controls something shrewdly or deviously.")
(comment Coloring-InfluencingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InfluencingAnAgent. In each #$Coloring-InfluencingAnAgent, someone modifies or bias theirself so as to influence someone else.")
(comment InfluencingAnAgentDecisively "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InfluencingAnAgent. In each #$InfluencingAnAgentDecisively, something influences someone decisively.")
(comment Adventure "A sub-collection of #$Project.  Each instance is a wild and exciting undertaking (not necessarily lawful).")
(comment VenturingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RiskTaking. In each #$VenturingSomething, someone puts something at risk.")
(comment GivingCareToACertainThing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Caregiving. In each #$GivingCareToACertainThing, someone takes charge of or deals with a certain thing.")
(comment Overprotecting-Caregiving "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Caregiving. In each #$Overprotecting-Caregiving, someone cares for someone else like a mother.")
(comment WinningApprovalForSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InfluencingAnAgent. In each #$WinningApprovalForSomething, someone wins approval or support from someone else for something.")
(comment InfluencingAnAgentByCorruption "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InfluencingAnAgent. In each #$InfluencingAnAgentByCorruption, someone influences someone else by corruption.")
(comment Tyranny-GoverningSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Dictating-GoverningSomething. In each #$Tyranny-GoverningSomething, someone rules a country as a tyrant.")
(comment GreetingSomeoneByFirstName "The collection of actions performed by one #$Agent-Generic to greet another by saying his first name.  For example, Tom walks down the hall and sees Bill; Tom greets Bill by saying \"Hey Bill\".")
(comment AskingWhereOneIsGoing "The collection of events in which some agent asks where someone is going.")
(comment Fork-Topographical "Each #$Fork-Topographical is a land feature consisting of a juncture of three or more channels that meet.  The fork could be a branch in a canyon or valley.")
(comment LeprosariumFacility "#$LeprosariumFacility is a medical facility where leprosy is treated.")
(comment MineralOreRefinery "Each #$MineralOreRefinery is a facility where #$MineralOre is refined (cf. #$Refining) to extract a desirable substance.  For example, at a #$CopperRefinery, a variety of copper-containing minerals can be separated into #$Copper and waste products.")
(comment CopperRefinery "Each instance of #$CopperRefinery is an industrial facility with the primary purpose of refining #$CopperOre.")
(comment Limekiln "A kiln or furnace for reducing limestone or shells to lime by burning.")
(comment SalinePond "Salt evaporation ponds or diked salt ponds used in the production of solar evaporated salt.")
(comment WaterPoweredMill "Each #$WaterPoweredMill is a building or group of buildings whose main purpose is the production of a more refined/usable product from raw materials, and whose power comes from gravity's action on water.  Such mills will have a waterwheel as a part, and a mechanism for diverting/directing water from a stream or pond to the waterwheel.  The water is caught by compartments on the waterwheel, and the weight of the water causes the wheel to turn, which drives a driveshaft that can be used to grind, squeeze, or operate mechanical devices such as looms or forge bellows.  In modern cultures, water mills have been replaced by first steam, then coal, and now electrically-powered mills that perform these functions and many more, but water power is still used in some parts of the world.")
(comment Moraine-Topographical "Each #$Moraine-Topographical is a mound or slope consisting of coarse and very heterogenous material (mostly earth and stones) which was carried along by a #$Glacier (either in it, on top of it, or underneath it), and deposited when the glacier melted or retreated.  The presence of a moraine can be used as proof that an area was once glaciated long after the glacier has melted.")
(comment MeanderNeck-Topographical "#$MeanderNeck-Topographical is a  narrow strip of land between the two limbs of a meander loop (i.e., a bend in a stream) at its narrowest point.")
(comment Novitiate-Building "Each #$Novitiate-Building is religious house or school dedicated to the training of people who aren't yet full members of the religious community that occupies the physical quarters of which the #$Novitiate-Building is a part.")
(comment Nunatak "#$Nunatak is a rock, hill, or mountain completely surrounded by glacial ice, but protruding above the ice.")
(comment ObservationPoint "A specialization of #$ObservationFacilitatingTerrain.  Each instance of #$ObservationPoint is an area that is an advantageous position from which to view wildlife or scenery.")
(comment RadioObservatory "#$RadioObservatory is a facility that is equipped with an array of antennae for receiving radio waves from space.")
(comment RadioObservatory "A specialization of #$AstronomicalObservatory.  Each instance of #$RadioObservatory is a facility that is equipped with an array of antennae for receiving radio waves from space.")
(comment StorageTerrainFeatureType "A collection of collections, a specialization of
#$CulturalTerrainFeatureType. Instances of
#$StorageTerrainFeatureType are collections of objects that are involved in the storage of things. One way of partitioning further
this collection type could be to consider the nature of the energy
produced.")
(comment WaterPump "A collection of pumps which pump water either from the ground or through a pipeline, circulate water in an aquarium, empty a swimming pool, draw irrigation water from a lake, or etc.")
(comment PumpingFacility "#$PumpingFacility is a facility that houses a #$Pump-Mechanical.")
(comment WaterPumpingFacility "#$WaterPumpingFacility is a facility that houses a #$WaterPump, which pumps water through a pipeline or from the ground.")
(comment Reformatory "The collection of facilities designed for detaining and reforming juvenile delinquents.")
(comment HydroelectricPowerPlantBuilding "The collection of buildings that serve primarily to house components of water powered electrical generators (see #$ElectricalGenerator-Hydroelectric).")
(comment Point-Topographical "#$Point-Topographical is a tapering piece of land projecting into a body of water, but less prominent than a cape.")
(comment Cape-Topographical "#$Cape-Topographical is a tapering piece of land projecting into a body of water and is more prominent than a #$Point-Topographical.")
(comment Portage "A route where boats, goods, etc., are carried overland between navigable waters.")
(comment NativeReservation "A collection of geopolitical entities; a sub-collection of #$AutonomousRegion.  Each instance of #$NativeReservation is a sub-region of a country that permits a degree of greater self-governing by peoples who are native to the region.")
(comment AbyssalPlain "An #$AbyssalPlain is any of the great flat sediment-covered areas of ocean floor.")
(comment AncientSite "A place that had significance during the ancient world - 5000 BC to 476 AD.")
(comment Arrondissement "An #$Arrondissement is an administrative district of some large French cities.")
(comment ExpatriateCommunity "A specialization of #$Community. Each instance of this
collection is a community of artists who are living in the same locale in order to share inspiration and focus on their art.")
(comment BattlefieldPark "A #$BattlefieldPark is an old #$Battlefield that has been made into a park to memorialize the battle.")
(comment Boomtown "A #$Boomtown is a town currently enjoying a business and population boom.")
(comment ByzantineSite "A place that had significance during the Byzantine Empire - roughly 476 to 1453.")
(comment Caliphate "A #$Caliphate is the dominion of a caliph, who is a successor of Muhammad as temporal and spiritual head of Islam.")
(comment Canton-Swiss "#$Canton-Swiss is one of the states of the Swiss confederation.")
(comment CeremonialSite "A #$CeremonialSite is a place where religious rites were once celebrated by a people or tribe.")
(comment Commandery-District "#$Commandery-District is a district under the control of a commander of an order of knights, such as the templars.")
(comment CountySeat "A specialization of #$CapitalCityOfRegion, a #$CountySeat is the town or city that also serves as the administrative headquarters of the #$County.")
(comment CowTown "A #$CowTown is a town whose economy currently thrives on ranching and cattle.")
(comment Despotate "A #$Despotate is a geopolitical entity with a system of government in which the ruler has unlimited power.")
(comment Duchy "A #$Duchy is a territory of a duke or duchess.")
(comment Earldom "An #$Earldom is a territory of an earl.")
(comment UnderwaterTopographicalFeature "A collection of geographical regions and a specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$UnderwaterLocation. Each instance of #$UnderwaterTopographicalFeature is a region of land under a body of water and is distinguishable from surrounding regions by salient physical differences in surface texture, three-dimensional shape, or elevation.")
(comment componentPartOfSpaceRegion "An binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and a specialization of #$partOfSpaceRegion (q.v.).  <code>(#$componentPartOfSpaceRegion COMPONENT REGION)</code> means that <code>COMPONENT</code> is a part of <code>REGION</code> and is \"maximally connected\" in the following sense: (i) <code>COMPONENT</code> is a #$ConnectedSpaceRegion (q.v.) and (ii) there is no larger connected space region that is part of <code>REGION</code> and contains <code>COMPONENT</code> as a proper part. 
<p>
See also the related predicate #$componentCardinalityOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment atomicPartOfSpaceRegion "An binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and a specialization of #$partOfSpaceRegion (q.v.).  <code>(#$atomicPartOfSpaceRegion ATOM REGION)</code> means that <code>ATOM</code> is an #$AtomicSpaceRegion and is a part of <code>REGION</code>.
<p>
See also the related predicate #$atomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment CrowdingSomething "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$OccupyingTheWholeOfSomething. In each #$CrowdingSomething, something fills or occupies something to the point of overflowing.")
(comment WranglingAnimals "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HerdingSomeAnimals. In each #$WranglingAnimals, someone herds and care for some animals.")
(comment Nesting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AnimalActivity. In each #$Nesting, some animal inhabits a nest, usually after building.")
(comment OverlappingSituations "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Accompaniment. In each #$OverlappingSituations, some situation coincides partially or wholly with some other situation.")
(comment SpreadingSomethingOverAWideExpanseOfSpace "A sub-collection of #$Translation-LocationChange.  Each instance is a process or result of distributing or extending something over a wide expanse of space.")
(comment Precession-Action "A sub-collection of #$Action.  Each instance is the act of some things preceding in time or order or rank (as in a ceremony).")
(comment EmbankingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnclosingSomething. In each #$EmbankingSomething, someone encloses something with banks, as for support or protection.")
(comment RailingInSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnclosingSomething. In each #$RailingInSomething, something encloses something else with rails.")
(comment BoxingInSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnclosingSomething. In each #$BoxingInSomething, something encloses something else or confines it as if in a box.")
(comment FramingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnclosingSomething. In each #$FramingSomething, someone encloses something else in a frame, as of a picture.")
(comment DefrayingExpenses "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Paying. In each #$DefrayingExpenses, someone bears the expenses of something.")
(comment DumpingGoodsAbroad-SalesActivity "A sub-collection of #$SalesActivity.  Each instance is a selling goods abroad at a price below that charged in the domestic market.")
(comment Dumping-Buying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Buying. In each #$Dumping-Buying, someone sells something at artificially low prices.")
(comment Undersell-Buying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Buying. In each #$Undersell-Buying, someone sells cheaper than their competition.")
(comment IssuingBonds "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnteringIntoAMortgageAgreement. In each #$IssuingBonds, someone issues bonds on something.")
(comment ImpulseBuyingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Buying. In each #$ImpulseBuyingSomething, someone buys something on impulse without proper reflection.")
(comment KillingByOrganism "A specialization of #$PhysicalDestructionEvent, #$AnimalActivity and #$ActionOnObject. In each instance of #$KillingByOrganism there exists at least one organism killed (see #$organismKilled) and at least one organism that is the killer.  Each organism that is killed in an instance KILL of #$KillingByOrganism is the #$bodilyDoer in an instance of #$Dying that is a sub-event of KILL.  For a more specialized collection of killing events where there is exactly one killer and exactly one killed see #$KillingByOrganism-Unique.")
(comment Dying "A collection of events.  Each instance of #$Dying is an event in which a living organism (an instance of #$Organism-Whole) ceases to live and thus ceases to exist.  The physical portion of the organism may remain, but that is not considered an instance of #$Organism-Whole (cf. #$DeadAnimal).  Note that the expiring animal is related to its dying event by #$bodilyDoer (q.v.)--in contrast to the role an organism plays when it is the #$organismKilled in a #$KillingByOrganism event (which will have some #$Dying event among its #$subEvents), and cf. #$bodilyActedOn.")
(disjointWith Bombing-RoadSideBomb TerroristSuicideBombing)
(comment Bombing-RoadSideBomb "The collection of bombings in which a \"roadside\" bomb is detonated.  Roadside bombs are almost always improvised explosive devices.  Many instances of this collection are also instances of #$AmbushingAnEnemy, but not all are.")
(comment abductivelyRelevant "(#$abductivelyRelevant EXPLANANS HYP-EXPLANANDUM) means that (1) if a ground form of #$CycLSentence-Assertible EXPLANANS is true, then it is very likely that the corresponding ground form of HYP-EXPLANANDUM is true; (2) the belief that a ground form of HYP-EXPLANANDUM would likely enrich one's understanding of the coresponding ground form of EXPLANANS.")
(comment AntiTankWeapon-Rocket "The collection of rocket propelled missiles designed to destroy tanks and other armored vehicles.")
(comment inheritRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType "A quaternary predicate. (#$inheritRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType SUPER ROLE1 SUB ROLE2) means that every instance of SUPER has an instance of SUB #$subSituations, and furthermore  if (#$isa INST1 SUPER) and (ROLE1 INST1 ACTOR) and (#$subSituations INST1 INST2) then (ROLE2 INST2 ACTOR). For example, in #$BiologyMt,  (#$inheritRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType #$KillingByOrganism-Unique #$organismKilled #$Dying #$bodilyDoer) would mean that every killing of a single organism has a subevent in which the killed organism dies.")
(comment cooperatesInInstancesOfEventTypeDuring "(#$cooperatesInInstancesOfEventTypeDuring AGT1 AGT2 EVENT-TYPE INTERVAL) holds just in case there is at least one instance of EVENT-TYPE, EVT, in which (#$cooperatingParticipants EVT AGT1) and (#$cooperatingParticipants EVT AGT2) holds and the date of EVT is subsumed by INTEVAL.")
(comment situationTypeDefaultConsequenceType "(#$situationTypeDefaultConsequenceType SIT-TYPE CONSEQ-TYPE) means that every instance of SIT-TYPE (a specialization of #$Situation) by default has an instance of CONSEQ-TYPE (another specialization of #$Situation) as a consequence (i.e. as a causal consequence, or as a sub-situation type).")
(comment WordNetSynsetImportStatus-ReadyForVerbSemtrans "The collection of #$WordNet-Version2_0 verb synsets that are ready for lexification with a semantic  translation assertion.")
(comment iGPertinentThing "An assertion of the form (#$iGPertinentThing IG THING) states that within the context of the #$InformationGathering IG, the #$Thing THING is relevant because of its bearing known relations to multiple entities that play the role of #$infoGatheredAbout in IG.  This is a weaker relation than the more specialized relation #$infoGatheredAbout, which expresses the fact that a particular thing is the actual subject about which information is being gathered about.")
(comment ComponentCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn "A unary function that takes a given space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) to the number of \"maximally connected\" component parts it has.  (#$ComponentCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn <b>REGION</b>) is the positive integer <b>N</b> such that there are exactly <b>N</b> distinct #$ConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are component parts of <b>REGION</b> (see #$componentPartOfSpaceRegion).  In particular, <b>N</b>=1 if and only if <b>REGION</b> is an instance of #$ConnectedSpaceRegion, and <b>N</b>>1 if and only if <b>REGION</b> is an instance of #$DisconnectedSpaceRegion (q.v.).
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$componentCardinalityOfSpaceRegion.  Also see the related function #$AtomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment componentCardinalityOfSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) to the number of \"maximally connected\" component parts it has.  <code>(#$componentCardinalityOfSpaceRegion REGION N)</code> means that there are exactly <code>N</code> distinct #$ConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are component parts of <code>REGION</code> (see #$componentPartOfSpaceRegion).  In particular, <code>N</code>=1 if and only if <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$ConnectedSpaceRegion, and <code>N</code>>1 if and only if <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$DisconnectedSpaceRegion (q.v.).
<p>
Compare this predicate with the related predicate
#$atomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegion (defined in the #$RCCWithAtomicRegionsMt).  Note that if <code>(#$atomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegion REGION M)</code>, then it is always the case that <code>M</code> is greater than or equal to <code>N</code> (as given above), with <code>M=N</code> if and only if <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$AtomicSpaceRegion (where <code>M=N=1</code>) or <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$ScatteredSpaceRegion (where <code>M=N</code>>1).
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$ComponentCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment atomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) to the number of \"atoms\" it has as parts.  <code>(#$atomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegion REGION N)</code> means that there are exactly <code>N</code> distinct #$AtomicSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are parts of <code>REGION</code> (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).  In particular, <code>N</code> =1 if and only if <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$AtomicSpaceRegion, and <code>N</code> >1 if and only if <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$CompositeSpaceRegion (q.v.).
<p>
Compare this predicate with the related predicate
#$componentCardinalityOfSpaceRegion.  Note that if <code>(#$componentCardinalityOfSpaceRegion REGION M)</code>, then it is always the case that <code>N</code> (as given above) is greater than or equal to <code>M</code>, with <code>M = N</code> if and only if <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$AtomicSpaceRegion (where <code>M = N = 1</code>) or <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$ScatteredSpaceRegion (where <code>M = N</code> > 1).
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$AtomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment AtomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn "A unary function that takes a given space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) to the number of \"atoms\" it has as parts.  (#$AtomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn <b>REGION</b>) is the positive integer <b>N</b> such that there are exactly <b>N</b> distinct #$AtomicSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are parts of <b>REGION</b> (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).  In particular, <b>N</b> =1 if and only if <b>REGION</b> is an instance of #$AtomicSpaceRegion, and <b>N</b> >1 if and only if <b>REGION</b> is an instance of #$CompositeSpaceRegion (q.v.).
<p>
See this function's corresponding predicate #$atomicCardinalityOfSpaceRegion.  And compare this function to the related function #$ComponentCardinalityOfSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment Disposing-Buying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Buying. In each #$Disposing-Buying, something gives, sells, or transfers to another.")
(comment Kickback "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Paying. In each #$Kickback, someone pays a kickback; makes an illegal payment.")
(comment Pouring-MakingSomethingAvailable "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$Pouring-MakingSomethingAvailable, something supplies something else in large amounts or quantities.")
(comment Moving-Buying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Buying. In each #$Moving-Buying, someone disposes of something by selling.")
(comment Pumping-MakingSomethingAvailable "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$Pumping-MakingSomethingAvailable, something supplies something else in great quantities.")
(comment Entrusting-HandingSomethingToSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HandingSomethingToSomeone. In each #$Entrusting-HandingSomethingToSomeone, someone puts something into the care or protection of someone else.")
(comment Oversupplying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$Oversupplying, something supplies something else with an excess of something.")
(comment Economizing "A sub-collection of #$EconomicAction. Each instance is an act of reduction in cost of something")
(comment Toggle-Connector "A sub-collection of #$Connector. Each instance is a fastener consisting of a peg or pin or crosspiece that is inserted into an eye at the end of a rope or a chain or a cable in order to fasten it to something (as another rope or chain or cable).")
(comment SeparatePhysicalPartOfObject "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalPartOfObject. Each instance is a separate part of a whole object.")
(comment PieceOfCloth "A sub-collection of #$Cloth. Each instance is a piece of fabric.")
(comment Patch-PieceOfCloth "A sub-collection of #$PieceOfCloth. Each instance is a piece of cloth used as decoration or to mend or cover a hole.")
(comment Leverage-Financial "A sub-collection of #$Investing. Each instance is an investing with borrowed money as a way to amplify potential gains (at the risk of greater losses).")
(comment LeveragingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$LeveragingSomething, someone provides something with leverage.")
(comment Unburdening "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TakingSomething. In each #$Unburdening, something frees or relieves someone of a burden.")
(comment Reflector "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalDevice. Each instance is a device that reflects radiation.")
(comment RetrofitingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$RetrofitingSomething, someone provides something with parts, devices, or equipment not available or in use at the time of the original manufacture.")
(comment Strip-Artifact "A sub-collection of #$Artifact. Each instance is an artifact consisting of a narrow flat piece of material.")
(comment StripNearTheBoundaryOfAnObject "A sub-collection of #$EdgeOnObject. Each instance is a strip near the boundary of an object.")
(comment Border-StripNearTheBoundaryOfAnObject "A sub-collection of #$StripNearTheBoundaryOfAnObject. Each instance is a strip forming the outer edge of something.")
(comment DefensiveStructure "A sub-collection of #$ConstructionArtifact. Each instance is a structure used for defense.")
(comment Fortification "A sub-collection of #$DefensiveStructure. Each instance is a defensive structure consisting of walls or mounds built around a stronghold to strengthen it.")
(comment Parapet "A sub-collection of #$Fortification. Each instance is a fortification consisting of a low wall.")
(comment Machicolation "A sub-collection of #$Parapet. Each instance is a projecting parapet supported by corbels on a medieval castle; has openings through which stones or boiling water could be dropped on an enemy.")
(comment Translocation-BiologicalEvent "A sub-collection of #$BiologicalEvent. Each instance is a an exchange of chromosome parts.")
(comment Covering-WorkingAsASubstituteForSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WorkingAsASubstituteForSomeone. In each #$Covering-WorkingAsASubstituteForSomeone, someone helps out by taking someone's place and temporarily assuming their responsibilities.")
(comment BaronetizingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnnoblingSomeone. In each #$BaronetizingSomeone, someone confers baronetcy upon someone.")
(comment LordingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnnoblingSomeone. In each #$LordingSomeone, someone makes a lord of someone.")
(comment KnightingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnnoblingSomeone. In each #$KnightingSomeone, someone raises someone else to knighthood.")
(comment Undertaking-WorkingEvent "A sub-collection of #$WorkingEvent. Each instance is any piece of work that is undertaken or attempted.")
(comment Labor-WorkingEvent "A sub-collection of #$WorkingEvent. Each instance is a productive work (especially physical work done for wages).")
(comment Advantaging-Preferment "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Preferment-PromotingSomeone. In each #$Advantaging-Preferment, something gives an advantage to something.")
(comment InstrumentalArtifact "A sub-collection of #$Artifact. Each instance is an artifact (or system of artifacts) that is instrumental in accomplishing some end.")
(comment Junction-Connector "A sub-collection of #$Connector. Each instance is a something that joins or connects.")
(comment Joint-Junction "A sub-collection of #$Junction-Connector. Each instance is a junction by which parts or objects are joined together.")
(comment directSpaceRegionSum "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of mutually discrete space regions to their mereological sum or union in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  <code>(#$directSpaceRegionSum SUM REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that (i) <code>SUM</code> is the sum (see #$spaceRegionSum)
of <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> and (ii) <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are discrete from each other (see #$discreteSpaceRegions).
<p>
Note that this predicate is an #$ELRelation (q.v.) whose #$expansion is given in terms of #$spaceRegionSum and #$discreteSpaceRegions.  See also the
corresponding function #$DirectSpaceRegionSumFn.")
(comment DirectSpaceRegionSumFn "A binary function that takes a pair of mutually discrete space regions to their mereological sum or union in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  (#$DirectSpaceRegionSumFn <b>SUM REGION1 REGION2</b>) is the #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion <b>SUM</b> such that (i) <b>SUM</b> is the sum (see #$spaceRegionSum) of <b>REGION1</b> and <b>REGION2</b> and (ii) <b>REGION1</b> and <b>REGION2</b> are discrete from each other (see #$discreteSpaceRegions).
<p>
Note that this predicate is an #$ELRelation (q.v.) whose #$expansion is given in terms of #$SpaceRegionSumFn.  See also the corresponding predicate #$directSpaceRegionSum.")
(comment Lining-ProtectiveCovering "A sub-collection of #$ProtectiveCovering. Each instance is a protective covering that protects an inside surface.")
(comment Bushing "A sub-collection of #$Lining-ProtectiveCovering. Each instance is a cylindrical metal lining used to reduce friction.")
(comment Partition-ConstructionArtifact "A sub-collection of #$ConstructionArtifact. Each instance is a vertical structure that divides or separates (as a wall divides one room from another).")
(comment Brattice "A sub-collection of #$Partition-ConstructionArtifact. Each instance is a partition (often temporary) of planks or cloth that is used to control ventilation in a mine.")
(comment Lumber "The collection of #$Wood that has been cut and prepared for use as a building material.")
(comment StripOfWoodOrMetal "A sub-collection of #$Lumber. Each instance is a thin piece of wood or metal.")
(comment Slat "A sub-collection of #$StripOfWoodOrMetal. Each instance is a thin strip (wood or metal).")
(comment Berth "A sub-collection of #$Bed-PieceOfFurniture. Each instance is a bed on a ship or train; usually in tiers.")
(comment Rafter "A sub-collection of #$Beam-Construction. Each instance is one of several parallel sloping beams that support a roof.")
(comment convexSpaceRegionSum "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of space regions to the smallest convex region containing their mereological sum or union in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  <code>(#$convexSpaceRegionSum CONVEX-SUM REGION1 REGION2)</code> holds if and only if <code>(#$convexSpaceRegionOf CONVEX-SUM SUM)</code> holds, where <code>SUM</code> is the unique space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) such that <code>(#$spaceRegionSum SUM REGION1 REGION2)</code>.")
(comment genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs "#$genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs is used to express a #$genlPreds -like relationship between a predicate SPEC-PRED that has at least one quoted argument place (see #$quotedArgument) and a predicate GENL-PRED of the same arity that<br><br> 1) has argIsa/argGenl constraints that are at least as general as those of SPEC-PRED,<br><br> 2) is <i>not</i> quoted in at least one of the argument places in which SPEC-PRED is, and<br><br>  3) is <i>only</i> quoted in argument places in which SPEC-PRED is also quoted.  <p>As such, GENL-PRED need not have any quoted argument places.  <p>In (#$genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs SPEC-PRED GENL-PRED SET), SET is the set of integers that correspond to quoted argument places in SPEC-PRED that are <i>not</i> quoted in GENL-PRED, thereby indicating a recipe for deriving a sentence GENL-SENT whose arg0 is GENL-PRED from a sentence SPEC-SENT whose arg0 is SPEC-PRED:  First, each argument in SPEC-SENT occuring in the Nth argument place, where N is neither 0 nor a member of LIST, will occur in the Nth argument place in GENL-SENT.  All other non-0 arguments in SPEC-SENT, which by definition occur in the Mth argument place, where M is an element of SET, will appear as explicitly quoted arguments in GENL-SENT, in those same argument places.  For example,
<pre>
   (#$genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs #$cycLActors #$situationConstituents (#$TheSet 2))
</pre>
entails that whenever
<pre>
   (#$cycLActors ExaminingTheCycLTermHashDollarDog #$Dog)
</pre>
holds,
<pre>
   (#$situationConstituents ExaminingTheCycLTermHashDollarDog (#$Quote #$Dog)).
</pre>
follows.")
(comment Profits "A sub-collection of #$MonetaryRate. Each instance is the excess of revenues over outlays in a given period of time (including depreciation and other non-cash expenses).")
(comment Accumulation-Profits "A sub-collection of #$Profits. Each instance is profits that are not paid out as dividends but are added to the capital base of the corporation.")
(comment StorageOfSomethingForFutureUse "A sub-collection of #$Accumulation-Profits. Each instance is a supply of something available for future use.")
(comment Yielding-MakingSomethingAvailable "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$Yielding-MakingSomethingAvailable, something is the cause or source of something else.")
(comment EquippingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$EquippingSomething, someone provides something with something else usually for a specific purpose.")
(comment SellingAtALoss "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Buying. In each #$SellingAtALoss, someone sells something at a loss.")
(comment Sparing-TransferringPossession "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TransferringPossession. In each #$Sparing-TransferringPossession, someone gives something up that is not strictly needed.")
(comment Adopting-SelectingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SelectingSomething. In each #$Adopting-SelectingSomething, someone chooses and follows theories, ideas, policies, strategies or plans.")
(comment Consigning-AbandoningSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AbandoningSomething. In each #$Consigning-AbandoningSomething, something commits forever; commits irrevocably.")
(comment Consigning-DeliveringSomeoneSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DeliveringSomeoneSomething. In each #$Consigning-DeliveringSomeoneSomething, someone sends something to an address.")
(comment CommittingSomeoneToAnInstitution "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PermittingEntrance. In each #$CommittingSomeoneToAnInstitution, someone causes someone to be admitted to an institution.")
(comment ReducingASalesPrice "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SalesActivity. In each #$ReducingASalesPrice, someone cuts the price of something.")
(comment Corbel "A sub-collection of #$Bracket-Support. Each instance is a triangular bracket of brick or stone (usually of slight extent).")
(comment Framework-SupportingStructure "A sub-collection of #$SupportingStructure. Each instance is a structure supporting or containing something.")
(comment Cornice-WindowCasing "A sub-collection of #$Framework-SupportingStructure. Each instance is a decorative framework to conceal curtain fixtures at the top of a window casing.")
(comment Constitution "A sub-collection of #$Law. Each instance is a law determining the fundamental political principles of a government.")
(comment WrittenCodeOfConduct "A sub-collection of #$Writing. Each instance is a set of rules or principles or laws (especially written ones).")
(comment SalicLaw-LegalCode "An instance of #$LegalCode. The code of laws of the Salian Franks and other German tribes.")
(comment JustinianCode "An instance of #$LegalCode. The legal code of ancient Rome; codified under Justinian; the basis for many modern systems of civil law.")
(comment CriminalLaw "A sub-collection of #$LegalCode. Each instance is the body of law dealing with crimes and their punishment.")
(comment PenalCode "A sub-collection of #$LegalCode. Each instance is the legal code governing crimes and their punishment.")
(comment UnitedStatesCode "An instance of #$LegalCode. A consolidation and codification by subject matter of the general and permanent laws of the United States; is prepared and published by a unit of the United States House of Representatives.")
(comment DistrainSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ImpoundingSomething. In each #$DistrainSomething, someone confiscates something by distress.")
(comment Index-List-Information "A sub-collection of #$List-Information. Each instance is an alphabetical listing of names and topics along with page numbers where they are discussed.")
(comment MakingSomethingAbstractAvailable "The collection of all activities in which an #$Agent-Generic makes it possible for another #$Agent-Generic to use (but not necessarily own) an abstract thing.")
(comment MakingAbstractAvailableFn "This function can be used to denote the set of events in which an agent makes it possible for another agent to use (but not own) a good or service.")
(comment RedisposeSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Disposing-Buying. In each #$RedisposeSomething, something is disposed anew.")
(comment SubjugatingByImposingTroops "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SubjugatingSomeone. In each #$SubjugatingByImposingTroops, someone subjugates something by imposing troops.")
(comment Administration-Management "A sub-collection of #$Handling-Management. Each instance is a method of tending to (especially business) matters.")
(comment Organization-Administration-Management "A sub-collection of #$Administration-Management. Each instance is the act of organizing a business or business-related activity.")
(comment Organization-AnimalActivity "A sub-collection of #$AnimalActivity. Each instance is the activity or result of distributing or disposing persons or things properly or methodically.")
(comment FormingAConfederation "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActingInConcert. In each #$FormingAConfederation, someone forms a confederation.")
(comment AllyingWithSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of ActingInConcert. In each AllyingWithSomething, someone formally acts in concert or formally unites in a common purpose or belief with someone else.")
(comment WorkingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Handling-Management. In each #$WorkingSomething, someone causes something to operate or function.")
(comment DisposingOf-Handling-Management "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Handling-Management. In each #$DisposingOf-Handling-Management, someone deals withs something or settles it.")
(comment TakingCare-Handling-Management "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Handling-Management. In each #$TakingCare-Handling-Management, someone is in charge of or deals with something.")
(comment CoordinatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Handling-Management. In each #$CoordinatingSomething, something brings something else into common action, movement, or condition.")
(comment HandlingSimultaneousSituations "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Handling-Management. In each #$HandlingSimultaneousSituations, someone deals with simultaneous situations.")
(comment Mismanagement "A sub-collection of #$Management. Each instance is a management that is careless or inefficient.")
(comment Misconduct-Management "A sub-collection of #$Management. Each instance is a bad or dishonest management by persons supposed to act on another's behalf.")
(comment Conducting-Administration-Management "A sub-collection of #$Administration-Management. Each instance is the way of administering a business.")
(comment Circularization-Advertising "A sub-collection of #$Advertising. Each instance is circulating printed notices as a means of advertising.")
(comment CircularizingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Circularization-Advertising. In each #$CircularizingSomeone, someone canvasses someone else by distributing letters.")
(comment RehouseSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HousingSomething. In each #$RehouseSomeone, someone puts someone else up in a new or different housing.")
(comment DecontrolingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Liberalizing-PoliticalAction. In each #$DecontrolingSomething, someone relaxes or removes controls on something.")
(comment ConsociatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActingInConcert. In each #$ConsociatingSomething, someone brings or comes into association or common action.")
(comment AgentsBandingTogether "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActingInConcert. In each #$AgentsBandingTogether, someone forms a group or unites.")
(comment ReunifySomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActingInConcert. In each #$ReunifySomething, something unifies again with respect to a country.")
(comment EnrollingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Registering-IBOCreation. In each #$EnrollingSomeone, someone registers formally as a participant or member.")
(comment MakingSomethingAbstract "A collection creation events, a specialization #$PurposefulAction in which some #$Agent-Generic creates an abstract thing such as a #$List-Information.")
(comment WritingOffADebt "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Canceling-Declaring-Evaluating. In each #$WritingOffADebt, someone cancels a debt.")
(comment InvalidatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Canceling-Declaring-Evaluating. In each #$InvalidatingSomething, someone declares something invalid.")
(comment MonetizingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$LegalizingSomethingPreviouslyIllegal. In each #$MonetizingSomething, someone gives legal value to something or establishes the legal tender of a country.")
(comment AskingWhetherOneHasEaten "The collection of events in which some agent asks somene whether one has eaten.")
(comment StatingWhatOtherIsDoing "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$StatingSomething.  Each instance of #$StatingWhatOtherIsDoing is a #$CommunicationAct-Single in which an agent makes an assertion about what someone else is doing.")
(comment interArgDifferent "A #$MetaRelation used for stating a certain kind of necessary condition for being an element of the domain of a given #$Relation (see #$relationDomain).  Thus (to put it in terms of the semantics of CycL expressions) #$interArgDifferent can be used for stating a certain kind of truth- or denotation-condition for a given relation-denoting expression.  (#$interArgDifferent RELN N M) means that RELN (if it is a #$Predicate or other #$TruthFunction) only holds among or (if it is a #$Function-Denotational) only has a value for sequences of arguments whose Nth and Mth members are distinct (see #$different).  That is (assuming without loss of generality that N < M): if RELN is a predicate or other \"truth-function\" then (RELN ... ARGN ... ARGM ...) holds only if ARGN and ARGM are distinct, and if RELN is a function then it is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <THING-1, ..., THING-N, ..., THING-M, ...> only if THING-N and THING-M are distinct.  For example, (#$interArgDifferent #$opponentsInConflict 1 2) means that no-one is his or her own opponent in a conflict.
<p>
Note that this predicate trivially holds for any relation with respect those argument-places it has that are subject to mutually disjoint (see #$disjointWith and cf. #$cosatisfiableInArgs) argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate).")
(comment ShootingABirdInFlight "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShootingSomeone. In each #$ShootingABirdInFlight, someone shoots a bird in flight.")
(comment PickingOff-ShootingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ShootingSomeone. In each #$PickingOff-ShootingSomeone, someone shoots their victims one by one.")
(comment Haltering-Hanging-Execution "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Hanging-Execution-Judicial. In each #$Haltering-Hanging-Execution, someone hangs and executes someone else with a halter.")
(comment Gibbeting-Hanging-Execution "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Hanging-Execution-Judicial. In each #$Gibbeting-Hanging-Execution, someone hangs someone else on an execution instrument.")
(comment Pairing-ActingInConcert "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActingInConcert. In each #$Pairing-ActingInConcert, at least two #$Agent-Generics form a pair or pairs.")
(comment EnslavingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SubjugatingSomeone. In each #$EnslavingSomeone, someone makes a slave of someone else; brings someone else into servitude.")
(comment Ticketing-MakingACriminalCharge "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BookingSomeone-MakingACriminalCharge. In each #$Ticketing-MakingACriminalCharge, someone issues a ticket or a fine as a penalty.")
(comment Deportation "A sub-collection of #$ExpellingFromStatus. Each instance is the expulsion from a country of an undesirable alien.")
(comment ExpatriatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ForcingSomeoneToLeave. In each #$ExpatriatingSomeone, someone expels a legal resident from a country.")
(comment SuspendingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ForcingSomeoneToLeave. In each #$SuspendingSomeone, someone bars someone else temporarily; from school, office, etc..")
(comment BanishingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ForcingSomeoneToLeave. In each #$BanishingSomeone, someone expels someone else as if by official decree.")
(comment BarringSomeoneByANegativeVote "A sub-collection of #$ExpellingFromStatus. Each instance is the act of excluding someone by a negative vote or veto.")
(comment OstracisingSomeone "A sub-collection of #$ForcingSomeoneToLeave. Each instance is the act of excluding someone from society by general consent.")
(comment BanishingSomeoneFromAPlaceOfResidence "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BanishingSomeone. In each #$BanishingSomeoneFromAPlaceOfResidence, someone bans someone else from a place of residence, as for punishment.")
(comment Ministering-GivingCareToACertainThing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GivingCareToACertainThing. In each #$Ministering-GivingCareToACertainThing, something attends to the wants and needs of others.")
(comment Care-WorkingEvent "A sub-collection of #$TakingCareOfSomething. Each instance is the work of caring for or attending to someone or something.")
(comment Tending-GivingCareToACertainThing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GivingCareToACertainThing. In each #$Tending-GivingCareToACertainThing, something has care of or looks after something.")
(comment Touching-Handling-Management "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Handling-Management. In each #$Touching-Handling-Management, someone deals with something; usually used with a form of negation.")
(comment Booking-MakingAReservation "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingAReservation. In each #$Booking-MakingAReservation, someone registers in a hotel book.")
(comment Accommodating-HelpingAnAgent "A sub-collection of #$HelpingAnAgent. Each instance is the act of providing something (lodging or seat or food) to meet a need of someone.")
(comment ProvidingLodging "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ServiceEvent. In each #$ProvidingLodging, someone provides housing for someone else.")
(comment ChamberingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HousingSomething. In each #$ChamberingSomething, someone places something in a chamber.")
(comment TakingIn-HousingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HousingSomething. In each #$TakingIn-HousingSomething, someone provides something with shelter.")
(comment Raking-SomethingDimensionallyCovering "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Sweeping-SomethingDimensionallyCovering. In each #$Raking-SomethingDimensionallyCovering, someone sweeps the length of something.")
(comment Transgressing-Spreading "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Spreading-SomethingDimensionallyCovering. In each #$Transgressing-Spreading, something spreads over land, especially along a subsiding shoreline.")
(comment Sharing-OverlappingSituations "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$OverlappingSituations. In each #$Sharing-OverlappingSituations, two or more things have something else in common.")
(comment ConfrontingSomething "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Fronting-LyingInACertainLocation. In each #$ConfrontingSomething, someone is face to face with something.")
(comment GallinaceousBird "A sub-collection of #$Bird. Each instance is a heavy-bodied largely ground-feeding domestic or game bird.")
(comment OcellatedTurkey "A sub-collection of #$GallinaceousBird. Each instance is a wild turkey of Central America and northern South America.")
(comment Orchis "A sub-collection of #$Orchid. Each instance is any of various deciduous terrestrial orchids having fleshy tubers and flowers in erect terminal racemes.")
(comment OrchisPapilionaceae "A sub-collection of #$Orchis. Each instance is a Mediterranean orchid having usually purple flowers with a fan-shaped spotted or striped rose-red lip.")
(comment EquippingFn "This function can be used to denote the set of events in which an agent equips something with an object.")
(comment BoundaryLine "A sub-collection of #$Line. Each instance is a line determining the limits of an area.")
(comment Rim-BoundaryLine "A sub-collection of #$BoundaryLine. Each instance is the shape of a raised edge of a more or less circular object.")
(comment Fret-MusicalInstrumentPart "A sub-collection of #$Bar-Object. Each instance is a small bar of metal across the fingerboard of a musical instrument; when the string is stopped by a finger at the metal bar it will produce a note of the desired pitch.")
(comment Rail-Bar "A sub-collection of #$Bar-Object. Each instance is a horizontal bar (usually of wood).")
(comment Barrier-ConstructionArtifact "A sub-collection of #$Obstruction-ConstructionArtifact. Each instance is a structure or object that impedes free movement.")
(comment Grate-Barrier "A sub-collection of #$Barrier-ConstructionArtifact. Each instance is a barrier that has parallel or crossed bars blocking a passage but admitting air.")
(comment MakingSomethingAvailable "The collection of all activities in which an #$Agent-Generic makes it possible for another #$Agent-Generic to use (but not necessarily own) a good or service.")
(comment Possession "A sub-collection of #$PartiallyTangible. Each instance is an anything owned or possessed.")
(comment Assets-Possession "A sub-collection of #$Possession. Each instance is an anything of material value or usefulness.")
(comment Capital "A sub-collection of #$Assets-Possession. Each instance is an asset available for use in the production of further assets.")
(comment Attempting "A specialization of #$PurposefulAction.  Each instance of #$Attempting is an event in which an agent attempts (whether successfully or not) to perform some type of action: attempts to use a telephone, attempts to solve a math problem, attempts to go to the store. Specializations of this collection can be created on the fly via #$AttemptingFn (q.v.).")
(disjointWith ExperiencingSomething PurposefulAction)
(comment typedGenlPredsWRTQuotedArgs "An instance of #$TaxonomicPredicateForPredicates that is a specialization of #$genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs, bearing a relationship to that predicate that is analogous to the relationship between #$typedGenlPreds and #$genlPreds.  #$typedGenlPredsWRTQuotedArgs is used to express a #$typedGenlPreds -like relationship between a predicate SPEC-PRED that has at least one quoted argument place (see #$quotedArgument) and a predicate GENL-PRED of the same arity that<br><br> 1) has argIsa/argGenl constraints that are at least as general as those of SPEC-PRED,<br><br> 2) is <i>not</i> quoted in at least one of the argument places in which SPEC-PRED is, and<br><br>  3) is <i>only</i> quoted in argument places in which SPEC-PRED is also quoted.  <p>As such, GENL-PRED need not have any quoted argument places.  <p>In (#$typedGenlPredsWRTQuotedArgs SPEC-PRED GENL-PRED SET), SET is the set of integers that correspond to quoted argument places in SPEC-PRED that are <i>not</i> quoted in GENL-PRED, thereby indicating a recipe for deriving a sentence SPEC-SENT whose arg0 is SPEC-PRED from a sentence GENL-SENT whose arg0 is GENL-PRED:  First, each argument ARG in GENL-SENT occuring in the Nth argument place, where N is neither 0 nor a member of LIST, will occur in the Nth argument place in SPEC-SENT if and only if ARG meets the Nth argument constraints of SPEC-PRED.  All other non-0 arguments OTHER-ARGS in GENL-SENT, which by definition occur in the Mth argument place, where M is an element of SET, will appear as explicitly quoted arguments in SPEC-SENT, in those same argument places, just in case OTHER-ARGS meets the quoted argument constraints of the Mth argument place in SPEC-PRED.  For example,
<pre>
   (#$typedGenlPredsWRTQuotedArgs #$cycLActors #$situationConstituents (#$TheSet 2))
</pre>
entails that whenever
<pre>
   (#$situationConstituents WritingACommentForHashDollarDog
       (#$Quote #$Dog))
</pre>
holds,
<pre>
   (#$cycLActors WritingACommentForHashDollarDog #$Dog)
</pre>
follows.")
(comment mtTopic "(#$mtTopic MT THING) means that context MT is \"about\" (is focused on information pertaining to) THING.  THING is one of the topics, or subject areas, of the information in MT.")
(comment Peer "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who is of equal standing with another in a group.")
(comment Associate "A sub-collection of #$Peer. Each instance is a person who joins with others in some activity.")
(comment Collaborator "A sub-collection of #$Associate. Each instance is an associate who works with others toward a common goal.")
(comment Indication "A sub-collection of #$Explanation. Each instance is a something that serves to indicate or suggest.")
(comment generalizedSpaceRegionSum "A binary predicate that relates a set of equidimensional space regions (see #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet) to their mereological sum or union. <code>(#$generalizedSpaceRegionSum SUM REGION-SET)</code> means that <code>SUM</code> is the spatial sum of all the regions in <code>REGION-SET</code>.  This predicate is an extension of the predicate #$spaceRegionSum, which relates two regions to their sum. 
<p>
See the corresponding function #$GeneralizedSpaceRegionSumFn.  Also see #$generalizedSpaceRegionProduct.")
(comment generalizedSpaceRegionProduct "A binary predicate that relates a set of equidimensional space regions (see #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet) to their mereological product or intersection. <code>(#$generalizedSpaceRegionProduct PROD REGION-SET)</code> means that <code>PROD</code> is the spatial product of all the regions in <code>REGION-SET</code>.  This predicate is an extension of the predicate #$spaceRegionProduct, which relates two regions to their sum. 
<p>
See the corresponding function #$GeneralizedSpaceRegionProductFn.  Also see  #$generalizedSpaceRegionSum.")
(comment MinimalSubsumingConvexSpaceRegionFn "A unary function that maps a given spatial thing to the space region enclosed by the thing's intangible \"convex hull\". (#$MinimalSubsumingConvexSpaceRegionFn  <b>OBJECT</b>) is the minimal #$ConvexSpaceRegion that #$spatiallySubsumes all of <b>OBJECT</b>.  Note that <b>OBJECT</b> might be a single object or a #$Group of spatially disjoint objects. 
<p>
To represent an object's intangible convex hull itself (rather than the entire space region it encloses), use #$ConvexHullFn.   
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$convexSpaceRegionSubsuming-Minimal.")
(comment OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn "A unary function that takes a given non-universal space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedNonUniversalSpaceRegion) to the maximal space region that is outside it (see #$outsideSpaceRegion).  
<p>
More precisely, (#$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn <b>REGION</b>) is the space region with which any space region <b>ANY-REGION</b> is connected (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) if and only if there is some region <b>OUTSIDE-REGION</b> that is outside <b>REGION</b> and connected to <b>ANY-REGION</b>.
<p>
Note that #$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn is the function known as <i>outside</i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$outsideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal; also see #$InsideOfSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment convexSpaceRegionSubsuming-Minimal "A binary #$SpatialPredicate that relates a spatial thing to the region of space enclosed by its \"convex hull\".  <code>(#$convexSpaceRegionSubsuming-Minimal REGION OBJECT)</code> means that <code>REGION</code> is the minimal convex #$SpaceRegion that #$spatiallySubsumes all parts of <code>OBJECT</code>.  To state things about the convex hull itself (rather than the entire region it encloses), use #$convexHullOf.  Note that <code>OBJECT</code> might be a single object or a #$Group of spatially disjoint objects. 
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$MinimalSubsumingConvexSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment (MakingAvailableFn Reflector) "The collection of events in which a reflector is made available.")
(comment Evidence-Indication "A sub-collection of #$Indication. Each instance is an indication that makes something evident.")
(comment Record "A sub-collection of #$Evidence-Indication. Each instance is anything (such as a document or a phonograph record or a photograph) providing permanent evidence of or information about past events.")
(comment WrittenRecord "A sub-collection of #$TextualMaterial. Each instance is a written document preserving knowledge of facts or events.")
(comment Translation-WrittenRecord "A sub-collection of #$WrittenRecord. Each instance is a written communication in a second language having the same meaning as the written communication in a first language.")
(comment Subtitle "A sub-collection of #$Translation-WrittenRecord. Each instance is a translation of foreign dialogue of a movie or TV program; usually displayed at the bottom of the screen.")
(comment bystanders "(#$bystanders EVENT AGT) means that AGT is a bystander with respect to EVENT.")
(comment causes-PropProp "A causal-relation predicate that holds between sentences (see #$CycLSentence-Assertible).   (#$causes-PropProp SENT1 SENT2) means that the state-of-affairs described by SENT1 causes the state-of-affairs described by SENT2.  Both of these states-of-affairs are presumed to be actual (from the point of view of the context in which the #$causes-PropProp sentence is asserted), i.e. both SENT1 and SENT2 are true.  Thus (#$implies SENT1 SENT2) will also hold.  But note that #$causes-PropProp differs from #$implies in four important ways:
<p>
(1) Unlike an #$implies sentence, a #$causes-PropProp sentence entails  a certain temporal ordering: the state of affairs described by SENT2  must not temporally precede the state of affairs described by SENT1  (though they may be simultaneous).
<p>
(2) Unlike an #$implies sentence, a #$causes-PropProp sentence presumes an underlying mechanism of causation.
<p>
(3) While #$implies is reflexive, #$causes-PropProp is irreflexive: (#$implies SENT SENT) holds for any SENT but (#$causes-PropProp SENT SENT) never holds.
<p>
(4) While #$implies is a truth-functional logical connective, #$causes-PropProp is a #$Predicate and is not truth-functional.  One consequence of this is that contraposition does not hold for  the latter: (#$causes-PropProp SENT1 SENT2) does NOT entail (#$causes-PropProp (#$not SENT2) (#$not SENT1)).
<p>
Note that (#$causes-PropProp SENT1 SENT2) can be a more informative way of talking about causation than saying that some event EVENT1 causes some event EVENT2 (see #$causes-EventEvent).  This is because often there are just a few key aspects of EVENT1 that cause a few key aspects of EVENT2, to which the remaining details of EVENT1 and EVENT2 are irrelevant; and these key aspects can be expressed directly in sentences.  For the same reason, #$causes-PropProp statements can be more informative than those that causally relate #$Situations (see #$causes-SitSit). See #$NoteAboutCausationPredicates for a full list of CycL causal-relation predicates.")
(comment attorneys "A #$PersonalAssociationPredicate and a specialization of #$receivesServicesFrom.  <code>(#$attorneys AGENT ATTORNEY)</code> means that <code>ATTORNEY</code> is an attorney for <code>AGENT</code>.")
(comment PersonalAssociationPredicate "A #$PredicateType and specialization of #$AgentPredicate.  Each instance of #$PersonalAssociationPredicate is a #$BinaryPredicate that can be used to express a social relationship between two instances of #$Person.  Some examples are #$affiliatedWith, #$employees, #$nephews, and #$rivals.
<p>
Note that the arguments of a #$PersonalAssociationPredicate need not be constrained (via #$argIsa) to #$Person; a #$PersonalAssociationPredicate might allow, e.g., #$Organizations as arguments.")
(comment ProvidingMatchingResources "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$ProvidingMatchingResources, someone provides resources (especially funds) complementary to something.")
(comment Hobnail "A sub-collection of #$Nail. Each instance is a short nail with a thick head; used to protect the soles of boots.")
(comment SurfeitingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingSomethingAvailable. In each #$SurfeitingSomething, someone supplies or feeds to surfeit.")
(comment SteamHeatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HeatingProcess. In each #$SteamHeatingSomething, someone heats something by means of steam.")
(comment ReservedSpaceRegion "A sub-collection of #$SpaceRegion. Each instance is an area reserved for some particular purpose.")
(comment PlateGlass "A sub-collection of #$Glass. Each instance is glass formed into a large thin sheet.")
(comment MakingConstructionArtifactFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  When applied to a specialization TYPE of #$PartiallyTangible (q.v.), #$MakingConstructionArtifactFn returns the collection of events in which one or more instances of TYPE are constructed (the collection in question will be a specialization of #$MakingConstructionArtifact).")
(comment Mound-ConstructionArtifact "A sub-collection of #$ConstructionArtifact. Each instance is a structure consisting of an artificial heap or bank usually of earth or stones.")
(comment Rampart "A sub-collection of #$Embankment. Each instance is an embankment built around a space for defensive purposes.")
(comment Battlement "A sub-collection of #$Rampart. Each instance is a rampart built around the top of a castle with regular gaps for firing arrows or guns.")
(comment RoughBed-PieceOfFurniture "A sub-collection of #$Bed-PieceOfFurniture. Each instance is a rough bed (as at a campsite).")
(comment Refueling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FuelingATransportationDevice. In each #$Refueling, someone provides something with additional fuel with respect to aircraft, ships, and cars.")
(comment Shield "A sub-collection of #$DefenseSystem. Each instance is a protective covering or structure.")
(comment Plate-Shield "A sub-collection of #$Shield. Each instance is a metal sheathing of uniform thickness (such as the shield attached to an artillery piece to protect the gunners).")
(comment Horseshoe "A sub-collection of #$Plate-Shield. Each instance is an U-shaped plate nailed to underside of horse's hoof.")
(comment DeviceRequiringSkillForProperUse "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalDevice. Each instance is a device that requires skill for proper use.")
(comment (EquippingFn MeasuringDevice) "The collection of events in which something is equiped with instruments for measuring, recording, or controlling.")
(comment Restraint-PhysicalDevice "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalDevice. Each instance is a device that retards something's motion.")
(comment WeatherStripping "A sub-collection of #$Strip-Artifact. Each instance is a strip of material to cover the joint of a door or window to exclude the cold.")
(comment incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion "A specialization of  #$incidentInSpaceRegion (q.v.).  <code>(#$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is incident in the interior of <code>REGION</code>.  In particular, <code>POINT</code> is <i>not</i> incident in the boundary of <code>REGION</code> (see #$incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion).  
<p>
Also compare this predicate with #$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion "A specialization of  #$incidentInSpaceRegion (q.v.).  <code>(#$incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is incident in the boundary of <code>REGION</code>.  In particular, <code>POINT</code> is <i>not</i> incident in the interior of <code>REGION</code> (see #$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion).  
<p>
Also compare this predicate with #$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment agentSwindled "An #$ActorSlot that relates an instance of #$SwindlingAnAgent to an #$IntelligentAgent who is swindled in that swindling event.")
(comment Introduction-CW "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$Introduction-CW is a #$ConceptualWork that serves as an introduction to another #$ConceptualWork (of which it may or may not be a subwork).  Examples: the introduction to a book, the introductory part of a musical composition.  Note that this does not, in general, include conceptual works that are introductions to some <i>topic</i> (cf. #$IntroductionToFn), such as an introductory logic textbook.")
(comment ActorTypePlaysRoleFn "#$ActorTypePlaysRoleFn is a binary  function that returns a collection of events when given a specialization of #$TemporalThing and a #$BinaryRolePredicate as arguments.  (#$ActorTypePlaysRoleFn ACTOR-TYPE ROLE) denotes the specialization of #$Situation which consists of all and only the events in which instances of ACTOR-TYPE play ROLE.  For example, (#$ActorTypePlaysRoleFn #$UnitedStatesPresident #$objectActedOn) denotes the collection of situations in which some U.S. president is the object acted on. See also #$ActorPlaysRoleFn.")
(comment Appointing-EquippingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EquippingSomething. In each #$Appointing-EquippingSomething, someone furnishes something.")
(comment RefitSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EquippingSomething. In each #$RefitSomething, someone fits something out again.")
(comment FulfillingAnObligation "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$FulfillingAnObligation is an #$Action in which some #$Obligation (moral, legal, etc.) is partially or completely fulfilled.")
(comment Paying-FulfillingAnObligation "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FulfillingAnObligation. In each #$Paying-FulfillingAnObligation, someone makes a compensation for something.")
(comment Consigning-Entrusting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Entrusting-HandingSomethingToSomeone. In each #$Consigning-Entrusting, something gives something else over to another for care or safekeeping.")
(comment contractee "'(#$contractee CONTRACT AGENT)' means that AGENT enters into a contract to receive a service in the #$ContractingAService CONTRACT.")
(comment contractor "'(#$contractor CONTRACT AGENT)' means that AGENT enters into a contract to perform a service in the #$ContractingAService CONTRACT.")
(comment incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion "A binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate that is a specialization of #$spatiallyDisjoint and a contrary (see #$negationPreds) of  #$incidentInSpaceRegion (q.v.).  <code>(#$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is incident in the exterior of <code>REGION</code>.  Thus, if <code>COMPLEMENT</code> is the complement (see #$spaceRegionComplement) of <code>REGION</code>, then <code>(#$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT COMPLEMENT)</code> holds. 
<p>
See also #$incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment properlySpatiallySubsumes "An asymmetric transitive #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that is a specialization of both #$spatiallySubsumes and #$different (qq.v.).  <code>(#$properlySpatiallySubsumes SUPER SUB)</code> means that the spatial extent of <code>SUB</code> lies within, and is not the same as (cf. #$cospatial), the spatial extent of <code>SUPER</code>.
<p>
Specializations of #$properlySpatiallySubsumes include  #$properPhysicalDecompositions and #$objectEdges.")
(comment CornerOnObject "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$CornerOnObject is a region centered around a point where two edges of a sheet-like object meet at an angle, or where three or more surfaces (together with three or more solid edges dividing them) meet at one place forming a solid angle.    An instance of #$CornerOnObject can be viewed as are either convex or concave, depending on one's perspective.")
(comment properlySpatiallySubsumes-Nontangential "An asymmetric transitive #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that is a specialization of #$properlySpatiallySubsumes (q.v.).  <code>(#$properlySpatiallySubsumes-Nontangential SUPER SUB)</code> means that (i) the spatial extent of <code>SUB</code> properly subsumes the spatial extent of <code>SUPER</code> and (ii) <code>SUB</code> is #$spatiallyDisjoint from the boundary (i.e. each external surface, edge, and corner) of  <code>SUPER</code>.
<p>
Specializations of this predicate include #$internalParts and #$nontangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse.")
(comment CodeOfConductMicrotheorySpindle "A specialization of #$MicrotheorySpindle.  Each instance
#$CodeOfConductMicrotheorySpindle is a spindle composed entirely of
microtheories (see #$mtSpindleMember) which represent the contents of
some or other #$CodeOfConduct.  More precisely, each instance of
#$CodeOfConductMicrotheorySpindle contains just those normative
contexts that apply to or govern some particular particular entity --
e.g., all the codes that operate in a #$Culture-Practice, that apply
within a given #$Organization, etc.")
(comment properlySpatiallySubsumes-Tangential "An asymmetric #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that is a specialization of #$properlySpatiallySubsumes (q.v.).  <code>(#$properlySpatiallySubsumes-Nontangential SUPER SUB)</code> means that (i) the spatial extent of <code>SUB</code> properly subsumes the spatial extent of <code>SUPER</code> and (ii) <code>SUB</code> #$spatiallyIntersects the boundary (i.e. intersects some external surface, edge, or corner) of <code>SUPER</code>.
<p>
Specializations of this predicate include #$externalParts and #$tangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse.
<p>
Cf. #$properlySpatiallySubsumes-Nontangential.")
(comment RecommitSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Entrusting-HandingSomethingToSomeone. In each #$RecommitSomething, someone commits again.")
(comment ObligatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Entrusting-HandingSomethingToSomeone. In each #$ObligatingSomething, someone commits something in order to fulfill an obligation.")
(comment HospitalizingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CommittingSomeoneToAnInstitution. In each #$HospitalizingSomeone, someone admits someone else into a hospital.")
(comment Implement "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalDevice. Each instance is an instrumentation (a piece of equipment or tool) used to effect an end.")
(comment Rod "A sub-collection of #$Implement. Each instance is a long thin implement made of metal or wood.")
(comment Pole-Rod "A sub-collection of #$Rod. Each instance is a long (usually round) rod of wood or metal or plastic.")
(comment Spar-Pole "A sub-collection of #$Pole-Rod. Each instance is a stout rounded pole of wood or metal used to support rigging.")
(comment Brace-StructuralMember "A sub-collection of #$StructuralMember. Each instance is a structural member used to stiffen a framework.")
(comment Crosspiece "A sub-collection of #$Brace-StructuralMember. Each instance is a transverse brace.")
(comment Rung-Crosspiece "A sub-collection of #$Crosspiece. Each instance is a crosspiece between the legs of a chair or ladder.")
(comment Coronation "A sub-collection of #$InitiationCeremony. Each instance is the ceremony of installing a new monarch.")
(comment Investment-PromotingSomeoneInACeremony "A sub-collection of #$PromotingSomeone. Each instance is the ceremonial act of clothing someone in the insignia of an office; the formal promotion of a person to an office or rank.")
(comment ConceptualAbstraction "A sub-collection of #$Thing. Each instance is a general concept formed by extracting common features from specific examples.")
(comment AttributeCharacteristicOfAnEntity "A sub-collection of #$ConceptualAbstraction. Each instance is an abstraction belonging to or characteristic of an entity.")
(comment Quality "A sub-collection of #$AttributeCharacteristicOfAnEntity. Each instance is an essential and distinguishing attribute of something or someone.")
(comment Power-Quality "A sub-collection of #$Quality. Each instance is a possession of controlling influence.")
(comment Control-Power-Quality "A sub-collection of #$Power-Quality. Each instance is a power to direct or determine.")
(comment Territorialization "A sub-collection of #$Organization-AnimalActivity. Each instance is the act of organizing as a territory.")
(comment Reorganization "A sub-collection of #$Organization-Administration-Management. Each instance is the act of imposing a new organization; organizing differently (often involving extensive and drastic changes).")
(comment Collectivization "A sub-collection of #$FoundingAnOrganization. Each instance is the organization of a nation or economy on the basis of collectivism.")
(comment Tending-TakingCare-Handling-Management "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TakingCare-Handling-Management. In each #$Tending-TakingCare-Handling-Management, someone manages or runs something.")
(comment PerformingAnInventory "A sub-collection of (#$MakingAbstractFn #$List-Information). Each instance is a making an itemized list of merchandise or supplies on hand.")
(comment EmpanelingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of (#$MakingAbstractFn #$List-Information). In each #$EmpanelingSomeone, someone enters someone else into a list of prospective jurors.")
(comment IndexingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of (#$MakingAbstractFn #$List-Information). In each #$IndexingSomething, someone lists something in an index.")
(comment BlacklistingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of (#$MakingAbstractFn #$List-Information). In each #$BlacklistingSomeone, someone puts someone else on a blacklist so as to banish or cause to be boycotted.")
(comment PostingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of (#$MakingAbstractFn #$List-Information). In each #$PostingSomething, something enters something else on a public list.")
(comment CommissioningSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EquippingSomething. In each #$CommissioningSomething, someone puts something into commission; equips something for service; of ships.")
(comment Breaking-InvalidatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InvalidatingSomething. In each #$Breaking-InvalidatingSomething, someone invalidates something by judicial action.")
(comment RetiringATradeableSecurity "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ProductRecall. In each #$RetiringATradeableSecurity, someone withdraws something from circulation or from the market with respect to bills, shares, and bonds.")
(comment Remarriage "A sub-collection of #$WeddingCeremony. Each instance is the act of marrying again.")
(comment Amercing-MakingACriminalCharge "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Ticketing-MakingACriminalCharge. In each #$Amercing-MakingACriminalCharge, someone punishes someone else by a fine imposed arbitrarily by the discretion of the court.")
(comment SuspendingSomeoneTemporarilyFromCollege "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SuspendingSomeone. In each #$SuspendingSomeoneTemporarilyFromCollege, someone suspends temporarily from college or university, in England.")
(comment RusticatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BanishingSomeoneFromAPlaceOfResidence. In each #$RusticatingSomeone, someone sends someone else to the country.")
(comment ShepherdingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Tending-GivingCareToACertainThing. In each #$ShepherdingSomething, someone tends something as a shepherd with respect to sheep or goats.")
(disjointWith AdverseSocialAction Perceiving)
(disjointWith AdverseSocialAction Culture-Practice)
(disjointWith AdverseSocialAction HostingASocialGathering)
(disjointWith AdverseSocialAction SocialGathering)
(comment Spiking-AdverseSocialAction "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AdverseSocialAction. In each #$Spiking-AdverseSocialAction, someone stands in the way of something that would otherwise benefit someone else.")
(comment AdverseSocialAction "A specialization of both #$PurposefulAction and #$SocialOccurrence.  Each instance of #$AdverseSocialAction is an event in which some agent performs an act adverse to some agent(s) in which multiple agents, at least one of whom is acting deliberately, take part.  Specializations of this collection include #$HarmingAnAgent.")
(comment StettingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InvalidatingSomething. In each #$StettingSomething, someone cancels, corrects or deletes something.")
(comment BarrackingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ProvidingLodging. In each #$BarrackingSomeone, someone lodges someone else in barracks.")
(comment Keeping-ProvidingLodging "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ProvidingLodging. In each #$Keeping-ProvidingLodging, someone supplies someone else with room and board.")
(comment QuarteringSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ProvidingLodging. In each #$QuarteringSomeone, someone provides housing for (military personnel).")
(comment EnfiladingSomething "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Raking-SomethingDimensionallyCovering. In each #$EnfiladingSomething, someone rakes or is in a position to rake an enemy with gunfire in a lengthwise direction.")
(comment PartakingSomething "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Sharing-OverlappingSituations. In each #$PartakingSomething, something has some of the qualities or attributes of something else.")
(comment Osculation "A sub-collection of #$TouchingEvent. Each instance is a contact of two curves (or two surfaces) at which they have a common tangent.")
(comment OsculatingSomething "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Sharing-OverlappingSituations. In each #$OsculatingSomething, something has at least three points in common with something else.")
(comment MicrotheoryDesignatingFunction-Denotational "The collection of denotational functions which specify that a #$CycLSentence-Assertible
is to be interpreted in a given #$Microtheory.  It will be canonicalized and
checked for well-formedness with respect to that microtheory.
Each microtheory-designating function has an
argument which denotes a microtheory, and another argument which denotes a
#$CycLSentence-Assertible.  It may have other arguments as well.
Use #$microtheoryDesignationArgnum and
#$sentenceDesignationArgnum to specify these argument positions for each
microtheory-designating function.")
(comment ConformingOnesActionsToSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HumanActivity. In each #$ConformingOnesActionsToSomething, someone conforms their action or practices to something.")
(comment Prosecution "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$LegalProcess. In each #$Prosecution, someone brings a criminal action against someone else (in a trial).")
(comment Representation-CooperationEvent "A sub-collection of #$CooperationEvent. Each instance is the act of representing; standing in for someone or some group and speaking with authority in their behalf.")
(comment DefendingSomeoneInALegalProceding "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Representation-CooperationEvent. In each #$DefendingSomeoneInALegalProceding, someone is the defense counsel for someone in a trial.")
(comment BeginningAnActivity "A #$TemporalObjectType and specialization of #$PurposefulAction.  Each instance of #$BeginningAnActivity is an event in which an agent begins an activity.")
(comment AttendingAPartyWithoutACompanion "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SociabilityBasedAction. In each #$AttendingAPartyWithoutACompanion, someone attends a dance or a party without a female companion.")
(comment AlmostDoingAnAction "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Action. In each #$AlmostDoingAnAction, something almost does or experiences something else.")
(comment Joining-SocialGathering "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SocialGathering. In each #$Joining-SocialGathering, someone comes into the company of someone else.")
(comment AgentSituation "The collection of #$Situations that involve an #$Agent-Generic.")
(comment Survival "A sub-collection of #$Living. Each instance is a state of surviving; remaining alive.")
(comment SupportingSomething-TransferringPossession "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TransferringPossession. In each #$SupportingSomething-TransferringPossession, someone supports something materially or financially.")
(comment EnduringATryingSituation "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Situation. In each #$EnduringATryingSituation, someone endures a trying situation.")
(comment Need-SystemCondition "A sub-collection of #$SystemCondition. Each instance is a condition requiring relief.")
(comment ObviatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event-Localized. In each #$ObviatingSomething, something does away with something else.")
(comment Deficiency "A sub-collection of #$Need-SystemCondition. Each instance is the state of needing something that is absent or unavailable.")
(comment AllottingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Action. In each #$AllottingSomething, something is awarded; is allotted.")
(comment DistributingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$TransferringPossession. In each #$DistributingSomething, someone administers or bestows, as in small portions.")
(comment ConformingToAcceptedStandards "A sub-collection of #$CooperationEvent. Each instance is an acting according to certain accepted standards.")
(comment Checking-Evaluating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Evaluating. In each #$Checking-Evaluating, something is verified or confirmed; passes inspection.")
(comment Examination-Investigation "A sub-collection of #$Investigation. Each instance is the act of examining something closely (as for mistakes).")
(comment Comparison-Examination "A sub-collection of #$Examination-Investigation. Each instance is an examining of resemblances or differences.")
(comment UnbalancingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Action. In each #$UnbalancingSomething, something throws something else out of balance or equilibrium.")
(comment Occupation-AnimalActivity "A sub-collection of #$AnimalActivity. Each instance is any activity that occupies a someone's attention.")
(comment Occupation-AnimalActivity "A sub-collection of #$AnimalActivity. Each instance is an activity that occupies someone's attention.")
(comment EndingScene "A sub-collection of #$AnEventEndingSomething. Each instance is the concluding part of an event or occurrence.")
(comment organismConceived "(#$organismConceived CONCEPTION ORGANISM) identifies CONCEPTION as the instance of #$ConceivingSomething-BiologicalReproductionEvent  in which ORGANISM, an instance of #$BiologicalLivingObject, is conceived.")
(comment intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Inclusive "<code>(#$intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Inclusive INTERVAL TYPE EARLY LATE)</code> means that the instance of #$TimeInterval <code>INTERVAL</code> is an interval of type <code>TYPE</code> that is subsumed by (c.f., #$temporallySubsumes) the interval started by (c.f., #$temporallyStartedBy) the earliest interval of <code>TYPE</code> that temporally intersects (c.f., #$temporallyIntersects) the instance of #$TemporalThing <code>EARLY</code>, and finished by (see #$temporallyFinishedBy) the latest interval of <code>TYPE</code> that temporally intersects the instance of #$TemporalThing <code>LATE</code>.  For example,
<pre>   (#$intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Inclusive
           (#$MonthFn #$June (#$YearFn 1990)) #$CalendarMonth
           (#$DayFn 4 (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 1968))) #$Now)
</pre>
means that the June 1990 is one of the instances of #$CalendarMonth that occurs during the interval formed from the first month that intersects (#$DayFn 4 (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 1968))) -- namely January 1968 -- and the last month that, as you read this, intersects the referent of the indexical #$Now.  <p>Note that though in the above example, <code>EARLY</code> and <code>LATE</code> are both instances of #$TimeInterval, <code>EARLY</code> and <code>LATE</code> can be any instance of #$TemporalThing for which a temporally intersecting interval of TYPE can be found: 
<pre>   (#$intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Inclusive
           (#$YearFn 1945) #$CalendarYear
           (#$EndFn #$WorldWarII)
           (#$OrganizationFoundingFn #$LebaneseHizballah))
</pre>means that the year 1945 is an instance of #$CalendarYear subsumed by the interval started by the year 1945 (the year in which WWII ended) and finished by the year 1982 (the year in which Hizballah was founded).  Similarly, 
<pre>   (#$intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Inclusive
           (#$YearFn 1945) #$CalendarYear #$WorldWarII
           (#$OrganizationFoundingFn #$LebaneseHizballah))
</pre>means that 1945 is a year that lies between the years 1939 and 1983.  See also #$intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Exclusive.")
(comment SomeUseRights "A specialization of #$Situation. Each instance of this collection is a situation that consists of someone having some use rights to an object. See the comments on #$ExclusiveUserRights and #$SituationTypeByUserRights for a fuller explanation of how this is cashed out. #$SomeUseRights is a very general instance of #$SituationTypeByUserRights that means that a user has some, as opposed to no, right to use of an object or facility. In most cases, a more specific #$SituationTypeByUserRights (q.v.) can be used.")
(comment OwningSomething "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$StaticSituation and #$PrimaryUserRights.  Each instance of #$OwningSomething is a situation consisting of some agent owning something (see #$owns).")
(comment geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Open "A binary predicate and specialization of #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion (q.v.) that relates two space regions (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) when the first is geometrically-inside the second and there is an open channel from the first to the outside of the second.  
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Open INSIDE REGION)</code> holds if and only if (i) <code>(#$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion INSIDE REGION)</code> holds) and (ii) the mereological sum of the inside of and the outside of <code>REGION</code> is strongly-connected, i.e. this holds:
<pre>
  (#$isa 
    (#$SpaceRegionSumFn 
      (#$InsideOfSpaceRegionFn <code>REGION</code>) 
      (#$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn <code>REGION</code>))
    #$StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion) .
</pre>
For example, if a ball is resting inside a baseball glove, the space region occupied by the ball is #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Open the space region occupied by the glove.
<p>
Cf. #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed.
<p>
Note that #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Open is the relation called <i>GEO-INSIDE-OPEN</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment Disc "A specialization of #$Cylinder. Each instance of #$Disc is a cylinder with a height much less than the radius of its base.")
(comment geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed "A binary predicate and specialization of #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion (q.v.) that relates two space regions (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) when one is geometrically-inside the other and there is <i>no</i> open channel from the first region to the outside of the second region.  Instead, the outside of the second region is closed-off to the first region by a point connection.  
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed INSIDE REGION)</code> holds if and only if (i) <code>(#$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion INSIDE REGION)</code> holds), (ii) the mereological sum <code>IN+OUT</code> of the inside and the outside of <code>REGION</code> -- i.e. the region denoted by
<pre>  (#$SpaceRegionSumFn 
    (#$InsideOfSpaceRegionFn <code>REGION</code>) 
    (#$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn <code>REGION</code>))
</pre>
-- is a #$ConnectedSpaceRegion, and (iii) <code>IN+OUT</code> is <i>not</i> a #$StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion.
<p>
Here's an example.  Let <code>C1</code>, <code>C2</code>, and <code>C3</code> be three circles such that (a) <code>C1</code> is properly inside and non-tangential with respect to <code>C2</code> and (b) <code>C2</code> is properly inside <code>C3</code> and tangent to it at some point <code>P</code>.  Then the region bounded by <code>C1</code> is #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed with respect to the region between <code>C2</code> and <code>C3</code>.
<p>
Cf. #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Open.
<p>
Note that #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed is the relation called <i>GEO-INSIDE-CLOSED</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment KeepingInACertainState "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Situation. In each #$KeepingInACertainState, something keeps something in a certain state, position, or activity.")
(comment Yielding-InfluencingAnAgent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InfluencingAnAgent. In each #$Yielding-InfluencingAnAgent, something gives in, as to influence or pressure.")
(comment SpeechAct "A sub-collection of #$CommunicationAct-Single. Each instance is the use of language to perform some act.")
(comment Description-SpeechAct "A sub-collection of #$SpeechAct. Each instance is the act of describing something.")
(comment ElucidatingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Explaining-CommunicationAct. In each #$ElucidatingSomething, someone makes something clear and (more) comprehensible.")
(comment ElaboratingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ElucidatingSomething. In each #$ElaboratingSomething, someone adds details, as to an account or idea; clarifies the meaning of and discourse of something in a learned way, usually in writing.")
(comment ExpoundingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ElaboratingSomething. In each #$ExpoundingSomething, someone states ideas in an organized fashion.")
(comment DescribingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExpoundingSomething. In each #$DescribingSomething, someone gives a description of something.")
(comment ContainingSomething "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Configuration. In each #$ContainingSomething, something is contained in something else.")
(comment resultArgIsaArg "(#$resultArgIsaArg FUNCTION N M) means that the result of applying the instance of #$PredicateDenotingFunction FUNCTION to a series of arguments for which FUNCTION is defined, including COLLECTION in the Mth argument position, will result in an instance PRED of #$Predicate such that (#$argIsa PRED N COLLECTION).")
(comment resultStrictlyFunctionalInArgs "(#$resultStrictlyFunctionalInArgs FUNCTION INTEGER) means that the result of applying FUNCTION to a series of arguments for which it is well defined is a predicate PRED such that (#$strictlyFunctionalInArgs PRED INTEGER).")
(comment OfDateFn "An instance of #$PredicateDenotingFunction that takes an instance of #$CalendarCoveringType and returns a binary predicate that relates a date to the instance of that instance of #$CalendarCoveringType that intersects that date.  For example, (#$OfDateFn #$CalendarDay) denotes a predicate PRED such that (PRED DATE-1 DATE-2) means that DATE-1 is the instance of #$CalendarDay that temporally intersects DATE-2.  For example, 
<pre>
   ((#$OfDateFn #$CalendarDay) 
       (#$DayFn 14 
          (#$MonthFn #$June 
             (#$YearFn 1972))) 
       (#$SecondFn 54 
          (#$MinuteFn 48 
             (#$HourFn 10
                (#$DayFn 14
                  (#$MonthFn #$June
                     (#$YearFn 1972)))))))
</pre> means that June 14, 1972 is the day of the date 10:48:54 June 14, 1972.")
(comment dateInRangePredDefinedForCalendarCoveringType "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that relates an instance of #$DateInRangePredicate to the unique instance of #$CalendarCoveringType that is implicit in the date-granularity implicitly defined for that predicate.  For example, (#$dateInRangePredDefinedForCalendarCoveringType #$yearInRange #$CalendarYear) means that the #$yearInRange relation holds only between dates that are years.")
(comment searchStringsUsed "An expression of the form (#$searchStringsUsed SEARCH STRING) means that during the instance of #$SearchingAnInformationStore SEARCH, the #$CharacterString STRING was used to guide the search process.  For instance, if one was searching the #$TheCurrentCycKB for a #$CycLConstant to describe humans and they entered the string 'person' into the search window.  That string would play the role #$searchStringsUsed in that event.")
(comment NullSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion and #$PositiveDimensionalThing (qq.v.).  #$NullSpaceRegion is the collection of \"null spatial regions\".  There is at most one known instance of #$NullSpaceRegion with respect to any context in which some #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegions (q.v.) are known to have locations, either absolutely or relatively (see #$LocationalPredicate and its immediate specializations).
<p>
Each #$NullSpaceRegion is of some particular positive number of dimensions (see #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions).  See the specializations #$NullSpaceRegion-1D, #$NullSpaceRegion-2D, and #$NullSpaceRegion-3D.  By definition, a null space region of a given dimensionality is disconnected from (see #$disconnectedSpaceRegions) every homogeneous space region of that same dimensionality (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional).
<p>
The introduction of null regions into #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory allows the functions #$SpaceRegionComplementFn, #$SpaceRegionProductFn, and #$SpaceRegionDifferenceFn to be defined as #$TotalFunctions.  For example, #$SpaceRegionComplementFn can then be defined on #$UniversalSpaceRegions (q.v.), and #$SpaceRegionProductFn can be defined for pairs of regions that are #$discreteSpaceRegions (q.v.).  (For more details, see the rules on the above functions.)")
(comment PolyDimensionalThing "A #$SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality and a specialization of #$PositiveDimensionalThing.  Each instance of #$PolyDimensionalThing is a spatial thing that has a dimensionality of two or higher, and thus is either a two- or three- (or higher-) dimensional spatial object.  
<p>
Examples of #$PolyDimensionalThings include tangible or intangible spatially-localized polydimensional objects, such as the flat surface of a tabletop and the table itself, as well as abstract geometrical objects that are at least two-dimensional, such as a Platonic cube.  Specializations of this collection include #$PartiallyTangible, #$BilateralObject, and #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing.")
(comment ThreeDimensionalThing "A #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions and a specialization of #$PolyDimensionalThing (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ThreeDimensionalThing is a three-dimensional spatial thing.  Examples include spatially-localized objects (such as buildings and people), 3-D pure space regions, and abstract 3-D geometric objects.  Specializations of this collection include #$PartiallyTangible, #$SpaceChunk, and #$ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing.")
(comment organizationFounded "(#$organizationFounded FOUNDING ORGANIZATION) means that the instance FOUNDING of #$FoundingAnOrganization is the founding of the instance ORGANIZATION of #$Organization.")
(comment against-Underspecified "An #$UnderspecifiedPredicate.  (#$before-Underspecified THING1 THING2) means that THING1 is in some sense before THING2.")
(comment against-Underspecified "An #$UnderspecifiedPredicate.  (#$against-Underspecified THING1 THING2) means that THING1 is in some sense against THING2.")
(comment SleepingLate "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Sleeping. In each #$SleepingLate, someone sleeps later than usual or customary.")
(comment Bed-PieceOfFurniture "The set of beds, a particular kind of furniture on which people or animals sleep.")
(comment KeepingAnAnimalAwake "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$KeepingInACertainState.  Each instance of #$KeepingAnAnimalAwake is an event in which something (e.g. a loud noise, excessive #$Caffeine, #$Fear) keeps an animal from #$GoingToSleep.")
(comment SpaceRegionProductFn "A binary function that takes a pair of space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) of the same dimensionality (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional)to their mereological product or intersection in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  (#$SpaceRegionProductFn <b>REG1 REG2</b>) is the unique #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion <b>PROD</b> such that any region connects with (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) <b>PROD</b> if and only if it connects with some region <b>REGION</b> such that <b>REGION</b> is a part of (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) both <b>REG1</b> and <b>REG2</b>. 
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$spaceRegionProduct.  Also see #$SpaceRegionSumFn and #$SpaceRegionDifferenceFn.
<p>
Note that #$SpaceRegionProductFn is the function called <i>prod</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment SpaceRegionSumFn "A binary function that takes a pair of space regions to their mereological sum in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  (#$SpaceRegionSumFn <b>REG1 REG2</b>) is the unique #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion <b>SUM</b> such that any region connects with (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) <b>SUM</b> if and only if it connects with either <b>REG1</b> or <b>REG2</b>. 
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$spaceRegionSum.")
(comment spaceRegionSum "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of space regions to their mereological sum or union in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  <code>(#$spaceRegionSum SUM REG1 REG2)</code> means that <code>SUM</code> is the unique #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion such that any region connects with (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) <code>SUM</code> if and only if it connects with either <code>REG1</code> or <code>REG2</code>. 
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpaceRegionSumFn.  Also see #$spaceRegionProduct and #$spaceRegionDifference.")
(comment topologicallyInsideSpaceRegion "A binary predicate and specialization of #$insideSpaceRegion (q.v.) that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to any space region that is \"topologically inside\" it in the sense used in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$topologicallyInsideSpaceRegion INSIDE REGION)</code> means that <code>INSIDE</code> is inside <code>REGION</code> and any #$ConnectedSpaceRegion that connects with both <code>INSIDE</code> and (#$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn <code>REGION</code>) necessarily overlaps <code>REGION</code> (see #$overlappingSpaceRegions).  Intuitively, this means that there is no way to reach <code>INSIDE</code> from the outside of <code>REGION</code>, or vice versa, except by cutting through <code>REGION</code>. 
<p>
Note that #$topologicallyInsideSpaceRegion is the relation called <i>TOP-INSIDE</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.
<p>
Cf. #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion.")
(comment geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion "A binary predicate and specialization of #$insideSpaceRegion (q.v.) that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to any space region that is \"geometrically inside\" it in the sense used in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion INSIDE REGION)</code> means that <code>INSIDE</code> is inside <code>REGION</code> but not \"topologically inside\" it (cf. #$topologicallyInsideSpaceRegion).  Intuitively, this means that <code>INSIDE</code> can be reached from the outside of <code>REGION</code>, and vice versa, without cutting through <code>REGION</code>. 
<p>
Note that #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion is the relation called <i>GEO-INSIDE</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment spaceRegionProduct "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) of the same dimensionality (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) to their mereological product or intersection in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  <code>(#$spaceRegionProduct PROD REG1 REG2)</code> means that <code>PROD</code> is the unique space region such that any region connects with (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) <code>PROD</code> if and only if it connects with some region <code>REGION</code> such that <code>REGION</code> is a part of (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) both <code>REG1</code> and <code>REG2</code>. 
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpaceRegionProductFn.  Also see #$spaceRegionSum and #$spaceRegionDifference.")
(comment GeneralizedSpaceRegionProductFn "A unary function that takes a set of equidimensional space regions (see #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet) to their mereological product or intersection. (#$GeneralizedSpaceRegionProductFn <b>REGION-SET</b>) is the #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion <b>PROD</b> that is the spatial product of all the regions in <b>REGION-SET</b>. If the members of <b>REGION-SET</b> do not all share a common region of overlap, then the function returns a #$NullSpaceRegion (q.v.). This function is an extension of the function #$SpaceRegionProductFn, which takes two regions to their product.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$generalizedSpaceRegionProduct. Also see #$GeneralizedSpaceRegionSumFn.")
(comment insideSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to any space region that is \"inside\" it in the sense used in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$insideSpaceRegion INSIDE REGION)</code> means that (i) <code>INSIDE</code> is discrete from (see #$discreteSpaceRegions) <code>REGION</code> and (ii) <code>INSIDE</code> is part of (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) the minimal convex space region subsuming <code>REGION</code> (see #$convexSpaceRegionOf). 
<p>
For example, the space occupied by the yolk of a chicken egg is #$insideSpaceRegion the space occupied by the unbroken eggshell that surronds it.
<p>
Note that #$insideSpaceRegion is the relation called <i>INSIDE</i>
in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.
<p>
See also #$insideSpaceRegion-Inverse, #$insideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal and #$outsideSpaceRegion.")
(comment insideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal "A binary predicate and specialization of #$insideSpaceRegion (q.v.) that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to the maximal space region that is inside it (in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory).
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$insideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal MAX-INSIDE REGION)</code> means that <code>MAX-INSIDE</code> is the space region (if there is one) with which any space region <code>ANY-REGION</code> is connected (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) if and only if there is some region <code>OTHER-INSIDE</code> that is inside <code>REGION</code> and connected to <code>ANY-REGION</code>.
<p>
Note that if a given space region has <i>any</i> region inside it, then there will be a (unique) maximal one.  By the same token, a space region (e.g. a convex, solid, hole-free region) might have <i>no</i> region inside it, and thus no maximal one.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$InsideOfSpaceRegionFn; also see #$outsideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal.")
(comment GeneralizedSpaceRegionSumFn "A unary function that takes a set of quidimensional space regions (see #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet) to their mereological sum or union. (#$GeneralizedSpaceRegionSumFn <b>REGION-SET</b>) is the #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion <b>SUM</b> that is the spatial sum of all the regions in <b>REGION-SET</b>.  This function is an extension of the  funcion #$SpaceRegionSumFn, which takes two regions to their sum. 
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$generalizedSpaceRegionSum.  Also see #$GeneralizedSpaceRegionProductFn.")
(comment ConvexSpaceRegionFn "A unary function and a specialization (via #$genlFunctions) of #$MinimalSubsumingConvexSpaceRegionFn (q.v.) that is defined only for #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions (q.v.).  (#$ConvexSpaceRegionFn <b>REGION</b>) is the #$ConvexSpaceRegion <b>CONVEX</b> delimited by the \"convex-hull\" of <b>REGION</b>.  That is, <b>CONVEX</b> is the minimal convex region that subumes <b>REGION</b>. 
<p>
This function is taken as a primitive notion (and called <i>conv</i>) in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory, and is used there to define other topological notions such as #$insideSpaceRegion, #$outsideSpaceRegion, and #$partiallyInsideSpaceRegion. 
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$convexSpaceRegionOf.")
(comment SymmetricSpaceRegionDifferenceFn "A binary function that takes a pair of space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) of the same dimensionality (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) to their \"symmetric difference\".  (#$SymmetricSpaceRegionDifferenceFn <b>SYMDIFF REGION1 REGION2</b>) means that <b>SYMDIFF</b> is the mereological difference (see #$spaceRegionDifference)
between the sum (see #$spaceRegionSum) of <b>REGION1</b> and <b>REGION2</b> and the product (see #$spaceRegionProduct) of <b>REGION1</b> and <b>REGION2</b>.
<p>
Currently, this function is an #$ELRelation whose #$expansion given in terms of
#$spaceRegionDifference, #$SpaceRegionProductFn, and #$SpaceRegionSumFn.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$symmetricSpaceRegionDifference.")
(comment spaceRegionDifference "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) of the same dimensionality (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) to their mereological difference in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  <code>(#$spaceRegionDifference DIFF REG1 REG2)</code> means that <code>DIFF</code> is the product (see #$spaceRegionProduct) of <code>REG1</code> and the complement (see #$spaceRegionComplement) of <code>REG2</code>.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpaceRegionDifferenceFn.  Also see #$spaceRegionSum.")
(comment SpaceRegionDifferenceFn "A binary function that takes a pair of space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) of the same dimensionality (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) to their mereological difference in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  (#$SpaceRegionDifferenceFn <b>REG1 REG2</b>) is the product (see #$SpaceRegionProductFn) of <b>REG1</b> and the complement (see #$SpaceRegionComplementFn) of <b>REG2</b>.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$spaceRegionDifference.  Also see #$SpaceRegionSumFn.
<p>
Note that #$SpaceRegionDifferenceFn is the function called <i>diff</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment symmetricSpaceRegionDifference "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) of the same dimensionality (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) to their \"symmetric difference\".  <code>(#$symmetricSpaceRegionDifference SYMDIFF REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>SYMDIFF</code> is the mereological difference (see #$spaceRegionDifference)
between the sum (see #$spaceRegionSum) of <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> and the product (see #$spaceRegionProduct) of <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code>.
<p>
Currently, this predicate is an #$ELRelation whose #$expansion given in terms of
#$spaceRegionDifference, #$spaceRegionProduct, and #$spaceRegionSum.
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$SymmetricSpaceRegionDifferenceFn.")
(comment identicalSpaceRegions "An equivalence relation and a #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight (q.v.) that is a specialization of both #$cospatial and #$equals.  <code>(#$identicalSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> holds if and only if <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) such that (i) <code>REGION1</code> is a part of (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) <code>REGION2</code> and (ii) <code>REGION2</code> is a part of <code>REGION1</code>.  Thus, #$identicalSpaceRegions is the #$equals relation restricted to space regions.
<p>
Note that #$identicalSpaceRegions is the relation called \"=\" in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.")
(comment properPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse "An asymmetric transitive #$RCCBinaryPredicate (q.v.) that is a specialization of #$partOfSpaceRegion-Inverse and the <i>inverse</i> of #$properPartOfSpaceRegion (q.v.).  <code>(#$properPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse REGION SUBREGION)</code> means that <code>SUBREGION</code> and <code>REGION</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionalty (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) such that <code>REGION</code> has <code>SUBREGION</code> as a part, but <code>SUBREGION</code> does <i>not</i> have <code>REGION</code> as a part.  It follows that <code>REGION</code> and <code>SUBREGION</code> are not identical (see #$identicalSpaceRegions). 
<p>
For example, the space region occupied by the US has that occupied by Texas as a proper part, but does <i>not</i> have itself as a proper part.
<p>
#$properPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse is \"partitioned into\" the two specializations #$tangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse and #$nontangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse.
<p>
Note that #$properPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse is the relation called <i>PP<sup>-1</sup></i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.")
(comment properPartOfSpaceRegion "An asymmetric transitive #$RCCBinaryPredicate (q.v.) that is a specialization of #$partOfSpaceRegion (q.v.).  <code>(#$properPartOfSpaceRegion SUBREGION REGION)</code> means that <code>SUBREGION</code> and <code>REGION</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionalty (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) such that <code>SUBREGION</code> is a part of <code>REGION</code>, but <code>REGION</code> is <i>not</i> a part of <code>SUBREGION</code>.  It follows that <code>SUBREGION</code> and <code>REGION</code> are not identical (see #$identicalSpaceRegions). 
<p>
For example, the space occupied by Texas is a proper part of that occupied by the US, but is <i>not</i> a proper part of itself.
<p>
#$properPartOfSpaceRegion is \"partitioned into\" the two specializations #$tangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion and #$nontangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion.  See also #$properPartOfSpaceRegion-Inverse.
<p>
Note that #$properPartOfSpaceRegion is the relation called <i>PP</i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.")
(comment overlappingSpaceRegions "A reflexive and symmetric #$RCCBinaryPredicate that is a specialization of both #$connectedSpaceRegions and #$spatiallyIntersects.  <code>(#$overlappingSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionalty (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) that have a common part (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).  
<p>
#$overlappingSpaceRegions is \"partitioned into\" three of its immediate specializations: #$partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions, #$partOfSpaceRegion, and #$partOfSpaceRegion-Inverse.
<p>
For example, the space regions respectively occupied by #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and the Sonoron desert (which straddles the US and Mexico)
are overlapping, while those of the US and #$Mexico are not.
<p>
The contrary predicate (see #$negationPreds) of #$overlappingSpaceRegions is #$discreteSpaceRegions.
<p>
Note that #$overlappingSpaceRegions is the relation called <i>O</i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.")
(comment rcc8RelationHoldsBetween "A ternary predicate that relates an #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight (q.v.) predicate to two #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions (q.v.).  <code>(#$rcc8RelationHoldsBetween RCC8PRED REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>RCC8PRED</code> holds between <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code>.
<p>
Note that there is <i>exactly one</i> instance of #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight that holds between any two instances of #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion in the
#$UniversalSpaceRegion (q.v.) of a given context.  Hence, this predicate could be useful for writing rules for a theory of topological state changes.")
(comment connectedSpaceRegions "A reflexive and symmetric #$RCCBinaryPredicate that is a specialization of both #$touches-Directly and #$spatiallyEquidimensional, (qq.v.).  #$connectedSpaceRegions is the primitive relation of connection in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  Intuitively, <code>(#$connectedSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionality that either touch or overlap.  
<p>
All fifteen #$RCCBinaryPredicates are defined, ultimately, in terms of  #$connectedSpaceRegions.  Of these fifteen, the eight mutually-exclusive and jointly-exhaustive <i>basic</i> relations defined in the RCC8 theory (see #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight) describe <i>degrees of connectedness</i> between regions.  (Seven of the eight base relations are specializations of #$connectedSpaceRegions; the remaining relation, #$disconnectedSpaceRegions, is defined as the relative complement or #$negationPreds of #$connectedSpaceRegions.)  

  
<p>
#$connectedSpaceRegions is denoted by \"C\" in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.
<p>
See also the specializations #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions and #$overlappingSpaceRegions.")
(comment disconnectedSpaceRegions "An irreflexive and symmetric #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight (q.v.) that is a specialization of both #$discreteSpaceRegions and #$spatiallySeparated.  #$disconnectedSpaceRegions holds between certain #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions that are #$spatiallyEquidimensional (qq.v.).  <code>(#$disconnectedSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are not connected (cf. #$connectedSpaceRegions).  Thus, they neither intersect (cf. #$overlappingSpaceRegions) nor touch (cf. #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions).
<p>
For example, the space regions respectively occupied by #$Canada and #$Mexico are mutually disconnected, while those occupied by #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and #$Mexico are not.
<p>
Note that #$disconnectedSpaceRegions is the relation called <i>DC</i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.")
(comment discreteSpaceRegions "An irreflexive and symmetric #$RCCBinaryPredicate that is a specialization of both #$spatiallyDisjoint and #$spatiallyEquidimensional.  <code>(#$discreteSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionality that do not spatially overlap (cf. #$overlappingSpaceRegions).  Thus, they are either spatially separated (see #$disconnectedSpaceRegions) or touching (see #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions).
<p>
For example, the space regions respectively occupied by #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and #$Mexico are discrete, while those of the US and Sonoron desert (which straddles the US and Mexico) are not.
<p>
Note that #$discreteSpaceRegions is the relation called <i>DR</i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.")
(comment SkinOfSpaceRegionFn "A unary function that takes a space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to its \"skin\" in the sense used in the extension of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory in which atoms are introduced (see #$RCCWithAtomicRegionsMt).  Intuitively, the skin of a space region can be thought of as its one-atom-thick outermost layer or external surface.
<p>
More precisely, (#$SkinOfSpaceRegionFn <b>REGION</b>) is the space region <b>SKIN</b> such that, for any space region <b>ANYREGION</b>, <b>ANYREGION</b> is connected to (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) to <b>SKIN</b> if and only if <b>ANYREGION</b> is connected to an #$AtomicSpaceRegion that is a #$tangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion <b>REGION</b>. 
<p>
Note that #$SkinOfSpaceRegionFn is #$undefined (q.v.) for atomic space regions and #$NullSpaceRegions. 
<p> 
#$SkinOfSpaceRegionFn is the function called <i>skin</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$skinOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment convexSpaceRegionOf "A binary predicate and a specialization of #$convexSpaceRegionSubsuming-Minimal (q.v.) that relates a given #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion (q.v.) to the minimal #$ConvexSpaceRegion that subsumes it.  <code>(#$convexSpaceRegionOf CONVEX REGION)</code> means that <code>CONVEX</code> is the space region delimited by the \"convex-hull\" or convex-cover of a <code>REGION</code>.
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$ConvexSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment insideSpaceRegion-Inverse "A binary predicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to any space region that is \"inside\" it in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  #$insideSpaceRegion-Inverse is the inverse of (see #$inverseBinaryPredicateOf) #$insideSpaceRegion (q.v.).
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$insideSpaceRegion-Inverse REGION OUTSIDE)</code> means that (i) <code>INSIDE</code> is #$discreteSpaceRegions <code>REGION</code> and (ii) <code>INSIDE</code> is #$partOfSpaceRegion the minimal convex space region subsuming <code>REGION</code> (see #$convexSpaceRegionOf). 
<p>
Note that #$insideSpaceRegion-Inverse is <i>not</i> the same relation as  #$outsideSpaceRegion.  For example, the geographical space region occupied by #$France bears the later relation, but not the former relation, to the geographical space region occupied by #$Germany.
<p>
#$insideSpaceRegion-Inverse is the relation called <i>INSIDE<sup>-1</sup></i>
in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment outsideSpaceRegion-Inverse "A binary predicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to any space region that is \"outside\" it in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  #$outsideSpaceRegion-Inverse is the inverse of (see #$inverseBinaryPredicateOf) of #$insideSpaceRegion (q.v.).
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$outsideSpaceRegion-Inverse REGION OUTSIDE)</code> means that <code>OUTSIDE</code> is discrete from (see #$discreteSpaceRegions) the minimal convex space region subsuming <code>REGION</code> (see #$convexSpaceRegionOf). 
<p>
Note that #$outsideSpaceRegion-Inverse is <i>not</i> the same relation as  #$insideSpaceRegion.  For example, the geographical space region occupied by #$France bears the former relation, but not the latter relation, to the geographical space region occupied by #$Germany.
<p>
#$outsideSpaceRegion-Inverse is the relation called <i>OUTSIDE<sup>-1</sup></i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment outsideSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to any space region that is \"outside\" it in the sense used in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$outsideSpaceRegion OUTSIDE REGION)</code> means that <code>OUTSIDE</code> is discrete from (see #$discreteSpaceRegions) the minimal convex space region subsuming <code>REGION</code> (see #$convexSpaceRegionOf). 
<p>
For example, the space occupied by the intact shell of a chicken egg at a given  time is #$outsideSpaceRegion the space occupied by the yolk of the egg.
<p>
Note that #$outsideSpaceRegion is the relation called <i>OUTSIDE</i>
in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.
<p>
See also #$outsideSpaceRegion-Inverse, #$outsideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal and #$insideSpaceRegion.")
(comment InsideOfSpaceRegionFn "A unary function that takes a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to the maximal space region that is inside it (see #$insideSpaceRegion).  
<p>
More precisely, (#$InsideOfSpaceRegionFn <b>REGION</b>) is the space region with which any space region <b>ANY-REGION</b> is connected (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) if and only if there is some region <b>INSIDE-REGION</b> that is inside (see #$insideSpaceRegion) <b>REGION</b> and connected to <b>ANY-REGION</b>.
<p>
Note that #$InsideOfSpaceRegionFn is the function known as <i>inside</i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$insideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal; also see #$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment outsideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal "A binary predicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to the maximal space region that is outside it (see #$outsideSpaceRegion) in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$outsideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal MAX-OUTSIDE REGION)</code> means that <code>MAX-OUTSIDE</code> is the space region with which any space region <code>ANY-REGION</code> is connected (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) if and only if there is some region <code>OTHER-OUTSIDE</code> that is outside <code>REGION</code> and connected to <code>ANY-REGION</code>.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn; also see #$insideOfSpaceRegion-Maximal.")
(comment RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight "A specialization of #$RCCBinaryPredicate (q.v.).  This collection has exactly eight instances, which constitute the jointly exhaustive and pairwise disjoint base relations in the #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory (q.v.).  Thus, for any pair of #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions, there is exactly one RCC8 base relation that holds between them (see #$rcc8RelationHoldsBetween).
<p>
For a list of all eight instances, see the \"Arg 2\" #$isa assertions on this collection.")
(comment externallyConnectedSpaceRegions "An irreflexive and symmetric #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight (q.v.) that is a specialization of #$connectedSpaceRegions, #$discreteSpaceRegions, and #$touches-Externally (qq.v.).  <code>(#$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionality that are connected but do <i>not</i> have a common part (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).  Intuitively put, they touch but don't overlap.
<p>
For example, the space regions respectively occupied by #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and #$Mexico are externally connected, as are the regions occupied by #$VaticanCity and #$CityOfRomeItaly.
<p>
#$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions is referred to as <i>EC</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment evaluatedSentence "(#$evaluatedSentence SENTENCE) means that a reviewer has looked at the #$CycLPropositionalSentence SENTENCE and felt that the information content of the sentence could be described by some specPred of #$evaluatedSentence. No assertions should be made on this predicate, as its extent should be entirely concludable via #$genlPreds.")
(comment uninterestingSentence "(#$uninterestingSentence SENTENCE) means that a reviewer who looked at the #$CycLPropositionalSentence SENTENCE felt that the information the sentence expresses, whether true or false, is not worth asserting into the KB.  An example sentence might be 'Cyndy is seated.'")
(comment functionalInArgs "A #$MetaPredicate for specifying the argument-place(s) with respect to which a given #$FunctionalPredicate (q.v) is \"functional\".  (#$functionalInArgs PRED N) means that PRED is functional in its Nth argument-place.  That is, for any particular way of fixing all of PRED's other arguments, there will be at most one thing that, if taken as the Nth argument, would result in a true sentence.  For example, #$biologicalMother is functional in its second argument, as nothing has more than one biological mother.
<p>
In most cases, the entry-format of a functional argument-place should be #$singleEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.).  In some cases, however, the  entry-format is #$intervalEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.), and then the meaning of (#$functionalInArgs PRED N) deviates slightly from that described above.  In such a case there might be more than one instance of #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) that, when taken as PRED's Nth argument (and keeping the other arguments fixed), yields a true statement; but if so, all such (equivalently \"truth-yielding\") scalar-intervals must <i>overlap</i> one another (see #$quantityIntersects).  For example, #$heightOfObject's second, functional argument-place has the interval-entry format, reflecting the fact that it might be true to say of a given basketball player (e.g.) <i>either</i> that he is 6-feet-11-inches in height or that he is between 6-feet-10 and 7-feet in height.
<p>
Obviously, the specified integer N must be less than or equal to the arity (see #$arity) of PRED.  Note that, for some ways of fixing the other arguments, there might be <i>nothing</i> that would yield a true sentence when put in PRED's functional argument-place.  For example, #$dictator is functional in its second argument because a given country can have at most one dictator at a given time; but of course some countries have no dictator.
<p>
See also the more specialized #$strictlyFunctionalInArgs.")
(comment NoddingOff "")
(comment PreventingFromHappening "A collection of events.  Each instance of #$PreventingFromHappening is an event which is the prevention of another event from taking place.  For example, the act of a police officer stopping a crime in progress would be the prevention of a crime.  To relate instances of #$PreventingFromHappening to the event types that they prevent, use the function #$PreventingFn.")
(comment BreakingABone "A #$TemporalObjectType and specialization of #$BreakingEvent and #$FracturingABone.  Each instance of #$BreakingABone is an event in which a bone that is part of a living animal is broken.")
(comment mtSpatialUniverse-3D "A binary #$MicrotheoryPredicate that relates a given context (i.e. #$Microtheory) to a three-dimensional space region (see #$SpaceChunk) that constitutes \"the spatial universe\" in that context.  
<p>
<code>(#$mtSpatialUniverse-3D MT UNIVERSE)</code> means that <code>UNIVERSE</code> is the maximal space region of interest in <code>MT</code>.  This implies that 
<code>(#$isa UNIVERSE #$UniversalSpaceRegion-3D)</code> holds in <code>MT</code>. Anything known, in <code>MT</code>, to be a #$SpaceChunk is a part (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) of <code>UNIVERSE</code>. 
<p>
See also #$mtSpatialUniverse-2D.")
(comment UniversalSpaceRegion-3D "A specialization of #$UniversalSpaceRegion  and #$SpaceChunk (qq.v.).  This is the collection of three-dimensional spatial universes. In a context whose spatial universe is 3-D (see #$mtSpatialUniverse-3D), every known #$SpatialThing is spatially extended in at most three dimensions, and is spatially subsumed by that universe; and every known 3-D homogeneous extended space region (i.e. #$SpaceChunk) is part of (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) that spatial universe.
<p>
A notable instance of #$UniversalSpaceRegion-3D is #$TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical.
<p>
See also #$UniversalSpaceRegion-1D and #$UniversalSpaceRegion-2D.")
(comment WordNetWorkflowConstant-ToBeRedone "The collection of all #$CycLConstants that, upon initial review, have been determined to represent concepts that should be given a more appropriate #$CycL representation.")
(comment afterRemoving "When a gaf is unasserted, #$afterRemovings are called on the gaf dependent on the predicate of the gaf.  (#$afterRemoving PRED HOOK) means that HOOK will be called on gaf whose predicate is PRED.  If the gaf predicate has multiple #$afterRemovings, the order of execution is not guaranteed.  Also, #$afterRemovings are retriggered on the removal of each of the arguments to a gaf.")
(comment Container "The collection of tangible objects whose primary function (see #$primaryFunction) is to contain something (see #$ContainingSomething).  Note that just about anything can serve as a container without actually being a container.  For example the palm of your hand when cupped may contain some marbles.  But because your palm is not always configured as and does not function primarily as a container, it would not be represented as a #$Container but would rather stand in a particular <i>relation</i> to the marbles via some containment predicate.  To relate contents to the thing containing them (whether or not that thing is a #$Container) use one or more specific predicates indicating the type of containment present (#$in-ContOpen, #$in-ContClosed, #$in-ContFullOf, #$in-Held, #$packagedIn, #$physicallyContains, #$protectiveContains, ...).  If the type of containment is not known use the general predicate #$in-ContGeneric.")
(comment (UnsuccessfulAttemptToFn Vomiting) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Attempting. In each (#$UnsuccessfulAttemptToFn #$Vomiting), someone makes an unsuccessful effort to vomit; strains to vomit.")
(comment ReplacementEvent "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$GeneralizedTransfer.  Each instance of #$ReplacementEvent is an #$Event in which something is (in some sense) replaced with something else.  
<p>
Note that #$ReplacementEvent is not a specialization of #$PurposefulAction, or even #$Action.  For example, some water replacing some other water in a puddle (because of a flood, say) would count as an instance of #$ReplacementEvent.
<p>
A notable specialization of this collection which <i>is</i> a specialization of #$PurposefulAction is #$ReplacingSomething (q.v.).")
(comment Interfering "A specialization of (#$AttemptingFn #$PreventingFromHappening) and #$ConflictEvent.  Each instance of #$Interfering is an #$Action aimed at thwarting or hindering another agent's action.  An instance of #$Interfering need not <i>prevent</i> an agent's action, but it must make that action more difficult for the agent to perform; otherwise, it is just an instance of (#$AttemptingFn #$Interfering).")
(comment DisruptingSomething "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$DisruptionEvent.  Each instance of #$DisruptingSomething is an #$Action in which some agent (intentionally or otherwise) disrupts an event, i.e. prevents it from occurring as usual, expected, or planned.")
(comment (ExperiencingEmotionFn Sympathy) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExperiencingEmotion. In each (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Sympathy), someone endures (emotional pain).")
(comment LearningThat "This is the set of events in which an agent is acquiring knowledge in the 'know-that' sense, as opposed to the 'know-how' sense.  For the latter type of event, see #$LearningAnAbility.   #$LearningThat may be accomplished by perception, or by just sitting and thinking, and inferring new information from what is already known.  For events which involve gathering information from an information source, see the spec #$LearningFromInformationSource.")
(comment partiallyInsideSpaceRegion "A binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) to another space region that it is partially (and not completely) \"inside\".  
<p>
<code>(#$partiallyInsideSpaceRegion REGION1 REGION2)</code> holds if and only if (i) <code>REGION1</code> is discrete from <code>REGION2</code> (see #$discreteSpaceRegions) and (ii) <code>REGION1</code> partially overlaps (see #$partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions) with the region delineated by the convex cover of <code>REGION2</code> (see #$convexSpaceRegionOf).  
<p>
For example, the space occupied by the popsicle stick is partially inside the space occupied by the frozen, edible part of the popsicle.
<p>
Note that #$partiallyInsideSpaceRegion is the relation called <i>P-INSIDE</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(comment partiallyInsideSpaceRegion-Inverse "A binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate that relates a given space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) to another space region that is partially (and not completely) \"inside\" it.  
This predicate is the inverse of #$partiallyInsideSpaceRegion.
<p>
<code>(#$partiallyInsideSpaceRegion-Inverse REGION1 REGION2)</code> holds if and only if (i) <code>REGION2</code> is discrete from <code>REGION1</code> (see #$discreteSpaceRegions) and (ii) <code>REGION2</code> partially overlaps (see #$partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions) with the region delineated by the convex cover of <code>REGION1</code> (see #$convexSpaceRegionOf).  
<p>
For example, the space occupied by the edible part of a popsicle has the space occupied by the popsicle stick partially inside it.
<p>
Note that #$partiallyInsideSpaceRegion-Inverse is the relation called <i>P-INSIDE<sup>-1</sup></i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.")
(disjointWith Line Surface)
(disjointWith Line SpaceChunk)
(comment EdgeOnObject "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible, #$Path-Spatial, and #$EdgeOnObject (qq.v.).  The collection of all edges on tangible objects.  For a two-dimensional object, its edges are its boundaries other than its corners.  For a three-dimensional object, its edges are the outer portions of those extremities, excluding any corners (see #$CornerOnObject), that are much more acute in cross-section in one direction than in most other directions at the same point.  Some objects -- e.g. spheres, hairs, round toothpicks, and typical burrs -- have no edges.  A discus has one, round, edge.  A mountain ridge might have only a single edge.  A rectangular lot of land has four edges.  A solid polyhedron has six or more edges.  To relate an object to its edges, use #$objectEdges.")
(comment Border "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Border is a spatial thing that constitutes the boundary between two other spatial things.  Specializations of #$Border include #$HumanRegionalBorder and #$TimeZoneBorder.")
(comment LinearObject-Straight "A specialization of #$LinearObject (q.v.). #$LinearObject-Straight is the collection of all linearly-shaped objects that are more-or-less straight (i.e. not curved), whether tangible or intangible.  
<p>
Note that the criterion for being \"straight\" in the present sense varies from context to context.  For example, the standard of straightness for limning a football field is less precise than that for pure geometry.  
<p>
Important geometrical specializations of #$LinearObject-Straight are #$Line-Straight, #$Ray-Geometrical, and #$LineSegment.")
(disjointWith Meat Water)
(comment exceptFor "A binary #$ExceptionPredicate that relates a general #$CycLAssertion to a thing (specified by a #$CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm) for which the assertion fails to hold, and thus under which the assertion should not be used as justification for other inferences.  <code>(#$exceptFor TERM ASSERTION)</code> means that <code>ASSERTION</code> fails to hold for the denotatum of <code>TERM</code>.  A default assumption is that <code>ASSERTION</code> <i>does</i> hold for everything other than the denotatum of <code>TERM</code> (or any other known exceptions there might be on <code>ASSERTION</code>).  For example,
<pre>
     (#$exceptFor
      #$Taiwan-RepublicOfChina
      (#$implies
        (#$isa ?X #$ChineseProvince)
        (#$geopoliticalSubdivision #$China-PeoplesRepublic ?X)))
</pre>
means that #$Taiwan-RepublicOfChina is an exception to the rule that every Chinese province is a geo-political subdivision of the People's Republic of China.
<p>
#$exceptFor is a special case of #$exceptWhen (q.v.), and is only applicable when <code>ASSERTION</code> has exactly one (implicitly) universally quantified variable.  <code>(#$exceptFor TERM ASSERTION)</code> prevents <code>TERM</code> from binding to that variable, thereby blocking any conclusions about the denotatum of <code>TERM</code> that Cyc might otherwise draw from <code>ASSERTION</code>.
<p>
Note that assertions made at the EL level with #$exceptFor are canonicalized into statements that do not contain #$exceptFor, but instead contain the predicate #$abnormal (q.v.).")
(comment exceptWhen "A binary #$ExceptionPredicate that relates a general #$CycLAssertion to a condition (specified by a #$CycLSentence-Assertible) under which the assertion fails to hold, and thus under which the assertion should not be used as justification for other inferences.  <code>(#$exceptWhen EXCEPTION ASSERTION)</code> means that <code>ASSERTION</code> fails to hold in the case of <code>EXCEPTION</code>.  A default assumption is that <code>ASSERTION</code> <i>does</i> hold for all cases other than those covered by <code>EXCEPTION</code> (or by any other exception assertions there might be on <code>ASSERTION</code>).  For example, 
<pre>
  (#$exceptWhen
    (#$spatiallySubsumes #$NorthPoleOfEarth ?THING)
    (#$implies 
      (#$isa ?THING #$GeographicalThing) 
      (#$thereExists ?OTHER 
        (#$northOf ?OTHER ?THING)))
</pre>  
means that every geographical thing has something to the north of it, except when the former is spatially subsumed by the North Pole.  
<p>
Normally, as in the foregoing example, the <code>ASSERTION</code> is an (implicitly) universally quantified sentence, and the <code>EXCEPTION</code>, in effect, places a restriction on the range of values (for the universally quantified variables in <code>ASSERTION)</code> for which <code>ASSERTION</code> is true.  Since implicit universal quantification is always interpreted as having the widest possible scope, in the above example the occurrence of the variable <code>?THING</code> in the exception-giving sentence is interpreted as if it were bound by the same wide-scope universal quantifer that binds the two occurrences of <code>?THING</code> in the #$implies rule.
<p>
A common special case of #$exceptWhen is handled by #$exceptFor (q.v.).  
<p>
Note that assertions made at the EL level with #$exceptWhen are canonicalized into statements that do not contain #$exceptWhen, but instead contain the predicate #$abnormal (q.v.).")
(comment UniversalSpaceRegion-1D "A specialization of #$UniversalSpaceRegion  and #$Line (qq.v.).  This is the collection of one-dimensional spatial universes.  In a context whose spatial universe is 1-D, every known #$SpatialThing is spatially extended in at most one dimension, and is spatially subsumed by that universe; and every known 1-D homogeneous extended space region (i.e. #$Line) is part of (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) that spatial universe.
<p>
See also #$UniversalSpaceRegion-2D and #$UniversalSpaceRegion-3D.")
(comment UnsuccessfulAction "The collection of purposeful actions that are considered \"unsuccessful\".  \"Success\" is a context dependent notion, but for the purposes of this constant can be thought to be relativized to the peformer of the action.  So, a purposeful action is an instance of this collection just in case the performer considered it unsuccessful given the reasons for (or the goals of) performing the action.")
(disjointWith LosingAConflict WinningAConflict)
(comment ActivatingATriggeringMechanism "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$TriggeringSomething.  Each instance of #$ActivatingATriggeringMechanism is a #$PhysicalEvent in which something activates a #$TriggeringMechanism.  Examples: a #$Mouse-Rodent setting off a #$MouseTrap, a #$Soldier pulling the trigger on a #$Rifle, a falling #$Tree-ThePlant triggering a #$CarAlarm.")
(comment GainingAnAgentsFavor "An #$AccomplishmentType.  Each instance of #$GainingAnAgentsFavor is an event in which one agent gains the favor of another (intentionally or otherwise).  In such an event, the latter agent comes to regard the former agent with support, approval, and the like.")
(comment (AttemptingFn GainingAnAgentsFavor) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Attempting. In each (#$AttemptingFn #$GainingAnAgentsFavor), something seeks someone's favor.")
(comment SituationBeginningFn "(#$SituationBeginningFn SITUATION) represents the #$Event in which SITUATION begins to exist.")
(comment BecomingFn "A unary, reifiable #$CollectionDenotingFunction that takes a specialization of #$TemporalThing COL and returns the collection of all #$Events in which something becomes an instance of COL.  For example, (#$BecomingFn #$Teacher) is the collection of events in which something becomes a teacher and (#$BecomingFn #$Transparent) is the collection of events in which something becomes transparent.")
(comment becoming "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that relates a specialization of #$TemporalThing COL to the collection of all events in which something becomes an instance of COL.  This is the predicate corresponding to #$BecomingFn (q.v.).")
(comment SpaceRegionComplementFn "An #$InvertibleFunction (q.v.) that takes a space region to its mereological complement in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  
<p>
(#$SpaceRegionComplementFn <b>REGION</b>) is the #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion <b>COMPLEMENT</b> such that for any such space region <b>SPACE</b> (i) <b>SPACE</b> connects with <b>COMPLEMENT</b> (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) if and only if <b>SPACE</b> is <i>not</i> a nontangential proper part of<b> REGION</b> (see #$nontangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion) and (ii) <b>SPACE</b> overlaps <b>COMPLEMENT</b> (see #$overlappingSpaceRegions) if and only if <b>SPACE</b> is not a part of <b>REGION</b> (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).
<p>
The complement of a given #$UniversalSpaceRegion (q.v.) is the #$NullSpaceRegion (q.v.) of the same dimensionality, and vice versa.  The introduction of null space regions allows #$SpaceRegionComplementFn to be defined as a #$TotalFunction.
<p>
Note that #$SpaceRegionComplementFn is the function called <i>compl</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.  See also the corresponding predicate #$spaceRegionComplement; and cf. #$SpaceRegionComplementOfInFn.")
(comment spaceRegionComplement "A symmetric predicate that relates a space region to its mereological complement in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.  
<p>
<code>(#$spaceRegionComplement COMPLEMENT REGION)</code> holds if and only if, for  any #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion <code>SPACE</code>, (i) <code>SPACE</code> connects with <code>COMPLEMENT</code> (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) if and only if <code>SPACE</code> is <i>not</i> a nontangential proper part of <code>REGION</code> (see #$nontangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion) and (ii) <code>SPACE</code> overlaps <code>COMPLEMENT</code> (see #$overlappingSpaceRegions) if and only if <code>SPACE</code> is not a part of <code>REGION</code> (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).
<p>
The complement of a given #$UniversalSpaceRegion (q.v.) is the #$NullSpaceRegion (q.v.) of the same dimensionality, and vice versa.  The introduction of null space regions allows this predicate's corresponding function, #$SpaceRegionComplementFn, to be defined as a #$TotalFunction.
<p>
See also #$spaceRegionComplementOfIn.")
(comment SpaceRegionComplementOfInFn "A binary function that takes a given space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to its \"relative complement\" with respect to another given region that subsumes both.  (#$SpaceRegionComplementOfInFn <b>REGION SUPERREGION</b>) is the space region <b>COMPLEMENT</b> that is the complement of <b>PART</b> in <b>WHOLE</b>.  This implies that <b>REGION</b> and <b>COMPLEMENT</b> are mutually-discrete (see #$discreteSpaceRegions) parts of <b>SUPERREGION</b> whose sum (see #$spaceRegionSum) is equal to <b>SUPERREGION</b>.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$spaceRegionComplementOfIn.  And cf. #$SpaceRegionComplementFn.")
(comment spaceRegionComplementOfIn "A ternary #$SpaceRegionPredicate that relates a space region (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion) to its \"relative complement\" with respect to a given region. <code>(#$spaceRegionComplementOfIn COMPLEMENT REGION SUPERREGION)</code> means that <code>COMPLEMENT</code> is the complement of <code>PART</code> in <code>WHOLE</code>.  This implies that <code>REGION</code> and <code>COMPLEMENT</code> are mutually-discrete (see #$discreteSpaceRegions) parts of <code>SUPERREGION</code> whose sum (see #$spaceRegionSum) is equal to <code>SUPERREGION</code>.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpaceRegionComplementOfInFn.  And cf. #$spaceRegionComplement.")
(comment iGPertinentSentences "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates an instance of #$InformationGathering to a (quoted) instance of #$CycLSentence.  <code>(#$iGPertinentSentences IG SENT)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> can be used to express a query that is likely to be in some way pertinent to <code>IG</code>.
<p>
Suppose, for example, that <code>IG-001</code> is an instance of #$InformationGathering that involves gathering information on professional baseball players from the Dominican Republic.  Then we might have:
<pre>
(#$iGPertinentSentences IG-001 
  (#$and 
    (#$isa ?X (#$ProfessionalFn #$BaseballPlayer)) 
    (#$birthPlace ?X #$DominicanRepublic)))
</pre>
And, given that Sammy Sosa satisfies the open sentence above, we might also have:
<pre>
(#$iGPertinentSentences IG-001 
  (#$hasMembers #$ChicagoCubs #$SammySosa-BaseballPlayer))
</pre>
and so on.")
(comment CountryCapital "A specialization of #$CapitalCityOfRegion.  An instance of #$CapitalCityOfRegion is an instance of #$CountryCapital just in case it is the capital city of some instance of #$Country (q.v.).")
(comment connectsWithAtomicTPPOfSpaceRegion "A binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and a specialization of #$connectedSpaceRegions (q.v.).  <code>(#$connectsWithAtomicTPPOfSpaceRegion REGION1 REGION2)</code> is true if and only if there is some <code>PART</code> such that (i) <code>PART</code> is an #$AtomicSpaceRegion, (ii) <code>PART</code> is a tangential proper part of <code>REGION1</code> (see #$tangentialProperPartOfSpaceRegion), and (iii) <code>PART</code> connects with <code>REGION2</code> (see #$connectedSpaceRegions).  
<p>
Note that this predicate was reified to simplify the defining axioms for #$skinOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment Creator-Person "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who grows or makes or invents things.")
(comment AlexanderGrahamBell "An instance of #$FamousHuman. United States inventor (born in Scotland) of the telephone (1847-1922).")
(comment NewYork-Colony "An instance of #$BritishColoniesThatFormedTheUnitedStates. One of the British colonies that formed the United States.")
(comment OldMaster-Painter "A sub-collection of #$Painter-FineArtist. Each instance is a great European painter prior to 19th century.")
(comment DomenikosTheotocopoulos "An instance of #$OldMaster-Painter. Spanish painter (born in Greece) remembered for his religious works characterized by elongated human forms and dramatic use of color (1541-1614).")
(comment AdventSunday "A sub-collection of #$ChristianHoliday. Each instance is the first of the four Sundays during Advent.")
(comment MarcelLajosBreuer "An instance of #$Architect. United States architect (born in Hungary) who was associated with the Bauhaus in the 1920's (1902-1981).")
(comment Illustrator "A sub-collection of #$Artist-Visual. Each instance is an artist who makes illustrations (for books or magazines or advertisements etc.).")
(comment NormanRockwell-TheArtist "An instance of #$FamousHuman. United States illustrator whose works present a sentimental idealized view of everyday life (1894-1978).")
(comment Draftsman-Artist "A sub-collection of #$Artist. Each instance is an artist skilled at drawing.")
(comment JulesFeifer-Cartoonist "An instance of #$Cartoonist. United States cartoonist who created a sarcastic comic strip (born in 1929).")
(comment AntoineDomino-Songwriter "An instance of #$Songwriter. United States rhythm and blues pianist and singer and composer (born in 1928).")
(comment AleksandrPorfirevichBorodin-MusicalComposer "An instance of #$MusicalComposer. Russian composer (1833-1887).")
(comment DanceBandLeader "A sub-collection of #$MusicalConductor. Each instance is the leader of a dance band.")
(comment AltonGlennMiller-DanceBandLeader "An instance of #$DanceBandLeader. United States bandleader of a popular big band (1909-1944).")
(comment NicolasPoussin-OldMaster "An instance of #$OldMaster-Painter. French painter in the classical style (1594-1665).")
(comment RobertFulton "An instance of #$FamousHuman. American inventor who designed the first commercially successful steamboat and the first steam warship (1765-1815).")
(comment Producer-Movie "A sub-collection of #$Leader. Each instance is a someone who finds financing for and supervises the making and presentation of a show (play or film or program or similar work).")
(comment WaltDisney "An instance of #$MaleHuman. United States film maker who pioneered animated cartoons and created such characters as Mickey Mouse and Donald Duck; founded Disneyland (1901-1966).")
(comment Whitmonday "A sub-collection of #$Monday. Each instance is the day after Whitsunday; a legal holiday in England and Wales and Ireland.")
(comment LevelOfDifficulty "A #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType and specialization of #$EvaluativeQuantity whose instances pertain to subjective assessments, by a performer of a particular action, of the perceived or experienced level of difficulty involved in performing that action.  The instances of #$LevelOfDifficulty range from #$Easy to #$Difficult, and more precise values can be specified using #$GenericValueFunctions (e.g. #$NoAmountFn).  Note
that since #$LevelOfDifficulty reflects subjective (concurrent or ex post facto) assessments of the difficulty of particular actions by their performers, they are not related in any direct way to more objective standards of difficulty, such as the \"levels of difficulty\" assigned to types of dives in Olympic diving competitions.")
(comment Easy "A rewrite of (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$LevelOfDifficulty).")
(comment Challenging "A rewrite of (#$MediumToVeryHighAmountFn #$LevelOfDifficulty).")
(comment Difficult "A rewrite of (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$LevelOfDifficulty).")
(comment mixedRule "(#$mixedRule RULE) means that RULE is \"mixed\" in the sense that it combines temporally qualifiable and non-temporally qualifiable
vocabulary in a way that renders it false under the standard
interpretation of its terms.  The general consensus seems to be that
such rules need to be edited, perhaps by temporally qualifying some of their
temporally qualifiable literals with reference to the times at which
events referenced by their non-temporally qualifiable literals occur.")
(comment WordNetIndividualMappingMt "The microtheory containing isa assertions for individuals made using the WordNet import workflow tool.")
(comment NorbertWiener-Mathematician "An instance of #$Mathematician. United States mathematician and founder of cybernetics (1894-1964).")
(comment CharlesWilkes-Explorer "An instance of #$Explorer. United States explorer of Antarctica (1798-1877).")
(comment MauriceHughFrederickWilkins-Biochemist "An instance of #$Biochemist. English biochemist who helped discover the structure of DNA (born in 1916).")
(comment GeorgeHubertWilkins-Explorer "An instance of #$Explorer. Australian who was the first to explore the Arctic by airplane (1888-1958).")
(comment SirGeoffreyWilkinson-Chemist "An instance of #$Chemist. English chemist honored for his research on pollutants in car exhausts (born in 1921).")
(comment ErikAdolfVonWillebrand-Neurophysiologist "An instance of #$Neurophysiologist. Finnish physician who first described vascular hemophilia (1870-1949).")
(comment TedWilliams-BaseballPlayer "An instance of #$MaleHuman. United States baseball player noted as a hitter (1918-2002).")
(comment BernanrdArthurOwenWilliams-Philosopher "An instance of #$Philosopher. English philosopher credited with reviving the field of moral philosophy (1929-2003).")
(comment HankWilliams "An instance of #$MaleHuman. United States country singer and songwriter (1923-1953).")
(comment IanWilmut-Geneticist "An instance of #$Geneticist. English geneticist who succeeded in cloning a sheep from a cell from an adult ewe (born in 1944).")
(comment CausingFn "A unary, reifiable, collection-denoting #$TermMacroFunction that takes a specialization of #$Event COL and returns the collection of all #$Events that cause an instance of COL.  For example, (#$CausingFn #$Rioting) is the collection of all events that cause rioting to occur.")
(comment (CausingFn CombustionProcess) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event. In each (#$CausingFn #$CombustionProcess), something causes something else to undergo combustion.")
(comment causing "This is the predicate corresponding to #$CausingFn (q.v.).")
(comment (ExperiencingEmotionFn Happiness) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExperiencingEmotion. In each (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Happiness), someone experiences a feeling of well-being or happiness, as from good health or an intense emotion.")
(comment SettlingIntoANewPlaceOfResidence "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Action. In each #$SettlingIntoANewPlaceOfResidence, someone takes up residence and become established.")
(comment (SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn CreationEvent outputsCreated Community) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CreationEvent. In each (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$CreationEvent #$outputsCreated #$Community), someone forms a community.")
(comment Dedifferentiating-BiologicalGrowthEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalGrowthEvent. In each #$Dedifferentiating-BiologicalGrowthEvent, something loses specialization in form or function.")
(comment AccumulatingSteadily "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$AccumulatingSteadily, something forms or accumulates steadily.")
(comment AffordingAccessToSomethingElse "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Situation-Localized. In each #$AffordingAccessToSomethingElse, something leads, extends, or affords access to something else.")
(comment Ankylosing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalGrowthEvent. In each #$Ankylosing, something undergoes ankylosis.")
(comment BecomingTense "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BodilyFunctionEvent. In each #$BecomingTense, something becomes tense or tenser.")
(comment BecomingWidelyKnown "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Movement-TranslationEvent. In each #$BecomingWidelyKnown, something becomes widely known and passed on.")
(comment CatchingOnFire "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SettingSomethingOnFire. In each #$CatchingOnFiire, something starts burning.")
(comment Churning "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MovementEvent. In each #$Churning, something liquid is agitated.")
(comment CoexistingWithSomethingElse "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$SomethingExisting. In each #$CoexistingWithSomethingElse, something exists together with something else.")
(comment Coming-CreationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CreationEvent. In each #$Coming-CreationEvent, something is the product or result.")
(comment ComingForth "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CreationEvent. In each #$ComingForth, something happens or occurs as a result of something.")
(comment CrossFertilizing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent. In each #$CrossFertilizing, something undergoes cross-fertilization; becomes fertile.")
(comment DevelopingSuddenly "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$DevelopingSuddenly, something develops suddenly.")
(comment DistributingMathematically "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$SomethingExisting. In each #$DistributingMathematically, something is distributed or spread mathematically, as in statistical analyses.")
(comment Estivating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Sleeping. In each #$Estivating, something sleeps during summer.")
(comment FlowingAbundantly "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FlowingContinously. In each #$FlowingAbundantly, someone is abundantly present.")
(comment GainingInWealth "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EconomicAction. In each #$GainingInWealth, something gains in wealth.")
(comment Gumming "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExudingSomething. In each #$Gumming, something exudes or forms gum.")
(comment HangingTogether "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ConnectingTogether. In each #$HangingTogether, something is connected.")
(comment Hibernating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Sleeping. In each #$Hibernating, some animal sleeps during winter.")
(comment Hypertrophying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GrowthEvent. In each #$Hypertrophying, something undergoes hypertrophy.")
(comment Interconnecting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ConnectingTogether. In each #$Interconnecting, something is interwoven or interconnected with something else.")
(comment InterconnectingThatAffordsPassage "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SomethingInterconnecting. In each #$InterconnectingThatAffordsPassage, something is interconnected, affording passage.")
(comment KickingAround "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$SomethingExisting. In each #$KickingAround, something is around; is alive or active.")
(comment Liquefying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$Liquefying, something becomes liquid.")
(comment MovingWhileCrashing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MovementEvent. In each #$MovingWhileCrashing, something moves with, or as if with, a crashing noise.")
(comment Neighboring "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DwellingInAPlace. In each #$Neighboring, someone lives or is located as a neighbor.")
(comment Nicking-SexualCopulation "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SexualCopulation. In each #$Nicking-SexualCopulation, some animal mates successfully; ofs livestock.")
(comment Plying-Travel-TripEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Travel-TripEvent. In each #$Plying-Travel-TripEvent, something travels a route regularly.")
(comment Preexisting "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$SomethingExisting. In each #$Preexisting, something exists beforehand or prior to a certain point in time.")
(comment Propagating-BiologicalReproductionEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent. In each #$Propagating-BiologicalReproductionEvent, something multiplies sexually or asexually.")
(comment Pupating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalGrowthEvent. In each #$Pupating, something develops into a pupa.")
(comment Resurging-CreationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CreationEvent. In each #$Resurging-CreationEvent, something rises again.")
(comment SelfReorganizing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$FoundingAnOrganization. In each #$SelfReorganizing, some organization organizes anew, as after a setback.")
(comment Separating-MovementEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MovementEvent. In each #$Separating-MovementEvent, something goes one's own away; moves apart from something else.")
(comment SpattingLikeRaindrops "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PrecipitationProcess. In each #$SpattingLikeRaindrops, something comes down like raindrops.")
(comment Staling-Urinating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Urinating. In each #$Staling-Urinating, something urinates, of cattle and horses.")
(comment Stooling-BiologicalGrowthEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalGrowthEvent. In each #$Stooling-BiologicalGrowthEvent, something grows shoots in the form of stools or tillers.")
(comment Tearing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Crying. In each #$Tearing, someone's eye fills with tears or sheds tears.")
(comment TransferringFromOneVehicleToAnother "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GeneralizedTransfer. In each #$TransferringFromOneVehicleToAnother, someone changes from one vehicle or transportation line to another.")
(comment Watering-SecretionEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SecretionEvent. In each #$Watering-SecretionEvent, something secretes or forms water, as tears or saliva.")
(comment WellingUp-CreationEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CreationEvent. In each #$WellingUp-CreationEvent, some ephemeral thing such as a feeling or thought is created.")
(comment Writhing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MovementEvent. In each #$Writhing, something moves in a twisting or contorted motion, (especially when struggling).")
(comment ActingByRelyingOnLuck "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RiskTaking. In each #$ActingByRelyingOnLuck, someone acts by relying on their luck.")
(comment Adhering-Adopting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Adopting-SelectingSomething. In each #$Adhering-Adopting, someone is a devoted follower or supporter of something.")
(comment AffiliatingWithAnOrganization "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$OrganizationalTransferIn. In each #$AffiliatingWithAnOrganization, someone joins in an affiliation.")
(comment AppearingInCourt "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$LegalProceeding. In each #$AppearingInCourt, someone presents theirself formally, as before a (judicial) authority.")
(comment AttachingOneselfToAGroup "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$OrganizationalTransferIn. In each #$AttachingOneselfToAGroup, someone attaches theirself to a group.")
(comment AttendingAsAVisitor "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AttendingSomething. In each #$AttendingAsAVisitor, someone attends something as a visitor.")
(comment Balding "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$HairLoss. In each #$Balding, someone grows bald; loses hair on their head.")
(comment Bawling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Crying. In each #$Bawling, someone cries loudly.")
(comment Beaming-Smiling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Smiling. In each #$Beaming-Smiling, someone smiles radiantly; expresses joy through their facial expression.")
(comment BecomingFullyEngagedWithAProblem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Handling-Management. In each #$BecomingFullyEngagedWithAProblem, someone becomes fully engaged with a problem or a subject.")
(comment Bellylaughing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Bellylaughing, someone laughs a deep, hearty laugh.")
(comment Blowing-EmittingAnObject "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EmittingAnObject. In each #$Blowing-EmittingAnObject, someone frees something of obstruction by blowing air through.")
(comment BoardingAtAPlace "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DwellingInAPlace. In each #$BoardingAtAPlace, someone lives and takes their meals at or in a place.")
(comment BreakingDown-BodilyFunctionEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BodilyFunctionEvent. In each #$BreakingDown-BodilyFunctionEvent, someone collapses due to fatigue, an illness, or a sudden attack.")
(comment BreakingUp-Laughing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$BreakingUp-Laughing, someone laughs unrestrainedly.")
(comment Bundling-Sleeping "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Sleeping. In each #$Bundling-Sleeping, someone sleeps fully clothed in the same bed with their betrothed.")
(comment Cachinnating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Cachinnating, someone laughs loudly and in an unrestrained way.")
(comment Cackling-Laughing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Cackling-Laughing, someone emits a loud, unpleasant kind of laughing.")
(comment CallingItQuits "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DiscontinuingAnActivity. In each #$CallingItQuits, someone stops doing what they are doing.")
(comment CallingTheShots "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$CallingTheShots, someone exercises authority or is in charge.")
(comment Choking "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Breathing. In each #$Choking, someone breathes with great difficulty, as when experiencing a strong emotion.")
(comment Chuckling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Chuckling, someone laughs quietly or with restraint.")
(comment ClearingTheirThroat "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Coughing. In each #$ClearingTheirThroat, someone clears mucus or food from their throat.")
(comment Clubbing-ActingInConcert "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ActingInConcert. In each #$Clubbing-ActingInConcert, people unite with a common purpose.")
(comment ComingTo-BodilyFunctionEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BodilyFunctionEvent. In each #$ComingTo-BodilyFunctionEvent, someone returns to consciousness.")
(comment Concerned "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$StrictlyMentalEvent. In each #$Concerned, something is on the mind of someone.")
(comment Convulsing-Laughing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Convulsing-Laughing, someone is overcome with laughter.")
(comment CraningOnesNeck "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$StretchingBody. In each #$CraningOnesNeck, someone stretches (their neck) so as to see better.")
(comment Crawling-Swimming "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Swimming-Generic. In each #$Crawling-Swimming, someone swims by doing the crawl.")
(comment CrickingOnesHead "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BecomingTense. In each #$CrickingOnesHead, someone twists their head into a strained position.")
(comment CrossDressing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$CrossDressing, someone dresses in the clothes of the other sex.")
(comment DevelopingAHabit "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BeginningAnActivity. In each #$DevelopingAHabit, someone develops a habit.")
(comment Dimpling-Smiling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Smiling. In each #$Dimpling-Smiling, someone produces dimples while smiling.")
(comment DoingTheHonors-HostingASocialGathering "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HostingASocialGathering. In each #$DoingTheHonors-HostingASocialGathering, someone acts as the host and receives or introduces their guests.")
(comment Dominating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$InfluencingAnAgent. In each #$Dominating, someone is in control of of someone else.")
(comment DressingDown "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$DressingDown, someone puts on clothes that make a careless or ill-dressed impression.")
(comment DressingUp "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$DressingUp, someone puts on special clothes to appear particularly appealing and attractive.")
(comment Driveling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Salivating. In each #$Driveling, someone lets saliva drivel from the mouth.")
(comment Drowsing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Resting-Relaxing. In each #$Drowsing, someone is on the verge of sleeping.")
(comment Emceeing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HostingASocialGathering. In each #$Emceeing, someone acts as a master of ceremonies.")
(comment Endeavoring "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Attempting. In each #$Endeavoring, something attempts some activity by employing effort.")
(comment Enrobing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DressingUp. In each #$Enrobing, someone adorns someone with a robe.")
(comment Excelling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HumanActivity. In each #$Excelling, someone distinguishes theirself.")
(comment FallingOver "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BreakingDown-BodilyFunctionEvent. In each #$FallingOver, someone falls forward and down.")
(comment FleshingOut "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GainingBodyWeight. In each #$FleshingOut, someone becomes round, plump, or shapely.")
(comment Fraternizing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Socializing. In each #$Fraternizing, someone is on friendly terms with someone, as if with a brother, especially with an enemy.")
(comment GesturingByWinking "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MakingAGesture. In each #$GesturingByWinking, someone signals by winking.")
(comment GettingInvolvedWithSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. In each #$GettingInvolvedWithSomething, someone gets involved in or with something.")
(comment Giggling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Giggling, someone laughs nervously.")
(comment GoingForBroke "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RiskTaking. In each #$GoingForBroke, someone risks everything in one big effort.")
(comment Grinning "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Smiling. In each #$Grinning, someone draws back their lips and reveals their teeth, in a smile, grimace, or snarl.")
(comment GrowingUp "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalMaturationEvent. In each #$GrowingUp, someone becomes an adult.")
(comment Guffawing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Guffawing, someone laughs boisterously.")
(comment Hacking-Coughing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Coughing. In each #$Hacking-Coughing, someone coughs spasmodically.")
(comment HavingAGo-Attempting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Attempting. In each #$HavingAGo-Attempting, someone makes an attempt at something.")
(comment HelpingOut "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HelpingAnAgent. In each #$HelpingOut, someone is of help, as in a particular situation of need.")
(comment Hyperventilating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Breathing. In each #$Hyperventilating, someone breathes excessively hard and fast.")
(comment Incorporating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$CommercialActivity. In each #$Incorporating, someone forms a corporation.")
(comment BeingIndifferentWhileSomethingIsHappening "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WatchingSomething. In each #$BeingIndifferentWhileSomethingIsHappening, someone is inactive or indifferent while something is happening.")
(comment Inmarrying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WeddingEvent-Generic. In each #$Inmarrying, someone marries within their own tribe or group.")
(comment Intermarrying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WeddingEvent-Generic. In each #$Intermarrying, someone marries within the same ethnic, social, or family group.")
(comment Laboring-BirthEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BirthEvent. In each #$Laboring-BirthEvent, someone undergoes the efforts of childbirth.")
(comment LimberingUp "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Exercising. In each #$LimberingUp, someone makes their body limber or suppler by stretching, as if to prepare for strenuous physical activity.")
(comment Lurking "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$HidingAnObjectByMovingIt. In each #$Lurking, someone lies in wait, lies in ambush, behaves in a sneaky and secretive manner.")
(comment MaintainingOnesPosition "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$MaintainingOnesPosition, someone maintains their position and is in control of a situation.")
(comment MakingAStrenousEffort "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Attempting. In each #$MakingAStrenousEffort, someone makes a strenuous or labored effort.")
(comment Matriculating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnrollingSomeone. In each #$Matriculating, someone enrolls as a student.")
(comment Mismarrying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WeddingEvent-Generic. In each #$Mismarrying, someone marries an unsuitable partner.")
(comment MovingInvoluntarily "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MovementEvent. In each #$MovingInvoluntarily, someone moves in an uncontrolled manner.")
(comment Overbearing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Dominating. In each #$Overbearing, someone overcomes something.")
(comment Overdosing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DruggingSomeone-DrugTherapy. In each #$Overdosing, someone doses a drug too heavily.")
(comment Palpebrating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Blinking. In each #$Palpebrating, someone winks or blinks, especially repeatedly.")
(comment AccidentallyLeavingSomethingSomewhere "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Accident. In each #$AccidentallyLeavingSomethingSomewhere, someone leaves something behind unintentionally.")
(comment PerkingUp "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RecoveringFromAilment. In each #$PerkingUp, someone gains or regains energy.")
(comment Pontificating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Administration-Management. In each #$Pontificating, someone administers a pontifical office.")
(comment Pouting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Grimacing. In each #$Pouting, someone makes a sad face and thrusts out their lower lip.")
(comment Preening "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DressingUp. In each #$Preening, someone dresses or grooms with elaborate care.")
(comment Presiding "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething. In each #$Presiding, someone acts as president.")
(comment PretendingToCooperate "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Pretending. In each #$PretendingToCooperate, someones cooperates or pretend to cooperate.")
(comment Primming-PuttingOnAClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$Primming-PuttingOnAClothingItem, someone dresses primly.")
(comment Prinking-PuttingOnAClothingItem "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$Prinking-PuttingOnAClothingItem, someone dresses very carefully and in a finicky manner.")
(comment Puffing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BlowingAir. In each #$Puffing, someone blows hard and loudly.")
(comment PullingThePlug-DiscontinuingAnActivity "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DiscontinuingAnActivity. In each #$PullingThePlug-DiscontinuingAnActivity, someone prevents some activity from happening or continuing.")
(comment PuttingOnAHat "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WearingSomething-PuttingOnAClothingItem. In each #$PuttingOnAHat, someone puts on or wears a hat.")
(comment RegisteringToVote "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EnrollingSomeone. In each #$RegisteringToVote, someone enrolls someone else to vote.")
(comment Remarrying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$WeddingEvent-Generic. In each #$Remarrying, someone marries, not for the first time.")
(comment Respiring-Breathing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Breathing. In each #$Respiring-Breathing, someone breathes easily again, as after exertion or anxiety.")
(comment RetiringGracefully "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RetirementEvent. In each #$RetiringGracefully, someone retires gracefully.")
(comment Roaring-Laughing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Roaring-Laughing, someone laughs unrestrainedly and heartily.")
(comment Romancing-Lying "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Lying. In each #$Romancing-Lying, someone tells romantic or exaggerated lies.")
(comment RunningWithABall-Sports "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RunningOnTwoLegs. In each #$RunningWithABall-Sports, someone runs with the ball; in such sports as football.")
(comment Scowling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Frowning. In each #$Scowling, someone frowns with displeasure.")
(comment ScrollingADisplayDevice "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GeneralizedTransfer. In each #$ScrollingADisplayDevice, someone moves through text or graphics in order to display parts that do not fit on the screen.")
(comment SittingBack-Resting-Relaxing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Resting-Relaxing. In each #$SittingBack-Resting-Relaxing, someone settles into a comfortable sitting position.")
(comment Sneering "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Smiling. In each #$Sneering, someone smiles contemptuously.")
(comment Snickering "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Snickering, someone laughs quietly.")
(comment Sniffing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Inhaling. In each #$Sniffing, someone inhales audibly through the nose.")
(comment Sniveling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Crying. In each #$Sniveling, someone cries or whines with snuffling.")
(comment Snorting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Exhaling. In each #$Snorting, someone makes a snorting sound by exhaling hard.")
(comment Snuffling "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Inhaling. In each #$Snuffling, someone snuffs up mucus through their nose.")
(comment Sobbing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Crying. In each #$Sobbing, someone weeps convulsively.")
(comment SocializingALot "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Socializing. In each #$SocializingALot, someone is a social swinger; socializes a lot.")
(comment SocializingWithElites "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Socializing. In each #$SocializingWithElites, someone socializes with elites.")
(comment SportsTraining "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Exercising. In each #$SportsTraining, someone exercises in order to prepare for an event or competition.")
(comment Squinting "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Grimacing. In each #$Squinting, someone partly closes one's eyes.")
(comment StandingBack-AvoidingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$AvoidingSomething. In each #$StandingBack-AvoidingSomething, someone stays clear of, avoids physically.")
(comment StreetWalking-Prostitution "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Prostitution. In each #$StreetWalking-Prostitution, some prostitute walks the streets in search of customers.")
(comment StrikingWithOnesFist "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MovementEvent. In each #$StrikingWithOnesFist, someone moves (one's fist).")
(comment Sweltering "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Sweating. In each #$Sweltering, someone suffers from intense heat.")
(comment TakingADaringRisk "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RiskTaking. In each #$TakingADaringRisk, someone takes a risk; performs a daring act.")
(comment Teething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BiologicalGrowthEvent. In each #$Teething, someone grows teeth; cuts the baby teeth.")
(comment Transpiring-ExudingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExudingSomething. In each #$Transpiring-ExudingSomething, someone gives off (water) through their skin.")
(comment TravellingRapidly "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Movement-TranslationEvent. In each #$TravellingRapidly, someone travels rapidly, by any (unspecified) means.")
(comment Tumbling-Exercising "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Exercising. In each #$Tumbling-Exercising, someone does gymnastics, rolls and turns skillfully.")
(comment Twinning-BirthEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$BirthEvent. In each #$Twinning-BirthEvent, someone gives birth to twins.")
(comment Twitching "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$MovingInvoluntarily. In each #$Twitching, someone makes an uncontrolled, short, jerky motion.")
(comment OccupyingAPlaceUninvited "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$DwellingInAPlace. In each #$OccupyingAPlaceUninvited, someone occupies a place, usually uninvited.")
(comment VegingOut-Resting-Relaxing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Resting-Relaxing. In each #$VegingOut-Resting-Relaxing, someone engages in passive relaxation.")
(comment VotingAgainst "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Voting. In each #$VotingAgainst, someone votes against something.")
(comment VotingAtAPollingStation "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Voting. In each #$VotingAtAPollingStation, someone votes in an election at a polling station.")
(comment Wailing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Crying. In each #$Wailing, someone cries weakly or softly.")
(comment Welshing-Cheating "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Cheating. In each #$Welshing-Cheating, someone cheats by avoiding payment of a gambling debt.")
(comment Wheezing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Breathing. In each #$Wheezing, someone breathes with difficulty.")
(comment Wincing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Grimacing. In each #$Wincing, someone makes a face indicating disgust or dislike.")
(comment ZonkingOut "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$GoingToSleep. In each #$ZonkingOut, someone falls asleep fast, as when one is extremely tired.")
(comment Braying-Laughing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Laughing. In each #$Braying-Laughing, someone laughs loudly and harshly.")
(comment closedUnder "A binary predicate that relates a given #$SetOrCollection to any function that it is closed under.  Roughly, <code>(#$closedUnder SETORCOL FUNC)</code> means that <code>FUNC</code> returns an element of <code>SETORCOL</code> for any (sequence of) element(s) of <code>SETORCOL</code> taken as argument(s).  
<p>
More precisely, where <code>FUNC</code> is an <code>N</code>-ary function, <code>(#$closedUnder SETORCOL FUNC)</code> holds if and only if, for any <code>N</code>-length sequence of elements of <code>SETORCOL</code> (<code>THING<sub>1</sub>, ..., THING<sub>N</sub></code>) for which <code>FUNC</code> returns a value <code>VALUE</code>, <code>VALUE</code> is itself an element of <code>SETORCOL</code>.  (We further stipulate that there must be <i>at least one</i> such argument-sequence, to rule out trivially vacuous cases such as #$RealNumber's being \"closed under\" #$MotherFn).  
<p>
For example, #$Integer is closed under #$TimesFn, but #$NegativeInteger is not.
<p>
See also #$totalFunctionOn; and note that <code>(#$closedUnder SETORCOL FUNC)</code> does <i>not</i> entail <code>(#$totalFunctionOn FUNC SETORCOL)</code>. ")
(comment TotalFunction "A specialization of 
#$Function-Denotational whose instances are <i>total functions</i>:
functions that have well-defined values for everything to which 
they can legally be applied.  More precisely, an (<code>N</code>-ary) function 
<code>FUNC</code> is total if it is defined -- i.e. returns a value -- for all
(length <code>N</code> sequences of) things that meet its (respective) 
argument-constraints (see #$admittedArgument).  
<p>
An example of a #$TotalFunction is #$SineFn; for any #$AngularDistance (the #$arg1Isa of #$SineFn) to which this unary function is applied, there is guaranteed to be a value returned.  
<p>
Any function that is not a total function is a #$PartialFunction.
See also #$totalFunctionOn.")
(comment LinearObject-Planar "A specialization of #$LinearObject (q.v.). #$LinearObject-Planar is the collection of all linearly-shaped objects, whether tangible or intangible, that are more-or-less embeddable in a #$Plane.  
<p>
Note that the criterion for being \"planar\" in the present sense varies from context to context.  For example, the standard of planarity for the manufacture of wrought-iron railing is less precise than that for pure geometry.  
<p>
Important geometrical specializations of #$LinearObject-Planar are #$LinearObject-Straight are #$Line-Planar.")
(comment BenjaminHarrison "An instance of #$UnitedStatesPresident. 23rd President of the United States (1833-1901).")
(comment MossHart "An instance of #$FamousHuman. United States playwright who collaborated with George S. Kaufman (1904-1961).")
(comment DavidHartley-Philosopher "An instance of #$Philosopher. English philosopher who introduced the theory of the association of ideas (1705-1757).")
(comment WilliamHarvey-Doctor-Medical "An instance of #$Doctor-Medical. English physician and scientist who described the circulation of the blood; he later proposed that all animals originate from an ovum produced by the female of the species (1578-1657).")
(comment FrederickChildeHassam-Painter-FineArtist "An instance of #$Painter-FineArtist. United States painter noted for brilliant colors and bold brushwork (1859-1935).")
(comment OddHassel-Chemist "An instance of #$Chemist. Norwegian chemist noted for his research on organic molecules (1897-1981).")
(comment ThomasHastings-Architect "An instance of #$Architect. United States architect who formed and important architectural firm with John Merven Carrere (1860-1929).")
(comment AnneHathaway-FemaleHuman "An instance of (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn #$FemaleHuman #$wife #$MaleHuman). Wife of William Shakespeare (1556-1623).")
(comment SirWalterNormanHaworth-Biochemist "An instance of #$Biochemist. English biochemist who was a pioneer in research on carbohydrates; when he synthesized vitamin C he became the first person to synthesize a vitamin artificially (1883-1950).")
(comment FranzJosephHaydn-MusicalComposer "An instance of #$MusicalComposer. Prolific Austrian composer who influenced the classical form of the symphony (1732-1809).")
(comment RutherfordBHayes "An instance of #$UnitedStatesPresident. 19th President of the United States; his administration removed federal troops from the South and so ended the Reconstruction Period (1822-1893).")
(comment ArthurGarfieldHays-Lawyer "An instance of #$Lawyer. United States lawyer involved in several famous court trials (1881-1954).")
(comment WilliamHarrisonHays-Lawyer "An instance of #$Lawyer. United States lawyer and politician who formulated a production code that prescribed the moral content of United states films from 1930 to 1966 (1879-1954).")
(comment OliverHeaviside-Physicist "An instance of #$Physicist. English physicist and electrical engineer who helped develop telegraphic and telephonic communications; in 1902 (independent of A. E. Kennelly) he suggested the existence of an atmospheric layer that reflects radio waves back to earth (1850-1925).")
(comment ChristianFriedrichHebbel-Playwright "An instance of #$Playwright. German dramatist (1813-1863).")
(comment Hegel "An instance of #$MaleHuman. German philosopher whose three stage process of dialectical reasoning was adopted by Karl Marx (1770-1831).")
(comment MartinHeidegger-Philosopher "An instance of #$Philosopher. German philosopher whose views on human existence in a world of objects and on Angst influenced the existential philosophers (1889-1976).")
(comment LillianHellman-Playwright "An instance of #$Playwright. United States playwright; her plays were often indictments of injustice (1905-1984).")
(comment BaronHermannLudwigFerdinandVonHelmholtz-Physicist "An instance of #$Physiologist. German physiologist and physicist (1821-1894).")
(comment cycLParsedTo "An expression of the form (#$cycLParsedTo TRANS  EXPRESSION) means that the quoted #$CycLExpression EXPRESSION is generated as a translation during the #$IBTTranslating TRANS.")
(comment LinearObject-OneDimensional "The \"collection-intersection\" of #$LinearObject and #$OneDimensionalThing (qq.v.). #$LinearObject-OneDimensional is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that are extended in only one spatial dimension.  They might be straight or curved, bounded or unbounded; being strictly one-dimensional, they are intangible.
<p>
Specializations of #$LinearObject-OneDimensional include #$Line, #$Ray-Geometrical, and #$LineSegment.")
(comment LinearObject-TwoDimensional "The \"collection-intersection\" of #$LinearObject and #$TwoDimensionalThing (qq.v.). #$LinearObject-TwoDimensional is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that are extended in exactly two spatial dimensions.  They might be straight or curved, bounded or unbounded; being strictly two-dimensional, they are intangible.
<p>
In certain two-dimensional geometrical contexts, a long and thin 2-D region might be treated as a line.  For example, a river, as represented on a map, might demarcate the boundary between two countries.")
(comment CompletingAnAction "A first-order collection and a sub-collection of #$EndingASituation.  #$CompletingAnAction represents a generic form of intentionally finishing or completing with the added content that the event being completed is successful.  Thus, its specializations cover events that mark the successful completion of some #$PurposefulAction.")
(comment EndingASituation "A first-order collection and a sub-collection of #$PurposefulAction.  #$EndingASituation represents a generic type of event in which an agent somehow ends another, larger #$Situation.  There is no implied success or failure associated with instances of this class. Those distinctions are made in its sub-collecitons:  #$AbandoningASituation and #$CompletingAnAction.")
(comment iGPertinentCycLQuerySpecification "<code>(#$iGPertinentCycLQuerySpecification IG Q-SPEC)</code> means that <code>Q-SPEC</code> is an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification that is likely to be in some way pertinent to <code>IG</code> (an instance of #$InformationGathering).
<p>
<code>Q-SPEC</code> may have as an #$InferenceSentenceParameter a fully-bound query sentence, or it may be a query specification which has open variables.  If <code>Q-SPEC</code> has open variables, it may be that some appropriate restriction of those variables will yield a more obviously pertinent query.

For example, if someone is studying a crime denoted by \"<code>Crime008</code>\", a CycL query specification with this inference parameter will be pertinent:
<p>
<pre>
(#$likelySuspects ?ACT ?AGENT)
</pre>
<p>
Further, a restriction of the open variables in that CycL query specification will yield a more obviously pertinent sentence:
<p>
<pre>
(#$likelySuspects Crime008 ?AGENT)
</pre>
<p>
.")
(comment AmphibiousOperation "A sub-collection of #$MilitaryOperation. Each instance is a military operation by both land and sea forces.")
(comment AmphibiousAssault "A sub-collection of #$AmphibiousOperation. Each instance is an amphibious operation attacking a land base that is carried out by troops that are landed by naval ships.")
(comment Era "A sub-collection of #$TimeInterval. Each instance is a period marked by distinctive character or reckoned from a fixed point or event.")
(comment Statesman "A sub-collection of #$Politician. Each instance is a man who is a respected leader in national or international affairs.")
(comment GreenwichMeanTime "A sub-collection of #$Time-Quantity. Each instance is the local time at the 0 meridian passing through Greenwich, England; it is the same everywhere.")
(comment CoordinatedUniversalTime "A sub-collection of #$GreenwichMeanTime. Each instance is a Greenwich Mean Time updated with leap seconds.")
(comment AdministrativeDistrict "A sub-collection of #$CountrySubsidiary. Each instance is a district defined for administrative purposes.")
(comment AsianCountry "A sub-collection of #$IndependentCountry. Each instance is a countries occupying the Asian continent.")
(comment Cognition "A sub-collection of #$MentalState. Each instance is the psychological result of perception and learning and reasoning.")
(comment Content "A sub-collection of #$Collection-Group. Each instance is an everything that is included in a collection, often having a container.")
(comment Concept "A sub-collection of #$Idea. Each instance is an abstract or general idea inferred or derived from specific instances.")
(comment (SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn Organization subOrganizations Organization) "A sub-collection of #$Organization. Each instance is an organization regarded as part of a larger social group.")
(comment AdministrativeUnit "A sub-collection of (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn #$Organization #$subOrganizations #$Organization). Each instance is a unit with administrative responsibilities.")
(comment UnitedNationsAgency "A sub-collection of #$AdministrativeUnit. Each instance is an agency of the United Nations.")
(comment LinearObject-ThreeDimensional "The \"collection-intersection\" of #$LinearObject and #$ThreeDimensionalThing (qq.v.). #$LinearObject-ThreeDimensional is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that are extended in three spatial dimensions.  They might be straight or curved, bounded or unbounded, tangible or intangible.
<p>
In certain contexts, a long and thin 3-D object or region might be treated as a line.  For example, a river might demarcate the boundary between two countries; a line drawn in the sand might serve as a volleyball court boundary.")
(comment CorroborationEvent "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$CorroborationEvent is an #$Event one of whose salient features is that it supports or provides evidence for the truth of something (a belief, claim, theory, hypothesis, etc.).  Obviously, what counts as a #$CorroborationEvent is highly context-dependent.")
(comment DismissingSomethingFromConsideration "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RejectingSomething. In each #$DismissingSomethingFromConsideration, someone dismisses something from consideration.")
(comment intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Exclusive "<code>(#$intervalOfTypeLiesBetween-Exclusive INTERVAL TYPE EARLY LATE)</code> holds just in case INTERVAL is an instance of TYPE and falls between the end of EARLY and the beginning of LATE according to the convention established for TYPE (an instance of #$NormalTimeParameter).")
(comment dateOfTypeInRange "<code>(#$dateOfTypeInRange DATE DATE-TYPE EARLY LATE)</code> holds just in case DATE is an instance of DATE-TYPE and falls between EARLY and LATE according to the convention established for DATE-TYPE (an instance of #$NormalTimeParameter.")
(comment sufficientToAchieve "An assertion of the form (#$sufficientToAchieve MOTIVE1 MOTIVE2) states that the #$NormativeSpecification MOTIVE1 has been adopted by some #$IntelligentAgent because its specification is perceived to be sufficient to bring about the specification of #$NormativeSpecification-Achievement MOTIVE2. If an agent were motivated to destroy a particular building (and so had the #$NormativeSpecification-Achievement M2), this motivation could give rise to the motivation to burn the building down (M1) and the following assertion would hold (#$sufficientToAchieve M1 M2).  This predicate is neutral on whether the specification of MOTIVE1 is sufficient to bring about the specification of MOTIVE2, see #$necessaryForAchieving for a predicate with that meaning.")
(comment IndividualAssignment "A specialization of #$Assignment-Specification (q.v.).  Each instance of #$IndividualAssignment is an #$Assignment-Specification that bears the #$allottedAgents relation to exactly one #$IntelligentAgent.  Thus, instances of this collection are satisfied when that agent performs the appropriate sort of action as specified by the #$Assignment-Specification.")
(comment Assignment-Specification "A sub-collection of #$NormativeSpecification.  #$Assignment-Specification is a collection of instances of #$ConceptualWork that describe a situation that some agent wants to be realized.  This collection's instances are differentiated from other instances of #$NormativeSpecification in that they typically relate to organizations where several parties are involved in developing the content of an assignment and then allocating available resources for the realization of the situations they represent.")
(comment Scale-Specification "A sub-collection of #$Specification. Each instance is an ordered reference standard.")
(comment Prayer-SacredText "A sub-collection of #$SacredText. Each instance is a fixed text used in praying.")
(comment Duet "A sub-collection of #$MusicPerformanceOrganization. Each instance is two performers or singers who perform together.")
(comment Intellectual "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who uses the mind creatively.")
(comment Scholar "A sub-collection of #$Teacher. Each instance is a learned person (especially in the humanities); someone who by long study has gained mastery in one or more disciplines.")
(comment CompassPoint "A sub-collection of #$GeographicalDirection-Direct. Each instance is an of 32 horizontal directions indicated on the card of a compass.")
(comment Municipality "A sub-collection of #$GeopoliticalEntity. Each instance is an urban district having corporate status and powers of self-government.")
(comment Division-AdministrativeUnit "A sub-collection of #$AdministrativeUnit. Each instance is an administrative unit in government or business.")
(comment GovernmentDepartment "A sub-collection of #$Department. Each instance is a department of government.")
(comment FederalDepartment "A sub-collection of #$GovernmentDepartment. Each instance is a department of the federal government of the United States.")
(comment ExecutiveDepartment-UnitedStates "A sub-collection of #$FederalDepartment. Each instance is a federal department in the executive branch of the government of the United States.")
(comment Racehorse "A sub-collection of #$Horse. Each instance is a horse bred for racing.")
(comment NaturalChildbirth "A sub-collection of #$VaginalChildBirth. Each instance is a labor and childbirth without medical intervention; no drugs are given to relieve pain or aid the birth process.")
(comment facets-Covering "A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections and a specialization of both #$facets-NearCovering and #$covering (qq.v.).   (#$facets-Covering COL COLTYPE) means that COLTYPE corresponds to  one way of dividing the entirety of COL into subcollections.   Specifically, (i) the instances of COLTYPE are all the same sort of  subcollection of COL and (ii) each instance of COL is an instance of  at least one instance of COLTYPE.  For example,  (#$facets-Covering #$BaseballPlayer #$BaseballPlayerTypeByPosition) holds, since each baseball player plays at least one position. Note that #$facets-Covering is _not_ a specialization of  #$partitionedInto; e.g. some baseball players play more than one position.  Cf. the more specialized #$facets-Partition, and also #$facets-Strict.")
(comment SpaceChunk "A specialization of both #$ConnectedSpaceRegion and #$ThreeDimensionalThing (qq.v.).  Instances of #$SpaceChunk are three-dimensional portions of a three-dimensional space. This is the kind of \"place\" that solid objects occupy. It makes sense to speak of, or compute, the volume of such objects.  
<p>
An important specialization of this collection is #$SpaceChunk-Empirical, whose instances are pieces of space in the empirical universe -- the kind of space that physical objects occupy.
<p>
See also #$Surface, #$Line, and #$Point.")
(comment HTMLImageFn "(#$HTMLImageFn SRC OPTIONS) denotes the #$HTMLSource for displaying the image file located at SRC with OPTIONS.")
(comment HTMLTableDataFn "(#$HTMLTableDataFn OPTIONS CONTENT) denotes the #$HTMLSource for displaying the table data CONTENT with OPTIONS.")
(comment PenetrationEvent "Each instance of #$PenetrationEvent is a #$PhysicalEvent in which something penetrates -- i.e. breaks the surface of and goes at least partially through -- something else.")
(disjointWith PurposefulAction (CollectionDifferenceFn (CausingFn Dying) PurposefulAction))
(comment PlayingAnAudioRecordedObject "A collection of events; a subcollection of (#$UsingAFn (#$CollectionIntersection2Fn #$MediaPlayingDevice #$AudioEquipment)). In each #$PlayingAnAudioRecordedObject, something causes something else to emit recorded sounds.")
(comment DisappearanceOfBaqirSadr "In 1978, Amal militia leader Baqir Sadr mysteriously disappered while on a visit to Libya, whereon leadership of Amal passed to #$NabihBerri, a secular lawyer with ties to the Syrian Baathists.  Coincidentally, militant Shiites #$AbbasAlMusawi and #$HassanAlSayyidNasrallah had returned to Lebanon at about this time, after having just been deported from #$Iraq after coming into conflict with #$SaddamHusseinAl-Takriti's Baath Party, and unsurprisingly, soon found themselves at odds with Berri.")
(comment CenterOfGeographicalRegion "A sub-collection of #$GeographicalRegion. Each instance is an area that is approximately central within some larger region.")
(comment CenterOfAuthority-GeographicalRegion "A sub-collection of #$GeographicalRegion. Each instance is a center of authority (as a city from which authority is exercised).")
(comment (ExperiencingEmotionFn Concern) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExperiencingEmotion. In each (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Concern), someone is worried, concerned, anxious, troubled, or uneasy.")
(comment (ExperiencingEmotionFn Dislike) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExperiencingEmotion. In each (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Dislike), someone has or feel a dislike or distaste for something.")
(comment (ExperiencingEmotionFn Fear) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ExperiencingEmotion. In each (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Fear), someone is uneasy or apprehensive about something.")
(comment JosephHenry-Physicist "An instance of #$Physicist. United States physicist who studied electromagnetic phenomena (1791-1878).")
(comment PureMathematics "An instance of #$FieldOfStudy. It is the branch of mathematics that studies and develops the principles of mathematics for their own sake rather than for their immediate usefulness.")
(comment Geometry "An instance of #$FieldOfStudy. It is is the #$PureMathematics of points and lines and curves and surfaces.")
(comment KingOfEngland "A sub-collection of #$King-HeadOfState. Each instance is the sovereign ruler of England.")
(comment SolarCalendar "An instance of #$CalendarSystem. It is a calendar based on solar cycles.")
(comment PuttingUpResistance "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Attempting. In each #$PuttingUpResistance, someone mounts resistance.")
(comment goalProperSubSituationTypes "<code>(#$goalProperSubSituationTypes PURP-ACT-TYPE SUB-SIT-TYPE)</code> indicates that instances of <code>PURP-ACT-TYPE</code>, which is a specialization of #$PurposefulAction, are performed with the goal of achieving instances of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code>.  This predicate is intended to complement temporal and causal predicates such that we can represent the reason why an #$IntelligentAgent would perform instances of <code>PURP-ACT-TYPE</code>.  More specifically, since an #$IntelligentAgent's goal can often be the satisfaction of another goal's preconditions, this predicate can help Cyc recognize causal chains that span several instances of distinct specializations of #$Situation during inference.")
(comment Attitude "A sub-collection of #$Cognition. Each instance is a complex mental state involving beliefs and feelings and values and dispositions to act in certain ways.")
(comment LexicalDatabase-CW "A sub-collection of #$ComputerDatabase-CW. Each instance is a database of information about words.")
(comment InternationalIntelligenceAgency "A sub-collection of #$IntelligenceAgency. Each instance is an intelligence agency outside the United States.")
(comment GeneralOfficer "A sub-collection of #$CommissionedOfficer. Each instance is an officers in the army or air force or marines above the rank of colonel.")
(comment (ResidentFn CityOfAthensGreece) "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a resident of Athens.")
(comment VedicLiterature "A sub-collection of #$SacredText. Each instance is a (from the Sanskrit word for `knowledge') any of the most ancient sacred writings of Hinduism written in early Sanskrit; traditionallly believed to comprise the Samhitas, the Brahmanas, the Aranyakas, and the Upanishads.")
(comment Samhita-VedicLiterature "A sub-collection of #$VedicLiterature. Each instance is one of four collections of sacred texts.")
(comment Manifestation "A sub-collection of #$SomethingAppearingSomewhere. Each instance is a clear appearance.")
(comment LinearObject-Finite "A specialization of #$LinearObject (q.v.). #$LinearObject-Finite is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that are finite in length.  They might be straight or curved, tangible or intangible.  A finite linear object might have two end points (e.g. a straight line segment), no end points (e.g. a closed curve or simple loop), or one end point (e.g. a loop with a \"tail\").
<p>
An important specialization of #$LinearObject-Finite is #$LineSegment, whose instances are strictly one-dimensional.
<p>
Cf. #$LinearObject-Infinite.")
(comment LinearObject-InfiniteUnbounded "A specialization of #$LinearObject-Infinite (q.v.). #$LinearObject-InfiniteUnbounded is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that are infinite in length and have no boundaries or end-points.  They might be straight or curved, intangible or (at least in principle) tangible.  
<p>
An important specialization of #$LinearObject-InfiniteUnbounded is #$Line, whose instances are strictly one-dimensional.
<p>
Cf. #$LinearObject-InfiniteSemiBounded.")
(comment LinearObject-Unbounded "A specialization of #$LinearObject (q.v.). #$LinearObject-Unbounded is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that have no boundaries or end-points.  They might be straight or curved, tangible or intangible.  
<p>
An important specialization of #$LinearObject-Unbounded is #$Line, whose instances are infinite in length and strictly one-dimensional.
<p>
Cf. #$LinearObject-Bounded and #$LinearObject-SemiBounded.")
(comment objectPerimeter "A binary #$SpatialPredicate relates two-dimensional (or \"flat\" three-dimensional) spatial objects to their perimeters, which are #$ClosedLinearObjects.   Also see the corresponding function #$PerimeterFn.")
(comment ClosedLinearObject "A specialization of #$LinearObject-Unbounded and  #$LinearObject-Finite (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ClosedLinearObject is a simple closed curved linear object or a connected series of such curves.
<p>
See also the #$comment on #$PerimeterFn.")
(disjointWith LinearObject-SemiBounded LinearObject-Bounded)
(disjointWith LinearObject-SemiBounded LinearObject-Unbounded)
(comment GinkoTestQuery "A holding tank for queries verified to be working that are potential targets for parses during the GINKO June 2005 demo.")
(comment equivalentRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType "'(#$equivalentRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType SIT-TYPE ROLE1 SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE2)' means that in any instance SIT of SITTYPE whatever plays ROLE1 also plays ROLE2 in SUBSIT, the subsituation of SIT that is an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE, and vice versa.")
(comment StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$ConnectedSpaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of \"strongly connected\" regions in the sense of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory (q.v.).  A homogeneous extended space region <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion if and only if (i) <code>REGION</code> is a connected region and (ii) any two homogeneous extended space regions <code>SUB1</code> and <code>SUB2</code> whose sum is <code>REGION</code> are strongly connected to each other (see #$stronglyConnectedSpaceRegions).  That is, there exists a part of <code>SUB1</code> (call it <code>P1</code>) and a part of <code>SUB2</code> (call it <code>P2</code>) such that the #$convexSpaceRegionOf (q.v.) the sum of <code>P1</code> and <code>P2</code> is also a part of <code>REGION</code>.   Intuitively, this means that a strongly-connected region is not the sum of two regions that are point- or edge-connected; there is a \"channel\" between them. 
<p>
Note that a strongly connected region may be hollow.  For example, a cup occupies a strongly-connected region.  A negative example is the sum of the spaces occupied by two tangentially connected balls, which is connected but not strongly- connected.")
(comment interestingSentence "<code>(#$interestingSentence THING SENTENCE)</code> means that <code>SENTENCE</code> (a quoted instance of #$CycLSentence-Assertible) is considered, by the salient audience, to be an interesting sentence about <code>THING</code> in the current context.")
(comment Institution "A sub-collection of #$Organization. Each instance is an organization founded and united for a specific purpose.")
(comment HonorarySociety "A sub-collection of #$Institution. Each instance is an institution for the advancement of art or science or literature.")
(comment Revolutionary "A sub-collection of #$Extremist. Each instance is a radical supporter of political or social revolution.")
(comment Remark "A sub-collection of #$Statement. Each instance is a statement that expresses a personal opinion or belief.")
(comment Observation-Remark "A sub-collection of #$Remark. Each instance is a remark expressing careful consideration.")
(comment LawEnforcementAgency "A sub-collection of #$GovernmentAgency. Each instance is an agency responsible for insuring obedience to the laws.")
(comment Campaign-MilitaryOperation "A sub-collection of #$MilitaryOperation. Each instance is several related operations aimed at achieving a particular goal (usually within geographical and temporal constraints).")
(comment Tyrant "A sub-collection of #$Leader. Each instance is a cruel and oppressive dictator.")
(comment Tyrant-AncientGreece "A sub-collection of #$HeadOfState. Each instance is a ruler in ancient Greece who had seized power without legal right to it.")
(comment (FrequentPerformerFn Dictating-GoverningSomething) "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who exercises power in a cruel way.")
(comment Code-CommunicationConvention "A sub-collection of #$CommunicationConvention. Each instance is a coding system used for transmitting messages requiring brevity or secrecy.")
(comment Communicator "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who communicates with others.")
(comment ArtisticMovement "A sub-collection of #$IdeologicalOrganization. Each instance is a group of artists who agree on general principles.")
(comment ConvexTangibleObject "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$ConvexTangibleObject is a tangible object that is convex; i.e., that has no significant concave surfaces, cavities or crevices (where the size of allowable minor concavities may depend on the context).  Each instance of #$ConvexTangibleObject occupies about the same space as its convex hull - see #$ConvexHullFn and #$MinimalSubsumingConvexSpaceRegionFn.  A solid physical sphere or cube is an instance of #$ConvexTangibleObject, but a cup or doughnut cannot be.")
(comment ConvexThing "A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of convex spatial things, tangible and intangible.  Each instance <code>CONVEX</code> of #$ConvexThing is such that, for any two points subumed by (see #$spatiallySubsumes) <code>CONVEX</code>, the straight line-segment between those points is itself subsumed by <code>CONVEX</code>.  
<p>
For example, a billiard ball is a convex thing but a boomerang is not; neither is a football (as it's hollow) or a sponge (as it has holes).
<p>
Specializations of #$ConvexThing include #$ConvexSpaceRegion and #$ConvexTangibleObject.  See also the generalization #$StarConvexThing.")
(comment Navigator-Explorer "A sub-collection of #$Explorer. Each instance is an in earlier times, a person who explored by ship.")
(comment Ballad-CW "A sub-collection of #$Poem-CW. Each instance is a narrative poem of popular origin.")
(comment Alliance-Organization "A sub-collection of #$Organization. Each instance is an organization of people (or countries) involved in a pact or treaty.")
(comment OilCartel "A sub-collection of #$Organization. Each instance is a cartel of companies or nations formed to control the production and distribution of oil.")
(comment (CausingFn Jumping) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event. In each (#$CausingFn #$Jumping), someone causes something to jump or leap.")
(comment foundingAgent "The relationship that obtains between an instance of #$Organization and an instance of #$IntelligentAgent who is a 'founder' of said #$Organization: more specifically, the relationship that obtains between any #$Organization that is an #$outputsCreated of an instance of #$FoundingAnOrganization and any #$IntelligentAgent who is the #$performedBy of that same instance.")
(comment ModelMicrotheory "A #$MicrotheoryType, all the instances of #$ModelMicrotheory are instances of #$Microtheory that contain a representation of the relevancy knowledge regarding some user of a #$UserProgram.  While instances of #$SoftwareInteractionSessionRecordMicrotheory are used to record the user's actions, knowledge that is inferred regarding what is relevant to the user is stored in an instance of #ModelMicrotheory.")
(comment ModelMtByUserAndTaskFn "An expression of the form (#$ModelMtByUserAndTaskFn PERSON TASK) denotes an instance of #$ModelMicrotheory that represents the context of the #$Person PERSON working on the #$Allotment TASK. Specifically, it is used to store inferred knowledge regarding the relevance of particular concepts, queries, or facts. Each instance of #$ModelMicrotheory bears a #$genlMt link to some #$TheoryMicrotheory and an instance of #$SoftwareInteractionSessionRecordMicrotheory denoted by (#$RecordMtByUserTaskAndApplicationFn PERSON TASK). It is from the contents of those microtheories that the contents of (#$ModelMtByUserAndTaskFn PERSON TASK) will be derived.")
(comment temporallyIntersectingEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that is used only with argument-places that are constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$TemporallyExistingThing (q.v.).  (#$temporallyIntersectingEntryFormatInArgs PRED N) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, for any two temporally existing things such that if either one is taken as the Nth argument to PRED, those temporally existing things must be such that they temporally intersect with one another (see #$temporallyIntersects).  That is, if the sequences <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then if (#$argIsa PRED N #$TemporallyExistingThing) then (#$temporallyIntersects ARGN ARGN-PRIME) holds (i.e. (#$not (#$temporallyDisjoint ARGN ARGN-PRIME)) holds).")
(comment spatiallyIntersectingEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that is used only with argument-places that are constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$SpatialThing (q.v.). (#$spatiallyIntersectingEntryFormatInArgs PRED N) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, for any two spatial things such that if either one is taken as the Nth argument to PRED, those spatial things must be such that they spatially intersect with one another (see#$spatiallyIntersects). That is, if the sequences <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then if (#$argIsa PRED N #$SpatialThing) then (#$spatiallyIntersects ARGN ARGN-PRIME) holds (i.e. (#$not (#$spatiallyDisjoint ARGN ARGN-PRIME)) holds).")
(comment StarConvexThing "A specialization of #$SimplyConnectedThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of \"star-convex\" spatial things, tangible and intangible.  Each instance <code>*CONVEX</code> of #$StarConvexThing subsumes (see #$spatiallySubsumes) a point <code>POINT</code> such that the straight line-segment between <code>POINT</code> and any other point in <code>*CONVEX</code> is itself subsumed by <code>*CONVEX</code>.  
<p>
For example, a five-point star figure and a typical printed asterisk (while not strictly convex) are both star-convex.  Conversely, a swastika and a printed parenthesis are not star-convex.
<p>
An important specialization of #$StarConvexThing is #$ConvexThing.")
(comment SimplyConnectedThing "A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of \"simply connected\" spatial things, tangible and intangible.  Each instance <code>SIMPCONN</code> of #$SimplyConnectedThing is a spatially continuous (i.e. \"connected\") thing such that any simple closed curve (see #$ClosedLinearObject) subsumed by (see #$spatiallySubsumes) <code>SIMPCONN</code> can be shunk to a point continuously and remain subsumed by <code>SIMPCONN</code>.  Intuitively, this means that <code>SIMPCONN</code> is a one-piece spatial thing with no holes (or pits or pockets) in it.
<p>
For example, a bowling pin is simply-connected, but a bowling ball is not.  A sewing pin is simply-connected but a sewing needle is not.
<p>
Notable subcollections of #$SimplyConnectedThing include #$StarConvexThing and (its specialization) #$ConvexThing.  Cf. #$MultiplyConnectedThing.")
(disjointWith SimplyConnectedThing MultiplyConnectedThing)
(comment MultiplyConnectedThing "A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of \"multiply connected\" spatial things, tangible and intangible.  Each instance <code>MULTCONN</code> of #$MultiplyConnectedThing is a spatially continuous (i.e. \"connected\") thing such that there is a simple closed curve (see #$ClosedLinearObject) subsumed by (see #$spatiallySubsumes) <code>MULTCONN</code> that, if shunk to a point continuously, would at some stage cease to be subsumed by <code>MULTCONN</code>.  Intuitively, this means that <code>MULTCONN</code> is a one-piece spatial thing with one or more <i>holes</i> (or pits or pockets) in it.
<p>
For example, a bowling ball is multiply-connected, but a bowling pin is not.  A sewing needle is multiply-connected, but a sewing pin is not.  (Both of the pins  are instead #$SimplyConnectedThings.)   
<p>
Note that the question as to whether a given connected <i>tangible</i> object (as opposed to an intangible #$SpaceRegion) is simply- or multiply-connected must be  considered a context-dependent matter of \"local granularity\".  Thus, in a microscopic context, a slight scratch on a bowling pin might constititute a \"pit\", rendering the pin a multiply-connected thing in that context.  Indeed, in certain subatomic contexts <i>any</i> material object would be viewed as full of holes (and perhaps even as a disconnected thing).")
(comment TwoDimensionalThing "A #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions and a specialization of #$PolyDimensionalThing.  A spatial thing is an instance of #$TwoDimensionalThing if and only if it is two-dimensional.  Examples include surfaces of tangible objects (such as a flat 2-D surface of a tabletop), planar space regions, and abstract 2-D objects.  Specializations include #$Surface and #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing.")
(comment LosingSightOfSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Action. In each #$LosingSightOfSomething, someone allows something to go out of sight.")
(comment Garbage-Generic "The collection of all tangible garbage in the world.  Requires some concept of things being no longer useful to the person who throws the object out.  Almost anything becomes garbage, once it has been thrown out.")
(comment CardinalCompassPoint "A sub-collection of #$CompassPoint. Each instance is one of the four main compass points.")
(comment ScandinavianCountry "A sub-collection of #$EuropeanCountry. Each instance is a country occupying Scandinavia.")
(comment ReligiousOrder "A sub-collection of #$ReligiousDenomination. Each instance is a group of person living under a religious rule.")
(comment FemaleAristocrat "A sub-collection of #$FemaleHuman. Each instance is a woman who is an aristocrat.")
(comment (SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn Time-Quantity duration TemporalThing) "A sub-collection of #$Time-Quantity. Each instance is the period of time during which something continues.")
(comment NaturalLaw-CodeOfConduct "A sub-collection of #$CodeOfConduct. Each instance is a rule or body of rules of conduct inherent in human nature and essential to or binding upon human society.")
(comment RelationalDatabase-CW "A sub-collection of #$ComputerDatabase-CW. Each instance is a database in which relations between information items are explicitly specified as accessible attributes.")
(comment Poet "A sub-collection of #$Author. Each instance is a writer of poems (the term is usually reserved for writers of good poetry).")
(comment (ResidentFn Lesbos-Island-Greece) "A sub-collection of #$GreekPerson. Each instance is a resident of Lesbos.")
(comment Miracle "A sub-collection of #$Situation. Each instance is a marvellous event manifesting a supernatural act of God.")
(comment Marshal-CommissionedOfficer "A sub-collection of #$CommissionedOfficer. Each instance is an (in some countries) a military officer of highest rank.")
(comment FieldMarshal "A sub-collection of #$Marshal-CommissionedOfficer. Each instance is an officer holding the highest rank in the army.")
(comment Sociologist "A sub-collection of #$Scientist. Each instance is a someone expert in the study of human society and its personal relationships.")
(comment Economist "A sub-collection of #$Professional-Adult. Each instance is an expert in the science of economics.")
(comment RingShapedObject "A #$GeometricShapeType and a specialization of #$RoundObject.  This is the collection of all two-dimensional ring-shaped objects, whether spatially-localized or abstract.  Each instance of #$RingShapedObject is a two-dimensional object having two roughly parallel circular or elliptical edges.  Typically, the thickness of the ring (i.e. the distance between the outer and inner edges) is small compared to the radius of the outer edge.  Examples include spatially-localized objects (e.g. a wedding ring or a washer), as well as abstract rings.  For three-dimensional \"ring-shaped\" objects, see #$Torus.")
(comment Singer "A sub-collection of #$MusicalPerformer. Each instance is a person who sings.")
(comment (CollectionIntersectionFn (TheSet FemaleHuman Actor)) "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a female actor.")
(comment KingOfFrance "A sub-collection of #$King-HeadOfState. Each instance is a sovereign ruler of France.")
(comment HolyRomanEmperor "A sub-collection of #$Emperor-HeadOfState. Each instance is a sovereign of the Holy Roman Empire.")
(comment actionRetaliatesFor "(#$actionRetaliatesFor REVENGE ACTION) means that for any agent who performs REVENGE, there is someone who that agent believes to have acted deliberately in ACTION and has the goal of harming with REVENGE, 'at' the time frame of REVENGE as indexed by the date at which REVENGE is carried out.  It also means that where the performer of REVENGE believes ACTOR to be the #$directingAgent or #$performedBy of ACTION, the likelihood that ACTOR is the #$intendedMaleficiary of REVENGE is high, and that where INTENDED-VICTIM is known to be the #$intendedMaleficiary of REVENGE and AVENGER is the performer, AVENGER believes INTENDED-VICTIM to have acted deliberately in REVENGE, 'at the time frame of REVENGE as indexed by the date.")
(comment ElapsedTime "A sub-collection of #$TimeInterval. Each instance is the time that elapses while some event is occurring.")
(comment EconomicAndSocialCouncilCommission "A sub-collection of #$Committee-Organization. Each instance is a commission of the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations.")
(comment Visionary "A sub-collection of #$Intellectual. Each instance is a person with unusual powers of foresight.")
(comment Forecaster "A sub-collection of #$Visionary. Each instance is a someone who makes predictions of the future (usually on the basis of special knowledge).")
(comment Astrologer "A sub-collection of #$Forecaster. Each instance is a someone who predicts the future by the positions of the planets and sun and moon.")
(comment MagnitudeRelation "A sub-collection of #$Relation. Each instance is a relation between magnitudes.")
(comment Ratio "A sub-collection of #$RealNumber. Each instance is the relative magnitudes of two quantities (usually expressed as a quotient).")
(comment Inscription "A sub-collection of #$TextualMaterial. Each instance is a letters inscribed (especially words engraved or carved) on something.")
(comment LunarCalendar "A sub-collection of #$CalendarSystem. Each instance is a calendar based on lunar cycles.")
(comment Borderline "A sub-collection of #$BoundaryLine. Each instance is a line that indicates a boundary.")
(comment StateLine "A sub-collection of #$Borderline. Each instance is the boundary between two states.")
(comment RocketEngineer "A sub-collection of #$Engineer. Each instance is an engineer who builds and tests rockets.")
(comment InternationalLawEnforcementAgency "A sub-collection of #$PoliceOrganization. Each instance is an international administrative unit responsible for law enforcement.")
(comment Commentary "A sub-collection of #$Statement. Each instance is a written explanation or criticism or illustration that is added to a book or other textual material.")
(comment Transformation "A sub-collection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. Each instance is a qualitative change.")
(comment Improvement-Transformation "A sub-collection of #$Transformation. Each instance is a change for the better; progress in development.")
(comment (VeryHighAmountFn Badness-Moral) "An instance of #$Badness-Moral. It causes harm or destruction or misfortune.")
(comment Carolingian-Monarch-HeadOfState "A sub-collection of #$Monarch-HeadOfState. Each instance is a member of the Carolingian dynasty.")
(comment Campanile-BellTower "A sub-collection of #$BellTower. Each instance is a bell tower; usually stands alone unattached to a building.")
(comment (MakingAbstractFn Explanation) "A sub-collection of #$SpeechAct. Each instance is the act of explaining; making something plain or intelligible.")
(comment Explanation-Thinking "A sub-collection of #$Thinking. Each instance is a thought that makes something comprehensible.")
(comment EconomicTheory "A sub-collection of #$Theorizing. Each instance is a theory of commercial activities (such as the production and consumption of goods).")
(comment IndependentGovernmentAgency "A sub-collection of #$GovernmentAgency. Each instance is an agency of the United States government that is created by an act of Congress and is independent of the executive departments.")
(comment ObtainingAndStoringKnowledge "A sub-collection of #$StrictlyMentalEvent. Each instance is a cognitive process involved in obtaining and storing knowledge.")
(comment RepresentationalCognitiveProcess "A sub-collection of #$ObtainingAndStoringKnowledge. Each instance is a basic cognitive process in which some entity comes to stand for or represent something else.")
(comment Emblem "A sub-collection of #$SymbolicThing. Each instance is a visible symbol representing an abstract idea.")
(comment Microscopist "A sub-collection of #$Scientist. Each instance is a scientist who specializes in research with the use of microscopes.")
(comment Archpriest "A sub-collection of #$Priest. Each instance is a senior clergyman and dignitary.")
(comment Pace-Speed "A sub-collection of #$Speed. Each instance is the rate of moving (especially walking or running).")
(comment EpicPoem-CW "A sub-collection of #$Poem-CW. Each instance is a long narrative poem telling of a hero's deeds.")
(comment InformationScience "An instance of #$ScientificFieldOfStudy. It is the science concerned with gathering and manipulating and storing and retrieving and classifying recorded information.")
(comment Advocate "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who pleads for a cause or propounds an idea.")
(comment (BelieverFn NationalistIdeology) "A sub-collection of #$Advocate. Each instance is an advocate of national independence of or a strong national government.")
(comment NationalistLeader "A sub-collection of (#$BelieverFn #$NationalistIdeology). Each instance is the leader of a nationalist movement.")
(comment AmericanRevolutionaryLeader "A sub-collection of #$NationalistLeader. Each instance is a nationalist leader in the American Revolution and in the creation of the United States.")
(comment EventCausingDistressOrPain "A sub-collection of #$AnimalActivity. Each instance is an event causing distress or pain.")
(comment Maker "A sub-collection of #$Creator-Person. Each instance is a someone who makes things.")
(comment ArmsManufacturer "A sub-collection of #$ManufacturingOrganization. Each instance is a someone who manufactures arms and munitions.")
(comment Colony-GeopoliticalEntity "A sub-collection of #$GeopoliticalEntity. Each instance is a body of people who settle far from home but maintain ties with their homeland; inhabitants remain nationals of their home state but are not literally under the home state's system of government.")
(comment Region "A sub-collection of #$Place. Each instance is the extended spatial location of something.")
(comment Belt-Region "A sub-collection of #$Region. Each instance is an elongated region where a specific condition is found.")
(comment spatioTemporallyIntersectingEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that is used only with argument-places that are constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$SpatialThing and #$TemporallyExistingThing (q.v.). (#$spatioTemporallyIntersectingEntryFormatInArgs PRED N) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, for any two spatio-temporal things such that if either one is taken as the Nth argument to PRED, those spatio-temporal things must be such that they spatially intersect with one another (see #$spatiallyIntersects) and they temporally intersect with one another (see #$temporallyIntersects). That is, if the sequences <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then if (#$argIsa PRED N #$SpatialThing) and (#$argIsa PRED N #$TemporallyExistingThing) then (#$spatiallyIntersects ARGN ARGN-PRIME) holds (i.e. (#$not (#$spatiallyDisjoint ARGN ARGN-PRIME)) holds) and (#$temporallyIntersects ARGN ARGN-PRIME) holds (i.e. (#$not (#$temporallyDisjoint ARGN ARGN-PRIME)) holds).")
(comment basicDataHTML "A #$BasicDataPredicate that relates something to a terse #$HTMLSource string containing its basic data.")
(comment informationRequirementQuerySpecification "(informationRequirementQuerySpecification INFORMATION-REQUEST QUERY-SPECIFICATION) relates a given #$InformationRequirement with its #$CycLQuerySpecification QUERY-SPECIFICATION.  See also #$QueryFn.")
(disjointWith WhenSituationWarrantsAssignment RegularlyRecurringAssignment)
(disjointWith WhenSituationWarrantsAssignment SingleEventAssignment)
(comment WhenSituationWarrantsAssignment "A specialization of #$Assignment-Specification (q.v.).  Each instance of #$WhenSituationWarrantsAssignment is an #$Assignment-Specification that requires a particular sort of action to be performed whenever a given situation occurs.  For example, the assignment of notifying security when ever an intruder is detected is an when-situation-warrants assignment because it involves the same type of action to be performed when ever a given situation occurs.")
(disjointWith WhenSituationWarrantsAssignment IteratedAssignment)
(comment RegularlyRecurringAssignment "A specialization of #$Assignment-Specification (q.v.).  Each instance of #$RegularlyRecurringAssignment is an #$Assignment-Specification that requires that a particular sort of action be performed once during each of the intervals of a specified type (#$assignmentRepetitionPeriod (q.v.)) that fall within the asssignment's period (#$assignmentPeriod (q.v.)).  Thus, the chore of cleaning the kitchen every week for a month is a #$RegularlyRecurringAssignment because although it requires only four performances, those performances must be distributed across the individual weeks of that month.  For the type of #$Assignment-Specification where the emphasis is on the particular number of repetitions, see  #$IteratedAssignment.")
(comment IteratedAssignment "A specialization of #$Assignment-Specification (q.v.).  Each instance of #$IteratedAssignment is an #$Assignment-Specification that requires a particular sort of action to be performed a specific number of times.  For example, the assignment of performing fifty push-ups is an iterated assignment because it involves the same type of action to be performed a particular number of times. For the type of #$Assignment-Specification where the emphasis is on the rate at which the repetitions are performed, see #$RegularlyRecurringAssignment.")
(comment Beam "A specialization of #$RectangularParallelepiped (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Beam has a maximum dimension (length) significantly greater than its next largest dimension (e.g. width).  Instances of this collection include tangible objects, such as wooden beams in houses, as well as intangible beam-shaped space regions.")
(comment ScrewShapedObject "An instance of #$ThreeDimensionalShapeType.  This is the collection of all screw-shaped objects, whether spatially-localized or abstract.  Each instance of #$ScrewShapedObject is roughly cylindrical or conical in shape, but has an external spiral \"thread\" starting at one end and running along most or all of its length.  A screw-shaped oject typically has a solid central axis.  
<p>
Specializations of this collection include #$Screw-Connector and #$DrillBit.  Note that #$Corkscrews are usually <i>not</i> #$ScrewShapedObjects, but are instead instances of #$Helix.")
(comment Tube-Tangible "A specialization of #$Tube (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Tube-Tangible is a hollow, tangible object whose shape is that of the side-surface of a #$Cylinder.")
(disjointWith LinearObject-Straight RoundObject)
(comment AnimalBodyShapeType "The collection of animal body shape types; instances are specializations of #$AnimalShapedThing which describe standard arrangements of animal body parts which are the standard body shapes.")
(disjointWith LinearObject-Planar LinearObject-NonPlanar)
(comment modelMtOfUser "The corresponding predicate of #$UserModelMtFn.  An assertion of the form (#$modelMtOfUser MT AGENT) means that MT is a #$Microtheory that stores information about the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT.")
(comment programArchitectureMt "An assertion of the form (#$programArchitectureMt PROGRAM MT) means that the defining characteristics of the of the #$ComputerProgram-CW, including how Cyc might be able to interact agentively with that program, are store in the #$Microtheory MT.")
(comment Complaining "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Objecting-CommunicationAct. In each #$Complaining, someone expresses complaints, discontent, displeasure, or unhappiness.")
(comment Line-Planar "A specialization of #$Line and #$LinearObject-Planar (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Line-Planar is a (straight or curved) intangible line that remains in a single two-dimensional plane. Specializations of this collection include #$Arc and #$Line-Straight.  And cf. #$Line-NonPlanar.")
(disjointWith Line-NonPlanar Line-Planar)
(comment LineSegment-NonPlanar "A specialization of #$LineSegment and #$LinearObject-NonPlanar (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$LineSegment-NonPlanar is a curved intangible line-segment that does <i>not</i> remain in a single two-dimensional plane, but can only be embedded in a 3-D space region.  For example, a one-dimensional bounded spiral or knot is a non-planar line-segment.  And cf. #$LineSegment-Planar.")
(comment LineSegment-Planar "A specialization of #$LineSegment and #$LinearObject-Planar (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$LineSegment-Planar is a straight or curved intangible line-segment that remains in a single two-dimensional plane.  For example, an #$Arc is a planar line-segment.  Cf. #$LineSegment-NonPlanar.")
(comment LinearObjectTypeByStraightness "A #$SecondOrderCollection that facets  and covers (see #$facets-Covering) the collection #$LinearObject, according to how close a linear object is to being completely straight. Relative straightness, in the sense relevent here, is measured roughly in terms of the dimensionality of a space in which a linear object can be embedded.  For example, a perfectly straight line segment (see #$LineSegment-Straight) can be embedded in a one-dimensional space; some curved line segments (e.g. #$Arcs) require at least a two-dimensional space for embedding; and other curved line segments (e.g. linear spirals) require three dimensions for embedding.  The reified instances of #$LinearObjectTypeByStraightness include the successively-subsuming collections #$LinearObject-Straight, #$LinearObject-Planar, and #$LinearObject itself.
<p>
Note that the qualifier \"<i>roughly</i>\" is used above because some #$LinearObjects are actually <i>poly-dimensional</i> (e.g. a piece of wire is 3-D), and we also want to distinguish between \"straight\" and \"planar\" and \"non-planar\" poly-dimensional linear objects (e.g. pins vs. staples vs. springs).  So some poly-dimensional linear objects will be borderline and/or context-dependent cases with respect to which straightness category they belong to.")
(comment LinearObjectTypeByDimensions "A second-order collection that facets  and partitions (see #$facets-Partition) the collection #$LinearObject (q.v.), according to how many spatial dimensions a linear-object has.  Geometric lines and line-segments are of course one-dimensional.  In a two-dimensional contexts, however, certain \"ribbon\"-shaped regions (e.g. rivers depicted on a map) might be treated as lines.  And in three-dimensional contexts (e.g. the physical world), certain long thin 3-D tangible objects (e.g. wires or pencil markings) are often treated as lines.  The instances of #$LinearObjectTypeByDimensions are thus: #$LinearObject-OneDimensional, #$LinearObject-TwoDimensional, and #$LinearObject-ThreeDimensional.
<p>
Note that some poly-dimensional objects will be borderline and/or context-dependent cases with respect to whether they are #$LinearObjects, as opposed to being #$Surfaces or solids.")
(comment HTMLTableFn "(#$HTMLTableFn OPTIONS . ROWS) denotes the #$HTMLSource for displaying the table whose rows are ROWS with OPTIONS.")
(comment (UnitOfMoneyOfGeopoliticalEntityFn Algeria) "A sub-collection of #$UnitOfMoney. Each instance is a monetary unit in Algeria.")
(comment ReligiousLeader "A sub-collection of #$ReligiousPerson. Each instance is a leader of a religious order.")
(comment GaelicLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$CelticLanguageFamily. Each instance is an of several related languages of the Celts in Ireland and Scotland.")
(comment (CollectionIntersectionFn (TheSet MaleHuman Aristocrat)) "A sub-collection of #$Aristocrat. Each instance is a man who is an aristocrat.")
(comment Prizefighter "A sub-collection of #$Boxer. Each instance is a professional boxer.")
(comment ElectromagneticUnitOfMeasure "A sub-collection of #$UnitOfMeasure. Each instance is an of various systems of units for measuring electricity and magnetism.")
(disjointWith UnitOfElectricalResistance UnitOfSpeed)
(disjointWith UnitOfElectricalResistance UnitOfFrequency)
(disjointWith UnitOfElectricalResistance UnitOfAngularSpeed)
(disjointWith UnitOfElectricalResistance UnitOfPower)
(comment UnitOfElectricalResistance "The collection of functions which return elements of #$ElectricalResistance.  E.g., (#$Ohm 2000) denotes an instance of #$ElectricalResistance that would measure two thousand ohms.")
(comment BarrierIsland "A sub-collection of #$Island. Each instance is a long narrow sandy island (wider than a reef) running parallel to the shore.")
(comment FinancialCondition "A sub-collection of #$SystemCondition. Each instance is the condition of finances.")
(comment Depression-FinancialCondition "A sub-collection of #$FinancialCondition. Each instance is a long-term economic state characterized by unemployment and low prices and low levels of trade and investment.")
(comment Historian "A sub-collection of #$AcademicProfessional. Each instance is a person who is an authority on history and who studies it and writes about it.")
(comment Chronicler "A sub-collection of #$Historian. Each instance is a someone who writes chronicles.")
(comment Explorer "A sub-collection of #$Researcher. Each instance is a someone who travels into little known regions (especially for some scientific purpose).")
(comment SkilledWorker "A sub-collection of #$PersonWithOccupation. Each instance is a worker who has acquired special skills.")
(comment Aviator "A sub-collection of #$SkilledWorker. Each instance is a someone who operates an aircraft.")
(comment PrinceOfWales "A sub-collection of #$Prince. Each instance is the male heir apparent of the British sovereign.")
(comment Assassin "A sub-collection of #$Murderer. Each instance is a murderer (especially one who kills a prominent political figure) who kills by a treacherous surprise attack and often is hired to do the deed.")
(comment Shoshonean-Uto-AztecanLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$Uto-AztecanLanguageFamily. Each instance is a subfamily of Uto-Aztecan languages spoken mainly in the southwestern United States.")
(comment Paleontologist "A sub-collection of #$Scientist. Each instance is a specialist in paleontology.")
(comment LiteratePerson "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who can read and write.")
(comment Diarist "A sub-collection of #$Author. Each instance is a someone who keeps a diary or journal.")
(comment HeroicPerson "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person distinguished by exceptional courage and nobility and strength.")
(comment (CollectionIntersectionFn (TheSet HeroicPerson MaleHuman)) "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a man distinguished by exceptional courage and nobility and strength.")
(comment (CollectionIntersectionFn (TheSet HeroicPerson FemaleHuman)) "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a woman possessing heroic qualities or a woman who has performed heroic deeds.")
(comment Discrimination-ObtainingAndStoringKnowledge "A sub-collection of #$ObtainingAndStoringKnowledge. Each instance is a cognitive process whereby two or more stimuli are distinguished.")
(comment AestheticDiscrimination "A sub-collection of #$Discrimination-ObtainingAndStoringKnowledge. Each instance is a delicate discrimination (especially of aesthetic values).")
(comment Trend "A sub-collection of #$AestheticDiscrimination. Each instance is the popular taste at a given time.")
(comment Reasoning "A sub-collection of #$Thinking. Each instance is thinking that is coherent and logical.")
(comment PointSystem-WritingSystem "A sub-collection of #$WritingSystem. Each instance is a system of writing or printing using patterns of raised dots that can be read by touch.")
(comment ServiceBook-SacredText "A sub-collection of #$SacredText. Each instance is a book setting forth the forms of church service.")
(comment Loch-Lake "A sub-collection of #$Lake. Each instance is a Scottish lake.")
(comment TravelRoute "A sub-collection of #$Path-Customary. Each instance is an established line of travel or access.")
(comment Loch-Cove "A sub-collection of #$Cove. Each instance is a long narrow inlet of the sea in Scotland (especially when it is nearly landlocked).")
(comment UnfortunatePerson "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person who suffers misfortune.")
(comment Victim-UnfortunatePerson "A sub-collection of #$UnfortunatePerson. Each instance is an unfortunate person who suffers from some adverse circumstance.")
(comment Martyr-Victim "A sub-collection of #$Victim-UnfortunatePerson. Each instance is one who voluntarily suffers death as the penalty for refusing to renounce their religion.")
(comment Ministry "A sub-collection of #$GovernmentDepartment. Each instance is a government department under the direction of a minister.")
(comment ForeignOffice "A sub-collection of #$Ministry. Each instance is the government department in charge of foreign relations.")
(comment Airlift "A sub-collection of #$Translocation. Each instance is a transportation of people or goods by air (especially when other means of access are unavailable).")
(comment Playwright "A sub-collection of #$Author. Each instance is a someone who writes plays.")
(comment FinnoUgric-UralicLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$UralicLanguageFamily. Each instance is a family of Uralic languages indigenous to Scandinavia and Hungary and Russia and western Siberia (prior to the Slavic expansion into those regions).")
(comment Fennic-UralicLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$FinnoUgric-UralicLanguageFamily. Each instance is one of two branches of the Finno-Ugric languages; a family of languages including Finnish and Estonian (but not Hungarian).")
(comment Volgaic-UralicLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$Fennic-UralicLanguageFamily. Each instance is a group of Finnic languages spoken around the Volga river.")
(comment Climber-Athlete "A sub-collection of #$Athlete. Each instance is someone who climbs as a sport; especially someone who climbs mountains.")
(comment MountainClimber "A sub-collection of #$Climber-Athlete. Each instance is someone who climbs mountains.")
(comment SouthDravidian-DravidianLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$DravidianLanguageFamily. Each instance is a Dravidian language spoken primarily in southern India.")
(comment Kannada-DravidianLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$SouthDravidian-DravidianLanguageFamily. Each instance is a Dravidian language spoken in southern India.")
(comment Expert "A sub-collection of #$Person. Each instance is a person with special knowledge or ability who performs skillfully.")
(comment Specialist "A sub-collection of #$Expert. Each instance is an expert who is devoted to one occupation or branch of learning.")
(comment LordsPrayer "A sub-collection of #$Prayer-SacredText. Each instance is the prayer that Christ gave his disciples in the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 6:9-13).")
(comment Copyist "A sub-collection of #$Writer. Each instance is someone employed to make written copies of documents and manuscripts.")
(comment Parliamentarian "A sub-collection of #$Expert. Each instance is an expert in parliamentary rules and procedures.")
(comment Mandate-ControlledLand "A sub-collection of #$ControlledLand. Each instance is a territory surrendered by Turkey or Germany after World War I and put under the tutelage of some other European power until they were able to stand by themselves.")
(comment (UnitOfMoneyOfGeopoliticalEntityFn Syria) "A sub-collection of #$UnitOfMoney. Each instance is a monetary unit in Syria.")
(comment Iroquois-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives "A sub-collection of #$EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives. Each instance is a member of the warlike North American Indian peoples formerly living in New York state; the Iroquois League were allies of the British during the American Revolution.")
(comment Onondaga-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives "A sub-collection of #$Iroquois-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives. Each instance is a member of the Iroquoian people formerly living between Lake Champlain and the Saint Lawrence River.")
(comment Penutian-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives "A sub-collection of #$EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives. Each instance is a member of a North American Indian people speaking one of the Penutian languages.")
(comment Shahaptian-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives "A sub-collection of #$Penutian-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives. Each instance is a member of a North American Indian people who lived in Oregon along the Columbia river and its tributaries in Washington and northern Idaho.")
(comment NezPerce-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives "A sub-collection of #$Shahaptian-EthnicGroupOfNewWorldNatives. Each instance is a member of a tribe of the Shahaptian people living on the pacific coast.")
(comment MythicalMonster "A sub-collection of #$MythologicalThing. Each instance is a monster renowned in folklore and myth.")
(comment (UnitOfMoneyOfGeopoliticalEntityFn ElSalvador) "A sub-collection of #$UnitOfMoney. Each instance is a monetary unit in El Salvador.")
(comment Kwa-CongoLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$Niger-CongoLanguageFamily. Each instance is an African language in the Niger-Congo group spoken from the Ivory Coast east to Nigeria.")
(comment Entomologist "A sub-collection of #$Zoologist. Each instance is a zoologist who studies insects.")
(comment Assembly-SocialGroup "A sub-collection of #$Gathering-SocialGroup. Each instance is a group of persons gathered together for a common purpose.")
(comment Federation "A sub-collection of #$Organization. Each instance is an organization formed by merging several groups or parties.")
(comment WorkingGroup-Team "A sub-collection of #$Team-Generic. Each instance is a group of people working together temporarily until some goal is achieved.")
(comment Expedition-Team "A sub-collection of #$WorkingGroup-Team. Each instance is an organized group of people undertaking a journey for a particular purpose.")
(comment Permic-UralicLanguageFamily "A sub-collection of #$Fennic-UralicLanguageFamily. Each instance is a Finnic language spoken in the northwest Urals.")
(comment Storyteller "A sub-collection of #$Artist-Performer. Each instance is a someone who tells a story.")
(comment Fabulist-Storyteller "A sub-collection of #$Storyteller. Each instance is a person who tells or invents fables.")
(comment Lobby-PoliticalOrganization "A sub-collection of #$PoliticalOrganization. Each instance is a group of people who try actively to influence legislation.")
(comment InferiorTerrestrialPlanet "A sub-collection of #$TerrestrialPlanet. Each instance is an of the planets whose orbit lies inside the earth's orbit.")
(comment (UnitOfMoneyOfGeopoliticalEntityFn Malawi) "A sub-collection of #$UnitOfMoney. Each instance is a monetary unit in Malawi.")
(comment SpiritualLeader "A sub-collection of #$Leader. Each instance is a leader in religious or sacred affairs.")
(comment Evangelist "A sub-collection of #$SpiritualLeader. Each instance is a (when capitalized) any of the spiritual leaders who are assumed to be authors of the Gospels in the New Testament: Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John.")
(comment Culture-Civilization "A sub-collection of #$Society. Each instance is a particular society at a particular time and place.")
(comment Executive-PublicOfficial "A sub-collection of #$PublicOfficial. Each instance is a persons who administers the law.")
(comment (GroupFn Assassin) "A sub-collection of #$Team-Generic. Each instance is a team of assassins.")
(comment Lama-Priest "A sub-collection of #$Priest. Each instance is a Tibetan or Mongolian priest of Lamaism.")
(comment UserModelMtFn "An expression of the form (#$UserModelMtFn AGENT) is used to create a #$Microtheory that allows for the storing of facts about a particular agent.  Specifically, within a single KB, this constitutes Cyc's knowledge of that user.")
(comment ComputerUserWithUserModel "A specialization of #$ComputerUser.  Each instance of #$ComputerUserWithUserModel is a computer user whose user profile is deemed worth storing for reasoning about.")
(comment TaskModelMtFn "An expression of the form (#$TaskModelMtFn TASK) is used to represent a #$Microtheory that represents the model for a particular #$Allotment TASK.  This microtheory holds information that is Cyc believes to be relevant to the performance of TASK.")
(disjointWith SimplyConnectedSpaceRegion MultiplyConnectedSpaceRegion)
(comment subcollectionFunctionDefiningRule "(#$subcollectionFunctionDefiningRule FUNC RULE) means that RULE is used by the #$CycInferenceEngine to prove literals of the form (#$isa :not-fully-bound (FUNC . :fully-bound)).  It will prove such literals via a recursive removal-only ask of the antecedent of RULE.")
(comment FoodWorker "A specialization of #$PersonWithOccupation.  Each instance of #$FoodWorker is a worker whose occupation is to prepare or serve food or drink.  Notable specializations of #$FoodWorker include #$Butcher, #$Brewer, #$Bartender, and #$Waitperson.")
(disjointWith SkilledWorker UnskilledWorker)
(comment earliestSubsumedIntervalOfType "<code>(#$earliestIntersectingIntervalOfType TEMPORAL-THING INTERVAL TYPE)</code> means that the instance of #$TimeInterval <code>INTERVAL</code> is the earliest interval of type <code>TYPE</code> that is temporally subsumed by (c.f., #$temporallySubsumes) the temporal extent of the instance of #$TemporalThing <code>TEMPORAL-THING</code>. For example, 
<pre>    
     (#$earliestSubsumedIntervalOfType #$WorldWarII 
        (#$YearFn 1940) #$CalendarYear)
</pre>means that the year 1940 is the first full calendar year that occurs during WWII.  See also #$latestIntersectingIntervalOfType and #$earliestIntersectingIntervalOfType.")
(comment earliestIntersectingIntervalOfType "<code>(#$earliestIntersectingIntervalOfType TEMPORAL-THING INTERVAL TYPE)</code> means that the instance of #$TimeInterval <code>INTERVAL</code> is the earliest interval of type <code>TYPE</code> that intersects (c.f., #$temporallyIntersects) the temporal extent of the instance of #$TemporalThing <code>TEMPORAL-THING</code>. For example, 
<pre>    
     (#$earliestIntersectingIntervalOfType #$WorldWarII 
        (#$YearFn 1939) #$CalendarYear)
</pre>means that the year 1939 is the first calendar year that intersects WWII.  See also #$latestIntersectingIntervalOfType and #$earliestSubsumedIntervalOfType.")
(comment latestIntersectingIntervalOfType "<code>(#$latestIntersectingIntervalOfType TEMPORAL-THING INTERVAL TYPE)</code> means that the instance of #$TimeInterval <code>INTERVAL</code> is the latest interval of type <code>TYPE</code> that intersects (c.f., #$temporallyIntersects) the temporal extent of the instance of #$TemporalThing <code>TEMPORAL-THING</code>. For example, 
<pre>
    (#$latestIntersectingIntervalOfType #$WorldWarII 
       (#$YearFn 1945) #$CalendarYear)
</pre>means that the year 1945 is the last calendar year that intersects WWII.  See also #$earliestIntersectingIntervalOfType and #$latestSubsumedIntervalOfType.")
(comment TwoDimensionalShapeType "A specialization of #$SpatialThingTypeByGeometricShape (q.v.).  Each instance of #$TwoDimensionalShapeType is a specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing (q.v.).  Instances include #$Circle and #$Polygon.  Cf. #$ThreeDimensionalShapeType.")
(comment latestSubsumedIntervalOfType "<code>(#$latestSubsumedIntervalOfType TEMPORAL-THING INTERVAL TYPE)</code> means that the instance of #$TimeInterval <code>INTERVAL</code> is the latest interval of type <code>TYPE</code> that is temporally subsumed by (c.f., #$temporallySubsumes) the temporal extent of the instance of #$TemporalThing <code>TEMPORAL-THING</code>. For example, 
<pre>
    (#$latestSubsumedIntervalOfType #$WorldWarII 
       (#$YearFn 1944) #$CalendarYear)
</pre>means that the year 1944 is the last full calendar year that occurs during WWII.  See also #$earliestIntersectingIntervalOfType and #$latestIntersectingIntervalOfType.")
(comment OneDimensionalThing "A specialization of #$SpatialThing and #$IntangibleIndividual (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all and only one-dimensional things.  An instance of #$OneDimensionalThing might be continuous or discontinuous, straight or curved, planar or non-planar (see e.g. #$Line, #$Line-Straight, and #$Line-Planar).")
(comment ExtendedSpaceRegion "A specialization of both #$SpaceRegion and #$PositiveDimensionalThing (qq.v.).  Instances of #$ExtendedSpaceRegion are portions of a one-or-higher-dimensional space that have an extent in at least one dimension.  Important specializations of this collection are #$Line, #$Surface, and #$SpaceChunk.")
(comment HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion and #$PositiveDimensionalThing.  This collection is by definition equal to (see #$rewriteOf) the union of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion and #$NullSpaceRegion (qq.v.).  Its instances include all homogeneous space regions except #$Points (q.v.).")
(disjointWith ExtendedSpaceRegion NullSpaceRegion)
(comment OpenSourceComputerProgram "A specialization of #$ComputerProgram-CW. #$OpenSourceComputerProgram is the collection of programs for which the #$ComputerCode-Source is available to anyone who owns a copy of the program.  That means owners are free to run, copy, distribute, study, change, and improve the software.  Some prominent instances of #$OpenSourceComputerProgram are the #$ApacheWebServer -- which operates a significant number of the world's web sites, #$BIND-TheProgram -- that provides the domain name service for the Internet, the #$Linux operating system, GNU software packages, and many computer games.")
(comment SimplyConnectedSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$ConnectedSpaceRegion and #$SimplyConnectedThing (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all and only simply-connected space regions.  A notable specialization of #$SimplyConnectedSpaceRegion is #$ConvexSpaceRegion.")
(comment FolderWithIntelligenceQueriesForSuggestion "Instances of this collection are (abstract) folders which contain queries that may be useful as suggested queries for research.")
(comment tinyIconTermImagePathname "(#$tinyIconTermImagePathname THING PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a pathname (i.e. a designator for a directory or a file) that contains the small version of the icon associated with THING.")
(comment iconForTermImagePathname "(#$iconForTermImagePathname THING PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a pathname (i.e. a designator for a directory or a file) that contains the icon associated with THING.")
(comment mapImageForTermImagePathname "(#$mapImageForTermImagePathname THING PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a pathname (i.e. a designator for a directory or a file) that contains the map image associated with THING.")
(comment flagImageForTermImagePathname "(#$flagImageForTermImagePathname THING PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a pathname (i.e. a designator for a directory or a file) that contains the flag icon associated with THING.")
(comment contentMtOfLinkage "(#$contentMtOfLinkage MICROTHEORY LINKAGE) identifies MICROTHEORY as the instance of #$Microtheory that records the linkage data for the instance LINKAGE of #$LinkParserLinkage.")
(comment TemporallyExistingThingWithEndingDateFn "(#$TemporallyExistingThingWithEndingDateFn DATE) is the collection of all #$TemporallyExistingThings TET such that (#$endingDate TET DATE).")
(comment TemporallyExistingThingWithStartingDateFn "(#$TemporallyExistingThingWithStartingDateFn DATE) is the collection of all #$TemporallyExistingThings TET such that (#$startingDate TET DATE).")
(comment CotemporalPredicate "A specialization of #$TemporalExistencePredicate (q.v.).  This is the collection of predicates that are used to make statements that imply the current temporal existence of any of their arguments that happen to be temporal things.  
<p>
More precisely, a predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$CotemporalPredicate if and only if the following two conditions hold.  (i) If a sentence of the form <code>(PRED ARG<sub>1</sub> ... ARG<sub>N</sub>)</code> is true at a given time <code>T</code>, then any <code>ARG<sub>i</sub></code> (<code>1<=i<=N</code>) that is a #$TemporalThing temporally-subsumes <code>T</code> (see #$temporallySubsumes).  (ii) <code>PRED</code> holds of at least one sequence of arguments that includes at least one instance of #$TemporalThing.
<p>
(Condition (ii) above prevents predicates that never hold of temporal things from trivially qualifying as #$CotemporalPredicates. Its practical import is that at least one of <code>PRED</code>'s argument-places must not be type-constrained (see #$argIsa) to any collection that is #$disjointWith #$TemporalThing.)
<p>
For example, #$owns is a #$CotemporalPredicate.  So the sentence <code>(#$owns Nick Spot)</code> with time-index (see #$mtTimeIndex) <code>(#$YearFn 1992)</code> implies (given that Nick and Spot are temporal things) that Nick and Spot were alive throughout 1992.  In contrast, the predicate #$awareOf is <i>not</i> a #$CotemporalPredicate.  The sentence <code>(#$awareOf Fred #$GeorgeWashington)</code>, with the same time-index as above, does <i>not</i> imply <code>(#$temporallySubsumes #$GeorgeWashington (#$YearFn 1992))</code>.
<p>
See also the specialization #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate, the collection of cotemporal predicates <i>all</i> of whose argument-places are constrained (at least) to #$TemporalThing.  And see the predicate #$contemporaryInArg.")
(comment MultiplyConnectedSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$ConnectedSpaceRegion and #$MultiplyConnectedThing (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all and only multiply-connected space regions: space regions having one or more \"holes\" in them.")
(comment basicDataForPerson "A #$BasicDataPredicate that relates a #$Person to a #$List of three instances of #$Set-Mathematical.  (#$basicDataForPerson PERSON (#$TheList NAME-SET COL-SET AFFIL-SET)) means that:
<p>
a) NAME-SET is a set of name strings for PERSON.
<p>
b) COL-SET is a set of collections of which PERSON is an instance.
<p>
c) AFFIL-SET is a set of agents AGENT such that (#$affiliatedWith PERSON AGENT).")
(comment linkageTokenization "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that relates an instance of #$LinkParserLinkage to its associated tokenization.  (#$linkageTokenization LINKAGE TOKENIZATION) means that TOKENIZATION is the instance of #$CycTokenization that is a tokenization of the string STRING that is the string for which LINKAGE is a linkage.")
(comment nthLinkParserLinkageOfInputString "(#$nthLinkParserLinkageOfInputString NUMBER LINKAGE STRING) means that LINKAGE is the NUMBER-th instance of #$LinkParserLinkage of STRING, an instance of #$SubLString.")
(comment InfectionCommunicabilityLevel "The #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType whose instances are #$Infections with a common level of communicability, or contagiousness. The value provides some quantitaive information of the risks of transmission from one infected host, for instance a #$Person, to another healthy organism of a given kind of #$Infection.")
(comment InfectionInfectivityLevel "The #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType whose instances are #$Infections with a common level of infectivity, which is the likelihood that an infection will cause disease. The value provides some quantitaive information of the risks of exposure to a given kind of #$Infection.")
(comment InfectionVirulenceLevel "The #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType whose instances are #$Infections with a common level of virulence, which is a measure of an infection's severity. For example, the common cold has low virulence, while anthrax is extremely virulent.")
(comment infectivityOfInfection "(#$infectivityOfInfection INFECTION LEVEL) means that instances of the #$InfectionType INFECTION have the level of infectivity LEVEL. For those infected with INFECTION, LEVEL represents the likelihood of INFECTION causing disease.")
(comment ExtendedRealNumber "A specialization of #$Number-General and a generalization of #$RealNumber. #$ExtendedRealNumber is the collection of real numbers extended to include two additional instances, #$PlusInfinity-Ordinal and #$MinusInfinity-Ordinal.  Extended reals are useful, for example, in measuring the size of an infinite spatial thing, such as a #$Line.")
(comment PlusInfinity-Ordinal "An instance of #$ExtendedRealNumber (q.v.) that is of infinitely large, positive magnitude.  Cf. #$MinusInfinity-Ordinal.")
(comment MinusInfinity-Ordinal "An instance of #$ExtendedRealNumber (q.v.) that is of infinitely large, negative magnitude.  Cf. #$PlusInfinity-Ordinal")
(comment InfiniteScalarInterval "A specialization of #$ScalarInterval whose instances are, or are based upon, an infinitely large numeric value, positive or negative.  An infinite scalar interval might be a #$NumericInterval (such as #$Aleph-1 or #$MinusInfinity-Ordinal) or a #$Quantity (such as an infinite distance or an infinite duration).")
(comment LinearObjectTypeByBoundedness "A #$SecondOrderCollection that facets  and partitions (see #$facets-Partition) the collection #$LinearObject (q.v.), according to how many end-points a linear object has.  A linear object can have zero, one, or two end-points.  Thus, the instances of #$LinearObjectTypeByBoundedness are #$LinearObject-Unbounded, #$LinearObject-SemiBounded, and #$LinearObject-Bounded.")
(disjointWith InfiniteScalarInterval RealNumber)
(comment VectorBorneInfection "The collection of all #$Infections that are commonly spread by vector organisms. Vectors are carriers of the pathogen.")
(comment basicDataForPlace "A #$BasicDataPredicate that relates a #$Place to a #$List of three instances of #$Set-Mathematical.  (#$basicDataForPlace PLACE (#$TheList NAME-SET COL-SET LOC-SET)) means that:
<p>
a) NAME-SET is a set of name strings for PLACE.
<p>
b) COL-SET is a set of collections of which PLACE is an instance.
<p>
c) LOC-SET is a set of locations LOC such that (#$underspecifiedLocation PLACE LOC).")
(comment deadPhysicalRemains "This is the predicate corresponding to #$DeadPhysicalRemainsFn (q.v.).")
(comment basicDataForOrg "A #$BasicDataPredicate that relates an #$Organization to a #$List of three instances of #$Set-Mathematical.  (#$basicDataForOrg ORG (#$TheList NAME-SET COL-SET REGION-SET)) means that:
<p>
a) NAME-SET is a set of name strings for ORG.
<p>
b) COL-SET is a set of collections of which ORG is an instance.
<p>
c) REGION-SET is a set of regions REG such that (#$operatesInRegion ORG REG).")
(comment LinearObject-Infinite "A specialization of #$LinearObject and #$InfiniteSpatialThing (qq.v.).  #$LinearObject-Infinite is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that are infinite in length.  They might be straight or curved, intangible or (at least in principle) tangible.  
<p>
An important specialization of #$LinearObject-Infinite is #$Line, whose instances are strictly one-dimensional.
<p>
Cf. #$LinearObject-Finite.")
(comment SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of surfaces that have an infintely long edge and no closed boundaries (such as a perimeter or hole).  More precisely, a surface is an instance of #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded if and only if (i) it has a boundary; (ii) its (total) boundary is -- or has a part that is -- spatially-continuous, infinite, and unbounded; and (iii) it is <i>not</i> an instance of #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded (cf.)  A semi-bounded surface might be intangible or (at least in principle) tangible.
<p>
For example, a #$HalfPlane is a semi-bounded surface whose boundary is a #$Line-Straight.  Also, cf. #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded.")
(comment SurfaceRegion-Bounded "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of tangible and intangible surfaces that have some closed boundary, such as a perimeter or hole.  More precisely, a surface is an instance of #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded if and only if it has a boundary, and its (total) boundary is -- or has a part that is -- a finite, unbounded, and  spatially-continuous (see #$ClosedLinearObject).  
<p>
For example, a #$Circle is a bounded surface; and so is the skin of an apple with a bite taken out of it.  Conversely, a #$Plane and intact apple skin are both  instances of #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded.  Also cf. #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded.")
(comment SurfaceRegion-Unbounded "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of surfaces that have <i>no boundary</i> whatsoever: no perimeter, hole, or edge.  An unbounded surface might be finite or infinite, tangible or intangible.
<p>
For example, a #$Plane is an unbounded, infinite, intangible surface.  And an intact apple skin is an unbounded, finite, tangible surface.  Conversely, a #$HalfPlane is a #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded (cf.), and an apple skin with a bite taken out is a #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded (cf.).")
(comment SurfaceRegion-Tangible "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion and #$PartiallyTangible.  This is the collection of all physical (and thus technically three-dimensional) surfaces, i.e. tangible surfaces of tangible objects.  Each instance of #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible has a (non-zero) thickness that is much less than its average length or width.  A tangible surface region might have holes, tears, or be unconnected and in multiple pieces; it might be flat, curved, or crumpled.  Examples include the skin of an apple and the top side of a tabletop. 
<p>
Cf. #$Surface, whose instances are two-dimensional, intangible regions of space.")
(comment titlesConventionallyQuoted "(#$titlesConventionallyQuoted WORK) means that titles of WORK (cf. #$titleOfWork) are generally quoted in written references.")
(comment performsActsOfTypeInLocation-Number "(#$performsActsOfTypeInLocation-Number AGENT ACTION-TYPE LOCATION NUM) holds just in case NUM of the instances of ACTION-TYPE that AGENT is known by Cyc to perform are performed in (#$eventOccursAt) location.  For instance, if 34 of the instances of #$TerroristAttack that Hamas is known to perform are performed in #$Israel, then (#$performsActsOfTypeInLocation-Number #$TerroristOrganization-Hamas #$TerroristAttack #$Israel 34) holds.")
(comment LinearObject "A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing. This is the collection of all lines, broadly construed.  Unlike its specialization #$Line (q.v.), this collection's instances need not actually be #$OneDimensionalThings, but may instead be simply near approximations of such (see e.g. #$LineOfDemarcationForSportsArea).  #$LinearObjects can be tangible or intangible, straight or curved.
<p>
Other specializations of this collection are #$LineSegment, #$LinearObject-Straight, and #$LinearObject-Tangible.")
(comment LinearObject-Tangible "The \"collection-intersection\" of #$LinearObject and #$PartiallyTangible (qq.v.). #$LinearObject-Tangible is the collection of all and only tangible, linearly-shaped objects.  They might be straight or curved, bounded or unbounded, finite or (at least in principle) infinite.  All tangible linear objects are in fact three-dimensional.
<p>
In certain contexts, a long and thin tangible object or region might be treated as a line.  For example, a wire might serve as a line in a communication network; a river might demarcate the boundary between two countries; a line drawn in the sand might serve as a volleyball court boundary.
<p>
Important specializations of #$LinearObject-Tangible are #$CordlikeObject and #$EdgeOnObject.")
(comment Point-Empirical "A specialization of both #$Point and #$SpaceRegion-Empirical (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Point-Empirical is a zero-dimensional object that belongs to, and thus has a fixed location in, the embedding space of the empirical universe (see #$TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical).  
<p>
Examples include the location of the center of mass of the Milky Way galaxy at the beginning of the 20th Century.  Note that empirical space points are embedded in time; if time is not a significant parameter (i.e. if in an atemporal or temporally agnostic context) consider using #$Point instead.
<p>
See also #$LineSegment-Empirical, #$Surface-Empirical, and #$SpaceChunk-Empirical.")
(comment LineSegment-Empirical "A specialization of both #$LineSegment and #$SpaceRegion-Empirical (qq.v.).  Instances of #$LineSegment-Empirical are one-dimensional, continuous line-segments in the space in the empirical universe (see #$TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical).  
<p>
For space-lines located on the Earth's surface, see the specialization #$GeographicalPlace-1D.
<p>
See also #$Point-Empirical, #$Surface-Empirical, and #$SpaceChunk-Empirical.")
(comment Surface-Empirical "A specialization of #$Surface and #$SpaceRegion-Empirical (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Surface-Empirical is a two-dimensional, continuous portion of #$TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical (q.v.).  
<p>
Note that a physical object, being three-dimensional, can only be partially located at a #$Surface-Empirical.  An empirical space surface might <i>intersect</i> the space occupied by a physical object, but is never #$cospatial with it.  (Cf. #$SpaceChunk-Empirical.)  The only objects properly localizable at instances of #$Surface-Empirical are intangible objects of two or less dimensions.  For example, the intangible surface that delimits (a) the space occupied by a table and (b) the space above the top surface of the table is an empirical space-surface.
<p>
See also #$LineSegment-Empirical and #$Point-Empirical.")
(comment GeographicalLine-Intangible "A specialization of both #$GeographicalThing-Intangible and #$LineSegment (qq.v.).  This is the collection of one-dimensional intangible geographical lines, fixed-location or otherwise.  Its specializations include #$LatitudeLine, #$LineOfMilitaryForces, and #$GeopoliticalBorder.")
(comment ratioOfCollectionToDifferentiatedCollection "(#$ratioOfCollectionToDifferentiatedCollection MOD COLL DIFFERENTIATOR-COLL NUM) holds just in case the ratio/percent/number of instances of COLL that also are instances of DIFFERENTIATOR-COLL is NUM.  MOD tells the inference engine what format to return the answer -- either a ratio, percent, or non-negative integer.  DIFFERENTIATOR-COLL is a collection that specifies the additional condition -- usually constructed via some subcollection function.  For example, If we wanted to ask what is the percentage of bombings performed by Hamas that are terrorist suicide bombings we would use the following formula:
<pre>
(#$ratioOfCollectionToDifferentiatedCollection #$Percent
 (#$CollectionSubsetFn #$TerroristAttack 
  (#$TheSetOf ?ACT
    (#$and
    (#$performedBy ?ACT #$TerroristOrganization-Hamas)
    (#$isa ?ACT #$Bombing))))
      #$TerroristSuicideBombing  ?NUM)
<pre>
.")
(comment GeographicalPlace-1D "A specialization of #$GeographicalLine-Intangible and #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.).  Instances of #$GeographicalPlace-1D are one-dimensional portions of the geographical surface of reference.  They can be used to indicate the (partial) geographical location of two- or three-dimensional things within the context of a geography (see #$locatedAtPoint-SurfaceGeographical).  They can also be used to indicate the  locations of borders (or portions of borders) and other types of demarcations.  The two main specializations of this collection are #$LongitudeLine and #$LatitudeLine.")
(comment GeographicalPlace-2D "A subcollection of #$GeographicalArea-Intangible and #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.). Instances of
#$GeographicalPlace-2D are two dimensional portions of the geographical surface of reference. They can be used to indicate the geographical location of two or three dimensional things within the context of a geography. See #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment GeographicalPlace "The #$collectionIntersection of #$GeographicalThing and #$Place (q.v.).")
(comment computerUserWithUserModelForApplication "(#$computerUserWithUserModelForApplication PROG USER-TYPE) means that USER-TYPE is the specialization of #$ComputerUserWithUserModel which is the collection of copmuter users modeled in Cyc as users of the application PROG.")
(comment ComputerUserWithUserModelForApplicationFn "(#$ComputerUserWithUserModelForApplicationFn PROG) denotes the specialization of #$ComputerUserWithUserModel which is the collection of computer users modeled in Cyc as users of the application PROG.")
(comment basicDataForEvent "A #$BasicDataPredicate that relates an #$Event to a #$List of three instances of #$Set-Mathematical.  (#$basicDataForEvent EVENT (#$TheList COL-SET DATE-SET LOC-SET)) means that:
<p>
a) COL-SET is a set of collections of which EVENT is an instance.
<p>
b) DATE-SET is a set of dates DATE such that (#$dateOfEvent EVENT DATE).
<p>
c) LOC-SET is a set of locations LOC such that (#$eventOccursAt EVENT LOC).")
(comment SurfaceRegion-Infinite "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion and #$InfiniteSpatialThing (qq.v.).  #$SurfaceRegion-Infinite is the collection of all and only surface regions that are infinitely large in at least one dimension.  They might be straight or curved, intangible or (at least in principle) tangible.  
<p>
Important specializations of #$SurfaceRegion-Infinite are #$Plane and #$HalfPlane, both of whose instances are strictly two-dimensional and infinite in two dimensions.  Another type of infinite surface is an infinitely long 2-D \"stripe\", which is infinite in one dimension.
<p>
Cf. #$SurfaceRegion-Finite.")
(comment SurfaceOnTangibleObject "A specialization of both #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible and #$SheetOfSomeStuff.  Each instance of #$SurfaceOnTangibleObject is a sheet-like surface (that is, a surface with one dimension significantly smaller than the other two) of a partially tangible object.  Such a surface is neither unconnected nor in multiple pieces.  Furthermore, the properties of such a surface differ noticeably from the properties of the partially tangible object covered by the surface. So a veneer surface of a table would be an instance of #$SurfaceOnTangibleObject, while the surface of a wooden table with no lining would not.  Further positive examples include carpet that is part of a floor, paint on a wall, the earth's crust, and the crust on a piece of bread.")
(comment SurfaceRegion-Finite "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion (q.v.). #$SurfaceRegion-Finite is the collection of all and only those surface regions  that are finite in both of their (salient) dimensions.  They might be flat or curved, tangible or intangible, bounded (e.g. a rectangle) or unbounded (e.g. a spheriod).
<p>
Cf. #$SurfaceRegion-Infinite.")
(comment Surface "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalThing, #$ConnectedSpaceRegion and #$SurfaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Surface is a two-dimensional portion of space, which might be flat or embeddable in a three-dimensional space.  It makes sense to speak of, or compute, the <i>area</i> of these regions. 
<p>
For example, the space occupied by a flat surface belongs to this collection. Conversely, the space occupied by a solid chunk of matter (such as a sheet of rice paper) is not a space-surface, but is instead a #$SpaceChunk (cf.).
<p>
An important specialization of #$Surface is #$Surface-Empirical, whose instances are 2-D pieces of space in the empirical universe.
<p>
Also cf. #$Line and #$Point.")
(disjointWith LinearObject-TwoDimensional SurfaceRegion-Unbounded)
(disjointWith SurfaceRegion-Infinite SurfaceRegion-Finite)
(disjointWith SurfaceRegion-Bounded SurfaceRegion-Unbounded)
(disjointWith SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded SurfaceRegion-Bounded)
(disjointWith SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded SurfaceRegion-Unbounded)
(comment FlatSurfaceRegion "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion.  Each instance of #$FlatSurfaceRegion is an (at least roughly) two-dimensional connected object that is (perfectly or nearly) flat.  Examples of flat surfaces include tangible objects like the top of a kitchen counter, and intangibles such as #$Planes.")
(comment HalfPlane "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing, #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded, and #$FlatSurfaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$HalfPlane is an semi-bounded portion of a #$Plane, having a single straight line as a boundary.  A straight line cuts any plane in which it lies into two half-planes.")
(comment Plane "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing, #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded and #$FlatSurfaceRegion (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all geometric planes.  Each instance of #$Plane is an unbounded, perfectly flat, two-dimensional region of space.  See also #$HalfPlane.")
(comment Resurgence "A sub-collection of #$Improvement-Transformation. Each instance is bringing something again into activity and prominence.")
(comment MathematicalStatement "A sub-collection of #$Statement. Each instance is a statement of a mathematical relation.")
(comment Equation-MathematicalStatement "A sub-collection of #$MathematicalStatement. Each instance is a mathematical statement that two expressions are equal.")
(comment DifferentialEquation "A sub-collection of #$Equation-MathematicalStatement. Each instance is an equation containing differentials of a function.")
(comment AngularPosition-SpatialPredicate "A sub-collection of #$SpatialPredicate. Each instance is a relation by which any position with respect to any other position is established.")
(comment Notation-CommunicationConvention "A sub-collection of #$CommunicationConvention. Each instance is a technical system of symbols used to represent special things.")
(comment Choreography-Notation "A sub-collection of #$Notation-CommunicationConvention. Each instance is a notation used by choreographers.")
(comment Reich "A sub-collection of #$GeopoliticalEntity. Each instance is a German state.")
(comment SamplingFrequency "A sub-collection of #$Frequency. Each instance is the frequency of sampling a continuously varying signal.")
(comment ObservanceDay "A sub-collection of #$ScheduledAction. Each instance is a day assigned to a particular purpose or observance.")
(comment VictoryDay "A sub-collection of #$ObservanceDay. Each instance is the day of a victory.")
(comment TalmudicLiterature "A sub-collection of #$SacredText. Each instance is an ancient rabbinical writing.")
(comment Passage-Chapter-PCW "A sub-collection of #$Chapter-PCW. Each instance is a section of text; particularly a section of medium length.")
(comment Excerpt-PCW "A sub-collection of #$Passage-Chapter-PCW. Each instance is a passage selected from a larger conceptual work.")
(comment AristocraticFamilyLine "A sub-collection of #$Family-SocialEntity. Each instance is an aristocratic family line.")
(comment (PlayerOfInstrumentFn Clarinet) "A sub-collection of #$MusicalInstrumentPlayer. Each instance is a musician who plays the clarinet.")
(comment CoefficientOfElasticity "A sub-collection of #$Modulus-Coefficient. Each instance is a ratio of the applied stress to the change in shape of an elastic body.")
(comment NonGovernmentalOrganization "A sub-collection of #$Organization. Each instance is an organization that is not part of the local or state or federal government.")
(comment relationInvokesTypeDerivatively "The most general predicate for relating a relation RELATION to a collection COLL that the relation invokes, where \"invokes\" has a very specific definition.  <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively RELATION COLL)</code> iff either one of two conditions holds.  <p><i>First</i>: RELATION has an explicit #$argIsa or #$argGenl constraint to a specialization SPEC of COLL; this, by convention, is an <i>explicit</i> (albeit derived) invocation of COLL by RELATION.  For example, since <code>(#$arg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code>, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds, since #$actionMotives has an explicit #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and <code>(#$genls #$Motive #$Motive)</code>.  Similarly, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> given the #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and the fact that <code>(#$genls #$Motive #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> holds.</p>  <p><i>Second</i>: RELATION is an instance of #$Predicate that, by its very definition, is such that from a closed sentence of the form, (RELATION . ARGS) one can conclude for some predicate PREDICATE (PREDICATE . PRED-ARGS) where there is some argument ARG in PRED-ARGS that is either an instance or a specialization of COLL.  For example, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$agentHasMotiveForAction #$Motive)</code> and <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$agentHasMotiveForAction #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> are both true, not because #$agentHasMotiveForAction has an explicit argument constraint of #$Motive, but because by the very definition of #$agentHasMotiveForAction, <code>(#$agentHasMotiveForAction AGENT ACTION)</code> <i>means</i> that there exists an instance MOTIVE of #$Motive such that AGENT has MOTIVE in performing ACTION.  This, by convention, is an <i>implicit</i> invocation of COLL.  Typically, in the case of implicit invocation, RELATION will be an instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.")
(comment relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively "The most general predicate for relating a relation RELATION to a collection COLL that the relation explicity and directly invokes, where \"explicitly and directly invokes\" has a very specific definition.  <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively RELATION COLL)</code> iff  RELATION has an explicit #$argIsa or #$argGenl constraint to a specialization SPEC of COLL.  For example, since 
<pre>
   (#$arg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</pre> and
<pre>
   (#$genls #$Motive #$Motive)</pre> both hold, 
<pre>
   (#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$Motive)</pre> holds.  Similarly,
<pre>
   (#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</pre> follows from the #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and the fact that 
<pre>
   (#$genls #$Motive #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</pre> holds.")
(comment nearestArgIsa "A specialization of #$argIsa (q.v.).  <code>(#$nearestArgIsa RELATION NUMBER COL)</code> means that not only is it the case that
<pre>
   (#$argIsa RELATION NUMBER COL)</pre> but also
<pre>
   (#$unknownSentence
     (#$thereExists ?GENL
        (#$and
           (#$argIsa RELATION NUMBER ?GENL)
           (#$different ?GENL COL)
           (#$genls ?GENL COL)))).</pre>  In other words, COL is a \"nearest\" -- i.e., non-subsuming -- #$argIsa constraint for RELATION in the NUMBER-th place.")
(comment nearestArg1Isa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg1Isa  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgIsa RELATION 1 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg3Isa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg3Isa  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgIsa RELATION 3 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg2Isa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg2Isa  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as  
  (#$nearestArgIsa RELATION 2 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg4Isa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg4Isa  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgIsa RELATION 4 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg5Isa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg5Isa  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgIsa RELATION 5 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArgGenl "A specialization of #$argGenl (q.v.).  <code>(#$nearestArgGenl RELATION NUMBER COL)</code> means that not only is it the case that
<pre>
   (#$argGenl RELATION NUMBER COL)</pre> but also
<pre>
   (#$unknownSentence
     (#$thereExists ?GENL
        (#$and
           (#$argGenl RELATION NUMBER ?GENL)
           (#$different ?GENL COL)
           (#$genls ?GENL COL)))).</pre>  In other words, COL is a \"nearest\" -- i.e., non-subsuming -- #$argGenl constraint for RELATION in the NUMBER-th place.")
(comment nearestArg1Genl "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg1Genl  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgGenl RELATION 1 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg2Genl "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg2Genl  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgGenl RELATION 2 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg3Genl "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg3Genl  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgGenl RELATION 3 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg4Genl "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg4Genl  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgGenl RELATION 4 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg5Genl "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg5Genl  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgGenl RELATION 5 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg6Isa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg6Isa  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgIsa RELATION 6 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg6Genl "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg6Genl  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgGenl RELATION 6 TYPE)).")
(comment nearestArg7Isa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  (#$nearestArg7Isa  RELATION TYPE) means the same thing as 
  (#$nearestArgIsa RELATION 7 TYPE)).")
(comment HTMLDivisionFn "(#$HTMLDivisionFn OPTIONS . CONTENT) denotes the #$HTMLSource for displaying the division whose content is CONTENT.")
(comment ConstructedCycLQuerySpecification "A #$FirstOrderCollection and sub-collection of #$CycLQuerySpecification, instances of #$ConstructedCycLQuerySpecification are minimally specified queries that are inferred by Cyc in response to it's knowledge of a user's interests and background (i.e., the user's current task).  Each instance of this collection has the default set of properties given by the Query Tool as of May, 2005.")
(comment Motorcade "A specialization of #$Convoy.  Each instance of #$Motorcade is a group of transportation vehicles that are travelling together (usually in a line) for some purpose other than simple cargo delivery.  Usually, a motorcade is provided to diplomats and government officials to safely convey them from one point to another when there are security concerns.")
(disjointWith DeemingAConceptIrrelevant DeemingAConceptRelevant)
(comment Tropic-LatitudeLine "A sub-collection of #$LatitudeLine. Each instance is either of two parallels of latitude about 23.5 degrees north and south of the equator representing the points farthest north and south at which the sun can shine directly overhead and constituting the boundaries of the torrid zone or tropics.")
(comment PolarCircle-LatitudeLine "A sub-collection of #$LatitudeLine. Each instance is a line of latitude at the north or south poles.")
(comment Eon-LongTime "A sub-collection of #$LongTime. Each instance is an immeasurably long period of time.")
(comment Charter "A sub-collection of #$OfficialDocument. Each instance is a document incorporating an institution and specifying its rights; includes the articles of incorporation and the certificate of incorporation.")
(comment RoyalCharter "A sub-collection of #$Charter. Each instance is a charter granted by the sovereign (especially in Great Britain).")
(comment MathematicalNotation "A sub-collection of #$Notation-CommunicationConvention. Each instance is a notation used by mathematicians.")
(comment ParenthesisFreeNotation "A sub-collection of #$MathematicalNotation. Each instance is a notation for forming mathematical expressions that does not use parentheses to delimit components.")
(comment BishopsCenterOfAuthority "A sub-collection of #$CenterOfAuthority-GeographicalRegion. Each instance is the seat within a bishop's diocese where his cathedral is located.")
(comment SamplingRate-Frequency "A sub-collection of #$Frequency. Each instance is a frequency of sampling per unit time.")
(comment QuantumMechanics "An instance of #$FieldOfStudy. It is the branch of physics based on quantum theory.")
(comment QuantumFieldTheory "An instance of #$FieldOfStudy. It is the branch of quantum physics that is concerned with the theory of fields; it was motivated by the question of how an atom radiates light as its electrons jump from excited states.")
(comment Elite "A sub-collection of #$UpperClass. Each instance is a group or class of persons enjoying superior intellectual or social or economic status.")
(comment Nobility "A sub-collection of #$Elite. Each instance is a privileged class holding hereditary titles.")
(comment Levy-Charging "A sub-collection of #$Charging-DocumentingResponsibilityForPayment. Each instance is a charge imposed and collected.")
(comment IndirectTax-COC "A sub-collection of #$Tax-COC. Each instance is a tax levied on goods or services rather than on persons or organizations.")
(comment ExciseTax-COC "A sub-collection of #$IndirectTax-COC. Each instance is a tax that is measured by the amount of business done (not on property or income from real estate).")
(comment Amendment-PCW "A sub-collection of #$TextualPCW. Each instance is a statement that is added to or revises or improves a proposal or document (a bill or constitution etc.).")
(comment Gospel "A sub-collection of #$SacredText. Each instance is one of four books in the New Testament that tell the story of Christ's life and teachings.")
(comment Infallibility "A sub-collection of #$Dependable-Personality. Each instance is the quality of never making an error.")
(comment Cuneiform "A sub-collection of #$WritingSystem. Each instance is an ancient wedge-shaped script used in Mesopotamia and Persia.")
(comment Regent-HeadOfState "A sub-collection of #$HeadOfState. Each instance is someone who rules during the absence or incapacity or minority of the country's monarch.")
(comment Queen-WifeOfAKing "A sub-collection of #$FemaleAristocrat. Each instance is the wife of a king, but not a head of state.")
(comment Questionnaire-CW "A sub-collection of #$PropositionalConceptualWork. Each instance is a form containing a set of questions; submitted to people to gain statistical information.")
(comment PersonalityInventory-CW "A sub-collection of #$Questionnaire-CW. Each instance is a questionnaire that is supposed to yield a description of a person's personality traits.")
(comment KnowHow-AgentCapacityQuantity "A sub-collection of #$AgentCapacityQuantity. Each instance is the knowledge and skill required to do something.")
(comment TeachingMethod "A sub-collection of #$TechniqueType. Each instance is a type of action in which principles and methods of instruction are used.")
(comment DistressSignal-IBT "A sub-collection of #$InformationBearingThing. Each instance is an internationally recognized signal sent out by a ship or plane indicating that help is needed.")
(comment DistressSignal-PCW "A sub-collection of #$PropositionalConceptualWork. Each instance is an internationally recognized signal sent out by a ship or plane indicating that help is needed.")
(comment factSheetTermDepictionHTML "(#$factSheetTermDepictionHTML TERM HTML) relates a fact-sheet topic to the #$HTMLSource that is to be used in a depiction of it in the fact sheet.")
(comment predExpansionListForType "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  #$predExpansionListForType relates a predicate PREDICATE to a collection TYPE and a list LIST of binary predicates such that: 1) Each member MEMBER-1 through MEMBER-N of LIST has an #$arg1Isa that is at least as general as TYPE; 2) The placement of MEMBER-M in list as its Mth member indicates that the #$arg2Isa of MEMBER is consistent with the argMthIsa of PREDICATE; a conjunction of the form,
<pre>
  (#$and
    (#$isa ?TYPE-INS TYPE)
    (MEMBER-1 ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-1)
       .
       .
       .
    (MEMBER-N ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-N))</pre>
entails a sentence of the form,
<pre>
  (PREDICATE ARG-2-MEMBER-1 ... ARG-2-MEMBER-N);</pre>
and 3) from 
<pre>
  (PREDICATE ARG-2-MEMBER-1 ... ARG-2-MEMBER-N)</pre>
a sentence of the form,
<pre>
  (#$thereExists ?TYPE-INS
    (#$and
      (#$isa ?TYPE-INS TYPE)
      (MEMBER-1 ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-1)
         .
         .
         .
      (MEMBER-N ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-N)))</pre>
follows.  Thus <code>(#$predExpansionListForType TYPE LIST PREDICATE)</code> means that PREDICATE is fully axiomatizable -- rules giving necessary and sufficient conditions for its application can be authored -- in the way indicated above.  An important specialization of #$predExpansionListForType is #$nonDavidsonianPredReln, which allows one to define a non-davidsonian predicate from a series of binary situation predicates and a given situation-type.")
(comment modelMtForTask "The corresponding predicate of the function #$TaskModelMtFn.  An assertion for the form (#$modelMtForTask MT TASK) states that the #$Microtheory MT serves to hold information that is relevant to the perfovrmance of the #$Allotment TASK.")
(comment InanimateObject "A subcollection of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$InanimateObject is an (at least partly) tangible thing that is not currently a living structure. Things that were never alive, dead organisms, and dead (or completely non-functioning) organism parts are included in this collection.  Examples: #$YaleUniversity, a piece of #$Meat, a dead armadillo, the #$StatueOfLiberty, and a pile of #$Sawdust.  Two important specializations of this collection are #$InanimateObject-Natural and #$InanimateObject-NonNatural.")
(comment relationInvokesType "The most general predicate for relating a relation RELATION to a collection COLL that the relation directly invokes, where \"directly invokes\" has a very specific definition.  <code>(#$relationInvokesType RELATION COLL)</code> iff either one of two conditions holds.  <p><i>First</i>: RELATION has a #$nearestArgIsa or #$nearestArgGenl constraint to COLL; this, by convention, is an <i>explicit</i> invocation of COLL by RELATION.  For example, since <code>(#$nearestArg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code>, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds, since #$actionMotives has an explicit #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive.</p>  <p><i>Second</i>: RELATION is an instance of #$Predicate that, by its very definition, is such that from a closed sentence of the form, (RELATION . ARGS) one can conclude for some predicate PREDICATE (PREDICATE . PRED-ARGS) where PREDICATE has either an #$argIsa or #$argGenl of COLL.  For example, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$agentHasMotiveForAction #$Motive)</code> is true, not because #$agentHasMotiveForAction has an explicit argument constraint of #$Motive, but because by the very definition of #$agentHasMotiveForAction, <code>(#$agentHasMotiveForAction AGENT ACTION)</code> <i>means</i> that there exists MOTIVE, such that (#$agentsActionHasMotive AGENT ACTION MOTIVE), where <code>(#$isa MOTIVE #$Motive)</code> due to the fact that <code>(#$arg3Isa #$agentsActionHasMotive #$Motive)</code>.  This, by convention, is an <i>implicit</i> invocation of COLL.  Typically, in the case of implicit invocation, RELATION will be an instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.")
(comment relationExplicitlyInvokesType "<code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType RELATION COLL)</code> means that RELATION has a #$nearestArgIsa or #$nearestArgGenl constraint to COLL, and that where that explicit constraint holds, there is not a more specific explicit constraint.  For example, since 
<code>(#$nearestArg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds.")
(comment relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgIsa "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgIsa RELATION COLL)</code> means that RELATION has a #$nearestArgIsa constraint of COLL.  For example, since 
<code>(#$nearestArg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds.")
(comment relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgGenl "<code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgGenl RELATION COLL)</code> means that RELATION has a #$nearestArgGenl constraint of COLL.  For example, since 
<code>(#$nearestArg2Genl #$addictedTo #$AddictiveSubstance)</code> holds, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgGenl #$addictedTo #$AddictiveSubstance)</code> holds.")
(comment relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgGenl "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgGenl RELATION COLL)</code> means that RELATION has a #$nearestArgGenl constraint of COLL.  For example, since 
<code>(#$nearestArg2Genl #$addictedTo #$AddictiveSubstance)</code> holds, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesType-ArgGenl #$addictedTo #$AddictiveSubstance)</code> holds.")
(comment NonDavidsonianPredFn "For a given #$PositiveInteger n, (#$NonDavidsonianPredFn EVENTTYPE (#$TheList SLOT_1 ... SLOT_n)) denotes the n-ary #$EventPredicate corresponding, in some sense, to the collection EVENTTYPE. For instance: (#$NonDavidsonianPredFn #$Cleaning (#$TheList #$performedBy #$objectActedOn)) corresponds to the #$ActionPredicate #$cleans (which is binary). Hence ((#$NonDavidsonianPredFn #$Cleaning (#$TheList #$performedBy #$objectActedOn)) Pierre PierresKitchen) is an #$CycLSentence-Assertible that is true at a time interval t iff Pierre cleans his kitchen at t. NOTE: the accepted OE practice is to use #$EventPredicates as primitively defined constants; use of this function should be limited to special cases in which the creation of a suitable action predicate is not justified by considerations of reusability, sufficient generality, etc.")
(comment relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgIsa "<code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgIsa RELATION COLL)</code>  means that RELATION has an explicit #$argIsa constraint to a specialization SPEC of COLL.  For example, since <code>(#$arg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code>, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgIsa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds, since #$actionMotives has an explicit #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and <code>(#$genls #$Motive #$Motive)</code>.  Similarly, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgIsa #$actionMotives #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> given the #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and the fact that <code>(#$genls #$Motive #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> holds.")
(comment relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgGenl "<code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgGenl RELATION COLL)</code>  means that RELATION has an #$argGenl constraint of COLL.  For example, since <code>(#$arg2Genl #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code>, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgGenl #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code>.  Similarly, since #$arg2Genl is transitive in its second argument via #$genls and #$Motive is a specialization of #$IntentionalMentalSituation, <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively-ArgIsa #$actionMotives #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> also holds.")
(comment ClosedSurfaceRegion "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded and  #$SurfaceRegion-Finite (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ClosedSurfaceRegion is a finite surface region with no boundary (i.e. no perimeter, edge, or hole).  
<p>
For example, the total surface of a billiard ball or a cube is a closed surface region.
<p>
Specializations of #$ClosedSurfaceRegion include #$Ellipsoid.")
(comment SurfaceRegion "A specialization of #$PolyDimensionalThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of all spatial surfaces, tangible or intangible (see the specializations #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible and #$Surface).  Each instance of #$SurfaceRegion is a spatial thing that has extent in at least two dimensions, and either has no thickness (i.e. is a purely two-dimensional object) or has an insignificant thickness compared to its length and width.  (If it is a closed surface, e.g. an apple skin, then any significant subregion of it must have insignificant thickness compared to that subregion's length and width.)  In sum, a surface region might be two- or three-dimensional; tangible or intangible; simply-connected or not; it might be flat, curved, folded, or crumpled.  
<p>
Other instances of #$SurfaceRegion are the skin of a basketball, the face-up side of a table top, and a particular face of a cube-shaped space region.  Other specializations are this collection include #$FlatSurfaceRegion, #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded, and #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded.")
(comment ConvexHullFn "A unary function that takes a given two- or three-dimensional spatial thing to its intangible \"convex hull\".  Let <b>HULL</b> = (#$ConvexHullFn <b>OBJECT</b>).  If <b>OBJECT</b> is a #$TwoDimensionalThing, then <b>HULL</b> is the entire one-dimensional boundary (see #$ClosedLinearObject) of the minimal convex 2-D space region that #$spatiallySubsumes <b>OBJECT</b> (see #$ConvexSpaceRegion and #$Surface).  If <b>OBJECT</b> is a #$ThreeDimensionalThing, then <b>HULL</b> is the entire closed two-dimensional surface (see #$ClosedSurfaceRegion) of the minimal convex 3-D space region that subsumes <b>OBJECT</b>.  
<p>
Note that the dimensionality of <b>HULL</b> is always one less than that of <b>OBJECT</b>.  Also note that, while <b>OBJECT</b> might or might not be a #$SpatiallyContinuousThing, <b>HULL</b> is spatially-continuous in either case.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$convexHullOf.  To represent the whole convex space region bounded by a given object's convex hull, use #$MinimalSubsumingConvexSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment SpatiallySemiBoundedThing "A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of self-connected (i.e. one-piece) spatial things that have a boundary, but are not fully-bounded geometrically.  In the case of a line, this means having exactly one end-point (see #$LinearObject-SemiBounded).  In the case of a surface, this means having a perimeter (or edge), but not a closed perimeter (see #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded).  In the case of a solid, this means having a surface, but not a closed surface (e.g. an infinitely long beam). 
<p>
Cf. #$SpatiallyBoundedThing and #$SpatiallyUnboundedThing.")
(comment agentHasMotiveForAction-Ternary "(#$agentHasMotiveForAction-Ternary AGENT ACT MOTIVE) means that the IntelligentAgent AGENT has the motive MOTIVE to see that Action ACT occurrs.  To say that AGENT has acted on MOTIVE so as to be the performer of ACT, use the more specific #$agentsActionHasMotive.")
(comment SpatiallyBoundedThing "A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of self-connected (i.e. one-piece) spatial things that are fully bounded geometrically.  In the case of a line, this means having two end-points (see #$LinearObject-Bounded).  In the case of a surface, this means having a perimeter or hole (see #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded).  In the case of a solid, this means having a closed external surface or an internal cavity.
<p>
Since every known tangible body is spatially-bounded, the intersection of #$PartiallyTangible and #$SpatiallyContinuousThing is asserted to be a specialization of #$SpatiallyBoundedThing in the #$NaivePhysicsMt. It is not asserted in any \"top\" microtheory (such as the #$UniversalVocabularyMt), however, so as not to rule out <i>a priori</i> the possibility of a plenum universe (which is what the ancient Greek philosopher #$Parmenides, for one, believed of the actual universe).
<p>
Cf. #$SpatiallyUnboundedThing and #$SpatiallySemiBoundedThing.")
(comment SpatiallyUnboundedThing "A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of self-connected (i.e. one-piece) spatial things that have no geometrical boundary.  In the case of a line, this means having no end-points (see #$LinearObject-Unbounded).  In the case of a surface, this means having no perimeter, edges, or holes (see #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded).  In the case of a solid, this means having no (internal or external) surface whatsoever -- which effectively means being hole-free and infinitely extended in all directions.
<p>
Cf. #$SpatiallyBoundedThing and #$SpatiallySemiBoundedThing.")
(comment standingInRelation "This is the predicate corresponding to #$StandingInRelationFn (q.v.).")
(comment NamingSomething "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$PurposefulAction. In each #$NamingSomething, someone assigns a name or title to something.")
(comment ratioOfSetToDifferentiatedSet "This is exactly the same as #$ratioOfCollectionToDifferentiatedCollection except it takes sets as its second and third argument
.")
(comment polygonDefinedByVerticesList "(#$polygonDefinedByVerticesList POLYGON LIST-OF-POINTS) means that POLYGON is the instance of #$PolygonalRegion which vertices are the POINT-i, with 1<i<N, in the same order as that of the elements of the LIST-OF-POINTS. Note that the order of the element in the list assumes counter-clockwise orientation.See the corresponding function #$VertexListToPolygonalRegionFn.")
(comment VertexListToPolygonFn "(#$VertexListToPolygonFn LIST-OF-POINTS) denotes the instance of #$PolygonalRegion which vertices are the POINT-i, with 1<i<N, that are listed as elements of LIST-OF-POINTS. The order of the element in the list assumes counter-clockwise orientation. Note that it takes at list three points to make a polygonal region, therefore the length of LIST-OF-POINTS is at list three.  See the corresponding predicate #$polygonDefinedByVerticesList.")
(comment SemicircularRegion "A specialization of #$PlaneFigure (q.v.).  This is the collection of all semi-circular two-dimensional space regions.  Each instance of #$SemicircularRegion is half of a #$CircularRegion.")
(comment EllipticalRegion "A specialization of both #$PlaneFigure and #$RoundObject (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all two-dimensional space regions whose boundaries are #$Ellipses (q.v.).  Geometrically, an elliptical region is an two-dimensional region bounded by a closed curve that is the path of a point that moves so that the sum of its distances from two fixed points (called \"foci\") is constant.  
<p>
An important specialization of #$EllipticalRegion is #$CircularRegion, the collection of elliptical regions such that the distance between their boundaries' two foci is zero. 
<p>
Cf. #$Ellipsoid.")
(comment CircularRegion "A specialization of #$EllipticalRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of elliptical regions whose boundaries are #$Circles.  Geometrically, a #$CircularRegion is a plane figure bounded by a closed curve each point on which is equidistant from a single (\"center\") point.  Cf. #$Disc.")
(comment Triangle "A specialization of #$Polygon (q.v.).  This is the collection of all three-sided polygons.")
(comment RightTriangle "A specialization of #$Triangle (q.v.).  This is the collection of all triangles having two adjacent sides that form a right (i.e. 90 degree) angle.")
(comment PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalShapeType (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides is the collection of all #$Polygons (q.v.) that have the same particular number of sides (see #$numberOfEdges).  Instances include #$Triangle and #$Octagon.")
(comment Quadrilateral "A #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides and a specialization of #$Polygon (q.v.).  This is the collection of all four-sided polygons.")
(comment Parallelogram "A specialization of #$Quadrilateral (q.v.).  This is the collection of all four-sided polygons such that each side is parallel to its opposite side.  Specializations of #$Parallelogram include #$Rectangle and #$Rhombus.")
(comment Trapezoid "A specialization of #$Quadrilateral (q.v.).  A #$Trapezoid is a four-sided polygon that has exactly two parallel sides.")
(comment Rectangle "A specialization of #$Parallelogram and #$EquiangularPolygon.  This is the collection of all rectangles: four-sided polygons whose opposing sides are parallel and whose internal angles are all of equal measure (viz. 90 degrees).")
(comment Rhombus "A specialization of #$Parallelogram and #$EquilateralPolygon (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all rhombi: four-sided polygons whose opposing sides are parallel and whose sides are all of equal length.")
(comment Square "A specialization of #$Rectangle, #$Rhombus, and #$RegularPolygon (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Square is a four-sided polygon whose sides are all of equal length and whose internal angles are all of equal measure (viz. 90 degrees).")
(comment Rhomboid "A specialization of #$Parallelogram (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Rhomboid is a four-sided polygon whose opposing sides are parallel and whose adjacent sides are of <i>unequal</i> length.  Cf. #$Rhombus.")
(comment RegularPolygon "A specialization of #$Polygon. This is the collection of all two-dimensional polygons that are both #$EquiangularPolygons and #$EquilateralPolygons (qq.v.).  One specialization of this collection is #$Square. See also #$RegularPolygonTypeFn.")
(comment EquiangularPolygon "A specialization of #$Polygon (q.v.).  Each instance of #$EquiangularPolygon is a polygon whose internal angles are all of equal measure.  See also #$EquilateralPolygon and #$RegularPolygon.")
(comment RegularPolygonTypeFn "A unary function that takes a collection of all polygons having a given number of sides to the subcollection containing just the <i>regular</i> (i.e. equilateral and equiangular) polygons with that number of sides.  
<p>
Where <b>N-GON</b> is a #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.), (#$RegularPolygonTypeFn <b>N-GON</b>) is <b>REG-N-GON</b>: the specialization of <b>N-GON</b> whose instances are all and only the <b>N</b>-sided regular polygonal regions.  <b>REG-N-GON</b> is thus the \"intersection\" of <b>N-GON</b> and #$RegularPolygon (q.v.).  For example, (#$RegularPolygonTypeFn #$Quadrilateral) is #$Square.  
<p>
See also #$EquilateralPolygon and #$EquiangularPolygon.")
(comment Pentagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all five-sided polygons.")
(comment Hexagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all six-sided polygons.")
(comment Heptagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all seven-sided polygons.")
(comment Octagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all eight-sided polygons.")
(comment Nonagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all nine-sided polygons.")
(comment Decagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all ten-sided polygons.")
(comment Hendecagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all eleven-sided polygons.")
(comment Dodecagon "A specialization of #$Polygon and an instance of #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides (q.v.).  This is the collection of all twelve-sided polygons.")
(comment orthogonalArgGenlsWithLink "An expression of the form (#$orthogonalArgGenlsWithLink  PRED1 ARG1 PRED2 ARG2 COLL1 COLL2) means that the collection that is the #$argGenl value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th argument and the collection that is the #$argGenl value for the #$Predicate PRED2's ARG2th argument are such that neither is a sub-collection of the other and are they are not disjoint. Further, COLL is the #$CollectionIntersectionFn of those arguments' constraining collections.")
(comment orthogonalArgIsasWithLink "An expression of the form (#$orthogonalArgIsasWithLink  PRED1 ARG1 PRED2 ARG2 COLL1 COLL2) means that the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED1's ARG1th argument and the collection that is the #$argIsa value for the #$Predicate PRED2's ARG2th value are such that neither is a sub-collection of the other and are they are not disjoint.  Further, COLL is the #$CollectionIntersectionFn of their arguments' constraining collections.")
(comment predicateInputModesForDetermination "An assertion of the form (#$predicateInputModesForDetermination PRED N TYPE DET BIN-PRED) means that relative to the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination DET, the Nth argument of the #$Predicate PRED should bear the relationship BIN-PRED to TYPE.")
(comment FactSheetBuilderQueryFn "A unary #$ReifiableFunction that takes a #$Predicate PRED and returns the #$CycLQuerySpecification that is used to populate the section of a fact sheet organized under the heading of PRED.")
(comment LineString "A specialization of #$LineSegment (q.v.).  Each instance of #$LineString is a finite segment of line that is the sum of one or more straight line-segments, connected end-to-end, and not necessarily colinear with each other.  An important specialization of this collection is #$LinearRing.")
(disjointWith ClosedLinearObject LinearObject-Straight)
(comment Ellipse "A specialization of both #$ClosedLinearObject and #$LineSegment (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Ellipse is a closed, one-dimensional curve that is the path of a point that moves so that the sum of its respective distances from two fixed points -- the ellipse's <i>foci</i> -- is constant.  
<p>
An important specialization of #$Ellipse is #$Circle, each instance of which is an ellipse such that the distance between its foci is zero. 
<p>
See also #$EllipticalRegion, each instance of which is a two-dimensional region whose boundary is an #$Ellipse.  And cf. #$Ellipsoid.")
(comment PerimeterFn "A unary function that takes a geometrically bounded surface (see #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded) to the closed curve (see #$ClosedLinearObject), or the mereological sum of such curves, that is the former's perimeter.  If <b>OBJ</b> is a self-connected (see #$SpatiallyContinuousThing) open surface with no holes in it, then (#$PerimeterFn <b>OBJ</b>) is a single #$ClosedLinearObject.  More generally, if <b>OBJ</b> is an open surface consisting of <b>M</b> (<b>M >= 1</b>) self-connected and pairwise-separated surfaces, and having a total of <b>N</b> (<b>N >= 0</b>) holes in it, then (#$PerimeterFn <b>OBJ</b>) is the mereological sum of <b>M+N</b> pairwise-separated closed curves.
<p>
For example, (#$PerimeterFn #$Kansas-State) is a single closed curve, while (#$PerimeterFn #$Italy) is the sum of several such curves (e.g. the perimeter of the mainland, the perimeter of #$Sicily, and the border between Rome and #$VaticanCity).")
(comment StandingInRelationFn "A unary, reifiable #$CollectionDenotingFunction that takes a #$Predicate PRED and returns the collection of situations that consist in some #$Tuple of objects standing in PRED.  For example, (#$StandingInRelationFn #$mother) is the collection of situations that consist in some ordered pair of #$Animals <CHILD, MOTHER> (e.g. <#$GeorgeWBush, #$BarbaraBush>, <#$CarrieFisher, #$DebbieReynolds>) standing in the #$mother relation.")
(comment (RefrainingFromFn ConsumingFoodOrDrink) "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Individual. In each (#$RefrainingFromFn #$ConsumingFoodOrDrink), someone chooses no to consume.")
(comment fieldValue "Let INDEXICAL denote a #$PhysicalFieldIndexical, and VALUE denote a #$KSID.  Then (#$fieldValue INDEXICAL VALUE) is true precisely if the value of the physical field referred to by INDEXICAL is VALUE in some row of some knowledge source in which the physical field is defined.  INDEXICAL is associated with a particular physical field using the predicate #$indexicalForPhysicalField, this physical field is associated with a physical schema using the predicate #$physicalFields, and the physical schema is associated with a structured knowledge source using the predicate #$physicalSchemaSourceMap.  See also #$rowInSourceClaims, which expresses in CycL (via a conjunction of one or more #$fieldValue literals) one or more specific field values appearing in a row of a structured knowledge source.")
(comment CorrelationCoefficient "(#$CorrelationCoefficient SET FUNCTION1 FUNCTION2) computes
the correlation coefficient of the data points obtained by applying
FUNCTION1 and FUNCTION2 to the elements of SET.")
(comment Pyramid "A #$ThreeDimensionalShapeType and a specialization of #$Polyhedron.  Each instance of #$Pyramid is a polyhedron having an n-sided #$Polygon as its base and n triangles (with a common vertex) as its other faces.  Examples include tangible objects, such as the Egyptian pyramids, as well as intangible pyramid-shaped space regions.")
(comment Covariance "(#$Covariance SET FUNCTION1 FUNCTION2) computes
the covariance of the data points obtained by applying
FUNCTION1 and FUNCTION2 to the elements of SET.")
(comment Ginko-BrokenQuery "A holding tank for queries designed for the GINKO demos that have failed their tests for some reason or other.")
(comment higherPriorityPredWRTFactSheetBuilderQuery "(#$higherPriorityPredWRTFactSheetBuilderQuery PRED1 PRED2 QUERY) means that sentences with PRED1 in arg0 should be displayed before/above sentences with PRED2 in arg0 in fact sheets generated using QUERY.")
(comment mergedConstantName "<code>(#$mergedConstantName CONSTANT NAME)</code> means that the #$CycLConstant <code>CONSTANT</code> has had another #$CycLConstant (one that was named with the #$SubLString <code>NAME)</code> merged onto it.  See also #$oldConstantName and #$mergedConstantGUID.")
(comment HTMLTableRowFn "(#$HTMLTableRowFn OPTIONS . CONTENT) denotes the #$HTMLSource for displaying the table row whose content is CONTENT with OPTIONS.")
(comment (CausingFn Dying) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event. In each (#$CausingFn #$Dying), someone deprives something of life.")
(comment (CausingFn Collision) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event. In each (#$CausingFn #$Colliding), someone causes something to collide.")
(comment (CausingFn (ExperiencingEmotionFn Resentment)) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event. In each (#$CausingFn (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Resentment)), something causes something else to be bitter or resentful.")
(comment ExpressingEmotion "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Action. In each #$ExpressingEmotion, someone gives verbal or other expression to their feelings.")
(comment (CausingFn (ExperiencingEmotionFn Happiness)) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event. In each (#$CausingFn (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Happiness)), someone makes glad or happy.")
(comment (CausingFn (CollectionUnionFn (TheSet (ExperiencingEmotionFn Happiness) (ExperiencingEmotionFn Satisfaction)))) "A collection of situations; a subcollection of #$Individual. In each (#$CausingFn (#$CollectionUnionFn (#$TheSet (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Happiness) (#$ExperiencingEmotionFn #$Satisfaction)))), someone makes happy or satisfied.")
(comment outsideRegion "A general predicate used to state a type of spatial disjointness.  <code>(#$outsideRegion SPATIAL-THING REGION)</code> means that <code>SPATIAL-THING</code> is not in or at <code>REGION</code>, or <code>(#$not (#$inRegion SPATIAL-THING REGION))</code>.")
(comment miniFactSheetFlagHtml "(#$miniFactSheetFlagHtml THING HTML) relates THING to the #$HTMLSource representing a flag image associated with THING, if we can come up with one.")
(comment experiencingEmotion-Pred "The predicate corresponding to #$ExperiencingEmotionFn (q.v.).")
(comment defaultTinyIconTermImagePathname "(#$defaultTinyIconTermImagePathname THING PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a pathname for a directory or file that contains the default small icon for THING.  This is the icon that should be used if no more specific icon is available via #$tinyIconTermImagePathname.")
(comment stronglyConnectedSpaceRegions "A binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and specialization of #$connectedSpaceRegions (q.v.).  <code>(#$stronglyConnectedSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that (i) <code>REGION1</code> is connected with <code>REGION2</code> and (ii) there exists a part (<code>PART1</code>) of <code>REGION1</code> and a part (<code>PART2</code>) of <code>REGION2</code> such that the convex region of the spatial sum of <code>PART1</code> and <code>PART2</code> is a part of the spatial sum of <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code>; i.e.
<pre>
  (#$partOfSpaceRegion 
    (#$ConvexSpaceRegionFn (#$SpaceRegionSumFn PART1 PART2))
    (#$SpaceRegionSumFn REGION1 REGION2))
</pre>
Intuitively, this means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are connected by a \"convex channel\" (of the same dimensionality as they are) that is entirely part of their spatial sum.  
<p>
Note that if <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are <code>N</code>-dimensional, then they are strongly-connected if and only if the border that they share is <code>N-1</code>-dimensional.  Thus, two 3-D cube-shaped regions with a common 2-D surface are strongly-connected; but if all they have in common is a (1-D) edge or (0-D) corner they are weakly-connected (cf. #$weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions).  Two 2-D polygon-shaped regions with a common edge are strongly-connected; but if they only have a common vertex they are weakly-connected.  
<p>
See also #$StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion.")
(comment weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions "An irreflexive and symmetric #$SpaceRegionPredicate (q.v.) that is a specialization of #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.).  <code>(#$weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionality that are externally-connected but <i>not</i> strongly-connected (cf. #$stronglyConnectedSpaceRegions). Intuitively, this means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are <i>not</i> connected by any \"convex channel\" (of the same dimensionality as they are) that is entirely part of their spatial sum.  
<p>
Note that if <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are <code>N</code>-dimensional, then they are weakly-connected if and only if the border that they share is no more than <code>N-2</code>-dimensional.  Thus, two 3-D cube-shaped regions with a common 2-D surface are strongly-connected; but if all they have in common is a (1-D) edge or (0-D) corner they are weakly-connected (cf. #$weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions).  Two 2-D polygon-shaped regions with a common edge are strongly-connected; but if they only have a common vertex they are weakly-connected.  
<p>
For example, the space regions occupied by adjacent black squares on a (geometrically ideal) checkerboard are weakly-connected.  Conversely, the space regions occupied by a black square and an adjacent red square are not weakly, but strongly, connected.")
(comment SpaceChunk-Empirical "A specialization of #$SpaceRegion-Empirical and #$SpaceChunk (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$SpaceChunk-Empirical is a three-dimensional portion of the intangible space of the empirical universe (see #$TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical).  This is the kind of space that physical objects occupy.
<p>
See also #$Surface-Empirical, #$LineSegment-Empirical, and #$Point-Empirical.")
(comment associatedPegPhrases "The union (see #$predicateUnion) of  #$rewritePhrases and #$originalPhrase. (#$associatedPegPhrases PEG PHRASE) means that PHRASE is one of the character strings that, either by virtue of being an exact record of the string content of PEG, or by virtue of being a rewrite of some of that content, reflects some of the sentential content of PEG.")
(comment rewritePhrases "(#$rewritePhrases PEG PHRASE) means that PHRASE is one of the character strings that rewrites of some of the sentential content of PEG, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.")
(comment expressionPegBasedOnToken "(#$expressionPegBasedOnToken PEG TOKEN) identifies PEG as the instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that has been introduced for purposes of parsing a sentence for which TOKEN is an instance of #$CycParsingToken token that results from a tokenization of that sentence.")
(comment metaFunctionBasis "<code>(#$metaFunctionBasis META-FUNCTION BINARY-PREDICATE)</code> means that <code>META-FUNCTION</code> is the instance of #$MetaFunction and #$BinaryPredicate such that for any instance <code>FN</code> of #$Function-Denotational such that <code>(META-FUNCTION FN THING)</code>, any thing <code>DENOTATION</code> that is denoted by <code>(FN . ARGS)</code> will be such that <code>(BINARY-PREDICATE DENOTATION THING)</code>.  <p>For example, <code>(#$metaFunctionBasis #$resultGenl #$genls)</code> means that where, for example, <code>(#$resultGenls #$FruitFn #$Fruit)</code>, <code>(#$genls (#$FruitFn TREE-TYPE) #$Fruit)</code> will hold, so long as <code>(#$FruitFn TREE-TYPE)</code> is defined (see #$undefined).")
(disjointWith ThreeDimensionalThing (MeaningInSystemFn SENSUS-Information1997 "ONE-OR-TWO-D-LOCATION"))
(comment GeometricFigure "A specialization of both #$GeometricallyDescribableThing and #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion (qq.v.).  This is the collection of geometrical figures: geometrically simple, bounded (one- or higher-dimensional) regions of space.  
<p>
Examples of #$GeometricFigures include straight line-segments, circles, spheres, and pyramids.  Excluded are points, sums of scattered points, figure-eights, and infinite lines.
<p>
An important specialization of this collection is #$PlaneFigure.")
(comment Point "A specialization of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion and #$ZeroDimensionalThing (qq.v.).  This is the collection of points that have a fixed location with respect to some coordinate system or frame of reference.  
Space points can be used to indicate the locations (or partial locations) of things in space (see #$locatedAtPoint-Spatial).
<p>
An important specialization of this collection is #$Point-Empirical (q.v.), whose instances have fixed locations with respect to some coordinate system or frame of reference in the empirically-observable universe, and thus are embedded in time.  An example of a space-point <i>not</i> in the empirical universe is a point in some abstract Euclidean space of the sort studied in geometry.
<p>
See also #$Line, #$Surface, and #$SpaceChunk.")
(comment ZeroDimensionalThing "A #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions and a specialization of #$SpatialThing and #$IntangibleIndividual.  Each instance of #$ZeroDimensionalThing is either a single #$Point (q.v.) or an arrangement of disconnected points in space.  Zero-dimensional objects have no length or breadth or height, nor do they have parts.")
(comment GeometricallyDescribableThing "A subcollection of #$SpatialThing. Each instance of #$GeometricallyDescribableThing is a spatially-connected spatial thing (of one, two, three, or four dimensions) that either (i) has or approximates a simple geometric shape (e.g. it is a #$Line or a #$Hemisphere) or (ii) consists of a small number of parts in a relatively simple (and stable) simple geometric configuration, where each such part has or approximates a simple geometric shape (e.g. a table consisting of a 3-D-disc-shaped top and four cylindrical legs).  A geometrically-describable thing might be tangible or intangible. 
<p>
Note that what counts as \"approximating\" a given simple geometric shape -- and thus what spatial things count as #$GeometricallyDescribableThings -- varies with context. In a context that was so fine-grained shape-wise that even the shapes of the individual molecules on the surface of an object were considered relevant to the object's shape, perhaps nearly every (connected, solid) tangible object would be geometrically-describable.  In more everyday contexts, on the other hand, an unopened can of soup would be geometrically-describable (as a cylinder), while a telephone or an animal's body would probably not.")
(comment ShapedObject "An #$ObjectType and a specialization of #$PositiveDimensionalThing (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ShapedObject is a spatial object that has some well-defined shape (though what counts as a \"well-defined shape\" can vary from context to context); see #$objectShapeType.  Specializations of #$ShapedObject include #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing and #$AnimalShapedThing.  Contrast with #$AmorphousThing.")
(comment SheetShapedObject "A specilization of #$ShapedObject and #$SolidTangibleThing.  Each instance of #$SheetShapedObject is an object that is flat and spread out, planar, and neither fully crumpled nor compactly folded.  Such an object has two opposing surfaces that are both #$FlatSurfaceRegions (q.v.); its other surfaces have much smaller area than the two main flat surfaces.   Thus, one of the three orthogonal dimensions (length, width or height) of a sheet-shaped object is much smaller than the other two.  
<p>
Examples of #$SheetShapedObject include a layer of ice over a pond and a piece of plywood.  A bedspread is a sheet-shaped object only if it is not folded into a small volume or crumpled.  (See #$CurrentShapeVsIntrinsicShape.)  
<p>
For describing a sheet of something (e.g. waxed paper, aluminum foil, cloth) regardless of its current shape, use #$SheetOfSomeStuff.  For \"one-sided\" surfaces, use a specialization of #$SurfaceRegion.")
(comment TwoDimensionalGeometricThing "A specialization of #$Surface, #$GeometricallyDescribableThing, and #$ShapedObject (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing is an intangible, two-dimensional, self-connected object whose shape is describable in (relatively simple) geometric terms.  Instances include planes, as well as all (planar or \"curved\") polygons and elliptical regions.  For bounded two-dimensional geometric objects that <i>can</i> be embedded in a #$Plane, see the specialization #$PlaneFigure.")
(comment SpaceRegionPredicate "A specialization of both #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and #$IntangibleObjectRelatingPredicate (qq.v.).  This is the collection of relative-location predicates that are used to relate only pure space regions.  Thus, any #$argIsa constraint on a #$SpaceRegionPredicate should be to #$SpaceRegion or a specialization thereof.
<p>
A notable specialization of this collection is #$RCCBinaryPredicate.")
(comment loggedInToAppOnComputer "An assertion of the form (#$loggedInToAppOnComputer AGENT PROGRAM COMPUTER) states that the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT is logged in to #$ComputerProgram-CW  PROGRAM using the #$Computer COMPUTER. This is a #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation.")
(comment RelativeLocationalPredicate "A specialization of #$LocationalPredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate relates two (or, occasionally, more) spatial things in terms of their relative locations with respect to one another, and does so in a way that does not simply amount to relating a spatial thing to a space region in which it is embedded.  (Cf. #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate.)  
<p>
Many relative-location predicates can take any #$SpatialThings (or at least any instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized) as arguments.  For example, #$touches-Directly holds of any two spatial things that either spatially intersect or are in direct spatial contact.  Other relative-location predicates have one or more of their argument-places restricted to tangible things of some sort (see #$PartiallyTangible); e.g. #$pathThroughRegion and #$abutsSurface.  Still others (the so-called #$SpaceRegionPredicates) only relate #$SpaceRegions to each other; e.g. #$overlappingSpaceRegions and #$properPartOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment AbsoluteLocationalPredicate "A specialization of #$LocationalPredicate (q.v.) instances of which are used to relate spatial things to their locations in space.  Each instance of #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate is a binary predicate that relates spatial things (including at least some that are not themselves pure space regions) to #$SpaceRegions in which they are (wholly or partly) embedded.  
<p>
By stipulation, the first argument-place of an #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate is constrained to instances of #$SpatialThing or some specialization thereof, but is <i>not</i> constrained so tightly as to only admit instances of #$SpaceRegion.  Conversely, the second argument-place is constrained to instances of #$SpaceRegion or some specialization thereof.
<p>
Examples of absolute-location predicates include #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial, #$partiallyLocatedAt-Spatial, and #$locatedAtPoint-SurfaceGeographical.
<p>
Cf. #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.")
(comment LocationalPredicate "A specialization of #$SpatialPredicate whose instances are used to indicate the locations of #$SpatialThings within a particular frame of reference.  #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicates (q.v.) are used to indicate a spatial thing's location by giving a pure #$SpaceRegion in which it is embedded.  #$RelativeLocationalPredicates (q.v.) are used to indicate the relative locations of two or more spatial things with respect to one another.")
(disjointWith AbsoluteLocationalPredicate RelativeLocationalPredicate)
(comment locatedAtPoint-SurfaceGeographical "(#$locatedAtPoint-SurfaceGeographical OBJECT GEO-POINT) means that
OBJECT, an instance of #$SpatialThing-Localized, is geographically located
at GEO-POINT on the geographical surface of reference. This relation expresses the fact that the geographical location of an extended #$SpatialThing includes some precise point. For non zero
dimensional objects, if GEO-SPACE is the region of two-dimensional
geographical space occupied by OBJECT
(see #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical), GEO-POINT is incident in
(see #$incidentInSpaceRegion) in GEO-SPACE. To locate precisely a point itself, use
#$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical")
(comment ending-Pred "The predicate corresponding to #$EndingFn (q.v.).")
(comment (EndingFn MovementEvent) "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event. In each (#$EndingFn #$MovementEvent), something comes to a halt, stop moving.")
(comment EndingFn "A unary, reifiable #$CollectionDenotingFunction that takes a specialization of #$TemporalThing COL and returns the #$TemporalObjectType consisting of all instances of #$EndingOfTemporalThing in which an instance of COL comes to an end.  For example, (#$EndingFn #$War) is the collection of all events in which a war comes to an end.")
(comment recordMtByUserTaskAndApplication "(#$recordMtByUserTaskAndApplication SESSION-CONTEXT USER TASK APP) means that SESSION-CONTEXT is the context in which data is stored about the session of the #$Person USER working on the #$Allotment TASK using the #$ComputerProgram-CW APP.")
(comment GlassBoxAllotment "A specialization of #$Allotment that represents #$NIMDProject Glass Box taskings.")
(comment GlassBoxAllotmentFn "(#$GlassBoxAllotmentFn TASKING-ID) denotes the #$NIMDProject Glass Box Tasking with TASKING-ID as its identification in the Glass Box system.")
(comment coveredBySpecPreds "A binary #$MetaPredicate that relates a given #$Predicate to a set of its specializations (see #$genlPreds) such that the extensions of the latter jointly include the extension of the former.
<p>
More precisely, the sentence 
<pre>
  (#$coveredBySpecPreds PRED (#$TheSet SPECPRED<sub>1</sub> ... SPECPRED<sub>N</sub>))
</pre> 
means that (i) each <code>SPECPRED<sub>i</sub></code> is a specialization of <code>PRED</code> and (ii) whenever <code>(PRED . ARGS)</code> holds of an argument-seqence <code>ARGS</code> at least one of the <code>(SPECPRED<sub>i</sub> . ARGS) holds (<code>1<=i<=N</code>).
<p>
Note that <code>PRED</code> might have other specializations that are not specializations of elements of <code>(#$TheSet SPECPRED<sub>1</sub> ... SPECPRED<sub>N</sub>)</code>.  For example, #$communicationParticipants is covered by (#$TheSet #$infoCommunicatedTo #$communicatorOfInfo).  #$onlineChatParticipants is also a specialization of the former; but it is not a specialization of either element of the latter.
<p>
See also #$partitionedIntoSpecPreds.")
(comment partitionedIntoSpecPreds "A binary #$MetaPredicate and specialization of #$coveredBySpecPreds (q.v.) that relates a given #$Predicate to a pairwise-disjoint set of its specializations such that the extensions of the latter jointly constitute the exact extension of the former.
<p>
More precisely, the sentence 
<pre>
  (#$partitionedIntoSpecPreds PRED (#$TheSet SPECPRED<sub>1</sub> ... SPECPRED<sub>N</sub>))
</pre> 
means that (i) each <code>SPECPRED<sub>i</sub></code> is a specialization of <code>PRED</code> (see #$genlPreds), (ii) each <code>SPECPRED<sub>i</sub></code> and <code>SPECPRED<sub>j</sub></code> are mutually-disjoint (see #$negationPreds), and (iii) whenever <code>(PRED . ARGS)</code> holds of an argument-seqence <code>ARGS</code> <i>exactly</i> one of the <code>(SPECPRED<sub>i</sub> . ARGS) holds (<code>1<=i,j<=N</code>).
<p>
Note that <code>PRED</code> might have other specializations that are not specializations of elements of <code>(#$TheSet SPECPRED<sub>1</sub> ... SPECPRED<sub>N</sub>)</code>.  For example, #$siblings is partitioned into (#$TheSet #$brothers #$sisters #$hermaphroditicSiblings).  #$twins is also a specialization of the former; but it is not a specialization of any element of the latter.")
(comment BlindingAnAnimal "Each instance of #$BlindingAnAnimal is an event that causes an animal to (temporarily or permanently) lose its ability to see.  This is a rewrite of:
<pre>
(#$CausingFn
(#$BecomingFn
  (#$CollectionSubsetFn #$Animal
    (#$TheSetOf ?X
      (#$behaviorIncapable ?X #$VisualPerception #$doneBy)))))
</pre>.")
(comment ComputerFn "(#$ComputerFn COMPUTER-NAME) denotes the #$Computer with the given name in the relevant context (e.g. within a local area network).")
(comment currentTasks "A #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation, #$currentTasks relates an #$IntelligentAgent to an #$Allotment that they are working on during a specific interval.  #$currentTasks is a more specific relation than its #$genlInverse #$allottedAgents in that it states that the agent is actually performing work on that assignment.")
(comment gbAllotmentOfGUID "An assertion of the form (#$gbAllotmentOfGUID ALLOT GUID) means that the #$GlassBoxAllotment ALLOT is picked out with the #$GUIDString GUID.  This is the corresponding predicate of the function #$GlassBoxAllotmentFn.")
(comment defaultTinyIconTermImagePathnameForType "(#$defaultTinyIconTermImagePathname COL PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a pathname for a directory or file that contains the default small icon for instances of COL. This is the icon that should be used if no more specific icon is available via #$tinyIconTermImagePathname.")
(comment partiallyLocatedAt-Spatial "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$spatiallySubsumes and #$genericallyLocatedAt-Spatial (q.v.) that relates a spatial thing to a space region.  <code>(#$partiallyLocatedAt-Spatial OBJ LOC)</code> means that part of <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at (see #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial) <code>LOC</code>.  
<p>
For example, my office is partially located in the space region occupied by my desk.
<p>
See also #$whollyLocatedAt-Spatial and #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment whollyLocatedAt-Spatial "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$genericallyLocatedAt-Spatial, #$inRegion, and (via #$genlInverse) #$spatiallySubsumes (qq.v.) that relates a spatial thing to a space region such that the entirety of the former is located within the latter.  <code>(#$whollyLocatedAt-Spatial OBJ LOC)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at (see #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial) a part of <code>LOC</code>.  
<p>
For example, my desk is wholly located in the space region occupied by my office.
<p>
See also #$partiallyLocatedAt-Spatial and #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$whollyLocatedAt-Spatial, #$partiallyLocatedAt-Spatial, and #$cospatial (qq.v.) that relates a spatial thing to the precise space region that it occupies.  <code>(#$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial OBJ LOC)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at <code>LOC</code>.  That is, <code>LOC</code> is the spatial extent of, or the complete and minimal space occupied by, <code>OBJ</code>.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpaceOccupiedByFn.  Also see #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment genericallyLocatedAt-Spatial "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$spatiallyIntersects (q.v.) that relates a spatial thing to any space region such that the two spatially overlap.  <code>(#$genericallyLocatedAt-Spatial OBJ LOC)</code> means that a part of <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at (see #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial) a part of <code>LOC</code>.  
<p>
For example, a loaf of french bread is spatially generically-located at the space that forms the interior of the grocery bag it's in, even if it sticks partly out of the bag.
<p>
See the specializations #$partiallyLocatedAt-Spatial and #$whollyLocatedAt-Spatial.  Also see #$genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment UserOfSystemWithLocalNameFn "An expression of the form (#$UserOfSystemWithLocalNameFn PROCESS ID) is used to represent a user (more specifically, an #$IntelligentAgent) of the #$ComputerProcessRunning PROCESS who is identified within the system by the #$IDString ID.  So, PROCESSS serves as a namespace for picking out this #$IntelligentAgent with the identifier ID.")
(comment userOfSystemWithLocalName "An expression of the form (#$userOfSystemWithLocalName USER PROCESS ID) states that USER is the individual denoted by the #$IDString ID relative to the #$ComputerProcessRunning PROCESS. This is the corresponding predicate of the function #$UserOfSystemWithLocalNameFn.")
(disjointWith CSQLFunction CSQLComparisonPredicate)
(comment CSQLToSQLTranslationSyntaxDirective "#$CSQLToSQLTranslationSyntaxDirective is a completely
asserted collection whose members are directives passed to the SKSI CSQL to
SQL translation code which specify how CSQL literals involving certain
operators and functions should be translated into SQL. Specifically,
they are used within #$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator assertions and
determine whether function syntax, operator infix syntax, operator
prefix syntax, or operator postfix syntax is to be used when
translating a CSQL literal into an SQL literal.
<p>
For example, the sentence
<pre>
(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator #$CSQLPlusFn \"+\" #$PostgreSQL #$SQLOperatorSyntax-Infix)
</pre>
tells the translator that whenever it encounters a literal such as
(#$CSQLPlusFn 1 2) within a CSQL query to be translated into an SQL
query and evaluated by a #$PostgreSQL dbms, the translated literal
should use the token \"+\" as the operator symbol and it should apply
as an infix operator to its arguments, so that the translated literal
would be \"1 + 2\".")
(comment csqlOperatorToSQLOperator "(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator CSQL-OP SQL-OP DBMS-TYPE
SYNTAX) tells the CSQL to SQL translator that in order to translate a
CSQL literal with operator CSQL-OP into an SQL string literal with
operator SQL-OP that is valid for evaluation by DBMS-TYPE, the
translator should use the translation syntax SYNTAX. For example, the
sentence
<pre>
(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator #$CSQLPlusFn \"+\" #$PostgreSQL #$SQLOperatorSyntax-Infix)
</pre>
tells the translator that when translating a literal such as
(#$CSQLPlusFn 1 2) into SQL which is to be evaulated by a #$PostgreSQL
DBMS, it should infix the operator between its arguments, so that
the resultant SQL literal would be \"1 + 2\".")
(comment SQLOperatorSyntax-Infix "A #$CSQLToSQLTranslationSyntaxDirective which directs the
CSQL to SQL translator to use operator infix syntax. This term
should appear only within a #$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator assertion. For
example, the sentence
<pre>
(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator #$CSQLPlusFn \"+\" #$PostgreSQL #$SQLOperatorSyntax-Infix)
</pre>
tells the translator that when translating a literal such as
(#$CSQLPlusFn 1 2) into SQL which is to be evaulated by a #$PostgreSQL
DBMS, it should infix the operator between its arguments, so that
the resultant SQL literal would be \"1 + 2\".")
(comment SQLOperatorSyntax-Prefix "A #$CSQLToSQLTranslationSyntaxDirective which directs the
CSQL to SQL translator to use operator prefix syntax. This term
should appear only within a #$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator assertion. For
example, the sentence
<pre>
(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator #$CSQLMinusFn \"-\" #$PostgreSQL #$SQLOperatorSyntax-Prefix)
</pre>
tells the translator that when translating a literal such as
(#$CSQLMinusFn 4) into SQL which is to be evaulated by a #$PostgreSQL
DBMS, it should prefix the operator before its argument(s), so that
the resultant SQL literal would be \"-4\".")
(comment SQLOperatorSyntax-Postfix "A #$CSQLToSQLTranslationSyntaxDirective which directs the
CSQL to SQL translator to use operator postfix syntax. This term
should appear only within a #$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator assertion. For
example, the sentence
<pre>
(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator #$CSQLFactorialFn \"!\" #$PostgreSQL #$SQLOperatorSyntax-Postfix)
</pre>
tells the translator that when translating a literal such as
(#$CSQLFactorialFn 4) into SQL which is to be evaulated by a
#$PostgreSQL DBMS, it postfix the operator after its argument(s), so
that the resultant SQL literal would be \"4!\".")
(comment SQLFunctionSyntax "A #$CSQLToSQLTranslationSyntaxDirective which directs the
CSQL to SQL translator to use function syntax. This term should appear
only within a #$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator assertion. For example, the
sentence
<pre>
(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator #$CSQLSineFn \"sin\" #$PostgreSQL #$SQLFunctionSyntax)
</pre>
tells the translator that when translating a literal such as
(#$CSQLSineFn 1.7323) into SQL which is to be evaulated by a
#$PostgreSQL DBMS, it should apply function syntax, so that the
resultant SQL literal would be \"sin(1.7323)\".")
(comment OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL "The #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL is a standard
adopted by the OpenGIS Consortium which defines a common SQL schema
that supports storage, retrieval, query, and update of simple
geospatial features.")
(comment geometryType "(#$geometryType GEOMETRY TYPE) means that the
#$OpenGISGeometry, GEOMETRY, is an instance of the
#$OpenGISGeometryType TYPE.")
(comment HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegionSet "A specialization of #$Set-Mathematical.  This is the collection of all sets of homogeneous and extended (or null) space regions.  A thing is an instance of #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegionSet if and only if it is an instance of (#$SetOfTypeFn #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion).  Indeed, the CycL constant <code>#$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegionSet</code> is a #$rewriteOf the non-atomic term <code>(#$SetOfTypeFn #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion)</code>.
<p>
See also the specialization #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet.")
(comment SpatialThingSet "A specialization of #$Set-Mathematical.  This is the
collection of all sets of spatial things broadly construed (see
#$SpatialThing).  A thing is an instance of #$SpatialThingSet if and
only if it is an instance of (#$SetOfTypeFn #$SpatialThing).  Indeed,
the CycL constant <code>#$SpatialThingSet</code> is a #$rewriteOf the
non-atomic term <code>(#$SetOfTypeFn #$SpatialThing)</code>.
<p>
See also the specialization #$SpaceRegionSet and its specializations.")
(comment HomogeneousSpaceRegionSet "A specialization of #$SpatialThingSet.  This is the
collection of all sets of homogeneous space regions (see
#$HomogeneousSpaceRegion).  A thing is an instance of
#$HomogeneousSpaceRegionSet if and only if it is an instance of
(#$SetOfTypeFn #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion).  Indeed, the CycL constant
<code>#$HomogeneousSpaceRegionSet</code> is a #$rewriteOf the
non-atomic term <code>(#$SetOfTypeFn #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion)</code>.
<p>
See also the specialization #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegionSet and
its specializations.")
(comment followupQuerySpecificationToSentence "(#$followupQuerySpecificationToSentence SENT Q-SPEC) means that if
the sentence SENT is asked as a query, the #$CycLQuerySpecification
Q-SPEC is a reasonable followup query to ask.")
(comment followupQuerySpecificationToTerm "(#$followupQuerySpecificationToTerm TERM Q-SPEC) means that if the sentence SENT conatins term TERM and is asked as a query, the #$CycLQuerySpecification Q-SPEC is a reasonable followup query to ask.")
(comment pegInterpretationMts "(#$pegInterpretationMts PEG MICROTHEORY) means that MICROTHEORY is the instance of #$Microtheory whose contents reflect one semantic interpretation of PEG, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.")
(comment kEInteractionFolderOfUserForTask "(#$kEInteractionFolderOfUserForTask AGENT ALLOTMENT FOLDER) means that
FOLDER is the #$KEInteractionFolder associated with the
#$IntelligentAgent AGENT, for the task ALLOTMENT.")
(comment UserKEInteractionFolderFn "(#$UserKEInteractionFolderFn AGENT) denotes the instance of
#$KEInteractionFolder associated with a particular user of the query
library.")
(comment kEInteractionFolderOfUser "(#$kEInteractionFolderOfUser AGENT FOLDER) means that FOLDER is the
#$KEInteractionFolder associated with the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT.")
(comment Paradox "A specialization of #$PropositionalConceptualWork.  Each instance of #$Paradox is a piece of reasoning in which plausible premises lead via plausible steps to a seemingly absurd conclusion.")
(comment Refusing-CommunicationAct "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$Declining-CommunicationAct and #$Insisting-CommunicationAct.  Each instance of #$Refusing-CommunicationAct is #$CommunicationAct-Single in which the #$senderOfInfo refuses to do something that he or she has been asked to do, or is otherwise expected to do.")
(comment Insisting-CommunicationAct "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$Insisting-CommunicationAct is a #$CommunicationAct-Single in which the #$senderOfInfo asserts, denies, demands, or refuses something vehemently and persistently.")
(comment notIdentical-AlreadyReviewed "This is a workflow constant which is used to mark potentially identical terms that have been reviewed, and determined to NOT be identical. (#$notIdentical-AlreadyReviewed OBJ1 OBJ2) means that OBJ1 and OBJ2 have been identified as non-identical. See also #$possiblyIdentical-NeedToReview.")
(comment WorldWideWebPage-PCW "#$WorldWideWebPage-PCW is a specialization of #$PropositionalConceptualWork and #$WorldWideWebPage-CW.  It is the collection of propositional works designed to be accessed on the #$WorldWideWeb-DynamicIndexedInfoSource via an implementation of the #$HypertextTransferProtocol.  Instances of this collection include all world wide web pages with significant propositional context.  Non-instances include web sites whose context is nearly entirely pictoral, musical, etc.")
(comment publicationLocation "(#$publicationLocation PUBLICATION LOCATION) means that PUBLICATION was published in LOCATION.")
(comment commentOnBy "(#$commentOnBy TERM STRING CYCLIST) means that STRING is a comment made about TERM by CYCLIST.")
(comment WorldWideWebPage-CW "#$WorldWideWebPage-CW is a specialization of #$ConceptualWork.  It is the collection of conceptual works designed to be accessed on the #$WorldWideWeb-DynamicIndexedInfoSource via an implementation of the #$HypertextTransferProtocol.  An important specialization is #$WorldWideWebPage-PCW which is the collection of all such web pages with substantial propositional content.")
(comment exactlyAssertedSentence "A #$KBDependentRelation (q.v.) and specialization of #$assertedSentence (q.v.) that is used to state that a given #$CycLSentence-Assertible has been asserted in the KB (in some accessible #$Microtheory). More exactly, (#$exactlyAssertedSentence SENT) is true in microtheory MT precisely when SENT canonicalizes without commutative handling of the sentence args into a set of clauses of the same form as the canonicalized form of some assertion in a microtheory accessible to MT. (The predicate is thus true both of assertions entered into the KB by hand and assertions deduced by Cyc from forward rules.)
<p>
This predicate enables Cyc to select a subset of information when answering queries, filtering out more generic (asserted-without-commutative-canonicalization) information. Thus, for example, the query:
<p>
(#$assertedSentence (#$bordersOn #$CentralUSATimeZone #$EasternUSATimeZone))
<p>
will return True (in appropriate microtheories), whereas:
<p>
(#$exactlyAssertedSentence (#$bordersOn #$CentralUSATimeZone #$EasternUSATimeZone))
<p>
will not.")
(comment Flat-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$FlatObject.  Each instance of #$Flat-Topographical relatively level area within a region of greater relief. Examples include forest clearings, mountain terraces, #$Playas, and #$Plateaus.")
(comment FlatObject "A flat surface is flat overall.  It may have texture or may have relatively small subregions which are concave, convex, have holes all the way through etc..  Need not be horizontal.  See #$SheetShapedObject for 'two-sided' or 'flat' objects such as boards, uncrumpled sheets of paper, or Compact Discs.")
(comment partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions "An irreflexive and symmetric #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight that is a specialization of #$overlappingSpaceRegions and #$partiallySpatiallyIntersects (qq.v.).  <code>(#$partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionalty (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) that overlap, but are such that neither is part of the other.  Thus, #$partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions is logically contrary to (i.e. a #$negationPreds of) both #$partOfSpaceRegion and #$partOfSpaceRegion-Inverse.
<p>
For example, the space region occupied by the Sonoran desert (which straddles the US and Mexico) partially overlaps with that occupied by the US.
<p>
Note that #$partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions is the relation called <i>PO</i> in #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory.")
(comment presumedDifferentDespiteSharedLexification "This is a workflow constant which is used to indicate that two potentially identical terms (e.g. terms which share some lexification) have been reviewd an are presumed to different. See also #$notIdentical-AlreadyReviewed and #$possiblyIdentical-NeedToReview.")
(comment notIdentical-AlreadyReviewed "This is a workflow constant which is used to mark potentially identical terms that have been reviewed, and determined to NOT be identical. (#$notIdentical-AlreadyReviewed OBJ1 OBJ2) means that OBJ1 and OBJ2 have been identified as not identical. See also #$possiblyIdentical-NeedToReview, and #$presumedDifferentDespiteSharedLexification.")
(comment possiblyIdentical-NeedToReview "This is a workflow constant which is used to mark potentially identical terms based on a shared lexification. (#$possiblyIdentical-NeedToReview OBJ1 OBJ2 STRING) means that OBJ1 and OBJ2 have been identified as potentially identical based on a shared lexification of STRING. In this case, their relationship should should be investigated and the objects should either be marked as different (#$different)  or should have their CycL terms merged. See also #$presumedDifferentDespiteSharedLexification, and #$notIdentical-AlreadyReviewed.")
(comment referencedAuthorOfPublication "(#$referencedAuthorOfPublication PERSON LITERARYWORK N) means that PERSON is one of the authors of LITERARYWORK, and is the Nth author credited in references to LITERARYWORK. For example, if ARTICLE's authors are referenced as 'SMITH, JONES, and BROWN,' then (referencedAuthorOfLiteraryWork-CW BROWN ARTICLE 3).")
(comment followupQuerySpecificationToType "(#$followupQuerySpecificationToType TYPE Q-SPEC) means that if the sentence SENT conatins term TERM (which denotes an instance of TYPE) and is asked as a query, the #$CycLQuerySpecification Q-SPEC is a reasonable followup query to ask.")
(comment except "(#$except ASSERTION) means that there is an exception to ASSERTION in the current microtheory such that ASSERTION is not known to be true.  If ASSERTION is a rule, it will not fire.")
(comment PlanarPointSet "A specialization of #$PointSet (q.v.).  A set of space-points <code>POINT-SET</code> is an instance of  #$PlanarPointSet if and only if, for any four elements (<code>A, B, C</code>, and <code>D</code>) of <code>POINT-SET</code>, <code>(#$coplanarPoints A B C D)</code> holds.")
(comment partiallySpatiallyIntersects "A symmetric, irreflexive #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that is a specialization of #$spatiallyIntersects (q.v.) and a <i>contrary</i> (see #$negationPreds) of #$spatiallySubsumes (q.v.).  <code>(#$partiallySpatiallyIntersects OBJECT1 OBJECT2)</code> means that the spatial extents of <code>OBJECT1</code> and <code>OBJECT2</code> overlap, but only partially, so that neither is completely included in the other.  Thus, there is some part of <code>OBJECT1</code> that is disjoint with <code>OBJECT2</code>, and vice versa.
<p>
For example, the sonoran desert, which straddles the US and Mexico, partially intersects both of those countries.  And the circular regions in a standard Venn diagram partially intersect one another.
<p>
See also the specialization #$partiallyOverlappingSpaceRegions.")
(comment coplanarLines "A symmetric, reflexive #$RelativeLocationalPredicates that relates straight lines (see #$Line-Straight) that lie in the same plane.
<code>(#$coplanarLines LINE1 LINE2)</code> is true if and only if there is a
#$Plane that #$spatiallySubsumes both <code>LINE1</code> and <code>LINE2</code>.
<p>
See also #$coplanarPoints.")
(comment parallelLines "An equivalence relation and specialization of #$coplanarLines (q.v.) that relates straight lines (see #$Line-Straight) that are parallel. <code>(#$parallelLines LINE1 LINE2)</code> is true if and only if (i) there is a #$Plane that #$spatiallySubsumes both <code>LINE1</code> and <code>LINE2</code> and (ii) <code>LINE1</code> and <code>LINE2</code> do <i>not</i> intersect (see #$spatiallyIntersects).")
(comment coplanarPoints "A quaternary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$coplanarPoints POINT1 POINT2 POINT3 POINT4)</code> is true if and only if
all of the #$Points <code>POINT1</code>, <code>POINT2</code>, <code>POINT3</code>, and <code>POINT4</code> lie in a common #$Plane.
<p>
See also #$coplanarLines.")
(comment concyclicPoints "A quaternary predicate and a specialization of #$coplanarPoints (q.v.).  <code>(#$concyclicPoints P1 P2 P3 P4)</code> holds if and only if the four #$Points <code>P1</code>, <code>P2</code>, <code>P3</code>, and <code>P4</code> all lie on some #$Circle.")
(comment CyclicQuadrilateral "A specialization of #$Quadrilateral (q.v.).
A quadrilateral is an instance of #$CyclicQuadrilateral if and only its vertices
are concyclic (see #$concyclicPoints): they all lie on a common #$Circle.")
(comment pegPartialInterpretationMts "(#$pegPartialInterpretationMts PEG MICROTHEORY) means that MICROTHEORY is an instance of #$Microtheory whose contents reflect a semantic interpretation of some instance SUB-PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that is a proper part (c.f., #$properSubExpressions) of the linguistic expression peg PEG.")
(comment LinguisticExpressionPegRewrite "The collection of all instances of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that do not occur naturally in the course of a dialogue or in an input text, but are proposed rewrites or partial rewrites of such pegs by the system, which the system can use to better understand the content associated with the rewritten peg.")
(disjointWith SemanticInterpretationMicrotheoryWithParsedContent SemanticInterpretationMicrotheoryWithHypothesizedContent)
(comment interpretedPhrase "(#$interpretedPhrase MICROTHEORY PHRASE) means that MICROTHEORY, an instance of #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory, is a microtheory that contains an interpretation of the string PHRASE.")
(comment SemanticInterpretationMicrotheoryWithParsedContent "The collection of all instances of #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory whose declared content (see #$interpretationMtContent-CycL) has been determined through parsing.  See also #$parsedPegInterpretations-CycL.")
(comment SemanticInterpretationMicrotheoryWithHypothesizedContent "The collection of all instances of #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory whose declared content (see #$interpretationMtContent-CycL) has been determined through some means of hypothesization, such as through sense-extension rules or rules that apply verb-argument analysis.  Contrast with #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheoryWithParsedContent.  See also #$hypothesizedPegInterpretations-CycL.")
(comment candidatePegInterpretations-CycL "(#$candidatePegInterpretations-CycL PEG CYCL) means that the CycL expression CYCL is one of the CycL interpretations of the content of the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that is/has been under consideration.")
(comment parsedPegInterpretations-CycL "(#$parsedPegInterpretations-CycL PEG CYCL) means that the CycL expression CYCL is one of the CycL interpretations of the content of the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that is/has been arrived at via parsing.  Contrast with #$hypothesizedPegInterpretations-CycL.")
(comment hypothesizedPegInterpretations-CycL "(#$hypothesizedPegInterpretations-CycL PEG CYCL) means that the CycL expression CYCL is one of the CycL interpretations of the content of the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that is/has been arrived at via some mechanism of hypothesization, such as sense-extension rules or verb/argument analysis.  Contrast with #$parsedPegInterpretations-CycL.")
(comment HomogeneousSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$SpaceRegion (q.v.).  A space region <code>REGION</code> is an instance of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion if and only if it is of uniform dimensionality.  That is, for some <code>N</code> >= 0: (i) <code>REGION</code> is <code>N</code>-dimensional, (ii) <code>REGION</code> does not include any protrusion or separated piece that is of a lower dimensionality than <code>N</code>, and (iii) there is no hole in <code>REGION</code> that is of a lower dimensionality than <code>N</code>.  Thus, a two-dimensional space region is homogeneous provided it has no protruding or separated or \"missing\" line(-segment) or point.  
<p>
Note that a homogeneous space region might or might not be self-connected (see #$ConnectedSpaceRegion); thus it might consist of two or more mutually-disconnected pieces, as long as each piece is of the same uniform dimensionality.
<p>
Specializations of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion include #$SpaceChunk, #$Surface, #$Line, and #$Point.")
(comment HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion "The intersection of #$ExtendedSpaceRegion and #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion (qq.v.); this is the collection of homogeneous extended regions of space.  Each instance <code>REGION</code> of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion is a portion of a pure space (i.e. a #$SpaceRegion) that (i) has positive spatial extent and (ii) is partwise homogeneous. 
<p>
Condition (i) above means that <code>REGION</code> is <code>N</code>-dimensional for some <code>N</code> >= 1 (see #$SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality; but cf. #$Point and #$NullSpaceRegion). Condition (ii) means that there is a sense of \"part\" (viz. #$partOfSpaceRegion) such that <code>REGION</code> is constituted wholly of parts that are of the same dimensionality (<code>N</code>) as <code>REGION</code> itself.
<p>
Note that in a given context we make the assumption that this collection is coextensional with the collection of all spatial things that connect with (see #$connectedSpaceRegions) the unique #$UniversalSpaceRegion (q.v.) recognized in that context. 
<p>
Specializations of #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion include #$ConnectedSpaceRegion, #$CompositeSpaceRegion, and #$GeometricFigure.")
(comment interpretationMtContent-CycL "(#$interpretationMtContent-CycL MT CYCL) means that, by definition, the instance MT of #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory  contains (see #$ist-Asserted) only the instance CYCL of #$CycLSentence-Assertible, plus any forwardly derived conclusions that the assertion of CYCL into MT has wrought.")
(comment pointSetOfSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) with the set of points that are incident in it. 
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$pointSetOfSpaceRegion REGION POINT-SET)</code> is true if an only if, for any instance <code>POINT</code> of #$Point, <code>(#$incidentInSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> implies <code>(#$elementOf POINT POINT-SET)</code> and vice versa.  
<p>
See also #$interiorPointSetOfSpaceRegion, #$boundaryPointSetOfSpaceRegion and #$exteriorPointSetOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment interiorPointSetOfSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) with the set of points that are incident in its interior. 
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$interiorPointSetOfSpaceRegion REGION POINT-SET)</code> is true if and only if, for any instance <code>POINT</code> of #$Point, <code>(#$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> implies <code>(#$elementOf POINT POINT-SET)</code> and vice versa.  
<p>
See also #$pointSetOfSpaceRegion, #$boundaryPointSetOfSpaceRegion, and #$exteriorPointSetOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment SpaceRegion "A specialization of both #$SpatialThing and #$IntangibleIndividual (qq.v.) whose instances are regions of space that exclusively act as possible locations for other spatial objects, and thus are immobile.  A space region might be three-, two-, one-, or zero-dimensional; and spatial objects \"occupy\" such regions accordingly.  Three-dimensional space regions (see #$SpaceChunk) can be occupied by solid objects.   Two-dimensional space regions (or #$Surfaces) can be occupied by a purely two-dimensional objects.  And similarly for one-dimensional space regions (#$Lines) and zero-dimensional space regions (#$Points). 
<p>
Another important specialization of #$SpaceRegion is #$SpaceRegion-Empirical, whose instances are pieces of the embedding space for spatio-temporal objects (see #$SpatialThing-Localized).
<p>
For more on spatial location and occupancy, see #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and its instances.")
(disjointWith Situation SpaceRegion)
(comment TextLearnerHypothesisEvaluationProceduresMt "A microtheory that contains rules for finalizing knowledge (promoting it to the relevant Microtheory -- typically the context of some instance of #$PropositionalConceptualWork) that results from parsing some text.")
(comment pegForPCW "(#$pegForPCW PEG PCW) identifies PCW as the instance of #$PropositionalConceptualWork for which PEG, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance, has been introduced into the discourse model.")
(comment SurfaceSet "A specialization of #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet.  This is the collection of all sets whose members are #$Surfaces.  The CycL constant <code>#$SurfaceSet</code> is the #$rewriteOf the term
<pre>
  (#$SetOfTypeFn #$Surface).")
(comment PolygonSet "A specialization of #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet.  This is the
collection of all sets whose members are #$Polygons.  The CycL constant <code>#$PolygonSet</code> is the #$rewriteOf the term
<pre>
  (#$SetOfTypeFn #$Polygon).")
(comment LineSegmentSet "A specialization of #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet.  This is the
collection of all sets whose members are #$LineSegments.  The CycL constant <code>#$LineSegmentSet</code> is the #$rewriteOf the term
<pre>
(#$SetOfTypeFn #$LineSegment).")
(comment LineStringSet "A specialization of #$LineSegmentSet (q.v.).  This is the
collection of all sets whose members are #$LineStrings.  The CycL constant <code>#$LineStringSet</code> is the #$rewriteOf the term
<pre>
  (#$SetOfTypeFn #$LineString).")
(comment facilitates-EventEvent "An #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate that relates an #$Event to another #$Event.  (#$facilitates-EventEvent E1 E2) means that E1 facilitates E2, i.e. E1 makes it easier for E2 to be done or more likely that E2 will occur.  A notable specialization is #$causes-EventEvent.")
(comment SetOfTypeFn "A unary #$CollectionDenotingFunction that takes a given collection to the collection of all sets (see #$Set-Mathematical) of instances of the former.  (#$SetOfTypeFn <b>COL</b>) is the collection of all sets such that  each element of the set is an instance of <b>COL</b>.  For example, (#$SetOfTypeFn #$Integer) is the collection of sets of integers, and (#$TheSet 1 2 3) is an instance of this collection.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$instanceElementType.")
(comment functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic "A #$MetaRelation and a generalization of #$functionCorrespondingPredicate (q.v.). #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic differs from the more specific predicate both in that (i) its second argument-position is constrained only to be a #$Predicate rather than a #$FunctionalPredicate and (ii) it can hold between a function and a predicate even when the second is a more broadly defined relation than the first.  Thus, for example, if <code>FUNC</code> is unary and is defined for <code>THING</code> (see #$inDomainOf), <code>(#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic FUNC PRED 2)</code> entails <code>(PRED THING (FUNC THING))</code>.  That is, <code>FUNC</code>'s returning some <code>VALUE</code> for <code>THING</code> is a sufficient (though not a necessary) condition for <code>PRED</code>'s holding between <code>THING</code> and <code>VALUE</code>.
<p>
When reifying an instance of #$Function-Denotational it is generally considered good ontological engineering practice also to reify a (or to find an already-existing) corresponding #$Predicate, as this enables one to describe more explicitly the relationship between the members of the function's domain and the associated members of its range (see #$relationDomain and #$relationRange).  #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic's particular reason-for-being is twofold.  First, there are some functions for which the intuitively most appropriate-to-reify corresponding predicate is not functional (see #$functionalInArgs) in the relevant argument place.  This is the case, for example, with the predicate #$streetIntersectionOf (which \"corresponds\" to the function #$StreetIntersectionFn), since it is possible for two given streets to intersect each other at more than one place.  Second, the predicate might have more general argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate) than the function.  For example, one of the function's arguments might be constrained to be an instance of #$PartiallyTangible, while the corresponding argument of the predicate might plausibly be constrained to be an instance of the more general #$SpatialThing-Localized.  In either of the above situations #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic can be used to relate such functions to \"corresponding\" predicates, even though #$functionCorrespondingPredicate could not.  Still, it is usually preferable to use the more specific #$functionCorrespondingPredicate or (even better) #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical when possible.")
(comment instanceElementType "A binary #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that relates types of sets to the types of elements they have in common.  More precisely, <code>(#$instanceElementType SETTYPE COLLECTION)</code> means that every #$elementOf every instance of (see #$isa) <code>SETTYPE</code> is an instance of <code>COLLECTION</code>.  For example, both of these hold:
<pre>
  (#$instanceElementType #$PointSet #$Point)

  (#$instanceElementType #$Set-Mathematical #$Thing) . 
</pre>
See the corresponding function #$SetOfTypeFn, which takes a given collection to the collection of <i>all</i> sets whose elements are instances of the former.  And see #$typeGenls for a similar predicate that relates collections of <i>collections</i> (rather than collections of sets) to their common element types.")
(comment instanceListMemberType "A binary #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that relates types of #$Lists to the types of members they have in common.  More precisely, <code>(#$instanceListMemberType LISTTYPE COLLECTION)</code> means that every member (see #$memberOfList) of every instance (see #$isa) of <code>LISTTYPE</code> is an instance of <code>COLLECTION</code>.  For example, both of these hold:
<pre>
  (#$instanceListMemberType (#$ListOfTypeFn #$Character-Abstract) #$Character-Abstract)

  (#$instanceListMemberType #$List #$Thing) . 
</pre>
The first example above mentions the corresponding function #$ListOfTypeFn, which takes a given collection to the collection of <i>all</i> lists whose members are instances of the former.  Also see #$typeGenls for a similar predicate that relates collections of <i>collections</i> (rather than collections of lists) to their common element types.")
(comment SpaceRegionSet "A specialization of #$SpatialThingSet.  This is the
collection of all sets of space regions (see #$SpaceRegion).  A set is an instance of #$SpaceRegionSet if and only if it is an instance of (#$SetOfTypeFn #$SpaceRegion).  Indeed, the CycL constant <code>#$SpaceRegionSet</code> is a #$rewriteOf the non-atomic term <code>(#$SetOfTypeFn #$SpaceRegion)</code>.
<p>
See also the specialization #$HomogeneousSpaceRegionSet and its
specializations.")
(comment Cone "A specialization of both #$ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing and #$RoundObject.  Each instance of #$Cone is a three-dimensional geometric object with a circular base; the rest of the surface of the object is formed by lines joining each point of the periphery of the base to a common vertex.  Examples include both tangible objects (e.g. a solid traffic cone) and intangible space regions (e.g. the space swept out by rotating a particular right triangle about one of its sides).")
(comment EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet "A specialization of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegionSet (q.v.).  This is the collection of all sets of <i>equidimensional</i> (see #$spatiallyEquidimensional) homogeneous space regions.  A thing is an instance of #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet if and only if it is a set of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegions all of whose elements are of the same particular dimensionality (see #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions).")
(comment PointSet "A specialization of #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet (q.v.).  This is the collection of all sets of #$Points (q.v.).  That is, #$PointSet is equal to (#$SetOfTypeFn #$Point).")
(comment OpenGISGeometryCompositeBaseSet "A specialization of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegionSet (q.v.).  This is the collection of all sets whose members are either #$Surfaces, #$LineSegments, or #$Points.  The CycL constant <code>#$OpenGISGeometryCompositeBaseSet</code> is the #$rewriteOf the term 
<pre>
  (#$SetOfTypeFn (#$CollectionUnionFn (#$TheSet #$Point #$LineSegment
#$Surface))) .  
</pre> 
It was introduced in order to simplify the definition of #$OpenGISGeometryComposite (q.v.).  ")
(comment LinearRing "A specialization of #$LineString and #$ClosedLinearObject  (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$LinearRing is a geometrically simple, closed line-segment consisting of multiple (i.e. three or more) straight line-segments.  
<p>
Note that, in #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesMt, #$LinearRing corresponds to the type called 'LinearRing' in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL document.")
(comment MultiPoint "A specialization of #$ZeroDimensionalThing.  Each instance of #$MultiPoint is the mereological sum (see #$generalizedSpaceRegionSum) of one or more (and no more than a countable number of) #$Points (q.v.).  
<p>
Note that, in #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesMt, #$MultiPoint corresponds to the type called 'MultiPoint' in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL document.")
(comment MultiPolygon "A specialization of #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegion (q.v.).  Each instance of #$MultiPolygon is the mereological sum of one or more non-overlapping #$Polygons.  The boundaries of any two polygons in a multi-polygon may share at most a finite number of points.  
<p>
Note that, in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesMt, #$MultiPolygon corresponds to the type called 'MultiPolygon' in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL document.")
(comment MultiLineSegment "A specialization of #$OneDimensionalThing.  Each instance of #$MultiLineSegment is the mereological sum of one or more non-overlapping #$LineSegments.  Two distinct line-segment parts of a
multi-line-segment may only meet at their endpoints. 
<p>
Note that, in #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesMt, #$MultiLineSegment corresponds to the
type called 'MultiCurve' in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL document.")
(comment MultiLineString "A specialization of #$OneDimensionalThing.  Each instance of #$MultiLineString is the mereological sum of one or more non-overlapping #$LineStrings (q.v.).  Two distinct line-string parts of a multi-line-string may intersect only at their endpoints. 
<p>
Note that, in #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesMt, #$MultiLineString corresponds to the type called 'MultiLineString' in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL document.")
(comment MultiSurface "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalThing.  Each instance of #$MultiSurface is the mereological sum of one or more non-overlapping #$Surfaces (q.v.).  The boundaries of any two #$Surfaces that are parts of a #$MultiSurface may share at most a finite number of points. 
<p>
Note that, in #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesMt, #$MultiSurface corresponds to the type called 'MultiSurface' in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL document. ")
(comment TextualPCW "A specialization of #$PropositionalConceptualWork. Each instance of #$TextualPCW is an abstract work whose propositional content is expressed, at least in substantial part, in text. Positive examples include instances of #$Novel-CW, #$Article-PCW, #$Resume, and #$RestaurantMenu.  Negative examples include episodes of an instance of #$TVShow-CW.  #$TextualPCWs may include non-textual components which expand on or otherwise contribute to the propositional content of the work, but the propositional content of the #$TextualPCW could, alternatively, be expressed entirely in text.")
(comment Paragraph-TextualPCW "A specialization of #$StructurallySpecificWork and #$TextualPCW.  An instance of #$Paragraph-TextualPCW is a propositional work whose informational structure (see #$infoStructureOfCW) is a paragraph (c.f., #$NLParagraph).")
(comment properSubcollectionOfNamedWithID "The corresponding predicate (#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical) of #$ProperSubcollectionNamedFn-Ternary.")
(comment Haven "The collection of sanctuaries, resting places of a certain sort and refuges.")
(comment boundaryPointSetOfSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) with the set of points that are incident in its boundary. 
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$boundaryPointSetOfSpaceRegion REGION POINT-SET)</code> is true if and only if, for any instance <code>POINT</code> of #$Point, <code>(#$incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> implies <code>(#$elementOf POINT POINT-SET)</code> and vice versa.  
<p>
See also #$pointSetOfSpaceRegion, #$interiorPointSetOfSpaceRegion, and #$exteriorPointSetOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment Escarpment "A specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  An instance of #$Escarpment is an elongated and comparatively steep slope separating flat or gently sloping areas.")
(comment parseVisualizationTable "(#$parseVisualizationTable WIDTH P-COUNT V-COUNT TOTAL-COUNT TABLE) means that TABLE is an HTML table source string graphically illustrating that of TOTAL-COUNT items to parse, P-COUNT were parsed, and V-COUNT had validated parses.")
(comment parseVisualizationTableForPeg "(#$parseVisualizationTableForPeg PEG WIDTH TABLE) means that TABLE is an HTML table source string graphically illustrating the extent to which PEG has rewrites with successful parses.")
(comment hypothesisVisualizationTableForPeg "(#$hypothesisVisualizationTableForPeg PEG WIDTH TABLE) means that TABLE is an HTML table source string graphically illustrating the extent to which PEG has rewrites with hypothesized interpretations.")
(comment hypothesisVisualizationTable "(#$hypothesisVisualizationTable WIDTH H-COUNT V-COUNT TABLE) means that TABLE is an HTML table source string graphically illustrating that for a given input, H-COUNT interpretations were hypothesized, and V-COUNT of those were validated.")
(comment pegUsesToken "(#$pegUsesToken PEG TOKEN) means that the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg was constructed using the instance of #$CycParsingToken TOKEN.  For functions that build pegs from tokens, see #$RewriteFromLinksFn and #$ConstituentPhraseOfTypeFromTokensFn.")
(comment Line "A specialization of #$LinearObject-OneDimensional, #$LinearObject-Unbounded, and #$LinearObject-Infinite (qq.v.).  Instances of #$Line are one-dimensional, unbounded, infinite portions of space.  They might be straight or curved.  
<p>
Specializations of #$Line include #$Line-Planar and #$Line-NonPlanar.  And  cf. #$Point, #$Surface, and #$SpaceChunk.")
(comment relationshipStatus "(#$relationshipStatus PERSON STATUS) means that PERSON has the #$PersonTypeByPersonalRelationshipStatus STATUS. For example, (#$relationshipStatus #$BillClinton #$Married).")
(comment EquidimensionalSpatialPredicate "The collection of polyadic #$SpatialPredicates that are used to relate only spatial things that occupy the same number of dimensions (see #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions).
<p>
The most general <i>binary</i> #$EquidimensionalSpatialPredicate is #$spatiallyEquidimensional.  A notable specialization of this predicate-type is #$RCCBinaryPredicate.
<p>
Note that most of the equidimensional spatial predicates currently refied in the Cyc Knowledge Base admit arguments (see #$admittedArgument) of different dimensions, but only <i>hold</i> between arguments that are of the same dimensionality (see e.g. #$connectedSpaceRegions).    Some equidimensional predicates, however, only admit arguments of one particular dimensionality (see e.g. #$parallelLines).")
(comment skewLines "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$disconnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.) that holds between straight lines (see #$Line-Straight) that do <i>not</i> lie in a common plane.  <code>(#$skewLines LINE1 LINE2)</code> holds if and only if <code>(#$not (#$coplanarLines LINE1 LINE2))</code> holds.")
(comment StraightLineSegmentSet "A specialization of #$LineStringSet (q.v.).  This is the
collection of all sets whose members are instances of #$LineSegment-Straight.  The CycL constant <code>#$StraightLineSegmentSet</code> is the #$rewriteOf the term
<pre>
  (#$SetOfTypeFn #$LineSegment-Straight).")
(comment folderContainsResource "A specialization of #$subInformation. (#$folderContainsResource FOLDER OBJ) means that FOLDER, an instance of #$KEInteractionFolder) has OBJ, a #$KEInteractionResource, as part of its contents.")
(comment normativeSpecificationDescribesSituationType "The predicate #$normativeSpecificationDescribesSituationType is used to associate instances of #$NormativeSpecification with the sub-collections of #$Situation that are their focal content.  <code>(#$normativeSpecificationDescribesSituationType SPEC TYPE)</code> means that the content of #$NormativeSpecification <code>SPEC</code> is directed at the occurence of an instance of <code>TYPE</code>.  This specification is vague because the attitude which an agent will have toward an instance of <code>TYPE</code> depends on the specialization of #$NormativeSpecification to which <code>SPEC</code> belongs.  For example, if <code>SPEC</code> is an instance of #$NormativeSpecification-Avoidance, then the relevant agents are trying to prevent an instance of TYPE from occurring.  The specification of <code>SPEC</code> can be completed using the predicates #$normativeSpecificationDescribesRoleForInd and #$normativeSpecificationDescribesRoleForType (q.v.).")
(comment collinearPoints "A ternary #$SpaceRegionPredicate. <code>(#$collinearPoints POINT1 POINT2 POINT3)</code> is true if and only if the #$Points <code>POINT1</code>, <code>POINT2</code>, and <code>POINT3</code> all lie on a common #$Line-Straight.")
(comment reflexiveInArgs "A #$MetaPredicate that is used to state that a given predicate is 'reflexive' in a certain sense with respect to two of its argument-places.   <code>(#$reflexiveInArgs PRED M N)</code> means that whenever <code>PRED</code> holds with a given thing in its <code>M</code>th (or <code>N</code>th) argument-place, then it also holds with that same thing in <i>both</i> its <code>M</code>th <i>and</i> <code>N</code>th argument-places (keeping all the other arguments fixed).
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$reflexiveInArgs PRED M N)</code> means that if <code>PRED</code> holds of a given sequence of arguments <code>ARGS</code>, then it also holds of both (i) the sequence obtained by replacing the <code>N</code>th item in <code>ARGS</code> with a (repeat occurrence) of the <code>M</code>th item and (ii) the sequence obtained by replacing the <code>M</code>th item in <code>ARGS</code> with a (repeat occurrence) of the <code>N</code>th item.  Put schematically (and supposing without loss of generality that <code>M<N)</code>, if this holds:
<p>
(1)     <code>(PRED ... THING<sub>M</sub> ... THING<sub>N</sub> ...)</code>
<p>
then so do both of these:
<p>
(2a)        <code>(PRED ... THING<sub>M</sub> ... THING<sub>M</sub> ...)</code>
<p>
(2b)        <code>(PRED ... THING<sub>N</sub> ... THING<sub>N</sub> ...)</code>
<p>
where the argument-sequence in (2a) is just like that in (1) except for having <code>THING<sub>M</sub></code> as its <code>M</code>th <i>and</i> <code>N</code>th items (and analogously for (2b) with respect to <code>THING<sub>N</sub>)</code>.
<p>
For example, a ternary predicate called <i><code>compatriotsOf</code></i> that took as its arguments two persons (<i>not</i> constrained to be distinct) and a geopolitical entity would be reflexive in its first and second arguments.
<p>
Every #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate (q.v.) is of course reflexive in its (only) two argument-places.  But note that the converse is not true: a binary predicate <code>BINPRED</code> such that <code>(#$reflexiveInArgs BINPRED 1 2)</code> holds might not be fully reflexive.  For the reflexive-in-args property only entails that if a thing <code>THING</code> stands in the <code>BINPRED</code> relation to something (in either argument-place) then <code>(BINPRED THING THING)</code> holds, while a #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate relates <i>everything</i> (that satisfies the predicate's argument-type constraints) to itself.  Thus, a binary predicate <i><code>attendsTheSameChurchAs</code></i> would be #$reflexiveInArgs in its two argument-places, but not a #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate.")
(comment copunctualLines "A ternary #$SpaceRegionPredicate that holds among certain straight lines (see #$Line-Straight).  <code>(#$copunctualLines LINE1 LINE2 LINE3)</code> is true if and only if <code>LINE1</code>, <code>LINE2</code>, and <code>LINE3</code> all intersect at a common #$Point.")
(comment OpenGISGeometry "A specialization of #$GeometricallyDescribableThing. This is the root collection defined in the simple features data model of the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL (q.v.) document.  #$OpenGISGeometry is #$partitionedInto the subcollections #$Point, #$LineSegment, #$Surface, #$OpenGISGeometryComposite, and #$NullBoundary.")
(comment OpenGISGeometryType "A #$SecondOrderCollection containing all of the types of geometries (specializations of #$OpenGISGeometry) that are defined in the geometry collection hierarchy of the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL (q.v.) document.")
(disjointWith InfiniteSpatialThing FiniteSpatialThing)
(disjointWith LinearObject-Infinite LinearObject-Finite)
(comment subLFunctionForNAUTVerification-GG "(#$subLFunctionForNAUTVerification-GG COL (#$SubLQuoteFn SUBL-FUNC)) means that SUBL-FUNC represents the NAUT verification method (within GAF-gathering) that should be used whenever the NAUT is an instance of COL.")
(comment Torus "A specialization of both #$RoundObject and #$ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all tori, or \"donut-shaped\" objects, whether tangible or intangible.  More precisely, each instance of #$Torus is a three-dimensional object generated by the revolution of an ellipse (especially a circle) about any line in its plane and external to it. Cf. #$RingShapedObject.")
(comment PlusAll "A #$NumericQuantifier function that takes a #$SetOrCollection and a #$UnaryFunction as its arguments. (#$PlusAll <b>SETORCOL FUNC</b>) is the result of adding up the results of successively applying <b>FUNC</b> to each element of <b>SETORCOL</b>.  For example:
<pre>
    (#$PlusAll (#$TheSet 4 9) #$SquareRootFn)
  = (#$PlusFn (#$SquareRootFn 4) (#$SquareRootFn 9))
  = (#$PlusFn 2 3)
  = 5.")
(comment Sphere "A specialization of #$Spheroid (q.v.). Each instance of #$Sphere is a two-dimensional unbounded surface, embedded in a three-dimensional space, and such that each point on it is equidistant from some given point. 
<p>
See also #$SphericalSolid, each instance of which is an (intangible or tangible) three-dimensional object whose boundary is or approximates a #$Sphere.")
(comment RoundObject "A specialization of #$ShapedObject. Each instance of #$RoundObject is a tangible or intangible thing with an (at least nearly) round or elliptical shape in a least one dimension.  A round object might be one-dimensional (e.g. a ring that bounds a 2-D #$Circle), two-dimensional (e.g. an intangible #$Ellipse) or three-dimensional (e.g. a #$Sphere or a chicken egg). 
<p>
Other specializations of #$RoundObject include #$Cone, #$Ball, #$Hemisphere, and #$RingShapedObject.")
(comment ShapePredicate "A #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$PhysicalFeaturePredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ShapePredicate is a predicate used to state something about the shape of a spatial object (see #$SpatialThingTypeByShape).  The most general such predicate is #$objectShapeType.")
(comment SphericalSolid "A specialization of #$EllipsoidalSolid (q.v.).  Each instance of #$SphericalSolid is a (tangible or intangible) three-dimensional object whose boundary is or approximates an #$Sphere (q.v.)
<p>
Examples of spherical solids include tangible objects such as billiard balls, as well as intangible solid-sphere-shaped #$SpaceRegions.")
(comment linkGrammarLinkTypeBasicLink "(#$linkGrammarLinkTypeBasicLink LINK-TYPE LINK) means that all instances of LINK-TYPE are variants (c.f., #$linkVariants) of the instance of #$LinkParserLinkPredicate LINK.")
(comment LexicalHypothesisMicrotheory "A specialization of #$LexicalDataMicrotheory.  Each instance contains lexical assertions that have been hypothesized by Cyc and will either be sufficiently confirmed and promoted to a lexicon of the relevant language, or disconfirmed and abandoned.")
(comment extensionParametersOfObject "This predicate relates an object to its quantified extensions in space.  (#$extensionParametersOfObject OBJ DIST) means that DIST is a magnitude in which OBJ is extended in some spatial dimension.  When making assertions about a particular object use a more specific predicate whenever possible, e.g. #$heightOfObject, #$widthOfObject, #$diameterOfObject.")
(comment maximumExtensionParameterOfObject "This predicate relates an object to the distance that is is largest extent.  For instance (#$maximumExtensionParameterOfObject OBJ DIST)
means that length, height and width and diameter (where applicable)
are all less than or equal to DIST and one or more of the extensions is equal to
DIST.  More straightforwardly, we might define this as the length of the longest line segment that passes through the object and whose end points are on the outer surface of the object.  Note that this will often be the length of the object but there are some situations in which the length won't be the greatest extension.  Also note that this predicate does not relate all spatial things to a distance.  For instance, spatially disconnected things would not have a greatest extension.")
(comment simpleQueryForFormula "This is the corresponding predicate of the function SimpleQueryForFormulaFn. An assertion of the form (simpleQueryForFormula QUERY FORMULA) means that the CycLQuerySpecification QUERY is a specification that takes the CycLSentence-Askable FORMULA as its InferenceSentenceParameter and assumes defaults for all of of the other software parameters.")
(comment SimpleQueryForFormulaFn "An expression of the form (SimpleQueryForFormulaFn FORMULA) is used to denote an instance of ConstructedCycLQuerySpecification. FORMULA is a instance of CycLSentence-Askable and this formula denotes the simplest query specification for that formula, where simplicity refers to the use of default parameters provided by the Query tool as elements of the specification.")
(comment Trestle "#$Trestle is a specialization of #$Bridge. #$Trestles are those brodges designed for use by #$Train-TransportationDevices. They may also feature footpaths, but the primary #$PathType of a #$Trestle is a #$Railway.")
(comment CargoAirplane "The collection of all #$Airplanes designed and/or used for carrying cargo, as opposed to passengers.")
(comment wordSenseExtension "<code>(#$wordSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION)</code> means that the use of the word <code>WORD</code> with speech part <code>SPEECH-PART-2</code> to mean <code>EXTENSION</code> is an extension of one sense of <code>WORD</code> -- in particular the sense of <code>WORD</code> with speech part </code>SPEECH-PART-1</code> and denotation <code>DENOTATION</code>.  For example, 
<pre>
(#$wordSenseExtension #$Chicken-TheWord #$CountNoun #$Chicken #$MassNoun #$Chicken-Meat)</pre>
means that the use of \"chicken\" as a mass noun to refer to chicken meat is an extension of the count noun sense of \"chicken\" meaning the bird.")
(comment predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromArgToArg "<code>(#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromArgToArg PREDICATE N M)</code> means that PREDICATE, an instance of #$SenseExtensionPredicate, licenses the extension of the sense of some word that denotes arguments appearing in its Nth place to arguments appearing in its Mth place.  For example, 
<pre>
(#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromArgToArg #$woodTypeOfPlantType 2 1)</pre>
means that for <code>WOOD-TYPE</code> and <code>PLANT-TYPE</code> such that
<pre>
(#$woodTypeOfPlantType WOOD-TYPE PLANT-TYPE),</pre>
there is likely to be some word(s) that denotes <code>PLANT-TYPE</code> whose sense can be extended to also denote <code>WOOD-TYPE</code>.  For example, there is a count-noun sense of the word \"redwood\" (see #$Redwood-TheWord) that denotes the tree (c.f., #$RedwoodTree); that the collection of such trees bears the #$woodTypeOfPlantType relation to the collection of samples of wood from such trees, licenses a mass-noun sense extension of \"redwood\" to denote that second collection.")
(comment binaryPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSToPOS1-2 "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  
<pre>
  (#$binaryPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSToPOS1-2 PREDICATE POS-1 POS-2)</pre> is shorthand for 
<pre>
  (#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg PREDICATE POS-1 1 POS-2 2)<pre>
where <code>PREDICATE</code> is restricted to instances of #$BinaryPredicate.")
(comment SenseExtendingPredicate "The collection of all instances of #$Predicate that broker a word sense extension -- i.e., license extending the sense of a word that is used to denote one of its arguments to cover another argument.")
(comment objectTypeDesigned "(#$objectTypeDesigned DESIGNING TYPE) means that in the instance of #$Designing, DESIGNING, the designing resulted in a design of a certain sub-type of TYPE.")
(comment servingTypeOfFoodOrDrinkType "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate. (#$servingTypeOfFoodOrDrinkType SERVING-TYPE FOOD-OR-DRINK-TYPE) identifies SERVING-TYPE as the collection of all complete servings of instances of FOOD-OR-DRINK-TYPE.  This is the predicate that corresponds to the function, #$FoodServingFn.")
(comment predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg "<code>(#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg PREDICATE POS-1 N POS-2 M)</code> means that PREDICATE, an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, licenses the extension of the sense of some word that denotes arguments appearing in its Nth place to arguments appearing in its Mth place.  For example, 
<pre>
(#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromArgToArg #$woodTypeOfPlantType #$CountNoun 1 #$MassNoun 2)</pre>
means that for <code>WOOD-TYPE</code> and <code>PLANT-TYPE</code> such that
<pre>
(#$woodTypeOfPlantType PLANT-TYPE WOOD-TYPE),</pre>
there is likely to be some word(s) that, as a count noun, denotes PLANT-TYPE whose sense can be extended to also denote WOOD-TYPE with a mass noun.  For example, there is a count-noun sense of the word \"redwood\" (see #$Redwood-TheWord) that denotes the tree (c.f., #$RedwoodTree); that the collection of such trees bears the #$woodTypeOfPlantType relation to the collection of samples of wood from such trees, licenses a mass-noun sense extension of \"redwood\" to denote that second collection.")
(comment slopeOfRegion "A #$NumericIntervalSlot that relates a geographical region to its amount of slope, typically given as a percentage. <code>(#$slopeOfRegion REGION (#$Percent NUM))</code> means that <code>REGION</code> has a slope of <code>NUM</code> percent (or <code>NUM</code> /100).  For example, if <code>REGION</code> has a slope of (#$Percent 10) then, when moving up (down) the slope, an object gains (loses) 10 units of vertical distance (elevation) for every 100 units of horizontal distance travelled.  A slope of 100% corresponds to a 1:1 ratio of vertical-over-horizontal distance, or a 45-degree angle.  A vertical (90-degree) cliff has an infinite slope.
<p>
The formula for converting an angle of <code>N</code> degrees (measured from the horizontal) to a slope is 
<pre>
  SLOPE = (#$TangentFn (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure N)) 
</pre>
To express <code>SLOPE</code> as a percentage, multiply it by 100 and apply the #$Percent function to the result:
<pre>
  (#$Percent (#$TimesFn SLOPE 100)) .
</pre>
Note that if <code>REGION</code> slopes in <i>two</i> directions with respect to the horizontal plane, #$slopeOfRegion relates it to whichever slope is the greatest.")
(comment Slope-FlatGradient "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$Slope-FlatGradient is a geographical region that has a slope of between 0% and 3% (see #$slopeOfRegion).
<p>
Note that, unlike the other instances of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel, #$Slope-FlatGradient is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.")
(comment Slope-VeryLowGradient "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-VeryLowGradient is a geographical region that has a slope of between 3% and 10% (see #$slopeOfRegion).")
(comment Slope-ModerateGradient "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-ModerateGradient is a geographical region that has a slope of between 15% and 30% (see #$slopeOfRegion).")
(comment Slope-HighGradient "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-HighGradient is a geographical region that has a slope of between 45% and 60% (see #$slopeOfRegion).")
(comment Slope-LowGradient "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-LowGradient is a geographical region that has a slope of between 10% and 15% (see #$slopeOfRegion).")
(comment Slope-ModeratelyHighGradient "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-ModeratelyHighGradient is a geographical region that has a slope of between 30% and 45% (see #$slopeOfRegion).")
(comment Slope-VeryHighGradient "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-VeryHighGradient is a geographical region that has a slope of between 60% and 100% (see #$slopeOfRegion).")
(comment Slope-ExtremelySteep "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-ExtremelySteep is a region with a slope (see #$slopeOfRegion) of between 100% and 375%.  In other words, such a region makes a 45- to 75-degree angle with the horizontal plane.")
(comment SlopeTypeByGradientLevel "A #$SecondOrderCollection instances of which are subcollections of #$LandTopographicalFeature that are grouped according to their level of slope.  More specifically, with the exception of #$Slope-FlatGradient (q.v.), each instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel is a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical (q.v.).")
(comment Slope-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature (q.v.) each instance of which is an inclined part of the Earth's surface.  See the instances of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel for some specializations of #$Slope-Topographical, grouped according to steepness.")
(comment VerticalProtrusion-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$Protrusion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$VerticalProtrusion-Topographical is a natural, upward protrusion from the surrounding part of the Earth's surface.  Specializations of this collection include #$Hill and #$Mountain.")
(comment Chickpea "A subcollection of #$Bean-Foodstuff. Also known as 'Garbanzo beans.'")
(comment expressionPegNumber "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot.  (#$expressionPegNumber PEG NUMBER) identifies NUMBER as the number of the instance PEG of #$NominalExpressionPeg.  For example, (#$expressionPegNumber ADog-Peg #$SingularExpression) means that the phrase that introduced ADog-Peg (presumably \"a dog\") was singular.")
(disjointWith SenseExtendingPredicate WFFConstraintSatisfactionPredicate)
(comment Protrusion "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$Protrusion is an outcropping that sticks out from, and is one of the #$externalParts of, some larger object.  Specializations of this collection include #$Mountain and #$Tail-BodyPart.")
(comment Slope-Vertical "An instance of #$SlopeTypeByGradientLevel (q.v.) and a specialization of #$Slope-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Slope-Vertical is a region with a slope so great as to be nearly (or absolutely) vertical.  Examples include the faces of #$Cliffs.
<p>
Note the the slope (see #$slopeOfRegion) of a absolutely vertical region is actually infinite.  Thus, such regions are better described in terms of the angles (see #$AngularDistance) they make with the horizontal plane.  We stipulate that a 
#$Slope-Vertical makes a 75- to 90-degree angle with the horizontal.")
(comment ContentModelMicrotheory "The collection of all instances of #$Microtheory that serve as content models for an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance.  A content model is the most general microtheory is a microtheory structure for reasoning about the meaning of a peg, and will have as spec-Mts a number of interpretation microtheories that posit possible interpretations for both the expression peg and sub-expression pegs.")
(comment fullName "(#$fullName X STRNG) means that #$Person X has the #$HumanNameString STRING as his or her complete name.  This includes #$firstName, #$lastName, and #$middleName or  #$middleNameInitial, but excludes titles.  Minimally, #$fullName should include a #$Person's #$firstName and #$lastName (or #$givenNames and #$HumanSurname).")
(comment culturallyConnectedToBeliefSystem "An assertion of the form (#$culturallyConnectedToBeliefSystem PERSON SYSTEM) states that the #$IndividualAgent PERSON is a member of a community that is characterized by the #$BeliefSystem SYSTEM; however, PERSON has not necessarily adopted the belief system. As an example, #$GeorgeCarlin bears this relationship to #$Catholicism because of his upbringing.  However, he does not conform to the strictures imposed by the religion and, so, it would be inaccurate to say (#$hasBeliefSystems #$GeorgeCarlin #$Catholicism).")
(comment officialBeliefSystem "An assertion of the form (#$officialBeliefSystem ORG SYSTEM) is used to relate an #$Organization to the #$BeliefSystem that it presents as its official ideology.  This predicate does not entail that the #$BeliefSystem will be useful in terms of predicting the behavior of #$Organization or that #$Organization will not violate the principles of #$BeliefSystem.")
(comment folderOfInterestToProject "<code>(#$folderOfInterestToProject FOLDER PROJECT)</code> means that the instance <code>FOLDER</code> of #$KEInteractionFolder is of interest to the instance <code>PROJECT</code> of #$Project.")
(disjointWith SenseExtendingPredicate MetaRelation)
(comment FamilyRelationsConcept "Instances of #$FamilyRelationsConcept are concepts which are included in the domain of family relationships.  Therefore, collections such as #$Family-SocialEntity, predicates such as #$siblings and #$children, and other family-related concepts are members of this class.")
(comment ConceptTypeByDomain "Each instance of #$ConceptTypeByDomain is a collection, all of whose elements can be considered concepts which belong to some domain of knowledge.  For example, #$FamilyRelationsConcept is the collection that includes predicates, such as #$siblings or #$children, collections, such as #$FamilyCohabitationUnit, and potentially other concepts that are part of the domain of family relationships.  It is important to note that instances of #$ConceptTypeByDomain are not quoted collections -- i.e., their elements are not the _terms_ used to represent the concepts, but instead are the concepts themselves.")
(comment MereotopologyConcept "Instances of #$MereotopologyConcept are concepts which are included in the domain of mereotopology; therefore, parts, boundaries and the topological relationships among them would be instances of this collection.")
(comment productTypeOriginatesIn "(#$productTypeOriginatesIn PROD-TYPE REGION) relates product types with the regions where the agents who are responsible for designing, financing, and planning the construction of that product type are found.  That is (#$productTypeOriginatesIn PROD-TYPE REGION) significant executive/creative/engineering/financial aspects of production of PROD-TYPE occur in REGION. Thus one would not assert (#$productTypeOriginatesIn #$JapaneseCar #$Texas-State) because mere fabrication of the cars occurs there; the funding and creative aspect happens in Japan.  One would assert  properly (#$productTypeOriginatesIn #$ToyotaCar #$Japan) and and describe their assembly with (#$productTypeManufacturedIn #$ToyotaCar #$Texas-State)")
(comment productTypeManufacturedIn "(#$productTypeManufacturedIn PROD-TYPE REGION) relates product types with the regions where instances of the collection are actually created.  So, REGION is one place where instances of PROD-TYPE actually come into being.  So, a product can be planned and designed in one place, but actually assembled in another.  The predicate #$productTypeManufacturedIn refers to this latter region.  The former relation between a product type and a region is covered by the predicate #$productTypeOriginatesIn. NOTE: there is no direct entailment relationship between these two predicates.")
(comment InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination "A sub-collection of #$ProgramSpecification.  Instances of #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination constitute part of the settings for inductive logic programs like #$ALEPH-ILPProgram.  Specifically, they enumerate the relevant predicates that will be investigated by the program while performing induction.")
(comment NounPhraseExpressionPeg "The collection of all instances of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that are used to represent occurences of instances of #$NounPhrase in a discourse.")
(comment Desertification "The collection of all events in which #$ArableLand becomes transformed into #$Desert.")
(comment FreeSpaceRegion "A specialization of #$SpaceChunk-Empirical and #$ConnectedSpaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of  #$FreeSpaceRegion is a connected, three-dimensional region of free (i.e. virtually empty) space located in the empirical universe (see #$TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical).  
<p>
Note that the precise meaning of \"free\" or \"empty\" here depends on the context.  In the context of subatomic physics, where \"empty\" is defined as containing no particles, a free space region would be a complete #$Vacuum.  In certain other contexts (e.g. #$AmbientConditionsMt) a free space region would be one occupied by nothing but air (see #$Atmosphere).  An undersea context might treat space regions filled with seawater as free.  
<p>
Note also that a #$FreeSpaceRegion is intangible, and not to be confused with the material (if any) that occupies it (cf. #$FreeSpaceContent).")
(comment contentOfFreeSpace "A specialization of #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial (q.v.) that relates a given #$FreeSpaceRegion to the content (if any) that fills it (see #$FreeSpaceContent).  <code>(#$contentOfFreeSpace CONTENT FREESPACE)</code> means that <code>CONTENT</code> is the piece of tangible fluid occupying the <code>FREESPACE</code>.  For example, this predicate relates the #$Air in your livingroom to the free space region subsumed by the interior of the room.")
(comment FreeSpaceContent "A specialization of #$FluidTangibleThing and #$SpatiallyContinuousThing.  Each instance of #$FreeSpaceContent is a connected, tangible fluid object that occupies a region of \"free\" space (see #$FreeSpaceRegion): a space region through which solid objects can move more or less freely.  
<p>
Examples of free space contents include the air in the interior of a room and the sky above a city.  In an underwater context, a free space content is likely to be an instance of liquid #$Water.
<p>
See also #$contentOfFreeSpace, which relates free space regions to their contents.")
(comment EquilateralPolygon "A specialization of #$Polygon (q.v.).  Each instance of #$EquilateralPolygon is a polygon whose sides are all of equal length.  See also #$EquiangularPolygon and #$RegularPolygon.")
(comment Hexagram "A subcollection of #$Star-PlaneFigure. Each instance is an equiangular polygon formed by extending each of the sides of a regular hexagon to form two partially-overlapping equilateral triangles.")
(comment AnalogyConstant "The collection of constants used in Northestern University's structure-mapping analogical reasoning system.")
(comment UbiquitousRelationForAnalogy "The collection of #$Relations that should be treated as uninteresting for 
analogical reasoning.")
(comment specPredOfPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg "<code>(#$specPredOfPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg PREDICATE POS-1 N POS-2 M)</code> means that there exists an instance SPECPRED of #$Predicate such that:<br>
<br>
<code>(#$genlPreds SPECPRED PREDICATE)</code>, and
<br><br><code>(#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg SPECPRED POS-1 N POS-2 M)</code>.<br><br>
As such, <code>SPECPRED</code> is an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate.  Since the complete extent of #$Predicate is not known, <code>SPECPRED</code> might not be reified in Cyc; #$specPredOfPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg thus allows us to use lexical and commonsense data to hypothesize the existence of a #$SenseExtendingPredicate.  For example, #$conceptuallyRelated is often used to relate two concepts denoted by the same word (e.g., #$Dancing and #$Dance-Party, both of which are denoted by #$Dance-TheWord (with different parts-of-speech).  However, #$conceptuallyRelated serves often as a promissory note, indicating the existence of a more specific relation that hasn't be represented in the KB yet.  If <code>(#$conceptuallyRelated VERB-DENOTATION COUNT-NOUN-DENOTATION)</code> can be proved for enough pairs of values for <code>VERB-DENOTATION</code> and <code>COUNT-NOUN-DENOTATION</code>, this could license concluding
<pre>
(#$specPredOfPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg #$conceptuallyRelated #$Verb 1 #$CountNoun 2),</pre> effectively launching a sense-extension hypothesis (c.f., #$SenseExtensionHypothesisMicrotheory) in which there exists a spec-pred of #$conceptuallyRelated that does license this sort of sense-extension.  For more information, see #$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg.")
(comment AgriculturalFacility "The collection of geographical regions occupied by instances of #$FarmTheOrganization. Instances of #$AgriculturalFacility have parts that can include buildings, fields or reservoirs. They can be in one piece or many.")
(comment FishFarm "Each instance of #$FishFarm is an #$AgriculturalFacility operated by an organization which raises and sells #$Fish.  It will typically have a number of ponds, tanks or enclosures within a larger body of water in which fish are kept in order to harvest for food.")
(comment AdverseWeatherArea "A specialization of #$OutdoorLocation.  Each instance of this collection is a location that is experiencing adverse weather, including #$HazyArea, #$SleetyArea, #$StormyArea, etc.")
(comment DegreeOfCloudiness "A specialization of #$OutdoorLocationByWeatherType.  Each instance of #$DegreeOfCloudiness is a collection of outdoor locations (and thus a specialization of #$OutdoorLocation) that all share a particular degree of cloud cover. Instances of #$DegreeOfCloudiness include #$CloudlessArea, #$PartiallyCloudCoveredArea, and #$CompletelyCloudCoveredArea.")
(comment specificationDocumentationDataSentence "A binary #$SummaryPredicate and specialization of #$interestingSentence that relates an #$NormativeSpecification to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  (#$specificationDocumentationDataSentence N-SPEC SENTENCE) means that SENTENCE documents some fact about N-SPEC.")
(comment between "An instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$between THIS THAT MID)</code> means that <code>MID</code> is in the space separating <code>THIS</code> and <code>THAT</code>, and is directly between them.  Depending on the context, \"directly between\" might imply that <code>MID</code> lies on a straight line between <code>THIS</code> and <code>THAT</code>, or that it lies on a great circle between them.
<p>
For the notion of \"betweenness\" along a particular physical path (e.g. Austin is between Dallas and San Antonio on Highway I-35), or on some trajectory, see #$betweenOnPath.")
(comment Hill "A specialization of #$VerticalProtrusion-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Hill is a convex protrusion from the surface of a planet that is smaller than a #$Mountain (q.v.).")
(comment wordSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred "<code>(#$wordSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION BINARY-PREDICATE)</code> means that the fact that <pre>
   (#$wordSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION)</pre> is explained <i>in part</i> by the fact that <pre>
   (BINARY-PREDICATE ARG1 ARG2)</pre> for some <code>ARG1</code> and <code>ARG2</code> such that 
<pre>
   (#$denotation WORD ARG1 SPEECH-PART-1 N)</pre> for some N. <p>The reason why this sense extension is only <i>partly</i> explained by the above considerations reflects the fact that due to Cyc's incomplete knowledge, there will be many cases where BINARY-PREDICATE is not an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and so cannot <i>reliably</i> broker sense extensions from SPEECH-PART-1 to SPEECH-PART-2 for any word denoting one of its arg1s.  Rather, there is a possibly unreified specialization SPEC-PRED of BINARY-PRED (see #$genlPreds) that is an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and the fact that (SPEC-PRED ARG1 ARG2) fully explains the sense extension in question.  ")
(comment wordSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred-Inverse "<code>(#$wordSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred-Inverse WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION BINARY-PREDICATE)</code> means that the fact that <pre>
   (#$wordSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION)</pre> is explained <i>in part</i> by the fact that <pre>
   (BINARY-PREDICATE ARG1 ARG2)</pre> for some <code>ARG1</code> and <code>ARG2</code> such that 
<pre>
   (#$denotation WORD ARG2 SPEECH-PART-1 N)</pre> for some N. <p>The reason why this sense extension is only <i>partly</i> explained by the above considerations reflects the fact that due to Cyc's incomplete knowledge, there will be many cases where BINARY-PREDICATE is not an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and so cannot <i>reliably</i> broker sense extensions from SPEECH-PART-1 to SPEECH-PART-2 for any word denoting one of its arg1s.  Rather, there is a possibly unreified specialization SPEC-PRED of BINARY-PRED (see #$genlPreds) that is an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and the fact that (SPEC-PRED ARG1 ARG2) fully explains the sense extension in question.  ")
(comment DimensionOfSpatialThingFn "<code>(#$DimensionOfSpatialThingFn REGION) = DIMENSION</code> where
<code>DIMENSION</code> is the maximum spatial dimension of
<code>REGION</code>. The <i>dimension.</i> of a #$SpatialThing is a
topological measure of its extension in space. Roughly speaking, it is
the number of coordinates needed to specify a point on the object. For
example, a #$Rectangle is two-dimensional, while a #$Cube is
three-dimensional. In some cases, <code>REGION</code> may be the
mereological sum of regions of different dimensions. In this case,
<code>DIMENSION</code> is the maximum dimension of all such parts of
<code>REGION</code>. The dimension of an object is sometimes also
called its <i>dimensionality</i>. See also the corresponding predicate,
#$maximalDimensionOfRegion.")
(comment minimumEnvelopeForGeometry "(#$minimumEnvelopeForGeometry GEOMETRY ENVELOPE) means that
ENVELOPE is the minimum subsuming bounding box (rectangular region)
which subsumes GEOMETRY.")
(comment WellKnownTextString "#$WellKnownTextString is the collection of all
#$CharacterStrings which are valid language constructs in the Well
Known Text (WKT) representation of a geometry, as defined in section
3.2.5 of the <b>OpenGIS Simple Features Specification for SQL</b>.
Positive examples of grammatically valid WKT strings include: 'POINT
(1 4)' and 'LINESTRING (10 3, 4 20, 30 40)'.")
(comment dimensionOfSpatialThing "<code>(#$dimensionOfSpatialThing REGION DIMENSION)</code>
means that the maximum spatial dimension of <code>REGION</code> is
<code>DIMENSION</code>. The <i>dimension.</i> of a #$SpatialThing is a
topological measure of its extension in space. Roughly speaking, it is
the number of coordinates needed to specify a point on the object. For
example, a #$Rectangle is two-dimensional, while a #$Cube is
three-dimensional. In some cases, <code>REGION</code> may be the
mereological sum of regions of different dimensions. In this case,
<code>DIMENSION</code> is the maximum dimension of all such parts of
<code>REGION</code>. The dimension of an object is sometimes also
called its <i>dimensionality</i>. See also the function
#$DimensionOfSpatialThingFn.")
(comment OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather is a collection of #$OutdoorLocations with similar weather. Instances include #$SnowyArea, #$SunnyArea, and #$CloudyArea.")
(comment RainyArea "An #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather and a specialization of #$CloudyArea.  Each instance of #$RainyArea is an #$OutdoorLocation at which it is raining.  If the rain is heavy, the location is probably also a #$StormyArea.")
(comment StormyArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather and 
a specialization of both #$CloudyArea and #$AdverseWeatherArea. Each instance of #$StormyArea is an #$OutdoorLocation that has moderate or heavy cloud cover and is undergoing moderate to heavy precipitation (in the form of rain, snow, sleet, or hail).")
(comment CloudyArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather and 
a specialization of #$OutdoorLocation. Each instance of #$CloudyArea is an #$OutdoorLocation that has at least some cloud cover, and this cloud 
cover is not located exclusively at ground-level (in which case the 
location would be an instance of #$FoggyArea instead).")
(comment MistyArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather that is characterized by the atmosphere being suspended with a collection of microscopic water droplets that reduces visibility, but not as much as fog.  Mist is often confused with drizzle.")
(comment FoggyArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather.  Each instance of #$FoggyArea is an instance of #$OutdoorLocation covered by a ground-level cloud.")
(comment GustyArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather.  Each instance of #$GustyArea is an instance of #$OutdoorLocation in which the wind is subject to sudden changes in direction and intensity.")
(comment SnowyArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather and a specialization of #$CloudyArea. Each instance of #$SnowyArea is an outdoor location where it is snowing.")
(comment SunnyArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather and a specialization of #$OutdoorLocation.  Each instance of #$SunnyArea is an instance of #$OutdoorLocation where the weather is sunny.")
(comment ClearWeatherArea "A current condition of clear visibility, not overcast, nor foggy, nor snowy, nor rainy. #$ClearWeatherArea does permit some minimal degree of cloud cover, but not greater than partially cloudy.")
(comment OutdoorLocationTypeByIlluminationSource "A collection of collections and specialization of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByAmbientCondition.  Each instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByIlluminationSource is a collection of #$OutdoorLocations with similar sources of illumination. Instances include #$AreaIlluminatedByMoonlight and #$AreaIlluminatedBySunlight.")
(comment OutdoorLocationTypeByAmbientCondition "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$OutdoorLocationTypeByAmbientCondition is a collection of #$OutdoorLocations with similar ambient conditions. An important specialization of this is #$OutdoorLocationTypeByWeather, which includes outdoor location types like  #$SnowyArea, #$SunnyArea, and #$CloudyArea.")
(comment Prairie "A specialization of #$Grassland.  Each instance of #$Prairie is an area where long grasses are dominant.  Praires are found in central North America, eastern South Africa and the Pampas of Argentina.")
(comment Savannah "A specialization of #$Grassland.  Each instance of  #$Savannah is an area in which grasses are interspersed with scattered thorn bushes or deciduous trees.  Savannahs are found to the north and south of the #$TropicalRainForest regions of South America and central Africa and around the desert fringes of Australia.")
(comment RegionTypeByTerrain "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$RegionTypeByTerrain is a collection of instances of #$GeographicalRegions with a similar type of terrain.  Each such instance represents a particular type of terrain feature.  Notable instances include #$Prairie, #$TropicalRainForest, and #$Tundra.")
(comment predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromNamestringPredInArgToNamestringPredInArg "<code>(#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromNamestringPredInArgToNamestringPredInArg PREDICATE NSPRED-1 N NSPRED-2 M)</code> means that <code>PREDICATE</code>, an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, licenses the extension of the sense of a name <code>NAME</code> for a thing <code>THING</code> to some other thing <code>OTHER</code> iff:
<p>1) <code>S</code> is a true sentence such that
<pre>
   (#$operatorFormulas PREDICATE S)

   (#$argN THING N S)

   (#$argN OTHER M S),</pre>
2) <code>NAME</code> is a name for <code>THING</code> such that
<pre>
   (NSPRED-1 THING NAME)</pre>
and<br><br>
3) the extension of <code>NAME</code> to <code>OTHER</code> is such that
<pre>
   (NSPRED-2 OTHER NAME).</pre>
For example, 
<pre>
   (#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromNamestringPredInArgToNamestringPredInArg #$territoryOf #$placeName-Standard 1 #$namestring 2)</pre>
means that where both of the following hold:
<pre>
   (#$territoryOf GEO-AGENT TERRITORY)

   (#$placeName-Standard GEO-AGENT NAME),</pre>
one is licensed to extend the use of <code>NAME</code> to refer to <code>TERRITORY</code> via
<pre>
   (#$nameString TERRITORY NAME).</pre>
See also #$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg, which operates in a way similar to this predicate, but pertains to the sense extension of instances of #$LexicalWord.")
(comment agentPerformsSimilarActs "<code>(#$agentPerformsSimilarActs AGT ACTION)</code> holds just in case AGT has been the performer of at least one act that is sufficiently similar to ACTION.  What counts as \"similar\" is context dependent.  In general, the same location, type, and device used are almost always going to count towards the acts being similar.  In terrorism contexts, similarity of target types also counts towards similarity of acts.")
(comment schoolSportsTeams "<code>(#$schoolSportsTeams SCHOOL SPORTS-TEAM)</code> means that the instance <code>SPORTS-TEAM</code> of #$SportsTeam is a team affiliated with the instance of #$EducationalOrganization, <code>SCHOOL</code>.  Typically, this means that the team's coaches are employees or students of <code>SCHOOL</code> and that the teams players are students at <code>SCHOOL</code>.  Note that #$schoolSportsTeams is functional in its first argument-place.")
(comment sourceOfExpression "(#$sourceOfExpression EXPR SOURCE) means that the #$InformationStore SOURCE is a source of EXPR, in that SOURCE claims that EXPR, or SOURCE is an author of a #$ConceptualWork that contains the information expressed in EXPR.")
(comment situationIsSimilarToSitWithRespectToType "<code>(#$situationIsSimilarToSitWithRespectToType SIT1 SIT2 TYPE)</code> holds just in case SIT1 has the same value for TYPE as SIT2 based on the paradigm relation that relates instances of TYPE to situation.")
(comment TimeDependentCollection "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$TimeDependentCollection is a collection whose membership changes over time.  For example, (#$LiquidFn #$Water) is a time dependent collection.  A given portion of water might be an instance of (#$LiquidFn #$Water) in the summer, and an instance of the disjoint collection (#$SolidFn #$Water) in the winter.  In contrast, #$Integer is a #$TimeIndependentCollection (q.v.) because the collection of integers does not change its membership over time.")
(comment populationGrowthRateDuring "An assertion of the form (#$populationGrowthRateDuring PLACE ORG-TYPE PERCENT DATE) states that measurements made of the population of #$OrganismClassificationType ORG-TYPE  for the #$Date DATE indicate that the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE experienced a PERCENT rate of growth in that population.  For example, an assertion of the form (#$populationGrowthRateDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens (#$PercentFn 1) (#$YearFn 2005)) says that records indicate that the human population of #$Syria grew by 1% during 2005. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment birthRateDuring "An assertion of the form (#$birthRateDuring  PLACE ORG-TYPE  REAL DATE) states that measurements made of the population for the #$Date DATE indicate that for every 1000 people in the population of the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE, REAL number of people were born. For instance, an expression of the form (#$birthRateDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens 10.2 (#$YearFn 2005)) states that records indicate that during 2005, for every 1000 citizens of #$Syria, 10.2 children were born. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment deathRateDuring "An assertion of the form (#$deathRateDuring  PLACE ORG-TYPE  REAL  DATE) states that measurements made of the population during the #$Date DATE indicate that for every 1000 people in the population of the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE, REAL number of people died. For example, an assertion of the form (#$deathRateDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens 3.4 (#$YearFn 2005)) states that records indicate that during 2005, for every 1000 citizens of #$Syria, 3.4 people died. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment infantMortalityRateDuring "An assertion of the form (#$infantMortalityRateDuring  PLACE ORG-TYPE  REAL DATE) states that measurements of the population indicate that for every 1000 infants born in the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE during the #$Date DATE, REAL of those infants died. For example, an assertion of the form (#$infantMortalityRateDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens 3.4 (#$YearFn 2005)) states that records indicate that during 2005, for every 1000 children that were born in #$Syria, 3.4 of those same children died.  This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment netMigrationRateDuring "An assertion of the form (#$netMigrationRateDuring  PLACE ORG-TYPE  REAL DATE) states that measurements made of the situation during the #$Date DATE indicate that there there was a total change in the population of PLACE of REAL persons for every 1000 people. If this number is positive, then the immigration surpassed emigration.  If this number is negative, then emigration surpassed immigration.  For example, an assertion of the form (#$netMigrationRateDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens 3.4 (YearFn 2005)) states that records indicate that for every 1000 citizens of #$Syria, 3.4 people migrated to #$Syria during 2005. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment prevelanceOfConditionDuring "An assertion of the form (#$prevelanceOfConditionDuring PLACE  ORG-TYPE CONDITION PERCENT DATE) states that measurements made of the population of the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE for the #$Date DATE indicate that PERCENT of the population were suffering from the #$PhysiologicalConditionType CONDITION.  For example, an assertion of the form (#$prevelanceOfConditionDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens #$AIDS (#$Percent .05) (#$YearFn 2005) would state that records indicate that during 2005, .05% of the human population of #$Syria suffer from #$AIDS.  This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment deathsDueToDuring "An assertion of the form (#$deathDueToDuring PLACE  ORG-TYPE TYPE N DATE) states that measurements made of the population of the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE during the #$Date DATE indicate that N instances of #$Person died as a result of their playing the role of #$objectActedOn in some occurrence of TYPE, a subcollection of #$Situation. For instance, an assertion of the form (#$deathsDueToDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens #$AIDS 200 (#$YearFn 2005)) would state that records indicate that during 2005, 200 people died from #$AIDS in #$Syria. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment percentDeathsDueToDuring "An assertion of the form (#$percentDeathsDueToDuring PLACE  ORG-TYPE SIT-TYPE PERCENT DATE) states that measurements made of the population of #$GeographicalRegion PLACE for the #$Date DATE indicate that PERCENT of the population died from playing the role of #$objectActedOn in some occurrence of TYPE, a sub-collection of #$Situation. For instance, an assertion of the form (#$percentDeathsDueToDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens #$AIDS (#$Percent .0001) (#$YearFn 2005)) would state that records indicate that .0001% of the human population of #$Syria died from #$AIDS during 2005. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment literacyRateOfDuring "An assertion of the form (#$literacyRateOfDuring PLACE PERSON-TYPE PERCENT DATE) states that measurements made of the population of the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE for the #$Date DATE indicate that the sub-population that is a set of #$PERSON-TYPE have a literacy rate of PERCENT. For example, an assertion of the form (#$literacyRateOfDuring #$Syria #$FemaleHuman (#$Percent 67) (#$YearFn 2005)) indicates that records indicate that during 2005, female citizens of #$Syria had a 67% literacy rate. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment regionContainsCountWithInfectionDuring "An assertion of the form (#$regionContainsCountWithInfectionDuring PLACE HOST-TYPE  COND-TYPE RANGE DATE) states  that measurements made of the population of HOST-TYPE, some subcollection of #$Organism-Whole, in the #$GeographicalRegion PLACE for the #$Date DATE indicates that somewhere in the range of RANGE members of that population suffer from the #$PhysiologicalConditionType COND-TYPE.  For example, (#$regionContainsCountWithInfectionDuring #$Syria #$HomoSapiens #$AIDS (#$IntervalRangeFn 0 500) (#$YearFn 2004)) states that measurements indicate that there are at most 500 members of the human population of #$Syria who were suffering from #$AIDS during 2004. This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment fertilityRateInOfDuring "An assertion of the form (#$fertilityRateInOfDuring PLACE TYPE N DATE) states that measurements made of the population of the type of #$Organism-Whole TYPE in the #$GeographicalRegion REGION for the period of DATE indicate that the average number of offspring for that population is N. For example, (#$fertilityRateInOfDuring #$Syria #$FemaleHuman 2.3 (#$YearFn 2000)) would state that records indicate that the women of #$Syria have an average of 2.3 children during 2005.  This represents a measurement and estimate.")
(comment Slide-Topographical "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature, #$LandStuff, and #$Pile.  Each instance of #$Slide-Topographical is a large deposit of earth and/or rocks at the base of a slope, formed by a #$Landslide.")
(comment Landslide "A specialization of #$NaturalHazard. This is the collection of landslides. A landslide occurs when soil becomes saturated with water and slides downhill.  See also #$Slide-Topographical.")
(comment DryCreekBed "A specialization of #$FlowTerrain.  Each instance of #$DryCreekBed is a usually-dry watercourse, also known as an <i>arroyo</i>.  They are typically found in arid parts of the world.  When it rains, they channel water; otherwise (i.e. most of the time) they are just dry depressions.")
(comment PresidentFn "This function takes an organization to the individual who is the president of that organization (at the time of evaluation).  If the organization doesn't have a president, or has more than one, then the function is undefined for that argument.")
(comment PastOrPresentPresidentFn "A collection denoting function.  This function returns the collection of all individuals who have held presidental office in the organization in arg1.")
(comment UnicodeDenotingASCIIString "#$UnicodeDenotingASCIIString is the collection of #$ASCIIString-Standard strings (hence 7-bit clean) that may include valid Cyc Unicode escapes, and hence may denote any string of Unicode characters (see #$UnicodeStringFn).  
<p>
Cyc's Unicode escapes are of the form &uhhhhhh; where the h's are 2 to 6 hexadecimal digits (0-9, A-F, a-f).  For example,  \"&uA3;\" denotes the \"fancy L\" currency symbol for #$Pound-GreatBritain.
<p>
See http://www.unicode.org/charts/ for the definitive encodings for all Unicode characters.  http://www.htmlhelp.com/reference/html40/entities/ is easier to use, but gives only the more common characters.  Note that the HTML encoding syntax differs slightly from Cyc's, but the hexadecimal values used are identical.  Only hexadecimal format is supported; named entities (e.g. &pound;) are not.")
(comment geometryCompositeCardinality "This predicate relates a #$OpenGISGeometryComposite to the
number of distinct disconnected geometric figures that it comprises.
(#$geometryCompositeCardinality GEOMETRYCOMPOSITE N) means that there
are exactly N geometric figures (instances of #$OpenGISGeometry) that
are disconnected, or weakly connected parts of GEOMETRYCOMPOSITE.")
(comment nthGeometryInComposite "This predicate relates a #$OpenGISGeometryComposite to its
Nth component part or maximally strongly connected part. That is,
(#$nthGeometryInComposite GEOMETRYCOMPOSITE N MEMBER) means that
MEMBER is the Nth such part of GEOMETRYCOMPOSITE.")
(comment geometryInComposite "(#$geometryInComposite GEOMETRYCOMPOSITE GEOMETRY) means
that GEOMETRY is a component part or maximally strongly connected part
of the composite GEOMETRYCOMPOSITE.")
(comment numberOfAnchorPointsInLineSegment "(#$numberOfAnchorPointsInLineSegment LINESEGMENT N) means that
there are exactly N points in LINESEGMENT which are either boundary
points of LINESEGMENT, or are points in the interior of LINESEGMENT
which are used as anchor points for displaying or storing LINESEGMENT
in an efficient fashion.")
(comment nthAnchorPointInLineSegment "(#$nthAnchorPointInLineSegment LINESEGMENT N POINT) means that POINT
is the Nth anchor point in LINESEGMENT.")
(comment anchorPointInLineSegment "(#$anchorPointInLineSegment POINT LINESEGMENT) means that POINT is a
point incident with LINESEGMENT that is either a boundary point of
LINESEGMENT, or is an interior anchor point of LINESEGMENT where two instances
of #$LineSegment that are part of LINESEGMENT meet.")
(comment exteriorRingOfPolygon "(#$exteriorRingOfPolygon POLYGON EXTERIOR) means that
EXTERIOR is the exterior ring of the polygon POLYGON. That is,
EXTERIOR is the #$LineString that constitutes the external boundary of
POLYGON. See also #$interiorRingOfPolygon, which relates a #$Polygon
to the #$LineStrings which demarcate the holes, if any, of the
polygon.")
(comment numberOfInteriorRingsOfPolygon "(#$numberOfInteriorRingsOfPolygon POLYGON N) means that
POLYGON has exactly N interior rings, or holes. See also
#$nthInteriorRingOfPolygon and #$interiorRingOfPolygon.")
(comment nthInteriorRingOfPolygon "(#$nthInteriorRingOfPolygon POLYGON N RING) means that RING
is the Nth interior ring of POLYGON.")
(comment interiorRingOfPolygon "(#$interiorRingOfPolygon POLYGON INTERIOR) means that
INTERIOR is an interior ring which demarcates a hole in POLYGON. See
also #$exteriorRingOfPolygon which relates a polygon to its unique
exterior ring.")
(comment vertexOfLineString "(#$vertexOfLineString POINT LINESTRING) means that POINT is
a vertex of LINESTRING. That is, POINT is a point at which two of the
constituent line segments (see #$LineSegment-Straight) of LINESTRING
join to form an angle strictly less than 180 degrees.")
(comment StraightLineSet "A specialization of #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet. This is
the collection of all sets whose members are instances of
#$Line-Straight. The CycL constant <code>#$StraightLineSet</code> is
the #$rewriteOf the term <pre>
(#$SetOfTypeFn #$Line-Straight)</pre>.")
(comment pencilOfLinesAtPoint "(#$pencilOfLinesAtPoint POINT PENCIL) means that PENCIL is
the set of all straight lines (see #$StraightLineSet) which are
incident with POINT.")
(comment ConcatenateStringsFn "A variable-arity #$IndividualDenotingFunction that takes one or more abstract character-strings and (in effect) concatenates them. For any instances <code>STRING1</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> of #$CharacterString, (#$ConcatenateStringsFn <code>STRING1</code> ... <code>STRING<sub>n</sub>)</code> is the character-string that is obtained by concatenating  <code>STRING1</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> end-to-end in the order from 1 to n.
<p>
For example, (#$ConcatenateStringsFn \"B\" \"o\" \"b\") is the string \"Bob\", and (#$ConcatenateStringsFn \"Bob\" \" \" \"Dy\" \"lan\") is the string \"Bob Dylan\".
<p>
Note that some (or all) of <code>STRING1</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> might be identical.  Also note that, when applied to a single argument, this function simply returns that argument unchanged; e.g. (#$ConcatenateStringsFn \"Bob\") = \"Bob\".
<p>
#$ConcatenateStringsFn is the restriction of #$JoinListsFn (which performs the same operation on #$Lists generally) to the realm of character-strings.")
(comment CycCreationTemplate "The collection of groups of CycLTerm that, together, form a template for the creation of constants and the generation of assertions needed for some application.  For example, consider a (fictional) SUBL function call (CREATE-USER-AND-MT \"user\") that creates two terms, User001 and Microtheory001, and three assertions, (isa User001 UserOfThisApplication), (isa Microtheory001 Microtheory) and (userApplicationMicrotheory User001 Microtheory001).  The creation template for CREATE-USER-AND-MT (see #$creationTemplateForSubLFunction) consists of an exemplar instance of #$UserOfThisApplication and #$Microtheory, as well as three assertions isomorphic with the assertions on User001 and Microtheory001 described above.  When CREATE-USER-AND-MT is called, the relevant creation template can be drawn upon to check for failure, or to optimize performance by restricting forward inference at creation time to only those rules that are known to succeed (by determining which rules successfully fired forward on FORTs associated with the template via #$creationTemplateFORTs (q.v.)), or to those rules explicitly associated with the template using #$creationTemplateAllowableRules.")
(disjointWith CylindricalSurface FlatSurfaceRegion)
(comment PlaneFigure "A specialization of #$GeometricFigure, #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing, #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded, and #$FlatSurfaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PlaneFigure is an intangible, self-connected, bounded, two-dimensional, geometrical figure that can be embedded in a #$Plane.  Specializations of this collection include #$Polygon and #$CircularRegion.
<p>
Note that the boundary of a plane figure is <i>not</i> itself an instance of #$PlaneFigure, but is rather a <i>one</i>-dimensional, planar, closed curve (see e.g. #$Circle).")
(comment syntacticNodePennTag "(#$syntacticNodePennTag NODE TAG) means that NODE has been tagged with TAG.")
(comment governmentOfGeoEntityMembers "A specialization of #$hasMembers with properties that facilitate efficient reasoning about membership in an instance of #$RegionalGovernment.  #$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers relates an instance of #$Person <code>PERSON</code> in its arg2 to an instance <code>GOVERNMENT</code> of #$RegionalGovernment of which <code>PERSON</code> is a member in one of two ways: Either directly, through an assertion of the form <code>(#$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers GOVERNMENT PERSON)</code>, or else indirectly, through <code>(#$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers ENTITY PERSON)</code>, where <code>(#$government ENTITY GOVERNMENT)</code> is the case.  As such, #$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers is essentially disjunctive in meaning, and is essentially a rule macro (c.f., #$RuleMacroPredicate) such that
<pre>
   (#$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers X Y)</pre>
iff
<pre>
   (#$or
      (#$and
         (#$isa X #$RegionalGovernment)
         (#$hasMembers X Y))
      (#$thereExists ?GOVERNMENT
         (#$and
            (#$isa X #$GeopoliticalEntity)
            (#$hasMembers ?GOVERNMENT Y)
            (#$government X ?GOVERNMENT)))).</pre>  This meaning is enforced via
<pre>
   (#$conservativeViaArg #$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers #$government 1)
   (#$conservativeViaArgInverse #$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers #$government 1)</pre> which means that the arg2 will be <i>guaranteed</i> to bear the #$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers relation, and thus the #$hasMembers relation, to the relevant government and country, regardless of which is specified.<p>This relation replaces the need for explicit rules to explicitly conclude membership in a government or a country from known membership in one.")
(comment GovernmentPositionSlot "The collection of instances of #$BinaryPredicates that relate an instance of #$GeopoliticalEntity to a person who is a member of that entity's government.  Each instance of #$GovernmentPositionSlot is a specialization of (see #$genlPreds) #$governmentOfGeoEntityMembers.")
(comment TheCharniakParser "A #$programCopies of #$CharniakParser, and an #$IndexicalConcept. #$TheCharniakParser denotes whichever copy of a #$CharniakParser a user is running.")
(disjointWith FlatSurfaceRegion Tube)
(comment OneDimensionalGeometricThing "A specialization of #$OneDimensionalThing, #$GeometricallyDescribableThing, and #$ShapedObject (qq.v.).   #$OneDimensionalGeometricThing is the collection of all one-dimensional, self-connected objects that can be described in relatively simple geometric terms.  Specializations of this collection include #$Line-Straight, #$LineSegment-Straight, #$Arc, and #$Ellipse.")
(comment Cylinder "A specialization of #$ThreeDimensionalThing.  Each instance of #$Cylinder is a three-dimensional object that has a straight or curved #$Tube (q.v.) as its \"side\" surface; any cross-section taken with respect to its side is (at least roughly) circular, and any two such cross-sections have (at least roughly) the same diameter.  A cylinder of finite length will also have a circular base and a circular top as parts of its boundary; an infinite cylinder has no top, and might have no base either.
<p>
Examples of cylinders include tangible objects (such as a roll of nickels) as well as intangible cylindrical space regions.
<p>
For cylinders with straight sides, see the specialization #$RightCylinder.  See also #$CylindricalSurface and #$HollowCylindricalObject.")
(comment OnlineArticle "The collection of all instances of #$Article-PCW that have been published online.")
(comment CWPublishedOnline "The collection of all instances of #$ConceptualWork that have been published online.")
(comment RightCylinder "A specialization of #$Cylinder and #$ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing (qq.v).  Each instance of #$RightCylinder is a cylinder that has a <i>straight</i> #$Tube (q.v.) as its \"side\" surface.  In other words, its radial axis is a straight line(-segment) (see #$LinearObject-Straight).
<p>
Examples of right cylinders include tangible objects (such as a roll of nickels) as well as intangible right cylindrical space regions.
<p>
See also #$CylindricalSurface and #$HollowCylindricalObject.")
(disjointWith ConicalSurface FlatSurfaceRegion)
(comment budgetExpendituresDuring "An assertion of the form (#$budgetExpendituresDuring GEO DATE AMOUNT) states that the #$GeopoliticalEntity GEO spent the #$MonetaryValue AMOUNT during the #$Date DATE.  For example, an assertion of the form (#$budgetExpendituresDuring  Syria (YearFn 2003) (Dollar-UnitedStates 10000000000)) states that #$Syria spent $10 billion from its budget in 2003.")
(comment budgetRevenuesDuring "An assertion of the form (#$budgetRevenuesDuring GEO DATE AMOUNT) states that the #$GeopoliticalEntity GEO took in the #$MonetaryValue AMOUNT during the #$Date DATE.  For example, an assertion of the form (#$budgetRevenuesDuring  Syria (YearFn 2003) (Dollar-UnitedStates 10000000000)) states that #$Syria took in $10 billion during 2003.")
(comment totalDebtDuring "A statement of the form (#$totalDebtDuring PLACE QUANTITY DATE) is used to give an estimate of the total debt  (borrowings minus repayments) of the government of the #$GeopoliticalEntity PLACE during the #$Date DATE.  For instance, (#$totalDebtDuring #$Syria (Dollar-UnitedStates 1.8E10) (#$YearFn 2005)) would state that during 2005, the Syrian government had a total debt of $18 billion.")
(comment Tube "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion (q.v.). Each instance of #$Tube is a hollow \"side\" surface of #$Cylinder.  They might be straight or curved lengthwise.  Examples include both tangible objects (such as pipes and hoses) and intangible tubular space regions.  
<p>
Each #$CylindricalSurface (q.v.) has a two-dimensional #$Tube as a part.  The surface of an infinitely long cylinder with no \"top\" or \"bottom\" just <i>is</i> an infinite #$Tube.")
(comment familyNameWithFollowingInitials "(#$familyNameWithFollowingInitials AGENT STRING) means that STRING is the name of agent that is created by taking AGENT's last name and concatenating a comma and AGENT's initials to it.")
(comment TagFreeLanguage "#$TagFreeLanguage is the \"null\" markup language -- the language with no tags.")
(comment contentModelFocalPeg "(#$contentModelFocalPeg MT PEG) identifies MT as the most general microtheory for recording discourse modelling information involving the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance.  The content model for a peg is to be distinguished from its interpretation microtheories (c.f., pegInterpretationMts, #$pegPartialInterpretationMts), each of which sees (c.f., #$genlMt) the contents of the content model.")
(comment contentModelPegs "(#$contentModelPegs MT PEG) identifies MT as the most general microtheory for recording discourse modelling information involving the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance, where PEG is either the focal peg for MT (see #$contentModelFocalPeg) or a rewrite of the focal peg for MT (see #$contentModelRewritePegs).  The content model for a peg is to be distinguished from its interpretation microtheories (c.f., pegInterpretationMts, #$pegPartialInterpretationMts), each of which sees (c.f., #$genlMt) the contents of the content model.")
(comment contentModelRewritePegs "(#$contentModelRewritePegs MT PEG) identifies MT as the most general microtheory for recording discourse modelling information involving the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance, where PEG is a rewrite of the focal peg for MT (see #$contentModelFocalPeg and #$associatedPegRewrites).  The content model for a peg is to be distinguished from its interpretation microtheories (c.f., pegInterpretationMts, #$pegPartialInterpretationMts), each of which sees (c.f., #$genlMt) the contents of the content model.")
(comment SemanticInterpretationMicrotheoryWithContentReinforcedByContext "The collection of all instances of #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory whose declared content (see #$interpretationMtContent-CycL) has been reinforced by other interpretations.  See also #$pegInterpretations-CycL-Reinforced.")
(comment CommonShapeFn "A unary #$GenlsPreservingFunction (q.v.) that takes a given type of tangible object to the collection of all objects that are shaped like objects of that type.  More precisely, (#$CommonShapeFn <b>OBJTYPE</b>) is the (most inclusive) specialization of #$ShapedObject all of whose instances have the same (most specific) shape shared by all non-pathological instances of <b>OBJTYPE</b>.  
<p>
For example, (#$CommonShapeFn #$DomesticCat) is the collection of all cat-shaped objects, which includes not just cats themselves, but also cat-shaped toys, cookie-cutters, cushions, space-regions, etc.
<p>
The fact that this function returns the <i>most inclusive</i> collection of <b>OBJTYPE</b>-shaped things ensures that (#$CommonShapeFn #$DomesticCat) includes <i>all</i> (not just some) cat-shaped objects.  The fact that the function returns the collection all of whose instances have the <i>most specific</i> shape common to (non-pathological) <b>OBJTYPE</b>s ensures that (#$CommonShapeFn #$DomesticCat) does <i>not</i> include, say, all animal-shaped objects.
<p>
See also the corresponding \"instance-level\" predicate #$objectShapeType.")
(comment firstPublicationDateForStyleAndRendering "(#$firstPublicationDateForStyleAndRendering WORK STYLE RENDERING STRING) means that STRING is the canonical string used to express of the first publication date for WORK according to STYLE-SPECIFICATION, for rendering using MARKUP-LANGUAGE.")
(comment titleOfWorkForStyleAndRendering "(#$titleOfWorkForStyleAndRendering WORK STYLE RENDERING STRING) means that STRING is the canonical string used to express the title of WORK according to STYLE-SPECIFICATION, for rendering using MARKUP-LANGUAGE.")
(comment publicationLocationForStyleAndRendering "(#$publicationLocationForStyleAndRendering WORK STYLE RENDERING STRING) means that STRING is the canonical string used to express the publication location for WORK according to STYLE-SPECIFICATION, for rendering using MARKUP-LANGUAGE.")
(comment publisherForStyleAndRendering "(#$publisherForStyleAndRendering WORK STYLE RENDERING STRING) means that STRING is the canonical string used to express the publisher of WORK according to STYLE-SPECIFICATION, for rendering using MARKUP-LANGUAGE.")
(comment citationStringForStyleAndRendering "(#$citationStringForStyleAndRendering WORK STYLE RENDERING STRING) means that STRING is the canonical string used to cite WORK according to STYLE-SPECIFICATION, for rendering using MARKUP-LANGUAGE.")
(comment containingWorkForStyleAndRendering "(#$containingWorkForStyleAndRendering WORK STYLE RENDERING STRING) means that STRING is the canonical string used to express the containing work for WORK according to STYLE-SPECIFICATION, for rendering using MARKUP-LANGUAGE.")
(comment pageNumbersOfSubworkForStyleAndRendering "(#$pageNumbersOfSubworkForStyleAndRendering WORK STYLE RENDERING STRING) means that STRING is the canonical string used to express the page numbers of WORK according to STYLE-SPECIFICATION, for rendering using MARKUP-LANGUAGE.")
(comment tokenizationOfTextOfPCW "(#$tokenizationOfTextOfPCW TOKENIZATION PCW) identifies PCW as the instance of #$PropositionalConceptualWork whose text was the input to the parsing mechanism that resulted in the tokenization TOKENIZATION of that input.")
(comment pressArmOfOrganization "(#$pressArmOfOrganization ORG PRESS) means that PRESS is the press-oriented organization associated with ORG.  For example, AAAI Press is the press-oriented organization associated with AAAI, so (#$pressArmOfOrganization #$AAAI #$AAAIPress) is true.")
(comment pegHasAssociatedParseTrees "<code>(#$pegHasAssociatedParseTrees PEG TREE)</code> means that the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg has the instance <code>TREE</code> of #$ParseTree as one of the parse trees that can be drawn upon to determine the semantics of <code>PEG</code>.")
(comment rectangleTypeOfExtensionParameters "(#$rectangleTypeOfExtensionParameters DIST1 DIST2 REC) means that REC is the collection of all and only #$Rectangles (q.v.) that have two sides of length DIST1 and two sides of length DIST2.")
(comment nlPhraseTypeForExpressionType "<code>(#$nlPhraseTypeForExpressionType PHRASE-TYPE EXPRESSION-TYPE)</code> means that <code>EXPRESSION-TYPE</code> is the collection of instances of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that serve to mark occurrences of instances of <code>PHRASE-TYPE</code> in a discourse.  For example, <code>(#$nlPhraseTypeForExpressionType #$NounPhrase #$NounPhraseExpressionPeg)</code> identifies #$NounPhraseExpressionPeg as the collection of expression pegs that mark occurrences of Noun Phrases.")
(disjointWith PolyhedralSurface FlatSurfaceRegion)
(comment PolyhedralSurface "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing, #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded, and #$SurfaceRegion-Finite (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PolyhedralSurface is the two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a #$Polyhedron (q.v.); it is the spatial sum of several (i.e. four or more) #$Polygons.")
(comment ThePartition "A variable-arity partial #$SetDenotingFunction that is a specialization (via #$genlFunctions) of #$TheCovering (q.v.), and is used to specify a set of two or more collections or mathematical sets that partition some set or collection (see #$partitionedInto).  For example, the fact that the collection #$Whale is partitioned into the collections #$BaleenWhale and #$ToothedWhale can be expressed as '(#$partitionedInto #$Whale (#$ThePartition #$BaleenWhale #$ToothedWhale))'.
<p>
If <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b> (<b>n</b>>=2) are pairwise-disjoint #$SetOrCollections, (#$ThePartition <b>SOC<sub>1</sub> ... SOCn</b>) is the set (see #$Set-Mathematical) whose elements are exactly <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b>.  If <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b> are not all mutually disjoint, then the function returns no value and '(#$ThePartition <b>SOC<sub>1</sub> ... SOCn</b>)' is #$undefined (q.v.).
<p>
Note that #$ThePartition is simply a restriction of the function #$TheSet (q.v.) to argument-sequences of length two or greater whose members are pairwise-disjoint (see #$disjointWith) sets or collections.")
(comment TheCovering "A variable-arity #$SetDenotingFunction that is a specialization (via #$genlFunctions) of #$TheSet (q.v.), and is used to specify a set of two or more collections or mathematical sets that cover some set or collection (see #$covering).  For example, the fact that the collection #$CreationOrDestructionEvent is covered by the collections #$CreationEvent and #$DestructionEvent can be expressed as '(#$covering #$CreationOrDestructionEvent (#$TheCovering #$CreationEvent #$DestructionEvent))'.
<p>
If <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b> (<b>n</b>>=2) are #$SetOrCollections, (#$TheCovering <b>SOC<sub>1</sub> ... SOCn</b>) is the set (see #$Set-Mathematical) whose elements are exactly <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b>.
<p>
Note that #$TheCovering is simply a restriction of the function #$TheSet (q.v.) to arguments that are #$SetOrCollections and to a minimum arity (see #$arityMin) of 2.")
(comment agreementSponsors "An assertion of the form (#$agreementSponsors  CREATION AGENT) means that during the #$CreatingALegalAgreement, the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT was the person who originally introduced a formal description of the #$LegalAgreement which was the #$outputsCreated in CREATION.  This does not assume that AGENT is the author or original source of the content of the #$LegalAgreement.")
(comment UNSecurityCouncilResolution "The sub-collection of #$Resolution that are created by the #$UnitedNationsSecurityCouncil.")
(comment CreatingALegalAgreement "A sub-collection #$LegalProceeding and #$CreationEvent.  Each instance of #$CreatingALegalAgreement involves a process where some authority (e.g. a #$RegionalGovernment) formally creates a #$LegalAgreement and, as a result, puts into effect some prescription or proscription on the behavior of the #$IntelligentAgents over which it has some authority.")
(comment specificationProscribesSituationType "The predicate #$specificationProscribesSituationType relates an instance of #$NormativeSpecification SPECIFICATION to a sub-collection of #$Situation.  An assertion of the form (#$specificationProscribesSituationType SPECIFICATION SIT-TYPE) states that the realization of an instance of SIT-TYPE runs counter to the content of SPECIFICATION and so, such a situation would violate SPECIFICATION.  This is a very general predicate.  A more specific form of this predicate is #$lawProscribesActType.")
(comment objectShapeType "A #$ShapePredicate (q.v.) and \"restricted specialization\" (see #$genlPredsWRTTypes)  of #$isa that relates a spatial object to a collection of things having (at least approximately) the same shape (see #$SpatialThingTypeByShape).  <code>(#$objectShapeType OBJECT SHAPETYPE)</code> means that <code>SHAPETYPE</code> characterizes <code>OBJECT</code>'s shape (at some level of generality).  If <code>SHAPETYPE</code> is a collection of things all having the same dimensionality as <code>OBJECT</code> (see #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions), <code>OBJECT</code> might actually be an instance of <code>SHAPETYPE</code>; but if 
<code>SHAPETYPE</code> is a collection of things all having the a different dimensionality than <code>OBJECT</code>, <code>OBJECT</code> can only approximate the shape in question. 
<p>
For example, <code>(#$objectShapeType MyCrystalBall #$SphericalSolid)</code> holds because my crystal ball <i>is</i> a solid sphere.  On the other hand <code>(#$objectShapeType MyMousePad #$Rectangle)</code>, while true, only means that my mouse pad is <i>roughly</i> rectangular in shape, as a three-dimensional object like a mousepad can't literally have a two-dimensional shape.
<p>
When making a ground-atomic assertion to the Knowledge Base regarding a particular object's shape it is normally advisable to use the most specific applicable #$SpatialThingTypeByShape available.  But note that the predicate #$objectShapeType itself implies no such thing; a more-inclusive shape-type will always characterize an object whenever a (correlatively) less-inclusive shape-type does.  Thus, <code>(#$objectShapeType MyMousePad #$Polygon)</code> holds if the second example above does.
<p>
See also the nearly corresponding type-level function #$CommonShapeFn.")
(comment TorusSurface "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing, #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded, and #$SurfaceRegion-Finite (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TorusSurface is the two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a #$Torus (q.v.).")
(disjointWith TorusSurface FlatSurfaceRegion)
(comment ParallelepipedSurface "A specialization of #$PolyhedralSurface (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ParallelepipedSurface is the two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a #$Parallelepiped (q.v.); it is the spatial sum of six externally-connected #$Parallelograms.")
(comment authorizingAgents "An assertion of the form (#$authorizingAgents CREATION AGENT) means that the #$CreatingALegalAgreement CREATION produced a genuine, binding #$LegalAgreement based on the participation of the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT.  As an example, if a #$USSenator votes on a piece of legislation and the legislation passes, then that performance of the voting would entail the role of #$authorizingAgents in the overall #$CreatingALegalAgreement.")
(comment abstainingVoter "An assertion of the form (#$abstainingVoter VOTE AGENT) means that AGENT refrained from voting either for or against the proposal that was the subject of the #$Voting VOTE.")
(comment noVoter "An assertion of the form (#$noVoter VOTE AGENT) means that AGENT against the proposal that was the subject of the #$Voting VOTE.")
(comment yesVoter "An assertion of the form (#$yesVoter VOTE AGENT) means that AGENT voted in favor of the proposal that was the subject of the #$Voting VOTE.")
(comment wasTheKillerOf "(#$wasTheKillerOf AGT1 AGT2)<TIME, GRAN> holds in MT at TIME and GRAN just in case there was some earlier time TIME*, and granularity GRAN*, such that AGT1 killed AGT2 at TIME* and GRAN*.")
(comment queryAnswers "(#$queryAnswers QUERY-SPEC ANSWER) means that the
#$CycInferenceEngine currently generates a proof for ANSWER in
response to the query specification QUERY-SPEC. ANSWER is a data
structure which specifies both an assignment of bindings to variables
and one or more justifications for that assignment.")
(comment CycInferenceBindingsDataStructure "A specialization of #$CycInferenceDataStructure.  Each
instance of #$CycInferenceBindingsDataStructure is a component of one
or more #$CycInferenceAnswers, and encodes an assignment of bindings
to the free variables in the query which yielded that answer
(i.e., the query which triggered the #$CycInference of which that
answer is a part).  If the triggering query is closed, the binding
list value is NIL.")
(comment InferenceBindingsDataStructureFn "(#$InferenceBindingsDataStructureFn BINDING-SET) denotes the
instance of #$CycInferenceBindingsDataStructure which encodes
BINDING-SET.")
(comment inferenceBindingsDataStructure "The corresponding predicate for #$InferenceBindingsDataStructureFn,
(#$inferenceBindingsDataStructure BINDING-SET BINDING-STRUCT) means
that the content of BINDING-STRUCT is BINDING-SET.")
(comment CycInferenceAnswerJustificationFn "(#$CycInferenceAnswerJustificationFn SET-OF-SUPPORTS)
denotes the instance of #$CycInferenceAnswerJustification which
contains just those supports (#$CycSupportDatastructure, q.v.) that
are elements of SET-OF-SUPPORTS.")
(comment inferenceJustificationSupportSet "The corresponding predicate to
#$CycInferenceAnswerJustificationFn.
(#$inferenceJustificationSupportSet JUSTIFICATION SET-OF-SUPPORTS)
means that JUSTIFICATION contains just those supports that are
elements of SET-OF-SUPPORTS.")
(comment inferenceAnswerJustification "(#$inferenceAnswerJustification ANSWER JUSTIFICATION) means
that JUSTIFICATION is the data structure component of ANSWER which
encodes the justification.  JUSTIFICATION thus contains (or is
represented as containing) the complete set of supports used in or
more proofs of ANSWER's binding list (see
#$CycInferenceBindingsDataStructure).")
(comment inferenceJustificationSupport "(#$inferenceJustificationSupport JUSTIFICATION SUPPORT)
means that SUPPORT is one of the supports that comprise JUSTIFICATION.
See also #$inferenceJustificationSupportSet.")
(comment CycInferenceAnswerJustification "A specialization of #$CycInferenceDataStructure.  Each
instance of #$CycInferenceAnswerJustification is a component of one or
more #$CycInferenceAnswers, and, in each case, encodes a complete set
of supports (cf., #$CycSupportDatastructure) for that answer's binding
list.  See also #$CycInferenceBindingsDataStructure.")
(comment ParseTreeRepresentationMicrotheory "A specialization of #$DataMicrotheory.  Each instance of #$ParseTreeRepresentationMicrotheory contains a representation of the structure of a microtheory.")
(comment ContentMtOfParseTreeFn "<code>(#$ContentMtOfParseTreeFn TREE)</code> denotes the instance <code>MT</code> of #$ParseTreeRepresentationMicrotheory that contains a representation of the structure of <code>TREE</code>, an instance of #$ParseTree.")
(comment contentMtOfParseTree "<code>(#$contentMtOfParseTree MT TREE)</code> means that <code>MT</code> is the instance of #$ParseTreeRepresentationMicrotheory that contains a representaton of the structure of <code>TREE</code>, an instance of #$ParseTree.  This is the predicate that corresponds to the function #$ContentMtOfParseTreeFn.")
(comment TheCycPhraseStructureParser "A #$programCopies of #$CycPhraseStructureParser, and an #$IndexicalConcept. #$TheCycPhraseStructureParser denotes whichever copy of #$CycPhraseStructureParser is part of the current #$Cyc.")
(comment grosslyIncompleteWithArgNotFullyBound "A binary #$InferenceCompletenessPredicate that can apply to a
predicate and a specified
argument-place. (#$grosslyIncompleteWithArgNotFullyBound PRED N) means
that for any sequence SEQ such that PRED holds of SEQ, the Cyc
inference engine can provide through removal tactics , at best, a minimal range of the values
(VALUE-1, VALUE-2, ..., VALUE-K) such that these values are the
Nth elements of SEQ. 
<p>
(#$grosslyIncompleteWithArgNotFullyBound PRED N) also means that the Cyc
inference engine will treat as \"grossly incomplete\" any removal tactic for a
problem with PRED in the arg0 position and with the Nth argument position not fully bound.
<p>
Cf. #$grosslyIncompleteWithAllArgsNotFullyBound and
#$grosslyIncompleteWithAnyArgsNotFullyBound.")
(comment TheLinkParser "A copy of #$LinkGrammarParser-CW and #$IndexicalConcept. #$TheLinkParser denotes whichever copy of a #$LinkGrammarParser-CW a user is running.")
(comment RectangularParallelepipedSurface "A specialization of #$ParallelepipedSurface (q.v.).  Each instance of #$RectangularParallelepipedSurface is the two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a #$RectangularParallelepiped (q.v.); it is the spatial sum of six #$Rectangles that are edge-connected at right angles.")
(comment CubicalSurface "A specialization of #$RectangularParallelepipedSurface (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CubicalSurface is the two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a #$Cube (q.v.); it is the spatial sum of six #$Squares that are edge-connected at right angles.")
(comment CruciformObject "A specialization of #$Artifact.  Each instance of #$CruciformObject is an artifact consisting of an upright beam and a transverse beam, with the two beams roughly orthogonal to each other.  Examples include Christian crosses.")
(comment PyramidalSurface "A specialization of #$PolyhedralSurface (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PyramidalSurface is the two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a #$Pyramid (q.v.); it is the spatial sum of an n-sided polygon that is its <i>base</i> with n-1 triangles that meet at a common vertex.")
(comment Wedge "A #$ThreeDimensionalShapeType and a specialization of #$Polyhedron.  The collection of all wedge-shaped spatial objects, whether tangible objects or intangible space regions.  Each instance of #$Wedge is a polyhedron with five sides, two of which are (parallel, congruent) triangles and three of which are rectangles; any cross-section of a wedge parallel to its two triangular ends is a triangle congruent to those ends.  For example, the space occupied by a pup tent is roughly a wedge.")
(comment linkGrammarLinkName "(#$linkGrammarLinkName LINK-PRED NAME) means that NAME is the name used to denote LINK-PRED in the output of the #$LinkGrammarParser-CW.")
(comment startOffset "(#$startOffset BIG LITTLE N) means that LITTLE is a (case-sensitive) #$substring of BIG that can be found N characters from the start of BIG.")
(comment correspondingDomainInArg "(#$correspondingDomainInArg QUERY-COLL DOMAIN-COLL ARG-NUM) holds just in case the normal domain for instances of QUERY-COLL in ARG-NUM is DOMAIN-COLL.  QUERY-COLL is restricted to collections of single literal queries.")
(comment usesProxyInEventsOfTypeWith "An assertion of the form (#$usesProxyInEventsOfTypeWith SOURCE PROXY TYPE TARGET) is used to represent that the #$IntelligentAgent SOURCE uses the #$IntelligentAgent PROXY to interact with the #$IntelligentAgent during instances of TYPE, some sub-collection of #$SocialOccurrence. So an assertion of (#$usesProxyInEventsOfTypeWith Bob Joe #$Negotiating Sue) would mean that Joe acts as a proxy for Bob during some #$Negotiating events involving Sue. See the related predicate #$proxyFor.")
(comment proxyFor "An assertion of the form (#$proxyFor EVENT PROXY SOURCE) states that during the #$Event EVENT the #$IntelligentAgent PROXY acts on behalf of the #$IntelligentAgent SOURCE.  For a related type-level predicate see #$usesProxyInEventsOfTypeWith.")
(comment MuslimBrotherhoodOrganization "#$MuslimBrotherhoodOrganization is intended to group together several organizations that are all typically referred to by the name 'Muslim Brotherhood,' but which have not been identified as a single group or sub-groups of one larger umbrella organization.  In the context of world affairs in 2005, #$MuslimBrotherhood-Egypt appears to be the most likely referent for the name because of its long history in Middle Eastern politics and its continuing political influence in Egypt ")
(comment GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction "A specialization of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  Each instance of #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction takes as arguments one or more #$Distances, and returns a specialization of a particular #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize whose instances all have those distances as their relevant size dimensions (see #$extensionParametersOfObject).
<p>
For example, #$RightCylinderTypeFn is a shape-type function that takes two distances as arguments, and returns the specialization of #$RightCylinder whose instances have the first distance as their height and the second distance as their diameter.")
(comment RightCylinderTypeFn "A #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that takes a pair of positive distances (see #$Distance-Absolute) to the collection of all straight cylinders that have the corresponding size dimensions.  More precisely, (#$RightCylinderTypeFn <b>HEIGHT DIAM</b>) is the collection of all and only those #$RightCylinders (q.v.) whose #$heightOfObject is <b>HEIGHT</b> and whose #$diameterOfObject is <b>DIAM</b>.
<p>
For example, a roll of nickels is an instance of (roughly) (#$RightCylinderTypeFn (#$Inch 3.5) (#$Inch 0.875)).")
(comment GeometricShapeTypeBySize "A #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$SpatialThingTypeByGeometricShape (q.v.).  Each instance of #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize is a specialization of #$GeometricallyDescribableThing (q.v.) that groups things according to their size (i.e. the #$Distance(s) that they are extended along one or more spatial dimensions).  
<p>
Instances of #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize include (#$RectangularSolidTypeFn  (#$Inch 12) (#$Inch 12) (#$Inch 12)), the collection of all 12'' x 12'' x 12'' cubes.
<p>
Note that the above-used function #$RectangularSolidTypeFn is an instance of #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction (q.v.), all of whose instances are functions that take #$Distances as arguments and return instances of #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize.")
(comment DocumentationPredicate "A #$PredicateType whose instances are #$Predicates specifically designed for in-KB documentation purposes, such as  facilitating #$Cyclists in their understanding of the Cyc system, tracking knowledge representation work being done in the Cyc Knowledge Base, or noting cleanup work to be done.  
<p>
Note that documentation predicates are usually not involved in inference.  They may be excluded from knowledge bases in which available memory is a premium without affecting the performance of applications. #$DocumentationPredicate is used in code to determine which documentation to show to the user when they request to see documentation for a first-order reified term (or \"FORT\").")
(comment tokenStartCharacterOffset "(#$tokenStartCharacterOffset TOKEN N) means that the string of TOKEN starts N characters into its tokenization's input string. If a token starts with the first character of its tokenization's input string, its offset is 0.")
(comment leaveArgPlaceOpenInTestQueries "(#$leaveArgPlaceOpenInTestQueries QUERY-COLL ARG-NUM) means that when running tests using the instances of QUERY-COLL, one should leave a variable in ARG-NUM position -- run the query open in that position.")
(comment forbiddenActType "'(#$forbiddenActType FORBIDDING ACT-TYPE)' means that ACT-TYPE is the type of #$Action that the #$forbidder in FORBIDDING forbids the #$forbiddee in FORBIDDING to perform.")
(comment ForbiddingSomething "A specialization of #$ObligationCreatingEvent and #$CommunicationAct-Single.  Each instance of #$ForbiddingSomething is an event in which some agent (the #$forbidder) forbids some other agent(s) (the #$forbiddee(s)) to do something.
<p>
The #$forbidder must have proper authority to forbid the #$forbiddee to perform the act type in question; for example, a five-year-old child typically cannot forbid his parents to serve spinach for dinner -- at least, we would not count such an act as an instance of #$ForbiddingSomething.")
(comment positionRecruitedFor "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a #$RecruitingActivity to an #$OrganizationalPositionSlot.  (#$positionRecruitedFor RECRUITING SLOT) means that RECRUITING is an attempt to recruit one or more people to occupy the organizational position corresponding to SLOT.")
(comment ControlledLand "A specialization of #$GeopoliticalEntity.  Each instance of #$ControlledLand is a geopolitical entity that is at least partially controlled by some other instance of #$GeopoliticalEntity.  Furthermore, the instance of #$ControlledLand is not a geopolitical sub-division (see the predicate #$geopoliticalSubdivisions) of the power that controls it.  Instances of #$ControlledLand include #$PuertoRico, #$Bermuda, #$Guadeloupe-TheDependency, #$Tibet.")
(comment PraisingSomeone "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$CommunicationAct-Single.  Each instance of #$PraisingSomeone is an event in which an agent expresses a strongly positive opinion of an agent.")
(comment ResumingAnActivity "Each instance of #$ResumingAnActivity is an event in which an agent resumes the performance of an activity, i.e. goes back to performing it after going a while without performing it.")
(comment AttackOnObject "A specialization of #$PurposefulPhysicalAction and #$ActionOnObject.  Each instance of #$AttackOnObject is an action intentionally performed by an actor or actors (see #$performedBy) with the goal (see #$purposeInEvent) that the action damages (see #$damages) the object attacked (see #$objectAttacked).  The object that is attacked in an instance of #$AttackOnObject need not be a tangible object -- for example, it could be a web site or a person's character.  For attacks against tangible objects, see the more specialized collection, #$AttackOnTangible.")
(comment ContentModelForPegFn "(#$ContentModelForPegFn PEG) denotes the the most general microtheory in a microtheoretic structure for representing and reasoning about the possible interpretations of an utterance (or info-transfer, generally) for which PEG has been introduced.")
(comment InterpretingDocumentMtFn "<code>(#$InterpretingDocumentMtFn TEXTUAL-PCW)</code> denotes the instance of  

#$ContentModelMicrotheory that is the most general microtheory in a microtheoretic structure used in interpreting the document <code>TEXTUAL-PCW</code>. <code>(#$InterpretingDocumentMtFn TEXTUAL-PCW)</code> will typically contain basic facts about the sentence-level expression pegs that have been introduced for purposes of reasoning about the content of <code>TEXTUAL-PCW</code>. ")
(comment pcwText "<code>(#$pcwText PCW STRING)</code> means that the instance of #$CharacterString <code>STRING</code> is the entire original string from the instance <code>PCW</code> of #$TextualPCW.")
(comment LearnByReadingDocumentDataMt "The microtheory for describing the most general features of instances of #$LearnByReadingDocument.")
(comment pegTokenizations "<code>(#$pegTokenizations PEG TOKENIZATION)</code> means that <code>TOKENIZATION</code> is one of the instances of
 #$CycTokenization that is a tokenization of the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.")
(comment RectangularSolidTypeFn "A #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that takes a triple of positive distances (see #$Distance-Absolute) to the collection of all rectangular solids (see #$RectangularParallelepiped) having those size dimensions.  (#$RectangularSolidTypeFn <b>DIST1 DIST2 DIST3</b>) is the collection of all and only those rectangular parallelepipeds whose size dimensions are <b>DIST1</b> by <b>DIST2</b> by <b>DIST3</b>.
<p>
For example, a 10-foot-long 2-by-4 board is an instance of (#$RectangularSolidTypeFn (#$Foot-UnitOfMeasure 10) (#$Inch 2) (#$Inch 4)).
<p>
Note that this function is a #$CommutativeRelation, and thus is indifferent to the order of its arguments.  None of its argument-places, of course, has an intrinsic correlation to the highly context-dependent notions of the \"height\", \"depth\", or \"breadth\" of a given rectangular solid.")
(comment CircularRegionTypeFn "A #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that takes a positive distance (see #$Distance-Absolute) to the collection of #$CircularRegions having that diameter.  (#$CircularRegionTypeFn <b>DIAMETER</b>) is the collection of all and only those circular regions whose #$diameterOfObject is <b>DIAMETER</b>.")
(comment SphericalSolidTypeFn "A #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that takes a positive distance (see #$Distance-Absolute) to the collection of #$SphericalSolids having that diameter.  (#$SphericalSolidTypeFn <b>DIAMETER</b>) is the collection of all and only those spherical solids whose #$diameterOfObject is <b>DIAMETER</b>.
<p>
For example, a billiard cue ball is an instance of (roughly) (#$SphericalSolidTypeFn (#$Inch 2.25).")
(comment RectangleTypeFn "A #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that takes a pair of positive distances (see #$Distance-Absolute) to the collection of all #$Rectangles having those size dimensions.  (#$RectangleTypeFn <b>DIST1 DIST2</b>) is the collection of all and only those rectangles whose size dimensions are <b>DIST1</b> by <b>DIST2</b>.
<p>
For example, the two-dimensional top surface of an 8'' x 10'' photograph is an instance of (#$RectangleTypeFn (#$Inch 8) (#$Inch 10)).
<p>
Note that this function is a #$CommutativeRelation, and is thus indifferent to the order of its arguments.  Neither of its argument-places, of course, has an intrinsic correlation to the highly context-dependent notions of the \"length\" or \"width\" of a given rectangle.")
(comment canContainShapesOfSize "A #$PhysicalFeatureBinaryPredicate that is used for indicating the maximal size of a thing (see #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize) that can fit inside a given tangible object.  <code>(#$canContainShapesOfSize OBJ SHAPETYPE)</code> means that there is sufficient room (see #$FreeSpaceRegion) inside <code>OBJ</code> that an arbitrary instance of <code>SHAPETYPE</code> can fit there (see e.g. #$fitsInsideObject).  For example,
<pre>
  (#$canContainShapesOfSize 
     <b>MyCarTrunk</b> 
     (#$RectangularSolidTypeFn (#$Meter 1) (#$Meter 0.5) (#$Meter 0.75))
</pre>
says that my car's trunk can hold a three-dimensional rectangular object whose dimensions are 1-meter by 1/2-meter by 3/4-meter.  
<p>
Note that assertions like the above are the most informative when they come as close as possible to giving the <i>largest</i> size of thing that fits inside an object.  This can be facilitated by choosing the #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that corresponds most closely to the shape of  the interior space in question.  For example, if the object of interest had been a soda can instead of a trunk, #$RightCylinderTypeFn would have been more appropriate to use than #$RectangularSolidTypeFn.")
(comment spatiallySubsumes-TypeType "A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a type-level correlate of #$spatiallySubsumes (q.v.).  It is used to state that things of one given type always spatially subsume something of another given type.  <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType BIGTYPE SMALLTYPE)</code> means that, for every instance <code>BIG</code> of<code> BIGTYPE</code>, there is an instance <code>SMALL</code> of <code>SMALLTYPE</code> such that <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes BIG SMALL)</code> holds.
<p>
For example, every 2'' x 4'' rectangle subsumes a 2'' x 3'' rectangle:
<pre>
  (#$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType
    (#$RectangleTypeFn (#$Inch 2) (#$Inch 4))
    (#$RectangleTypeFn (#$Inch 2) (#$Inch 3)))
</pre>
An important specialization of #$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType is #$physicalPartTypes.")
(comment spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType "A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a #$SpatialThing to a type of spatial thing such that the former is spatially subsumed by something of that type.  <code>(#$spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType OBJ TYPE)</code> means that there is at least one instance <code>INST</code> of <code>TYPE</code> such that <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes INST OBJ)</code> is true.  For example,
<pre>
  (#$spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType #$CityOfAustinTX #$Country)
</pre>
means that Austin is subsumed by at least one country.
<p>
This predicate is often used with a #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize (q.v.) as its second argument, in order to give an approximation of the external size of an object.  For example,
<pre>
  (#$spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType 
    <b>TheHopeDiamond</b> 
    (#$RightCylinderTypeFn (#$Centi (#$Meter 10) (#$Centi (#$Meter 10)))
</pre>
entails that the Hope Diamond can fit inside a #$RightCylinder that is 10 centimeters high and 10 centimeters in diameter.  Its truth follows from the fact that (at any given moment) the Hope is indeed subsumed by a #$SpaceRegion of that shape and size.
<p>
See also #$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType.")
(comment syntacticNodeInitialToken "(#$syntacticNodeInitialToken NODE TOKEN) means that TOKEN is the first (i.e. leftmost) token covered by NODE.")
(comment syntacticNodeFinalToken "(#$syntacticNodeFinalToken NODE TOKEN) means that TOKEN is the last (i.e. rightmost) token covered by NODE.")
(comment rectangularSolidTypeOfSize "A quaternary predicate that relates a triple of positive #$Distances to the collection of three-dimensional rectangles that have those distances as their spatial dimensions. <code>(#$rectangularSolidTypeOfSize DIST1 DIST2 DIST3 SOLIDTYPE)</code> means that <code>SOLIDTYPE</code> is the #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize (q.v.) whose instances are all and only those #$RectangularParallelepipeds (q.v.) whose dimensional measurements are <code>DIST1</code> by <code>DIST2</code> by <code>DIST3</code>.
<p>
Note that #$rectangularSolidTypeOfSize is a #$PartiallyCommutativeRelation that is  commutative in its first three arguments.
<p>
See also the corresponding function  #$RectangularSolidTypeFn.")
(comment candidatePegInterpretations-NonPartial-CycL "(#$candidatePegInterpretations-CycL PEG CYCL) means that the CycL expression CYCL is one of the CycL interpretations of the content of the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that is/has been under consideration.")
(comment creationTemplateIndexicals "<code>(#$creationTemplateIndexicals TEMPLATE TERM)</code> means that <code>TERM</code> is an indexical concept that appears in creation-template assertions (see #$creationTemplateAssertions) for the instance <code>TEMPLATE</code> of #$CycCreationTemplate and whose referent must be resolved by the relevant code (see #$creationTemplateForSubLFunction) before the assertion can be made.  Thus <code>TERM</code> is not a creation template FORT (see #$creationTemplateFORTs)</code> that is reified when the code is run, nor is <code>TERM</code> a term that will appear in any assertions that result from the running of the relevant code.  Rather, <code>TERM</code> will be resolved to the appropriate term and that term will appear in the relevant assertions.")
(comment ExemplarSentenceLevelPeg001 "The exemplar instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance used to guide the reification of instances of that collection vis a vis #$SentenceLevelPegCreationTemplate.")
(disjointWith ExcavationPit ShelterConstruction)
(disjointWith ExcavationPit Dock)
(disjointWith ExcavationPit DefenseSystem)
(comment LilliputQuery "The collection of all queries created by Project Lilliput's automatic trivial inference generator, and actually used as one of the corpora for Project Lilliput.")
(comment LilliputQuery-Open "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are open.")
(comment LilliputQuery-Closed "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are closed.")
(comment LilliputQuery-Good "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys known to be answerable - can generate at least one answer. The queries are answerable removal only, with no new terms allowed.")
(comment LilliputQuery-NoGood "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys known to be unanswerable. The queries are unanswerable removal only, with no new terms allowed.")
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-Open LilliputQuery-Closed)
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-Good LilliputQuery-NoGood)
(comment LilliputQuery-OpenGood "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are open and known to be answerable - can generate at least one answer. The queries are answerable removal only, with no new terms allowed.")
(comment LilliputQuery-OpenNoGood "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are open and known to be unanswerable. The queries are unanswerable removal only, with no new terms allowed.")
(comment LilliputQuery-ClosedGood "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are closed and known to be answerable - can generate at least one answer. The queries are answerable removal only, with no new terms allowed.")
(comment LilliputQuery-ClosedNoGood "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are open and known to be unanswerable. The queries are unanswerable removal only, with no new terms allowed.")
(comment LilliputQuery-Training "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are used for some training test suite for Project Lilliput.")
(comment LilliputQuery-Blind "The subcollection of #$LilliputQuerys that are used for some blind test suite for Project Lilliput. These queries are not permitted to be analyzed until the end of the project.")
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-Training LilliputQuery-Blind)
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-OpenGood LilliputQuery-OpenNoGood)
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-OpenGood LilliputQuery-ClosedGood)
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-OpenGood LilliputQuery-ClosedNoGood)
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-OpenNoGood LilliputQuery-ClosedGood)
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-OpenNoGood LilliputQuery-ClosedNoGood)
(disjointWith LilliputQuery-ClosedGood LilliputQuery-ClosedNoGood)
(comment SubmarineFleet-Organization "A specialization of #$NavalFleet-Organization.  This is the collection of all military organizations that operate some #$FleetOfSubmarines: a group of submarines that are in a relatively well-defined area and are under a single command.")
(disjointWith NonCombatReadyMilitaryUnit CombatReadyMilitaryUnit)
(comment NuclearBiologicalOrChemicalWeaponsUnit "A specialization of #$MilitaryOrganization.  Instances of #$NuclearBiologicalOrChemicalWeaponsUnit are those military units specialized in nuclear, biological, or chemical weapons.  See also its specializations, #$NBCWeaponsUnit-Offensive and #$NBCWeaponsUnit-Defensive")
(comment Monument "A specialization of #$MemorialMarker. Each instance of #$Monument this collection is a large, imposing, and awe-inspiring memorial marker. See also the predicate #$monumentHonors.")
(comment creationTemplateForSubLFunction "(#$creationTemplateForSubLFunction TEMPLATE S-EXPRESSION) identifies TEMPLATE as the instance of #$CycCreationTemplate that is used to gauge the success and optimize the performance of calls of the SUBL function denoted by the S-expression S-EXPRESSION.  See the #$comment on #$CycCreationTemplate for more details about how creation templates used with SUBL function calls.")
(comment StorageTower "A specialization of #$Tower and #$ContainerArtifact.
#$StorageTowers are freestanding, fixed structures that are used to
store various types of materials. Notable specs include #$WaterTower
and #$GrainSilo. This collection is #$disjointWith
#$CommunicationTower, which are used primarily for supporting
communications.")
(comment CommunicationTower "A specialization of #$Tower and #$CommunicationsFacility.
#$CommunicationTowers are freestanding, fixed structures that are used
primarily to support various communications platforms, such as radio
transmitters. A notable specialization of this collection is
#$RadioTower. Also, this collection is #$disjointWith #$StorageTower,
which are used primarily for storing materials such as water and
grain.")
(comment assertPriorToInCreationTemplate "(#$assertPriorToInCreationTemplate ASSERTION-1 ASSERTION-2 TEMPLATE) means that when using TEMPLATE to create a new term, the instance of #$CycLAssertion corresponding to ASSERTION-1 should be asserted prior to asserting the instance of #$CycLAssertion corresponding to ASSERTION-2.")
(disjointWith CommunicationTower (CollectionDifferenceFn Tower CommunicationTower))
(comment MilitaryBase-Organization "A specialization of #$MilitaryOrganization.  Typically, an instance of #$MilitaryBase-Organization is a military organization that is part (see #$subOrganizations) of a larger force, and is associated with and in control of one of that force's military bases (see #$MilitaryBase-Grounds).")
(comment MilitaryBase-Grounds "A specialization of #$MilitaryFacility and #$GroundsOfOrganization.  Each instance of #$MilitaryBase-Grounds is a base, post, or fort controlled by a military organization (see #$MilitaryBase-Organization).")
(comment IrregularMilitaryForce "A specialization of #$MilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$IrregularMilitaryForce is a military organization that is not regularly established, but rather, exists in connection with (and usually only for the duration of) a specific conflict or situation.  A group member of an irregular military force might be a regular (instance of) #$MilitaryPerson who is assigned a special military task very different from his usual ones, or might be someone who is not regularly a #$MilitaryPerson at all but is nevertheless assigned a military task.  
<p>
For example, suppose a Central American army officer organized a group of villagers to help defend against an impending invasion.  The group would for a time constitute a part of the country's irregular army force -- which force would itself be an instance of (#$IrregularForcesFn #$Army-BranchOfService).")
(comment UnitedStatesMilitaryOrganization "A specialization of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  This collection includes the (contemporary) U.S. military forces: the #$UnitedStatesArmy, #$UnitedStatesNavy, #$UnitedStatesAirForce, #$UnitedStatesMarineCorps (and, during wartime, the #$UnitedStatesCoastGuard), as well as all of their #$subOrganizations.  Each  US military organization is a suborganization of the #$USExecutiveBranch.")
(comment Reserves-ArmedForces "A specialization of #$GovernmentMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$Reserves-ArmedForces is a military organization belonging to some country (and perhaps associated with a particular branch of that country's military) but not as part of its regular forces.  Reservists may be called to active duty in wartime.")
(disjointWith CoastGuardOrganization Army-BranchOfService)
(disjointWith MarineCorps Army-BranchOfService)
(disjointWith CoastGuardOrganization MarineCorps)
(comment Army-BranchOfService "A specialization of #$BranchOfMilitaryService (q.v.).  This is the collection of all modern nation-states' armies, considered as a whole branch of military service.  
<p>
Note that this sense of \"army\" is different from that used to describe certain military units, e.g. the American 5th Army in the European Theater in WWII.  Use #$Army-MilitaryEchelon for the latter meaning.")
(comment CoastGuardOrganization "A specialization of #$BranchOfMilitaryService (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CoastGuardOrganization is a military organization
responsible for defending its nation's coastline and waterways.")
(comment ExecutiveGovernmentalOrganization "A specialization of #$LegalGovernmentOrganization.  Each instance of #$ExecutiveGovernmentalOrganization is an executive (i.e. law-enforcement, state department, or national security) organization that is part of some #$RegionalGovernment.")
(comment GovernmentMilitaryOrganization "A specialization of both #$MilitaryOrganization and #$ExecutiveGovernmentalOrganization.  Each instance of #$GovernmentMilitaryOrganization is a military organization belonging to, and  directed by, the governing body of a geographical region or international organization.  The governing body in question may be a national government, a state or provincial government, or an international governing body such as the #$UnitedNationsOrganization. Important specializations of this collection include #$Army-BranchOfService, #$Navy, and #$AirForce.  This collection excludes private armies and mercenary forces.")
(comment NationalGuardOrganization "A specialization of #$GovernmentMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$NationalGuardOrganization serves to protect the interior, boundaries, and territories of its home region.")
(comment RepublicanGuard "A specialization of #$GovernmentMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$RepublicanGuard is a part or branch of a nation's military force, separate from its regular forces, whose main purpose is to protect the nation's government, leader, or top officals.
<p>
For example, during #$SaddamHusseinAl-Takriti's presidency, the Iraqi Republican Guard Forces Command were considered a branch of #$Iraq's military.  #$France and #$Syria also have republican guards.")
(comment MilitarySpecialForce "A specialization of #$MilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$MilitarySpecialForce is an elite suborganization of
some other military organization.  Examples include the U.S. Army
Green Berets, the U.S. Navy SEALs, and the Gestapo Schutzstaffel.")
(comment PersonalStaff-Military "A specialization of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$PersonalStaff-Military
is the sub-organization of some #$StaffOrganization, comprising those members of the staff who attend to personal concerns and responsibilities of the unit commander (see #$unitStaff and #$unitCommander).")
(comment unitStaff "A binary predicate that relates a #$ModernMilitaryOrganization to the sub-organization that is its #$StaffOrganization (q.v.).  <code>(#$unitStaff UNIT STAFF)</code> means that <code>STAFF</code> functions as the staff for <code>UNIT</code>.  The members of <code>STAFF</code> are officers (see #$staffOfficers) who advise the commander of <code>UNIT</code> (see #$unitCommander).")
(disjointWith ConventionalWeaponsUnit-MilitarySpecialty NuclearBiologicalOrChemicalWeaponsUnit)
(comment supportsResponseToOf "(#$supportsResponseToOf PASSAGE QUESTION RESPONSE) means that the content of the NL text PASSAGE supports a response of RESPONSE to the NL question QUESTION.")
(comment textSupportsSomeAnswerTo "(#$textSupportsSomeAnswerTo PASSAGE QUESTION) means that the content of the NL text PASSAGE supports some answer for the NL question QUESTION.")
(comment textSupportsAffirmativeAnswerTo "(#$textSupportsAffirmativeAnswerTo PASSAGE QUESTION) means that the content of the NL text PASSAGE supports an affirmative answer for the NL question QUESTION.")
(comment textSupportsNegativeAnswerTo "(#$textSupportsNegativeAnswerTo PASSAGE QUESTION) means that the content of the NL text PASSAGE supports a negative answer for the NL question QUESTION.")
(comment textDoesNotSupportAnswerTo "(#$textDoesNotSupportAnswerTo PASSAGE QUESTION) means that the content of the NL text PASSAGE does not support an answer for the NL question QUESTION.")
(comment ExpressionTypeByFamiliarity "A specialization of #$LinguisticExpressionType.  Instances of this collection facet #$NounPhraseExpressionPeg according to definiteness marking of the expression used to introduce the referent peg into the discourse. Instances include #$IndefiniteExpression and #$DefiniteExpression.")
(comment DragonTeeth "#$DragonTeeth are regularly spaced concrete or metal
barriers laid in single or multiple rows to prevent vehicle
movement.")
(comment NPTypeByDefiniteness "An instance of #$FacetingCollectionType that facets the collection of instances of #$NounPhrase by whether an instance has a definite or indefinite determiner.")
(comment DefiniteNounPhrase "A specialization of #$NounPhrase.  Each instance is a noun phrase with a definite determiner, such as \"the\" or \"his\".")
(comment IndefiniteNounPhrase "A specialization of #$NounPhrase.  Each instance is a noun phrase with an indefinite determiner, such as \"a\" or \"an\".")
(comment editionOfPeriodicalByDate "A #$TernaryPredicate.  <code>(#$editionOfPeriodicalByDate SERIES DATE ISSUE)</code> means that <code>ISSUE</code> is the #$PeriodicalIssue of the #$PeriodicalSeries <code>SERIES</code> for the #$Date <code>DATE</code>.  This is the predicate corresponding to #$EditionFn (q.v.).")
(comment notAbducibleForArg "An assertion of the form (#$notAbducibleForArg PRED N) represents 
a restriction on hypothesis formation relative to abductive inferences.  Specifically, Cyc cannot hypothesize values for 
the Nth argument position in an assertion whose zeroth argument is PRED. 
Abductive inferences often seek to explain an assertion or set of 
assertions by hypothesizing the truth of other assertions.  However, we 
wish to restrict the kinds of assertions which Cyc will hypothesize such 
that Cyc does not hypothesize new theoretical or conceptual knowledge.  
The predicate #$notAbducibleForArg is used to restrict the values which 
Cyc could hypothesize for an assertion involving PRED.  For example, 
consider the assertion (#$notAbducibleForArg #$makesProductType 2). The 
assertion (#$makesProductType ORG TYPE) states that the instance of 
#$Organization ORG produces instances of #$ProductType TYPE.  While we 
don't want to hypothesize that a known organization makes novel types of 
products, we would be more willing to accept that an unknown organization 
makes a particular type of product.  More specifically, while we wouldn't 
want to hypothesize (#$makesProductType #$DunkinDonuts-Corporation 
#$Handgun), it would be useful to hypothesize the existence of 
organizations that produce the kinds of products that known organizations 
also produce.  Thus, (#$makesProductType (The Organization) #$Doughnut) 
might be a useful hypothesis within an abductive inference.")
(comment syntacticNodeAgreementPredicate "<code>(#$syntacticNodeAgreementPredicate NODE PRED)</code> means that the instance <code>NODE</code> of #$SyntacticNode has the agreement properties of <code>PRED</code>, an instance of #$TermPhrasesConstraintPredicate.  For example, where <code>(#$syntacticNodeString NODE \"a democracy\")</code>, </code>(#$syntacticNodeAgreementPredicate NODE #$singular)</code> would hold, as \"a democracy\" is a singular NP.")
(comment expressionTypeAgreementPredicate "<code>(#$expressionTypeAgreementPredicate EXPRESSION-TYPE PRED)</code> means that the instance <code>EXPRESSION-TYPE</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionType has the agreement properties of <code>PRED</code>, an instance of #$TermPhrasesConstraintPredicate. ")
(comment OwlSemanticDataType "#$OwlSemanticDataType is the collection of Owl data types.  The datatypes can be characterized in two ways -- (1) by the type of thing it is; e.g. a positive integer, (2) by the way it is denoted; e.g. positive integers that are denoted by instances of #$SubLPositiveInteger.")
(comment sectionTitle "<code>(#$sectionTitle WORK TITLE)</code> relates an instance <code>WORK</code> of #$TextualPCW to a string <code>TITLE</code> that is its title, where <code>WORK</code> must be a sub-section of some larger work (see #$textualPCWSubSections).")
(comment subWorkPriorToSubWorkInWork "An instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate. <code>(#$subWorkPriorToSubWorkInWork EARLY-SUBWORK LATER-SUBWORK WORK)</code> means that the instance <code>WORK</code> of #$ConceptualWork is structured in such a way that the sub-work (see #$subWorks) <code>EARLY-SUBWORK</code> comes before  the sub-work <code>LATER-SUBWORK</code> (or in a performance of <code>WORK</code>, a performance of <code>EARLY-SUBWORK</code> is typically presented to the audience before a performance of <code>LATER-SUBWORK</code>).  For example, <code>(#$subWorkPriorToSubWorkInWork TaxMan-TheSong TomorrowNeverKnows-TheSong Revolver-TheAlbum)</code> means that the song \"Tax Man\" comes before the song \"Tomorrow Never Knows\" on the Beatles album, <i>Revolver</i>.")
(comment TreeStructuredOrganization "A specialization of #$Organization.  Each instance of #$TreeStructuredOrganization is an organization whose members (see #$hasMembers) and/or sub-organizations (see #$subOrganizations) are arranged in a strict command hierarchy tree (see #$TreeOrganizationalChart), with a single organization member at the root of the tree who commands the entire organization.")
(comment NonCombatReadyMilitaryUnit "A specialzation of #$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable.  This is the collection of military units that have been reduced (through fighting or other enemy action) to half or less of their starting combat power.  Units in this condition are not combat-ready (cf. #$CombatReadyMilitaryUnit) and are unlikely to take offensive action.")
(disjointWith NonCombatReadyMilitaryUnit MilitaryUnit-NonCombat)
(disjointWith CombatReadyMilitaryUnit MilitaryUnit-NonCombat)
(comment CombatReadyMilitaryUnit "A specialization of #$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable.  This is the collection of military units that 
have most or all of their starting combat power, and thus are combat-ready (cf. #$NonCombatReadyMilitaryUnit).")
(comment MilitaryAgent "A specialization of #$IntelligentAgent.  This is the collection of all agents -- individual persons and organizations -- that are part of some military group or service.  Specializations of #$MilitaryAgent include #$MilitaryPerson and #$MilitaryOrganization.")
(comment ParamilitaryOrganization "A specialization of #$MilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$ParamilitaryOrganization is an organization formed according to a military structure, but not as a part of any mainstream military force.  Paramilitary organizations sometimes operate as auxiliaries to or in place of mainstream military forces (e.g. as border patrols or police units), but generally they operate independently of any government or national allegiance, often with loyalty to some political program or party.  
<p>
Examples of #$ParamilitaryOrganizations include the #$MilitiaOfMontana and the Ulster Defence Association (UDA), the Protestant militant group operating in Northern Ireland.")
(comment NavalFleet-Organization "a specialization of #$MilitaryOrganization.  This is the collection of military organizations whose primary equipment consists of groups of naval ships and (sometimes) submarines that are in a relatively defined area and are under a single command (see #$FleetOfMilitaryCraft).  
<p>
Note that this collection corresponds to the broad, generic sense of the English word <i>fleet</i>, rather than the more specialized, technical sense in which the #$UnitedStatesNavy uses the word, as in e.g. <i>U.S. Atlantic Fleet</i>.")
(comment DeliberatelyTouchingSomething "In each instance of #$DeliberatelyTouchingSomething, the salient
touching (cf., #$PhysicalContactEvent) is a deliberate action on the
part of at least one agent.  The following will both be
true of that agent: (#$performedBy TOUCH AGT) and  (#$objectsInContact TOUCH AGT). 
In cases in which two or more agents are in
physical contact (#$objectsInMutualContact, qv.), it is sufficient for the touching to be deliberate 
if just one participant is
acting deliberately. E.g. if AGT1 grasps the hand of AGT2, this
grasping is an instance of #$DeliberatelyTouchingSomething, even if AGT2 is not
participating voluntarily.")
(disjointWith TernaryOrderingPredicate CapabilityPredicate)
(disjointWith TernaryOrderingPredicate CognitivePredicate)
(comment TernaryOrderingPredicate "A collection of ternary predicates, each instance of which is a ternary, rule-macro predicate for describing an ordering of two elements in a context in which that ordering holds.  A notable example is #$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType, which relates a situation type to two proper sub-situation-types that stand in a quasi-temporal order in the context of that situation type (specifically, in any instance of that situation type, there will be instances of the specified sub-situation-types that stand in the #$startsAfterStartingOf relation.  Every instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate is transitive in two of its arguments (the arguments where the ordered elements occur), which is specified using #$transitiveInArgs.  The context-argument is specified using #$orderingContextArg.")
(comment expressionPegDefiniteness "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot.  (#$expressionPegDefiniteness PEG DEFINITENESS) identifies DEFINITEESS as the instance of #$ExpressionTypeByFamiliarity of which the instance PEG of #$NominalExpressionPeg is an instance.  For example, (#$expressionPegDefiniteness ADog-Peg #$IndefiniteExpression) means that the phrase that introduced ADog-Peg (presumably \"a dog\") was indefinite (had an indefinite determiner).")
(comment orderingContextArg "<code>(#$orderingContextArg PREDICATE ARG-NUM)</code> identifies <code>ARG-NUM</code> as the argument-place for the context against which the other arguments related by <code>PREDICATE</code> are assigned an order.  <p>For example, 
<pre>     (#$orderingContextArg #$subWorkPriorToSubWorkInWork 3)</pre> means that in <pre>     (#$subWorkPriorToSubWorkInWork WORK-1 WORK-2 WORK-3)</pre> <code>WORK-1</code> and <code>WORK-2</code> are ordered in the context of <code>WORK-3</code> (in this case, <code>WORK-1</code> \"comes before\" <code>WORK-2</code> in <code>WORK-3</code>.  Similarly, <pre>     (#$orderingContextArg #$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType 1)</pre> means that in <pre>     (#$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE-1 SIT-TYPE-2 SIT-TYPE-3)</pre> <code>SIT-TYPE-2</code> and <code>SIT-TYPE-3</code> are assigned an order in the context of <code>SIT-TYPE-1</code> (in this case, <code>SIT-TYPE-2</code> is a \"stage\" that starts after the start of the stage <code>SIT-TYPE-3</code> in the larger script or process <code>SIT-TYPE-1</code>.")
(comment assertedSentence "A #$KBDependentRelation (q.v.) and specialization of #$knownSentence (q.v.) that is used to state that a given #$CycLSentence-Assertible has been asserted in the KB (in some accessible #$Microtheory). More exactly, (#$assertedSentence SENT) is true in microtheory MT precisely when SENT canonicalizes into a set of clauses of the same form as the canonicalized form of some assertion in a microtheory accessible to MT. (The predicate is thus true both of assertions entered into the KB by hand and assertions deduced by Cyc from forward rules.) For the more specific claim that SENT is asserted <b>in MT</b>, see the predicate #$ist-Asserted.
<p>
This predicate enables Cyc to select a subset of information when answering queries, filtering out more generic (inferrable-but-not-asserted) information. Thus, for example, the query:
<p>
(#$genls #$Dog #$Thing)
<p>
will return #$True (in appropriate microtheories), whereas:
<p>
(#$assertedSentence (#$genls #$Dog #$Thing))
<p>
will not.")
(comment tokenizationTokenizer "(#$tokenizationTokenizer TOKENIZATION TOKENIZER) means that the TOKENIZATION was created by the software agent TOKENIZER.")
(comment creationTemplateAllowsAllRulesFromMt "<code>(#$creationTemplateAllowableRules TEMPLATE MT)</code> means that for any value of <code>RULE</code> such that <code>(#$quotedIsa RULE #$CycLRuleAssertion)</code> and <code>(#$assertionMt RULE MT)</code>, <code>RULE</code> may be considered when calling the code-level function associated with <code>TEMPLATE</code>, an instance of #$CycCreationTemplate.  Thus #$creationTemplateAllowsAllRulesFromMt allows one to specify allowable rules (see #$creationTemplateAllowableRules) for a template at the level of an entire microtheory.  For more information about how creation templates are used, see the #$comment on

#$CycCreationTemplate.")
(comment programUsesCycCreationTemplate "<code>(#$programUsesCycCreationTemplate PROGRAM TEMPLATE)</code> means that <code>PROGRAM</code>, an instance of #$CycRelatedProgram, makes use of <code>TEMPLATE</code>, an instance of #$CycCreationTemplate.")
(comment typePrimaryFunction "A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate and a specialization of #$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable (q.v.) that is used to represent the <i>primary</i> use associated with some type of object (usually instances of #$PhysicalDevice but also instances of #$Organization, or anything           else that can be said to have a function) as a consequence of the <i>intent</i> with which that type of object was designed. <code>(#$typePrimaryFunction OBJ-TYPE SIT-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that instances of <code>OBJ-TYPE</code> are primarily intended to participate in situations of type <code>SIT-TYPE</code> in the instance of #$Role <code>ROLE</code>.  For example, the primary use associated with any instance of #$ElectricalToaster is making toast: we can represent this by (#$typePrimaryFunction #$ElectricalToaster #$MakingToast #$deviceUsed). As another example, the primary use associated with a government intelligence organization is performing instances of #$IntelligenceAnalysisProcess.           We can represent this as (#$typePrimaryFunction #$GovernmentIntelligenceOrganization #$IntelligenceAnalysisProcess #$performedBy).
<p>
Note that this predicate should be used to represent only the very specific <code>SIT-TYPE</code> in which something is intended to function in some role.  For example, in most contexts, while it is correct to assert (#$typePrimaryFunction #$Telephone #$AudioCommunicating #$deviceUsed), it is not correct to assert (#$typePrimaryFunction #$Telephone #$Communicating #$deviceUsed) since #$Communicating is not specific enough. Instead of the latter sentence, (#$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable #$Telephone #$Communicating #$deviceUsed) is preferable.  When asserting a #$typePrimaryFunction GAF, use the most specific #$Situation <code>SIT-TYPE</code> available in the microtheory in which you are making the assertion, as long as you do not believe <code>SIT-TYPE</code> is too specific.  If no #$Situation specific enough is available, consider creating one.
<p>
This predicate presupposes that instances of the first argument have the function that they do in virtue of the <i>design</i> of some intelligent agent. If the \"functionality\" in question does not obviously derive from some agent's design or artifice, #$typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional (q.v.) should be used instead. See also (#$TypeCapableFn #$behaviorCapable), #$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable and #$primaryFunction.")
(comment Militia "A specialization of #$ParamilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$Militia is a non-governmental military force.  Many #$Militias consider themselves patriotic; but they are often viewed with suspicion (and may be considered terrorist groups) by the government and the general citizenry.
<p>
Note that this collection reflects a different sense of <i>militia</i> than that sometimes used to describe certain auxilliary governmental military organizations, such as #$NationalGuardOrganizations.")
(comment TerroristGroup "A specialization of #$ParamilitaryOrganization and #$TerroristAgent.  Each instance of #$TerroristGroup is an organization that tries to achieve its political ends by engaging in terrorist activities (see #$TerroristAct), such as bombing public buildings or attacking civilians on the street.  Instances of this collection include #$PalestineIslamicJihad and the #$IrishRepublicanArmy.")
(comment IndependentCountryOrTerroristGroup "A specialization of #$LegalAgent.  This collection is by definition (see #$rewriteOf) the union of #$IndependentCountry and #$TerroristGroup.  It is the collection of all agents who may be considered to be \"players\" or \"agents\" in the scenarios generated by Cyc's scenario generation technology.")
(comment AirForceBase-MilitaryOrganization "A specialization of #$MilitaryBase-Organization (q.v.).  This is the collection of military organizations that run air force bases (cf. #$AirForceBase-Grounds).  Each instance of #$AirForceBase-MilitaryOrganization is a sub-organization of an #$AirForce.")
(comment ArmyBase-MilitaryOrganization "A specialization of #$MilitaryBase-Organization (q.v.).  This is the collection of military organizations that run army bases (cf. #$ArmyBase-Grounds).  Each instance of #$ArmyBase-MilitaryOrganization is a sub-organization of an #$Army-BranchOfService.")
(comment NavyBase-MilitaryOrganization "A specialization of #$MilitaryBase-Organization (q.v.).  This is the collection of military organizations that run naval bases (see #$NavalShipBase).  Each instance of #$NavyBase-MilitaryOrganization is a sub-organization of a #$Navy.")
(comment ArmyReserves "A specialization of #$Reserves-ArmedForces.  Each instance of #$ArmyReserves is a reserve military organization that is #$affiliatedWith an #$Army-BranchOfService.")
(comment NavyReserves "A specialization of #$Reserves-ArmedForces.  Each instance of #$NavyReserves is a reserve military organization that is #$affiliatedWith a #$Navy.")
(comment AirForceReserves "A specialization of #$Reserves-ArmedForces.  Each instance of #$AirForceReserves is a reserve military organization that is #$affiliatedWith an #$AirForce.")
(comment CoordinatingStaff-USArmy "A specialization of #$UnitedStatesMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$CoordinatingStaff-USArmy  is  the sub-organization of some #$StaffOrganization-USArmy (q.v.) comprising the coordinating staff of the unit in question.")
(comment SpecialStaff-USArmy "A specialization of #$UnitedStatesMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$SpecialStaff-USArmy is a sub-organization of some #$StaffOrganization-USArmy (q.v.).  Members of a US Army special staff provide support in specialized technical or functional areas, such as fire support, medical services, or air defense operations.")
(comment PersonalStaff-USArmy "A specialization of #$PersonalStaff-Military and #$UnitedStatesMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$PersonalStaff-USArmy is the organization comprising the personal staff of the commander of some #$StaffOrganization-USArmy (q.v.).")
(comment StaffOrganization-USArmy "A specialization of #$UnitedStatesMilitaryOrganization and #$StaffOrganization.  This is the collection of staff organizations of the United States Army, at the level of corps or lower.   Such a  staff organization's primary purpose is to assist a commander in the discharge of his or her responsibilites.  Each #$StaffOrganization-USArmy includes several sub-organizations, e.g. a #$CoordinatingStaff-USArmy and a #$PersonalStaff-USArmy.")
(comment UnifiedCombatantCommand-US "A specialization of #$UnitedStatesMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$UnifiedCombatantCommand-US is a US military organization composed of forces from two or more branches of service (see #$BranchOfMilitaryService) that has a broad and continuing mission.  Typically, a unified combatant command has operational control of the US combat forces within a particular geogaphical region.  Instances include #$US-PacificCommand-HonoluluHI.
<p>
See also #$DefenseLINKMt.")
(comment PeaceKeepingForce "A specialization of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  This is the collection of those military organizations whose mission is to maintain peace in some volatile region.")
(comment CarrierBattleGroup-MilitarySpecialty "A specialization of #$NavalFleet-Organization.  This is the collection of specialized military units that have a #$CarrierBattleGroup (q.v.) as (the group consisting of) its primary equipment.")
(comment TextLearnerReificationTemplate "The collection of instances of #$CycCreationTemplate that are used by the Text Learner code (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram).")
(comment strictlyFunctionalInArgs "A #$MetaPredicate for specifying the argument-place(s) with respect to which a given #$FunctionalPredicate (q.v) is \"functional\" in a strict sense.  <code>(#$strictlyFunctionalInArgs PRED N)</code> means that <code>PRED</code> is strictly functional in its <code>N</code><sub>th</sub> argument-place.  That is, for any particular way of fixing all of <code>PRED</code>'s other arguments, there will be at most one thing that, if taken as the <code>N</code><sub>th</sub> argument, would result in a true sentence.  For example, #$biologicalMother is strictly functional in its second argument, as nothing has more than one biological mother.
<p>
In most cases, the entry-format (see #$argFormat) of a strictly functional argument-place should be #$singleEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.).  Obviously, the specified integer <code>N</code> must be less than or equal to the arity (see #$arity) of <code>PRED</code>.  Note that, for some ways of fixing the other arguments, there might be <i>nothing</i> that would yield a true sentence when put in <code>PRED</code>'s functional argument-place.  For example, #$dictator is functional in its second argument because a given country can have at most one dictator at a given time; but of course some countries have no dictator.
<p>
For a more general predicate that encompasses a looser sense in which a predicate's argument-place might be called \"functional\", see #$functionalInArgs.")
(comment FolderOfResultsFromQuerySearchByAgentWithStringFn "An expression of the form (#$FolderOfResultsFromQuerySearchByAgentWithStringFn AGENT STRING) is used to represent a folder that will hold all of the instances of #$CycLQuery that were found or generated in response to a #$SearchingForACycLQuery event by the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT in whichAGENT used the #$CharacterString STRING as the basis for the search.")
(comment folderOfResultsFromQuerySearchByAgentWithString "An assertion of the form (#$folderOfResultsFromQuerySearchByAgentWithString FOLDER AGENT STRING) states that the #$KEInteractionFolder FOLDER stores a set of #$CycLQuerySpecifications that the system found or generated in response to the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT's searches (see #$SearchingForACycLQuery) using the #$CharacterString STRING.  This is the corresponding predicate of the function #$FolderOfResultsFromQuerySearchByAgentWithStringFn.")
(comment SearchingForACycLQuery "A sub-collection of #$StringBasedSearching, #$SearchingForACycLQuery is the process of using NL strings to interact with some application in order to discover a #$CycLQuerySpecification whose content matches, by some metric, the content of the search string.")
(disjointWith BiologicalDevelopmentEvent AbnormalSystemCondition)
(disjointWith Dying EyeDisorder)
(disjointWith IndividualAgent GeographicalThing)
(comment formulaForStringPertinentToDegreeFromFolderOfType "An assertion of the form (#$formulaForStringPertinentToDegreeFromFolderOfType STRING FORMULA DEGREE FOLDER-TYPE) states that the #$CharacterString STRING would translate into the #$CycLSentence-Askable FORMULA and be interpreted to have the #$Relevance DEGREE within the context of the activity type that is supported by the contents of instances of FOLDER-TYPE, a sub-collection of #$KEInteractionFolder.  For instance, (#$formulaForStringPertinentToDegreeFromFolderOfType \"When was Arafat born?\" (#$birthDate #$YassirArafat ?DATE) (#$HighAmountFn #$Relevance) #$FolderWithIntelligenceQueriesForSuggestion) would state that the string \"When was Arafat born?\" would be translated to the #$CycLSentence-Askable (#$birthDate #$YassirArafat DATE) and been deemed to have a high amount of relevance relative to queries that are stored in instances of #$KEInteractionFolder that are also instances of #$FolderWithIntelligenceQueriesForSuggestion.")
(comment genlCreationTemplate "<code>(#$genlCreationTemplates SPECIFIC GENERAL)</code> means that the instance <code>SPECIFIC</code> of #$CycCreationTemplate inherits the creation-template properties specified in <code>GENERAL</code>, also an instance of #$CycCreationTemplate.  Specifically, any rules, FORTs, or indexicals that are applicable for <code>GENERAL</code> will also apply for <code>SPECIFIC</code>.")
(comment AreaIlluminatedBySunlight "A specialization of #$OutdoorLocation which subsumes areas illuminated directly or indirectly by #$Sunlight.")
(comment Unknown-TEPResponsePolarity "Instances of this collection are #$TEPResponses which express propositional content which is uncertain.")
(comment provenanceStringForTEP "(#$queryTextForTEP TEP STRING) means that STRING references the source for background passage used for the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment SubExpressionAtOffsetFn "(#$SubExpressionAtOffsetFn PEG STRING OFFSET) denotes the subexpression of PEG whose string is STRING and whose start offset is OFFSET.")
(comment ConventionalWeaponsUnit-MilitarySpecialty "A #$MilitaryUnitSpecialtyType and a specialization of #$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable.  This is the collection of all military units trained, organized and equipped for the handling, deployment, employment, or containment of some kind of conventionional weapons system. 
<p>
\"Conventional\" in this context refers to weapons relying upon conventional explosives for motive force and/or destructive potential.  Cf. #$NuclearBiologicalOrChemicalWeaponsUnit.
<p>
A notable specialization of this collection is #$ArtilleryUnit-MilitarySpecialty.
")
(comment TheCycLLiarSentence "An instance of #$CycLPropositionalSentence.  This is a CycL version of a so-called <i>Liar sentence</i>, which says of itself that it is false.  To be precise, #$TheCycLLiarSentence is the following CycL sentence:
<pre>  
    (#$forAll ?SENT-1 
      (#$implies 
        (#$equals #$TheCycLLiarSentence ?SENT-1) 
        (#$sentenceTruth ?SENT-1 #$False))) .")
(comment CommandUnit-MilitarySpecialty "A #$MilitaryUnitSpecialtyType and thus a specialization of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  This is the collection of all military units that play a command role.  An important specialization of this collection is #$HeadquartersUnit-MilitarySpecialty.")
(comment HeadquartersUnit-MilitarySpecialty "A #$MilitaryUnitSpecialtyType and a specialization of #$CommandUnit-MilitarySpecialty.  This is the collection of all military units that specialize in the operation of a military headquarters.")
(comment HeadquartersAndHeadquartersCompany "A specialization of #$HeadquartersUnit-MilitarySpecialty, #$Company-MilitaryEchelon, and #$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable. This is the collection of all military companies that operate a military headquarters.  Instances of #$HeadquartersAndHeadquartersCompany are commomly referred to as <i><b>HHC</b></i>s.")
(comment TaskForce-Military "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByOrganizationalFeature and thus a specialization of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$TaskForce-Military is a temporary military unit formed to accomplish a particular objective.")
(comment JointTaskForce-Military "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByOrganizationalFeature and a specialization of #$TaskForce-Military (q.v.).  Each instance of #$JointTaskForce-Military is a task force made up of military units of different types.")
(comment Section-MilitaryOrganization "A specialization of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$Section-MilitaryOrganization is a tactial military unit of a size that puts it in-between two standard echelons (see #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon).  Different branches of military service consider different sorts of units to be \"sections\".   For example, in the #$UnitedStatesArmy and #$UnitedStatesMarineCorps, a section is a unit that is smaller than (and is a #$subOrganizations of) a #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon and larger than a (and has as a sub-oganization at least one) #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon.  In the #$UnitedStatesNavy, a section of ships is one-half of a #$Division-MilitaryEchelon, and a section of aircraft consists of two aircraft.")
(disjointWith Section-MilitaryOrganization Platoon-MilitaryEchelon)
(disjointWith Squad-MilitaryEchelon Section-MilitaryOrganization)
(disjointWith Division-MilitaryEchelon Section-MilitaryOrganization)
(comment TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat "These are tests which require a determination of whether or not a response to a question (both expressed in a a #$NaturalLanguage) is justified on the basis of a particular background text. The predicates #$backgroundTextForTEP and #$queryTextForTEP, should be used to specify the background query texts for a particlar #$TextualEntailmentProblem.  The predicate #$textStringForTEPResponse should be used to specify the response text for a particular #$TEPResponse.")
(comment advisorFor "(#$advisorFor AGT1 AGT2) means that #$IntelligentAgent
AGT2 is an advisor for AGT1.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblem "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were created for (and contributed by participants in) the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromCycorp "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by Cycorp for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromStanford "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by Stanford for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromMIT "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by MIT for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment cwTypeHasInfoStructureType "<code>(#$cwTypeHasInfoStructureType WORK-TYPE AIS-TYPE)</code> means that every instance of <code>WORK-TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$StructurallySpecificWork, has an instance of <code>AIS-TYPE</code>, a specializaton of #$AbstractInformationStructure, as its information structure.")
(comment InterAgentQuantity "The collection of instances of #$SocialQuantity that agents have with respect to another agent. (c.f. (#$HighAmountFn #$Prestige)).")
(comment openingTagForStructure "(#$openingTagForStructure STRUC SOURCE TAG) means that STRUC, an instance of either #$GraphicalStructure or #$TextString generated from SOURCE, an #$HTMLSource, has, in SOURCE, an opening tag of TAG, a tag that is the result of #$OpenTagFn.")
(comment htmlSourceOf "(#$htmlSourceOf SOURCE DOC) means that SOURCE, an #$HTMLSource, is the source of DOC, an instance of either #$TextString or #$GraphicalStructure.  SOURCE generates DOC on most web browsers.")
(comment MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon "A #$SecondOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon is a collection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganizations that are of the same standard echelon (though not necesarily part of the same #$BranchOfMilitaryService, or even sub-organizations of the same country).  Examples include #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon and #$Division-MilitaryEchelon.
<p>
Specializations of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon include the partially-overlapping collections #$USMarineCorpsEchelon, #$USArmyEchelon, and #$IranianArmyEchelon.
<p>
To state that a military unit is of a particular echelon, use 
#$echelonOfUnit.")
(comment USNavyEchelon "A specialization of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon (q.v.), pertaining to the echelon structure of the #$UnitedStatesNavy.")
(comment USAirForceEchelon "A specialization of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon (q.v.), pertaining to the echelon structure of the #$UnitedStatesAirForce.")
(comment USArmyEchelon "A specialization of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon (q.v.), pertaining to the echelon structure of the #$UnitedStatesArmy.")
(comment USMarineCorpsEchelon "A specialization of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon (q.v.), pertaining to the echelon structure of the #$UnitedStatesMarineCorps.")
(comment IranianArmyEchelon "A specialization of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon (q.v.), pertaining to the echelon structure of the Iranian Army.")
(comment Company-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization. #$Company-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, a company consists of between 60 and 190 soldiers, and is typically made up of three to five platoons (see #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon).  Two to five companies typically make up an army #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon.
<p>
In the US Marine Corps, a company consists of roughly 240 marines, and is typically made up of four platoons.  Five companies typically make up a marine #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment Battalion-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization. #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, a battalion consists of between 300 and 1,000 soldiers, and is typically made up of four to six companies (see #$Company-MilitaryEchelon).  Two to five battalions typically make up an army #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon.
<p>
In the US Marine Corps, a battalion consists of roughly 1,200 marines, and is typically made up of five companies.  Five battalions typically make up a marine #$Regiment-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment Platoon-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, a platoon consists of between 16 and 44 soldiers, and is typically made up of two to four squads (see #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon) or sections (see #$Section-MilitaryOrganization).  Three to five platoons typically make up an army #$Company-MilitaryEchelon.
<p>
In the US Marine Corps, a platoon consists of roughly 60 marines, and is typically made up of four squads.  Four platoons typically make up a marine #$Company-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment Squad-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, a squad consists of between 9 and 10 soldiers, and is the smallest type of unit in the Army force structure.  Two to four squads typically make up an army #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon.
<p>
In the US Marine Corps, a squad consists of roughly 15 marines, and is typically made up of three fire teams (see #$FireTeam-MilitaryEchelon).  Four squads typically make up a marine #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment FireTeam-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Marine Corps, a fire team is a small, task-oriented unit consisting of roughly 5 marines.  Three fire teams typically make up a marine #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment Regiment-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Regiment-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USMarineCorpsEchelon and #$USArmyEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Marine Corps, a regiment consists of roughly 6,000 marines, and is typically made up of five battalions (see #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon)
<p>
In the US Army, certain brigade-sized units (see #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon) are sometimes called \"regiments\".")
(comment Brigade-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon is an instance of #$USArmyEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, a brigade is a special-purpose unit consisting of between 3,000 and 5,000 soldiers, and is typically made up of two to five battalions (see #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon).  Three brigades (or brigade-sized units) typically make up an army #$Division-MilitaryEchelon.  An army brigade is usually formed to fulfill a specific objective, and is commanded by a brigadier general.  
<p>
Note that certain brigade-sized US army units are called \"regiments\" (see #$Regiment-MilitaryEchelon).")
(comment Division-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Division-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USArmyEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, a division consists of between 10,000 and 15,000 soldiers, and is typically made up of three brigade-sized units (see #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon).  Two to five divisions typically make up an army #$Corps-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment Corps-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Corps-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USArmyEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, a corps consists of between 20,000 and 45,000 soldiers, is typically made up of two to five divisions (see #$Division-MilitaryEchelon), and is commanded by a lieutenant general.  Two or more corps typically make up an #$Army-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment Battery-MilitaryUnit "A specialization of both #$Company-MilitaryEchelon and #$ArtilleryUnit-MilitarySpecialty.  Each instance of  
#$Battery-MilitaryUnit is a company unit that specializes in field artillery or operating stationary artillery emplacements.")
(comment Fleet-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a specialization of #$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable.  #$Fleet-MilitaryEchelon is an instance of #$USNavyEchelon.  As a rule, fleets are composed of several large navy units equipped with sizeable #$ModernNavalShips.  In most country's orders-of-battle, fleets are commanded by admirals (see #$Admiral-Rank).")
(comment FontStyleMarkupTag "The collection of instances of #$MarkupLanguageTag that serve to assign a particular font style to text characters.")
(comment UnderlinedCharacter "An instance of #$FontTypeByStyle.  Instances are individual instances of #$Character-Abstract that appear as underlined text.")
(comment StrikedOutCharacter "An instance of #$FontTypeByStyle.  Instances are individual instances of #$Character-Abstract that appear as striked-out.")
(comment TeletypeCharacter "An instance of #$FontTypeByStyle.  Instances are individual instances of #$Character-Abstract that appear in a teletype, or monospace font.")
(comment TeletypeFormat "An instance of #$GraphicalStructureByFormat.  Instances are graphical structures formatted to appear in a teletype style.")
(comment fontStyleFormat "<code>(#$fontStyleFormat FONT FORMAT)</code> means that the instance <code>FONT</code> of #$FontTypeByStyle is the collection of characters (see #$Character-Abstract), strings formed from which are all formatted in the instance <code>FORMAT</code> of #$GraphicalStructureByFormat. <p>For example, <code>(#$fontStyleFormat #$BoldedCharacter #$BoldfacedFormat)</code> means that all bolded character strings are structures with a boldfaced format.")
(comment markupTagIndicatesFontStyle "<code>(#$markupTagIndicatesFontStyle TAG FONT)</code> means that the markup language tag <code>TAG</code> is used to indicate that the characters that comprise the tagged string are to be rendered as  <code>FONT</code> characters.<p>For example,
<code>(#$markupTagIndicatesFontStyle #$I-HTMLTag #$ItalicizedCharacter)</code> means that the occurrence of \"i\" HTML tags around a string indicate that the characters that comprise that string to be rendered in italics.")
(comment PhraseStyleMarkupTag "The collection of all so-called \"phrase style,\" or \"logical style\" markup tags.  Phrase style tags provide information about how an end-user should be presented with the \"execution\" of the tagged string, without assuming a particular mode of \"execution\" (e.g., visually, through a browser).  For example, the <strong>...</strong> tag might tell a browser to display a string as bold, but might also tell an audio program to read the text with a certain kind of emphasis.")
(comment MarkupTagTypeByPurpose "An instance of #$FacetingCollectionType.  #$MarkupTagTypeByPurpose is the collection of all specializations of #$MarkupLanguageTag that share a common function in a given markup language.  Notable examples include #$FontStyleMarkupTag and #$PhraseStyleMarkupTag.")
(comment virtualPhysicalFields "A spec pred of #$physicalFields, (#$virtualPhysicalFields SCHEMA
VIRTUAL-FIELD) means that VIRTUAL-FIELD is a virtual physical field of
SCHEMA. A <i>virtual</i> physical field of a schema is one that is not
explicitly present in the schema defintion found on the native
knowledge source, but is introduced in the mapping process, typically
in order to facilitate translating more complex functional expressions
which are used to construct more expressive meaning sentences.")
(comment not "An instance of both #$LogicalConnective and #$UnaryRelation.  which takes an instance of #CycLSentence-Assertible as its argument. (#$not SENT) is true if and only if SENT is false (and false if and only if SENT is true).")
(comment Troop-MilitaryUnit "A specialization of both #$Company-MilitaryEchelon and #$CavalryUnit-MilitarySpecialty.  In the US Army, a #$Battery-MilitaryUnit is a company that is also an armored or air cavalry unit.")
(comment Squadron-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Squadron-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USAirForceEchelon.  The squadron is the basic fighting unit of an air force.
<p>
In the #$UnitedStatesAirForce, a squadron typically consists of two or more flights, and some ground personnel. (A <i>flight</i>, in this sense, is a subordinate echelon consisting of two or more airplanes.)")
(comment Army-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable.  #$Army-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USArmyEchelon.  
<p>
In the US Army, an army consists of 50,000 or more soldiers, is made up of two or more corps (see #$Corps-MilitaryEchelon), and is commanded by a lieutenant general or higher.
<p>
Note that army units have not been deployed by the #$UnitedStatesArmy since World War II.  Also cf. #$Army-BranchOfService.")
(comment MilitaryOrganizationConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$MilitaryOrganizationConcept are collections and relations having to do with military organizations.  Specializations of #$MilitaryOrganization, and relations used for describing the structure, size, capabilites, equipment, commanders, etc., of various types of military units are instances of this collection.")
(comment echelonOfUnit "A binary predicate that is a specialization of #$isa that relates a given #$ModernMilitaryOrganization to the (unique) #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon (q.v.) of which it is an instance.  <code>(#$echelonOfUnit UNIT ECHELON)</code> means that UNIT has the official unit ranking ECHELON in the armed service branch of which it is a part.   
<p>
Note that the echelon of a unit generally provides an indication of its size; the higher the unit's echelon in an echelon hierarchy, the larger it is.  See also #$subEchelonsInOrg and #$militaryUnitSize.")
(comment personalStaff "A binary predicate that relates a given military unit commander (see #$unitCommander) to his or her #$PersonalStaff-Military (q.v.)  <code>(#$personalStaff COMMANDER STAFF)</code> means that <code>STAFF</code> serves as the personal staff of <code>COMMANDER</code>, and as such is collectively responsible for attending to some of <code>COMMANDER</code>'s personal needs and responsibilities.
<p>
See also #$personalStaffOfficer.")
(comment Reservist "A specialization of #$MilitaryPerson.  Each instance of #$Reservist is a member of some #$Reserves-ArmedForces organization.")
(comment expectedTenureInUnitPosition "(#$expectedTenureInUnitPosition COUNTRY BOS ECHELON POSITION DURATION) means that an agent who holds POSITION in a military unit of the branch-of-service BOS with #$sovereignAllegianceOfOrg COUNTRY and #$echelonOfUnit ECHELON is expected to hold that position for DURATION.")
(comment IrregularForcesFn "#$IrregularForcesFn is a unary collection-denoting function that maps a    given collection of the appropriate type to a certain specialization of that   collection.  More precisely: for any specialization BRANCH of #$MilitaryOrganization,   (#$IrregularForcesFn BRANCH) is the specialization IRREG of BRANCH such that   IRREG is the collection of all instances of #$IrregularMilitaryForce that are of   the type BRANCH.")
(comment dateOfDeath "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate and a specialization of #$objectEndingDate (q.v.) restricted in its first argument to #$BiologicalLivingObjects.  <code>(#$dateOfDeath BLO ENDDATE)</code> means that <code>BLO</code> died during <code>ENDDATE</code>.  See also #$birthDate.")
(comment hyperlinkTo "<code>(#$hyperlinkTo LINK AIS)</code> means that the instance <code>LINK</code> of #$Hyperlink is a link to the instance <code>AIS</code> of #$AbstractInformationStructure.  Note that though it is an instance of #$FunctionalSlot, #$hyperlinkTo is not <i>strictly</i> functional (c.f., #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot); two distinct arg2 values <code>AIS-1</code> and <code>AIS-2</code> can hold for a given arg1 value just in case one of those values is a sub-structure of the other: <code>(#$or (#$subInfoStructures AIS-1 AIS-2) (#$subInfoStructures AIS-2 AIS-1))</code>.")
(comment Hyperlink "The collection of links in hypertext documents that, when the document is rendered by an appropriate program, such as a web browser, allow an end-user to open new files or view other parts of the original document.")
(comment PublicationDataMt "An instance of #$DataMicrotheory that contains publication information about conceptual works.")
(comment hyperlinkHrefValue "<code>(#$hyperlinkHrefValue LINK URI)</code> means that the instance <code>LINK</code> of #$Hyperlink is a link to the document identified by the instance <code>URI</code> of #$UniformResourceIdentifier.")
(comment webPageBaseURI "<code>(#$webPageBaseURI WP URI)</code> means that URI is the #$UniformResourceIdentifier from which relative links in the instance WP of #$WorldWideWebPage-AIS are computed. This information is specified via #$Base-HTMLTag.")
(comment ContextualURLFn "<code>(#$ContextualURLFn LOCAL-URL BASE-URI)</code> denotes the absolute #$UniformResourceLocator constructed from LOCAL-URL with respect to BASE-URI.")
(comment NonAbducibleRelation "The collection #$NonAbducibleRelation is a sub-collection of #$Relation.  Each instance of #$NonAbducibleRelation is such that if it occurs as the arg0 in a literal that Cyc is trying to abduce to be true, Cyc will fail to abduce that sentence.")
(comment parliamentOf "The predicate corresponding to #$ParliamentFn (q.v.).")
(comment AppointingSomeoneToAPosition "The collection of instances of #$PositionSelection in which the position is selected through an act of appointment (versus, e.g., election).")
(disjointWith AppointingSomeoneToAPosition Election)
(comment appointee "<code>(#$appointee APPOINTMENT AGENT)</code> means that the instance <code>AGENT</code> of #$IntelligentAgent is the agent selected to the relevant position in <code>APPOINTMENT</code>, an instance of #$AppointingSomeoneToAPosition.")
(comment AbstractIndividual "A specialization of #$AbstractThing and #$IntangibleIndividual (q.v.).  This is the collection of all abstract (i.e. aspatial, atemporal) individuals.  Specializations of #$AbstractIndividual include #$Relation, #$Tuple, and #$AbstractStructure.")
(comment IntangibleObjectPredicate "A specialization of #$ObjectPredicate.  This is the collection of predicates used to ascribe characteristics or properties (taken in a very broad sense) to intangible objects.  As such, each instance of  #$IntangibleObjectPredicate has at least one argument place (quite often including the first) constrained to accept only instances of #$IntangibleIndividual or some subcollection thereof.  Examples include #$arg3Isa and #$religionOfRule.")
(comment TangibleObjectPredicate "A specialization of #$ObjectPredicate.  This is the collection of all predicates that are used to ascribe characteristics or properties (taken in a very broad sense) to tangible objects.  As such, each instance of #$TangibleObjectPredicate has at least one argument place (often including the first) constrained (see #$argIsa) to instances of #$PartiallyTangible or some subcollection thereof.  Examples include #$viscosityOfSubstance, #$objectHasColor, #$porosityOfObject, and #$physicalBuild.")
(comment birthDate "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate and a specialization of #$objectStartingDate (q.v.) restricted in its first argument to #$Animals.  <code>(#$birthDate ANIMAL BIRTHDATE)</code> means <code>BIRTHDATE</code> is the date at which <code>ANIMAL</code> was born (hence the name of this predicate).  See also #$dateOfDeath.")
(comment Face-SocialStanding "A specialization of
#$InterAgentQuantity. #$Face-SocialStanding is the collection of
amounts of 'face' that agents possess in various social
contexts. AGENT's 'face' is, roughly, a kind of esteem accorded to him
by others, especially as regards his suitability to perform his
assigned social roles. In many collectivist cultures, ensuring that
persons maintain 'face' in social interactions is a central value, one
that underlies many behavioral norms. The concept is most often
invoked in US culture in references to 'saving' or 'losing' face.")
(comment syntacticNodeStartCharacterOffset "<code>(#$syntacticNodeStartCharacterOffset NODE N)</code> means that the string of <code>NODE</code> starts <code>N</code> characters into its tokenization's input string. If a node starts with the first character of its tokenization's input string, its offset is 0.")
(comment Checkpoint "An official place to register, declare or check goods and people.")
(comment MobileHomePark "A site for the permanent parking of trailer(s) used as
dwellings and designed without a permanent foundation.")
(comment PicnicSite "A parcel of land that has picnic tables for public use.")
(comment Fairgrounds "An area where permanent facilities exist to hold outdoor fairs, circuses or exhibitions.")
(comment AnimalSanctuary "A natural area set aside and for the preservation and protection of wildlife.")
(comment BuiltUpArea "An area containing a concentration of buildings and other structures.")
(comment HistoricalBuiltUpArea "An area that constitutes a concentration of historical
buildings or other historical structures within a built up area.")
(comment CliffDwelling "A dwelling built in the recesses of cliffs.")
(comment decreasesStatus-RoleInSitTypeWithRelationToSitType "(#$decreasesStatus-RoleInSitTypeWithRelationToSitType relates
certain types of event sequences to certain types of social
consequences.  (#$decreasesStatus-RoleInSitTypeWithRelationToSitType ROLE
SIT-TYPE SIT-RELATION RELATED-TYPE QUANTITY) means that, if an
instance of SIT-TYPE (INS-SIT-TYPE) and an instance of RELATED-TYPE
(INS-RELATED-TYPE) are related by SIT-RELATION -- i.e., if
(SIT-RELATION INS-SIT-TYPE INS-RELATED-TYPE) holds -- then the
agent playing ROLE in INS-SIT-TYPE will suffer a decrease in the
#$SocialQuantity QUANTITY.")
(comment TotallyOrderedCollection "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder (q.v.).  Each instance of #$TotallyOrderedCollection is a collection whose instances are conventionally regarded as being ordered by some relation <code>RELN</code>, where <code>RELN</code> totally orders <code>COL</code>.  A relation <code>RELN</code> <i><b>totally orders</b></i> a collection <code>COL</code> just in case <code>RELN</code> is <i>reflexive</i>, <i>antisymmetric</i>, and <i>transitive</i> on <code>COL</code>, and <code>COL</code> is <i>connected</i> with respect to <code>RELN</code> (i.e. for any instances <code>INST1</code> and <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL</code>, either <code>(RELN INST1 INST2)</code> or <code>(RELN INST2 INST1)</code> holds).
<p>
For example, the integers are conventionally regarded as being ordered by #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, which totally orders #$Integer. Thus #$Integer is an instance of #$TotallyOrderedCollection, and (since all integers are scalars) of its specialization #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType (q.v.).")
(comment Side "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible.  Each instance of #$Side is a surface of some partially tangible object, and serves as a boundary between the inside and outside of that object.")
(disjointWith ProlateSpheroid OblateSpheroid)
(comment straightLineOnPoints "An #$IndividualDenotingFunction for denoting a line which intersects ARG1 and ARG2.")
(comment objectRightSide "(#$objectRightSide OBJECT RIGHT-SIDE) relates an instance of #$LeftAndRightSidedObject OBJECT to the entire surface part that is
considered the right side, RIGHT-SIDE.  See also #$LeftAndRightSidedObject.")
(comment objectLeftSide "(#$objectLeftSide OBJECT LEFT-SIDE) relates an instance of #$LeftAndRightSidedObject OBJECT to the entire surfact part that is
considerd the left side LEFT-SIDE.  See also #$LeftAndRightSidedObject.")
(comment centroidOfObject "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$centroidOfObject OBJ CENTROID)</code> means that <code>CENTROID</code> is the centroid of <code>OBJ</code>.  The <i>centroid</i> of an object is its unweighted center of mass.  The centroid of an object is not guaranteed to be part of, or on, the object.  For instance, the centroid of a perfect sphere is the center of the sphere, which is not on the sphere itself.")
(comment AxisymmetricObject "A specialization of #$SpatialThing.  Each instance of #$AxisymmetricObject is a (tangible or intangible) spatial thing that is symmetric with respect to an axis.  Such an axis, which is a #$Line-Straight, is an <i>axis of symmetry</i> (see #$symmetricAxes) of the spatial thing.  An axially symmetric object might be a #$BilaterallySymmetricObject or a #$RadiallySymmetricObject (qq.v.).")
(comment bilateralAxes "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a tangible object with at least one well-defined pair of opposing sides (see #$BilateralObject) to an axis it has with respect to some such pair of sides.  <code>(#$bilateralAxes OBJECT AXIS)</code> means that <code>AXIS</code> is a #$Line-Straight that passes through the respective centers (see #$centroidOfObject) of each of two opposing sides (i.e. the front-back, left-right, or top-bottom sides) of <code>OBJECT</code>.
<p>
Cf. #$symmetricAxes.  Note that a bilateral axis of an object might or might not also be a symmetric axis of it.  For example, the left-right axis of a bicycle wheel is also an axis of symmetry of the wheel, but the top-bottom axis of a frying pan is not an axis of symmetry.
<p>
See also #$objectSides and #$BilateralAxisFn.")
(comment objectAxes "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a (tangible or intangible) spatial thing to an intrinsic axis (if it has any).  By an \"intrinsic\" here we mean an axis an object has either purely in virtue of its geometric shape or in virtue of some natural or conventional feature it has regarding its directional orientation.  More precisely, <code>(#$objectAxes OBJECT AXIS)</code> means that either (i) <code>AXIS</code> is an <i>axis of symmetry</i> of <code>OBJECT</code> (see the specialization #$symmetricAxes) or (ii) <code>OBJECT</code> is an #$BilateralObject (q.v.) and <code>AXIS</code> passes through the centers (see #$centroidOfObject) of two if its opposing sides (see the specialization #$bilateralAxes).")
(comment objectTopSide "(#$objectTopSide OBJECT TOPSIDE) means that TOPSIDE is the entire top surface part of OBJECT. See also #$TopAndBottomSidedObject and #$TopSide.")
(comment BilateralAxisFn "A ternary function that takes a #$BilateralObject and a pair of its opposing sides to the straight line that passes through the centers (see #$centroidOfObject) of those two sides.  See #$bilateralAxes.
<p>
For example, (#$BilateralAxisFn MyCar (#$BackFn MyCar) (#$FrontFn MyCar)) is the back-front axis of my car.")
(comment OwlExportableRelation "This is the collection of relations that we currently are able to define translations for into OWL Full.  Usually these are higher arity \"macro\" predicates or functions that require a specific mapping from our ontology into OWL Full.  For instance, *#$affiliatedWith* is not an instance of this collection because that is a binary predicate that can be defined in the normal way in OWL.  On the other hand, *#$relationAllExists* is an instance because assertions made with that predicate require a special translation to be expressible in OWL.")
(comment near "This intentionally-vague predicate relates spatial things that are relatively near each other.  <code>(#$near THIS THAT)</code> means that the distance between <code>THIS</code> and <code>THAT</code> is such that -- given the situation at hand and the sorts of things that <code>THIS</code> and <code>THAT</code> are -- they would be considered near each other by most observers.  Note that an object is #$near itself, anything it touches, and any part of itself.  When appropriate, use a more precisely-defined predicate such as #$nearToDegree, #$nearRelativeTo, #$nearForRolesInEventType, #$nearnessForRolesInEventType, #$touches-Directly, or #$physicalParts.")
(comment equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid "A specialization of #$sizeParameterOfObject.  <code>(#$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half the length of <code>SPHEROID</code>'s equatorial axes.  
<p>
Whenever two #$symmetricAxes (q.v.) of a #$Spheroid that is <i>not</i> a perfect sphere are of equal length, they are both <i>equatorial axes</i> of the spheroid.  All of the equatorial axes of such a spheroid lie in a single plane; the (unique) symmetric-axis of the spheroid that is perpendicular with its equatorial axes is known as its <i>polar axis</i> (cf. #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).
<p>
If a spheroid's equatorial radius is greater than its polar radius, it is an #$OblateSpheroid; if its polar radius is greater than its equatorial radius, it is a #$ProlateSpheroid.  The greater radius (whichever it might be) measures the spheroid's <i>semi-major axis</i> (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength).  The equatorial and polar radii of a #$Sphere are both equal to it ordinary #$radius.
<p>
Note that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>DISTANCE</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has length <code>DISTANCE</code>.")
(comment Spheroid "A #$TwoDimensionalShapeType and a specialization of #$Ellipsoid (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Spheroid is an ellipsoid that has two axes (see #$symmetricAxes) of equal length (if <i>all</i> of an ellipsoid's axes are of equal length, it is a #$Sphere).  With respect to a non-spherical spheroid, the axes that are of equal length are <i><b>equatorial axes</b></i>, and an axis that is perpendicular to an equatorial axis is a <i><b>polar axis</b></i>.  (Since a non-spherical spheroid's equatorial axes are coplanar, it therefore has a unique polar axis.) 
<p>
A spheroid whose equatorial axes are longer than its polar axis is an #$OblateSpheroid, and one whose polar axis is longer than its equatorial axes is a #$ProlateSpheroid.  See also #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid and #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid. 
<p>
Note that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>LENGTH</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has <code>LENGTH</code>.")
(comment ProlateSpheroid "A specialization of #$Spheroid (q.v.). Each instance of #$ProlateSpheroid is a \"pointy\" spheroid: one whose polar radius is greater than its equatorial radius (see #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid and #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).
<p>
Cf. #$OblateSpheroid.")
(comment OblateSpheroid "A specialization of #$Spheroid (q.v.). Each instance of #$OblateSpheroid is a \"squashed\" spheroid: one whose polar radius is less than its equatorial radius (see #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid and #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).  
<p>
Cf. #$ProlateSpheroid.")
(comment WellKnownBinaryString "A #$WellKnownBinaryString is an #$IDString which represents
an instance of #$OpenGISGeometry in binary form, encoded in
hexadecimal.")
(comment CSQLAsTextFn "This #$CSQLFunction is translated into SQL as the function
\"AsText\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLSpatialThingSumFn "This #$CSQLFunction is translated into SQL as the
function \"union\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLSpatialThingProductFn "This #$CSQLFunction is translated into SQL as the
function \"intersection\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLSpatialThingDifferenceFn "This #$CSQLFunction is translated into SQL as the
function \"difference\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLSpatialThingSymmetricDifferenceFn "This #$CSQLFunction is translated into SQL as the
function \"symdifference\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLSpatiallySubsumes "This #$CSQLComparisonPredicate is translated into SQL as the
predicate \"contains\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLTouches-Externally "This #$CSQLComparisonPredicate is translated into SQL as the
predicate \"touches\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLSpatiallyIntersects "This #$CSQLComparisonPredicate is translated into SQL as the
predicate \"intersects\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLSpatiallySeparated "This #$CSQLComparisonPredicate is translated into SQL as the
predicate \"disjoint\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLPartiallySpatiallyIntersects "This #$CSQLComparisonPredicate is translated into SQL as the
predicate \"overlaps\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment CSQLCospatial "This #$CSQLComparisonPredicate is translated into SQL as the
predicate \"equals\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment spheroidSemiminorAxisLength "A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot (q.v.) that is used to specify (partially) the size a #$Spheroid. <code>(#$spheroidSemiminorAxisLength SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half of the length of <code>SPHERIOID</code>'s minor axis.  The <i>minor axis</i> of a spheroid is by definition the shortest of its three principle axes.  For an #$OblateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-minor axis length is equal to its polar radius (see #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).  For a #$ProlateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-minor axis length is equal to its equatorial radius (see #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).
<p>
Together, the semi-minor axis length and the semi-<i>major</i> axis length (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength) of a spheroid determine its <i>flattening</i> and  
<i>inverse flattening</i> (see #$spheroidInverseFlattening). For example, if the  
semi-major and semi-minor axis lengths are <code>MAJ</code> and <code>MIN</code>, respectively, then the inverse flattening is <code>INVFLAT = MAJ/(MAJ-MIN)</code>.
<p>
Note that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>LENGTH</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has <code>LENGTH</code>.")
(comment disjointWith "A #$TaxonomicSlot predicate that relates #$Collections  (q.v.) that have no instances in common. <code>(#$disjointWith COL1 COL2)</code> means that <code>COL1</code> is disjoint with <code>COL2</code>: nothing is an instance of both. That is, there is no <code>THING</code> such that both <code>(#$isa THING COL1)</code> and <code>(#$isa THING COL2)</code> hold. For example, <code>(#$disjointWith #$Herbivore #$Carnivore)</code> holds because no animal is both a herbivore and a carnivore. Note that #$disjointWith is <b>not</b> irreflexive (see #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate): it is possible for a collection to be disjoint with itself, though only if it is empty (i.e. has no instances). Thus, for example, <code>(#$disjointWith #$Nothing #$Nothing)</code> holds.  Cf. #$intersectsWith.
<p>
Also note that it is quite possible for two second (or higher) order collections to be disjoint even if an instance of one collection and an instance of the other collection themselves share instances.  Consider, for example, the disjoint collections #$SpatialThingTypeByShape and #$SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality.  #$RoundObject is an instance of the first and #$ThreeDimensionalThing is an instance of the second; and any solid spherical object is an instance of both of these latter collections.")
(comment spheroidInverseFlattening "A #$ScalarIntervalSlot (q.v.) that is used to specify the shape of a #$Spheroid in terms of a real number (or numeric interval).  <code>(#$spheroidInverseFlattening SPHEROID INVFLAT)</code> means that <code>SPHEROID</code> has the inverse flattening <code>INVFLAT</code>.  
Where <code>SPHEROID</code>'s semi-major axis length (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength) is <code>MAJ</code> and its semi-minor axis length (see #$spheroidSemiminorAxisLength),
<pre>
  INVFLAT = MAJ/(MAJ-MIN) .
</pre>
An important use of the predicate #$spheroidInverseFlattening is in representing certain geospatial reference systems.  If it is known whether a given spheroid is an #$OblateSpheroid or a #$ProlateSpheroid (qq.v.), its  inverse flattening and semi-major axis length determine its precise shape and size.")
(comment spheroidSemimajorAxisLength "A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot (q.v.) that is used to specify (partially) the size a #$Spheroid. <code>(#$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half of the length of <code>SPHERIOID</code>'s major axis.  The <i>major axis</i> of a spheroid is by definition the longest of its three principle axes (see #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid and #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).  For an #$OblateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-major axis length is equal to its equatorial radius (see #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).  For a #$ProlateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-major axis length is equal to its polar radius (see #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).
<p>
An important use of the predicate #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength is in representing certain geospatial reference systems.  See also #$spheroidInverseFlattening.  If it is known whether a given spheroid is oblate or prolate, then its semi-major axis length and inverse flattening determine its precise shape and size.
<p>
See also #$spheroidSemiminorAxisLength.
<p>
Note that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>LENGTH</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has <code>LENGTH</code>.")
(comment relationExistsInstance "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate holds between some instance of a given collection and a given thing.  <code>(#$relationExistsInstance BINPRED COL THING)</code> means that there exists an instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST THING)</code> holds.  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
(#$thereExists ?INST
  (#$and
     (#$isa ?INST COL)
     (PRED ?INST THING))) .
</pre>
<p>
For example, (#$relationExistsInstance #$eventOccursAt #$IllegalDrugActivity #$Sweden) means that there exists illegal drug activity in Sweden.  See also the shared  #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment approximatelyLocatedAt-Spatial "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$genericallyLocatedAt-Spatial (q.v.) that relates a spatial thing to a space region such that the former is (at least) approximately or roughly located at the latter.  <code>(#$approximatelyLocatedAt-Spatial OBJ LOC)</code> means that <code>LOC</code> is at least a close approximation to the exact location (see the specialization #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial) of <code>OBJ</code>.  This entails that (i) <code>OBJ</code> and <code>LOC</code> spatially intersect (often, but not necessarily, to a significant degree), (ii) the lengths/areas/volumes (as the case may be, dimensionality-wise) of <code>OBJ</code> and <code>LOC</code> are approximately the same, and (iii) each  part of <code>OBJ</code> is #$near <code>LOC</code>, and vice versa.
<p>
The built-in fuzziness of #$approximatelyLocatedAt-Spatial makes it useful for representing situations in which a physical object is modelled by a geometric shape.  For example, spatial reference systems typically model the Earth's surface as a #$Spheroid, and different reference systems use spheroids of slightly different dimensions (see #$spheroidInverseFlattening, #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength, and #$CartographicSpheroidType).  Although the Earth's surface is not perfectly spheroidal, it can be said to be <i>approximately</i> located at a spheroid of any of these types.")
(comment basicPrice-Event "#$basicPrice-Event is a binary predicate that relates a #$Buying to a #$MonetaryValue (qq.v.). <code>(#$basicPrice-Event BUYING MONEY)</code> means that the sum of the basic price of all the merchandise and services bought in the #$Buying event <code>BUYING</code> was the #$MonetaryValue <code>MONEY</code>.  <code>MONEY</code> does not include tax or service charges -- for that, see #$totalCharge-Event. This predicate is not to be confused with #$basicPrice (q.v.), which relates <i>bought things</i> (i.e. purchased instances of #$TemporalThing) to their basic prices.")
