(comment NationalAtlasContentTest "#$NationalAtlasContentTest is a collection of #$SKSIContentTests which test the content of the #$NationalAtlas-KS database.")
(comment permitted-SitRelationToSitType "(#$permitted-SitRelationToSitType SIT PRED PERMITTED-TYPE)
means that, in some specified #$CodeOfConduct, it is
permissible that there be an instance of PERMITTED-TYPE such that
(PRED SIT PERMITTED-INS).  The #$CodeOfConduct which grants this
permission should be specified by assertion or rule microtheory. Note
that 'permitted' here is meant only in the weak sense that the relationship is not prohibited.  In particular, 
(#$permitted-SitRelationToSitType SIT PRED TYPE) is consistent with such a relationship being required, cf., #$oughtToBe-SitRelationToSitType.")
(comment AllegedTypeFn "An instance of #$UnaryFunction. This function takes for its value a specialization of #$TemporallyExistingThing, <b>TYPE</b>, and returns the collection of alleged instances of <b>TYPE</b>. Thus, for any instance <b>INDIVIDUAL</b> of the collection (#$AllegedTypeFn <b>TYPE</b>), an agent has asserted <i>without proof</i> that <b>INDIVIDUAL</b> is an instance of <b>TYPE</b>. The system should not consider (#$isa <b>INDIVIDUAL</b> <b>TYPE</b>) to be true, but rather only (#$thereExists <b>?AGENT</b> (#$alleged <b>?AGENT</b> (#$isa <b>INDIVIDUAL</b> <b>TYPE</b>))) is true.")
(comment InstructionsForMakingFn "This function maps a given type of object to the collection of all instructions for making things of that type. (#$InstructionsForMakingFn OBJTYPE) denotes the specialization of #$Instructions for making instances of OBJTYPE.  For example, (#$InstructionsForMakingFn #$Beer) returns the collection of all (complete sets of) instructions for making beer.")
(comment SubcollectionOfDuringFn "(#$SubcollectionOfDuringFn COLLECTION TEMPORALTHING) specifies the collection of all instances of COLLECTION which are in existence during TEMPORALTHING.  Thus (#$SubcollectionOfDuringFn #$Author #$TheTwentiethCenturyCE) would be the collection of all #$Authors who were alive during the Twentieth Century.")
(comment ScheduleFn-Type "#$ScheduleFn-Type takes as argument any spec of #$TemporallyExistingThing and returns the collection of all instances of #$Schedule that include schedule information for things of that type.  For example, (#$ScheduleFn-Type #$FootballTeam) returns the collection of all Schedules for any football team, and (#$ScheduleFn-Type #$OlympicGames) returns the collection of all schedules for the Olympics.")
(comment supplyThroughAmountDuring "(#$supplyThroughAmountDuring PT1 COL PT2 THING AMOUNT) means that the #$ScalarInterval AMOUNT is a measure of the amount of instances of the #$Collection COL with which the instance of #$PartiallyTangible PT1 is supplied through the instance of #$PartiallyTangible PT2 (on their way from their origin) during the temporal extent of the #$TemporalThing THING.  Like the predicate #$suppliedWith (q.v.), #$supplyThroughAmountDuring is very general.  In various assertions PT1 might, for instance, be a #$Cell, a #$Person, a #$Business, or a #$GeographicalRegion; and PT2 might, for example, be a #$Hose, a #$Railway, a #$GeographicalRegion, or a #$BloodVessel.  The range of possible supplies is similarly broad.  THING will often be a calendar time period; but it may also be, for instance, a particular #$Event.  If AMOUNT is a #$Rate, AMOUNT indicates the average rate of supply of instances of PRODUCT-TYPE to PT1 through PT2 during the temporal extent of THING.")
(comment supplyFromAmount "(#$supplyFromAmount PT1 COL PT2 AMOUNT)
means that the ScalarInterval AMOUNT is a measure of the amount of
instances of the Collection COL with which the instance of
PartiallyTangible PT1 is supplied from the instance of
PartiallyTangible PT2.  Like the predicate #$suppliedWith (q.v.), #$supplyFromAmount is very general. In various assertions PT1 might, for instance, be a #$Cell, a #$Person, a #$Business, or a #$GeographicalRegion; and likewise for PT2. The range of possible supplies is similarly broad.")
(comment fieldInvolvesResearchOnType "A binary predicate that relates fields of study to the sorts of things researched by people active in those fields.  <code>(#$fieldInvolvesResearchOnType AREA TYPE)</code> means that research in the #$FieldOfStudy <code>AREA</code> involves research on instances of <code>TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$TemporallyExistingThing).")
(comment fromPossessor "This predicate identifies a #$SocialBeing who loses some right to use an object.  <code>(#$fromPossessor LOSS AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> enjoys being in some #$SituationTypeByUserRights over the #$objectOfPossessionTransfer at the start of the #$LosingUserRights event <code>LOSS</code>, but no longer is in that particular #$SituationTypeByUserRights after the <code>LOSS</code> ends.")
(disjointWith ShakingHands (CollectionDifferenceFn DeliberatelyTouchingSomething ShakingHands))
(comment objectBottomSide "(#$objectTopSide OBJECT BOTTOMSIDE) means that BOTTOMSIDE is the entire bottom surface part of OBJECT. See also #$TopAndBottomSidedObject and #$TopSide.")
(comment BilaterallySymmetricObject "A specialization of #$AxisymmetricObject (q.v.).  Each instance of #$BilaterallySymmetricObject is a (tangible or intangible) spatial object that is symmetric with respect to some bisecting straight line (if the object is planar) or plane (if it's non-planar).  In this context, <i>symmetric</i> means that the line or plane divides the object into two equal halves with the same shape (or mirror-image shapes).  For example, a #$Circle, an isosceles #$Triangle, a #$Cone, and a human body are all bilaterally symmetric objects.
<p>
If a #$BilaterallySymmetricObject <code>OBJ</code> is a one- or two-dimensional planar region, then any bisecting line with respect to which <code>OBJ</code> is symmetric is an <i>axis of symmetry</i> (see #$symmetricAxes) of <code>OBJ</code>.  If <code>OBJ</code> is a two- or three-dimensional non-planar object that is symmetric with respect to a bisecting plane <code>PLANE</code>, then any straight line with respect to which the space-region intersection of <code>OBJ</code> and <code>PLANE</code> is symmetric is itself an axis of symmetry of <code>OBJ</code>.  Thus, a circle has an infinite number of axes of symmetry (viz. any straight line that intersects the circle and passes through its center); an isosceles triangle has exactly one axis of symmetry; a cone has at least one axis of symmetry; and a human body has no axis of symmetry (though it does have three #$objectAxes (q.v.)).
<p>
See also #$RadiallySymmetricObject.")
(comment testAnswers-Cardinality-Exact "An expression of the form (#$testAnswers-Cardinality-Exact TEST N) states that when the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST is run against a #$CycKB there are exactly N distinct instances of #$CycInferenceAnswer that should bear the relation #$queryAnswers to the #$CycLQuerySpecification, let's call it SPEC, that is associated with TEST via #$testQuerySpecification.  By extension, since each instance of #$CycInferenceAnswer contains exactly one instance of #$ELInferenceBindingSet, N should be the cardinality of the #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets that bears the relation #$queryAnswersCycL-Exact to an instance of #$CycQueryRunning in which SPEC plays the role #$inferenceQuerySpecification (and which is run against the same #$CycKB).")
(comment testAnswers-Cardinality-Min "An expression of the form (#$testAnswers-Cardinality-Min TEST N) states that when the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST is run against a #$CycKB there should be at least N distinct instances of #$CycInferenceAnswer that should bear the relation #$queryAnswers to the #$CycLQuerySpecification that is associated with TEST via #$testQuerySpecification. By extension, since each instance of #$CycInferenceAnswer contains exactly one instance of #$ELInferenceBindingSet, the cardinality of the #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets that bears the relation #$queryAnswersCycL-Exact to any instance of #$CycQueryRunning in which SPEC plays the role #$inferenceQuerySpecification should be greater than or equal to N (and which is run against the same #$CycKB).")
(comment testAnswers-Cardinality-Max "An expression of the form (#$testAnswers-Cardinality-Max TEST N) states that when the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST is run against a #$CycKB there should be at most N distinct instances of #$CycInferenceAnswerthat should bear the relation #$queryAnswers to the #$CycLQuerySpecification that is associated with TEST via #$testQuerySpecification.  By extension, since each instance of #$CycInferenceAnswer contains exactly one instance of #$ELInferenceBindingSet, the cardinality of the #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets that bears the relation #$queryAnswersCycL-Exact to any instance of #$CycQueryRunning in which SPEC plays the role #$inferenceQuerySpecification should be less than or equal to N (and which is run against the same #$CycKB).")
(disjointWith LumberYard WoodedArea)
(comment LumberYard "An open area for the storage of wooden lumber and timbers.")
(comment MilitaryContractor "A specialization of #$GovernmentContractor.  Each instance of #$MilitaryContractor is an organization that contracts with a government to provide goods or services for that government's armed forces.")
(comment MilitaryContract "A specialization of #$SalesContract.  Each instance of #$MilitaryContract is a sales contract between a military contractor (see the collection #$MilitaryContractor) and a government in which the government buys some goods or services for its armed forces from the contractor.")
(comment RoyalFamily "A specialization of #$Family-Human.  Each instance of #$RoyalFamily consists of all the living relatives of some ruling monarch (see the constant #$Monarch-HeadOfState).  See also the related constant #$DynasticFamily.")
(comment StalkingSomething "A specialization of #$PursuingSomething.  Each instance of #$StalkingSomething is an event in which some agent pursues or follows someone or something in a stealthy or furtive manner.")
(disjointWith StalkingSomething ChasingAThrownBall)
(disjointWith StalkingSomething ChasingAHitBall)
(disjointWith StalkingSomething ChasingAPitchedBall)
(disjointWith MilitaryContract FranchiseContract)
(disjointWith MilitaryContract PersonalContractPurchase-UK)
(disjointWith MilitaryContract HirePurchaseAgreement-UK)
(comment numberCorrespondsToRelevance "(#$numberCorrespondsToRelevance NUM REL) menas that the #$NonNegativeNumber NUM (in the range from 0 to 1) corresponds to the #$Relevance REL.
<p>
Assertions made with the predicate #$numberCorrespondsToRelevance provide the basis for translating between #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree and #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric (qq.v).")
(comment MilitaryPersonnelBunker "A #$Bunker used by a military force, either for
location of command/control centers or for troop encampment.")
(comment Bunker "A #$Bunker is a fortified, underground military facility which is resistant to ordnance, where military personnel or equipment are protected. See also the specializations #$MilitaryPersonnelBunker and #$MilitaryStorageBunker.")
(comment MilitaryStorageBunker "A #$Bunker which is used to store #$MilitaryEquipment, not personnel.")
(comment MineralPile "A man-made #$Pile of mining or quarrying products, excluding waste materials.")
(comment RoadVehicleChase "The collection of all instances of #$PursuingSomething in which the #$pursuingAgent, plus a person or persons constituting the #$objectPursued, are transported in instances of #$RoadVehicle.  There may be more than one vehicle pursued, and more than one pursuing vehicle.  The chase may or may not take place at high speed.")
(comment VehicleBarrier "A permanent obstruction placed across a route to prevent vehicular traffic.")
(comment Culvert "A #$Culvert is a #$DrainageDitch which crosses under a #$Roadway, #$Railway, or #$Embankment.")
(comment FerryCrossing "A route in a body of water where a ferry crosses from one shoreline to another.")
(comment Stalking-CriminalAct "A specialization of #$CriminalAct. Each instance of #$Stalking-CriminalAct is a complex event consisting of repeated actions (such as repeatedly maintaining unwanted physical proximity to the victim, or repeatedly making verbal or written threats to the victim) by a stalker directed at a specific victim.   In order to constitute criminal stalking, the actions in question must induce in the victim fear of death or bodily injury to herself or to a member of her immediate family, this fear must be a reasonable response to those actions, and the stalker must (or should) know that his actions will produce that response in the victim.")
(disjointWith Arson Stalking-CriminalAct)
(disjointWith Stalking-CriminalAct Murder)
(comment FerryStation "A #$FerryStation is a #$TransportFacility located near some
#$BodyOfWater whose primary purpose is to be a place where #$Ferrys
discharge and receive passengers.")
(comment Terrorism-Topic "An instance of #$Topic.  Each instance of #$Terrorism-Topic is a #$Thing which (in some significant sense) has to do with the topic of terrorism.")
(comment numberOfHostagesTaken "(#$numberOfHostagesTaken CAPTURE N) means that the number of persons taken hostage in the instance of #$TakingHostage is N.")
(comment CycInferenceAnswerFn "(#$CycInferenceAnswerFn <b>BINDING-SET JUSTIFICATION-SET</b>) denotes
the instance of #$CycInferenceAnswer which is composed of (i) an encoding of <b>BINDING-SET</b>, an
instance of #$ELInferenceBindingSet, and (ii) <b>JUSTIFICATION-SET</b>, a set of instances of #$CycInferenceAnswerJustification).")
(comment CycInferenceAnswer "A specialization of #$CycInferenceDataStructure.  Each
instance of #$CycInferenceAnswer is a complex data structure that is a
component of one or more #$CycInferences (specifically, of certain
inferences whose root problem is proven).  A #$CycInferenceAnswer has
two components: an assigment of bindings to query variables and sets of justifications for those bindings.  For information about the components, see the predicates #$inferenceAnswerBindings and #$inferenceAnswerJustification.")
(comment inferenceAnswerBindingsAndJustifications "The corresponding predicate for #$CycInferenceAnswerFn.
(#$inferenceAnswerBindingsAndJustifications ANSWER BINDING-SET
JUSTIFICATION-SET) means that the #$CycInferenceAnswer ANSWER is
composed of (i) an encoding of BINDING-SET, and (ii) the set of #$CycInferenceAnswerJustifications JUSTIFICATION-SET.")
(comment inferenceAnswerBindings "(#$inferenceAnswerBindings ANSWER BINDING-SET) means that one component of ANSWER is an encoding of BINDING-SET,
a set of variable bindings for which a proof has been found (or NIL, if the
triggering query is closed).  See also #$CycInferenceAnswer.")
(comment folderTypePertinentToTask "(#$folderTypePertinentToTask TASK FOLDER-TYPE) means that each instance of collection FOLDER-TYPE (a specialization of #$KEInteractionFolder) is a folder of queries that is pertinent to TASK.")
(comment topicPertinentToTask "(#$topicPertinentToTask TASK TOPIC) means that the #$Allotment TASK is about TOPIC.")
(comment ogcGeometriesIntersect "#$ogcGeometriesIntersect is a typed spec pred of #$spatiallyIntersects and was introduced exclusively to be supported by
SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly into SQL
queries using the predicate <code>intersects</code> defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment ogcGeometriesDisjoint "#$ogcGeometriesDisjoint is a typed spec pred of
#$spatiallySeparated and was introduced exclusively to be supported by
SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly into SQL
queries using the predicate <code>disjoint</code> defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment ogcGeometrySubsumes "#$ogcGeometrySubsumes is a typed spec pred of
#$spatiallySubsumes and was introduced exclusively to be supported by
SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly into SQL
queries using the predicate <code>contains</code> defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment ogcGeometriesCospatial "#$ogcGeometriesCospatial is a typed spec pred of
#$cospatial and was introduced exclusively to be supported by
SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly into SQL
queries using the predicate <code>equals</code> defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment ogcGeometriesTouch "#$ogcGeometriesTouch is a typed spec pred of
#$touches-Externally and was introduced exclusively to be supported by
SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly into SQL
queries using the predicate <code>touches</code> defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment ogcGeometriesPartiallyOverlap "#$ogcGeometriesPartiallyOverlap is a typed spec pred of
#$partiallySpatiallyIntersects and was introduced exclusively to be
supported by SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly
into SQL queries using the predicate <code>overlaps</code> defined in
the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment LightingACigarette "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$LightingUp-SettingSomethingOnFire. In each #$LightingACigarette, a cigarette is lit and begins to smoke and burn.")
(comment PeaceNegotiation "A specialization of #$Negotiating.  Each instance of #$PeaceNegotiation is a negotiation having as its end the peaceful resolution of some armed conflict.")
(comment CivilWar "A specialization of #$War.  Each instance of #$CivilWar is a war in which the opposing factions are both part of the same nation.")
(comment Narcissism "A specialization of #$PsychologicalAilment.  Each instance of #$Narcissism is a psychological disorder marked by a subject's infatuation or obsession with himself to the exclusion of others.  Persons with this disorder will, above all, seek to pursue their own gratification, dominance, and ambition.")
(comment Blogging "A specialization of #$EditingAnElectronicDocument.  Each instance of #$Blogging is an event in which a user makes or edits a #$Weblog post.")
(comment DiscriminatoryAction "A specialization of #$HostileSocialAction.  Each instance of #$DiscriminatoryAction is an event in which an agent (either a person or group) is unfairly treated by some other agent, due to the latter agent's unfair bias against the former agent.")
(comment grosslyIncompleteWithAllArgsNotFullyBound "A unary #$InferenceCompletenessPredicate that can apply to a
predicate. (#$grosslyIncompleteWithAllArgsNotFullyBound PRED) means
that the Cyc inference engine will treat as \"grossly incomplete\" any
removal (but not transformation) tactic for a problem with PRED in the
arg0 position and with all other argument positions not fully bound.
<p> A good example of a predicate that is
#$grosslyIncompleteWithAllArgsNotFullyBound is #$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType.
Generally, for no two shapes will it ever be asserted that one fits in
the other.  <p> Cf. the specialization of this predicate,
#$grosslyIncompleteWithAnyArgsNotFullyBound, as well as
#$grosslyIncompleteWithArgNotFullyBound.")
(comment grosslyIncompleteWithAnyArgsNotFullyBound "A unary #$InferenceCompletenessPredicate and a specialization of
#$grosslyIncompleteWithAllArgsNotFullyBound (q.v.) that can apply to a
predicate.  (#$grosslyIncompleteWithAnyArgsNotFullyBound PRED) means that the
Cyc inference engine will treat as \"grossly incomplete\" any removal tactic
for a problem with PRED in the arg0 position and with any other
argument positions open.
<p>
A good example of a predicate that is
#$grosslyIncompleteWithAnyArgsNotFullyBound is #$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType.  Generally,
for no two shapes will it ever be asserted that one fits in the other. 
<p>
Cf. #$grosslyIncompleteWithArgNotFullyBound.")
(comment allotmentApproximateDuration "An assertion of the form #$allotmentApproximateDuration  ALLOT QUANT) is used to represent an <b>approximation</b> of the duration of the interval for which the #$Allotment ALLOT is supposed to hold (as specified with an #$allotmentForInterval assertion).  For the sake of completing the calculations, it smooths over indeterminate points during intervals. For the sake of efficiency, it ignores some of the special cases involving the month #$February. This is intended to help inference in cases where allotments have complex time intervals.")
(comment OutdoorDiningArea "A specialization of  #$RestaurantSeatingArea and #$OutdoorLocation.  Instances of this collection are seating areas which are outdoors.")
(comment zAxisPredicateOfSystem "A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem that relates a #$MeasurableQuantitySlot predicate to a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem (qq.v.). <code>(#$zAxisPredicateOfSystem Z-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>Z-PRED</code> is the binary predicate used to state <b>z</b>-coordinate values in the 3-D Cartesian coordinate system <code>SYS</code>, i.e. #$Distances from the <b>xy</b>-plane.  
<p>
Thus, for example, <code>(Z-PRED POINT (#$Meter 5))</code> would mean that, in  <code>SYS</code>, the <b>z</b>-coordinate value of <code>POINT</code> is 5 meters.

<p>
See also #$xAxisPredicateOfSystem and #$yAxisPredicateOfSystem.")
(comment xAxisPredicateOfSystem "A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem that relates a #$MeasurableQuantitySlot predicate to a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem (qq.v.). <code>(#$xAxisPredicateOfSystem X-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>X-PRED</code> is the binary predicate used to state <b>x</b>-coordinate values in the Cartesian coordinate system <code>SYS</code>, i.e. #$Distances from the <b>y</b>-axis (if <code>SYS</code> is 2-D) or from the <b>yz</b>-plane (if <code>SYS</code> is 3-D).  
<p>
Thus, for example, <code>(X-PRED POINT (#$Meter 5))</code> would mean that, in  <code>SYS</code>, the <b>x</b>-coordinate value of <code>POINT</code> is 5 meters.

<p>
See also #$yAxisPredicateOfSystem and #$zAxisPredicateOfSystem.")
(comment yAxisPredicateOfSystem "A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem that relates a #$MeasurableQuantitySlot predicate to a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem (qq.v.). <code>(#$yAxisPredicateOfSystem Y-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>Y-PRED</code> is the binary predicate used to state <b>y</b>-coordinate values in the Cartesian coordinate system <code>SYS</code>, i.e. #$Distances from the <b>x</b>-axis (if <code>SYS</code> is 2-D) or from the <b>xz</b>-plane (if <code>SYS</code> is 3-D).  
<p>
Thus, for example, <code>(Y-PRED POINT (#$Meter 5))</code> would mean that, in  <code>SYS</code>, the <b>y</b>-coordinate value of <code>POINT</code> is 5 meters.

<p>
See also #$xAxisPredicateOfSystem and #$zAxisPredicateOfSystem.")
(comment radialDistancePredicate "A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem (q.v.).  <code>(#$radialDistancePredicate RAD-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>RAD-PRED</code> is the binary predicate used to express the radial distance coordinates of #$SpatialThings in the #$PolarCoordinateSystem (q.v.) <code>SYS</code>.  <code>RAD-PRED</code>'s two argument-places will be restricted (via #$argIsa) to instances of #$SpatialThing (or some specialization thereof) and #$Distance, respectively.
<p>
Thus, for example <code>(RAD-PRED POINT (#$Meter 5))</code> would mean that, in <code>SYS</code>, the distance from <code>POINT</code> to the pole is 5 meters.
<p>
See also #$azimuthAnglePredicate and #$altitudeAnglePredicate.")
(comment CartesianCoordinateSystem "A specialization of #$CoordinateSystem.  Each instance of #$CartesianCoordinateSystem is based on two or three perpendicular axes (see #$Line-Straight) that intersect at a point, called the <i>origin</i>.  Two axes are used for coordinating a two-dimensional space; three axes are used for coordinating a three-dimensional space.  Any point in the space  coordinated by the system can be specified by a pair (or triple) of #$Distances, representing distances from the origin along each of the two (or three) axes.
<p>
More precisely, the axes of a given Cartesian coordinate system are respectively designated its <i><b>x</b>-axis</i>, its <i><b>y</b>-axis</i>, and (if it has one) its <i><b>z</b>-axis</i>.  The <i><b>x</b>-coordinate</i> of a given #$Point measures its least distance from the <b>y</b>-axis (in a 2D system) or from the #$Plane defined by the <b>y</b> and <b>z</b> axes (in a 3D system).  And similarly for the <i><b>y</b>-coordinate</i> and (if it has one) the <i><b>z</b>-coordinate</i> of the point.  
<p>
Given a particular Cartesian coordinate system, binary predicates for giving a point's <b>x</b>-, <b>y</b>-, and <b>z</b>-coordinates can be defined using the meta-predicates #$xAxisPredicateOfSystem, #$yAxisPredicateOfSystem, and #$zAxisPredicateOfSystem, respectively.
<p>
Cf. #$PolarCoordinateSystem.")
(comment PolarCoordinateSystem "A specialization of #$CoordinateSystem (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PolarCoordinateSystem consists of a of a fixed #$Point called the <i>origin</i> or <i>pole</i>, and a fixed #$Ray-Geometrical that terminates at the pole, called the <i>polar axis</i>, which is usually taken to be the positive half of the <i><b>x</b>-axis</i>.  A fixed #$Line-Straight passing through the pole and perpendicular to the <b>x</b>-axis is taken to be the <i><b>y</b>-axis</i>.  For three-dimensional systems, the straight line passing through the pole and perpendicular to both the <b>x</b> and <b>y</b> axes is the <i><b>z</b>-axis</i>. 
<p>
In a two-dimensional polar coordinate system (also called a <i>circular coordinate system</i>), the location of a given point <b>P</b> in the plane is specified by giving (i) the #$Distance from the pole to <b>P</b> (called the <i>radius</i>) and (ii) the size of the angle (see #$AngularDistance) that the ray from the pole through <b>P</b> makes with the polar axis.
<p>
Two common types of three-dimensional polar coordinate systems are <i>cylindrical coordinate systems</i> and <i>spherical coordinate systems</i>.  In a cylindrical system, the location of a point <b>P</b> is specified by giving (i') the distance (or <i>radius</i>) between the <b>z</b>-axis and <b>P</b>, (ii') the angular distance (called the <i>azimuth</i> or <i>longitude</i>) between the polar axis and the line from the origin to the projection of <b>P</b> onto the <b>xy</b>-plane, and (iii') the signed distance (or <i>height</i>) from the <b>xy</b>-plane to <b>P</b>.  In a spherical system, the location of a point <b>P</b> is specified by giving (i'') the radius [defined as in (i) above], (ii'') the azimuth or longitude [defined as in (ii') above], and (iii'') the angular distance (called the <i>colatitude</i> or <i>polar angle</i>) between the <b>z</b>-axis and the line from the origin to <b>P</b>.  The #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem (q.v.) is an example of a spherical coordinate system.
<p>
Given a particular polar coordinate system, predicates for specifying the radius, the azimuth, and the colatitude of a point can be defined using the meta-predicates #$radialDistancePredicate, #$azimuthAnglePredicate, and #$altitudeAnglePredicate, respectively.
<p>
Cf. #$CartesianCoordinateSystem.")
(comment altitudeAnglePredicate "A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem (q.v.).  <code>(#$altitudeAnglePredicate ALT-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>ALT-PRED</code> is the binary predicate used to express colatitude or polar angle coordinates of #$SpatialThings in the #$PolarCoordinateSystem (q.v.) <code>SYS</code>.  <code>ALT-PRED</code>'s two argument-places will be restricted (via #$argIsa) to instances of #$SpatialThing (or some specialization thereof) and #$AngularDistance, respectively.
<p>
Thus (e.g.), <code>(ALT-PRED POINT (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 30))</code> would mean that, in <code>SYS</code>, <code>POINT</code> is on a ray that terminates at the pole and makes a 30-degree angle with the <b>z</b>-axis.
<p>
For example, #$latitude is the #$altitudeAnglePredicate for the  #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem.
<p>
See also #$radialDistancePredicate and #$azimuthAnglePredicate.")
(comment azimuthAnglePredicate "A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem (q.v.).  <code>(#$azimuthAnglePredicate AZI-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>AZIPRED</code> is the binary predicate used to express azimuth angle coordinates of #$SpatialThings in the #$PolarCoordinateSystem (q.v.) <code>SYS</code>.  <code>PRED</code>'s two argument-places will be restricted (via #$argIsa) to instances of #$SpatialThing (or some specialization thereof) and #$AngularDistance, respectively.
<p>
Thus (e.g.), <code>(AZI-PRED POINT (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 30))</code> would mean that, in <code>SYS</code>, <code>POINT</code> (or, in a 3-D system, <code>POINT</code>'s image on the <b>xy</b> plane) is on a ray that terminates at the pole and makes a 30-degree angle (counter-clockwise) with the polar axis.
<p>
For example, #$longitude is the #$azimuthAnglePredicate for the  #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem.
<p>
See also #$radialDistancePredicate and #$altitudeAnglePredicate.")
(comment CoordinateSystem "A specialization of #$FrameOfReference (q.v.).  This is the collection of systems with respect to which the locations of #$SpatialThings can be specified.  Each #$CoordinateSystem has an associated suite of coordinate value predicates (see #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem and its specializations), which assign coordinates to spatial things.  
<p>
The two most common types of coordinate systems are #$CartesianCoordinateSystems and #$PolarCoordinateSystems (qq.v.).  The #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem is an example of the latter; it has two coordinate value predicates, #$latitude and #$longitude, both of which assign #$AngularDistances to spatial things.")
(comment ProvidingSubsidy "A specialization of #$GrantingMoney. Each instance of #$ProvidingSubsidy is a monetary gift from an instance of #$RegionalGovernment to some other agent (usually, either a private company or individual, or some other instance of #$RegionalGovernment), with the understanding that the recipient will use the money in an enterprise that benefits the public.")
(comment approximateAllottedQuantityForMonth "An assertion of the form (#$approximateAllottedQuantityForMonth ALLOTMENT QUANTITY MONTH) represents, in some cases only approximately, the amount of work time that is allotted for a particular #$Assignment-Specification during a particular #$CalendarMonth.  In cases where the #$allotmentForInterval value of #$Allotment intersects several #$CalendarMonths, the calculation of QUANTITY assumes a uniform distribution of effort across that interval and multiplies the original quantity (as represented with the predicate #$allottedQuantityOfWorkTime) by the fraction of the duration of the original interval that is temporally assumed by the #$CalendarMonth MONTH.  The rules supporting this predicate are not senstive to such things as week-ends and holidays. So, those factors can contribute to errors in some of the error in the calculations.")
(comment TwoDimensionalCoordinateSystem "A specialization of #$CoordinateSystem (q.v.).  Each instance of #$TwoDimensionalCoordinateSystem is a frame of reference for specifying the locations of spatial things with respect to a #$Plane.  Typically, such a system will have just two coordinate-value predicates associated with it (see #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem).  A two-dimensional coordinate system might be a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem or a #$PolarCoordinateSystem (qq.v.).  
<p>
Cf. #$ThreeDimensionalCoordinateSystem.")
(comment ThreeDimensionalCoordinateSystem "A specialization of #$CoordinateSystem (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ThreeDimensionalCoordinateSystem is a frame of reference for specifying the locations of spatial things in a three-dimensional space.  Typically, such a system will have three coordinate-value predicates associated with it (see #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem).  A three-dimensional coordinate system might be a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem or a #$PolarCoordinateSystem (qq.v.).  
<p>
Cf. #$TwoDimensionalCoordinateSystem.")
(comment GreekOrthodoxPerson "The collection of people who
 consider themselves to be adherents of the #$GreekOrthodoxReligion,
 regardless of whether they really live by (or understand) 
 most Greek Orthodox principles.  As such, this collection
is designed to capture the 'common sense' notion of 'being
a Greek Orthodox,' and not the prescriptive sense that a devout
Greek Orthodox person or a Greek Orthodox church leader might attach to the phrase.")
(comment queryHasPertinenceForThing "(#$queryHasPertinenceForThing QUERY THING DEGREE)  means that QUERY (an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification) has the pertinence DEGREE whenever THING  has a non-zero pertinence.")
(comment queryHasVeryHighPertinenceForThing "(#$queryHasVeryHighPertinenceForThing QUERY THING) means that QUERY (an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification) has the pertinence (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$Relevance)  whenever THING has a non-zero pertinence.")
(comment cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric "<code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric Q-SPEC DEGREE)</code> means that <code>Q-SPEC</code> is an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification that is, to the degree of relevance <code>DEGREE</code>, pertinent to an instance of information gathering, due to the pertinence of the terms in <code>SET</code>).
<p>
This predicate is similar in meaning to #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree (q.v.).  Whereas, however, #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree takes an instance of #$Relevance as an argument in the second argument position, #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric takes a number in the range of 0 to 1. 
<p>
<code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric Q-SPEC 0)</code> means that <code>Q-SPEC</code> has no relevance (<i>i.e.</i> it is equivalent to <code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric Q-SPEC (#$NoAmountFn #$Relevance))</code>.
<p>
NOTE: This predicate should only be used in microtheories which specify the appropriate qualifications of assertions made with it: e.g., for whom the query is pertinent, on what task, at what time and place, <i>etc.</i>")
(comment TaskGroup-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a specialization of
#$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable. #$TaskGroup-MilitaryEchelon is an
instance of #$USNavyEchelon. A Task Group is typically composed of an
aircraft carrier and all of her escort ships, and in the
#$UnitedStatesNavy is typically commanded by a rear admiral.")
(comment TaskUnit-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a specialization of
#$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable. #$TaskUnit-MilitaryEchelon is an
instance of #$USNavyEchelon. A typical Task Unit is composed of a
squadron of destroyers assigned to protect the carrier, and is usually
commanded by a senior captain.")
(comment TaskElement-MilitaryEchelon "A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a specialization of
#$ModernMilitaryUnit-Deployable. #$TaskElement-MilitaryEchelon is an
instance of #$USNavyEchelon. A Task Element typically consists of
a single ship or squadron of aircraft. The commanding officer of a
ship is generally a Captain or Commander in rank.")
(disjointWith ProvidingSubsidy LoanPayment)
(disjointWith Narcissism Phobia)
(disjointWith PeaceNegotiation ContractNegotiation)
(disjointWith Blogging EditingACycKB)
(comment Weblog "A specialization of #$WebSite.  Each instance of #$Weblog is a website containing periodic, chronologically ordered posts.  These posts typically share a theme, and are typically made either by a single author, or a small group of authors. Weblog posts are often comments on material found elsewhere on the web, and usually contain hyperlinks to such material.  Weblogs often allow visitors to the sites to comment on the various posts.")
(comment pastOrPresentPresident "This predicate relates an organization to the historical collection of its present and past presidents.")
(comment UserKEInteractionFolderForTaskWRTConceptFn "(#$UserKEInteractionFolderForTaskFn AGENT ALLOTMENT THING) denotes the instance of #$KEInteractionFolder associated with a particular user of the query
library, for a particular task, ALLOTMENT, with focus on the concept THING that is relevant to ALLOTMENT.")
(comment kEInteractionFolderOfUserForTaskWRTConcept "(#$kEInteractionFolderOfUserForTaskWRTConcept AGENT ALLOTMENT THING FOLDER) means that FOLDER is the #$KEInteractionFolder associated with the
#$IntelligentAgent AGENT, for the task ALLOTMENT, with respect to the concept THING that is relevant to ALLOTMENT.")
(comment topicForInstancesOfTypeInSuperTopic "<code>(#$topicForInstancesOfTypeInSuperTopic SUB-TOPIC SUPER-TOPIC COLLECTION)</code> means that <code>SUB-TOPIC</code> is a topic that contains all and only the instances of the #$Collection <code>COLLECTON</code>, which is in turn contained in <code>SUPER-TOPIC</code> (a super topic of <code>SUB-TOPIC</code>).  For example:
<p>
<pre>
(#$topicForInstancesOfTypeInSuperTopic 
#$GeopoliticalEntities-Individuals-Geography-Topic
#$GeopoliticalEntities-Geography-Topic
#$GeopoliticalEntity)
</pre>
<p>
means that #$GeopoliticalEntities-Geography-Topic is a supertopic of #$GeopoliticalEntities-Individuals-Geography-Topic, and that #$GeopoliticalEntity is in the topic #$GeopoliticalEntities-Geography-Topic, and that  #$GeopoliticalEntities-Individuals-Geography-Topic contains all and only the instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity.")
(comment OutdoorDiningArea-Patio "A specialization of  #$OutdoorDiningAreaa.  Instances of this collection are seating areas which are on patios.")
(comment OutdoorDiningArea-Rooftop "A specialization of  #$OutdoorDiningArea.  Instances of this collection are seating areas which are on rooftops.")
(comment OutdoorDiningArea-Sidewalk "A specialization of  #$OutdoorDiningArea.  Instances of this collection are seating areas which are on sidewalks.")
(comment CeremonialMound "A specialization of #$MemorialMarker and #$Landmark. A #$CeremonialMound is usually a man-made mound of earth that serves as a memorial for either the death of an important person or of a significant battle for a particular people or tribe.  The ceremonies are religious.")
(comment barrierBlocksPath "(#$barrierBlocksPath BARRIER PATH) means that there exists
some BARRIERSITUATION (see #$BarrierSituation) such that BARRIER is
the #$barrier in BARRIERSITUATION and PATH is the #$blockedPath in BARRIERSITUATION.")
(comment TestOWLExportConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$TestOWLExportConcept are concepts each of which tests some aspect of the new export schema.")
(comment BuildingConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$BuildingConcept are collections and relations having to do with buildings.  Specializations of #$Building, and relations used for describing the construction, parts, size, uses, and other features of various types of buildings are instances of this collection.")
(comment MilitaryFacilityConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$MilitaryFacilityConcept are collections and relations having to do with military facilities.  Specializations of #$MilitaryFacility, and relations used for describing the construction, parts, size, uses, and personnel of various types of military facilities are instances of this collection.")
(comment WaterBodyConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$WaterBodyConcept are collections and relations having to do with bodies of water.  Specializations of #$BodyOfWater, and relations used for describing the parts, sizes, locations, uses, and other features of various types of water-bodies are instances of this collection.")
(comment ClimateConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$ClimateConcept are collections and relations having to do with climate.  Specializations of #$AnnualClimateCycle, those specializations of #$GeographicalRegion that are closely associated with particular climates, and relations used for describing the locations, meteorological features, inhabitants,  and other features of regions in various types of climates are instances of this collection.")
(comment VegetationConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$VegetationConcept are collections and relations having to do with vegetation.  Instances of #$RegionTypeByVegetation, and relations used for describing the locations and associated climates and terrain features of various types of vegetation are instances of this collection.")
(comment IndustrialConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$IndustrialConcept are collections and relations having to do with types of industries and industrial facilities.  Instances of #$IndustryType, specializations of #$ManufacturingFacility, and relations used for describing the products, resources, parts, size, functions, and personnel of various types of industries are instances of this collection.")
(comment ProductionIndustryTypeFn "#$ProductionIndustryTypeFn is a collection denoting function. (#$ProductionIndustryTypeFn PRODUCT-TYPE) denotes the collection all of geographically defined industries (instances of #$Industry-Localized) that produce and/or sell (see #$sellsProductType) PRODUCT-TYPE.  The resulting #$IndustryType will subclass of #$Industry-Localized.  See also #$IndustryOfRegionFn to denote the particular #$Industry-Localized characterized by being in a certain country and producing a certain kind of product.")
(comment Polygon "A specialization of #$PlaneFigure and #$ConnectedSpaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Polygon is a plane figure whose boundary is one or more closed curves, each consisting of three or more straight line-segments joined end-to-end (see #$LineString).  Specializations of this collection include #$Triangle, #$Hexagon, and #$RegularPolygon.  See also #$PolygonTypeByNumberOfSides.")
(disjointWith Stalking-CriminalAct Smuggling)
(disjointWith Stalking-CriminalAct Manslaughter)
(disjointWith Stalking-CriminalAct NumbersPool)
(disjointWith PeaceNegotiation (AttemptingFn TerroristAct))
(disjointWith PeaceNegotiation (AttemptingFn MilitaryAttack))
(disjointWith PeaceNegotiation JobInterview)
(disjointWith Narcissism Ache-AilmentCondition)
(disjointWith MakingABankWithdrawal ProvidingSubsidy)
(comment typedGenlPreds "A #$TaxonomicSlotForPredicates that is a specialization of #$genlPreds (q.v.).  <code>(#$typedGenlPreds SPECPRED GENLPRED)</code> means that <code>GENLPRED</code> is a more general version of <code>SPECPRED</code> that allows for more general argument types.  Futhermore, any sequence of things that stand in the relation <code>GENLPRED</code> and that satisfy all of the respective argument-constraints on <code>SPECPRED</code> also stand in the relation <code>SPECPRED</code>.  More precisely, the #$typedGenlPreds sentence above entails each of the following: 
<pre>
1. GENLPRED is a generalization of SPECPRED (see #$genlPreds). 

2. The individual argument-type constraints (see #$ArgIsaPredicate 
   and #$ArgGenlPredicate) on SPECPRED are all at least as narrow 
   as, and in at least one argument-place narrower than, the 
   corresponding constraints on GENLPRED. 

3. For any list of things ARGS such that (GENLPRED . ARGS) 
   holds and where the items in ARGS satisfy both (a) the 
   respective individual argument-type constraints on the 
   corresponding argument-places of SPECPRED and (b) any 
   <i>inter-argument</i> constraints (see #$InterArgTypePredicate) 
   on SPECPRED, (SPECPRED . ARGS) holds.
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$typedGenlPreds #$sisters #$siblings)</code> entails that if <code>(#$siblings John Alex)</code> holds and Alex is a #$FemaleAnimal (and thus satisfies the relatively narrower type-constraint on the second argument of #$sisters), then <code>(#$sisters John Alex)</code> holds.")
(comment CyclicPolygon "A #$CyclicPolygon is a #$Polygon whose vertices all lie on a
common #$Circle. Every #$RegularPolygon is a #$CyclicPolygon. Since
three noncolinear points determine a unique #$Circle, every #$Triangle
is a #$CyclicPolygon as well.")
(comment sridForCoordinateSystem "(#$sridForCoordinateSystem SRID SYSTEM) means the SYSTEM is
the spatial reference system (see #$CoordinateSystem) which is
identified by SRID in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
See also the corresponding function #$CoordinateSystemFromSRIDFn.")
(comment CSQLSRIDForGeometryFn "#$CSQLSRIDForGeometryFn is the #$CSQLFunction
corresponding to #$SRIDForGeometryFn.")
(comment resultTemporallySubsumedByArg "<code>(#$resultTemporallySubsumedByArg FUNC)</code> holds just in case, for anything, ARG, such that ARG is an admitted argument to FUNC, the result of applying FUNC to ARG is such that (#$temporallySubsumes ARG (FUNC ARG)).  This predicate is restricted to unary functions whose #$arg1Isa is a specialization of #$TemporalThing and whose #$resultIsa is a specialization of #$TemporalThing. ")
(comment TEPQueryContentFromStringForContextFn "(#$TEPQueryContentFromStringForContextFn STRING MT) is an evaluatable function which, for a given sentence STRING and context MT, returns the #$CycLSentence-Askable which reflects (the disjunction of possible) cyclifier outputs for STRING in MT.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromPARC "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by PARC for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment Group "A collection of temporal objects.  Each instance of #$Group is a composite object made up of one or more individual objects or events.  A group is related to each of its members by the predicate #$groupMembers (q.v.).  Note that instances of #$Group are <i>not</i> collections.  A group has temporal extent and might have spatial location, while a collection is timeless and nonspatial.  It is of course possible to define a collection parallel to any given group, so that the instances of the collection are exactly the group-members of that group; e.g. each toe on my left foot (and nothing else) is both an instance of the collection of my left toes and a member of the group of toes on my left foot.  But that group (of my left toes) is a spatiotemporal thing while the correlated collection (of my left toes) is not.  Similarly, if a certain flock of pigeons is considered as having a location, a spatial extent, and a time of existence, then the flock is being considered a <i>group</i> and not a collection.  Finally, unlike a collection, a group cannot be empty, but must have <i>at least one</i> group-member.  As a default, a group whose group-members all are instances of #$SomethingExisting is itself an instance of #$SomethingExisting, and a group whose group-members all are #$Events is itself an #$Event.  Instances of #$Group include #$QueensGuard, #$ThreeWiseMen, #$SantasReindeer, and #$InternationalCommunity.")
(comment querySpecificationDefaultIrrelevantToConcept "(#$querySpecificationDefaultIrrelevantToConcept CONCEPT CYCL-Q-SPEC) means that, by default,  CYCL-Q-SPEC should not be deemed pertinent to CONCEPT.  
<p>
This predicate is useful for blocking default-strong arguments to the effect that CYCL-Q-SPEC is relevant to CONCEPT.")
(comment Gunman "A sub-collection of #$MaleHuman, this collection is characterized by the possession of and frequent use of instances of #$Gun-Portable during instances of #$ViolentAction.")
(comment Observer-Formal "A sub-collection of #$Person and an instance of both #$PersonTypeByDutyStatus and #$PersonTypeByActivity.  When a person is an instance of #$Observer-Formal, they participate in some #$Event but are not expected to (and may even have an #$Obligation to not) be actively involved in shaping the progression of that #$Event.  Because of this formal participation, they are a somewhat more specific collection that all of the #$observers of an event.")
(comment CivilLaw "A sub-collection of #$Law.  Each instance of #$CivilLaw is a particular law that governs relations among people, especially disputes involving a #$Contract, but also cases where one party is seeking redrees for a  accidents or libel. Contrast with #$CriminalLaw.")
(disjointWith CriminalLaw CivilLaw)
(comment CriminalLaw "A sub-collection of #$Law.  Each instance of #$CriminalLaw represents a particular law that restricts the performance of certain types of #$Action.  The performance of instances of those types of #$Action are considered #$CriminalActs and the #$maleficiary of those acts is either the #$Government or population of the region in which the event occurred. #$CriminalAct is the subcollection of #$IllegalAction in which the #$obligationsViolated is an instance of #$CriminalLaw.  Contrast with #$CivilLaw.")
(comment BreakingIntoSomething "A sub-collection of #$PurposefulPhysicalAction, each instance of #$BreakingIntoSomething involves the damage to some #$Container is damaged so that either it can be entered by the performer of this action or its contents can be removed.")
(comment BreakingAndEntering "A sub-collection of #$Trespassing.  Each instance of #$BreakingAndEntering involves the intrusion into a #$HumanOccupationConstruct through some forcible means (i.e., damaging a lock so as to be able to open a door) with the intent of committing some other #$CriminalAct once inside.")
(comment SealingAnArea "A sub-collection of #$ExercisingAuthoritativeControlOverSomething.  Each instance of #$SealingAnArea involves one agent controlling a #$Place to such a degree that other agents are prevented from physically entering that area.")
(comment ConfirmingTruthEvent "A sub-collection of #$CorroborationEvent.  In this particular sub-collection, the corrobation takes the form of some agent communicating (by some medium) that a particular claim is true or that a particular #$Situation has occurred.")
(comment ConfirmationForAPosition "A sub-collection of #$ChangeOfStatusEvent.  Each instance of #$ConfirmationForAPosition involves an instance of #$Evaluating and then an instance of #$Accept. During the evaluation an agent is judged for the ability to perform the set of duties associated with some position and the agent performing the evaluation decides that the evaluated agent meets the criteria and so, accepts the applicant / nominee.")
(comment CallingThePolice "A sub-collection of #$EmergencyCall-Script in which a phone-call is made to some #$LocalPoliceOrganization so as to request help.  This is distinct from #$EmergencyCall-Script, which involves the extended process of making the call to an #$EmergencyDispatchService and the associated instance of #$EmergencyResponse.")
(comment HavingGoneMissing "A sub-collection of #$Situation.  Each instance of #$HavingGoneMissing is a situation where some #$SomethingExisting plays the role of #$missingIndividual such that the none of the places where the entity <i>should</i> be are occupied by the entity in the  #$missingIndividual role.  The existence of an instance of #$HavingGoneMissing is importantly related to the beliefs of individuals who have some interest in the entity that fills the #$missingIndividual role, especially since it is from those beliefs that the normative location is fixed.  However, instances of this collection only refer to the actual state of having moved from that location.")
(comment missingIndividual "An assertion of the form (#$missingIndividual MISSING THING) states that in the instance of #$HavingGoneMissing MISSING, the #$PartiallyTangible THING is the entity that has gone missing.")
(comment hasDeadline "An assertion of the form (#$hasDeadline SPEC DATE) means that the #$NormativeSpecification SPEC describes some state of affairs and that the satisfaction of that state of affairs be brought about before the #$Date DATE in order for the resulting state of affairs to satisfy SPEC.")
(comment DescribingSomething "A sub-collection of #$Reporting. Each instance of #$DescribingSomething involves the communication of information about some #$Thing.  This is a very general collection and so, the #$topicOfInfoTransfer could be concrete or abstract, an individual or a collection, etc.")
(comment SettingADeadline "A sub-collection of #$Demanding-CommunicationAct and #$DescribingSomething. Each instance of a #$SettingADeadline involves describing a #$Date by which some #$Specification must be satisfied. More specifically, stating the #$Date that bears the relation #$hasDeadline from some instance of #$NormativeSpecification.")
(comment DisarmingSomeone "A sub-collectionn of #$AffectingSomething, all instances of #$DisarmingSomeone involve removing some #$Weapon from the possession of some agent.  This refers to the process of change and not the state of beinging disarmed.")
(comment EyeWitnessTestimony "A sub-collection of #$DescribingSomething.  Each instance of #$EyeWitnessTestimony involves the action of describing some #$Situation to which the performer of the instance of #$EyeWitnessTestimony bears the relation #$spectators. So, if AGENT bears the relation #$spectators to EVENT and later AGENT plays the #$performedBy role in DESCRIBES, an instance of #$DescribingSomething, and EVENT plays the role of #$topicOfInfoTransfer in DESCRIBES, then DESCRIBES is also an instance of #$EyeWitnessTestimony.")
(comment ViolatingACivilLaw "A sub-collection of #$IllegalAction, instances of #$ViolatingACivilLaw are those actions in which an instance of #$CivilLaw plays the role of #$obligationsViolated.")
(comment Trespassing "A sub-collection of #$IllegalAction and #$GoingSomewhere.  Each instance of #$Trespassing involves a movement into or onto a piece of property against the wishes of the agent that has legal rights over how that property is used.  Whether those instances are also instances of #$CriminalAct or a #$ViolatingACivilLaw depends on the social environment in which it occurs.")
(comment vertexOfPolygon "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a #$Polygon to its \"corners\".  <code>(#$vertexOfPolygon POINT POLYGON)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is a vertex #$Point of one of the #$LineStrings that  constitute the boundary of <code>POLYGON<code>.")
(comment EventOrRoleConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$EventOrRoleConcept are collections and relations having to do with general kinds of events and the roles that things can play in them.  Major specializations of #$Event and #$Role, and other relations used for stating significant facts about events (such as time, place, causes, and effects) are instances of this collection.")
(comment Posture-Configuration "A subcollection of #$Configuration.  Each instance of
#$Posture-Configuration is a #$Situation that consists of the body of
an #$Animal being in a certain configuration or orientation
(e.g., crouching or lying prone).  Note that bodily postures are also
represented in #$Cyc as subcollections of #$Animal (see #$AnimalTypeByPosture) -- that is, as collections of animals who possess the relevant posture.  To relate collections in these two different schemes, use
#$configurationForPostureType and its corresponding function,
#$Posture-ConfigurationFn .")
(comment animalInPosture "(#$animalInPosture POSTURE ANIMAL) means that POSTURE is an instance of #$Configuration that consists of ANIMAL's body being in a certain posture.")
(comment Kappa "A binary #$PredicateDenotingFunction and a #$ScopingRelation (qq.v.), #$Kappa is used to define a predicate on the basis of a sentence (see #$CycLSentence-Assertible) and a list of variables (see #$CycLVariableList) some or all of which typically occur free in the sentence.  The resulting predicate holds of just those sequences that would make the sentence true.
<p>
More precisely, if <code>VAR1,...,VAR<sub>N</sub></code> include all of the #$CycLVariables that occur free in <code>SENT</code>, then the term
<pre>
  (#$Kappa (VAR1 ... VAR<sub>N</sub>) SENT)
</pre>
denotes the <code>N</code>-ary relation (i.e. the #$Predicate) that holds of any admitted sequence of arguments <code>ARG1...ARG<sub>N</sub></code> if and only if <code>SENT[ARG<sub>I</sub>/VAR<sub>I</sub>]</code> is true, where <code>SENT[ARG<sub>I</sub>/VAR<sub>I</sub>]</code> is the result of simultaneously replacing each free occurrence of <code>VAR<sub>I</sub></code> in <code>SENT</code> with a CycL name of the corresponding <code>ARG<sub>I</sub></code> (for 1 <= <code>I</code> <= <code>N</code>). 
<p>
(With respect to the above, <code>ARG1...ARG<sub>N</sub></code> is an <i>admitted sequence of arguments</i> to <code>(#$Kappa (VAR1 ... VAR<sub>N</sub>) SENT)</code> just in case (i) each <code>ARG<sub>I</sub></code> satisfies any argument-type constraints applicable to the position(s) occupied by <code>VAR<sub>I</sub></code> in <code>SENT</code> and (ii) if any two variables <code>VAR<sub>I</sub></code> and <code>VAR<sub>J</sub></code> are the same then the corresponding sequence-members <code>ARG<sub>I</sub></code> and <code>ARG<sub>J</sub></code> are identical.)
<p>
A few examples.  (#$Kappa (<code>?X</code>) (#$greaterThan <code>?X</code> 1)) is a unary predicate that holds of all numbers greater than 1. (#$Kappa (<code>?X ?Y</code>) (#$greaterThan <code>?X</code> 1)) is a binary predicate that holds of any ordered pair of argument values whose first member is a number greater than 1. (#$Kappa (<code>?X</code>) (#$greaterThan 2 1)) is a unary predicate that holds of any thing.  (#$Kappa (<code>?X</code>) (#$greaterThan 1 2)) is a unary predicate that holds of no thing.  (#$Kappa (<code>?X ?X</code>) (#$greaterThan <code>?X 1</code>)) is a binary predicate that holds of any ordered pair of numbers whose first and second members are the same number <b>K</b>, where <b>K</b> is greater than 1.
<p>
Most of the above examples are uses of #$Kappa that have little utility.  The first example, however, is an example of a common and important use of #$Kappa, which is to reduce the arity of a predicate by fixing the value for one or more of its arguments. For example, (#$Kappa (<code>?COUNTRY ?RATE</code>) (#$exportRate <code>?COUNTRY</code> #$Oil <code>?RATE</code>)) defines a binary predicate that relates a country to its export rate of oil, whereas (#$Kappa (<code>?COUNTRY</code>) (#$exportRate <code>?COUNTRY</code> #$Oil (#$MillionBarrelsPerDay 2)))</code> defines a unary predicate that holds of any country that exports oil at the rate of two million barrels per day.
<p>
The sentence that is an argument to #$Kappa may be arbitrarily complex.  For example, it may be an existentially quantified conjunction.  Thus,
<pre>
   (#$Kappa (?PERSON ?NAME)
    (#$thereExists ?FATHER
      (#$and
        (#$isa ?PERSON #$Person)
        (#$father ?PERSON ?FATHER)
        (#$lastName ?FATHER ?NAME))
</pre>
<p>
returns a predicate that holds of any ordered pair <b><PERSON, NAME></b>, such that <b>PERSON</b> is a person whose father's last name is <b>NAME</b>.
<p>
Sometimes it is useful to construct an expression of the form <code>(#$Kappa LIST SENT)</code> where some variables that occur free in <code>SENT</code> do <i>not</i> occur in <code>LIST</code>.  For example, <code>(#$Kappa (?X) (#$greaterThan ?X ?Y))</code> might be used in a context where the <code>?Y</code> appears within the scope of a quantifier that occurs outside of the #$Kappa expression.
<p>
#$Kappa is a cousin of the function #$Lambda (q.v.), which is used to define a <i>function</i> on the basis of a list of variables and a non-logical term.")
(comment issuerOfCW "<code>(#$issuerOfCW PCW AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> issued the published work <code>PCW</code>. <code>AGENT</code> 'issued' <code>PCW</code>, in the sense meant here, just in case <code>AGENT</code> (i) created or authored <code>PCW</code>
(see #$creatorOfCW), (ii) distributed <code>PCW</code>, or otherwise made it available, to an outside audience (#$publisher).  Note that
#$Organizations, as well as #$IndividualAgents, may issue conceptual
works, as in the case where members of an organization <code>ORG</code> author the
work on <code>ORG</code>'s behalf, and the work is subsequently distributed as
<code>ORG</code>'s product.  Also note that making a work 'available' may involve
either distributing physical copies of it, or placing copies of it in
a location accessible to the target audience, e.g., on a website.  See
the comment on #$PublishedConceptualWork.")
(comment authorListStringForStyleAndRendering-Maximal "<code>(#$authorListStringForStyleAndRendering-Maximal WORK STYLE-SPECIFICATION MARKUP-LANGUAGE STRING)</code> means that <code>STRING</code> is the canonical representation of the author list for <code>WORK</code> according to <code>STYLE-SPECIFICATION</code>, for rendering using <code>MARKUP-LANGUAGE</code>.")
(comment WorkWithNoAttributedCreator "A specialization of #$ConceptualWork.  Each instance of #$WorkWithNoAttributedCreator is a conceptual work that has no creator attribution.")
(comment UnitOfMeasureConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$UnitOfMeasureConcept are collections and relations having to do with unit of measurement functions and the kinds of quantities they return as values.  Important specializations and instances of #$UnitOfMeasure, major specializations or #$ScalarInterval, and relations that are important to measurement theory are instances of this collection.")
(comment postureConnotesFeelingToward "(#$postureConnotesFeelingToward POSTURE FEELING THING) means
that POSTURE, an instance of #$Posture-Configuration, in some way communicates FEELING
toward THING.  More precisely, POSTURE communicates that the animal who is
'in' POSTURE (see #$animalInPosture) has 
FEELING towards THING.  Note that it is possible that ANIMAL neither
has nor wishes to communicate such a feeling, but her being in POSTURE
nevertheless suggests this.  For example, there are a number of bodily
postures that, in Arab cultures, communicate a lack of #$Respect towards
the person to whom one is speaking.")
(comment postureConnotesFeeling "(#$postureConnotesFeeling POSTURE FEELING) means that
POSTURE, an instance of #$Posture-Configuration, in some way
communicates FEELING. More precisely, it communicates that the
animal who is 'in' POSTURE (see #$animalInPosture) has FEELING. Note that it is possible 
for (#$postureConnotesFeeling POSTURE FEELING) to hold even if ANIMAL does not in fact have FEELING.  For example, certain bodily postures may
suggest #$Embarrassment or #$Anticipation-Feeling,  because they are common manifestations of these feelings.  See also #$postureConnotesFeelingToward.")
(comment termInSentenceOfQuerySpecificationOfType "<code>(#$termInSentenceOfQuerySpecificationOfType TERM Q-SPEC FOLDER-TYPE)</code> means that the #$CycLTerm '<code>TERM</code>' occurs in the #$InferenceSentenceParameter '<code>SENTENCE</code>' associated with <code>Q-SPEC</code> (an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification), where <code>Q-SPEC</code> is the query specification for a template that is a resource contained in <code>FOLDER-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$KEInteractionFolder).")
(comment pertinentQuerySentenceWithTerms "<code>(#$pertinentQuerySentenceWithTerms SOURCE-SENTENCE TERM-SET NEW-SENTENCE SUBSTITUTION-COUNT)</code> means that <code>NEW-SENTENCE</code> is a #$CycLSentence-Askable constructed by taking the #$CycLSentence-Askable <code>SOURCE-SENTENCE</code> and replacing terms or free variables in it with  <code>SUBSTITUTION-COUNT</code> many terms from <code>TERM-SET</code>. 
<p>
#$pertinentQuerySentenceWithTerms is supported by a removal module that can be called by the Cyc inference engine.  The removal module requires the first two argument positions to be bound, the third to be unbound, and the fourth can be either bound or unbound.
<p>
Here is an example of how the removal module works.  Given this query sentence: 
<p>
<pre>
(#$pertinentQuerySentenceWithTerms 
   (#$Quote (#$objectFoundInLocation #$OsamaBinLaden ?X)) 
   (#$TheSet #$MohamedAtta #$Iran) 
   ?SENT 
   ?SUBST-COUNT)
</pre>
<p>
The following will be returned as bindings for <?SENT, ?SUBST-COUNT>:
<pre>
<(#$Quote (#$objectFoundInLocation #$MohamedAtta ?X)), 1>
<(#$Quote (#$objectFoundInLocation #$OsamaBinLaden #$Iran)), 1>
<(#$Quote (#$objectFoundInLocation #$MohamedAtta #$Iran)), 2>
</pre>
")
(comment subExpressionWithStringAtOffset "<code>(#$subExpressionWithStringAtOffset SUB SUPER STRING INTEGER)</code> means that the instance <code>SUB</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg is the subexpression of <code>SUPER</code> whose string is <code>STRING</code> and which starts <code>OFFSET</code> characters into the string of <code>SUPER</code>.")
(comment SeaplaneStation "A #$SeaplaneStation is an #$Airport-Physical which is adjacent to some #$Sea or other large body of water and supports the takeoff and landing of #$FloatPlanes.")
(comment molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample "A ternary predicate that relates a sample of something -- typically a solution -- to the molar concentration of *chemical objects* of a given species which it contains. (#$molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample SAMPLE CHEM-OBJ-TYPE VALUE) holds just in case SAMPLE has VALUE, expressed in moles per liter, of ions/molecules of type CHEM-OBJ-TYPE.  The concentration of ions in a sample is expressed in terms of ((#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter) NUM). Note that for ionic compounds this expresses the molarity of the *dissociated* species in a solution: e.g., if SAMPLE is a 1M aqueous solution of HCl,  it would be incorrect to state (#$molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample SAMPLE (#$MoleculeFn #$HydrogenChloride) ((#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter) 1)) since HCl, a strong acid, dissociates completely in #$HydrogenPlusIons and #$ChlorideIons, so that SAMPLE contains virtually no amount of HCl *molecules*. On the other hand, it would be correct to assert (#$molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample (q.v.) SAMPLE #$HydrogenPlusIons ((#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter) SOME-NUM)).")
(comment SentenceAndMtOnlyVersionOfTestFn "An instance of both #$ReifiableFunction and #$UnaryFunction. When applied to an instance KBCT-FS of #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified, #$SentenceAndMtOnlyVersionOfTestFn returns an instance KBCT-SMO of #$KBContentTest-QuerySentenceAndMicrotheoryOnly. The test query specification (see the predicate #$testQuerySpecification) for KBCT-SMO has the same inference sentence and inference microtheory parameters as the specification for KBCT-FS, but has no other specified parameters.")
(comment testHasSentenceAndMtOnlyVersion "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate and a specialization of #$genlKBContentTests. (#$testHasSentenceAndMtOnlyVersion KBCT-FS KBCT-SMO) means that the query specification for the #$KBContentTest-QuerySentenceAndMicrotheoryOnly KBCT-SMO has only the inference sentence and inference microtheory parameters specified, and these parameters are the same as those in the query specification for the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified KBCT-FS (which also has other inference parameters specified). See also the function #$SentenceAndMtOnlyVersionOfTestFn.")
(comment SubLString "A subcollection of #$SubLAtomicTerm (q.v.) whose instances can be used to denote character-strings.  In the SubL language (as in English), one can refer to a particular character-string by simply putting a pair of quotation marks around it.  Semantically, what this amounts to is using a certain character-string to denote itself.  
<p>
More precisely: a given #$SubLString STRING is a character-string such that, when it appears inside a pair of double-quotation marks, STRING is interpreted as denoting itself (i.e. STRING).  The surrounding quotation-marks are <i>not</i> considered to be parts of STRING; rather, they are syncategorematic symbols whose role is merely to indicate that a string is being referred to.  Thus, in the context of the sentence '(#$firstName #$Lenat \"Doug\")', the four-character string 'Doug' denotes itself: the string `Doug'.  
<p>
Note that <i>not</i> every #$CharacterString is a #$SubLString: there are some restrictions on what characters a SubL-string can contain. Note also that #$SubLStrings are <i>atomic</i> terms (see #$SubLAtomicTerm and #$CycLAtomicTerm), as they cannot be constructed out of other SubL (or CycL) terms via the syntax (grammar) of SubL (or CycL).  Thus although the string 'hotdog' can indeed be obtained from 'hot' and 'dog' via concatenation (see #$ConcatenateStringsFn), the <i>syntax</i> of SubL (or CycL) itself contains no rule that licenses such a construction (see e.g. the comments on #$SubLSExpression, #$CycLExpression, #$CycLTerm, #$CycLFormula, and various subcollections thereof).  Note finally that #$SubLString is a \"quoted-collection\" (see #$quotedCollection).  Thus the sentence '(#$isa \"hotdog\" #$SubLString)' means, just as one would expect, that the six-character string 'hotdog' (not the eight-character quote-inclusive '\"hotdog\"'; see above) is an instance of #$SubLString.")
(comment FerryCrossing-Toll "A route in a body of water where a ferry crosses from one shoreline to another and a toll is collected.")
(comment CollectorRoad "A specialization of #$Roadway.  A #$CollectorRoad
is needed to connect traffic between #$LocalRoads and #$ArterialRoads; 
also used for relatively short-distance travel in town.  A #$CollectorRoad 
has a moderate balance between mobility and accessibility; typically traffic 
speeds are from 20 to 45 mph.")
(comment ArterialRoad "A specialization of #$Highway.  An #$ArterialRoad permits faster 
traffic by having less stops and accessibility.  It usually is at least 
two-lanes in either direction.  In city traffic, it is not uncommon to 
have occassional traffic lights, or in the country, four-way stops at the 
juncture of other major roads. However, long stretches of road will have 
unobstructed traffic and speeds of over 60 mph.")
(comment FrontageRoad "A specialization of #$Roadway.  Each instance of #$FrontageRoad 
typically runs parallel to an #$ArterialRoad or #$Freeway (i.e., the 
main road) and has intermittent ramps that merge to and from the main road.  
A #$FrontageRoad also is usually one way: traffic moves in the same 
direction as traffic on the lane of the main road that is immediately 
adjacent.  Another #$Frontage road has traffic that moves in the opposite 
direction on the other side of the main road.  #$FrontageRoads often 
have easy access to properties or side streets that are on the side opposite
of the main road (usually the right side).")
(comment CollectorDistributorRoad "A specialization of #$Roadway.  Each #$CollectorDistributorRoad
is a part of a #$Freeway or #$ArterialRoad that allows a vehicle to 
merge or get off the main road without weaving or interfering with the 
flow of traffic in the faster lanes.  Like the typical #$FrontageRoad, the 
#$CollectorDistributorRoad is one way.  However, the latter is part of the 
main road, while the #$FrontageRoad is not.")
(comment QueryLibraryPreference "A #$QueryLibraryPreference is a #$SoftwareParameter which
states a particular mode of Query Library behavior which a user may
state as a preferred behavior when using the Query Library. A user's
preference is stated using #$preferenceForQueryLibrary.")
(comment preferenceForQueryLibrary "A predicate for stating a user's preferred query library
behaviors. (#$preferenceForQueryLibrary USER PREFERENCE) means that
when USER is using the Query Library, the tool's behavior should
conform to PREFERENCE.")
(comment PrintQueryLibraryAnswersInCycL "An instance of #$QueryLibraryPreference which instructs
the Query Library to display query results in #$CycL by default.")
(comment PrintQueryLibraryAnswersInEnglish "An instance of #$QueryLibraryPreference which instructs
the Query Library to display query results in the #$EnglishLanguage by
default.")
(comment ReviewOntologyExportContentInCycL "An instance of #$QueryLibraryPreference which instructs
the Query Library's ontology export review module to display
exportable content in #$CycL by default.")
(comment ReviewOntologyExportContentInOWL "An instance of #$QueryLibraryPreference which instructs
the Query Library's ontology export review module to display
exportable content in the #$OWL-WebOntologyLanguage by default.")
(comment ReviewOntologyExportContentInEnglish "An instance of #$QueryLibraryPreference which instructs
the Query Library's ontology export review module to display
exportable content in the #$EnglishLanguage by default.")
(comment UseDeclarativeModeToParaphraseQueries "An instance of #$QueryLibraryPreference which instructs
the Query Library to paraphrase queries as declarative sentences without
quantifying variables.")
(comment UseInterrogativeModeToParaphraseQueries "An instance of #$QueryLibraryPreference which instructs
the Query Library to paraphrase queries as interrogatives, with quantified
variables.")
(comment LocalRoad "A specialization of #$Roadway.  Each instance of #$LocalRoad
is typically one lane with two-way traffic, frequent stops, and slow 
travel.   #$LocalRoads tend to be short with a small volume of traffic.  
Most streets - especially those in neighborhoods - are #$LocalRoads.")
(comment RadarReflector "A specialization of #$NavigationDevice. Each instance of #$RadarReflector is intended to be highly detectable by #$RadarDevices. Examples would include stationary reflectors for use with vehicle-mounted #$RadarDevices, and vehicle-mounted reflectors (such as those on boats) for use with all #$RadarDevices.")
(comment RadarReflector-Runway "A specialization of #$RadarReflector. #$RadarReflector-Runways are mounted at one end of an #$AirplaneRunway in order to make that runway highly detectable to aircraft-mounted #$RadarDevices.")
(comment RadarReflector-Mobile "A specialization of #$RadarReflector. #$RadarReflector-Mobiles are most typically mounted on boats and ships, in order to make them more detectable to #$RadarDevices on other vehicles. These devices are essential anti-collision safety equipment for instances of #$WaterTransportationVehicle.")
(comment AircraftArrestingGear "A specialization of #$PhysicalDevice. Each instance of #$AircraftArrestingGear is a device used to stop an aircraft immediately upon landing. Common examples include the cable and hook systems on #$AircraftCarriers and barrier nets.")
(comment AircraftBlastBarrier "A specialization of #$Barrier-ConstructionArtifact. Each instance of #$AircraftBlastBarrier is a barrier (typically a wall), used to divert or dissipate jet or propeller blast. #$AircraftBlastBarriers perform both safety and noise reduction functions.")
(comment Helipad "A paved or improved area used by vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) aircraft for #$TakeOff-Flights and #$Landing-Aircrafts.")
(comment AirplaneRunway-Overrun "An area beyond an #$AirplaneRunway designated as able to support an #$Airplane during an aborted #$TakeOff-Flight.")
(comment AirplaneRunway-TouchdownZone "A specialization of #$PathForWheeledVehicles. The first 3000 feet of an #$AirplaneRunway, starting at the threshold, is called a #$AirplaneRunway-TouchdownZone. #$Airplanes generally make their first contact with the ground in the #$AirplaneRunway-TouchdownZone during #$AirplaneLandings.")
(comment Revetment "A specialization of #$Barrier-ConstructionArtifact and #$PhysicalImpactBuffer. A #$Revetment protects equipment or facilities from hostile action, specifically #$Explosions.")
(comment TravelRoute-Water "A specialization #$TravelRoute. Each instance of #$TravelRoute-Water is an established line of travel or access that crosses some #$BodyOfWater, intended for use by #$WaterTransportationDevices.")
(comment DecontaminationPad-Airport "A specialization of #$Apron-Airport designated area where a Nuclear, Biological, or Chemical (NBC) contaminated aircraft, checked at landing, is sent to be decontaminated by special teams.")
(comment Shoulder-Roadway "Adjacent area along either side of a #$PathForWheeledVehicles not normally used by vehicles or aircraft, but provided as an allowable margin in case of emergency situations.")
(comment Taxiway "A specialization of #$Roadway. Each instance of #$Taxiway is a prepared surface providing acess to and from an #$AirplaneRunway. #$Taxiways could be considered as the collection of non-runway ground paths for airplanes.")
(comment SeaplaneLandingTakeOffArea "A specialization of #$WaterSurfacePath. Each instance of #$SeaplaneLandingTakeOffArea is a designated portion of water outlined by visual surface markings, used by seaplanes to land and take off.")
(comment InertialNavigationSystem "A specialization of #$NavigationDevice. Each instance of #$InertialNavigationSystem measures the position and altitude of a vehicle by measuring the accelerations and rotations applied to the system's inertial frame.")
(comment ResettingASensor "The collection of events in which some agent returns a #$Sensor-Device to its default state. Postive examples of #$ResettingASensor would include resetting an #$Odometer or 'zeroing out' a #$Voltmeter. Note that #$ResettingASensor is distinct from <i>calibrating</i> a sensor, which may or may not involve resetting.")
(comment INSAlignmentPad "A specialization of #$Apron-Airport. Each instance of #$INSAlignmentPad is a designated area where the inertial navigation system instruments of an aircraft are reset before take-off.")
(comment AirplaneRunway-DisplacedThreshold "The designated beginning of the portion of the #$AirplaneRunway usable for an #$AirplaneLanding.  This is located at a point on the runway other than the designated beginning of the runway.   In addition, the feature typically exists at each end of the runway.")
(comment OntologyExporterUser "The #$OntologyExporterUser is the default user of the Query
Library's Ontology Exporter tool which is being developed as part of
the #$SemanticMarkupProject.")
(comment NotForAnalogyPredicate "The collection of #$Predicates that should not be considered at 
all for analogical reasoning (e.g. expressions using this
predicate should not even be put into the cases or
dgroups being compared.)")
(comment USCityOrCounty "#$USCityOrCounty is the #$collectionUnion of #$USCity and #$USCounty.")
(comment USAdministrativeDivision "#$USAdministrativeDivision is the collection of all US
states (#$State-UnitedStates), territories (#$Territory-US) plus the
#$DistrictOfColumbia.")
(comment Apron-Airport "A specialization of #$ParkingLot. Each instance of #$Apron-Airport is a defined paved or hard-packed area at an #$Airport-Physical or #$Heliport-Physical intended for parking #$AirTransportationDevices.")
(comment OGCNumPointsFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'NumPoints' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCNumPointsFn LINESTRING) returns the number of anchor points
(including endpoints) that are part of LINESTRING. See also the
corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLNumPointsFn.")
(comment CSQLNumPointsFn "#$CSQLNumPointsFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCNumPointsFn.")
(comment OGCStartPointFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'StartPoint' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCStartPointFn LINESEGMENT) returns the 'start' point of
LINESEGMENT. Ontologically speaking, #$LineSegments don't have an
orientation, so designating one endpoint as its 'start' point is
arbitrary. However, in OGC compliant databases, stored #$LineSegments
do have start and end points, even if they are arbitrarily assigned as
such. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLStartPointFn.")
(comment CSQLStartPointFn "#$CSQLStartPointFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCStartPointFn.")
(comment OGCEndPointFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'EndPoint' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCEndPointFn LINESEGMENT) returns the 'end' point of LINESEGMENT.
Ontologically speaking, #$LineSegments don't have an orientation, so
designating one endpoint as its 'end' point is arbitrary. However, in
OGC compliant databases, stored #$LineSegments do have start and end
points, even if they are arbitrarily assigned as such. See also the
corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLEndPointFn.")
(comment CSQLEndPointFn "#$CSQLEndPointFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCEndPointFn.")
(comment OGCBoundaryFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'Boundary' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCBoundaryFn GEOM) returns the (possibly empty) boundary of GEOM.
See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLBoundaryFn.")
(comment CSQLBoundaryFn "#$CSQLBoundaryFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCBoundaryFn.")
(comment OGCEnvelopeFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'Envelope' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCEnvelopeFn GEOM) returns the minimal subsuming bounding box
(polygon) containing GEOM. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLEnvelopeFn.")
(comment CSQLEnvelopeFn "#$CSQLEnvelopeFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCEnvelopeFn.")
(comment OGCLengthFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'Length' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCLengthFn LINE) returns the length of LINE. See also the
corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLLengthFn.")
(comment CSQLLengthFn "#$CSQLLengthFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCLengthFn.")
(comment OGCPointNFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'PointN' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCPointNFn LINE N) returns the Nth anchor point of LINE, where the
order is determined by the actual data storage structure of LINE on
the underlying database. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLPointNFn")
(comment CSQLPointNFn "#$CSQLPointNFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCPointNFn.")
(comment OGCCentroidFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'Centroid' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCCentroidFn SURFACE) returns the centroid point of SURFACE, which
may, or may not be actually on SURFACE. See also the corresponding
#$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLCentroidFn")
(comment CSQLCentroidFn "#$CSQLCentroidFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCCentroidFn.")
(comment CSQLXCoordinateFn "#$CSQLXCoordinateFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCXCoordinateFn.")
(comment CSQLYCoordinateFn "#$CSQLYCoordinateFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCYCoordinateFn.")
(comment OGCAreaFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'Area' which is defined
in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL. (#$OGCAreaFn
SURFACE) returns the area of SURFACE. See also the corresponding
#$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLAreaFn")
(comment CSQLAreaFn "#$CSQLAreaFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCAreaFn.")
(comment OGCIsEmptyFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'IsEmpty' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCIsEmptyFn GEOM) is equal to 1 iff GEOM is empty, and equal to 0
iff GEOM is not empty. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLIsEmptyFn")
(comment CSQLIsEmptyFn "#$CSQLIsEmptyFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCIsEmptyFn.")
(comment OGCIsSimpleFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'IsSimple' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCIsSimpleFn GEOM) is equal to 1 iff GEOM is simple, and equal to
0 iff GEOM is not simple. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLIsSimpleFn")
(comment CSQLIsSimpleFn "#$CSQLIsSimpleFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCIsSimpleFn.")
(comment OGCIsClosedFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'IsClosed' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCIsClosedFn LINE) is equal to 1 iff LINE is closed, and equal to
0 iff LINE is not simple. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLIsClosedFn")
(comment CSQLIsClosedFn "#$CSQLIsClosedFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCIsClosedFn.")
(comment OGCIsRingFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'IsRing' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCIsRingFn LINE) is equal to 1 iff LINE is a ring (#$LinearRing),
and equal to 0 iff LINE is not a linear ring. See also the
corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLIsRingFn")
(comment CSQLIsRingFn "#$CSQLIsRingFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCIsRingFn.")
(comment OGCExteriorRingFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'ExteriorRing' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCExteriorRingFn POLYGON) returns the exterior ring (#$LineString)
of POLYGON. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLExteriorRingFn")
(comment CSQLExteriorRingFn "#$CSQLExteriorRingFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCExteriorRingFn.")
(comment OGCNumInteriorRingFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'NumInteriorRing' which
is defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCNumInteriorRingFn POLYGON) returns the number of interior rings
(#$LineString) of POLYGON. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLNumInteriorRingFn")
(comment CSQLNumInteriorRingFn "#$CSQLNumInteriorRingFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCNumInteriorRingFn.")
(comment OGCInteriorRingNFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'InteriorRingN' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCInteriorRingNFn POLYGON N) returns the Nth interior ring
(#$LineString) of POLYGON. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLInteriorRingNFn")
(comment CSQLInteriorRingNFn "#$CSQLInteriorRingNFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCInteriorRingNFn.")
(comment OGCNumGeometriesFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'NumGeometries' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCNumGeometriesFn GEOMCOMP) returns the number of geometries in
GEOMCOMP. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLNumGeometriesFn")
(comment CSQLNumGeometriesFn "#$CSQLNumGeometriesFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCNumGeometriesFn.")
(comment OGCGeometryNFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'GeometryN' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCGeometryNFn GEOMCOMP N) returns the Nth geometry in GEOMCOMP.
See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLGeometryNFn")
(comment CSQLGeometryNFn "#$CSQLGeometryNFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCGeometryNFn.")
(comment nonWffResultsForContentModel "(#$nonWffResultsForContentModel MT RESULT-LIST) means that the list RESULT-LIST is comprised of non-wff parses which were generated as possible interpretations for the utterance associated with the ContentModelMicrotheory MT.")
(disjointWith ContextualizedInformationStructure AbstractInformationStructure)
(comment ContextualizedInformationStructure "A specialization of #$AbstractStructure whose instances are individuated by not only their internal structure, but by their relationships to other parts of a larger abstract structure.  As such, #$ContextualizedInformationStructure is disjoint with (see #$disjointWith) #$AbstractInformationStructure, a collection of individuals whose identity is determined soley by their decomposition into abstract symbols and the relations between them.  

<p>Consider, for example, a document that contains the sentence (an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure), \"What you see is what you get.\"  Despite being seven words in length, this sentence contains exactly six instances of #$AbstractInformationStructure that are also text-strings for words: \"What,\" \"you,\" \"see,\" \"is,\" \"what,\" and \"get.\"  However, it contains seven instances of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure: the one and only occurrence of \"What,\" the <i>first</i> occurrence of \"you,\" and so on.  #$ContextualizedInformationStructure is the collection of such AIS-occurrences; they are abstract objects that are identified by both the AISes they \"contain\" (see #$cisContainsAIS) and the relations they bear to other info-structures in some larger AIS.
<p>The <i>occurrence</i> metaphor used to describe contextualized information structures can mislead one into thinking of contextualized information structures, or CISes, as physical instantiations of AISes, in the way that physical character string tokens (c.f., #$CharacterStringToken) instantiate (c.f., #$instantiationOfAIS) a character string (c.f., #$CharacterString) AIS.  On the contrary, #$ContextualizedInformationStructure is not a collection of physical or spatial entities -- they are not instances of #$InformationBearingThing -- but are abstract objects derivable from a complete description of an AIS.")
(comment cisContainsAIS "<code>(#$cisContainsAIS CIS AIS)</code> means that the instance <code>CIS</code> of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure is a contextualized occurrence of <code>AIS</code>, an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure.")
(comment asHtmlSourceString "<code>(#$asHtmlSourceString AS STRING)</code> identifies <code>STRING</code> as the HTML source string of <code>AS</code>, an instance of #$AbstractStructure, typically an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure or #$ContextualizedInformationStructure.")
(comment spanningLink "<code>(#$spanningLink AIS LINK)</code> means that the entire span of the instance <code>AIS</code> of #$AbstractStructure (typically an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure or #$ContextualizedInformationStructure that is part of an HTML document) is linked to some other resource via the instance <code>LINK</code> of #$Hyperlink. In a typical browsing environment, clicking on any part of AIS will result in <code>LINK</code> being followed.")
(comment structureContainsLinks "<code>(#$structureContainsLinks STRUCTURE LINK)</code> means that the instance <code>STRUCTURE</code> of #$AbstractStructure (typically a hypertext document or a part thereof) contains an instance <code>LINK</code> of #$Hyperlink.")
(comment structureContainsSpanningLinkToAIS "<code>(#$structureContainsSpanningLinkToAIS STRUCTURE AIS)</code> means that there is a an instance of #$Hyperlink to the instance <code>AIS</code> of #$AbstractInformationStructure that is a spanning link (see #$spanningLink) contained in <code>STRUCTURE</code>, an abstract information structure.  A spanning link for a structure is one that spans the string that is the largest AIS inside the link-tags that is contained in (#$subStructures) that structure.")
(comment aisFormat "<code>(#$aisFormat AIS FORMAT)</code> means that the instance <code>AIS</code> of #$GraphicalStructure and #$AbstractInformationStructure is an instance of <code>FORMAT</code>, an instance of #$GraphicalStructureByFormat.  For example, 
<pre>
   (#$aisFormat \"<i>green eggs and ham</i>\" #$ItalicizedFormat)</pre>
means that \"<i>green eggs and ham</i>\" is an italicized string.  #$aisFormat speaks to the format of a particular structure itself, not to the format of a structure in relation to other structures or within an encompassing structure.  To describe the format of a contextualized structure, use the more general #$hasFormat.")
(comment hasFormat "<code>(#$hasFormat STRUCTURE FORMAT)</code> means that the instance <code>STRUCTURE</code> of #$GraphicalStructure is formatted with <code>FORMAT</code>, an instance of #$GraphicalStructureByFormat.  For example, 
<pre>
   (#$hasFormat TheFirstOccurrenceOf'GreenEggsAndHam'InDocument001 #$ItalicizedFormat)</pre>
means that the first occurrence of \"green eggs and ham\" in document 001 is formatted with italics.  Where <code>(#$hasFormat STRUCTURE FORMAT)</code> one can conclude that there is a sub-structure (#$subStructures) of <code>STRUCTURE</code> that is an instance <code>AIS</code> of #$GraphicalStructure and #$AbstractInformationStructure that is an <i>instance</i> of <code>FORMAT</i>, where <code>AIS</code> might be <code>STRUCTURE</code> itself.")
(comment GeometryFromTextFn "(#$GeometryFromTextFn WKTSTRING) denotes the
instance of #$OpenGISGeometry which is encoded in Well Known Text form
as the string WKTSTRING. See also the corresponding predicate,
#$wellKnownTextStringForGeometry")
(comment GeometryFromBinaryFn "(#$GeometryFromBinaryFn WKBSTRING) denotes
the instance of #$OpenGISGeometry which is encoded in Well Known
Binary form as the hexadecimal string WKBSTRING. See also the
corresponding predicate, #$wellKnownBinaryStringForGeometry")
