(comment BinaryForGeometryFn "This function returns the #$WellKnownBinaryString for a valid
instance of #$OpenGISGeometry. It is an #$SKSISupportedFunction, which
means that when it appears as the functor of a term in a query, the
term is translated by SKSI into an intermediate CSQL form using the
#$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLAsBinaryFn, and subsequently translated into
SQL.")
(comment TextForGeometryFn "This function returns the #$WellKnownTextString for a valid
instance of #$OpenGISGeometry. It is an #$SKSISupportedFunction, which
means that when it appears as the functor of a term in a query, the
term is translated by SKSI into an intermediate CSQL form using the
#$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLAsTextFn, and subsequently translated into
SQL.")
(comment CSQLAsBinaryFn "This #$CSQLFunction is translated into SQL as the function
\"AsBinary\" which is defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment cisPriorToCISInStructure "An instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate that identifies an ordering among two instances of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure in some larger structure.
<pre>
   (#$cisPriorToCISInStructure CIS-1 CIS-2 STRUCTURE)</pre>
means that <code>CIS-1</code> occurs prior to <code>CIS-2</code> within the context <code>STRUCTURE</code>, an instance of #$AbstractStructure.  \"occurs prior to\" in this context means that <code>CIS-1</code> comes before <code>CIS-2</code> as understood by the conventions used in interpreting <code>STRUCTURE</code>.  So if <code>CIS-1</code> and <code>CIS-2</code> are textual occurrences of English strings and <code>STRUCTURE</code> is a document formatted in the typical way that English documents are formatted, then any physical instantiation <code>PRINTOUT</code> of <code>STRUCTURE</code> will be such that an instantiation of the text for <code>CIS-1</code> will appear to the left of or higher than an instantiation of the text for <code>CIS-2</code> on <code>PRINTOUT</code>.")
(comment structurePriorToStructureInStructure "A very general instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate that identifies an ordering among two instances of #$AbstractStructure in some larger structure.
<pre>
   (#$structurePriorToStructureInStructure AS-1 AS-2 STRUCTURE)</pre>
means that <code>AS-1</code> occurs prior to <code>AS-2</code> within the context <code>STRUCTURE</code>, itself an instance of #$AbstractStructure.  \"occurs prior to\" in this context means that <code>AS-1</code> comes before <code>AS-2</code> as understood by the conventions (interpreted loosely, to include man-made conventions as well as natural ones, such as the processes by which information in DNA is interpreted) used in interpreting <code>STRUCTURE</code>.  So if <code>AS-1</code> and <code>AS-2</code> are abstract character strings (c.f., #$CharacterString) of English and <code>STRUCTURE</code> is a document formatted in the typical way that English documents are formatted, then any physical instantiation <code>PRINTOUT</code> of <code>STRUCTURE</code> will be such that an instantiation of (#$instantiationOfAIS) <code>AIS-1</code> will appear to the left of or higher than or on an earlier page than an instantiation of <code>AIS-2</code> on <code>PRINTOUT</code>.")
(comment aisPriorToAISInAIS "An instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate that identifies an ordering among two instances of #$AbstractInformationStructure in some larger structure.
<pre>
   (#$aisPriorToAISInAIS AIS-1 AIS-2 STRUCTURE)</pre>
means that <code>AIS-1</code> occurs prior to <code>AIS-2</code> within the context <code>STRUCTURE</code>, itself an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure.  \"occurs prior to\" in this context means that <code>AIS-1</code> comes before <code>AIS-2</code> as understood by the conventions used in interpreting <code>STRUCTURE</code>.  So if <code>AIS-1</code> and <code>AIS-2</code> are abstract character strings (c.f., #$CharacterString) of English and <code>STRUCTURE</code> is a document formatted in the typical way that English documents are formatted, then any physical instantiation <code>PRINTOUT</code> of <code>STRUCTURE</code> will be such that an instantiation of (#$instantiationOfAIS) <code>AIS-1</code> will appear to the left of or higher than or on an earlier page than an instantiation of <code>AIS-2</code> on <code>PRINTOUT</code>.<p>Note that because the same #$AbstractInformationStructure can appear multiple times in a larger structure, #$aisPriorToAISInAIS is neither asymmetric (see #$asymmetricInArgs) in its first and second argument-places, nor irreflexive (see #$interArgDifferent) in those argument-places.  Thus where <code>STRUCTURE</code> is an abstract information structure for the book <i>Green Eggs and Ham</i>, the following will hold true: <code>(#$aisPriorToAISInAIS \"Sam I am\" \"Sam I am\" STRUCTURE)</code>.  This feature makes #$aisPriorToAISInAIS somewhat unusual as an instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate; contrast with #$cisPriorToCISInStructure.")
(comment structureContainsCIS "<code>(#$structureContainsCIS STRUCTURE CIS)</code> means that the instance <code>STRUCTURE</code> of #$AbstractStructure is a structure in which <code>CIS</code> is a contextualized sub-structure.  See also #$ContextualizedInformationStructure.")
(comment wellKnownBinaryStringForGeometry "(#$wellKnownBinaryStringForGeometry GEOMETRY WKBSTRING)
means that WKBSTRING is the Well Known Binary string representation of
GEOMETRY. Well Known Binary (#$WellKnownBinaryString) is an encoding
format adopted by the Open Geospatial Consortium (OGC) and used in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL as the canonical
representation of geometric objects. See also the corresponding
function, #$GeometryFromBinaryFn")
(comment TheExemplarSentenceLevelPeg "The exemplar instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance used to guide the reification of instances of that collection vis a vis #$SentenceLevelPegCreationTemplateForDocumentSlurper.")
(comment CommonSenseTest-Answerable "An instance of #$KBContentTestSpecificationType, and a specialization of #$CommonSenseTest. Each instance of #$CommonSenseTest-Answerable is a common sense test that returns the correct answer or set of answers (see the predicates #$testAnswersCycL-Exact and #$testAnswersCycL-Wanted), while returning no incorrect answers (see the predicate #$testAnswersCycL-NotWanted).")
(comment FETIntermediateFieldValue "A collection of non-denoting concepts for use with the fact entry tool. Instances of #$FETIntermediateFieldValue are used in the fact entry tool to provide users with sensible choices to questions that don't have simple renderings in CycL. These instances are used as the arguments for special-purpose predicates which are then reformulated into inferentially-useful assertions.")
(comment BeheadingAnOrganism "A specialization of #$KillingByOrganism and
#$CuttingOffAPiece.  Each instance of this collection is an event in
which an organism's head is removed from its body.")
(comment completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs "An instance of #$InferenceCompletenessPredicate that relates a predicate to a set, the members of which are lists that serve to specify values and argument places in assertions formed with the predicate.  #$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs is similar to the more specialized and focused predicate, #$completeExtentEnumerableForValueInArg, which states completeness information for a predicate given a single value and argument-place.  Where
<pre>
    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValueInArg PRED VALUE N)</pre>
means that every fact expressible in the form
<pre>
    (PRED . ARGS)</pre>
in which <code>VALUE</code> appears in the <code>N</code>th argument-place is enumerable,
<pre>
    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs PRED SET),</pre>
where <code>SET</code> consists of a single list whose first member is <code>VALUE</code> and second is <code>N</code>, expressible as,
<pre>
    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs PRED 
      (#$TheSet 
        (#$TheList VALUE N)))</pre>
will have the same meaning.
<p>In general, 
<pre>
    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs PRED 
      (#$TheSet 
        (#$TheList VALUE-1 INTEGER-1) ... (#$TheSet VALUE-N INTEGER-N))),</pre>
means that every fact expressed in the form of the sentence <code>S</code> =
<pre>
    (PRED . ARGS)</pre>
is enumerable if for each pair <code>VALUE</code> and <code>INTEGER</code> in <<code>VALUE-1</code>, <code>INTEGER-1</code>> ... <<code>VALUE-N</code>, <code>INTEGER-N</code>> is such that <code>VALUE</code> appears in the <code>INTEGER</code>th argument place of <code>S</code>.
<p>
For example,
<pre>
    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs #$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure 
      (#$TheSet 
        (#$TheList #$NounPhrase 3)
        (#$TheList EMailMessageText001 4)))</pre>
means that every noun phrase in the text of this particular e-mail is known.  I.e., every fact expressible in the form,
<pre>
    (#$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure OCCURRENCE NUMBER #$NounPhrase EMailMessageText001)</pre>
is enumerable by the system.  In practical terms, this means that when querying for 
<pre>
    (#$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure ?OCCURRENCE ?NUMBER #$NounPhrase EMailMessageText001)</pre>
all values of <code>?OCCURRENCE</code> and <code>?NUMBER</code> can be found (using removal only).")
(comment contextOfPeg "<code>(#$contextOfPeg CONTEXT PEG)</code> means that the microtheory <code>CONTEXT</code> is the complete context -- contains a complete representation of the semantic content -- of <code>PEG</code>, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance.")
(comment ContextOfPegFn "<code>(#$ContextOfPegFn PEG)</code> denotes the instance <code>MT</code> of #$Microtheory such that <code>(#$contextOfPeg MT PEG)</code> -- i.e., <code>MT</code> is the Microtheory that contains a complete semantic representation of <code>PEG</code>.")
(comment USHighwayRoute "A specialization of #$Highway.  Each instance of #$USHighwayRoute
is coordinated by the US federal government, but they may be
funded and maintained by state governments.")
(comment USHighwayRoute-Major "A specialization of #$Freeway and #$USHighwayRoute.  Most 
instances of #$USHighwayRoute-Major have controlled-access and 
multiple lanes either way.  #$USHighwayRoute-Major typically either  
runs for a long distance or connects major cities.  Route 66 is a famous 
example; it extends 2448 miles and connects Chicago to Santa Monica, 
California.  #$USInterstateSystemRoute is 
an important specialization.")
(comment USHighwayRoute-Minor "A specialization of #$ArterialRoad and #$USHighwayRoute.
Most instances of #$USHighwayRoute-Minor have less 
controlled-access and supplement main routes.   US highway routes that 
are specialized as alternate, bypass, city, historic, scenic, spur, or 
truck are considered instances of #$USHighwayRoute-Minor.")
(comment USInterstateSystemRoute "A specialization of #$USHighwayRoute-Major.  Instances
of #$USInterstateSystemRoute are controlled-access and permit high speed
limits.   The federal government coordinates the highway system and 
provides funding to the states for maintenance.")
(comment StateRoute "A specialization of #$Highway.  Instances of #$StateRoute 
are controlled and maintained by a #$State-UnitedStates.  Unlike 
#$USHighwayRoute, state routes are exclusively intra-state.")
(comment StateRoute-Primary "A specialization of #$StateRoute and #$Freeway.  Instances 
of #$StateRoute-Primary are major expressways, multi-laned, and 
controlled-access.  They are found primarily in major cities.")
(comment StateRoute-Major "A specialization of #$StateRoute and #$ArterialRoad.  Instances
of #$StateRoute-Major are multi-laned and may extend many miles, but 
they lack controlled-access in certain parts, especially via towns or
cities.")
(comment StateRoute-Minor "A specialization of #$StateRoute and #$ArterialRoad.  An instance
of #$StateRoute-Minor is often a road with a painted dividing line, which
separates bi-directional traffic.   In order for a vehicle to pass a
slower vehicle, it must temporarily enter the lane for oncoming traffic. 
The minor roads include state farm roads and business roads.")
(comment AlternateRoute "A specialization of #$Roadway.  An instance of #$AlternateRoute
is a route that separates from a main route and returns to the 
main route at a later point.  The #$AlternateRoute is often longer 
and of less construction quality as the main route.  The purpose of the 
alternate route is often to relieve congestion from the main route.")
(comment BusinessRoute "A specialization of #$CollectorRoad.  An instance of 
#$BusinessRoute is a route that separates from the main route in 
order to detour through the commerical district of a town or city. 
After passing through the commerical district, the business route
reconnects to the main route.  The business route usually has easy 
access to the surrounding stores and properties and several traffic
stops.")
(comment BypassRoute "A specialization of #$Roadway and #$Bypass-Path.  An 
instance of #$BypassRoute is a route that separates from the main 
route in order to avoid entering into a commercial district or congested 
area.  The bypass route reconnects to the main route after the congestion 
is passed.")
(comment TruckRoute "A specialization of #$Roadway.  An instance of #$TruckRoute
is sometimes like a #$BypassRoute - used to bypass a congested area - 
but it is also a road that is more suitable for trucks.  For example, 
the #$TruckRoute may have wider lanes, better leveled surface, or 
properly elevated bridges with underpasses.  The #$TruckRoute is a 
temporary detour from the main route.")
(comment CountyNamedFn "The Cyc function #$CountyNamedFn is an #$IndividualDenotingFunction. (#$CountyNamedFn STRING GEOPOL) applied to a (quoted) #$ProperNameString STRING that is the name of a county in region GEOPOL returns the County in that region with that name. Examples: (#$CountyNamedFn \"Marin\" #$California-State); (#$CountyNamedFn \"Szeged\" #$Hungary); (#$CountyNamedFn \"Travis\" #$Texas-State).")
(comment Advocate-CourtesyTitle "The title ''Advocate'' that may attach to a person's name.")
(comment L2RDocumentSlurperCreationTemplate "The collection of instances of #$CycCreationTemplate that are used by the part of the Text Learner code that represents documents, aspects of their internal structure, and introduces pegs (see #$LinguisticExpressionPeg) for their sentences. (See also #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram).")
(comment URLSourceFn "An expression of the form (#$URLSourceFn URL) evaluates to the current #$HTMLSource located at the #$UniformResourceLocator URL.  This is a temporally qualifiable relation.")
(comment KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal "The goal-category of acquiring knowledge.")
(comment Tuple "A specialization of both #$MathematicalObject and #$AbstractIndividual (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Tuple is a complex consisting of one or more indexed (and possibly ordered) components; it might be a single, a pair, a triple, or so on; and the components might be things of any sort whatsoever (see #$memberOfTuple).  For example, a specialization of #$Tuple is #$TupleOfIntervals (q.v.), whose instances are tuples consisting exclusively of #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.); e.g. complex numbers and physical vectors are n-tuple-intervals.  Another specialization of #$Tuple is #$List (q.v.), whose instances are ordered. 
<p>
Each tuple has an associated \"index set\": the set of things that serve (via an associated \"indexing function\")  to index or individually represent the tuple's members (see  #$tupleIndexSet and #$tupleMemberIndex).  If the index set for a given tuple is the set of positive integers (or an initial segment thereof), then the integers' usual ordering serves to  order the tuple's components, and the tuple is in fact an <i>ordered n-tuple</i>, i.e. it is a #$List.  But in general any set (e.g. the column names in a relational database) may be used to index the components of a tuple.")
(comment MakingAbstractFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  When applied to a specialization TYPE of #$IntangibleIndividual (q.v.), #$MakingAbstractFn returns the collection of events in which one or more instances of TYPE are made (the collection in question will be a specialization of #$MakingSomethingAbstract).  For example, (#$MakingAbstractFn #$List-Information) is the collection of events in which at least one list is created.")
(comment CSQLGeometryTypeFn "#$CSQLGeometryTypeFn is the #$CSQLFunction corresponding to
#$OGCGeometryTypeFn.")
(comment OGCDistanceBetweenGeometriesFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'Distance' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCDistanceBetweenGeometriesFn GEOM1 GEOM2) returns the distance
from GEOM1 to GEOM2. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLDistanceBetweenGeometriesFn")
(comment CSQLDistanceBetweenGeometriesFn "#$CSQLDistanceBetweenGeometriesFn is the #$CSQLFunction
corresponding to #$OGCDistanceBetweenGeometriesFn.")
(comment OGCGeometryTypeFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'GeometryType' which is
defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.
(#$OGCGeometryTypeFn GEOM) returns the string name of the #$OpenGISGeometryType for GEOM. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLGeometryTypeFn")
(comment DisplayingFactSheetForConcept "The collection of all events in which some agent uses a running instantiation of a #$CycProgramModule-CW to display some of the content that a #$CycKB contains for a particular entity and that display takes the form of a 'fact sheet.'")
(comment cycLQueryAnswerBindingSet "An assertion of the form (#$cycLQueryAnswerBindingSet. QUERY BINDING-SET) states that the #$CycQueryRunning QUERY, an event, returned the #$ELInferenceBindingSet BINDING-SET as the set of bindings for the #$CycLQuerySpecification that was asked during QUERY.")
(disjointWith FictionalOrMythologicalContext NonfictionalContext)
(comment FunctionDenotingFunction "A specialization of #$IndividualDenotingFunction (q.v.) and the collection of functions that always return functions.  A function <b>FUNC</b> is an instance of #$FunctionDenotingFunction if and only if, for any (sequence of) argument(s) <b>ARGS</b> for which <b>FUNC</b> is defined, the value it yields -- i.e. <b>(FUNC . ARGS)</b> -- is an instance of #$Function-Denotational.
<p>
Instances of #$FunctionDenotingFunction include #$Lambda, #$FunctionToArg, and #$FunctionCompositionFn.  Thus (e.g.) (#$FunctionCompositionFn #$SuccessorFn #$SquaredFn) is the function that takes any integer and returns the square of its successor.
<p>
Specializations of #$FunctionDenotingFunction include #$MetricUnitPrefix.")
(comment WeatherNews "A collection of abstract (intangible) informational items; a specialization of #$News. A #$WeatherNews consists of information about recent, current or future #$WeatherEvents. Typically disseminated via TV or radio broadcasts, the internet, or newspapers.")
(comment associates "A specialization of #$affiliatedWith.  (#$associates AGT1 AGT2) holds just in case AGT1 and AGT2 have a certain sort of ongoing affiliation with each other.  In almost all cases, this is some sort of professional, organizational, or business relationship.  For instance, two individuals who are on the same committee in a particular charitiable organization may be considered associates if they interact sufficiently within the scope of their committee work.  Being members of the same organization is neither necessary nor sufficient for this relation to hold.")
(comment LinguisticObject-NoParseAvailable "A specialization of #$LinguisticObject.  Instances are objects that correspond to instances of #$TextString (either by being an actual text-string or by being a peg that stands for a contexualized text-string occurrence, as in a discourse model) for which it is known that there are no NL-to-CycL parsing methods that will produce a CycL interpretation.  As such, this is an instance of #$TimeDependentCollection.")
(comment ExpFn "A unary #$EvaluatableFunction that is the #$CycL version of the exponential operator. It takes instances of #$ComplexNumber and returns instances of #$ComplexNumber. (#$ExpFn NUM) is e^NUM, i.e. e raised to the power of NUM (where e is #$E-LogarithmBase).  When NUM is a #$RealNumber, (#$ExpFn NUM) is necessarily a #$PositiveNumber. In particular, (#$ExpFn 1) is e.  The inverse of this function (cf. #$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric
) is #$LogFn. Note that although the arg constraint for #$ExpFn is #$ComplexNumber, its #$evaluationDefn only evaluates to a value when NUM is a #$RealNumber. See also #$ExponentFn, a binary function which returns the result of raising a specified number to a specified exponent.")
(comment CSQLConcatenateStringsFn "#$CSQLConcatenateStringsFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$ConcatenateStringsFn.")
(comment CSQLStringUpcaseFn "#$CSQLStringUpcaseFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$StringUpcaseFn.")
(comment CSQLStringDowncaseFn "#$CSQLStringDowncaseFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$StringDowncaseFn.")
(comment CSQLSquareRootFn "#$CSQLSquareRootFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$SquareRootFn.")
(comment CSQLLogFn "#$CSQLLogFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$LogFn.")
(comment CSQLArcTangentFn "#$CSQLArcTangentFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$ArcTangentFn.")
(comment CSQLExpFn "#$CSQLExpFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$ExpFn.")
(comment CSQLTruncateFn "#$CSQLTruncateFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$TruncateFn.")
(comment CSQLArcSineFn "#$CSQLArcSineFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$ArcSineFn.")
(comment CSQLExponentFn "#$CSQLExponentFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$ExponentFn.")
(comment CSQLAbsoluteValueFn "#$CSQLAbsoluteValueFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$AbsoluteValueFn.")
(comment CSQLSquaredFn "#$CSQLSquaredFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$SquaredFn.")
(comment CSQLMinusFn "#$CSQLMinusFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$MinusFn.")
(comment CSQLTangentFn "#$CSQLTangentFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$TangentFn.")
(comment CSQLRoundUpFn "#$CSQLRoundUpFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$RoundUpFn.")
(comment CSQLRoundDownFn "#$CSQLRoundDownFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$RoundDownFn.")
(comment CSQLModuloFn "#$CSQLModuloFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$ModuloFn.")
(comment antisymmetricOn "A binary #$MetaPredicate that is used to state that a #$BinaryPredicate is antisymmetric on a given #$Collection.
<code>(#$antisymmetricOn PRED COL)</code> is true if and only if both (i) <code>(#$cosatisfiableInArgs PRED 1 2)</code> holds and (ii) </code>for any instances <code>INST1</code> and <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL</code>, if both <code>(PRED INS1 INS2)</code> and <code>(PRED INS2 INS1)</code> hold, then so does <code>(#$equals INS1 INS2)</code>.
<p>
For example, #$subsetOf, though not generally antisymmetric, is antisymmetric on the collection #$Set-Mathematical.  Trivially, any #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate 
is antisymmetric on #$Thing.
<p>
See also #$irreflexiveOn and #$asymmetricOn.")
(comment exteriorPointSetOfSpaceRegion "A binary predicate that relates a space region (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) with the set of points that are incident in its exterior. 
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$exteriorPointSetOfSpaceRegion REGION POINT-SET)</code> is true if an only if, for any instance <code>POINT</code> of #$Point, <code>(#$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> implies <code>(#$elementOf POINT POINT-SET)</code> and vice versa.  
<p>
See also #$pointSetOfSpaceRegion, #$interiorPointSetOfSpaceRegion and #$boundaryPointSetOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment InferenceParameterTypeByValueDomainType "A collection of collections that group #$InferenceParameters by the types of the domains of their values.")
(comment BooleanInferenceParameter "The specialization of #$InferenceParameters whose instances take #$TruthValue (boolean) values; i.e. #$True and #$False.")
(comment IntegerInferenceParameter "The specialization of #$InferenceParameters whose instances take #$Integer values.")
(comment RealNumberInferenceParameter "The specialization of #$InferenceParameters whose instances take #$RealNumber values.")
(comment EnumerationInferenceParameter "The specialization of #$InferenceParameters whose instances take values from a set of possible enumerated values.")
(comment softwareParameterValueDomainInSoftwareObject "(softwareParameterValueDomainInSoftwareObject SOFTWARE-PARAMETER SET-OR-COL SOFTWARE) means that the value of SOFTWARE-PARAMETER [see softwareParameterValue] is restricted to being an element/instance of the set/collection SET-OR-COL in SOFTWARE.")
(comment softwareParameterShortDescription "This predicate is used to present the users of various
Cyc interfaces with short descriptions of #$SoftwareParameters
that they can assign values to.")
(comment softwareParameterLongDescription "This predicate is used to present the users of various
Cyc interfaces with long descriptions of #$SoftwareParameters
that they can assign values to.")
(comment Ravine-Topographical "A specialization of #$Crevice-Topographical. Each instance of #$Ravine-Topographical is a deep, narrow, steep-walled passage, but shallower than a #$Gorge.")
(comment spatiallyDisjoint "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$spatiallyRelated (q.v.) that holds between  #$SpatialThings that do not spatially overlap.  <code>(#$spatiallyDisjoint THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> are in the same spatial universe (see #$UniversalSpaceRegion), but there is no space region that they both subsume (see #$spatiallySubsumes).  It follows that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> have no spatial #$parts in common.
<p>
For example, the United States and the Soviet Union were spatially disjoint, as were Plato and Aristotle, and as are the planets Saturn and Jupiter.
<p>
Cf. #$spatiallyIntersects.")
(comment cospatial "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$spatiallySubsumes (q.v.).  <code>(#$cospatial THING1 THING2)</code> means that the <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> have exactly the same spatial extent.  This implies that they have the same location, shape, and size.  But note that it does <i>not</i> imply that they are one and the same, or that they have any parts in common.  For we allow that distinct things (e.g. a tangible object and the pure space region it occupies) can spatially coincide without sharing any common parts.  #$SpaceRegions that are cospatial, however, are identical (see #$identicalSpaceRegions).")
(comment DividingTextIntoParagraphs "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SeparationEvent. In each #$DividingTextIntoParagraphs, someone divides text into paragraphs.")
(comment Quay "A specialization of #$Dock.  Each instance of #$Quay is a dock that is parallel to the bank of a waterway.")
(comment Reach-Topographical "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater. 
Each instance of #$Reach-Topographical is a long and straight section in
the course of a #$Stream.")
(comment Bend-Road "#$Bend-Road is a specialization of
#$TurnInPath.  Each instance of #$Bend-Road is a part of the #$Roadway that
curves in direction.")
(comment BeachRidge "A specialization of Ridge-Hill.  Each #$BeachRidge is found on a 
#$SandyBeach.")
(comment Roadstead "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each
instance of #$Roadstead  is a body of water where ships may ride at anchor,
but is not as enclosed as a #$Harbor.")
(comment NatureReserve "A specialization of #$Reserve-OutdoorRegion and
#$NaturalRegion; each instance of #$NatureReserve is intended for the
maintenance of a natural habitat.")
(comment WildlifeReserve "A
specialization of #$NatureReserve and #$WildernessArea; each instance of
#$WildlifeReserve is intended for wildlife preservation.")
(comment ForestReserve "A specialization
of #$NatureReserve and #$Forest, each instance of #$ForestReserve is
intended for forest preservation; it is often publicly managed.")
(comment HuntingReserve "A specialization of #$Reserve-OutdoorRegion, each instance of #$HuntingReserve is intended for hunting.")
(comment Rockfall "A specialization of
#$LandTopographicalFeature, #$Ground, and #$InanimateObject-Natural.  Each
instance of #$Rockfall is a pile of rocks or stony debris that has accumulated along a steep incline.")
(comment Rookery "A specialization of
#$NaturalRegion and #$Habitat.  Each instance of #$Rookery is a place used
for breeding by birds or seals.")
(comment Retreat-Place "A specialization of #$Place.  Each
instance of #$Retreat-Place is a place where people can go to escape from 
their typical daily activities for the sake of relaxation, meditation, or 
reflection.")
(comment LayeredObject "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible (q.v.).  This is the collection of tangible objects having the physical structure of being layered, laminate, or made of #$Layers.  For example, stratified rock and an onion are both layered object.  A #$LayeredObject has two or more parts that are each #$Layers.")
(comment CavityInteriorRegion "A specializaion of both #$SpaceChunk-Empirical and #$CavityInterior-Generic (qq.v.).  This is the collection of space regions that constitute cavities inside the walls or boundaries of solid objects.  Such regions need not be completely sealed off.  As a space region, an instance of  #$CavityInteriorRegion is intangible, and thus not to be confused with the partially tangible matter that might fill or occupy it (cf. #$CavityInteriorContent).  
<p>
See also #$Cavity, whose instances, unlike those of #$CavityInteriorRegion, can include walls (in which cases they are also instances of #$CavityWithWalls).  And see #$CavityInteriorRegionFn.")
(comment CavityInteriorRegionFn "A unary function that takes a tangible object to the sum of its interior spaces.  More precisely, <code>(#$CavityInteriorRegionFn OBJECT)</code> is the (possibly discontinuous) spatial sum of the interiors (see #$CavityInteriorRegion) of any #$Cavity[ies] (e.g. chambers, passages, pockets, bubbles, etc.) that occur inside of <code>OBJECT</code>.  Note that this does not include the walls of the cavities, just the interior space itself.")
(comment hijackingTypeByVehicleType "An assertion of the form (#$hijackingTypeByVehicleType EVENT-TYPE VEHICLE-TYPE) is used to relate EVENT-TYPE, some subcollection of #$Hijacking, to the type of #$TransportationDevice VEHICLE-TYPE that is hijacked in every instance of that collection.  More formally, in any instance of EVENT-TYPE, the individual in the role of #$transporterHijacked is an instance of VEHICLE-TYPE.")
(comment feelsTowardsObject-AssignedValueOnScaleWRTPurpose "(#$feelsTowardsObject-AssignedValueOnScaleWRTPurpose AGENT OBJECT NUMBER SCALE EVALUATION-TYPE) says that on a scale SCALE used in carrying out evalations of type EVALUATION-TYPE, where NUMBER is a point on SCALE, AGENT feels the feeling that corresponds to NUMBER towards OBJECT.  To assign a number to a feeling on SCALE, use #$assignedValueOfQuantityForEvaluationType.")
(comment SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory "A specialization of #$HypotheticalContext.  Instances are microtheories that contain the results of processing some piece of natural language.")
(comment EmptySetOrCollection "A #$SetOrCollectionTypeByCardinality for object types that have no instantiations.")
(comment learningTypeMethod "<code>(#$learningTypeMethod LEARNING-TYPE METHOD)</code> means that every instance of <code>LEARNING-TYPE</code> is a type of learning (see #$Learning) in which the information learned is arrived at through events of type <code>METHOD</code>.  <p>For example, <code>(#$learningTypeMethod #$LearningByReading #$Reading)</code> means that reading is the method by which one acquires information when engaged in a learning-by-reading event.")
(comment LearningTypeByMethod "An instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType.  Instances are specializations SPEC of #$Learning such that each instance INS of SPEC is such that there is a specialization ACTION-TYPE of #$Action that INS is achieved (learning takes place) primarily through the learner's performing sub-events of type ACTION-TYPE.")
(comment fileGeneratedByApplication "(#$fileGeneratedByApplication FILE APPLICATION) means that #$ComputerFile-CW FILE was created via the use of ComputerProgram-CW APPLICATION. For example, we could say (#$fileGeneratedByApplication #$MSWordDoc1001 #$MSWord). Note that this is not a specPred of #$createdBy, as APPLICATION does not perform an agentive role.")
(comment ComputerFileTypeByApplication "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$ComputerFileTypeByApplication is a collection of all and only those #$ComputerFile-CWs that have been generated by a given #$ComputerProgram-CW. See also #$fileGeneratedByApplication.")
(comment fileTypeGeneratedByApplication "A type-level version of #$fileGeneratedByApplication. (#$fileTypeGeneratedByApplication FILE-TYPE APPLICATION) means that all instances of FILE-TYPE are generated by APPLICATION, or (#$relationAllInstance #$fileGeneratedByApplication FILE-TYPE APPLICATION).")
(comment FileTypeGeneratedByApplicationFn "(#$FileTypeGeneratedByApplicationFn APPLICATION) denotes the #$ComputerFileTypeByApplication uniquely generated by APPLICATION. See also #$fileTypeGeneratedByApplication.")
(comment MSPowerPointDocument-CW "#$MSPowerPointDocument-CW is the collection of all #$ComputerFile-CWs generated by the #$MSPowerPoint application.")
(comment MSWordDocument-CW "#$MSWordDocument-CW is the collection of all #$ComputerFile-CWs generated by the #$MSWord application.")
(comment MSExcelDocument-CW "#$MSExcelDocument-CW is the collection of all #$ComputerFile-CWs generated by the #$MSExcel application.")
(comment AdobePhotoshopDocument-CW "#$AdobePhotoshopDocument-CW is the collection of all #$ComputerFile-CWs generated by the #$AdobePhotoshop-TheProgram application.")
(comment LearningUsingACombinationOfMethods "The collection of instances of #$Learning in which learning is achieved by applying two or more instances of #$LearningTypeByMethod.")
(comment relationOnlyInstance "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a binary predicate relates to a given thing only instances of a certain collection.  <code>(#$relationOnlyInstance PRED COL GIVENTHING)</code> means that, for any thing <code>THING</code>, <code>(PRED THING GIVENTHING)</code> holds only if <code>THING</code> is an instance of <code>COL</code>.  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (PRED ?X GIVENTHING)
    (#$isa ?X COL)) .
</pre>
Note the the above does <i>not</i> entail that that there <i>is</i> some instance <code>THING</code> of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(PRED THING GIVENTHING)</code> holds.
<p>
Compare with #$relationAllInstance and #$relationExistsInstance. See also the shared  #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(disjointWith ConcaveTangibleObject ConvexThing)
(comment ConcaveTangibleObject "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  This is the collection of tangible objects that are concave.  The surface of a concave object is \"indented\", i.e. contains points such that the straight line-segment between them is not subsumed by the object (see #$spatiallySubsumes).  
<p>
Cf. #$ConvexTangibleObject.")
(comment SheetOfSomeStuff "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$SheetOfSomeStuff is a partially tangible thing which (in its \"intrinsic\" shape -- see #$CurrentShapeVsIntrinsicShape) is continuous and, when flattened, uncrumpled, unfurled, etc., has one dimension which is significantly smaller than the other two dimensions.  Although each instance of #$SheetOfSomeStuff is a _continuous_ sheet, it may be penetrable, so #$Screen-Mesh, #$Netting, and #$LaceCloth are specializations of #$SheetOfSomeStuff.  Instances of #$SheetOfSomeStuff  may be in solid, liquid, or gaseous form.  An instance of #$Puddle on a relatively flat surface (but not in a deep pothole) or an instance of #$AltoStratusCloud would be instances of this collection.  Note that being an instance of #$SheetOfSomeStuff implies nothing about the current configuration of the instance; in particular, instances of #$SheetOfSomeStuff need not be perpetually lying flat.  For example, aluminum foil on a roll or a bedspread crumpled up on a bed are instances of #$SheetOfSomeStuff. Cf. #$SheetShapedObject.")
(comment Corner-TwoEdge "A specialization of #$CornerOnObject and #$LinearObject-Tangible (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Corner-TwoEdge is a corner of a sheet-like tangible object (see #$SheetOfSomeStuff and #$SheetShapedObject): a part of the object wherein two more-or-less straight linear edges meet to form an angle of substantially less than 180 degrees.  A polygonal-shaped object has at least three two-edge corners.
<p>
See also #$Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdge.")
(comment Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdge "A specialization of #$CornerOnObject (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdge is a corner of a #$SolidTangibleThing: a part of the object wherein three (or more) more-or-less straight linear edges (see #$EdgeOnObject) meet in such a way that any two of them form an angle of substantially less than 180 degrees.
<p>
Examples of #$Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdges include corners of the boxes and the tops of pyramids.
<p>
See also #$Corner-TwoEdge.")
(comment Crevice "A speicialization of #$CavityWithWalls and #$LongThinObject.  This is the collection of all long, slender cavities or cracks or furrows in otherwise solid objects.  The width of a crevice is significantly less than its length.  The depth of a crevice is often greater than its width, and is never significantly less than its width.")
(comment EntireExternalSurface-Tangible "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible and  #$SurfaceRegion-Finite.  This is the collection of all entire outer physical surfaces of tangible objects.  It this excludes mere patches or portions of the whole surface of an object.")
(disjointWith EntireExternalSurface-Tangible FreeSheet)
(comment FlatPhysicalSurface "A specialization of #$FlatSurfaceRegion and #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible.  This is the collection of all tangible surfaces that are substantially flat (by the tolerance standards of the the context).  This means that there are no \"significant\" concave depressions or convex bulges or bumps, and that the surface approximates some portion of a Euclidian plane in space.  Note that the surface may have minor holes or cracks.  A typical table top is a #$FlatPhysicalSurface.")
(comment InteriorSurface-Tangible "A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible.  This is the collection of all surfaces of the interiors of tangible things.  The tangible thing may have a #$Cavity or several cavities or passageways.  Often the tangible thing can be thought of as a container of some sort.")
(disjointWith InteriorSurface-Tangible FreeSheet)
(comment SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory "A specialization of #$HypotheticalContext.  Instances are microtheories that contain the assertions purporting to identify the correct interpretation of an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.")
(comment pegInterpretation-CycL "<code>(#$pegInterpretation-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> identifies <code>CYCL</code> as the correct CycL interpretation of the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.")
(comment semanticInterpretationHypothesisMtForPeg "<code>(#$semanticInterpretationHypothesisMtForPeg MT PEG)</code> identifies <code>PEG</code> as the instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg for which <code>MT</code> is a microtheory contains the hypothesis that some interpretation <code>I</code> is the intended, or correct semantic interpretation of <code>PEG</code>, stated formally as <code>(#$pegInterpretation-CycL PEG I)</code>.")
(comment TerrainConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$TerrainConcept are collections and relations having to do with soil and vegetation.  Instances of #$RegionTypeByTerrain, and relations used for describing the locations, make-up, soil, vegetation, associated climates, and other features of various types of  terrain are instances of this collection.")
(comment hypothesisMtConfidence "<code>(#$hypothesisMtConfidence MT CONFIDENCE)</code> means that the system's confidence in the truth of the content of the instance of #$HypotheticalContext <code>MT</code> is <code>CONFIDENCE</code>, an instance of #$Real0-1.")
(comment interpretationMtCorrespondsToHypothesis "<code>(#$interpretationMtCorrespondsToHypothesis INTERP-MT HYP-MT)</code> means that for some instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance, <code>INTERP-MT</code> is an interpretation context that contains a possible CycL representation of <code>PEG</code>, while <code>HYP-MT</code> is an instance of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that contains the hypothesis that the interpretation contained in <code>INTERP-MT</code> is the correct interpretation.
<p>More formally, for <code>PEG</code>, <code>(#$pegInterpretationMts PEG INTERP-MT)</code> and <code>(#$semanticInterpretationHypothesisMtForPeg HYP-MT PEG)</code> where there is an interpretation <code>CYCL</code> such that <code>(#$interpretationMtContent-CycL INTERP-MT CYCL)</code> and <code>(#$mtFocalContent HYP-MT (#$pegInterpretation-CycL PEG CYCL))</code>.")
(comment quantityMeasured "<code>(#$quantityMeasured MEASURING SLOT)</code> means that in <code>MEASURING</code>, an instance of #$DeterminingTheValueOfASlot, the (arg2) value of <code>SLOT</code> was determined for some #$evaluee-Direct.  For example, <code>(#$quantityMeasured MeasuringTheHeightOfTheEiffelTower-001 #$heightOfObject)</code> means that this particular event of measuring the height of the Eiffel Tower resulted in learning the value for <code>HEIGHT</code> in the sentence <code>(#$heightOfObject #$EiffelTower HEIGHT)</code>.")
(comment GeodesyConcept "Instances of #$GeodesyConcept are concepts which are included in the domain of #$Geodesy; therefore, #$Spheroid models of the Earth, map projections, and coordinate systems would be instances of this collection.")
(comment GeographicalRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalThing and #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible.  Each instance of #$GeographicalRegion is a tangible spatial region that includes some piece of the surface of a planet (usually #$PlanetEarth), and may be represented on a map of the planet.  This includes purely topographical regions like mountains and underwater spaces, places defined by demographics (e.g. language areas) and territory otherwise demarcated (e.g. #$TimeZones).  In \"dualist\" geopolitical contexts (see #$DualistGeopoliticalMt, instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity are also considered to be instances of #$GeographicalRegion.  
<p>
In all cases the region in question must contain some tangible component with which it is possible to make physical contact.  The instances of #$GeographicalRegion contrast in this respect with the instances of #$GeographicalThing-Intangible, which are wholly intangible.  
<p>
Examples of #$GeographicalRegion include #$RockyMountainStates-USRegion, the #$ContinentOfAustralia, #$SinaiPeninsula, and -- in \"dualist\" geopolitical contexts -- #$YaleUniversity and #$CityOfPittsburghPA.  Some important types of regions are represented by the sub-collections #$LanguageArea, #$TimeZone, #$PostalCodeRegion, #$EcologicalRegion, #$ConstructionSite, and -- in \"dualist\" geopolitical contexts -- #$GeopoliticalEntity.  No instances of #$GeographicalRegion are wholly indoor locations.")
(comment cavityHasWall "A specialization of #$physicalParts that relates a #$CavityWithWalls (q.v.) to its walls  <code>(#$cavityHasWall CAVITY WALL)</code> means that <code>CAVITY</code> has <code>WALL</code> as a wall (or part of a wall) or partly-enclosing inner surface.  
<p>
A common use of #$cavityHasWall is for describing the relationship between a part of a #$ConstructionArtifact and the substructures that bound or enclose it; e.g. the relationship between a room and its walls, floor, and ceiling.")
(comment containsPortals "A #$PhysicalPartPredicate and a specialization of #$hasAsCavity and #$parts.  <code>(#$containsPortals OBJECT PORTAL)</code> means that the <code>OBJECT</code> has<code> PORTAL</code> as one of its holes, passages, exits, or entrances.")
(comment hasPortalToRegion "A #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate that is a specialization of both #$near and #$temporallyCoexist.  <code>(#$hasPortalToRegion OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that <code>OBJ1</code> is connected to <code>OBJ2</code> by some type of #$Portal (q.v.).  For example, 
<code>(#$hasPortalToRegion MyMasterBedroom MyMasterBathroom)</code>.  See also 
#$portalConnectsRegions.")
(comment MtOfCWFn " (#$MtOfCWFn WORK) denotes the microtheory that houses the
contents of WORK, i.e., #$CycL translations of those contents.  It is
possible for assertions in other microtheories to correspond, by
happenstance, to some of the semantic content of WORK (e.g., if WORK
asserts widely-known facts).  But placement of ASSERTION in
(#$MtOfCWFn WORK) indicates that ASSERTION was derived from, and is
intended to translate, part of the content of WORK.")
(comment PCWInformationAboutFn "(#$PCWInformationAboutFn <b>PCW</b> <b>TOPIC</b>) denotes the
#$Microtheory whose contents are #$CycL expressions of all and only those propositions
in <b>PCW</b> that are focally 'about' <b>TOPIC</b>.  (For the sense of 'aboutness'
meant here, see the predicate #$containsInformationAbout-Focally).")
(comment pcwInformationAbout "(#$pcwInformationAbout <b>PCW</b> <b>TOPIC</b>
<b>ABOUT-MT</b>) means that the expressions contained in
<b>ABOUT-MT</b> capture the portion of the propositional content of
<b>PCW</b> that is focally 'about' <b>TOPIC</b> (in the sense of the
predicate #$containsInformationAbout-Focally, qv.).")
(comment CSQLRoundClosestFn "#$CSQLRoundClosestFn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$RoundClosestFn.")
(comment cavityInteriorRegionPortals "A specialization of #$touches.  <code>(#$cavityInteriorRegionPortals REGION HOLE)</code> means that <code>HOLE</code> is a #$Portal into the #$CavityInteriorRegion <code>REGION</code>.")
(comment surfaceFeatureOfSurface "A specialization of both #$surfaceFeatureOfObject and #$isa.  <code>(#$surfaceFeatureOfSurface SURFACE FEATURE)</code> means that the #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible <code>SURFACE</code> has the surface feature represented by the #$ObjectTypeBySurfaceFeature <code>FEATURE</code> over most or all of its external surface area.  Note that a given surface might have more than one such surface feature at the same time.")
(disjointWith RelationDenotingFunction ScalarDenotingFunction)
(disjointWith RelationDenotingFunction DerivedWordFormingFunction)
(comment RelationDenotingFunction "A specialization of #$IndividualDenotingFunction.  #$RelationDenotingFunction is the collection of all functions (instances of #$Function-Denoatational) that, when applied to appropriate arguments, return instances of #$Relation.  It includes all #$PredicateDenotingFunctions, e.g. #$Kappa, and also all #$FunctionDenotingFunctions, e.g. #$FunctionToArg.")
(comment resultArity "Where FN is a #$RelationDenotingFunction, (#$resultArity FN N) means that every #$Relation denoting CycL term that is generated with the CycL term denoting FN in the arg 0 position denotes a #$Relation with #$arity N.  For example, (#$resultArity #$OfDateFn 2) means that every CycL predicate reified via a term that uses #$OfDateFn as the main operator, e.g. (#$OfDateFn #$CalendarDay), has arity 2.")
(comment EvaluationTypeByEvalueeType "The collection of all specializations of #$Evaluating that share a common type of evaluee (see #$evaluee-Direct).")
(comment evaluationTypeDirectEvalueeType "<code>(#$evaluationTypeDirectEvalueeType EVALUATION-TYPE TYPE)</code> identifies <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code> as the collection of all specializations of #$Evaluating for which some instance of the collection <code>TYPE</code> is the evaluee (in the sense of #$evaluee-Direct).  As such, <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code> is an instance of #$EvaluationTypeByEvalueeType, the collection of all types of evaluating that share a common evaluee.")
(comment RemoveHTMLTagsFn "<code>(#$RemoveHTMLTagsFn HTML-STRING)</code> evaluates to a version of <code>HTML-STRING</code> with all of its tags removed.")
(comment SoftwareParameterValueObject "A collection whose instances can be used as values for
#$SoftwareParameters in instances of #$SoftwareObject.")
(comment softwareParameterValueShortDescription "This predicate is used to present the users of various
Cyc interfaces with short descriptions of values
that they can assign to certain #$SoftwareParameters.")
(comment softwareParameterValueLongDescription "This predicate is used to present the users of various
Cyc interfaces with long descriptions of values
that they can assign to certain #$SoftwareParameters.")
(comment alternateSoftwareParameterValueInSoftwareObject "(alternateSoftwareParameterValueInSoftwareObject SOFTWARE-PARAMETER SOFTWARE-PARAMETER-VALUE SOFTWARE) means that in addition to the usual domain of values that SOFTWARE-PARAMETER can normally take in SOFTWARE (see #$softwareParameterValueDomainInSoftwareObject) it can also take SOFTWARE-PARAMETER as an alternate value.")
(comment substring-CaseInsensitive "<code>(#$substring-CaseInsensitive SUBSTRING STRING)</code> means that the <code>#$CharacterString SUBSTRING</code> is a substring of the <code>#$CharacterString STRING</code> with case ignored in both <code>SUBSTRING</code> and <code>STRING</code>. For case-sensitive tagging use the more specialized predicate, <code>#$substring</code>.")
(comment substring "A binary predicate that relates an instance of #$CharacterString to another intance of #$CharacterString. <code>(#$substring SUBSTRING STRING)</code> means that <code>SUBSTRING</code> is a substring of <code>STRING</code>.")
(comment interArgResultReln "A #$MetaRelation predicate used for stating that one of the arguments to a given function always stands in a specified #$Relation to the corresponding result returned by the function.  <code>(#$interArgResultReln FUNC N RELN)</code> means that, for any admitted sequence of arguments <code>ARGS</code> to <code>FUNC</code>, the <code>N</code>th item <code>ARG<sub>N</sub></code> of <code>ARGS</code> stands in <code>RELN</code> to the result of applying <code>FUNC</code> to <code>ARGS</code>.  Stated schematically: if <code>(FUNC . ARGS) = RESULT</code>, then <code>(RELN ARG<sub>N</sub> RESULT)</code> holds.  For example,
<pre>
  (#$interArgResultReln #$SubPathInsideRegionFn 1 #$subPaths) 
</pre>
means that the result of applying #$SubPathInsideRegionFn to a given <code>PATH</code> and <code>REGION</code> is itself a sub-path of <code>PATH</code>.  
<p>
See also #$interResultArgReln.")
(comment interResultArgReln "A #$MetaRelation predicate used for stating that   the result returned by a given function always stands in a specified #$Relation to a certain one of its arguments. <code>(#$interResultArgReln FUNC N RELN)</code> means that the result of applying <code>FUNC</code> to any admitted sequence of arguments <code>ARGS</code> stands in <code>RELN</code> to the <code>N</code>th item (<code>ARG<sub>N</sub></code>) of <code>ARGS</code>.  Stated schematically: if <code>(FUNC . ARGS) = RESULT</code>, then <code>(RELN RESULT ARG<sub>N</sub>)</code> holds.  For example,
<pre>
  (#$interResultArgReln #$SubPathInsideRegionFn 2 #$pathInsideRegion) 
</pre>
means that the result of applying #$SubPathInsideRegionFn to a given <code>PATH</code> and <code>REGION</code> is itself a path that is wholly inside of <code>REGION</code>.  
<p>
See also #$interArgResultReln.")
(comment inRangeOf "A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates instances of #$Relation to things in their ranges.  (#$inRangeOf ELEMENT RELATION) means that ELEMENT is an element of the #$relationRange (q.v.) of RELATION.  That is, RELATION relates at least one thing in its domain (see #$relationDomain and #$inDomainOf) to ELEMENT.  If the #$relationalArity (q.v.) of RELATION is N (for N > 1), then ELEMENT is the Nth item of an N-length #$List that is an element of the extension (see #$relationExtension) of RELATION.  (Note that if the #$relationalArity of RELATION is 1 then it has no range, and #$inRangeOf is undefined for such a RELATION.)  If RELATION is a #$VariableArityRelation (q.v.)
there will be no particular length N shared by each of the lists in its extension; but ELEMENT will still be the last item of some list in that extension.
<p>
Though this predicate can be applied to polyadic relations generally, asserting that something is in RELATION's range will normally be of particular interest only when RELATION is a function (i.e. an instance of #$Function-Denotational), in
which case RELATION associates each element of its domain with exactly one element of its range.  See also #$RangeOfFunctionFn.")
(comment FullySpecifiedEvaluationProcedure "The collection of all specializations of #$Evaluating that have been specified in the knowledge base at a level of detail such that Cyc can perform instances of that evaulation-type through inference.")
(comment TextLearnerHypothesisEvaluationProcedure "A specialization of #$FullySpecifiedEvaluationProcedure, #$TextLearnerHypothesisEvaluationProcedure is the collection of specializations of #$Evaluating that the TextLearner program (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) can use to judge the the hypotheses it forms while trying to read text.")
(disjointWith DeterminingAMedian DeterminingAMode)
(comment ApplyingANumericQuantifier "A specializaton of #$DoingMath and #$SolvingAProblem.  Instances are events in which one determines the numeric value of a function as applied to the elements of a set.  See also #$appliedNumericQuantifier.")
(comment techniqueAppliesNumericQuantifier "<code>(#$techniqueAppliesNumericQuantifier TECHNIQUE QUANTIFIER)</code> means that the instance <code>TECHNIQUE</code> of #$TechniqueType is a collection of applications of <code>QUANTIFIER</code>, an instance of #$NumericQuantifier.  For example, <code>(#$techniqueAppliesNumericQuantifier #$DeterminingAMean #$Average)</code> means that #$DeterminingAMean is a problem-solving technique, instances of which are applications of the #$Average function against some set and function.")
(comment appliedNumericQuantifier "An instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate. <code>(#$appliedNumericQuantifier APPLICATION QUANTIFIER)</code> means that the instance <code>APPLICATION</code> of #$ApplyingANumericQuantifier is an application of the instance <code>QUANTIFIER</code> of #$NumericQuantifier.  For example, <code>(#$appliedNumericQuantifier DeterminingTheNationwideMedianTestScoreForThe2005SATs #$Median)</code> means that the determination of the nationwide median test score for the 2005 SATs is an event in which the #$Median function is applied against a set and function.")
(comment SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheoryValidatedByWebSearch "The collection of instances of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that have been validated by generating search or query strings from the hypothesized content, and submitting them against the web.")
(comment queriesWithTermHaveDefaultTimeCutoff "(#$queriesWithTermHaveDefaultTimeCutoff TERM TIME) means that queries in which TERM is a constituent should have a minimum default time cutoff of TIME.")
(comment SocialGatheringAtResidence "A subcollection of #$HostedSocialGathering, each instance
#$SocialGatheringAtResidence takes place at someone's residence, be it
a house, apartment, dorm room, etc.  It is common in many cultures to
host certain types of social gatherings at one's residence.  Examples
include both certain types of #$Party-Celebration (e.g.,
#$DinnerParty, #$BirthdayParty), and more informal get-togethers for
conversation, #$PlayingACardGame, etc.  In many cultures there are
specific social expectations regarding behavior of hosts and guests at
such occasions.  The #$hostOfEvent for such a gathering usually,
though not always, lives at the residence.  By contrast, a resident
will rarely if ever be a guest at such a gathering (see
#$guestAtSocialGathering).")
(comment guestAtSocialGathering "(#$guestAtSocialGathering GATHERING INDIV) means that INDIV
is a guest at GATHERING.  Guests are #$attendees (typically, invited)
to whom some form of hospitality or entertainment is being offered,
and who have little or no responsibility for administering
GATHERING.  (#$guestAtSocialGathering GATHERING INDIV) thus entails
that INDIV is neither the #$hostOfEvent, nor there primarily to
provide help -- e.g., hired entertainers or
cooks. (#$guestAtSocialGathering GATHERING INDIV) entails that
GATHERING has a (distinct) #$hostOfEvent.")
(comment DeterminingAnArithmeticMean "A specialization of #$DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues.  One determines a mean by adding up N values and dividing the sum by N.")
(comment DeterminingAMode "A specialization of #$DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues.  One determines a mode by finding the most frequently occurring value in a group of values.")
(comment DeterminingAMedian "A specialization of #$DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues.  One determines a median by finding the middle value between the highest and lowest values of an ordered set of values.")
(comment DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem, #$DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues is the collection of all activities that, using some method or other (e.g, determining a median) that allows one to determine a central tendency for some group of values, frequently, though imprecisely described as determining an \"average.\"  Notable specializations of this collection are instances of #$TechniqueType, such as #$DeterminingAnArithmeticMean, #$DeterminingAMode, and #$DeterminingAMedian.  These specializations are disjoint with one another, not because one cannot arrive at the same answer using each method (i.e., one could accidently \"determine\" the mean by determining the mode -- where the values in question are 5, 5, 4, 6, the mode, mean and median are identical), but because these collections are defined procedurally.")
(disjointWith DeterminingAMode DeterminingAMean)
(disjointWith DeterminingAMedian DeterminingAMean)
(disjointWith DeterminingAnArithmeticMean DeterminingAGeometricMean)
(comment DeterminingAGeometricMean "An instance of #$TechniqueType and specialization of #$DeterminingAMean.  Typically used in figuring rates of change, #$DeterminingAGeometricMean is the process of calculating the <i>n</i>th root of the <i>pi</i> product of each <i>i</i> of <i>n</i> values.")
(comment DeterminingAMean "A specialization of #$DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues.  Instances are any of various problem-solving activities that are conventionally described as determining a \"mean.\"  Notable specializations include #$DeterminingAnArithmeticMean (often described simply as determining a \"mean\") and #$DeterminingAGeometricMean.")
(comment interpretationMtForContentModel "(#$interpretationMtForContentModel INTERP-MT MODEL-MT) means that INTERP-MT, an instance of #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory, is a microtheory that contains an interpretation of the utterance modeled by MODEL-MT, an instance of #$ContentModelMicrotheory.")
(comment contentModelForString "(#$contentModelForString MODEL-MT STRING) means that STRING is the text which is modeled by MODEL-MT, an instance of #$ContentModelMicrotheory.")
(comment Replying-Noncommittal "A specialization of #$Replying.  In each instance of
#$Replying-Noncommittal, the #$senderOfInfo replies to some prior
communication (PRIOR-COMM), but does not express a firm commitment
concerning its content.  The type of commitment that is withheld
depends on the
#$IllocutionaryForce of PRIOR-COMM.  For example, when PRIOR-COMM is an
instance of #$Requesting-CommunicationAct, replies which are
instances of #$Replying-Noncommittal neither agree to nor
 reject the request.  If PRIOR-COMM is an instance of
#$Offering-CommunicationAct, a #$Replying-Noncommittal response neither expressly
accepts nor declines the offer (cf., #$Accepting-CommunicationAct,
#$Declining-CommunicationAct).")
(comment EndingOfTemporalThing "A #$TemporalObjectType and specialization of #$Event.  Each instance of #$EndingOfTemporalThing is an event in which some #$TemporallyExistingThing comes to a (permanent) end, i.e. ceases to temporally subsume the present moment (and does not temporally subsume any future moment).  (We use the vague \"moment\" instead of the precise #$TimePoint, as we do not want to rule out the possibility that a #$TemporallyExistingThing may <i>gradually</i> come to an end.)
<p>
Instances of #$EndingOfTemporalThing include the ending of #$WorldWarII, the death of #$Socrates, and #$TheTwentiethCenturyCE's coming to an end. 
<p>
Specializations of this collection can be specified compositionally using #$EndingFn.  A notable specialization, in which the thing coming to an end is an instance of #$SomethingExisting, is #$DestructionEvent.")
(comment folderContainsTemplate "A specialization of #$folderContainsResource. (#$folderContainsTemplate FOLDER TEMPLATE) means that FOLDER, an instance of #$KEInteractionFolder, has TEMPLATE, an instance of #$FormulaTemplate, as part of its contents.")
(comment subFolders "A specialization of #$folderContainsResource. (#$subFolders SUPER SUB) means that SUPER, an instance of #$KEInteractionFolder, has SUB, a different instance of #$KEInteractionFolder, as part of its contents.")
(comment HumanResidenceHunting "Instances are complicated actions in which the performer looks for a house/condo/apt that he/she wishes to either rent or buy.")
(comment OfferingSomethingToDrink "A specialization of #$Offering-CommunicationAct; each instance
of #$OfferingSomethingToDrink entails one person offering a #$Drink
to another person.")
(comment ClosedIntersectionCollectionType "#$ClosedIntersectionCollectionType is a collection of
collection types which all share the following property: Whenever
COLLTYPE is an instance of this collection, and COLL1 and COLL2 are
instances of COLLTYPE, then the intersection of COLL1 and COLL2 is
also an instance of COLLTYPE. For example, #$SpatialThingTypeByShape is an instance
of this collection. #$SomewhatRoundObject and #$Cube are both instances of #$SpatialThingTypeByShape,
so (#$CollectionIntersectionFn (#$TheSet #$SomewhatRoundObject #$Cube)), the
collection of somewhat round cubes, is also an instance of  #$SpatialThingTypeByShape.
<p>
Note that it is
possible that this intersection may be empty. #$Square and #$Circle
are both instances of #$SpatialThingTypeByShape, so is (#$CollectionIntersectionFn (#$TheSet
#$Square #$Circle)), even though this collection is necessarily
empty.")
(comment GraphicalCIS "The intersection of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure and #$GraphicalStructure.  Instances are graphical structures that are individuated, not only by their internal structure (an instance of #$GraphicalAIS) but by their relationships to other parts of a larger abstract structure.")
(comment interpretationMtFromParser "<code>(#$interpretationMtFromParser MT PARSER)</code> identifies <code>PARSER</code> as the NL-to-CycL parser that is the source of the CycL contained in <code>MT</code>, an instance of  #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheoryWithParsedContent.")
(comment TextStringOccurrence "Instances of this collection are specific passages  within some larger textual structure (an instance of #$GraphicalStructure) such as a document (c.f., #$NLDocument), paragraph (c.f., #$NLParagraph), or sentence (c.f., #$NLSentence).  #$TextStringOccurrence is a specialization of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure; it's instances are thus individuated not only in terms of their internal information structure (e.g.. in terms of #$TextStrings) but also by their relative position within the larger structure. So, for example, if some document contains several occurances of the string \"green eggs and ham,\" each is a distinct instance of #$TextStringOccurrence (one for each occurance) that instantiates that string (in the sense of instantiation represented by #$cisContainsAIS).")
(comment FormerFn "A #$UnaryFunction that takes a specialization COL of #$SomethingExisting and returns the collection of instances of #$SomethingExisting that are former instances of COL -- i.e., things that used to be, but no longer are, instances of COL.  For example, (#$FormerFn #$Teacher) is the collection of former teachers.")
(comment NationalParkService "The National Park Service is an agency of the U.S. Department 
of Interior, which manages U.S. #$NationalParks.")
(comment USArmyCorpOfEngineers "The US Army Corp of Engineers is a military organization subordinate
to the #$UnitedStatesArmy.  Most members, however, are civilian 
engineers who work on civilian project throughout the United States and 
the world.")
(comment BureauOfLandManagement "The Bureau of Land Management is an agency of the U.S. Department 
of Interior, which manages public lands in the Western part of the 
United States.")
(comment NationalMonument-NPS "A specialization of #$NationalMonument; each instance of 
#$NationalMonument-NPS is a monument of historical, natural, or 
scientific importance located in the United States
and managed by the #$NationalParkService.")
(comment NationalPark-NPS "A specialization of #$NationalPark; each instance of 
#$NationalPark-NPS is located in the United States
and managed by the #$NationalParkService.")
(comment ScenicRoadway "A specialization of #$Roadway.  Each instance of #$ScenicRoadway 
typically runs parallel to a #$WildernessPark.   People usually take the
#$ScenicRoadway for recreational driving.")
(comment NationalParkway-NPS "A specialization of #$ScenicRoadway.  Each instance of 
#$NationalParkway-NPS is found in the United States and managed by 
the NationalParkService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver "A specialization of #$NationalPark.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver is a protected natural 
geographical area that includes the segment of a river and 
the land along side it.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-ACOE-USFS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-ACOE-USFS is found in the United States
and jointly managed by the #$USArmyCorpOfEngineers and the 
#$UnitedStatesForestService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM is found in the United States
and managed by the #$BureauOfLandManagement.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-NPS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-NPS is found in the United States
and managed jointly by the #$BureauOfLandManagement and the #$NationalParkService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-NPS-USFS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-NPS-USFS is found in the United States
and managed jointly by the #$BureauOfLandManagement, the #$UnitedStatesForestService, and the #$NationalParkService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-USFS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-USFS is found in the United States
and managed jointly by the #$BureauOfLandManagement and the #$UnitedStatesForestService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-USFWS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-BLM-USFWS is found in the United States
and managed jointly by the #$BureauOfLandManagement and the #$USFishAndWildlifeService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-NPS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-NPS is found in the United States
and managed by the #$NationalParkService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-NPS-USFS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-NPS-USFS is found in the United States
and managed jointly by the #$NationalParkService and the #$UnitedStatesForestService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-NPS-USFWS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-NPS-USFWS is found in the United States
and managed jointly by the #$NationalParkService and the #$USFishAndWildlifeService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-USFS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-USFS is found in the United States
and managed by the #$UnitedStatesForestService.")
(comment NationalWildAndScenicRiver-USFWS "A specialization of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver.  Each instance 
of #$NationalWildAndScenicRiver-USFWS is found in the United States
and managed by the #$USFishAndWildlifeService.")
(comment Circle "A specialization of both #$Ellipse and #$RadiallySymmetricObject (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Circle is a closed, one-dimensional curve that is the path of a point that moves so that its distance from another point -- the circle's <i>center</i> -- is constant.  
<p>
See also #$CircularRegion, each instance of which is a two-dimensional region whose boundary is a #$Circle.")
(comment on-Physical "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate (q.v.) that holds between certain tangible objects. <code>(#$on-Physical OVER UNDER)</code> means that <code>OVER</code> is above, touching, and supported by <code>UNDER</code> (see #$above-Touching and #$supportedBy).  <code>OVER</code> and <code>UNDER</code> might be at rest or in motion; one might even be in motion and the other at rest.  For example, this predicate holds of a statue on a pedestal, a person on a bicycle, and an ice skater on a sheet of ice.")
(comment onLine "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that holds between a #$SpatialThing and certain lines (see #$LinearObject) it intersects with or touches.  <code>(#$onLine OBJ LINE)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is an object or region that touches (see #$touches-Directly) <code>LINE</code> but does not subsume it, and at least one of <code>OBJ</code>'s axes (see #$objectAxes) is much shorter than the length of the <code>LINE</code>.
<p>
See also #$onPath.")
(comment RowOfObjectsFn "(#$RowOfObjectsFn TYPE) denotes the collection of all rows of objects of TYPE.")
(comment suspendedIn "A binary #$SpatialPredicate.  <code>(#$suspendedIn OBJ FLUID)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is fully immersed in <code>FLUID</code> (see #$in-ImmersedFully), and <code>FLUID</code> provides (via fluid dynamics) a significant supporting or upward force on <code>OBJ</code> so that OBJ does not undergo rapid falling.")
(comment touches "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$near that holds between certain tangible objects.  <code>(#$touches OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> are in contact, either directly or indirectly.  Two spatial things are directly in contact just in case there is nothing between them; to indicate that such a situation obtains between two spatial things, use the specialization #$touches-Directly.  Two spatial things touch indirectly if there is only a very thin object or substance between them.  Here 'very thin' means that the distance between the surfaces of the two spatial things is very much less than the distance between their center points.  
<p>
Here are some examples in which #$touches holds: an airplane touches the air around it; my feet touch the floor (even though I am wearing shoes); a blanket touches the person sleeping under it (even if there is a sheet); a hovercraft touches the water (even though a cushion of air is between it and the water).")
(comment touches-Directly "A specialization of #$touches (q.v.).  <code>(#$touches-Directly REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are #$SpatialThings that are in direct spatial contact.  That is, there is a point or region in <code>REGION1</code> and a point or region in <code>REGION2</code> such that the distance between those points/regions is zero.  
<p>
Note that either one of <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> might or might not be #$PartiallyTangible.
<p>
Note also that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> might or might not be #$spatiallyDisjoint (q.v.).  For the case where they are disjoint, see the specialization #$touches-Externally; for the case where they're not disjoint, see 
the specialization #$spatiallyIntersects.")
(comment trajectoryPassesThrough "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$nonDeliberateActors.  <code>(#$trajectoryPassesThrough MOVE LOC)</code> means that the trajectory (see #$trajectory-Complete) of the object moving (see #$objectMoving) <code>OBJ</code> in the #$Translation-SingleTrajectory <code>MOVE</code> passes through the location (see #$EnduringThing-Localized) <code>LOC</code>.  Here \"passes through <code>LOC</code>\" implies that, in the course of moving from its #$fromLocation to its #$toLocation, <code>OBJ</code> first arrives in and then leaves <code>LOC</code>.  
<p>
Note that <code>LOC</code> is <i>not</i> constrained to be #$spatiallyDisjoint with this from-location or to-location; <code>LOC</code> might even subsume (see #$spatiallySubsumes) either of them.  Thus, if (say) <code>LOC</code> subsumes the from-location, <code>OBJ</code> must first leave <code>LOC</code>, then arrive back in <code>LOC</code>, and then leave <code>LOC</code> again in order to pass through it.  
<p>
Also note that <code>LOC</code> must be #$Stationary during <code>MOVE</code>, relative to the associated #$FrameOfReference.")
(comment traversalPassesThrough "A specialization of #$pointOnTraversal.  <code>(#$traversalPassesThrough TRAV POINT)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is a point that the #$Traversal (q.v.) <code>TRAV</code> <i>passes through</i>, which requires that <code>TRAV</code> must first enter and then leave <code>POINT</code>.  Thus, <code>TRAV</code> does <i>not</i> pass through its starting-point (or ending-point) unless it happens to meet that point twice.")
(comment subObligations "A #$NonPhysicalPartPredicate that is a specialization of #$normativeSpecificationParts.  (#$subObligations OBLIG SUBOBLIG) means that the #$Obligation (q.v.) SUBOBLIG is a part of the wider #$Obligation OBLIG. SUBOBLIG might be (i) a part of what all of the #$obligatedAgents to OBLIG are obligated to, (ii) the whole of what some of the obligated agents to OBLIG are obligated to, or (iii) some combination of both of the foregoing cases.
<p>
For example, (#$subObligations #$TheTenCommandments #$TheFirstCommandment) holds.  And a given state's body of laws pertaining to minors is a subobligation of the state's entire penal code.
<p>
Cf. #$subAgreements.  Note that, although not all #$Obligations are #$Agreements, if (#$subObligations OBLIG SUBOBLIG) holds and both OBLIG and SUBOBLIG are agreements, then (#$subAgreements OBLIG SUBOBLIG) holds.")
(comment subAgreements "A #$NonPhysicalPartPredicate that is a specialization of #$normativeSpecificationParts.  (#$subAgreements AGREE SUBAGREE) means that the #$Agreement (q.v.) SUBAGREE is a part of the larger #$Agreement AGREE.  SUBAGREE might be (i) a part of what all of the #$agreeingAgents to AGREE agree to, (ii) the whole of what some of the agreeing agents to AGREE agree to, or (iii) some combination of both of the foregoing cases.
<p>
For example, (#$subAgreements #$LawOfTheSea-UnitedNationsConvention #$MineralRightsClause-UNLawOfTheSea) holds.  And the agreement that I will not attack you first is one of the #$subAgreements of our mutual truce.
<p>
Cf. #$subObligations.  Note that, although not all #$Agreements are #$Obligations (q.v.), if (#$subAgreements AGREE SUBAGREE) holds and both AGREE and SUBAGREE are obligations, then (#$subObligations AGREE SUBAGREE) holds as well.")
(comment newDiscourseReferentInUDS "(#$newDiscourseReferentInUDS UDS PEG) means that PEG is a discourse
entity introduced in the discourse by the utterance to which UDS
corresponds.")
(comment unitStructureOfTokenization "(#$unitStructureOfTokenization T UDS) means that UDS is an
instance of #$UnitDiscourseStructure corresponding to a parse tree
generated from the tokenization T of some particular utterance in
discourse. This relation is one-to-many.")
(comment nodeCoveredByPeg "<code>(#$nodeCoveredByPeg NODE EXPRESSION-PEG)</code> means that the instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg <code>EXPRESSION-PEG</code> has is an occurrence of an instance <code>AIS</code> of #$GraphicalAIS (typically an instance of #$TextString) that corresponds to a syntactic node <code>NODE</code> in a parse tree for a structure of which <code>AIS</code> is a (possibly non-proper) part.")
(comment candidateConditionOnReferentExpression "A predicate to characterize salient features of a referential expression used in a discourse context. (#$candidateConditionOnReferentExpression EXPRESSION-PEG EXPRESSION-TYPE) is true iff EXPRESSION-TYPE is a feature of EXPRESSION-PEG in the local context. 

Note that 'candidate conditions' in the sense of this predicate can be incompatible syntactic attributes. That is why #$candidateConditionOnDiscourseReferent is not a specialization of #$localConditionOnReferentExpression (q.v.). For a similar predicate applicable to #$DiscourseReferentPegs, see #$candidateConditionOnDiscourseReferent.")
(comment localConditionOnReferentExpression "A predicate to characterize the features (cf. #$LinguisticExpressionType) of the referential expressions in a discourse. (#$localConditionOnReferentExpression EXPRESSION-PEG EXPRESSION-TYPE) means that (#$isa EXPRESSION-PEG EXPRESSION-TYPE) is a provisional constraint on the referential expression EXPRESSION-PEG that we can extract from the current discourse state. Properties constraining a discourse-referent expression can be either agreement features expressed as #$LinguisticExpressionTypes. The properties of the <i>referents</i> themselves, i.e., the collections of which they are instances, can be expressed with #$localConditionOnDiscourseReferent (q.v.).")
(comment PresupposedDiscourseReferentFn "This function denotes the implicit discourse entity presupposed
by a definite NP discourse referent. (#$PresupposedDiscourseReferentFn PEG
UDS) is the entity presupposed by PEG, and UDS is the discourse structure
to which it is assigned (i.e., in which it is created). Example: bridging
anaphora")
(comment viableBridgingCriterionForReferents "(#$viableBridgingCriterionForReferents PEG ANAPHOR PRED) means
that the conditions of familiarity and uniqueness presuppositions required
for picking the coreferent of ANAPHOR are satisfied by PEG with respect to
(some specialization of) the relation PRED. For example
(viableBridgingCriterionForReferents PEG1 
PEG2 #$parts) when it is known in the discourse that (isa PEG1 Automobile)
and (isa PEG2 SteeringWheel).")
(comment NthUtteranceInDialogueFn "(#$NthUtteranceInDialogueFn DIALOG N) designates the N-th
utterance in the instance DIALOG of #$CycInterlocutorDialogue.")
(comment CycInterlocutorDialogue "Instances of this collection are the instances of #$Conversation
that take place in a session between a user and the #$CycInterlocutor. An
instance of #$CycInterlocutorDialogue consists of a series of
#$CycInterlocutorUtterances.")
(comment userSelectedCoreferentInUDS "(#$userSelectedCoreferentInUDS UDS PEG1 PEG2) is true if PEG2 has been explicitly selected by the #$CycInterlocutor user as a coreference target for PEG1. This is a special case of #$viableProposedCoreferentInUDS (q.v.), which implies that user-selected coreferents must not violate semantic or syntactic constraints on anaphoric reference, as applicable to the more general predicate.")
(comment userExcludedCoreferentInUDS "(#$userExcludedCoreferentInUDS UDS PEG1 PEG2) is true if PEG1
is an anaphoric discourse entity in UDS and PEG2 has been explicitly ruled out by the #$CycInterlocutor user as a
coreference candidate for PEG2. This is a special case of #$excludedCoreferentInUDS. Note that (#$userExcludedCoreferentInUDS UDS PEG1 PEG2) does not imply that PEG2 violates semantic or positional (accessibility) constraints.")
(comment candidateConditionOnDiscourseReferent "A predicate to capture those properties of a discourse entity that can be evinced directly from the local discourse context in which it is first introduced. (#$candidateConditionOnDiscourseReferent PEG COND) is true iff (#$isa PEG COND) is a constraint on PEG entailed by at least one of the #$proposedMeaning of PEG. 

Because they depend on #$proposedMeaning, 'candidate conditions' in the sense of this predicate can be inconsistent (e.g., disjoint collections, incompatible attributes, etc.). That is why #$candidateConditionOnDiscourseReferent is not a specialization of #$localConditionOnDiscourseReferent (q.v.). For a similar predicate applicable to #$LinguisticExpressionPegs, see #$candidateConditionOnReferentExpression.")
(comment localConditionOnDiscourseReferent "A predicate to characterize those properties of a discourse entity that can be evinced directly from the local discourse context. (#$localConditionOnDiscourseReferent PEG COND) means that (#$isa PEG COND) is a provisional constraint on PEG that we can extract from the current discourse state. Properties constraining a discourse referent can be  either the collection (which are the semantic values of nouns, in general) or  agreement features of the discourse expression itself, represented as #$LinguisticExpressionTypes (for which, however, see #$localConditionOnReferentExpression). Such properties can be used to determine appropriate coreferent targets even when the full semantics of a node is not yet available.")
(comment excludedCoreferentInUDS "(#$excludedCoreferentInUDS UDS PEG1 PEG2) is true if PEG1

is an anaphoric discourse entity in UDS and PEG2 has been ruled out as a

coreference candidate for PEG2. This could mean that PEG2 is not accessible

from UDS, or that it violates applicable syntactic or semantic

constraints.")
(comment viableProposedCoreferentInUDS "(#$viableProposedCoreferentInUDS UDS PEG1 PEG2) means that PEG2 is a
possible antecedent for PEG1 in the discourse structure UDS: more
precisely, (i) PEG1 is a discourse anaphor in UDS (cf. #$anaphoricDiscourseReferentInUDS), (ii) PEG2 is an available referent marker in UDS 
(cf. #$availableCoreferentInUDS), and (iii) PEG2 has not been
excluded by other semantic or syntactic constraints.")
(comment UDSForParseTreeFn "(UDSForParseTreeFn TREE). denotes the unit discourse
structure associated with the #$CycParseTree TREE.")
(comment unitStructureOfParseTree "(#$unitStructureOfParseTree TREE UDS) means that UDS is the
instance of #$UnitDiscourseStructure corresponding to the particular
#$CycParseTree TREE. This doesn't imply that UDS is the only
discourse structure generated in the process of constructing the parse tree
for a given utterance: UDS could have additional embedded
sub-substructures, e.g. if the utterance is (interpreted as) a
conditional.")
(comment accessibleDiscourseReferentInUDS "(#$accessibleDiscourseReferentInUDS UDS1 PEG) is true iff there
exists another discourse structure UDS2 such that PEG is an
#$availableCoreferentInUDS in UDS2 and
 (#$subordinateTo-DiscourseStructure UDS1 UDS2). In other words, the
discourse entities that are candidate coreference targets in a given 
unit discourse structure are accessible from any of its subordinate 
structures (except itself).")
(comment availableCoreferentInUDS "(#$availableCoreferentInUDS UDS PEG) is true if PEG is a discourse entity that is a candidate co-reference target for anaphors in UDS. In particular, this means that PEG is either new (in the sense of #$newDiscourseReferentInUDS) or accessible from another discourse structure (cf. #$accessibleDiscourseReferentInUDS) that is super-ordinate to, i.e., \"visible\" to, UDS.")
(comment anaphoricDiscourseReferentInUDS "(#$anaphoricDiscourseReferentInUDS UDS PEG) means that PEG is a

discourse entity introduced in UDS the reference of which needs to be

resolved via the identification of an appropriate coreferent among the

available discourse entities.")
(comment moreRecentInDiscourseThan "<code>(#$moreRecentInDiscourseThan PEG-NEW PEG-OLD)</code> is true iff the discourse entity expression <code>PEG-NEW</code> is more recent within the context of the implicitly presupposed discourse than <code>PEG-OLD</code>. This means that PEG-OLD was introduced by a previous utterance or step in the discourse, or by previous clauses in the same utterance.")
(comment subordinateTo-DiscourseStructure "This predicate expresses a partial ordering relation between 
local discourse structures that could  be described as 
`accessibility with respect to discourse context'. (#$subordinateTo-DiscourseStructure SUB SUPER) means that
SUB is a discourse structure whose local context is a specification of the
local context of SUPER. A key derivate feature is that any peg (cf.
#$DiscourseReferentMarker) available in SUPER is also available in SUB. Read:
`SUB is subordinate to SUPER' or `SUPER is accessible from SUB'.")
(comment unitStructureOfUtterance "(#$unitStructureOfUtterance UTT UDS) means that UTT is the utterance to which the #$UnitDiscourseStructure UDS corresponds. Note that this relation is functional in the arg1 position, but not in the arg2 one: each discourse structure is generated by a particular parse tree, and each utterance tokenization can generate multiple parse trees.")
(comment DiscourseStructureBinaryRole "The collection of binary predicates used to characterize
discourse properties of instances of #$UnitDiscourseStructure (q.v.):
corresponding utterance, accessible (super-ordinate) discourse structures,
newly introduced pegs (cf. #$DiscourseReferentMarkers), etc.")
(comment UnitDiscourseStructure "An instance of this collection is a local discourse context, that
is, the smallest unit of context in a discourse. Each new utterance
introduces an additional #$UnitDiscourseStructure in a discourse; some
parts of utterances have their own UDSs. The main properties of a UDS are represented by the predicates in #$DiscourseStructureBinaryRole (q.v.). Any number of
reference-resolving conditions (cf #$coReferring) may also be asserted in a
UDS.")
(comment DiscourseReferentMarker-Pronominal "A specialization of #$DiscourseReferentMarker-NounPhrase: instances of this collection are pegs corresponding to noun phrases consisting of pronouns.  Note that although pronominal pegs are always introduced by NPs, their antecedents need not be in turn NP pegs.")
(comment DiscourseReferentMarker-IndefiniteNP "A specialization of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg: instances of this collection are pegs introduced by dialogue processing in correspondence of indefinite noun phrases such as 'a yellow dog'.")
(comment DiscourseReferentMarker-DefiniteNP "A specialization of #$DiscourseReferentMarker-NounPhrase: instances of this collection are pegs introduced by dialogue processing in correspondence of definite noun phrases such as 'the queen of England'.")
(comment DiscourseReferentMarker-NounPhrase "A specialization of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg: instances of this collection are pegs introduced by dialogue processing in correspondence of noun phrases.")
(comment FormalSemanticStructureType "The collection of all types of entities used for representing in
CycL the formal structures of natural-language semantics.")
(disjointWith DiscourseReferentMarker-IndefiniteNP DiscourseReferentMarker-Pronominal)
(comment agentHasBeenInPlace "An assertion of the form (#$agentHasBeenInPlace AGENT PLACE) means that at some point in time the #$Agent-PartiallyTangible has been located at the #$Place PLACE.  More specific forms of the relationship represented by this predicate include #$residesInRegion, #$birthPlace, #$placeOfDeath.")
(comment orgHasKeyIndividualMembers "An assertion of the form (#$orgHasKeyIndividualMembers ORG PERSON) means that the #$Person PERSON is a member of the #$Organization ORG and that PERSON plays a significant role in guiding or implementing the actions of ORG.  Important specializations of this predicate include #$companyHasCEO, #$seniorExecutives, #$primeMinister.")
(comment SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory-SentenceLevel "The collection of instances of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that contain hypothesized sentence-level semantic interpretations.")
(comment SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory-PhraseLevel "The collection of instances of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that contain hypothesized phrase-level semantic interpretations.")
(comment SIHM-ValidatedByInterlocutor "The collection of instances of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that have been validated by the Cyc Interlocutor (c.f., #$CycInterlocutor).  These microtheories represent phrase-level interpretations that the Interlocutor has chosen (see #$chosenMeaning) as correct.")
(comment EtiquetteNorm "An #$EtiquetteNorm is an individual rule of etiquette, i.e.,
a particular requirement on social conduct that is part of
the system of manners and social obligations within some culture (see
#$cultureCodeOfEtiquette).  Note that some #$EtiquetteNorms are accepted by multiple cultures or groups, and so, belong to multiple systems of etiquette. To relate
an instance of #$EtiquetteNorm to such a system, use
#$subObligations.")
(disjointWith Agent-NonGeographical GeographicalAgent)
(comment Horizontal-Generally "An instance of #$TerrestrialNonGeographicDirection.  With respect to a given reference point, #$Horizontal-Generally comprises the disc-shaped interval of vectors pointing from the reference point and perpendicular to any #$VerticalDirection.")
(comment TerrestrialNonGeographicDirection "A specialization of #$TerrestrialDirection (q.v.) whose instances are those directions that are <i>not</i> defined strictly in terms of the standard \"North-South\" and \"East-West\" directional axes.  Instead, they are defined (at least in part) in terms of the notions of \"up\" and \"down\".  Instances of this collection include #$Down-Directly, #$Up-Generally, and #$Horizontal-Generally.")
(comment direction-Pointing "A binary predicate that relates a spatial thing to the direction in which it's pointing.  <code>(#$direction-Pointing OBJ DIR)</code> means that the intrinsic pointing axis of <code>OBJ</code> points in the direction <code>DIR</code> (represented as a #$UnitVectorInterval).  
<p>
Pointing axes may be ascribed to certain objects, especially in relation to their functions or uses; e.g. to indicate direction (as with a pointer stick or a compass needle), to cause motion in a certain direction (as with a gun or a train), or to be accessed from a certain direction (as with a cupboard or a couch).
<p>
Some objects, like the train and the cupboard, point in the same direction that they face (see #$facesDirection).  Other objects, such as a tower, point and face in different directions.  Still other objects, such as a sewing needle, point in a certain direction but don't face in any direction at all.")
(comment VerticalDirection "A #$VectorIntervalType and a specialization of #$TerrestrialNonGeographicDirection.  #$VerticalDirection is defined to be the collection of all vectors that are parallel to #$Up-Directly and #$Down-Directly.  Cf. #$Horizontal-Generally.")
(comment facesDirection "A binary predicate that relates a tangible object to the direction in which it's facing.  <code>(#$facesDirection OBJ DIR)</code> means that the vector (see #$UnitVectorInterval) that is parallel to <code>OBJ</code>'s front-back axis, and which is directed as from back to front, is <code>DIR</code>.
<p>
Note that an object has a front-back axis only if it has intrinsic front and back sides by virtue of its design or function (see #$FrontAndBackSidedObject).  For example, trains, cars, and bullets have intrinsic front faces by virtue of the direction in which they are intended to travel.  Other objects, such as refrigerators, bookshelves against walls, and televisions, have front faces by virtue of how people usually view the object.  Spheres, being symmetric, do not have front-back axes.
<p>
See also #$direction-Pointing and #$orientationToObject.")
(comment orientationToObject "A ternary predicate that is used to describe the spatial orientation of one thing in relation to the another, by specifying an axis of the former that intersects with the latter.  <code>(#$orientationToObject
OBJ AXIS THING)</code> means that an intrinsic axis (see #$objectAxes) of <code>OBJ</code>, <code>AXIS</code>, intersects with <code>THING</code>.  Typically, <code>AXIS</code> will be specified using #$BilateralAxisFn, thus indicating which axis of <code>OBJ</code>'s we're talking about.  For example, <pre>(#$orientationToObject 
    <b>MyHouse</b> 
    (#$BilateralAxisFn <b>MyHouse</b> #$LeftSide #$RightSide) 
    <b>MyGarage</b>)
</pre>
says that my garage is beside my house.  But note that it does <i>not</i> specify which side of my house, the left or the right, the garage is on; for the second and third arguments of #$BilateralAxisFn commute.
<p>
See also #$facesDirection.")
(comment oppositeDirection-Interval "A symmetric binary predicate and a specialization of #$disjointDirections.  <code>(#$oppositeDirection-Interval COMING GOING)</code> means that the vectors <code>COMING</code> and <code>GOING</code> (see #$UnitVectorInterval) point in opposite directions.  That is, (i) the respective midpoints of <code>COMING</code> and <code>GOING</code> are separated by 180 degrees and (ii) <code>COMING</code> and <code>GOING</code> are of equal breadth (as measured by the absolute value of the number of degrees difference between their greatest and least points).  For example,
<pre>
  (#$oppositeDirection-Interval #$North-Generally #$South-Generally)
  (#$oppositeDirection-Interval #$DorsalDirection #$VentralDirection) .
</pre>
For stating precise opposite direction see the specialization #$oppositeDirection-Precise.")
(comment oppositeDirection-Precise "A symmetric binary predicate and a specialization of #$oppositeDirection-Interval (q.v.).  <code>(#$oppositeDirection-Precise COMING GOING)</code> means that the precise vectors <code>COMING</code> and <code>GOING</code> (see #$UnitVector-Precise) point in respective directions that are 180 degrees apart.  For example,
<pre>
  (#$oppositeDirection-Precise #$North-Directly #$South-Directly)
  (#$oppositeDirection-Precise #$Up-Directly #$Down-Directly) .")
(comment orientation "A binary predicate that is used for stating the general orientation (see #$OrientationVector) of a tangible object.  <code>(#$orientation OBJECT ORIENT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is oriented in the way described by <code>ORIENT</code>.  Examples: <code>(#$orientation OBJ #$RightSideUp)</code> and <code>(#$orientation OBJ #$VerticalOrientation)</code>.
<p>
An #$orientation statement must of course be always interpreted with respect to some #$FrameOfReference.")
(comment symmetricAxes "A binary predicate that relates a (tangible or intangible) spatial thing to an axis of symmetry (if it has any).  <code>(#$symmetricAxes OBJECT AXIS)</code> means that <code>AXIS</code> is an <i>axis of symmetry</i> of <code>OBJECT</code>: a #$Line-Straight with respect to which <code>OBJECT</code> is either bilaterally or radially symmetric (see #$AxisymmetricObject).
<p>
For example, any straight line that passes through the center of a given #$Sphere is an axis of symmetry of the sphere.  The axle of a bicycle wheel spatially subsumes a segment of the wheel's (radial) axis of symmetry.
<p>
For a broader notion of axis, which includes the partly conventionally-determined \"axes\" of certain non-symmetric objects, see the generalization #$objectAxes.")
(comment aligned "(#$aligned OBJ1 OBJ2) means that the two objects are aligned so as to be oriented in the same way.  Specifications of this would be #$alignedAlong to specify that the two objects are side-by-side, and #$alignedEndToEnd to specify that the two objects share a common length axis as well. See also a related predicate #$parallel.")
(comment upAxisPoints "A binary #$SpatialPredicate that is used to describe a tangible object's general orientation.  <code>(#$upAxisPoints OBJ DIR)</code> means <code>OBJ</code>'s intrinsic top-bottom axis -- i.e. (#$BilateralAxisFn <code>OBJ</code> #$TopSide #$BottomSide) -- when considered \"from bottom to top\", points in the direction <code>DIR</code>.  
<p>
Objects that have intrinsic top-bottom axes (see #$TopAndBottomSidedObject) do so by virtue of their design or function.  Examples include people, rockets, cars, and cups.  Conversely, a sphere has no intrinsic top or bottom, due to its symmetry.  
<p>
Note that <code>(#$upAxisPoints OBJ #$Up-Directly)</code> holds only when  <code>OBJ</code> is perfectly right-side-up.  If <code>OBJ</code> is on its side, <code>(#$upAxisPoints OBJ #$Horizontal-Generally)</code> holds.  If OBJ is upside-down, <code>(#$upAxisPoints OBJ #$Down-Generally)</code> holds, and <code>(#$upAxisPoints OBJ #$Down-Directly)</code> might hold as well.
<p>
See also #$orientation.")
(comment AboveGroundFuelStorageTank "Each instance of #$AboveGroundFuelStorageTank is a large, cylindrical tank intended for the storage of #$Oil, #$GasolineFuel or other liquid petrochemicals.  The capacity of these tanks can range upwards of 10,000 gallons.")
(comment TankFarm "Each #$TankFarm is an outdoor area occupied by several (two or more, but usually more) large, cylindrical, metal tanks in which oil or liquid petrochemicals are stored.")
(comment concatenateStrings "A #$VariableArityPredicate that takes one or more abstract character strings.  Its first argument is the concatenation of its successive arguments.  For any instances <code>STRING1</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> of #$CharacterString, (#$concatenateStrings <code>STRING1</code> ... <code>STRING<sub>n</sub>)</code> holds just in case <code>STRING1</code> is the character-string that is obtained by concatenating  <code>STRING2</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> end-to-end in the order from 2 to n.
<p>
For example, (#$concatenateStrings \"Bob\" \"B\" \"o\" \"b\"), and (#$concatenateStrings \"Bob Dylan\" \"Bob\" \" \" \"Dy\" \"lan\").
<p>
Note that some (or all) of <code>STRING2</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> might be identical.  Also note that, when applied to a single argument, this predicate always holds, and when applied to two arguments, this predicate holds just in case the two arguments are equal;
e.g. (#$concatenateStrings \"Bob\" \"Bob\").")
(comment Fjord "Each instance of #$Fjord is an arm of the sea characterized by sheer parallel walls often extending far below the water surface and by many branches.  #$Fjords are found where the land next to the sea is mountainous, usually at high latitudes.")
(comment Stream "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater, #$InanimateObject-Natural, and #$FlowPath.  Each instance of #$Stream is a natural body of water that flows when it is not frozen.  A stream can either be a great river (see #$River) or a  tiny creek (see #$Creek).  Most often, streams are filled with fresh water, but can in certain cases be filled with saline or brackish water (see #$TidalCreek). Every non-frozen stream flows in (see #$in-ContOpen) some conduit (see #$FluidConduit) such as a streambed or riverbed.  Notable instances of #$Stream include #$AmazonRiver, #$LittleMissouriRiver, #$WoundedKnee-Creek.  
<p>
Note that riverbanks are not streams nor are they parts of streams, but rather they mark the edges of streams.")
(comment TerritorialWaters "Each instance of #$TerritorialWaters is the sum of all subregions of one or more #$Seas which are adjacent to one particular country such that they are subject to the political jurisdiction that country.")
(comment Down-Directly "A #$TerrestrialNonGeographicDirection (q.v.) and a #$UnitVector-Precise corresponding to the direction \"straight down\".  In the terrestrial context, #$Down-Directly points in the same direction as Earth's gravitational force vector.  See also #$Down-Generally and #$Up-Directly.")
(comment Up-Directly "A #$TerrestrialNonGeographicDirection (q.v.) and a #$UnitVector-Precise corresponding to the direction \"straight up\".  In the terrestrial context, #$Up-Directly points in the exactly opposite direction to Earth's gravitational force vector.  See also #$Up-Generally and #$Down-Directly.")
(comment Down-Generally "An instance of #$TerrestrialNonGeographicDirection (q.v.).  With respect to a given reference point, #$Down-Generally is the \"cone-shaped\" vector interval that subsumes any precise vector (see #$UnitVector-Precise) that points within forty-five degrees of where #$Down-Directly points.  See also #$Up-Generally.")
(comment Up-Generally "An instance of #$TerrestrialNonGeographicDirection (q.v.).  With respect to a given reference point, #$Up-Generally is the \"cone-shaped\" vector interval that subsumes any precise vector (see #$UnitVector-Precise) that points within forty-five degrees of where #$Up-Directly points.  See also #$Down-Generally.")
(comment OrientationVector "A specialization of #$PositionVector (q.v.).  #$OrientationVector is the collection of vectors that characterize an object's orientation relative to a given #$FrameOfReference (q.v.).  In most contexts, orientation is taken with respect to the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference (q.v.)
<p>
Instances of #$OrientationVector include #$RightSideUp and #$HorizontalOrientation.")
(comment disjointDirections "A predicate that relates #$VectorIntervals (q.v.) whose directions do not overlap.  <code>(#$disjointDirections VECTOR1 VECTOR2)</code> means that the respective direction-interval components of <code>VECTOR1</code> and <code>VECTOR2</code> are disjoint with each other.  Specializations of this predicate include #$oppositeDirection-Interval and #$perpendicularVectors.")
(comment VerticalOrientation "An #$OrientationVector (q.v.).  <code>(#$orientation OBJECT #$VerticalOrientation)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is vertical with respect to the current #$FrameOfReference.  A #$LongThinObject is vertical if and only if its longest axis is perpendicular to the horizontal plane.  A #$SheetShapedObject is vertical if and only if its two longest axes are both perpendicular to the horizontal plane.  Typical vertical objects include towers, trees, telephone poles, and skyscapers.
<p>
Cf. #$HorizontalOrientation.  See also #$VerticalDirection.")
(comment HorizontalOrientation "An #$OrientationVector (q.v.).  <code>(#$orientation OBJECT #$HorizontalOrientation)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is horizontal with respect to the current #$FrameOfReference.  A #$LongThinObject is horizontal if and only if its longest axis lies in the horizontal plane.  A #$SheetShapedObject is horizontal if and only if its two longest axes lie in the horizontal plane.  Typical horizontal objects include dinner plates, mousepads, ice-skating rinks, parking lots, and stratus clouds.
<p>
Cf. #$VerticalOrientation.  See also the direction vector #$Horizontal-Generally.")
(comment oppositeIntervals "This specialization of #$different (q.v.) relates incompatible quantities or vectors that are \"direct opposites\" of each other. (#$oppositeIntervals X Y) means that X is the directly opposite of Y (and vice versa).  The sense of \"opposite\" intended here is intuitively familiar, but difficult to define precisely. In some cases, X and Y will constitute the only two instances of a certain specialization of #$Quantity QUANT-TYPE, and there is a certain \"natural\" class of things QUANT-TYPE-RANGE each member of which has X if and only if it fails to have Y.  In other cases, X and Y will be instances of a specialization of #$ScalarOrVectorInterval that has other instances as well, but is structured such that X and Y are \"maximally different from\" or \"diametrically opposed to\" each other with respect to their positions in this structure; e.g with respect to the instances of #$GeographicalDirection-General, #$North-Generally and #$South-Generally are opposites.")
(comment RightSideUp "An #$OrientationVector (q.v.).  <code>(#$orientation OBJECT #$RightSideUp)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code>'s intrinsic top side (e.g. the top of a person's head) is directly above its intrinsic bottom side (e.g. the bottoms of their feet).  To have this orientation, <code>OBJECT</code> must be a #$TopAndBottomSidedObject (q.v.).
<p>
Cf. #$UpsideDown.")
(comment UpsideDown "An #$OrientationVector (q.v.).  <code>(#$orientation OBJECT #$UpsideDown)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code>'s intrinsic top side (e.g. the top of a person's head) is directly <i>below</i> its intrinsic bottom side (e.g. the bottoms of their feet).  To have this orientation, <code>OBJECT</code> must be a #$TopAndBottomSidedObject (q.v.).
<p>
Cf. #$RightSideUp.")
(comment FrameOfReference "A specialization of #$MathematicalObject.  Each instance of #$FrameOfReference is a mathematical (and hence intangible) representation of the context in which certain data are to be interpreted.  Such contexts are typically physical, but may be purely mathematical.  For example, a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem can be used to represent the positions of things on the surface of the Eath, but can also be used to represent an abstract gepometric space.")
(comment Water-Fresh "The collection of portions of #$Water in any physical state (solid, liquid, or gas) which is considered fresh water rather than sea water.  Thus, the water in most lakes and rivers, drinking water, steam, and precipitation are all fresh water.")
(comment BodyOfWater "A specialization of #$TopographicalFeature.  Each instance of #$BodyOfWater is a natural or artificial topographical feature consisting of a relatively large volume of water primarily in liquid form, contained by an instance of #$Basin-Topographical.  Both the basin and the water are considered parts of the #$BodyOfWater; see #$basinOfBodyOfWater and #$waterOfBodyOfWater.
<p>
Specializations of #$BodyOfWater include #$Ocean, #$Lake, #$River, #$Canal, and #$Harbor. 
<p>
Note that relatively small, purely artificial containers of water (such as swimming pools or tanks of water) are <i>not</i> instances of #$BodyOfWater.  Moreover, topographical features such as #$Glaciers or snowfields, which contain a large volume of water primarily in solid form, are also <i>not</i> instances of #$BodyOfWater.")
(comment Pond "Each instance of #$Pond is a relatively small body of standing water.  #$Ponds can be freshwater or saline, natural or artificial.")
(comment Overfalls-RegionOfWater "#$Overfalls-RegionOfWater is subregion of a #$BodyOfWater in which waves typically break due either to opposing currents meeting or due to waves moving against the current.")
(comment Harbor "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$Harbor  is a body of water along the coast of a #$Sea which is protected from the open sea and deep enough to furnish fairly safe anchorage for ships.  Instances of #$Harbor include #$PearlHarbor and #$AdenHarbor-Yemen.")
(comment WaterReservoir "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater.  Each instance of #$WaterReservoir is an artificial pond or lake used for the storage and preservation of water.")
(comment Marina "A type of #$TransportFacility which serves small boats or pleasure boats.  Each instance of #$Marina is in the region of some #$Harbor and its shoreline, and provides docks or achorages, and often other services, such as a place to buy fuel and other supplies.")
(disjointWith Canal WaterReservoir)
(comment expressionPegCategory "A specialization of (see #$genlPreds) #$isa.  <code>(#$expressionPegCategory PEG PEG-TYPE)</code> means that the instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg <code>PEG</code> is a peg of type <code>PEG-TYPE</code>, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionType.")
(comment evaluationProcedureEvalueeType "<code>(#$evaluationProcedureEvalueeType EVALUATION-TYPE TYPE)</code> means that <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$Evaluating is, by design, an procedure for evaluating instances of <code>TYPE</code>, a collection.  For any given instance of <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code>, there could be things other than an instance of <code>TYPE</code> that get evaluated, but that is incidental: To be an instance of <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code> it must be the intention of the performer that an instance of <code>TYPE</code> be evaluated.  See also #$evaluee-Direct and #$evaluationTypeDirectEvalueeType.")
(comment MainCampusFn "An expression of the form (#$MainCampusFn ORG) is used to denote the instance of #$Campus that is the main #$Campus for the #$Organization ORG.  If ORG is an instance of #$SchoolInstitution, then the result of this function is an instance of #$SchoolCampus.  As the 'main' campus, this is intended to pick out the #$Place where the buildings that house the core administrative and some functional sub-organizatons of ORG perform their daily operations.")
(comment hasCampus "A specialization of #$operatesInRegion.  An assertion of the form (#$hasCampus ORG CAMP) is used to relate an #$Organzation ORG to a piece of real estate that it occupies, typically with there being multiple buildings on that one piece of real estate.  This is a general relationship and can be used to relate one #$Organization to several instances of #$Campus.  The more specialized predicate #$hasMainCampus can be used to relate the #$Organization to the #$Campus that houses the headquarters or primary administrative units of ORG.")
(comment evaluationProcedureEvaluationDimension "<code>(#$evaluationProcedureEvaluationDimension EVALUATION-TYPE PREDICATE)</code> means that <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$Evaluating is, by design, a procedure for evaluating instances of <code>TYPE</code>, for some instance of #$Collection <code>TYPE</code>, along the dimension <code>PREDICATE</code>, an instance of #$ScalarIntervalSlot.  In particular, for every instance <code>EVALUATION</code> of <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code> where <code>THING</code> is evaluated (see #$evaluee-Direct), the intended outcome of <code>EVALUATION</code> is the determination of <code>VALUE</code> such that <code>(PREDICATE THING VALUE)</code>.  Or, to be more formal, it is the intention of the performer of <code>EVALUATION</code> that for some <code>VALUE</code> <code>(#$evaluationOutputSentences EVALUATION (PREDICATE THING VALUE))</code>.")
(comment KBCTForAquaintKBPE-TIPInvolvingPresupposition "These are KBCTs which test whether Cyc can solve a particular #$AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblem which involve presupposition.")
(comment KBCTForAquaintKBPE-TIPInvolvingImplicature "These are KBCTs which test whether Cyc can solve a particular #$AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblem which involve conversational implicature.")
(comment TextualInferenceProblemInvolvingPresupposition "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which involve presuppositon.")
(comment TextualInferenceProblemInvolvingImplicature "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which involve conversational implicature.")
(comment playsRoleTypicalOf "(#$playsRoleTypicalOf OBJ OBJ-TYPE EVENT) means that, in a particular 
instance of Event EVENT, one would usually expect an instance of 
OBJ-TYPE to play an instrumental role.  In this particular event, however,
that role is being played by OBJ, an object of another type.  For instance, (playsRoleTypicalOf JimsScalpel CarvingKnife CuttingTheTurkeyAtThanksgiving2000) means that there is some role in that particular #$CuttingAPieceOfFood event that one would expect to be played by a carving knife, but in this case that role is being played by Jim's scalpel.")
(comment NBCWeaponStorage "#$NBCWeaponStorage is the collection of events in which some number of nuclear, biological, or chemical weapons are stored, typically in a #$NBCWeaponStorageFacility.")
(comment NBCWeaponStorageFacility "#$NBCWeaponStorageFacility is the collection of storage facilities where nuclear, biological, or chemical weapons are typically stored.")
(comment Bank-Underwater "Each instance of #$Bank-Underwater is an elevated area, usually on a shelf.  Though shallower than surrounding areas, the depth of water over a #$Bank-Underwater is usually sufficient for most surface vessels to pass.  For shallower areas, see #$Shoal.")
(comment Shoal "A specialization of both #$Bank-Underwater.  Each instance of #$Shoal is an underwater elevation which is composed of relatively loose material, and poses a navigation hazard to surface vessels because the water over it is fairly shallow.")
(comment NaturalElevation "A sub-collection of #$LandTopographicalFeature. Each instance is a raised or elevated geological formation.  Each instance is an area that has a greater elevation than the surrounding area, and thus includes more elevated parts of a whole, such as #$Summit and #$RidgeLine-Topographical.  The more specialized collection, #$VerticalProtrusion-Topographical, should be used for types of elevated areas that are in some sense whole, e.g., #$Hill or #$Mountain.")
(comment Bar-Topographical "A natural accumulation of sand, gravel, or alluvium forming an underwater embankment that may be partially exposed. A shallow ridge or mound of coarse unconsolidated material in a stream channel, at the mouth of a stream, estuary, or lagoon and in the wave-break zone along coasts.")
(comment connectedAtEnd "(#$connectedAtEnd OBJ1 OBJ2) means that an end of #$LongThinObject OBJ1 is #$connectedTo OBJ2.")
(comment BodyOfWater-Intermittent-WetPhase "Each instance of #$BodyOfWater-Intermittent-WetPhase is a region which often is inundated with a relatively large volume of stationary or flowing water, and which in fact has water present at the time it is known to be a member of this collection.")
(disjointWith BodyOfWater-Intermittent-DryPhase BodyOfWater-Intermittent-WetPhase)
(comment BodyOfWater-Intermittent "Each instance of #$BodyOfWater-Intermittent is a #$TopographicalFeature characterized by the frequent presence of a relatively large volume of water, but which is usually dry part of the time (cf. #$BodyOfWater-Intermittent-DryPhase and #$BodyOfWater-Intermittent-WetPhase).  Often the presence of water is seasonal, accumulating during the wet part of the year and drying at other times.  For some intermittent bodies of water, the cycle is longer or erratic, but on some humanly-perceivable time scale.  Bodies of water that disappear over a very long time scale or because of particular geologic events (e.g., a spring that stops flowing due to an earthquake) are not considered intermittent in this sense.")
(disjointWith BodyOfWater-Intermittent Sea)
(comment IntermittentBodyOfWaterFn "(IntermittentBodyOfWaterFn BOW) denotes the collection of bodies of water of type BOW which are intermittent, meaning that their instances are characterized by the frequent presence of a relatively large volume of water, but usually dry out and refill many times during their existence.")
(comment numberOfEdges "This predicate relates a spatial thing to the number of edges it has. Also see #$objectEdges and #$EdgeOnObject.")
(comment Star-PlaneFigure "A specialization of #$Polygon. Each instance of #$Star-PlaneFigure is a star-shaped plane figure with three or more points.  The perimeter of a star figure is a closed #$LineString whose internal angles alternate between acute (< 180-degrees) and obtuse (> 180-degrees); the vertices of the acute angles are the \"points\" of the star.")
(comment NullBoundary "A specialization of #$NullSpaceRegion (q.v.).  Each instance of #$NullBoundary is a null space region that is the (empty) boundary of a #$SpatiallyUnboundedThing (q.v.), according to a convention adopted in certain geometrical contexts.  This convention is a simplifying assumption that allows a \"boundary function\" (the function from things in space to their boundaries; see #$boundaryOfSpatialThing) to be defined as a #$TotalFunction on the collection #$SpatialThing.  At most one null boundary is recognized with respect to any given context.  
<p>
Note that the dimensionality (see #$dimensionOfSpatialThing) of any null boundary is set by fiat to -1.  This conforms with the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment mutuallyOverlappingCircles "A #$SpaceRegionPredicate and #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$mutuallyOverlappingCircles CIRCLE1 CIRCLE2 CIRCLE3)</code> means that the three #$CircularRegions <code>CIRCLE1</code>, <code>CIRCLE2</code>, and <code>CIRCLE3</code> share a common part (see #$partOfSpaceRegion).")
(comment TextualInferenceProblemInvolvingAbduction "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which require abduction.")
(comment TextualInferenceProblemInvolvingSenseExtension "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which involve a sense extension of some sort (e.g. metonymy ometaphor).")
(comment pegInterpretations-CycL-Rejected "<code>(#$pegInterpretations-CycL-Rejected PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL semantics <code>CYCL</code> assigned to the instance PEG of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg has been rejected and is no longer considered a viable possibility.")
(comment bodyOfWaterTypeIntermittentForm "(#$bodyOfWaterTypeIntermittentForm BOW-TYPE INTERMITTENT-TYPE) means that INTERMITTENT-TYPE is a kind of BOW-TYPE whose instances intermittently contain water.  In other words, all instances of INTERMITTENT-TYPE 1) are bodies of water, 2) are instances of BOW-TYPE, and 3) are often full of water, but are dry at many times during their existence.")
(comment SaltLake "Each instance of #$SaltLake is a body of standing water that is inland (so is a #$Lake), and is filled with (though possibly intermittently) with salt water.")
(comment MissileFacility "A #$MissileFacility is a #$MilitaryFacility where #$Missiles are designed, assembled, or stored.")
(comment NuclearBiologicalOrChemicalWeapon "The collection of nuclear, biological, or chemical weapons. Such weapons have massive destructive power and cause great loss of life.")
(comment MilitaryAirfield "The collection of all military airfields. Similar to instances of its superset #$Airport-Physical, all #$MilitaryAirfields have a runway where airplanes take off and land. They generally also contain buildings for the military personnel that operate the military airplanes that are stationed at the airfield.")
(comment MarineBase-Grounds "A specialization of #$MilitaryBase-Grounds
.  Each instance of #$MarineBase-Grounds is a base, post, or fort controlled by a #$MarineCorps.")
(comment lineOfSight-PointToPoint "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates geographical points.  <code>(#$lineOfSight-PointToPoint POINT1 POINT2)</code> means that <code>POINT1</code> is \"in the line of sight of\" <code>POINT2</code>, and vice versa.  When <code>POINT1</code> and <code>POINT2</code> are different, it follows that there is a #$FreeSpaceRegion (q.v.) that subsumes the straight line-segment whose endpoints (see #$endPointOfLineSegment) are <code>POINT1</code> and <code>POINT2</code>.
<p>
See also #$lineOfSight-PointToRegion.")
(comment lineOfSight-PointToRegion "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates geographical points to georaphical regions.  <code>(#$lineOfSight-PointToPoint POINT REGION)</code> means that <code>REGION</code> is the largest region such that every point in it is \"in the line of sight of\" <code>POINT</code>, and vice versa (see #$lineOfSight-PointToPoint).  See also the corresponding function #$LineOfSightFn.")
(comment farFrom "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$farFrom THING1 THING2)</code> means that the object or place <code>THING1</code> is far away from the object or place <code>THING2</code>.  The minimal distance that constitutes being \"far\" is vague, and exceeds, in a particular context, the maximal limit of #$near (q.v.).  See also #$notFarFrom.")
(comment notFarFrom "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate (q.v.) and a contrary (see #$negationPreds) of #$farFrom (q.v.).  <code>(#$not FarFrom THING1 THING2)</code> means that the object or place <code>THING1</code> is not far away from the object or place <code>THING2</code>.  The minimal distance that constitutes being \"far\" is vague, and exceeds, in a particular context, the maximal limit of #$near (q.v.).")
(comment occupiesMostOf "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$spatiallyIntersects (q.v.).  <code>(#$occupiesMostOf THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>THING1</code> spatially occupies most (over 50 percent) or all of the space occupied by <code>THING2</code> (see #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial). They must overlap.  To state the fraction of <code>THING2</code> that <code>THING1</code> occupies, use #$occupiesFraction.")
(comment inRegionBetween "A ternary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that holds of three spatial things when the third is located in the region generally between the first two.  <code>(#$inRegionBetween PLACE1 PLACE2 THING)</code> means that <code>PLACE1</code> is closer to <code>THING</code> than to <code>PLACE2</code>, and <code>PLACE2</code> is closer to <code>THING</code> than to <code>PLACE1</code>.  More precisely, <code>THING</code> is subsumed by the space region <code>REGION</code> such that, for every point <code>POINT</code> in <code>REGION</code>, (i) <code>PLACE1</code> is closer to <code>POINT</code> than to <code>PLACE2</code> and (ii) <code>PLACE2</code> is closer to <code>POINT</code> than to <code>PLACE1</code>.
<p>
For cases in which a spatial thing is <i>directly</i> between two others, see the specialization #$between.")
(comment locatedAtPoint-Spatial "A #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$partiallyLocatedAt-Spatial (q.v.) that holds between #$SpatialThings and #$Points.  <code>(#$locatedAtPoint-Spatial OBJECT POINT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is located at <code>POINT</code>. This is a cross-dimensional locational predicate that can be used to indicate when the region occupied by some extended spatial object includes some point. See also #$incidentInSpaceRegion.
<p>
Note that #$locatedAtPoint-Spatial is irreflexive; in order to locate points in space use #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial.")
(comment distanceBetween "A ternary instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$distanceBetween PLACE1 PLACE2 DIST)</code> means that the #$Distance between <code>PLACE1</code> and <code>PLACE2</code> is <code>DIST</code>.  More precisely, <code>DIST</code> is the length of the shortest path through the space (when there is such a space) separating <code>PLACE1</code> and <code>PLACE2</code>.  Usually, a straight line between a point on <code>PLACE1</code> and a point on <code>PLACE2</code> will constitute the shortest such path.  However, there are some circumstances in which this will <i>not</i> be the case; for instance, if <code>PLACE1</code> and <code>PLACE2</code> are #$GeographicalRegions, the shortest path between them may be a great circle, rather than a straight line.  See also #$distanceAlongPath.")
(comment spatiallyContains "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$spatiallyIncludes (q.v.).  <code>(#$spatiallyContains CONT OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is within the convex hull of <code>CONT</code>.  (See #$convexHullOf and #$convexSpaceRegionSubsuming-Minimal.)  
<p>
Note that #$spatiallyContains is agnostic as to whether <code>OBJ</code> is a part of <code>CONT</code>, fully inside of <code>CONT</code> (as opposed to sharing an outer surface with it), or moves with <code>CONT</code> (see #$movesWith).  #$spatiallyContains is a very general predicate, so a more specific predicate is almost always applicable. For example, if <code>OBJ</code> #$movesWith <code>CONT</code>, the slightly more specific predicate #$physicallyContains is applicable.")
(comment IceCapClimateCycle "The climate of polar ice caps, such as Antarctica or central Greenland.  Temperatures never rise above freezing, the precipitation is always snow, and the days are 24 hours long in summer and nights 24 hours long in winter.")
(comment MediterraneanClimateCycle "This is the collection of climage cycles like that of European countries bordering the Mediterranean Sea, such as Greece and Italy. Characteristic weather patterns include hot, dry summers and mild, rainy winters.")
(comment MonsoonClimateCycle "This is the collection of climate cycles of tropical areas that have distinct wet and dry seasons, such as Bangladesh.  Such climates are always hot.")
(comment MountainClimateCycle "This is the collection of climate cycles of moutainous areas.  They are usually cooler and wetter than the climates of the surrounding lowlands.")
(comment RainForestClimateCycle "This is the collection of climate cycles of the rain forests. Such areas are always hot and wet, with little or no seasonal temperature changes at all.")
(comment RainyClimateCycle "This is thee collection of climate cycles that have lots of rain.")
(comment TaigaClimateCycle "This is the climate of the great forests of Northern #$Canada and #$Russia.  They are characterized by long cold winter and short, cool summers.")
(comment TemperateMarineClimateCycle "This is the climate of coastal areas in temperate regions, such as Seattle, Washington. Such climates are characterized by warm summers, cool winters, and precipitation throughout.")
(comment TundraClimateCycle "This is the climate of tundra regions near the poles. Always cold, with short summers and little precipitation.")
(comment Desert "A #$Desert is a #$GeographicalRegion that receives little precipitation, by fiat less than 200 mm per year. Desert soil is often composed mostly of sand, and sand dunes may be present. Exposures of rocky terrain are typical, and reflect minimal soil development and sparse vegetation.")
(comment Forest-Temperate "#$Forest-Temperate is the collection of #$Forests which are found in temperate climates.")
(comment hasMainCampus "A specialized form of #$hasCampus and #$hasHeadquartersInRegion.  An assertion of the form (#$hasMainCampus ORG CAMPUS) is used to relate an #$Organization to the #$Campus where the core administrative and functional sub-organziations of ORG are located.")
(comment terrainClimateType "(#$terrainClimateType TERRAINTYPE CLIMATE) mean that #$GeographicalRegions of type TERRAINTYPE can be
expected to have the #$ClimateCycleType CLIMATE.  See also
#$hasClimateType.")
(comment queriesWithTermHaveDefaultQueryMt "(#$queriesWithTermHaveDefaultQueryMt TERM MT) means that queries in which TERM is a constituent should have the inference microtheory parameter set to MT.")
(comment Hot "#$Hot is a generic temperature quantity that corresponds to a higher than desirable temperature, often causing a sensation of heat or burning.")
(comment EatingFoodFromContainer "A specialization of #$EmptyingAContainer and #$EatingEvent;
each instance of #$EatingFoodFromContainer is an action where some 
animal is eating food off a plate, a dish, from a bowl, a trough, a
box, or some sort of container.")
(comment TEPSentenceContentFromStringFn "(#$TEPSentenceContentFromStringFn STRING) is an evaluatable function which, for a given sentence STRING, returns the #$CycLSentence-Askable which reflects (the disjunction of possible) cyclifier outputs for STRING.")
(comment Warm "#$Warm is a generic temperature quantity which corresponds to a comfortable and agreeable degree of heat.")
(comment Cool "#$Cool is a generic temperature quantity which is between #$Warm and #$Cold and often gives relief from the heat.")
(comment Cold "#$Cold is a generic temperature quantity that corresponds to temperatures that are lower than #$Cool and are often found to be unpleasant.")
(comment VeryHot "#$VeryHot is a generic temperature quantity that corresponds to very #$Hot temperatures. Such temperatures are typically unpleasant to experience, and may be dangerous for animals exposed to them for long periods of time.")
(comment VeryCold "#$VeryCold is a generic temperature quantity that corresponds to very #$Cold temperatures. Such temperatures are typically very unpleasant to experience and may be dangerous for animals exposed to them for long periods of time.")
(comment CoolToCold "#$CoolToCold is a generic temperature value that corresponds to temperatures that roughly fall between #$Cool and #$Cold.")
(comment BitterlyCold "#$BitterlyCold is a generic temperature value that corresponds to temperatures that are extremely, or bitterly, #$Cold. Such temperatures are lower than #$VeryCold temperatures and exposure to them for long periods of time can be dangerous for animals.")
(comment TemperateToHot "#$TemperateToHot is a generic temperature quantity that is not uncomfortably cool for people.  In this range, we would not expect a person to have to wear much, if any, clothing for warmth.  Approximately 55 to 130 degrees Fahrenheit.")
(comment ModeratelyHot "#$ModeratelyHot is a generic temperature quantity that corresponds to temperatures that are #$Hot but not quite #$VeryHot.")
(comment ModeratelyCold "#$ModeratelyCold is a generic temperature quantity that corresponds to temperatures that are #$Cold but not quite #$VeryCold.")
(comment HumanHabitableTemperature "#$HumanHabitableTemperature is a generic temperature quantity that includes the temperatures at which humans normally find habitable. Such temperatures range from #$ModeratelyCold to #$ModeratelyHot.")
(comment AboveFreezingOnEarth "#$AboveFreezingOnEarth is a generic temperature quantity that includes the range of naturally occurring above-freezing temperatures on the surface of the #$PlanetEarth.  Approx. 32 to 130 degrees Fahrenheit.")
(comment BelowFreezingOnEarth "#$BelowFreezingOnEarth is a generic temperature quantity which includes the range of naturally occurring temperatures on the surface of the #$PlanetEarth that are below the freezing point of fresh water.  Approx. -70 to +32 degrees Fahrenheit.")
(comment InchesPerYear "#$InchesPerYear is unit of speed which correspond to the number of #$Inches translated per #$YearsDuration.")
(comment ManufacturingFacility "A #$ManufacturingFacility is the grounds of a #$ManufacturingOrganization, where the actual #$Manufacturing of some product takes place.")
(comment FertilizerProductionFacility "A #$FertilizerProductionFacility is a facility where #$Fertilizer is produced from phosphates.")
(comment OilRefinery "The collection of places where #$Petroleum-CrudeOil is refined. Each instance of #$OilRefinery includes at least one or more distillation or absorption units that separate the crude oil into various compounds (see #$PetroleumProduct-Refined). But an #$OilRefinery will also be equiped with loading facilities for pumping the oil from trucks or ships, as well as office buildings for personnel.")
(comment ExpressionTypeByAgreementFeature "The collection of specializations of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg whose instances share a common agreement feature (e.g., such as being singular or mass number).  Each instance of #$ExpressionTypeByAgreementFeature stands in the #$expressionTypeAgreementPredicate relation to some instance of #$TermPhrasesConstraintPredicate.")
(comment ExpressionTypeWithAgreementPredicateFn "<code>(#$ExpressionTypeWithAgreementPredicateFn PRED)</code> denotes the instance of #$ExpressionTypeByNumber whose instances are expressions introduced into a discourse or text-interpretation context by an instance of #$NLPhrase with the agreement property (such as being singular or plural) indicated by PRED.<p>For example, <code>(#$ExpressionTypeWithAgreementPredicateFn #$massNumber)</code> is the collection of mass number expressions -- i.e., pegs that mark the occurrence of a mass number phrase, such as \"pizza.\"")
(comment exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical and #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical; it relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.).   <code>(#$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent is the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.
<p>
Note that #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  Use #$locationState to supply such additional information.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$GeographicalSpaceRegionFn.")
(comment whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical that relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.) in which all of the former is located.   <code>(#$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at some part of <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent subsumes the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.
<p>
Note that #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  Use #$locationState to supply such additional information.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.
<p>
See also the specialization #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical; and see #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical that relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.) in which part of the former is located.   <code>(#$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that part of <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent is subsumed by the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.
<p>
Note that #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  Use #$locationState to supply such additional information.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.
<p>
See also the specialization #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical; and see #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical "An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$notFarFrom that relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.) such that part of the former is located at a part of the latter.   <code>(#$genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that part of <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at a part of <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent intersects the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.
<p>
Note that #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code> (see #$locationState); that is why this predicate is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$spatiallyIntersects.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.
<p>
See also the specializations #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical and #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.")
(comment polarRadiusOfSpheroid "A specialization of #$sizeParameterOfObject.  <code>(#$polarRadiusOfSpheroid SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half the length of <code>SPHEROID</code>'s polar axis.  
<p>
Whenever two #$symmetricAxes (q.v.) of a #$Spheroid that is <i>not</i> a perfect #$Sphere have the same length, they are both <i>equatorial axes</i> of the spheroid (cf. #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).  All of the equatorial axes of such a spheroid lie in a single plane; and the (unique) symmetric-axis of the spheroid that is perpendicular with its equatorial axes is known as its <i>polar axis</i>. 
<p>
If a spheroid's equatorial radius is greater than its polar radius, it is an #$OblateSpheroid; if its polar radius is greater than its equatorial radius, it is a #$ProlateSpheroid.  The greater radius (whichever it might be) measures the spheroid's <i>semi-major axis</i> (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength).  A #$Sphere's polar and equatorial radii are both equal to its ordinary #$radius.
<p>
Note that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>DISTANCE</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has length <code>DISTANCE</code>.")
(comment OliveOilMill-ManufacturingFacility "An #$OliveOilMill-ManufacturingFacility is a mill where oil is extracted from olives.")
(comment OliveOilManufacturingOrganization "An #$OliveOilManufacturingOrganization is a manufacturing organization whose primary activity is the production of #$OliveOil.")
(comment OliveOil-Manufacturing "#$OliveOil-Manufacturing is the process of pressing the dried fruit of an olive tree and extracting its oil.")
(comment Sawmill-ManufacturingFacility "A #$Sawmill-ManufacturingFacility is a mill where logs of wood are sawn into lumber of specified shapes and sizes.")
(comment LumberManufacturingOrganization "A #$LumberManufacturingOrganization is an organization whose primary activity is #$LumberManufacturing.")
(comment LogOfWood "A #$LogOfWood is a log or section of a tree which is no longer living. Logs of wood may be sawn into short pieces and hence be considered #$Firewood, but not so processed as to be considered #$Lumber.")
(comment WaterPoweredDevice "A #$WaterPoweredDevice is a mechanical device that receives its power from the conversion of the kinetic energy of flowing water into another form of mechanical energy.")
(comment ManufacturingRun "A #$ManufacturingRun is a single iteration of a process that intentionally produces a stream of products.  A #$ManufacturingRun may consist of several steps, each of which is typically a #$Manufacturing.  A #$ManufacturingRun does not include ancillary events associated with the manufacturing, such as collection of materials, disposal of waste, or quality control inspection of the product.  Those other events are sibling #$subEvents of the #$ManufacturingRun, within a #$ProductionEvent.")
(comment IndustrialEquipment "#$IndustrialEquipment is the collection of tools and other artifacts that are routinely used in the manufacturing processes occuring at industrial facilities.")
(comment MachineTool "#$MachineTool is the collection of powered machine tools used for cutting or shaping or finishing metals or other materials. It includes metal and wood cutting tools, lathes, and milling machines to name a few.")
(comment spatiallyIncludes "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that is the common generalization of #$spatiallyContains and #$spatiallySubsumes (neither of which is a generalization of the other).  <code>(#$spatiallyIncludes SURROUND OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is subsumed by the space contained in the convex hull of <code>SURROUND</code>.  
<p>
Note that #$spatiallyIncludes is agnostic as to whether <code>OBJ</code> is a part of <code>SURROUND</code>,  is #$spatiallyDisjoint with <code>SURROUND</code>, #$partiallySpatiallyIntersects <code>SURROUND</code>, is properly inside of <code>SURROUND</code> (as opposed to sharing an outer surface), moves with <code>SURROUND</code> (see #$movesWith), or shares a complete outer surface with <code>SURROUND</code>. 
<p>
As #$spatiallyIncludes is very general, a more specific predicate is usually applicable.")
(comment occurrenceNOfStringInStructure "<code>(#$occurrenceNOfStringInStructure CIS INTEGER STRING STRUCTURE)</code> means that the instance <code>CIS</code> of #$TextStringOccurrence is the <code>INTEGERth</code> occurrence of the instance <code>STRING</code> of #$CharacterString in (is a #$subInfoStructures of) the instance of #$AbstractStructure <code>STRUCTURE</code>.  <p>For example, 
<pre>
     (#$occurrenceNOfStringInStructure CIS 2 \"insurgent\" 
        (#$NewsArticleNamedFn #$ReutersPressAgency
          (#$DayFn 28
             (#$MonthFn #$January
               (#$YearFn 2004))) \"Bomb Explodes Outside Baghdad Hotel, at Least One Dead\"))</pre>
means that <code>CIS</code> is the second occurrence of the string \"insurgent\" to appear in the abstract structure of the January 28, 2004 Reuter's article, \"Bomb Explodes Outside Baghdad Hotel, at Least One Dead.\"")
(comment NthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructureFn "<code>(#$NthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructureFn INT TYPE STRUCTURE)</code> denotes the instance <code>CIS</code> of
 #$ContextualizedInformationStructure that is the <code>INT</code>th <code>TYPE</code>-occurrence in the instance <code>STRUCTURE</code> of #$AbstractStructure.<p>For example, <code>(#$NthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructureFn 3 #$NLParagraph (#$AISForFn #$PotbelliedPigs-Article))</code> denotes the third paragraph of the #$AbstractInformationStructure denoted by <code>(#$AISForFn #$PotbelliedPigs-Article))</code>, the total abstract info-structure for the article, \"Potbellied Pigs.\"")
(comment nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure "An instance of #$QuaternaryPredicate used to individuate instances of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure.
<p><code>(#$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure CIS N TYPE STRUCTURE)</code> means that <code>CIS</code>, an instance of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure is the <code>N</code>th occurrence of the specialization <code>TYPE</code> of #$AbstractInformationStructure in <code>STRUCTURE</code>.
<p>For example, 
<pre>
   (#$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure 
     (#$TextualOccurrenceFn 10 \"green eggs and ham\" (#$AISForFn GreenEggsAndHam)) 54 #$NounPhrase (#$AISForFn GreenEggsAndHam))</pre>
means that the 10th occurrence of the string \"green eggs and ham\" is the 54th occurrence of a noun phrase in the #$AbstractInformationStructure of the work <i>Green Eggs and Ham<i>.")
(comment cisIsAnOccurrenceOfStructureType "<code>(#$cisIsAnOccurrenceOfStructureType CIS STRUCTURE-TYPE)</code> means that <code>CIS</code>, an instance of

#$ContextualizedInformationStructure, is a contextualized occurrence of some instance <code>STRUCTURE</code> of <code>STRUCTURE-TYPE</code>.  For example, the third occurrence of the string \"eggs\" in the structure of the book <i>Green Eggs and Ham</i> is an occurrence of #$NounPhrase.")
(comment cisContainsStructure "<code>(#$cisContainsStructure CIS STRUCTURE)</code> means that the instance <code>CIS</code> of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure contains (has a sub-structure (see #$subStructures) <code>STRUCTURE</code>, an instance of #$AbstractStructure.")
(comment AnimalShapedThing "A specialization of #$ShapedObject. Each instance of this collection is an object with a shape that resembles the typical shape of some animal.")
(comment anatomicallyExteriorTo "(#$anatomicallyExteriorTo EXTERIOR-OBJ INTERIOR-OBJ) means that EXTERIOR-OBJ lies in the #$ExteriorDirection of INTERIOR-OBJ, when viewed in the frame of reference of the object with #$AnimalBodyShapeType (often, a #$HomoSapiens) that they are both located in.  Note that INTERIOR-OBJ is therefore interior to EXTERIOR-OBJ; thus, no distinct predicate is needed to represent `interior to'.  EXTERIOR-OBJ and INTERIOR-OBJ are often, but not always, #$AnimalBodyRegions.")
(comment oughtToBe-SitTypeRolePlayerOfType "(#$oughtToBe-SitTypeRolePlayerOfType <b>SIT-TYPE</b> <b>ROLE</b>
<b>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</b>) means that, given any instance <b>INS</b> of <b>SIT-TYPE</b>, at
least one object playing <b>ROLE</b> in <b>INS</b> should be an instance of
<b>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</b>. That is, there should be an instance <b>OBLIGATORY-INS</b>
of <b>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</b> such that
(<b>ROLE</b> <b>INS</b> <b>OBLIGATORY-INS</b>). Assertions involving this predicate --
which specify what <i>should</i> be the case -- should be made in a
normative context, e.g., one that reflects the contents of a
particular #$CodeOfConduct.")
(comment forbidden-SitTypeRolePlayerOfType "(#$forbidden-SitTypeRolePlayerOfType <b>SIT-TYPE</b> <b>ROLE</b>
<b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b>) means that in no instance of <b>SIT-TYPE</b>
should any instance of <b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b> play
<b>ROLE</b>.  That is, there should be no instance <b>INS</b> of
<b>SIT-TYPE</b> and <b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b> of <b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b> such
that
(<b>ROLE</b> <b>INS</b> <b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b>).  Assertions involving this predicate --
which specify what <i>should</i> not be the case -- should be made in
a normative context, e.g., one that reflects the contents of a
particular #$CodeOfConduct.")
(comment oughtToBe-SitRelationToType "(#$oughtToBe-SitRelationToType <b>SIT</b> <b>BIN-PRED</b>
<b>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</b>) means that there should be an instance
<b>OBLIGATORY-INS</b> of <b>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</b> such that
(<b>BIN-PRED</b> <b>SIT</b> <b>OBLIGATORY-INS</b>).
#$oughtToBe-SitRelationToType is a very general requirement predicate
most useful for querying.  Statements of requirements should usually
employ specializations of this predicate.  E.g., if <b>BIN-PRED</b> is
an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate, use
#$oughtToBe-SitRolePlayerOfType.  If <b>OBLIGATORY-INS</b> is a type
of #$Situation (e.g., one an instance of which should be a #$subEvents
of <b>SIT</b>), use #$oughtToBe-SitRelationToSitType.")
(comment forbidden-SitRolePlayerOfType "(#$forbidden-SitRolePlayerOfType <b>SIT</b> <b>ROLE</b>
<b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b>) means that no instance of <b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b>
should play <b>ROLE</b> in <b>SIT</b>.  In other words, there should
be no <b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b> such that (<b>ROLE</b> <b>SIT</b>
<b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b>). Assertions involving this predicate -- which
specify what <i>should</i> not be the case -- should be made in a
normative context, e.g., one that reflects the contents of a
particular #$CodeOfConduct. Usually, <b>SIT</b> will be subject to
such a prohibition in virtue of satisfying some general description,
e.g., one stated in the antecedent of a #$CycLRuleAssertion.")
(comment forbidden-SitRelationToType "(#$forbidden-SitRelationToType <b>SIT</b> <b>BIN-PRED</b> <b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b>) means
that there should be no instance <b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b> of <b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b> such
that (<b>BIN-PRED</b> <b>SIT</b> <b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b>).  #$forbidden-SitRelationToType is a
very general prohibition predicate most useful for querying.
Statements of prohibitions should usually employ specializations of
this predicate.  E.g., if <b>BIN-PRED</b> is an instance of
#$BinaryRolePredicate, use #$forbidden-SitRolePlayerOfType.  If
<b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b> is a type of #$Situation (e.g., one no instance of which
may be a #$subEvents of <b>SIT</b>), use #$forbidden-SitRelationToSitType.")
(comment SIHMSemanticClosenessMeasure "The collection of predicates that indicate some measure of semantic closeness between instances of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory.  \"Semantic closeness\" in this context is cashed out in terms of the degree to which the contents of any pair of these microtheories, given their role in the interpretation of some instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg, support one another.")
(comment sksiSaturateRule "This predicate is used to trigger SKSI's database saturation
capability. If <code><RULE></code> is a CycL conditional formula with
free variables in its consequent, <code><SOURCE_MT></code> and
<code><TARGET_MT></code> are microtheories, then asking a query such
as
<p>
<code>
(#$sksiSaturateRule <RULE> <SOURCE_MT> <TARGET_MT>)
</code>
<p>
will recursively ask the antecedent of <code><RULE></code> in the
<code><SOURCE_MT></code>, inheriting all of its other inference
parameters from the parent query, and then via SKSI attempt to assert
the consequent bindings for <code><RULE></code> into any databases
relevent within the context of <code><TARGET_MT></code>. The
consequent bindings are returned as the top level query results.")
(comment coreConceptOfDomain "(#$coreConceptOfDomain ?CONCEPT ?DOMAIN) means that ?CONCEPT belongs to the domain, ?DOMAIN.  For example, (#$coreConceptOfDomain #$properPartOfSpaceRegion #$MereotopologyConcept) means that #$properPartOfSpaceRegion is a part of the domain of mereotopology.")
(comment conceptOfDomain "(#$conceptOfDomain ?CONCEPT ?DOMAIN) means that ?CONCEPT is associated with the domain, ?DOMAIN, either because it is one of the core concepts in the domain (see #$coreConceptOfDomain), or because it is required in order to state useful facts about a concept which is actually part of DOMAIN (see #$peripheralConceptOfDomain).  #$conceptOfDomain is a common generalization (#$genlPreds) of both #$coreConceptOfDomain and #$peripheralConceptOfDomain.")
(comment RemovingMaterialFromAContainer "A specialization of #$RemovingSomething.  Each instance of 
#$RemovingMaterialFromAContainer involves a decrease in the amount
of material in a container.  Someone eating food off a 
plate or siphoning water from a pool are examples 
of the process of #$RemovingMaterialFromAContainer.")
(comment sihmsMutuallySupportive-RelationInvokesType "An instance of #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate and #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.  <code>(#$sihmsMutuallySupportive-RelationInvokesType SIHM-1 SIHM-2)</code> means that there are two instances of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg <code>PEG-1</code> and <code>PEG-2</code> such that one is a proper sub-expression of the other (see #$properSubExpressions) and the interpretations of those pegs captured in the instances <code>SIMH-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory support one-another, in the sense that one interpretation contains a relation that invokes (see #$relationInvokesType) the second interpretaton.  <p>For example, where <code>(#$interpretationMtContent-CycL SIHM-1
(#$naturalResourcesInRegion (#$TerritoryFn #$Angola) #$Diamond))</code> and <code>(#$interpretationMtContent-CycL SIHM-2 #$NaturalResourceType)</code>, it follows that <code>(#$sihmsMutuallySupportive-RelationInvokesType SIHM-1 SIHM-2)</code>.")
(comment causes-EventEvent "A #$CausationPredicate and specialization of #$causes-SitSit and #$eventOutcomes (qq.v.) that holds between two #$Events.  <code>(#$causes-EventEvent CAUSE EFFECT)</code> means that the event <code>CAUSE</code> was sufficient to cause the event <code>EFFECT</code>.  
<p>
See the shared #$NoteAboutCausationPredicates for a map of related causation predicates.")
(comment primaryObjectMoving "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$objectMoving (q.v.) that is used to specify a focal object that is moving in a particular #$MovementEvent. <code>(#$primaryObjectMoving MOVE OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is in motion at some point during the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> and this movement is focal in <code>MOVE</code>.  
<p>
<code>OBJECT</code> moves from the #$fromLocation to the #$toLocation.  <code>OBJECT</code>'s motion in <code>MOVE</code> may be translational, rotational, or some combination of these. <code>OBJECT</code> may be a solid (such as an animal, an automobile, or a tennis ball) or a fluid (such as lava flowing or a warm air mass rising) -- but must be #$PartiallyTangible. If a #$Group of objects is moving along the same trajectory, that #$Group, each #$subGroups, and each member of that <code>GROUP</code> is a #$primaryObjectMoving in the movement.  An individual part of a #$primaryObjectMoving may be but is not necessarily an #$objectMoving in <code>MOVE</code>.  An object contained by a #$primaryObjectMoving is generally, but not always, also a #$primaryObjectMoving in the same event.  Other objects moving in the #$MovementEvent are referred to by the more general predicate, #$objectMoving.  A #$transporter which follows a separate #$Trajectory (e.g. a #$Ladle) or a conveyor which engages in a different form of motion (e.g. a rotating #$ConveyorBelt that translates a load) is not a #$primaryObjectMoving in the conveying event.  Any #$objectMoving in a particular event is a #$primaryObjectMoving in one or more #$subEvents.")
(comment inputsCommitted "A specialization of #$inputs and #$commitsForFutureUses that is used to state that an input (see #$inputs) to an event is incorporated into some output (see #$outputs) of that event, but remains recognizable rather than being destroyed.  <code>(#$inputsCommitted EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> exists before <code>EVENT</code> and continues to exist afterwards, and as a result of <code>EVENT</code>, <code>OBJECT</code> becomes incorporated into something created during <code>EVENT</code>.  Once incorporated into the output of <code>EVENT</code>, <code>OBJECT</code> can't be independently incorporated in any other creation event.  
<p>
For example, bricks that are used to build a wall continue to exist as bricks once the wall has been built.  While a part of the wall, a brick cannot be used as an independent input in another creation event. (See also #$outputsCreated.)
<p>
Note that there is a grey area between #$inputsCommitted and #$inputsDestroyed; the less possible it is to take apart the relevant #$outputs of <code>EVENT</code> and get <code>OBJECT</code> back as an independent thing, the more likely it is that the relationship between <code>EVENT</code> and <code>OBJECT</code> should be #$inputsDestroyed, rather than #$inputsCommitted.")
(comment motionPathway-Partial "An #$ActorSlot and specialization of #$genericPathway-Partial that relates a #$Movement-TranslationEvent to a #$Path-Spatial such that at least part of the movement is along part of the path.  It can be used to indicate one of the paths followed in a movement event.
More formally, <code>(#$motionPathway-Partial MOVE PATH)</code> means that <code>MOVE</code> has a trajectory (see #$trajectory-Complete) with a sub-trajectory in common with some #$subPaths of <code>PATH</code>.
<p>
For example, if a certain trip to Dallas from Austin is via highway I-35, this does not imply either that the whole journey is along I-35 (it also may be along
driveways and side streets), or that it is along all of I-35 (which would take you from the Mexican border all the way to the Canadian border).  A part of the journey is along a part of I-35.  
<p>
Contrast this predicate with #$motionPathway-Complete, which relates a movement event to a spatial path followed throughout all of the movement.")
(comment genericPathway-Partial "An #$ActorSlot that relates a \"transfer\" event of any sort (see #$GeneralizedTransfer) to a spatial path (see #$Path-Spatial) that is followed during at least part of the event.  More formally, <code>(#$genericPathway-Partial TRANS PATH)</code> means that at least part of the route of <code>TRANS</code> is spatial, and at least part of this part follows a part of <code>PATH</code>.
<p>
For example, if a certain trip <code>TRANS</code> takes a person from a home in Austin to one in Dallas via (mostly) I-35, part of the trip may occur along highways other than I-35 and part of it will occur along ordinary streets in Austin and Dallas.  Thus only part of <code>TRANS</code> occurs along I-35.  We may represent this in CycL by stating <code>(#$genericPathway-Partial TRANS Interstate35)</code>.  As another example, consider an e-mail sent from one PC to another. Suppose this transfer of information <code>INFOTRANS</code> occurs along several distinct cables. If one of these cables is <code>CABLE001</code>, then we may correctly assert <code>(#$genericPathway-Partial INFOTRANS CABLE001)</code>. 
<p>
See also #$genericPathway-Complete and its #$cyclistNotes for a comparison with this predicate.  Note that in both examples above, it would be incorrect to replace #$genericPathway-Partial with #$genericPathway-Complete, since the entire transfers did not occur along I-35 and <code>CABLE001</code>, respectively. 
<p>
See also the more specific predicates #$infoPathway-Partial and #$motionPathway-Partial.")
(comment infoPathway-Partial "An #$ActorSlot and specialization of #$genericPathway-Partial that relates an #$InformationTransferEvent to a #$Path-Spatial that is followed, at least in part, during the event. <code>(#$infoPathway-Partial TRANS PATH)</code> means that part of the route of <code>TRANS</code> is spatial and follows part of <code>PATH</code>.  
<p>
For example, consider a transfer <code>INFOTRANS</code> in which an e-mail is sent from one PC to another, and the information content of the #$InformationBearingThing transferred is <i>successfully</i> transferred.  Suppose <code>INFOTRANS</code> is routed along several distinct cables.  If one of these cables is <code>CABLE001</code>, then <code>(#$infoPathway-Partial INFOTRANS CABLE001)</code> holds.  
<p>
See also #$infoPathway-Complete.  Note that in the example above it would be incorrect to replace #$infoPathway-Partial with #$infoPathway-Complete, since the entire transfer did not occur along <code>CABLE001</code>.")
(comment CanopyClosure-Sparse "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. #$CanopyClosure-Sparse is the collection of #$OutdoorLocations that are 5-25% covered by the crowns of trees. Such locations offer poor concealment from aerial detection.")
(comment CanopyClosure-Congested "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. #$CanopyClosure-Congested is the collection of #$OutdoorLocations that are 75-100% covered by the crowns of trees. Such locations offer excellent concealment from aerial detection.")
(comment RegionTypeByCanopyClosure "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$RegionTypeByCanopyClosure is a collection of instances of #$GeographicalRegion that share a degree of canopy closure. 'Canopy closure' refers to the amount of a given area that is covered by the crowns of trees.")
(comment CanopyClosure-Moderate "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. #$CanopyClosure-Moderate is the collection of #$OutdoorLocations that are 25-50% covered by the crowns of trees. Such locations offer fair concealment from aerial detection.")
(comment CanopyClosure-Open "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. #$CanopyClosure-Open is the collection of #$OutdoorLocations that are 0-5% covered by the crowns of trees. Such locations offer extremely poor to no concealment from aerial detection.")
(comment CanopyClosure-Dense "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. #$CanopyClosure-Dense is the collection of #$OutdoorLocations that 50-75% covered by the crowns of trees. Such locations offer good concealment from aerial detection.")
(comment UrbanAreaTypeByRoofCoverage "A collection of collections. Each instance of  #$UrbanAreaTypeByRoofCoverage is a type of #$UrbanArea with a particular degree of roof coverage.  The degree of roof coverage is determined by the percentage of buildings or other over-hangs in a given area.")
(comment RoofCoverage-Sparse "A specialization of #$UrbanArea. #$RoofCoverage-Sparse is the collection #$UrbanAreas that are characterized by roof coverage closure of 2-20%. Such regions offer fair concealment from aerial detection and poor cover from direct fire.")
(comment RoofCoverage-Dense "A specialization of #$UrbanArea. #$RoofCoverage-Dense is the collection
#$UrbanAreas that are characterized by roof coverage closure of over
40%. Such regions offer excellent concealment from aerial detection and
good cover from direct fire.")
(comment RoofCoverage-Moderate "A specialization of #$UrbanArea. #$RoofCoverage-Moderate is the collection
#$UrbanAreas that are characterized by roof coverage closure of
20-40%. Such regions offer good concealment from aerial detection and
fair cover from direct fire.")
(comment RoofCoverage-Negligible "A specialization of #$UrbanArea. #$RoofCoverage-Negligible is the collection
#$UrbanAreas that are characterized by roof coverage closure of
0-2%. Such regions offer extremely poor concealment from aerial detection and
poor cover from direct fire.")
(comment WetlandType "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$WetlandType is a type of #$WetlandRegion. Instances include #$SaltMarsh and #$Swamp.")
(comment OrganicSoilRegion "#$OrganicSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions characterized by soils, like top soil and peat, that are organic and usually composed of decaying vegetation and other #$OrganicMaterial, and exhibit a very strong odor.")
(comment CoarseGrainedSoilRegion "A specialization of #$InorganicSoilRegion. #$CoarseGrainedSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of at least 50% gravel or sand.")
(comment WellGradedSoilRegion "A specialization of #$CoarseGrainedWithoutFinesSoilRegion. #$WellGradedSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of many different particle sizes and contains little or no fines. Well-graded soil is designated by the secondary letter of (W) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem.")
(comment CoarseGrainedWithFinesSoilRegion "A specialization of #$CoarseGrainedSoilRegion. #$CoarseGrainedWithFinesSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of gravel or sand with 5% or more of fines. In other words, coarse-grained soil that is intermixed with silt, clay, or both.")
(comment ClayishSoilRegion "A specialization of #$FineGrainedSoilRegion. #$ClayishSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil  generally consists of microscopic particles.  Its plasticity and adhesiveness are outstanding characteristics.  Depending on mineral composition and proportion of coarser grains, clayish soils vary from lean (low plasticity) to fat (high plasticity).  Many clayish soils which are brittle or stiff in their undisturbed state become soft and plastic when worked.  When thoroughly dry, clayish soils provide a hard surface with excellent trafficability; however, it is seldom dry except in arid climates.  It absorbs water very slowly but takes a long time to dry and is very sticky and slippery.  Slopes with a clay surface are difficult or impassable, and deep ruts form rapidly on level ground.  A combination of silt and clay makes a particularly poor surface when wet.  Clayish soil is designated by the primary letter of (C) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem.")
(comment ClayBasedFinesSoilRegion "A specialization of #$CoarseGrainedWithFinesSoilRegion. #$ClayBasedFinesSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of gravel or sand intermixed with clay. Soil with clay-based fines is designated by the secondary letter of (C) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem.")
(comment LowPlasticitySoilRegion "A specialization of #$FineGrainedSoilRegion. #$LowPlasticitySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil has low plasticity, meaning it consists of silt or clay with a liquid limit of less than 50%. Low-plasiticty soil is designated by the secondary letter of (L) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem.")
(comment GravellySoilRegion "A specialization of #$CoarseGrainedSoilRegion. #$GravellySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is composed of angular to rounded, bulky rock particles ranging in size from about 0.6 to 7.6 cm in diameter. Gravelly soil is designated by the primary letter of (G) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem. It offers excellent traction for tracked vehicles; however, if it is not mixed with other soil, the loose particles may roll under pressure, hampering the movement of wheeled vehicles. Weather has little or no effect on its trafficability.")
(comment InorganicSoilRegion "#$InorganicSoilRegion is the collection of GeographicalRegions where the soil (#$Soil-Generic) which is primarily composed of #$InorganicMaterial.  Coarse-grained soils are always inorganic, as are most silts and clays.")
(comment FineGrainedSoilRegion "#$FineGrainedSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is composed of at least 50% silt or clay.")
(comment SiltySoilRegion "A specialization of #$FineGrainedSoilRegion. #$SiltySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is composed of silt. Silty soil is designated by the primary letter of (M) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem. When dry, silty soil provides excellent trafficability, although it is very dusty. However, it absorbs water quickly and turns to a deep, soft mud (a quick condition), which is definite obstacle to movement.")
(comment PoorlyGradedSoilRegion "A specialization of #$CoarseGrainedWithoutFinesSoilRegion. #$PoorlyGradedSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of either gravel or sand, few different particle sizes, and little or no fines. Poorly-graded soil is designated by the secondary letter of (P) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem.")
(comment SiltBasedFinesSoilRegion "A specialization of #$CoarseGrainedWithFinesSoilRegion. #$SiltBasedFinesSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of gravel or sand intermixed with silt. Soil with silt-based fines is designated by the secondary letter of (M) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem.")
(comment CoarseGrainedWithoutFinesSoilRegion "A specialization of #$CoarseGrainedSoilRegion. #$CoarseGrainedWithoutFinesSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of 5% or less fines (clay or silt). Sometimes soil with this quality is referred to as 'clean soil.'")
(comment SandySoilRegion "A specialization of both #$CoarseGrainedSoilRegion and #$SoftTerrain. #$SandySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil consists of sand: rock grains 0.6cm in diameter and smaller. Sandy soil is designated by the primary letter of (G) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem. Sand is classified as coarse, medium, or fine, and is angular or rounded. When wet enough to become compacted or when mixed with clay, sand provides excellent trafficability. Very dry, loose sand is an obstacle to vehicles, particularly on slopes. Under wet conditions, remoldable sands react to traffic in a manner similar to fine-grained soils.")
(comment HighPlasticitySoilRegion "A specialization of #$FineGrainedSoilRegion. #$HighPlasticitySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil has high plasticity, meaning it consists of silt or clay with a liquid limit of more than 50%. Low-plasiticty soil is designated by the secondary letter of (H) in the #$UnifiedSoilClassificationSystem.")
(comment FatClaySoilRegion "#$FatClaySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is fat clay: fine-grained, inorganic, with high plasticity. ")
(comment ClaySoilRegion "#$ClaySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is clay: fine-grained, inorganic, with low to moderate plasticity.")
(comment ClayishGravelSoilRegion "#$ClayishGravelSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is clayish gravel: predominantly gravel with a percentage of clay intermixed.")
(comment SiltyGravelSoilRegion "#$SiltyGravelSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is silty gravel: predominantly gravel with a percentage of silt intermixed.")
(comment PoorlyGradedGravelSoilRegion "#$PoorlyGradedGravelSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is poorly graded gravel: coarse-grained, with few different particle sizes, and little or no intermixed fines.")
(comment WellGradedGravelSoilRegion "#$WellGradedGravelSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is well-graded gravel: coarse-grained, with many different particle sizes, and little or no intermixed fines.")
(comment InorganicSiltSoilRegion "#$InorganicSiltSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is inorganic silt: fine-grained, inorganic, with moderate to high plasticity.")
(comment SiltSoilRegion "#$SiltSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is silt: fine-grained, inorganic, with slight to no plasticity.")
(comment FatOrganicClaySoilRegion "#$FatOrganicClaySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is fat organic clay: fine-grained, organic, with high plasticity.")
(comment OrganicSiltSoilRegion "#$OrganicSiltSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is organic silt: fine-grained, organic, with low plasticity.")
(comment PeatSoilRegion "#$PeatSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is peat: highly organic with high plasticity.")
(comment RockySoilRegion "#$RockySoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is terrain consists of large rock particles, more than 7.6 cm in diameter. and hardly classifies as a soil.")
(comment ClayishSandSoilRegion "#$ClayishSandSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is predominantly sand with a percentage of clay intermixed.")
(comment SiltySandSoilRegion "#$SiltySandSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is predominantly sand with a percentage of silt intermixed.")
(comment PoorlyGradedSandSoilRegion "#$PoorlyGradedSandSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is poorly graded sand: coarse-grained, with few different particle sizes, and little or no intermixed fines.")
(comment WellGradedSandSoilRegion "#$WellGradedSandSoilRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions where the soil is well-graded sand: coarse-grained, with many different particle sizes, and little or no intermixed fines.")
(comment VeryPoorlyDrainedRegion "A specialization #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$VeryPoorlyDrainedRegion are regions where water is at or near the soil surface during much of the growing season.                       Internal free water is shallow and persistent or permanent.  Unless the           soil is artificially drained, most mesophytic crops cannot be grown.           Commonly, the soil occupies a depression, or is level.  If rainfall is           persistent or high, the soil can be sloping.  (<i>Field Book for Describing           and Sampling Soils, U.S. Department of Agriculture</i>)")
(comment ExcessivelyDrainedRegion "A specialization #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$ExcessivelyDrainedRegion are regions where water moves through the soil very rapidly. Internal free-water commonly is very rare or very deep. The soils are commonly coarse-textured, have very high saturated hydraulic conductivity, and lack redoximorphic features. (<i>Field Book for Describing and Sampling Soils, U.S. Department of Agriculture</i>)")
(comment ModeratelyWellDrainedRegion "A specialization #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$ModeratelyWellDrainedRegion are regions where water moves through the soil slowly during some periods of the year. Internal free-water commonly is moderately deep and may be transitory or permanent. The soil is wet for only a short time within the rooting depth during the growing season. The soil commonly has a moderately low, or lower, saturated hydraulic conductivity within one meter of the surface, or periodically receives high rainfall, or both. (<i>Field Book for Describing and Sampling Soils, U.S. Department of Agriculture</i>)")
(comment PoorlyDrainedRegion "A specialization #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$PoorlyDrainedRegion are regions where the soil is wet at shallow depths periodically during the growing season or remains wet for long periods. Interna free-water is shallow or very shallow and common or persistent. Unless the soil is artifically drained, most mesophytic crops cannot be grown. The soil, however, is not continuously wet directly below plow depth. The water table is commonly the result of low or very low saturated hydraulic conductivity, persistent rainfall, or a combination of both factors. (<i>Field Book for Describing and Sampling Soils, U.S. Department of Agriculture</i>)")
(comment SomewhatExcessivelyDrainedRegion "A specialization #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$SomewhatExcessivelyDrainedRegion are regions where water moves through the soil rapidly. Internal free water commonly is very rare, or very deep. The soils are commonly coarse-textured, have high saturated hydraulic conductivity, and lack redoximorphic features. (<i>Field Book for Describing and Sampling Soils, U.S. Department of Agriculture</i>)")
(comment SomewhatPoorlyDrainedRegion "A specialization #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$SomewhatPoorlyDrainedRegion are regions where the soil is wet at shallow depths for significant periods during the growing season. Internal free-water is commonly shallow to moderately deep and transitory to permanent. Unless the soil is artificially drained, the growth of most mesophytic plants is markedly restricted. The soil commonly has (i) a low to very low saturated hydraulic conductivity, (ii) a high water table, (iii) receives water from a lateral flow, (iv) experiences persistent rainfall, or a combination of these factors. (<i>Field Book for Describing and Sampling Soils, U.S. Department of Agriculture</i>)")
(comment WellDrainedRegion "A specialization #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$WellDrainedRegion are regions where water moves through the soil readily, but not rapidly. Internal free-water commonly is deep or very deep available to plants in humid regions during much of the growing season. Wetness does not inhibit growth of roots for significant periods during most growing seasons. The soil is deep to, or lacks redoximorphic features. (<i>Field Book for Describing and Sampling Soils, U.S. Department of Agriculture</i>)")
(comment PeriodicallyFloodedRegion "An instance of #$RegionTypeByTerrain. #$PeriodicallyFloodedRegion is the collection of #$GeographicalRegions characterized by periodic seasonal flooding.  In many cases, such regions can almost become standing bodies of water during the flood season.  In some cases the periodic flooding may be the deliberate work of human agents.")
(comment MudFlat "A specialization of #$FlatRegion. Each instance of #$MudFlat is a relatively level area of mud either between
high and low tide lines, or subject to flooding.")
(comment SoftTerrain "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each instance of #$SoftTerrain is a region where the ground is not rigid or solid at the surface.")
(comment SnowCoveredArea "A specialization of #$SoftTerrain. Each instance of #$SnowCoveredArea is a region with a standing layer of snow over
it")
(comment RubbleStrewnArea "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion.  Each instance of #$RubbleStrewnArea is a region strewn with the remains of
#$ConstructionArtifacts, to the extent that progress over the
terrain might be slowed for the average person or vehicle.")
(comment MonsoonForest "A specialization of #$Forest. Each instance of #$MonsoonForest is a tropical moist forest that gets 4-12 feet of rain annually. They are distinguished from #$TropicalRainForests by a cooler dry season. During this dry season, many trees shed some or even all their leaves, creating a seasonal reduction of canopy cover and allowing more sunlight to reach the forest floor. The increased sunlight reaching the forest floor allows the growth of vigorous understory vegetation not found in #$TropicalRainForests. Examples are
found in eastern India, in parts of Southeast Asia, and in
northeastern Australia. Growth is associated with the
#$MonsoonClimateCycles prevailing in those regions. Source: <i>The Times
Atlas of the World (1995)</i>.")
(comment DryTropicalScrubAndThornRegion "A specialization of #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion. Each instance of #$DryTropicalScrubAndThornRegion is a region having
low-growing, widely spaced shrubs, bushes, and succulents. Instances
comprise extensive areas of Central and South America, Africa, the
Indian subcontinent, and Australia. Source: <i>The Times Atlas of the
World (1995)</i>.")
(comment MuddyArea "A specialization of #$SoftTerrain. Each instance of #$MuddyArea is a region where the terrain consists primarily of mud")
(comment MinedArea "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each instance of #$MinedArea is a region (whether on land or at sea) in which #$Mine-Weapons have been placed.")
(comment PermaFrostRegion "A specialization of
#$GeographicalRegion. Each instance of #$PermaFrostRegion has a layer of permanently frozen
ground insulated by a shallow layer of soil that thaws during the
spring and summer.")
(comment DryTropicalForest "A specialization of #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion. Instances of #$DryTropicalForest are areas of semi-deciduous forest
growing in semi-arid areas of South America and the Indian
subcontinent. Rainfall in these areas usually averages less than 250mm
per annum. Thorny scrub and low- to medium-sized trees with thick bark
and deep roots characterize the vegetation. Source: <i>The Times Atlas of
the World (1995)</i>.")
(comment RegionTypeByHilliness "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$RegionTypeByHilliness is a collection of #$GeographicalRegions with a common degree of hilliness.")
(comment FlatRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Instances of #$FlatRegion are regions that are level, without substantial hills")
(comment HillyRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each instance of #$HillyRegion is a region consisting of many hills (not flat, not mountainous).  Instances have both up and down grades.")
(comment HighMountainousRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each instance of #$HighMountainousRegion is a region on the upper slopes of mountains at mid- and high-altitude.  Instances have little plant growth apart from mosses and lichens.")
(comment ratingConeIndex "A #$TernaryPredicate that expresses the rating cone index of a soil type at a given degree of wetness. (#$ratingConeIndex SOIL-TYPE WETNESS INDEX) means that SOIL-TYPE has a rating cone index of INDEX when it has the degree of wetness WETNESS. The rating cone index of a soil type is an indicator of its trafficability. See also #$vehicleConeIndex.")
(comment terrainDecompositions "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates region types to their defining characteristics. (#$terrainDecompositions TERRAIN-TYPE FEATURE-TYPE) means that if a particular #$GeographicalRegion REGION is an instance of TERRAIN-TYPE, then there exists an instance FEATURE of FEATURE-TYPE such that (#$physicalDecompositions REGION FEATURE). For example, (#$terrainDecompositions #$Savannah (#$MobFn #$Grass-Plant)) means that grass can be found in every savannah.")
(comment terrainFeatures-Soil "A #$BinaryPredicate that holds between a #$GeographicalRegion and its dominant soil type. (#$terrainFeatures-Soil REGION SOIL-TYPE) means that #$GeographicalRegion REGION is an instance of the #$RegionTypeBySoilType SOIL-TYPE. For example, (#$terrainFeatures-Soil #$GreatSandyDesert #$SandySoilRegion) means that the Great Sandy Desert is a sandy soil region.")
(comment vehicleTypeForTerrainType "A #$BinaryPredicate that holds between types of vehicles and the types of terrain where they are suitable for travel. (#$vehicleTypeForTerrainType VEHICLE-TYPE TERRAIN-TYPE) means that some specialization of #$LandTransportationDevice VEHICLE-TYPE is suitable for travel over the specialization of #$RegionTypeByTerrain TERRAIN-TYPE.")
(comment UsingAForFn "This function can be used to denote the collection of instances of #$Event in which some instance of #$Agent-PartiallyTangible deliberately uses some instance of a subcollection of #$SomethingExisting in some subcollection of #$PurposefulAction. (#$UsingAForFn OBJ_TYPE ACT_TYPE ROLE) denotes the subcollection of  #$PurposefulAction in which an instance OBJ of the  subcollection OBJ_TYPE of #$SomethingExisting is used in an instance ACT of some subcollection ACT_TYPE of #$PurposefulAction in the instance ROLE of #$Role. For example,  (#$UsingAForFn #$Hammer #$ConstructingABuilding #$deviceUsed) denotes the subcollection of #$PurposefulAction in which an instance of #$Hammer is used in an instance of #$ConstructingABuilding as a #$deviceUsed. (#$UsingAForFn #$Recipe-CW #$CookingFood #$instructionsFollowed) denotes the subcollection of #$PurposefulAction in which an instance of #$Recipe-CW is used in an instance of #$CookingFood as the #$instructionsFollowed.  Note that ACT_TYPE need not be the  #$typePrimaryFunction for OBJ_TYPE, e.g. one could in principle reify (#$UsingAForFn #$HuntingKnife #$Whittling #$deviceUsed), though #$Whittling is not a #$typePrimaryFunction of #$HuntingKnife.")
(comment TextString "A subcollection of #$CharacterString.  Each instance of #$TextString is a #$CharacterString that constitutes a                                                                                                   meaningful unit (with respect to some actual language or other                                                                                                             #$CommunicationConvention), such as words, phrases, sentences,                                                                                                             multi-sentence constructions, or symbols that contribute to the meaning of                                                                                                 phrases or sentences (including syncategormatic objects such as                                                                                                            punctuation symbols).  Formal languages may define multi-symbol constructs                                                                                                  that have meaning but are not considered words -- these, too are                                                                                                          
instances of #$TextString. For example, this sequence of characters:
<pre>
  er#q2
</pre>
is a #$CharacterString but <i>not</i> (as far as I know) a #$TextString. Conversely, the sentence you are now reading is a #$TextString, as is the text of this entire comment. 
<p>
Note that, as with all #$CharacterStrings, a #$TextString is a sequence of abstract symbols, not a tangible instantiation of it (cf. #$CharacterStringToken).")
(comment InferenceSupportedCollection "The collection of all CycL terms that denote collections, where these terms are supported in some way by the #$CycInferenceEngine.  If a term T denoting the collection COL is an #$InferenceSupportedCollection, this means that the inference engine treats instances of COL specially.")
(comment HTMLStringTypeByTagFn "<code>(#$HTMLStringTypeByTagFn TAG)</code> denotes the collection of instances of #$CharacterString that are well-formed HTML strings for which <code>TAG</code> is the opening tag.  See also #$htmlStringTypeTag, the predicate that corresponds to this function (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate).")
(comment htmlStringTypeTag "<code>(#$htmlStringTypeTag STRING-TYPE TAG)</code> identifies <code>STRING-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$CharacterString that are well-formed HTML strings for which <code>TAG</code> is the opening tag.  See also #$HTMLStringTypeByTagFn, the function for which this is the corresponding predicate (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate).")
(comment HTMLStringTypeByTag "The collection of specializations <code>SPEC</code> of #$CharacterString, such that each instance of <code>SPEC</code> is an HTML string with the same  outermost tag (see #$HTMLTag).")
(comment forbidden-SitRelationToSitType "(#$forbidden-SitRelationToSitType <b>SIT</b> <b>PRED</b>
<b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b>) means that there should be no instance
<b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b> of <b>FORBIDDEN-TYPE</b> such that
(<b>PRED</b> <b>SIT</b> <b>FORBIDDEN-INS</b>).  Assertions involving
this predicate -- which specify what <i>should</i> not be the case --
should be made in a normative context, e.g., one that reflects the
contents of a particular $#CodeOfConduct.  Usually, <b>SIT</b> will be
subject to such a prohibition in virtue of satisfying some general
description, e.g., one stated in the antecedent of a
#$CycLRuleAssertion. ")
(comment oughtToBe-SitRelationToSitType "(#$oughtToBe-SitRelationToSitType <b>SIT</b> <b>PRED</b>
<b>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</b>) means that there should be an instance <b>OBLIGATORY-INS </b>
of <b>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</b> such that (<b>PRED</b> <b>SIT</b>
<b>OBLIGATORY-INS</b>). Assertions involving this predicate -- which
specify what <i>should</i> be the case -- should be made in a
normative context, e.g., one that reflects the contents of a
particular #$CodeOfConduct.  Usually, <b>SIT</b> will be subject to
such a requirement in virtue of satisfying some general description,
e.g., one stated in the antecedent of a #$CycLRuleAssertion. ")
(comment ConvertingASpecificationToAnAIS "The collection of all events in which an agent's knowledge of some #$Specification is used to generate an #$AbstractInformationStructure for some work that is motivated by the content of that specification.  For example, an instance of #$InformationNeed can lead to the generation of instances of #$TextString for use with a #$SearchEngine.")
(comment InformationNeed "A subcollection of #$NormativeSpecification.  Instances of #$InformationNeed represent a description of the types of knowledge that an agent needs in relation to some goal.  An example of one would be a list of questions that need to be answered.")
(comment oughtToBe-SubSitTypeRelationToSubSitType "(#$oughtToBe-SubSitTypeRelationToSubSitType <b>SIT</b>
<b>SUB-TYPE1</b> <b>PRED</b> <b>SUB-TYPE2</b>) means that, for
any instance <b>SUB-INS1</b> of <b>SUB-TYPE1</b> that occurs as a
#$subSituations of <b>SIT</b>, there should be an instance
<b>SUB-INS2</b> of <b>SUB-TYPE2</b> such that (<b>PRED</b>
<b>SUB-INS1</b> <b>SUB-INS2</b>).  Assertions involving this predicate
-- which specify what <i>should</i> be the case -- should be made in a
normative context, e.g., one that reflects the contents of a
particular #$CodeOfConduct.")
(comment ObjectTypeBySurfaceFeature "A second-order collection and a specialization of #$PartiallyTangibleTypeByPhysicalFeature (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ObjectTypeBySurfaceFeature is a collection of objects that share a particular surface feature.  Thus, these collections represent various surface properties of objects.  Instances include #$Slippery, #$Paved, and #$Imprinted.  To state that an object has a particular surface feature, use #$surfaceFeatureOfObject.  To state that the surface itself is of a certain sort, use #$surfaceFeatureOfSurface.")
(comment CavityWithWalls "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible and #$Cavity.  Each instance of #$CavityWithWalls is an individual that spatially subsumes both the intangible space region \"inside\" a cavity (see #$CavityInteriorRegion) and the surfaces that constitute the inner walls of the cavity.  Compare #$CavityWithWalls to the collection #$Cavity; some instances of #$Cavity have their walls as parts, and some do not.")
(comment Traversal "The collection of all traversals of paths and networks.  A traversal is a trace of a pathway along pre-existing #$Path-Generics.  It is the \"path-representation\" of certain actual trajectories (instances of #$Trajectory) that are confined to paths.  Different actual trajectories may have the same traversal; to see this, consider two cars driving along a block of 10th St.  The actual car movements have different trajectories -- say, one drove straight while the other switched lanes many times.  The two different trajectories are both characterized by the same traversal of that block of 10th Street.  
<p>
A traversal of a network of paths can be represented by (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) where <b>CHAIN</b> is a #$PathChain.  We may choose to use a more detailed representation of the traversal, i.e. use a #$PathChain made of more, but shorter, paths.  For example, we may use several paths to replace a cycle in <b>CHAIN</b> if we want to be clear about the direction in that part of the traversal, or we may have to replace a path in <b>CHAIN</b> by several 'shorter' paths if we want to preserve the fact that the object moved backward at a small part of <b>PATH</b> and then resumed its original direction.  In summary, a traversal is a constraint on a trajectory and a characterization of it in terms of traversing pre-existing #$Path-Generics.   Technically, a #$Traversal from <b>A</b> to <b>B</b> can be expressed as (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) where <b>CHAIN</b> is a #$PathChain such that the first item in <b>CHAIN</b> is <b>A</b> and the last is <b>B</b> (we can assert: (#$traversalFrom (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) <b>A</b>) and (#$traversalTo (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) <b>B</b>)).  
<p>
Note that a #$Traversal, unlike a #$Path-Generic, can cross itself, i.e. can \"pass through\" a point on it many times.  Mathematically: given a #$PathSystem or #$CustomarySystemOfLinks, a #$Traversal of it is a directed path that has a homomorphism into that system.
<p>
Related constants include #$TraversalFn, #$traversalFrom, #$traversalTo, #$pointOnTraversal, #$subTraversals, and #$traversalPassesThrough, #$traversalInSystem.")
(comment recipientOfInfo-Intended "A specialization of both #$recipientOfInfo and #$communicationTarget that is used to indicate who among those who receive information in a communication event were also <i>intended</i> to receive the information. (#$recipientOfInfo-Intended COMM RECIPIENT) holds whenever (i) RECIPIENT successfully receives the information communicated in the communication event COMM (see #$recipientOfInfo) and (ii) RECIPIENT is one of the #$Agent-Generics that the #$senderOfInfo in                                        the #$CommunicationAct-Single COMM intended to receive the information (see #$communicationTarget).  
<p>
For example, suppose I am speaking at a closed meeting to one or more persons on classified information and, unbeknownst to me, a spy outside the room is listening in and hears everything I am saying.  If each of the persons in the meeting hears everything I am saying, then each bears the relation #$recipientOfInfo-Intended to each instance of #$CommunicationAct-Single COMM during the meeting of which I am the #$senderOfInfo.  The spy, however, bears only the relation #$recipientOfInfo to each of these instances.  Suppose, however, that one person at the meeting is hard of hearing and does not hear what I am saying.  Each event COMM is still a #$CommunicationAct-Single since it had at least one #$subEvents that is an instance of #$AccessingAnIBT (at least the spy heard what was said) but the person who is hard of hearing is a #$communicationTarget of COMM but not a #$recipientOfInfo-Intended since he or she did not receive the information, i.e. was not a #$recipientOfInfo.  
<p>
See also #$intendedAudience.")
(comment inputs "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$objectActedOn that relates a given event to an object or material that is used in that event and thereby either \"used up\" (i.e. destroyed) or incorporated into another object. <code>(#$inputs EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is a pre-existing participant in (see #$preActors) the #$CreationOrDestructionEvent <code>EVENT</code> which, during and due to <code>EVENT</code>, is either destroyed (see #$inputsDestroyed) or incorporated into a new entity (see #$inputsCommitted). 
<p>
For example, the pigments used to paint the Mona Lisa were #$inputs to Leonardo's painting process; however, his brushes were not #$inputs, even though they were changed a little by it.  In general, in instances of #$Manufacturing, materials or objects are inputs if they find their way into the product manufactured or if they are destroyed in the manufacturing process (e.g. the coke used in manufacturing steel).")
(comment outputsRemaining "A specialization of both #$outputs and #$preActors that relates physical destruction events to surviving parts of the things destroyed in those events.  <code>(#$outputsRemaining EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> persists after <code>EVENT</code>, and that it existed before <code>EVENT</code> as an identifiable part of one of the things destroyed during <code>EVENT</code> (see #$inputsDestroyed).  
<p>
Examples: (1) when a sheet of plywood is sawn through, the original board is destroyed and the remaining-outputs are the two smaller plywood pieces; (2) when a mixture undergoes a #$FluidFiltrationProcess, the mixture itself is destroyed, while both the material that was in suspension (see #$suspendedPart) and the fluid it was suspended in (see #$suspendingFluid) are remaining-outputs.")
(comment eventOccursAt "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$inRegion that relates a particular event to the instance of #$EnduringThing-Localized at which it occurs.  <code>(#$eventOccursAt EVENT LOC)</code> means that the spatial extent of <code>EVENT</code> is within <code>LOC</code>. For example, (#$eventOccursAt LomaPrietaEarthquake SanFranciscoBayArea).  
<p>
See also the more general predicate #$eventPartiallyOccursAt, which relates an event to any instance of #$SpatialThing-Localized in which some sub-event of it occurs.")
(comment communicationParticipants "A predicate for relating a #$Communicating event to an agent  participating in the event. (#$communicationParticipants COMM AGENT) means that AGENT is a participant in COMM.")
(comment recipientOfInfo "A binary predicate which relates instances of #$InformationTransferEvent to instances of #$Agent-Generic. <code>(#$recipientOfInfo TRANSFER RECIP)</code> means that the information transferred in the #$InformationTransferEvent <code>TRANSFER</code> is received by the #$Agent-Generic <code>RECIP</code>.  After <code>TRANSFER</code>, <code>RECIP</code> understands or conceives of the information transferred in <code>TRANSFER</code>; Cyc does NOT conclude that s/he #$knows the information or counts it among his/her #$beliefs, because <code>RECIP</code> might not believe it.  In receiving the information transferred, <code>RECIP</code> may be acting intentionally or unintentionally.  Note that if the communication event used in the first argument slot of this predicate is a #$CommunicationAct-Single and the recipient of the information was intended to receive the information by the originator of the communication event (see #$communicationTarget) then #$recipientOfInfo-Intended should probably be used instead of #$recipientOfInfo.  See also #$infoTransferred, and the slightly orthogonal #$intendedAudience (which relates instances of #$InformationBearingThing to instances of #$Agent-Generic).")
(comment to-Generic "<code>(#$to-Generic GEN-TRANS THING)</code> means that the #$GeneralizedTransfer <code>GEN-TRANS</code> has a destination or endpoint <code>THING</code>.  Because #$GeneralizedTransfer covers many kinds of transfer, both physical and abstract, this endpoint can be of many types.  In a #$Translocation it is a #$PartiallyTangible location (see #$toLocation), while in #$TransferringPossession it is an #$Agent-Generic (see #$toPossessor).  #$to-Generic encompasses all kinds of endpoints; as a rule, it is better to use more the specific predicates when possible.")
(comment from-Generic "<code>(#$from-Generic GEN-TRANS THING)</code> means that the #$GeneralizedTransfer <code>GEN-TRANS</code> has an origin or starting point <code>THING</code>.  Because #$GeneralizedTransfer covers many kinds of transfer, both physical and abstract, this starting point can be of many types.  In a #$Translocation it is a #$PartiallyTangible location (see #$fromLocation), while in #$TransferringPossession it is an #$Agent-Generic (see #$fromPossessor).  #$from-Generic encompasses all kinds of starting points; as a rule, it is better to use more the specific predicates when possible.")
(comment Translation-MultiTrajectory "A specialization of #$Movement-TranslationEvent.  In any instance of #$Translation-MultiTrajectory, motion may occur simultaneously along two or more instances of #$Trajectory (which are not related by sub-trajectory relationships.)  Therefore, there must be at least two #$objectMovings in such an event.  There may be more than one #$fromLocation and/or more than one #$toLocation.  Instances of #$Translation-MultiTrajectory include scattering, distributing, and collecting things. 
<p>
For example, all of the following exhibit multi-trajectory translation movements: a rainshower or snowfall; an exploding firework; water being ejected from a sprinkling system; the surrounding objects being sucked up by a tornado; participants running a 100K footrace; pool balls during the opening break.
<p>
Cf. #$Translation-SingleTrajectory.")
(comment Movement-TranslationProcess "A subcollection of #$Movement-TranslationEvents whose instances are, practically speaking, continuous processes.  In any instance of #$Movement-TranslationProcess, all time-slices of that process are also themselves instances of #$Movement-TranslationProcess.
<p>
Note that walking is a type of #$Movement-TranslationProcess, even though it involves some nonzero accelerations and jerks.  Non-examples include Captain Kirk beaming up to the Enterprise and the \"tunneling\" of an electron in a tunnel diode.")
(comment MovementOrShapeChangeEvent "A specialization of #$PhysicalEvent whose instances include translational or rotational movements of whole objects (see #$MovementEvent), objects changing shape (see #$ShapeChangeEvent), as well as events in which salient parts of an object move or change shape in relation to the reference frame of the whole object.  
<p>
For example, although a ticking grandfather clock is not normally undergoing either a #$MovementEvent or a #$ShapeChangeEvent, it can still be said to be moving -- and thus undergoing a #$MovementOrShapeChangeEvent -- in virtue of the fact that a salient part of the clock, the pendulum, is swinging.  (Of course, the pendulum itself is periodically undergoing #$Movement-Rotation, and the tip of the pendulum is undergoing a #$Movement-TranslationEvent.)  As a negative example, a catatonic person should not be thought of as undergoing any #$MovementOrShapeChangeEvent.  Although blood moves through such a person's veins, her lungs expand and contract, and so on, these things would not normally be considered salient enough to entail that the person herself was undergoing a movement or shape change event.")
(comment BiologicalProcess "A specialization of #$PhysicalEvent.  Each instance of #$BiologicalProcess is an event in which some biological thing(s) (see #$BiologicalLivingObject) plays an actor role (see #$actors).")
(comment inLaw "(#$inLaw PERS1 PERS2) means that PERS1 and PERS2 are in-laws, in the conventional American sense.  In other words, they are #$relativesByMarriage, but not necessarily #$stepRelatives nor directly married (though this could be the case in a particular situation).  Either PERS1 has married a mother-father-sister-brother of PERS2, or PERS2 has married such a relative of PERS1.  More specific #$inLaw relationships include #$sonInLaw, #$daughterInLaw, #$motherInLaw, #$fatherInLaw, #$sisterInLaw, and #$brotherInLaw.")
(comment spatialSum "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological sum or union.  <code>(#$spatialSum SUM THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>SUM</code> is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>) such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <code>SUM</code> if and only if it intersects with either <code>THING1</code> or <code>THING2</code>.  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <code>SUM</code>,
since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpatialSumFn.  Also see #$spatialProduct and #$spatialDifference.")
(comment SpatialProductFn "A binary function that takes a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological product or intersection.  (#$SpatialProductFn <b>PRODUCT THING1 THING2</b>) is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <b>THING1</b> and <b>THING2</b>) <b>PRODUCT</b> such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <b>PRODUCT</b> if and only if it intersects with both <b>THING1</b> and <b>THING2</b>.  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <b>PRODUCT</b>, since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$spatialProduct.  Also see #$SpatialSumFn and #$SpatialDifferenceFn.")
(comment spatialProduct "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological product or intersection.  <code>(#$spatialProduct PRODUCT THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>PRODUCT</code> is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>) such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <code>PRODUCT</code> if and only if it intersects with both <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>.  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <code>PRODUCT</code>, since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpatialProductFn.  Also see #$spatialSum and #$spatialDifference.")
(comment SpatialDifferenceFn "A binary function that takes a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological difference.  (#$SpatialDifferenceFn <b>DIFFERENCE THING1 THING2</b>) is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <b>THING1</b> and <b>THING2</b>) <b>DIFFERENCE</b> such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <b>DIFFERENCE</b> if and only if it intersects with <b>THING1</b> and is disjoint with (see #$spatiallyDisjoint) <b>THING2</b>.  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <b>DIFFERENCE</b>, since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$spatialDifference.  Also see #$SpatialSumFn and #$SpatialProductFn.")
(comment OGCGeometryDifferenceFn "#$OGCGeometryDifferenceFn is a spec function of #$SpatialDifferenceFn and was introduced exclusively to be supported by SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly into SQL queries using the function <code>difference</code> defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment OpenSpaceContent "A subcollection of #$FreeSpaceContent whose
instances are pieces of partially tangible fluids occupying \"open\" spaces
such as free spaces outdoors and certain #$OuterSpaceRegions.")
(comment Atmosphere "A specialization of #$OpenSpaceContent (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Atmosphere is a gaseous mass or envelope surrounding an #$AstronomicalBody, especially a planet.  Examples include #$TheEarthsAtmosphere.")
(comment interpretationWorkspaceMtForDocument "<code>(#$interpretationWorkspaceMtForDocument MT PCW)</code> identifies <code>MT</code> as the most general instance of #$ContentModelMicrotheory in a microtheoretic structure used in interpreting the document <code>PCW</code>. See also #$InterpretingDocumentMtFn.")
(comment InformationRequirement "A sub-collection of #$Assignment-Specification.  Each instance of #$InformationRequirement is a particular task that focuses on the gathering of certain forms of information.  The required knowledge, considered independently of a particular assignment to gather that knowledge, would be an instance of #$InformationNeed.")
(comment validSpecificationForProgram "<code>(#$validSpecificationForProgram SPECIFICATION PROGRAM)</code> means that the instance <code>SPECIFICATION</code> of #$ProgramSpecification is a valid description of one way in which the computer program <code>PROGRAM</code> can run.")
(comment programRunToSpecificationWithInputYieldsOutput "<code>(#$programRunToSpecificationWithInputYieldsOutput PROGRAM SPECIFICATION INPUT OUTPUT)</code> means that whenever the computer program <code>PROGRAM</code> is run with <code>INPUT</code> in accordance with the instance <code>SPECIFICATION</code> of #$ProgramSpecification, <code>OUTPUT</code> will be one of its outputs.")
(comment specificationTypeBasedOnParameterType "(<code>(#$specificationTypeBasedOnParameterType SPECIFICATION-TYPE PARAMETER-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>SPECIFICATION-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$ProgramSpecification that are constructed by specifying values for instances of #$SoftwarePparameter of type <code>PARAMETER-TYPE</code>.")
(comment SpecificationTypeFromParameterTypeFn "<code>(#$SpecificationTypeFromParameterTypeFn PARAMETER-TYPE)</code> denotes the collection of instances of #$ProgramSpecification that are the result of specifying values for instances of #$SoftwareParameter that are instances of <code>PARAMETER-TYPE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$SpecificationTypeFromParameterTypeFn #$InferenceParameter)</code> denotes the collection of specifications for inference.")
(comment NLParsingParameter "The collection of instances of #$SoftwareParameter that, broadly speaking, are applicable to instances of #$NLParser-CW.")
(comment parserRunToSpecificationWithInputStringReturnsCyclification "<code>(#$parserRunToSpecificationWithInputStringReturnsCyclification PARSER SPECIFICATION STRING CYCL)</code> means that <code>CYCL</code> is the CycL output from the instance <code>PARSER</code> of #$CyclificationParser with the string input <code>STRING</code> when <code>PARSER</code> is set to run according to the instance <code>SPECIFICATION</code> of #$ProgramSpecification.")
(comment defaultSpecificationForProgram "<code>(#$defaultSpecificationForProgram SPECIFICATION PROGRAM)</code> means that the default specification (the way that the values of parameters for are normally set) for running the computer program <code>PROGRAM</code> is <code>SPECIFICATION</code>, an instance of #$ProgramSpecification.")
(comment parameterTypeOfProgram "<code>(#$parameterTypeOfProgram PARAMETER-TYPE PROGRAM)</code> identifies <code>PARAMETER-TYPE</code> as the collection of all instances of #$SoftwareParameter that are defined for (c.f., #$parameterOfProgram) <code>PROGRAM</code>, an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW.")
(comment ParameterTypeOfProgramFn "<code>(#$ParameterTypeOfProgramFn PROGRAM)</code> denotes the collection of instances of #$SoftwareParameter that are defined for (c.f., #$parameterOfProgram) <code>PROGRAM</code>, an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW.")
(comment softwareParameterValues "<code>(#$softwareParameterValues PARAMETER VALUE)</code> means that <code>VALUE</code> is a possible value for <code>PARAMETER</code>, an instance of #$SoftwareParameter.")
(comment ProgramSpecificationWithParameterValueSetFn "<code>(#$ProgramSpecificationWithParameterValueSetFn SET)</code> denotes the instance of #$ProgramSpecification that consists of <code>VALUE-1</code> ... <code>VALUE-N</code>, instances of #$SoftwareParameterValueObject specified in <code>SET</code>, an instance of #$SetOrCollection.")
(comment programSpecificationWithParameterValueSet "<code>(#$programSpecificationWithParameterValueSet SPECIFICATION SET)</code> identifies <code>SPECIFICATION</code> as the instance of #$ProgramSpecification arrived at by setting <code>VALUE-1</code> ... <code>VALUE-N</code>, instances of #$SoftwareParameterValueObject specified in <code>SET</code>, an instance of #$SetOrCollection.")
(comment Air "A specialization of #$GaseousTangibleThing.  Each instance of #$Air is one 'piece' among all the portions of the atmosphere of the Earth, considered as a substance present in various places, in various quantities, under various pressures, etc.  Examples: the AirInAustin; the stuffy air in my office; the thin air atop Annapurna.  See also #$TheEarthsAtmosphere, which is all ambient #$Air on the planet taken as a single object.")
(comment GaseousTangibleThing "A specialization of #$FluidTangibleThing.  Each instance of #$GaseousTangibleThing is a partially tangible thing which possesses a number of the salient properties exhibited by substances in a gaseous state (see the constant #$Gaseous-StateOfMatter).   To be more specific, each instance of #$GaseousTangibleThing is compressible, and the shape of each instance would completely conform to the boundaries of a vessel containing it.  Examples of #$GaseousTangibleThing include pieces of matter in a gaseous state (such as the helium in a child's balloon), mixtures of gases such as #$TheEarthsAtmosphere, and suspensions of liquids and solids in gas, such as instances of #$Fog or #$Smoke.")
(comment correlatedWith "(#$correlatedWith SET DEP-PRED INDEP-PRED) means that the second
arguments to DEP-PRED are either positively or negatively correlated
with those for INDEP-PRED.  Note that #$correlatedWith assertions
don't entail a particular directionality of the causal relationship
between arg2 values for DEP-PRED and INDEP-PRED. It might go either
direction, or there may be no direct causal link, with instead arg2
values for DEP-PRED and INDEP-PRED actually being independent but
subject to some common causal mechanism. See also #$dependsOn,
#$positivelyCorrelatedWith and #$negativelyCorrelatedWith.")
(comment EBMTConstant "The collection of all instances of #$CycLConstant that are used by the Cyc Example-Based Machine Translation code.")
(comment mtTypeFocalContentFocalPred "<code>(#$mtTypeFocalContentFocalPred MT-TYPE PREDICATE)</code> means that every instance <code>MICROTHEORY</code> of type <code>MT-TYPE</code> is such that <code>(#$mtFocalContent MT (PREDICATE . ARGS))</code>.")
(comment dependsOn "(#$dependsOn SET DEP-SLOT INDEP-SLOT) means that the values for DEP-SLOT are either positively or negatively dependent on
the values for INDEP-SLOT, for elements of SET.  Unlike the more
general #$correlatedWith (q.v.), #$dependsOn means that there is some
causal link from INDEP-SLOT affecting DEP-SLOT.")
(comment pegDerivedSemanticInterpretationMts "<code> (#$pegDerivedSemanticInterpretationMts PEG MT)</code> means that the #$Microtheory <b>MT</b> holds a semantic interpretation (call this interpretation <b>I</b>) of the instance <b>PEG</b> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg, such that 1) there exists some other interpretation of <b>PEG</b>, call it <b>I2</b>, that entails <b>I</b> and, 2) <b>I</b> is true in the content model (see #$contentModelFocalPeg) of <b>PEG</b>.")
(comment derivedHypothesisMt "<code>(#$derivedHypothesisMt <b>SOURCE-MT DERIVED-MT</b>)</code> means that the #$Microtheory <code>DERIVED-MT</code> has focal content that is derived from the content of the instance of #$HypotheticalContext <code>SOURCE-MT</code>.")
(comment spatialSymmetricDifference "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological symmetric difference.  <code>(#$spatialSymmetricDifference SYMDIFF THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>SYMDIFF</code> is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>) such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <code>SYMDIFF</code> if and only if it intersects with <code>THING1</code> but is disjoint with (see #$spatiallyDisjoint) <code>THING2</code> <i>or</i> it intersects with
<code>THING2</code> but is disjoint with
<code>THING1</code>.  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <code>SYMDIFF</code>, since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpatialSymmetricDifferenceFn.  Also compare with  #$spatialDifference.")
(comment SpatialSymmetricDifferenceFn "A binary function that takes a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological symmetric difference.  (#$SpatialSymmetricDifferenceFn <b>SYMDIFF THING1 THING2</b>) is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <b>THING1</b> and <b>THING2</b>) <b>SYMDIFF</b> such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <b>SYMDIFF</b> if and only if it intersects with <b>THING1</b> and is disjoint with (see #$spatiallyDisjoint) <b>THING2</b> <i>or</i> it intersects with <b>THING2</b> and is disjoint with <b>THING1</b> .  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <b>SYMDIFF</b>, since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$spatialSymmetricDifference.  Also compare with #$SpatialDifferenceFn.")
(comment spatialDifference "A ternary predicate that relates a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological difference.  <code>(#$spatialDifference DIFFERENCE THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>DIFFERENCE</code> is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>) such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <code>DIFFERENCE</code> if and only if it intersects with <code>THING1</code> and is disjoint with (see #$spatiallyDisjoint) <code>THING2</code>.  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <code>DIFFERENCE</code>, since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SpatialDifferenceFn.  Also see #$spatialSum and #$spatialProduct.")
(comment considerSpecializing "An instance of DocumentationPredicate that indicates a need for work that will improve KB hygiene and reduce one aspect of KB incompleteness. (considerSpecializing SPEC-COLL TOO-GENL-COLL) means that '(genls SPEC-COLL TOO-GENL-COLL)' is currently asserted, and that there either exists or should exist some collection LESS-GENL-COLL such that: 1) (genls SPEC-COLL LESS-GENL-COLL) is true and 2) the assertion (genls SPEC-COLL TOO-GENL-COLL) should be replaced in the KB with (genls SPEC-COLL LESS-GENL-COLL).")
(comment ForwardReifiableCycLFunctor "A specialization of #$CycLFunctor, this is the collection of instances <code>FN</code> of function-terms of the CycL language that are foward-reifiable -- i.e., can occur in #$termOfUnit clauses in forward rules with the effect that those rules will, when necessary, generate instances of #$CycLNonAtomicReifiedTerm of the form <code>(FN . ARGS)</code>.")
(comment ReifiableCycLFunctor "A specialization of #$CycLFunctor, this is the collection of instances <code>FN</code> of function-terms of the CycL language that can occur as operators in <b>reified</b> non-atomic terms -- i.e., in instances of #$CycLNonAtomicReifiedTerm of the form <code>(FN . ARGS)</code>.")
(disjointWith ReifiableCycLFunctor UnreifiableCycLFunctor)
(comment UnreifiableCycLFunctor "A specialization of #$CycLFunctor, this is the collection of instances <code>FN</code> of function-terms of the CycL language that <b>cannot</b> occur as operators in <b>reified</b> terms -- i.e., there can be no expression of the form <code>(FN . ARGS)</code> that is an instance of #$CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm.")
(comment HTMLHeadingTag "The collection of instances of #$HTMLTag that are used to identify headings.")
(disjointWith PhysicalQualityOfTangibleOnly SpatiallyDisjointRegionType)
(comment MediterraneanScrub "A specialization of #$OutdoorLocation. Each instance is an area of shrub-dominated vegetation located in
the Mediterranean basinand similar climatic regions in coastal parts
of California, Chile, South Africa, and southern Australia.  A variety
of aromatic herbaceous plants grow beneath low shrub thickets.
Source: <i>The Times Atlas of the World (1995).</i>")
(comment BroadleafForest "A specialization of #$Forest. Each #$BroadleafForest is a forest region where deciduous trees predominate, found mainly in the #$NorthernHemisphere-Region. In Western Europe and North America before 1600, a wide variety of tree species occupied these regions, e.g., oak, ash, beech, elm, maple, hickory, alder, and birch, but due to exploitation little of the original forest regions remain. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$BroadleafForests a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>D</code>).")
(comment BroadleafForest-D1 "A specialization of #$BroadleafForest. Each #$BroadleafForest-D1 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 5-25% covered with deciduous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$BroadleafForest-D1 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>D1</code>).")
(comment BroadleafForest-D2 "A specialization of #$BroadleafForest. Each #$BroadleafForest-D2 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 25-50% covered with deciduous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$BroadleafForest-D2 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>D2</code>).")
(comment BroadleafForest-D3 "A specialization of #$BroadleafForest. Each #$BroadleafForest-D3 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 50-75% covered with deciduous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$BroadleafForest-D3 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>D3</code>).")
(comment BroadleafForest-D4 "A specialization of #$BroadleafForest. Each #$BroadleafForest-D4 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 75-100% covered with deciduous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$BroadleafForest-D4 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>D4</code>).")
(comment ConiferForest "A specialization of #$Forest. Each #$ConiferForest is a forest in which conifers predominate, but with a different pattern of forestation from that found in instances of #$Taiga region. Examples are found in western North America, southeastern USA, and southern Brazil. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$ConiferForests a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>C</code>).")
(comment ConiferForest-C1 "A specialization of #$ConiferForest. Each #$ConiferForest-C1 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 5-25% covered with coniferous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns ConiferForest-C1 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>C1</code>).")
(comment ConiferForest-C2 "A specialization of #$ConiferForest. Each #$ConiferForest-C2 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 25-50% covered with coniferous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns ConiferForest-C2 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>C2</code>).")
(comment ConiferForest-C3 "A specialization of #$ConiferForest. Each #$ConiferForest-C3 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 50-75% covered with coniferous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns ConiferForest-C3 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>C3</code>).")
(comment ConiferForest-C4 "A specialization of #$ConiferForest. Each #$ConiferForest-C4 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 75-100% covered with coniferous trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns ConiferForest-C4 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>C3</code>).")
(comment MidLatitudeMixedForest "A specialization of #$Forest. Each #$MidLatitudeMixedForest is a forest featuring both coniferous and deciduous trees, but where neither type of tree exceeds the other by 60% of all the trees present. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$MidLatitudeMixedForest a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>E</code>).")
(comment MidLatitudeMixedForest-E1 "A specialization of #$MidLatitudeMixedForest. Each #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E1 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 5-25% covered with coniferous and deciduous trees, but where neither type of tree exceeds the other by 60% of all the trees present. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E1 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>E1</code>).")
(comment MidLatitudeMixedForest-E2 "A specialization of #$MidLatitudeMixedForest. Each #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E2 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 25-50% covered with coniferous and deciduous trees, but where neither type of tree exceeds the other by 60% of all the trees present. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E2 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>E2</code>).")
(comment MidLatitudeMixedForest-E3 "A specialization of #$MidLatitudeMixedForest. Each #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E3 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 50-75% covered with coniferous and deciduous trees, but where neither type of tree exceeds the other by 60% of all the trees present. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E3 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>E3</code>).")
(comment MidLatitudeMixedForest-E4 "A specialization of #$MidLatitudeMixedForest. Each #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E4 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 75-100% covered with coniferous and deciduous trees, but where neither type of tree exceeds the other by 60% of all the trees present. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$MidLatitudeMixedForest-E4 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>E4</code>).")
(comment OrchardForest "A specialization of #$Forest. Each #$OrchardForest is a forest where #$FruitTrees predominate. These forests are often cultivated for agricultural purposes. The USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns OrchardForests a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>F</code>).")
(comment OrchardForest-F1 "A specialization of #$OrchardForest. Each #$OrchardForest-F1 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 5-25% covered with cultivated fruit trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns OrchardForest-F1 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>F1</code>).")
(comment OrchardForest-F2 "A specialization of #$OrchardForest. Each #$OrchardForest-F2 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 25-50% covered with cultivated fruit trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns OrchardForest-F2 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>F2</code>).")
(comment OrchardForest-F3 "A specialization of #$OrchardForest. Each #$OrchardForest-F3 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 50-75% covered with cultivated fruit trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns OrchardForest-F3 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>F3</code>).")
(comment OrchardForest-F4 "A specialization of #$OrchardForest. Each #$OrchardForest-F4 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is 75-100% covered with cultivated fruit trees. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns OrchardForest-F4 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>F4</code>).")
(comment Swamp "A specialization of both #$WetlandRegion and #$Forest. Each instance of #$Swamp is an area characterized by shallow water, vegetation, small patches of dry land, and mud. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns Swamp-I1 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>I</code>).")
(comment Swamp-I1 "A specialization of #$Swamp. Each instance of #$Swamp-I1 is a #$WetlandRegion that is 5-25% covered with trees and woody bushes. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns Swamp-I1 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>I1</code>).")
(comment Swamp-I3 "A specialization of #$Swamp. Each instance of #$Swamp-I3 is a #$WetlandRegion that is 50-75% covered with trees and woody bushes. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns Swamp-I3 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>I3</code>).")
(comment Swamp-I4 "A specialization of #$Swamp. Each instance of #$Swamp-I4 is a #$WetlandRegion that is 75-100% covered with trees and woody bushes. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns Swamp-I4 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>I4</code>).")
(comment ScrubbyVegetationRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion is a semiarid to temperate area where the vegetation pattern consists primarily of low shrubs interspersed with grasses. It sometimes called 'brushland'. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>B</code>).")
(comment ScrubbyVegetationRegion-B1 "A specialization of #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion. Each #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion-B1 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is with scrubby brushland, less than 5m high and open to medium spacing. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion-B1 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>B1</code>).")
(comment ScrubbyVegetationRegion-B2 "A specialization of #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion. Each #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion-B2 is a #$GeographicalRegion that is with scrubby brushland, less than 5m high and medium to dense spacing. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion-B2 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>B2</code>).")
(comment BambooCoveredRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$BambooCoveredRegion is a #$GeographicalRegion that is predominantly covered with #$BambooPlants. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$BambooCoveredRegion a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>M</code>).")
(comment BarrenRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$BarrenRegion is a #$GeographicalRegion which for any one of a number of climatic reasons will not support much plant growth beyond lichen, moss, and very small shrubs. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$BarrenRegion a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>N</code>).")
(comment BushyTerrain "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$BushyTerrain is an #$OutdoorLocation covered with bushes.")
(comment CropCoveredRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$CropCoveredRegion is a #$GeographicalRegion that is covered with a type or types of #$CropPlant. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$CropCoveredRegion a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>A</code>).")
(comment Forest "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$Forest    is a region with a high density of #$Tree-ThePlants.")
(comment ForestClearing "A specialization of #$Flat-Topographical. Each #$ForestClearing is an area in a #$Forest that is bare of trees.")
(comment Grassland "A specialization of #$GrassyRegion and #$Plain-Topographical. Each #$Grassland is a grassy region with deep, rich soil and few trees or shrubs.")
(comment GrassyRegion "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$GrassyRegion is an arid to temperate #$EcologicalRegion where grasses predominate, including human-tended regions where #$GrassTurf has been extensively planted and maintained. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$GrassyRegion a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>G</code>).")
(comment Heath "A specialization of #$EcologicalRegion. Each #$Heath is an upland moor or sandy area dominated by low shrubby vegetation including heather.")
(comment LowLyingWetland "A specialization of #$WetlandRegion. Each #$LowLyingWetland is a wetland distinguished from #$Marshes by occuring in in low-lying wet areas, like the southern coast of Louisiana or Bangladesh. The U.S. military The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$LowLyingWetland a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>K</code>).")
(comment Marsh "A specialization of #$WetlandRegion. Each #$Marsh is a wetland characterized by vegetation composed primarily of marsh grasses and rushes. Its vegetation map code is (J</code>).")
(comment MediterraneanScrub "A specialization of #$OutdoorLocation. Each instance is an area of shrub-dominated vegetation located in the Mediterranean basinand similar climatic regions in coastal parts of California, Chile, South Africa, and southern Australia. A variety of aromatic herbaceous plants grow beneath low shrub thickets. Source: <i>The Times Atlas of the World (1995).</i>")
(comment SaltMarsh "A specialization of #$WetlandRegion. #$SaltMarsh is a wetland characterized by vegetation composed primarily of marsh grasses and rushes, subject to periodic salt water inundation, and dominated by grassy salt-tolerant plants.")
(comment Steppe "A specialization of #$Grassland. Each #$Steppe is an area where short grasses are dominant. Traditionally, the term 'steppe' referred to the wild grasslands of Eurasia; but steppes are also found extensively in central North America, central and southern Africa, and Australia.")
(comment SubtropicalForest "A specialization of #$Forest. Each #$SubtropicalForest is a hardleaf evergreen forest growing between the latitudes of 15-40 degrees north and south of the equator in China, Japan, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa. Source: <i>The Times Atlas of the World (1995).</i>")
(comment Taiga "A specialization of #$Forest. Each #$Taiga is characterized by extensive forestation, short, cool summers, and long, cold winters with heavy snowfall. Also known as boreal forests. Predominant trees are pine, larch, spruce, and fir. Source: <i>The Times Atlas of the World (1995).</i>")
(comment Tundra "A specialization of #$PermaFrostRegion and #$Plain-Topographical. Each #$Tundra is a treeless plain characterized by year-round low temerature and a short growing season. The subsoil of #$Tundra is permafrost. Vegetation is predominantly grasses, mosses, lichens, rushes, and very few other forms of plant life.")
(comment VinyArea "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$VinyArea is an area dominated by cultivated vines, such as wine vineyards. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns #$VinyAreas a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>L</code>).")
(comment WoodedArea "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each #$WoodedArea is a place with a lot of trees. Some wooded areas are in inhabitated regions. Some are not. For example, the area to the west of #$CycorpCorporateHeadquarters is a wooded area that is adjacent to `civilized' areas. #$Forests, on the other hand, are #$WoodedAreas that are large expanses most of which are very far from any civilized region.")
(comment BiomeType "A collection of collections. Each #$BiomeType is a type of broad plant and animal community, such as #$Grassland.")
(comment RegionTypeBySurfaceGrowth "A collection of collections. Each #$RegionTypeBySurfaceGrowth is a specialization of #$GeographicalRegion distinguised by the type of surface growth it has (which, in turn, is typically a function of the region's climate). See also #$FixedTerrainGeologicalRegion.")
(comment RegionTypeByVegetation "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$RegionTypeByVegetation is a specialization of #$GeographicalRegion characterized by some type of dominant vegetation.")
(comment terrainFeatures-Vegetation "A #$BinaryPredicate that holds between a #$GeographicalRegion and its dominant vegetation type. (#$terrainFeatures-Vegetation REGION VEG-TYPE) means that #$GeographicalRegion REGION is an instance of the #$RegionTypeByVegetation VEG-TYPE. For example, (#$terrainFeatures-Vegetation #$AmazonRainForest #$TropicalRainForest</code>).")
(comment TEPConjunctiveQueryContentFromStringForContextFn "(#$TEPQueryContentFromStringForContextFn STRING MT) is an evaluatable function which, for a given sentence STRING and context MT, returns the #$CycLSentence-Askable which reflects the (conjunction of possible) cyclifier output for STRING in MT.")
(comment AbstractVisualStructure "A specialization of #$AbstractStructure (q.v.).  Each instance of #$AbstractVisualStructure (AVS) is a structure that can be discerned visually.   Any concrete <i>instantiation</i> of a given AVS consists of a particular spatial (or spatio-temporal) arrangement of shapes and/or colors.  A given AVS might have multiple instantiations.   By the same token, a given concrete visual arrangment (appearing, say, on a sheet of paper or a computer monitor screen) might simultaneously instantiate multiple AVSs, corresponding to different degrees of abstractness.
<p>
For example, when displaying a particular image of a sunset, my computer screen might instantiate (among others) the following AVSs: (i) an AVS such that each of its instantiations consists of a round region and a contrasting surrounding region, (ii) an AVS such that each of its instantiations consists of a round <i>orange</i> region and a surrounding <i>blue</i> region, and (iii) an AVS such that each of its instantiations consists of an image that is pixel-for-pixel indiscernable from my screen in its current state.
<p>
#$AbstractVisualStructures can be thought of as the abstract commonality in virtue of which objects, or arrangements of objects, look the same (to some degree of granularity).  Abstract visual structures can be instantiated by objects or groups of objects.  Thus, a group of objects in a room, together with the room itself, instantiate shapes and colors in a certain spatial arrangement; and any group of objects instantiating all the same #$AbstractVisualStructures as the first group would be visually indiscernible from that group.
<p>
A notable specialization of this collection is #$GraphicalStructure.")
(comment TemporalThing "A specialization of #$Individual.  This is the collection of all things that have temporal extent or location -- things about which one might sensibly ask \"<i>When?</i>\".  #$TemporalThing thus contains many kinds of things, including events, physical objects, agreements, and pure intervals of time.  Major specializations of this collection are #$SomethingExisting, #$Situation, and #$TimeInterval (qq.v.).
<p>
Things that are timeless -- such as mathematical sets, #$Collections, and numbers -- are of course  <i>not</i> instances of #$TemporalThing (cf. #$AtemporalThing).")
(comment temporallySubsumes "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.). <code>(#$temporallySubsumes LONG SHORT)</code> means that all time points in the #$temporalExtent (q.v.) of <code>SHORT</code> are contained in that of <code>LONG</code>.   This implies that <code>SHORT</code> does not start before, or end after, <code>LONG</code>.  Moreover, at any time point or interval when <code>LONG</code> does not exist or occur or obtain, neither does <code>SHORT</code>.
<p>
Note that #$temporallySubsumes is a strictly temporal relation; it is thus weaker than its specialization #$subEvents, which further entails that its first (temporally longer) argument has the second as a \"part\".
<p>
See also the specialization #$cotemporal.")
(disjointWith TemporallyExtendedThing SomethingExisting)
(comment cyclifierOutputForTEP "(#$cyclifierOutputForTEP TEP DATE PASSAGE-INTERPS QUERY-INTERPS) means that PASSAGE-INTERPS and QUERY-INTERPS are, respectively, the disjunctions of possible interpretations returned for the premise and query text associated with TEP on DATE.")
(comment cyclifierOutputForTextualInferenceKBCT "(#$cyclifierOutputForTEP TEP DATE PASSAGE-INTERPS QUERY-INTERPS) means that PASSAGE-INTERPS and QUERY-INTERPS are, respectively, the disjunctions of possible interpretations returned for the premise and query text associated with TEP on DATE.")
(comment informationStoreMappingMt "<code>(#$informationStoreMappingMt STORE MT)</code> identifies <code>MT</code> as the instance of #$MappingMicrotheory that contains assertions mapping concepts found in the instance of #$InformationStore <code>STORE</code> into Cyc.  Where <code>STORE</code> is an instance of #$StructuredKnowledgeSource and <code>MT</code> is an instance of #$SKSIMappingMicrotheory, use the more specialized #$mappingMt.")
(comment LSCOMObjectAndSituationOntology "An instance of #$Ontology.  The LSCOM object and situation ontology is, at its most general level, an ontology of specializations of #$TemporallyExistingThing, with each concept in the ontology serving as the basis of the LSCOM Visual IBT ontology (#$LSCOMVisualIBTOntology), an ontology of specializations of #$VisualInformationBearingThing, nodes in which are defined in terms of various relations to nodes in this ontology.")
(comment LSCOMVisualIBTOntology "An instance of #$Ontology.  The LSCOM visual IBT ontology is, at its most general level, an ontology of specializations of #$VisualInformationBearingThing, used for video and image classification and retrieval.  Many concepts in this ontology are defined in terms of their relaionships to concepts in the LSCOM objects and situations ontology (#$LSCOMObjectAndSituationOntology), an ontology of specializations of #$TemporallyExistingThing.")
(comment informationTerminal "This predicate is used to indicate any instance of #$TemporalThing that plays the role of either #$informationDestination or #$informationOrigin in any instance of #$InformationTransferEvent, i.e. plays the role of endpoint.  <code>(#$informationTerminal TRANS THING)</code> means the #$TemporalThing <code>THING</code> was either the origin or destination of the #$InformationTransferEvent <code>TRANS</code>.")
(comment scopingArg "A binary #$MetaRelation that relates a #$ScopingRelation (q.v.) to an integer indicating the argument-place of the former that takes a variable or list of variables.  (#$scopingArg RELN N) means that RELN's Nth argument is a variable or variable-list (see #$CycLVariable and #$CycLVariableList) such that it (they) and any other occurrences of the same variable(s) that occur free in the CycL formula serving as the \"scoped\" argument (see #$scopedFormulaArg) to RELN is (are) bound with respect to the entire RELN-based formula.  For example, (#$scopingArg #$thereExists 1) entails that in the sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$thereExists ?KIT
    (#$and
      (#$isa ?KIT #$Cat)
      (#$isa ?KIT #$BlackColor)))
</pre>
<p>
each occurrence of the variable '?KIT' is bound by the quantifier #$thereExists.")
(comment resultIsa "A binary #$MetaFunction that is used to indicate that any value returned by a given function (see #$Function-Denotational) is an instance of a given collection.  <code>(#$resultIsa FUNC COL)</code> means that <code>FUNC</code> returns an instance of <code>COL</code> for any sequence of arguments for which <code>FUNC</code> is defined.  That is, <code>(#$isa (FUNC . ARGS) COL)</code> holds for any sequence <code>ARGS</code> for which <code>FUNC</code> has a value (see #$valueOfFunctionForArgs and #$relationDomain).  
<p>
For example, <code>(#$resultIsa #$PlusFn #$ScalarInterval)</code> holds, and it entails (e.g.) <code>(#$isa (#$PlusFn 2 2) #$ScalarInterval)</code>.  
<p>
See also #$resultGenl.")
(comment resultGenl "A #$MetaFunction that is used to specify that a 
given collection is a supercollection of any value returned by a given #$CollectionDenotingFunction (q.v.).  <code>(#$resultGenl COLFUNC COL)</code> means that when <code>COLFUNC</code> is applied to any sequence of arguments <code>ARGS</code> for which it is defined, the resulting value will be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(#$genls (COLFUNC . ARGS) COL)</code> holds.  
<p>
For example, <code>(#$resultGenl #$UsingAFn #$Action)</code> implies that the collection of acts done using any given type of object is a type of action, <code>(#$resultGenl #$AttemptingFn #$PurposefulAction)</code> implies that the attempts at a given type of action constitute a type of purposeful act, and <code>(#$resultGenl #$JointTypeBetweenFn #$Joint-Physical)</code> means that the collection of joints between two given types of things is a type of physical joint.  
<p>
See also #$resultIsa.")
(comment DurationOfAMonarchsPower "A sub-collection of #$Time-Quantity.  This is by definition the collection of all time-quantities that are the #$duration of some monarch's #$Reign.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromLCC-H "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by the LCC group for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment sacredTextOfReligion "(#$sacredTextOfReligion RELIGION TEXT) means that TEXT is a #$PropositionalConceptualWork that is sacred according to the #$Religion, RELIGION.")
(comment Taxonomy "The collection of all taxonomic systems.  A taxonomy minimally includes a description of a domain along class/subclass lines (see #$ClassSubclassHierarchy), but can also include other binary relations (e.g., partonomic relations) in its description.  A taxonomy can be usefully contrasted with an ontology (see #$Ontology), the latter of which includes a rich description of a domain, often including non-binary relations and complex rules.")
(disjointWith Taxonomy Ontology)
(comment ClassSubclassHierarchy "The collection of all taxonomic systems that describe a domain only along class (#$elementOf) and sub-class (#$subsetOf) lines.  In some circles, only this kind of system is appropriately described as a \"taxonomy\".")
(comment IsaGenlsHierarchy "The collection of all instances of #$ClassSubclassHierarchy that describes a domain along class (#$elementOf) and sub-class (#$subsetOf) lines, where the domain is restricted to individuals (#$Individual) and collections (#$Collection).  Given that <code>(#$typedGenlPreds #$isa #$elementOf)</code> and <code>(#$typedGenlPreds #$genls #$subsetOf)</code>, this collection can also be described as a collection of taxonomic systems that describe a domain along #$isa and #$genls lines.")
(comment algorithmComputes "A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to the functions that they constitute effective procedures for computing <code>(#$algorithmComputes ALGORITHM FUNCTION)</code> means that <code>ALGORITHM</code> is a sequence of operations that constitute a strictly mechanical procedure for determining the value of <code>FUNCTION</code> for any argument in its domain (see #$valueOfFunctionForArgs and #$relationDomain).  By their very nature, all algorithms compute some function or other.  In some cases, distinct algorithms will compute the same function (see #$equivAlgorithms).")
(comment algorithmInputs "A predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to their allowable inputs.  <code>(#$algorithmInputs ALGORITHM THING)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is a possible input to <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  That is, if <code>ALGORITHM</code> is applied to <code>THING</code>, a finite sequence of intermediate results, and possibly a final output (see #$algorithmOutputFor), will be generated, after which <code>ALGORITHM</code> will halt.  Depending on the nature of <code>ALGORITHM</code>, <code>THING</code> might be a character-string, a number, a physical object, a set, a function, a list, or something else.  
<p>
An algorithm need not generate a final output for all allowable inputs (e.g. a division algorithm when the divisor is zero).  While most algorithms of interest have a range of allowable inputs (e.g. all integers) for which they generate a  corresponding range of result-sequences, an algorithm might be so specific as to have only a single allowable input (e.g. a dedicated algorithm for squaring the number 2).  Unless stated otherwise, a thing is an allowable input for a given algorithm if and only if it is an element of the domain of the first operation within the algorithm.")
(comment algorithmOutputFor "A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to their outputs for given inputs.  <code>(#$algorithmOutputFor ALGORITHM INPUT OUTPUT)</code> means that <code>ALGORITHM</code>, when applied to <code>INPUT</code>, produces <code>OUTPUT</code> as its final result.  Note that <code>ALGORITHM</code> might not produce a final output for every allowable input (see #$algorithmInputs).")
(comment algorithmProps "A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to propositions describing the individual steps or operations in which they consist.  <code>(#$algorithmProps ALGORITHM PROP)</code> means that <code>PROP</code> describes a particular step in <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  
<p>
<code>PROP</code> can be thought of as having the force of a command within the context of <code>ALGORITHM</code>: it tells an agent or machine that is following <code>ALGORITHM</code> what to do at some particular juncture.  <code>PROP</code> will normally make reference to a (generic) input (which might itself consist of the outputs of one or more previous steps in <code>ALGORITHM</code>) and describe an operation to be performed on the input, the result of which will be the output (if any) of the current step for that input.  If <code>PROP</code> is not the final step in <code>ALGORITHM</code>, this output might serve as an input to a subsequent step.  <code>PROP</code> can be thought of as describing a \"minimal\" subalgorithm (i.e. operation sequence of length 1) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.")
(comment computerProcessMaxAlgorithm "A specialization of the predicate #$computerProcessAlgorithms (q.v.) that relates a computer process to the unique
\"maximal\" #$Algorithm (q.v.) followed via that process.  <code>(#$computerProcessMaxAlgorithm PROCESS ALGORITHM)</code> means that  <code>PROCESS</code> involves carrying out <code>ALGORITHM</code> on one or more inputs and does <i>not</i> involve carrying out any proper superalgorithm (see #$subAlgorithms) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.")
(comment equivAlgorithms "A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms that are procedures for completing the same task(s) with respect to the same inputs --  i.e. algorithms that compute the same function (see #$algorithmComputes).  <code>(#$equivAlgorithms ALG1 ALG2)</code> means that <code>ALG1</code> and <code>ALG2</code> have the same set of allowable inputs and, for any such input, they both produce the same output (or both produce no output).  No further relationship is implied regarding the constituent operations or #$subAlgorithms of <code>ALG1</code> and <code>ALG2</code>; only the initial inputs and final outputs are relevant.  
<p>
For example, two different procedures for alphabetizing a list of names, or two explicit sets of directions for getting to the same destination from a given starting point, constitute equivalent algorithms, regardless of the details of either algorithm's internal structure.")
(comment expressesAlgorithms "A specialization of the predicate #$containsInformation that relates an #$InformationBearingThing (q.v.) to an #$Algorithm (q.v.) that it expresses or fully describes.  <code>(#$expressesAlgorithms IBT ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>IBT</code> unambiguously describes each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  <code>IBT</code> might be a written or spoken set of directions, an instance of #$ComputerProgramCopy, or something else.  Any agent or machine capable of interpreting <code>IBT</code> should be able, upon doing so, actually to carry out <code>ALGORITHM</code> on any allowable input (assuming that the agent or machine possesses the knowledge or abilities required to perform each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code>).  
<p>
See also the specialization #$expressesMaxAlgorithm.")
(comment expressesMaxAlgorithm "A specialization of the predicate #$expressesAlgorithms that relates an #$InformationBearingThing (q.v.) to
a \"maximal\" algorithm that it expresses or fully describes. <code>(#$expressesMaxAlgorithm IBT ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>IBT</code> unambiguously describes each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code> and does not express (see #$expressesAlgorithms) any proper superalgorithm (see #$subAlgorithms) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  <code>IBT</code> might be a written or spoken set of directions, an instance of #$ComputerProgramCopy, or something else.  
<p>
Any agent or machine capable of interpreting <code>IBT</code> should be able, upon doing so, actually to carry out <code>ALGORITHM</code> on any allowable input (assuming that the agent or machine possesses the knowledge or abilities required to perform each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code>).  
<p>
Note that, in general, an IBT might express more than one maximal algorithm (e.g. a page might have instructions for two separate algorithms printed on it).  A computer program copy, however, expresses <i>exactly one</i> maximal algorithm: the maximal algorithm used by the code for the copy.")
(comment genlAlgorithms "A specialization of the predicate #$equivAlgorithms that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to equivalent ones that are structurally similar to them except for possibly being less detailed.  <code>(#$genlAlgorithms SPEC-ALG GENL-ALG)</code> means that <code>SPEC-ALG</code> and <code>GENL-ALG</code> are functionally equivalent algorithms (see #$equivAlgorithms), and that <code>SPEC-ALG</code> is at least as detailed a sequence of operations as <code>GENL-ALG</code>.  
<p>
More precisely, for each successive operation <code>OP</code> in <code>GENL-ALG</code>, <code>SPEC-ALG</code> contains (in the same relative position) either <code>OP</code> itself or a series of operations <code>OP<sub>1</sub></code>, ..., <code>OP<sub>N</sub></code> such that, whenever <code>OP</code> produces output <code>OUT</code> from input <code>IN</code>, then the relative product of <code>OP<sub>1</sub></code>, ..., <code>OP<sub>N</sub></code> for <code>IN</code> is <code>OUT</code>.  
<p>
For example, <code>GENL</code> might contain a step that simply says \"Sort list <code>L</code> alphabetically\", whereas the analogous segment of <code>SPEC-ALG</code> delineates the series of steps involved in doing an alphabetical bubble-sort on <code>L</code>.  A third algorithm, which was just like <code>SPEC-ALG</code> except for specifying another type of alphabetizing procedure, would constitute another specialization of <code>GENL</code>.  Arguably, corresponding executable code, source code, and pseudocode versions of a given instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW often use maximal algorithms that are strictly speaking distinct, but ordered by #$genlAlgorithms.")
(comment instructionsAlgorithms "A specialization of the predicate  #$containsProposition-CW that relates a instance of #$Instructions to an #$Algorithm (q.v.) that is part of its information content. <code>(#$instructionsAlgorithms INSTR ALGORITHM)</code> means that following <code>INSTR</code> (see #$instructionsFollowed) requires carrying out <code>ALGORITHM</code> (see #$algorithmUsed).  
<p>
See also the specialization #$instructionsMaxAlgorithm.")
(comment instructionsMaxAlgorithm "A specialization of the predicate #$instructionsAlgorithms (q.v.) that relates an instance of #$Instructions to the \"maximal\" #$Algorithm that it involves.  <code>(#$instructionsMaxAlgorithm INSTR ALGORITHM)</code> means that following <code>INSTR</code> requires carrying out <code>ALGORITHM</code> and does <i>not</i> require carrying out any proper superalgorithm (see #$subAlgorithms) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.")
(comment programAlgorithms "A binary predicate that relates code for a computer program to an #$Algorithm (q.v.) used by that code.  <code>(#$programAlgorithms CODE ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>CODE</code> uses <code>ALGORITHM</code>: the event of running a copy of <code>CODE</code> on a computer system involves the carrying out of <code>ALGORITHM</code> on one or more inputs (see #$computerProcessAlgorithms).  <code>ALGORITHM</code> might correspond to part or all of <code>CODE</code>; in the former case <code>ALGORITHM</code> will be a proper #$subAlgorithms of the \"maximal\" algorithm (see #$programMaxAlgorithm) used by <code>CODE</code>.  
<p>
Different computer code, whether written in the same or in different languages, can use the same algorithm; and if the codes differ only in ways that are irrelevent to the (entire) sequence of operations that they represent, they will use all of the same algorithms.  Note, however, that two <i>versions</i> of code for the same instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW will not necessarily use all (or even many) of the same algorithms; and the algorithms used by one of these versions might not all even be functionally equivalent (see #$equivAlgorithms) to their counterparts used by the other version, if (say) some of the program's functionality changes from version to version (e.g. <i>WordPerfect</i> 5.1 versus <i>WordPerfect</i> 6.0).")
(comment programMaxAlgorithm "A specialization of the predicate #$programAlgorithms that relates code for a computer program to the \"maximal\" algorithm used by that code.  <code>(#$programMaxAlgorithm CODE ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>CODE</code> uses <code>ALGORITHM</code> (see #$programAlgorithms) and uses no proper superalgorithm of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  <code>ALGORITHM</code> corresponds to all of <code>CODE</code>, and any #$subAlgorithms of <code>ALGORITHM</code> will be  #$programAlgorithms of <code>CODE</code>.  
<p>
Different computer code, whether written in the same or in different languages, can use the same maximal algorithm; this will be the case if the codes differ only in ways that are irrelevent to the (entire) sequence of operations that they represent.  Note, however, that two <i>versions</i> of code for the same instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW will not necessarily use the same maximal algorithm; such codes' respective maximal algorithms might not even be functionally equivalent (see #$equivAlgorithms), if (say) some of the program's functionality changes from version to version (e.g. <i>WordPerfect</i> 5.1 versus <i>WordPerfect</i> 6.0).")
(comment subAlgorithms "A specialization of the predicate #$subInformation that holds between #$Algorithms (q.v.).  <code>(#$subAlgorithms SUPER-ALG SUB-ALG)</code> means that <code>SUPER-ALG</code> contains <code>SUB-ALG</code> as a part.  That is, the sequence of operations that constitutes <code>SUB-ALG</code> is a subsequence of that which constitutes <code>SUPER-ALG</code>.  <code>SUB-ALG</code> is essential to <code>SUPER-ALG</code> in that, if the former were replaced in the latter with a distinct algorithm, the result would be distinct from <code>SUPER-ALG</code>.  
<p>
Note that a given algorithm might be a subalgorithm of various different algorithms, even when the latter are not related to one another by the subalgorithm relation.")
(comment UsingAnAlgorithm "A specialization of #$Action whose instances are events in which one or more intelligent agents (possibly software agents) accurately, completely, and \"purposely\" follow the steps of an #$Algorithm (q.v.) with respect to some input(s) (see #$algorithmInputs).  
<p>
In using an algorithm, one is of course thereby also doing whatever it is that the algorithm is an algorithm <i>for</i>.  For example, in following an algorithm for long division one is in fact dividing one number by another.  Instances of #$UsingAnAlgorithm are by definition \"successful\" in that the algorithm in question must not be deviated from or incompletely followed.  
<p>
An event in which a computer follows an algorithm (see #$computerProcessAlgorithms) might be an instance of #$UsingAnAlgorithm, but only if that event is an agentive computer process associated with some pre-existing instance of #$SoftwareAgent (see #$softwareAgentProcesses).  
<p>
For actions in which an agent, rather than carrying out an algorithm directly, uses a device that embodies an algorithm, see #$Computing.")
(comment FollowingInstructions "A specialization of #$Action whose instances are events in which one or more intelligent agents (possibly software agents)   accurately, completely, and \"purposely\" follow some instance of    #$Instructions (q.v.).  In following instructions, one is of course thereby also doing whatever it is that the instructions are instructions <i>for</i>.  For example, in following a certain set of instructions for baking an apple pie one is in fact baking an apple pie.  
<p>
Instances of #$FollowingInstructions are by    definition \"successful\" in that the instructions in question must not be deviated from or incompletely followed.  If the instructions being followed involve one or more instances of #$Algorithm (see #$instructionsAlgorithms), then the instance of #$FollowingInstructions will contain that many instances of    #$UsingAnAlgorithm (q.v.) as subevents.")
(comment algorithmUsed "A specialization of the predicate #$informationUsed that relates an act in which an agent (possibly a software agent) uses an #$Algorithm (q.v.) to the algorithm s/he/it is following.  <code>(#$algorithmUsed USING ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>USING</code> involves some agent \"purposefully\" and accurately using <code>ALGORITHM</code> with respect to some input.
<p>
See also #$instructionsFollowed.")
(comment instructionsFollowed "A specialization of the predicate #$informationUsed that relates acts in which an agent follows a set of instructions to the instance of #$Instructions that agent follows. <code>(#$instructionsFollowed FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS)</code> means that <code>FOLLOWING</code> involves some agent purposefully, accurately, and completely following <code>INSTRUCTIONS</code>.
<p>
See also #$algorithmUsed.")
(comment compiledVersionOfCode "A ternary predicate that relates source code for a computer program, an executable machine code translation of it, and the compiler that translates the first into the second.  <code>(#$compiledVersionOfCode SOURCE EXEC COMPILER)</code> means that <code>EXEC</code> is the output of <code>COMPILER</code> for the input <code>SOURCE</code>.")
(comment Algorithm "A specialization of #$MathematicalOrComputationalThing and #$AspatialInformationStore (qq.v.).  An instance of #$Algorithm is a particular finite sequence of operations that constitutes an effective, or strictly mechanical, procedure for completing some task, computing some function, or simply performing a number of unrelated operations.  
<p>
Instances of #$ComputerProgram-CWIS use algorithms (see #$programAlgorithms), and some instances of #$Instructions (e.g. some instances of its specializations #$Recipe-CW and #$DirectionsToGetSomewhere), if sufficiently explicit, involve algorithms.  
<p>
An algorithm can be viewed as a specific way of computing a particular instance of #$Function-Denotational, i.e. a way determining the value of the function for any argument in the function's domain (see #$algorithmComputes).")
(comment expertRegardingDeviceType "An assertion of the form (#$expertRegardingDeviceType AGENT DEVICE-TYPE) means that the #$Person AGENT has extensive knowledge about instances of the sub-collection of #$SolidTangibleArtifact DEVICE-TYPE.  This predicate does not specify the nature of that knowledge, but it could include both the creation, use, and maintenance and repair of instances of this collection.")
(comment expertRegardingActivityType "An assertion of the form (#$expertRegardingActivityType AGENT ACT-TYPE) means that the #$Person AGENT has extensive knowledge, and, most likely, formal training, of the sub-collection of #$PurposefulAction ACT-TYPE.")
(comment expertOnOrg "An assertion of the form (#$expertOnOrg EXPERT SUBJECT) means that the #$Person EXPERT has extensive knowledge of the #$Organization SUBJECT.")
(comment expertOnIndividual "An assertion of the form (#$expertOnIndividual EXPERT SUBJECT) means that the #$Person EXPERT has extensive knowledge of the #$IndividualAgent SUBJECT.")
(comment expertInField "An assertion of the form (#$expertInField AGENT FIELD) means that the #$Person AGENT has extensive knowledge of the #$FieldOfStudy FIELD.")
(comment guestInHosting "(#$guestInHosting HOSTING AGENT) means that AGENT is a guest in the  instance of #$Hosting, HOSTING.")
(comment computerProcessAlgorithms "A binary predicate that relates computer processes to the #$Algorithms (q.v.) followed via those processes.  <code>(#$computerProcessAlgorithms PROCESS ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>PROCESS</code> involves carrying out <code>ALGORITHM</code> on one or more inputs.  
<p>
For a specialization of this predicate that relates a computer process to the \"maximal\" algorithm followed via that process, see #$computerProcessMaxAlgorithm.")
(comment Facility-Construct "#$Facility-Construct is a specialization of #$HumanOccupationConstruct and #$Facility-Generic (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Facility-Construct is used primarily for one purpose and    will usually be a building (such as a   particular #$Laboratory) or building(s) *and*   the surrounding property (e.g., a   particular #$MilitaryFacility).  As such, this   collection is a proper subcollection of   #$ConstructionArtifact, as it excludes    things like railroad tracks, and is a distinct   collection from #$FixedStructure, which  includes non-#$Facility-Constructs like the #$EiffelTower, but  excludes most (perhaps all) instances of  #$MilitaryFacility).")
(comment peripheralConceptOfDomain "(#$peripheralConceptOfDomain ?CONCEPT ?DOMAIN) means that ?CONCEPT is peripherally associated with the domain, ?DOMAIN, usually because it is required in order to state useful facts about a concept which is actually part of DOMAIN (see #$coreConceptOfDomain). For example, (#$peripheralConceptOfDomain #$cavityHasWall #$WaterBodyConcept) means that #$cavityHasWall is peripherally associated with the domain of concepts related to bodies of water.  This was stated because, in order to talk about the structure of the basins that contain the waters of lakes, etc., we need to talk about the walls of those basins.")
(comment VisualIBTTypeByVisualFeatureType "The collection of all subcollections of #$VisualInformationBearingThing whose instances share a common visually discernible feature-type with respect to its visual content, such as <i>depicts a celebrity</i> or <i>is of an underwater scene.</i> <p>Every instance <code>IBTTYPE</code> of this collection stands in the #$visualIBTTypeVisualFeatureTypeDefinedByRelnAndType relation to an instance <code>PRED</code> of #$VisualIBTVisualFeaturePredicate and a collection <code>COLL</code>, by which it is defined as the most general subcollection of #$VisualInformationBearingThing whose instances all stand in the <code>PRED</code> relation to some instance of <code>COLL</code> (i.e., <code>IBTTYPE</code> is the most general subcollection of #$VisualInformationBearingThing such that <code>(#$relationAllExists PRED IBTTYPE COLL)</code>).</p> <p>For example, <code>(#$visualIBTTypeVisualFeatureTypeDefinedByRelnAndType VisualIBTThatDepictsACelebrity #$visuallyDepicts #$Celebrity)</code> identifies VisualIBTThatDepictsACelebrity as the collection of visual IBTs that depict at least one celebrity, i.e., as the most general collection <code>IBTTYPE</code> of instances of #$VisualInformationBearingThing such that <code>(#$relationAllExists #$visuallyDepicts IBTTYPE #$Celebrity)</code>.")
(comment VisualIBTVisualFeaturePredicate "A collection of binary predicates.  Each instance <code>PRED</code> of #$VisualIBTVisualFeaturePredicate can be used in conjunction with the predicate #$visualIBTTypeVisualFeatureTypeDefinedByRelnAndType and a collection consistent with its #$arg2Isa to define an instance of #$VisualIBTTypeByVisualFeatureType, the collection of all subcollections of #$VisualInformationBearingThing whose instances share a common visually discernible feature-type.  For example, #$visuallyDepicts can be used to define <code>IBTTYPE</code> as the most general subcollection of #$VisualInformationBearingThing that visually depicts some instance of a collection <code>COLL</code>, i.e., <code>(#$visualIBTTypeVisualFeatureTypeDefinedByRelnAndType IBTTYPE #$visuallyDepicts COLL)</code>.")
(comment visualIBTTypeVisualFeatureTypeDefinedByRelnAndType "<code>(#$visualIBTTypeVisualFeatureTypeDefinedByRelnAndType IBTTYPE RELN COLL)</code> means that, by definition, <code>IBTTYPE</code> is the most general subcollection of #$VisualInformationBearingThing whose instances bears the relation <code>RELN</code> to some instance of the collection <code>COLL</code>.  <p>For example, <code>(#$visualIBTTypeVisualFeatureTypeDefinedByRelnAndType VisualIBTThatDepictsACelebrity #$visuallyDepicts #$Celebrity)</code> identifies VisualIBTThatDepictsACelebrity as the collection of visual IBTs (#$VisualInformationBearingThing, q.v.) that depict at least one celebrity, i.e., as the most general collection <code>IBTTYPE</code> of instances of #$VisualInformationBearingThing such that <code>(#$relationAllExists #$visuallyDepicts IBTTYPE #$Celebrity)</code>.")
(comment VisualIBTTypeByVisualFeatureTypeFn "<code>(#$VisualIBTTypeByVisualFeatureTypeFn RELN COLL)</code> denotes the most general subcollection <code>IBTTYPE</code> of #$VisualInformationBearingThing such that each of its instances bears the <code>RELN</code> relation to at least one instance of <code>COLL</code>, i.e., <code>(#$relationAllExists RELN IBTTYPE COLL)</code>.  For example, <code>(#$VisualIBTTypeByVisualFeatureTypeFn #$visuallyDepicts #$Celebrity)</code> denotes the most general type <code>IBTTYPE</code> of visual IBT such that <code>(#$relationAllExists #$visuallyDepicts IBTTYPE #$Celebrity)</code>.")
(comment Facility-Generic "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$Facility-Generic is a #$FixedStructure or #$Place that is designed for a specific use or to accomodate a specific type of (human or animal) activity.")
(comment Texture "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$Texture is a collection of instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized such that each has the same (main exterior) texture.  Instances of #$Texture include #$Rough, #$Smooth, and #$Slimy.")
(disjointWith Smooth Rough)
(comment sihmsMutuallySupportive-ConceptReferredToInDistinctWays "An instance of #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate and #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate.  <code>(#$sihmsWithCommonSuperExpressionMutuallySupportive SIHM-1 SIHM-2)</code> means that the instances <code>SIHM-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory are mutually supportive, in a very specific way:  Given three instances of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg <code>PEG</code>, <code>SUB-PEG-1</code> and <code>SUB-PEG-2</code> such that <code>(#$properSubExpressions PEG SUB-PEG-1)</code> and <code>(#$properSubExpressions PEG SUB-PEG-2)</code> where <code>SUB-PEG-1</code> and <code>SUB-PEG-2</code> are not sub-expressions of each other, and <code>SIHM-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> contain hypothesized semantic interpretations of <code>SUB-PEG-1</code> and <code>SUB-PEG-2</code>,  <code>(#$sihmsWithCommonSuperExpressionMutuallySupportive SIHM-1 SIHM-2)</code> holds just in case <code>SIHM-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> distinguish themselves from other pairs of interpretations of parts of <code>PEG</code> by virtue of a semantic closeness between them that could help form a sense of what <code>PEG</code> is about.  <p>For example, if <code>PEG</code> is an occurrence of the sentence, \"Oil and other natural resources will increase in value as the earth's population continues to grow,\" and among the various interpretation hypotheses for the sub-expressons of <code>PEG</code> are <code>SIHM-1</code>, that \"oil\" in this context maps to #$Petroleum-CrudeOil; <code>SIHM-2</code>, that \"oil\" maps to #$ArtistsOilPaint, and <code>SIHM-3</code>, that \"natural resources\" maps to #$NaturalResourceType,  then because <code>(#$isa #$Petroleum-CrudeOil #$NaturalResourceType)</code>, <code>SIHM-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> count as mutually supportive, whereas <code>SIHM-2</code> and <code>SIHM-3</code> do not.")
(comment sihmsMutuallySupportive-ConceptReferredToInDistinctWays "An instance of #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate and #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.  <code>(#$sihmsMutuallySupportive-ConceptReferredToInDistinctWays SIHM-1 SIHM-2)</code> means that the instances <code>SIHM-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory are mutually supportive in a very specific way:  Given three instances of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg <code>PEG</code>, <code>SUB-PEG-1</code> and <code>SUB-PEG-2</code> such that <code>(#$properSubExpressions PEG SUB-PEG-1)</code> and <code>(#$properSubExpressions PEG SUB-PEG-2)</code> where <code>SUB-PEG-1</code> and <code>SUB-PEG-2</code> are not sub-expressions of each other, <code>SIHM-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> contain hypothesized semantic interpretations <code>I1</code> of <code>SUB-PEG-1</code> and <code>I2</code> of <code>SUB-PEG-2</code>, and these pegs are occurrences of distinct strings,  <code>(#$sihmsMutuallySupportive-ConceptReferredToInDistinctWays SIHM-1 SIHM-2)</code> holds just in case <code>I1</code> is identical to <code>I2</code>.  <p>For example, if <code>PEG</code> is an occurrence of the sentence, \"Oil is a natural resource whose value will continue to grow, as the demand for crude will rise as the economies and populations of developing countries continue to grow,\" and among the various interpretation hypotheses for the sub-expressons of <code>PEG</code> are <code>SIHM-1</code>, that \"oil\" in this context maps to #$Petroleum-CrudeOil and <code>SIHM-2</code>, that \"crude\" maps to #$Petroleum-CrudeOil, then <code>SIHM-1</code> and <code>SIHM-2</code> count as mutually supportive.")
(comment DirectTV1-Satellite "#$DirectTV1-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 109.8 West.")
(comment DirectTV1R-Satellite "#$DirectTV1R-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 101.2 West.")
(comment DirectTV2-Satellite "#$DirectTV2-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 100.8 West.")
(comment DirectTV3-Satellite "#$DirectTV3-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 100.8 West.")
(comment DirectTV4S-Satellite "#$DirectTV4S-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 101.2 West.")
(comment DirectTV6-Satellite "#$DirectTV6-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 118.8 West.")
(comment EchoStar5-Satellite "#$EchoStar5-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 110.0 West.")
(comment EchoStar2-Satellite "#$EchoStar2-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 119.0 West.")
(comment EchoStar6-Satellite "#$EchoStar6-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 119.0 West.")
(comment EchoStar7-Satellite "#$EchoStar7-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 119.0 West.")
(comment EchoStar4-Satellite "#$EchoStar4-Satellite is a geostationary communications
satellite used for television broadcasting. It maintains a fixed
#$longitude of 119.5 West.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromATM "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by the ATM group for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromBrandeis "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by Brandeis for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment AquaintKBPilotEvaluationProblemFromLCC-M "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems which were contributed by the LCC-M group for the Aquaint KB pilot evaluation.")
(comment EarthRadius-Standard "An approximate distance from the center of the #$PlanetEarth to the surface. Equal to ((#$PerFn #$Mile #$Radian) 3980.91).")
(comment AnomalousSituation "A sub-collection of #$Situation. Each instance is somehow out of the normal range of situations of its (other) types.")
(comment considerMerging "An instance of #$DocumentationPredicate that indicates a need for work that will improve KB hygiene. (#$considerMerging COLL-1 COLL-2) means that COLL-1 should possibly be merged onto COLL-2. Rules which generate taxonomies from KB content assumes the merge consideration to be correct. Accordingly, a forward rule asserts (#$taxonomyUseTermForTerm TAXONOMY COLL-2 COLL-1) for every taxonomy for which (nodeInSystem COLL-1 TAXONOMY) or (nodeInSystem COLL-2 TAXONOMY) is true. This is, in turn, used by taxonomy display functions to combine the terms in output and look to COLL-2 for lexification.")
(disjointWith SKSISupportedConstant SKSISupportedComparisonPredicate)
(comment SKSISupportedConstant "This is the collection of mathematical constants (such as #$Pi-Number) which are
supported by SKSI in the sense that they are translatable to their SQL
equivalents.")
(comment AssociativeRelation "The collection of polyadic #$Relations having the property of <i>associativity</i>.  Roughly speaking, a relation is associative if certain different sequences of recursive applications of it lead to the same final result.
<p>
More precisely:  If <b>RELN</b> is binary, it is an instance of #$AssociativeRelation if and only if (for any appropriate arguments <b>A</b>, <b>B</b>, and <b>C</b>) the corresponding formulas <b>(RELN A (RELN B C))</b> and <b>(RELN (RELN A B) C)</b> are (extensionally) equivalent: if <b>RELN</b> is a #$Function-Denotational both formulas have the same denotatum (or both have no denotatum), while if <b>RELN</b> is a #$Predicate or #$LogicalConnective both formulas have the same truth value.  If <b>RELN</b> has a higher arity than two, the definition is similar, but with the relevant formula schemata appropriately rewritten.  If <b>RELN</b> is variable-arity (see #$VariableArityRelation), it is an #$AssociativeRelation if and only of it behaves associatively with respect to each of the particular arities it subsumes (its #$arityMin to #$arityMax).  No unary relation is associative. 
<p>
It follows from the above definition that that each argument-place of an associative relation must admit exactly the same class of arguments (see #$admittedArgument), and that its results must also be of that same class (so that it can be applied recursively).
<p>
Examples of associative relations include #$PlusFn, #$TimesFn, #$JoinListsFn, #$SpatialSumFn, #$or, and #$and.
<p>
Note that, while many #$AssociativeRelations are #$CommutativeRelations (q.v.), this is not always the case.  Consider for example #$ConcatenateStringsFn.  Since it is associative, (('c'^'a')^'t') = ('c'^('a'^'t')) = 'cat'.  But it is certainly <i>not</i> commutative: 'cat' =/ 'act'.")
(comment anatomicallyCapableOf "The predicate #$anatomicallyCapableOf indicates that an agent is anatomically able to play a certain role in a certain type of situation or event.  (#$anatomicallyCapableOf AGENT SIT-TYPE ROLE) means that an individual #$EmbodiedAgent AGENT has the anatomical prerequisites (natural or prosthetic) needed to play ROLE in normal instances of SIT-TYPE.

We mean 'anatomical' to be construed fairly broadly in this case.  Strictly speaking, 'anatomical' may mean 'pertaining to the structures of organisms (e.g. the structures of plants or animals)'.  In the case of this predicate, however, 'anatomical' means 'pertaining to the physical structures of embodied agents.  Thus androids and even non-humanoid robots can be considered to have anatomical capabilities.

Note that #$anatomicallyCapableOf is different inmeaning from #$physicallyCapableOf (q.v.).
For instance, (#$anatomicallyCapableOf  #$MarthaThePassengerPigeon #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy), but if Martha were caged, then (#$not (#$physicallyCapableOf #$MarthaThePassengerPigeon  #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy)).")
(comment PerceptualAgent-Embodied "A specialization of #$EmbodiedAgent (q.v.). #$PerceptualAgent-Embodied is the collection of all sentient agents.  Instances of #$PerceptualAgent-Embodied are embodied agents capable of performing acts of #$Perceiving (q.v.).  In many cases information that a perceptual agent gathers through perceiving can influence certain other of the agent's actions.")
(comment TaxonomyLinkPredicate "The collection of predicates, including e.g. #$broaderThan, used to describe links within a #$Taxonomy. For taxonomy TAXON, GAFs involving these predicates are asserted (usually by rules) into (ContextOfPCWFn TAXON). Certain population rules which apply to all of these predicates are forward generated based on (#$isa PRED #$TaxonomyLinkPredicate).")
(comment mapsToTaxonomyReln "(mapsToTaxonomyReln TAXON KBPRED TAXPRED) means that assertions of the form (KBPRED TERM1 TERM2) will appear in TAXON as (TAXPRED TERM1 TERM2).")
(comment classificationSystemOf-Focally "<code>(#$classificationSystemOf-Focally ONTOLOGY TYPE)</code> means that <code>SYSTEM</code>, an instance of #$ClassificationSystem, is, focally, an system that classifies elements of the set or collection (#$SetOrCollection) <code>TYPE</code>.  That a classification system is <i>focally</i> a system of classifying some type <code>TYPE</code> does not mean that every concept in the system is an instance of <code>TYPE</code>.  For example, an ontology (#$Ontology) will also contain relations and operators that enable it to represent facts and rules, where those facts and rules will serve primarily to define the instances of <code>TYPE</code> that occur in the ontology, and, to a lesser extent, to define the concepts that are needed to define those instances of <code>TYPE</code>.  For example, an ontology of biological species might also contain concepts, such as height, weight, and anatomical concepts that, though not the focus of the ontology per se, must have some representation in the ontology to be of use in representing biological species.")
(comment LinearObjectConcept "Instances of #$LinearObjectConcept are concepts which are types or relations about #$LinearObjects.")
(comment Line-NonPlanar "A specialization of #$Line and #$LinearObject-Planar (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Line-NonPlanar is a curved intangible line that does <i>not</i> remain in a single two-dimensional plane, but can only be embedded in a 3-D space region.  For example, a one-dimensional unbounded spiral is a non-planar line.  And cf. #$Line-Planar.")
(comment structurePrimaryLanguage "<code>(#$structurePrimaryLanguage STRUCTURE LANGUAGE)</code> identifies <code>LANGUAGE</code> as the instance of #$Language that is the primary language in which the textual portions of the instance <code>STRUCTURE</code> of #$GraphicalStructure are written.")
(comment Wikipedia-WebSite "The English-language edition of the online encylopeida.")
(comment UnicodeString "#$UnicodeString is the collection of strings of unicode code-points, Code points have a value greater than equal to 0 and less
than or equal to 1,114,111 (10ffff in base-16). They are defined in the unicode standard available at: http://www.unicode.org/")
(comment LexiconExtensionHypothesisMicrotheory "The collection of instances of #$HypotheticalContext that represent hypothesized extensions to a lexicon.  Typically, these will be generated by Cyc or a related program on the fly when that program encounters what appear to be heretofore unknown words while processing text.")
(comment lexiconExtensionHypothesisMtTypeForLanguage "<code>(#$lexiconExtensionHypothesisMtTypeForLanguage MT-TYPE LANGUAGE)</code> identifies <code>MT-TYPE</code> as the specialization of #$LexiconExtensionHypothesisMicrotheory each instance of which is a microtheory that hypothesizes an extension to the lexicon for the instance of #$Language <code>LANGUAGE</code>.")
(comment LexiconExtensionHypothesisMtTypeForLanguageFn "<code>(#$LexiconExtensionHypothesisMtTypeForLanguageFn LANGUAGE)</code> denotes the subcollection of instances of #$LexiconExtensionHypothesisMicrotheory that hypothesize extensions to the lexicon (see #$languageHasRootLexicon) for the instance of #$Language <code>LANGUAGE</code>.")
(comment wordExtractedFromStructure "<code>(#$wordExtractedFromStructure WORD STRUCTURE)</code> means that the instance of #$LexicalWord <code>WORD</code> was first encountered by a Cyc-based text processing program (e.g., the TextLearner system (#$TheCycTextLearnerProgram)) while processing text found in <code>STRUCTURE</code> an instance of #$AbstractStructure.")
(comment Meal-ReadyToEat "Each instance of #$Meal-ReadyToEat is a military field ration, also known as 'meal ready to eat'.")
(comment WordWithSpeechPartPredAndStringFn "<code>(#$WordWithSpeechPartPredAndStringFn PRED STRING)</code> denotes the instance <code>WORD</code> of #$LexicalWord such that <code>(PRED WORD STRING)</code>, where <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$SpeechPartPredicate and <code>STRING</code> is an instance of #$CharacterString.  For example, <code>(#$WordWithSpeechPartPredAndStringFn #$singular \"exclave\")</code> denotes the word for which \"exclave\" is a valid singular speech part.")
(comment Cardinal-Mathematical "A specialization of #$Number-General (q.v.).  This is the collection of all cardinals, finite and infinite.  It includes both the #$NonNegativeIntegers and the #$TransfiniteCardinals, such as Aleph-0 (see #$PlusInfinity) and #$Aleph-1.")
(comment PlusInfinity "An instance of #$TransfiniteCardinal (q.v.).  #$PlusInfinity, commonly known as <i>aleph-zero</i> or <i>aleph-null</i>, is the smallest infinite cardinal number.")
(comment angleBetweenEdgesOfObject "A quaternary spatial predicate that relates adjacent edges of an object to the measure of the angle they form at their common endpoint, as measured from the interior of the object. <code>(#$angleBetweenEdgesOfObject EDGE1 EDGE2 DEGREES OBJ)</code> means that <code>EDGE1</code> and <code>EDGE2</code> form an angle that measures <code>DEGREES</code> within <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment ExitingAnAutomobile "The collection of all instances of #$ExitingAVehicle in which the vehicle exited is an instance of #$Automobile.")
(comment TravelRoute-Air "A specialization #$TravelRoute and #$PathThroughAir. Each instance of #$TravelRoute-Air is an established line of travel or access that traverses some #$AirspaceRegion, intended for use by #$AirTransportationDevices.")
(comment queryBindings "<code>(#$queryBindings QUERY-SPEC BINDING-SET)</code> means that in response to the query specification <code>QUERY-SPEC</code>, the #$CycInferenceEngine currently generates an assignment <code>BINDING-SET</code> of bindings to variables.")
(comment TemporallyContinuousThing "A specialization of #$TemporalThing.  Each instance of #$TemporallyContinuousThing <code>CONT</code> is a thing that occupies (see #$temporalExtent) or is one continuous chunk of time.  More precisely: <code>CONT</code> #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.) every time-interval between its #$startingPoint and #$endingPoint.   Thus, there is no time-interval between the start and end of <code>CONT</code> during which <code>CONT</code> does not exist (if a #$SomethingExisting) or occur (if an #$Event) or obtain (if a #$StaticSituation) or which <code>CONT</code> does not have as one of its #$timeSlices (if a #$TimeInterval).
<p>
For example, all #$Dates are temporally-continuous (see the specialization #$ContinuousTimeInterval), and most or all ordinary physical objects are temporally-continuous.
<p>
See also #$SpatiallyContinuousThing.")
(comment interrupts "A specialization of #$temporallyDisjoint that holds between two events such that the occurring of one interrupts the occuring of the other.  <code>(#$interrupts INTERRUPTOR INTERRUPTEE)</code> means that <code>INTERRUPTOR</code> at least temporarily puts a stop to <code>INTERRUPTEE</code>.  
<p>
Note that if <code>INTERRUPTOR</code> is a continuous event (see #$TemporallyContinuousThing) then it must start after <code>INTERRUPTEE</code> starts.  If <code>INTERRUPTEE</code> resumes after the interruption, it does so only after <code>INTERRUPTOR</code> (permanently or temporarily) stops occurring.")
(comment realizes "An instance of #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot. (#$realizes AGENT PROP) means that AGENT realizes that PROP is true. (#$realizes AGENT PROP) implies (#$knows AGENT PROP); moreover, (#$realizes AGENT PROP) pressuposes that prior to (#$realizes AGENT PROP) becoming true, AGENT did not believe (see the predicate #$beliefs) that PROP is true.")
(disjointWith Frigate-SailingShip ModernFrigate)
(comment SloopOfWar "The collection of instances of #$Corvette-Ship that are instances of #$SailingWarship.")
(disjointWith SailingVessel Watercraft-Subsurface)
(disjointWith Sailboat TallShip)
(comment SailingVessel "A specialization of #$TransportationDevice and #$WindPoweredDevice.  Instances are transportation devices that are powered by wind blowing against sails supported by masts.")
(comment Cutter-SailingVessel "A specialization of #$SailingVessel and #$Watercraft-Surface.  A cutter is distinguished by having two headsails.")
(comment HeadSail "The collection of all instances of #$Sail-Device that, by design or use, are headsails -- sails that are connected to the vessel at both the mast (#$Mast-Device) and the bow (front) of the boat.")
(comment Sloop "The collection of all sailing watercraft that have a single mast (#$Mast-Device) and two main sails, one connected at the mast and bow (front) of the boat, the other connected at the mast and aft (back) of the boat.")
(comment WaterTransportationDeviceTypeByDesign "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$WaterTransportationDeviceTypeByDesign is a collection of instances of #$WaterTransportationDevice which share a certain distinctive design.  Notable instances of this collection include #$Submarine and #$Rowboat.")
(disjointWith SelfPoweredDevice AncientNavalShip)
(comment IntelligenceAnalysisProcess "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$IntelligenceActivity.  Each instance of #$IntelligenceAnalysisProcess is an event in which an #$IntelligenceAnalyst gathers and analyzes information for intelligence purposes.")
(comment CulturalScenarioEvaluationProcess "A specialization of #$Research and #$Evaluating.  Each instance of #$CulturalScenarioEvaluationProcess is an event in which a person assesses the way that cultural norms are likely to bear on the development of a situation.  Often a person undertakes such an assessment in order to determine how to act in a planned cross-cultural encounter.")
(comment ContinuousTimeInterval "The intersection of #$TimeInterval and #$TemporallyContinuousThing (qq.v.).  Specializations of #$ContinuousTimeInterval include #$Date and #$TimePoint.")
(comment temporallyContinuousDuring "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that relates  a #$TemporalThing to any #$ContinuousTimeInterval (q.v.) that it temporally subsumes.  <code>(#$temporallyContinuousDuring TEMP INTERVAL)</code> means that <code>TEMP</code> #$temporallySubsumes the continuous chunk of time <code>INTERVAL</code>.  That is, <code>TEMP</code> subsumes every time-interval between the #$startingPoint and #$endingPoint of <code>INTERVAL</code>.   Thus, there is no time-interval between the start and end of <code>INTERVAL</code> during which <code>TEMP</code> does not exist (if a #$SomethingExisting) or occur (if an #$Event) or obtain (if a #$StaticSituation) or which <code>TEMP</code> does not have as one of its #$timeSlices (if a #$TimeInterval).
<p>
Note that a given thing might be temporally continuous during some but not all of the continuous intervals between its own #$startingPoint and #$endingPoint.  A #$TemporallyContinuousThing (q.v.), however, is continuous during all such intervals.")
(comment BUTLERVerificationProcess-1 "A sub-collection of #$VerificationOfTruth.  All events of this category involve the #$BUTLERAgent using the #$Google-SearchEngine to verify the truth of some set of #$CycLFullyGroundAtomicSentence.  This process involves the generation of a natural language form of the GAF as well as an additional string that contains a more precise formulation of one of the key constants in that formula.  The conjunction of those strings is passed to a copy of the #$Google-SearchEngine.  This type of verification is achieved if Google returns any results from its search of the web.")
(comment forgets "An instance of #$ActionPredicate.  (#$forgets AGENT PROP) means that there is an instance of #$ForgettingThat (q.v.) done by AGENT in which PROP is forgotten.")
(comment InvitedDiningAtResidence "A subcollection of #$SocialGatheringAtResidence.  In each
instance of #$InvitedDiningAtResidence, the focal sub-event is a meal
provided for the guest or guests (#$guestAtSocialGathering, qv.) by
the host.  Typically, the guest and host share the meal, often with
others (e.g., family members of the host).  It is also typical for the
meal to have been prepared by the host and/or some of her close
associates (family, roomates, servants), though it is possible for the
meal to be catered.  In some instances of #$InvitedDiningAtResidence,
guests contribute to the meal in minor ways, e.g., by bringing side
dishes they purchased or prepared.  But a true 'pot-luck' dinner would
not be an instance of #$InvitedDiningAtResidence.")
(comment subjectivelyEvaluates "This predicate can be used to assert that an agent has made a certain essentially subjective assessment or value judgment about a particular individual.  (#$subjectivelyEvaluates AGENT INDIV EVAL) means that the AGENT projects upon INDIV the #$EvaluativeQuantity (q.v.) EVAL.  As the evaluation in question is essentially subjective, the above formula of course does _not_ entail either (i) that INDIV actually has the feature it is subjectively thought to have (or some \"objective\" counterpart thereof) or (ii) that any other given agent would also attribute EVAL to INDIV.  Indeed, outside of contexts explicitly defined so as to represent some particular agent's point of view (e.g. some sentient agent's inner mental world or the world-view espoused by a particular religion), it arguably makes no sense to attribute an #$EvaluativeQuantity to an individual directly. 
<p>
For example, (#$subjectivelyEvaluates Sally SallyLearningToRideABike #$Easy) says that Sally herself finds/found learning to ride a bike easy; and this might be true regardless of any \"objective\" ranking of the relative level of difficulty involved in learning to ride a bike, and regardless even of any third-person observer's judgment as to how hard or easy a time Sally had learning to ride.  (Of course, assuming Sally is at least minimally self-aware and the observer similarly perceptive, we would expect their respective judgments on this point likely to match fairly well.)  
<p>
Note that AGENT's basis for the assessment might be largely emotional, logical-inferential (e.g. having a plausible argument), empirical, or some combination of these.  Note also that, while there is an almost trivially sense in which _any_ judgment or attribution made by a sentient agent has a subjective (i.e. conscious, inner) aspect or manifestation, #$subjectivelyEvaluates pertains specifically to #$EvaluativeQuantity, and thus to what are normally considered value judgments (broadly construed).  Hence the use of the expressions \"essentially subjective\" and \"projects upon\" in the foregoing to describe the sort of attributions at issue.  
<p>
For a similar predicate that relates an agent to a state of affairs onto which s/he projects an evaluative attribute, see #$evaluates-Propositional.")
(comment Speedboat "The collection of instances of #$Motorboat that are designed to travel very fast across the water.")
(comment CigaretteBoat "A subcollection of #$Speedboat.  Instances are characterized by long, sleek hulls.")
(comment Bow-BoatOrShipPart "The collection of bows, the frontmost portion of a boat or ship.")
(comment ClosedBow "The collection of instances of #$Bow-BoatOrShipPart that have no (largish) openings along the top surface.")
(disjointWith Spiral LinearObject-Straight)
(comment Spiral "A specialization of both #$GeometricallyDescribableThing and #$LinearObject.  This is the collection of all spiral-shaped things, tangible or intangible.  Each instance of #$Spiral is a curved linear-object that makes one or more turns about an axis.  As with #$LinearObjects generally, a spiral might be one-, two-, or three-dimensional.")
(comment InfusionDrink "The collection of liquid extracts that result from steeping a substance (esp. herbs, see #$Herb-HumanUse) in water to produce a drink.  An infusion may be taken as either a beverage or a medicine.")
(comment HerbalTea "The collection of liquid extracts that result from steeping an herbal substance (see Herb-HumanUse) in water to produce a drink.")
(comment SailingShip "The collection of ships that are sailing vessels.")
(comment Galley "The collection of instances of #$Ship that are propelled using oars.")
(disjointWith Boat Ship)
(comment Stroking-Rowing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$RowingABoat. In each #$Stroking-Rowing, someone rows at a particular rate.")
(disjointWith Submarine (CommonShapeFn Boat))
(comment Boat "The collection of boats -- surface watercraft (see  #$Watercraft-Surface) consisting of a hull (see #$Hull) and with some (at least intended) method of propulsion, such as a mast-and-sail rigging, an attached motor, or (the in-prinicple availability of) oars or paddles.  Boats are distinguished from instances of #$Ship by size (\"a boat can fit on a ship, but not vice versa,\" the saying goes) or convention (e.g., a sailing vessel with three or more masts is typically considered a ship, whereas one with fewer than three is considered a boat).")
(comment movementsPowered "<code>(#$movementsPowered POWERING MOVEMENT)</code> means that <code>MOVEMENT</code>, an instance of #$MovementEvent is powered by the instance of #$PoweringADevice <code>POWERING</code>.")
(comment devicePowered "<code>(#$devicePowered POWERING DEVICE)</code> means that the instance <code>DEVICE</code> of #$PhysicalDevice is the device being powered in the instance <code>POWERING</code> of #$PoweringADevice.")
(comment Rowing "The collection of instances of #$VoluntaryBodyMovements used to power a device with oars, such as a boat or rowing machine.")
(comment OarPropelledWatercraft "The collection of all instances of #$Watercraft-Surface that are propelled by (#$movementsPowered) instances of #$Rowing.")
(comment AquaintKBPEProblem-Under100Char "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems for which the premise and query are (separately) no more than 100 characters in length.")
(comment BusTicket "An instance of #$TicketTypeBySitTypeAuthorized.  Instances are tickets (see #$Ticket) that license one to board and ride a bus (see #$Bus-RoadVehicle).")
(comment commonNicknameForName "A binary predicate relating two instances of HumanGivenName.  (commonNicknameForName NAME NICKNAME) means that the string(s) associated with NICKNAME is commonly used for people with the name NAME.")
(comment temporalExtent "A binary #$TemporalPredicate and specialization of #$cotemporal (q.v.) that relates each #$TemporalThing to the #$TimeInterval that it occupies.  <code>(#$temporalExtent TEMP TIME)</code> means that <code>TIME</code> is the time-interval of longest #$duration (q.v.) during which <code>TEMP</code> exists (if <code>TEMP</code> is a #$SomethingExisting) or occurs (if an #$Event) or obtains (if a #$StaticSituation).  If <code>TEMP</code> is itself a time-interval, the above #$temporalExtent sentence holds if and only if <code>TIME</code> is identical to <code>TEMP</code>.
<p>
Note that if <code>(#$temporalExtent TEMP TIME)</code> holds, <code>TIME</code> is a #$ContinuousTimeInterval (q.v.) if and only if <code>TEMP</code> is an instance of #$TemporallyContinuousThing (q.v.).
<p>
Note also that it is <i>not</i> the case that anything having a #$temporalExtent is a #$TemporallyExtendedThing (cf.), as that collection excludes all #$SomethingExistings. 
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$TemporalExtentFn.")
(comment ArcTangent2Fn "The trigonometric function <code>(#$ArcTangent2Fn Y
X)</code> performs the trigonometric arctangent operation on
<code>Y/X</code> and returns an angle in the range from -PI to PI
expressed in radians, using the signs of the parameters to determine
the quadrant. The result is valid even if <code>X</code> is 0 (angle
is PI/2 or -PI/2).")
(comment CSQLArcTangent2Fn "#$CSQLArcTangent2Fn is the #$CSQLFunction that corresponds to #$ArcTangent2Fn.")
(disjointWith Chimney PathForWheeledVehicles)
(disjointWith Smokestack Chimney-HousePart)
(disjointWith Pipe-GenericConduit FloorInAConstruction)
(disjointWith Chimney FloorInAConstruction)
(comment Chimney "The collection of chimneys, vertically-oriented portals that serve to direct hot or dangerous gases to an outdoor location, either by acting as a flue (see #$Flue) or (as with most modern home chimneys (#$Chimney-HousePart)) by serving as a rigid, protective (and often decorative) structure that contains the final part of the flue.")
(comment Smokestack "The collection of industrial chimneys.")
(comment managesToPerformActOfType "An instance of #$BinaryPredicate and #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate. (#$managesToPerformActOfType AGENT ACT-TYPE) means that AGENT manages to perform (see #$performedBy) an instance of ACT-TYPE. (#$managesToPerformActOfType AGENT ACT-TYPE) presupposes that AGENT attempts to perform an instance of ACT-TYPE, and implies that AGENT's attempt was successful. (#$managesToPerformActOfType AGENT ACT-TYPE) also generally implies that AGENT overcame some obstacles in successully performing an instance of ACT-TYPE.")
(comment queryVariable "<code>(#$queryVariable QUERY-SPEC SET)</code> means that in response to the query specification <code>QUERY-SPEC</code> (which is a query with exactly one free variable), the #$CycInferenceEngine currently generates an assignment <code>SET</code> of bindings.
<p>
For example, the following is currently true:
<p>
<pre>
 (#$queryVariable
   (#$QueryFn  
     (#$ist #$SimpleAstronomyDataVocabularyMt
     (#$isa ?PLANET #$Planet))
   (#$TheSet
       #$PlanetMars    #$PlanetEarth   #$PlanetTauBooB  #$PlanetMercury     
       #$PlanetVenus   #$PlanetSaturn  #$PlanetNeptune  #$PlanetJupiter
       #$PlanetUranus  #$PlanetPluto))
</pre>
<p>
See also #$queryAnswers and #$queryBindings.
")
(comment Flue "The collection of flues, instances of #$Pipe-ConduitArtifact designed to guide harmful gases into the atmosphere.")
(comment MicrowaveTower "A subcollection of #$CommunicationTower.  Instances of #$MicrowaveTower are towers that are designed to send and receive microwaves (see #$MicrowaveRadiation) to other such towers, which form large communication networks.")
(comment TransmissionOfRadiationTypeFn "<code>(#$TransmissionOfRadiationTypeFn RADIATION-TYPE)</code> denotes the collection of transmissions of information using wave propagations of a frequency defined by <code>RADIATION-TYPE</code>, an instance of #$ElectromagneticRadiationTypeByWaveFrequency.  For example, <code>(#$TransmissionOfRadiationTypeFn #$RadioWave)</code> denotes the collection of radio wave signals.")
(comment transmissionTypeOfRadiationType "<code>(#$transmissionTypeOfRadiationType TRANSMISSION-TYPE RADIATION-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>TRANSMISSION-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$InformationBearingWavePropagation that carry information through waves with a wavelength defined by <code>RADIATION-TYPE</code>, an instance of #$ElectromagneticRadiationTypeByWaveFrequency.")
(comment receptionOfWaveType "<code>(#$receptionOfWaveType RECEPTION-TYPE WAVE-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>RECEPTION-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$ReceivingAWave where the wave received is an instance of <code>WAVE-TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$WavePropagation.")
(comment emissionOfWaveType "<code>(#$emissionOfWaveType EMISSION-TYPE WAVE-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>EMISSION-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$EmittingAWave where the wave emitteded is an instance of <code>WAVE-TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$WavePropagation.")
(disjointWith HouseholdAppliance Tower)
(comment WaterCoolingDevice "The collection of instances of #$CoolingDevice whose primary function (see #$primaryFunction) is to cool water.  Contrast with #$WaterCooler, instances of which need not cool water, only maintain its temperature.")
(comment CoolingWater "The collection of instances of #$CoolingProcess in which water is cooled.")
(comment CoolingTower "The collection of cooling towers.  Cooling towers circulate water and put it in contact with air to cool it for re-use, typically in utility and chemical processing facilities.")
(comment supportingTypeOfType "<code>(#$supportingTypeOfType SUPPORT-TYPE TYPE)</code> identifies <code>SUPPORT-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$SupportingSomething where what is supported is an instance of <code>TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.")
(comment SupportingFn "<code>(#$SupportingFn TYPE)</code> denotes the collection of instances of #$SupportingSomething where what is supported is an instance of <code>TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.")
(comment Grenade "Each instance of #$Grenade is a small bomb that can be thrown or launched.  #$Grenade is partitioned into #$HandGrenade or #$RifleGrenade.")
(comment parallelVectors "A specialization of #$parallel that holds only 
between #$VectorIntervals (qq.v.).  <code>(#$parallelVectors VECTOR1 VECTOR2)</code> means that <code>VECTOR1</code> is \"parallel\" to <code>VECTOR2</code>.  Parallel vectors might either point in the same 
direction (see #$vectorDirection) or directly opposite directions (see 
#$oppositeDirection-Precise).")
(comment parallel "An #$EquivalenceRelation and a very general quasi-spatial predicate that is used to state that two lines, aligned objects, or vectors are parallel or point in parallel directions.  <code>(#$parallel INDIV1 INDIV2)</code> means that <code>INDIV1</code> and <code>INDIV2</code> are parallel.  One or both of <code>INDIV1</code> and <code>INDIV2</code> might be a (tangible or intangible) line or line-segment (see #$LinearObject), a physical object with a well-defined lengthwise axis, or a vector (see #$VectorInterval).  
<p>
When appropriate, consider using one a more specialized predicate, such as #$parallelLines, #$parallelVectors, #$aligned, or #$parallelPaths.  And cf. #$perpendicular.")
(comment perpendicular "A very general quasi-spatial predicate that is used to state that two lines, surfaces, aligned objects, or vectors are perpendicular or oriented in perpendicular directions.  <code>(#$perpendicular INDIV1 INDIV2)</code> means that <code>INDIV1</code> and <code>INDIV2</code> are perpendicular.  One or both of <code>INDIV1</code> and <code>INDIV2</code> might be a (tangible or intangible) line or line-segment (see #$LinearObject), a surface (see #$SurfaceRegion), physical object with a well-defined lengthwise axis, or a vector (see #$VectorInterval).  
<p>
See also the specializations #$perpendicularObjects and #$perpendicularVectors.  And cf. #$parallel.")
(comment NationalFlag "The collection of instances of #$Flag that symoblize an instance of #$Country.")
(comment FlagTypeByAgentSymbolized "A collection of subcollections of #$Flag.  Each instance of #$FlagTypeByAgentSymbolized is a type of flag <code>FLAG-TYPE</code> such that each instance of <code>FLAG-TYPE</code> symbolizes the same instance of #$MultiIndividualAgent.")
(disjointWith NationalFlag (CollectionDifferenceFn Flag NationalFlag))
(disjointWith (FlagFn UnitedStatesOfAmerica) (CollectionDifferenceFn NationalFlag (FlagFn UnitedStatesOfAmerica)))
(comment collaborateViaMemberActions "(collaborateViaMemberActions ORG1 ORG2) means that ORG1 and ORG2 can be said to be collaborating in view of certain actions deliberately undertaken by their members in certain types of events or situation.  The relevant event types and action types vary with the type of organizations involved.")
(comment NumericIntervalSlot "A specialization of #$ScalarIntervalSlot (q.v.).  Each instance of #$NumericIntervalSlot is a binary predicate that is used to attribute a property to a thing, by relating it to a #$NumericInterval of some sort, typically a #$RealNumber.
<p>
Examples of #$NumericIntervalSlot include #$pulseRate, #$bloodAlcoholContent, and #$iQ.
<p>
See also #$MeasurableQuantitySlot.")
(comment Situation "A specialization of both #$IntangibleIndividual and #$TemporalThing.  Each instance of #$Situation is a state or event consisting of one or more objects having certain properties or bearing certain relations to each other.  Notable specializations of #$Situation are #$Event and #$StaticSituation; it is disjoint with #$SomethingExisting.")
(comment QuantityType "A #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$ObjectType.  Each instance of #$QuantityType is a specialization of #$Quantity (q.v.).  Notable specializations of #$QuantityType include #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType and #$UnorderedQuantityType.")
(comment FamousHuman "A specialization of #$HomoSapiens.  This is the collection of all human beings that are #$Famous (i.e. identifiable by a large number of other human beings). See also #$HistoricHuman.")
(disjointWith LinguisticObject TupleOfIntervals)
(comment gamePlayingType "<code>(gamePlayingType GAME ACTIVITY-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>ACTIVITY-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$PlayingAGame in which the game being played (see #$Game) is <code>GAME</code>.  For example, <code>(#$gamePlayingType #$Cricket-TheGame (#$PlayingFn #$Cricket-TheGame))</code> means that <code>(#$PlayingFn #$Cricket-TheGame)</code> is the collection of events in which people are playing cricket.")
(comment instantiationsOfGameAre "<code>(#$instantiationsOfGameAre GAME EVENT-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>EVENT-TYPE</code> as the collection of all complete games played of the game <code>GAME</code>.")
(comment resultGenlWhenArgIsa "<code>(#$resultGenlWhenArgIsa FN COLL NUM TYPE)</code> means that when the <code>NUM</code>th argument to a well-formed application of the function <code>FN</code> is an instance of the collection <code>TYPE</code>, that application of <code>FN</code> will denote a subcollection of <code>COLL</code>.  <p>For example, <code>(#$resultGenlWhenArgIsa #$FlagFn #$NationalFlag 1 #$Country)</code> means that any expression of the form <code>(#$FlagFn AGENT)</code> will denote a subcollection of #$NationalFlag if <code>(#$isa AGENT #$Country)</code>.")
(comment SportsCourt "Any area used to play sports that is also referred to as a \"court\" in English.")
(comment PointAtDistanceAndBearingFn "<code>(#$PointAtDistanceAndBearingFn THING DISTANCE AZIMUTH)</code> returns the #$Point which is obtained by translating from <code>THING</code> a fixed distance <code>DISTANCE</code> along the great circle with the geographical bearing of <code>AZIMUTH</code>.")
(comment Observing "A collection of information-gathering events in which an agent or group of agents acquire information by observing some thing or event.  The observation may be mediated by equipment, such a binoculars, video cameras, body wires, an intercom, and other aids to real-time observation, and direct sensory perception on the part of a #$PerceptualAgent-Embodied is not required, although in cases of group observation, we require that there be at least one member of the group playing the role of #$recipientOfInfo in the #$Observing. Positive examples: a military unit's following air traffic by means of radar or a submarine crew's tracking the progress of another submarine by means of sonar.")
(comment minQuantValue "A specialization of #$scalarBounds (q.v.) that relates a given #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) to the minimum #$ScalarPointValue (q.v.) it subsumes (see #$quantitySubsumes). <code>(#$minQuantValue SCALAR POINT)</code> means that the lower limit of <code>SCALAR</code> is <code>POINT</code>.
<p>
For example, if all mailroom employees earned from five to eight dollars per hour, <code>(#$minQuantValue \"MailroomPayRange\" (#$DollarsPerHour 5))</code> would hold.  Other examples:
<pre>
  (#$minQuantValue (#$Mile 15 20) (#$Mile 15))

  (#$minQuantValue (#$Unity 6 12) 6).
</pre>
<p>
See also #$maxQuantValue and #$pointQuantValue.")
(comment maxQuantValue "A specialization of #$scalarBounds (q.v.) that relates a given #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) to the maximum #$ScalarPointValue (q.v.) it subsumes (see #$quantitySubsumes). <code>(#$maxQuantValue SCALAR POINT)</code> means that the upper limit of <code>SCALAR</code> is <code>POINT</code>.
<p>
For example, if all mailroom employees earned from five to eight dollars per hour, <code>(#$maxQuantValue \"MailroomPayRange\" (#$DollarsPerHour 8))</code> would hold.  Other examples:
<pre>
  (#$maxQuantValue (#$Mile 15 20) (#$Mile 20))

  (#$maxQuantValue (#$Unity 6 12) 12).
</pre>
<p>
See also #$minQuantValue and #$pointQuantValue.")
(comment CharacterStringType "A #$SecondOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$CharacterStringType is a collection of concrete character-string tokens (regardless of their physical form: written, spoken, or whatever) that all represent the same abstract character-string.  That is, each instance of #$CharacterStringType is the collection of all character-string tokens that #$tokensString some particular character-string.  (Thus #$CharacterStringType might have been called \"<i>TokenTypeByCharacterString</i>\".)  An example is the collection of all tokens of the string 'hello', whether inscribed, sounded, or electromagnetically encoded.  
<p>
For more specialized collections that correspond to particular physical forms, see 
#$CharacterStringMode and its specializations.  Note that although instances of #$CharacterStringType group character-string tokens according what abstract character-strings they represent, character-string types are not to be confused with the abstract character-strings themselves; see #$typeForString.")
(comment GeographicalAgent "A specialization of both #$MultiIndividualAgent and #$GeographicalThing.  Each instance of #$GeographicalAgent is a group of people and/or organizations cohesive enough to be treated as an agent (see the collection #$Agent-PartiallyTangible, of which #$GeographicalAgent is also a specialization), and which occupies a particular instance of #$GeographicalRegion.  Important specializations of #$GeographicalAgent include #$GeopoliticalEntity, #$University, and #$Neighborhood.  
<p>
Note that instances of #$GeographicalAgent are viewed in two significantly different ways with respect to two different types of geography-related microtheories. In a \"physical\" geography microtheory (i.e. #$PhysicalGeographyMt and its submicrotheories), geographical agents are clearly distinguished from the regions they occupy. (#$TerritoryFn GEO-AGENT) is used in these contexts to denote the land mass (an instance of  #$GeographicalRegion) occupied by a given geographical agent GEO-AGENT. In a \"dualist\" geography microtheory (i.e. #$DualistGeopoliticalMt and its submicrotheories), on the other hand, geographical agents are viewed as being <i>both</i> agents <i>and</i> land masses (instances of #$GeographicalRegion). Thus, there is little need for #$TerritoryFn in the latter sort of context. (Despite their somewhat paradoxical flavor, dualist microtheories arguably allow Cyc to mimic commonsense reasoning about geographical agents and regions more closely than do the stricter physical microtheories.) There are also some \"generic\" geography microtheories (e.g. #$WorldGeographyMt and #$UnitedStatesGeographyMt) which are neutral with respect to the physical and dualist views. 
<p>
Also see the shared-note #$Dualist-Physical-AndGenericGeographyMtNote.")
(comment subSituationTypes "A specialization of #$temporallySubsumes-TypeType and #$partTypes (qq.v.).   This predicate is a type-level correlate of #$subSituations (q.v.), and is used to state that situations (see #$Situation) of one given type always have temporal situations of another given type as sub-situations.  <code>(#$subSituationTypes SUPERTYPE SUBTYPE)</code> means that for every instance <code>SUPERINST</code> of <code>SUPERTYPE</code>, there is an instance <code>SUBINST</code> of <code>SUBTYPE</code> such that <code>(#$subSituations SUPERINST SUBINST)</code>. 
<p>
For example, <code>(#$subSituationTypes UnderstandingBachelorhood UnderstandingManhood)</code> might mean that anyone's being in a situation (i.e. #$MentalState) of understanding the concept of bachelorhood involves being in a situation of understanding the concept of manhood.  
<p>
An important specialization of this predicate is #$subEventTypes.")
(comment orgTypeFacilityType "This predicate is a type-level version of #$orgFacilities. (#$orgTypeFacilityType ORG-TYPE FAC-TYPE) means that every instance of ORG-TYPE is related to some instance of FAC-TYPE via the #$orgFacilities relation, and that every instance of FAC-TYPE is related to some instance of ORG-TYPE via the #$orgFacilities relation.  This is also the predicate corresponding to #$FacilityTypeOfOrganizationTypeFn (q.v.).")
(comment circularRegionAtCenterWithRadius "<code>(#$circularRegionAtCenterWithRadius CIRCULARREGION POINT DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>CIRCULARREGION</code> is a #$CircularRegion which is bounded by a #$Circle centered at <code>POINT</code> and has a radius of <code>DISTANCE</code>.")
(comment eventTypeDataSentence "A binary #$TermSummaryPredicate and specialization of #$interestingSentence that relates an #$Event to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  (#$eventTypeDataSentence EVENT SENTENCE) means that SENTENCE expresses a fact about what type of event EVENT is.")
(comment FruitOrVegetableFood "A specialization of #$EdibleStuff and an instance of #$FoodGroup.  Each instance of #$FruitOrVegetableFood is a fruit or a vegetable.")
(comment relatives "A symmetric binary predicate that relates organisms (see #$Organism-Whole).  <code>(#$relatives ORGANISM1 ORGANISM2)</code> means that <code>ORGANISM1</code> is a relative of <code>ORGANISM2</code>.  In the case of organisms other than humans, a #$relatives relationship is established only via reproduction (sexual or asexual); see the specialization #$biologicalRelatives.  In the case of humans, however, a #$relatives relationship can be established also via certain social arrangements, such as #$Adoption, marriage, or fostering.")
(disjointWith TemporalPartPredicate TemporallyQualifiableRelation)
(comment performsActsOfType-Percentage "(#$performsActsOfType-Percentage AGENT ACTION-TYPE-1 ACTION-TYPE-2 PERCENT) holds just in case the percentage of the instances of ACTION-TYPE-1 that AGENT is known by Cyc to perform that are also instances of ACTION-TYPE-2 is PERCENT.  For instance, if 40% of the instances of #$TerroristAttack that Hamas is known to perform are also instances of #$Bombing, then (#$performsActsOfType-Percentage #$TerroristOrganization-Hamas #$TerroristAttack #$Bombing 40) holds.")
(comment HotAirBalloon "A specialization of #$LighterThanAirCraft.  Each instance of #$HotAirBalloon consists of a large balloon (the lifting envelope) which is filled with hot air and used to transport passengers in a basket, or gondolla suspended underneath.")
(comment HotAirBalloonGondola "A collection of instances of #$PassengerCompartment. Each instance of #$HotAirBalloonGondola is a smallish compartment that is designed to transport passengers in instances of (#$TransportViaFn #$HotAirBalloon).")
(comment interpretationMtContent-ParseResult "(#$interpretationMtContent-ParseResult MT CYCL) means that MT, an of #$SemanticInterpretationMicrotheory, is supposed to represent (see #$ist-Asserted) the (possibly strengthened) parse resultCYCL, a formula which will typically (but not necessarily) be an instance #$CycLSentence-Assertible.")
(comment CycLPredicator "A #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of #$CycLOperator (q.v.). Characterized synatactically, this is the collection of all and only those CycL terms that can appear as the main operator (i.e. in the \"arg0\" position) of a #$CycLClosedSentence. Characterized semantically, this is also the collection of all and only those CycL terms that denote #$Predicate relations. Examples of #$CycLPredicators include these three CycL expressions:
<pre>
  #$genls 
  #$loves
  (#$TypeCapableFn #$behaviorCapable)
</pre>
These examples reflect the fact that while most #$CycLPredicators encountered in practice are #$CycLConstants, a predicator might be a #$CycLNonAtomicTerm.
<p>
See also #$CycLFunctor.")
(comment CycLTruthValueSentence "A collection of CycL sentences containing only the CycL sentences <code>#$True</code> and <code>#$False</code>, both of which are sentential constants.")
(comment ImagePathnamePredicate "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$ImagePathnamePredicate is a #$Predicate that links a #$Thing to a corresponding image #$Pathname.")
(comment CollectionSubsetFn "A binary #$SubcollectionDenotingFunction that takes a #$Collection and a #$Set-Mathematical to the largest subcollection of the former that is a #$subsetOf the latter.  (#$CollectionSubsetFn <b>COL SET</b>) is, by definition, the collection of all and only those instances of <b>COL</b> that are also elements of <b>SET</b>.
<p>
For example,
<p>
<pre>
(#$CollectionSubsetFn
  #$TemporalThing
  (#$TheSetOf ?X (#$isa ?X #$TemporallyContinuousThing)))
</pre>
<p>
is the collection of all and only temporal things that are temporally-continuous.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$subcollectionOfAndSubsetOf.")
(comment SubSituationOfTypeSuchThatFn "<code>(#$SubSituationOfTypeSuchThatFn SIT SIT-TYPE SUB-SIT-TYPE
LIST-OF-LISTS)</code> denotes the instance <code>INST</code> of
<code>SUB-SIT-TYPE</code> that is a subsituation of <code>SIT</code>
<i>qua</i> scripted instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>.
<code>LIST-OF-LISTS</code> provides identifying features of
<code>INST</code>: it os a list of binary lists, the first element of
each of which is a binary role <code>ROLE</code>, and the second
element of each of which is a role-player such that plays the role
<code>ROLE</code> in <code>(#$SubSituationOfTypeSuchThatFn SIT
SIT-TYPE SUB-SIT-TYPE ROLE-LIST ACTOR-LIST)</code>.

<p>

For example, 

<pre>

(#$SubSituationOfTypeSuchThatFn 
#$BillGatesBrushesTeeth 
#$BrushingTeeth
#$TeethCleaning
(#$TheList  (#$TheList #$performedBy #$BillGates) 
(#$TheList #$deviceUsed BillGatesToothbrush))

</pre>
<p>

denotes the instance of #$TeethCleaning that is a sub-event of
#$BillGatesBrushesTeeth which is performed by Bill Gates, and which
involves Bill Gates' toothbriush as the device used.")
(comment ConjecturalContext "A specialization of #$Microtheory.  The assertions present in an instance of #$ConjecturalContext are conjectural relative to some other context.  The assertions in any #$ConjecturalContext CNTXT need not be true or provable in that other context: some might be true and some might be false; some might be provable and some might not be provable.  They all may be assumed as hypotheses for the sake of argument (see #$HypotheticalContext), or some may be posited as having potential explanatory value for the assertions in other contexts (see #$ScenarioContext).  Also, some instances of #$ConjecturalContext may contain assertions that are widely believed to be false (see #$FictionalOrMythologicalContext).")
(comment codeConstrainsSituationType "(#$codeConstrainsSituationType <b> CODE SIT-TYPE</b>) means
<b>CODE</b> puts forward constraints on conduct during (or otherwise
closely related to) instances of <b>SIT-TYPE</b>.  <b>CODE</b> may be either
an individual norm or a normative system.  If the latter, then at
least some of its component requirements must govern behavior related
to <b>SIT-TYPE</b>.  <b>CODE</b> may specify obligations,
prohibitions, permissions, or combinations thereof.")
(comment AgriculturalVehicle "The collection of vehicles that are also instance of #$AgriculturalImplement.")
(comment School-AcademicOrganization "A specialization of #$AcademicOrganization.  This collection represents a generalization of the various types of academic organizations which could typically be described as schools.  Notable specializations include #$SchoolInstitution, #$SchoolInstitution-KThrough12, and #$SchoolInUniversity-DegreeGranting.")
(comment KickScooter "The collection of \"kick,\" or \"push\" scooters, which are propelled by the user kicking or pushing off the ground with one foot, while standing on a wheeled platform with another.  Kick scooters roughly resemble thin skateboards with a long thin pole with handles in front.")
(comment Scooter "An instance of #$ConventionalClassificationType.  Instances of #$Scooter are pedal-less instances of #$MountableTransporter that typically have two wheels and a low, flat area where the rider puts one or both feet.")
(comment BoxTruck "A subcollection of #$Truck.  Instances are trucks with a cab and a cargo area that resembles a smallish tractor-trailer rig; however, unlike the trailer on such a rig, the cargo area on a box truck is not a detachable unit, but a permanent part of the truck.  Like tractor-trailers, there is no direct pathway out of the cab into the cargo area.")
(comment temperatureOfObject "A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot that relates a given tangible thing to its current temperature.  <code>(#$temperatureOfObject OBJECT TEMPERATURE)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code>  has <code>TEMPERATURE</code>.  Note that <code>OBJECT</code> need not be a discrete entity; <i>e.g.</i> it might be a particular spot in the ocean.  A notable specialization of this predicate is #$bodyTemperature.  See also #$ambientTemperature and #$UnitOfTemperature.")
(comment RoomInAConstruction "#$RoomInAConstruction is a specialization of #$SpaceInAFixedHOC.  Each instance of #$RoomInAConstruction is a room found in a #$ConstructionArtifact.")
(comment allParseResultsForContentModel "(#$allParseResultsForContentModel MT RESULT-LIST) means that the list RESULT-LIST is comprised of parses which were generated as possible interpretations for the utterance associated with the ContentModelMicrotheory MT.")
(comment ResidueFn "This function is used in conjunction with #$quantityCongruenceBase to compute the <i>common residue</i> of a quantity. See the #$comment on #$quantityCongruenceBase for more details about the notion of congruence and common residue.")
(comment commonResidue "<code>(#$commonResidue QUANTITY RESIDUE)</code> means that the quantity <code>RESIDUE</code> is the <i>common residue</i> of <code>QUANTITY</code> with respect to some quantity congrence base. See the #$comment on #$quantityCongruenceBase for more details about the notion of congruence and common residue.")
(comment AQUAINTKBPEProblem-WorkingFromText "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems that return the correct answers using parses obtained from the original texts.")
(comment AQUAINTKBPEProblem-WorkingFromCycL "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems that return the correct answers using the desired CycL as recorded on the textual inference problem.")
(comment pointAtDistanceAndBearingFromThing "<code>(#$pointAtDistanceAndBearingFromThing THING DISTANCE AZIMUTH
POINT)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is the #$Point which is obtained by
translating from <code>THING</code> a fixed distance <code>DISTANCE</code> in the direction of <code>AZIMUTH</code>.")
(comment AcuteAngularDistance "#$AcuteAngularDistance is the collection of all #$AngularDistances that are greater than or equal to 0 degrees and less than 90 degrees.")
(comment ObtuseAngularDistance "#$ObtuseAngularDistance is the collection of all #$AngularDistances that are greater than 90 degrees and less than 180 degrees.")
(comment ReflexAngularDistance "#$ReflexAngularDistance is the collection of all #$AngularDistances that are greater than 180 degrees and less than 360 degrees.")
(comment reflexAngles "<code>(#$reflexAngles ANGLE1 ANGLE2)</code> means that the sum of <code>ANGLE1</code> and <code>ANGLE2</code> is 360 degrees.")
(comment complementaryAngles "<code>(#$complementaryAngles ANGLE1 ANGLE2)</code> means that the sum of <code>ANGLE1</code> and <code>ANGLE2</code> is 90 degrees.")
(comment supplementaryAngles "<code>(#$supplementaryAngles ANGLE1 ANGLE2)</code> means that the sum of <code>ANGLE1</code> and <code>ANGLE2</code> is 180 degrees.")
(comment FullAngularDistance "#$FullAngularDistance is the collection of all #$AngularDistances that are exactly equal to 360 degrees. In fact, this collection consists of exactly one member, <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 360)</code>, however it is valuable to create the single member collection so that we have specs of #$AngularDistance that correspond to the well established divisions of angular measures in geometry (acute, right, obtuse, straight, reflex, and full angles), and to serve as argument constraints on angle relating predicates, such as #$supplementaryAngles.")
(comment StraightAngularDistance "#$StraightAngularDistance is the collection of all #$AngularDistances that are exactly equal to 180 degrees. In fact, this collection consists of exactly one member, <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 180)</code>, however it is valuable to create the single member collection so that we have specs of #$AngularDistance that correspond to the well established divisions of angular measures in geometry (acute, right, obtuse, straight, reflex, and full angles), and to serve as argument constraints on angle relating predicates, such as #$supplementaryAngles.")
(comment RightAngularDistance "#$RightAngularDistance is the collection of all #$AngularDistances that are exactly equal to 90 degrees. In fact, this collection consists of exactly one member, <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 90)</code>, however it is valuable to create the single member collection so that we have specs of #$AngularDistance that correspond to the well established divisions of angular measures in geometry (acute, right, obtuse, straight, reflex, and full angles), and to serve as argument constraints on angle relating predicates, such as #$supplementaryAngles.")
(comment ReflexAngleFn "If <code>ANGLE</code> is an #$AngularDistance that is greater than or equal to <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0)</code> and less than or equal to <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 360)</code> then <code>(#$ReflexAngleFn ANGLE)</code> returns the <i>reflex angle</i> of <code>ANGLE</code>, that is <code>360 - ANGLE</code> degrees. Note that this function is defined only for angular distances within the above stated bounds, otherwise it is #$undefined.")
(comment ComplementaryAngleFn "If <code>ANGLE</code> is an #$AngularDistance that is greater or equal to <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0)</code> and less than or equal to <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 90)</code> then <code>(#$ComplementaryAngleFn ANGLE)</code> returns the <i>complementary angle</i> of <code>ANGLE</code>, that is <code>90 - ANGLE</code> degrees. Note that this function is defined only for angular distances within the above stated bounds, otherwise it is #$undefined.")
(comment SupplementaryAngleFn "If <code>ANGLE</code> is an #$AngularDistance that is greater than or equal to <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0)</code> and less than or equal to <code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 180)</code> then <code>(#$SupplementaryAngleFn ANGLE)</code> returns the <i>supplementary angle</i> of <code>ANGLE</code>, that is <code>180 - ANGLE</code> degrees. Note that this function is defined only for angular distances within the above stated bounds, otherwise it is #$undefined.")
(comment angleBetweenPoints "If <code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, and <code>C</code> are #$Points, <code>AB</code> is the straight line segment between <code>A</code> and <code>B</code>, and <code>BC</code> is the straight line segment between <code>B</code> and <code>C</code>, then <code>(#$angleBetweenPoints A B C ANGLE)</code> means that <code>ANGLE</code> is the non-reflex angle (see #$ReflexAngularDistance) between <code>AB</code> and <code>BC</code>, measured in the #$Plane in which <code>A</code>, <code>B</code> and <code>C</code> all lie.")
(comment pointOfIntersectingLines "<code>(#$pointOfIntersectingLines P LINE1 LINE2)</code> means that the straight lines <code>LINE1</code> and <code>LINE2</code> intersect at the point <code>P</code>.")
(comment EndFn "#$EndFn is a unary function that takes an instance of #$TemporalThing as an argument and returns an instance of  #$TimePoint.  (#$EndFn TT) denotes the time point at which TT ends.  Thus: (#$endingPoint ?PERIOD (#$EndFn ?PERIOD)) is true.")
(comment Denting "A specialization of #$ShapeChangeWithoutLosingMaterial (q.v.).  Each
instance of #$Denting is an event in which a depression is created in
an object by the application of pressure, where the depression would
remain even if the application of pressure ceased.  Often,
dent-inducing pressure is applied with a sudden blow, as when a
baseball bat is swung with force to dent a car fender.  However, the
pressure may be applied slowly, as an empty aluminum soda can may be
dented when slowly squeezed by someone's hand.  Cf. #$Dented.")
(comment curveOnSurface "<code>(#$curveOnSurface CURVE SURFACE)</code> means that the instance of #$LinearObject-OneDimensional, <code>CURVE</code>, is subsumed by the #$Surface, <code>SURFACE</code>.")
(comment tangentLineAtPointOnCurve "A line, <code>LINE</code> is <i>tangent</i> to a curve, <code>CURVE</code> at a point, <code>POINT</code> if and only if both <code>LINE</code> and <code>CURVE</code> pass through <code>POINT</code> with the same direction and slope. In Cyc, this relation is stated as <code>(#$tangentLineAtPointOnCurve POINT LINE CURVE)</code>.")
(comment ellipsoidalSection "An ellipsoidal section is the curve formed by the intersection of a plane with an ellipsoid. Excluding the degenerate case of the plane tangent to the ellipsoid, an ellipsoidal section is always an ellipse. We exclude the degenerate case from consideration in the predicate #$ellipsoidalSection. So <code>(#$ellipsoidalSection ELLIPSE ELLIPSOID)</code> means that the #$Ellipse, <code>ELLIPSE</code> is a curve formed by the intersection of the #$Ellipsoid, <code>ELLIPSOID</code> with a unique #$Plane.")
(comment spheroidalSection "An spheroidal section is the curve formed by the intersection of a plane with a spheroid. Excluding the degenerate case of the plane tangent to the spheroid, a spheroidal section is always an ellipse. If the plane is perpendicular to the polar axis of the spheroid, the intersection is a circle. We exclude the degenerate case from consideration in the predicate #$spheroidalSection. So <code>(#$spheroidalSection ELLIPSE SPHEROID)</code> means that the #$Ellipse, <code>ELLIPSE</code>, is a curve formed by the intersection of the #$Spheroid, <code>SPHEROID</code>, with a unique #$Plane.")
(comment sphericSection "A spheric section is the curve formed by the intersection of a plane with a sphere. Excluding the degenerate case of the plane tangent to the sphere, all spheric sections are circles. We exclude the degenerate case from consideration in the predicate #$sphericSection. So <code>(#$sphericSection CIRCLE SPHERE)</code> means that the #$Circle, <code>CIRCLE</code>, is a curve formed by the intersection of the #$Sphere, <code>SPHERE</code>, with a unique #$Plane.")
(comment circleAtCenterWithRadius "<code>(#$circleAtCenterWithRadius CIRCLE CENTER RADIUS)</code> means that <code>CIRCLE</code> is the #$Circle centered at <code>CENTER</code> with #$radius <code>RADIUS</code>.")
(comment CircleFn "<code>(#$CircleFn CENTER RADIUS)</code> denotes the #$Circle centered at <code>CENTER</code> with #$radius <code>RADIUS</code>.")
(comment sphereAtCenterWithRadius "<code>(#$sphereAtCenterWithRadius SPHERE CENTER RADIUS)</code> means that <code>SPHERE</code> is the #$Sphere centered at <code>CENTER</code> with #$radius <code>RADIUS</code>.")
(comment SphereFn "<code>(#$SphereFn CENTER RADIUS)</code> denotes the #$Sphere centered at <code>CENTER</code> with #$radius <code>RADIUS</code>.")
(comment greatCircleOfSphere "A <i>great circle</i> is a section of a sphere (see #$sphericSection) that is centered at the same point as the sphere. <code>(#$greatCircleOfSphere CIRCLE SPHERE)</code> means that the #$Circle, <code>CIRCLE</code>, is a great circle of the #$Sphere, <code>SPHERE</code>. See also #$smallCircleOfSphere.")
(comment smallCircleOfSphere "A <i>small circle</i> is a section of a sphere (see #$sphericSection) that is not centered at the same point as the sphere. <code>(#$smallCircleOfSphere CIRCLE SPHERE)</code> means that the #$Circle, <code>CIRCLE</code> is a small circle of the #$Sphere, <code>SPHERE</code>. See also #$greatCircleOfSphere.")
(comment Business-Fortune500 "A specialization of #$LargeIncorporatedBusiness.  Each instance of #$Business-Fortune500 is among the largest 500 corporations in the U.S. during a particular year, according to the editors of Fortune Magazine.")
(comment OuterGarment "A specialization of #$Garment.  Each instance of
#$OuterGarment is a garment designed to be worn 'on the outside' in public, or at least, one that may be appropriately worn in this
fashion.  Wearing a garment 'on the outside' means wearing it
uncovered by another garment.  #$OuterGarment is disjoint with
#$Underwear (q.v).  For the collection of 'overclothes' such as
#$Coats, i.e., #$OuterGarments meant to be worn on top of other
instances of #$OuterGarment, see #$OverGarment.")
(comment OverGarment "A specialization of #$OuterGarment.  Each instance of #$OverGarment is a piece of clothing designed to be worn outside of other garments, usually other #$OuterGarments.  A common purpose of outer garments is to protect their wearers and/or their wearers' inner garments from outside elements (for example, instances of the specialization #$RainCoat protect their wearers and their wearers' inner garments from getting wet during a rain storm).  An instance of #$OverGarment may also be a robe of office or position, such as an academic gown or a bishop's mantle.")
(comment targetCycLForTIPremise "(#$targetCycLForTIPremise TEP CYCL) means that CYCL is a #$CycLSentence-Assertible which should be used to represent the background text associated with the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment targetCycLForTIQuery "(#$targetCycLForTIQuery TEP CYCL) means that CYCL is a #$CycLSentence-Askable which should be used to represent the question text associated with the #$TextualEntailmentProblem TEP.")
(comment RatioOfPrimitiveQuantities "A specialization of #$RatioQuantity (q.v.) is a quantity that is derivable (via #$QuotientFn) as a ratio of two non- #$numericallyComparable primitive quantities (i.e. instances of two respective #$PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityTypes).  

For example, (#$MilesPerHour 65) is a ratio of primitive quantities obtainable by dividing (#$Mile 65) by (#$HoursDuration 1).

Note that each #$RatioOfPrimitiveQuantities has a #$scalarDimensionalRank (q.v.) of 2.")
(comment IntervalEndedByFn-Inclusive "A unary function that takes an instance of #$TemporalThing as its argument and returns an instance of #$ContinuousTimeInterval.  (#$IntervalEndedByFn-Inclusive TEMP-OBJ) denotes the time interval which ends when TEMP-OBJ ends.  The beginning of this interval coincides with the beginning of all time (#$Always-TimeInterval), if time has a beginning.  It differs from #$IntervalEndedByFn in including (temporally subsuming) the temporal thing taken as its argument.")
(comment honorsThing "A binary predicate which relates instances of #$TemporalThing to instances of #$Thing. <code>(#$honorsThing OBJ THING)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> commemorates or memorializes <code>THING</code>.  This is a very general predicate which can be used to talk about monuments, holidays and many other things. For instance, (#$honorsThing #$StatueOfLiberty #$Freedom-Emotion). See also the more specialized #$artifactHonors and #$monumentHonors.")
(comment cycLFound-Set "An expression of the form (#$cycLFound-Set GATHER SET) states that the #$InformationGathering GATHER generated a set of (quoted) instances of #$CycLTerm as results.  For example, an #$InformationGathering process that took a text string and passed it to the #$EvaluatableFunction #$TopicsOfTextStringFn returns a set of #$CycLDenotationalTerm would bear this relation to the set of #$CycLDenotationalTerm returned by that function.")
(comment Dent "A specialization of #$CavityWithWalls (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Dent is a depression that an object has acquired in its surface as the causal consequence of the application of pressure, where the depression remains even after the application of pressure ceases.  Cf. #$Dented and #$Denting.")
(disjointWith Dent BodilyOrifice)
(comment relationExistsRangeInstance "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to a given collection and a given thing.  <code>(#$relationExistsRangeInstance BINPRED COL THING RANGE)</code> means that there are between the minimum and maximum of <code>RANGE</code> (see #$NonNegativeIntegerRange) many instances <code>INST</code>(i) of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST</code>(i) <code>THING)</code> holds.  Thus it is equivalent to the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$thereExistRange RANGE ?INST
     (#$and
        (#$isa ?INST COL)
        (PRED ?INST THING))) .
</pre>
<p>
For example, <code>(#$relationExistsRangeInstance #$objectFoundInLocation #$Star #$MilkyWay-Galaxy #$Billions-Quant)</code> means there are billions of stars in the Milky Way.  Cf. #$relationExistsRangeAll, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment exceptionalPerformerTypeOfCharacteristicActivity "(#$exceptionalPerformerTypeOfCharacteristicActivity PERSON-TYPE EXCEPTIONAL-PERSON-TYPE) means that EXCEPTIONAL-PERSON-TYPE is the specialization of PERSON-TYPE (an instance of #$PersonTypeByActivity) that is exceptionally good at the action type characteristic of PERSON-TYPE.  .")
(comment ExceptionalPerformerTypeOfCharacteristicActivityFn "(#$ExceptionalPerformerTypeOfCharacteristicActivityFn PERSON-TYPE) denotes the specialization of PERSON-TYPE (an instance of #$PersonTypeByActivity) that is exceptionally good at the action type characteristic of PERSON-TYPE. (See #$characteristicActivityTypeOfPersonType.).")
(comment angleBetween "This is a very general predicate used to state the angle between two spatial things that intersect. <code>(#$angleBetween THING1 THING2 ANGLE)</code> means that the #$AngularDistance between <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> is <code>ANGLE</code>. There are several points to note about the meaning of this predicate:
<p>
1. While it is necessary that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> #$spatiallyIntersects in order for there to exist some <code>ANGLE</code> between them, it is not sufficient. Two things may intersect in such a complex fashion that it is impossible to determine a measurable angle between them.
<p>
2. The arg3 of this predicate is <i>not</i> functional. In many cases, the measured angle between two intersecting things may form more than one angle. For example, two intersecting straight lines, <code>LINE1</code> and <code>LINE2</code>, form two angles, <code>ANGLE</code> and <code>SUPP-ANGLE</code>, which are supplementary to one another. (see #$supplementaryAngles) In such a case we posit that both <code>(#$angleBetween LINE1 LINE2 ANGLE)</code> and <code>(#$angleBetween LINE1 LINE2 SUPP-ANGLE)</code> are true. In addition, it may be that the measured angle depends on the orientation of the observer, or the context of the measurement. For example, measurement of the vertex angles of polygons often distinguishes between the <i>interior angles</i> and <i>exterior angles</i>. 
<p>
3. In almost all cases, the best way to use this predicate is to write rules which state sufficient conditions for concluding #$angleBetween for special cases, such as for the angles of intersection between two lines. See the consequent rules on this predicate for examples of such.")
(comment many-GenQuantRelnTo "(#$many-GenQuantRelnTo RELN COL THING) is true if for many of the instances X of COL (RELN X THING) holds. \"Many\" in this sense need not mean \"many relative to the total number of THING that exist\".")
(comment many-GenQuantRelnFrom "(#$many-GenQuantRelnFrom RELN THING COL) is true if for many of the instances X of COL (RELN THING X) holds. \"Many\" in this sense need not mean \"many relative to the total number of THING that exist\".")
(comment many-GenQuantRelnToType "(#$many-GenQuantRelnToType RELN COL1 COL2) means that for many instances X of COL1 there is an instance INST of COL2 such that (RELN X INST) holds. \"Many\" in this sense need not mean \"many relative to the total number of the instances of COL1 that exist\".")
(comment many-GenQuantRelnFromType "(#$many-GenQuantRelnFromType RELN COL2 COL1) means that for many instances X of COL1 there is an instance INST of COL2 such that (RELN INST X) holds. \"Many\" in this sense need not mean \"many relative to the total number of instances of COL1 that exist\".")
(comment Sanction "A specialization of #$NormativeSpecification. Each instance of #$Sanction describes the 'content' of some element of #$ImposingSanctions.")
(comment VeryLongNovel-CW "A specialization of #$Novel-CW (q.v.). Each instance of #$VeryLongNovel-CW is a novel with very many more words than most novels.  <i>War and Peace</i> is a typical example.")
(comment AverageLengthNovel-CW "A specialization of #$Novel-CW (q.v.). Each instance of #$AverageLengthNovel-CW is a novel with around the average number of words. <i>In Cold Blood</i> or <i>A Clockwork Orange</i> are good examples.")
(comment PrimeRib-MeatCut "A specialization of #$Beef (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PrimeRib-MeatCut is a cut of beef taken from the loin area of some cattle.")
(disjointWith PrimeRib-MeatCut FiletMignon)
(disjointWith VeryLongNovel-CW AverageLengthNovel-CW)
(comment typeCoerceableToType "(#$typeCoerceableToType TYPE1 TYPE2) means that TYPE1 can be coerced to TYPE2 in the interests of rendering the interpretation of some NL input expression WFF.")
(comment equatorialDiameterOfObject "An instance of #$MeasurableQuantitySlot. (#$equatorialDiameterOfObject ROUND-OBJ DISTANCE) means that ROUND-OBJ has an equatorial diameter of DISTANCE. In the special case where ROUND-OBJ is an instance of #$Spheroid (q.v.), DISTANCE is a quantity equal to twice the equatorial radius of ROUND-OBJ (see the predicate #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).")
(comment polarDiameterOfObject "An instance of #$MeasurableQuantitySlot. (#$polarDiameterOfObject ROUND-OBJ DISTANCE) means that ROUND-OBJ has a polar diameter of DISTANCE. In the special case where ROUND-OBJ is an instance of #$Spheroid (q.v.), DISTANCE is a quantity equal to twice the polar radius of ROUND-OBJ (see the predicate #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).")
(comment PlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional "#$PlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional is the collection intersection of #$LinearObject-OneDimensional and #$LinearObject-Planar. That is, it is the collection of all one dimensional linear objects that are embedded in a single #$Plane.")
(comment SurfaceOfRevolution "A <i>surface of revolution</i> is a surface generated by rotating a planar curve about an axis contained in the same plane. The generating curve is called a <i>generatrix</i> and may be either closed or open, bounded or unbounded. Here are some examples of surfaces of revolution:
<p>
1. A #$Spheroid is obtained by rotating an #$Ellipse about either its major or minor axes. In the first case, the surface is a #$ProlateSpheroid, while in the second case, the surface is an #$OblateSpheroid. If the #$Ellipse is a #$Circle, the surface is a #$Sphere.
<p>
2. A #$TorusSurface is obtained by rotating a #$Circle about a line which is in the same plane as the circle, but is separated from it.
<p>
See also the predicates #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution and #$generatrixOfSurfaceOfRevolution.")
(comment generatrixOfSurfaceOfRevolution "<code>(#$generatrixOfSurfaceOfRevolution GENERATRIX SURFACE)</code> means the <code>GENERATRIX</code> is the generating curve for the surface of revolution, <code>SURFACE</code>, about some axis of revolution (see #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution. A surface of revolution may be generated by more than one pair of generatrices and axes. For example, a #$Sphere is generated by any axis that passes through the center of the sphere and any great circle of the sphere (see #$greatCircleOfSphere).")
(comment PlanarLinearObjectSet-OneDimensional "A specialization of #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet.  This is the collection of all sets whose members are instances of #$PlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional.  The CycL constant <code>#$PlanarLinearObjectSet-OneDimensional</code> is the #$rewriteOf the term
<p>
</code>(#$SetOfTypeFn #$PlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional)</code>.")
(comment PlanarLinearObjectComposite "#$PlanarLinearObjectComposite is the collection of all one dimensional figures which are embedded in a single #$Plane and are the spatial sums of one or more one dimensional planar linear objects (#$PlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional).")
(comment EvaluatableFunction-Contextualized "A #$FunctionType and a sub-collection of #$EvaluatableFunction.  As a
sub-collection of #$EvaluatableFunction, each instance of this collection is
associated with a piece of procedural code (see #$evaluationDefn); however, the
behavior of that code and, as a consequence, the value returned by this
function, can vary in even minutely different contexts.   Moreover, the factors that govern this behavior are complex or diffuse such that they cannot be provided as arguments to the function.  This collection has an important relationship to forward inference because, during truth maintenance, the verification of the value returned by such a function cannot be accomplished simply by calling the function again (unlike, say #$PlusFn).  Thus, there is special handling for the evaluations of these functions in order to provide stability to forward inference. For example, the function #$RandomIntegerFn is intended to return a random integer. Since that function should not return the same digit every time it is called,  a forward inference based on an earlier value that was returned should not be undone because a new value was returned on a subsequent call.  #$EvaluatableFunction-Contextualized and #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation are 'orthogonal.' So, a #$Function-Denotational could be instances of both; however,  #$EvaluatableFunction-Contextualized is not a sub-collection of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation because a function like #$RandomIntegerFn could have two simultaneous calls on different processes and return different results.")
(comment EvaluatableRelation-Contextualized "A sub-collection of #$EvaluatableRelation.  As a sub-collection of #$EvaluatableRelation, each instance of this collection is associated with a piece of procedural code (see #$evaluationDefn); however, the behavior of that code and, as a consequence, the behavior of these relations can vary in even minutely different contexts.  Moreover, the factors that govern this behavior are complex or diffuse such that they cannot be provided as additional arguments . #$EvaluatableRelation-Contextualized and #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation are 'orthogonal.' So, a relation could be an instance of both; however, #$EvaluatableRelation-Contextualized is not a sub-collection of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation because simultaneous evaluations could produce different results.")
(comment EvaluatablePredicate-Contextualized "A sub-collection of #$EvaluatablePredicate.  As a sub-collection of #$EvaluatablePredicate, each instance of this collection is associated with a piece of procedural code (see #$evaluationDefn); however, the behavior of that code and, as a consequence, the behavior of this predicate can vary in even minutely different contexts.  Moreover, the factors that govern this behavior are complex or diffuse such that they cannot be provided as additional arguments . #$EvaluatablePredicate-Contextualized and #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation are 'orthogonal.' So, a predicate could be an instance of both; however, #$EvaluatablePredicate-Contextualized is not a sub-collection of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation because simultaneous evaluations could produce different results.")
(comment meridianForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution "A <i>meridian</i> of a surface of revolution with respect to an axis of revolution is the intersection of the surface with a plane containing the axis of revolution. <code>(#$meridianForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution MERIDIAN AXIS SURFACE)</code> means that <code>MERIDIAN</code> is a meridian for the axis, <code>AXIS</code>, of the #$SurfaceOfRevolution, <code>SURFACE</code>. It is necessary to specify the axis in this relation because some surfaces of revolution may be generated by revolving a generatrix about different axes, and a meridian of <SURFACE> with respect to one axis is not a meridian with respect to a different axis. Note also that our use of the term <i>meridian</i> is consistent with the mathematical vocabulary of surface geometry, however it is not consistent with the cartographic use of the term.")
(comment parallelForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution "A <i>parallel</i> for an axis of a surface of revolution is the intersection of the surface with any plane orthogonal to the axis. That is, if <code>AXIS</code> is an axis of revolution (see #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution) for the #$SurfaceOfRevolution <code>SURFACE</code>, then <code>(#$parallelForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution PARALLEL AXIS SURFACE)</code> means that there exists some #$Plane <code>PLANE</code> such that <code>AXIS</code> is orthogonal to <code>PLANE</code> and the intersection of <code>PLANE</code> and <code>SURFACE</code> is <code>PARALLEL</code>.")
(comment axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution "<code>(#$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution AXIS SURFACE)</code> means that <code>AXIS</code> is the axis about which a curve (the <i>generatrix</i>) is rotated in order to obtain <code>SURFACE</code>. <code>AXIS</code> may or may not spatially intersect <code>SURFACE</code>. For instance, a #$TorusSurface is a #$SurfaceOfRevolution such that its axis of revolution is spatially separated from the surface. Furthermore, the same surface may be gnerated by different generatrices and axes. See also #$generatrixOfSurfaceOfRevolution.")
(comment shootingTypeUsesLauncherType "<code>(#$shootingTypeUsesLauncherType SHOOTING-TYPE LAUNCHER-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>SHOOTING-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$ShootingAProjectileWeapon in which the projectile weapon used is an instance of <code>LAUNCHER-TYPE</code>.  This is the predicate that corresponds to the function, #$ShootingByLauncherTypeFn.")
(comment FlyingViaAirplane "The collection of all instances of #$Flying in which an airplane flies.  Every instance of #$FlyingViaAirplane is a proper sub-event of some instance of (#$TransportViaFn #$Airplane).")
(comment NonPlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional "#$NonPlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional is the collection intersection of #$LinearObject-OneDimensional and #$LinearObject-NonPlanar. That is, it is the collection of all one dimensional linear objects that cannot be embedded in a single #$Plane.")
(comment loxodromeForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution "A <i>loxodrome</i>, also known as a <i>rhumb line</i>, is a curve which crosses the meridians for the axis of some surface of revolution (see #$meridianForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution) at any constant angular distance (in degrees) <code>A</code> such that <code>-90 < A < 0</code> or <code>0 < A < 90</code>. We rule out the case where <code>A = 0</code> degrees, since this case corresponds to the meridians of the surface (for some axis of revolution), and we rule out the case where <code>A = 90</code> degrees, since this case corresponds to the parallels of the surface (for some axis of revolution). In Cyc, <code>(#$loxodromeForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution LOXODROME AXIS SURFACE)</code> means the <code>LOXODROME</code> is such a curve with respect to the axis of revolution <code>AXIS</code> for the surface of revolution <code>SURFACE</code>.")
(comment enforcementOf "(#$enforcementOf ENFORCE COC) means that the enforcement action ENFORCE enforces the #$CodeOfConduct COC.")
(comment forbids "A #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot and a specialization of  #$negativeInterest-Prop.  <code>(#$forbids AGT PROP)</code> means that <code>AGT</code> forbids that <code>PROP</code> anything happen that would make <code>PROP</code> true.")
(comment quantityCongruenceBase "A binary predicate used to state the modulus or base used to compute equivalent quantity expressions for units of measure that are congruent with respect to a fixed quantity (the base). <code>(#$quantityCongruenceBase BASE QUANTITYTYPE)</code> means that if <code>UNIT</code> is a #$UnitOfMeasure which denotes quantities of type <code>QUANTITYTYPE</code> and <code>VAL1</code> and <code>VAL2</code> are both #$admittedArguments of <code>UNIT</code>, then <code>(UNIT VAL1)</code> is said to be congruent to <code>(UNIT VAL2)</code> modulo <code>UNIT</code> if and only if there exists some #$Integer <code>N</code> such that
<pre>
  (UNIT VAL1) = (UNIT VAL2) + N*BASE.
</pre>
It is easy to see that <code>(UNIT VAL1)</code> is conguent modulo <code>UNIT</code> to <code>(UNIT VAL1) + N*BASE</code> for any #$Integer <code>N</code>, and so there are a countably infinite number of quantities that are congruent to <code>(UNIT VAL1)</code>. There is exactly one, however, that is greater than or equal to <code>(UNIT 0)</code> and strictly less than <code>BASE</code>. This quantity is called the <i>common residue</i> of <code>(UNIT VAL1)</code>. In Cyc, the function <code>#$ResidueFn</code> and the predicate <code>#$commonResidue</code> compute the common residue of a quantity using the asserted extent of <code>#$quantityCongruenceBase</code>.")
(comment signalsEndOf-Performative "(#$signalsEndOf-Performative <b>SIT</b> <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b>)
means that, as dictated by some governing convention
(written or unwritten), the occurrence of <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> both
(i) causes <b>SIT</b> to come to an end, and (ii) signals the fact of
<b>SIT's</b> termination to relevant participants and/or observers.
For example, in instances of (#$GameFn #$Boxing-SportCW), the referee
waving his hands in a certain motion serves as an official indication
-- and a signal to all participants -- that the fight has ended.
Typically, only select participants in <b>SIT</b> -- often officiators
or hosts of some sort -- are granted the power to perform such
actions.  For more on the conventional status of such signals, see the
comment on #$signalsEndOf.")
(comment areaOfObject "A #$PhysicalAmountSlot that is a specialization of #$sizeParameterOfObject (q.v.), this predicate relates a two- or three-dimensional spatial thing to its two-dimensional size or #$Area (q.v.).  <code>(#$areaOfObject OBJ AREA)</code> means that the two-dimensional size of <code>OBJ</code> is <code>AREA</code>.
<p>
Note that <code>OBJ</code> might be either an intangible two-dimensional space-region (such as a #$Polygon or a #$CircularRegion) or a tangible three-dimensional object that has only two \"salient\" dimensions in typical contexts (such as a desk top, a cross-section of wire, a floor, a geographical region, or a tennis court).
<p>
For the areas of geographical regions, see the specialization #$areaOfRegion.  See also #$surfaceAreaOfTopOfObject and #$surfaceAreaOfWholeObject.")
(comment ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing "A specialization of #$ThreeDimensionalThing, #$GeometricallyDescribableThing, and #$ShapedObject (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing is a three-dimensional, self-connected object whose shape is (at least roughly) describable in relatively simple geometric terms.  Examples include intangible 3-D objects (such as ideal cones and cylinders) as well as certain tangible objects (such as billard balls and dice).  Specializations of this collection include #$Polyhedron, #$Cone and #$Torus.")
(comment SpatialThingTypeByShape "A specialization of #$FirstOrderCollection whose instances are collections of things sorted purely in terms of their shape.  Each instance of #$SpatialThingTypeByShape is a subcollection of #$SpatialThing (q.v.) whose own instances are all and only those things, either tangible or intangible, that have some particular thing in common as regards their shape.  
<p>
For example, #$Wedge is the collection of all wedge-shaped objects; it includes both physical wedges and wedge-shaped #$SpaceRegions.  Similarly, #$Square is the collection of all two-dimensional squares; but note that all of its instances are <i>intangible</i>, since tangible objects are by their nature three-dimensional.  Finally, note that #$Block (e.g.) is <i>not</i> a #$SpatialThingTypeByShape; for it collects its instances not merely by their shape, but also by the fact that they are #$SolidTangibleThings.  
<p>
Specializations of #$SpatialThingTypeByShape include #$SpatialThingTypeByGeometricShape and #$SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality.")
(comment Ellipsoid "A specialization of both #$RoundObject, #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing, and #$ClosedSurfaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Ellipsoid is a two-dimensional unbounded surface, embedded in a three-dimensional space, and such that the planar sections along its respective internal axes are #$Ellipses (q.v.).
<p>
Note that #$Sphere and its generalization #$Spheroid are specializations of #$Ellipsoid.  See also #$EllipsoidalSolid, each instance of which is an (intangible or tangible) three-dimensional object whose boundary is or approximates an #$Ellipsoid.")
(comment NaturalLanguageTranslation "A specialization of #$IBTTranslating. Each instance is an event which generates some  new #$InformationBearingThing by reexpressing the contents of a source IBT (expressed in some #$NaturalLanguage) into a new representation which uses a different #$HumanLanguage (i.e. it need not be a #$NaturalLanguage).  The new IBT encodes approximately the same information as the source IBT.")
(comment CrudeFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction. When applied to an instance <code>STUFF-TYPE</code> of #$ExistingStuffType, #$CrudeFn returns all instances of <code>STUFF-TYPE</code> that are products entering a market for the first time that are not manufactured, processed or refined, and are not sold directly to consumers. ")
(comment signalsEvent "(#$signalsEvent <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> <b>EVENT</b>) means that
<b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> signals the existence or occurrence of
<b>EVENT</b>.  The type of 'signaling' relation
intended here is conventional, i.e., is determined by a convention which dictates that
events of one type (i.e., one to which <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> belongs)
indicate the occurrence of events of another type.
(The relevant convention here may be unwritten, limited to a small
group of agents, temporary, etc.).  For example, the selection of a
new pope by the #$CollegeOfCardinals is signaled, in accordance with
Church law, by white smoke issuing from the chimney of the
#$SistineChapel.  No temporal order is entailed by this general
relation -- <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> may signal that <b>EVENT</b> has
occurred, will occur, or has started.")
(comment permittee "'(#$permittee GRANT AGENT)' means that AGENT is the #$IntelligentAgent who is granted permission in the #$GrantingPermission GRANT.")
(comment agreementRequires "This quaternary predicate is used to indicate that a particular agent is required to play a particular role in a particular kind of situation under the terms of a particular agreement.  <code>(#$agreementRequires AGREEMENT AGENT SIT-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that the #$Agreement <code>AGREEMENT</code> specifies that the #$IntelligentAgent <code>AGENT</code> is required to play the #$Role <code>ROLE</code> in some situation that is an instance of the subcollection of #$Situation <code>SIT-TYPE</code>.  For example, a peace accord #$agreementRequires the signatories to withdraw their troops behind specified boundaries.")
(comment permittedProp "(#$permittedProp GP FORM) means that in the #$GrantingPermission event, GP, the doer permits situations described by the formula FORM.")
(comment forbiddenProp "(#$forbiddenProp FS FORM) means that in the #$ForbiddingSomething event, FS, the doer forbids situations described by the formula FORM.")
(comment signalsEndOf "(#$signalsEndOf <b>SIT</b> <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b>) means that
<b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> serves as a signal to participants in and/or
observers of <b>SIT</b> that <b>SIT</b> is ending or has ended.   Such a 'signaling' relation is  determined by convention -- i.e., it holds in virtue of there being some operative convention 
which relates instances of one event-type to instances of another.
 (Note that the relevant convention may be unwritten, limited to a small group of agents, temporary, etc.)  Many
specializations of #$Event-Organized have such conventional signals
regarding their completion.  For example, the end of a primary or
secondary school #$ClassMeeting is often announced by the ringing of a
bell, and the end of a #$DramaticPerformance is often marked by the
lowering of a curtain (a signal to the audience).  Organized sports
provides many examples -- e.g., an official blowing his whistle
signals the end of live action in an organized
(#$GameFn #$Football-American).  Note an important distinction:
<b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> may simply communicate to observers that SIT has
already ended, as is (often) the case with a curtain lowering at the
end of a performance.  Or <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> may itself <i>bring
about</i> the end of SIT, by convention, as does the whistle-blowing
in football.  To make the second, stronger, claim, use
#$signalsEndOf-Performative.")
(comment HomeOffice "The collection of offices located inside the living quarters of the person who works there.  Typically, but not necessarily, such offices will be a single room set aside for work purposes.")
(comment SentenceEvaluationOutput "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$SentenceEvaluationOutput denotes a property that can be assigned to a #$CycLPropositionalSentence by some judge/reviewer, when it is presented to the reviewer in either #$CycL or in a translation to some #$HumanLanguage).   Examples of #$SentenceEvaluationOutput include #$RidiculousCycLSentence, #$PlausibleCycLSentence, and #$TrueCycLSentence.  Since, these collections represent a judgment that results from some #$Evaluating of a sentence , each instance of this collection is a #$notAssertibleCollection.  Each instance of this collection has a related #$UnaryPredicate.")
(comment evaluationOfAs "A sentence of the form (#$evaluationOfAs EVAL HYP-SENTENCE EVALUATION)  states that during the #$Evaluating EVAL, the sentence represented by the #$HypotheticalContext HYP-SENTENCE was categorized as an instance of EVALUATION, which is some collection that is an instance of #$SentenceEvaluationOutput, a second order collection whose instances are collections of sentences that share a common evaluation.")
(comment believedProp "(#$believedProp BT FORM) means that in the #$BelievingThat situation, BTS, the doer believes a proposition expressed by the formula FORM.")
(comment cotemporal "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate and an #$EquivalenceRelation that is a specialization of both #$temporalBoundsIdentical and #$temporallySubsumes (qq.v.).  <code>(#$cotemporal TEMP1 TEMP2)</code> means that <code>TEMP1</code> and <code>TEMP2</code> have precisely the same temporal extent (see #$temporalExtent).
<p>
Note that if both <code>TEMP1</code> and <code>TEMP2</code> are instances of #$TemporallyContinuousThing (q.v.), the above is equivalent to <code>(#$temporalBoundsIdentical TEMP1 TEMP2)</code>.  But if at least one of them is not continuous, they might have identical temporal bounds (i.e. start and end points) and yet not be cotemporal.
<p>
Specializations of #$cotemporal include #$cotemporalSubEvents (which applies to #$Events) and #$simultaneousWith (which applies to #$TimePoints).")
(comment assumedProp "(#$assumedProp ASTBT FORM) means that in the #$AssumingSomethingToBeTrue (q.v.) situation, ASTBT, the doer assumes a proposition expressed by the formula FORM.")
(comment KnowingThat "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$BelievingThat. Each instance of #$KnowingThat is a #$MentalSituation in which an agent knows (see #$knows and #$knownProp) that a particular proposition is true.")
(comment knownProp "(#$knownProp KT FORM) means that in the #$KnowingThat (q.v.) situation, KT, the doer knows the proposition expressed by the formula FORM.")
(comment DesiringThat "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$IntentionalMentalState. Each instance of #$DesiringThat is a situation in which an agent desires (see #$desires and #$desiredProp) that a particular proposition is true.")
(comment desiredProp "(#$desiredProp DT FORM) means that in the #$DesiringThat (q.v.) situation, DT, the doer desires that the proposition expressed by the formula FORM be true.")
(comment HopingThat "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$DesiringThat. Each instance of #$HopingThat is a situation in which an agent hopes (see #$hopes and #$hopedProp) that a particular proposition is true.")
(comment hopedProp "(#$hopedProp HT FORM) means that in the #$HopingThat (q.v.) situation, HT, the doer hopes that the proposition expressed by the formula FORM is true.")
(comment doubtedProp "(#$doubtedProp DS FORM) means that in the #$DoubtingSomething (q.v.) situation, DS, the doer doubts that the proposition expressed by the formula FORM is true.")
(comment knows "(#$knows AGENT PROP) means that AGENT (an instance of #$IntelligentAgent) knows PROP (an instance of #$CycLSentence-Assertible).  (#$knows AGENT PROP) implies that (i) the proposition PROP is true, (ii) AGENT believes the proposition PROP, and (iii) AGENT is justified in believing PROP.")
(comment RealizingThat "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$KnowingThat. Each instance of #$RealizingThat is a situation in which an agent realizes (see #$realizes and #$realizedProp) that a particular proposition is true.")
(comment realizedProp "(#$realizedProp RT FORM) means that in the #$RealizingThat (q.v.) situation, RT, the doer realizes that the proposition expressed by the formula FORM is true.")
(comment worriedProp "(#$worriedProp WT FORM) means that in the #$WorryingThat (q.v.) situation, WT, the doer worries that the proposition expressed by the formula FORM is true.")
(comment FearingSomething "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$WorryingAboutSomething. Each instance of #$FearingSomething is a situation in which an agent fears (see #$fears and #$fearedProp) that a particular proposition is true.")
(comment fearedProp "(#$fearedProp FS FORM) means that in the #$FearingSomething (q.v.) situation, FS, the doer fears that the proposition expressed by the formula FORM is true.")
(comment WorryingAboutSomething "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$IntentionalMentalState. Each instance of #$WorryingAboutSomething is a situation in which an agent worries (see #$worries and #$worriedProp) that a particular proposition is true.")
(comment HappyAboutSomething "A #$TemporalStuffType and specialization of #$IntentionalMentalState. Each instance of #$HappyAboutSomething is a situation in which an agent is happy (see #$pleasingProp) that a particular proposition is true.")
(comment pleasingProp "(#$pleasingProp HAS FORM) means that in the #$HappyAboutSomething (q.v.) situation, HAS, the doer is happy that the proposition expressed by the formula FORM is true.")
(comment AQUAINTKBPEProblem-Working "A specialization of #$TextualInferenceProblem-AQUAINTFormat.  Instances of this collection are textual inference problems that return the correct answers.  They may or may not return the correct answer based on the text of the problem.  See also #$AQUAINTKBPEProblem-WorkingFromCycL and #$AQUAINTKBPEProblem-WorkingFromText.")
(comment AlawitePerson "The collection of all persons who consider themselves to be Alawite (see #$AlawiteIslam).")
(comment unicodeStringDenotedByAsciiString "(#$unicodeStringDenotedByAsciiString UNC-STRING ASCII-STRING)
relates a UNICODE string to the ASCII String that denotes this string.")
(comment OperationEnduringFreedom "The  military operation carried out by the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica against #$Afghanistan.")
(comment groupMemberType "The predicate #$groupMemberType is used to characterize the membership of a particular group.  <code>(#$groupMemberType GROUP TYPE)</code> means that all of <code>GROUP</code>'s members are instances of the collection <code>TYPE</code>.  
<p>
For example, to state that the #$groupMembers of the Austin City Council are humans, one would write (#$groupMemberType AustinCityCouncil #$Person).  The members of a particular group might be characterized in more than one way; e.g., a Hispanic Business Council may have both the #$groupMemberType #$EthnicGroupOfHispanics and the #$groupMemberType #$BusinessPerson (provided that all of its members are Hispanic business people).  For 'typical' or 'expected' characteristics of members, see also #$memberTypes and #$orgTypeTypicallyHasMemberTypes.")
(comment regionOfCountryTypeByGeneralRegion "(#$regionOfCountryTypeByGeneralRegion COUNTRY-TYPE REGION) means that every #$Country whose complete territorial extent is a #$geographicalSubRegions of REGION is an instance of COUNTRY-TYPE.  For example, (#$regionOfCountryTypeByGeneralRegion #$CaucasusCountry #$Caucasus-Region) means that every #$Country that is geographically subsumed by the #$Caucasus-Region is a #$CaucasusCountry.")
(comment cityWithNameInGeoEntity "The predicate that corresponds to #$CityNamedFn, <code>(#$cityWithNameInGeoEntity CITY NAME GEO-ENTITY)</code> identifies <code>CITY</code> as the city named <code>NAME</code> that is a geopolitical sub-division (see #$geopoliticalSubdivision) of <code>GEO-ENTITY</code> an instance of #$GeopoliticalEntity.")
(comment TextOfTextuallySpecificWorkFn "A #$UnaryFunction that takes a #$TextuallySpecificWork and returns the #$TextString associated with that work.  For example, (#$TextOfTextuallySpecificWorkFn <i>FrenchTranslationOfMobyDick001</i>) would denote the specific string of French text associated with <i>FrenchTranslationOfMobyDick001</i>.
<p>
Note that a non-textually-specific conceptual work such as #$MobyDickNovel does not have any unique #$TextString associated with it, since it can be instantiated in different languages.")
(comment TextuallySpecificWork "A #$LiteraryWork-CW with which a single complete text can be associated (via #$TextOfTextuallySpecificWorkFn). This is not generally true of a #$LiteraryWork-CW, such as #$MobyDickNovel (a work which has no specific text associated with it -- see the comment on #$LiteraryWork-CW).")
(comment mapsToTaxonomyRelnStartingAt "(#$mapsToTaxonomyRelnStartingAt TAXON KBPRED TAXPRED TERM1) means that assertions of the form (KBPRED TERM1 TERM2) will appear in TAXON as (TAXPRED TERM1 TERM2). For example, (#$mapsToTaxonomyRelnStartingAt #$FoodAndDrinkTaxonomy #$partTypes #$parts-Underspecified #$FoodComposite) means that TERMs such that (partTypes FoodComposite TERM) will appear in the food and drink taxonomy as (parts-Underspecified FoodComposite TERM).")
(comment MenagerieMaintenanceAndDisplayOrganization "The collection of instances of #$Organization whose primary focus is the maintenance and display of a collection of (typically wild) animals.  Notable subcollections include #$ZooOrganization and #$AquariumOrganization.")
(disjointWith CaptiveNonHumanAnimal HomoSapiens)
(comment ZooAnimal "The collection of animals that belong to some #$ZooOrganization.")
(comment CaptiveNonHumanAnimal "The intersection (see #$collectionIntersection) of #$CaptiveAnimal and #$NonHumanAnimal.")
(comment Menagerie "A subcollection of #$Holdings.  Instances are groups of live animals put on display, such as in a zoo or aquarium.  In the typical case, the animals in question are wild animals (see #$WildAnimal), but in some contexts (science fiction in particular) this is not always the case.")
(comment IntransitiveSubjectControlFrame "This frame is used for words that subcategorize for (i) an NP and (ii) an infinitive phrase which is controlled by the subject of the matrix clause, i.e. the semantic subject of the infinitive clause is identical to the syntactic subject of the matrix clause. Example: 'Mary promised John to go to the party'. Note that this could be paraphrased as 'Mary promised John that *Mary* (not John) go to the party', thus that the *subject* (not object) of the matrix clause is the one going to the party. Contrast this with #$ObjectControlFrame.")
(comment TransitiveSubjectControlFrame "This frame is used for words that subcategorize for an infinitive phrase which is controlled by the subject of the matrix clause, i.e. the semantic subject of the infinitive clause is identical to the syntactic subject of the matrix clause. Example: 'Mary wants to go to the party'. Note that this could be paraphrased as 'Mary wants that *Mary* goes to the party', thus that the *subject* (not object) of the matrix clause is the one going to the party. Contrast this with #$ObjectControlFrame.")
(comment Snoozing "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Napping. In each #$Snoozing, someone sleeps lightly or for a short period of time.")
(comment DisinfectingProcess "#$DisinfectingProcess is a collection of events on which a #$DisinfectantSubstance is used to clean something by killing pathogenic #$Microorganisms.")
(comment Quarantine "The collection of #$Events during which an individual, or a group, suspected of being contaminated is kept for a determined time isolated in observation to prevent spreading of pathogenic #$Microorganisms in a larger community.")
(comment HandlingAnAnimal "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$ControllingSomething. In each #$HandlingAnAnimal, someone shows and trains some animal.")
(comment Sneezing "This is the collection of acts of making a violent spasmodic expiration of breath from the mouth or nose.  See also #$Sneezing-AilmentCondition.")
(comment WoundFestering "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EmittingAnObject. In each #$WoundFestering, some wound ripens and generates pus.")
(comment Pulling-Stretching "Applying force upon an object (#$pullee) to tend the object to move towards the #$puller, causing the #$pullee to deform
in the direction of the #$puller.  Instances of #$Pulling-Stretching may often also be instances of #$PullingWithoutTranslation, but this is not necessarily the case.")
(comment UITemplateEditorInput "Instances of #$UITemplateEditorInput are specialized editors within a user interface that allow the user to specify, edit, or view a #$FormulaTemplate.")
(comment CausingToBeInACertainCondition "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Event-Localized. In each #$CausingToBeInACertainCondition, something causes something to be in a certain intrinsic condition.")
(comment tokenPennTag "(#$tokenPennTag TOKEN TAG) means that TOKEN has been tagged with TAG.")
(comment VisualDisplayScreen "The collection of visual display screens -- relatively flat surfaces (though some projection screens are rolled up when not in use) designed to be the area where images appear during the projection or generation of an image.  Instances include movie screens and the slightly curved panes of glass at the end of a cathode ray tube.  Though a sufficiently flat and uniformly colored wall can be used as a screen, this collection only includes devices for which this is their primary function.  So though it is possible for a wall to belong to this collection, such instances are atypical.")
(comment ProjectingAnImage "A specialization of #$VisualInformationGeneratingAct.  Instances are emissions of light that result in the generation of an image (c.f., #$VisualImage).")
(disjointWith ProjectionScreen VideoScreen)
(comment ProjectionScreen "The collection of all flat surfaces that, by design, are intended to be location onto which visual images are projected during an instance of #$ProjectingAnImage.")
(comment defined "A unary predicate, whose argument-place is \"quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument), that is used to state that a given CycL non-atomic term does denote something.  Where <code>NAT</code> has the form <code>(FUNC . ARGS)</code>, <code>(#$defined NAT)</code> means that <code>NAT</code> has a denotatum because <code>FUNC</code> (the function denoted by the main functor in <code>NAT</code>) returns a value for the argument sequence <code>ARGS</code>.  In other words,  <code>FUNC</code> is defined on the sequence <code>ARGS</code>.
<p>
Cf. #$undefined.")
(comment candidateNounCompound "(#$candidateNounCompound MOD HEAD) means that the phrase consisting of MOD and HEAD has the necessary syntactic properties to be a noun compound.")
(disjointWith Water Milk)
(disjointWith VegetableMatter Milk)
(disjointWith Milk Oil)
(disjointWith Milk Juice)
(disjointWith BiologicalLivingObject Milk)
(comment nounCompoundWithRule "(#$nounCompoundWithRule MOD MOD-DENOT HEAD HEAD-DENOT RULE) means that the phrase consisting of MOD and HEAD, under the semantic interpretations MOD-DENOT and HEAD-DENOT, meets the syntactic and semantic constraints specified by RULE.")
(comment projectionSource "A binary predicate that relates a given #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.) to its source #$Surface.  <code>(#$projectionSource PROJ SOURCE)</code> means that <code>SOURCE</code> is the surface whose subregions <code>PROJ</code> maps onto another surface (cf. #$projectionTarget).")
(comment projectionTarget "A binary predicate that relates a given #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.) to its target #$Surface.  <code>(#$projectionTarget PROJ TARGET)</code> means that <code>TARGET</code> is the surface onto which <code>PROJ</code> maps subregions of another surface (cf. #$projectionSource).")
(comment DimensionPreservingProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$DimensionPreservingProjection is a surface projection function that preserves (see #$preservesRelnInArg) the relation #$spatiallyEquidimensional (q.v.).")
(comment ConnectednessPreservingProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ConnectednessPreservingProjection is a surface projection function that preserves (see #$preservesRelnInArg) the relation #$connectedSpaceRegions (q.v.).")
(comment OverlapPreservingProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$OverlapPreservingProjection is a surface projection function that preserves (see #$preservesRelnInArg) the relation #$overlappingSpaceRegions (q.v.).")
(comment PartsPreservingProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ConnectednessPreservingProjection is a surface projection function that preserves (see #$preservesRelnInArg) the relation #$connectedSpaceRegions (q.v.).")
(comment RCC8PreservingProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$RCC8PreservingProjection is a surface projection function that preserves (see #$preservesRelnInArg) each of the eight instances of #$RCCBinaryPredicate-BaseEight (q.v.).")
(comment ConformalProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ConformalProjection is a surface projection function that preserves the sizes of angles.  That is, if a triple of points or pair of lines (or edges) in the function's domain are related via a certain #$AngularDistance, then their respective images in the function's range will be related via the same angular distance.  (See #$angleBetween, #$angleBetweenPoints, and #$angleBetweenEdgesOfObject.)")
(comment projectionScale "A binary predicate that relates a #$SurfaceProjection function to its <i>scale</i>: the number by which the distance between any two things in the source surface (see #$projectionSource) can be multiplied to yield the distance between their respective images in the target surface (see #$projectionTarget).  Clearly, not every projection has a scale; a projection might distort its source in such a way that the relative distances between things are not maintained.  
<p>
A #$MapProjection (q.v.) typically has a scale that is significantly less than 1.  Often such a scale will be 1 divided by some positive integer.  For example, a scale of \"1:2500\" entails that a distance of 2500 meters in the source (e.g the Earth's surface) corresponds to 1 meter in the target (a map of the world).  Note that a map might be designated as having a particular scale despite exhibiting some minor relative-distance distortion, if the latter is deemed to be negligable for practical purposes.")
(comment EqualAreaProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$EqualAreaProjection is a surface projection function that preserves the 
the areas of two-dimensional regions.  That is, if a two-dimensional #$SpaceRegion in the function's domain has a certain #$Area (see #$areaOfObject), then the region's image in the function's range will have the same area.")
(disjointWith EqualAreaProjection AphylacticProjection)
(disjointWith ConformalProjection AphylacticProjection)
(comment AphylacticProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$AphylacticProjection is a surface projection function that preserves <i>neither</i> the areas of (cf. #$EqualAreaProjection) <i>nor</i> the angles between (cf. #$ConformalProjection) the regions subsumed by its source surface (see #$projectionSource).")
(disjointWith SpheroidalSurface FlatSurfaceRegion)
(comment SpheroidalSurface "A specialization of #$Surface and #$SurfaceRegion-Finite (q.v.).  This is the collection of all intangible, finite surfaces the are either #$Spheroids or self-connected proper parts of spheroids (see #$SpheroidPatch).
<p>
A #$MapProjection (q.v.) has a #$SpheroidalSurface as its source surface (see #$projectionSource).")
(comment SpheroidPatch "A specialization of #$SpheroidalSurface and #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded (q.v.).  This is the collection of all intangible, finite surfaces the are self-connected proper parts of spheroids (see #$properPartOfSpaceRegion).")
(comment MapProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$MapProjection is a surface projection function whose #$projectionSource (q.v.) is either a #$Spheroid or a portion of a spheroid (see #$SpheroidalSurface), and whose #$projectionTarget (q.v.) is a flat two-dimensional surface.")
(comment SurfaceProjectionType "A #$SecondOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$SurfaceProjectionType is a specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.), all of whose instances have some feature in common, such as preserving a particular relation (see #$preservesRelnInArg) or having a particular type of target surface (see #$projectionTarget).")
(comment SubFunctionTypePreservingRelnInArgFn "A ternary #$SubcollectionFunction that takes a #$FunctionType to the specialization of it comprising those instances that preserve a specified binary relation with respect to a specified argment-place.  (#$SubFunctionTypePreservingRelnInArgFn <b>FUNCTYPE PRED N</b>) is the collection <b>SUBFUNCTYPE</b> consisting of all and only those instances of <b>FUNCTYPE</b> that preserve the relation <b>PRED</b> with respect to their <b>N</b>th argument-place (see #$preservesRelnInArg).  That is, if <b>FUNC</b> is an instance of <b>SUBFUNCTYPE</b>, then whenever two members of <b>FUNC</b>'s domain stand in the relation <b>PRED</b>, their respective images in <b>FUNC</b>'s range stand in <b>PRED</b> also.
<p>
For example, (#$SubFunctionTypePreservingRelnInArgFn #$SurfaceProjection #$partOfSpaceRegion 1) is the collection of #$SurfaceProjections (q.v.) that preserve the part-whole relation between space-regions.")
(comment aisCopied "A sentence of the form (#$aisCopied COPYING AIS) means that the #$IBTCopying COPYING is a process where some #$InformationBearingThing that bears the relation #$instantiationOfAIS the #$AbstractInformationStructure  AIS is used to create another #$InformationBearingThing that also bears the relation #$instantiationOfAIS to AIS).")
(comment instrumentalCW-Focal "A more specialized form of the predicate #$instrumentalCW.  A sentence of the form (#$instrumentalCW-Focal  EVENT CW) is used to state that the #$ConceptualWork CW is somehow used in #$Event EVENT. Contrast with #$instrumentalCW-Auxiliary.")
(comment instrumentalCW-Auxiliary "A more specialized form of the predicate #$instrumentalCW.  A sentence of the form (#$instrumentalCW-Auxiliary EVENT CW) is used to state that the #$ConceptualWork CW is somehow used in the #$Event EVENT, but that it is of secondary importance. Contrast with #$instrumentalCW-Focal.")
(comment downloadTypeOfAIS "A sentence of the form (#$downloadTypeOfAIS TYPE AIS) states that TYPE is the sub-collection of #$DownloadingAFile for which all of its instances have the #$AbstractInformationStructure AIS in the role of #$aisCopied.")
(comment DownloadTypeOfAISFn "#$DownloadTypeOfAISFn is a #$UnaryFunction and #$CollectionDenotingFunction. An expression of the form (#$DownloadTypeOfAISFn AIS) is used to represent the sub-collection of #$DownloadingAFile in which the role of #$aisCopied is filled with the #$AbstractInformationStructure AIS.")
(comment Disambiguating "A subcollection of #$Comprehending.  Each instance of #$Disambiguating is a case in which an agent attempts to assign the correct meaning to some symbol, sign, or representation and this accomplishment involves the elimination of other possible interpretations.  This is not necessarily meant to be a spec of #$ConsciousActivity since it is possible for this activity to be done by a #$ComputerProgram-CW.")
(comment disambiguationTypeOfAS "A sentence of the form (#$disambiguationTypeOfAS TYPE AS) states that TYPE is a sub-collection of #$Disambiguating in which the #$AbstractStructure AS is the entity to which a correct meaning is being assigned.")
(comment DisambiguatingTypeOfASFn "#$DisambiguatingTypeOfASFn is a #$UnaryFunction and #$CollectionDenotingFunction. An expression of the form (#$DisambiguatingTypeOfASFn AS) represents the sub-collection of #$Disambiguating in which the #$AbstractStructure AS is the structure being disambiguated.")
(disjointWith SphericalSolid OblateSpheroidalSolid)
(comment SupportingStructure "A sub-collection of #$HumanScaleObject. Each instance is a physical structure that serves to support something.")
(comment Stemware "The collection of all pieces of stemware -- instances of #$DrinkingVessel such that a fluid resevoir (#$FluidReservoir, q.v.) is connected to a base (#$Base-Support, q.v.) via a glass stem (#$Stem-DrinkingVesselPart, q.v.).")
(comment Stem-DrinkingVesselPart "The collection of all stems on drinking vessels (see #$Stemware).")
(comment GlassStemware "The collection of instances of #$Stemware made primarly of glass.")
(disjointWith Cup HighballGlass)
(disjointWith Stemware Cup)
(disjointWith Stemware DrinkingMug)
(comment Cup "The collection of all instances of #$DrinkingVessel that have an open top and a somewhat bowl-like shape.  If the height of a vessel is out of proportion to its width to a point that it begins to look tall and thin, the vessel is usually classified as a glass (even if it's plastic).")
(disjointWith Cup PilsnerGlass)
(comment objectHasBase "A specialization (see #$genlPreds) of #$properPhysicalParts, <code>(#$objectHasBase OBJECT BASE)</code> identifies <code>BASE</code> as the base (see #$Base-Support) of the instance of #$PartiallyTangible, <code>OBJECT</code>.")
(comment TextTopicStructureFn "A #$UnaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction-Contextualized. Evaluating an expression of the form (#$TextTopicStructureFn TEXT) will return a list of lists of lists of length three.  The function presumes  that it is receiving a natural language expression containing at least one sentence.  The elements of the overall list will represent the individual sentences contained in TEXT.  Those elements are ordered based on the sequence of their appearance in TEXT. Each of those 'middle' lists represents a sentence.  Its elements represent #$Cyc's understanding of the words in the sentence.  Each element is a list of length 3 and they are ordered relative to the words appearance in the individual sentences.  The first element is a #$CharacterString that represents a particular word in the sentence.  The second element is a #$SpeechPart. The third element is a #$Set-Mathematical containing the possible denotations of the first element of the list based on the part of speech that is the second element.  The possible denotations receive some filtering to remove overly abstract or 'ke irrelevant' concepts.")
(comment Subsist-EconomicEvent "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$EconomicEvent. In each #$Subsisting-EconomicEvent, something supports itself.")
(comment RailroadConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$RailroadConcept are collections and relations having to do with railroad systems, transportation, facilities, equipment, and personnel.")
(comment ClassificationSystemSuggestionAndRepairMt "A microtheory that contains rules for generating alternative formulations of a classification system.  These formulations represent suggestions for repairing or augmenting the given classification system.")
(comment ClassificationSystemRepairOrAugmentationSuggestion "A subcollection of #$Microtheory.  Each instance is a set of propositions that describe a possible formulation of a classification system (#$ClassificationSystem, q.v.,) that differs from the formulation that Cyc knows about.")
(comment classificationSystemRepairOrAugmentationSuggestionMts "<code>(#$classificationSystemRepairOrAugmentationSuggestionMts SYSTEM SUGGESTION)</code> identifies <code>SYSTEM</code> as the instance of #$ClassificationSystem for which <code>SUGGESTION</code>, an instance of
#$ClassificationSystemRepairOrAugmentationSuggestion, describes a suggested alternative formulation.")
(comment strategyPurpose-SitType "<code>(#$strategyPurpose-SitType STRATEGY SITUATION-TYPE)</code> means that, in general, the purpose of executing the instance of #$Strategy <code>STRATEGY</code> is in order to bring about a situation of type <code>SITUATION-TYPE</code>.")
(comment ClassificationSystemImprovementStrategy "A collection of strategies for improving any instance of #$ClassificationSystem.")
(comment StrategyApplicationOutputsMtFn "<code>(#$StrategyApplicationOutputsMtFn FOCAL-OBJECT STRATEGY)</code> denotes the instance of #$Microtheory that results from applying the strategy (#$Strategy, q.v.) <code>STRATEGY</code> to <code>FOCAL-OBJECT</code>.")
(comment mtContainsResultsOfApplyingStrategy "<code>(#$mtContainsResultsOfApplyingStrategy MT THING STRATEGY)</code> identifies <code>MT</code> as the instance of #$Microtheory that contains the results of Cyc applying the strategy (#$Strategy, q.v.) <code>STRATEGY</code> to <code>THING</code>.")
(comment ImprovingAClassificationSystem "A specialization of #$ImprovingAConceptualWork.  Instances are improvement events in which the #$objectImproved is an instance of #$ClassificationSystem.")
(comment ImprovingAConceptualWork "The collection of instances of #$ImprovementEvent in which the #$objectImproved is an instance of #$ConceptualWork.")
(comment ImprovingADevisedPracticeOrWork "The collection of instances of #$ImprovementEvent in which the #$objectImproved is an instance of #$DevisedPracticeOrWork. To improve a work is also to generate a derived work (see #$genlWorks, #$derivedConceptualWork); as such #$ImprovingADevisedPracticeOrWork  is a specialization of #$CreationEvent, where among the #$outputsCreated is a better version of the original.")
(comment SpheroidalSolid "A specialization of both #$EllipsoidalSolid (q.v.).  Each instance of #$EllipsoidalSolid is a (tangible or intangible) three-dimensional object whose boundary is or approximates an #$Spheroid (q.v.)
<p>
Examples of spheroidal solids include tangible objects such as planets, as well as similarly-shaped intangible #$SpaceRegions.
<p>
See also the specializations #$SphericalSolid and #$OblateSpheroidalSolid.")
(comment EllipsoidalSolid "A specialization of both #$RoundObject and #$ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$EllipsoidalSolid is a (tangible or intangible) three-dimensional object whose boundary is or approximates an #$Ellipsoid (q.v.)
<p>
Examples of ellipsoidal solids include tangible objects such as jelly beans, as well as intangible #$SpaceRegions.
<p>
See also the specialization #$SpheroidalSolid.")
(disjointWith SphericalSolid ProlateSpheroidalSolid)
(disjointWith OblateSpheroidalSolid ProlateSpheroidalSolid)
(comment ProlateSpheroidalSolid "A specialization of #$SpheroidalSolid (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ProlateSpheroidalSolid is a (tangible or intangible) three-dimensional object whose boundary is or approximates an #$ProlateSpheroid (q.v.).  Thus, its polar diameter is greater than its equatorial diameter.
<p>
Examples of prolate spheroidal solids include tangible objects such as Nerf footballs, as well as similarly-shaped intangible #$SpaceRegions.
<p>
Cf. #$OblateSpheroidalSolid and #$SphericalSolid.")
(comment OblateSpheroidalSolid "A specialization of #$SpheroidalSolid (q.v.).  Each instance of #$OblateSpheroidalSolid is a (tangible or intangible) three-dimensional object whose boundary is or approximates an #$OblateSpheroid (q.v.).  Thus, its equatorial diameter is greater than its polar diameter.
<p>
Examples of oblate spheroidal solids include tangible objects such as some planets, as well as intangible solid-spheroidal-shaped #$SpaceRegions.
<p>
Cf. #$ProlateSpheroidalSolid and #$SphericalSolid.")
(comment AssignNodesMoreAppropriateExistingParents-Strategy "An instance of #$ClassificationSystemImprovementStrategy.  Applied to an instance of #$ClassificationSystem, this strategy will yield a number of suggested #$broaderThan links among existing nodes in that system.")
(comment ConicalSurface-WithBase "A specialization of #$ConicalSurface, #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded, and #$SurfaceRegion-Finite (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ConicalSurface-WithBase is the entire two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a finite #$Cone (q.v.).  It is the unbounded sum of a finite #$ConicalSurface-WithoutBase (cf.) and a #$CircularRegion (i.e. the base).")
(disjointWith ConicalSurface-WithBase ConicalSurface-WithoutBase)
(comment ConicalSurface "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing and #$SurfaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ConicalSurface is either (i) the entire two-dimensional, non-planar, unbounded surface of a #$Cone (see #$ConicalSurface-WithBase) or (ii) the same, but without the circular base (see #$ConicalSurface-WithoutBase).")
(comment ConicalSurface-WithoutBase "A specialization of #$ConicalSurface (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ConicalSurface-WithoutBase is the two-dimensional, non-planar, surface of a #$Cone (q.v.) minus the base (cf. #$ConicalSurface-WithBase).  The entire surface of an infinite cone is a #$ConicalSurface-WithoutBase.")
(comment HomelandDefense-Magazine "An instance of #$MagazineSeries.")
(comment bloTypeProducesSubstanceType "An assertion of the form (#$bloTypeProducesSubstanceType OBJECT-TYPE SUBSTANCE-TYPE) means that instances of the sub-collection of #$BiologicalLivingObject OBJECT-TYPE during the course of its normal activities will produce instances of the sub-collection of #$OrganicCompound SUBSTANCE-TYPE.")
(comment Neuromodulator "A sub-collection of #$OrganicCompound.  Sub-collections of #$Neuromodulator are substances that are released from neurons and which affect the behavior of other neurons.  Neuromodulators tend to reside in the medium outside of neurons (the cerebro-spinal fuild) and are not absorbed and metabolized by neurons in the manner that is characteristic of #$Neurotransmitter. However, that is not a hard distinction and these collections are not disjoint.")
(comment DualUseDeviceType "A #$SecondOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$DualUseDeviceType is such that its instances can be used for both military and civilian applications.  A prime example is #$GPSNavigator, which can be used by hikers as well as military units.")
(comment resultIsaForType "A #$SetOrCollectionPredicate that is used to state that a particular #$resultIsa (q.v.) condition holds of all functions of a given type.  <code>(#$resultIsaForType FUNCTYPE COL)</code> means that <code>(#$resultIsa FUNC COL)</code> holds for any instance <code>FUNC</code> of <code>FUNCTYPE</code>.  
<p>
Note that the above does <i>not</i> preclude <code>(#$argIsa FUNC SUBCOL)</code> holding for some specialization <code>SUBCOL</code> of <code>COL</code>; e.g. there might in some cases be an asserted condition on <code>FUNC</code>'s result that is stricter than that given by <code>COL</code>.  For example, <code>(#$resultIsaForType #$RelationDenotingFunction #$Relation)</code> holds even though some relation-valued functions have #$Predicate as their asserted #$resultIsa condition.  
<p>
See also #$argIsaForType.")
(comment TerroristGroupTypeByBeliefSystem "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristGroupTypeByBeliefSystem is a collection of #$TerroristGroups sharing the same #$BeliefSystem.")
(comment TerroristTypeByBeliefSystem "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByBeliefSystem is a collection of #$Terrorists that share a certain #$BeliefSystem.  (See #$hasBeliefSystems.)")
(comment TerroristTypeByCitizenship "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByCitizenship is a collection of #$Terrorists who are #$citizens of the same #$GeopoliticalEntity.")
(comment TerroristTypeByRegionOfResidence "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByRegionOfResidence is a collection of #$Terrorists who reside in the same region.  (See #$residesInRegion.)")
(comment TerroristTypeByLanguageSpoken "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByLanguageSpoken is a collection of #$Terrorists who speak the same #$Language.  (See #$languageSpoken.)")
(comment TerroristTypeByHighestEducationLevel "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByHighestEducationLevel is a collection of #$Terrorists who have the same highest level of education.  (See #$highestEducationLevel.)")
(comment TerroristTypeByOccupation "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByOccupation is a collection of #$Terrorists who have the same #$occupation.")
(comment TerroristTypeByEthnicity "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByEthnicity is a collection of #$Terrorists who have the same #$ethnicity.")
(comment TerroristTypeByBirthplace "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeByBirthplace is a collection of #$Terrorists who have the same #$birthPlace.")
(comment TerroristTypeBySex "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$TerroristTypeBySex is a collection of #$Terrorists who have the same biological sex.  (See #$hasBiologicalSex.)")
(comment ElectronicMailingList "An #$ObjectType and a sub-collection of #$MediaSeriesProduct.  Instances of #$ElectronicMailingList are the collected content of a group of emails, each of which has been distributed to a group of people in a #$EMailMailingListCommunication by some #$MailingListManagementProgram.")
(comment StructureOfClassificationSystemMtFn "<code>(#$StructureOfClassificationSystemMtFn SYSTEM)</code> denotes the microtheory that contains a description of the structure of the instance <code>SYSTEM</code> of #$ClassificationSystem.")
(comment structureMtOfClassificationSystem "<code>(#$structureMtOfClassificationSystem MT SYSTEM)</code> identifies <code>MT</code> as the instance of #$Microtheory that contains a description of the structure of the instance <code>SYSTEM</code> of #$ClassificationSystem.")
(comment Dented "An instance of #$PartiallyTangibleTypeByPhysicalStructuralFeature,
and a specialization of both #$Deformable and #$SolidTangibleThing.
Each instance of #$Dented is an object which has acquired a depression
in its surface as the causal consequence of the application of
pressure, where the depression remains even after the application of
pressure ceases. Cf. #$Denting.")
(comment hasAsCavity "<code>(#$hasAsCavity OBJECT CAVITY)</code> means that #$PartiallyTangible <code>OBJECT</code> has <code>CAVITY</code> as a hole or cavity.  #$hasAsCavity accepts both instances of #$CavityWithWalls and #$CavityInteriorRegion for <code>CAVITY</code>.  If the type of cavity is known, use the more specific predicate #$containsCavityInteriorRegion or #$containsCavityWithWalls, as appropriate.")
(comment ExampleBasedMachineTranslation "A sub-collection of #$LinguisticTranslationProcess that is essentially a translation by analogy and can be viewed as an implementation of the case-based reasoning approach of machine learning.")
(comment EBMT-EnglishToCycL "The process of translating a string in #$EnglishLanguage to #$CycL using the #$EBMTCyclifier.")
(disjointWith Border FixedFunctionalSystem)
(comment PavedRoadway "The collection of instance of #$Roadway that are also #$Paved.  Notable specializations include #$Highway.")
(comment UnpavedRoadway "The collection of instances of #$Roadway that are also instances of #$UnpavedPathArtifact.")
(comment ElevatedRoadway "The collection of instances of #$Roadway that are elevated (see #$Bridge).")
(comment possesses "<code>(#$possesses AGENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is in the physical possession of <code>AGENT</code>.  Compare with #$owns.")
(comment ReduceNumberOfSourceNodesThroughSubsumption-Strategy "An instance of #$ClassificationSystemImprovementStrategy.  Applied to an instance of #$ClassificationSystem, this strategy will yield a number of suggested #$broaderThan links among existing nodes in that system.")
(comment ContentMtOfCDAFromEventTypeFn "An expression of the form (#$ContentMtOfCDAFromEventTypeFn FILE EVENT-TYPE) denotes an instance of #$Microtheory.  Specifically, the content represented by the assertions in the denoted microtheory is the interpretation of at least part of the FILE using some instance of EVENT-TYPE.")
(comment CasualtyPredicate "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$CasualtyPredicate is a #$Predicate that, when applied to an admissible tuple of arguments, expresses casualty data about one of those arguments.  For example, #$organismKilled is a #$CasualtyPredicate since a sentence of the form (#$organismKilled EVENT ORG) expresses casualty data about EVENT.")
(comment physicallyCapableOf "<code>(#$physicallyCapableOf OBJ SIT-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> can physically play <code>ROLE</code> in a situation of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>.  This does not imply that <code>OBJ</code> is capable of doing so in the sense of know-how (see  #$skillCapableOf), nor does it mean that <code>OBJ</code> has the resources to do so (see #$resourceCapableOf).  For instance, (#$anatomicallyCapableOf  #$MarthaThePassengerPigeon #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy), but if Martha were caged, then (#$not (#$physicallyCapableOf #$MarthaThePassengerPigeon  #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy)).  Similarly, if Martha's feet were stuck in lime, she would also not be physically capable of flying.  It is this sense of  capability that this predicate is meant to express.")
(comment LinkPreservingClassificationSystemImprovementStrategy "A collection of strategies for improving instances of #$ClassificationSystem.  Instances of this strategy-type suggest the addition of links among existing nodes that do not violate the hierarchical assumptions of the system as given.")
(comment Railway-Siding "A specialization of #$Railway;
each instance of #$Railway-Siding is a short track that separates, runs
parallel, and rejoins the main track.  A #$Railway-Siding allows one train
to pass another when both are using the same main track.")
(comment RailwaySignal "A specialization of #$Signal; each
instance of #$RailwaySignal indicates the beginning of a section of track
that may have to be shared with other trains; thus it regulates whether
trains can move or stop.")
(comment RailroadYard "A specialization of #$Railway,
#$OpenArea, #$VehicleStorageConstruct, and #$SurfaceOnTangibleObject.
Each instance of #$RailroadYard is a fairly large open area of #$Railways
intended to be used for switching and storing
#$Train-TransportationDevice.")
(comment Railhead "A specialization of #$TransportFacility.  Each instance of #$Railhead is a terminus of a railway line where materials can be loaded or unloaded from railroad wagons.  Typically, a railway will interface with another mode of transport (such as shipping) at a railhead.")
(comment RailroadTurntable "A specialization of #$TransportFacility.  Each instance of #$RailroadTurntable is a rotating platform with railroad tracks that is used for turning locomotives or railroad cars.")
(comment RailroadSwitch "A specialization of #$TransportFacility.  Each instance of #$RailroadSwitch is a location on a railroad at which rails may be switched to permit access to another line.  Also known as \"railway points\".")
(comment cisTextTopicStructure "An assertion of the form (#$cisTextTopicStructure CIS LIST-STRUCT) means that when the actual text associated with the #$ContextualizedInformationStructure CIS is processed through the #$EvaluatableFunction #$TextTopicStructureFn (q.v.) it produces the (#$ListOfType (#$ListOfTypeFn (#$ListOfLengthFn 3))) STRUCT.")
(comment ProposeUpwardClosureFromLeavesToSourceNodes-Strategy "An instance of #$ClassificationSystemImprovementStrategy.  Applied to an instance <code>SYSTEM</code> of #$ClassificationSystem, this strategy will yield a series of <code>(#$broaderThan X Y)</code> suggestions for each appropriate concept <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> that occur in the #$broaderThan closure between the leaf and source nodes, such that either <code>X</code> or <code>Y</code> is a novel node (i.e., a concept not currently a node in <code>SYSTEM</code>).")
(comment EnduringThing-Localized "A specialization of both #$SpatialThing-Localized and #$SomethingExisting (qq.v.). Each instance of #$EnduringThing-Localized is a spatial enduring thing at which an event can occur or a situation can obtain.  Positive examples include planets, the borders between countries, human beings, rocks, and atoms.  Negative example include situations, events, abstract objects, and regions of space that exclusively act as possible locations for other spatial objects (see #$SpaceRegion).  An important specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized is #$Place (q.v.). The salient distinction between places (instances of #$Place) and locations (instances of #$EnduringThing-Localized) is that places are assumed to have relatively permanent locations, whereas locations need not have permanent locations.  Thus, from the perspective of someone standing on a beach, the crest of a breaking wave can be a location at which foaming is occuring (thus an #$EnduringThing-Localized), but it cannot be such a place (i.e. it cannot be an instance of #$Place).")
(comment RecordPlayer "A device used to play phonograph records")
(comment Garment "#$Garment is a specialization of #$ClothingItem.  Each instance of #$Garment is an item of clothing that is <i>not</i> an accessory (see the collection #$ClothingAccessory).  Notable specializations of #$Garment include #$Clothing-Top and #$Clothing-Bottom.")
(comment conceptInSystemDefaultDisplayString "<code>(#$conceptInSystemDefaultDisplayString THING SYSTEM STRING)</code> means that when displaying the concepts of the system <code>SYSTEM</code>, an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore, it is preferred that <code>THING</code> be represented using the string <code>STRING</code>.")
(comment inheritsConstraintsViaArg "A quaternary instance of #$MetaRelation (q.v.) that is used to state that a relation that is an argument to another relation imposes its own argument constraints on some argument positions of the latter relation.  The latter relation \"inherits\" argument constraints from the relation that is an argument to it.
<p>
<code>(#$inheritsConstraintsViaArg RELN INT-J INT-K INT-L)</code> means that when a #$Relation <code>SUB-RELN</code> occurs as the <code>INT-J</code>th argument position of #$Relation <code>RELN</code>, the argument constraints of the <code>INT-K</code>th argument position of <code>SUB-RELN</code> apply to the <code>INT-L</code>th argument position of <code>RELN</code>.  The inherited constraints are semantic well-formedness constraints on all expressions in which both <code>RELN</code> occurs as the arg0 term and <code>SUB-RELN</code> occurs as the <code>INT-J</code>th term.
<p>
For example, (#$inheritsConstraintsViaArg #$decreasesCausally 3 1 2) means that <code>(#$decreasesCausally ARG1 ARG2 ARG3)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>ARG2</code> satisfies the semantic well-formedness constraints on the arg1 position of <code>ARG3</code>.  So (#$inheritsConstraintsViaArg #$decreasesCausally 3 1 2) means that <code>(#$decreasesCausally ARG1 ARG2 #$agentPopularity)</code> (for instance) is semantically well-formed only if <code>ARG2</code> is an instance of #$IntelligentAgent (since (#$arg1Isa  #$agentPopularity #$IntelligentAgent) is true).  For an explanation of  semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.")
(comment Railway-StandardGauge "A specialization of #$Railway.  Each instance of #$Railway-StandardGauge is a railway that is of <i>standard</i> (or <i>international</i>) gauge.  <i>Rail gauge</i> is the distance between the inner sides of the two rails of a railroad, and standard gauge is ((#$Milli #$Meter) 1435) (or 4 feet 8.5 inches).  Standard gauge is currently used by about 60% of the world's railroads.  
<p>
Cf. #$Railway-NarrowGauge, #$Railway-BroadGauge, and #$Railway-DualGauge.")
(comment Railway-NarrowGauge "A specialization of #$Railway.  Each instance of #$Railway-NarrowGauge is a railway whose gauge is less than that of #$Railway-StandardGauge (or <i>international</i> gauge).  <i>Rail gauge</i> is the distance between the inner sides of the two rails of a railroad, and standard gauge is ((#$Milli #$Meter) 1435) (or 4 feet 8.5 inches).  Narrow gauge is currently used in a fair number of countries, including parts of South America, Asia, and Eastern Europe.
<p>
See also #$Railway-BroadGauge and #$Railway-DualGauge.")
(comment Railway-BroadGauge "A specialization of #$Railway.  Each instance of #$Railway-BroadGauge is a railway whose gauge is greater than that of #$Railway-StandardGauge (or <i>international</i> gauge).  <i>Rail gauge</i> is the distance between the inner sides of the two rails of a railroad, and standard gauge is ((#$Milli #$Meter) 1435) (or 4 feet 8.5 inches).  Broad gauge is currently used in a fair number of countries, including India, Spain, Ireland, Brazil, and parts of Australia.
<p>
See also #$Railway-NarrowGauge and #$Railway-DualGauge.")
(comment Railway-DualGauge "A specialization of #$Railway.  Each instance of #$Railway-DualGauge is a railway consisting of three or four rails in order to accommodate cars of two different gauges.  <i>Rail gauge</i> is the distance between the inner sides of the two rails of a railroad, and  #$Railway-StandardGauge is ((#$Milli #$Meter) 1435) (or 4 feet 8.5 inches).  
<p>
See also #$Railway-NarrowGauge and #$Railway-BroadGauge.")
(comment DieselFuel "A specialization of #$LiquidFuel and #$PetroleumProduct-Refined.  This is the collection of all portions of diesel fuel.")
(comment CommuterRailTransportation "A specialization of #$TrainPassengerTransportation and #$Commuting.  This is the collection of all transportation events in which passengers are transported in a #$CommuterTrain.")
(comment TrainPassengerTransportation "A specialization of #$CommercialPassengerTransportation.  This is the collection of all transportation events in which passengers ride in a #$PassengerTrain.")
(comment TrainEngineerLicense "A specialization of #$TransportationLicense.  The collection  of licenses of the sort required to operate a #$Train-TransportationDevice.")
(comment TrainEngineer "A specialization of #$Driver-TransportationProfessional.  This is the collection of persons whose job it is to operate a #$Train-TransportationDevice.")
(comment Monorail "A specialization of #$Train-TransportationDevice.  This is the collection of all trains that ride on a single rail.  The train might either straddle over or hang suspended from the rail.")
(disjointWith CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom CylindricalSurface-WithTopAndBottom)
(comment CylindricalSurface-WithTopAndBottom "A specialization of #$CylindricalSurface and #$ClosedSurfaceRegion (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$CylindricalSurface-WithTopAndBottom is the entire two-dimensional surface of a finite #$Cylinder (q.v.).
<p>
Note that each #$CylindricalSurface-WithTopAndBottom has as proper parts a #$CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom (q.v.) and two #$CircularRegions.")
(comment boundaryOfSpatialThing "This predicate relates a #$SpatialThing to its boundary.  Specifically, <code>(#$boundaryOfSpatialThing REGION BOUNDARY)</code> means that <code>BOUNDARY</code> is the entire (but possibly null, see #$NullBoundary) of <code>REGION</code>. 
<p>
See also #$SpatiallyBoundedThing and #$SpatiallyUnboundedThing.")
(disjointWith CylindricalSurface SurfaceRegion-Bounded)
(comment CylindricalSurface "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CylindricalSurface is the two-dimensional, non-planar, surface of a #$Cylinder (q.v.).  A cylindrical surface might be the entire surface (see #$boundaryOfSpatialThing) of a finite cylinder (see #$CylindricalSurface-WithTopAndBottom), or it might be just the tubular \"side\" surface of a finite or infinite cylinder (see #$CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom).  Note that, in the case of an infinitely long cylinder with no top or bottom, the side surface <i>is</i> the entire surface.")
(comment CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom "A specialization of #$CylindricalSurface and #$Tube (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom is the two-dimensional, non-planar, tubelike \"side\" surface of a #$Cylinder (q.v.).
<p>
Note that a #$CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom (like the cylinder it is the side of) might be finite or infinite in height.  If finite, it will be a proper part of some #$CylindricalSurface-WithTopAndBottom (q.v.).")
(comment externallyTangentAtPoint "A ternary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and a specialization of #$tangentAtPoint (q.v.) that relates two space regions (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) and a point (see #$Point) at which they are tangent, when those regions are such that some interior point of one is an exterior point of the other, and vice versa.
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$externallyTangentAtPoint REGION1 REGION2 BOUNDARY-POINT)</code> holds if and only if (i) <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are tangent at <code>BOUNDARY-POINT</code> (see #$tangentAtPoint), (ii) there exists a <code>POINT1</code> that is incident in the interior of <code>REGION1</code> (see #$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion) and incident in the exterior of <code>REGION2</code> (see #$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion) and is arbitrarily close to <code>BOUNDARY-POINT</code> (see #$arbitrarilyClosePoints), and (iii) there exists a <code>POINT2</code> that is incident in the exterior of <code>REGION1</code> and incident in the interior <code>REGION2</code> and is arbitrarily close to <code>BOUNDARY-POINT</code>.
<p>
Conditions (ii) and (iii) above can be stated succinctly using the predicate
#$boundaryClusterOfSpaceRegion, which relates a boundary point of a region to
two arbitrarily close points that are incident in the region's
interior and exterior, respectively.
<p>
See also the related predicate #$internallyTangentAtPoint.")
(comment tangentAtPoint "A ternary #$SpaceRegionPredicate that relates two space regions (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) and a point (see #$Point) where their boundaries coincide.  <code>(#$tangentAtPoint
REGION1 REGION2 POINT)</code> holds if and only if both of the these hold:
<pre>
  (#$incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION1)
  (#$incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion POINT REGION2) .
</pre>
See also the specializations #$internallyTangentAtPoint and #$externallyTangentAtPoint.")
(comment internallyTangentAtPoint "A ternary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and a specialization of #$tangentAtPoint (q.v.) that relates two space regions (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) and a point (see #$Point) at which they are tangent, when those regions share both interior and exterior points.
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$internallyTangentAtPoint REGION1 REGION2 BOUNDARY-POINT)</code> holds if and only if (i) <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are tangent at <code>BOUNDARY-POINT</code> (see #$tangentAtPoint), (ii) there exists a <code>POINT1</code> that is incident in the interior of both <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> (see #$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion) and is arbitrarily close to <code>BOUNDARY-POINT</code> (see #$arbitrarilyClosePoints), and (iii) there exists a <code>POINT2</code> that is incident in the exterior of both <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> (see #$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion) and is arbitrarily close to <code>BOUNDARY-POINT</code>.
<p>
Conditions (ii) and (iii) above can be stated succinctly using the predicate
#$boundaryClusterOfSpaceRegion, which relates a boundary point of a region to
two arbitrarily close points that are incident in the region's
interior and exterior, respectively.
<p>
See also the related predicate #$externallyTangentAtPoint.")
(comment arbitrarilyClosePoints "A symmetric, reflexive, binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate that relates two #$Points that are \"arbitrarily close\" to one another.  
<p>
More precisely, <code>(#$arbitrarilyClosePoints POINT1 POINT2)</code> entails that, for any region <code>REGION</code>, if <code>(#$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT1 REGION)</code> holds then so does <code>(#$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT2 REGION)</code>.  Equivalently, if <code>(#$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT1 REGION)</code> holds then so does <code>(#$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion POINT2 REGION)</code>.  Put yet another way, the predicates #$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion and #$incidentInExteriorOfSpaceRegion are both truth-preserving in their first argument-places with respect to #$arbitrarilyClosePoints (see #$conservativeViaArg).")
(comment RailwayCrossing "The collection of all junctions where a #$Roadway intersects a #$Railway.")
(comment BulletTrain "A specialization of #$HighSpeedTrain whose instances are  modern trains designed to travel at very high speeds.")
(comment TGVTrain "A specialization of #$HighSpeedTrain.  this is the collection of all trains used in the TGV railroad network in Europe.")
(comment SubwayTrain "A specialization of #$PassengerTrain whose instances are trains designed to run on #$Subway tracks.")
(comment TrainTicket "An instance of #$TicketTypeBySitTypeAuthorized and thus a specialization of #$Ticket.  Each instance of #$TrainTicket is a ticket that licenses one to ride a #$PassengerTrain.")
(comment CommuterTrain "The collection of all #$PassengerTrains designed primarily to be used on commuter lines: relatively short trips typically taken by people going to and from work in an urban area.")
(comment SenseExtensionDataMicrotheory "The collection of all microtheories containing lexical entries that represent sense extensions.  Each instance of this collection bears in the relation #$senseExtensionDataMtForLanguage to some instance of #$NaturalLanguage.")
(disjointWith SenseExtensionDataMicrotheory ParaphraseDataMicrotheory)
(comment senseExtensionDataMtForLanguage "<code>(#$senseExtensionDataMtForLanguage MT LANGUAGE)</code> means that <code>MT</code> is the instance of #$SenseExtensionDataMicrotheory whose assertions reflect sense-extension lexical entries for the language <code>LANGUAGE</code>.")
(comment SenseExtensionDataMtForLanguageFn "<code>(#$SenseExtensionDataMtForLanguageFn LANGUAGE)</code> denotes the instance of #$SenseExtensionDataMicrotheory that contains sense-extending lexical entries for the language <code>LANGUAGE</code>.")
(comment HumanPhysicalDescriptionPredicate "A #$PredicateTypeByTopic.  Each instance of #$HumanPhysicalDescriptionPredicate is a #$Predicate that can be used to ascribe common identifying physical features such as height, weight, hair color, eye color, skin color, etc. to a #$HomoSapiens.")
(comment queryFolderForSKS "<code>(#$queryFolderForSKS SKS FOLDER)</code> means that <code>FOLDER</code> is the top-level #$KEInteractionFolder for the top-level #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>. It contains subfolders for the example queries and builder queries for <code>SKS</code>.")
(comment QueryFolderForSKSFn "#$QueryFolderForSKSFn is the function corresponding to the predicate #$queryFolderForSKS.")
(comment exampleQueryFolderForSKS "<code>(#$exampleQueryFolderForSKS SKS FOLDER)</code> means that <code>FOLDER</code> is the #$KEInteractionFolder for the top-level #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code> which contains example queries to the <code>SKS</code> knowledge source.")
(comment ExampleQueryFolderForSKSFn "#$ExampleQueryFolderForSKSFn is the function corresponding to the predicate #$exampleQueryFolderForSKS.")
(comment builderQueryFolderForSKS "<code>(#$builderQueryFolderForSKS SKS FOLDER)</code> means that <code>FOLDER</code> is the #$KEInteractionFolder for the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>. It contains all of the builder query templates for <code>SKS</code>. (See #$builderQueryTemplateForSchematicElementOfSKS and its spec preds.)")
(comment BuilderQueryFolderForSKSFn "#$BuilderQueryFolderForSKSFn is the function corresponding to the predicate #$builderQueryFolderForSKS.")
(comment builderQueryTemplateForMeaningSentenceOfSKS "<code>(#$builderQueryTemplateForMeaningSentenceOfSKS SKS PREDICATE TEMPLATE)</code> means that <code>TEMPLATE</code> is the #$FormulaTemplate for the meaning sentence predicate <code>PREDICATE</code> in the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>.")
(comment builderQueryTemplateForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS "<code>(#$builderQueryTemplateForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS SKS COLLECTION TEMPLATE)</code> means that <code>TEMPLATE</code> is the #$FormulaTemplate for the logical field type <code>COLLECTION</code> in the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>.")
(comment BuilderQueryTemplateForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKSFn "#$BuilderQueryTemplateForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKSFn is the function corresponding to the predicate #$builderQueryTemplateForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS.")
(comment builderQueryTemplateForSchematicElementOfSKS "<code>(#$builderQueryTemplateForSchematicElementOfSKS SKS ELEMENT TEMPLATE)</code> means that <code>TEMPLATE</code> is the #$FormulaTemplate for the schematic element <code>ELEMENT</code> in the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>. Here, a <i>schematic element</i> is either a meaning sentence predicate or a logical field type for <code>SKS</code>. See also the disjoint typed spec preds #$builderQueryTemplateForMeaningSentenceOfSKS and #$builderQueryTemplateForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS.")
(comment builderQueryForMeaningSentenceOfSKS "<code>(#$builderQueryForMeaningSentenceOfSKS SKS PREDICATE QUERY)</code> means that <code>QUERY</code> is the #$CycLQuerySpecification for the meaning sentence predicate <code>PREDICATE</code> in the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>.")
(comment BuilderQueryForMeaningSentenceOfSKSFn "#$BuilderQueryForMeaningSentenceOfSKSFn is the function corresponding to the predicate #$builderQueryForMeaningSentenceOfSKS.")
(comment builderQueryForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS "<code>(#$builderQueryForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS SKS COLLECTION QUERY)</code> means that <code>QUERY</code> is the #$CycLQuerySpecification for the logical field type <code>COLLECTION</code> in the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>.")
(comment BuilderQueryForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKSFn "#$BuilderQueryForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKSFn is the function corresponding to the predicate #$builderQueryForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS.")
(comment builderQueryForSchematicElementOfSKS "<code>(#$builderQueryForSchematicElementOfSKS SKS ELEMENT QUERY)</code> means that <code>QUERY</code> is the #$CycLQuerySpecification for the schematic element <code>ELEMENT</code> in the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code>. Here, a <i>schematic element</i> is either a meaning sentence predicate or a logical field type for <code>SKS</code>. See also the disjoint typed spec preds #$builderQueryForMeaningSentenceOfSKS and #$builderQueryForLogicalFieldTypeOfSKS.")
(comment lexicalNodeMeetsNounCompoundConstraint "(#$lexicalNodeMeetsNounCompoundConstraint NODE CONSTRAINT) means that NODE meets the syntactic constraint specified by CONSTRAINT.")
(comment lexicalNodeDenotMeetsNounCompoundConstraint "(#$lexicalNodeDenotMeetsNounCompoundConstraint NODE DENOT CONSTRAINT) means that NODE meets the semantic constraint specified by CONSTRAINT, by virture of denoting DENOT.")
(comment FormulaTemplate-ReviewedForCandidateReplacementAssertions "The collection of #$FormulaTemplates that have been thoroughly reviewed for potentially useful #$candidateReplacementForPosition assertions that can be made on them.")
(comment keySuborganizations "(#$keySuborganizations BIG-ORG LITTLE-ORG) holds just in case LITTLE-ORG is a key suborganization of BIG-ORG.  This predicate is similar to #$keyMembers except it deals with suborganizations of an organization instead of its members.  For example, (#$keySuborganizations #$InternationalFrontForJihadAgainstJewsAndCrusaders #$JihadMovement) holds because the terrorist group #$JihadMovement is a key suborganization of #$InternationalFrontForJihadAgainstJewsAndCrusaders.")
(comment hasAsBoundary "(#$hasAsBoundary REG BOUND) means that BOUND either borders on REG (see #$bordersOn) or part of the boundary of REG is spatially subsumed by BOUND.  It is particularly useful when one wishes to be agnostic about whether BOUND is or is not a sub-region of REG or whether BOUND contains a sub-region which is a sub-region of REG, or whether REG simply borders on (see #$bordersOn) BOUND.  Some examples: (#$hasAsBoundary #$Virginia-State #$PotomacRiver) and (#$hasAsBoundary #$Maryland-State #$PotomacRiver).  Whereas the Potomac merely borders on Virginia, the non D.C. portion of the length of the Potomac is a sub-region of Maryland.  Alternatively, BOUND can be shared by two regions which border on each other.  Thus, since the northern and southern halves of the Red River belong to Oklahoma and Texas respectively, #$hasAsBoundary is an appropriate relationship 
between Oklahoma and the Red River as well as between Texas and the Red River.  Note that unlike #$formsBorderBetween, the bounding region need not be thin or small relative to the bounded object(s).  Thus, both England and the United States have the Atlantic Ocean as the second argument in #$hasAsBoundary assertions.")
(comment MtWithFocalContentSentenceFn "An expression of the form (#$MtWithFocalContentSentenceFn SENTNECE) is used to represent a hypothetical context in which the #$LinguisticObject (either an instance of #$TextString that encodes a #$NLSentence or a #$CycLSentence) is true.")
(comment mtHasFocalContentSentence "A sentence of the (mtHasFocalContentSentence MT SENTENCE) relates an instance of #$Microtheory to a #$LinguisticObject (either, an instance of #$TextString that encodes a #$NLSentence or a #$CycLSentence) is true.")
(comment evaluatedHypothesisMt "A sentence of the form (#$evaluatedHypothesisMt EVAL HYP-MT) relates an instance of #$Evaluating to an instance of #$HypotheticalContext.  Such a sentence asserts that the content of the MT (as repreented with the predicate #$mtHasFocalContentSentence) was the #$evaluee-Direct of the evaluation.")
(comment evaluationOfMtWithDirectEvalueeForCycL "A sentence of the form (#$evaluationOfMtWithDirectEvalueeForCycL  EVAL MT SENTENCE CYCL) connects an #$Evaluating EVAL to a #$HypotheticalContext MT whose content was (either directly or indirectly) the subject of the that evaluation. More specifically, SENTENCE represents the actual entity that was presented to the evaluator, while CYCL is the corresponding #$CycLSentence that is assumed to have the same content as SENTENCE when it was presented to the evaluator.")
(comment UserRejectedSentence "A #$SentenceEvaluationOutput.  This evaluation means that a user has, will, or would rejecte a sentence for some reason.  For example, the user is simply not interested in the subject and so, refuses to answer questions about it.")
(comment groupMaxCardinality "<code>(#$groupMaxCardinality GROUP CARDINALITY)</code> means that the instance <code>GROUP</code> of #$Group has at most <code>CARDINALITY</code> members.  So for example, where <code>(#$groupCardinality GROUP 5)</code>, then <code>(#$groupMaxCardinality GROUP 5)</code> and where <code>(#$groupCardinality GROUP (#$Unity 3 5))</code>, then  <code>(#$groupMaxCardinality GROUP 5)</code>.")
(comment groupMinCardinality "<code>(#$groupMinCardinality GROUP CARDINALITY)</code> means that the instance <code>GROUP</code> of #$Group is such that its cardinality (see #$groupCardinality) is no smaller than <code>CARDINALITY</code>.  So for example, where <code>(#$groupCardinality GROUP 5)</code>, then <code>(#$groupMinCardinality GROUP 5)</code> and where <code>(#$groupCardinality GROUP (#$Unity 3 5))</code>, then  <code>(#$groupMinCardinality GROUP 3)</code>.")
(comment perpendicularObjects "An instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  (#$perpendicularObjects OBJ1 OBJ2) means that the longest axis (see #$objectAxes) of OBJ1 is perpendicular to the longest axis of OBJ2.")
(comment Beggar "The collection of people who subsist primarily or to any significant degree by asking others for goods.")
(disjointWith Panhandling AthleticActivity)
(disjointWith Labor-WorkingEvent Panhandling)
(disjointWith Panhandling JobPosition)
(disjointWith Panhandling HomeWorkingEvent)
(comment Panhandling "The activity of stopping people and asking them for money or food.")
(comment BoyScout "The collection of boy scouts, young males (typically boys) who belong to a group of peers led by one or more adults, who are not themselves boy scouts (but may have been in the past).  Typically, the number of boy scouts is greater than the number of adults.")
(disjointWith Scout-MemberOfScoutingOrganization HumanPreSchoolageChild)
(comment Scout-MemberOfScoutingOrganization "The collection of instances of #$YoungHuman who belong to an instance of #$ScoutingOrganization, such as the Boy Scouts of America.")
(comment GirlScout "The collection of Girl Scouts, young females (typically girls) who belong to a group of peers led by one or more adults, who are not themselves girl scouts (but may have been in the past).  Typically, the number of Girl Scouts in a scouting organization is greater than the number of adults.")
(comment functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical "A #$MetaRelation and specialization of #$functionCorrespondingPredicate that is used to state that a given function and a given predicate amount to essentially the same relationship and have the same argument order, modulo the given argument number of the predicate corresponding to the result of the function.
<p>
<code>(#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical FUNC PRED N)</code> means that <code>FUNC</code> maps a given argument-sequence <code>ARGS</code> (say of length <code>M</code>) to the value <code>VAL</code> if and only if <code>PRED</code> holds of the (<code>M</code>+1 length) sequence <code>ARGS-N-VAL</code>, where <code>ARGS-N-VAL</code> is just like <code>ARGS</code> except for having <code>VAL</code> as its <code>N</code>th member and having <code>ARGS</code>'s <code>N</code>th to <code>M</code>th members as its (<code>N</code>+1)th to (<code>M</code>+1)th members.  Thus <code>PRED</code> will be a #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate (q.v.), with its <code>N</code>th argument-place being a \"functional\" one (see #$strictlyFunctionalInArgs).
<p>
For example, <code>(#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical #$StreetCornerFn #$streetCornerOf 1)</code> implies that <code>(#$StreetCornerFn XING DIRECTION) = LOT</code> just in case <code>(#$streetCornerOf LOT XING DIRECTION)</code> holds.  
<p>
When reifying an instance of #$Function-Denotational it is generally considered good ontological engineering practice also to reify a (or to find an already-existing) corresponding #$Predicate, as this allows one to describe more explicitly the relationship between the members of the function's domain and the corresponding members of its range (see #$relationDomain and #$relationRange).  
<p>
See also #$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate, #$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate-Symmetric, and #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic.")
(comment subordinatingConjunctionSemTrans "(#$subordinatingConjunctionSemTrans WORD INT FRAME TRANS) means that the TRANS is the translation template which can be used to relate the semantics of the matrix and subordinate clauses of a sentence for which WORD is used as a #$SubordinatingConjunction.")
(comment DisambiguationStrategy "The collection of methods for resolving ambiguity.")
(comment BlastFurnace "A #$BlastFurnace is a type of #$Furnace used for smelting metallic ores, such as #$IronOre and #$CopperOre.")
(comment CatalyticCracker "A #$CatalyticCracker is a unit is an #$OilRefinery where the long hydrocarbon chains in #$Petroleum-CrudeOil are broken, or 'cracked' into smaller hydrocarbon chains as part of the oil refining process. See (#$RefiningFn #$Petroleum-CrudeOil).")
(comment CrackingReaction "A #$CrackingReaction is a type of #$ChemicalDecomposition in which long hydrocarbon chains are broken, or 'cracked' into smaller hydrocarbons, typically with the aid of a #$catalyst.")
(comment SludgePond "A #$SludgePond is a type of #$FluidReservoir in which solid matter is precipitated from a liquid by evaporating the liquid or allowing the solid matter to settle.")
(comment PigFarm "A #$PigFarm is a type of #$LivestockFarm that raises pigs. See #$Pig-Domesticated.")
(comment pointsScored "An assertion of the form (#$pointsScored SCORING N) means that the N points were earned by the #$Agent-Generic that performed the #$ScoringPoints SCORING.")
(comment agentHasScoreInGame "#$agentHasScoreInGame is a #$TernaryPredicate and a #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation. An assertion of the form (#$agentHasScoreInGame AGENT SCORE EVENT) states that, at a particular period in time, the #$Agent-Generic has a score of SCORE within the #$GameEvent EVENT.")
(comment agentFinalScoreInGame "#$agentFinalScoreInGame is a #$TernaryPredicate and a #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation. An assertion of the form (#$agentHasScoreInGame AGENT SCORE EVENT) states that the #$Agent-Generic had scored a total of SCORE points within the entirety of #$GameEvent EVENT.")
(comment scoreRankForGame "An assertion of the form (#$scoreRankForGame SCORE RANK GAME) states that, within a given context, there is a non-empty set of instances of #$GameEvent where the #$Game GAME was played.  Further, SCORE represents the final score that was achieved by some player in at least one of the elements of that set (see #$agentFinalScoreInGame).  Finally, if the set of scores of those games were ranked in order from highest to lowest, then SCORE would be the RANKth element of that list.")
(comment agentScoresForGame "An assertion of the form (agentScoresForGame AGENT SCORE GAME) means that the #$Agent-Generic AGENT has been a player in some instantiation of the #$Game GAME and received a final score of SCORE.")
(comment ProcessingEvent "The collection of individual processing events. In a processing event, something is acted on in a string of events that might change it somehow (e.g., processing cheese) or simply change its status in some system (e.g., processing a form).  The thing acted on  (the thing that plays the #$objectProcessed role) is neither created nor destroyed as a result of the processing. #$ProcessingEvent is to be distinguished from the general notion of a process, which we currently represent at the type-level via #$CumulativeEventType.")
(comment processesTypes "(#$processesTypes PROCTYPE OUTPUTTYPE) says that every instance of PROCTYPE, a spec of #$ProcessingEvent, is a processing of some instance of OUTPUTTYPE.  In other words, (#$relationAllExists #$objectProcessed PROCTYPE OUTPUTTUPE).")
(comment LatitudeLine "The collection of lines around the earth which are parallel to the #$EarthsEquator.")
(comment PetroleumEngineer "The collection of engineers involved in oil and gas exploration, extraction, and refining. Some subspecialties include drilling engineers, production engineers, reservoir engineers, and petroleum safety engineers.")
(comment conceptPertinentToDegree "<code>(#$conceptPertinentToDegree CONCEPT DEGREE)</code> means that <code>CONCEPT</code> is an instance of #$Thing that is, to the degree of relevance <code>DEGREE</code>, pertinent to an instance of information gathering #$InformationGathering.
<p>
<b>NOTE</b>: This predicate should only be used in microtheories which specify the appropriate qualifications of assertions made with it: e.g., for whom the concept is pertinent, on what task, at what time and place, etc.")
(comment cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree "<code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree Q-SPEC DEGREE SET)</code> means that <code>Q-SPEC</code> is an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification that is, to the degree of relevance <code>DEGREE</code>, pertinent to the instance of information gathering #$InformationGathering), as a consequence of the pertinence of the terms in the #$Set-Mathematical <code>SET</code>.
<p>
<code>Q-SPEC</code> may have as an #$InferenceSentenceParameter a fully-bound query sentence, or it may be a query specification which has open variables.  If <code>Q-SPEC</code> has open variables, it may be that some appropriate restriction of those variables will yield a more obviously pertinent query.

For example, if someone is studying a crime denoted by \"<code>Crime008</code>\", a CycL query specification with this inference sentence parameter will be pertinent:
<p>
<pre>
(#$likelySuspects ?ACT ?AGENT)
</pre>
<p>
Further, a restriction of the open variables in that CycL query specification will yield a more obviously pertinent sentence:
<p>
<pre>
(#$likelySuspects Crime008 ?AGENT)
</pre>
<p>
In such a case, it will be true that a CycL query specification with the latter inference sentence parameter will be pertinent to a higher degree of relevance than a CycL query specification with the former inference sentence parameter.
<p>
See also #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric.
<p>
<b>NOTE</b>: This predicate should only be used in microtheories which specify the appropriate qualifications of assertions made with it: e.g., for whom the query is pertinent, on what task, at what time and place, etc.")
(comment cultureOperativeForEvent "(#$cultureOperativeForEvent <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b>
<b>CULTURE</b>) means that most or all #$socialParticipants in
<b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b> expect or accept that the social customs of
<b>CULTURE</b> will be largely followed during <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b>.
Some allowances might be made for the ignorance or discomfort of
participants who are not members of <b>CULTURE</b>. For example,
(#$cultureOperativeForEvent <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b> <b>CULTURE</b>)
typically holds when all the participants in <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b> are
members of <b>CULTURE</b> (though in this case, the
expectation of conformance is often not a conscious one).  In the
case of events which involve participants from multiple cultures, cross-cultural
guidelines sometimes dictate that one particular culture is
'operative.'  E.g., in many instances of #$HostedSocialGathering
involving participants from multiple cultures, it is the culture of
the #$hostOfEvent which is largely followed, since he or she 
controls much of the structure and form of the events.")
(comment BucketElevator "A #$BucketElevator is a type of #$Elevator that comprises a series of #$Buckets attached to a conveyor belt, used to raise liquids or otherwise porous materials to a higher level.")
(comment ShearLegs "A #$ShearLegs is a device, often temporary, for lifting heavy weights. It consisted of three poles lashed together at the top with a block and tackle attached. Shear legs were often used for placing masts in sailing ships.")
(comment EngineTestCell "A #$EngineTestCell is a #$MechanicalDevice which is used to test the performance of #$Engines. Typically, the engine to be tested is mounted, or otherwise attached to the test cell, which provides fuel and air inputs to the engine and monitors the engine's output (exhaust, torque, horsepower, etc.).")
(comment FlarePipe "A #$FlarePipe is a #$Flue, typically found in petrochemical refineries and other industrial complexes, where waste gases are expelled and burned off into the atmosphere.")
(comment Hopper "A #$Hopper is a top-loaded, funnel-shaped structure for temporary holding of loose material which will be dispensed from its bottom.")
(comment parallelOfSpheroid "A specialization of the binary predicate #$spheroidalSection (q.v.) that relates any #$Spheroid that is not a perfect #$Sphere to each of its <i>parallels</i>, or lines of latitude.  <code>(#$parallelOfSpheroid PARALLEL SPHEROID)</code> means that <code>PARALLEL</code> is a #$Circle that is a section of <code>SPHEROID</code> centered at a point on <code>SPHEROID</code>'s #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution (q.v.) and #$perpendicular to that axis.  Thus any two parallels of a given spheroid are indeed #$parallel to each other.  The parallel centered at the spheroid's center is its <i>equator</i> (see #$equatorOfSpheroid).")
(comment ManuscriptOfPCWFn "A function that denotes the #$Manuscript-CW of a given #$PublishedConceptualWork. For example, (#$ManuscriptOfPCWTypeFn #$TheCognitiveStructureOfEmotions-Book) is the manuscript of <i>The Cognitive Structure of Emotions.</i> The #$ConceptualWork thus denoted is said to be pre-publication.")
(comment ManuscriptOfPCWTypeFn "A function that denotes the #$Manuscript-CWs of a given type of #$PublishedConceptualWork. For example, (#$ManuscriptOfPCWTypeFn #$Article-PCW) is the collection of manuscripts of articles. Instances of the collection thus denoted are said to be pre-publication.")
(comment EarthsTopographicSurface "The entire contiguous topographic surface of #$PlanetEarth composed of the solid physical surfaces of Earth's land masses and liquid physical surfaces of Earth's oceans, and not inclusive of any part of the #$TheEarthsAtmosphere.")
(comment Zeolite "#$Zeolites are #$Minerals which have a porous structure, which permits a variety of #$Cations to occupy the space between their crystal lattices. These positive ions are rather loosely held and can readily be exchanged for others in a contact solution. This property makes #$Zeolites useful as #$catalysts in a variety of #$ChemicalReactions, notably in #$CrackingReactions.")
(comment Mineral "A specialization of #$InanimateObject-Natural.  Each instance of #$Mineral is a piece of homogeneous inorganic physical substance that has a crystalline structure.  Specializations of #$Mineral include the collections #$Diamond, #$Turquoise-Gem, #$Jade-Gem, and #$Corundum.")
(disjointWith Sphere (CollectionDifferenceFn Spheroid Sphere))
(comment CulturalAdvisorInternalConstant "The collection of #$CycLConstants which play a role in the
internal operation of the Cultural Advisor application.  More
precisely, each instance of #$CulturalAdvisorInternalConstant refers
directly to some property of this operation, and so denotes something
about the computational procedure that this application follows.")
(comment evaluationTypeAcceptanceThresholdForAgent "A sentence of the form (#$evaluationTypeAcceptanceThresholdForAgent SENT-EVAL-TYPE N AGENT) means that, within a particular context, the #$Agent-Generic will 'accept' that any #$Sentence that has received N different evaluations of the value SENT-EVAL-TYPE (an instance of #$SentenceEvaluationOutput) has the property associated with that #$SentenceEvaluationOutput.  For example, the sentence (#$evaluationTypeAcceptanceThresholdForAgent #$TrueCycLSentence 1000 #$Cyc) says that if, in a given context, 1000 people assert that a particular sentence is true, then #$Cyc will accept that it is true in that context.")
(comment TheModeledUser "#$TheModeledUser denotes the computer user with a user model who is modeled in a given context.")
(comment nearestGenls "A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections and a #$KBDependentRelation (qq.v.) that is a specialization of #$genls. 
<code>(#$nearestGenls COL NEAR-COL)</code> means that <code>NEAR-COL</code> is a \"nearest\" generalization of <code>COL</code>.  More precisely:
<p>
(i) <code>(#$genls COL NEAR-COL)</code> holds, <br>
(ii) <code>COL</code> is not identical to <code>NEAR-COL</code>, <br>
(iii) both <code>COL</code> and <code>NEAR-COL</code> are reified, and <br>
(iv) there is no reified #$Collection <code>NEARER-COL</code> distinct from <code>COL</code> and <code>NEAR-COL</code> such that both <code>(#$genls COL NEARER-COL)</code> and <code>(#$genls NEARER-COL NEAR-COL)</code> hold.
<p>
Note that #$nearestGenls is non-assertible (see #$notAssertible), since its extension at any given time depends upon (and so must be recomputed from) the current state of the #$genls hierarchy in the Cyc Knowledge Base.")
(comment PathSystemType-Structural "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$PathSystemType-Structural is a collection of path systems that share a certain kind of structure.  Examples include #$PointFinitePathSystem, #$NodeFinitePathSystem, #$LinkFinitePathSystem, #$FinitaryJunctionPathSystem, #$SimplePathSystem, #$Multigraph, #$FiniteMultigraph, #$SimpleGraph-GraphTheoretic, #$ConnectedPathSystem, #$Semi-DirectedPathSystem, #$DirectedPathSystem, #$BidirectedPathSystem, #$DirectedAcyclicPathSystem, #$BoundedDirectedPathSystem, #$DirectedMultigraph, #$DirectedGraph, #$DirectedAcyclicGraph, #$BoundedDirectedAcyclicGraph, and #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded.")
(comment focalEventOfCulturalScenarioEvaluation "<code>(#$focalEventOfCulturalScenarioEvaluation TASK EVENT)</code> means that the focal event subject to evaluation in <code>TASK</code> is <code>EVENT</code>.")
(comment directionFromTo "<code>(#$directionFromTo THING1 THING2 BEARING)</code> means that <code>BEARING</code> is the angular bearing from <code>THING1</code> to <code>THING2</code> where <code>BEARING</code> is the angle between the segment of the great circle joining #$AxialNorthPoleOfEarth and <code>THING1</code> and the segment of the great circle joining <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>.")
(comment Field-Mathematical "A #$Field-Mathematical is a unary function which assigns a scalar or vector (#$ScalarOrVectorInterval) to each #$Point in a region of space. Fields are often used in physics to model quantities that vary throughout space. For example, the speed and direction of a moving fluid; the strength and direction of some force, such as the magnetic or gravitational force; the distribution of temperature throughout a region; the distribution of pressure in a gaseous region. The first two examples are types of #$VectorFields, while the latter two examples are types of #$ScalarFields.")
(comment MagneticForceVector "A #$MagneticForceVector is a #$ForceVector that measures the magnitude and direction of the magnetic force exerted on a body at a given point.")
(comment ElectricForceVector "An #$ElectricForceVector is a #$ForceVector that measures the magnitude and direction of the electric force exerted on a body at a given point.")
(comment VectorField "A #$VectorField is a #$Field-Mathematical which assigns a #$VectorInterval to each #$Point in some #$SpaceRegion.")
(comment ScalarField "A #$ScalarField is a #$Field-Mathematical which assigns a #$ScalarInterval to each #$Point in some #$SpaceRegion.")
(comment MagneticField "A #$MagneticField is a #$VectorField which assigns a #$MagneticForceVector to each #$Point in some #$SpaceRegion.")
(disjointWith ElectricField GravitationalField)
(disjointWith MagneticField GravitationalField)
(disjointWith VectorField ScalarField)
(comment ElectricField "An #$ElectricField is a #$VectorField which assigns an #$ElectricForceVector to each #$Point in some #$SpaceRegion.")
(comment sampleInstanceOfTypeForProgram "The predicate corresponding to #$SampleInstanceOfTypeForProgramFn (q.v.).")
(comment SampleInstanceOfTypeForProgramFn "A reifiable #$BinaryFunction that takes a #$Collection COL and a #$ComputerProgram-CW PROGRAM and returns the collection of things that are intended to be used as \"sample\" instances of COL in PROGRAM.")
(comment SitSitBinaryPredicate "The collection of all #$BinaryPredicate both of whose arguments are required (via #$argIsa) to be #$Situations.")
(comment DateSlot "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$DateSlot is a #$BinaryPredicate whose second argument is required (via #$argIsa) to be a #$Date.")
(comment GeographicalRegionSlot "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$GeographicalRegionSlot is a #$BinaryPredicate whose second argument is required (via #$argIsa) to be a #$GeographicalRegion.")
(comment fieldInRegion "A predicate which relates a field (#$Field-Mathematical) with the region of space (#$SpaceRegion) in which the field is defined. <code>(#$fieldInRegion FIELD REGION)</code> means that for each #$Point, <code>POINT</code> that is incident in <code>REGION</code> (see #$incidentInSpaceRegion), the quantity <code>(FIELD POINT)</code> is #$defined.")
(comment MagneticNorthPoleOfEarth "One of two instances of #$MagneticPoleOfPlanet found on #$PlanetEarth. The #$MagneticNorthPoleOfEarth is the point where the Earth's magnetic field (see #$MagneticFieldOfPlanetEarthFn) points directly down (#$Down-Directly). Due to the dynamics of the field, the pole is not stationary.")
(comment MagneticSouthPoleOfEarth "One of two instances of #$MagneticPoleOfPlanet found on #$PlanetEarth. The #$MagneticSouthPoleOfEarth is the point where the Earth's magnetic field (see #$MagneticFieldOfPlanetEarthFn) points directly up (#$Up-Directly). Due to the dynamics of the field, the pole is not stationary.")
(comment atLeastN-GenQuant "(#$num-GenQuant COL1 COL2 N) means that there are exactly N elements of COL1 which are also elements of COL2.  This predicate is a sort of macro-predicate which can be used to represent the meaning of arbitrarily many English numeric determiners like \"two\", \"three\", etc.  More specialiuzed predicates for specific numbers can be defined in terms of #$num-GenQuant.  Thus, for instance, #$two-GenQuant can be defined as (#$num-GenQuant COL1 COL2 2).")
(comment GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing is a #$GeneralizedQuantifier <code>PRED</code> such that:
<pre>
(#$transitiveViaArg PRED #$subsetOf 2).
</pre>
Instances of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing include #$some, #$most, and (via its tranitivity) #$subsetOf. See also  #$GeneralizedQuantifier-LeftMonotoneIncreasing #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneDecreasing.")
(comment GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneDecreasing "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneDecreasing is a #$GeneralizedQuantifier <code>PRED</code> such that:
<pre>
(#$transitiveViaArgInverse PRED #$subsetOf 2).
</pre>
Instances of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneDecreasing include #$no and #$few.  See also #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing.")
(comment GeneralizedQuantifier-LeftMonotoneIncreasing "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing is a #$GeneralizedQuantifier <code>PRED</code> such that <pre>(#$transitiveViaArg PRED #$subsetOf 1).</pre> Instances of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing include #$some and #$atLeastN-GenQuant. See also #$GeneralizedQuantifier-LeftMonotoneIncreasing #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneDecreasing.")
(comment GeneralizedQuantifier-LeftMonotoneDecreasing "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing is a #$GeneralizedQuantifier <code>PRED</code> such that <pre>(#$transitiveViaArgInverse PRED #$subsetOf 1).</pre> Instances of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing include #$no and #$subsetOf. See also #$GeneralizedQuantifier-LeftMonotoneIncreasing #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneDecreasing.")
(comment both "An instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-RightMonotoneIncreasing.  <code>(#$both SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)</code> means that some contextually salient elements of <code>SETORCOL1</code>, of which it is presupposed that there are two, are also elements of <code>SETORCOL2</code>.")
(comment KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal-VerifyingKnowledgeFromCorpus "The goal-category of acquiring knowledge by verifying beliefs that were gained via a search of some corpus (e.g., the World Wide Web).")
(comment KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal-VerifyingInduction "The goal-category of verifying knowledge formed via induction.")
(comment KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal-VerifyingAbduction "The goal-category of acquiring knowledge by verifying beliefs that were formed via abduction.")
(comment CulturalScenarioContext "A specialization of #$ConjecturalContext (q.v.).  Each instance of
#$CulturalScenarioContext is a context in which the scenario central
to a Cultural Advisor task is represented.")
(comment CulturalScenarioContextForTaskFn "<code>(#$CulturalScenarioContextForTaskFn TASK)</code> denotes the
conjectural context in which the scenario central to the task <code>TASK</code> is
represented. See also the corresponding predicate, #$culturalScenarioContextForTask.")
(comment CulturalScenarioEvaluationAllotment "A specialization of #$Allotment (q.v.).  Each instance of
#$CulturalScenarioEvaluationAllotment is authorized designation of use of
resources, including a copy of #$CulturalAdvisor-TheProgram, to bringing about the
analysis of a cultural scenario.")
(comment culturalScenarioContextForTask "<code>(#$culturalScenarioContextForTask TASK CONTEXT)</code> means
that CONTEXT is the context in which the scenario central to the task
<code>TASK</code> is represented.  See also the corresponding function, #$CulturalScenarioContextForTaskFn.")
(comment Culture-Specification "A subcollection of #$Specification.  Each instance of #$Culture-Specification is a broad -- often
extremely broad and heterogenous -- set of norms and
descriptive claims that is pervasively, and somewhat unconsciously,
followed within some broad social grouping.

Many social groups follow a distinctive #$Culture-Specification,
including many nationalities
(#$PersonTypeByNationality), ethnicities
(#$PersonTypeByEthnicity), and professions
(#$PersonTypeByOccupation).  

<p>Typically, the elements of a #$Culture-Specification include
norms for social behavior and assignments of social
significance to actions (see, for example, #$EtiquetteOfCultureFn).
But cultures may also specify actions or beliefs related to various other subjects, including
religious practice, artistic style and activity, and sociological
phenomena.</p><p>

The process of learning of #$Culture-Specification usually begins at a
young age, and results in its elements becoming largely internalized.
Some elements of a #$Culture-Specification -- e.g., aspects of the
#$NaturalLanguage spoken -- may not be directly accesssible to
consciousness even in principle.</p>

A #$Culture-Specification emerges from and is sustained by the activity of agents, and
may go out of existence (see #$TemporallyExistingThing).  Component specifications may be added, subtracted, or modified over time.  Associated with each #$Culture-Specification is a corresponding
#$Culture-Practice, which is the complex event composed of the many
'followings' of the specification, as well shifts in it, by culture
members over time.")
(comment AerodromeConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.). Instances of AerodromeConcept are collections and relations having to do with air transportation facilities, equipment, and personnel.")
(comment IsogonicLine "An #$IsogonicLine is an intangible curve on the Earth's surface (#$EarthsTopographicSurface) connecting points of constant magnetic declination.")
(comment AgonicLine "An #$AgonicLine is an intangible curve on the Earth's surface (#$EarthsTopographicSurface) connecting points of 0 degrees magnetic declination.")
(comment GeopoliticalBorder "The collection of all boundaries, borders, and lines of demarcation that are used to separate one geopolitical region from another.  This includes such things as national borders, the boundaries of #$TimeZones, the boundaries of free trade zones, and maritime and airspace limits.  Borderline cases might be boundaries at the state level--eg, state lines and municipal boundaries.  Note also that instances of #$GeopoliticalBorder are cases of #$GeographicalThing-Intangible.  Although the geographical regions which they serve to demarcate are #$PartiallyTangible enough, the borders that serve to do the demarcating are not.  They are abstractions:  you can't see them or touch them physically.")
(comment cultureAdheresToSpecification "(#$cultureAdheresToSpecification <b>CULTURE</b> <b>SPECIFICATION</b>)
means that <b>SPECIFICATION</b> is generally followed within
<b>CULTURE</b>.  Instances of #$Specification that are followed in this way include <b>CULTURE</b>'s social etiquette -- see #$EtiquetteOfCultureFn -- and its various customs.")
(comment AntipodeFn "Given a pair of #$latitude and #$longitude assertions for <code>THING</code>, <code>(#$AntipodeFn THING)</code> returns the antipodal point for <code>THING</code>. See #$antipodesOf.")
(comment CulturalConvention-Specification "A subcollection of #$Specification.    Each instance of
#$CulturalConvention-Specification is the specification of an activity
or practice that is a regular part of certain
cultures, but not of others (though see below).  Practices
specified by #$CulturalConvention-Specifications are learned, and
typically involve a particular form of activity that is not fully
necessitated by biological need or physical environment (e.g., eating
is not a convention, but eating with certain utensils may be.)
  To be an
instance of #$CulturalConvention-Specification, a specification must
be widely followed within a culture by persons in applicable
circumstances.  Note that a #$CulturalConvention-Specification is an
abstract standard for behavior (mentally encoded by culture members),
rather than the collection of actions conforming to this standard.
 Each is thus a #$subWorks of certain instances of
#$Culture-Specification. (If there are universals in learned human
behavior, they can be considered parts of a general human
culture).
 Also note that
#$CulturalConvention-Specification includes both norms of behavior --
see #$EtiquetteOfCultureFn -- and customary forms of activity
deviations from which would not be considered offensive.")
(comment ServiceEventsOfTypeFn "This is a function which, when applied to a collection of events COL, denotes the specialization of COL which were performed as a service for some agent.")
(comment ConventionsOfCultureFn "(#$ConventionsOfCultureFn <b>CULTURE</b>) denotes the 
compound #$Specification whose #$subWorks are all and only those conventions and customs that exist within <b>CULTURE</b>.  (For more on customs as instances of #$Specification, see
#$CulturalConvention-Specification.)
See also the corresponding, but more general, predicate
#$cultureAdheresToConvention.")
(comment cultureAdheresToConvention "(#$cultureAdheresToConvention <b>CULTURE</b>
<b>CONVENTION</b>) means that <b>CONVENTION</b> is an individual
custom or set of customs (see #$CulturalConvention-Specification) that
is followed within <b>CULTURE</b>.")
(comment CulturalCodeOfEtiquette "A specialization of
#$MannersCodeOfConduct.  Each instance of #$CulturalCodeOfEtiquette is
the complete system of etiquette accepted or followed within some
#$Culture-Practice.  To denote the #$CulturalCodeOfEtiquette for a
particular culture, use #$EtiquetteOfCultureFn.")
(comment coplanarCurves "Two curves, <code>CURVE1</code> and <code>CURVE2</code> (see #$PlanarLinearObject-OneDimensional) are <i>coplanar</i> if and only if there exists some #$Plane, <code>PLANE</code> which subsumes them both (see #$curveOnSurface). In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$coplanarCurves CURVE1 CURVE2)</code>.")
(comment concentricCircles "Two coplanar circles, <code>CIRCLE1</code> and <code>CIRCLE2</code> are <i>concentric</i> if and only if they share the same center point. In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$concentricCircles CIRCLE1 CIRCLE2)</code>.")
(comment IndividualCustom "A subcollection of #$CulturalConvention-Specification.  Each
instance of #$IndividualCustom is a particular convention that is
followed within some culture.  More precisely, each #$IndividualCustom
is the specification of some particular learned practice that forms part of some
overall #$Culture-Specification.  For more on this notion of a
cultural convention, see #$CulturalConvention-Specification")
(comment ogcSpatialEnvelopesIntersect "A specialization of #$near. <code>(#$ogcSpatialEnvelopesIntersect GEOMETRY1 GEOMETRY2)</code> means that the minimal envelope, or bounding box for <code>GEOMETRY1</code>, (see #$minimumEnvelopeForGeometry) spatially intersects with the minimal envelope for <code>GEOMETRY2</code>.")
(comment CSQLSpatialEnvelopesIntersect "This #$CSQLComparisonPredicate is translated into SQL as the predicate \"&&\" which is defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL standard.")
(comment setOrCollectionUnion "A #$TaxonomicSlot for #$SetOrCollections.  <code>(#$setOrCollectionUnion UNION SET-OF-CLASSES)</code> means that <code>UNION</code> is the set-or-collection that is the union of all of the set-or-collections in <code>SET-OF-CLASSES</code>.  More precisely, any thing <code>THING</code> is an #$elementOf <code>UNION</code> if and only if <code>THING</code> is an element of at least one element of <code>SET-OF-CLASSES</code>.  Furthermore, we stipulate that (i) if each element of <code>SET-OF-CLASSES</code> is a set (see #$Set-Mathematical) then <code>UNION</code> is a set and (ii) if each element of <code>SET-OF-CLASSES</code> is a #$Collection then <code>UNION</code> is a collection.  If the elements of <code>SET-OF-CLASSES</code> include both a set and a collection then <code>UNION</code> will either be a set or a collection, though in many cases we might not be able to determine which, and can only conclude that <code>UNION</code> is a #$SetOrCollection.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$SetOrCollectionUnionFn.  Also see #$setOrCollectionUnion2 and #$setOrCollectionIntersection.")
(comment SetOfSets "This is the collection of all sets (see #$Set-Mathematical) all of whose members are themselves sets.  The constant <code>#$SetOfSets</code> is a rewrite of the non-atomic term <code>(#$SetOfTypeFn #$Set-Mathematical)</code>.")
(comment Topology "A <i>topology</i>, <code>T</code>, on a set <code>X</code> is a set of subsets of <code>X</code> which satisfies the following closure properties:
<p>
(O<sub>1</sub>) The union of every subset of <code>T</code> is an element of <code>T</code>.
<p>
(O<sub>2</sub>) The intersection of every <i>finite</i> subset of <code>T</code> is an element of <code>T</code>.
<p>
Axiom (O<sub>1</sub>) implies in particular that the union of the empty set is an element of <code>T</code>, i.e. the empty set itself is an element of <code>T</code>. Similarly, axiom (O<sub>2</sub>) implies that the intersection of the empty set is an element of <code>T</code>, i.e. <code>X</code> itself, is an element of <code>T</code>. The elements of <code>T</code> are called the <i>open sets</i> of the <i>topological structure</i> defined by <code>T</code> on <code>X</code>. The #$Tuple, <code>(X,T)</code> is called a #$TopologicalSpace.")
(comment TopologicalSpace "A #$TopologicalSpace is a #$Tuple consisting of a set, <code>SET</code>, together with a #$Topology, <code>TOPOLOGY</code>, of subsets of <code>SET</code>. The elements of <code>SET</code> are typically called the <i>points</i> of the topological space, however points in the topological sense should not be confused with instances of the collection <code>#$Point</code>, which are zero dimensional spatial things. In principle, any set can be endowed with a topology. See also the predicates used for identifying the base set and topology in a topological space, #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace and #$topologyOfTopologicalSpace.")
(comment baseSetOfTopologicalSpace "<code>(#$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace SET SPACE)</code> means that <code>SET</code> is the base set of the #$TopologicalSpace, <code>SPACE</code>.")
(comment topologyOfTopologicalSpace "<code>(#$topologyOfTopologicalSpace TOPOLOGY SPACE)</code> means that <code>TOPOLOGY</code> is the #$Topology of the #$TopologicalSpace, <code>SPACE</code>.")
(comment openSetOfTopologicalSpace "An <i>open set</i> in a #$TopologicalSpace is a subset of the base set (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace) that is an element of the topology of the space (see #$topologyOfTopologicalSpace). In CycL, the sentence <code>(#$openSetOfTopologicalSpace SET SPACE)</code> means that <code>SET</code> is an open set of the #$TopologicalSpace, <code>SPACE</code>.")
(comment neighborhoodOfThingInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace), <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, and <code>THING</code> is an element of <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code>, then <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> is a <i>neighborhood</i> of <code>THING</code> if and only if there exists an open set <code>OPENSET</code> of <code>SPACE</code> which contains <code>THING</code> and is a subset of <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code>. In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$neighborhoodOfThingInTopologicalSpace THING NEIGHBORHOOD SPACE)</code>.")
(comment fundamentalSystemOfNeighborhoodsOfThing "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace), <code>THING</code> is an element of <code>BASE</code>, and <code>SYSTEM</code> is a set of subsets of <code>BASE</code>, then <code>SYSTEM</code> is called a <i>fundamental system of neighborhoods</i> of <code>THING</code>, and we write in #$CycL <pre>(#$fundamentalSystemOfNeighborhoodsOfThing THING SYSTEM SPACE)</pre> if and only if for each neighborhood, <code>NEIGHBORHOOD1</code>, of <code>THING</code>, (see #$neighborhoodOfThingInTopologicalSpace) there exists a neighborhood, <code>NEIGHBORHOOD2</code>, of <code>THING</code>, that is an element of <code>SYSTEM</code> and is a subset of <code>NEIGHBORHOOD1</code>.")
(comment fundamentalSystemOfNeighborhoodsOfSet "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace), <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, and <code>SYSTEM</code> is a set of subsets of <code>BASE</code>, then <code>SYSTEM</code> is called a <i>fundamental system of neighborhoods</i> of <code>SET</code>, and we write in #$CycL <pre>(#$fundamentalSystemOfNeighborhoodsOfSet SET SYSTEM SPACE)</pre> if and only if for each neighborhood, <code>NEIGHBORHOOD1</code>, of <code>SET</code>, (see #$neighborhoodOfSetInTopologicalSpace) there exists a neighborhood, <code>NEIGHBORHOOD2</code>, of <code>SET</code>, that is an element of <code>SYSTEM</code> and is a subset of <code>NEIGHBORHOOD1</code>.")
(comment basisOfTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and topology <code>TOPOLOGY</code> (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace and #$topologyOfTopologicalSpace), then the subset <code>BASIS</code> of <code>TOPOLOGY</code> is called a <i>basis</i> of <code>TOPOLOGY</code> if and only if every element of <code>TOPOLOGY</code> is the union of elements of <code>BASIS</code>. In CycL, this relation is stated as <code>(#$basisOfTopologicalSpace BASIS SPACE)</code>.
<p>
The following equivalent definition follows from the above and the definition of a fundamental system of neighborhoods. (See #$fundamentalSystemOfNeighborhoodsOfThing) The set <code>BASIS</code> is a basis for <code>TOPOLOGY</code> if and only if for each element <code>THING</code> of <code>BASE</code>, the set of elements <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> of <code>BASIS</code> which contain <code>THING</code> as an element constitute a fundamental system of neighborhoods for <code>THING</code>.")
(comment closedSetOfTopologicalSpace "A <i>closed set</i> in a topological space is the complement of an open set. That is, if <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code>, (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace) and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then <code>(#$closedSetOfTopologicalSpace SET SPACE)</code> means that <code>(#$openSetOfTopologicalSpace BASE\\SET SPACE)</code> is true, where <code>BASE\\SET</code> is the complement of <code>SET</code> in <code>BASE</code>.")
(comment locallyFiniteFamilyInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace) and <code>FAMILY</code> is a set of subsets of <code>BASE</code>, then <code>FAMILY</code> is <i>locally finite</i> if and only if for every <code>THING</code> in <code>BASE</code> there exists a subset <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> of <code>BASE</code> which is a neighborhood of <code>THING</code> (see #$neighborhoodOfThingInTopologicalSpace) and is such that the intersection of <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> with the elements of <code>FAMILY</code> is non-empty for at most a finite number of such elements.")
(comment interiorOfSetInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then the <i>interior</i> of <code>SET</code> is the set of elements of <code>SET</code> for which <code>SET</code> is a neighborhood. (See #$neighborhoodOfThingInTopologicalSpace) In CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$interiorOfSetInTopologicalSpace INTERIOR SET SPACE)</code>.")
(comment closureOfSetInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then the <i>closure</i> of <code>SET</code> is the set of all elements <code>THING</code> of <code>SPACE</code> for which the intersection of <code>SET</code> with every neighborhood of <code>THING</code> is non-empty. (See #$neighborhoodOfThingInTopologicalSpace) In CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$closureOfSetInTopologicalSpace CLOSURE SET SPACE)</code>.")
(comment frontierOfSetInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then the <i>frontier</i> of <code>SET</code> is the intersection of the closure of <code>SET</code> with the closure of the complement of <code>SET</code> in <code>BASE</code> In CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$frontierOfSetInTopologicalSpace FRONTIER SET SPACE)</code>.")
(comment denseSetInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then <code>SET</code> is <i>dense</i> in <code>SPACE</code> if and only if the closure of <code>SET</code> is <code>BASE</code>. In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$denseSetInTopologicalSpace SET SPACE)</code>.")
(comment separationOfSetInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then the sets <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> form a <i>separation of</i> <code>SET</code> if and only if the following conditions are met:
<p>
(S<sub>I</sub>) <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> are both non-empty, i.e. #$different from #$TheEmptySet.
<p>
(S<sub>II</sub>) <code>SET</code> is the union of <code>A</code> and <code>B</code>.
<p>
(S<sub>IIIa</sub>) The intersection of the closure of <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> is empty.
<p>
(S<sub>IIIb</sub>) The intersection of the closure of <code>B</code> and <code>A</code> is empty.
<p>
In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$separationOfSetInTopologicalSpace A B SET SPACE)</code>.")
(comment connectedSetInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then <code>SET</code> is <i>connected</i> if and only if there exists no separation of <code>SET</code> into separated subsets (see #$separationOfSetInTopologicalSpace). In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$connectedSetInTopologicalSpace SET SPACE)</code>.")
(comment GeographicDatum "A #$GeographicDatum is defined as any numerical or geometrical quantity, or set of quantities, which serves as a reference point from which to accurately measure geographic position.")
(disjointWith HorizontalDatum VerticalDatum)
(comment VerticalDatum "A #$VerticalDatum is a #$GeographicDatum which is used to accruately determine the height of a geographical location above (or below) some reference surface. For example, #$VerticalGeodeticDatums are used to determine a location's height relative to the #$EarthsGeoidSurface, while #$TidalDatums are used to determine a location's height relative to mean sea level.")
(comment VerticalGeodeticDatum "A type of #$VerticalDatum. #$VerticalGeodeticDatums are used to determine a location's height relative to the #$EarthsGeoidSurface")
(disjointWith VerticalGeodeticDatum TidalDatum)
(comment TidalDatum "A type of #$VerticalDatum. #$TidalDatums are used to determine a location's height relative to mean sea level. Tidal datums are local datums and should not be extended into areas which have differing hydrographic characteristics without substantiating measurements.")
(comment GeodeticControlPoint "A #$GeodeticControlPoint is a stationary point on the #$PlanetEarth whose horzontal and/or vertical position with respect to the #$EarthsGeoidSurface has been accruately determined.")
(comment GeodeticControlNetwork "A #$GeodeticControlNetwork is a #$Network of #$GeodeticControlPoints which is used to establish an accurate #$Datum for determining the position of locations on some portion of the #$PlanetEarth.")
(comment spheroidTypeForDatum "<code>(#$spheroidTypeForDatum SPHEROID-TYPE DATUM)</code> means that some instance of <code>SPHEROID-TYPE</code> is used to locally approximate a patch of the #$EarthsGeoidSurface near the benchmark point for <code>DATUM</code>.")
(comment CartographicSpheroidType "#$CartographicSpheroidType is a #$SecondOrderCollection of types of #$OblateSpheroids that are used by cartographers to approximate the #$EarthsGeoidSurface.")
(comment NorthAmericanDatum1927 "The North American Datum 1927. This datum was established by the United States National Ocean Service (NOS), later known as National Oceanographic and Atmospheric Agency (NOAA). From 1927 to 1987 all U.S. government charts of the contiguous United States were based on the #$NorthAmericanDatum1927. After 1989, the U.S. officially switched to the #$NorthAmericanDatum1983.")
(comment NorthAmericanDatum1983 "The North American Datum 1983. This datum was established by the National Oceanographic and Atmospheric Agency in 1983 and has been the official datum for all U.S. government charts of the contiguous United States since 1989. It is a refinement of the #$NorthAmericanDatum1927.")
(comment datumForCoordinateSystem "<code>(#$datumForCoordinateSystem DATUM SYSTEM)</code> means that <code>DATUM</code> is the #$GeographicDatum upon which an object's coordinates in <code>SYSTEM</code> are referenced.")
(comment NAD27CoordinateSystem "The #$PolarCoordinateSystem based on the #$NorthAmericanDatum1927 datum.")
(comment NAD83CoordinateSystem "The #$PolarCoordinateSystem based on the #$NorthAmericanDatum1983 datum.")
(comment primeMeridianForCoordinateSystem "<code>(#$primeMeridianForCoordinateSystem MERIDIAN SYSTEM)</code> means that <code>MERIDIAN</code> is the #$LongitudeLine from which the azimuth angle for <code>SYSTEM</code> (see #$azimuthAnglePredicate) measures angles. That is, if <code>AZIMUTH-PRED</code> is the azimuth angle predicate for <code>SYSTEM</code>, then <code>(AZIMUTH-PRED MERIDIAN (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0))</code> and the value of <code>AZIMUTH-PRED</code> is positive for locations to the east of <code>MERIDIAN</code> and negative for locations west of <code>MERIDIAN</code>. Often, <code>MERIDIAN</code> is the #$PrimeMeridian-Greenwich, however there are some coordinate systems that use other meridians.")
(comment WorldGeodeticSurvey1984 "The datum established by the World Geodetic Survey of 1984. The WGS system is not based on a single #$GeodeticControlPoint, but on many points, fixed with extreme precision by the Global Positioning Satellite system. It is applicable worldwide and has been widely adopted around the world as the base reference datum.")
(comment WGS84CoordinateSystem "The #$PolarCoordinateSystem based on the #$WorldGeodeticSurvey1984 datum.")
(comment CoordinateSystemFromSRIDFn "(#$CoordinateSystemFromSRIDFn SRID) denotes the spatial
reference system (see #$CoordinateSystem )defined within the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL with id SRID. See also the
corresponding predicate #$sridForCoordinateSystem.")
(comment AltitudeAnglePredicateFn "The function corresponding to the predicate #$altitudeAnglePredicate. If <code>SYSTEM</code> is a #$PolarCoordinateSystem, <code>(#$AltitudeAnglePredicateFn SYSTEM)</code> denotes the altitude angle predicate for <code>SYSTEM</code>.")
(comment AzimuthAnglePredicateFn "The function corresponding to the predicate #$azimuthAnglePredicate. If <code>SYSTEM</code> is a #$PolarCoordinateSystem, <code>(#$AzimuthAnglePredicateFn SYSTEM)</code> denotes the azimuth angle predicate for <code>SYSTEM</code>.")
(comment RadialDistancePredicateFn "The function corresponding to the predicate #$radialDistancePredicate. If <code>SYSTEM</code> is a #$PolarCoordinateSystem, <code>(#$RadialDistancePredicateFn SYSTEM)</code> denotes the radial distance predicate for <code>SYSTEM</code>.")
(comment MapProjectionConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain (q.v.).  Instances of #$MapProjectionConcept are collections and relations having to do with general kinds of map projections.  Specializations of #$MapProjection, and other collections and relations used for describing features and components of map projections are instances of this collection.")
(comment negationQuantities "This predicate relates quantities that are mutually incompatible.  (#$negationQuantities QUANT1 QUANT2) means nothing has, or could have, both QUANT1 and QUANT2 as quantities at the same time.  This implies
more than mere material non-coincidence (e.g. being very happy and being very poor are not negation-quantities, even if there happen to be no very happy very poor people); but #$negationQuantities does subsume both \"physical\" and \"logical\" (or \"conceptual\") incompatibility between quantities.)")
(comment culturalSpecificationParts "(#$culturalSpecificationParts <code>CULTURE</code>
<code>SUB-SPEC</code>) means that <code>SUB-SPEC</code> is one of the
components of the overall #$Culture-Specification,
<code>CULTURE</code>.  Common examples of such component specifications include
instances of #$CulturalCodeOfEtiquette and of
#$CulturalConvention-Specification (qq.v.).  Note that
#$culturalSpecificationParts may hold between two complete
#$Culture-Specifications, in the case where <code>CULTURE-SPEC</code>
is a specialized form of <code>SUB-SPEC</code> that includes and
supplements the guidelines of the latter.")
(comment cultureContainsCulture "(#$cultureContainsCulture <code>SPECIALIZED</code>
<code>GENERAL</code>) means that <code>SPECIALIZED</code> is a
specialized form of the more general culture <code>GENERAL</code>.
That is, <code>SPECIALIZED</code> includes most of the component
specifications of <code>CULTURE</code> (see
#$culturalSpecificationParts), but also includes additional directives
and claims.")
(comment HorizontalDatum "A #$HorizontalDatum is a #$GeographicDatum which is used to accurately determine the horziontal position of a geographic location.")
(comment SurfaceGeometryConcept "A #$ConceptTypeByDomain containing the core concepts related to the geometry of #$Surfaces.")
(comment negligibleForces "(#$negligibleForces SIT FORCE) means that in the #$Situation SIT,  FORCE is a #$ForceVector that is negligible.  This predicate is useful for describing physics problems where it is common to state that particular normal forces (e.g., friction, wind resistance) are not meant to factor in.")
(comment AirResistance "A specialization of #$FrictionVector (q.v.). Air resistance is friction between an object and the air.")
(comment duration "A #$TemporalPredicate and #$MeasurableQuantitySlot (qq.v.) that relates a temporal thing to the length of time (see #$Time-Quantity) during which it existed, happened, or obtained.  <code>(#$duration TEMPTHING TIME)</code> means that <code>TIME</code> is the amount of time during which <code>TEMPTHING</code> existed (if a #$SomethingExisting) or happened (if an #$Event) or obtained (if a #$StaticSituation).
<p>
Note that if <code>TEMPTHING</code> is an instance of #$TemporallyContinuousThing (q.v.), its #$duration is the same as its #$timeSpan (q.v.): the total elapsed time from its start to its end.  But if <code>TEMPTHING</code> is discontinuous, its duration is strictly less than its time-span.  For example, the playing of the 2004 Super Bowl game has a duration of one hour, but a time-span of several hours.")
(comment occursWhile "#$occursWhile is a binary predicate relating an event to an instance of #$TemporallyExtendedThing.  <code>(#$occursWhile EVENT TET)</code> means that the event <code>EVENT</code> occurs entirely within the temporal extent of <code>TET</code>.  Thus, <code>(#$occursDuring EVENT TET)</code> implies that <code>(#$temporallySubsumes TET EVENT)</code>. For example, if <code>ASSN</code> is the assassination of Martin Luther King, Jr., then <code>(#$occursWhile ASSN (#$YearFn 1968))</code>.  This is a generalization  of #$occursDuring.  The latter requires an instance of #$TimeInterval while this predicate allows the use of instances of #$TemporallyExtendedThing.")
(disjointWith DevelopableSurface FlatSurfaceRegion)
(disjointWith DevelopableSurface SurfaceRegion-Unbounded)
(comment DevelopableSurface "A specialization of #$Surface.  Each instance of #$DevelopableSurface is a non-flat surface that can be \"flattened\" or \"developed\" (see #$DeveloperProjection) into a two-dimensional flat surface (see #$PlaneFigure), without being stretched or compressed.  Such flattening might involve \"cutting\" the developable surface, however.  Thus, the distance between any two points on a developable surface remains the same after the surface is flattened -- as long as the points are not separated by a cut.  For example, a #$CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom can be cut lengthwise and flattened into a #$Rectangle.  A #$ConicalSurface-WithoutBase can be cut from center to edge and flattened into a flat \"pie-shaped\" region.")
(disjointWith MapProjection DevelopableProjection)
(comment DevelopableProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$DevelopableProjection is a surface projection whose #$projectionSource is a #$SpheroidalSurface (q.v.) and whose #$projectionTarget is a #$DevelopableSurface (q.v.).  While not itself a proper #$MapProjection, a #$DevelopableProjection can be combined with (via #$FunctionCompositionFn) a #$DeveloperProjection (q.v.) to yield a #$CompositeMapProjection (q.v.).")
(comment DeveloperProjection "A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$DeveloperProjection is a surface projection whose #$projectionSource is a #$DevelopableSurface (q.v.) and whose #$projectionTarget is a flat surface (see #$PlaneFigure).  While not itself a proper #$MapProjection, a #$DeveloperProjection can be combined with (via #$FunctionCompositionFn) a #$DevelopableProjection (q.v.) to yield a #$CompositeMapProjection (q.v.).")
(comment DirectMapProjection "A specialization of #$MapProjection.  Each instance of #$DirectMapProjection is a map projection in which points on the #$Spheroid (see #$projectionSource) are projected directly onto points on the flat target surface (see #$projectionTarget) rather than via an intermediate \"developable\" surface (see #$projectionIntermediate).  See the specialization #$AzimuthalProjection.  And cf. #$CompositeMapProjection.")
(disjointWith DirectMapProjection CompositeMapProjection)
(comment CompositeMapProjection "A specialization of #$MapProjection.  Each instance of #$CompositeMapProjection is a map projection in which points on the #$SpheroidalSurface (q.v.) are projected indirectly onto points on the flat target surface (see #$projectionTarget), via an intermediate #$DevelopableSurface (such as a cylinder or cone).
<p>
A #$CompositeMapProjection is actually the composition (see #$FunctionCompositionFn) of two functions: a #$DevelopableProjection, which maps points on the spheroid to a developable surface, and a #$DeveloperProjection, which maps points on the developable surface to a flat surface.
<p>
Cf. #$DirectMapProjection.")
(comment projectionIntermediate "A binary predicate that relates a given #$CompositeMapProjection (q.v.) to a #$DevelopableSurface (q.v.).  <code>(#$projectionIntermediate PROJ SURFACE)</code> means that <code>PROJ</code> uses <code>SURFACE</code> as an intermediate surface in projecting points from its source #$Spheroid (see #$projectionSource) onto its flat target surface (see #$projectionTarget).  Such an intermediate surface is used in those types of projections that are not done directly onto the target surface, but onto another surface (such as a cylindrical or a conic surface) that is subsequently \"flattened\" (or <i>developed</i>) into the target surface.  
<p>
In the above, <code>PROJ</code> is actually the composition (see #$compositionOfFunctions) of a #$DevelopableProjection (q.v.) with a #$DeveloperProjection (q.v.), where <code>SURFACE</code> is the target surface of the former and the source surface of the latter.")
(comment projectionDevelopableComponent "A binary predicate that relates a #$CompositeMapProjection (q.v.) to to its #$DevelopableProjection (q.v.) component, which projects subregions of the source #$Spheroid to an intermediate #$DevelopableSurface.  <code>(#$projectionDevelopableComponent PROJ STAGE1)</code> means that there is some #$DeveloperProjection (q.v.) <code>STAGE2</code> such that <code>PROJ</code> is the functional composition of <code>STAGE1</code> with <code>STAGE2<code>, i.e. <code>(#$compositionOfFunctions PROJ STAGE1 STAGE2)</code>.  <code>STAGE1<code>, in turn, is related to <code>PROJ</code> by the predicate #$projectionDeveloperComponent.")
(comment projectionDeveloperComponent "A binary predicate that relates a #$CompositeMapProjection (q.v.) to to its #$DeveloperProjection (q.v.) component, which projects subregions of the intermediate #$DevelopableSurface to the flat target surface.  <code>(#$projectionDeveloperComponent PROJ STAGE2)</code> means that there is some #$DevelopableProjection (q.v.) <code>STAGE1</code> such that <code>PROJ</code> is the functional composition of <code>STAGE1</code> with <code>STAGE2<code>, i.e. <code>(#$compositionOfFunctions PROJ STAGE1 STAGE2)</code>.  <code>STAGE1<code>, in turn, is related to <code>PROJ</code> by the predicate #$projectionDevelopableComponent.")
(comment PerspectiveProjection "A specialization of #$MapProjection.  Each instance of #$PerspectiveProjection is a map projection such that the points on the #$SpheroidalSurface that is the #$projectionSource are projected from a single point (see #$projectionPointOfOrigin).  If the projection is also a #$DirectProjection (q.v.), the source points are projected directly onto the flat target surface (see #$projectionTarget).  Conversely, if it is a #$DevelopedProjection (q.v.), the source points are initially projected onto an intermediate #$DevelopableSurface (such as the surface of cylinder or a cone; see #$projectionIntermediate) that is subsequently \"flattened\" into the target surface.  #$PerspectiveProjections are also known as \"geometric projections\".")
(comment projectionPointOfOrigin "A binary predicate that relates a given #$MapProjection (q.v.) to the #$Point from which points in the #$projectionSource are projected to either the #$projectionIntermediate (if there is one) or the #$projectionTarget.  <code>(#$projectionPointOfOrigin PROJ ORIGIN)</code> means that in <code>PROJ</code> some or all points on the source surface <code>SOURCE</code> are projected from <code>ORIG</code>.  That is, there is some point <code>SOURCEPOINT</code> in <code>SOURCE</code> is projected to the point <code>TARGETPOINT</code> in the intermediate or target surface (as the case may be) at which that surface intersects the line passing through <code>ORIG</code> and <code>SOURCEPOINT</code>.  If <code>PROJ</code> is a #$PerspectiveProjection (q.v.), then <i>every</i> point in <code>SOURCE</code> is projected in that same way with respect to<code>ORIG</code>.")
(comment TangentProjection "A specialization of #$MapProjection (q.v.) instances of which involve projection to a surface that is tangent to the #$projectionSource (q.v.) at either a point or a line.   That is, the target surface (see #$projectionTarget) -- or, if there is one, the intermediate surface  (see #$projectionIntermediate) -- used in the projection is tangent with the source #$Spheroid (an idealization of the the Earth's surface; see #$projectionSource) at some #$Point or #$Line-Segment in such a way that no part of the target/intermediate surface is inside the #$Spheroid (see #$touches-Externally).
<p>
Specializations of #$TangentProjection include #$EquatorialProjection, #$PolarProjection, and #$ObliqueProjection.  Cf. #$SecantProjection.")
(comment SecantProjection "A specialization of #$MapProjection (q.v.) instances of which involve projection onto a surface that intersects with and \"cuts through\" the #$projectionSource (q.v.).   That is, the target surface (see #$projectionTarget) -- or, if there is one, the intermediate surface  (see #$projectionIntermediate) -- used in the projection is tangent with the source #$Spheroid (an idealization of the the Earth's surface) along one or two #$Ellipses in such a way that some part of the target/intermediate surface is inside the #$Spheroid.
<p>
Cf. #$TangentProjection.")
(comment EquatorialProjection "A specialization of #$TangentProjection (q.v.) instances of which are centered on the equator (see #$equatorOfSpheroid) of the source spheroid.   That is, the target surface (see #$projectionTarget) -- or, if there is one, the intermediate surface  (see #$projectionIntermediate) -- used in the projection is tangent with the source #$Spheroid (an idealization of the the Earth's surface; see #$projectionSource) at the equator, either at a point (as with a direct equatorial projection) or along the entire equator (as with a cylindrical equatorial projection).
<p>
Cf. #$PolarProjection and #$ObliqueProjection.")
(comment antipodesInSpatialThing "A ternary predicate that relates a given spatial thing to a pair of points in it that are in a broad sense \"polar opposites\".  <code>(#$antipodesInSpatialThing POINT1 POINT2 OBJ)</code> holds if and only if <code>POINT1</code> and <code>POINT2</code> are maximally distant points in <code>OBJ</code> with respect to some line that passes through <code>OBJ</code>'s center.  More precisely: (i) <code>POINT1</code> and <code>POINT2</code> are both subsumed (see #$spatiallySubsumes) by <code>OBJ</code>; (ii) <code>POINT1</code>, <code>POINT2</code>, and the centroid (see #$centroidOfObject) <code>CENTER</code> of <code>OBJ</code> are #$collinearPoints on a line <code>LINE</code> such that <code>CENTER</code> lies between the other two points; (iii) there is no point <code>POINT1A</code> in <code>OBJ</code> and on <code>LINE</code> such that <code>POINT1</code> is between <code>CENTER</code> and <code>POINT1A</code>; and (iv) there is no point <code>POINT2A</code> in <code>OBJ</code> and on <code>LINE</code> such that <code>POINT2</code> is between <code>CENTER</code> and <code>POINT2A</code>.
<p>
See also the specialization #$polesOfSpheroid.")
(comment polesOfSpheroid "A ternary predicate that relates a given #$Spheroid to its \"poles\".  <code>(#$polesOfSpheroid POINT1 POINT2 SPHEROID)</code> holds if and only if <code>POINT1</code> and <code>POINT2</code> are the antipodal $#Points at which <code>SPHEROID</code> intersects one of its axes (see #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution).
<p>
Note that if <code>SPHEROID</code> is an #$OblateSpheroid or a #$ProlateSpheroid it has exactly one #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution and thus exactly one pair of poles.  If <code>SPHEROID</code> is a perfect #$Sphere, on the other hand, any #$Line-Straight that passes through its center (see #$centroidOfObject) is an #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution; thus it will have an infinite number of pairs of poles.")
(disjointWith EquatorialProjection ObliqueProjection)
(comment ObliqueProjection "A specialization of #$MapProjection (q.v.) instances of which are centered somewhere between the equator and the poles.  That is,  the target surface (see #$projectionTarget) -- or, if there is one, the intermediate surface  (see #$projectionIntermediate) -- used in the projection is tangent with the source #$Spheroid (an idealization of the the Earth's surface; see #$projectionSource) on either (i) a point that is between the equator and one of the poles or (ii) a line other than the equator that does not pass through the poles that is pole.  
<p>
Cf. #$EquatorialProjection and #$PolarProjection.")
(comment MercatorProjection "A type of map projection and a specialization of both #$CylindricalProjection and #$EquatorialProjection (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$MercatorProjection involves projecting a #$Spheroid onto a cylinder (see #$CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom) that is tangent with the spheroid along the equator (see #$equatorOfSpheroid).  The projection is such that rhumb lines appear as straight lines, and their directions can be measured directly in the map.  Both meridians and parallels are expanded at a ratio equal to the secant of the latitude (with a minor correction made for the oblate-spheroidal shape of the Earth's surface); since the secant of 90 degrees is infinity, the poles cannot be shown.")
(disjointWith MercatorProjection TransverseMercatorProjection)
(comment TransverseMercatorProjection "A type of map projection and a specialization of #$CylindricalProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$TransverseMercatorProjection involves projecting a spheroid to a cylinder that is tangent with the spheroid along a meridian (see #$meridianForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution).  Also known as \"transverse (or inverse) orthomorphic projections\", they are useful for maps covering a wide band of latitude and a relatively short distance on either side of the tangent meridian. 
<p>
Transverse Mercator projections use a ficticious graticule, with a ficticious equator at the tangent meridian; and ficticious poles, meridians, parallels, and so forth are defined accordingly.  The <i>Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) grid </i> is a military grid superimposed upon a transverse Mercator graticule.")
(disjointWith MercatorProjection ObliqueMercatorProjection)
(disjointWith TransverseMercatorProjection ObliqueMercatorProjection)
(comment ObliqueMercatorProjection "A type of map projection and a specialization of #$CylindricalProjection.  Each instance of #$ObliqueMercatorProjection involves projecting a spheroid to a cylinder that is tangent to it along a \"great ellipse\" other than the equator or a meridian.  
<p>
Cf. #$MercatorProjection and #$TransverseMercatorProjection.")
(disjointWith MercatorProjection RectangularProjection)
(disjointWith TransverseMercatorProjection RectangularProjection)
(disjointWith ObliqueMercatorProjection RectangularProjection)
(comment RectangularProjection "A type of map projection and a specialization of #$CylindricalProjection.  Each instance of #$RectangularProjection is similar to a #$MercatorProjection (q.v.) but has uniform spacing of the parallels.  Parallels of latitude(including the equator and poles) are represented by lines of equal length.")
(disjointWith CylindricalProjection ConicProjection)
(comment ConicProjection "A specialization of #$CompositeMapProjection (q.v.).   Each instance of #$ConicProjection is a map projection whose intermediate surface (see #$projectionIntermediate) is a cone or series of cones (see #$ConicalSurface-WithoutBase).  If the cone's axis coincides (cones' axes coincide) with the source #$Spheroid's axis (see #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution),  parallels are represented as arcs of circles, and meridians as lines converging toward the nearer pole (see #$polesOfSpheroid).
<p>
A conic projection that uses a single cone is a #$SimpleConicProjection, and one that uses a series of cones is a #$PolyconicProjection.  Among the simple conic projections, some are such that the cone is tangent with the spheroid only along a single line, and others have the cone tangent with the spheroid along two lines (see #$SecantConicProjection).  Conic projections distort least near the lines of tangency, and distortion is zero at the lines themselves.  If the cone's and the spheroid's axes coincide, the line(s) of tangency is
called a <i>standard parallel</i>.")
(disjointWith PerspectiveProjection SimpleConicProjection)
(comment SimpleConicProjection "A specialization of #$ConicProjection (q.v.).   Each instance of #$SimpleConicProjection is a conic projection whose intermediate surface (see #$projectionIntermediate) is a single cone (cf. #$PolyconicProjection).
<p>
Note that #$CylindricalProjections and #$AzimuthalProjections can be viewed as special cases of simple conic projections, where the height of the cone is infinite or zero, respectively.  (We do not represent them that way here, however.)")
(comment SecantConicProjection "A specialization of #$SimpleConicProjection (q.v.).   Each instance of #$SecantConicProjection is a simple conic projection whose intermediate conical surface (see #$projectionIntermediate) is tangent to the source spheroid along <i>two</i> lines.  Since distortion is low near either of the two lines of tangency, secant conic projections exhibit an increased useful latitude range compared to other simple conic projections.")
(comment LambertConformalProjection "A specialization of #$SecantConicProjection and #$ConformalProjection (qq.v.).   Each instance of #$LambertConformalProjection is a secant conic projection in which the spacing of the parallels on the conical surface is altered so that distortion along them them is equal to that along the meridians.")
(disjointWith ConformalProjection ModifiedLambertConformalProjection)
(comment ModifiedLambertConformalProjection "A specialization of #$SecantConicProjection whose instances are similar to ordinary #$LambertConformalProjections, but have been modified so as to be better suited for representing polar regions.  These are also known as <i>Ney's projections</i>.  Typically, when a conic projection is centered at a pole, the parallels are shown as arcs instead of circles, and the pole is shown as as arc instead of a point.  In a #$ModifiedLambertConformalProjection, a parallel near the pole is chosen as its higher standard parallel; then a little \"stretching\" is done to complete the circles of the parallels and to eliminate the arc of the pole
<p>
A modified Lambert conformal projection is virtually conformal over its entire extent; but since not strictly a #$ConformalProjection it's not suited for highly accurate work.")
(comment PolyconicProjection "A specialization of #$ConicProjection (q.v.).   Each instance of #$PolyconicProjection is a conic projection that uses a <i>series</i> of tangent cones, consisting of a cone tangent with each parallel (or, more precisely, each of those that is some whole number of degrees above or below the equator).
Such projections are free of distortion along any parallel and along the central meridian.  They are used (e.g.) in atlases to show large areas; they are not used in navigation.")
(disjointWith SecantProjection AzimuthalProjection)
(disjointWith ObliqueProjection PolarProjection)
(comment PolarProjection "A specialization of #$AzimuthalProjection (q.v.) instances of which are centered on a pole.  That is, the flat target surface (see #$projectionTarget) used in the projection is tangent with the source #$Spheroid (an idealization of the the Earth's surface; see #$projectionSource) at one of its poles.  #$AzimuthalProjections are often also #$PolarProjections.
<p>
Cf. #$EquatorialProjection and #$ObliqueProjection.")
(comment GnomonicProjection "A specialization of #$AzimuthalProjection and #$PerspectiveProjection (qq.v.).   Each instance of #$GnomonicProjection is a map projection whose #$projectionPointOfOrigin (q.v.) is the center of the source spheroid.")
(disjointWith ConformalProjection ObliqueGnomonicProjection)
(disjointWith EqualAreaProjection ObliqueGnomonicProjection)
(comment ObliqueGnomonicProjection "A specialization of #$GnomonicProjection and #$ObliqueProjection (qq.v.).   In a #$ObliqueGnomonicProjection, all \"great circles\" in the spheroid appear as straight lines in the target surface, and the meridians appear as straight lines converging toward the nearer pole.  They exhibit a large amount of distortion as one moves away from the point of tangency.")
(comment StereographicProjection "A specialization of #$AzimuthalProjection and #$PerspectiveProjection (qq.v.).  These are also known as <i>azimuthal orthomorphic</i> projections.  In a #$StereographicProjection, the origin of the projecting rays (see #$projectionPointOfOrigin) is the point on the spheroid directly opposite the point of tangency with the target surface.  The scale of a stereographic projection increases (and thus, distortion increases) with distance from the point of tangency, but the increase is gradual enough that an entire hemisphere can be shown without excessive distortion.
<p>
Stereographic projections are commonly used for maps of polar regions.  A <i>Universal Polar Stereographic</i> (UPS) grid, mathematically adjusted to the graticule, is used as a reference system.")
(comment PolarStereographicProjection "A specialization of #$StereographicProjection and #$PolarProjection (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PolarStereographicProjection is conformal over its entire exent (see #$ConformalProjection).  Straight lines in the target surface closely approximate great circles on the spheroid.  The scale distortion of these projections is relatively low (but greater than that of #$ModifiedLambertConformalProjections).")
(disjointWith ConformalProjection OrthographicProjection)
(disjointWith EqualAreaProjection OrthographicProjection)
(disjointWith PerspectiveProjection AzimuthalEquidistantProjection)
(comment AzimuthalEquidistantProjection "A specialization of #$AzimuthalProjection that is disjoint with #$PerspectiveProjection.  In a #$AzimuthalEquidistantProjection, the scale of distances is constant along any radial line from the point of tangency.  If the point of tangency is at a pole (see #$PolarAzimuthalEquidistantProjection), then meridians appear as straight lines eminating from the pole, and parallel appear as equally-spaced concentric circles cenered at the pole.  An azimuthal equidistant projection can be used to show the entire spheroid, but the distortion becomes great as the distance from the point of tangency increases.")
(disjointWith ConformalProjection PolarAzimuthalEquidistantProjection)
(comment PolarAzimuthalEquidistantProjection "A specialization of #$AzimuthalEquidistantProjection and #$PolarProjection (qq.v.).  In a #$PolarAzimuthalEquidistantProjection, the scale is constant along the meridians, but not along the parallels.")
(comment Reserve-OutdoorRegion "A
specialization of #$OutdoorLocation and #$PublicProperty.  Each instance of
#$Reserve-OutdoorRegion is an area of land set aside for protection, conservation, or
controlled use of a given resource.")
(comment in-Rooted "A #$ConnectionPredicate and specialization of #$sticksInto (q.v.) that relates certain #$SolidTangibleThings to each other.  <code>(#$in-Rooted OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that some end portion (and not the whole) of <code>OBJ1</code> is embedded in <code>OBJ2</code>.  As a consequence, <code>OBJ1</code> is connected to <code>OBJ2</code> (see #$connectedAtContact), and <code>OBJ2</code> surrounds the embedded portion of <code>OBJ1</code> (see #$surrounds-2D).")
(comment TopologyConcept "#$TopologyConcept is the collection of concepts in Cyc that are germane to the #$Topology-FieldOfStudy.")
(comment OpenGISConcept "#$OpenGISConcept is the collection of all Cyc concepts that correspond to some concept standardized by the Open Geospatial Consortium.")
(comment KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal-VerifyingKBContent "The goal-category of wanting to verify the truth of some portion of the content of a #$KnowledgeBase.")
(comment nad27Latitude "The altitude angle predicate for the #$NAD27CoordinateSystem.")
(comment nad27Longitude "The azimuth angle predicate for the #$NAD27CoordinateSystem.")
(comment nad83Latitude "The altitude angle predicate for the #$NAD83CoordinateSystem.")
(comment nad83Longitude "The azimuth angle predicate for the #$NAD83CoordinateSystem.")
(comment wgs84Latitude "The altitude angle predicate for the #$WGS84CoordinateSystem.")
(comment wgs84Longitude "The azimuth angle predicate for the #$WGS84CoordinateSystem.")
(comment OGCGeometrySymmetricDifferenceFn "#$OGCGeometrySymmetricDifferenceFn is a spec function of
#$SpatialSymmetricDifferenceFn and was introduced exclusively to
be supported by SKSI so that queries using it can be translated
directly into SQL queries using the function
<code>symdifference</code> defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment FrictionProcess "A specialization of #$ForceAffectingAnObjectsMovement.  Each instance of #$FrictionProcess is an event in which at least one of the acting forces (see #$actingForces) isa a #$FrictionVector.  When a #$FrictionProcess occurs it inhibits the relative motion between objects which are in physical contact (see #$touches).  If motion is already happening, the force acts in a direction opposite to motion.  If the motion has not started, as in the case of static friction, the frictional force acts in a direction opposite that of the net applied forces.  Examples include air friction, rolling friction, and mechanical friction.")
(comment ForceVector "A specialization of #$VectorInterval (q.v.). Each instance of #$ForceVector is an n-tuple of intervals (where n > 1), one of which is a direction, and one of which expresses a quantity of force. Like the instances of #$ScalarInterval, the intervals in an instance of #$ForceVector may be point-valued or cover a range of values. The minimal interval (i.e., point-valued) type of vector interval is exemplified by a vector such as \"10 pounds in a horizontal direction\". Vectors may also cover a range of values; e.g. \"between 10 and 12 Newtons in a vertical direction\".")
(comment Celebrity-Entertainment "The collection of instances of #$Celebrity who owe their celebrity primarily due having played a lead or memorable role (or perhaps a large number of roles) in instances of #$TVShow-CW.")
(comment ActingInAMovie "The collection of all instances of #$Acting that are filmed and used in any version of an instance of #$Movie-CW.")
(comment TelevisionStar "The collection of instances of #$ActorOnTV who have achieved celebrity status by virtue of their appearance on television shows.")
(comment PopularMusicStar "The collection of instances of #$Musician who have achieved celebrity due their success as performers of popular music.")
(comment KillingByOrganism-Unique "A specialization of both #$PhysicalDestructionEvent and #$KillingByOrganism.  Each instance of #$KillingByOrganism-Unique consists of one living organism ending (either intentionally or unintentionally) the life of one other living organism.  Events of this type start sometime prior to the death of the individual (We leave it open whether, in some cases, some of the events leading up the the death of the individual are part of the killing event) and ends at the last moment of the individual's death.")
(comment coefficientOfFriction "Friction is typically characterized by a coefficient of friction which is the ratio of the frictional resistance force to the normal force which presses the surfaces together.   <code>(#$coefficientOfFriction OBJ1 OBJ2 NUM)</code> means that <code>NUM</code> is the ratio of the frictional resistance force (see #$FrictionVector) to the normal force which presses the surfaces of <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> together. See also #$coefficientOfFriction-Static and #$coefficientOfFriction-Kinetic.  When coefficients of friction are quoted for specific surface combinations, it is the kinetic coefficient which is generally quoted since it is typically the more reliable number.")
(comment coefficientOfFriction-Static "Static frictional forces from the interlocking of two surfaces work against any relative motion up until some threshold of motion which is characterized by the coefficient of static friction. The coefficient of static friction is typically larger than the coefficient of kinetic friction (see #$coefficientOfFriction-Kinetic).  <code>(#$coefficientOfFriction-Static OBJ1 OBJ2 NUM)</code> means that <code>NUM</code> is the ratio of the static frictional resistance force (see #$FrictionVector) to the normal force which presses the surfaces of <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> together at the threshold of motion.  See also #$coefficientOfFriction.")
(comment SpecificationOfCultureFn "(#$SpecificationOfCultureFn CULTURE-PRACTICE) denotes the
instance of #$Culture-Specification which details the
full set of norms, practices, and beliefs characteristic of
CULTURE-PRACTICE.  (#$SpecificationOfCultureFn CULTURE-PRACTICE) thus
denotes the full set of guidelines that #$cultureParticipants learn
and follow (often unconsciously) while engaging in CULTURE-PRACTICE's
characteristic activities and adopting/maintaining its characteristic
beliefs.  (#$SpecificationOfCultureFn CULTURE-PRACTICE) includes just
those norms, practices, etc., that are adhered to generally within
CULTURE-PRACTICE, and not those restricted to special sub-cultures or
regions.")
(comment specificationOfCulture "(#$specificationOfCulture <code>PRACTICE</code>
<code>SPECIFICATION</code>) means that <code>SPECIFICATION</code>, an
instance of #$Culture-Specification, fully details the set of norms,
practices, and beliefs that are characteristic of
<code>PRACTICE</code>.  See also the corresponding function
#$SpecificationOfCultureFn.")
(comment DrinkingFountain "An instance of #$ArtifactTypeByGenericCategory.  Each instance of #$DrinkingFountain is a device that releases a (usually upward-arcing) stream of water, from which a person can directly drink.")
(comment positiveAmountOf "The instance of #$GenericValuePredicate (q.v.) that corresponds to (see #$correspondingGenericValuePredicate) the generic-value function #$PositiveAmountFn (q.v.).  <code>(#$positiveAmountOf QUANTTYPE QUANTITY)</code> holds just in case <code>QUANTITY</code> is the #$GenericQuantity that corresponds to a <i>positive amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code>: the interval whose minimum value is the same as that of <code>(#$VeryLowAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> and whose maximum value is the maximum possible for <code>QUANTTYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$positiveAmountOf #$Happiness (#$PositiveAmountFn #$Happiness))</code> holds.  And <code>(#$levelOfCompatibility OLDTHING NEWTHING #$positiveAmountOf)</code> holds just in case <code>OLDTHING</code> and <code>NEWTHING</code> are compatible to at least a very low degree.")
(comment HemisphericalSolid "A specialization of #$ThreeDimensionalGeometricThing and #$RoundObject.  Each instance of #$HemisphericalSolid is half of a #$SphericalSolid (q.v.), obtainable by slicing the latter along a great circle.  Examples include tangible objects (such as the halves of a grapefruit) as well as intangible 3-D hemispherical space regions.
<p>
See also the two-dimensional shape-type #$Hemisphere.")
(comment Hemisphere "A specialization of #$Hemispheroid (q.v.). Each instance of #$Hemisphere is a half- #$Sphere.  Any great circle of a given sphere divides it into two hemispheres.
<p>
See also #$HemisphericalSolid and #$GeographicalHemisphere.  Note that, despite the latter collection's name, its instances do not approximate #$Hemispheres as much as they do halves of oblate spheroids (see #$Hemispheroid).")
(comment greatEllipseOfSpheroid "A generalization of the notion of a great circle to sections of #$Spheroids. <code>(#$greatEllipseOfSpheroid ELLIPSE SPHEROID)</code> means that <code>ELLIPSE</code> is a <i>great ellipse</i> of <code>SPHEROID</code>: the former is a #$spheroidalSection of the latter that is centered at the same point as it.  
<p>
Note that, for any spheroid that is not a perfect #$Sphere, exactly one of its great ellipses is a #$Circle: its equator (see #$equatorOfSpheroid).")
(comment greatEllipseOfEllipsoid "A generalization of the notion of a great circle to sections of #$Ellipsoids. <code>(#$greatEllipseOfEllipsoid ELLIPSE ELLIPSOID)</code> means that <code>ELLIPSE</code> is a <i>great ellipse</i> of <code>ELLIPSOID</code>: the former is an #$ellipsoidalSection of the latter that is centered at the same point as it.")
(disjointWith ProperSpheroid Sphere)
(comment ProperSpheroid "A specialization of #$Spheroid (q.v.). Each instance of #$ProperSpheroid is a spheroid that is not a #$Sphere.  Thus its polar radius is different than its equatorial radius (see #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid and #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).  If its polar radius is longer it's a #$ProlateSpheroid; if its equatorial radius is longer it's an #$OblateSpheroid.")
(comment Hemispheroid "A specialization of #$Hemiellipsoid (q.v.). Each instance of #$Hemispheroid is a half- #$Spheroid.  Any great ellipse of a given spheroid (see #$greatEllipseOfSpheroid) divides it into two hemispheroids.
<p>
Note that, despite the collection's name, instances of #$GeographicalHemisphere do not approximate #$Hemispheres (cf.) as much as they do #$Hemispheroids.")
(disjointWith Hemiellipsoid FlatSurfaceRegion)
(comment Hemiellipsoid "A specialization of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing, #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded, and #$SurfaceRegion-Finite (qq.v.). Each instance of #$Hemiellipsoid is a half- #$Ellipsoid.  Any great ellipse of a given ellipsoid (see #$greatEllipseOfEllipsoid) divides it into two hemiellipsoids.")
(comment PolarGnomonicProjection "A specialization of #$GnomonicProjection and #$PolarProjection (qq.v.).   In a #$PolarGnomonicProjection, all \"great ellipses\" in the spheroid (see #$greatEllipseOfSpheroid) appear as straight lines in the target surface.  These projections cannot show an entire hemisphere, as the distance increase goes to infinity at 90 degrees.")
(comment initialSpeedOfPrimaryObjectMoving "<code>(#$initialSpeedOfPrimaryObjectMoving MOVE SPEED)</code> means in the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> where the primary moving object (see #$primaryObjectMoving) is <code>OBJ</code>, at the start of <code>MOVE</code>, <code>SPEED</code> is the speed of <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment directionOfTranslation-Initial "<code>(#$directionOfTranslation-Throughout MOVEMENT INITIAL-DIR)</code> means that the primary moving object in <code>MOVEMENT</code> (see #$primaryObjectMoving) initially moves in the direction <code>DIR</code>.  See also #$directionOfTranslation-Throughout and #$directionOfTranslation-Avg.")
(comment Ground "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion. Each instance of #$Ground is a land surface of a #$PlanetaryBody. Note that an instance of #$Ground is not necessarily a portion of #$EarthsTopographicSurface.  Any #$PlanetaryBody with solid ground has an instance of this collection as part of its surface.")
(comment Cliff "A specialization of #$Slope-Vertical and #$Wall-Vertical.  Each instance of #$Cliff is a very steep, vertical or overhanging face on a mass of rock, earth, or ice.")
(comment genValPredSubsumes "A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a #$GenericValuePredicate to a #$GenericValuePredicate.  <code>(#$genValPredSubsumes GVP1 GVP2)</code> means that <code>GVP1</code> \"subsumes\" <code>GVP2</code> in the following (indirect) sense:
<pre>
(#$implies
  (#$and
    (GVP1 ?QUANT-TYPE ?QUANT-1)
    (GVP2 ?QUANT-TYPE ?QUANT-2))
  (#$quantitySubsumes ?QUANT-1 ?QUANT-2))
</pre>")
(disjointWith EllipticalCurve LinearObject-Straight)
(comment LongitudeLine "A line between the North Pole (see #$AxialNorthPoleOfEarth) and the South Pole (see #$AxialSouthPoleOfEarth), intersecting the #$EarthsEquator at 90 degrees.")
(comment Arc "A specialization of #$EllipticalCurve (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Arc is a continuous proper part of a #$Circle.")
(comment LinearObject-Bounded "A specialization of #$LinearObject (q.v.). #$LinearObject-Bounded is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that have two boundaries or end-points.  They might be straight or curved, tangible or intangible.  All bounded linear objects are finite in length (see #$LinearObject-Finite), as infinite linear objects can have at most one end-point. 
<p>
Specializations of #$LinearObject-Bounded include #$LineSegment-Straight and #$EllipticalCurve, both of whose instances are strictly one-dimensional.
<p>
Cf. #$LinearObject-Unbounded and #$LinearObject-SemiBounded.")
(comment EllipticalCurve "A specialization of both #$LineSegment-Planar and #$LinearObject-Bounded (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$EllipticalCurve is a continuous proper part of an #$Ellipse.  For those that are parts of #$Circles, see the specialization #$Arc.")
(comment Semiellipse "A specialization of #$EllipticalCurve (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Semicircle is a half- #$Ellipse.")
(comment ObjectTopSideFn "A unary #$IndividualDenotingFunction that takes a #$TopAndBottomSidedObject and returns the #$TopSide of that object.  For example (#$ObjectTopSideFn #$SistineChapel) is the top side of the Sistine Chapel.")
(comment MoonOfEarth "#$MoonOfEarth (an instance of #$MoonOfAPlanet) is the only natural satellite of the #$PlanetEarth.")
(comment ObjectBottomSideFn "<code>(#$ObjectBottomSideFn OBJ)</code> represents the #$BottomSide of the tangible object <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment averageSpeedOfPrimaryObjectMoving "<code>(#$averageSpeedOfPrimaryObjectMoving MOVE SPEED)</code> means in the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> where the primary moving object (see #$primaryObjectMoving) is <code>OBJ</code>, <code>SPEED</code> is the average speed of <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment directionOfTranslation-Throughout "<code>(#$directionOfTranslation-Throughout MOVEMENT DIR)</code> means that the primary moving object in <code>MOVEMENT</code> (see the predicate #$primaryObjectMoving) moves in the direction <code>DIR</code> throughout <code>MOVEMENT</code>, so that <code>DIR</code> is the direction of the object moving during each time slice (see #$timeSlices) of <code>MOVEMENT</code>.")
(comment RightSide "A specialization of #$Side.  Each instance of this
collection is the right side (as conventionally understood) of an
object.  See also #$objectRightSide and #$LeftAndRightSidedObject.")
(comment LeftSide "A specialization of #$Side.  Each instance of this
collection is the left side (as conventially understood) of an object.
See also #$objectLeftSide and #$LeftAndRightSidedObject.")
(comment terminalPointOfLineSegment "A binary predicate that relates a #$LineSegment to each of its terminal (or \"end\") #$Points. <code>(#$terminalPointOfLineSegment POINT LINESEGMENT)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is a terminal point of <code>LINESEGMENT</code>.  A given line-segment might have zero, one, or two terminal points.")
(comment startPointOfLineSegment "(#$startPointOfLineSegment POINT LINESEGMENT) means that POINT is the start point of LINESEGMENT. See also #$endPointOfLineSegment.")
(comment endPointOfLineSegment "(#$endPointOfLineSegment POINT LINESEGMENT) means that POINT is the end point of LINESEGMENT. See also #$startPointOfLineSegment.")
(comment symbolizes "<code>(#$symbolizes SYMBOL OBJ)</code> means that the #$SymbolicObject <code>SYMBOL</code> represents the #$Thing <code>OBJ</code> to some interpreting agent.  For example, (#$symbolizes (#$NationalAnthemFn #$Canada) #$Canada) is true because the Canadian national anthem represents Canada to many interpreters (for example, most Canadian citizens). Note that a given instance of #$SymbolicObject may represent different things to agents in different cultures, so that assertions involving #$symbolizes can be highly context-dependent.")
(comment OrthographicProjection "A specialization of #$AzimuthalProjection and #$PerspectiveProjection (qq.v.).  In a #$OrthographicProjection, the #$projectionPointOfOrigin is at infinity, making the projecting rays parallel. 
<p>
If an #$OrthographicProjection is also an #$EquatorialProjection (q.v.), then parallels (see #$parallelOfSpheroid) appear as straight lines and meridians appear as half-ellipses (except the meridian through the point of tangency, which appears straight, and the ones 90 degrees from that, which appear as halves of a circle).
<p>
Orthographic projections are useful for solving the navigational triangle and illustrating celestial coordinates.  Unlike other types of Azimuthal projections, in practice they are never done as #$PolarProjections.")
(comment GravitationalForceVector "A specialization of #$BodyForceVector.  Each instance of #$GravitationalForceVector is a tuple consisting of a direction (see #$Direction) and a magnitude.")
(comment tupleIndexSet "A predicate that relates a given tuple (see #$Tuple) to its \"index set\".  <code>(#$tupleIndexSet TUPLE INDEXSET)</code> means that <code>INDEXSET</code>  indexes <code>TUPLE</code> (see #$memberOfTuple).  That is, each member of <code>TUPLE</code> is associated, via an \"indexing function\" (see #$tupleMemberIndex), with at least one element of <code>INDEXSET</code>, and no element of <code>INDEXSET</code> is associated with more than one member  of <code>TUPLE</code>.  The elements of <code>INDEXSET</code> might be any sort of things whatsoever.   If <code>INDEXSET</code> is the set of all positive integers (or the set of members of  some initial segment thereof), then <code>TUPLE</code> is an <i>ordered n-tuple</i> -- i.e.  an instance of #$List (q.v.).")
(comment CylindricalProjection "A specialization of #$MapProjection (q.v.) instances of which involve projecting a #$Spheroid onto a cylinder (see #$CylindricalSurface-WithoutTopAndBottom) that is tangent with the spheroid along a \"great ellipse\": a #$spheroidalSection centered at the same point as the spheroid.  (Cf. #$greatCircleOfSphere, which only holds of perfect #$Spheres.)  For example, the cylinder might be tangent with the spheroid along the equator or a meridian (see #$equatorOfSpheroid and #$meridianForAxisOfSurfaceOfRevolution).
<p>
Specializations of #$CylindricalProjection include #$MercatorProjection and #$TransverseMercatorProjection.")
(comment oughtToBe-SitRolePlayerOfType "(#$oughtToBe-SitRolePlayerOfType <code>SIT</code> <code>ROLE</code>
<code>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</code>) means that at least one object playing
<code>ROLE</code> in <code>SIT</code> should be an instance of
<code>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</code>. That is, there should be an instance
<code>OBLIGATORY-INS</code> of <code>OBLIGATORY-TYPE</code> such that (<code>ROLE</code>
<code>SIT</code> <code>OBLIGATORY-INS</code>).  Assertions involving this
predicate -- which specify what <i>should</i> be the case -- should be
made in a normative context, e.g., one that reflects the contents of a
particular #$CodeOfConduct.  Usually, <code>SIT</code> will be subject to
such a requirement in virtue of satisfying some general description,
e.g., one stated in the antecedent of a #$CycLRuleAssertion.")
(comment customary-SitRolePlayerOfType "(#$customary-SitRolePlayerOfType <code>SIT</code>
<code>ROLE</code> <code>CUSTOMARY-TYPE</code>) means that it would
follow custom or convention in some culture (see
#$CulturalConvention-Specification) if at least one object playing
<code>ROLE</code> in <code>SIT</code> were an instance of
<code>CUSTOMARY-TYPE</code>. That is, it would follow custom if there
were an instance <code>CUSTOMARY-INS</code> of
<code>CUSTOMARY-TYPE</code> such that (<code>ROLE</code>
<code>SIT</code> <code>CUSTOMARY-INS</code>).  Assertions involving
this predicate should be made in a context that reflects the contents
of some #$IndividualCustom, or larger
#$CulturalConvention-Specification.")
(comment customary-SitRelationToType "(#$customary-SitRelationToType <code>SIT</code>
<code>BIN-PRED</code> <code>CUSTOMARY-TYPE</code>) means that there it
would follow custom or convention in some culture (see
#$CulturalConvention-Specification) if there were an instance
<code>CUSTOMARY-INS</code> of <code>CUSTOMARY-TYPE</code> such that
(<code>BIN-PRED</code> <code>SIT</code> <code>CUSTOMARY-INS</code>).
#$customary-SitRelationToType is a very general requirement predicate
most useful for querying.  Representations of particular conventions
will normally employ specializations of this predicate.  E.g., if
<code>BIN-PRED</code> is an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate, use
#$customary-SitRolePlayerOfType.  If <code>CUSTOMARY-INS</code> is a
type of #$Situation (e.g., one an instance of which should be a
#$subEvents of <code>SIT</code>), use
#$customary-SitRelationToSitType.")
(comment customary-SitRelationToSitType "(#$customary-SitRelationToSitType <code>SIT</code> <code>PRED</code>
<code>CUSTOMARY-SIT-TYPE</code>) means that it would follow
custom or convention in some culture (see #$CulturalConvention-Specification) if <code>SIT</code> were
related by <code>PRED</code> to a situation of
<code>CUSTOMARY-SIT-TYPE</code>.  That is, it would follow custom if
there were an instance <code>CUSTOMARY-INS</code> of
<code>CUSTOMARY-SIT-TYPE</code> such that (<code>PRED</code>
<code>SIT</code> <code>CUSTOMARY-INS</code>).  Assertions involving
this predicate should be made in a context that reflects the contents
of some #$IndividualCustom, or larger
#$CulturalConvention-Specification.")
(comment SpatialSumFn "A binary function that takes a pair of tangible objects, space regions, or spatially-localized events to their mereological sum or union.  (#$SpatialSumFn <b>THING1 THING2</b>) is the unique #$SpatialThing (of the same basic type as <b>THING1</b> and <b>THING2</b>) <b>SUM</b> such that a spatial thing intersects (see #$spatiallyIntersects) <b>SUM</b> if and only if it intersects with either <b>THING1</b> and <b>THING2</b>.  
<p>
Note that \"<i>of the same basic type</i>\" in the above means that the three relata are either all #$PartiallyTangibles, all #$SpaceRegions, or all #$Event-Localizeds.  This restriction is needed in order to ensure the uniqueness of <b>SUM</b>, since a tangible object, a space region, and an event can have the same spatial extent (see #$cospatial), despite their being distinct things.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$spatialSum.  Also see #$SpatialProductFn and #$SpatialDifferenceFn.")
(comment UnitOfConcentration "A specialization of #$UnitOfMeasure. Each instance of #$UnitOfConcentration is a function which returns an instance of #$ConcentrationStrength (q.v.) when applied to a real number (see the collection #$SubLRealNumber) or a sequence of two real numbers. For instance, (#$GramsPerMilliliter 1) is a concentration of 1 gram per milliliter.  Instances of #$UnitOfConcentration include #$GramsPerMilliliter, #$MilligramsPerMilliliter, and #$MicrogramsPerMilliliter.")
(comment Concentration-PartsPerNumber "The collection of #$ConcentrationStrengths expressed in terms of parts per some number.  See e.g. #$PartsPerMillion and #$PartsPerBillion, both of which return instances of #$Concentration-PartsPerNumber.")
(comment UnitOfMeasureWithPrefix "A specialization of #$UnitOfMeasure.  Each instance of #$UnitOfMeasureWithPrefix is a unit-of-measure function that is conventionally denoted by a functor that has a multiplicative prefix, such as 'kilo' or 'centi'.  Cf. #$UnitOfMeasureNoPrefix.  For more explanation, see the shared #$NoteForUnitOfMeasureWithPrefixAndNoPrefix.")
(comment UnitOfMeasureNoPrefix "A specialization of #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.).  Each instance of #$UnitOfMeasureNoPrefix is a unit-of-measure function that is conventionally denoted by a functor that has <i>no</i> multiplicative prefix, such as 'kilo' or 'centi'.  Cf. #$UnitOfMeasureWithPrefix.  For more explanation, see the shared #$NoteForUnitOfMeasureWithPrefixAndNoPrefix.")
(comment UnitOfAbsoluteViscosity "A specialization of #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.).      Each instance of #$UnitOfAbsoluteViscosity is a unit-of-measure function used for measuring dynamic, or absolute viscosity -- the frictional resistance to flow.  Examples include #$Poise-UnitOfAbsoluteViscosity.")
(comment Molarity "A specialization of #$ConcentrationStrength (q.v.). Each instance of #$Molarity is a ratio of the number of moles (see #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit) of some chemical species per liter of a sample substance.")
(comment Concentration-MassPerVolume "A specialization of #$ConcentrationStrength (q.v.) statable in terms of the mass of some type of substance per a certain volume of some mixture.  See e.g. #$GramsPerLiter, which returns instances of #$Concentration-MassPerVolume.")
(comment UnitOfEnergy "A specialization of #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.).  Each instance of this collection is a unit-of-measure function that returns instances of #$EnergyQuantity.  Examples include #$KilowattHour and #$Joule.")
(comment SeriesCircuit "A specialization of #$ElectricalCircuit (q.v.).  Each instance of #$SeriesCircuit is an electrical circuit which is designed to have the same current pass through all the components in the circuit.")
(disjointWith SeriesCircuit ParallelCircuit)
(comment ParallelCircuit "A specialization of #$ElectricalCircuit (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ParallelCircuit is an electrical circuit in which each device is placed in a different branch. The branch lines in a parallel circuit enable the current to  traverse the external circuit in multiple ways.")
(comment voltageSuppliedBySource "(#$voltageSuppliedBySource SOURCE V) means that SOURCE supplies V voltage.")
(comment electricalResistanceOfObject "<code>(#$electricalResistanceOfObject OBJ DEGREE)</code> means that the tangible object <code>OBJ</code> has this <code>DEGREE</code> of #$ElectricalResistance.  A lower value of <code>DEGREE</code> indicates <code>OBJ</code> is a better conductor, i.e., one that conducts with less energy lost to heat.")
(comment BaseOfLandProtrusion "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature. Each instance of #$BaseOfLandProtrusion is the region of land that constitutes the base of a large topographical protrusion, such as a hill or a mountain.")
(comment Hilltop "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$ConvexTangibleObject.  Each instance of #$Hilltop is the region of land that constitutes the top of a hill.")
(comment FPSUnitOfMeasure "An instance of #$UnitOfMeasureTypeBySystem and a specialization of #$USUnitOfMeasure.  Each instance of #$FPSUnitOfMeasure is a #$USUnitOfMeasure that can be given an <i>exact</i> definition in terms of #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure, #$Pound-UnitOfMass, and/or #$SecondsDuration.  Examples include:
<pre>
  #$Mile               = (#$Foot-UnitOfMeasure 5280) 
  #$ShortTon           = (#$Pound-UnitOfMass 2000)
  #$CubicFoot          = (#$UnitProductFn 
                          (#$UnitProductFn #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure)     
                           #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure)      
  #$CubicFootPerSecond = (#$PerFn #$CubicFoot #$SecondsDuration).")
(comment Tablespoon-UnitOfVolume "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  A tablespoon is equal to three teaspoons (see #$Teaspoon-UnitOfVolume), or half a fluid ounce (see #$Ounce-UnitOfVolume.).")
(comment Teaspoon-UnitOfVolume "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  A teaspoon is equal to one-third of a tablespoon (see #$Tablespoon-UnitOfVolume).")
(comment Ounce-UnitOfVolume "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  A fluid ounce is equal to two tablepoons (see #$Tablespoon-UnitOfVolume), and eight fluid ounces is equal to one cup (see #$Cup-UnitOfVolume).")
(comment Cup-UnitOfVolume "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  One cup is equal to eight fluid ounces (see #$Ounce-UnitOfVolume); two cups equal one pint (see #$Pint-US) and four cups equal one quart (see #$Quart-US).")
(comment Pint-US "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  One pint is equal to two cups (see #$Cup-UnitOfVolume), and two pints equal one quart (see #$Quart-US).")
(comment Quart-US "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  One quart is equal to two pints (see #$Pint-US), and four quarts equal one gallon (see #$Gallon-US).")
(comment Gallon-US "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  One gallon is equal to four quarts (see #$Quart-US).")
(comment Minim-UnitOfVolume "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  #$Minim-UnitOfVolume is the smallest US unit of fluid volume.  One fluid minim is equal to one-sixtieth (1/60) of a dram (see #$Dram-UnitOfVolume), or about a drop.")
(comment Foot-UnitOfMeasure "A #$UnitOfDistance and an #$FPSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.). #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure is the basic unit of distance in the British FPS system.  One foot is equal to twelve #$Inches, and three feet equals one yard (see #$Yard-UnitOfDistance).")
(comment Inch "A #$UnitOfDistance and an #$FPSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  Twelve #$Inches is equal to one foot (see #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure).")
(comment Yard-UnitOfDistance "A #$UnitOfDistance and an #$FPSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One yard is equal to three feet (see #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure) or thirty-six #$Inches.")
(comment Furlong "A #$UnitOfDistance and a #$USUnitOfMeasure.  #$Furlong is a unit often used to give distances in horse racing.  One furlong is equal to 220 yards (see #$Yard-UnitOfDistance) or one-eighth (1/8) of a #$Mile.")
(comment Mile "A #$UnitOfDistance and an #$FPSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One mile is equal to 1760 yards (see #$Yard-UnitOfDistance) or 5280 feet (see #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure).")
(comment MinutesDuration "A #$UnitOfTime (q.v.) function that takes one or two real numbers as arguments and returns, as its value, a comparable #$Time-Quantity  (q.v.) measured in minutes.  Thus an expression of the form <code>(#$MinutesDuration NUM)</code> denotes the (\"point-value\") time-quantity of being exactly <code>NUM</code> minutes in duration, and an expression of the form <code>(#$MinutesDuration MIN MAX)</code> denotes the (properly interval-like) time-quantity of being at least <code>MIN</code> minutes and at most <code>MAX</code> minutes in duration.
<p>
See also #$SecondsDuration and #$HoursDuration.")
(comment Ounce "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$FPSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One ounce is equal to one-sixteenth (1/16) of a pound (see #$Pound-UnitOfMass).")
(comment Pound-UnitOfMass "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$FPSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  #$Pound-UnitOfMass is the basic unit of mass in the British (FPS) system.  
(#$Pound-UnitOfMass 1) is the amount of mass possessed by an object weighing one pound (cf. #$Pound-UnitOfForce) at sea-level on the Earth's surface.  One pound is equal to sixteen #$Ounces (q.v.).
<p>
Strictly speaking, the FPS <i>pound</i> is the unit of force, analogous to the #$Newton in the MKS system.  #$Pound-UnitOfMass is introduced here primarily to facilitate interconversions between pounds and kilograms in the terrestrial frame of reference.")
(comment UnitOfMass "A specialization of #$UnitOfMeasure and an instance of #$FundamentalUnitType. Each function in this collection returns instances of #$Mass.  For example, (#$Kilogram 5) is the quantity 5 kilograms.")
(comment Kilogram "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$MKSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One kilogram is equal to 1000 #$Grams, and 1000 kilograms is equal to one #$MetricTon.")
(comment Dram-UnitOfVolume "A #$USUnitOfMeasure and a #$UnitOfVolume (qq.v.).  A fluid dram is equal to one-quarter of a tablepoon (see #$Tablespoon-UnitOfVolume), or one-eighth of a fluid ounce (see #$Ounce-UnitOfVolume).")
(comment Rod-UnitOfDistance "An instance of #$UnitOfDistance and #$USUnitOfMeasure, equal to 5.5 yards (see #$Yard-UnitOfDistance).")
(comment highestPointInRegion "<code>(#$highestPointInRegion GEO POINT)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is the geographical location which has the highest elevation in (the territory of) <code>GEO</code>. For example,  (#$highestPointInRegion #$Canada #$MountLogan) means that #$MountLogan is the  highest point in the territory of #$Canada.")
(comment distanceOfRotation "A predicate that relates a particular rotational motion event to the total angular distance travelled by the moving object (see the predicate #$objectMoving) in that event.  <code>(#$distanceOfRotation ROT DIST)</code> means that in the event <code>ROT</code>, the #$objectMoving travels the angular distance <code>DIST</code>.  Note that <code>DIST</code> is the total angular distance travelled by the #$objectMoving, and not just the net angular displacement of the #$objectMoving. So if a person spins around ten times, then the #$distanceOfRotation in that spinning event is 3600 degrees, not zero.")
(comment terminalSpeedOfPrimaryObjectMoving "<code>(#$terminalSpeedOfPrimaryObjectMoving MOVE SPEED)</code> means in the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> where the primary moving object (see #$primaryObjectMoving) is <code>OBJ</code>, at the end of <code>MOVE</code>, <code>SPEED</code> is the speed of <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment stuckTo "<code>(#$stuckTo OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that either <code>OBJ1</code> or <code>OBJ2</code> has a surface or surfaces covered with some adhesive material, and <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> are held together by that adhesive material.  Moreover, the adhesive force holding <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> together is at least strong enough to support the whole weight of (the lighter of) <code>OBJ1</code> or <code>OBJ2</code>.  Note that if a third object (such as glue) supplies the adhesive force holding <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> together, or if <code>OBJ1</code> or <code>OBJ2</code> were acted on non-trivially (e.g., by welding, not simply contact or pressure), then #$bondedTo (or one of its specializations), rather than #$stuckTo, would relate <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code>.")
(comment connectedTo-Rigidly "<code>(#$connectedTo-Rigidly OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> are joined so that no relative motion between them can occur at the point of connection unless the connection is severed by breakage of some part of the connected objects or by disassembly of the connection.  Further, no relative motion between the contact surfaces of <code>OBJ1</code> nor <code>OBJ2</code> may occur.  Thus, if a nail is rigidly connected to a board, the only way for that relationship to no longer hold is if the nail is pulled out of the hole (e.g. by a hammer or crowbar) or if the nail or board is broken in a critical location.  Note that <code>(#$connectedTo-Rigidly OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> does admit cases in which <code>OBJ1</code> or <code>OBJ2</code> undergo  plastic deformation.  For example, consider the case of a disc rigidly connected via glue (see #$gluedTo) to a rubber sheet.  The rubber sheet and disc may undergo significant relative motion.  Both of the connected objects are solid.")
(comment MetricTon "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$MetricUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One metric ton is equal to 1000 #$Kilograms, or about 2200 pounds.  Cf. #$ShortTon.")
(comment ShortTon "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$FPSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One short ton is equal to 2000 pounds (see #$Pound-UnitOfMass).  Cf. #$MetricTon.")
(comment Milligram "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$MetricUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One milligram is equal to 1/1000 of a #$Gram.")
(comment Grain-UnitOfMass "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$USUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One grain is equal to 1/437.5 of an #$Ounce.")
(comment Ounce-Troy "A #$UnitOfMass and a #$USUnitOfMeasure. #$Ounce-Troy is an apothecary's unit of mass.  A troy ounce is roughly equal to 1.1 avoirdupois ounce (see #$Ounce).  And cf. #$Ounce-UnitOfVolume.")
(comment MillionOuncesTroy "A #$UnitOfMass and a #$USUnitOfMeasure. A #$MillionOuncesTroy is equal to one 1,000,000 troy ounces (see #$Ounce-Troy).  It is used, e.g., to measure the size of a nation's gold reserves.")
(comment AtomicMassUnit "A #$UnitOfMass (q.v.). #$AtomicMassUnit (AMU) is  used for measuring the mass of single atoms. One #$AtomicMassUnit is equal to 1.66054 * 10^-24 #$Grams.  Thus, (#$AtomicMassUnit #$AvogadrosNumber) is equal to one gram.")
(comment Gram "A #$UnitOfMass and an #$CGSUnitOfMeasure (qq.v.).  One gram is equal to 1000 (#$Milli #$Gram)s, and 1000 grams is equal to one #$Kilogram.  Also,  a gram is the mass of Avogadro's number of individual hydrogen atoms:
<pre>
  (#$Gram 1) = (#$AtomicMassUnit #$AvogadrosNumber)</pre>.")
(comment AzimuthalProjection "A specialization of #$DirectMapProjection (q.v.).   Each instance of #$AzimuthalProjection is a map projection whereby points on the source spheroid are mapped directly to a flat target surface tangent to the spheroid, rather than by way of an intermediate #$DevelopableSurface.  They are also known as <i>zenithal</i> projections.  Typically, the target surface wil be tangent to the spheroid at one of its poles (see #$PolarProjection; but cf. #$ObliqueGnomonicProjection).  An azimuthal projection correctly represents the bearing of any point from the point of tangency.  \"Great circles\" through the point of tangency (e.g. the meridians, if the projection is polar) appear as straight lines.")
(comment setOrCollectionUnion2 "A binary predicate that relates a pair of #$SetOrCollections to their union.  <code>(#$setOrCollectionUnion2 UNION CLASS1 CLASS2)</code> entails that, for any thing <code>THING</code>, <code>THING</code> is an #$elementOf <code>UNION</code> if and only if <code>THING</code> is an element of <code>CLASS1</code> or an element of <code>CLASS2</code>.  Furthermore, we stipulate that (i) if both <code>CLASS1</code> and <code>CLASS2</code> are sets (see #$Set-Mathematical) then <code>UNION</code> is a set and (ii) if both <code>CLASS1</code> and <code>CLASS2</code> are #$Collections then <code>UNION</code> is a collection.  If one of them is a set and the other a collection then <code>UNION</code> will either be a set or a collection, though in many cases we might not be able to determine which, and can only conclude that <code>UNION</code> is a #$SetOrCollection.
<p>
Also see #$setOrCollectionUnion and #$setOrCollectionIntersection2.")
(comment neighborhoodOfSetInTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a topological space with base set <code>BASE</code> (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace), <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code>, then <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> is a <i>neighborhood</i> of <code>SET</code> if and only if there exists an open set <code>OPENSET</code> of <code>SPACE</code> which is a subset of <code>NEIGHBORHOOD</code> and contains <code>SET</code> as a subset. In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$neighborhoodOfSetInTopologicalSpace SET NEIGHBORHOOD SPACE)</code>.")
(comment groupRetainsMembershipThroughoutInterval "A #$BinaryPredicate and specialization of #$temporallySubsumes that relates a #$Group to a #$TimeInterval.  (#$groupRetainsMembershipThroughoutInterval GROUP INTERVAL) means that GROUP neither loses nor gains any #$groupMembers at any time subsumed by INTERVAL  In other words, GROUP has the same members throughout INTERVAL.")
(comment setOfGroupMembers "(#$setOfGroupMembers GROUP SET) means that SET is the set containing all and only members of GROUP.  (Note that this predicate may relate a group to different sets at different times, though it not relate a group to more than one set at any given time.)  This is the predicate corresponding to #$SetOfGroupMembersFn (q.v.).")
(disjointWith Hilltop BaseOfLandProtrusion)
(disjointWith BaseOfLandProtrusion Summit)
(comment NaturalFeatureType "#$NaturalFeatureType is the collection of
#$CartographicFeatureTypes which naturally occur on the surface of the
Earth. Positive examples include #$Mountain, #$Ocean, and #$Mesa.
Negative examples include #$WaterReservoir, #$Canal, and #$Airfield. Also
see #$ArtifactualFeatureType, the corresponding collection for feature
types which are #$Artifacts.")
(comment accomplishmentMessage "A sentence of the form (#$accomplishmentMessage EVENT-TYPE MESSAGE) is used to relate an #$AccomplishmentType to a #$CharacterString.  When a #$ComputerProcessRunning, which involves a copy of some sub-collection #$CycProgram,  includes the successful peformance of some #$AccomplishmentType, then MESSAGE is an appropriate message for the process to display to the user.")
(comment CreatingASpecificEBMTTemplate "An #$AccomplishmentType and a sub-collection of #$CreatingAnEBMTTemplate. #$CreatingASpecificEBMTTemplate is the collection of all events in which a computer process successfully generates a specific template for translating one particular sentence from one #$Language to another.")
(comment CreatingAGeneralEBMTTemplate "An #$AccomplishmentType and a sub-collection of #$CreatingAnEBMTTemplate. #$CreatingAGeneralEBMTTemplate is the collection of all events in which a computer process successfully generates a template (with place-holders or variables) for translating sentences that are similarly constructed, from one #$Language to another.")
(comment AssertingAnEBMTExample "An #$AccomplishmentType and a sub-collection of #$ComputerProcessRunning.  #$AssertingAnEBMTExample is the collection of all events in which a computer process successfully asserts an NL and CycL sentence pair as an EBMT training example using #$ebmtNLToCycLTrainingExample.")
(comment CreatingAnEBMTTemplate "An #$AccomplishmentType and a sub-collection of both #$LanguageProcessing and #$ComputerProcessRunning.  #$CreatingAnEBMTTemplate is the collection of all events in which a computer process successfully generates a template for translating from one #$Language to another.")
(comment DockWorker "A specialization of #$UnskilledWorker.  Instances are people who load and unload frieght from docked ships.")
(comment Dishwasher-Occupation "A subcollection of #$UnskilledWorker.  Instances are people who wash dishes.")
(comment HostedSocialGathering "A subcollection of #$SocialGathering.
#$HostedSocialGathering is the collection of social gatherings which
have both a #$hostOfEvent and one or more guests
(cf., #$guestAtSocialGathering).  Each instance of
#$HostedSocialGathering thus has a 'hosting' sub-event -- see
#$HostingASocialGathering and #$hostedGathering.")
(comment hostedGathering "<code>(#$hostedGathering HOSTING GATHERING)</code> identifies <code>GATHERING</code> as the instance of #$HostedSocialGathering that is hosted in <code>HOSTING</code>, an instance of #$HostingASocialGathering.")
(comment CumulativeStuffType "A #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$ExistingStuffType (q.v.).  Each instance <code>CUMULTYPE</code> of #$CumulativeStuffType is a collection of #$PartiallyTangible objects that has the following property of <i>cumulativity</i>: if <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> are instances of <code>CUMULTYPE</code>, then their spatial sum (#$SpatialSumFn <code>OBJ1 OBJ2</code>) is also an instance of <code>CUMULTYPE</code>.")
(comment ownsDuring "(#$ownsDuring AGT OBJ TEMP-THING) states that AGT owns OBJ during the temporal extent of TEMP-THING.")
(comment Semicircle "A specialization of #$Arc and #$Semiellipse (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Semicircle is a half- #$Circle.")
(disjointWith Hunter HumanPreSchoolageChild)
(comment Hunter "The collection of people who hunt.")
(comment officialArmedForces "The predicate #$officialArmedForces relates a particular organization to its armed forces.  In most all of the cases the organization will be a country, but in certain exceptional circumstances, the organization could be other than a country.   (#$officialArmedForces COUNTRY FORCE) means that FORCE is the armed forces of COUNTRY.")
(comment creationTemplateAssertions "(#$creationTemplateAssertions TEMPLATE ASSERTION) means that ASSERTION is one of the assertions that forms the instance of #$CycCreationTemplate, TEMPLATE. This means that the code-level function associated with TEMPLATE (see #$creationTemplateForSubLFunction) is responsible for adding an analogous assertion to the KB.  The assertion is analgous in the following way:  Let ASSERTION = (ARG-0 ARG-1 ... ARG-N).  For any arg ARG in ARG-0 - ARG-N that is known to be an exemplar FORT for TEMPLATE (see #$creationTemplateFORTs), an analogous assertion will be one just like ASSERTION, but with ARG replaced by an appropriately analogous FORT reified during the call to that code-level function.")
(comment JustificationDisplayCreationTemplate "A creation template for new instances of #$DisplayingACycQueryJustification in the stylized way they are created by the CAE.")
(comment AngularMomentumVector "A #$VectorIntervalType.  An #$AngularMomentumVector is the analog to #$MomentumVector in cases where the motion of an object is an instance of #$Movement-Rotation, as opposed to a #$Movement-TranslationEvent.")
(comment RotationalInertia-Value "A #$QuantityType and a sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantity.  A #$RotationalInertia-Value represents an object's resistance to rotational change.")
(comment UnaryPolynomialFunction "A sub-collection of #$PolynomialFunction and #$UnaryFunction.  Each instance of this collection is a polynomial function with exactly one variable.  For example, f(x) = 3x - 2  and g(x) = x^2 + 4x +3  are both instances of #$UnaryPolynomialFunction.")
(comment UnaryPolynomialOfDegreeWithCoefficientsFn "An expression of the form (#$UnaryPolynomialOfDegreeWithCoefficientsFn DEGREE LIST) denotes a #$UnaryPolynomialFunction of degree DEGREE (that is the highest power to which the variable is raised), where the Nth element of list is the coefficient of the term in function that is raised to the power N-1.  For instance, an expression of the form (#$UnaryPolynomialOfDegreeWithCoefficientsFn 2 (TheList 3 4 1) could be written out as f(x) = x^2 + 4x + 3.  This function abstracts away from the actual choice of variables in the formula.")
(comment ConstantPolynomialFunction "A sub-collection of #$PolynomialFunction, each of which returns exactly one value. For example, f(x) = 3")
(comment LinearPolynomialFunction "A sub-collection of #$PolynomialFunction, each of which returns has a degree of 1.")
(comment QuadraticPolynomialFunction "A sub-collection of #$PolynomialFunction, each of which has a degree of 2.")
(comment ExponentiallyDecreasingFunction "A sub-collection of #$NetDecreasingFunction, each instance of this function decays at an exponential rate.")
(comment ExponentiallyIncreasingFunction "A sub-collection of #$NetIncreasingFunction, each instance of this function increases at an exponential rate.")
(comment unaryPolynomialOfDegreeWithCoefficients "An expression of the form (#$unaryPolynomialOfDegreeWithCoefficients FUNCTION DEGREE LIST) states that the #$UnaryPolynomialFunction FUNCTION is of degree DEGREE (that is the highest power to which the variable is raised) and the (#$ListOfTypeFn #$RealNumber) where the Nth element of list is the coefficient of the term in function that is raised to the power N-1.  For instance, an expression of the form (#$UnaryPolynomialOfDegreeWithCoefficientsFn 2 (#$TheList 3 4 1)) could be written out as f(x) = x^2 + 4x + 3.  This function abstracts away from the actual choice of variables in the formula.")
(comment ImaginaryNumber "A specialization of #$ComplexNumber (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ImaginaryNumber is a number of the type <b><i>a</i>+i<i>b</i></b>, where: (1) <b>i</b> is the square root of negative one (see #$I-TheNumber), (2) <b><i>a</i></b> and <b><i>b</i></b> are real numbers (see #$RealNumber), and (3) <b><i>b</i></b> is non-zero.")
(comment I-TheNumber "<i>i</i> (usually spelled lower-case)
is the square root of negative 1. It is an element of
#$ImaginaryNumber.")
(comment TranscendentalNumber "A specialization of #$IrrationalNumber.  A #$RealNumber, <code>x<sub>T</sub></code>, is a #$TranscendentalNumber if and only if for all <code>n>0</code> <code>x<sub>T</sub></code> is not the solution to any polynomial equation of the form
<pre>
a<sub>n</sub>x<sup>n</sup> + a<sub>n-1</sub>x<sup>n-1</sup> + ... + a<sub>1</sub>x + a<sub>0</sub> = 0
</pre>
where the <code>a<sub>i</sub></code> are #$Integers. Cf. #$AlgebraicNumber.
<p>
#$TranscendentalNumbers are important in the history of mathematics because their investigation provided the first proof that it is impossible using only a straightedge and compass to construct a #$Square of equal area to a given #$Circle. The impossibility of such a construction follows from the proof that <pre>(#$isa #$Pi-Number #$TranscendentalNumber).")
(comment AlgebraicNumber "A #$RealNumber, <code>x<sub>A</sub></code>, is an #$AlgebraicNumber if and only if it is the solution to some polynomial equation of the form
<pre>
a<sub>n</sub>x<sup>n</sup> + a<sub>n-1</sub>x<sup>n-1</sup> + ... + a<sub>1</sub>x + a<sub>0</sub> = 0
</pre>
where the <code>a<sub>i</sub></code> are #$Integers. The smallest <code>n</code> for which <code>x<sub>A</sub></code> is the solution to some polynomial equation of order <code>n</code> is called the <i>degree</i> of <code>x<sub>A</sub></code>. Cf. #$TranscendentalNumber.")
(comment PrimeNumber "A specialization of #$PositiveInteger.  An integer <code>N</code> is a  #$PrimeNumber if and only if it is greater than 1 and its only integral divisors are 1 and <code>N</code> itself.")
(comment OddNumber "A specialization of #$Integer.  An integer is an #$OddNumber if and only if it is <i>not</i> divisible by 2 without remainder.  It might be positive or negative.")
(disjointWith ScalarNullValue (CollectionDifferenceFn RealNumber ScalarNullValue))
(disjointWith NonZeroNumber ScalarNullValue)
(comment Zero "The number zero, the lowest #$NonNegativeInteger.  This is not to be confused with #$0-TheDigit, which is the character '0'.")
(comment MoneyPerVolumeRatio "A specialization of #$MoneyRatio (q.v.).  Each instance of #$MoneyPerVolumeRatio is an amount of #$MonetaryValue (q.v.) taken with respect to an amount of #$Volume.  Examples include (#$DollarsPerGallon 2.25) and (#$EurosPerLiter 5).")
(comment UnitOfRate "A specialization of #$UnitOfRatio (q.v.).  Each instance of #$UnitOfRate is a function that returns an instance of #$Rate (q.v.) when applied to a real number (or pair of real numbers).  A given unit-of-rate <code>RATEUNIT</code> is the result of \"dividing\" some unit-of-measure <code>UNIT</code> by some #$UnitOfTime <code>TIMEUNIT</code>.  That is:
<pre> 
   <code>RATEUNIT</code> = (#$PerFn  <code>UNIT TIMEUNIT </code>) 
</pre>
Specializations of #$UnitOfRate include #$UnitOfSpeed, #$UnitOfFrequency, and #$UnitOfPower.")
(comment DollarsPerGallon "A #$UnitOfRatio (q.v.) function that is used to specify the cost of something in terms US dollars (see #$Dollar-UnitedStates) per US gallon (see #$Gallon-US).  For example, (#$DollarsPerGallon 2) is the #$MoneyPerVolumeRatio (q.v.) <i>two-dollars-per-gallon</i>.")
(comment EurosPerLiter "A #$UnitOfRatio (q.v.) function that is used to specify the cost of something in terms of #$Euros per #$Liter.  For example, (#$EurosPerLiter 2) is the #$MoneyPerVolumeRatio (q.v.) <i>two-euros-per-liter</i>.")
(comment DollarsPerSquareFoot "A #$UnitOfRatio (q.v.) function that is used to specify the cost of something in terms US dollars (see #$Dollar-UnitedStates) per #$SquareFoot.  For example, (#$DollarsPerSquareFoot 2) is the #$MoneyRatio (q.v.) <i>two-dollars-per-square-foot</i>.")
(comment DollarsPerHour "An instance of #$UnitOfMonetaryRate. When applied to a number or pair of numbers, #$DollarsPerHour returns an instance of #$MonetaryRate.  For example, (#$DollarsPerHour 20) is a monetary rate of 20 US dollars per hour.")
(comment DollarsPerPound "A #$UnitOfRatio (q.v.) function that is used to specify the cost of something in terms US dollars (see #$Dollar-UnitedStates) per pound (see #$Pound-UnitOfMass).  For example, (#$DollarsPerPound 6) is the #$MoneyPerMassRatio (q.v.) <i>six-dollars-per-pound</i>.")
(comment vectorDirection "<code>(#$vectorDirection VECTOR DIRECTION)</code> means that the direction of the #$VectorInterval <code>VECTOR</code> is the #$Direction <code>DIRECTION</code>. See also #$vectorMagnitude.")
(comment VectorIntervalSlot "A #$PredicateType and specialization of #$BinaryPropertyPredicate.  Each instance of #$VectorIntervalSlot is a predicate that is used to attribute a property to a thing by relating it to a #$VectorInterval (q.v.) of some sort.
<p>
Instances of #$VectorIntervalSlot include #$velocityOfObject, #$facesDirection, and #$angularMomentum.")
(comment GlassBoxAllotmentCreationTemplate "A creation template for new instances of #$GlassBoxAllotment in the stylized way they are created by the CAE.")
(comment ParliamentBuilding "The collection of instances of #$GovernmentalBuilding wherein instances of #$Parliament gather to do legislative business.")
(comment newsSubject "<code>(#$newsSubject NEWS SUBJECT)</code> means that <code>SUBJECT</code> is a subject of <code>NEWS</code>, an instance of #$News.  <code>SUBJECT</code> will either be a situation, or a constituent (#$situationConstituents) of a situation that is also a subject of <code>NEWS</code>.")
(comment DairyBeverageDeliveryProfessional "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation and specialization of #$FoodDeliveryPerson.  This is the collection of people, traditionally males described as \"milkmen,\" who deliver bottles of milk to people's doorstep each morning.")
(comment objectsDelivered "<code>(#$objectsDelivered DELIVERY OBJECT)</code> means that the instance <code>OBJECT</code> of #$PartiallyTangible is delivererd in <code>DELIVERY</code>, an instance of #$DeliveringSomeoneSomething.")
(comment ebmtNLToCycLTrainingExample "<code>(#$ebmtNLToCycLTrainingExample STRING (#$Quote CYCL))</code> means that <code>CYCL</code> is a (quoted) instance of #$CycLExpression that is an acceptable parsing target for the NL string <code>STRING</code>, for purposes of training the Cyc Example-Based Machine Translation system.")
(comment PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction "A sub-collection of #$IndividualDenotingFunction.  #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction  is a collection of functions that are used to denote the properties of objects within a physical system/event (e.g., a ball's velocity, acceleration or displacement along an X axis).  The various sub-collections of these functions differ based on the number of input values which their instances take as part of determining their output values.  For instance, #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction-SingleInput is a collection of functions that denote an object's properties during a physical event in cases where the function's output value depends on a single input value.")
(comment PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction-SingleInput "A sub-collection of #$TernaryFunction and #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction.  These functions are used to represent the properties of objects during a physical system/event (e.g., a ball's velocity, acceleration or displacement along an X axis) when those properties depend on some other quantity that is being measured within the system (e.g., elapsed time).")
(comment DistanceFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType.  Each instance of #$DistanceFunction function is used to denote a #$Distance.")
(comment VelocityFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType. Each instance of #$VelocityFunction is used to denote a #$LinearVelocityVector.")
(comment LinearMomentumFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType. Each instance of #$VelocityFunction is used to denote a #$LinearVelocityVector.")
(comment AngularVelocityFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType. Each instance of #$VelocityFunction is used to denote a #$LinearVelocityVector.")
(comment AngularMomentumFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType. Each instance of #$VelocityFunction is used to denote a #$LinearVelocityVector.")
(comment ForceFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction.  Each instance is an abstract representation of some force within a physical system and will return instances of #$ForceVector.")
(comment GravitationalForceFunction "A sub-collection of #$ForceFunction, each instance represents a force that is due to the gravitational attaction between several massive objects.")
(comment CentripetalForceFunction "A sub-collection of #$ForceFunction, each instance represents a force that acts on some object to keep it moving along some curved trajectory.")
(comment eventForceFunctions "A sentence of the form (#$eventForceFunctions EVENT FORCE) states that within the #$PhysicalSituation SIT, the #$ForceFunction FORCE describes a force that affects some of the #$PartiallyTangible entities that are constituents of SIT.")
(comment functionDescribesThingDuring "A sentence of the form (#$functionDescribesThingDuring FUNCTION OBJECT EVENT) means that the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction FUNCTION describes some property (e.g., velocity, position along the x axis, etc.) of the #$PartiallyTangible OBJECT during the #$Event EVENT.")
(comment functionDescribesForceOnObjectDuring "An assertion of the form (#$functionDescribesForceOnObjectDuring FORCE-FUNC OBJ SIT) states that relative to the #$PhysicalSituation SIT, the #$ForceFunction FORCE-FUNC describes a force that is acting on the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ.")
(comment valueOfDuringForQuantity "A sentence of the form (#$valueOfDuringForQuantity FUNC THING EVENT IN-QUANT OUT-QUANT) states that the property of the #$Thing THING that is denoted by the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction-SingleInput during the #$Event EVENT, has a value of  OUT-QUANT, some determining #$Quantity, when the input quanity to FUNC is the #$Quantity IN-QUANT.")
(comment Optician "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  An optician is someone who builds or repairs devices to enhance vision.  Commonly, this involves the building of eyeglass lenses to fill a presciption written by an optometrist (see #$Optometrist) or opthamologist (see #$Ophthalmologist), but also includes constructing telescopic lenses and other vision-enhancing devices.")
(disjointWith DevicePrescription DrugPrescription)
(comment FillingACorrectiveLensPrescription "The collection of all instances of #$FillingADevicePrescription in which the prescription filled is a prescription for one or more instances of #$CorrectiveLens.  See also #$CorrectiveLensPrescription -- the type of prescription that every instance of this collection fills.")
(comment FocalFieldOfStudyFn "<code>(#$FocalFieldOfStudyFn TYPE)</code> denotes the instance of #$FieldOfStudy that, by definition, is the focal field for which instances of <code>TYPE</code> have received their training. For example, <code>(#$FocalFieldOfStudyFn #$Gynecologist)</code> is the field of study known as 'gynecology'.")
(comment CorrectiveLensPrescription "The collection of instances of #$MedicalPrescription that are prescriptions for corrective lenses (c.f., #$CorrectiveLens).")
(comment DevicePrescription "The collection of prescriptions (see #$MedicalPrescription) requesting that a patient receive and use a device intended to help him or her overcome a physiological condition.  See also #$DisabilityAidEquipment.")
(comment MedicalPrescription "The collection of prescriptions -- orders from doctors that authorize that a patient to receive and use a substance or device that will help them treat or overcome a physiological condition.")
(comment functionFirstVariableDependsOnPropertyForEvent "A sentence of the form (#$functionFirstVariableDependsOnPropertyForEvent FUNC THING PROPERTY-FUNCTION EVENT) means that the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction  FUNC is dependent on the property of the #$Thing THING denoted by the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction  PROPERTY-FUNCTION when it is calculated relative to the #$Event EVENT.  More specifically, the formula that represents the transformation of the input into the output has a 'first' variable (see #$functionPolynomialFunctionForSit and #$functionFirstInputUnitOfMeasureForSit) and the values that are plugged into this are based on the associated property of THING that is denoted by  PROPERTY-FUNCTION.")
(comment functionFirstInputUnitOfMeasureForSit "A sentence of the form (#$functionFirstInputUnitOfMeasureForSit FUNCTION UNIT SITT) means that the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction  FUNCTION takes as its 'first' input a value that is measured in UNIT, some #$UnitOfMeasure relative to the #$PhysicalSituation SIT. The notion of 'first' here is intended only to allow for knowledge about the function to be shared across the predicates such as #$functionFirstInputValue.")
(comment functionOutputUnitOfMeasureForSit "A sentence of the form (#$functionOutputUnitOfMeasureForSit FUNCTION UNIT SIT) means that the result of the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction  FUNCTION is measured in the #$UnitOfMeasure UNIT relative to the #$PhysicalSituation SIT.")
(comment PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction-ConstantValue "A #$FunctionType and a sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction.  Each instance of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction-ConstantValue describes some physical property of some element of a system during some specific instance of #$PhysicalSituation.  What separates this class is that the functions return a constant value.")
(comment functionConstantVectorDuringSit "A sentence of the form (#$functionConstantVectorDuringSit FUNCTION VECTOR SIT) means that the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction-ConstantValue denotes only the #$VectorInterval VECTOR relative to the #$PhysicalSituation SIT.")
(comment functionConstantQuantityDuringSit "A sentence of the form (#$functionConstantVectorDuringSit FUNCTION VALUE SIT) means that the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction-ConstantValue denotes only the #$PhysicalQuantity VECTOR relative to #$PhysicalSituation SIT.")
(comment SimpleVector "The collection of all instances of #$VectorInterval that whose dimensions are measured using #$ScalarPointValue.  These are best understood are syntactic / symbolic representations that are useful for mathematical manipulation but which gain full meaning when interpreted relative to incorporated into a larger semantic unit that associate them with a specific #$CoordinateSystem.")
(comment BinarySimpleVector "The collection of all instances of #$SimpleVector that have two dimensions and whose dimensions are measured using #$ScalarPointValue.")
(comment BinarySimpleVectorFn "An expression of form (#$BinarySimpleVectorFn N M) denotes a #$BinarySimpleVector whose value in the first dimension is the #$ScalarPointValue N and whose value in the second dimension is the #$ScalarPointValue M.")
(comment binarySimpleVectorWithDimensions "A sentence of the form (#$binarySimpleVectorWithDimensions VECTOR DIM-1-VALUE DIM-2-VALUE) states that VECTOR is the #$BinarySimpleVector whose first dimension has the #$ScalarPointValue DIM-1-VALUE and whose second dimension has the #$ScalarPointValue DIM-2-VALUE.")
(comment angularDistanceBetweenVectors "A sentence of the form (#$angularDistanceBetweenVectors VECT1 VECT2 ANGLE) means that the instances of #$SimpleVector VECT1 and VECT2 have the #$AngularDistance ANGLE between them when they are interpreted as having a common origin in the same #$CoordinateSystem.")
(comment correspondingCoordinateSystem "A sentence of the form (#$correspondingCoordinateSystem SIT SYSTEM) means that, relative to a particular context, constitutents of the #$PhysicalSituation SIT (and their properties) can be modeled relative to the #$CoordinateSystem SYSTEM.")
(comment pointIsOriginForAssociatedCoordinateSystem "A sentence of the for (#$pointIsOriginForAssociatedCoordinateSystem POINT SIT SYSTEM) means that the #$Point POINT is, relative to a particular context, corresponds to the origin of the #$CoordinateSystem SYSTEM when SYSTEM is used for representing the constituents of the #$PhysicalSituation SIT.")
(comment HangingFromSituation "A sub-collection of #$ContinuousPhysicalContactSituation.  Each instance of #$HangingFromSituation is a physical arrangement in which one object acts as a #$hangingFixedPoint from which some other objects hang (see #$hangsFromDuring).  This involves the configuration only and is not meant to represent that there is no movement.  Thus, the physical arrangement of a swinging pendulum would be an instance of this collection.")
(comment hangingFixedPoint "A sentence of the form (hangingFixedPoint HANGING OBJECT) means that the #$HangingFromSituation HANGING has the #$PartiallyTangible OBJECT as the fixed point from which some other object hangings.  So, if some #$Mistletoe hangs from a #$Nail, that #$Nail would fill the fole of the #$hangingFixedPoint in that #$HangingFromSituation.")
(comment hangsFromDuring "An sentence of the form (#$hangsFromDuring  HANGING OBJ1 OBJ2) means that during the #$HangingFromSituation HANGING, the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ1 is suspended from the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ2 throughout HANGING.")
(comment TurningInACircle "A sub-collection of #$TurningAlongAnArc, each instances is an event where the path that the #$primaryObjectMoving approximates a #$Circle.")
(comment radiusOfTurn "A sentence of the form (#$radiusOfTurn TURNING RADIUS) is used to describe the distance between the entity that is the #$primaryObjectMoving in TURNING, an instance of #$TurningInACircle and the point that represents the axis of rotation around which the object is moving.")
(comment touchesAtDuring "A sentence of the form (#$touchesAtDuring OBJ1 OBJ2 POINT EVENT) states that during the #$ContinuousPhysicalContactSituation EVENT, the two instances of #$PartiallyTangible OBJ1 and OBJ2 are in physical contact throughout EVENT and the #$Point POINT is subsumed by that region of contact .")
(comment uniqueProperSubSituationTypes "A specialization of #$properSubSituationTypes. (#$uniqueProperSubSituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUB-SIT-TYPE) means that SIT-TYPE has exactly one specialization of #$Situation SUB-SIT-TYPE as a proper subsituation type.  #$uniqueProperSubSituationTypes is the #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical for #$ProperSubSituationTypeFn.")
(comment SkilledPerson "The collection of instances of #$Person who exhibit or have acquired a high level of skill in an area, where having such skills is generally seen as valuable or the obtaining of such is generally accepted as a mark of high achievement.")
(comment NonUniformedFighter "A specialization of #$Warrior.  Instances are people engaged in some form of #$War, who, in order to facilitate access to targets with minimal detection, and sometimes to create levels of mistrust and fear among enemy soldiers and the surrounding civilian population, dress in civilian clothing as they go about the business of carrying out attacks.")
(comment inheritRolePlayers-SubSitTypeToSitType "'(#$inheritRolePlayers-SubSitTypeToSitType SIT-TYPE ROLE1 SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE2) means that in any instance SIT of SIT-TYPE whatever things play ROLE2 in sub-situations of SIT that are instances of SUBSIT-TYPE also play ROLE1 in SIT.")
(comment EnteringAResidence "A subcollection of #$EnteringAnHOC.  In each instance of
#$EnteringAResidence, one or more agents enter a #$HumanResidence.")
(comment spatiallyIntersects "<code>(#$spatiallyIntersects THING1 THING2)</code> means that the spatial extent of the #$SpatialThing <code>THING1</code> overlaps the spatial extent of the #$SpatialThing <code>THING2</code>.  Note that spatial intersection doesn't imply that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> have any physical parts (see the predicate #$physicalParts) in common. For example, an instance of #$IntangibleIndividual, such as a shadow, can spatially intersect an instance of #$PartiallyTangible, such as a wall; in such a case, the instance of #$IntangibleIndividual will obviously not share any physical part with the instance of #$PartiallyTangible.")
(comment Galaxy "A specialization of (#$MobFn #$Star) and  #$AstronomicalObject.  Each instance of #$Galaxy is an enormous group of stars that is relatively isolated from other large groups of stars.")
(comment SteelMillWorker "The collection of factory workers who work at at steel mill.")
(comment StandupComedian "The collection of instances of #$Comedian who perform standup routines at nightclubs.")
(comment StreetVendor "The collection of instances of #$SalesPerson who pitch and sell their wares at an instance of #$RetailBusinessStand.")
(comment Goatherd "The collection of people who herd goats.")
(comment herdTypeOfType "<code>(#$herdTypeOfType HERD-TYPE ANIMAL-TYPE)</code> means that <code>HERD-TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$Herd, is the collection of all herds comprised of animals of type <code>ANIMAL-TYPE</code>.")
(comment SteelMill-ManufacturingFacility "A specialization of #$HeavyIndustryFacility.  This is the collection of plants where steel is manufactured.")
(comment Bazaar-Marketplace "A type of #$Marketplace-Object that is very common in the middle east.")
(comment inheritRolePlayer-SitTypeToDistinctSubSitType "(#$inheritRolePlayer-SitTypeToDistinctSubSitType
<code>SIT-TYPE</code> <code>ROLE </code> <code>SUB-TYPE</code>
<code>SUB-ROLE</code>) means that, for each instance
<code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> and each object
that plays <code>ROLE</code> in
<code>SIT</code> (<code>ROLE-PLAYER</code>), there exists a distinct instance of <code>SUB-TYPE</code> which is a sub-situation 
of <code>SIT</code> and in which <code>ROLE-PLAYER</code>
plays <code>SUB-ROLE</code>.  In
other words, there exists some sub-situation of <code>SUB-TYPE</code> such that <code>ROLE-PLAYER</code> plays <code>SUB-ROLE</code> in that sub-situation, and no other
player of <code>ROLE</code> in <code>SIT</code> plays <code>SUB-ROLE</code> in that
sub-situation.")
(disjointWith Team BranchOfMilitaryService)
(comment TribalLeader "The collection of all people who are leaders of some instance of #$Tribe.")
(comment Vandal "An instance of #$CriminalTypeByCrimeType.  Every instance of #$Vandal is a person who has committed an act of vandalism (see #$Vandalism).")
(comment associatedCriminalActivity "<code>(#$associatedCriminalActivity CRIMINAL-TYPE CRIME-TYPE)</code> means that <code>CRIMINAL-TYPE</code>, an instance of #$CriminalTypeByCrimeType, is the collection of instances of #$Criminal who have perpetrated an act of <code>CRIME-TYPE</code>, a type of #$ActsCommonlyConsideredCriminal.")
(comment interestingForArgOfTypeInReln "A #$SummaryPredicate. (#$interestingForArgOfTypeInReln N COL PRED) means that for any assertion ASSERTION of the form (RELN . ARGS) such that (#$genlPreds RELN PRED) and (#$assertionSentence ASSERTION SENTENCE), (#$interestingSentence THING SENTENCE) will be true for the Nth argument ARG-N of SENTENCE, <i><b>provided that ARG-N is an instance of COL</b></i>.
<p>
For example, if (#$interestingForArgOfTypeInReln 1 #$Politician #$affiliatedWith) and (#$isa #$BillClinton #$Politician) are true, then (#$interestingSentence #$BillClinton (#$headOfStateOf #$BillClinton #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)) holds, since #$BillClinton is the arg 1 of (#$headOfStateOf #$BillClinton #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica), that sentence is asserted in the KB, and (#$genlPreds #$headOfStateOf #$affiliatedWith).
<p>
See also #$interestingForArgInReln.")
(comment displacementInDirection "This predicate relates a translational movement event to the distance travelled by the #$objectMoving in that event in some particular direction.  <code>(#$displacementInDirection MOVE DIST DIRECTION)</code> means that at the end of the event <code>MOVE</code>, the #$objectMoving is distance <code>DIST</code> away from its original position in the direction <code>DIRECTION</code>. <p>
For example (#$displacementInDirection Movement001 (#$Meter 400) #$Horizontal-Generally) could be true of a projectile launched from the top of a building, even if the total distance travelled by the projectile is over (#$Meter 500) because the projectile also happened to fall downward a distance of (#$Meter 300).  In that case, (#$displacementInDirection Movement001 (#$Meter 300) #$Down-Directly) would be true and the total distance travelled by the projectile could be over 500 meters (see #$distanceTranslated).")
(comment genlMtWithMonadIndexAndParameter "<code>(#$genlMtWithMonadIndexAndParameter MT GENL-MT MONAD TIME PAR)</code> means that the following hold:
<p>
1. <code>(#$genlMt MT GENL-MT)</code>       <br>
2. <code>(#$mtMonad MT MONAD)</code>        <br>
3. <code>(#$mtTimeIndex MT TIME)</code>   <br>
4. <code>(#$mtTimeParameter MT PAR)</code>")
(comment SKSITransitivityTest "A collection of SKSI content tests which test the interaction between SKSI removal modules and the transitivity removal modules.")
(comment UniformlyAcceleratedTranslationEvent "A specialization of #$Accelerating-Translational. Each instance of #$UniformlyAcceleratedTranslationEvent is an event in which the magnitude of the acceleration constant is unchanging.  The motion of the #$primaryObjectMoving  in each instance of #$UniformlyAcceleratedTranslationEvent is in a straight line.  In physics, there are simple kinematic equations for constant acceleration that apply to instances of #$UniformlyAcceleratedTranslationEvent.")
(comment attackAgainst "(#$attackAgainst ATTACK OBJECT) means that ATTACK was an event in which OBJECT was targeted or harmed in some way in ATTACK.  This is a very general predicate it it rarely should be used when entering knowledge.  The utility of this predicate comes from its use at query time.")
(comment speedOfObject-Translation "The predicate #$speedOfObject-Translation is used to give the speed with which an object is moving in a particular translational motion.  Velocity has a speed and direction.  Speed is a scalar quantity, i.e., it has no direction, only magnitude.  Thus, \"100 miles per hour straight up\" is a velocity, and \"100 miles per hour\" is the corresponding speed. (See #$velocityOfObject.) #$speedOfObject-Translation can be used to state the speed of an object at an arbitrarily long or short interval.")
(comment averageAccelerationOfPrimaryObjectMoving "<code>(#$averageAccelerationOfPrimaryObjectMoving MOVE ACCEL)</code> means in the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> where the primary moving object (see #$primaryObjectMoving) is <code>OBJ</code>, #$Acceleration <code>ACCEL</code> is the average acceleration of <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment initialAccelerationOfPrimaryObjectMoving "<code>(#$initialAccelerationOfPrimaryObjectMoving MOVE ACCEL)</code> means in the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> where the primary moving object (see #$primaryObjectMoving) is <code>OBJ</code>, #$Acceleration <code>ACCEL</code> is the initial acceleration of <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment TopologicalSpaceType "#$TopologicalSpaceType is a #$CollectionType whose instances are types of #$TopologicalSpaces.")
(comment TopologicalSpaceTypeBySeparationProperty "#$TopologicalSpaceTypeBySeparationProperty is a specialization of #$TopologicalSpaceType which identifies #$TopologicalSpaces according to their separation properties. More precisely, each instance of #$TopologicalSpaceTypeBySeparationProperty is a specialization of #$TopologicalSpace determined by how tightly a closed subset can be contained within an open set. The measure of tightness is the extent to which this envelope can separate the subset from other subsets. Following the standard mathematical convention, the names of these collections are all prefixed by the letter 'T' followed by a numeral between 0 and 4. The incresasing sequence of numerals refers to the increasing degree of separation.")
(comment RegularTopologicalSpace "A #$RegularTopologicalSpace is a topological space in which every neighborhood of a point contains a closed neighborhood of the same point. See #$neighborhoodOfThingInTopologicalSpace and #$closedSetOfTopologicalSpace.")
(comment NormalTopologicalSpace "A #$NormalTopologicalSpace is a #$TopologicalSpace in which for any two disjoint closed sets <code>C</code> and <code>D</code> there exist disjoint open sets <code>U</code> and <code>V</code> such that <code>C</code> is a subset of <code>U</code> and <code>D</code> is a subset of <code>V</code>. See #$openSetOfTopologicalSpace and #$closedSetOfTopologicalSpace.")
(comment T0-TopologicalSpace "A #$T0-TopologicalSpace is a #$TopologicalSpace in which for any two points <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> there exists an open set <code>U</code> such that either:
<p>
1. <code>X</code> is an element of <code>U</code> and <code>Y</code> is not an element of <code>U</code>, or
<p>
2. <code>Y</code> is an element of <code>U</code> and <code>X</code> is not an element of <code>U</code>.
<p>
#$T0-TopologicalSpaces are often called <i>Kolmogorov spaces</i>. See also #$openSetOfTopologicalSpace.")
(comment T1-TopologicalSpace "A #$T1-TopologicalSpace is a #$TopologicalSpace in which for any two points <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> there exist open sets <code>U</code> and <code>V</code> such that
<p>
1. <code>X</code> is an element of <code>U</code> and <code>Y</code> is not an element of <code>U</code>, and
<p>
2. <code>Y</code> is an element of <code>V</code> and <code>X</code> is not an element of <code>V</code>.
<p>
#$T1-TopologicalSpaces are often called <i>Frechet spaces</i>. See also #$openSetOfTopologicalSpace.")
(comment T2-TopologicalSpace "A #$T2-TopologicalSpace is a #$TopologicalSpace in which for any two points <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> there exist <i>disjoint</i> open sets <code>U</code> and <code>V</code> such that <code>X</code> is an element of <code>U</code>, <code>Y</code> is an element of <code>V</code>. #$T2-TopologicalSpaces are often called <i>Hausdorff spaces</i>. See also #$openSetOfTopologicalSpace.")
(comment T3-TopologicalSpace "A #$T3-TopologicalSpace is a #$T1-TopologicalSpace which is also a #$RegularTopologicalSpace. #$T3-TopologicalSpaces are often called <i>Vietoris spaces</i>.")
(comment T4-TopologicalSpace "A #$T4-TopologicalSpace is a #$T1-TopologicalSpace which is also a #$NormalTopologicalSpace. #$T4-TopologicalSpaces are often called <i>Tietze spaces</i>.")
(disjointWith T1-TopologicalSpace TrivialTopologicalSpace)
(comment TrivialTopologicalSpace "A #$TrivialTopologicalSpace is a #$TopologicalSpace in which the only elements of its #$Topology (see #$topologyOfTopologicalSpace) are #$TheEmptySet and the base set of the topological space (see #$baseSetOfTopologicalSpace).")
(comment DiscreteTopologicalSpace "A #$DiscreteTopologicalSpace is a #$TopologicalSpace in which every subset of the base set is a member of the topology.")
(comment InstantaneousEvent "A specialization of #$Event.  Each instance of #$InstantaneousEvent is an even whose duration (see #$duration) is infinitesimally short.")
(comment instantaneousSubEvents "<code>(#$instantaneousSubEvents EVENT INSTANT-EVENT)</code> means that <code>INSTANT-EVENT</code> is a proper sub-event  of <code>EVENT</code> that is infinitesimally short (see also #$InstantaneousEvent).")
(comment organizationKeyMembers "This predicate is used to identify an important member of a particular organization, i.e., one who plays a crucial role in its successful operation.  (#$organizationKeyMembers ORG PER) means that the #$Person PER holds a key position in the #$Organization ORG.  A key position in an organization is one whose vacancy would severely and negatively impact the functioning of the organization.  Common key roles are: a leader (e.g., President, C.E.O., Admiral), a senior executive, a board member, a cabinet member, a junta member, a major owner, or other important decision-makers.  Because #$organizationKeyMembers is closely tied to the functioning of an organization, the role and characteristics of a key member may be highly specialized; for example, #$organizationKeyMembers of a professional sports team, or #$organizationKeyMembers of a surgical practice.  A key member is, or often gives input to, the organization's leader and thus may substantially influence the decisions of the organization.")
(disjointWith Sunglasses (CollectionDifferenceFn Eyeglasses-Corrective Sunglasses))
(comment LikelySuspect "A specialization of #$IntelligentAgent.  Each instance of
#$LikelySuspect is an agent who is considered to be likely to have
performed some action.")
(comment foreignObjectMap "This quaternary predicate is the logical analogue to #$foreignKeyMap and is used to generate #$fieldDecoding and #$fieldEncoding assertions. <code>(#$foreignObjectMap ODS OF LS LF)</code> means that the #$LogicalField <code>LF</code> in the #$LogicalSchema <code>LS</code> is a foreign reference to the #$LogicalField <code>OF</code> in the #$ObjectDefiningSchema <code>ODS</code>.")
(comment typeOfThingOffered "(#$typeOfThingOffered
<code>OFFERING</code> <code>TYPE</code>) means that
<code>OFFERING</code> is an offer to perform, or furnish, an instance
of <code>TYPE</code>.  #$typeOfThingOffered is the correct predicate
to use in cases where <code>OFFERING</code> concerns only some
indefinite instance of <code>TYPE</code>, e.g., one that has not yet
been (and may never be) created or performed. Examples include an
offer to repair someone's car (an event that has not yet occurred),
and an offer of a martini before one has actually been prepared. When what
is offered in <code>OFFERING</code> is a particular object, use the
more specific #$thingOffered (q.v.)")
(comment regularSetOfTopologicalSpace "If <code>SPACE</code> is a #$TopologicalSpace with base set <code>BASE</code> and <code>SET</code> is a subset of <code>BASE</code>, then <code>SET</code> is a <i>regular</i> set of <code>SPACE</code> if and only if the closure of <code>SET</code> is equal to the closure of its interior and the interior of <code>SET</code> is equal to the interior of its closure. (See #$closureOfSetInTopologicalSpace and #$interiorOfSetInTopologicalSpace.) In #$CycL, this relation is expressed as <code>(#$regularSetOfTopologicalSpace SET SPACE)</code>.")
(comment odsExtendsODS "This predicate is used to capture the notion of object inheritence for knowledge sources that are object oriented in their conceptual design. That is, <code>(#$odsExtendsODS SUB ODS)</code> means that the #$ObjectDefiningSchema <code>SUB</code> extends the #$ObjectDefiningSchema <code>ODS</code>.")
(comment TheCulturalScenarioAnalysisTask "An indexical placeholder for the currently active instance of #$CulturalScenarioEvaluationAllotment.")
(comment FirstLady "An instance of #$ConventionalClassificationType and specialization of #$Married.  This is the collection of instances of #$AdultFemaleHuman who, by virtue of being married to a sitting head of state or head of government, are referred to as the \"first lady\" of the relevant polity.")
(comment FirstGentleman "An instance of #$ConventionalClassificationType and specialization of #$Married.  This is the collection of instances of #$AdultMaleHuman who, by virtue of being married to a sitting head of state or head of government, are referred to as the \"first gentleman\" of the relevant polity.")
(comment HeadOfState-Elected "The collection of instances of #$HeadOfState who are heads of state as the result of a free and fair election.")
(disjointWith UnderwaterTopographicalFeature FloodedArea)
(comment Foxhole "The collection of foxholes, instances of #$Ditch, typically dug by soldiers during wartime, designed to provide a defensive fighting position.")
(comment SpiderHole "The collection of small (1-person), deep instances of #$Foxhole, used frequently by Japanese soldiers during World War II and by the Vietcong during the Vietnam war.")
(comment FloodedArea "The collection of areas that are instances of #$UnderwaterLocation due to an instance of #$Flooding.")
(comment capableOf "This predicate is a #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation that can be used to assert that an agent is <i>fully</i> able to carry out a certain role in a certain type of situation.  <code>(#$capableOf AGENT SITTYPE ROLE)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is able to perform <code>ROLE</code> in #$Situations of the type <code>SITTYPE</code>.  This entails that <code>AGENT</code> is qualified in all the relevant ways to fill that <code>ROLE</code>; i.e. <code>AGENT</code> is #$physicallyCapableOf playing that <code>ROLE</code> in instances of <code>SITTYPE</code> -- and, given that <code>AGENT</code> is the appropriate sort of agent -- is also #$anatomicallyCapableOf, #$fiscallyCapableOf, #$legallyCapableOf, and #$skillCapableOf playing that <code>ROLE</code> in instances of <code>SITTYPE</code>.
<p>
Contrast this predicate with the more general #$behaviorCapable: <code>(#$behaviorCapable OBJ SITTYPE ROLE)</code>  does not imply that <code>OBJ</code> can unquestionably play <code>ROLE</code> in instances of <code>SITTYPE</code> since extrinsic factors may prevent it from doing so; for example, (#$behaviorCapable #$RonaldReagan #$Reading #$performedBy) may be true at a time when (#$capableOf #$RonaldReagan #$Reading #$performedBy) is not (e.g. at a time when Reagan is literate but blindfolded).")
(comment ParliamentFn "A unary, partial function that takes a #$GeopoliticalEntity and returns its #$Parliament, if it has one.  For example, <code>(#$ParliamentFn #$Canada)</code> denotes the Canadian parliament.  Conversely, since the US has no parliament, <code>(#$ParliamentFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)</code> is #$undefined.
<p>
Of course all #$ParliamentaryGovernments (q.v.) have parliaments; but note that  geopolitical entities with certain other systems of goverment might still have parliaments (e.g. #$Iran).")
(comment parliamentOf "A specialization of the predicate #$subOrganizations that relates a #$GeopoliticalEntity to its #$Parliament (if it has one).  <code>(#$parliamentOf GEOPOL PARLIAMENT)</code> means that <code>PARLIAMENT</code> is the parliament of <code>GEOPOL</code>.
<p>
Of course all #$ParliamentaryGovernments (q.v.) have parliaments; but note that  geopolitical entities with certain other systems of goverment might also have parliaments (e.g. #$Iran).
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$ParliamentFn.")
(comment ParliamentMember "A specialization of #$Legislator.  #$ParliamentMember is the collection of all (and only) public officials who are members of a #$Parliament.")
(comment Arboretum "The collection of instances of #$BotanicalGarden that are devoted to the preservation and study of trees.")
(comment CollectionMaintenanceAndDisplayOrganization "The collection of organizations whose focal activity is the maintenance and display of items belonging to a collection (see #$Collection-Group), usually to the public.")
(disjointWith CollectionMaintenanceAndDisplayOrganization EntertainmentOrRecreationOrganization)
(comment CollectionDisplaySetting "The collection of instances of #$Place that, by design, are settings for the display of items in some collection (#$Collection-Group).  This is a very general collection that includes entire facilities (#$Facility-Construct), such as zoos and arboreta; display structures (#$DisplayStructure), such as museum buildings; and spaces in structures (#$SpaceInAFixedHOC), such as showrooms.")
(comment settingTypeForDisplayOfType "<code>(#$settingTypeForDisplayOfType PLACE-TYPE TANGIBLE-TYPE)</code> means that typically, objects of type <code>TANGIBLE-TYPE</code> are on display at any instance of <code>PLACE-TYPE</code>.")
(comment BanquetHall "The collection of instances of #$DiningRoom that are used as the locations for banquets (see #$Banquet).")
(comment DiningHall "A specialization of #$DiningRoom and #$PersonAssemblyRoom, this is the collection of larg-ish, open dining areas, frequently with a kitchen adjoining or nearby, used for communal dining, and, in some cases, for hosting large dinner parties.")
(comment Banquet "The collection of large dinner parties, frequently formal or semi-formal in nature, often held as part of a fundraiser or to celebrate some cause or achievement.")
(comment Chaplain "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Chaplains are instances of #$Cleric typically employed by a non-overtly-religious organization to meet the spiritual needs of its members (as in a military chaplain) or clients (as in a hospital chaplain) or, in the case of prison chaplains, its captives.")
(comment DangerousActivity "The collection of activities that pose an inherent risk to the health or well-being of its doer or those nearby when the event unfolds.")
(comment BushFlying "The collection of instances of #$FlyingViaAirTransportationDevice that involve taking off or landing in remote areas.")
(comment Choir "A specialization of #$VocalEnsemble.  A choir is a typically largish group of singers who specialize in performing spiritual music.")
(comment cotemporalProperSubEvents "The intersection (see #$predicateIntersection) of #$properSubEvents and #$cotemporalSubEvents.  <code>(#$cotemporalProperSubEvents EVENT SUB-EVENT)</code> means that <code>SUB-EVENT</code> is a distinct event from <code>EVENT</code>, is a part of <code>EVENT</code>, and has the same temporal extent as <code>EVENT</code>, such as the event of one person marching in step during a coordinated marching event.")
(comment Singing-Human "The collection of all instance of #$Singing performed by one or more humans.")
(comment GroupSinging "The collection of instances of #$Singing performed by multiple animals engaged in simultaneous instances of #$Singing-SingleDoerAction.")
(comment Singing "A specialization of #$MakingMusicalSound.  This is the most general collection of all singing events, including both human singing (#$Singing-Human) as well as the singing of non-human animals, such as birds; single-doer singing (#$Singing-SingleDoerAction), and coordinated singing activity, made up of single doer singing (#$GroupSinging).")
(comment VocalHarmonization "The collection of instances of #$GroupSinging-Human that involve singers singing different notes, forming chords.")
(comment BushPilot "The collection of instances of #$AirplanePilot who fly planes into remote and dangerous areas.")
(comment cotemporalProperSubEventTypes "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  A sentence of the form <code>(#$cotemporalProperSubEventTypes SUPER-EVENT SUB-EVENT)</code> has the same truth conditions as a sentence of the form, <code>(#$relationAllExists cotemporalProperSubEvents SUPER-EVENT SUB-EVENT)</code>, which means that every instance <code>INSTANCE</code> of <code>SUPER-EVENT</code> is cotemporal (#$cotemporal) with a distinct event of type <code>SUB-EVENT</code> that is a part of <code>INSTANCE</code>.")
(comment EnsembleSinging "The collection of instances of #$GroupSinging-Human performed by an instance of #$VocalEnsemble.")
(comment compoundStringSenseExtension "<code>(#$wordSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION LIST)</code> means that the use of the word <code>WORD</code> with speech part <code>SPEECH-PART-2</code> followed by the string picked out by <code>LIST</code>  to mean <code>EXTENSION</code> is an extension of the sense of the #$compoundString associated with <code>LIST</code> and <code>WORD</code> -- in particular the sense with speech part </code>SPEECH-PART-1</code> and denotation <code>DENOTATION</code>.  For example, 
<pre>
(#$compoundStringSenseExtension #$Bird-TheWord #$CountNoun #$BirdOfPrey #$MassNoun (MeatFn BirdOfPrey))</pre>
means that the use of \"bird of prey\" as a mass noun to refer to meat from birds of prey is an extension of the count noun sense of \"bird of prey\" meaning the #$BirdOfPrey.")
(comment multiWordStringSenseExtension "(#$multiWordStringSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION BINARY-PREDICATE LIST) means that the fact that

   (#$multiWordStringSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION LIST)

is explained in part by the fact that

   (BINARY-PREDICATE ARG1 ARG2)

for some ARG1 and ARG2 such that

   (#$multiWordString WORD SPEECH-PART-1 ARG1)

for some N.

The reason why this sense extension is only partly explained by the above considerations reflects the fact that due to Cyc's incomplete knowledge, there will be many cases where BINARY-PREDICATE is not an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and so cannot reliably broker sense extensions from SPEECH-PART-1 to SPEECH-PART-2 for any word denoting one of its arg1s. Rather, there is a possibly unreified specialization SPEC-PRED of BINARY-PRED (see genlPreds) that is an instance of SenseExtendingPredicate, and the fact that (SPEC-PRED ARG1 ARG2) fully explains the sense extension in question. ")
(comment multiWordStringSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred-Inverse "<code>(#$multiWordStringSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred-Inverse WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION BINARY-PREDICATE LIST)</code> means that the fact that <pre>
   (#$multiWordStringSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION LIST)</pre> is explained <i>in part</i> by the fact that <pre>
   (BINARY-PREDICATE ARG1 ARG2)</pre> for some <code>ARG1</code> and <code>ARG2</code> such that 
<pre>
   (#$multiWordString LIST WORD SPEECH-PART-1 ARG2)</pre> for some N. <p>The reason why this sense extension is only <i>partly</i> explained by the above considerations reflects the fact that due to Cyc's incomplete knowledge, there will be many cases where BINARY-PREDICATE is not an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and so cannot <i>reliably</i> broker sense extensions from SPEECH-PART-1 to SPEECH-PART-2 for any word denoting one of its arg1s.  Rather, there is a possibly unreified specialization SPEC-PRED of BINARY-PRED (see #$genlPreds) that is an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and the fact that (SPEC-PRED ARG1 ARG2) fully explains the sense extension in question.  ")
(comment multiWordStringSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred "<code>(#$multiWordStringSenseExtensionBrokeredByPred WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION BINARY-PREDICATE LIST)</code> means that the fact that <pre>
   (#$multiWordStringSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION LIST)</pre> is explained <i>in part</i> by the fact that <pre>
   (BINARY-PREDICATE ARG1 ARG2)</pre> for some <code>ARG1</code> and <code>ARG2</code> such that 
<pre>
   (#$multiWordString LIST WORD SPEECH-PART-1 ARG1)</pre>. <p>The reason why this sense extension is only <i>partly</i> explained by the above considerations reflects the fact that due to Cyc's incomplete knowledge, there will be many cases where BINARY-PREDICATE is not an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and so cannot <i>reliably</i> broker sense extensions from SPEECH-PART-1 to SPEECH-PART-2 for any word denoting one of its arg1s.  Rather, there is a possibly unreified specialization SPEC-PRED of BINARY-PRED (see #$genlPreds) that is an instance of #$SenseExtendingPredicate, and the fact that (SPEC-PRED ARG1 ARG2) fully explains the sense extension in question.  ")
(comment nominee "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$objectSelected and #$affectedAgent-Direct.  <code>(#$nominee NOMINATION AGENT)</code> means that in <code>NOMINATION</code>, <code>AGENT</code> is formally proposed for membership in, or for a particular position within (see #$PersonTypeByPositionInOrg), some #$Organization.
<p>
Unlike the recipient of a direct appointment (cf. #$appointees), a nominee must
successfully undergo a confirmation process or win an election in order to be granted the membership or position.")
(comment NominatingSomeone "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$SelectingSomething. In each #$NominatingSomeone, someone puts forward someone else; nominates someone else for appointment to an office, position, or membership in an organization.")
(comment topicOfStatusChange "A binary predicate that relates a #$ChangeOfStatusEvent (q.v.) to its \"topic\", in the sense of the thing with respect to which the relevant object changes its status.
<code>(#$topicOfStatusChange EVENT THING)</code> means that <code>EVENT</code> centrally involves a certain object's (the #$objectOfStatusChange) changing its status with respect to <code>THING</code>.
<p>
The nature of <code>THING</code> varies, depending on the sort of status change involved.  For example, if its a matter of an agent's membership status in a certain organization (see #$AgentTypeByOrganizationStatus), then the topic of status  change is the organization itself.  If it's a matter of the agent's status regarding a particular position in the organization, then the topic of status  change is the position, which we construe as a #$PersonTypeByPositionInOrg. ")
(comment multiWordStringSenseExtension "<code>(#$multiWordStringSenseExtension WORD SPEECH-PART-1 DENOTATION SPEECH-PART-2 EXTENSION LIST)</code> means that the use the string picked out by <code>LIST</code> followed by the word <code>WORD</code> with speech part <code>SPEECH-PART-2</code> to mean <code>EXTENSION</code> is an extension of the sense of the #$multiWordString associated with <code>LIST</code> and <code>WORD</code> -- in particular the sense with speech part </code>SPEECH-PART-1</code> and denotation <code>DENOTATION</code>.  For example, 
<pre>
(#$multiWordStringSenseExtension 
#$Lion-TheWord #$CountNoun #$Chicken #$MassNoun (MeatFn SeaLion))</pre>
means that the use of \"sea lion\" as a mass noun to refer to sea lion meat is an extension of the count noun sense of \"sea lion\" meaning the #$SeaLion.")
(comment posConstraintForNCR "(#$posConstraintForNCR RULE INDEXICAL POS) means that RULE requires its #$NounCompoundRuleIndexicalTerm-Direct term INDEXICAL to satisfy the #$TermPhrasesConstraint POS. In general, this means that there is a #$ncRuleConstraint relating rule RULE to the constraint (#$NCPOSConstraintFn INDEXICAL POS).")
(comment isaConstraintForNCR "(#$isaConstraintForNCR RULE INDEXICAL COL) means that RULE constrains its #$NounCompoundRuleIndexicalTerm-Direct term INDEXICAL to denote an instance of COL. In general, the means that there will be an #$ncRuleConstraint assertion linking RULE to (#$NCIsaConstraintFn INDEXICAL TYPE) where TYPE is some spec of COL.")
(comment equalsConstraintForNCR "(#$equalsConstraintForNCR RULE INDEXICAL COL) means that RULE constrains its #$NounCompoundRuleIndexicalTerm-Direct term INDEXICAL to denote COL. In general, the means that there will be an #$ncRuleConstraint assertion linking RULE to (#$NCIsaConstraintFn INDEXICAL COL).")
(comment posPredConstraintForNCR "(#$posPredConstraintForNCR RULE INDEXICAL POSPRED) means that RULE requires its #$NounCompoundRuleIndexicalTerm-Direct term INDEXICAL to satisfy the #$TermPhrasesConstraint associated with POSPRED. In general, this means that there will a #$ncRuleConstraint relating rule RULE to the constraint (#$NCPOSPredConstraintFn INDEXICAL POSPRED).")
(comment wordUnitConstraintForNCR "(#$wordUnitConstraintForNCR RULE INDEXICAL WORD) means that RULE requires its #$NounCompoundRuleIndexicalTerm-Direct term INDEXICAL to be a form of the #$LexicalWord WORD. In general, this means that there will a #$ncRuleConstraint relating rule RULE to the constraint (#$NCWordUnitConstraintFn INDEXICAL WORD).")
(comment CommonSense "A #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and specialization of #$PersonalQuantity and #$NonAspectualQuantity.  Each instance of #$CommonSense is an amount or degree of \"common sense\" -- basic, down-to-earth knowledge about ordinary things.")
(comment preventedProp "A binary #$PropositionPredicate.  <code>(#$preventedProp PREVENTING SENT)</code> means that in the #$PreventingSomething event <code>PREVENTING</code>, the doer prevents the situation described by <code>SENT</code> from obtaining.")
(comment countryPassportIDIssuedToPerson "<code>(#$countryPassportIDIssuedToPerson COUNTRY PIN PERSON)</code> means that <code>COUNTRY</code> has issued a #$Passport with ID number <code>PIN</code> to <code>PERSON</code>.")
(comment testFollowupTestFormula-All "An expression of the form (#$testFollowupTestFormula-All TEST FORMULA) is used to partially describe a followup test that should be run on the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST to test the properties of the answers returned by TEST.  Specifically, FORMULA is both an #$CycLOpenSentence and a #$CycLSentence-Askable.  This formula will support the creation of a second test that would have the same query formula as the #$CycLQuerySpecification of TEST but with FORMULA conjoined to the relevant query, within the scope of any explicit quantifiers. (In the case of a hypothetical query, it would be conjoined to the consequent of the implication.)  As an example, assume that TEST has the hypothetical query <pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y))
</pre>
The assertion (#$testFollowupTestFormula-All TEST (and (#$isa ?Y #$FemaleHuman)(#$isa ?X #$FemaleHuman))) would generate the followup query formula:<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)
    (#$and
      (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y)
      (#$isa ?X #$FemaleHuman)
      (#$isa ?Y #$FemaleHuman)))
</pre>The #$CycLQuerySpecification of the followup test will assume the same parameter settings as the #$CycLQuerySpecification for TEST, most significantly the #$InferenceMicrotheoryParameter. The success of TEST requires that <b>all bindings</b> returned by TEST are also returned by the followup query. It is assumed that the introduction of FORMULA does not cause the query formula for the followup query to have a different set of free variables.  See also #$testFollowupTestFormula-Some and #$testFollowupTestFormula-None.")
(comment testFollowupTestFormula-None "An expression of the form (#$testFollowupTestFormula-None TEST FORMULA) is used to partially describe a followup test that should be run on the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST to test the properties of the answers returned by TEST.  Specifically, FORMULA is both an #$CycLOpenSentence and a #$CycLSentence-Askable.  This formula will support the creation of a second test that would have the same query formula as the #$CycLQuerySpecification of TEST but with FORMULA conjoined to the relevant query, within the scope of any explicit quantifiers. (In the case of a hypothetical query, it would be conjoined to the consequent of the implication.)  As an example, assume that TEST has the hypothetical query <pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y))
</pre>
The assertion (#$testFollowupTestFormula-None TEST (#$isa ?Y #$NonPersonAnimal)) would generate the followup query formula:<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)
    (#$and
      (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y)
      (#$isa ?Y #$NonPersonAnimal)))
</pre>The #$CycLQuerySpecification of the followup test will assume the same parameter settings as the #$CycLQuerySpecification for TEST, most significantly the #$InferenceMicrotheoryParameter. The success of TEST requires that <b>no bindings</b> are returned by the followup test. That is, this test specification can be used to screen the answers to the first query so as to ensure that certain classes of answers are not returned.  It is assumed that the introduction of FORMULA does not cause the query formula for the followup query to have a different set of free variables.  See also #$testFollowupTestFormula-All and #$testFollowupTestFormula-Some.")
(comment testFollowupTestFormula-Some "An expression of the form (#$testFollowupTestFormula-Some TEST FORMULA) is used to partially describe a followup test that should be run on the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST to test the properties of the answers returned by TEST.  Specifically, FORMULA is both an #$CycLOpenSentence and a #$CycLSentence-Askable.  This formula will support the creation of a second test that would have the same query formula as the #$CycLQuerySpecification of TEST but with FORMULA conjoined to the relevant query, within the scope of any explicit quantifiers. (In the case of a hypothetical query, it would be conjoined to the consequent of the implication.)  As an example, assume that TEST has the hypothetical query <pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y))
</pre>
The assertion (#$testFollowupTestFormula-Some TEST (#$equals ?Y ?X)) would generate the follow up query formula:<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)
    (#$and
      (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y)
      (#$equals ?X ?Y)))
</pre>The #$CycLQuerySpecification of the followup test will assume the same parameter settings as the #$CycLQuerySpecification for TEST, most significantly the #$InferenceMicrotheoryParameter. The success of TEST requires that the set of bindings returned by the followup query is <b>non-empty.</b>  Furthermore, this predicate assumes that the introduction of FORMULA does not cause the query formula for the followup query to have a different set of free variables.  See also #$testFollowupTestFormula-All and #$testFollowupTestFormula-None.")
(comment TermSuggestorExpertOnlyTerm "#$TermSuggestorExpertOnlyTerm is the collection of #$CycLTerms that are omitted from the results that the #$TermSuggestor returns unless the #$TermSuggestor is run in \"expert mode\".")
(comment imageSource "<code>(#$imageSource VISUAL-IBT-TYPE IMAGE)</code> means that the instance <code>IMAGE</code> of #$VisualImage is a light wave propogation whose source is the instance <code>VISUAL-IBT-TYPE</code> of #$VisualInformationBearingThing.  For example, if <code>PICTURE</code> is the image (see #$Picture-Image) generated by subjecting a given printed developed photograph (see (#$HardcopyOfIBTTypeFn #$Photograph)) <code>PRINT</code> to the right amount of light, then <code>(#$imageSource PICTURE PRINT)</code> holds.")
(comment MapCopy "A specialization of #$StillImageSource.  A #$MapCopy is an  object (often physical -- see (#$HardcopyOfIBTTypeFn #$MapCopy)) depicting in two dimensions and instantiating a conceptual #$Map.  A map often consists of printed sheets or similar displays on screens.  Map copies often contain #$TextualMaterial, but sometimes contain only images.  A specialization of #$MapCopy is #$Globe-Map.")
(comment BilateralObject "A specialization of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  Each instance of #$BilateralObject is an object that has a well-defined pair of opposing sides (see #$objectSides) -- such as front and back, bottom and top, or left and right.  \"Well-defined\" here just means either naturally-determined (e.g. the front-back of a mammal and the top-bottom of a hill) or conventionally-determined (e.g. the left-right of a car and the top-bottom of a soda can).  
<p>
Examples of non-bilateral objects are ball bearings and sugar cubes.
<p>
See also the specializations #$FrontAndBackSidedObject, #$LeftAndRightSidedObject, and #$TopAndBottomSidedObject.  An object that is an instance of all three of these collections is a #$HexalateralObject.
<p>
Also see #$bilateralAxes and #$NoteAboutSidedObjects.")
(comment hardcopyTypeOfIBTType "<code>(#$hardcopyTypeOfIBTType HARDCOPY-VERSION TYPE)</code> identifies <code>HARDCOPY-VERSION</code> as the collection of all instances of
#$HardcopyInformationBearingObject that are hard-copies of an instance of <code>TYPE</code>, a specialization of #$InformationBearingThing..")
(disjointWith Graphic-VisualIBT TextualMaterial)
(disjointWith ActionOnObject WavePropagation)
(disjointWith BilateralObject ActionOnObject)
(disjointWith Graphic-VisualIBT Sculpture)
(comment Graphic-VisualIBT "A subcollection of #$VisualInformationBearingThing.  Instances of this collection are approximately 2-dimensional spatial objects that are the sources (see #$imageSource) of still images (see #$StillImage).  This collection includes many specializations of #$TwoDimensionalArtObject, such as paintings (#$Painting-ArtForm), as well as spatial regions where a still visual image is displayed (such as the area on a wall where an image might be projected).  Counter-examples include high-relief objects (sculptures), motion pictures, and text, though many graphics can include bits of text (and vice versa).")
(comment Infographic "A subcollection of #$Graphic-VisualIBT.  Instances are still-image sources (see #$imageSource), such as charts and graphs, that serve to display data in a way that makes it easy for the target audience to digest.")
(comment Chart "A subcollection of #$Infographic.  Instances are still-image sources (see #$imageSource), such as charts and graphs, that serve to display data in a chart format -- i.e., one that distinguishes comparable data through different-sized shapes and different colors.")
(comment Graph-VisualIBT "A subcollection of #$Infographic.  Instances are still-image sources (see #$imageSource) that serve to display system data in the familiar node-and-edge format.")
(comment Picture-Image "The collection of all instances of #$StillImage that depict some state of affairs (typically by being a picture of situated objects).  Prototypical examples include the pictures one sees on a printed flyer or on a photograph.  Examples exclude the image of a statue one has before them when one visits one at a museum, though the (perhaps visually indistiguishable) image one gets by photographing that statue, developing the photo and illuminating it under sufficient light, does count.  Similarly, this collection excludes mere symbols or arrangements as they might occur in a chart or graph, though one can form a instance of #$Picture-Image by filming or photographing such things and producing the relevant instance of #$StillImageSource from which the picture can be viewed.")
(comment PowerLine "the collection of electrical power lines")
(comment IranianHeadOfJudiciary "A specialization of #$PublicOfficial and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianHeadOfJudiciary is the head of the #$JudicialSystem of Iran.  He ensures that the Islamic laws are enforced and defines legal policy.  The head of judiciary also nominates the six lay members of the #$IranianGuardianCouncil.")
(comment IranianCabinet "An instance of #$Council-Organization.  The #$IranianCabinet, or <i>Council of Ministers</i>, advises the Iranian president.    Its members are chosen by the president, but must be approved by the Iranian  parliament (see #$Majlis-Iran).  The parliament also has the power to impeach ministers.")
(comment IranianExpediencyCouncil "An instance of #$Council-Organization.  The #$IranianExpediencyCouncil is an advisory body for the #$IranianSupremeLeader.  It has ultimate adjudicating power in disputes over legislation between the parliament (see #$Majlis-Iran) and the #$IranianGuardianCouncil. The Supreme Leader appoints the Council's members, who are prominent religious, social and political figures.")
(comment Majlis-Iran "An instance of #$Parliament.  The #$Majlis-Iran is the parliament of (post-revolutionary) #$Iran.")
(comment PresidentOfIran "A specialization of #$President-HeadOfGovernmentOrHeadOfState.  #$PresidentOfIran is the collection of persons who hold the office of President in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment PersonAssemblyArea "The collection of all spaces designed or set-aside primarily to hold a group of people who are participating in some event.")
(disjointWith DriveInTheater (CollectionDifferenceFn MovieTheaterSpace DriveInTheater))
(comment objectOfStatusChange "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$objectActedOn and #$postActors (q.v.).  <code>(#$objectOfStatusChange EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> undergoes some kind of change of conventional (i.e. social, legal, economic, historic, etc.) status in the #$ChangeOfStatusEvent <code>EVENT</code>.  <code>OBJECT</code> exists before and after <code>EVENT</code>, and might or might not be directly involved in <code>EVENT</code>. See also #$topicOfStatusChange.
<p>
Note that a change of status is an <i>extrinsic</i> change.  Cf. #$objectOfStateChange, which pertains to intrinsic changes (see #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent).")
(comment Muscle "A specialization of #$MuscleTissue.  Each instance of #$Muscle is an organ that is essentially a mass of muscle tissue controlled by a nerve (see #$Nerve) and that by contracting moves or restricts the motion of one or more #$AnimalBodyParts to which it is connected.")
(comment CircusClown "The collection of clowns that work at a circus. Every good circus should have one.")
(comment Sentient "The collection of all sentient beings -- instances of #$IndividualAgent that have sufficient mental capabilities that they are self-aware.")
(comment LionTamer "A specialization of #$EntertainmentOrArtsProfessional.  Each instance is an animal trainer who directs public performances of routines performed by lions.")
(comment PerceptualAgent "A specialization of #$IndividualAgent (q.v.). #$PerceptualAgent is the collection of all individuals capable of perception.  For agents capable of sensory perception, see #$PerceptualAgent-Embodied.")
(comment AnimalPerformanceRoutine "An instance of #$LearnedActivityType and specialization of #$ConformingToASpecification.  This is the collection of all actions done by animals (human and non-human alike) that, by the intention of either the doer or director, instantiate a script-like specification, typically as a performance for an audience or in the course of practicing from such a performance.  Positive examples include a pet performing a trick, a gymnast executing a floor routine, and a band performing a musical piece.  Negative examples include brushing teeth, unlocking a car door, or any other somewhat mundane learned behaviors that are rarely, if ever, intended as performances or demonstrations.")
(comment appointedByAgentToOrg "A ternary predicate that relates pairs of agents to an organization. <code>(#$appointedByAgentToOrg APPOINTEE APPOINTER ORG)</code> means that <code>APPOINTEE</code> is a member of <code>ORG</code> in virtue of having been appointed by <code>APPOINTER</code>.  This entails that <code>(#$hasMembers ORG APPOINTEE ORG)</code> is true and, at least at the time of the appointment, that <code>(#$agentAppointsMembersOfOrg APPOINTER ORG)</code> is true. 
<p>
Note that #$appointedByAgentToOrg is #$contemporaryInArg (q.v.) in its first and third argument-places, but <i>not</i> in its second argument-place.  So the above sentence can be true only so long as <code>APPOINTEE</code> and <code>ORG</code> exist (and, indeed, the former remains a member of the latter); but the sentence 
might be true even after <code>APPOINTER</code> ceases to exist.
<p>
See also #$agentAppointsPositionInOrg.")
(comment termOfPositionInOrg "A ternary predicate that is used to state the prescribed duration of a \"term\" for a given position in an organization. Positions with such built-in term-lengths are usually elected or appointed ones.  
<code>(#$termOfPositionInOrg DURATION POSITION ORG)</code> means that it is stipulated by <code>ORG</code> that <code>POSITION</code> is to be held by any given agent for a (maximum) period of <code>DURATION</code>, at which point there is another election or appointment to <code>POSITION</code>.  Of course, <code>ORG</code>'s rules allowing, the same agent might hold <code>POSITION</code> for consecutive terms.  Conversely, an agent might leave <code>POSITION</code> voluntarily, be forced out, or die before her term is over.
<p>
Note that if <code>POSITION</code> is one that is normally held for the remainder of the electee/appointee's life (or until s/he resigns), <code>DURATION</code> should be #$LifetimesDuration.
<p>
For organizations in which the same term-length applies to all members, use #$termOfMembershipInOrg")
(comment termOfMembershipInOrg "A binary predicate that is used to state the prescribed duration of a \"term\" of membership in a given organization. Organizations with such built-in terms-lengths are usually ones whose members are all elected or appointed.  <code>(#$termOfMembershipInOrg DURATION ORG)</code> means that it is stipulated that an agent who becomes a member of <code>ORG</code> remains so for a period of <code>DURATION</code>, at which point there is another election or appointment (or whatever recurrent event it is that confers membership in <code>ORG</code>).  Of course, <code>ORG</code>'s rules allowing, a member might be re-elected (re-appointed) to <code>ORG</code> for another term.  Conversely, an agent might leave <code>ORG</code> voluntarily, be forced out, or die before her term is over.
<p>
Note that in the case of \"lifetime membership\", <code>DURATION</code> should be #$LifetimesDuration.
<p>
See also #$termOfPositionInOrg.")
(comment agentAppointsMembersOfOrg "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates an #$IntelligentAgent to an #$Organization.  (#$agentAppointsMembersOfOrg AGENT ORG) means that AGENT has the right/responsibility to appoint members of ORG (under appropriate circumstances).
<p>
For example, (#$agentAppointsMembersOfOrg (#$PresidentFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica) #$UnitedStatesSupremeCourt) means that the president of the United States has the right/responsibility to appoint members of the U.S. Supreme Court (under appropriate circumstances, e.g. when a current Supreme Court justice resigns).")
(comment agentNominatesMembersOfOrg "A binary predicate that relates an #$IntelligentAgent to an #$Organization.  <code>(#$agentNominatesMembersOfOrg AGENT ORG)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> has the responsibility or right to nominate persons for membership in <code>ORG</code> (under appropriate circumstances).
<p>
Note that the above does <i>not</i> entail that <code>AGENT</code> is uniquely responsible for putting forth such nominations to <code>ORG</code>; there might be others who have the same responsibility.  Nor, of course, does it entail that new membership in <code>ORG</code> is always preceded by a nomination.
<p>
When a given <code>NOMINEE</code> is nominated for membership in <code>ORG</code>, some further condition must be satisfied in order for <code>NOMINEE</code> actually to become a member.  In some cases, this further condition is that the nomination be approved by some agent or group (from inside or outside <code>ORG</code>); and if approved, <code>NOMINEE</code> becomes an <i>appointed member</i> of <code>ORG</code> (see #$appointedToOrganization; and cf. #$agentAppointsMembersOfOrg for appointments not subject to approval).  In other cases, the further condition is that <code>NOMINEE</code> win an election in which s/he competes against other nominees for the same position; if s/he wins, <code>NOMINEE</code> becomes an <i>elected member</i> of <code>ORG</code> (see #$electedToOrganization). 
<p>
For example, <code>(#$relationAllInstance #$agentNominatesMembersOfOrg #$IranianHeadOfJudiciary #$IranianGuardianCouncil)</code> means that the Head of Judiciary in Iran may nominate members to the Guardian Council.  The \"appropriate circumstances\" for this obtain every three years, when the terms of three of the six lay members of the Guardian Council end.  These nominations are subject to approval by parliament (see #$Majlis-Iran).")
(comment organismTypeExistentialThreatState "(#$organismTypeExistentialThreatState ORGANISMTYPE STATE) means that the specified type of organism has the specified state.")
(comment renownWithinGroup "A ternary predicate.  <code>(#$renownWithinGroup THING AGENTTYPE RENOWN)</code> means that <code>THING</code>'s #$LevelOfRenown among agents of <code>AGENTTYPE</code> is <code>RENOWN</code>.")
(comment ResolvingByAdjudication "A specialization of #$ResolvingAConflict.  Each instance of #$ResolvingByAdjudication is the resolution of a conflict via a decision by some agent empowered to do so.  That agent is the performer (see #$performedBy) of the resolution.")
(comment ResolvingPeacefully "A collection of #$Actions.   An instance of #$ResolvingPeacefully is an event in which  some #$ConflictEvent is resolved peacefully, i.e. without one or other of the adversaries attacking or harming the other.")
(comment nounLearnerConjecturedTypeForNoun "(#$nounLearnerConjecturedTypeForNoun NOUN-STRING COLLECTION) means that, based on statistical analysis of its usages in some text corpus (like, perhaps, the New York Times from 2001), the Noun Learner conjectures that the noun NOUN-STRING denotes an instance or spec of COLLECTION.  For example, (#$nounLearnerConjecturedTypeForNoun \"rutabaga\" #$EdibleStuff) means that \"rutabaga\" is conjectured to denote an instance or spec of #$EdibleStuff.  Use non-plural forms of nouns in queries.")
(comment BinaryQuantityPredicate "A specialization of #$BinaryPredicate and #$IntangibleObjectPredicate whose instances relate things to quantities.  A binary predicate is an instance of #$BinaryQuantityPredicate if and only if its #$arg1Isa or its #$arg2Isa (or both) is a specialization of #$Quantity.  Examples include #$sizeParameterOfObject, #$followingValue, #$lifeExpectancy, and many others.  
<p>
Note that if a binary quantity predicate is such that (i) its #$arg2Format is #$intervalEntryFormatInArgs and (ii) its #$arg1Isa is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$Quantity, it is an instance of the more specialized collection #$QuantitySlot.")
(comment vector "The predicate corresponding to #$VectorFn (q.v.).")
(comment ForceQuantity "A #$QuantityType and a sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantity.  A #$ForceQuantity represents the magnitude compnent of a #$ForceVector (q.v.).")
(comment IranianAssemblyOfExperts "A #$GovernmentalOrganization and a sub-organization of the Iranian government.  The #$IranianAssemblyOfExperts is a group of clerics responsible for appointing the #$IranianSupremeLeader, monitoring his performance, and removing him if they deem him incapable of performing his duties.  The assembly's 86 members are elected directly every eight years.")
(comment IranianGuardianCouncil "An instance of #$Council-Organization and an Iranian govenmental organization.  This is the most influential body in Iran.  It consists of six clerics appointed by the #$IranianSupremeLeader and six jurists nominated by the #$IranianHeadOfJudiciary and approved by the parliament (see #$Majlis-Iran).  The Guardian Council has the power to veto any bill passed by parliament that it deems inconsistent with the constitution or Islamic law.  
The Council can also bar candidates from running for parliament, the presidency, or membership in the #$IranianAssemblyOfExperts.  Members of the Council serve for terms of six years.")
(comment ConfirmationInOrganization "A specialization of #$OrganizationalTransferIn (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ConfirmationInOrganization is an action in which some applicant or nominee for membership in an organization is officially approved for membership; it involves an instance of #$Evaluating (in which the applicant/nominee is judged with respect to the organization's membership criteria) and then an instance of #$Accept (in which it is decided that the evaluee meets the criteria).  Upon confirmation, the agent becomes a member of 
the organization.
<p>
See also #$ConfirmationForAPosition.")
(comment CulturalAnalysis-FETReformulation "This reformulation points all CKB template output to the reformulation rules gathered under the meta-predicate #$useReformulationRuleForCulturalAnalysis.")
(comment Maoist "The collection of persons who believe in #$Maoism.")
(comment ensures-AgentProp "A binary #$PropositionPredicate that is used to state that an agent makes sure that something is the case.  <code>(#$ensures-AgentProp AGENT SENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> ensures that <code>SENT</code> is true: <code>AGENT</code> determines whether <code>SENT</code> is true and, if it's not, intentionally performs an action that causes <code>SENT</code> to become true.  This implies that <code>SENT</code> expresses a contingent truth: although <code>SENT</code> is (at least upon being ensured) in fact true, it might not have been true (and perhaps wasn't true before being ensured).  Typically, immediately before ensuring <code>SENT</code>, <code>AGENT</code> wants <code>SENT</code> to be true (and might even believe it's likely to be true already), but is not certain that it's true.  
<p>
For example, I sometimes ensure that the back door is locked when I'm about to leave my house.  First, I go and check the door.  If I find it's locked, I'm done.  Or, if it's unlocked, I lock it, and then I'm done.  
<p>
See also #$ensures-SitProp.")
(comment CandleFlame "The collection of instances of #$Flame generated as a part of a candle-burning.  To count as an instance of #$CandleFlame, the flame must be on the wick of the candle; flames on nearby curtains caused by a candle-burning mishap don't count.")
(comment ObjectTypeByLightSource "An instance of #$FacetingCollectionType that facets #$Lit, the collection of all illuminated tangibles (see #$PartiallyTangible).  Each instance of #$ObjectTypeByLightSource is a collection of illuminated things whose instances all, intuitively, share a common source of illumination.  For example, #$Candlelit is the collection of all things whose source of illumination is the flame of a candle.")
(comment Sunlit "The collection of all tangible things that are illuminated (#$Lit) and whose source of illumination is an instance of #$Sun.  In #$AmbientConditionsMt, that instance is our sun (#$TheSun).")
(comment Moonlit "The collection of all tangible things that are illuminated (#$Lit) and whose source of illumination is a moon, reflecting light from a sun.  In #$AmbientConditionsMt, that particular moon is the Earth's moon (#$MoonOfEarth).")
(comment Starlit "The collection of all tangible things that are illuminated (#$Lit) by stars (other than the sun).")
(disjointWith Headscarf Hat)
(comment BaptisedSikhMalesShouldWearATurban "#$BaptisedSikhMalesShouldWearATurban denotes the Sikh religious requirement that baptized men wear turbans.")
(comment baptizedIntoReligion "<code>(#$baptizedIntoReligion PERSON RELIGION)</code> means that the person <code>PERSON</code> has undergone a baptism ceremony (c.f., #$Baptism) initiating him or her into <code>RELIGION</code>, an instance of #$Religion.")
(comment Headscarf "The collection of headscarves, headdresses frequently worn by women in Arab countries as part of a cultural norm (hijab) requiring modesty.")
(comment ceremonyOfBeliefSystem "<code>(#$ceremonyOfBeliefSystem CEREMONY BELIEF-SYSTEM)</code> means that the instance <code>CEREMONY</code> of #$Ceremony is a ceremony that takes place as part of (#$subSituations) a larger system of practices and beliefs, the instance <code>BELIEF-SYSTEM</code> of #$BeliefSystem.  For example, a particular rite of passage, such as receiving a tribal tattoo, might instantiate a cultural practice of that tribe, and thus occur as part of that tribe's belief system.   To relate religious ceremonies to their respective religions, use #$ceremonyOfReligion.")
(comment ceremonyOfReligion "<code>(#$ceremonyOfReligion CEREMONY RELIGION)</code> means that the instance <code>CEREMONY</code> of #$ReligiousCeremony is a ceremony of the religion <code>RELIGION</code>.  For example, a given wedding ceremony might be a Christian or Muslim ceremony.")
(comment Candlelit "An instance of #$ObjectTypeByLightSource.  Instances are illuminated tangibles whose source of illumination is the flame of a candle.")
(comment Lit "The collection of tangible objects or regions that are illumiated to some level beyond #$LitTooDarkForSeeing.  See also #$ObjectTypeByLightSource for facetings of #$Lit based on source of illumination, and #$ObjectTypeByLightIntensity, most instances of which are specializations of #$Lit, organized by intensity (brightness).")
(comment illuminatedObjectTypeLightSourceType "<code>(#$illuminatedObjectTypeLightSourceType OBJECT-TYPE SOURCE-TYPE)</code> means that <code>OBJECT-TYPE</code> is the instance of #$ObjectTypeByLightSource, a collection of illuminated objects/areas that share a common source-type of illumination, for which <code>SOURCE-TYPE</code> is that common source-type.  For example, <code>(#$illuminatedObjectTypeLightSourceType #$Sunlit #$Sun)</code> means that every object/area illuminated by sunlight has a sun (on earth, that would be our sun (#$TheSun)) as the source of that illumination.")
(comment ReligiousCodeOfConduct "A specialization of #$CodeOfConduct.  The collection of rules and/or expectations for governing the behavior, where the source of these prescriptions are religious in nature -- e.g., entrenched in the practices of some #$Religion or dictated by its holy texts or decreed by its leaders.")
(comment ReligiousNorm "The collection of individual rules/expectations that belong to some #$ReligiousCodeOfConduct.")
(comment mealServed "(#$mealServed <code>HAVING-A-MEAL</code> <code>MEAL</code>)
means that <code>MEAL</code> is the instance of #$Meal that is acted
on during <code>HAVING-A-MEAL</code>.  That is, <code>MEAL</code> is
the #$Meal that is served or otherwise provided for consumption during
<code>HAVING-A-MEAL</code>, and which is subsequently consumed during
that event.  Note that <code>MEAL</code> need not be <i>completely</i>
consumed during <code>HAVING-A-MEAL</code> -- portions may remain to
be finished later or discarded.  Thus, #$mealServed is not a
specialization of #$consumedObject.  Also, <code>MEAL</code> need not
be served to the participants in <code>HAVING-A-MEAL</code> in the
formal sense of #$ServingFoodOrDrink, since participants may fetch
their own portion of <code>MEAL</code> themselves.")
(comment cotemporalSubSituations "The #$predicateIntersection of #$subSituations and
#$cotemporal.  (#$cotemporalSubSituations <code>SIT</code>
<code>SUB-SIT</code>) means that (i) (#$subSituations <code>SIT</code>
<code>SUB-SIT</code>), and (ii) <code>SIT</code> and
<code>SUB-SIT</code> have the same temporal extent.  For example, many
instances of (#$WearingFn #$Tuxedo) have as sub-situations instances
of (#$WearingFn #$TuxedoJacket) which persist throughout them.  To
relate pairs of #$Events which satisfy this relation, use the
specialization #$cotemporalSubEvents.")
(comment IranianSupremeLeader "A specialization of #$Leader and #$IranianPerson.  #$IranianSupremeLeader is the highest position in the post-revolutionary Iranian government.  The Supreme Leader appoints the #$IranianHeadOfJudiciary, six (of twelve) members of the #$IranianGuardianCouncil, and the commanders of all the Iranian armed forces.  The Supreme Leader is chosen by the #$IranianAssemblyOfExperts.
<p>
Since the #$IranRevolution-1978-1979 there have been two Supreme Leaders: Ayatollah Khomeini (see #$RuhollahKhomeini) and the current leader Ayatollah Khamenei (see #$AliKhamenei).")
(comment presentTenseVersion "<code>(#$presentTenseVersion ROLE PRED)</code> identifies <code>PRED</code> as the instance of #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate that is a present tense version of <code>ROLE</code>, an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate. This means that both <code>(#$arg2SometimesIsa ROLE TYPE)</code> and <code>(ROLE SITUATION ARG2)</code> are timelessly true just in case there is some time interval <code>T</code> such that at every moment throughout T, <code>(PRED SITUATION ARG2)</code> and <code>(#$isa ARG2 TYPE)</code> holds.")
(comment correctsMixedRule "<code>(#$correctsMixedRule CORRECTION ERROR)</code> means that the #$Individual <code>CORRECTION</code>, a quoted instance of #$CycLAssertion, is a corrected version of <code>ERROR</code> a (different) quoted instance of #$CycLAssertion that incorrectly mixes temporally qualifiable relations with non-temporally qualifiable relations so as to produce falsehood.  See also #$mixedRule.")
(comment ControllingTraffic "The collection of instances of #$ControllingSomething in which the flow of vehicles on or through a system of paths is controlled.")
(comment ControllingRoadTraffic "The collection of instances of #$ControllingTraffic in which the flow of vehicles on a system of roadways is controlled.")
(comment Landscape "A specialization of #$TopographicalFeature.  Each landscape is an area of landforms that can be viewed by a perceptual agent, or can be represented visually in a landscape image (c.f., #$LandscapeImage).")
(comment Steeple "The collection of steeples, towers that form parts of building, especially churches.")
(disjointWith ForestClearing Forest)
(disjointWith Dump HumanOccupationConstruct)
(comment Dump "The collection of places where garbage winds up for indefinite storage.")
(comment LectureHall "The collection of lecture halls, large classrooms (see #$Classroom) where lectures (see #$Lecturing) are given.")
(comment LivestockCarrier "The collection of instances of #$Truck designed to transport livestock from place to place.")
(comment Refinery "The collection of plants (see #$HeavyIndustryFacility) where the processing/refining of substances takes place.")
(comment NewYorkTimes2001TextCorpus "A text corpus, currently used by the noun learner.")
(comment IranianForeignMinister "A specialization of #$MinisterOfForeignAffairs and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianForeignMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving other countries.")
(comment IranianDefenseMinister "A specialization of #$MinisterOfDefense and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianForeignMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving military defense.")
(comment MostafaPour-Mohammadi "The #$IranianInteriorMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment ManuchehrMottaki "The #$IranianForeignMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MostafaMohammadNajjar "The #$IranianDefenseMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment DavoudDanesh-Jaafari "The #$IranianEconomyMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment nounLearnerConjecturedTypeFromNounUsages "<code>(#$nounLearnerConjecturedTypeFromNounUsages USAGES COLLECTION)</code> means that where <code>USAGES</code> represents all observed usages of an unknown noun in the subject or direct object relation to verbs, the Noun Learner (see #$CycNounLearnerTool) conjectures that the noun denotes an instance or specialization of <code>COLLECTION</code>.  The Noun Learner's conjecture is based on statistical analysis of verb-argument cooccurrences in some text corpus (like, perhaps, the New York Times from 2001).  <code>USAGES</code> must take the form of an extensional list <code>(#$TheList SUBJECT-USAGES OBJECT-USAGES)</code> where <code>SUBJECT-USAGES</code> is the list of infinitive verb-strings of which the noun occurred as the subject and <code>OBJECT-USAGES</code> is the list of infinitive verb-strings of which the noun occurred as the direct object.  A given verb can appear more than once in a list to reflect multiple occurrences with the noun.<p>For example, <code>(#$nounLearnerConjecturedTypeFromNounUsages (#$TheList (#$TheList \"explode\") (#$TheList \"fire\" \"fire\" \"load\")) #$Weapon)</code> means that, given that a noun occurred once as the subject of the verb \"explode\", twice as the direct object of \"fire\", and once as the direct object of \"load\", the Noun Learner conjectures that it denotes an instance or specialization of #$Weapon.")
(comment nounLearnerConjecturedTypeForNounFromCorpus "<code>(#$nounLearnerConjecturedTypeForNounFromCorpus NOUN-STRING COLLECTION CORPUS)</code> means that, based on statistical analysis of its usages in the text corpus <code>CORPUS</code>, the Noun Learner conjectures that the noun <code>NOUN-STRING</code> denotes an instance or specialization of <code>COLLECTION</code>.  For example, <code>(#$nounLearnerConjecturedTypeForNounFromCorpus \"rutabaga\" #$EdibleStuff #$NewYorkTimes2001TextCorpus)</code> means that \"rutabaga\" is conjectured to denote an instance or specialization of #$EdibleStuff.  Be sure to use non-plural forms of nouns in queries.")
(disjointWith Airplane (CollectionDifferenceFn AirTransportationVehicle Airplane))
(comment Junkyard "Places where people go to buy possibly still-useful things that others have discarded as garbage.")
(comment SimilarityMetricFn "An instance of #$EvaluatableFunction, <code>(#$SimilarityMetricFn THING-1 THING-2)</code> evaluates to an instance of #$Real0-1 that indicates a measure of similarity between <code>THING-1</code> and <code>THING-2</code>. Higher values indicate a larger degree of similarity; by definition <code>(#$SimilarityMetricFn THING THING)</code> evaluates to 1.  <p>Similarity here is based on the ontology, and is computed as the intersection of the number of generalizations of <code>THING-1</code> and <code>THING-2</code> over the union of their generalizations, where a \"generalization\" of a thing <code>THING</code> includes every collection <code>COLL</code> such that <code>THING</code> is either an instance or specialization of <code>COLL</code>.")
(disjointWith SpaceInAHOC (CollectionDifferenceFn HumanScaleObject SpaceInAHOC))
(comment CycExtendedSystemFn "<code>(#$CycExtendedSystemFn SYSTEM)</code> denotes a derived version of the instance <code>SYSTEM</code> of #$ClassificationSystem that results from Cyc filling gaps in <code>SYSTEM</code> that were found by comparing <code>SYSTEM</code> to the Cyc ontology.")
(comment cycExtendedClassificationSystem "<code>(#$cycExtendedClassificationSystem INPUT OUTPUT)</code> identifies <code>OUTPUT</code> as an instance of #$ClassificationSystem that is a derived version of <code>INPUT</code>, another classification system, where <code>OUTPUT</code> was arrived at by Cyc filling gaps in <code>INPUT</code> that if found by comparing <code>SYSTEM</code> to the Cyc ontology.")
(comment transportMinister "A #$GovernmentPositionSlot for a cabinet-level position.  <code>(#$transportMinister COUNTRY PERSON)</code> means that <code>PERSON</code> is the transportation (or roads and transport) minister of <code>COUNTRY</code>.")
(comment IranianRoadsAndTransportMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianRoadsAndTransportMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving roads nd transportation.")
(comment speaker-OrganizationalRole "An #$OrganizationalPositionSlot and a specialization of #$chairperson-OrganizationalRole.  <code>(#$speaker-OrganizationalRole ORG SPEAKER)</code> means that <code>SPEAKER</code> presides over the proceedings or deliberations of <code>ORG</code> in the capacity of speaker.  As a rule, an organization can have only one speaker.")
(comment GholamaliHaddad-Adel "A member of the Iranian parliament (see #$Majlis-Iran) elected in February 2004; then elected speaker of that body in May 2004.  #$GholamaliHaddad-Adel is the first non-Cleric speaker of Iran's parliament since the 1979 revolution.")
(comment SystemPresentationDataMtFn "<code>(#$SystemPresentationDataMtFn SYSTEM)</code> denotes the microtheory that contains information about how <code>SYSTEM</code> should be presented in an application.")
(comment systemPresentationDataMt "<code>(#$systemPresentationDataMt SYSTEM MT)</code> identifies <code>MT</code> as an instance of #$DataMicrotheory that contains information about how <code>SYSTEM</code> should be presented in an application.")
(comment AirTrafficServiceRoute "A specialization of #$TravelRoute-Air. Each #$AirTrafficServiceRoute is a route within the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica designated for air travel by the #$FederalAviationAdministration. Instances include federal airways, jet routes and area navigation routes.")
(comment NAVAID "A specialization of #$NavigationDevice. Each #$NAVAID is a visual or electronic device which provides point-to-point guidance information or position data.")
(comment AirTrafficServiceRouteSegment "A specialization of #$TravelRoute-Air. Each #$AirTrafficServiceRouteSegment is a proper subpart of some #$AirTrafficServiceRoute. #$AirTrafficServiceRouteSegment are identified by the two #$NAVAIDS at its terminal points.")
(comment SpecialUseAirspace "A specialization of #$AirspaceRegion. Each #$SpecialUseAirspace is an airspace of defined dimension identified by area on the Earth's surface where activities must be confined because of their nature and/or where limitations may be imposed on aircraft operations not associated with those activities. Such airspaces are most commonly designated by military organizations and aviation regulatory agencies.")
(comment airspaceOfRegion "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that holds between a #$GeographicalRegion and its corresponding #$AirspaceRegion. Airspaces are conventionally defined as the volume directly above a #$GeographicalRegion. The top surfaces of the spaces thus designated are usually undefined, though in practice they are limited by the maximum operating altitude of #$AirTransportationVehicles.  (#$airspaceOfRegion REGION AIRSPACE), then, means that AIRSPACE is the volume directly above REGION.")
(comment AirspaceFn "An #$IndividualDenotingFunction that takes a #$GeographicalRegion and returns its corresponding #$AirspaceRegion. (#$AirspaceFn REGION) denotes the volume directly above REGION. See also #$airspaceOfRegion.")
(comment AirWarningLight "A specialization of #$NavigationDevice. Each #$AirWarningLight is a light or group of lights marking an obstacle which constitutes a danger to air navigation.")
(comment Foreshore "The #$Foreshore, or littoral zone, is that portion of a #$Seacoast located between the average high tide and average low tide.")
(comment AhmadJannati "An #$IranianPerson. #$AhmadJannati is the current (2005) chairman of the #$IranianGuardianCouncil.")
(comment MahmoudHashemiShahrudi "An #$IranianPerson.  #$MahmoudHashemiShahrudi  is the current (2005) #$IranianHeadOfJudiciary.")
(comment SQLOperatorSyntax-InverseInfix "A #$CSQLToSQLTranslationSyntaxDirective which directs the CSQL to SQL translator to invert the argument order and then use operator infix syntax. This term should appear only within a #$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator assertion. For example, the sentence
<pre>                                                                                              
(#$csqlOperatorToSQLOperator #$CSQLRegexStringMatch \"~\" #$PostgreSQL #$SQLOperatorSyntax-InverseInfix)
</pre>
tells SKSI's CycL to SQL translator that to translate a literal such as
<pre>
(#$CSQLRegexStringMatch \"[DL]og\" \"Dog\")
</pre>
into an SQL expression to be evaulated by a #$PostgreSQL DBMS, it should invert the argument order and then infix the operator between its arguments, so that the resultant SQL literal would be
<pre>
'Dog' ~ '[DL]og'.
</pre>")
(comment IranianAgricultureMinister "A specialization of #$MinisterOfAgriculture and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianAgricultureMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters agriculture.")
(comment IranianInteriorMinister "A specialization of #$MinisterOfInterior and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianInteriorMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving land.")
(comment IranianLaborMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianLaborMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving labor.")
(comment MinisterOfEducation "The collection of #$Minister-Governmental who
head the branch of a regional government responsible for its
#$EducationalSystem.")
(comment IranianEconomyMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianEconomyMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving the economy.")
(comment MinisterOfIntelligence "The collection of #$Minister-Governmentals who head the branch of a regional government responsible for its intelligence activities, including both #$IntelligenceGathering and analysis.")
(comment IranianIntelligenceMinister "A specialization of #$MinisterOfDefense and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianIntelligenceMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving intelligence gathering and analysis (see #$IntelligenceActivity).")
(comment IranianCommerceMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianCommerceMinister heads the Iranian governmental department that oversees commerce, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving commerce.")
(comment IranianJusticeMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianJusticeMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving the judicial system.
<p>
Cf. #$IranianHeadOfJudiciary.")
(comment IranianOilMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianOilMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving the production and trade of oil.")
(comment IranianHousingAndUrbanDevelopmentMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianHousingAndUrbanDevelopmentMinister heads the ministry that oversees housing and urban developement, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on these matters.")
(comment IranianEnergyMinister "A specialization of #$MinisterOfEnergy and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianCommerceMinister heads the Iranian governmental department that oversees the production and distribution of energy, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on energy-related matters.")
(comment IranianHealthMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianHealthMinister heads the Iranian governmental department that oversees public health issues, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on matters of public health.")
(comment IranianCooperativesMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianCooperativesMinister heads the Iranian ministry that oversees #$CooperativeOrganizations, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on matters pertaining to cooperatives.")
(comment IranianIndustriesAndMinesMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianIndustriesAndMinesMinister heads the Iranian ministry that oversees industry and mining, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving these things.")
(comment IranianScienceResearchAndTechnologyMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianScienceResearchAndTechnologyMinister heads the Iranian ministry that oversees scientific research and technology, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on scienticfic and technological matters.")
(comment IranianEducationMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianEducationMinister heads the Iranian ministry that oversees education, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on matters involving education.")
(comment templateFunctionForNCR "(#$templateFunctionForNCR RULE FUNC) means that FUNC is the function which appears as the arg0 of the #$ncRuleTemplate for RULE.  See also #$semanticRelationForNCR.")
(comment semanticRelationForNCR-Inverse "(#$semanticRelationForNCR-Inverse RULE RELATION) means that RELATION is the semantically noteworthy relation which is assumed to hold inversely between the head (arg2) and modifier (arg1) of a noun compound licensed by the #$NounCompoundRule RULE.  In general, we'll expect that RELATION will be either: i)  the arg0 of a NAT which appears as the arg2 of some #$ncRuleTemplate assertion; or ii) the second argument to some subcollection NAT which appears in this same position. See also #$semanticRelationForNCR.")
(comment semanticRelationForNCR "(#$semanticRelationForNCR RULE RELATION) means that RELATION is the semantically noteworthy relation which is assumed to hold between the head (arg1) and modifier (arg2) of a noun compound licensed by the #$NounCompoundRule RULE.  In general, we'll expect that RELATION will be either: i)  the arg0 of a NAT which appears as the arg2 of some #$ncRuleTemplate assertion; or ii) the second argument to some subcollection NAT which appears in this same position. See also #$semanticRelationForNCR-Inverse and #$templateFunctionForNCR.")
(comment PerformerOfFn "(#$PerformerOfFn ACTION) is, by definition, the collection of individuals, IND, such that (#$performedBy ACTION IND)")
(comment collectionOfPerformersOfAction "(#$collectionOfPerformersOfAction ACTION COLL) relates, by definition, ACTION to the collection of individuals, IND, such that (#$performedBy ACTION IND).  This the the corresponding predicate to #$PerformerOfFn. ")
(comment GovernmentRetirementProgram "A specialization of #$GovernmentProgram and #$RetirementSystem.  #$GovernmentRetirementProgram is the collection of all government-sponsored programs that provide for income for system enrollees after their retirement.  An example is the US #$SocialSecuritySystem.")
(comment IranianWelfareAndSocialSecurityMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianWelfareAndSocialSecurityMinister heads the Iranian ministry that oversees the welfare and social security programs, and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on welfare and social security matters.")
(comment IranianIslamicCultureAndGuidanceMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianIslamicCultureAndGuidanceMinister is the member of the #$IranianCabinet whose job is to advise the president on matters involving Islamic culture and Islamic law.")
(comment IranianPostTelegraphAndTelephoneMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianPostTelegraphAndTelephoneMinister heads the ministry that oversees the Iranian postal, telegraph, and telephone services.  S/he is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on matters pertaining to these services.
<p>
Note that this position has lately come to be commonly referred to as \"Minster of Communication and Information Technology\".")
(comment MohammadRezaEskandari "The #$IranianAgricultureMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MohammadRahmati "The #$IranianRoadsAndTransportMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.  #$MohammadRahmati is the only minister retained from the cabinet of the previous president, #$MohammedKhatami.")
(comment MahmudFarshidi "The #$IranianEducationMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MasudMir-Kazemi "The #$IranianCommerceMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MohammadSoleimani "The #$IranianPostTelegraphAndTelephoneMinister (aka <i>Minister of Communication and Information Technology</i>) serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MohammadNazemiArdakani "The #$IranianCooperativesMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment ParvizFatah "The #$IranianEnergyMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment KamranBaqueri-Lankarani "The #$IranianHealthMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MohammadSaidi-Kia "The #$IranianHousingAndUrbanDevelopmentMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment AliRezaTahmasbi "The #$IranianIndustriesAndMinesMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment Gholam-HosseinMohseni-Ejeie "The #$IranianIntelligenceMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MohammadHosseinSaferHarandi "The #$IranianIslamicCultureAndGuidanceMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment JamalKarimi-Rad "The #$IranianJusticeMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MohammadJahromi "The #$IranianLaborMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment KazemVaziriHamaneh "The #$IranianOilMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment MohammadMehdiZahedi "The #$IranianScienceResearchAndTechnologyMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment ParvizKazemi "The #$IranianScienceResearchAndTechnologyMinister serving in the #$IranianCabinet of president #$MahmoudAhmadinejad.")
(comment altitudeOfHighestPointInRegion "(#$altitudeOfHighestPointInRegion REG ALT) means that there is a place in REG which has the altitude (#$altitudeAboveSeaLevel) ALT and this ALT is greater than or equal to the altitudes of all the places within REG.")
(comment EastAsianCuisine "The collection of all #$EdibleStuff that is considered to be East Asian cuisine (authentic or otherwise).")
(comment WebGatheredGAFMicrotheory "A collection of microtheories in which are asserted GAFs that were gathered automatically from the Worldwide Web.")
(comment KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal-VerifyingHypothesizedInformation "The goal-category of wanting to verify hypothesized information.")
(comment goalTypeOfVerifyingHypothesizedInfoAboutTopic "The predicate corresponding to #$GoalOfVerifyingHypothesizedInfoAboutTopicFn (q.v.).")
(comment IranianConstructionJihadMinister "A specialization of #$Minister-Governmental and #$IranianPerson.  An #$IranianConstructionJihadMinister heads the ministry that oversees the <i>Construction Jihad</i> (a program to reduce poverty through rural development and construction), and is the member of the #$IranianCabinet who advises the president on matters involving the Construction Jihad.
<p>
This position was created shortly after the 1979 Iranian revolution.  But note that there is (at least as of late 2005) no longer a Minister of Construction Jihad in the Iranian cabinet.")
(comment IranianFirstVicePresident "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).  #$IranianFirstVicePresident is the collection of persons who hold the office of First Vice President in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment IranianVicePresident "A specialization of #$VicePresident-GovernmentOfficial and #$IranianPerson.  #$IranianVicePresident is the collection of persons who hold one of the vice presidential offices in the Iranian government.  For the specific VP offices, see the specializations of this collection.")
(comment IranianVicePresidentForAtomicEnergy "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).  #$IranianVicePresidentForAtomicEnergy is the collection of persons who hold the VP office for the development and production of atomic energy in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment IranianVicePresidentForManagementAndPlanning "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).  #$IranianVicePresidentForAtomicEnergy is the collection of persons who hold the VP office for the development and production of atomic energy in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment IranianVicePresidentForPhysicalEducation "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).  #$IranianVicePresidentForPhysicalEducation is the collection of persons who hold the VP office in charge of athletic training and competition in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment EnvironmentalProtectionGovernmentOrganization "The collection of government organizations whose purpose is to help protect the physical environment .  An #$EnvironmentalProtectionGovernmentOrganization might enforce environmental laws, conduct (or fund) environmental research, and/or run programs intended to improve the environment in some way.  Instances include the EPA (see #$EnvironmentalProtectionAgency-US).")
(comment IranianVicePresidentForLegalAndParliamentaryAffairs "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).  #$IranianVicePresidentForLegalAndParliamentaryAffairs is the collection of persons who hold the VP office for overseeing legal and parliamentary affairs in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment IranianVicePresidentForCulturalHeritageAndTourism "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).  #$IranianVicePresidentForCulturalHeritageAndTourism is the collection of persons who hold the VP office for that oversees the preservation and promotion of Iranian cultural heritage and promotes #$Tourism in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment IranianVicePresidentForEnvironmentalProtection "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).  #$IranianVicePresidentForEnvironmentalProtection is the collection of persons who hold the VP office for overseeing environmental protection in #$Iran.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment SurfaceProjection "A collection of unary functions that map subregions of one two-dimensional surface (the #$projectionSource) to subregions of another (the #$projectionTarget).  Each instance <code>PROJ</code> of #$SurfaceProjection (with source <code>SOURCE</code> and target <code>TARGET</code>) is a function that (i) is defined only on space regions subsumed by <code>SOURCE</code>, (ii) is defined on every <i>part</i> (see #$partOfSpaceRegion) of <code>SOURCE</code>, and (iii) takes anything it's defined on to some space region subsumed by <code>TARGET</code>.  
<p>
Note that, clause (ii) above notwithstanding, <code>PROJ</code> might <i>not</i> be defined on every region <i>subsumed by</i> (see #$spatiallySubsumes) <code>SOURCE</code>.  For example, a given #$ConicProjection might be defined on every subregion of a hemisphere except the pole.
<p>
Of particular interest are those surface projections whose source is all or part of a #$Spheroid (such as an idealization of the Earth's surface) and whose target is a #$FlatSurfaceRegion (such as might serve as the \"spatial universe\" of a two-dimensional #$Map).  See #$MapProjection.")
(comment IdempotentBinaryFunction "A specialization of #$BinaryFunction.  A binary function <code>FUNC</code> is an instance of #$IdempotentBinaryFunction if and only if it has the following property: for any thing <code>THING</code> that is an #$admittedArgument to both of <code>FUNC</code>'s argument-places, <code>(FUNC THING THING) = THING</code>.  
<p>
Examples of idempotent binary functions include #$CollectionIntersection2Fn, #$SpatialSumFn, and the binary version of #$Unity, viz. (#$Lambda (?X ?Y) (#$Unity ?X ?Y)).")
(comment EarthsGeoidSurface "The shape of the #$PlanetEarth's gravitational equipotential surface. That is, the #$EarthsGeoidSurface is the unique surface which satisfies the following conditions:
<p>
1. Given any #$Point, <code>POINT</code>, on the surface, the normal line at <code>POINT</code> (see #$normalLineAtPointOnSurface) is parallel to the #$GravitationalForceVector at <code>POINT</code>;
<p>
2. The magnitude of the #$GravitationalForceVector at <code>POINT</code> is constant for all <code>POINT</code>s on the surface.
<p>
A consequence of this definition is that the #$EarthsGeoidSurface is the surface to which the oceans would conform over the entire planet, if they were free to adjust to the combined effect of the Earth's mass attraction and the centrifugal force of the Earth's rotation.")
(comment normalLineAtPointOnSurface "A line is <i>normal</i> to a surface if and only if it is perpendicular to the tangent plane of the surface at the point at which the line intersects the surface. <code>(#$normalLineAtPointOnSurface POINT LINE SURFACE)</code> means that <code>LINE</code> is the #$Line-Straight which passes through the #$Point, <code>POINT</code>, and is perpendicular to (see #$perpendicularObjects) the tangent plane of the #$Surface, <code>SURFACE</code>, at <code>POINT</code> (see #$tangentPlaneAtPointOnSurface).")
(comment IranianVicePresidentForMartyrsAndWarVeteransAffairs "A specialization of #$IranianVicePresident (q.v.).   #$IranianVicePresidentForMartyrsAndWarVeteransAffairs is the collection of persons who hold the VP office for martyrs and war veterans affairs.  At any given time this collection will have at most one instance.")
(comment SiblingVariantHypothesizingIrrelevantPred "A specialization of #$CycLTerm.  Each instance of 
#$SiblingVariantHypothesizingIrrelevantPred is a CycL predicate that 
it is not appropriate to do sibling variant hypothesizing with.")
(comment binaryPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSToPOS2-1 "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  
<pre>
  (#$binaryPredicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSToPOS2-1 PREDICATE POS-1 POS-2)</pre> is shorthand for 
<pre>
  (#$predicateLicensesSenseExtensionFromPOSInArgToPOSInArg PREDICATE POS-1 2 POS-2 1)</pre>
where <code>PREDICATE</code> is restricted to instances of #$BinaryPredicate.")
(comment SiblingVariantHypothesizingPred "A specialization of #$Predicate.  Each instance of 
#$SiblingVariantHypothesizingPred is a CycL predicate that 
it is appropriate to do sibling variant hypothesizing with.")
(disjointWith Weapon (CollectionDifferenceFn PhysicalDevice Weapon))
(disjointWith SpatialThing-NonSituational Situation)
(disjointWith Situation (CollectionDifferenceFn SpatialThing Situation))
(comment SpatialThing-NonSituational "The collection of all spatial things that are not instances of #$Situation.  This is, in essence, the collection difference of #$SpatialThing and #$Situation.")
(comment ResultStandsInRelationFn "<code>(#$ResultStandsInRelationFn BINARY-PREDICATE)</code> denotes the instance <code>PRED</code> of #$MetaFunction and #$BinaryPredicate such that <code>(#$metaFunctionBasis PRED BINARY-PREDICATE)</code>.  For example,  <code>(#$ResultStandsInRelationFn #$isa)</code> denotes the same thing as #$resultIsa -- namely, a meta-function that relates a function to a collection such that any defined result of applying that function will be an instance of that collection.  Similarly, <code>(#$ResultStandsInRelationFn #$genls)</code> denotes the same thing as #$resultGenl -- a meta function that relates a function to a collection such that any defined result of applying it will be a specialization of that collection.  #$ResultStandsInRelationFn allows one to construct such meta functions on the fly, and avoids the need for separate forward rules for each such meta function.")
(comment modalFeatureTransfers "<code>(#$modalFeatureTransfers PRED-1 PRED-2 OPERATOR)</code> means that when <code>(PRED-1 . ARGS)</code> holds, <code>(OPERATOR (PRED-2 . ARGS))</code> also holds, where <code>OPERATOR</code> is a modal operator.  For example, <code>(#$modalFeatureTransfers #$foundingAgent #$createdBy #$willAlwaysBe)</code> means that when <code>(#$foundingAgent ORGANIZATION AGENT)</code> holds, <code>(#$willAlwaysBe (#$createdBy ORGANIZATION AGENT))</code> holds.")
(comment modalFeatureTransfers-Inverse "<code>(#$modalFeatureTransfers-Inverse PRED-1 PRED-2 OPERATOR)</code> means that when <code>(PRED-1 ARG1 ARG2)</code> holds, <code>(OPERATOR (PRED-2 ARG2 ARG1))</code> also holds, where <code>OPERATOR</code> is a modal operator.  For example, <code>(#$modalFeatureTransfers #$foundingAgent #$foundsOrganization #$willAlwaysBe)</code> means that when <code>(#$foundingAgent ORGANIZATION AGENT)</code> holds, <code>(#$willAlwaysBe (#$foundsOrganization AGENT ORGANIZATION))</code> holds.")
(disjointWith Situation StrategicTarget)
(comment SpatialPredicate "Each instance of #$SpatialPredicate is a spatial relation that can (only) hold between one or more #$SpatialThings, and is used to state something about its/their spatial location(s), position(s), orientation(s), size(s), or dimensionality.  
<p>
Note that when a #$SpatialPredicate has a #$Group as one of its arguments, a certain spatial relationship is usually being asserted to hold of all or most of the members of that group; but there are exceptions (e.g. #$in-Among) for which this is not the case.")
(comment SpatialCotemporalPredicate "The collection of instances of #$SpatialPredicates that are also instances of #$CotemporalPredicate.")
(comment lastSitOfTypeWithRolePlayerBefore "(#$lastSitOfTypeWithRolePlayerBefore <code>SIT</code>
<code>SIT-TYPE</code> <code>ROLE</code> <code>PLAYER</code>
<code>TEMP-THING</code>) means that (i) <code>SIT</code> is an
instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> in which <code>PLAYER</code> plays
<code>ROLE</code>, and (ii) of all such items, <code>SIT</code> is the
last to occur prior to <code>TEMP-THING</code>.  We can express that
<code>SIT</code> is the most recent situation of the relevant type --
the last to occur prior to the present moment -- by substituting #$Now
for <code>TEMP-THING</code>.  For example, we can express that
<code>SPEECH</code> is the most recent public speech by #$GeorgeWBush
by writing (#$lastSitOfTypeWithRolePlayerBefore <code>SPEECH</code>
#$PublicSpeaking #$senderOfInfo #$GeorgeWBush #$Now).")
(comment goalTypeOfVerifyingKnowledgeFromCorpusAboutTopic "The predicate corresponding to #$GoalOfVerifyingKnowledgeFromCorpusAboutTopicFn (q.v.).")
(comment TextLearnerConstantTypeByFunctionalArea "An instance of #$FacetingCollectionType and specialization of #$CycKBSubsetCollection.  Every instance of this collection is a subcollection of #$TextLearnerInfrastructureConstant such that each instance of that subcollection is a term that is primarily useful to TextLearner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) in its performance of a common high-level task-type.  For example, #$TextSimplificationConstant is the collection of constants that are primarily useful for text simplification.")
(comment TextLearnerHypothesisGenerationAndEvaluationConstant "An instance of #$TextLearnerConstantTypeByFunctionalArea, #$TextLearnerHypothesisGenerationAndEvaluationConstant is the collection of constants that are primarily useful to the TextLearner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) in its generation and evaluation of hypotheses.")
(comment TextSimplificationConstant "An instance of #$TextLearnerConstantTypeByFunctionalArea, #$TextSimplificationConstant is the collection of constants that are primarily useful to the TextLearner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) for doing text simplification.")
(comment LinkParserLinkageRepresentationConstant "The collection of instances of #$CycLConstant used to represent linkages produced by the Link parser. (See #$TheLinkParser.)")
(comment DocumentStructureRepresentationConstant "The collection of instances of #$CycLConstant used to represent the structure of documents.")
(comment CycLexiconPredicator "The collection of all instances of #$CycLPredicator that denote (see #$denotes) an instance of #$NLSemanticPredicate.")
(comment knowledgeAcquisitionGoalTypesForTopic "(#$knowledgeAcquisitionGoalTypesForTopic TOPIC KAG-TYPE) means that KAG-TYPE is a type of #$KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal that involves gaining knowledge about TOPIC.")
(comment nounStringOccursAsObjectForVerbStringsInCorpus "<code>(#$nounStringOccursAsObjectForVerbStringsInCorpus STRING USAGES SOURCE)</code> means that <code>USAGES</code> represents all observed usages of the noun with nonplural string <code>STRING</code> as an object in the instance of #$InformationStore <code>SOURCE</code>.  <code>USAGES</code> must take the form of an extensional list (see #$List-Extensional) of infinitive verb-strings.  A given verb can appear more than once in a list to reflect multiple occurrences with the noun.<p>For example, <code>(#$nounStringOccursAsObjectForVerbStringsInCorpus \"apple\" (#$TheList \"eat\" \"eat\" \"slice\" \"toss\") #$NewYorkTimes2001TextCorpus)</code> means that in the New York Times 2001 corpus, the noun 'apple' occurred twice as the object of the verb 'to eat,' once as the object of the verb 'to slice,' and once as the object of the verb 'to toss.'<p> See also #$nounStringOccursAsSubjectForVerbStringsInCorpus.")
(comment nounStringOccursAsSubjectForVerbStringsInCorpus "<code>(#$nounStringOccursAsSubjectForVerbStringsInCorpus STRING USAGES SOURCE)</code> means that <code>USAGES</code> represents all observed usages of the noun with nonplural string <code>STRING</code> as a subject in the instance of #$InformationStore <code>SOURCE</code>.  <code>USAGES</code> must take the form of an extensional list (see #$List-Extensional) of infinitive verb-strings.  A given verb can appear more than once in a list to reflect multiple occurrences with the noun.<p>For example, <code>(#$nounStringOccursAsSubjectForVerbStringsInCorpus \"apple\" (#$TheList \"fall\" \"fall\" \"look\" \"taste\") #$NewYorkTimes2001TextCorpus)</code> means that in the New York Times 2001 corpus, the noun 'apple' occurred twice as the subject of the verb 'to fall,' once as the subject of the verb 'to look,' and once as the subject of the verb 'to taste.'<p>See also #$nounStringOccursAsObjectForVerbStringsInCorpus.")
(comment AssertionDirectionSpecificationMicrotheory "A specialization of #$ProjectMicrotheory.  Each instance is a microtheory that contains data pertinent about the direction of assertions as required by a particular instance of #$Cyc-BasedProject.")
(comment assertionDirectionSpecificationMtForProject "<code>#$assertionDirectionSpecificationMtForProject MT PROJECT)</code> identifies <code>MT</code> as the instance of
#$AssertionDirectionSpecificationMicrotheory that contains assertion direction requirements for the project <code>PROJECT</code>.")
(comment AssertionDirectionChangeSpindle "An instance of #$MicrotheorySpindle whose members are all instances of #$AssertionDirectionSpecificationMicrotheory that contain assertion direction assertions required for a specific project.")
(comment liftTo "This predicate, used primarily for rules, allows one to override a particular microtheory dimension.  For example, '(#$liftTo (#$MtTimeDimFn (#$YearFn 1997)) (#$objectFoundInLocation OBJ LOC))' says that '(#$objectFoundInLocation OBJ LOC)' is true in the microtheory that would result from replacing the time index (see #$mtTimeIndex) of the current context (the result of querying '(#$indexicalReferent #$QueryMt ?X)') with '(#$MtTimeDimFn (#$YearFn 1997))'.  ")
(comment wantedForCriminalOffenseType "(#$wantedForCriminalOffenseType AGENT CRIMINAL-ACT-TYPE) holds just in case AGENT has committed/performed at least one instance of CRIMINAL-ACT-TYPE and they are wanted in some country for that act.  This is a temporally qualifiable relation.  This relation holds until they are no longer wanted for the act of type CRIMINAL-ACT-TYPE.")
(comment locationHasTargetType "(#$locationHasTargetType LOC TARG-TYPE) means that #$Place LOC presents some instance of #$FirstOrderCollection TARG-TYPE as a likely target for attackers.  For example, (#$locationHasTargetType #$CityOfNewYorkNY #$HumanHighOccupancyConstruct) means that New York presents some large group occupation construct as a target, and (#$locationHasTargetType #$CityOfNewYorkNY #$MassTransitSystem) means that New York presents a mass transit system as a target.")
(comment maximalInitialStringOmittingLast "(#$maximalInitialStringOmittingLast AGENT STRING) means that STRING is the sequence of initials of AGENT that is created by taking all of AGENT's names except for AGENT's last name and puiting them in sequence from first to next-to-last.")
(comment IntegratingSKSWithCycKB "A specialization of #$ActionOnObject.  Each instance of #$IntegratingSKSWithCycKB is an event in which some instance of #$StructuredKnowledgeSource <code>SKS</code> is integrated with the #$CycKB in the sense that, after the integration,  CycL queries can be issued against <code>SKS</code> to retrieve information appropriately, or Cyc can effectively transform the data in <code>SKS</code>.")
(comment CreatingCycLTermDenotingInstanceOfTypeFn "A unary reifiable function.  (#$CreatingCycLTermDenotingInstanceOfTypeFn COL) is the collection of events (the specialization of #$CreatingNewConceptInCyc) in which some instance of COL is created in a Cyc KB.")
(comment MappingPhysicalToLogicalSchema "A specialization of #$CWCreation.  Each instance of #$MappingPhysicalToLogicalSchema is an event in which the relationships between the indexical terms of logical and physical schemata of a structured knowledge source are stated.  The CycL predicates used to state this mapping are #$fieldEncoding and #$fieldDecoding (qq.v.).")
(comment sksIntegrated "<code>(#$sksIntegrated INTEG SKS)</code> means that in the event <code>INTEG</code> (an instance of #$IntegratingSKSWithCycKB), the structured knowledge source that is integrated is <code>SKS</code>.")
(comment termCreated "<code>(#$termCreated CREATION TERM)</code> means that in the event <code>CREATION</code> (an instance of #$CreatingNewConceptInCyc), the CycL term created is  <code>TERM</code>.")
(comment creatingCycLTermDenotingInstanceOfType "(#$creatingCycLTermDenotingInstanceOfType COL CREATION-TYPE) means that in any instance of the collection CREATION-TYPE, a term denoting some instance of COL is created.")
(comment forwardNonTriggerLiteral "An #$HLPredicate that is used to state which literals in a forward rule should not be allowed to re-trigger forward inference.  This is useful for placing pragmatic restrictions on the applicability of forward rules.  It is only useful when used via #$pragmaticRequirement on a forward rule.
<p>
A pragmatic requirement of the form
<pre>
(#$pragmaticRequirement (#$forwardNonTriggerLiteral FORMULA) RULE),
</pre>
where <code>FORMULA</code> is a literal in the antecedent of <code>RULE</code>, ensures that new assertions that are instantiations of <code>FORMULA</code> do not trigger <code>RULE</code>.")
(comment assertionAsserted "<code>(#$assertionAsserted ASSERTING ASSERTION)</code> means that in the event <code>ASSERTING</code> (an instance of #$AssertingAnAssertionToACycKB), the assertion asserted is <code>ASSERTION</code>.")
(comment heightOfObjectWRTDatum "<code>(#$heightOfObjectWRTDatum OBJECT HEIGHT DATUM)</code> means that when measured with respect to the #$VerticalDatum <code>DATUM</code> the height of <code>OBJECT</code> above or below the reference surface for <code>DATUM</code> (typically either the #$EarthsGeoidSurface or #$EarthsTopographicSurface) is <code>DISTANCE</code>.")
(comment ContourLine "A #$ContourLine is a geographical line that connects points of equal height above or below the reference surface of a vertical datum.")
(comment DepthContour "A #$DepthContour is an underwater contour line (#$ContourLine) of equal height below the reference surface of a #$TidalDatum.")
(comment Sounding "A place, ostensibly on the surface of a body of water, at which the distance above the reference surface for a tidal datum is known.")
(comment DepthCurve "A #$DepthCurve is a geographical line used in ocean navigation indicating that no #$Sounding of a lesser depth exists seaward of the line, but greater depths may occur on the shallow side of the line.")
(comment SpotElevation "A #$SpotElevation is a location for which the elevation relative to a vertical datum has been measured.")
(comment meetingParticipants "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$socialParticipants. (#$meetingParticipants MEETING PERSON) means that PERSON is one of the #$attendees of MEETING.")
(comment MooringDolphin "A specialization of #$FixedStructure. Instances of #$MooringDolphin are fixed man-made structures which are not connected to the shore and are used for mooring #$Watercraft-Surfaces. See also #$MooringABoat.")
(comment LightShip "A specialization of #$Ship. Instances of #$LightShip are distinctively marked manned vessels anchored or moored at a defined point to serve as an aid to maritime navigation.")
(comment gerund-Generic "An instance of #$GenericSpeechPartPredicate, #$gerund-Generic is the common generalization of #$gerund, #$gerund-Singular, and #$gerund-Plural.  <code>(#$gerund-Generic WORD STRING)</code> means that the lexical word (see #$LexicalWord) <code>WORD</code> has at least one gerundive form  <code>STRING</code>.  #$gerund-Generic is the predicate that corresponds to the speech part #$GerundiveNoun-NumberAgnostic.")
(comment GerundiveNoun-NumberAgnostic "The collection of all gerunds, that is, deverbal nouns formed (in English) with the suffix '-ing.'   Gerunds that have count-noun agreement are instances of #$GerundiveCountNoun, while those that have mass noun agreement are instances of #$GerundiveNoun.")
(comment NounLearnerConjectureFn "(#$NounLearnerConjectureFn SENTENCE) denotes the microtheory whose focal content is the noun learner conjecture <code>SENTENCE</code>, typically a #$denotation assertion for a word not known to Cyc for which the Noun Learner (see #$CycNounLearnerTool) has posited a meaning.")
(comment nounLearnerConjectureMtContent "<code>(#$nounLearnerConjectureMtContent CONJECTURE-MT SENTENCE)</code> identifies <code>SENTENCE</code> as the conjecture produced by the Noun Learner (see #$CycNounLearnerTool) that is the focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) of the instance <code>CONJECTURE-MT</code> of #$Microtheory.")
(comment AbstractLexiconSpecification "The base class from which all useful lexicons are derived.  Because it is an abstract class, it should not be expected to be fully functional, and should not be used in applications, except for inheritance purposes.")
(comment objectClassName "(#$objectClassName SPEC NAME) means that SubL instances of the class SPEC should be created using NAME.  This is the main hook between SubL and CycL to be used when shadowing SubL objects in CycL.")
(comment lexiconSuperClass "(lexiconSuperClass SUB SUPER) means that SUB is a subclass of SUPER.  In practice this means that if something is not specified in the KB on SUB, it should be inherited from SUPER.")
(comment SemTransLexiconSpecification "Specification for Lexicon objects that produce semTrans semantics, for use by any application that desires simple semTranses for words")
(comment CyclifierLexiconSpecification "Specification for Lexicon objects that produce cyclifier semTrans semantics.  Lexicons intended for use by any of the #$Cyclifiers must be instances of #$CyclifierLexicon")
(comment PackedCyclifierLexiconSpecification "A specialization of #$CyclifierLexiconSpecification.  Instances of #$PackedCyclifierLexiconSpecification will return semTrans semantics that will pack as much ambiguity as possible into a single lexical entry.  For example, if the word \"blork\" has two denotations, #$Blork and #$Blonk, that fit in the same syntactic contexts, the lexical entry returned by a #$PackedCyclifierLexiconSpecification will be of the form (#$isa :NOUN (#$TheIntendedOneOfFn \"blork\" #$Blork #$Blonk)).  This contrasts with a #$CyclifierLexiconSpecification, which would return two separate entries: (#$isa :NOUN #$Blork) and (#$isa :NOUN #$Blonk).")
(comment TermLexiconSpecification "A specialization of #$LexiconSpecification.  A term lexicon returns 'denots' of words, such as #$Dog for \"dog\".  This contrasts with #$SemTransLexiconSpecifications which would return (#$isa :NOUN #$Dog) for the word \"dog\".")
(comment disallowedLexicalMt "(#$disallowedLexicalMt LEXICON MT) means that in any instance of LEXICON, assertions in MT will not be visible.  This property is inherited all genls of LEXICON")
(comment allowedLexicalMt "(#$allowedLexicalMt LEXICON MT) means that in any instance of LEXICON, assertions in MT should be visible.")
(comment TextualInferenceLexiconSpecification "A specialization of #$CyclifierLexiconSpecification.  It has access to determiner semantics that are different from those used by #$CyclifierLexiconSpecification.")
(comment sentenceWeight "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that relates a (quoted) instance of #$CycLSentence to a #$RealNumber.  <code>(#$sentenceWeight SENTENCE NUM)</code> means that <code>NUM</code> is the weight assigned to <code>SENTENCE</code> in the current context.
<p>
This predicate is intended to be used for representing Markov logic networks in Cyc.  When used in this way, the weight associated with a sentence reflects its strength as a constraint on possible worlds.  The predicate may have other uses, however.
<p>
#$sentenceWeight should not be confused with #$likelihood (q.v.).")
(comment assertionWeight "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot and (typed) specialization of #$sentenceWeight (q.v.) that relates a (quoted) instance of #$CycLAssertion to a #$RealNumber.  <code>(#$assertionWeight ASSERTION NUM)</code> means that <code>NUM</code> is the weight assigned to <code>ASSERTION</code> in the current context.
<p>
This predicate is intended to be used for representing Markov logic networks in Cyc.  When used in this way, the weight associated with an assertion reflects its strength as a constraint on possible worlds.  The predicate may have other uses, however.
<p>
#$assertionWeight should not be confused with #$likelihood (q.v.).")
(comment AlchemyUSGovtOrgTestMt "This microtheory contains assertions about Cyclists to be exported into Alchemy for testing purposes.")
(comment MooringABoat "A specialization of #$ParkingAVehicle. Instances of #$MooringABoat involve fastening some #$Watercraft-Surface to a #$StationaryObject, usually with ropes or cables. Boats are usually moored to #$Docks and #$MooringDolphins, but it is also possible to moor a boat to a rock or a tree. Anchoring a boat at sea is not an example of #$MooringABoat.")
(comment CableDrivenConveyance "A specialization of #$Conveyance-Stationary. Each #$CableDrivenConveyance consists of a long loop of #$Cable that is mechanically cycled. Transport via a #$CableDrivenConveyance may as simple as a handle attached to the cable, or involve seats or entire cabins suspended from it. Examples include #$SkiLifts and cable cars. Trolleys are not #$CableDrivenConveyances, as the cable is not providing motive force.")
(comment Ship-PermanentlyMoored "#$Ship-PermanentlyMoored is the subcollection of #$Ships that have been permanently moored, for whatever reason. They can be navigation hazards.")
(comment occupiedActors "This predicate relates an agent to an event that s/he is occupied with participating in, in the sense of being busy  with or absorbed in the event.  <code>(#$occupiedActors EVENT AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is occupied with participating in <code>EVENT</code>.  
<p>
Note  that #$occupiedActors is a specialization of #$deliberateActors,  and thus the foregoing is not to be confused with <code>AGENT</code>'s being  occupied with <code>EVENT</code> in the sense that s/he merely #$thinksAbout <code>EVENT</code> in a focused way, as (say) a contemporary historian might be  \"occupied\" with the reign of King Louis XIV.  For this latter sense of 'occupied', see #$occupiedWith.")
(comment satisfiesModifierSyntacticConstraintsForNCR "(#$satisfiesModifierSyntacticConstraintsForNCR NODE RULE) means that the #$SyntacticNode NODE satisfies all of the syntactic constraints (cf. #$NounCompoundRuleConstraint-Syntactic) imposed by #$NounCompoundRule RULE on the modifier.")
(comment violatesConstraintForNCR "(#$violatesConstraintForNCR NODE RULE CONSTR) means that the #$SyntacticNode NODE violates the syntactic constraint CONSTR (cf. #$NounCompoundConstrain) for the #$NounCompoundRule RULE.")
(comment compoundRequiresGeneralizationOfWRTConstraint "(#$compoundRequiresGeneralizationWRTConstraint MOD-NODE MOD-DENOT HEAD-NODE HEAD-DENOT RULE CONSTR) means that it may be possible to hypothesize a noun-compound interpretation for the MOD-NODE and HEAD-NODE (w. the semantics of MOD-DENOT and HEAD-DENOT, respectively) by generalizing the #$NounCompoundRuleConstraint CONSTR of the #$NounCompoundRule RULE.")
(comment GeneralizedNCRFn "(#$GeneralizedNCRFn OLD-RULE OLD-CONSTRAINT NEW-CONSTRAINT) denotes a new #$NounCompoundRule derived from OLDRULE by replacing OLD-CONSTRAINT (cf. #$NounCompoundRuleConstraint) with NEW-CONSTRAINT.")
(comment generalizationOfNCR "(#$generalizationOfNCR NEW-RULE OLD-RULE OLD-CONSTRAINT NEW-CONSTRAINT)means that NEW-RULE is a new #$NounCompoundRule derived from OLDRULE by replacing OLD-CONSTRAINT (cf. #$NounCompoundRuleConstraint) with NEW-CONSTRAINT.")
(comment prospectiveRuleForCompound "(#$prospectiveRuleForCompound MOD MOD-DENOT HEAD HEAD-DENOT RULE) means that the phrase consisting of MOD and HEAD, under the semantic interpretations MOD-DENOT and HEAD-DENOT, may meet the syntactic and semantic constraints specified by RULE.")
(comment Actor "The collection of people whose job it is to entertain others by taking on the roles of other characters in #$DramaticPerformances.  Note that an #$Actor may be #$Male or #$Female.")
(comment CycLFullyGroundAtomicSentence "A #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of both #$CycLFullyGroundExpression and #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all and only the \"fully ground\" atomic sentences of CycL.  A <i>fully ground</i> expression is one that contains no variables, free or bound.  (An expression that contains bound variables but no free ones is merely \"ground\"; see e.g. #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence.)  An <i>atomic sentence</i> is one that is not constructible out of another sentence or sentences via #$LogicalConnectives or #$Quantifiers.  (But note that a CycL atomic sentence <i>is</i> constructible out of other, normally <i>non-sententential</i>, expressions: a #$Predicate expression and its arguments.  An atomic sentence might even contain another <i>sentence</i> as a constituent, if the latter appears as an argument to the atomic sentence's initial predicate; e.g. the second argument in a #$beliefs sentence.)  #$CycLFullyGroundAtomicSentences are commonly referred to as \"GAFs\" (for \"ground atomic formula\"), though that informal label is most often used so as to apply to any #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence.")
(comment CycLExpression "The collection of all (and only) expressions in the CycL language.  This includes all constants, variables, and formulas (i.e. non-atomic terms and sentences).  Each CycL expression is, trivially, a <i>syntactically</i> well-formed expression of CycL; but note that it might or might not be \"<i>semantically</i> well-formed\" (see #$CycLExpression-Assertible).  Also note that, since the CycL syntax allows any CycL expression to be used as a term, #$CycLExpression is coextensional with #$CycLTerm (q.v.).")
(comment tributaries "A #$SpatialPredicate that is used to indicate the tributaries of a given stream or river.  (#$tributaries STREAM TRIBUTARY) means that STREAM has TRIBUTARY as  one of its tributaries.  That is, TRIBUTARY is a #$Stream (q.v.) that flows into, and not out of, STREAM.  For example, the #$MississippiRiver has the #$WisconsinRiver, #$OhioRiver, #$ArkansasRiver, #$RedRiverOfTexas, and others as #$tributaries. Compare this predicate with its two negations <code>#$distributaries</code> and <code>#$anabranches</code>. In cases where STREAM and TRIBUTARY are both #$Rivers, consider whether the more specialized predicate #$fedByConfluenceOfRivers (q.v.) is applicable.")
(comment distributaries "A #$SpatialPredicate that is used to indicate the distributaries of a given stream or river. (#$distributaries STREAM DISTRIBUTARY) means that STREAM has DISTRIBUTARY as one of its distributaries. That is, DISTRIBUTARY is a #$Stream (q.v.) that flows out of STREAM and does not rejoin it. Compare this predicate with its two negations <code>#$tributaries</code> and <code>#$anabranches</code>.")
(comment anabranches "A #$SpatialPredicate that is used to indicate the anabranches of a given stream or river. (#$anabranches STREAM ANABRANCH) means that STREAM has ANABRANCH as one of its anabranches. That is, ANABRANCH is a #$Stream (q.v.) that flows out of and later rejoins STREAM. Compare this predicate with its two negations <code>#$tributaries</code> and <code>#$distributaries</code>.")
(comment PredicateUnionFn "A binary #$PredicateDenotingFunction that takes two predicates to their \"union\" or \"disjunction\": the predicate that is by definition their <i>nearest possible common generalization</i>.  <code>(#$PredicateUnionFn PRED1 PRED2)</code> is the predicate <code>UNIONPRED</code> such that, for any sequence <code>ARGS</code> and context <code>MT</code>, <code>(UNIONPRED . ARGS)</code> holds in <code>MT</code> if and only if either <code>(PRED1 . ARGS)</code> or <code>(PRED2 . ARGS)</code> holds in <code>MT</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$PredicateUnionFn #$biologicalDaughters #$biologicalSons) = #$biologicalOffspring.
<p>
If either <code>PRED1</code> or <code>PRED2</code> is of fixed arity <code>N</code>, then the other must either be of arity <code>N</code> or be variable-arity, and <code>UNIONPRED</code> is itself of arity <code>N</code>; if both <code>PRED1</code> and <code>PRED2</code> are variable-arity, then so is <code>UNIONPRED</code>.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$predicateUnion.")
(comment IsraeliSettlement "The collection of all Israeli settlements in the West Bank and Gaza Strip, which #$Israel established and has held since the end of the #$Arab-IsraeliWar-1967. #$Israel formally withdrew its settlements from the #$GazaStrip and ended its military occupation of Gaza on September 12, 2005.")
(comment Saddle-TerrainFeature "A broad, open pass crossing a ridge or between hills or mountains.")
(disjointWith ControlledLand (CollectionDifferenceFn GeopoliticalEntity ControlledLand))
(comment causes-PropSit "(#$causes-PropSit PROPOSITION SITUATION) means that the instance of #$CycLSentence-Assertible of PROPOSITION causes the instance of #$Situation SITUATION.")
(comment Guyot "A #$Guyot is a flat-topped #$Seamount.")
(comment ForestManagement "The collection of all activities undertaken to establish or manage #$Forests.")
(comment ForestStation "A #$ForestStation is a group of buildings and facilities located in some #$Forest that is intended to support forest management activities.")
(comment ObservationTower "The collection of observation towers, typically located in #$Forests and used to visually detect forest fires.")
(comment ArtifactualFeatureType "#$ArtifactualFeatureType is the collection of
#$CartographicFeatureTypes which are #$Artifacts, as opposed to
naturally occuring. Positive examples include #$WaterReservoir, #$Canal,
and #$Airfield. Negative examples include #$Mountain, #$Ocean, and
#$Mesa. Also see #$NaturalFeatureType, the corresponding collection
for naturally occuring feature types.")
(comment Sebkha "A specialization of both #$Desert and #$ClaySoilRegion. Each instance of #$Sebkha is a natural depression in arid or semi-arid regions whose bed is covered with salt encrusted clayey soil. #$Sebkhas can be hazardous obstacles to land transportation.")
(comment CollegeCampus "The grounds of a #$College.")
(comment gafGatheringTriggerSentence "<code>(#$gafGatheringTriggerSentence GATHER SENT)</code> means that
in the instance of #$GAFGathering, <code>GATHER</code>, the sentence
that triggers the generation of search strings is <code>SENT</code>.")
(comment numberOfSearchResultsObtained "<code>(#$numberOfSearchResultsObtained SEARCH N)</code> means
that in the instance of #$LookingForAThingOrTypeOfThing,
<code>SEARCH</code>, the number of items found that meet the search
criteria is <code>N</code>.  For example, if in the event
<code>Search001</code> a person sets out to find all of the unmatched
socks behind their washing machine, and they find 7 such socks, then
<code>(#$numberOfSearchResultsObtained Search001 7)</code> is true.")
(comment numberOfResultsThatSupportBinding "<code>(#$numberOfResultsThatSupportBinding SEARCH TERM N)</code>
means that in the <code>SEARCH</code>, which is either an instance of
#$StringBasedSearching or #$GAFGathering, <code>N</code> is the number
of search results found that justify <code>TERM</code> as a binding
for the open variable in the #$gafGatheringTriggerSentence (for
<code>SEARCH</code> if <code>SEARCH</code> is an instance of
#$GAFGathering, or for the instance of #$GAFGathering that is a
super-event of <code>SEARCH</code>, if search is an instance of
#$StringBasedSearching).")
(comment ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult "A specialization of #$FindingSomething (q.v.).  Each instance of
#$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult is an event in which a result of
an instance of #$StringBasedSearching is obtained.")
(comment rankOfResult "<code>(#$rankOfResult OBTAINING-A-RESULT N)</code> means that in the
instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult,
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, the result obtained (some instance of
#$ComputerDataArtifact) is ranked <code>N</code> by the search engine
that is the #$searchEngineUsed in the instance of
#$StringBasedSearching that is a super-event of
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>.")
(comment focalBasisString "<code>(#$focalBasisString OBTAINING-A-RESULT STRING)</code> means that
in the instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult,
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, <code>STRING</code> is the specific
string occurring within the result returned that qualifies the result
as a result that meets the search criteria.  For example, if the
#$searchStringUsed in the super-event of
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code> which is an instance of
#$StringBasedSearching is \"Austria is a *\", then it could be that
<code>(#$focalBasisString OBTAINING-A-RESULT \"Austria is a democracy\")
</code> holds.")
(comment evincesBinding "<code>(#$evincesBinding OBTAINING-A-RESULT TERM)</code> means that in
the instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult,
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, <code>TERM</code> is the #$CycLTerm
that is supported by the result returned as the binding for the
#$gafGatheringTriggerSentence of the #$GAFGathering which is a
super-event of <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>.")
(comment GAFGatheringToGenerateAssertions "A specialization of #$GAFGathering (q.v.).  Each instance of
#$GAFGatheringToGenerateAssertions is an event in which GAFs are
gathered with the aim and intent of immediately placing them into a
source microtheory spindle as assertions.")
(comment Seamount "A #$Seamount is an underwater #$Mountain rising from the seafloor that does not reach to the surface of the #$EarthsOceanSea.")
(comment OxbowLake "An #$OxbowLake is a crescent-shaped #$Lake (often temporary) that is formed when a meander of a #$River is cut off from the main channel.")
(comment gatheredGAFs "<code>(#$gatheredGAFs GATHER GAF)</code> means that in the instance
of #$GAFGathering, <code>GATHER</code>, <code>GAF</code> is a
#$CycLSentence-Askable that is gathered.")
(comment stagesEvent "A specialization (via #$genlInverse) of #$responsibleFor  and the #$predicateUnion (q.v.) of #$directingAgent and #$primarySponsorOfEvent (qq.v.).  <code>(#$stagesEvent EVENT STAGER)</code> means that <code>STAGER</code> either directs, or plays a crucial sponsorship role in, <code>EVENT</code>.")
(comment primarySponsorOfEvent "A specialization of #$sponsorOfEvent and (via #$genlInverse) #$responsibleFor (qq.v.).  <code>(#$primarySponsorOfEvent EVENT SPONSOR)</code> means that <code>SPONSOR</code> plays a crucial sponsorship role in <code>EVENT</code>, such that without <code>SPONSOR</code>'s support (everything else being equal) <code>EVENT</code> would not have occurred.  Primary-sponsorship roles include providing a significant portion of the funding, materials, incentive, or other support for <code>EVENT</code> and its participants.
<p>
Note that, although this predicate is not strictly functional in its second argument-place, a given social event will often have no more than one primary sponsor.")
(comment SaltFlat "A #$SaltFlat is a shallow basin or flat where salt accumulates after periodic inundation or the evaporation of a intermittent body of water.")
(comment Mound-NGA "A specialization of #$Hill. #$Mound-NGA is a specialized concept representing a low, isolated, rounded #$Hill.")
(comment Ravine "A specialization of #$Crevice-Topographical. Each instance of #$Ravine is a deep, narrow, steep-walled passage. #$Ravines are typically smaller than #$Gorges.")
(comment Inlet "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater. Instances of #$Inlet are narrow waterways extending into the land, or connecting a #$Bay or #$Lagoon with a larger body of water.")
(comment IntermittentWetlandRegion "#$IntermittentWetlandRegion is the subcollection of #$WetlandRegions that are periodically dry.")
(comment RailroadStop "An #$ExistingObjectType.  Each instance
of #$RailroadStop is a #$TransportFacility where one
#$Train-TransportationDevice at a time stops to pick up and drop off
passengers (usually at predetermined times). #$RailroadStops imply a very minimal sense of #$TransportFacility and do not necessarily include #$Buildings. #$RailroadStops inside or adjacent to #$Buildings are more accurately represented by #$RailroadStation.")
(comment Keld "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater. #$Kelds are deep, relatively still areas in #$Rivers.")
(disjointWith SmallGroup Mob)
(comment SpatialThing-Localized "A specialization of both #$SpatialThing and #$TemporalThing.  This is the collection of all spatial things, tangible or intangible, that can meaningfully be said to have location or position in the empirical universe.   
<p>
Examples of spatially-localized things include all #$PartiallyTangible things, such as trees and ships, as well as certain #$Intangible spatial things, like the #$EarthsEquator.  Also included are those events that can be pinned-down to specific places (see #$Event-Localized), and thus all #$PhysicalEvents.  Excluded from this collection are any #$SpatialThings that are <i>not</i> localized, such as -- arguably -- purely abstract geometrical figures (e.g. a Platonic sphere). 
<p>
Note that a fictional or imaginary object (such as Frodo, Captain Queeg, or #$HAL9000-TheComputer) is typically localized with respect to the universe of the fictional/imagnary context in which it is found, and so is an instance of #$SpatialThing-Localized with respect to that context.")
(comment GeneralizedSpatialSumFn "A unary function that takes a set of spatial things (see #$SpatialThingSet) of the same basic type (i.e. #$PartiallyTangible, #$SpaceRegion, or #$Event-Localized) to their mereological sum or union.  Where <b>SET</b> is a set of tangible objects (space regions, spatially-localized events), (#$GeneralizedSpatialSumFn <b>SET</b>) is the tangible object (space region, spatially-localized event) that is the spatial sum of all the elements of <b>SET</b>.  
<p>
This function is a generalization of the function #$GeneralizedSpaceRegionSumFn, which takes equidimensional sets of homogeneous space regions (see #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet) to their mereological sum.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$generalizedSpatialSum.  Also see #$GeneralizedSpatialProductFn.")
(comment generalizedSpatialSum "A binary predicate that relates a set of spatial things (see #$SpatialThingSet) of the same basic type (i.e. #$PartiallyTangible, #$SpaceRegion, or #$Event-Localized) to their mereological sum or union.  <code>(#$generalizedSpatialSum SUM SET)</code> means that <code>SUM</code> is the spatial sum of all the elements of <code>SET</code>.  
<code>SUM</code> is itself of the same basic type as the elements of <code>SET</code>; e.g. if <code>SET</code> is a set of tangible objects, then <code>SUM</code> is a tangible object.
<p>
This predicate is a generalization of the predicate #$generalizedSpaceRegionSum, which relates a set of equidimensional, homogeneous space regions (see #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet) to their sum.
<p>
See the corresponding function #$GeneralizedSpatialSumFn.  Also see #$generalizedSpatialProduct.")
(comment GeneralizedSpatialProductFn "A unary function that takes a set of spatial things (see #$SpatialThingSet) of the same basic type (i.e. #$PartiallyTangible, #$SpaceRegion, or #$Event-Localized) to their mereological product or intersection.  Where <b>SET</b> is a set of tangible objects (space regions, spatially-localized events), (#$GeneralizedSpatialProductFn <b>SET</b>) is the tangible object (space region, spatially-localized event) that is the spatial product of all the elements of <b>SET</b>.  
<p>
This function is a generalization of the function #$GeneralizedSpaceRegionProductFn, which takes equidimensional sets of homogeneous space regions (see #$EquidimensionalSpaceRegionSet) to their mereological sum.
<p>
#$GeneralizedSpatialProductFn is #$undefined (or, in certain cases, returns a #$NullSpaceRegion) for any set of spatial things such that some of its elements are #$spatiallyDisjoint from others.  It is not defined on any set whose members are not all of the same basic spatial type.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$generalizedSpatialProduct.  Also see #$GeneralizedSpatialSumFn.")
(comment ProbabilityDistributionFunction "A #$FunctionType.  Intuitively, a #$ProbabilityDistributionFunction is a #$UnaryFunction that distributes probability amongst the elements of a set of outcomes (or possible worlds).  More formally, a #$ProbabilityDistributionFunction is a function <b>Pr</b> from a #$SetOrCollection <b>S</b> (the \"sample space\") into #$Real0-1 such that the results of applying <b>Pr</b> to all elements of the sample space add up to 1.  <b>Pr</b> must also be additive for the (possibly infinite) union of disjoint sets in some sigma algebra of <b>S</b>.")
(comment probabilityOfSet "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that relates a #$Set-Mathematical to a #$Real0-1.  (#$probabilityOfSet <b>SET N</b>) means that the generic probability of <b>SET</b> (with respect to the #$mtSampleSpace of the current context) is <b>N</b>.  This is the predicate corresponding to #$ProbabilityOfSetFn (q.v.).")
(comment suicideAttacker "A specialization of both #$perpetrator and
#$organismKilled-Suicide.  (#$suicideAttacker <code>ATTACK</code>
<code>PERP</code>) means that <code>PERP</code> is a #$perpetrator of
<code>ATTACK</code> who intentionally kills herself as part of
executing the attack.")
(comment SouthernCone-SouthAmerica "A #$GeographicalRegion and the southern portion of #$ContinentOfSouthAmerica.  More precisely, #$SouthernCone-SouthAmerica comprises the territories of #$Argentina, #$Chile, #$Uruguay, #$Paraguay, and the southern part of #$Brazil.")
(comment WestAfrica "A subregion of #$ContinentOfAfrica.  #$WestAfrica is generally taken to include the following countries: #$Benin, #$BurkinaFaso, #$IvoryCoast, #$Gambia, #$Ghana, #$Guinea, #$Guinea-Bissau, #$Liberia, #$Mali, #$Mauritania, #$Niger, #$Nigeria, #$Senegal, #$SierraLeone, and #$Togo.  It is considered by some also to include the island countries of #$CapeVerde and #$SaoTomeAndPrincipe.
<p>
Note that #$WestAfrica does <i>not</i> constitute all of (#$WesternRegionFn #$ContinentOfAfrica), but is rather a proper subregion of the latter.")
(disjointWith AltarServer Cleric)
(comment AltarBoy "The collection of young, male instances of #$AltarServer.")
(comment AltarServer "The collection of altar servers, typically young males (see #$AltarBoy) who assist a priest during his or her performance of a church service, by performing minor tasks, such as carrying candles, accepting the eucharistic gifts on behalf of the priest, etc.  The altar server is a traditional role in Catholic Mass and in Anglican/Episcopal services (where altar servers are referred to as \"acolytes\").")
(disjointWith Robe Dress)
(comment personTypeAssociatedClothingType "A binary predicate that relates an instance of #$PersonTypeByActivity to types of clothing and accessories that instances of that person-type normally wear when performing the official duties or characteristic activities that type.  (#$personTypeAssociatedClothingType PERSON-TYPE SOMETHING-TO-WEAR-TYPE) means that if an instance of <code>PERSON-TYPE</code> is engaged in the characteristic activities associated with that type, he or she will be wearing (see #$wearsClothing) an instance of <code>SOMETHING-TO-WEAR-TYPE</code> during some significant portion of that activity.")
(comment Robe "The collection of instances of #$Gown that typically have sleeves, and differs from #$Gown primarily in that certain types of gowns -- specifically gowns that are also dresses (e.g., wedding gowns, formal ball gowns, etc.) -- are excluded.")
(comment Tunic "A specialization of #$Robe and, during the time of Ancient Rome, a specialization of #$MensClothing.  Tunics were worn under togas on formal occasion, and are still worn today, but typically only as a liturgical vestment.")
(comment latestDateOfFact "(#$latestDateOfFact FACT DATE) means that DATE is the
latest-ending date of any context in which FACT is known to hold.
I.e., FACT is known to hold 'at' DATE, and there is no other date
known at which FACT holds that ends any later than DATE.")
(comment earliestDateOfFact "(#$earliestDateOfFact FACT DATE) means that DATE is the
earliest-starting DATE of any context in which FACT is known to hold.
I.e., FACT is known to hold 'at' DATE, and there is no other date
known at which FACT holds that begins any earlier than DATE.")
(comment ArmedForcesMedic "A subcollection of #$MilitaryPerson and #$Medic.  The instances of this collection are all military medics that serve in some branch of the armed forces for some nation's military.  Note that this is not simply the intersection of #$MilitaryPerson and #$Medic.  For instance, a paramedic that is in the National Guard, but is not acting in the capacity of a medic in that organization is not a member of this collection. ")
(comment Medic "A subcollection of  #$MedicalCareProfessional.  The instances of this collection are persons who are medically trained in some way or other including doctors, nurses, paramedics, and military medics.  Note that this represents the most general meaning of \"medic\".  Most uses of the word \"medic\" refer the the more specialized #$ArmedForcesMedic.")
(comment ActionAttributeLevelQuantity "A specialization of #$Quantity.  Each instance of #$ActionAttributeLevelQuantity is a particular <i>level</i> or amount of a certain type of quantity that an #$Action, or the performance of it, can exhibit.  
<p>
For example, (#$HighAmountFn #$Dangerousness) is an #$ActionAttributeLevelQuantity
possessed by actions that are highly dangerous.  (#$MediumAmountFn #$Gracefulness)  is an action-attribute-level quantity exhibited by any moderately graceful performance of an action.  The latter example is also an instance of the more specialized collection #$PerformanceLevelQuantity (q.v.).")
(comment actsInCapacityAsMember "(#$actsInCapacityAsMember AGENT OCCUPATION-TYPE ORGANIZATION) holds just in case AGENT is a member of ORGANIZATION and works as OCCUPATION-TYPE for ORGANIZATION.  For instance, asserting the conjunction of (#$isa AGENT #$ComputerScientist) and (#$employees ORGANIZATION AGENT) isn't sufficient to represent the situation where AGENT works as a computer scientist in ORGANIZATION -- the mere conjunction is consistent with the fact that AGENT is a computer scientist somewhere else and moonlights as a janitor for ORGANIZATION.")
(comment facilityIntendedForPersonType "(#$facilityIntendedForPersonType FACILITY PERSON-TYPE) means
that FACILITY is intended primarily as a resource for instances of
PERSON-TYPE. Use of FACILITY need not be strictly limited to such
persons -- others may make use of it under certain circumstances --
but its primary purpose is use by or service of instances of
PERSON-TYPE. The 'purpose' of a facility in this sense is determined
by the policies or goals of the agents who oversee it. Examples of
facilities whose use is so qualified include a #$CommunityCenter for
members of a minority population, a fitness center provided by a
company for its employees, and a university student health center.")
(comment actionUsedQuantity "(#$actionUsedQuantity AGENT ACTION PERFORMANCE-QUANT) holds just in case AGENT experiences the degree of experience represented by PERFORMANCE-QUANT while performing ACTION.  The third argument of this predicate will most often be occupied by a NART created by an instance of #$GenericValueFunction.  The argument to the value function will, in those cases, be an instance of #$PerformanceLevelQuantity.  that instance will mark the type of experience and the value function will return the #$Quantity of that experience expressed by the value function.  For instance, '(#$actionUsedQuantity #$WilliamShakespeare ReadingHamlet04 (#$LowAmountFn #$PhysicalEffortLevel))' means that #$WilliamShakespeare found his reading of Hamlet (on that particular occasion) to require only a slight physical effort.")
(comment PhysicalEffortLevel "A specialization of #$PerformanceLevelQuantity.  This is the collection of all the instances of #$PerformanceLevelQuantity that represent various levels of physical effort with respect to events.  Instances of this collection are usually formed with the #$GenericValueFunctions")
(comment MentalEffortLevel "A specialization of #$PerformanceLevelQuantity.  This is the collection of all the instances of #$PerformanceLevelQuantity that represent various levels of mental effort with respect to events.  Instances of this collection are usually formed with the #$GenericValueFunctions")
(comment latestDateOfFactOfCalendarCoveringType "(#$latestDateOfFactOfCalendarCoveringType ASSERT CAL-TYPE DATE) means that either DATE is itself an instance of CAL-TYPE and (#$latestDateOfFact ASSERT DATE), or that there is a date, SOME-DATE, such that (#$latestDateOfFact ASSERT SOME-DATE), such that DATE is a 'component'of SOME-DATE (i.e., (#$termFormulas DATE SOME-DATE)), DATE is an instance of CAL-TYPE, and there is no other component of SOME-DATE that is an instance of CAL-TYPE that is any later than DATE.  Intuitively, (#$latestDateOfFactOfCalendarCoveringType ASSERT CAL-TYPE DATE) means that DATE is the latest date of type CAL-TYPE at which the content expressed by ASSERT is known to be true in the KB.")
(comment earliestDateOfFactOfCalendarCoveringType "(#$earliestDateOfFactOfCalendarCoveringType ASSERT CAL-TYPE DATE) means that either DATE is itself an instance of CAL-TYPE and (#$earliestDateOfFact ASSERT DATE), or that there is a date, SOME-DATE, such that (#$earliestDateOfFact ASSERT SOME-DATE), such that DATE is a 'component'of SOME-DATE (i.e., (#$termFormulas DATE SOME-DATE)), DATE is an instance of CAL-TYPE, and there is no other component of SOME-DATE that is an instance of CAL-TYPE that is any earlier than DATE.  Intuitively, (#$latestDateOfFactOfCalendarCoveringType ASSERT CAL-TYPE DATE) means that DATE is the earliest date of type CAL-TYPE at which the content expressed by ASSERT is known to be true in the KB.")
(comment Theorem-Proposition "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$Theorem-Proposition is a significant #$Proposition that has been proved on the basis of explicit assumptions.  Examples include #$BayesTheorem and #$GoedelsFirstIncompletenessTheorem.")
(disjointWith Angling Trawling)
(disjointWith Angling Archery)
(comment Angler "The collection of instances of #$Fisherman who fish with a rod and reel.")
(comment professionalTypeOfType "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate and #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate.  <code>(#$professionalTypeOfType PROFESSIONAL-TYPE TYPE)</code> identifies <code>PROFESSIONAL-TYPE</code> as the subcollection of instances of #$PersonWithOccupation such that the #$occupation of each instance is <code>TYPE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$professionalTypeOfType #$ProfessionalTennisPlayer #$TennisPlayer)</code> identifies #$ProfessionalTennisPlayer as the collection of persons who play tennis professionally.  #$professionalTypeOfType is the predicate that corresponds to the function #$ProfessionalFn (see #$functionCorrespondsToPredicate).")
(comment Angling "The collection of instances of #$Fishing in which the performer uses a rod and hook.")
(comment Smuggler "The collection of all smugglers.  Smugglers are people who perform acts of #$Smuggling.")
(comment WNRankedCandidateDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default "A #$BinaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction that returns a (#$ListOfTypeFn #$Set-Mathematical) where the elements of each set are (quoted) instances of #$CycLExpression.   An evaluation of an expression of the form (#$WNRankedCandidateDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default LEXICAL-WORD POS)  function consults the #$WordNetKnowledgeBase #$WordNet-Version2_0 to find synonyms for the #$LexicalWord LEXICAL-WORD based on the #$SpeechPart POS.  Based on the #$LexicalWords that it finds, it will return known #$denotations for those words for POS.  The ordering of the list is based on the assumed frequency of the meanings of the word.  As its arguments indicate, this function operates to relate a #$LexicalWord to (possibly many) instances of #$CycLExpression, it does not associate instances of #$WordNetSynset and  #$CycLExpression, therefore, its results will reflect the ambiguity in mapping from words to concepts.  The 'default' aspect of the name indicates that this functions employs the simplest method for going from lexical words to concepts.  Other functions could incorporate different available methods.")
(comment WNAllCandidateDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default "A #$BinaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction that returns a #$Set-Mathematical of (quoted) instances of #$CycLExpression.   An evaluation of an expression of the form (#$WNAllCandidateDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default LEXICAL-WORD POS)  function consults the #$WordNetKnowledgeBase #$WordNet-Version2_0 to find synonyms for the #$LexicalWord LEXICAL-WORD based on the #$SpeechPart POS.  Based on the #$LexicalWords that it finds, it will return all known #$denotations for those words for POS.  As its arguments indicate, this function operates to relate a #$LexicalWord to (possibly many) instances of #$CycLExpression, it does not associate instances of  #$WordNetSynset and #$CycLExpression, therefore, its results will reflect the ambiguity in mapping from words to concepts.  The 'default' aspect of the name indicates that this functions employs the simplest method for going from lexical words to concepts.  Other functions could incorporate different available methods.")
(comment WNRankedHypernymDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default "A #$BinaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction that returns a (#$ListOfTypeFn #$Set-Mathematical) where the elements of each set are (quoted) instances of #$CycLExpression.   An evaluation of an expression of the form (#$WNRankedHypernymDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default LEXICAL-WORD POS) function consults the #$WordNetKnowledgeBase #$WordNet-Version2_0 to find direct hypernyms of all the synsets for the #$LexicalWord LEXICAL-WORD based on the #$SpeechPart POS.  A direct hypernym of the #$WordNetSynset BASE-SS is a more general #$WordNetSynset where there is an asserted, rather than implied,  #$wnHypernym relation.    Based on the #$LexicalWords that it finds, it will return known #$denotations for those hypernym words for POS.  The ordering of the list is based on the assumed frequency of the meanings of the word.  As its arguments indicate, this function operates to relate a #$LexicalWord to (possibly many) instances of #$CycLExpression, it does not associate instances of #$WordNetSynset and  #$CycLExpression, therefore, its results will reflect the ambiguity in mapping from words to concepts.  The 'default' aspect of the name indicates that this functions employs the simplest method for going from lexical words to concepts.  Other functions could incorporate different available methods.")
(comment WNAllHypernymDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default "A #$BinaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction that returns a #$Set-Mathematical of (quoted) instances of #$CycLExpression.   An evaluation of an expression of the form (#$WNAllHypernymDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default LEXICAL-WORD POS)  function consults the #$WordNetKnowledgeBase #$WordNet-Version2_0 to find direct hypernyms for the #$LexicalWord LEXICAL-WORD based on the #$SpeechPart POS.   A direct hypernym of the #$WordNetSynset BASE-SS is a more general #$WordNetSynset where there is an asserted, rather than implied,  #$wnHypernym relation.    Based on the #$LexicalWords that it finds, it will return all known #$denotations for those hypernym words for POS.  As its arguments indicate, this function operates to relate a #$LexicalWord to (possibly many) instances of #$CycLExpression.  It does not associate instances of #$WordNetSynset and  #$CycLExpression, therefore, its results will reflect the ambiguity in mapping from words to concepts.  The 'default' aspect of the name indicates that this functions employs the simplest method for going from lexical words to concepts.  Other functions could incorporate different available methods.")
(comment hasNonAspectualQuantity "The most general of the #$NonAspectualQuantitySlots (q.v.): binary predicates that relate a thing to a #$NonAspectualQuantity (q.v.) that characterizes it.   An object's <i>non-aspectual</i> quantities are those that it has simpliciter, without being qualified to any particular aspect or dimension of the object.  Thus <code>(#$hasNonAspectualQuantity OBJ NONASPECT-QUANT)</code> means that <code>NONASPECT-QUANT</code> is a unqualified property of <code>OBJ</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$hasNonAspectualQuantity HotPotato004 (#$DegreeCelsius 70))</code>  means that HotPotato004 has the temperature of 70 degrees celsius.  (In contrast, a #$Distance such as (#$Inch 5) can only characterize a 3-dimensional object with respect to a particular dimension; e.g. it might be HotPotato004's <i>length</i>.)")
(comment DirectorOfAdministration "Someone who is in charge of administrative duties in an organization.")
(comment Presence "A #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and a specialization of #$SocialQuantity.  #$Presence is the collection of degrees to which an agent can have influence, importance, or high-profile in some region, situation, group, or field.")
(comment controlsIndex "(#$controlsIndex ORGANIZATION INDEX) means that the instance of #$Organization ORGANIZATION controls, or establishes the value of the #$EconomicIndexPredicate INDEX). For example, (#$controlsIndex #$FederalNationalMortgageAssociation #$conventionalFixedRateActualYield) means that Fannie Mae is responsible for setting the Conventional Fixed Rate Actual Yield, an index commonly used to determine the #$fullyIndexedRate for loans.  Other commonly used indexes are the Treasury Constant Maturity index, the 11th District Cost of Funds Index, and the prime rate.")
(comment Dome "The collection of domes, man-made instances of #$HemisphericalSolid used most frequently as roofs (see #$DomedRoof) but also as stand-alone structures, particularly geodesic domes (see #$GeodesicDome).")
(comment ShelteringArtifact "The collection of artifacts that are used primarily to provide shelter to some object.")
(comment Strut "The collection of struts, devices used to add support to the shape or stability of an object.")
(comment GeodesicDome "A specialization of #$Dome.  Geodesic domes are domes whose support-structure consists of a series of intersecting struts that form  triangles along the surface of the dome.  Each strut of each triangle simultaneously contributes to a line of struts that, were the dome a sphere, trace a geodesic, or great circle, around its surface.  These lines of struts  distribute stress across the triangles.")
(comment levelOfPresenceIn "A ternary predicate that relates an agent to an object or situation (see #$TemporallyExistingThing) to a level of #$Presence (q.v.).  <code>(#$levelOfPresenceIn AGENT TEMP-THING PRESENCE)</code> means that in <code>TEMP-THING</code> <code>AGENT</code> exhibits <code>PRESENCE</code>.  <code>TEMP-THING</code> might be a #$GeographicalRegion in which <code>AGENT</code> is located or is active, a #$Group of which <code>AGENT</code> is a member, or an #$Event (or #$StaticSituation) in which <code>AGENT</code> plays a role (or is a constituent).  For example,
<pre>
  (#$levelOfPresenceIn #$UnitedStatesArmy #$Iraq (#$HighAmountFn #$Presence))
</pre>
means that the US Army has a strong presence in Iraq.")
(comment EmbodiedAgent "A specialization of #$IndividualAgent and #$Agent-NonGeographical (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$EmbodiedAgent is an individual partially tangible agent.  
<p>
A notable specialization of #$EmbodiedAgent is #$PerceptualAgent-Embodied (q.v.).  Most instances of #$EmbodiedAgent are also instances of that specialization, but some embodied agents may not have the capacity to perceive anything.  For instance, there might be an embodied creature that has no perception of the world, yet can choose what to think about.  Such a creature would be an embodied agent but not a perceptual agent.")
(comment orgPresentInRegion "A binary predicate that relates #$Organizations to #$GeographicalThings.  <code>(#$orgPresentInRegion ORG REGION)</code> means that <code>ORG</code> \"has a presence\" in <code>REGION</code>.  This implies that at least some of <code>ORG</code>'s members (see the #$hasMembers) are in <code>REGION</code>, and are involved in activities somehow connected to <code>ORG</code>.  Note that it does <i>not</i> imply that <code>ORG</code> has any physical quarters (see #$physicalQuarters) in <code>REGION</code>.")
(comment DirectionFromToFn "This function calculates the geographic bearing from one spatial thing to another using their #$latitude and #$longitude coordinates and the law of cosines from spherical trigonometry. <code>(#$DirectionFromToFn THING1 THING2</code>) returns the angle between the segment of the great circle joining #$AxialNorthPoleOfEarth and <code>THING1</code> and the segment of the great circle joining <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>.")
(comment syntacticSubNodes "The most general relation that holds between a syntactic node (#$SyntacticNode (q.v.)) in a parse tree and any node (including itself) in the sub-tree for which that node is the parent. <code>(#$syntacticSubNodes SUPER SUB)</code> means that <code>SUB</code> is a node included in such a sub-tree, such that <code>SUPER</code> is the top-level node.")
(comment syntacticNodeSyntacticHead "<code>(#$syntacticNodeSyntacticHead NODE HEAD)</code> identifies <code>HEAD</code> as the syntactic head of the instance <code>NODE</code> of #$SyntacticNode.  Note that where <code>(#$isa NODE #$LexicalNode)</code>, <code>(#$syntacticNodeSyntacticHead NODE NODE)</code> holds.")
(comment syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeArgumentNodes "<code>(#$syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeArgumentNodes V-NODE ARG-NODE)</code> identifies <code>ARG-NODE</code> as an instance of #$SyntacticNode that is a node for one of the arguments (subject or object) for the verbal phrase (either a sentence of a VP) represented by <code>V-NODE</code> in the parse tree (see #$ParseTree) that contains both nodes.")
(comment syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeSubjectNode "<code>(#$syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeSubjectNode V-NODE SUBJ-NODE)</code> identifies <code>SUBJ-NODE</code> as the instance of #$SyntacticNode that represents the subject of <code>V-NODE</code>, an instance of #$VerbalPhraseSyntacticNode.")
(comment SyntacticNodeTypeByCategory "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection.  #$SyntacticNodeTypeByCategory is the collection of all specializations of #$SyntacticNode whose instances share a common linguistic category (an instance of #$SententialConstituentType). For example, #$SyntacticNode-VP is the collection of instances of syntactic node whose syntactic category (see #$syntacticNodeCategory) is #$VerbPhrase.")
(comment VerbalPhraseSyntacticNode "The collection of syntactic nodes (#$SyntacticNode, q.v.) that represent verbal phrases (phrases that can take a grammatical subject, such as sentences or verb phrases).")
(comment syntacticNodeTypeCategory "<code>(#$syntacticNodeTypeCategory NODE-TYPE CATEGORY)</code> identifies <code>NODE-TYPE</code> as the instance of #$SyntacticNodeTypeByCategory, every instance of which is a syntactic node (#$SyntacticNode, q.v.,) representing a sentential constituent (see #$SententialConstituent) of type <code>CATEGORY</code>, an instance of #$SententialConstituentType.  For example, <code>(#$syntacticNodeTypeCategory #$SyntacticNode-VP #$VerbPhrase)</code> identifies #$SyntacticNode-VP as the collection of nodes that represent verb phrases.")
(comment syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeDirectObjectNode "<code>(#$syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeDirectObjectNodes V-NODE OBJ-NODE)</code> means that <code>OBJ-NODE</code> is the syntactic node (#$SyntacticNode, q.v.) that represents the direct object of the verbal phrase represented by <code>V-NODE</code>, an instance of #$VerbalPhraseSyntacticNode.")
(comment syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeIndirectObjectNode "<code>(#$syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeIndirectObjectNodes V-NODE OBJ-NODE)</code> means that <code>OBJ-NODE</code> is the syntactic node (#$SyntacticNode, q.v.) that represents the indirect object of the verbal phrase represented by <code>V-NODE</code>, an instance of #$VerbalPhraseSyntacticNode.")
(comment syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeObjectNodes "<code>(#$syntacticVerbalPhraseNodeObjectNodes V-NODE OBJ-NODE)</code> means that <code>OBJ-NODE</code> is a syntactic node (#$SyntacticNode, q.v.) that represents an object of the verbal phrase represented by <code>V-NODE</code>, an instance of #$VerbalPhraseSyntacticNode.")
(comment logicallyTrueSentence "A specialization of #$trueSentence and #$necessary that is used to state that a given CycL sentence is logically true (in the intended semantics of CycL) -- i.e. it is equivalent to #$True.  Note that this is not a #$KBDependentRelation: we do not assume that all logical truths are provable in Cyc.  Also, note that #$logicallyTrueSentence is #$notAssertible.")
(comment DeReAttitudePredicate "A specialization of #$CognitiveAttitudePredicate (q.v.).  A de re attitude predicate is a relation that (loosely stated) holds among an agent, a specified thing, and a property, such that the agent bears a certain <i>de re</i> propositional attitude (e.g. de re belief, de re doubt, de re knowledge) regarding that object's having that property.  More precisely, each instance <code>DE-RE-PRED</code> of #$DeReAttitudePredicate is a ternary predicate that takes as arguments an #$IntelligentAgent, a #$CycLPropositionalSentence (that is also an #$CycLSentence-Assertible), and an integer; and <code>(DE-RE-PRED AGENT SENT N)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> has a certain de re attitude to the thing <code>OBJ</code> denoted by the <code>N</code>th-place constituent of <code>SENT</code>, with respect to <code>OBJ</code>'s (the thing itself, regardless of what expression is used to denote it) having the property that the proposition expresssed by <code>SENT</code> attributes to it.  For example,
<pre>
 (#$believesOfArg #$Meyer (#$isa #$Patches-Cat #$TabbyCat) 2)
</pre>
means that Meyer believes of Patches that he is a tabby cat.  A defining feature of de re attitude predicates is that they allow substitutions of coreferring terms in the specified (i.e. <code>N</code>th) position within the predicate's own second argument (i.e. <code>SENT</code>).  Thus, if the expression 'GarysPet'  happens also to denote Patches, then the sentence displayed above entails
<pre>
  (#$believesOfArg #$Meyer (#$isa GarysPet #$TabbyCat) 2) .
</pre>
Cf. #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot.  And see the function #$DeReVersionFn, which takes as argument a \"de dicto\" propositional attitude slot (e.g. #$beliefs) and returns the corresponding #$DeReAttitudePredicate (#$believesOfArg).")
(comment bordersOn-AgentAgnostic "A common generalization (see #$genlPreds) of #$bordersOn and #$politiesBorderEachOther, used to state that two objects share a border, without requiring inter-argument consistency with regard to agency or region-hood. <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic GEO-THING1 GEO-THING2)</code> means that the instances <code>GEO-THING1</code> and <code>GEO-THING2</code> of #$GeographicalThing border each other, allowing for a CycL representation of, e.g., \"Canada borders the Atlantic Ocean,\" namely <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic #$Canada #$AtlanticOcean)</code> that is neither unduly complex (e.g., <code>(#$thereExists ?TERRITORY (#$and (#$territoryOf ?TERRITORY #$Canada) (#$bordersOn ?TERRITORY #$AtlanticOcean)))</code>, nor requires reasoning from a special \"dualist\" microtheory that wouldn't otherwise be used for a given application.  #$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic is conservative (see #$conservativeViaArg) via #$territoryOf in both its arguments, allowing one to conclude, e.g., <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Mexico)</code> from <code>(#$bordersOn (#$TerritoryFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica) (#$TerritoryFn #$Mexico))</code>.  It is conservative via (#$InverseBinaryPredicateFn #$territoryOf) in both arguments, as well, allowing the derivation of <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic (#$TerritoryFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica) (#$TerritoryFn #$Mexico))</code> from <code>(#$politiesBorderEachOther #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Mexico)</code>.  As a corollary, \"mixed\" derivations, e.g., <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica (#$TerritoryFn #$Mexico))</code> are also possible.")
(comment borderingNeighbors "(#$borderingNeighbors SITUATION GEO-THING) means that in the instance SITUATION of #$BorderingSomething, GEO-THING is one of the instances of #$GeographicalThing that borders another instance of that collection.")
(comment accusedOf "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates an #$Accusing to an #$Action.  <code>(#$accusedOf ACCUSING ACTION)</code> means that the agent playing the role of #$accused in <code>ACCUSING</code> is accused of performing <code>ACTION</code>.  See also #$accusedOfRoleInEvent, #$accusedOfRoleInType, and #$accusedOfPerformingActionType.")
(comment primarilyContainsInfo "A binary predicate that relates an #$InformationBearingThing (q.v.) to the #$AspatialInformationStore (q.v.) that is its primary information content. <code>(#$primarilyContainsInfo IBT INFO)</code> means that <code>IBT</code> contains <code>INFO</code> as its main or primary content or meaning.  <code>IBT</code> might also #$containsInformation additional bits of information, but not primarily.  
<p>
Note that <code>IBT</code>'s primary information content is supposed to be \"objective\" in that it is unique and such that if somebody  interpreted <code>IBT</code> as primarily containing some other piece of information instead of <code>INFO</code>, s/he would be misinterpreting or  incompletely interpreting <code>IBT</code>.  In this way the predicate differs  from its generalization #$containsInformation, by which a given <code>IBT</code> can simultaneously contain different -- even conflicting -- bits of information based on the fact that different people interpret it in different ways.  
<p>
The relation #$primarilyContainsInfo is often referred to in ordinary English by the phrase '(overtly) means', and it is most often applied to uses of language (e.g. a written sentence).  It would be misleading, however, to think of <code>IBT</code>'s primary information content as its \"intended meaning\", as <code>IBT</code> need not have been created or used with a direct intention on the part of some agent that it convey <code>INFO</code>.  An intentionless software agent or a mechanical device, for example, might create and send a meaningful message.")
(comment completeInfoTransferred-Explicitly "A specialization of the predicate #$infoTransferred-Explicitly that relates an #$InformationTransferEvent (q.v.) to the <i>totality</i> of information that it explicitly conveys.  <code>(#$completeInfoTransferred-Explicitly TRANS INFO)</code> means that <code>INFO</code> is the most inclusive piece of information (see #$AspatialInformationStore) explicitly transferred in <code>TRANS</code>.  It follows that <i>any</i> information that is explicitly transferred in <code>TRANS</code> is #$subInformation (q.v.) of <code>INFO</code>.  
<p>
Note that, for certain types of information-transfers, the type of (total) information explicitly transferred can be specified more precisely.  For example, 
if <code>TRANS</code> is a #$CommunicationAct-Single (q.v.), <code>INFO</code> is an instance of #$Information-Content; if <code>TRANS</code> is an #$Informing (q.v.), <code>INFO</code> is an instance of #$Proposition.")
(comment Actuary "A specialization of #$DeskWorker and #$Professional.  Each instance of #$Actuary is a professional in the insurance business whose job is to estimate how money can be contributed to a pension plan, insurance, or other related area to fund that plan over time.")
(comment AquariumOrganization "Instances of #$AquariumOrganization  are organizations, usually non-profit but not always, which keep a collection of aquatic organisms for research and display. ")
(comment authorizedToControl "(#$authorizedToControl CONTROLLER THING) means that
CONTROLLER is authorized (in some fashion, see below) to control the
activities or disposition of THING.  #$authorizedToControl is
essentially a <i>de jure</i> version of #$controls -- it expresses the
<i>authority</i> to control, rather than actual control.  These two
relationships often go together, of course.  But it is also possible
to have such authority but not use it (or be unable to), and possible
to exert control without relevant authorization.  For example, if
PERSON1 #$owns THING and PERSON2 #$steals it, then PERSON1 retains the
(legal) authority to control THING, but actual control passes to
PERSON2.  The 'authority' expressed by this predicate often has a
legal basis, as in the case of ownership.  But certain other sources
may also confer authority.  For example, such authority might attach
to a certain position within an organization (i.e., be recognized
within the organization), even if that authority is not legally
enforceable (in fact, even if the organization is illegal, e.g., a
criminal organization).")
(comment occursDuring "#$occursDuring is a binary predicate relating an event to a time interval in which it occurs.  <code>(#$occursDuring EVENT TIMEINTERVAL)</code> means that the event <code>EVENT</code> occurs entirely within the time interval <code>TIMEINTERVAL</code>.  Thus, <code>(#$occursDuring EVENT TIMEINTERVAL)</code> implies that <code>(#$temporallySubsumes TIMEINTERVAL EVENT)</code>. For example, if <code>ASSN</code> is the assassination of Martin Luther King, Jr., then <code>(#$occursDuring ASSN (#$YearFn 1968))</code>.  See also #$dateOfEvent.")
(disjointWith TwoWheeledMotorizedVehicle RecreationalVehicle)
(comment Moped "A specialization of #$MotorScooter.  #$Mopeds are motor scooters with limited power and speed (30-35 mph).  They often have pedals.
The moped classification was designed to allow the use of small motorized vehicles while avoiding certain safety restrictions and licensing charges attached to more powerful scooters and motorcycles.")
(comment TheCollection "A collection denoting function.  <code>(#$TheCollection ARG1,...,ARGN)</code> denotes the collection that is, by definition, the collection of individuals such that each one of them is identical with one of the individuals in the series ARG1 through ARGN.")
(comment pegDerivedSentenceFromInterpretation "A sentence of the form (#$pegDerivedSentenceFromInterpretation PEG CYCL MT) means that the #$Microtheory MT is a possible interpretation of the #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance PEG and that CYCL is a #$CycLSentence that is both entailed by the content of MT (see #$mtFocalContent) and that is known to be true from the content model of PEG (see, #$contentModelFocalPeg).")
(comment derivedCandidatePegInterpretations-CycL "A sentence of the form (#$derivedCandidatePegInterpretations-CycL PEG CYCL) means that one possible interpretation of the #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance PEG is the #$CycLSentence CYCL.  More specifically, CYCL is a sentence that is (1) entailed by some other proposed interpretation of PEG and (2) CYCL is known to be true relative to the content model mt of PEG (see #$contentModelFocalPeg.)")
(comment LeaderTypeByActivityTypeInfluenced "A collection of specializations of #$Leader.  Each instance of #$LeaderTypeByActivityTypeInfluenced is such that all of the individuals in its extent share a common sphere of influence, understood as a type of activity (a subcollection of #$PurposefulAction), instances of which are shaped, in part, by the leadership efforts of those individuals.  To tie an instance of #$LeaderTypeByActivityTypeInfluenced to the relevant activity-type, use #$leaderTypeHasInfluenceOverActivityType-Definitional.")
(comment leaderTypeHasInfluenceOverActivityType-Definitional "<code>(#$leaderTypeHasInfluenceOverActivityType-Definitional LEADER-TYPE ACT-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>LEADER-TYPE</code> as the subcollection of #$Leader whose instances use their leadership to help shape the unfolding or outcome of events of type <code>ACT-TYPE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$leaderTypeHasInfluenceOverActivityType-Definitional #$ReligiousLeader #$ReligiousActivity)</code> identifies #$ReligiousLeader as the collection of agents who, by their leadership, help define instances of #$ReligiousActivity.  Note that #$leaderTypeHasInfluenceOverActivityType-Definitional gives a necessary condition for being an instance of its arg1, and not a sufficient one.")
(comment ReligiousActivity "The collection of all instances of #$ReligiousEvent that are also instances #$CulturalActivity.  Notable specializations include #$Praying, #$ObservingAReligiousHoliday, and #$ReligiousCeremony.")
(comment ObservingASecularHoliday "The collection of all instances of #$ObservingAHoliday in which the holiday observed (#$holidayObserved, q.v.) is a religious holiday (#$SecularHoliday, q.v.).")
(comment ObservingAReligiousHoliday "The collection of all instances of #$ObservingAHoliday in which the holiday observed (#$holidayObserved, q.v.) is a religious holiday (#$ReligiousHoliday, q.v.).")
(comment ScopingRelation "A specialization of #$Relation whose instances are used to determine a \"scope\" with respect to one or more variables and to bind any free occurrences of those variables that are inside that scope.  Each instance of #$ScopingRelation takes as its arguments (at least) a variable or list of variables (see #$CycLVariable and #$CycLVariableList) and a formula (see #$CycLExpression-Assertible).  The former argument is the scoping relation's \"scoping\" argument (see #$scopingArg), and the latter its \"scoped formula\" argument (see #$scopedFormulaArg).
<p>
Syntactically, the formula (SCOPINGRELN VAR SENT) might either be a sentence or a non-atomic term (see #$CycLNonAtomicTerm), depending on which #$ScopingRelation it is built from.  Suppose for simplicity that the sentence SENT itself contains no #$ScopingRelation expressions, so that any variables occurring in SENT are not bound (i.e. they are _free_).  Then, in the formula (SCOPINGRELN VAR SENT), the _scope_ of the occurrence of the term SCOPINGRELN is SENT, and any free occurrence of the variable VAR in SENT is said to be _bound_ by that occurrence of SCOPINGRELN, and thus is bound (and hence not free) with respect to the entire formula.  (The occurrence of VAR immediately to the right of SCOPINGRELN is also considered bound with respect to the entire formula.)
<p>
Semantically, if the formula (SCOPINGRELN VAR SENT) is _closed_ (i.e. if it contains no free occurrence of any variable), then it will in some sense be \"about\" the value(s) of VAR that satisfy SENT.  That is, if (SCOPINGRELN VAR SENT) is a closed sentence it will state something about those values, while if (SCOPINGRELN VAR SENT) is a closed NAT it will (purport to) denote something related to those values.  For an example of the former case see #$thereExists; for the latter case see #$TheSetOf.  (If there are no free occurrences of VAR inside SENT, then (SCOPINGRELN VAR SENT) is a \"vacuous\" application of SCOPINGRELN that means exactly what SENT means.)
<p>
Instances of #$ScopingRelation include all of the #$Quantifiers and #$Lambda.")
(comment Motorcycle "A specialization of #$TwoWheeledMotorizedVehicle.  Each instance of #$Motorcycle is a two-wheeled motorized personal transport device other than a #$MotorScooter (cf.).")
(comment MotorScooter "A specialization of #$TwoWheeledMotorizedVehicle and #$Scooter that resembles the typical #$Motorcycle but for its smaller size and unique step-through frame.")
(disjointWith MotorScooter Motorcycle)
(comment TwoWheeledMotorizedVehicle "A specialization of #$RoadVehicle and #$WheeledTransportationDevice.  Each instance of  #$TwoWheeledMotorizedVehicle is a two-wheeled motorized personal transport device.  Unlike a typical #$Automobile, such a vehicle has no body compartment that surrounds (and shelters and protects) the operator.
<p>
Specializations of this collection include #$Motorcycle and #$MotorScooter.")
(comment evincesUnverifiedGAF "<code>(#$evincesUnverifiedGAF OBTAINING-A-RESULT GAF)</code> means that in the
instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult,
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, <code>GAF</code> is the
#$CycLSentence-Assertible that is supported by the unverified result returned as
the fully-bound form of the #$gafGatheringTriggerSentence of the
#$GAFGathering which is a super-event of
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>.")
(comment GAFGathering "A specialization of (#$AttemptingFn #$Learning) .  Each instance of #$GAFGathering is a process in which the goal is for Cyc to learn one or more #$CycL 'ground atomic formulas' (see #$CycLFullyGroundAtomicSentence) based on querying against some natural language corpus with some search string that is generated from a target #$Predicate and, typically, some values to its arguments.  Each successful result of the process requires at least that the resultant formula is semantically well-formed, though other requirements are possible.")
(comment backchainForbiddenWhenUnboundInArg "#$backchainForbiddenWhenUnboundInArg is a predicate that
allows users to prevent the inference engine from backchaining on a
literal when one or more of its arguments are unbound. That is, if
PRED is a #$Predicate, N a positive integer less than or equal to the
arity of PRED, then (#$backchainForbiddenWhenUnboundInArg PRED N) that
no rules concluding to a positive literal with PREDICATE as the arg0
(in other words, no #$implies assertions in which a literal with
PREDICATE as the arg0 appears in the consequent) will be used when
attempting to prove (non-negated) literals with PREDICATE as the arg0
*unless* argN in the literal is fully bound. For instance, the sentence
<pre>
(#$backchainForbiddenWhenUnboundInArg #$interestingSentence 1)
</pre>
prevents the inference engine from applying any rule concluding
#$interestingSentence, such as 
<pre>
(#$implies
  (#$maleficiary ?PROGRAM ?AGENT) 
  (#$interestingSentence ?AGENT (#$maleficiary ?PROGRAM ?AGENT)))
</pre>
unless and until <code>?AGENT</code> is bound.")
(comment TerroristAct-InitiatedByCommunicationAct "A specialization of #$TerroristAct. Each instance of #$TerroristAct-InitiatedByCommunicationAct is a terrorist act first set in motion by a communication act addressed to the parties carrying out the act.")
(comment EmbassyBuildingFn "(#$EmbassyBuildingFn COUNTRY CITY) returns the physical #$EmbassyBuilding which houses the #$Embassy-TheMission of COUNTRY in CITY.")
(comment leaderTypeOrganizationType "<code>(#$leaderTypeOrganizationType LEADER-TYPE ORGANIZATION-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>LEADER-TYPE</code> as the subcollection of #$Leader whose instances are, by definition, leaders in an organization of type <code>ORGANIZATION-TYPE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$leaderTypeOrganizationType #$ReligiousLeader #$ReligiousOrganization)</code> identifies #$ReligiousLeader as the subcollection of leaders such that, for any instance of that subcollection, that instance is a leader of a religious organization.  Note that #$leaderTypeOrganizationType gives a necessary condition for being an instance of its arg1, and not a sufficient one.")
(comment LeaderTypeByOrgType "A collection that facets (see #$facets-Generic) #$Leader.  Every instance of #$LeaderTypeByOrgType is a collection <code>LEADER-TYPE</code> such that there is some specialization of #$Organization <code>ORGANIZATION-TYPE</code> such that one is an instance of <code>LEADER-TYPE</code> only if one is a leader of some instance of <code>ORGANIZATION-TYPE</code>.")
(comment LeaderTypeByOrgType "A collection that facets (see #$facets-Generic) #$Leader.  Every instance of #$LeaderTypeByOrgType is a collection <code>LEADER-TYPE</code> such that there is some specialization of #$Organization <code>ORGANIZATION-TYPE</code> such that one is an instance of <code>LEADER-TYPE</code> only if one is a leader of some instance of <code>ORGANIZATION-TYPE</code>.  Notable instances include #$ReligiousLeader and #$GovernmentLeader.")
(disjointWith TerroristSuicideBombing-CarriedOnPerson (AttackTypeByWeaponTypeFn Bomb-VehicleDelivered))
(disjointWith TerroristSuicideBombing-CarriedOnPerson AerialBombing)
(comment TerroristSuicideBombing "A specialization of #$TerroristAttack, #$SuicideAttack, and #$Bombing in which a  bomb is delivered  to a target location by one or more perpetrators. The perpetrators intentionally kill themselves in the attack.  This includes acts in which the perepetrator carries the bomb on their person as well as cases where a vehicle is loaded with high explosives and the perpetrator drives it to the target location and explodes it.  A vehicle bomb exploded remotely where the perpetrator does not intend to die in the blast is not considered an instance of this collection.")
(comment assertionOfRuleSpecification "<code>(#$assertionOfRuleSpecification RULE-SPEC ASSERT)</code> means
that the #$CycLRuleSpecification, <code>RULE-SPEC</code> is expressed
in CycL as the #$CycLRuleAssertion, <code>ASSERT</code>.")
(comment mtOfRuleSpecification "<code>(#$mtOfRuleSpecification RULE-SPEC MT)</code> means
that the #$CycLRuleSpecification, <code>RULE-SPEC</code> should
correspond to an assertion in the #$Microtheory, <code>MT</code>.")
(comment sentenceOfRuleSpecification "<code>(#$mtOfRuleSpecification RULE-SPEC MT)</code> means
that the #$CycLRuleSpecification, <code>RULE-SPEC</code> should
correspond to an assertion in the #$Microtheory, <code>MT</code>.")
(comment CycLRuleQuerySpecification "The collection of all instances of #$CycLQuerySpecification that have assertible sentences (i.e., instances of #$CycLSentence-Assertible) with #$implies in the arg0 position specified as the #$InferenceSentenceParameter, via #$softwareParameterValueInSpecification.")
(comment MagneticPoleOfPlanet "A specialization of #$GeographicalThing-Intangible-MovableLocation. Each instance of #$MagneticPoleOfPlanet is a point on the surface of a #$Planet where the lines of force of its magnetic field are vertical. Two notable instances of this collection are #$MagneticNorthPoleOfEarth and #$MagneticSouthPoleOfEarth.")
(comment magneticInclinationAtPointOnEarth "The <i>magnetic inclination</i> at any geographical point, <code>POINT</code>, on #$PlanetEarth is the angle between the gravitational vector at <code>POINT</code> and the vertical component of the Earth's magnetic field at <code>POINT</code>. In #$CycL, this relation is denoted by <code>(#$magneticInclinationAtPointOnEarth POINT INCLINATION)</code>. The #$MagneticNorthPoleOfEarth is by definition the point on #$PlanetEarth where the inclination is (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0), and the #$MagneticSouthPoleOfEarth is the point on #$PlanetEarth where the inclination is (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 180).")
(comment GravitationalForceAtPointOnEarthFn "An instance of #$GravitationalField. If <code>POINT</code> is a point on #$PlanetEarth, then <code>(#$GravitationalForceAtPointOnEarthFn POINT)</code> denotes the gravitational force vector (#$GravitationalForceVector) at <code>POINT</code>.")
(comment GravitationalField "A #$GravitationalField is a #$VectorField which assigns a #$GravitationalForceVector to each #$Point in some #$SpaceRegion.")
(comment magneticDeclinationAtPointOnEarth "The <i>magnetic declination</i> at any geographical point, <code>POINT</code>, on #$PlanetEarth is the angle between the vector positioned at <code>POINT</code> and pointing at the #$AxialNorthPoleOfEarth and the horizontal component of the Earth's magnetic field at <code>POINT</code>, measured clockwise from the axial North pole. In #$CycL, this relation is denoted by <code>(#$magneticDeclinationAtPointOnEarth POINT DECLINATION)</code>.")
(comment MagneticForceAtPointOnEarthFn "An instance of #$MagneticField. If <code>POINT</code> is a point on #$PlanetEarth, then <code>(#$MagneticForceAtPointOnEarthFn POINT)</code> denotes the magnetic force vector (#$MagneticForceVector) at <code>POINT</code>.")
(comment specifiedRuleContributesFunctionalityTo "<code>(#$specifiedRuleContributesFunctionalityTo RULE-SPEC SOFTWARE)</code> means that the rule specified by #$CycLRuleSpecification <code>RULE-SPEC</code> should contribute directly to the functionality of the #$SoftwareObject <code>SOFTWARE</code>.")
(comment FACToryIrrelevantTerm "A collection of #$CycLTerms TERM such that (NL paraphrases of) sentences containing TERM should not be displayed to users of the #$FACTory-WebGame.")
(comment sentenceAssignedStatusViaInfoStore "A #$TernaryPredicate that relates a #$CycLSentence-Assertible to a #$SentenceEvaluationOutput to an #$InformationStore.  <code>(#$sentenceAssignedStatusViaInfoStore SENT STATUS INFOSTORE)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> was assigned <code>STATUS</code> via <code>INFOSTORE</code> -- i.e. <code>INFOSTORE</code> played an #$instrumentalRole in assigning <code>STATUS</code> to <code>SENT</code>.  The #$InformationStore in question might be, e.g., a #$Person, a #$Database-AbstractContent, or an #$OnlineGameProgram.
<p>
For example,
<pre>
(#$sentenceAssignedStatusViaInfoStore 
   (#$capitalCity #$Brazil #$CityOfSaoPauloBrazil)
    #$FalseCycLSentence 
    #$FACTory-WebGame)
</pre>
means that (#$capitalCity #$Brazil #$CityOfSaoPauloBrazil) was deemed false via the FACTory web game.")
(comment satisfiesHeadSemanticConstraintsForNCR "(#$satisfiesHeadSemanticConstraintsForNCR NODE RULE DENOT) means that wrt. the meaning DENOT (cf. #$proposedMeaning) NODE satisfies all of the semantic constraints (cf. #$NounCompoundRuleConstraint-Semantic) imposed by the #$NounCompoundRule RULE on a head.")
(comment satisfiesModifierSemanticConstraintsForNCR "(#$satisfiesHeadSemanticConstraintsForNCR NODE RULE DENOT) means that wrt. the meaning DENOT (cf. #$proposedMeaning) NODE satisfies all of the semantic constraints (cf. #$NounCompoundRuleConstraint-Semantic) imposed by the #$NounCompoundRule RULE on a modifier.")
(comment BoatBombing "A specialization of #$Bombing. Each instance of #$BoatBombing is an event in which a watercraft is used to deliver a bomb to its intended detonation location. See also #$BoatBomb-VehicleDelivered.")
(comment Cavus-Topographical "The collection of all topographical features characterized by irregular steep-sided depressions, usually in arrays or clusters.  A hollow is an example of a #$Cavus-Topographical.")
(disjointWith CycLTruthValueSentence (CollectionDifferenceFn CycLSentence-Assertible CycLTruthValueSentence))
(comment AutomobileSuspensionSystem "A specialization of both #$MechanicalAutoPart and #$SystemOfDevices.  Each instance of #$AutomobileSuspensionSystem is a system of devices (often including either #$ShockAbsorbers or #$AutomotiveSuspensionStruts) supporting the upper part of an automobile on its axles.")
(comment TerroristSuicideBombing-CarriedOnPerson "A specialization of #$TerroristSuicideBombing in which a  bomb is delivered  to a target location by one or more persons who keep the bomb or bombs concealed until detonated. The perpetrators intentionally kill themselves in the attack.  This constant is intended to cover the stereotypical tactic in which a perpetrator carries (or wears) a conventional #$HighExplosive to a target. Driving vehicles loaded with explosives into a target are not considered to be instances of #$TerroristSuicideBombing-CarriedOnPerson.  For the general collection of terrorist suicide bombings that include vehicle delivered suicide bombings see #$TerroristSuicideBombing.")
(comment CycLFormulaicSentence-Assertible "A specialization of #$CycLFormulaicSentence, #$CycLSentence-Assertible, and #$CycLPropositionalSentence.  This is the collection of all instances of #$CycLSentence-Assertible except for the two #$CycLTruthValueSentences: <code>#$True</code> and <code>#$False</code> (i.e. (#$Quote #$True) and (#$Quote #$False)).")
(comment CycLSentence-Assertible "An instance of #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of #$CycLExpression-Assertible and #$CycLSentence-Askable.  This is the collection of all CycL sentences that could be asserted to the Cyc Knowledge Base.  More precisely, each instance of #$CycLSentence-Assertible is a CycL sentence that is both syntactically and semantically well-formed.  By definition, any CycL sentence is syntactically well-formed.  To be semantically well-formed, a CycL sentence must be constructible via the syntax of CycL without violating any applicable arity or argument-type constraints (see #$arity and #$ArgTypePredicate).  A CycL sentence must be semantically well-formed in order to be interpretable as having a \"semantic value\", which for sentences means having a truth value.  Note that being \"assertible\" in the present sense does not require a sentences's actually being asserted in the KB.")
(comment orgAffiliateTypeHasRelationToRegionAtTime "An instance of
#$QueryMacroPredicate. (#$orgAffiliateTypeHasRelationToRegionAtTime
ORG ORG-ROLE AGENT LOCATION-RELATION REGION INTERVAL) means that,
throughout INTERVAL, AGENT both (i) is related by ORG-ROLE to ORG, and
(ii) is related by LOCATION-RELATION to REGION.  If AGENT is a
#$Person, then ORG-ROLE is an instance of
#$OrganizationalRolePredicate, and AGENT occupies the arg2 position.
If AGENT is an #$Organization, then ORG-ROLE is a specialization or
specInverse of #$subGroups, and AGENT occupies the 'sub-group' arg
position.")
(comment Arson-NonBombing "The collection of instances of Arson that don't have instances of #$Bombings as subevents.")
(disjointWith Arson-NonBombing Bombing)
(comment DemandingARansom "A collection of events; a subcollection of #$Demanding-CommunicationAct. In each #$DemandingARansom, someone demands a ransom.")
(comment functionFirstInputValue "A sentence of the form (#$functionFirstInputValue  FUNCTION QUANTITY) means that the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction FUNCTION (some arbitrary function for use in calculating the value of some property of some system) tasks as its 'first' input a QUANTITY, some specific QUANTITY that is measured in the #$UnitOfMeasure that is related to FUNCTION by the predicate #$functionFirstInputUnitOfMeasureForSit. The notion of 'first' here is intended only to allow for knowledge about the function to be shared across the predicates #$functionFirstInputValue, #$functionFirstInputUnitOfMeasureForSit.")
(comment functionPolynomialFunctionForSit "A sentence of the form (#$functionPolynomialFunctionForSit FUNCTION FORMULA EVENT) is used to state that the #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction FUNCTION is defined by the #$PolynomialFunction FORMULA relative to the #$Event EVENT.")
(comment TensionForceFunction "A sub-collection of #$ForceFunction, each instance represents a force that acts on some 3D object with the potential affect of expanding its volume.")
(comment angularVelocityOfDuringForQuantity "A sentence of the form (#$angularVelocityOfDuringForQuantity OBJECT EVENT QUANTITY AV) means that the #$PartiallyTangible OBJECT, considered relative to the #$Event EVENT and some determining #$Quantity QUANTITY (e.g., a time) has #$RateOfRotation of AV.")
(comment TurningAlongAnArc "A sub-collection of both and #$RotationAxisPerpendicularToDirectionOfMotion #$Yaw-Orientational.  Each instance of this collection has a #$primaryObjectMoving that simultaneously undergoes the changes associated with both a #$Movement-TranslationEvent and a #$Yaw-Orientational.  An example of this would be the movement of a car along a circular race track.  The car is performing a #$Movement-TranslationEvent, but it is also performing a #$Yaw-Orientational such that the direction in which it is facing approximates a line tangent to the race track at the point where the car is located.")
(comment ComparativeModifierType "A collection of terms that serve as argument places in instances of #$QueryMacroPredicate.  The terms in this collection allow one to bring back, e.g., the deadliest member of a set, the longest duration between things in some set.")
(comment GrainElevator "The collection of buildings used to store and distribute grain (#$GrainOfCereal, q.v.).  See also #$BucketElevator, a device used in grain elevators to move grain into bins.")
(disjointWith CoinFlipResultingInHeads CoinFlipResultingInTails)
(comment CollectionCoverageRatioFn "<code>(#$CollectionCoverageRatioFn COLLECTION-SET MT)</code> returns the ratio of specializations of #$Thing that are specializations of at least one member of <code>COLLECTION-SET</code>, computed from the #$Microtheory <code>MT</code>.")
(comment Waterhole "A specialization of #$Pond. Instances of #$Waterhole are small holes, hollows or depressions containing water. Such water sources are of great importance to wildlife as well as humans and livestock in arid regions.")
(comment TribalRegion "Each #$TribalRegion is a #$GeographicalRegion used by indigenous tribes, nomadic or otherwise, for hunting, habitation or other activities.")
(comment WindPowerPlant "A specialization of #$PowerGenerationComplex.  Each instance of #$WindPowerPlant is a complex that harvests and distributes energy collected by multiple wind turbines.")
(comment WindPoweredTurbine "The subcollection of #$Turbine such that each instance uses instances of #$Translation-SingleTrajectory of instances of #$WindProcess to generate #$Electricity.")
(comment DeadliestFn "A collection denoting unary function.  (#$DeadliestFn COLL) denotes the subcollection of COLL that consists of the deadliest (most number of People killed) events in COLL -- there could be more than one \"deadliest\" because of ties.  Most often though, this will return a collection with a single instance.")
(disjointWith WindPowerPlant ThermalPowerGenerationComplex)
(disjointWith ThermalPowerGenerationComplex HydroelectricDam)
(disjointWith FossilFuelPowerGenerationComplex PowerGenerationComplex-Nuclear)
(comment FossilFuelPowerGenerationComplex "A specialization of #$ThermalPowerGenerationComplex.  Instances generate electricity by converting mechanical energy produced by a turbine that converts thermal energy generated by the burning of fossil fuels (#$FossilFuel, q.v.).")
(comment ThermalPowerGenerationComplex "A specialization of #$PowerGenerationComplex.  Instances are complexes for generating electricity by converting mechanical energy produced by a turbine that converts thermal energy.")
(comment PowerGenerationFacilityTypeByPrimaryMover "An instance of #$FacetingCollectionType.  Instances are subcollections of #$PowerGenerationComplex whose instances generate electricity through a turbine system where the turbines are moved by instances of a common specialization of #$FluidFlow-Translation.  For example, #$WindPowerPlant is the instance of #$PowerGenerationFacilityTypeByPrimaryMover such that every instance is a plant that generates electricity with turbines moved by wind (see #$WindProcess).")
(comment SteamElectricPowerGenerationComplex "A specialization of #$ThermalPowerGenerationComplex.  Each instance is a power plant that generates electricity by converting mechanical energy collected via turbines moved with steam.")
(comment ELIntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn "A binary evaluatable function which is an EL version of #$IntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn.  This is a pragmatic function to aid canonicalization.")
(comment IntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn "A binary evaluatable function which takes a #$TimeInterval and a #$TimeParameter and returns a #$TimeInterval.  <code>(#$IntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn INTERVAL TYPE)</code> denotes the time interval ended by the last subinterval of <code>INTERVAL</code> of type <code>TYPE</code>.  Note that this is only defined if <code>INTERVAL</code> subsumes an instance of <code>TYPE</code>.  So, for example, (#$IntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn (#$YearFn 2004) #$CalendarMonth) denotes (#$IntervalEndedByFn (#$MonthFn #$December (#$YearFn 2004))).  On the other hand, (#$IntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn (#$YearFn 2004) #$CalendarDecade) is undefined.  See also #$ELIntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn and #$IntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn.")
(comment ELIntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn "A binary evaluatable function which is an EL version of #$IntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn. This is a pragmatic function to aid canonicalization.")
(comment IntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn "A binary evaluatable function which takes a #$TimeInterval and a #$TimeParameter and returns a #$TimeInterval.  <code>(#$IntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn INTERVAL TYPE)</code> denotes the time interval started by the first subinterval of <code>INTERVAL</code> of type <code>TYPE</code>.  Note that this is only defined if <code>INTERVAL</code> subsumes an instance of <code>TYPE</code>.  So, for example, (#$IntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn (#$YearFn 2004) #$CalendarMonth) denotes (#$IntervalStartedByFn (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 2004))).  On the other hand, (#$IntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn (#$YearFn 2004) #$CalendarDecade) is undefined.  See also #$ELIntervalStartedByFirstSubintervalOfTypeFn and #$IntervalEndedByLastSubintervalOfTypeFn.")
(comment ELNthMetricallyPrecedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn "A ternary evaluatable function which is an EL version of #$NthMetricallyPrecedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn.  This is a pragmatic function to aid canonicalization.")
(comment NthMetricallyPrecedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn "A ternary evaluatable function which takes a #$PositiveInteger, a #$TimeInterval, and a #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries and returns a #$TimeInterval.  <code>(#$NthMetricallyPrecedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn N INTERVAL TYPE)</code> denotes the time interval of type <code>TYPE</code> which precedes <code>INTERVAL</code> by <code>N</code> intervals of type <code>TYPE</code>.  See also #$ELNthMetricallyPrecedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn and #$NthMetricallySucceedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn.")
(comment ELNthMetricallySucceedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn "A ternary evaluatable function which is an EL version of #$NthMetricallySucceedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn. This is a pragmatic function to aid canonicalization.")
(comment NthMetricallySucceedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn "A ternary evaluatable function which takes a #$PositiveInteger, a #$TimeInterval, and a #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries and returns a #$TimeInterval.  <code>(#$NthMetricallySucceedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn N INTERVAL TYPE)</code> denotes the time interval of type <code>TYPE</code> which succeeds <code>INTERVAL</code> by <code>N</code> intervals of type <code>TYPE</code>.  See also #$ELNthMetricallySucceedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn and #$NthMetricallyPrecedingTimeIntervalOfTypeFn")
(comment evincesKnownGAF "<code>(#$evincesKnownGAF OBTAINING-A-RESULT GAF)</code> means that in the instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult, <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, <code>GAF</code> is the #$CycLSentence-Assertible that is supported by the result returned as the fully-bound form of the #$gafGatheringTriggerSentence of the #$GAFGathering which is a super-event of <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>.  Further, <code>GAF</code> corresponds to the CycL sentence of an assertion in a reliable context in the Cyc KB.")
(comment evincesRefutableGAF "<code>(#$evincesRefutableGAF OBTAINING-A-RESULT GAF)</code> means that in the instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult, <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, <code>GAF</code> is the #$CycLSentence-Assertible that is supported by the result returned as the fully-bound form of the #$gafGatheringTriggerSentence of the #$GAFGathering which is a super-event of <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>.  Further, the negation of <code>GAF</code> can be proved using the inference settings set by the Cyc GAF-gathering technology.")
(comment KBDependentRelation "A collection of relations.  Members of this collection are those predicates that are used (in the zeroth place of formulas) to express propositions whose truth depend on facts about the KB or functions whose mappings depend on the state of the #$CycKB (KB).  A good example of a #$KBDependentRelation is #$assertedSentence.  Whether or not it is true that (#$assertedSentence FORM) depends entirely upon whether or not FORM is asserted in the KB.
<p>
See also #$KBDependentCollection.")
(comment CommercialHoldingCompany "A specialization of #$HoldingCompany and
#$CommercialOrganization.  Each instance of #$CommercialHoldingCompany
is a #$CommercialOrganization that exists primarily to own controlling
interests in other #$CommercialOrganizations.")
(comment relationToDuringRelationTo "(#$relationToDuringRelationTo <code>OBJECT</code>
<code>BIN-PRED-1</code> <code>INDIVIDUAL1</code> <code>BIN-PRED-2
<code>INDIVIDUAL2) means that the #$TimeInterval during which the
proposition (<code>BIN-PRED-1</code> <code>OBJECT</code>
<code>INDIVIDUAL1</code>) holds at least partly overlaps the
#$TimeInterval during which the proposition
(<code>BIN-PRED-2 <code>OBJECT</code> <code>INDIVIDUAL2) holds.  In
other words, the two propositions hold during intersecting
#$TimeIntervals (see #$temporallyIntersects). Since
#$relationToDuringRelationTo is a query-only predicate used to compare
the time indices of temporally-qualified assertions,
<code>BIN-PRED-1</code> and <code>BIN-PRED-2</code> are restricted to
#$TemporallyQualifiableRelations")
(comment KBDependentCollection "A specialization of #$TimeDependentCollection (q.v.).  This is the collection of all and only those collections <code>COL</code> such that any thing's being an instance of <code>COL</code> depends on the current state of the #$CycKB.  Such a <code>COL</code> corresponds to a property defined (at least partly) in terms of the contents, features, or implementation of the Cyc Knowledge Base itself, as opposed to a property that \"exists out there\" in the KB-independent world.  
<p>
For example, #$CycLAssertion is a KB-dependent collection, membership in which requires of a given CycL sentence that it has in fact been asserted to the KB.  #$CycLSentence is also KB-dependent, as being a CycL sentence requires being composed (ultimately) out of #$CycLReifiedDenotationalTerms (and perhaps variables) -- i.e. terms that are currently reified in the KB.  Conversely, #$Sentence is <i>not</i> a KB-dependent collection.
<p>
See also #$KBDependentRelation.")
(comment WNAllSynonymStringsForWordAndPOSFn "A #$BinaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction that returns a #$Set-Mathematical of #$CharacterStrings.   An evaluation of an expression of the form (#$WNAllSynonymStringsForWordAndPOSFn LEXICAL-WORD POS)  function consults the #$WordNetKnowledgeBase #$WordNet-Version2_0 to find synonyms for the #$LexicalWord LEXICAL-WORD based on the #$SpeechPart POS.  Based on the #$LexicalWords that it finds, it will return the <b>union</b> of the words (as strings) that appear in all the synsets in which LEXICAL-WORD appears.")
(comment WNRankedSynonymStringsForWordAndPOSFn "A #$BinaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction that returns a #$List of #$Set-Mathematical of #$CharacterStrings.   An evaluation of an expression of the form (#$WNAllSynonymStringsForWordAndPOSFn LEXICAL-WORD POS)  function consults the #$WordNetKnowledgeBase #$WordNet-Version2_0 to find synonyms for the #$LexicalWord LEXICAL-WORD based on the #$SpeechPart POS.  For each synset that in which LEXICAL-WORD appears, it will construct a set of strings for the terms in that synset.  The list is composed of those sets and the ordering of the list roughly reflects the frequency with which LEXICAL-WORD is used to express the meaning assocaited with that the synset.")
(comment keRequirementInverse "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate that is a specialization of #$requiredArg2Pred (q.v.).  <code>(#$keRequirementInverse COL BIN-PRED)</code> means that for every instance <code>INS</code> of <code>COL</code> that is known to (reified in) the knowledge base, there is an expectation that there exist some <code>VALUE</code> such that <code>(BIN-PRED VALUE INS)</code> is known to (asserted in) the knowledge base.
<p>
See also #$keRequirementPreds.")
(comment keRequirementPreds "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate that is a specialization of #$requiredArg1Pred (q.v.).  <code>(#$keRequirementPreds COL BINPRED)</code> means that for every instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> that is known to (reified in) the knowledge base, there is an expectation that there exist some reified <code>VALUE</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST VALUE)</code> is known to (asserted in) the knowledge base.
<p>
See also #$keRequirementInverse.")
(comment mainBusinessTypeOfBusiness "(#$mainBusinessTypeOfBusiness BUSINESS BUSINESS-TYPE) holds just in case BUSINESS is an instance of BUSINESS-TYPE and BUSINESS-TYPE is the collection of businesses that engage in the same sort of activity types as BUSINESS.  BUSINESS-TYPE will be an instance of #$BusinessTypeByActivityType.")
(comment drivingForce "<code>(#$drivingForce MOVEMENT FORCE)</code> means that <code>FORCE</code> is a physical force which acts to influence the acceleration of the primary object moving in <code>MOVEMENT</code>.")
(comment MechanicalEquilibrium "A physical situation is in mechanical equilibrium if and only if the sum of the applied forces is zero. See also, #$StaticEquilibrium, which is a special case characterized by the lack of all motion throughout the situation. For example, a book sitting on a table is in static equilibrium, whereas a child sliding down a slide is in mechanical equilibrium but not static equilibrium.")
(comment StaticEquilibrium "A physical situation is in static equilibrium if and only if it is in mechanical equilibrium (see #$MechanicalEquilibrium) and no motion occurs within the situation.")
(comment SimpleHarmonicOscillation "A simple harmonic oscillation is a harmonic oscillation which is neither damped nor driven. That is, the primary object moving in the oscillation is not acted upon by either a frictional force or a driving force. A simple harmonic oscillation is a special case of a conservative physical event for which, by definition, the sum of the potential and kinetic energy of the primary object moving is constant.")
(disjointWith SimpleHarmonicOscillation DampedHarmonicOscillation)
(disjointWith SimpleHarmonicOscillation DrivenHarmonicOscillation)
(comment HarmonicOscillation "A harmonic oscillation is a periodic movement in which the motion of the primary object moving satisfies the following properties.
<p>
1. The motion is symmetric about an equilibrium position at which point no net force acts on the system.
<br>
2. The restoring force is proportional to and oppositely directed to the displacement at any point along the oscillating object's trajectory. 
<br>
3. The motion is periodic.
<p>
Harmonic oscillations are further characterized by the additional forces which may act upon the system. If the object's velocity is attenuated by a fricitonal force, the oscillation is <i>damped</i>. (See #$DampedHarmonicOscillation.) If the object's acceleration is acted on by an external, periodic force, the oscillation is <i>driven</i>. (See #$DrivenHarmonicOscillation) If the oscillation is neither damped nor driven, it is <i>simple</i>. (See #$SimpleHarmonicOscillation). See also #$DrivenAndDampedHarmonicOscillation.")
(comment ChemicalReaction-Intentional "A specialization of #$PurposefulAction-ResourceDriven.  Every instance of #$ChemicalReaction-Intentional is an act of intentionally causing a #$ChemicalReaction.  That is, either the #$ChemicalReaction itself or the #$chemicalProducts-Macroscopic which result from the #$ChemicalReaction is the goal of some agent.  The script involves four subscenes.  First, there is an instance of #$GainingControl. In this first scene, the #$ChemicalSubstanceTypes that are the #$precursors-Chemical of the reaction's products are acquired. Second there is a #$FollowingInstructions scene.  This scene involves executing the standardized methodology for transforming the precursors into the product.  This second scene overlaps the third scene, an instance of #$Incorporation-Physical in which the precursors are brought together.  Next, there is a #$ChemicalReaction which produces an instance of the intended #$ChemicalSubstanceType. Finally, there is a scene of type (#$GainingControlOfFn #$ChemicalSubstanceType), in which the agent performing the script gains control of the products of the chemical reaction.  Specifications of this script can be picked out, based on the produced #$ChemicalSubstanceType, using #$ChemicalReaction-IntentionalFn .")
(comment DampedHarmonicOscillation "A damped harmonic oscillation is a harmonic oscillation in which the velocity of the primary object moving is is attenuated by a frictional force which acts proportionally to the magnitude of velocity. Compare with #$SimpleHarmonicOscillation.")
(comment DrivenAndDampedHarmonicOscillation "A driven and damped harmonic motion is a harmonic motion in which the velocity of the primary object moving is is attenuated by a frictional force which acts proportionally to the magnitude of velocity and in which the acceleration of the primary object moving is acted upon by an external driving force. Compare with #$SimpleHarmonicOscillation.")
(comment DrivenHarmonicOscillation "A driven harmonic oscillation is a harmonic oscillation in which the acceleration of the primary object moving is acted upon by an external driving force. Compare with #$SimpleHarmonicOscillation.")
(comment KineticEnergyFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType. Each instance of #$KineticEnergyFunction is a function which measures the amount of #$KineticEnergy in some physical situation or event.")
(disjointWith PotentialEnergy KineticEnergy)
(comment PotentialEnergyFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType. Each instance of #$PotentialEnergyFunction is a function which measures the amount of #$PotentialEnergy in some physical situation or event.")
(comment PragmaticallyDecontextualizedCollection "A #$CollectionType.  Each instance of #$PragmaticallyDecontextualizedCollection is a collection that, while not a genuine ANECT (see #$AtemporalNecessarilyEssentialCollectionType) is for purely pragmatic reasons handled by the Cyc system as if it were an ANECT.  Instances include certain #$KBDependentCollections such as #$ReifiableFunction, and certain non-KB-dependent collections such as #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.")
(comment AtemporalNecessarilyEssentialCollectionType "A collection of collections.  Each instance <code>COL</code> of #$AtemporalNecessarilyEssentialCollectionType (<i>ANECT</i>) is a collection satisfying three conditions: (1) <code>COL</code> is disjoint with (see #$disjointWith) #$TemporalThing, (2) every instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> is an instance of <code>COL</code> essentially (i.e. <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>COL</code>, and could not exist without being an instance of <code>COL</code>), and (3) condition (2) is a necessary truth about <code>COL</code>.
<p>
Positive examples of ANECTs include #$Collection, #$Integer, and #$Relation (each of which is a collection of atemporals and is such that, necessarily, all of its instances are in it essentially).  Negative examples include #$SpatialThing (though arguably it is necessary that all of its instances are essentially instances of it, it is not disjoint with #$TemporalThing) and #$UniqueAnatomicalPartType (which, though disjoint with #$TemporalThing, has instances, such as #$Heart, that could exist even if they weren't instances of it; e.g. it might have been the case that every creature with a heart had at least two hearts).  There are no known examples of Cyc-reified collections satisfying conditions (1) and (2) but not (3), but one can be contrived.  Suppose that all of today's winning lottery numbers were primes.  Now consider the collection:
<p>
 (#$CollectionUnionFn 
   (#$TheSet #$PrimeNumber <i>TodaysWinningLotteryNumbers</i>))
<p>
This collection is clearly disjoint with #$TemporalThing and, by hypothesis, all of its instances are in it essentially (as each prime number is essentially a prime number).  But this last fact is not <i>necessarily</i> true of this collection: the collection might have had instances that belonged to it only contingently (i.e. not essentially), as it might have been the case that one of today's winning lottery numbers was non-prime, and no number is such that it is essentially one of today's winning lottery numbers.
<p>
When asserting that something is an instance or specialization of a given instance of ANECT, it is appropriate to do so in the #$UniversalVocabularyMt (q.v.).  Indeed, ANECT was specially defined to facilitate the movement of appropriate assertions to that microtheory.
<p>
Cf. #$PragmaticallyDecontextualizedCollection.")
(comment amplitudeOfOscillation "The <i>amplitude</i> of a harmonic oscillation is the maximum displacement of the primary object moving from its position of equilibrium. <code>(#$amplitudeOfOscillation OSCILLATION AMPLITUDE)</code> means that the amplitude of <code>OSCILLATION</code> is <code>AMPLITUDE</code>. See also #$periodOfOscillation.")
(comment periodOfOscillation "The <i>period</i> of a harmonic oscillation is the amount of time it takes for the primary object moving to complete one full cycle from a fixed point in its trajectory. <code>(#$periodOfOscillation OSCILLATION PERIOD)</code> means that <code>PERIOD</code> is the period of oscillation in <code>OSCILLATION</code>. See also #$amplitudeOfOscillation.")
(comment hypothesizesDenotationOfWordForPOS "A sentence of the form (#$hypothesizesDenotationOfWordForPOS HYP-MT LEXICAL-WORD POS) states that the #$LexicalHypothesisMicrotheory HYP-MT posits some denotation for the #$LexicalWord LEXICAL-WORD relative to the part of speech.")
(comment potentialCompetitorLHMs "A sentence of the form (#$potentialCompetitorLHMs LHM-1 LHM2) states that the two instances of #$LexicalHypothesisMicrotheory LHM-1 and LHM-2 posit denotations for the same #$LexicalWord for the same #$SpeechPart.")
(comment AccelerationFunction "A sub-collection of #$PhysicalQuantityOrVectorFunction and instance of #$FunctionType. Each instance of #$AccelerationFunction is used to denote an #$Acceleration.")
(comment PhysicalQuantityFunction "A specialization of #$BinaryFunction whose instances measure some physical quantity of an object that can be determined by observing the object's state at a fixed time. By convention, we require that all instances of this collection have #$SpatialThing as their #$arg1Isa and #$TimeInterval as their #$arg2Isa. Example specializations include #$KineticEnergyFunction and #$PotentialEnergyFunction.")
(comment maximumValueOfFunctionForThingThroughoutEvent "If <code>FUNCTION</code> is a #$PhysicalQuantityFunction, <code>EVENT</code> is a #$PhysicalEvent, and <code>THING</code> is an actor in <code>EVENT</code>, then <code>(#$maximumValueOfFunctionForThingThroughoutEvent FUNCTION THING EVENT MAXIMUM)</code> means that the maximum value of <code>(FUNCTION THING TIME)</code> where <code>TIME</code> ranges over all #$TimePoints subsumed by <code>EVENT</code>.")
(comment multipleChoiceSingleOptionList "<code>(#$multipleChoiceSingleOptionList TEST LIST)</code> means that for the KBContentTest <code>TEST</code>, the <code>LIST</code> whose first element is a CycL term and whose second element is a string corresponds to one answer choice out of several answer choices offered in the context in which <code>TEST</code> is asked.")
(comment PositiveDimensionalThing "A #$SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality and a specialization of #$SpatialThing.  Each instance of #$PositiveDimensionalThing is a spatial thing having a positive (i.e. non-zero) number of dimensions, and thus is either a one- or two- or three- (or higher-) dimensional spatial object.
<p>
Examples of #$PositiveDimensionalThings include tangible or intangible spatially-localized objects, such as the edge of a tabletop, the surface of the tabletop, and the table itself, as well as abstract geometrical objects that are at least one-dimensional, such as a Platonic circle or cube.  Excluded from this collection are geometrical #$Points and any other #$ZeroDimensionalThings.
<p>
Specializations of #$PositiveDimensionalThing include #$ExtendedSpaceRegion, #$ShapedObject, and #$PolyDimensionalThing.")
(comment SuspensionBridge "A specialization of #$Bridge. On suspension bridges the main load-bearing cables are suspended between towers and vertical suspender cables attached to the main load-bearing cables carry the weight of the deck below.")
(comment MovableBridge "A specialization of #$Bridge. Movable bridges are fixed structures, parts of which move to allow objects to travel past them.")
(comment CantileverBridge "A specialization of #$Bridge. Cantilever bridges are supported by cantilevers positioned towards the middle of the bridge.")
(comment BridgeTypeBySupportType "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$ArtifactType.  Each instance of #$BridgeTypeBySupportType is a collection of #$Bridges grouped together according to the technique used to support their spans.")
(comment ArchBridge "A specialization of #$Bridge. Arch bridges have abutments at each end and are shaped as curved arches. They transferring the weight of the bridge and its loads partially into a horizontal thrust against the abutments at either side.")
(comment CompressionArchSuspendedDeckBridge "A specialization of #$ArchBridge. Compression arch suspended-deck bridges are arch bridges form which the transport decks are suspended, usually by cables.")
(comment TrussBridge "A specialization of #$Bridge. Truss bridges are supported by truss structures -- static structures consisting of straight slender members inter-connected at joints into triangular units.")
(comment CableStayedBridge "A specialization of #$Bridge. Cable-stayed bridges consist of one or more pillars from which with supporting cables descend to the deck.")
(comment above-Overhead "<code>(#$above-Overhead ABOVE BELOW)</code> means that <code>ABOVE</code> is directly above <code>BELOW</code> (see the predicate #$above-Directly), all points of <code>ABOVE</code> are higher than all points of <code>BELOW</code>, and <code>ABOVE</code> and <code>BELOW</code> do <i>not</i> touch.")
(comment above-Directly "<code>(#$above-Directly ABOVE BELOW)</code> means either that (1) the volumetric center of <code>ABOVE</code> is directly above some point of <code>BELOW</code>, if <code>ABOVE</code> is smaller than <code>BELOW</code>; or that (2) some point of <code>ABOVE</code> is directly above the volumetric center of <code>BELOW</code>, if <code>ABOVE</code> is larger than, or equal in size to, <code>BELOW</code>.")
(comment mtWithMonadIndexAndParameter "<code>(#$mtWithMonadIndexAndParameter MT MONAD TIME PAR)</code> means that the following hold:
<p>
1. <code>(#$mtMonad MT MONAD)</code>        <br>
2. <code>(#$mtTimeIndex MT TIME)</code>   <br>
3. <code>(#$mtTimeParameter MT PAR)</code>
<p>
See also #$genlMtWithMonadIndexAndParameter.")
(comment KnowledgeAcquisitionGoal-FalsifyingLies "The goal-category of wanting to falsify (i.e. verify the negation of) \"lies,\" i.e. sentences that are intended to be false but (at least somewhat) plausible.")
(comment FACToryIrrelevantMicrotheory "A specialization of #$Microtheory.  Any assertion <code>ASSERT</code> that is
directly asserted in any instance of #$FACToryIrrelevantMicrotheory is such
that (NL paraphrases of) <code>ASSERT</code> should not be displayed to users of
the #$FACTory-WebGame; further, no (NL paraphrases of) hypothesized
sentences presented to users of the #$FACTory-WebGame should ultimately
derive from <code>ASSERT</code>.")
(comment FACToryLoginAccountFn "(#$FACToryLoginAccountFn STRING) denotes the the Fact Factory #$LoginAccount associated with the username STRING.")
(comment fACToryLoginAccount "This is the predicate corresponding to #$FACToryLoginAccountFn (q.v.).")
(comment pointOfEquilibriumOfPhysicalSituation "The <i>point of equilibrium</i> in a physical sitaution is a point at which the sum of the applied forces acting on the situation constituents is zero.")
(comment hookeSpringConstant "In physics, <i>Hooke's law of elasticity</i> is an approximation which states that if an instance of #$Spring-Device is elongated by some distance, <code>x</code>, then the restoring force exherted by the spring is proportional to <code>x</code> by a constant factor. This predicate relates a spring to its constant of proportionality. That is, <code>(#$hookeSpringConstant SPRING CONSTANT)</code> means that <code>CONSTANT</code> is the constant of proportionality for the restoring force exerted by SPRING. Note that <code>CONSTANT</code> is a quantity of #$Pressure.")
(comment frequencyOfEventType "(#$frequencyOfEventType TYPE FREQUENCY) holds just in case instances of type occur with at least FREQUENCY.  For instance, during baseball season, there is at least one game of baseball being played a day.  So, for any time period, T, subsumed by a baseball season (#$frequencyOfEventType #$BaseballGame #$Daily) holds at T.")
(comment collectionOfCitiesInCountry "The predicate corresponding to #$CityInCountryFn.")
(comment OGCGeometryProductFn "#$OGCGeometryProductFn is a spec function of #$SpatialProductFn and was
introduced exclusively to be supported by SKSI so that queries using
it can be translated directly into SQL queries using the function
<code>intersection</code> defined in the
#$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment FactFactoryClassificationFrequency "A collection of instances of #$Frequency.  The members of this collection broadly cover a wide range of frequencies from once a half century to continuously.")
(comment querySentenceOfQuery "<code>(#$querySentenceOfQuery QUERY SENTENCE)</code> means that <code>SENTENCE</code> is the query sentence for <code>QUERY</code>.")
(comment testAnswers-SingleCorrectMultipleChoiceValue "<code>(#$testAnswers-SingleCorrectMultipleChoiceValue TEST ANSWER)</code> means that <code>ANSWER</code> is the single correct choice among the values of the multiple choice answers for the question associated with <code>TEST</code>.")
(comment FrequencyOfHumanActs-Topic "An instance of #$Topic.  Each instance of #$FrequencyOfHumanActs-Topic is a #$Thing which (in some significant sense) has to do with the topic of the frequency in which people perform types of actions.")
(comment objectStationary "<code>(#$objectStationary EVENT OBJ)</code> means that in the #$PhysicalEvent <code>EVENT</code>, the instance of #$PartiallyTangible, <code>OBJ</code>, does not move.
<p>
For example, during a baseball game, the field that the game is played on does not move, so (#$relationAllExists #$objectStationary #$BaseballGame #$BaseballField) is true.")
(comment WordDenotationFn "<code>(#$WordDenotationFn WORD POS NUM)</code> denotes the <code>NUM</code>th denotation of the lexical word (#$LexicalWord) <code>WORD</code> with part of speech (#$SpeechPart) <code>POS</code>.  This function corresponds to the predicate #$denotation.")
(comment candidateFallsBetweenArgsWRTOrderingByPred-Exclusive "(#$candidateFallsBetweenArgsWRTOrderingByPred-Exclusive PRED THING LOWER-BOUND UPPER-BOUND) means that as a result of some user interaction it has been determined that it is likely that THING falls between LOWER-BOUND and UPPER-BOUND, exclusive, in the (partial) ordering determined by PRED.  PRED is a predicate that induces at least a partial ordering of the members of its domain.  For instance, #$heightOfObject induces a partial ordering of the members of its domain by virtue of the fact that all objects in the domain of that predicate can be partially ordered by height.")
(comment increasesThroughoutEvent "This predicate is used to state that the value of a quantity slot increases for a fixed object throughout an event. <code>(#$increasesThroughoutEvent SLOT THING EVENT)</code> is true if and only if for every pair of sub-events <code>SUB1</code> and <code>SUB2</code> of <code>EVENT</code> there exist values <code>VAL1</code> and <code>VAL2</code> such that if
<pre>
(#$and
  (#$startsAfterEndingOf SUB1 SUB2)
  (#$holdsIn SUB1 (SLOT THING VAL1))
  (#$holdsIn SUB2 (SLOT THING VAL2)))
</pre>
it follows that
<pre>
(#$greaterThanOrEqualTo VAL1 VAL2)
</pre>
is true. See also #$decreasesThroughoutEvent and #$constantThroughoutEvent.")
(comment decreasesThroughoutEvent "This predicate is used to state that the value of a quantity slot decreases for a fixed object throughout an event. <code>(#$decreasesThroughoutEvent SLOT THING EVENT)</code> is true if and only if for every pair of sub-events <code>SUB1</code> and <code>SUB2</code> of <code>EVENT</code> there exist values <code>VAL1</code> and <code>VAL2</code> such that if
<pre>
(#$and
  (#$startsAfterEndingOf SUB1 SUB2)
  (#$holdsIn SUB1 (SLOT THING VAL1))
  (#$holdsIn SUB2 (SLOT THING VAL2)))
</pre>
it follows that
<pre>
(#$greaterThanOrEqualTo VAL2 VAL1)
</pre>
is true. See also #$increasesThroughoutEvent and #$constantThroughoutEvent.")
(comment constantThroughoutEvent "This predicate is used to state that the value of a quantity slot remains constant for a fixed object throughout an event. <code>(#$constantThroughoutEvent SLOT THING EVENT)</code> is true if and only if for every pair of sub-events <code>SUB1</code> and <code>SUB2</code> of <code>EVENT</code> there exist values <code>VAL1</code> and <code>VAL2</code> such that if
<pre>
(#$and
  (#$startsAfterEndingOf SUB1 SUB2)
  (#$holdsIn SUB1 (SLOT THING VAL1))
  (#$holdsIn SUB2 (SLOT THING VAL2)))
</pre>
it follows that
<pre>
(#$numericallyEquals VAL1 VAL2)
</pre>
is true. See also #$decreasesThroughoutEvent and #$increasesThroughoutEvent.")
(comment TextLearnerStrategyTerm "A sub-collection of #$TextLearnerInfrastructureConstant. Each quoted instance of #$TextLearnerStrategyTerm is a term that is used for reasoning about how to evaluate hypotheses about the correct #$CycL representation of a piece of text. This can work at the level of words, sentences, paragraphs, or entire documents.")
(comment DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter "A specialization of #$SoftwareParameter. Instances of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter control how the #$DocumentDisambiguator carries out a disambiguation.")
(comment DocumentDisambiguatorParameter "A specialization of #$SoftwareParameter. Instances of #$DocumentDisambiguatorParameter control how the #$DocumentDisambiguator handles non-strategy-specific aspects of disambiguation.")
(comment softwareParameterRangeInSpecification "(#$softwareParameterRangeInSpecification SPEC PARAM VAL1 VAL2) states that, according to the #$ProgramSpecification SPEC, the #$SoftwareParameter PARAM is greater than VAL1 and lesser than VAL2.")
(comment MultiStrategyBonus "An instance of #$DocumentDisambiguatorParameter.")
(comment MinimumCorrectCookieScore "An instance of #$DocumentDisambiguatorParameter.")
(comment CookieScoreTolerance "An instance of #$DocumentDisambiguatorParameter.")
(comment CookieJarIterativity "An instance of #$DocumentDisambiguatorParameter.")
(comment checkConceptuallyRelated "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment conceptuallyRelated-MaxSentenceDistance "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment conceptuallyRelated-MaxWordDistance "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment interSentenceDegradationBase "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment intraSentenceDegradationBase "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment isaGenlsGeneralityCutoff "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment maxScorePerHit "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment wordWeightCutoff "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment strategyForPred "A sentence of the form (#$strategyForPred STRAT-TYPE PRED) means that the #$DisambiguationStrategy is a sub-collection of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategy and that the strategy is applicable (i.e., it rewards points to semantic hypotheses) based on the the #$Predicate PRED holding between at least some denotation posited by some #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory.")
(comment strategyRewardsArg "An assertion of the form (#$strategyRewardsArg STRAT N) means that the instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategy STRAT-TYPE awards hypotheses based on the holding of a particular predicate among the denotations of a set of hypotheses and that it will reward points to the hypotheses whose denotations can be proven to fill the Nth argument of a sentence involving the predicate that is associated with STRAT-TYPE (see #$strategyForPred).")
(comment LexicalHypothesisScoringStrategy "A sub-collection of #$Strategy.  Each instance of this collection is a strategy for evaluating hypotheses concerning the #$denotation of a #$LexicalWord.")
(comment ScoringStrategy "A sub-collection of #$Strategy. Each instance of #$ScoringStrategy represents a set of criteria for when points should be awarded and how many points should be given to some #$Thing in the course of an #$Evaluating. For instance, a strategy might represent the degree of belief given to a particular hypothesis based on the logical relations of its contents to other pieces of knowledge.")
(comment ScoringSoftwareStrategy "A specialization of #$ScoringStrategy and #$ProgramSpecification. Each instance of #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy is a #$ProgramSpecification that characterizes how a scoring program should behave. For example, a video game that awards points based on players' performance might have several strategies that reflect different levels of difficulty.")
(comment DisambiguationScoringStrategy "A sub-collection of #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy. Each instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategy represents a set of criteria used by the #$DocumentDisambiguator for awarding points to possible denotata of a particular natural language expression.")
(comment strategyParameterValueRange "A sentence of the form (#$strategyParameterValueRange STRATEGY PARAMETER MIN-VAL MAX-VAL) states that the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy STRATEGY incorporates the Parameter PARAMETER and some system employing STRATEGY can set PARAMETER to some value that is no less than the ScalarInterval MIN-VAL and no greater than the ScalarInterval MAX-VAL.")
(comment strategyGivesHypothesisScore "A sentence of the form (#$strategyGivesHypothesisScore STRAT HYP SCORE) means that the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy STRAT assigns the #$HypotheticalContext HYP a numeric score of SCORE within the context in which the sentence is asserted.  The value of SCORE that STRAT assigns to HYP can differ across context and can differ when STRAT's optimized form (see #$optimizedStrategyForm) differs.")
(comment strategyParameters "A sentence of the form (#$strategyParameters STRAT PARAM) states that the #$Parameter PARAM is one of the parameters that the  STRAT includes.  Thus, this the value assigned to PARAM can be modified and, possibly, change the behavior associated with executing STRAT.")
(comment strategyParameterValue "A sentence of the form (#$strategyParameterValue STRAT PARAM VALUE) means that the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy STRAT contains the #$Parameter PARAM and that it has assigned PARAM a value of VALUE, some #$ScalarInterval.")
(comment SymmetricDisambiguationStrategyForBinaryPredFn "An expression of the form (#$SymmetricDisambiguationStrategyForBinaryPredFn PRED) denotes an instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategy for the #$BinaryPredicate PRED.  The is a strategy for how to score hypotheses about the meaning of of some text (ranging from a single word in a document to the entire document).  For the strategies that are denoted by this function, (1) there will actually be two hypotheses that are evaluated, the scoring strategy will focus on the interpretations positing denotations that bear the relationship PRED between them, and (3) both hypotheses will receive the same score from this strategy for entering into this relationship.  So, the 'symmetry' of name refers to the distribution of points, not the logical properties of the #$BinaryPredicate PRED.")
(comment symmetricDisambiguationStrategyForBinaryPred "A sentence of the form (#$symmetricDisambiguationStrategyForBinaryPred STRAT-TYPE PRED) means that the #$DisambiguationStrategy is applicable (i.e., it rewards points to semantic hypotheses) based on the the #$BinaryPredicate PRED holding between the two denotations posited by two hypotheses and that it rewards equal points to both hypotheses by having this relationship.  So, the 'symmetry' of name refers to the distribution of points, not the properties of the #$BinaryPredicate PRED.  This is the corresponding predicate of the function #$SymmetricDisambiguationStrategyForBinaryPredFn.")
(comment exampleSentenceForValueInFACTTopic "(#$exampleSentenceForValueInFACTTopic VALUE SENTENCE FACTORY-TOPIC) means that SENTENCE is a good example, in the context of FACTORY-TOPIC, of a true statement using VALUE.")
(comment MergerOfAdobeAndMacromedia "The December, 2005 acquisition by Adobe Systems of Macromedia Inc.")
(comment BaseballGame "The collection of instances of #$GameEvent that are #$performanceOfWorks of #$Baseball-TheGame. Whichever team scores more baseball runs is the #$winner-First. #$BaseballGames typically last 9 #$BaseballInnings, but if it would be a #$TieGame, enough extra #$BaseballInnings are played to determine a #$winner-First.")
(comment PlayingAGame "A specialization of #$Playing and #$Event-Organized.  Each instance of #$PlayingAGame is an event in which a person plays a game.  Examples include people playing chess and people long jumping.  Instances of #$GameEvent (q.v.) include instances of #$PlayingAGame as sub-events.")
(comment accelerationOfObject-Translation "This predicate relates an object to its magnitude of acceleration. <code>(#$accelerationOfObject-Translation OBJECT ACCELERATION)</code> means that the acceleration of <code>OBJECT</code> has magnitude <code>ACCELERATION</code>. Note that physically, acceleration is a vector valued quantity, possessing both a direction and a magnitude. This predicate is intended to relate only the magnitude of acceleration to the object, irrespective of the directional component of acceleration.")
(comment maximumWidthOfObject "An instance of #$MeasurableQuantitySlot. (#$maximumWidthOfObject OBJ MAX-WIDTH) means that the maximum distance across OBJ from one of its side boundaries to the opposite side boundary is MAX-WIDTH.  See also #$widthOfObject, the generalization of this predicate.")
(comment SortingPhysicalObjects "A specialization of #$DistributionEvent: the collection of all events in which some agent(s) sorts some objects by physically moving them.  Se also #$SortingSomethingMentally.")
(comment SortingSomethingMentally "A specialization of #$GroupingThingsMentally, the collection of all events in which some agent(s) puts some group of things into some kind of order (often according to some schema). Examples include #$SortingE-MailMessages and  #$CataloguingWrittenMaterial. For cases where an agent sorts objects by physically moving them, see #$SortingPhysicalObjects.")
(comment Thinking-NonPurposeful "A specialization of  #$Thinking (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Thinking-NonPurposeful is a non-purposeful mental process.  Some compulsive thoughts and daydreams are positive examples.")
(comment personsInvolvedInActTypeWithFrequency "(#$personsInvolvedInActTypeWithFrequency ACT-TYPE FREQ) means that ACT-TYPE is something people, in general, do or are involved (as #$actors) in with some frequency greater than never and the frequency at which some instance of #$Person does or is involved in instances of ACT-TYPE is FREQ.")
(disjointWith DentalCareEvent-Generic GynecologicalCareEvent)
(disjointWith DentalCareEvent-Generic EyeCareEvent)
(comment DentalCareEvent-Generic "The collection of events in which dental care is administered. This includes personal teeth cleaning.")
(comment DentalCareEvent "The collection of events in which professional dental care is administered. This excludes personal teeth cleaning.")
(comment completeByExternalConvention "An instance of #$CompletenessGroundsPredicate that is used to state, about a collection or predicate whose extent is inferentially complete (see #$InferenceCompletenessPredicate), that the completeness derives mainly from a conventional stipulation, rather than from the nature of the items in the extent themselves, and that the convention is not something stipulated by the designers of the Cyc system, but exists in the world outside of Cyc. For example, <code>(#$completelyAssertedCollection #$DayOfWeekType)</code> holds, and so does <code>(#$completeByExternalConvention #$DayOfWeekType)</code>.  Cf. #$completeByCycConvention.")
(comment completeByConvention "An instance of #$CompletenessGroundsPredicate (q.v.) that is used to state, about a collection or predicate whose extent is inferentially complete (see #$InferenceCompletenessPredicate), that the completeness derives mainly from a conventional stipulation, rather than from the nature of the items in the extent themselves. 
<p>
For example, <code>(#$completelyAssertedCollection #$DayOfWeekType)</code> holds, and so does <code>(#$completeByConvention #$DayOfWeekType)</code>.  In contrast, #$completeByConvention does <i>not</i> hold of the completely-decidable collection #$Integer. Cf. #$completeByNature.  
<p>
See also the specializations #$completeByExternalConvention and #$completeByCycConvention.")
(comment SharedNote "A subcollection of #$DocumentationConstant.
Each instance of #$SharedNote is a CycL constant whose #$comment is a piece of documentation that can be attached, via the predicate #$sharedNotes, to various CycL terms.
<p>
Use shared-notes in cases where doing so would be more effective or efficient
than putting the same (or very similar) information into a separate comment or cyclist-note for each of the relevant CycL terms.")
(comment optimizedStrategyForm "A sentence of the form (#$optimizedStrategyForm STRATEGY SETTINGS-MT) relates a #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy to a #$Microtheory that describes the optimized instantiation of that strategy. STRATEGY is a #$Strategy and determines the set of available parameters and constraints on their allowable values. SETTINGS-MT stores specific values for those parameters that, within a particular context, are understood to be the optimal values.")
(comment disambiguationStrategyFromPredAndRewardedArgs "The corresponding predicate of the function #$DisambiguationStrategyFromPredAndRewardedArgsFn.  A sentence of the form (#$disambiguationStrategyFromPredAndRewardedArgs STRAT PRED SET-OF-INTEGERS) states that the #$DisambiguationScoringStrategy STRAT is based on the #$Predicate PRED and that it will assign scores (or reward) instances of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that posit meanings (see #$mtFocalContent and #$pegInterpretation-CycL) that (1) can be proven to enter into the relation expressed by PRED and which fill the Nth argument of that relationship, where N is an element of SET-OF-INTEGERS.")
(comment OptimizedStrategyFormFn "An expression of the form (#$OptimizedStrategyFormFn STRAT) denotes a #$Microtheory that stores the information on the best group of #$Parameter settings for the usage of the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy STRAT.")
(comment TypicalHumanActivityType-AtLeastOnce "The collection of specializations of #$HumanActivity such that persons typically are involved in (#$actors) at least one instance of the activity type in their lifetime.")
(comment DurationOfHumanActs-Topic "An instance of #$Topic.  Each instance of #$DurationOfHumanActs-Topic is a #$Thing which (in some significant sense) has to do with the topic of the duration of acts that peopple are involved with or perform.")
(comment InductiveInferenceProcess "A specialization of #$Reasoning.  Each instance of #$InductiveInferenceProcess is an inference in which a general fact or rule is generated in order to serve as an explanation of a less general fact or set of facts.")
(comment Thinking "A specialization of #$IntentionalMentalEvent.  Each instance of this collection is a thinking process in which it is essential that an agent uses information about the current state of the world (or some portion of the world).  Notable specializations of #$Thinking include #$Evaluating, #$MakingAPlan, #$Negotiating, and #$InventingSomething.")
(comment AbductiveInferenceProcess "A specialization of #$Reasoning.  Each instance of
#$AbductiveInferenceProcess is an inference is which hypotheses are
generated as possible explanations of further facts.  The hypotheses abduced are typically less certian that the facts to be explained.  This distinguishes #$AbductiveInferenceProcess from #$DeductiveInferenceProcess (q.v.), in each instance of which the conclusion produced is at least as certain its premises.")
(comment InferenceToTheBestExplanation "A specialization of #$Reasoning.  Each instance of #$InferenceToTheBestExplanation is an inference in which one generates hypotheses through abduction or induction (see #$AbductiveInferenceProcess and #$InductiveInferenceProcess), and then applies a selection mechanism to choose the best hypothesis out of the candidates generated.")
(comment DeductiveInferenceProcess "A specialization of #$Reasoning.  Each instance of
#$DeductiveInferenceProcess is an inference in which the conclusion is
at least as certain as the premises.")
(comment typicalOwnerTypeForArtifactType "(#$typicalOwnerTypeForArtifactType OWNER-TYPE ARTIFACT-TYPE) means that owners (#$owns) of instances of ARTIFACT-TYPE are typically instances of OWNER-TYPE.")
(comment connectedAtContact "<code>(#$connectedAtContact OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> are #$connectedTo each other at a point of contact.  I.e. <code>OBJ1</code> #$touches <code>OBJ2</code>.  This includes all cases of #$connectedTo except some when the two object are #$connectedViaConnector.")
(comment sticksInto "<code>(#$sticksInto INNER OUTER)</code> means that <code>INNER</code>, has one end inside of <code>OUTER</code>, its other end is at the edge of or outside of <code>OUTER</code>.  The end of <code>INNER</code> inside of <code>OUTER</code> may be surrounded in either two dimensions (such as the #$IndianSubcontinent and the #$IndianOcean) or three dimensions (such as a #$Nail in a #$Board-PieceOfWood).  More specific forms of #$sticksInto are #$protrudesInto for objects which are mostly outside, but have a portion sticking into another object; #$in-Rooted, for a solid object which is somewhat held in place and therefore also #$touches and is #$connectedTo another object; and #$sticksInto-2D, for cases of effectively two dimensional objects where <code>INNER</code> is not surrounded by <code>OUTER</code> in a perpendicular direction (as in the #$IndianOcean case mentioned above).")
(comment AxiomaticTheory "The collection of axiomatized #$ScientificTheory[ies].  Each #$AxiomaticTheory <b>T</b> includes a set of basic principles or <i>axioms</i>, and <b>T</b> is (or at least is uniquely determined by) the deductive closure of the set of its axioms.  For example, #$ZermeloFraenkelSetTheory is the theory of sets supported by the Zermelo and Fraenkel axiomatization.")
(comment CCCPointSet "A #$CCCPointSet is a set of points in a plane (see #$PlanarPointSet) with the additional properties that no three distinct elements are collinear (see #$collinearPoints) and no four distinct elements lie on a common circle (see #$concyclicPoints).")
(comment CCPointSet "A #$CCPointSet is a set of points in a plane (see #$PlanarPointSet) with the additional property that no three distinct elements are collinear (see #$collinearPoints). See also #$CCCPointSet.")
(comment conceptExtractedViaReferenceInStructure "<code>(#$conceptExtractedViaReferenceInStructure THING STRUCTURE)</code> means that the thing <code>THING</code> was first encountered by a Cyc-based data processing program (e.g., the TextLearner system (TheCycTextLearnerProgram)) that discovered a reference to it while processing <code>STRUCTURE</code>, an instance of #$AbstractStructure.")
(comment extractedFromStructure "<code>(#$extractedFromStructure THING STRUCTURE)</code> means that Cyc's knowledge of the existence of <code>THING</code> has its origins in the processing of a data-rich structure <code>STRUCTURE</code> performed by a Cyc-based data processing program (e.g., the TextLearner system (TheCycTextLearnerProgram)).")
(comment inCircle "If <code>P</code>, <code>Q</code>, <code>R</code>, and <code>S</code> are four points lying in a common plane, then <code>(#$inCircle P Q R S)</code> is true if and only if <code>(#$counterClockwise P Q R)</code> is true and <code>S</code> lies inside of the circle passing through <code>P</code>, <code>Q</code>, and <code>R</code>.")
(comment expressionHead "A functional relation (see #$FunctionalPredicate) that holds among sub-expressions (see #$subExpressions).  <code>(#$expressionHead EXPRESSION HEAD)</code> identifies <code>HEAD</code> as the instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that represents the syntactic head of the phrase represented by <code>EXPRESSION</code>.  For example 
<pre>
  (#$expressionHead
   (#$SubExpressionAtOffsetFn PEG-N \"an emu\" 11) 
   (#$SubExpressionAtOffsetFn PEG-N \"emu\" 14))</pre>
identifies the occurrence of \"emu\" within <code>PEG-N</code> at offset 14 is the head of the occurrence of \"an emu\" within <code>PEG-N</code> at offset 11.")
(comment LineSegment "A specialization of #$LinearObject-OneDimensional and #$LinearObject-Finite (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$LineSegment is a finite, continuous portion of a #$Line (q.v.). It might be straight or curved.  It might be  bounded (i.e. have two endpoints), semi-bounded (one endpoint; e.g. noose-shaped), or unbounded (no endpoints; e.g. ring-shaped).  Specializations of #$LineSegment include #$LineSegment-Straight and  #$LineString.")
(comment LinearObject-SemiBounded "A specialization of #$LinearObject (q.v.). #$LinearObject-SemiBounded is the collection of all and only linearly-shaped objects that have exactly one end-point.  They might be straight or curved, tangible or intangible, finite or infinite in length.
<p>
An important specialization of #$LinearObject-SemiBounded is #$Ray-Geometrical, whose instances are infinite in length and strictly one-dimensional.
<p>
See also #$LinearObject-Bounded and #$LinearObject-Unbounded.")
(comment interArgNotIsa1-2 "(#$interArgNotIsa1-2 PRED COL1 COL2) means that, when an instance of COL1 appears as the first argument to PRED, the second argument in that assertion is constrained <i>not</i> to be an instance of COL2 and vice versa.")
(comment linearObjectEnds "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and specialization (via #$genlInverse) of #$properlySpatiallySubsumes that relates a #$LinearObject to each of its ends.  <code>(#$linearObjectEnds LINE END)</code> means that <code>END</code> is one of the ends of <code>LINE</code>.
<p>
Note that the dimensionality and (in)tangibility of <code>END</code> depends partly on those of <code>LINE</code>.  For example, an end of a #$LinearObject-OneDimensional is always a #$Point, and the end of a #$LinearObject-ThreeDimensional is either a two- or three-dimensional bounded finite #$SurfaceRegion. If <code>LINE</code> is (in)tangible, then so is <code>END</code>.")
(comment rotationallyConnectedTo "A #$ConnectionPredicate (q.v.) and thus a specialization of #$connectedTo (q.v.).  <code>(#$rotationallyConnectedTo OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> are connected 
in such a way that rotational motion, and only rotational motion, can happen 
between them.  The range of rotational motion possible might be full or 
partial.  Non-rotational movement between two rotationally connected objects 
can occur only if the connection is broken, deformed, or disassembled.  If 
<code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> do rotate relative to one another, then this may be due to sliding of their surfaces, articulation of some joint part, or deformation of <code>OBJ1</code> or <code>OBJ2</code> (so long as that deformation only allows rotation between <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code>).  Positive examples: Femurs are rotationally connected to hips, doors are rotationally connected to door frames, doorknobs are rotationally connected to doors, and propellers are rotationally connected to airplanes; in computer trackballs the ball is rotationally connected to the housing.  Also a book cover is rotationally connected to its binding (but #$flapHingedTo is even more appropriate for describing such a connection because it is more specific). Negative examples: a planet orbiting a star (they are not connected; cf. #$MovingInACircle) and a toothpick stuck in a person's leg (although elastic deformation of flesh allows there to be rotational motion between toothpick and leg, it also may allow a  small amount of translational motion to occur between them; #$in-Lodged is more appropriate for describing this case).")
(comment VectorFn "An unreifiable #$BinaryFunction.  <code>(#$VectorFn MAGNITUDE DIRECTION)</code> denotes the #$VectorInterval whose #$vectorMagnitude and #$vectorDirection are <code>MAGNITUDE</code> and <code>DIRECTION</code>, respectively.")
(comment creatorOfCW "An #$IntangibleObjectPredicate that relates #$IntelligentAgents to  #$DevisedPracticeOrWorks that they have created. <code>(#$creatorOfCW AGENT WORK)</code>  means that <code>AGENT</code> is responsible for the creation of a substantial portion of the content of <code>WORK</code>.  To indicate the creator of a part of a written  #$ConceptualWork, use #$creatorOfPartOfWork.")
(comment SupermarketSized "Something that has the approximate volume of a house")
(comment FACToryCandidateSpatialThingForComparison "A placeholder concept.  Instances of this are candidate spatial thing types that may be suitable for being offered up in some sentence in the web game.")
(comment netForceActingOnObject "<code>(#$netForceActingOnObject OBJ FORCE)</code> means that the vector sum of all the forces acting on <code>OBJ</code> is <code>FORCE</code>.")
(comment accelerationOfObject-Direction "This predicate relates an object to the direction in which it is accelerating. <code>(#$accelerationOfObject-Direction OBJECT DIRECTION)</code> means that the acceleration of <code>OBJECT</code> has the vector direction <code>DIRECTION</code>. Note that physically, acceleration is a vector valued quantity, possessing both a direction and a magnitude. This predicate is intended to relate only the direction of acceleration to the object, irrespective of the magnitude component of acceleration.")
(comment accelerationOfObject-Vector "This predicate relates an object to the vector that expresses the magnitude and direction of its acceleration. <code>(#$accelerationOfObject-Direction OBJECT VECTOR)</code> means that the acceleration of <code>OBJECT</code> has the vector direction and magnitude of <code>VECTOR</code>. See also #$accelerationOfObject-Translation and #$accelerationOfObject-Direction.")
(comment PersonTypeByGender "A #$SecondOrderCollection and specialization of #$PersonTypeByCulture (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PersonTypeByGender is the collection of all #$Persons of a particular gender, understood as a set of attitudes, beliefs, and behaviors (and not strictly a matter of one's biological sex).  Instances include #$FemalePerson and #$MalePerson.")
(comment FemininePerson "A #$PersonTypeByGender (q.v.).  #$FemininePerson is the collection of all #$Persons of feminine gender.  Note that a human #$FemininePerson is typically, but not necessarily, a #$FemaleHuman (c.f.).")
(comment MasculinePerson "A #$PersonTypeByGender (q.v.).  #$MasculinePerson is the collection of all #$Persons of masculine gender.  Note that a human #$MasculinePerson is typically, but not necessarily, a #$MaleHuman (c.f.).")
(comment contributorToTheory "<code>(#$contributorToTheory AGENT THEORY)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> contributed intellectually to the elaboration of <code>THEORY</code>, a scientific theory. <code>AGENT</code> needs to have played an active and significant role in the elaboration of <code>THEORY</code>. In particular supplying means or logistic infrastructure isn't considered a contribution, as for instance funding research or typewriting copies of manuscripts. Similarly, animals or human being used as test subjects for experimental purposes by scientists aren't considered to be contributors in this sense. Positive example: Zermelo, Skolem and Fraenkel contibuted to the elaboration of #$ZermeloFraenkelSetTheory. Note that this theory is named after whom are traditionally considered to be its two most significant contributors. We do not formalize the contingente and subjective notions of 'most' or 'more'
significant contributor. We'll use the same predicate to give to Skolem
credit for its contribution to the theory.")
(comment mtSpindleOfTheory "<code>(#$mtSpindleOfTheory THEORY SPINDLE)</code> means that <code>SPINDLE</code> is the microtheory spindle that contains the laws or axioms (see #$LawOfNature and #$Axiom) of the #$PhilosophicalTheory <code>THEORY</code>.")
(comment theoreticalField "(#$theoreticalField THEORY FIELD) means that the #$PhilosophicalTheory THEORY belongs to FIELD, a #$ScientificFieldOfStudy. Many theories can belong to a single field, for instance #$Systematics-Taxonomy knows of different theories that classify organisms. It is also the case when a field has many #$subFields such as #$Physics, any theories in one of the subfield of the latter will be a theory of the broader field. On the other hand, some theories may belong to more than one field.")
(comment Connection-Configuration "A specialization of #$Configuration (q.v.).  #$Connection-Configuration is the collection of all configurations in which the objects configured are connected to each other.")
(comment Coupling-Configuration "A specialization of #$PhysicalConnection-Configuration.  Each instance of  #$Coupling-Configuration is a connection which enables the flow of some stuff, such as electricity, water or gas.  For example, the connection made by plugging a phone into a phone jack is a #$Coupling-Configuration -- it would enable an electrical signal to move to the phone from the jack.")
(comment PhysicalConnection-Configuration "A specialization of #$Connection-Configuration and #$Attachment (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PhysicalConnection-Configuration is a configuration situation in which the objects configured are physical objects that are attached to one another.")
(comment RigidConnection "A specialization of #$PhysicalConnection-Configuration.  Each instance of #$RigidConnection is a situation in which the connected objects are rigidly connected.")
(comment configuredObjects "This predicates relates a #$Configuration to the objects that are configured in the situation.   For instance, if a set of links is configured into a chain, then the chain and each of its links would be related to the configuration situation by #$configuredObjects.")
(comment FACToryObjectTypeVolumeBuckets "Each instance of this collection will be a collection of object types that, via the web game, have all been categorized as having instances that have similar volumes.")
(comment FACToryObjectTypeValueBuckets "Each instance of this collection will be a collection of object types that, via the web game, have all been categorized as having a similar sort of value.  These values can range from being found in a particular room in a house -- \"things (types) found in a kitchen\" -- to having instances that are similar volumes or size.")
(comment comparisonClassForFACTory "(#$comparisonClassForFACTory BUCKET VALUE TOPIC) holds just in case BUCKET is the collection of object types that have values similar to VALUE in the context of TOPIC.")
(comment predicateForComparisonClassForFACTory "(#$predicateForComparisonClassForFACTory BUCKET-TYPE BIN-PRED) holds just in case BIN-PRED is the predicate whose values are being compared in the specialization of #$FACToryObjectTypeValueBuckets BUCKET-TYPE.  For instance, (#$predicateForComparisonClassForFACTory #$FACToryObjectTypeVolumeBuckets #$volumeOfObject) holds because volumeOfObject is the predicate used to determine the similarity classes for volume that are the instances of FACToryObjectTypeVolumeBuckets.  Associate a particular value with an instance of FACToryObjectTypeValueBuckets with the predicate #$comparisonClassForFACTory.")
(comment MicroscopicSized-ObjectType "The collection of object types that have instances that are all, roughly, microscopic.")
(comment AttackSubmarine "A specialization of #$Submarine-Military.  Each instance of #$AttackSubmarine is a submarine whose primary function is to seek and destroy other submarines or surface vessels.")
(comment lawContributesToSlotValue "<code>(#$lawContributesToSlotValue LAW SLOT THING SIT)</code> means that the law of nature, <code>LAW</code>, applies to the thing <code>THING</code> in the situation <code>SIT</code>, and partially determines the value of <code>SLOT</code> applied to <code>THING</code> in<code> SIT</code>.")
(comment lawAppliesToObjectInSituation "<code>(#$lawAppliesToObjectInSituation LAW THING SIT)</code> means that the law of nature, <code>LAW</code>, applies to the thing <code>THING</code> in the situation <code>SIT</code>.")
(comment centerOfMass "<code>(#$centerOfMass OBJ POINT)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is the center of mass of the object <code>OBJ</code>.  In other words, <code>POINT</code> is the single point, relative to the parts of <code>OBJ</code> that, if it were a point mass, would move in the same way as <code>OBJ</code> were <code>OBJ</code> subject to the same external forces.")
(comment tangentPlaneAtPointOnSurface "A plane, <code>PLANE</code> is <i>tangent</i> to a surface, <code>SURFACE</code> at a point <code>POINT</code>, if and only if for every curve, <code>CURVE</code>, on <code>SURFACE</code> passing through <code>POINT</code> there is some straight line, <code>LINE</code>, contained in <code>PLANE</code> which is tangent to <code>CURVE</code>. <code>POINT</code> is often called the <i>point of tangency</i>. In Cyc, this relation is stated as <code>(#$tangentPlaneAtPointOnSurface POINT PLANE SURFACE)</code>.")
(comment HomosexualPersonTypeBySex "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$HomosexualPersonTypeBySex is a specialization of #$HomosexualHuman whose instances are all and only those of the same sex.")
(comment subCollectionTypes "A #$TaxonomicSlot (q.v.) that relates pairs of  #$CollectionTypes (q.v.).  <code>(#$subCollectionTypes COLTYPE SUBCOLTYPE)</code> means that every instance of <code>SUBCOLTYPE</code> is a specialization (see #$genls) of some instance of <code>COLTYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$subCollectionTypes #$ObjectTypeByGender #$PersonTypeByGender)</code> holds because each (most-inclusive) collection of <i>persons</i> grouped by gender (e.g. #$FemininePerson) is a specialization of some (most-inclusive) collection of <i>objects</i> grouped by gender (viz. #$FeminineObject).
<p>
Note that the instances of <code>SUBCOLTYPE</code> and <code>COLTYPE</code> mentioned in the above definition might or might not actually be reified in 
the Cyc Knowledge Base.  Thus, the above example would still be true even if the collection #$FeminineObject were not represented in the KB.")
(comment Battle "A specialization of #$HostileSocialAction.  Each instance of #$Battle is an event in which two or more large groups of people meet and fight one another, each group seeking to vanquish (some of) the others.  Instances of #$Battle are often conducted by instances of #$MilitaryOrganization (q.v.), and they may influence the outcomes of instances of #$War (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Battle will have as sub-events at least two #$PhysicallyAttackingAnAgent events, in which one of the fighting groups attacks the other.")
(comment UrbanCombat "A specialization of #$Battle.  Each instance of #$UrbanCombat is a battle that occurs in an urban setting.")
(comment CustomsHouse "A building in a port where customs and duties are paid, and where vessels are entered and cleared.")
(comment PersonTypeByContinentAffiliation "This is the collection of types of person distinguished by the continent on which their native country or country of citizenship person has its national territory. A person native to an area of a continent but not under the jurisdiction of any GeopoliticalEntity would also be an instance of the #$PersonTypeByContinentAffiliation corresponding to that continent.")
(comment ArtisticTypeByStyle "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$ArtisticTypeByStyle is a collection of the things that are of the represented genre/school/type.  The various specializations of this collection are collections of collections that all are genres of a single type of thing.  For instance, #$ArchitecturalTypeByStyle is the collection of 
collections of #$ConstructionArtifacts each one representing a particular architectural style.")
(comment ArchitecturalTypeByStyle "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$ArchitecturalTypeByStyle is a collection of instances of #$ConstructionArtifact having a particular architectual style.  A building or artifact may be an instance of several architectural styles at the same time.")
(comment VirginiaClassSubmarine "A specialization of #$AttackSubmarine. Each instance of #$VirginiaClassSubmarine is an attack submarine built for the U.S. Navy.")
(comment SeawolfClassSubmarine "A specialization of #$AttackSubmarine. Each instance of #$SeawolfClassSubmarine is an attack submarine built for the U.S. Navy.")
(comment LosAngelesClassSubmarine "A specialization of #$AttackSubmarine. Each instance of #$LosAngelesClassSubmarine is an attack submarine built for the U.S. Navy.")
(comment OhioClassSubmarine "A specialization of #$BallisticMissileSubmarine. Each instance of #$OhioClassSubmarine is a ballistic missile submarine built for the U.S. Navy.")
(comment CycLClosedSentence "A specialization of both #$CycLSentence and #$CycLClosedExpression.  The collection of CycL sentences that contain no free variables.  Note that those CycL sentences that are treated (in the context of representing assertions in the Cyc Knowledge Base) as implicitly containing universal quantifiers are <i>not</i> closed sentences (cf. #$CycLOpenSentence).")
(comment CycLClosedFormulaicSentence "A specialization of #$CycLFormulaicSentence, #$CycLClosedSentence, and #$CycLClosedFormula.  This is the collection of all closed CycL sentences that are not constants (cf. #$CycLTruthValueSentence).")
(disjointWith CycLTruthValueSentence (CollectionDifferenceFn CycLClosedSentence CycLTruthValueSentence))
(comment languagesSpokenHere "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a #$GeographicalRegion to a #$NaturalLanguage.  <code>(#$languagesSpokenHere REGION LANGUAGE)</code> means that a signficant percentage (roughly, at least 5-10%) ) of the residents of <code>REGION</code> speak <code>LANGUAGE</code> at least somewhat fluently (in the sense of #$languageSpoken).")
(comment LocallyEuclideanSpatialThing "A specialization of #$SpatialThing.    Roughly speaking, a spatial thing is a #$LocallyEuclideanSpatialThing if it has no self-intersections.   For example, a line-segment is geometrically simple if it can be traced without visiting the same point twice; a two-dimensional region is geometrically simple if its boundary is simple.
<p>
More formally, an <code>N</code>-dimensional spatial thing <code>THING</code> is geometrically simple if and only if, for every point in the interior of <code>THING</code>, there exists a neighborhood of the point contained in the interior of <code>THING</code> and a homeomorphism (open and continuous bijection) that maps the neighborhood onto an open subset of <code>N</code> dimensional Euclidean space.
<p>
While CycL currently lacks the vocabulary to codify the formal definition, we can state restricted forms of it for certain classes of spatial things, and do so in the cases that are of immediate interest.  For these, see the reified specializations of this collection.")
(comment Parameter "A sub-collection of #$AspatialInformationStore.  A #$Parameter is a component of some larger #$AspatialInformationStore that is capable of storing a value and having that value change.  An important sub-class of this collection is #$SoftwareParameter, but this collection is more general and includes such things as how much weight a college admissions officer gives to extracurricular activities when rating applications or how much a chess program values pawns when calculating the value of the board that results from a move.")
(comment BooleanParameter "A specialization of #$Parameter. Instances of #$BooleanParameter can have one of two values.  From a logical perspective, this would be #$True and #$False.  However, 0 and 1 will also work.")
(disjointWith EnumerableSetOrCollection NonDenumerableSetOrCollection)
(comment EnumerableSetOrCollection "A specialization of #$SetOrCollection.  A set or collection is an instance of #$EnumerableSetOrCollection if and only if it is either finite (see #$FiniteSetOrCollection) or enumerably infinte (see #$DenumerableSetOrCollection) in size.  Thus, its #$cardinality is no greater than aleph-0 (i.e. #$PlusInfinity).")
(comment hypothesisRelevantScoringSetsOrCollections "A sentence of the form (#$hypothesisRelevantScoringSetsOrCollections HYP-MT SET-OR-COLL) relates a #$HypotheticalContext HYP-MT to a #$SetOrCollection SET-OR-COLL.  The sentence means that, relative to the current context, the evaluation of HYP-MT (for instance, in considering its #$hypothesisMtConfidence) can sensibly include reference to HYP-MT being an element of SET-OR-COLL.  In this case, membership in SET-OR-COLL will typically be a shorthand for a more complex set of properties and relations to other hypotheses or pieces of knowledge.")
(comment AllotmentWithFollowupQuestionsAndConceptsDisabledInCAE "A specialization of #$Allotment (q.v.).  Each instance of #$AllotmentWithFollowupQuestionsAndConceptsDisabledInCAE is an allotment for which all of the forward inference that generates followup questions and concepts is disabled.")
(comment modelMtForUserAndTask "The corresponding predicate of the function #$ModelMtByUserAndTaskFn. An assertion of the form <code>(#$modelMtForUserAndTask MT PERSON TASK)</code> states that the #$Microtheory <code>MT</code> is the context for representing relevant facts regarding the #$Person <code>PERSON</code>'s work on the #$Allotment <code>TASK</code> such that a Cyc-enabled application (e.g., #$Nooescape-TheProgram) can act agentively based on that knowledge.")
(comment CycLTerm "The collection of all syntactically well-formed   expressions in the CycL language that can be used as terms, i.e. that can be combined with other expressions to form non-atomic terms or formulas.  Since the grammar of the CycL language allows any CycL expression to be used as a term, #$CycLTerm and #$CycLExpression are coextensional collections.")
(comment SubLSExpression "The collection of all expressions of the #$SubL language.  #$SubL is the implementation language of the Cyc system, and it subsumes the #$CycL language.  The #$CycL grammar is more strict than the #$SubL grammar. Thus, while every #$CycLExpression is a #$SubLSExpression, the converse is not true. Examples of #$SubLSExpressions that are not #$CycLExpressions include Arabic decimal numeric expressions such as '123', expressions like '(1 2 3)' that denote lists of numbers, and \"dotted pair\" expressions like '(#$Cat . #$Dog)'.")
(comment FacetingCollectionType "A collection of #$CollectionTypes. Each instance of #$FacetingCollectionType is a collection-type that \"facets\" -- i.e. stands in the #$facets-Generic (q.v.) relation to -- another collection. Examples include #$AutomobileTypeByBrand and #$MusicTypeByGenre, which facet #$Automobile and #$Music, respectively.")
(comment FacetInstanceCollection "A #$CollectionType.  Each instance of #$FacetInstanceCollection is a collection that is an instance of a #$FacetingCollectionType (q.v.).  For example, the facet-instance-collection (#$WineOfVintageFn (#$YearFn 1926)) is an instance of the faceting-collection-type #$WineTypeByVintage.")
(comment facetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPred "A ternary #$FacetBasisPredicate (q.v.) that relates a collection to a type-level binary predicate to a #$FacetingCollectionType such that the latter facets the collection on the basis of the predicate.  <code>(#$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPred COL BINPRED FACET)</code> means that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPred FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPredFn.")
(comment FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredFn "A binary function that takes a collection and a binary predicate to the #$FacetingCollectionType (if any) that facets the collection on the basis of the predicate.  (#$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredFn <b>COL BINPRED</b>) is the faceting-collection-type <code>FACET</code> such that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnBinaryPred FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPred.")
(comment FacetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPredFn "A binary function that takes a collection and a type-level binary predicate to the #$FacetingCollectionType (if any) that facets the collection on the basis of the predicate.  (#$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPredFn <code>COL BINPRED</code>) is the faceting-collection-type <code>FACET</code> such that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPred FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPred.")
(comment initialLinearVelocityOfPrimaryObjectMoving "<code>(#$initialLinearVelocityOfPrimaryObjectMoving MOVE VEL)</code> means in the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> where the primary moving object (see #$primaryObjectMoving) is <code>OBJ</code>, at the start of <code>MOVE</code>, <code>VEL</code> is the linear velocity of <code>OBJ</code> (see #$LinearVelocityVector).")
(comment terminalLinearVelocityOfPrimaryObjectMoving "<code>(#$terminalLinearVelocityOfPrimaryObjectMoving MOVE VEL)</code> means in the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> where the primary moving object (see #$primaryObjectMoving) is <code>OBJ</code>, at the end of <code>MOVE</code>, <code>VEL</code> is the linear velocity of <code>OBJ</code> (see #$LinearVelocityVector).")
(comment planeThroughPoints "It is an elementary fact of geometry that if <code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, and <code>C</code> are three noncollinear points, then there exists a unique plane <code>PLANE</code> which subsumes them. In #$CycL, we write <code>(#$planeThroughPoints A B C PLANE)</code>.")
(comment hiringAgent "(#$hiringAgent HIRING HIRER) means that HIRER is the agent doing the hiring in the #$MakingAnAgreement event HIRING.  HIRER may be either a person or an organization.")
(comment agentHired "(#$agentHired HIRING HIRED) means that HIRED is the #$Person or #$Organization hired in the #$MakingAnAgreement HIRING.")
(comment totalDisplacement "This predicate relates a translational movement event to the accumulated total displacement by the #$primaryObjectMoving in that event. <code>(#$totalDisplacement MOVE DIST)</code> means that in the event <code>MOVE</code>, the #$primaryObjectMoving covered the distance <code>DIST</code>. Note that <code>DIST</code> is the net change in position by the object, not its total distance travelled; so if the #$motionPathway-Complete of <code>MOVE</code> is a round trip a mile each way, the #$totalDisplacement would be zero miles, not two. For a way to express the total distance travelled, see the predicate #$distanceTranslated.")
(comment distanceTranslated "This predicate relates a translational movement event to the total distance travelled by the #$objectMoving in that event.  <code>(#$distanceTranslated MOVE DIST)</code> means that in the event <code>MOVE</code>, the #$primaryObjectMoving covered the distance <code>DIST</code>.  Note that <code>DIST</code> is the actual distance travelled by the object, not merely its net change in position; so if the #$motionPathway-Complete of <code>MOVE</code> is a round trip a mile each way, the #$distanceTranslated would be two miles, not zero. For a way to express the net change in position, see the predicate #$totalDisplacement.")
(comment evaluate "A binary #$MetaLanguagePredicate that relates a thing to a thing that is known to denote it.  <code>(#$evaluate THING TERM)</code> means that <code>TERM</code> evalutes to <code>THING</code>: the Cyc system \"knows\" that <code>TERM</code> denotes <code>THING</code>.
<p>
Note that #$evaluate is #$notAssertible (q.v.).  Its most common uses are in rules or queries, with its first argument-place filled (syntactically speaking) with a variable.  In a query, #$evaluate is typically used to establish a binding between such a variable and an evaluatable expression.  If VAR is a variable, a literal of the form <code>(#$evaluate VAR TERM)</code> is satisfied by an HL module that evaluates <code>TERM</code> and binds <code>VAR</code> to the result.  For example, the literal '<code>(#$evaluate ?SUM (#$PlusFn 1 2))</code>' would bind '<code>?SUM</code>' to 3.  If #$evaluate's arg1 position is filled instead with a closed expression, then the HL module checks to see if the arg2 term evaluates to the denotatum of that arg1 expression.  For example, if asked the sentence '<code>(#$evaluate 3 (#$PlusFn 1 2))</code>' the system will return '#$True'.")
(comment TheTermToSolveForFn "#$TheTermToSolveForFn takes a #$Collection as its argument and returns an instance of #$IndexicalConcept that in the context functions as the indexical instance of the collection that is equal to the entity to be solved for in a query.  Generally, terms denoted with #$TheTermToSolveForFn are used as arguments to the query sentence referenced by #$querySentenceOfQuery.")
(comment functionDescribesSlotValueForThingInArgDuring "<code>(#$functionDescribesSlotValueForThingInArgDuring FUNCTION SLOT THING N EVENT)</code> means that the #$Function-Denotational <code>FUNCTION</code> determines the values of the functional slot <code>SLOT</code> with #$Thing <code>THING</code> as its <code>N</code>th argument during the #$Event <code>EVENT</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$functionDescribesSlotValueForThingInArgDuring Function001 #$altitudeAboveSeaLevel Object001 1 Event001)</code> means that at each time point during <code>Event001</code>, <code>Function001</code> returns the functional value of #$altitudeAboveSeaLevel given <code>Object001</code> as the first argument to #$altitudeAboveSeaLevel.")
(comment altitudeAboveGround "<code>(#$altitudeAboveGround OBJECT DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is the distance between the bottom or lowest point of <code>OBJECT</code> and the instance of #$Ground which is directly below it.  On earth, the relevant ground would, of course, be #$EarthsGround.
<p>
As an airplane flies over a mountain range, at a constant altitude above sea level (expressable with #$altitudeAboveSeaLevel (q.v.)), the airplane's #$altitudeAboveGround will vary: it will be small when the plane is above mountain peaks, and large when the plane is above valleys.")
(comment velocityOfObject "<code>(#$velocityOfObject OBJ VEL)</code> relates a tangible object <code>OBJ</code> to a #$LinearVelocityVector <code>VEL</code>; <code>VEL</code> represents both the speed of <code>OBJ</code> and its direction of motion.  See also #$speedOfObject-Translation, #$directionOfTranslation-Throughout, and #$direction-Pointing.")
(comment translationalKineticEnergy "A #$PhysicalAmountSlot and specialization of #$hasPhysicalQuantity.  <code>(#$translationalKineticEnergy OBJECT QUANTITY)</code> means that <code>QUANTITY</code> is a measure of <code>OBJECT</code>'s translational kinetic energy.
<p>
The #$translationalKineticEnergy <i>KE</i> of a body is
<p>
<i>KE = 1/2 * m * v</i><sup>2</sup>,
<p>
where <i>m</i> is the body's #$massOfObject and <i>v</i> is its #$speedOfObject-Translation.  See also #$rotationalKineticEnergy.")
(comment rotationalKineticEnergy "A #$PhysicalAmountSlot and specialization of #$hasPhysicalQuantity.  (#$rotationalKineticEnergy OBJECT QUANTITY) means that QUANTITY is a measure of OBJECT's rotational kinetic energy.
<p>
The #$rotationalKineticEnergy <i>KE</i> of a body rotating about a fixed axis is
<p>
<i>KE = 1/2 * I * w</i><sup>2</sup>,
<p>
where <i>I</i> is the body's #$rotationalInertia and <i>w</i> is its #$angularVelocity. see also #$translationalKineticEnergy.")
(comment indexReferences "<code>(#$indexReferences INDEX REFERENT)</code> means that <code>REFERENT</code> is one of the references, or entries in the index <code>INDEX</code>.")
(comment CycEBMTTemplateIndex "The collection of all EBMT template indexes.  Such indexes associate a linkage/CycL tuple (an instance of #$List consisting of an instance of #$LinkParserLinkage and an instance of #$CycLFormula) to one or more instances of #$CycEBMTTemplate.")
(comment unsuccessfulAttemptTypeOfActionType "<code>(#$unsuccessfulAttemptTypeOfActionType FAILURE-TYPE TYPE)</code> identifies <code>FAILURE-TYPE</code> as the collection of failed attempts to bring about an instance of <code>TYPE</code>.  #$unsuccessfulAttemptTypeOfActionType is the predicate that corresponds to the function  #$UnsuccessfulAttemptToFn (see
 #$functionCorrespondsToPredicate).  See also #$AttemptingFn and #$attemptsToDoActType.")
(comment AddingAnEntryToACycEBMTTemplateIndex "The collection of events in which a template is added to an instance of #$CycEBMTTemplateIndex.")
(comment assertionFailedToBeUsedInProcessType "<code>(#$assertionFailedToBeUsedInProcessType ASSERTION PROCESS)</code> means that there has been at least one failed attempt (typically by a computer process) to use the CycL assertion <code>(#$Quote ASSERTION)</code> to bring about an instance of <code>PROCESS</code>, a specializaton of #$Action.")
(comment ParsingTemplate "The collection of instances of #$AbstractInformationStructure that serve as patterns for mapping one sort of text structure into another.")
(comment CycEBMTParsingTemplate "The collection of all instances of #$ParsingTemplate used by the Cyc EBMT system.")
(comment Sun "A specialization of #$Star (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Sun is a star that forms part of a solar system (see #$PlanetarySystem).")
(comment forceActingOnObject "(#$forceActingOnObject OBJ VECTOR) means that the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ has the force VECTOR acting on it.  See also #$netForceActingOnObject.")
(comment gravitationalForceAtPointOnEarth "<code>(#$gravitationalForceAtPointOnEarth POINT VECTOR)</code> means that <code>VECTOR</code> is the #$GravitationalForceVector at <code>POINT</code>, an instance of #$GeographicalPoint-Intangible. See also the corresponding function #$GravitationalForceAtPointOnEarthFn.")
(comment magneticForceAtPointOnEarth "<code>(#$magneticForceAtPointOnEarth POINT VECTOR)</code> means that <code>VECTOR</code> is the #$MagneticForceVector at <code>POINT</code>, an instance of #$GeographicalPoint-Intangible. See also the corresponding function #$MagneticForceAtPointOnEarthFn.")
(comment increaseEventTypeForSlot "(#$increaseEventTypeForSlot INCREASE-EVT-TYPE
SLOT) means that INCREASE-EVT-TYPE is the collection of events in
which some object increases its value on the #$QuantitySlot SLOT.  See the corresponding function #$IncreaseOnSlotFn.")
(comment decreaseEventTypeForSlot "(#$decreaseEventTypeForSlot DECREASE-EVT-TYPE
SLOT) means that DECREASE-EVT-TYPE is the collection of events in
which some object decreases its value on the #$QuantitySlot SLOT. See the corresponding function #$DecreaseOnSlotFn. ")
(comment QuantityChangeEvent "#$QuantityChangeEvent is the collection of events whose focal characteristic is a change in some quantity -- more
specifically, a change in value on some instance of
#$QuantitySlot.")
(comment IncreaseOnSlotFn "(#$IncreaseOnSlotFn  SLOT) denotes the collection of events
in which something increases its value on SLOT, an instance of #$QuantitySlot.
For example, (#$IncreaseOnSlotFn #$basicPriceOfType) is the collection of
events in which some type of good increases in price.")
(comment Mereotopology "An instance of #$FieldOfStudy.  Mereotopology combines Mereology (#$Mereology, q.v.) and Topology (#$Topology-FieldOfStudy, q.v.) to study the topological relations among parts and wholes, and the boundaries that exist among parts of a whole.")
(comment conceptTypeDomain "<code>(#$conceptTypeDomain COLL THING)</code> identifies <code>COLL</code> as the instance of #$ConceptTypeByDomain whose instances are all concepts relevant to <code>THING</code>.  For example, <code>(#$conceptTypeDomain #$MereotopologyConcept #$Mereotopology)</code> identifies #$MereotopologyConcept as the collection of concepts relevant to Mereotopology.")
(comment stateOfHealth "The binary predicate #$stateOfHealth relates an animal to its state of health.  The first argument is a #$BiologicalLivingObject; the second argument must be an #$BLOTypeByGenericHealthStatus, such as #$Sick or #$Healthy. To specify a BLO's state relative to a given type of #$AilmentCondition, use the predicate #$stateOfAilment.")
(disjointWith SenseExtendingPredicate NLParsingDirectivePredicate)
(disjointWith SenseExtendingPredicate SummaryPredicate)
(comment endingQuantity "#$endingQuantity relates an instance of
#$QuantityChangeEvent to its 'ending value,' that is, the value
possessed by the #$objectChangingValue on the relevant #$QuantitySlot
at the end of the event.  So, if (#$changesSlot
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> <code>SLOT</code>) and
(#$objectChangingValue <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> <code>OBJ</code>),
then (#$endingQuantity <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>
<code>VALUE</code>) iff (<code>SLOT</code> <code>OBJ</code>
<code>VALUE</code>) holds at the conclusion of
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>.")
(comment startingQuantity "#$startingQuantity relates an instance
of #$QuantityChangeEvent to its 'starting value,' that is, the value
possessed by the #$objectChangingValue on the relevant #$QuantitySlot
immediately before the change occurs.  So, if (#$changesSlot
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> <code>SLOT</code>) and
(#$objectChangingValue <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> <code>OBJ</code>),
then (#$startingQuantity <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>
<code>VALUE</code>) iff (<code>SLOT</code> <code>OBJ</code>
<code>VALUE</code>) holds just before
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>.")
(comment heightOfObject "A #$PhysicalAmountSlot used for stating the heights of tangible objects and other (polydimensional) spatial things.  (#$heightOfObject OBJECT HEIGHT) means that the #$Distance (q.v.) from OBJECT's bottom boundary to its top boundary is HEIGHT.  \"Top\" and \"bottom\" are often determined by an object's intrinsic default orientation, if it has one (see #$TopAndBottomSidedObject).  But some objects have tops and bottoms -- and thus heights -- only by virtue of their spatial relations to certain other objects in their environments.  In any case, having a height requires being at least two-dimensional (see #$PolyDimensionalThing).  See also #$lengthOfObject, #$widthOfObject, and #$depthOfObject.")
(comment Height "A specialization of #$Distance. #$Height is the collection of all distances that are heights of objects (see #$heightOfObject). Each instance of #$Height is the distance from some object's bottom boundary to its top boundary.")
(comment widthOfObject "<code>(#$widthOfObject OBJECT WIDTH)</code> means that the #$Distance across <code>OBJECT</code> from one side boundary to the opposite side boundary is <code>WIDTH</code>.  Side boundaries are roughly perpendicular to the top and bottom of the object.  Note that some objects have widths by virtue of having one or more intrinsic default orientations (see #$BilateralObject and its specializations), while some objects have widths by virtue of having orientations determined only in relation to their environments.  In any case, having a width requires being
at least two-dimensional (see #$PolyDimensionalThing).  See also #$lengthOfObject, #$heightOfObject, and #$depthOfObject.")
(comment Width "A specialization of #$Distance. #$Width is the collection of all distances that are widths of objects (see #$widthOfObject). Each instance of #$Width is the #$Distance across an object from one of its side boundaries to its opposite side boundary.")
(comment Altitude "A specialization of #$Distance. #$Altitude is the collection of all distances that are altitudes of objects (see #$altitudeAboveGround). Each instance of #$Altitude is the #$Distance between a spatial thing and the ground of the planet nearest to that thing.")
(comment objectChangingValue "(#$objectChangingValue
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>
<code>OBJ</code>) means that <code>OBJ</code> is the item whose value on some #$QuantitySlot undergoes a change during <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>.
Supposing that (#$changesSlot <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>
<code>SLOT</code>), then (#$objectChangingValue <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> <code>OBJ</code>) means that the value of
<code>OBJ</code> on <code>SLOT</code> increased or decreased during
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>.")
(comment admittedArgument "A ternary #$MetaRelation predicate that is used to state that a given thing meets the argument-type constraints on a given argument-place of a given relation. <code>(#$admittedArgument THING ARGNUM RELN)</code> means that <code>THING</code> satisfies all of the (single-argument) argument-type constraints (see #$ArgIsaPredicate and #$ArgGenlPredicate) for the <b><code>ARGNUM</b>-th</code> argument position of <code>RELN</code>.  
<p>
For example, both of the following sentences hold:
<pre>  (#$admittedArgument #$AbrahamLincoln 1 #$wife)

  (#$admittedArgument #$Cougar 1 #$JuvenileFn) .</pre>
See also #$admittedAllArgument and #$admittedSentence.")
(comment UnorderedQuantityType "A #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$QuantityType.  Each instance <code>TYPE</code> of #$UnorderedQuantityType is a collection of #$Quantity[ies] having the following property: for any two instances <code>Q1</code> and  <code>Q2</code> of <code>TYPE</code>, neither <code>(#$followingValue Q1 Q2)</code> nor <code>(#$followingValue Q2 Q1)</code> holds (cf. #$colinearQuantities).  In other words, any two instances of <code>TYPE</code> are incommensurable.
<p>
Cf. #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType.")
(comment requiredArg1Pred "A #$MetaPredicate for stating that every instance of a given collection is related to some thing or things by a given predicate.  <code>(#$requiredArg1Pred COL PRED)</code> means that for every  instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code>, there exists some sequence <code>ARGS</code> such that <code>(PRED INST . ARGS)</code> holds.  For example, (#$requiredArg1Pred #$Organization #$hasMembers) means that every organization has at least one member.  
<p>
See also #$requiredArg2Pred and #$requiredArg3Pred.")
(comment MeasurementAtFn "(#$MeasurementAtFn QUANTITY TEMPORAL-THING) denotes the value of the quantity QUANTITY as measured during TEMPORAL-THING.")
(comment accelerationDueToGravityOnPlanetaryBody "<code>(#$accelerationDueToGravityOnPlanetaryBody BODY RATE)</code> means that near the surace of the planetary body <code>BODY</code>, the force of gravity causes objects to accelerate at <code>RATE</code> towards the center of mass of <code>BODY</code>.  For example, (#$accelerationDueToGravityOnPlanetaryBody   #$PlanetEarth (#$MetersPerSecondPerSecond 9.80619)) is true.")
(comment baseWordFormTypeEndingLetters "<code>(#$baseWordFormTypeEndingLetters TYPE STRING)</code> identifies <code>TYPE</code> as the instance of #$BaseWordFormTypeByEndingLetters each instance of which is a word unit with the ending letters <code>STRING</code>.")
(comment Bog "A specialization of #$WetlandRegion. Each #$Bog is a wetland characterized by wet, spongy soil composed mostly of dead vegetation which is not rapidly decomposing. This soil is very nutrient-poor, so it can't be cultivated and only some plants can live there.  Typical bog plants are mosses, sedges and reeds. Bogs definitely don't have trees.")
(comment tensionForceOnObject "<code>(#$tensionForceOnObject OBJ TENS)</code> relates a tangible object <code>OBJ</code> to the #$Tension vector <code>TENS</code>, which is the tension force acting on <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment GrassyScrubbyVegetationRegion "A specialization of #$ScrubbyVegetationRegion. Each #$GrassyScrubbyVegetationRegion is a #$GeographicalRegion that is populated with grasses and short woody plants.")
(comment ErosionProtectionStructure "Each instance of #$ErosionProtectionStructure is a fixed structure built to protect a region against soil erosion caused by water.  Such structures include seawalls, breakwaters, groynes.  These are built in or near the water and either run parallel to the shore or perpendicular to it.")
(comment Groyne "Each #$Groyne is a wall built across a beach and into the sea, perpendicular to the shore.  Such walls are effective at controlling beach erosion, but while they cause sand to tend to be deposited on one side, there will also be a corresponding loss of beach material on the other.")
(comment Seawall "Each #$Seawall is a hard wall built to protect a coastal region from erosion.  Seawalls usually are built on land, parallel to and next to the shore, as opposed to #$Groynes, which are built perpendicular to the shore, or #$Breakwaters, which are built in the water to break up the action of waves.  Seawalls can be vertical, sloped, or curved.")
(comment subcollectionOfWithRelationFromType "<code>(#$subcollectionOfWithRelationFromType SUPER-COL PREDICATE TYPE SUB-COL)</code> means that <code>SUB-COL</code> is the collection of all instances <code>INSTANCE</code> of <code>SUPER-COL</code> for which there is some instance of <code>TYPE, <code>INS-TYPE such that <code>(PREDICATE INS-TYPE INSTANCE) holds</code>. See the function that corresponds to this predicate, #$SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn.")
(comment CountrySubsidiaryTypeByAdministrativeDivision "#$CountrySubsidiaryTypeByAdministrativeDivision is a faceting of #$CountrySubsidiary according to administrative subdivision. It's instances are #$FirstOrderAdministrativeDivision, #$SecondOrderAdministrativeDivision, #$ThirdOrderAdministrativeDivision, and #$FourthOrderAdministrativeDivision. First order subdivisions of a country are directly below the federal level, such as states in the United States. Second order subdivisions are the immediate subdivisions of a first order subdivision, such as counties in a state in the United States. Similarly for third and fourth order subdivisions.")
(comment FirstOrderAdministrativeDivision "A primary administrative division of a country, such as a state in the United States.")
(comment SecondOrderAdministrativeDivision "A subdivision of a first order administrative division, such as a county in a state of the United States.")
(comment ThirdOrderAdministrativeDivision "A subdivision of a second order administrative division.")
(comment FourthOrderAdministrativeDivision "A subdivision of a third order administrative division.")
(comment admittedAllArgument "A ternary #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a #$Collection to an integer (representing an argument-place) to a #$Relation such that every instance of the collection is an #$admittedArgument (q.v.) to the specified argument-place of the relation.  <code>(#$admittedAllArgument COL ARGNUM RELN)</code> means that every instance of <code>COL</code> satisfies all of the (single-argument) argument-type constraints (see #$ArgIsaPredicate and #$ArgGenlPredicate) for the <code>ARGNUM</code>th argument position of <code>RELN</code>.  Note that <code>COL</code> is required <i>not</i> to be an  #$EmptyCollection.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$admittedAllArgument #$FemaleHuman 1 #$spouse)</code> and <code>(#$admittedAllArgument #$PersonTypeByActivity 1 #$JuvenileFn)</code> both hold.")
(comment NotEmpty "An instance of #$Fullness (q.v.).  #$NotEmpty is the fullness quantity of being at least partially filled.")
(comment collectionClosedUnderRelation "A predicate used to state that a certain closure relation holds between a collection and a binary predicate. Specifically, <code>(#$collectionClosedUnderRelation COL RELN)</code> means that, if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(RELN INST THING)</code> holds for some <code>THING</code>, then <code>THING</code> is also an instance of <code>COL</code>.  Note that the argument-type constraints on <code>RELN</code> must <i>not</i> preclude all instances of <code>COL</code> from either of <code>RELN</code>'s argument-places (see #$admittedSomeArgument). 
<p>
See also #$collectionClosedUnderRelation-Inverse.")
(comment collectionClosedUnderRelation-Inverse "A predicate used to state that a certain closure relation holds between a collection and a binary predicate. Specifically, <code>(#$collectionClosedUnderRelation-Inverse COL RELN)</code> means that, if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(RELN THING INST)</code> holds for some <code>THING</code>, then <code>THING</code> is also an instance of <code>COL</code>.  Note that the argument-type constraints on <code>RELN</code> must <i>not</i> preclude all instances of <code>COL</code> from either of <code>RELN</code>'s argument-places (see #$admittedSomeArgument). 
<p>
See also #$collectionClosedUnderRelation.")
(comment admittedSomeArgument "A ternary #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a #$Collection to an integer (representing an argument-place) to a #$Relation such that some instance of the collection is an #$admittedArgument (q.v.) to the specified argument-place of the relation.  <code>(#$admittedSomeArgument COL ARGNUM RELN)</code> means there is at least one instance of <code>COL</code> that satisfies all of the (single-argument) argument-type constraints (see #$ArgIsaPredicate and #$ArgGenlPredicate) for the <code>ARGNUM</code>th argument position of <code>RELN</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$admittedSomeArgument #$Animal 2 #$spouse)</code> and <code>(#$admittedSomeArgument #$ExistingObjectType 1 #$JuvenileFn)</code> both hold.
<p>
See also the more specialized predicate #$admittedAllArgument.")
(comment collectionHypothesizedGenlsSet "A sentence of the form (#$collectionHypothesizedGenlsSet COLL SET-OF-COLLS) states that the #$Collection COLL is such that some form of reasoning (e.g., a consultation with a #$WordNetKnowledgeBase) has generated the elements of SET-OF-COLLS, a (#$SetOfTypeFn #$Collection) as candidates for more general forms of COLL.")
(comment mtVisible "A unary #$EvaluatablePredicate that applies to a #$Microtheory.  <code>(#$mtVisible MT)</code> means that the contents of <code>MT</code> are visible in the current query context.  

In general, 
<pre>
(#$genlMt MT1 MT2) 
</pre>
entails 
<pre>
(#$ist MT1 (#$mtVisible MT2),
</pre>
but the converse does not hold.  In particular, it fails when <code>MT1</code> is a #$ProblemSolvingCntxt.  For example, 
<pre>
(#$ist #$EverythingPSC (#$mtVisible #$HumanSocialLifeMt))
</pre>
holds (in #$BaseKB), but
<pre>
(#$genlMt #$EverythingPSC #$HumanSocialLifeMt)
</pre>
does not hold (in #$BaseKB).")
(comment genlMt "A reflexive and transitive #$BinaryPredicate that relates a #$Microtheory to a #$Microtheory.  <code>(#$genlMt SPEC-MT GENL-MT)</code> means that <code>SPEC-MT</code> is a specialization or extension of <code>GENL-MT</code>.  (Note that it need not be a <i>proper</i> specialization or extension.)  In particular, this entails that everything that is true in <code>GENL-MT</code> is also true in <code>SPEC-MT</code>.  This relation is sometimes expressed informally by saying that <code>SPEC-MT</code> \"sees\" <code>GENL-MT</code>.")
(comment numericallyEquals "A #$NumericComparisonPredicate that is applicable to #$ScalarIntervals (<i>q.v.</i>) of all sorts, including quantitative intervals (see #$NumericInterval and #$Quantity) as well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).  <code>(#$numericallyEquals VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> means that <code>VALUE1</code> is equal to <code>VALUE2</code> with respect to some scale that they are both on.  More precisely, there is some #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType <code>SCALE</code> that <code>VALUE1</code> and <code>VALUE2</code> are instances of and the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1</code> is equal to the minimum of <code>VALUE2</code> and the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1</code> is equal to the maximum of <code>VALUE2</code>.
<p>
Note that #$numericallyEquals is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$equals (<i>q.v.</i>), as numeric values of different sorts can be \"numerically  equivalent\" without being considered identical (e.g. the #$Integer 1 and the #$RealNumber 1.0).")
(comment facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverse "A ternary #$FacetBasisPredicate (q.v.) that relates a collection to a binary predicate to a #$FacetingCollectionType such that the latter facets the collection on the basis of the <i>inverse</i> of the predicate.  <code>(#$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverse COL BINPRED FACET)</code> means that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnBinaryPredInverse FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPred.")
(comment facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPred "A ternary #$FacetBasisPredicate (q.v.) that relates a collection to a binary predicate to a #$FacetingCollectionType such that the latter facets the collection on the basis of the predicate.  <code>(#$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPred COL BINPRED FACET)</code> means that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnBinaryPred FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredFn.  And see #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPred and #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverse.")
(comment facetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverse "A ternary #$FacetBasisPredicate (q.v.) that relates a collection to a type-level binary predicate to a #$FacetingCollectionType such that the latter facets the collection on the basis of the inverse of the predicate.  <code>(#$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverse COL BINPRED FACET)</code> means that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverse FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPred.")
(comment SKSIProfilingTest "#$SKSIProfilingTest is a collection of SKSI content tests that are intended to be used as a test suite for measuring how certain changes to the inference engine affect the answerability of queries that require access to external sources. There are several notable specializations of this suite, including #$SKSIProfilingTest-SingleLiteral, #$SKSIProfilingTest-MultiLiteral, #$SKSIProfilingTest-SingleSource, and #$SKSIProfilingTest-MultiSource.")
(comment SKSIProfilingTest-SingleLiteral "#$SKSIProfilingTest-SingleLiteral is a specialization of #$SKSIProfilingTest which contains only single literal queries.")
(comment SKSIProfilingTest-SingleSource "#$SKSIProfilingTest-SingleSource is a specialization of #$SKSIProfilingTest which contains queries which access a single external knowledge source.")
(comment SKSIProfilingTest-MultiLiteral "#$SKSIProfilingTest-MultiLiteral is a specialization of #$SKSIProfilingTest which contains only multi literal queries.")
(comment SKSIProfilingTest-MultiSource "#$SKSIProfilingTest-MultiSource is a specialization of #$SKSIProfilingTest which contains queries which access multiple external knowledge sources.")
(comment Question-PCW "A specialization of #$PropositionalConceptualWork (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Question-PCW is a propositional conceptual work that expresses a request for information (see #$InformationRequest-IBT).")
(comment WorkedSolution "A specialization of #$TextualPCW.  Each instance of #$WorkedSolution is a description of a sequence of steps taken to solve a problem, such as a physics problem, a calculus problem, or a chemistry problem.")
(comment PhysicsWorkedSolution "A specialization of #$WorkedSolution (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PhysicsWorkedSolution is a description of a sequence of steps taken to solve a physics problem.")
(comment WorkedSolutionStep "A specialization of #$TextualPCW.  Each instance of #$WorkedSolutionStep is a description of a specific step taken to solve a problem, such as a physics problem, a calculus problem, or a chemistry problem. See #$WorkedSolution.")
(comment PhysicsWorkedSolutionStep "A specialization of #$TextualPCW.  Each instance of #$PhysicsWorkedSolutionStep is a description of a specific step in a sequence of steps taken to solve a physics problem.")
(comment PhysicsProblem "A specialization of #$Question-PCW.  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem is a description of a problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment hasSolutionSteps "A specialization of #$textualPCWSubSections (q.v.). (#$hasSolutionSteps SOL STEP) means that STEP is a step in the larger solutioon, SOL.")
(comment firstSolutionStep "A specialization of #$hasSolutionSteps (q.v.). (#$firstSolutionStep SOL STEP) means that STEP is the unique first step in the larger solution, SOL.")
(comment lastSolutionStep "A specialization of #$hasSolutionSteps (q.v.). (#$lastSolutionStep SOL STEP) means that STEP is the unique last step in the larger solution, SOL.")
(comment priorSolutionStep "(#$priorSolutionStep LATER EARLIER) means that in the larger solution of which they are steps, EARLIER is a step that comes after LATER.  #$priorSolutionStep imposes a total ordering on the steps in a worked solution.")
(comment solutionStepUses "(#$solutionStepUses STEP SENT) means that #$WorkedSolutionStep STEP appeals to the sentence SENT. Generally, SENT will be a component of the statement of the problem to the worked solution of which STEP is a part, or it will be the statement of a general principle that is pertinent to solving the problem.")
(comment solutionStepOperationType "(#$solutionStepOperationType STEP OPERATION-TYPE) means that #$WorkedSolutionStep STEP involves the ocurrence of an instance of the specialization of #$Event, OPERATION-TYPE.  Typically, the instance of OPERATION-TYPE will be an action performed by the agent solving the pertinent problem with the worked solution of which STEP is a part.  For example, it may be the process of instantiating a physical principle, or it may be balancing an equation.")
(comment NCRuleWithLabelFn "<code>(#$NCRuleWithLabelFn STRING)</code> denotes the instance <code>RULE</code> of #$NounCompoundRule such that <code>(#$ncRuleLabel RULE STRING)</code>.")
(comment workedSolutionForKBContentTest "(#$workedSolutionForKBContentTest TEST SOLUTION) means that SOLUTION is a worked solution for TEST.")
(comment geopoliticalSuperEntityTypes "<code>(#$geopoliticalSuperEntityTypes SUPER-TYPE ENTITY-TYPE)</code> means that every geopolitical entity of type <code>TYPE</code>, is a subdivision of a geopolitical entity of type <code>SUPER-TYPE</code>. For example, ''Every county in the United States is a subdivision of some state'' may be expressed as <code>(#$geopoliticalSuperEntityTypes #$State-UnitedStates #$USCounty)</code>.")
(comment AccelerationDueToGravity "A specialization of #$Acceleration. #$AccelerationDueToGravity is the collection of all accelerations that are accelerations of objects due to gravity. See #$accelerationDueToGravityOnPlanetaryBody.")
(comment SubstitutingKnownValuesIntoAnEquation "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of
#$SubstitutingKnownValuesIntoAnEquation is an event in which an agent
substitutes known values into an mathematical equation.  Typically
this is done with the ultimate goal of solving for the unknowns. See
#$SolvingAMathematicalEquation.")
(comment DistanceTimeUnderConstantAccelerationMt "When a is constant, x=x<sub>0</sub>+v<sub>0</sub>t+at<sup>2</sup>/2.")
(comment ncRuleExtractedFromStructure "<code>(#$ncRuleExtractedFromStructure RULE STRUCTURE)</code> means that the instance <code>RULE</code> of #$NounCompoundRule has been posited as a result of information contained in <code>STRUCTURE</code> an instance of #$AbstractStructure.")
(comment lawOfScientificTheory "<code>(#$lawOfScientificTheory THEORY LAW)</code> means that <code>LAW</code> contains propositional information that is also wholly contained in <code>THEORY</code>.")
(comment ncRuleTemplate-Compact "<code>(#$ncRuleTemplate-Compact RULE LIST)</code> means that the list <code>LIST</code> is a structure that contains information sufficient for stating the semantic constraints and interpretation template for <code>RULE</code>, an instance of #$NounCompoundRule.")
(comment quantityChangeFraction "#$quantityChangeFraction
relates an instance of #$QuantityChangeEvent to a fractional measure
of the change in quantity that constitutes that event.
(#$quantityChangeFraction <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code>
<code>FRACTION</code>) means, given (#$quantityChangeAmount <code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> <code>AMOUNT</code>), that
<code>AMOUNT</code> is an increase or decrease in the
#$startingQuantity (q.v.) of <code>FRACTION</code> (e.g., a
#$Percent).  <code>FRACTION</code> may be positive or negative.  See
also #$changesQuantityByFraction, a #$QuaternaryPredicate which allows
us to relate changes in #$QuantitySlot values to broader events and
time intervals, as well as to #$QuantityChangeEvents.")
(comment quantityChangeAmount "#$quantityChangeAmount relates an instance of
#$QuantityChangeEvent to the amount by which the relevant quantity
changed during that event.  (#$quantityChangeAmount
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> <code>AMOUNT</code>) means that the difference
between the #$startingQuantity and #$endingQuantity (qq.v.)  of
<code>QUANTITY-CHANGE</code> is
<code>AMOUNT</code>. <code>AMOUNT</code> may be positive or negative.
See also #$changesQuantityByAmount, a #$QuaternaryPredicate which
allows us to relate changes in #$QuantitySlot values to broader events
and time intervals, as well as to #$QuantityChangeEvents.")
(comment CycLRuleSpecification "A specialization of #$ProgramStepSpecification (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CycLRuleSpecification is a description of a rule.  It may be an actual rule in the Cyc KB or it may be a description of how a rule <i>should</i> function in Cyc or in a Cyc-based application. At minimum, this description includes the CycL assertion that expresses the rule, via #$assertionOfRuleSpecification. It can also include a number of other parameters.  For example, it can specify the applications whose functionality is  supported by the rule.")
(comment WordNetSpeechPart "A collection of collections.  Each element of #$WordNetSpeechPart is a #$SpeechPart.  As of #$WordNet-Version2_0, this is restricted to #$Noun, #$Verb, #$Adverb, and #$Adjective.  This collection is significant because it allows us to restrict the values that are sent to functions such as #$WNAllCandidateDenotsForWordPOSFn-Default.")
(comment netFrictionForceOnObject "<code>(#$netFrictionForceOnObject OBJ FRICT)</code> relates a tangible object <code>OBJ</code> to the #$FrictionVector  <code>FRICT</code>, which is the net friction force acting on <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment InverseBinaryPredicateFn "A #$PredicateDenotingFunction that takes a #$BinaryPredicate <code>BIN-PRED</code> and returns its inverse, i.e. the predicate <code>INV-PRED</code> such that for any <code>ARG1</code> and <code>ARG2</code>, <code>(INV-PRED ARG1 ARG2)</code> holds just in case <code>(BIN-PRED ARG2 ARG1)</code> holds.  Note that for every instance of #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate <code>S-BIN-PRED</code>, <code>(#$InverseBinaryPredicateFn S-BIN-PRED)</code> is equivalent to <code>S-BIN-PRED</code>.")
(comment totalRoledTypeByAffectedTypeInType "<code>(#$totalRoledTypeByAffectedTypeInType NUM AFFECT-TYPE ROLE TYPE-PLAYING-ROLE TYPE)</code> means that <code>NUM</code> is the total number of things of <code>TYPE-PLAYING-ROLE</code> that played <code>ROLE</code> in situations of <code>TYPE</code> in which some instance of <code>AFFECT-TYPE</code> plays some role or other.  For instance, 

         <code>(#$totalRoledTypeByAffectedTypeInType 180 #$BankBuilding #$organismKilled #$Person #$TerroristAttack)</code> means that, as far as we know in the KB, a total of 180 persons were killed in terrorist attacks where a bank was somehow involved.")
(comment TemporallyQualifiableFunction "A specialization of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation and #$Function-Denotational (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TemporallyQualifiableFunction is a function whose results may vary when applied to the same argument or arguments at different times.
<p>
In other words, if <code>FUNC</code> is an instance of #$TemporallyQualifiableFunction and the result of <code>(FUNC ARG-1 ARG-N)</code> is <code>RESULT-1</code> at <code>TIME-1</code>, it is possible (although not necessary) that at <code>TIME-2</code> (where <code>TIME-2</code> is different from <code>TIME-1</code>) the result of <code>(FUNC ARG-1 ARG-N)</code> may be <code>RESULT-2</code>, where <code>RESULT-2</code> is different from <code>RESULT-1</code>.
<p>
Examples of #$TemporallyQualifiableFunction include #$PresidentFn and #$MassOfObjectFn.")
(comment pegWithoutNodeInTree "<code>(#$pegWithoutNodeInTree PEG TREE)</code> means that <code>PEG</code> is a proper sub-expression (#$properSubExpressions, q.v.) of a linguistic expression peg with the parse tree <code>TREE</code>, such that <code>PEG</code> corresponds to no syntactic node in <code>TREE</code>.")
(comment massOfObject "An instance of #$MeasurableQuantitySlot and a specialization of #$sizeParameterOfObject. <code>(#$massOfObject OBJ MASS)</code> means that the tangible object <code>OBJ</code> has #$Mass <code>MASS</code>.")
(comment totalRoledTypeInType "<code>(#$totalRoledTypeInType NUM ROLE TYPE-PLAYING-ROLE TYPE)</code> means that <code>NUM</code> is the total number of things of <code>TYPE-PLAYING-ROLE</code> that played <code>ROLE</code> in situations of <code>TYPE</code>.  For instance, 

         <code>(#$totalRoledTypeInType 10000 #$organismKilled #$Person #$TerroristAttack)</code> means that, as far as we know in the KB, a total of 10000 persons were killed in terrorist attack.")
(comment MetaRelation "A subcollection of #$Predicate.  Instances of #$MetaRelation are used to make definitional (e.g. arity or argument-constraint) assertions about relations.  As such, each instance of #$MetaRelation has (at least) one of its own argument-places (usually, but not always, the first or \"arg1\") type-constrained to instances of #$Relation (or some subcollection thereof).  
<p>
Note that all meta-relations are themselves <i>predicates</i> (as opposed to functions or other non-predicate relations); the collection #$MetaRelation is so-named because its instances (unlike those of its specialization #$MetaPredicate) may admit non-predicate relations as arguments.")
(comment restaurantTypeSpecializingInCuisine "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot.  <code>(#$restaurantTypeSpecializingInCuisine CUISINE REST-TYPE)</code> means that <code>REST-TYPE</code> is the collection of #$Restaurant-Organizations that specialize in the #$Cuisine <code>CUISINE</code>.  This is the predicate corresponding to #$RestaurantSpecializingInCuisineFn (q.v.).")
(comment RestaurantTypeByCuisine "The collection of subcollections of #$Restaurant-Organization whose instances all specialize in a common instance of #$Cuisine.  See also #$restaurantTypeSpecializingInCuisine.")
(comment Quantifier "A specialization of #$SententialRelation and #$ScopingRelation (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Quantifier takes as its arguments (at least) a variable (see #$CycLVariable) and a sentence (see #$CycLSentence-Assertible), and is used to make a certain kind of generic quantitative statement regarding the things that satisfy the sentence.  Typically, the variable VAR will occur free in the sentence SENT, and in the quantified sentence (QUANT VAR SENT ...) these occurrences of VAR are bound by that occurrence of QUANT.  (If VAR does not occur free in SENT, then the quantified sentence is a \"vacuous quantification\" that is equivalent to SENT by itself.  For the definitions of 'free' and 'bound' occurrences of variables, see #$ScopingRelation.)  For example, '(#$thereExists ?X (#$isa ?X #$Dog))' means that there exists at least one dog.  Other instances of #$Quantifier are #$forAll, #$thereExistExactly, #$thereExistAtLeast, and #$thereExistAtMost.")
(comment EdibleStuff "An instance of #$ExistingStuffType and a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each specialization of #$EdibleStuff is a type of partially tangible stuff which all normal instances of some specialization of #$Organism-Whole can consume and successfully metabolize a significant portion of any instance of, with or without resulting harm to themselves. This collection includes virtually all animal and vegetable matter, as well as salts, potable water, and instances of #$OralDrug. It does not necessarily include things that are ingested but not metabolized, such as the stones that birds swallow to aid in digestion, or dirt, paint chips, and coins ingested by children. The function #$EdibleByFn is used to specify the collections of edible stuff that are ingestible by all normal instances of certain specializations of #$Organism-Whole, including #$Person. Note that whether an edible substance <i>also</i> has a detrimental effect if eaten is a separate question. Something can be both edible and poisonous.")
(comment MassOfObjectFn "An instance of #$TemporallyQualifiableFunction (q.v.).  <code>(#$MassOfObjectFn OBJ)</code> denotes the mass of <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment NetForceVector "#$NetForceVector is the collection of force vectors  that measure the magnitude and direction of the net forces exerted on a body at a given point in time.")
(comment NetForceOnObjectFn "An instance of #$TemporallyQualifiableFunction (q.v.).  <code>(#$NetForceOnObjectFn OBJ)</code> denotes the net force on <code>OBJ</code> at a given time.")
(comment LinearAccelerationVectorOfObjectFn "An instance of #$TemporallyQualifiableFunction (q.v.).  <code>(#$LinearAccelerationVectorOfObjectFn OBJ)</code> denotes the linear acceleration of <code>OBJ</code> at a given time.")
(comment MathematicalFunctionOnVectors "A specialization of #$IndividualDenotingFunction (q.v.).  Each instance of #$MathematicalFunctionOnVectors is a function that takes one or more #$VectorIntervals (q.v.) as its argument(s) (and also possibly one or more #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.)), and returns a vector interval as its value.")
(comment PhysicalPrincipleMicrotheory "A specialization of #$PropositionalInformationThing.  Each instance of #$PhysicalPrincipleMicrotheory is a context containing assertions that express some particular more or less idealized aspect of the physical universe: some instance of #$PhysicalPrinciple (q.v.).  For example, some physical principles describe how objects behave under constant acceleration, which is an idealization that discounts any frictional forces.  See also the specialization of this collection, #$LawOfNatureMicrotheory.")
(comment LawOfNatureMicrotheory "A specialization of #$PhysicalPrincipleMicrotheory (q.v.). Each instance of #$LawOfNatureMicrotheory is a scientific generalization about the physical world.  
<p>
In Cyc, a law of nature is denoted by a specific assertion or set of assertions in a #$LawOfNatureMicrotheory that contains assertions which express the regularity that the law captures.

For example, #$NewtonsSecondLawMt is an instance of #$LawOfNatureMicrotheory. Newton's second law, \"F=ma\", is expressed as a #$LawOfNature CycL assertion.")
(comment NewtonsThirdLawMt "An instance of #$LawOfNatureMicrotheory (q.v.).  #$NewtonsThirdLawMt is the context in which #$NewtonsThirdLaw is true.")
(comment NewtonsSecondLawMt "An instance of #$LawOfNatureMicrotheory (q.v.). The context in which #$NewtonsSecondLaw is stated.")
(comment SoundLaw-LawOfNature "An instance of #$LawOfNatureMicrotheory. Each instance is a law describing sound changes in the history of a language.")
(comment LawOfNatureSpecification "A specialization of #$CycLRuleSpecification.  Each instance of #$LawOfNatureSpecification is a specification of a CycL rule assertion that expresses a fact about the physical universe that is a nearly exceptionless regularity.")
(comment NewtonsFirstLaw-Specification "An instance of #$LawOfNatureSpecification (q.v.).  #$NewtonsFirstLaw-Specification states that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue in motion with constant velocity, except insofar as it is acted upon by external forces.")
(comment NewtonsSecondLaw-Specification "An instance of #$LawOfNatureSpecification (q.v.).  Newton's second law states that the net force acting on an object is equal to the mass of the object times its acceration.")
(comment BookkeepingPredicate "A #$PredicateType whose instances are predicates used to make assertions about the reification (or \"creation\") and internal representation of particular CycL terms (usually constants) in the Cyc system.  Bookkeeping predicates neither specify nor constrain the semantics of CycL terms.  Examples include  #$myCreator, #$myCreationTime, #$termOfUnit, and #$defnSufficient.  Many (but not all) bookkeeping predicates are also #$MetaKnowledgePredicates (q.v.).")
(comment NCRuleWithTemplateFn "<code>(#$NCRuleWithTemplateFn LIST)</code> denotes the instance of #$NounCompoundRule whose #$ncRuleTemplate-Compact is given in the list <code>LIST</code>.")
(disjointWith LawOfNature Proposition-ConceptuallyTrue)
(comment PhysicalPrinciple "A specialization of #$Proposition. Each instance of #$PhysicalPrinciple is a proposition about some particular more or less idealized aspect of the physical universe. For example, some physical principles describe how objects behave under constant acceleration, which is an idealization that discounts any frictional forces. See also the specialization of this collection, #$LawOfNature.")
(comment LawOfNature "A specialization of #$Proposition-True and #$PhysicalPrinciple (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$LawOfNature is a relatively simple, universal, and true proposition which serves to explain some fundamental aspect of the functioning of the physical universe.  
<p>
Scientists discover instances of #$LawOfNature by working to confirm hypotheses through successful and repeatable experimentation.  Laws of nature are at once components of larger scientific theories (see #$ScientificTheory) and the subject matter of those theories.  Scientific theories incorporate laws of nature and supplement them with additional propositions which tie the laws together in an effort to produce a cohesive explanatory system.")
(comment NewtonsFirstLaw "An instance of #$LawOfNature (q.v.). #$NewtonsFirstLaw states that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue in motion with constant velocity, except insofar as it is acted upon by external forces.")
(comment NewtonsSecondLaw "An instance of #$LawOfNature (q.v.).  Newton's second law states that the net force acting on an object is equal to the mass of the object times its acceration.")
(comment NewtonsThirdLaw-Specification "An instance of #$LawOfNatureSpecification (q.v.).  Newton's third law states that for each force acting on a body <code>B</code>, there is an equal but oppositely directed force on some other body interacting with <code>B</code>.")
(comment specifiedRuleExpressesProposition "(#$specifiedRuleExpressesProposition RULE-SPEC PROP) means that the rule specification RULE-SPEC describes a CycL rule that expresses the #$Proposition PROP.")
(comment TriggeringOfEventTypeFn "(#$TriggeringOfEventTypeFn <code>EVENT-TYPE</code>) denotes
the collection of #$Event sequences which culminate in an instance of
<code>EVENT-TYPE</code>, and which begin with an event or events which
can be said to have 'triggered' that instance (cf.,
#$TriggeringSomething).  Some degree of directness must hold of the
causal link between the triggering event (<code>TRIGGER</code>) and
the final event (<code>EVENT-INS</code>), i.e., there are limits on
the number and nature of intermediate steps between
<code>TRIGGER</code> and <code>EVENT-INS</code>, though it is
difficult to state precisely what those limits are.")
(comment Petroleum-SweetCrudeOil "A subset of #$Petroleum-SweetCrudeOil and a #$TangibleStuffCompositionType,
sweet crude oil contains a relatively small amount of sulfur and carbon dioxide, which is easier to refine into gasoline.  See also #$PetroleumProduct
and #$PetroleumProduct-Refined.")
(comment FuturesContract "A specialization of #$SalesContract.  Each instance of #$FuturesContract is a sales contract between a buyer and seller of a certain kind of asset.  The price is fixed and the deliverable date is set in advance.    The risk for the buyer is that the market price for the asset will drop by the time he receives it; the seller is worried the price will go higher.  The trade of stocks, bonds, and currency are all technically futures contracts.  However, the term is used to mostly describe commodity futures contacts: such as the trade of crude oil, lumber wheat, gold, and etc.")
(comment CommodityFuturesContract "A specialization of #$FuturesContract.  Each instance of #$CommodityFuturesContract is a sales contract between a buyer and seller of a certain kind of #$CommodityProduct.  Commodity trading mostly occur at futures exchanges like at the Chicago Mercantile Exchange.  The price is fixed and the deliverable date is set in advance.    The risk for the buyer is that the market price for the asset will drop by the time he receives it; the seller is worried the price will go higher.   Commodity futures contacts often involve #$CommodityProducts: such as crude oil, lumber wheat, gold, and etc.")
(comment InvestmentVehicle "Each instance of #$InvestmentVehicle is an item that generally appreciates or depreciates in value, thereby affecting the financial worth of its owner.  Important specializations include some kinds of #$RealEstate,  #$TradeableSecurity, and #$CommodityFinancialProduct (qq.v).")
(comment typeLevelVersionInArg "A quintary #$MetaRelation predicate. <code>(#$typeLevelVersionInArg RELN TYPERELN N M QUANT)</code> means that <code>TYPERELN</code> is a type-level version of <code>RELN</code> with regard to their respective <code>N</code>th and <code>M</code>th argument-places.  This implies that if <code>RELN</code>'s <code>N</code>th argument-place is constrained to take <i>instances</i> of a collection <code>COL</code> (see #$argIsa), then <code>TYPERELN</code>'s <code>M</code>th argument-place is constrained to take <i>specializations</i> of <code>COL</code> (see #$argGenl).  (Usually, <code>N</code> and <code>M</code> will be the same.)  Moreover, where <code>INST</code> and <code>SPEC</code> are respectively an instance and a specialization of <code>COL</code>, and <code>RELN</code> and <code>TYPERELN</code> are both predicates, what the sentence <code>(TYPERELN ... SPEC<sub>M</sub> ...)</code> states is a generalization of what the sentence <code>(RELN ... INST<sub>N</sub> ...)</code> states.  If <code>QUANT</code> is a universal quantifier (i.e. #$forAll), the former sentence says something about <i>all</i> of the instances of some collection -- which, depending on the case, could either be <code>SPEC</code> or the collection of things <code>RELN</code> relates via its <code>N</code>th argument-place to the rest of the given arguments.  If <code>QUANT</code> is an existential quantifier (e.g. #$thereExists), it says something about <i>some</i> (unspecified) instance of one of these collections.  (Typically, <code>TYPERELN</code> will be a #$MacroRelation whose #$expansion involves <code>RELN</code> and <code>QUANT</code>.)
<p>  
When <code>RELN</code> and <code>TYPERELN</code> are <i>functions</i> rather than predicates, the situation is analogous to the above.
<p>
For example, (#$typeLevelVersionInArg #$groupMembers #$groupMemberType 2 2 #$forAll) holds.  While <code>(#$groupMembers GROUP THING)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is a member of <code>GROUP</code>, <code>(#$groupMemberTypes GROUP TYPE)</code> means that <i>all</i> the members of <code>GROUP</code> are instances of <code>TYPE</code>.  For another example, (#$typeLevelVersionInArg #$instrument-Generic #$instrumentType-Generic 2 2 #$thereExists) holds. <code>(#$instrumentType-Generic EVENT TYPE)</code> means that <i>some</i> instance of <code>TYPE</code> plays the role of #$instrument-Generic in <code>EVENT</code>.
<p>
See also the binary generalization #$typeLevelCognates.")
(comment FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverseFn "A binary function that takes a collection and a binary predicate to the #$FacetingCollectionType (if any) that facets the collection on the basis of the <i>inverse</i> of the predicate.  (#$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredFn <b>COL BINPRED</b>) is the faceting-collection-type <code>FACET</code> such that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnBinaryPredInverse FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverse.    And see #$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredFn.")
(comment Appositive "The collection of instances of #$DefiniteNounPhrase consisting of a definite noun phrase modifying a name or noun phrase.  The head semantics of an appositive are identical with those of the entire phrase.")
(comment SandyDesert "A #$SandyDesert is a #$Desert that is composed almost entirely of sand and sand dunes. The #$SaharaDesert is a typical example of a #$SandyDesert.")
(comment SubSurfaceDam "A #$SubSurfaceDam is a #$Dam that extends below the basin of a body of water, down into the bedrock. Such dams are needed when the basin is composed of porous material that would permit the water to flow under the dam if it did not extend into the ground.")
(comment ContemporaryInResultFunction "A specialization of #$TemporallyQualifiableFunction and #$TemporalExistenceRelation.  Each instance of this collection is a function that is \"contemporary\" in the value it returns.  That is, if FUNC is an instance of this collection and '(FUNC ARG-1 ARG-N)' appears as a denotational term in an assertion ASSERT<MT TIME PAR>, then FUNC has a value for the given series of args ARG-1 through ARG-N only if that value's temporal extent subsumes TIME.  For instance,
<p>
(#$isa (#$TerritoryFn #$France) #$GeographicalRegion)<MT 3,000B.C. TimePoint>
<p>
is false because #$TerritoryFn is an instance of #$ContemporaryInResultFunction and hence '(#$TerritoryFn #$France)' does not return a value for the time index 3,000B.C..
<p>
A sufficient condition for membership in this collection for any function, FUNC, is that there exists a PRED, that is #$contemporaryInArg in arg N and
<p>
(#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical FUNC PRED N).")
(comment TimeDependentRelation "A specialization of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation (q.v.).  #$TimeDependentRelation is the collection of all (not eternally-empty) predicates and functions that are such that nothing remains in their extensions eternally.  
<p>
A predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$TimeDependentRelation if and only if both (i) for any sequence of things <code>SEQ</code> and time <code>T</code>, if <code>PRED</code> holds of <code>SEQ</code> at <code>T</code>, then there is some different time <code>T'</code> such that <code>PRED</code> does <i>not</i> hold of <code>SEQ</code> at <code>T'</code> and (ii) there is some sequence and some time such that <code>PRED</code> holds of that sequence at that time.  (Condition (ii) is needed to exclude relations with necessarily empty extensions, such as <i>is neither identical to nor distinct from</i>.)   For example, #$likesAsFriend is time-dependent. <code>(#$likesAsFriend Amy Bob)</code> can be true today but false tomorrow (and will certainly not be true after Amy and Bob are dead).  On the other hand, #$isa is not time-dependent but merely temporally-qualifiable; although it holds of some things temporarily, it holds of some other things eternally.
<p>
A function <code>FUNC</code> is an instance of #$TimeDependentRelation if its value for any given sequence of arguments in its (non-empty) domain, and/or the fact that is <i>has</i> a value for that sequence, changes over time.  #$TerritoryFn is an example of a time-dependent function.")
(disjointWith TemporallyQualifiableRelation TimeIndependentRelation)
(comment QuantitySlotForArg2Fn "(#$QuantitySlotForArg2Fn <code>THING</code>
<code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code>) returns the binary relation which results from binding the second argument position of <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code> (a ternary predicate) to
<code>THING</code> -- i.e., the binary relation which results from
restricting the extension of <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code> to triples
whose second member is <code>THING</code>.  The
constraints on the first and second arguments of
(#$QuantitySlotForArg2Fn<code>THING</code>
<code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code>) will thus be identical to those on the first and
third arguments of <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code>.  The second argument of
the new predicate thus must be a specialization of #$Quantity (see
comment on #$ComplexQuantityPredicate), and so the new predicate will
itself be a #$QuantitySlot.  For example, (#$QuantitySlotForArg2Fn
#$Automobile #$productionRate) returns a #$QuantitySlot which relates a #$PartiallyTangible
(e.g., a #$GeographicalRegion or factory) to the rate at which it produces #$Automobiles.")
(comment quantitySlotForArg2 "(#$quantitySlotForArg2 <code>SLOT</code> <code>THING</code> <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code>) means that <code>SLOT</code>
is the #$QuantitySlot which results from restricting the second
argument of <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code> to <code>THING</code>.  See the
corresponding function #$QuantitySlotForArg2Fn.")
(comment QuantitySlotForArg1Fn "(#$QuantitySlotForArg1Fn <code>THING</code>
<code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code>) returns the binary relation which results from binding the first argument position of the (ternary) <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code> to
<code>THING</code> -- i.e., the binary relation which results from
restricting the extension of <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code> to triples
whose first member is <code>THING</code>.  The
constraints on the first and second arguments of
(#$QuantitySlotForArg1Fn <code>THING</code>
<code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code>) will thus be identical to those on the second and
third arguments of <code>COMPLEX-SLOT</code>.  The second argument of
the new predicate thus must be a specialization of #$Quantity (see
comment on #$ComplexQuantityPredicate), and so the new predicate will
itself be a #$QuantitySlot.  For example, (#$QuantitySlotForArg1Fn
#$Canada #$importRate) returns a #$QuantitySlot which relates a type
of good to the rate at which it is imported into Canada.")
(comment anRootPathForMachineName "Used by the query library visualization facility to record the location of Analyst's Notebook installations on a per-machine basis.")
(comment performsPercentageOfType "(#$performsActsOfType-Percentage AGENT ACTION-TYPE PERCENT) holds just in case the percentage of the instances of ACTION-TYPE-1 that AGENT is known by Cyc to perform is PERCENT.  For instance, if 40% of the terrorist attacks in Israel are performed by Hamas, then (#$performsPercentageOfType #$TerroristOrganization-Hamas (#$CollectionSubsetFn #$TerroristAttack (#$TheSetOf ?ATT (#$eventOccursAt ?ATT #$Israel))) 40) holds.")
(comment TransitiveBinaryPredicate "A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and thus a specialization of #$BinaryPredicate.  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate only if it has the following property: For any <code>THING1</code>, <code>THING2</code>, and <code>THING3</code>, if both of these hold:
<pre>
  (PRED THING1 THING2)
  (PRED THING2 THING3)
</pre>
Then so does this:
<pre>
  (PRED THING1 THING3) .
</pre>
Note that a necessary condition on <code>PRED</code>'s being transitive is that its two argument-places must be co-satisfiable; see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement.
<p>
Instances of #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate include #$greaterThan,
#$geographicalSubRegions, and #$cotemporal.  
<p>
Cf. #$QuasiTransitiveBinaryPredicate and #$AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate.")
(comment NumericIntervalMaxFn "An instance of #$ScalarDenotingFunction.
#$NumericIntervalMaxFn takes as arguments a type of number
(NUMBER-TYPE, a spec of #$RealNumber) and an instance of that type
(MAX), and returns the #$IntervalOnNumberLine composed of instances of
NUMBER-TYPE that are #$lessThanOrEqualTo MAX. For example,
(#$NumericIntervalMaxFn #$Integer 3) returns the sequence of integers
that ends with 3.")
(comment PhrasalWordForm "The collection of individual word forms, such as proper nouns (#$ProperNoun, qv) and intransitive verbs that occur as phrases.")
(comment crewMemberOfVessel "The binary, temporally qualifiable relation that relates a 'crewed' transportation device, such as a #$Spacecraft, #$Ship, dirigible, etc., to each member of the crew.")
(comment vesselsWholeCrew "A functional predicate and the corresponding predicate of #$VesselsWholeCrewFn.  At any given time, #$vesselsWholeCrew relates a 'crewed' #$TransportationDevice to the #$Organization that is the crew of that 'vessel' at that time.")
(comment VesselsWholeCrewFn "A temporally parameterized function:  at any given time T, (#$VesselsWholeCrewFn VESSEL) returns the organization that is the entire crew complement of the 'crewed' vessel VESSEL.  See also #$crewMemberOfVessel.")
(comment QuasiTransitiveBinaryPredicate "A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and thus a specialization of #$BinaryPredicate.  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$QuasiTransitiveBinaryPredicate only if it has the following property: For any things <code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, and <code>C</code>, if all of these hold:
<pre>
  (PRED A B)
  (PRED B C)
  (#$different A C)
</pre>
Then so does this:
<pre>
  (PRED A C) .
</pre>
Note that a necessary condition on <code>PRED</code>'s being quasi-transitive is that its two argument-places must be co-satisfiable; see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement.
<p>
Notable specializations of #$QuasiTransitiveBinaryPredicate include
#$TransitiveBinaryPredicate and #$CoEquivalenceBinaryPredicate.  Instances include #$connectedTo and #$relatives.
<p>
Cf. #$AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate.")
(comment TimeIndependentRelation "The collection of all #$Relations whose extentions (see #$relationExtension) do not change over time.  A relation <code>RELN</code> is an instance of #$TimeIndependentRelation if and only if there is <i>no</i> sequence <code>SEQ</code> and times <code>T1</code> and <code>T2</code> such that <code>RELN</code> holds among <code>SEQ</code> at <code>T1</code> but not at <code>T2</code>.  Thus, if <code>RELN</code> holds of a given sequence at any time, it holds of it at all times.
<p>
Examples of time-independent relations include #$greaterThan, which relates numbers and other #$ScalarIntervals eternally, and #$simultaneousWith, which relates #$TimePoints eternally.
<p>
Note that a relation whose extension is necessarily empty, such as <i>is neither identical to nor distinct from</i>, trivially qualifies as time-independent.
<p>
Cf. #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation.")
(disjointWith ConstructionArtifact FluidTangibleThing)
(disjointWith ConstructionArtifact (GroupFn BiologicalLivingObject))
(comment Stream-Canalized "A specialization of #$Stream.  Each instance of #$Stream-Canalized is a natural stream of water which has been significanly modified by deepening, straightening, or installing dikes.")
(comment Sanatorium "A #$Hospital that specializes in the long term care of illness or other physical conditions, such as tuberculosis.")
(comment Common-Region "Each instance of #$Common-Region is an area of land owned by a community (town, city, etc.) and used by members of that community for recreation, pastureland, crops, and so forth.")
(comment Ledge-Topographical "A linear, flat, rocky shelf, especially one that projects, as from a wall of rock. Instances of #$Ledge-Topographical can occur on dry land as well as underwater.")
(comment ShelfEdge-Undersea "The linear region at the edge of an island shelf or continental shelf, at which there is significant increase in negative slope as the ocean floor drops away to deeper water.")
(comment Shelf-Undersea "A region of relatively shallow water with a gently sloping bottom, ringing or at least next to a continent or island.  Each #$Shelf-Undersea starts at the low water line and extends to the #$ShelfEdge-Undersea, beyond which the slope of the bottom drops off much more steeply.  Each #$Shelf-Undersea is next to a land mass, but not all land masses are completely ringed by or even next to shelves.")
(comment Promontory-OverLand "A specialization of #$Promontory.  Each instance of #$Promontory-OverLand is a prominent hill or bluff that overlooks and possibly jutts out into a lowland. ")
(comment objectSurfaces "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a spatial object to the surfaces that are parts (or the whole) of the boundary between the object and that which is not the object. <code>(#$objectSurfaces OBJECT SURFACE)</code> means that <code>SURFACE</code> is one of the surfaces of <code>OBJECT</code>.")
(comment objectEdges "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$properlySpatiallySubsumes-Tangential (q.v.) that relates a (two- or higher-dimensional) spatial object to its edges.  <code>(#$objectEdges OBJ EDGE)</code> means that <code>EDGE</code> is one of the edges of <code>OBJ</code>.  More precisely, <code>EDGE</code> is a (not necessarily straight) \"line\" segment (see #$LinearObject) that defines a boundary of <code>OBJ</code>.  Where two or more edges of <code>OBJ</code> meet (see #$spatiallyIntersects), their point of intersection is a <i>corner</i> of <code>OBJ</code> (see #$CornerOnObject).")
(comment collRelation "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a predicate to a pair of collections such that each instance of the first (second) collection is related by the predicate to (from) some instance of the second (first) collection.  Thus, <code>(#$collRelation PRED COL1 COL2)</code> is true if and only if both of these are true:
<pre>
  (#$relationAllExists PRED COL1 COL2)

  (#$relationExistsAll PRED COL1 COL2)
</pre>
For example,
<pre>
  (#$collRelation #$groupMembers #$PlanetarySystem #$Sun)
</pre>
means that (i) every planetary system includes a sun and (ii) every sun is a member of a planetary system.
<p>
#$collRelation is used to represent plural sentences.  For instance, \"The cats slept\" means that for some (non-empty non-singleton) class of cats and some (non-empty) class of sleeping events, each cat in the cat-class was in the #$bodilyDoer relation to some event in the sleeping-class, and each sleeping was in the inverse relation to some cat in the cat-set.")
(comment assertedArgCardinality "An #$ArgCardinalityPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to put a \"state-of-the-KB\" cardinality constraint on a predicate with respect to a given argument-place.  Unlike with most argument-constraint predicates, a constraint imposed by #$assertedArgCardinality is not intended necessarily to reflect the intrinsic nature of the constrained predicate per se, but to limit the number of sentences built from (the CycL 
term for) the predicate that can coexist as assertions in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  Thus a cardinality constraint imposed by #$assertedArgCardinality can be (and normally is) stricter than the appropriate analogous constraint imposed by #$argCardinality (q.v.).  
<p>
<code>(#$assertedArgCardinality PRED N CARD)</code> means that, at any given time, the KB should contain no more than <code>CARD</code> different assertions of the form <code>(PRED ... ARGN<sub>I</sub> ...)</code> that are all exactly alike except that they each have a different term <code>ARGN<sub>I</sub></code> in their respective <code>N</code>th argument-positions.  
<p>
See also #$argCardinality and #$interArgCardinality.")
(comment admittedAllAndOnlyArgument "A ternary #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a #$Collection to an integer (representing an argument-place) to a #$Relation such that collection contains all of and only the #$admittedArguments (q.v.) to the specified argument-place of the relation.  <code>(#$admittedAllAndOnlyArgument COL ARGNUM RELN)</code> means that, for any thing <code>THING</code>, <code>THING</code> is an instance of <code>COL</code> if and only if <code>THING</code> satisfies all of the (single-argument) argument-type constraints (see #$ArgIsaPredicate and #$ArgGenlPredicate) for the <code>ARGNUM</code>th argument position of <code>RELN</code>.  
<p>
If a given relation has an argument-place on which there is only one arg-isa constraint, and no arg-genl constraints, the collection used in the arg-isa constraint is related to the given relation by #$admittedAllAndOnlyArgument.  Thus <code>(#$admittedAllAndOnlyArgument #$HomoSapiens 1 #$spouse)</code> holds.  Otherwise, things get a bit more complicated.  For example, the collection of all and only the admitted-arguments for the first argument-position of #$JuvenileFn is (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$ExistingObjectType #$genls #$Animal).
<p>
See also #$admittedAllArgument and #$admittedSomeArgument.")
(comment relationOnly "A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a given unary predicate to a collection of things that includes everything the predicate holds of.  <code>(#$relationOnly PRED COL)</code> means that, for any thing <code>THING</code>, if <code>(PRED THING)</code> holds, then do does <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code>.  Thus, <code>(#$relationOnly PRED COL)</code> is equivalent to (the universal closure of) the more complex form:
<pre>
  (#$implies 
    (PRED <b>?X</b>) 
    (#$isa <b>?X</b> COL)) .
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$relationOnly #$completelyAssertedCollection #$FiniteSetOrCollection)</code> holds because a collection must be of finite size in order for its extent to be completely asserted in the Knowledge Base.  
<p>
See also #$relationAll the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment relationAll "A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a given unary predicate to a collection of things that the predicate holds of.  <code>(#$relationAll PRED COL)</code> means that, for every instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code>, <code>(PRED INST)</code> holds.  Thus <code>(#$relationAll PRED COL)</code> is equivalent to -- and so can be used in effect as shorthand for -- (the unversal closure of) the more complex form:
<pre>
  (#$implies 
    (#$isa <b>?INST</b> COL) 
    (PRED <b>?INST</b>)) .
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$relationAll #$assertedSentence #$CycLAssertion)</code> means that all #$CycLAssertions are asserted sentences.  
<p>
See also #$relationOnly and the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment PresentTenseVersionFn "A function that allows one to define temporally qualifiable versions of role predicates used in Davidsonian-style representations of situations.  <code>(#$PresentTenseVersionFn ROLE)</code> denotes the instance PRED of #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate that is a present tense version of <code>ROLE</code>, an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate.  This means that both <code>(#$arg2SometimesIsa ROLE TYPE)</code> and <code>(ROLE SITUATION ARG2)</code> are timelessly true just in case there is some time interval <code>T</code> such that at every moment throughout T, <code>(PRED SITUATION ARG2)</code> and <code>(#$isa ARG2 TYPE)</code> holds.  
<p>
#$PresentTenseVersionFn thus allows one to compose terse representations of situation-types than Davidsonian-only vocabulary will allow.  For example, to say that only transportation devices act as transporters using Davidsonian vocabulary, one must write a complex rule: <pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$and
      (#$isa ?EVENT (#$TransportViaFn #$TransportationDevice))
      (#$temporalExtent ?EVENT ?EVENT-EXTENT)
      (#$indexicalReferent #$QueryMt ?MT)
      (#$mtMonad ?MT ?MONAD)
      (#$transporter ?EVENT ?TRANSPORTER)) 
   (#$thereExists ?TIME
     (#$ist
       (#$MtSpace ?MONAD
         (#$MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn ?TIME #$TimePoint))
       (#$isa ?TRANSPORTER #$TransportationDevice))))</pre>  However, with #$PresentTenseVersionFn, one may simply express this as <pre>
   (#$relationAllOnly 
     (#$PresentTenseVersionFn #$transporter)
     (#$TransportViaFn #$TransportationDevice) #$TransportationDevice).</pre>")
(comment Headland "A specialization of #$Promontory.  Each instance of #$Headland is a high bluff that jutts out into a large body of water.")
(comment totalRoledOfTypeInTypeByAgent "<code>(#$totalRoledTypeByAffectedTypeInType NUM AGENT ROLE TYPE-PLAYING-ROLE TYPE)</code> means that <code>NUM</code> is the total number of things of <code>TYPE-PLAYING-ROLE</code> that played <code>ROLE</code> in situations of <code>TYPE</code> which <code>AGENT</code> performs.  For instance, 

         <code>(#$totalRoledTypeByAffectedTypeInType 180 #$LebaneseHizballah #$organismKilled #$Person #$CarBombing)</code> means that, as far as we know in the KB, a total of 180 persons were killed in bombings performed by Hizballah.")
(comment numberOfTypeWithRelationFrom "A quaternary predicate that is used to state the number of instances of a given collection that are related to a given thing by a given binary predicate.  <code>(#$numberOfTypeWithRelationFrom COL BINPRED THING N)</code> means that there are exactly <code>N</code> instances (<code>INST<sub>1</sub>, ..., INST<sub>N</sub></code>) of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(BINPRED THING INST<sub>I</sub>)</code> holds of each of the <code>INST<sub>I</sub></code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$numberOfTypeWithRelationFrom #$Professor #$employees #$UCLA 500)</code> means that exactly 500 professors are employed by UCLA.")
(comment GasOilSeparatorFacility "The collection of places where #$Petroleum-CrudeOil is stripped of natural gas and other gases (some toxic) that it contains. Each instance of #$GasOilSeparatorFacility includes at least one or more separation units that remove natural gas from crude oil.  A #$GasOilSeparatorFacility will produce both #$NaturalGas and slightly more refined #$Petroleum-CrudeOil, both of which are usually piped to a different facility for further refining.")
(comment OilGasSeparation-PetroleumRefining "#$OilGasSeparation-PetroleumRefining is a type of #$SeparatingAMixture in which raw crude oil (#$Petroleum-CrudeOil) is stripped of the gasses it contains by flashing the mixture, or heating the mixture to 65 degrees fahrenheit.  This causes the dissolved gasses to leave the mixture.  Much of the gas removed in this way is #$NaturalGas.")
(comment Gateway "Opening in a fence or wall enclosing an #$OutdoorLocation that can be closed with a gate to control access to the region inside.")
(comment Canyon "A specialization of #$Valley.  Each instance of #$Canyon is a deep, narrow valley with very steep sides and a river flowing through the bottom.  #$Canyons are produced by water erosion.")
(comment ResearchInstitute-Facility "A facility, most likely consisting of several structures, which comprises the entire grounds of a #$ResearchOrganization.")
(comment Sheepfold "A walled or fenced enclosure intended to contain sheep or small herd animals,")
(comment Hermitage "A #$Hermitage is a place where a religious person lives on his own, apart from the rest of society.")
(comment Moor "A #$Moor is an area of open ground overlaid with wet peaty soils.")
(disjointWith Boatyard Shipyard)
(comment Boatyard "A #$Boatyard is a waterside facility for servicing, repairing, and building #$Boats. It is #$disjointWith #$Shipyard.")
(comment Cuesta "A #$Cuesta is a #$Ridge-Hill formed by gently tilted hard rock layers. Every cuesta has an #$Escarpment where the rock layers are exposed on their edges.")
(comment DockingBasin "A #$DockingBasin is the part of a #$Harbor where watercraft dock.")
(comment HerdAnimal-Small "A subcollection of #$HerdAnimal, consisting of those types of #$HerdAnimal that in general are smaller (such as #$Sheep and #$Goats) than the other types (such as #$Mastodons or #$Cattle).")
(disjointWith Hammock-Topographical Mountain)
(comment Hammock-Topographical "A #$Hammock-Topographical is a #$Hill which is distinct from and slightly above the surrounding plain or wetland.")
(comment LandMass "A generalization of #$LandBody.  Each instance of #$LandMass is a large topographical features distingished from the surrounding features by abrupt and clearly delineated boundaries, generally by being of higher altitude. Examples include #$Continents, large #$Islands, and bulges rising above the 'datum' of planetary bodies are all elements of this collection. Instances include #$ContinentOfNorthAmerica on #$PlanetEarth, Ishtar Terra on #$PlanetVenus, Tempa Terra on #$PlanetMars, etc.")
(comment Pier "A #$Pier is a fixed, typically wooden walkway or platform that sticks into a body of water. Notable specializations include #$Dock and #$RecreationalPier.")
(comment RecreationalPier "A #$RecreationalPier is a pier that is primarily intended for recreational activities, such as #$Fishing.")
(disjointWith WatercraftLanding SeaportFacility)
(comment WatercraftLanding "A #$WatercraftLanding is a place where watercraft receive or discharge passengers and freight, but lacking most port facilities.")
(comment Stable-Structure "Each #$Stable-Structure is a building typically found on a farm or ranch which is intended to house large domesticated animals such as horses.  Stables are often divided up into stalls intended for occupation by single animals.")
(comment BalancingReservoir "A specialization of #$WaterReservoir.  Each instance of #$BalancingReservoir is a body of water contained in a holding basin in which variations in flow and composition are averaged. Such basins are typically used to provide a flow of reasonably uniform volume and composition to a treatment plant. Balancing reservoirs are also often called \"equalizing basins.")
(comment ArtificialWaterFeature "#$ArtificialWaterFeature is a specialization of #$GeographicalThing, so each instance is located in a fixed position and could reasonably be placed on a map.  Each #$ArtificialWaterFeature is a human-created body of water whose basin is either a partially modified topographical feature, in the case of #$WaterReservoirs, or is a #$ConstructionArtifact, in the case of open tanks, swimming pools, and so forth.")
(comment StockTank "Instances of #$StockTank are small, human-made pools of water intended for use for livestock (horses, cattle, sheep, etc), either as drinking water or for disease treatment.  In regions where the topography will allow, #$StockTanks are usually created by moving earth to dam a watercourse.  Such instances can be considered instances of #$WaterReservoir.  In areas where this is not possible, #$StockTanks are established by pumping well water to fill large metal containers.  Those pools of water have some instance of  #$LiquidStorageTank as their basin.")
(comment BodyOfWater-Generic "Each instance of #$BodyOfWater-Generic is a mass of stationary or moving water in a fixed, outdoor location, contained either by a #$Basin-Topographical or by a #$Basin which is an artifact.  Positive examples include naturallying occurring #$Oceans, #$Rivers and #$Ponds, #$WaterReservoirs which are created with #$Dams, and other #$ArtificialWaterFeatures like #$StockTanks, reflecting pools, and swimming pools.  Negative examples include instances of #$LiquidStorageTank, because those are containers only and don't include their contents as parts.  Each #$BodyOfWater-Generic has a part which is a basin, and a part which is a mass of water.")
(comment basinOfBodyOfWater "(#$basinOfBodyOfWater BASIN BODY) means that the #$Basin BASIN is the basin in which the water of #$BodyOfWater-Generic BODY rests.")
(comment waterOfBodyOfWater "(#$waterOfBodyOfWater WATER BODY) means that WATER is the entire aqueous contents of #$BodyOfWater-Generic BODY's basin, at a specific time.")
(comment BodyOfWaterWaterFn "(#$BodyOfWaterWaterFn BODY) denotes the single, entire mass of #$Water that is contained by the basin of the #$BodyOfWater-Generic BODY.")
(comment BodyOfWaterBasinFn "(#$BodyOfWaterBasinFn BODY) denotes the basin in which the water portion of #$BodyOfWater-Generic BODY rests.")
(comment mathEquals "<code>(#$mathEquals INTERVAL1 INTERVAL2)</code> means that <code>INTERVAL1</code> is mathematically equal to <code>INTERVAL1</code> with respect to some scale that they are both on.  If <code>INTERVAL1</code> and <code>INTERVAL2</code> have upper and lower bounds, those bounds are identical.
<p>
Note that #$mathEquals is not a specialization of #$equals (q.v.), as mathematical values of different sorts can be mathematically equivalent without being considered identical (e.g. the #$Integer 1 and the #$RealNumber 1.0).
<p>
See also the typed specialization of #$mathEquals, #$numericallyEquals, which applies only to scalar values.")
(comment CattleDippingTank "Instances of #$CattleDippingTank are small, human-made pools of chemically treated water in which livestock is immersed for disease treatment or prevention.")
(comment Weir-Dam "The collection of all #$Dams with uncontrolled spillage.")
(comment damCreatesReservoir "This predicate relates a dam to the reservoir it creates by damming a stream.  (#$damCreatesReservoir DAM RES) means that the #$Dam DAM creates the #$WaterReservoir RES. For example, (#$damCreatesReservoir #$HooverDam #$LakeMead) is true.  Note that some #$Dams don't really create a reservoir, but simply raise the water level of a small stream or divert water to a desired channel.")
(comment Weir-Dam "Each instance of #$Weir-Dam is a small dam over a stream or small river.  Water flows over the top of a weir.  A weir really doesn't create much of a reservoir, but can deepen a small river to make it more navigable.")
(comment EnvironmentalQuantity "A specialization of #$ScalarQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$EnvironmentalQuantity is feature that applies to one's surrounding environment in some degree or other.")
(comment TypeLevelQuantity "The collection of #$ScalarQuantity[ies]
that sensically apply only to classes of #$Individuals, and not to the #$Individuals themselves, per se.")
(comment GenericQuantity "A specialization of #$NonNumericScalarQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$GenericQuantity is a properly interval-like, generic or approximate measure that is not numerically specified.  (Cf. #$MeasurableQuantity).
<p>
For example, (#$HighAmountFn #$Elasticity) is a \"high amount of elasticity\" (with respect to some contextually-determined, conventional elasticity scale), but is not explicitly associated with any particular number or numbers.  (But see #$NoteAboutGivingGenericValueFunctionsNumericValues.)
<p>
The values returned by #$GenericValueFunctions (such as #$HighAmountFn) are all instances of #$GenericQuantity.")
(comment EconomicalQuantity "A specialization of #$ScalarQuantity (q.v.), this is the collection of measurements of economical quantities such as the monetary value of some item or the inflation rate in some economy.")
(comment NonAspectualQuantity "A specialization of #$ScalarQuantity (q.v.).  This is the collection of all \"non-aspectual\" quantities: those that can always be attributed to an object simpliciter, without specifying a particular aspect or dimension of the object that the quantity has to do with.
<p>
For example, the degrees of #$Flammability are non-aspectual; an object could be attributed its flammability-level with a generic binary \"hasQuantity\" predicate.  Conversely, a particular #$Distance is aspect-relative; attributing a distance to any multi-dimensional object is relative to the dimension along which the distance is being measured (compare e.g. #$lengthOfObject and #$widthOfObject).")
(comment groupMembersPredominantlyOfType "(#$groupMembersPredominantlyOfType <code>GROUP</code>
<code>TYPE</code>) means that a significant majority (and possibly
all) of the members of <code>GROUP</code> are instances of
<code>TYPE</code>.  A bit more precisely,
(#$groupMembersPredominantlyOfType <code>GROUP</code>
<code>TYPE</code>) supports a defeasible inference to the conclusion that an
arbitrary member of <code>GROUP</code> is an instance of
<code>TYPE</code>.  This predicate is a generalization of
#$groupMemberType (qv.), and is especially useful in cases where
evidence does not quite support the strict universal generalization of
the latter predicate.")
(comment ScalarQuantity "A specialization of #$ScalarInterval and #$Quantity (qq.v.) that includes all scalars other than those that are purely numeric (cf. #$NumericInterval).  This is the collection of scalar <i>quantities</i>: at most partly numeric abstract individuals used as measurements or ratings of things.  Specializations of #$ScalarQuantity include #$Distance, #$Temperature, #$Acceleration, #$Toxicity, and #$Loyalty (all of which save the last are in fact specializations of #$PhysicalQuantity (q.v.) as well).
<p>
Another broad specialization of #$ScalarQuantity is #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.), which includes any quantity that could actually be used to <i>measure</i> something, as opposed to merely ranking it on a scale.  Any quantity that is (or in principle could be) returned by a #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) function is a measurable quantity.  In CycL, particular measurable quantities are usually represented as the result of applying a unit-of-measure function to a real number or pair of reals.  For example, (#$Meter 5) is the distance 5 meters.
<p>
For predicates that relate things to quantities that characterize them, see #$QuantitySlot.")
(comment Quantity "A specialization of #$ScalarOrVectorInterval (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Quantity is an abstract individual that is at most partly numeric and that is used as measurement or a rating of a thing.  #$Quantity has two significant specializations: #$ScalarQuantity and #$VectorQuantity (qq.v.).")
(comment vectorMagnitude "<code>(#$vectorMagnitude VECTOR MAG)</code> means that the magnitude of the #$VectorInterval <code>VECTOR</code> is the #$ScalarInterval <code>MAG</code>.  See also #$vectorPreciseMagnitude and #$vectorDirection.")
(comment UnderspecifiedIntervalFunctionFn "<code>(#$UnderspecifiedIntervalFunctionFn INTERVAL-TYPE)</code> is a
function-denoting function which returns temporally qualifiable functions.
<code>((#$UnderspecifiedIntervalFunctionFn INTERVAL-TYPE) THING)</code> is
the quantity of <code>INTERVAL-TYPE</code> that <code>THING</code> has, wheter or not that quantity is measured or even measurable.  For example,
((#$UnderspecifiedIntervalFunctionFn #$Mass) #$EiffelTower) denotes a mass: namely, the mass of the Eiffel Tower.  Functions denoted by
#$UnderspecifiedIntervalFunctionFn are typically used in conjunction with #$valueOf
(q.v.). For example, (#$valueOf ((#$UnderspecifiedIntervalFunctionFn #$Mass)
#$EiffelTower) (#$MetricTon 10100)) is true.")
(comment Furrow-Topographical "A specialization of both #$Depression-Topographical and #$LongThinObject.  Each instance of #$Furrow-Topographical is a long, narrow trench or depression that can be relatively small in scale, as the furrows dug for planting seeds. Furrows are much longer than wide, but usually not a whole lot deeper than they are wide.  If much deeper than wide, a long thin depression would be classified as a #$Crevice-Topographical.")
(comment Crevice-Topographical "A collection of topographical features and a specialization of #$Crevice and #$Depression-Topographical.  Each instance of #$Crevice-Topographical is a long, slender cavity or crack.  The width of a crevice is significantly less than its length.  The depth of a crevice is often greater than its width, and is never significantly less than its width.")
(comment Trough-Topographical "Each instance of #$Trough-Topographical is a long, narrow depression in a planetary surface, not any deeper than wide, especially with relatively steep sides and flat bottom, but less steep and deep than a trench.")
(comment PartiallyUndergroundStream "Each instance of #$PartiallyUndergroundStream is a stream or river which for one or more sections does not flow above ground, but flows below the surface, possibly reappearing further downstream, possibly not.")
(comment Upland "A specialization of #$NaturalElevation.  Each instance of #$Upland is a large region of land away from the sea having a consistently high elevation.  A single #$Hill or #$Mountain would not be considered an #$Upland. A #$MountainRange would not be considered an #$Upland, because it varies too much in elevation.")
(comment VectorQuantity "A specialization of #$VectorInterval and #$Quantity (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$VectorQuantity is a vector composed of a direction and a quantity (where the quantity ios the vector's magnitude).
<p>
For example, the vector with the direction #$Up-Generally and the magnitude (#$Meter 5) is a #$VectorQuantity, whereas the  vector with the direction #$Up-Generally and the magnitude 5 is a #$VectorInterval that is not a vector quantity.")
(comment VectorQuantityOfFn "<code>(#$VectorQuantityOfFn VECTOR-QUANT-TYPE)</code> is a
function-denoting function which returns a temporally qualifiable function when given a specialization of #$VectorQuantity as an argument. <code>((#$VectorQuantityOfFn VECTOR-QUANT-TYPE) THING)</code> is the quantity of <code>VECTOR-QUANT-TYPE</code> that <code>THING</code> has, whether or not that quantity is measured or even measurable.  For example, ((#$VectorQuantityOfFn #$LinearVelocityVector) #$RMS-Lusitania-TheShip) denotes a linear velocity: namely, the linear velocity of the Lusitania.  Functions denoted by #$QPQuantityFn are typically used in conjunction with #$valueOf (q.v.).  For example, (#$valueOf ((#$VectorQuantityOfFn #$LinearVelocityVector) #$RMS-Lusitania-TheShip) (#$VectorFn #$North-Generally (#$MetersPerSecond 0.5))) could have been true at some time.")
(comment primaryObjectConfigured "This predicate relates an object configuration to the primary object configured in the situation.  <code>(#$primaryObjectConfigured CONFIG OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is the main thing configured in <code>CONFIG</code>.  All things besides <code>OBJ</code> configured in the situation, <code>SUBOBJ</code>, (i.e. bearing the #$configuredObjects relation to <code>CONFIG</code>), will be part (see #$parts) of <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment DistanceVelocityUnderConstantAccelerationMt "When a is constant, v<sup>2</sup>=v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>+2ay.")
(comment TranslatedDistance "A specialization of #$Distance. #$TranslatedDistance is the collection of all distances that are translated by the primary objects moving in events (see #$distanceTranslated). Each instance of #$TranslatedDistance is the #$Distance between an object's starting location and ending location in a #$Movement-TranslationEvent.")
(comment sksiUnknownSentence "A #$negationPreds of #$sksiSentence, #$sksiUnknownSentence is used in inference to translate CycL queries into SQL queries that employ the SQL subquery expression \"<code>NOT EXISTS SELECT ...</code>\". <code>(#$sksiUnknownSentence SENT)</code> is true if and only if <code>SENT</code> can be entirely translated into one or more SQL queries to some #$StructuredKnowledgeSource that is applicable within the context of the inference, and the SQL query \"<code>NOT EXISTS SQL-SENT</code>\" returns true for every SQL translation <code>SQL-SENT</code> of <code>SENT</code> and for every such structured knowledge source.")
(comment sourceUnknownSentence "A #$negationPreds of #$sourceSentence, #$sourceUnknownSentence is used in inference to translate CycL queries into SQL queries that employ the SQL subquery expression \"<code>NOT EXISTS SELECT ...</code>\". <code>(#$sourceUnknownSentence SKS SENT)</code> is true if and only if <code>SENT</code> can be entirely translated into one or more SQL queries to <code>SKS</code>, and the SQL query \"<code>NOT EXISTS SQL-SENT</code>\" returns true for every SQL translation <code>SQL-SENT</code> of <code>SENT</code>.")
(comment Interfluve "A specialization of #$NaturalElevation.  Each instance of #$Interfluve is the region of higher elevation between two adjacent valleys or streams that drain in the same direction.")
(comment MangroveIsland "A sub-collection of #$Island. Each instance of #$MangroveIsland is a swampy island, surrounded by a body of water, and occupied by #$MangroveTrees.")
(comment ArtificialIsland "A man-made island created by depositing fill, potentially contained by dikes, into a wetland, bay or lagoon.")
(comment KidnappingAndHoldingHostage "A specialization of #$SeizingAndHoldingHostage.  In each
instance of #$KidnappingAndHoldingHostage, one or more #$Persons are
kidnapped and subsequently held hostage.  Instances of this collection
thus have instances of both #$KidnappingSomeone and #$HoldingHostages
as sub-events.  Typically, the #$perpetrator issues demands for the
release of the #$hostages, often for a ransom (see #$DemandingARansom)
or for some action by government authorities.")
(comment MangroveSwamp "A type of wetland region, each instance of which is occupied by many #$MangroveTrees.")
(comment Mine-ExcavationFacility "Each instance of #$Mine-ExcavationFacility is a place where people excavate in order to extract valuable materials from the soil.  Some mines are deep underground (for example, the #$Diamond mines in the #$RepublicOfSouthAfrica or some #$Coal mines in the #$AppalachianMountains), but others, such as #$PlacerMines do not result in very deep excavation.")
(comment PlacerMine "Each instance of #$PlacerMine is a location at which shallow excavation in loose, alluvial soil is performed and water is used to separate heavier, valuable gemstones and metals from the surrounding sediments.  Panning for gold is the simplest form of placer mining, but a #$PlacerMine can be more elaborate with infrastructure such as sluice boxes and excavating machinery.")
(comment PortableFn "An instance of both #$UnaryIntersectionFunction and #$ReifiableFunction. When applied to a specialization <code>OBJ</code> of #$PartiallyTangible, #$PortableFn returns as its value the intersection of #$PortableObject and <code>OBJ</code>.")
(comment RigidPortableObject "A specialization of #$PortableObject. Each instance of #$RigidPortableObject is a portable object with a medium to very high level of rigidity (see the predicate #$rigidityOfObject).")
(comment siblingDisambiguationCollectionType "<code>(#$siblingDisambiguationCollectionType TYPE)</code> means that the Learning Reader should give cookies to parses whose #$nearestIsaOfType <code>TYPE</code> are shared with other parses.")
(comment PortableObject "A specialization of #$HumanScaleObject (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PortableObject is a tangible object that is not \"fastened down\" and is light enough for an average human (or, more to the point, for its average intended user) to move easily.  
<p>
For many instances of #$PortableObject, portability is important for the fulfillment of their primary functions;  for example, an article of clothing (an instance of #$SomethingToWear),  a hand-guided tool (an instance of #$HandTool), a coin or dollar bill (an instance of #$Currency), or a flashlight (an instance of #$Flashlight) would be pretty useless if they weren't portable.  In other cases, making a device portable, or making a portable version of a device, simply provides a convenience, as with the subcollections #$PortableTelevision and #$PortableStereoSystem.  
<p>
Note that #$Tool is not a subcollection of #$PortableObject, since many tools are heavy and/or stationary.  A borderline example of a #$PortableObject would be a heart/lung machine; although the beneficiary of its primary function is in no position to move it around, the physicians and nurses who also are \"using\" it can and do exactly that.  The same goes for a playpen; the kids in it had better <i>not</i> be able to move it around, but the adults who set it up and put them there can and do move it.   A borderline non-example would be a car; even though its user can make it move around, that is more a controlling action than a transporting action -- i.e., the car is doing the transporting in that case, not the driver.  A borderline example is a cat; even though it moves under its own power, it is light enough for its owner to pick it up and move it around.  A borderline example is a bed or a dresser; though too heavy to lift, its owners can generally move it around (by sliding, disassembling and reassembling, and so on).")
(comment HumanlyOccupiedSpatialObject "A specialization of #$InanimateObject.  Each instance of #$HumanlyOccupiedSpatialObject is a place that humans occupy. Instances include both movable things, such as cars and ships, and things having a more or less permanent location, such as houses or office buildings.  Instances include things made by human beings, such as subway stations or concert halls, as well as things not made by human beings, such as caves.")
(comment siblingsWithRespectToType "<code>(#$siblingsWithRespectToType THING1 THING2 TYPE)</code> means that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> share a common #$nearestIsaOfType <code>TYPE.</code>")
(comment Weight-NonPoweredDevice "An instance of #$ArtifactTypeByFunction and a specialization of #$NonPoweredDevice. Each instance of #$Weight-NonPoweredDevice is a rigid object having some exact specified weight (for example, 10 grams or 1 kilogram), and used for weighing other objects (for instance, on a balance scale).")
(comment ContinuousQuantity "A specialization of #$ScalarQuantity (q.v.).  This is the collection of quantities that are components of some continuous (i.e. gap-free) scale.")
(comment demandsActOfType "(#$demandsActOfType <code>DEMANDING</code>
<code>ACT-TYPE</code>) means that in <code>DEMANDING</code>, an
instance of #$Demanding-CommunicationAct, the #$senderOfInfo demands
that the #$recipientOfInfo-Intended (or whomever the demand is
ultimately addressed to) perform an instance of
<code>ACT-TYPE</code>.")
(comment NLKBContentTest "The subcollection of KB Content Tests which test some aspect of Cyc's natural language parsing and/or generation abilities.")
(comment NLQuery "The subcollection of #$CycLQuerySpecifications which rely on some aspect of Cyc's natural language parsing and/or generation abilities.")
(comment SKSIQuery "The subcollection of #$CycLQuerySpecifications which need one or more SKSI sources to be registered in order to succeed.")
(comment BuilderQuery "The subcollection of #$CycLQuerySpecifications which are not intended to be asked by themselves, but instead are intended to be combined with other builder queries or otherwise added to or constrained.")
(comment diachronicallyDisjoint "A specialization of #$disjointWith (q.v.) that is used to state that two collections are \"disjoint across time\" in a certain sense.  <code>(#$diachronicallyDisjoint COL1 COL2)</code> holds just in case if something is an instance of <code>COL1</code> (<code>COL2</code>) at some time, then that thing is <i>not</i> an instance of <code>COL2</code> (<code>COL1</code>) at <i>any</i> time.  More precisely, for any <code>T</code>, <code>T'</code>, and <code>X</code>,
<pre> 
  if (#$isa X COL1) holds at T,  

  then (#$isa X COL2) does <i>not</i> hold at T'.
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$diachronicallyDisjoint #$Cat #$Dog)</code> means that nothing that's a cat (at any time) is <i>ever</i> a dog, and vice versa.  Note that the weaker statement <code>(#$disjointWith #$Cat #$Dog)</code> simply means that nothing is both a cat and a dog at the <i>same</i> time.")
(comment DrivingDistanceBetweenFn "#$DrivingDistanceBetweenFn is an evaluatable function which uses Google Maps to calculate the driving distance between two geographical places.<code>(#$DrivingDistanceBetweenFn PLACE1 PLACE2)</code> is evaluated by querying Cyc for the lat/long coordinates of <code>PLACE1</code> and <code>PLACE2</code>, using them to construct a URL targeted at the Google Maps website, and extracting the distance from the resulting html.")
(comment LocalGoogle-SearchEngine "The #$LocalGoogle-SearchEngine is Google's search engine that employs its map making capability to return maps as query results.")
(comment drivingDistanceBetween "The predicate corresponding to #$DrivingDistanceBetweenFn. <code>(#$drivingDistanceBetween PLACE1 PLACE2 DISTANCE)</code> means that according to the Google Maps website, the driving distance between <code>PLACE1</code> and <code>PLACE2</code> is <code>DISTANCE</code>.")
(comment Layer "A specialization of #$SheetOfSomeStuff.  Each instance of #$Layer is a sheet-like object situated adjacent, surface-to-surface, to at least one surface or layer or region on one side, and possibly to other layers or surfaces on both sides.  An instance of #$Layer may be part of a larger object, or it may be an independent whole object, such as a hide confined in a stack of hides.  An instance of #$Layer is <i>not</i> an instance of #$FreeSheet.")
(comment eventOutcomes "A specialization of #$eventResults (q.v.). The predicate #$eventOutcomes relates a particular event to the situations that follow from it either by causation or by agency (where the first event figures in the agent's reason for the subsequent action).  <code>(#$eventOutcomes EVT OUTCOME)</code> means that, in some sense, <code>OUTCOME</code> occurred because <code>EVT</code> occurred.  #$eventOutcomes is very general and includes <code>OUTCOME</code>s from #$causes-EventEvent, #$inReactionTo, #$postEvents, and #$postSituation.  For knowledge representation, always use the more specific relations where they are known.  #$eventOutcomes is useful in querying for the broad range of outcomes of an event.")
(comment eventResults "<code>(#$eventResults EVENT RESULT)</code> means that <code>RESULT</code> is a #$TemporalThing that in some way came about due to <code>EVENT</code> occurring.  This is a very general predicate, and <code>RESULT</code> may be an #$Event caused by <code>EVENT</code>, or a #$SomethingExisting created by <code>EVENT</code>.  Also, <code>RESULT</code> may come about directly or indirectly as a result of <code>EVENT</code>.")
(comment TransferringPossessionOfTypeFn "(#$TransferringPossessionOfTypeFn <code>OBJECT-TYPE</code>) denotes the
sub-collection of #$TransferringPossession events in which the
#$objectOfPossessionTransfer is an instance of <code>OBJECT-TYPE</code>.")
(comment transferringPossessionOfType "(#$transferringPossessionOfType <code>TRANSFER-TYPE</code> <code>OBJECT-TYPE</code>)
means that <code>TRANSFER-TYPE</code> is the sub-collection of 
#$TransferringPossession events in which an instance of <code>OBJECT-TYPE</code> is
the #$objectOfPossessionTransfer.  See also the corresponding function
#$TransferringPossessionOfTypeFn.")
(comment intendedMaleficiary "<code>(#$intendedMaleficiary ACTION AGENT)</code> means that either (i) some agent who directs (see #$directingAgent) <code>ACTION</code> intends that <code>AGENT</code> be harmed in (see #$maleficiary) <code>ACTION</code> or (ii) there is no directing agent, but some agent who performs (see #$performedBy) <code>ACTION</code> intends that <code>AGENT</code> be a maleficiary of <code>ACTION</code>.")
(comment damages "<code>(#$damages EVT OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is acted on in <code>EVT</code> in such a way as to end up damaged.  Destruction is considered an extreme form of damage.")
(comment givesSupportToAgentOfCategory "<code>(#$givesSupportToAgentOfCategory GIVER RECEIVER CATEGORY)</code> means that <code>GIVER</code> gives some positive amount of support, of category <code>CATEGORY</code>, to <code>RECEIVER</code>. For example, (#$givesSupportToAgent #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Egypt #$MilitarySupportQuantity) means that the US gives some positive amount of military support to Egypt. This predicate is the 'type-level' version of #$givesSupportToAgent, and so whenever an assertion with #$givesSupportToAgentOfCategory as arg zero is true, so will be some possible assertion with #$givesSupportToAgent as arg zero not, in general, be 'redundant with' the assertion using the latter predicate, as the latter will generally be more specific. This predicate is suited to making assertions about inter-agent support relationships in the presence of limited information either about the amount of support given, or about the criteria, in context, for a given amount of support counting as 'high', 'low' 'medium', and so forth. This predicate is also suited to formulating rules for which particular degrees of support are irrelevant.")
(comment ElectronicTransferOfCWTypeFn "(#$ElectronicTransferOfCWTypeFn <code>CW-TYPE</code>) denotes the
sub-collection of #$InformationTransferEvent-Electronic events in
which an instance (<code>CW-INS</code>) of <code>CW-TYPE</code> is the #$transferredThing.  Such
events typically involve transferring an
#$AbstractInformationStructure corresponding to <code>CW-INS</code> (see
#$infoStructureOfCW) by producing a new instantiation of this
structure through electronic means.")
(comment electronicTransferOfCWType "(#$electronicTransferOfCWType <code>TRANSFER-TYPE</code> <code>CW-TYPE</code>) means
that <code>TRANSFER-TYPE</code> is the sub-collection of
#$InformationTransferEvent-Electronic events in which an instance of
 <code>CW-TYPE</code> is the #$transferredThing.")
(comment typeOfThingRequested "'(#$typeOfThingRequested REQUEST TYPE)' means that TYPE is the
type of thing requested in REQUEST.")
(comment solutionStepResult "(#$solutionStepResult STEP SENT) means that #$PhysicsWorkedSolutionStep STEP results in the addition of SENT to the worked solution of which it is a component.  This predicate is not functional in argument 2, for a solution step may have more than one result.")
(comment StrategyScoreForHypothesesFn "An #$EvaluatableFunction. An expression of the form (#$StrategyScoreForHypothesesFn MT HYP1 HYP2) will return a #$RealNumber that is the score that is received by the two instances of #$HypotheticalContext HYP1 and HYP2 relative to the #$Microtheory MT which stores the representation of the parameter values that describe an instance of some sub-collection of #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy. (see #$optimizedStrategyForm, the relation that ties the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy type to MT). The function acts as an interface to a set of underlying (but unreferenced) reasoning modules. The first parameter, MT represents the particular strategy that is being invoked, possibly with additional settings for the strategy. The second and third arguments represent the two hypotheses that are the subject of scroing strategy.")
(comment distanceTravelledByInInterval "<code>(#$distanceTravelledByInInterval OBJ INT DIST)</code> means that during the time interval <code>INT</code>, <code>OBJ</code> travels the distance <code>DIST</code>.")
(comment DeterminingRelevantForcesInAPhysicsProblem "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$DeterminingRelevantForcesInAPhysicsProblem
is an event in which an agent determines which forces apply in a physics problem.")
(comment CategorizingAPhysicsProblem "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem and #$GroupingThingsMentally. Each instance of #$CategorizingAPhysicsProblem
is an event in which an agent determines which category a specific
physics problem belongs to.")
(comment IdentifyingARelevantEquationForProblem "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of
#$IdentifyingARelevantEquationForProblem is an event in which an agent
identifies a mathematical equation as relevant to solving a mathematical equation.")
(comment TransformingAMathematicalEquation "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of
#$TransformingAMathematicalEquation is an event in which an agent
transforms a mathematical equation.  Often this is done so that an
unknown quantity may easily be calculated based on known quantities.")
(comment equationForSolution "(#$equationForSolution SOLUTION EQUATION) means that EQUATION is a mathematical equation that is useful for working out the #$WorkedSolution, SOLUTION.")
(comment SubstitutingBindingsForVariables "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of
#$SubstitutingBindingsForVariables is an event in which an agent
substitutes known bindings for variables in a formula.")
(comment SettingInitialValuesOfQuantitiesInAPhysicsProblem "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$SettingInitialValuesOfQuantitiesInAPhysicsProblem 
is an event in which an agent determines what the initial values of quantities are in a physics problem.  For example, in a distance-time under constant acceleration problem, if the object moving is released and not thrown, this step would involve setting the initial velocity of the object to zero meters per second.")
(comment SanityCheckingPhysicsProblemSolution "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$SanityCheckingPhysicsProblemSolution 
is an event in which an agent checks the results of calculations performed to solve a physics problem to see whether they are reasonable.")
(comment consistent "A predicate which asserts of sentences that they do not contradict currently known facts. (#$consistent SENTENCE) means that the #$CycLSentence-Assertible SENTENCE is consistent with assertions known in the current context.  Consequently, (#$consistent SENTENCE) holds in a #$Microtheory MT just in case, for any assertion ASSERT that holds in MT, SENTENCE does not imply the negation of ASSERT.  Note that this predicate is #$notAssertible. See also #$inconsistentWithMt.")
(disjointWith PathThroughAir WaterSurfacePath)
(disjointWith SolidSurfacePathThroughAir PathThroughAir)
(disjointWith PathThroughAir PathThroughAirHangingFromSolid)
(comment CaravanRoute "A specialization #$TravelRoute. Each instance of #$CaravanRoute is an established line of travel used by #$Caravans.")
(comment FiringRange "An area intended for safely practicing the firing of ballistic weapons.  Various types of target practice facilities exist, ranging from private operations intended for the general public to practice with rifles and handguns, to military facilities designed to allow the firing of tank weaponry.")
(comment ScalarDivisionOfVectorFn "A binary, unreifiable #$MathematicalFunctionOnVectors that takes a #$VectorInterval <code>VI</code> and a #$ScalarInterval <code>SI</code> and returns the #$VectorInterval <code>RESULT-VI</code> that results from dividing <code>VI</code> by <code>SI</code>.  The magnitude of <code>RESULT-VI</code> is the result of dividing the magnitude of <code>VI</code> by <code>SI</code>, and the direction of <code>RESULT-VI</code> is the same as the direction of <code>VI</code>.
<p>
For example, the value of 
<pre>
(#$ScalarDivisionOfVectorFn
  (#$VectorFn (#$Newton 20) #$Down-Directly)
  2)
</pre>
is
<pre>
(#$VectorFn (#$Newton 10) #$Down-Directly),
</pre>
and the value of 
<pre>
(#$ScalarDivisionOfVectorFn
  (#$VectorFn (#$Newton 20) #$Down-Directly)
  (#$MinusFn 5))
</pre>
is
<pre>
(#$VectorFn (#$Newton (#$MinusFn 4)) #$Down-Directly),
</pre>
which is equivalent to
<pre>
(#$VectorFn (#$Newton 4) #$Up-Directly).
</pre>
See also #$ScalarMultiplicationOfVectorFn.")
(comment ScalarMultiplicationOfVectorFn "A binary, unreifiable #$MathematicalFunctionOnVectors that takes a #$VectorInterval <code>VI</code> and a #$ScalarInterval <code>SI</code> and returns the #$VectorInterval <code>RESULT-VI</code> that results from multiplying <code>VI</code> by <code>SI</code>.  The magnitude of <code>RESULT-VI</code> is the result of multiplying the magnitude of <code>VI</code> by <code>SI</code>, and the direction of <code>RESULT-VI</code> is the same as the direction of <code>VI</code>.
<p>
For example, the value of 
<pre>
(#$ScalarMultiplicationOfVectorFn
  (#$VectorFn (#$Newton 20) #$Down-Directly)
  2)
</pre>
is
<pre>
(#$VectorFn (#$Newton 40) #$Down-Directly),
</pre>
and the value of 
<pre>
(#$ScalarMultiplicationOfVectorFn
  (#$VectorFn (#$Newton 20) #$Down-Directly)
  (#$MinusFn 3))
</pre>
is
<pre>
(#$VectorFn (#$Newton (#$MinusFn 60)) #$Down-Directly),
</pre>
which is equivalent to
<pre>
(#$VectorFn (#$Newton 60) #$Up-Directly).
</pre>
See also #$ScalarDivisionOfVectorFn.")
(comment VectorSumFn "A binary, unreifiable #$MathematicalFunctionOnVectors that takes two #$VectorIntervals, <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> and returns the #$VectorInterval <code>SUM-VI</code> that results from adding <code>VI-1</code> to <code>VI-2</code>.  If <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> have the same direction, <code>DIR</code>, then <code>SUM-VI</code> is the vector whose magnitude is the sum of the magnitudes of <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> and whose direction is <code>DIR</code>.  When <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> have different directions, things are slightly more complicated.  Basically, the sum of the vectors is determined by separately adding their individual dimensional components.
<p>
See also #$VectorDifferenceFn.")
(comment VectorDifferenceFn "A binary, unreifiable #$MathematicalFunctionOnVectors that takes two #$VectorIntervals, <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> and returns the #$VectorInterval <code>SUM-VI</code> that results from subtracting <code>VI-2</code> from <code>VI-1</code>.  If <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> have the same direction, <code>DIR</code>, then <code>SUM-VI</code> is the vector whose magnitude is the difference of the magnitudes of <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> and whose direction is <code>DIR</code>.  When <code>VI-1</code> and <code>VI-2</code> have different directions, things are slightly more complicated.  Basically, the difference of the vectors is determined by performing subtraction on their individual dimensional components.
<p>
See also #$VectorSumFn.")
(comment VectorDirectionFn "<code>(VectorDirectionFn VECTOR)</code> denotes the direction which is the direction of <code>VECTOR</code>.")
(comment DeterminingTheNetForceActingOnAnObject "A specialization of #$DoingAddition and #$DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues. Each instance of #$DeterminingTheNetForceActingOnAnObject
is an event in which an agent determines the vector sum of all forces acting on a particular object.")
(comment SolutionStepEventType "#$SolutionStepEventType is the collection of types of events that can be associated with the solution steps used to generate a worked solution to a problem.")
(comment thingIrrelevantToThing "<code>(#$thingIrrelevantToThing THING1 THING2)</code> means that when reasoning about <code>THING2</code>, it is not necessary to draw in considerations about <code>THING1</code>.  For example, <code>(#$thingIrrelevantToThing ((#$VectorQuantityOfFn #$FrictionVector) Obj001) Obj001))</code> means that the friction vectors that might ordinarily be applicable to <code>Obj001</code> are in fact irrelevant to considerations about <code>Obj001</code>.")
(comment DeterminingTheForceActingOnAnObjectInASpecificDirection "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$DeterminingTheForceActingOnAnObjectInASpecificDirection
is an event in which an agent determines the force acting on an object in a specific direction.")
(comment UpwardForceVector "The collection of all #$ForceVectors whose directional component is #$Up-Directly.  See also #$DownwardForceVector.")
(comment DownwardForceVector "The collection of all #$ForceVectors whose directional component is #$Down-Directly.  See also #$UpwardForceVector.")
(disjointWith DeterminingTheNetForceActingOnAnObject DeterminingTheNetForceOfASpecificTypeActingOnAnObject)
(comment DeterminingTheNetForceOfASpecificTypeActingOnAnObject "A specialization of #$DoingAddition and #$DeterminingACentralTendencyAmongASetOfValues. Each instance of #$DeterminingTheNetForceOfASpecificTypeActingOnAnObject
is an event in which an agent determines the vector sum of all forces <i>of a specific type</i> (e.g. gravity, magnetism, or friction) acting on a particular object.
<p>
To be precise, by \"a specific type\" of force, we mean: any <i>proper</i> specialization of #$ForceVector.  Hence this collection is disjoint with #$DeterminingTheNetForceActingOnAnObject, which involves determining the vector sum of <i>all</i> forces (of <i>any</i> type) acting on an object.")
(comment utmLongitudeZone "<code>(#$utmLongitudeZone THING LONG-ZONE)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is found in UTM Longitude Zone <code>LONG-ZONE</code>.  There are 60 total UTM LongitudeZones, numbered from 1 to 60. Each zone is six degrees wide. Zone 1 is bounded by longitude 180 to 174 W and is centered on the 177th West meridian. Zone numbering increases in an easterly direction. See also #$utmLatitudeZone.")
(comment UTMCoordinateSystem "A #$CartesianCoordinateSystem that
sections the earth into many longitude and latitude zones. See #$utmLongitudeZone and #$utmLatitudeZone. The cartesian coordinates of points within a single UTM zone are expressed using the predicates <code>#$easting</code> and <code>#$northing</code>.")
(comment utmLatitudeZone "<code>(#$utmLatitudeZone THING LAT-ZONE)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is found in UTM Latitude Zone <code>LAT-ZONE</code>.  The #$UTMCoordinateSystem segments each longitude zone (see #$utmLongitudeZone)into 16 latitude zones. Each latitude zone is 8 degrees high, and is lettered starting from \"C\" at 80 S latitude, increasing up the (English) alphabet up to \"X\", omitting the letters \"I\" and \"O\" (because of their similarity to the digits one and zero). The last latitude zone, \"X\", is extended an extra 4 degrees, so it ends at 84 N latitude, thus covering the northern most land on the #$EarthsTopographicSurface.")
(comment coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem "A binary predicate that relates a #$FunctionalSlot predicate to a #$CoordinateSystem (qq.v.).  <code>(#$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>PRED</code> is one of the binary predicates used to describe the location of spatial things in the coordinate system <code>SYS</code>.  The first argument of <code>PRED</code> will be restricted (via #$arg1Isa) to instances of #$SpatialThing or some specialization thereof.  
<p>
In geometry, typically only <i>points</i> have specific coordinate values; the location of a spatially extended object is given by an equation that is satisfied by (all and only) the points spatially subsumed by the object.  But here we allow for spatially extended objects to have coordinate values attributed to them directly.  So in an assertion of the form <code>(PRED THING VALUE)</code>, the #$SpatialThing <code>THING</code> need not be an instance of #$Point.  In certain contexts we might stipulate, for example, that such an assertion is true if and only if <code>THING</code> subsumes a point <code>POINT</code> such that <code>(PRED POINT VALUE)</code> holds.
<p>
There are various specializations of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem currently reified in the Cyc knowledge base, each of which is applicable to one or the other of the two main types of coordinate systems.  #$xAxisPredicateOfSystem, #$yAxisPredicateOfSystem, and #$zAxisPredicateOfSystem all apply to #$CartesianCoordinateSystems.  #$radialDistancePredicate, #$azimuthAnglePredicate, and #$altitudeAnglePredicate all apply to #$PolarCoordinateSystems.")
(comment SymmetricScoringStrategy "A specialization of #$ScoringStrategy and #$ProgramSpecification. Each instance of #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy is a #$ProgramSpecification that characterizes how a scoring program should behave. For example, a video game that awards points based on players' performance might have several strategies that reflect different levels of difficulty.")
(comment TextLearnerFinalizationQuerySpecification "The collection of instances of #$CycLQuerySpecification that are run by the Text Learner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) to determine what knowledge should be asserted for retention independent of the infrastructure (parse trees, etc.) used to deduce it. These queries should mention (#$TheFn #$LinguisticExpressionPeg) -- for which will be substituted the peg for the sentence just read -- and return #$ist sentences suitable to be asserted and transmitted.")
(comment LivestockRoute "A specialization #$TravelRoute. Each instance of #$LivestockRoute is an established line of travel used to transport #$Livestock by herding them along.")
(comment Demanding-Threatening "A subcollection of #$Demanding-CommunicationAct.  In each
instance of #$Demanding-Threatening, an agent presents to another
a forceful request for a certain type of action, and threatens an
undersirable result if this request is not fulfilled.  Instances of
#$Demanding-Threatening are complex communication events in which both a
#$Request and #$Threat are expressed and related in a certain way.")
(comment ConvertingTranslatedDistanceToDistanceTravelled "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$ConvertingTranslatedDistanceToDistanceTravelled is an event in which an agent determines how far an object has travelled in a time period, based on how far it has travelled in an event with the duration of that time period.")
(comment CalculatingTranslatedDistance "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$CalculatingTranslatedDistance
is an event in which an agent determines how far an object has travelled.")
(comment DistanceTravelled "A specialization of #$Distance. #$DistanceTravelled is the collection of all distances that are translated by the primary objects moving in events. Each instance of #$DistanceTravelled is the #$Distance between an object's starting location and ending location over a time period.")
(comment candidatePegInterpretations-Partial-CycL "<code>(#$candidatePegInterpretations-Partial-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL expression <code>CYCL</code> is a CycL interpretation of some part of the content of <code>PEG</code>, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.  Note that #$candidatePegInterpretations-Partial-CycL is not the #$negationPreds of #$candidatePegInterpretations-NonPartial-CycL, as it is possible that an interpretation of part of a peg (e.g., #$JohnLennon as an interpretation of \"Lennon\" in \"John Lennon\") can also be an interpretation for the whole peg (e.g., \"John Lennon\").")
(comment pegDerivedSentenceDrawsOnInterpretations "<code>(#$pegDerivedSentenceDrawsOnInterpretations PEG CYCL MT)</code> means that the #$Microtheory <code>MT</code> represents a possible interpretation of the #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance <code>PEG</code> that properly contains (see #$termFormulas and #$different) a closed CycL sentence <code>S</code>, provable from the content model of <code>PEG</code> (see #$contentModelFocalPeg), that is also contained in <code>CYCL</code>, a CycL sentence that (thus) entails <code>S</code> and, like <code>S</code> is also true from the content model of PEG.")
(comment JointOperationsGraphic "#$JointOperationsGraphic (JOG) maps are #$TopographicMaps which are based on standard 1:250,000 medium-scale military topographic maps, but they contain additional information needed to conduct joint air-ground operations. JOG maps are produced both in ground (JOG-G) and air (JOG-A) formats. See #$JointOperationsGraphic-Ground and #$JointOperationsGraphic-Air. The topographic information is identical on both, but the ground version shows elevations and contour in meters and the air version shows them in feet. Layer (elevation) tinting and relief shading are added as an aid to interpolating relief. Both versions emphasize air landing facilities (shown in purple), but the air version has additional symbols to identify aids and obstructions to air navigation. Each JOG map is identified by an ID string which is determined by the geographic location which the map covers. See #$JOGMapFn and #$idStringForJOGMap")
(comment JointOperationsGraphic-Ground "#$JointOperationsGraphic-Ground maps are JOG maps that are prepared for use by ground units. Only stable or permanent air facilities are identified. Elevations and contours are located in the same positions as on the air version, (see #$JointOperationsGraphic-Air) but are given in meters instead of feet.
")
(disjointWith JointOperationsGraphic-Ground JointOperationsGraphic-Air)
(comment JointOperationsGraphic-Air "#$JointOperationsGraphic-Air maps are #$JointOperationsGraphic maps that are specially prepared for planning and conducting air operations. In addition to the information provided on all JOG maps, the air versions contain detailed information on air facilities such as radio ranges, runway lengths, and landing surfaces. The highest terrain elevation in each 15-minute quadrangle is identified by the large open-faced figures shown in the legend. Elevations and contours on JOG (AIR) maps are given in feet.")
(comment idStringForJOGMap "<code>(#$idStringForJOGMap SHEET-ID JOG-MAP)</code> means that the #$JointOperationsGraphic map <code>JOG-MAP</code> is identified by the sheet ID <code>SHEET-ID</code>. The sheet ID is determined by the geographic location which the map covers. See also the function corresponding to this predicate, #$JOGMapFn.")
(comment QuestionTypeByAnswerTypeRequested "A #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$QuestionTypeByAnswerTypeRequested is a collection of instances of #$Question-PCW that request the same type of answer.  Examples include the collection of all multiple choice questions and the collection of all yes/no questions.")
(comment DeterminingTheForceOneObjectExertsOnAnother "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$DeterminingTheForceOneObjectExertsOnAnother
is an event in which an agent determines the force that one object exerts on another object.")
(comment nearestScalarInSet "A #$TernaryPredicate that relates a #$ScalarInterval to a #$ScalarInterval to a set of #$ScalarIntervals.  (#$nearestScalarInSet A B SET) means that B is a member of SET and the absolute value of A minus B is less than the absolute value of the difference between A and any other member of SET.  For example, the following is true:
<pre>
(#$nearestScalarInSet 7 8 (#$TheSet 5 8 10)).
</pre>")
(comment Policy "A specialization of #$NormativeSpecification (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Policy is a specification which contains the terms of some policy of a particular agent or agents.  #$Policy is thus stronger than #$NormativeSpecification, since some normative specifications may describe the way the world should be without describing what agents should do.
<p>
Instances of #$Policy would include U.S. Federal government hiring policies (see #$OrganizationPolicy), an individual's policy to refrain from violating rules of grammar when writing, to go to the gym three times a week, or to be honest (it is widely noted that honesty is the best policy).
<p>
Note that most policies with more than one committed agent are instances of #$Agreement (q.v.), a specialization of #$Policy.")
(comment opposesAgent "<code>(#$opposesAgent OPPOSER OPPOSEE)</code> means that <code>OPPOSER</code> opposes <code>OPPOSEE</code>. Note that this does not imply that <code>OPPOSEE</code> opposes <code>OPPOSER</code>, nor even that <code>OPPOSEE</code> is aware of <code>OPPOSER</code>.")
(comment opponents "A specialization of #$opposesAgent (q.v.). <code>(#$opponents AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> means that <code>AGENT1</code> and <code>AGENT2</code> oppose each other.  See also #$considersAsEnemy.")
(comment DeterminingTheAbsoluteValueOfAScalar "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$DeterminingTheAbsoluteValueOfAScalar
is an event in which an agent determines the absolute value of a scalar.  An example would be determining that the absolute value of -5 is 5.")
(comment sympathizers "A specialization of #$positiveVestedInterest (q.v.). <code>(#$sympathizers AGENT SYMPATHIZER)</code> means that <code>SYMPATHIZER</code> is a sympathizer of <code>AGENT</code>; that is, <code>SYMPATHIZER</code> shares at least some of the ideological goals of <code>AGENT</code>, and hopes that <code>AGENT</code> will be successful in bringing about those goals.  For example, (#$sympathizers #$LabourParty-UK #$Bob) means that Bob is a sympathizer of the UK Labour Party.")
(comment DeterminingTheElementOfASetOfScalarsNearestToAGivenScalar "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$DeterminingTheElementOfASetOfScalarsNearestToAGivenScalar
is an event in which an agent determines which element of a set of scalars is nearest to a given scalar.  An example would be determining that the number that is nearest to 7 in the set {5, 8, 10} is 8.")
(comment TranslationInBackwardsDirection "A specialization of #$Movement-TranslationEvent. Each instance of #$TranslationInBackwardsDirection is a translation event in which the direction of translation (see the predicate #$directionOfTranslation-Throughout) of the moving object is the same as the direction in which the back side of that object (see #$objectBackSide) is facing.")
(comment WalkingBackwards "A specialization of both #$Walking-Generic and #$TranslationInBackwardsDirection. Each instance of #$WalkingBackwards is a walking event in which the walker moves in the direction in which his back side is facing.")
(comment RunningBackwards "A specialization of both #$Running and #$TranslationInBackwardsDirection. Each instance of #$RunningBackwards is a running event in which the runner moves in the direction in which his back side is facing.")
(comment TranslationInForwardDirection "A specialization of #$Movement-TranslationEvent. Each instance of #$TranslationInForwardDirection is a translation event in which the direction of translation (see the predicate #$directionOfTranslation-Throughout) of the moving object is the same as the direction in which the front side of that object (see #$objectFrontSide) is facing.")
(disjointWith TranslationInBackwardsDirection TranslationInForwardDirection)
(comment WalkingForward "A specialization of both #$Walking-Generic and #$TranslationInForwardDirection. Each instance of #$WalkingForward is a walking event in which the walker moves in the direction in which his front side is facing.")
(comment RunningForward "A specialization of both #$Running and #$TranslationInForwardDirection. Each instance of #$RunningForward is a running event in which the runner moves in the direction in which his front side is facing.")
(comment AgriculturalTerrainFeatureType "A collection of collections, a specialization of #$ArtifactualFeatureType. Instances of #$AgriculturalTerrainFeatureType
are collections of sites used for agricultural activities or structures of significant importance that are found in such sites.")
(comment AdministrativeRegionFeatureType "A collection of collections. Instances of #$AdministrativeRegionFeatureType are collections of terrain features which are types of #$GeopoliticalEntity.")
(comment ResidentialTerrainFeatureType "A collection of collections, a specialization of #$ArtifactualFeatureType. Instances of #$ResidentialTerrainFeatureType are collections of constructed sites amenable for human beings to reside at.  Also included are collections of structures of significant importance, whether functional or physical, that are found in such sites.")
(comment LandTransportationTerrainFeatureType "A collection of collections. Instances of #$LandTransportationTerrainFeatureType are collections of terrain features which are used for or relate to transportation of vehicles, people, or materials over land.")
(comment distanceTravelledBy "(#$distanceTravelledBy OBJ DIST) means that the distance OBJ has travelled in the current context is DIST.  This is intended to capture English expressions like \"The car has moved 10 meters,\" where context provides the relevant background information for evaluating this as true or false.  See also #$distanceTravelledByInInterval.")
(comment Ducking-DodgingSomething "A specialization of #$DodgingSomething. In each instance of #$Ducking-DodgingSomething, an agent dodges an object by lowering its head or body.")
(comment Sprinting "A specialization of #$Running. Each instance of #$Sprinting is a running event in which the runner runs at a top rate of speed for a short duration.")
(disjointWith Sprinting Jogging)
(comment SprintingForward "A specialization of both #$Sprinting and #$RunningForward. Each instance of #$SprintingForward is a sprinting event in which the runner moves in the direction in which his front side is facing.")
(comment SprintingBackwards "A specialization of both #$Sprinting and #$RunningBackwards. Each instance of #$SprintingBackwards is a sprinting event in which the runner moves in the direction in which his back side is facing.")
(comment UnderseaTerrainFeatureType "A collection of collections.  Instances of #$UnderseaTerrainFeatureType are collections of terrain features which can be found on the ocean floor.")
(comment genlPreds "A #$MetaPredicate for stating that one predicate is a generalization of another.  <code>(#$genlPreds SPEC-PRED GENL-PRED)</code> means that <code>GENL-PRED</code> is a generalization of <code>SPEC-PRED</code>.  That is, <code>(GENL-PRED ARG1..ARGN)</code> holds whenever <code>(SPEC-PRED ARG1..ARGN)</code> holds.  For example, <code>(#$genlPreds #$touches #$near)</code> holds, as touching something implies being near it.
<p>
As for the respective arities (see #$arity) of <code>SPEC-PRED</code> and <code>GENL-PRED</code>, (i) both predicates might have the same fixed-arity (see #$FixedArityRelation), (ii) both might have variable-arity (see #$VariableArityRelation), or (iii) <code>SPEC-PRED</code> might have a fixed-arity and <code>GENL-PRED</code> variable-arity.  (It cannot be the case that <code>SPEC-PRED</code> has variable-arity and <code>GENL-PRED</code> has a fixed-arity.)
<p>
See also #$negationPreds, #$genlInverse, and #$negationInverse.")
(comment Geocode "A #$Geocode is an #$IDString which uniquely identifies a point or area at the surface of the #$PlanetEarth. Examples include #$PostalCodes and #$MGRSZoneCodes.")
(comment MGRSZoneCode "The Military Grid Reference System is the geographic standard used by the United States Armed Forces and NATO for locating any point on the #$PlanetEarth with a 2 to 10 character #$Geocode. It is always displayed in an even number of numeral characters preceded by an alpha-numeric code describing the larger Earth area to which it belongs. A two digit numeral code after the alpha-numeric prefix implies a precision of 10km, and a ten digit code corresponds to a 1m precision with intermediate steps of 1km, 100m, and 10m.")
(comment mgrsZone "<code>(#$mgrsZone THING MGRS-CODE)</code> means that the geographic location of <code>THING</code> is identified by the #$MGRSZoneCode <code>MGRS-CODE</code>.")
(comment memberOpposesOrgNorm "<code>(#$memberOpposesOrgNorm MEM ORG NORM)</code> means that <code>MEM</code> is a member of <code>ORG</code> (see #$hasMembers), <code>ORG</code> is a proponent of <code>NORM</code> (see #$normProponents), and <code>MEM</code> opposes <code>NORM</code> (see #$normOpponents).")
(comment interArgEqualsIsa1-2 "A ternary instance of #$InterArgEqualsIsaPredicate (q.v.). (#$interArgEqualsIsa1-2 RELN INDEPENDENT-ARG DEPENDENT-ARG-COL) means that, when INDEPENDENT-ARG appears as the first argument to RELN, a necessary condition for semantic well-formedness is that the second argument must be an instance of DEPENDENT-ARG-COL.  That is, (RELN INDEPENDENT-ARG ARG2 ...) is semantically well-formed only if ARG2 is an instance of DEPENDENT-ARG-COL. For example, (#$interArgEqualsIsa1-2 #$softwareParameterValueInSpecification #$InferenceSentenceParameter #$CycLSentence-Assertible) means that (#$softwareParameterValueInSpecification #$InferenceSentenceParameter VALUE SPEC) is semantically well-formed only if VALUE is an instance of #$CycLSentence-Assertible.
<p>
For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its direct specializations.  See #$interArgEqualsIsa for a similar but more general predicate that is quintary, allowing the user to specify the positions of the independent and dependent arguments.")
(comment LookingDownward "A specialization of #$WatchingSomething. Each instance of #$LookingDownward is an event in which a perceptual agent directs its visual sensors downward.")
(comment LookingUpward "A specialization of #$WatchingSomething. Each instance of #$LookingUpward is an event in which a perceptual agent directs its visual sensors upward.")
(disjointWith LookingDownward LookingUpward)
(comment relationExtractedViaReferenceInStructure "<code>(#$relationExtractedViaReferenceInStructure RELN STRUCTURE)</code> means that the thing <code>RELN</code> was a relation first encountered by a Cyc-based data processing program (e.g., the TextLearner system (#$TheCycTextLearnerProgram)) that discovered a reference to it while processing <code>STRUCTURE</code>, an instance of #$AbstractStructure.")
(comment testAnswers-SingleCorrectMultipleChoice "<code>(#$testAnswers-SingleCorrectMultipleChoiceValue TEST ANSWER)</code> means that <code>ANSWER</code> is the single correct choice among the labels (typically numbers of letters) of the multiple choice answers for the question associated with <code>TEST</code>.")
(comment TensileForceQuantity "A #$QuantityType and a sub-collection of #$ForceQuantity.  A #$TensileForceQuantity represents the magnitude component of a #$TensileForceVector (q.v.).")
(comment directionOfPulling "(#$directionOfPulling PULL DIR) means that in the pulling event PULL, the object pulled is pulled in the direction DIR. ")
(comment directionOfForceApplication "(#$directionOfForceApplication APPLY DIR) means that in the application of force APPLY, a force is applied to an object in the direction DIR. ")
(comment increasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent "This predicate is used to state that the value of a fluent remains constant for a fixed object throughout an event. <code>(#$increasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent FLUENT EVENT)</code> is true if and only if for every pair of sub-events <code>SUB1</code> and <code>SUB2</code> of <code>EVENT</code> there exist values <code>VAL1</code> and <code>VAL2</code> such that if
<pre>
(#$and
  (#$startsAfterEndingOf SUB1 SUB2)
  (#$valueOf (#$MeasurementAtFn FLUENT SUB1) VAL1))
  (#$valueOf (#$MeasurementAtFn FLUENT SUB2) VAL2))
</pre>
it follows that
<pre>
(#$lessThan VAL1 VAL2)
</pre>
is true. See also #$decreasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent and #$constantThroughoutEvent-Fluent.")
(comment decreasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent "This predicate is used to state that the value of a fluent decreases for a fixed object throughout an event. <code>(#$decreasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent FLUENT EVENT)</code> is true if and only if for every pair of sub-events <code>SUB1</code> and <code>SUB2</code> of <code>EVENT</code> there exist values <code>VAL1</code> and <code>VAL2</code> such that if
<pre>
(#$and
  (#$startsAfterEndingOf SUB1 SUB2)
  (#$valueOf (#$MeasurementAtFn FLUENT SUB1) VAL1))
  (#$valueOf (#$MeasurementAtFn FLUENT SUB2) VAL2))
</pre>
it follows that
<pre>
(#$greaterThan VAL1 VAL2)
</pre>
is true. See also #$constantThroughoutEvent-Fluent and #$increasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent.")
(comment constantThroughoutEvent-Fluent "This predicate is used to state that the value of a fluent remains constant for a fixed object throughout an event. <code>(#$constantThroughoutEvent-Fluent FLUENT EVENT)</code> is true if and only if for every pair of sub-events <code>SUB1</code> and <code>SUB2</code> of <code>EVENT</code> there exist values <code>VAL1</code> and <code>VAL2</code> such that if
<pre>
(#$and
  (#$startsAfterEndingOf SUB1 SUB2)
  (#$valueOf (#$MeasurementAtFn FLUENT SUB1) VAL1))
  (#$valueOf (#$MeasurementAtFn FLUENT SUB2) VAL2))
</pre>
it follows that
<pre>
(#$numericallyEquals VAL1 VAL2)
</pre>
is true. See also #$decreasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent and #$increasesThroughoutEvent-Fluent.")
(comment LookingStraightAhead "A specialization of #$WatchingSomething. Each instance of #$LookingStraightAhead is an event in which a perceptual agents directs its visual sensors straight ahead, and neither upward or downward. See also the related collections #$LookingUpward and #$LookingDownward")
(comment RadioStation-Place "An instance of #$ExistingObjectType and a specialization of #$ModernShelterConstruction.  Each instance of #$RadioStation-Place is a construction artifact equipped with a tower (see the collection #$RadioTower) for making radio broadcasts.  See also the related collection #$RadioStation-Organization.")
(comment extractedRelationBasedOnPrepositionInStructure "Nouns and prepositional complements pose special problem to compositional approaches to the semantic interpretation of natural language sentences.  Consider the sentence \"Chirac was president of France.\"  One cannot successfully construct the right semantic interpretation by building a CycL sentence that <i>merely</i> combines the semantics of the \"president\" (e.g., #$PresidentOfOrganization) with those of \"of\" (say #$possessiveRelation), as there are lots of ways in which Chirac might be an instance of #$PresidentOfOrganization (e.g., he's president of his local book club) and be \"of\" France (e.g., he was born there). The correct semantics involves recognizing that the noun \"president\" and its complement form a unit (\"president of\") that can be mapped more or less directly to a relation (#$president) that can relate the respective denotations of \"Chirac\" and \"France.\"
<p>In many cases where a noun is known to Cyc, we have ways of handling this correctly (see, e.g., #$denotesArgInReln and #$RelationalNounSlot).  But where a noun is unknown, (e.g., \"exclave\" in \"Cabinda is an exclave of Angola\"), the relation is frequently missing, and so must, along with the denotation for the new noun, be first represented in Cyc in order to form a semantic interpretation of the sentence. 
  <code>(#$extractedRelationBasedOnPrepositionInStructure RELN PREP STRUCTURE)</code> means the relation <code>RELN</code> has been reified by Cyc in order to fill just this sort of ontological gap, made evident by the occurrence of a preposition-string (see #$prepositionStrings) associated with the preoposition <code>PREP</code> in the abstract structure <code>STRUCTURE</code>.")
(comment candidateForRelationExtractionBasis "<code>(#$candidateForRelationExtractionBasis STRUCTURE COLLECTION WORD)</code> means that the instance <code>STRUCTURE</code> of #$AbstractStructure is a candidate for the reification of a new relation due to its relationship to the collection <code>COLLECTION</code> and the word <code>WORD</code>.  In particular, <code>COLLECTION</code> is a concept that has been extracted from (see #$conceptExtractedViaReferenceInStructure) a structure <code>PRIOR</code> such that a larger structure <code>WHOLE</code> spans <code>PRIOR</code> and <code>STRUCTURE</code> in the right way (e.g., in the way that \"president of\" spans \"president\" and \"of\") and where <code>WORD</code> (e.g., #$Of-TheWord) is related to <code>STRUCTURE</code> by virtue of sharing a common string.")
(comment AgriculturalSchool "A specialization of #$SchoolInstitution. Each instance of #$AgriculturalSchool is a school whose curriculum is focused on agriculture.")
(comment MilitarySchool "A specialization of #$SchoolInstitution. Each instance of #$MilitarySchool is a school whose curriculum is focused on the field of #$MilitaryScience.")
(comment GeographicalPlaceByNGAImportanceLevel "A #$typeGenls of #$GeographicalPlace whose instances are types of populated places which can be ordered by their relative importance according to the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US (NGA). The NGA ranks certain populated places on a scale from 1 to 5, with 1 corresponding to places of extreme importance, and 5 to places of negligible importance. Currently (03/21/2006) the NGA's criteria for determining relative importance of a place is unknown.")
(comment UnimportantGeographicalPlace "An instance of #$GeographicalPlaceByNGAImportanceLevel. Instances of #$UnimportantGeographicalPlace are of negligible importance according to the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US.")
(comment SomewhatImportantGeographicalPlace "An instance of #$GeographicalPlaceByNGAImportanceLevel. Instances of #$SomewhatImportantGeographicalPlace are of minor importance according to the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US.")
(comment ModeratelyImportantGeographicalPlace "An instance of #$GeographicalPlaceByNGAImportanceLevel. Instances of #$ModeratelyImportantGeographicalPlace are of moderate importance according to the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US.")
(comment HighlyImportantGeographicalPlace "An instance of #$GeographicalPlaceByNGAImportanceLevel. Instances of #$HighlyImportantGeographicalPlace are of high importance according to the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US.")
(comment VeryHighlyImportantGeographicalPlace "An instance of #$GeographicalPlaceByNGAImportanceLevel. Instances of #$VeryHighlyImportantGeographicalPlace are of utmost importance according to the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US.")
(comment Sluice "A specialization of both #$FlowControlDevice and #$FluidConduit. Each instance of #$Sluice is a device designed to carry off surplus water from a body of water (see the collection #$BodyOfWater). The flow of water through a sluice is usually regulated by a gate.")
(comment UnderspecifiedPrepositionPredicate "A specialization of #$UnderspecifiedPredicate.  Each instance is a predicate used to provide temporary or stub-like semantics to prepositions encountered during parsing.")
(comment underspecifiedPredicateForPreposition "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate that relates an instance of #$UnderspecifiedPrepositionPredicate to the preposition that the predicate is, by definition, used to supply underspecified, or stub semantics for.")
(comment maximumMotionInDirection "<code>(#$maximumMotionInDirection EVENT SUBEVENT DIRECTION)</code> means that in the subevent <code>SUBEVENT</code> of
<code>EVENT</code>, the object that is the primary object moving in
<code>SUBEVENT</code> moves a distance in direction
<code>DIRECTION</code> that is no less than the distance the object moves
in that direction in any other subevent of <code>EVENT</code>.")
(comment givenInProblem "(#$givenInProblem QUESTON SENT) means that the in the question QUESTION, certain points are given as part of the statement of QUESTION (typically in terms of setting up a hypothetical situation, or providing other background information) -- furthermore, among these points is the proposition expressed by SENT.")
(comment Spillway "A specialization of #$FluidConduit. Each instance of #$Spillway is a conduit through which surplus water flows over or around a dam (see the collection #$Dam).")
(comment Island-LandTied "A specialization of #$Island. Each instance of #$Island-LandTied is a coastal island connected to the mainland by levees, barrier beaches, or dikes.")
(comment PalmTreeReserve "A specialization of #$NatureReserve. Each instance of #$PalmTreeReserve is a use-controlled area of land containing palm trees.")
(comment NetForceQuantity "A #$QuantityType and a sub-collection of #$ForceQuantity.  A #$NetForceQuantity represents the magnitude component of a #$NetForceVector (q.v.).")
(comment JOGMapFn "<code>(#$JOGMapFn SHEET-ID)</code> denotes the #$JointOperationsGraphic sheet identified by the ID string <code>SHEET-ID</code>. See also the corresponding predicate #$idStringForJOGMap.")
(comment DrivingTimeBetweenFn "#$DrivingTimeBetweenFn is an evaluatable function which uses Google Maps to calculate the approximate time it takes to drive between two geographical places.<code>(#$DrivingTimeBetweenFn PLACE1 PLACE2)</code> is evaluated by querying Cyc for the lat/long coordinates of <code>PLACE1</code> and <code>PLACE2</code>, using them to construct a URL targeted at the Google Maps website, and extracting the approximate elapsed time from the resulting html.")
(comment drivingTimeBetween "The predicate corresponding to #$DrivingTimeBetweenFn. <code>(#$drivingTimeBetween PLACE1 PLACE2 DURATION)</code> means that according to the Google Maps website, the approximate driving time between <code>PLACE1</code> and <code>PLACE2</code> is <code>DURATION</code>.")
(comment pegExtractedContents-CycL "<code>(#$pegExtractedContents-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> means that <code>CYCL</code> is a correct CycL representation of the content (be it a correct translation or a presupposition) of the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.")
(comment PresuppositionHypothesisMicrotheory "A specialization of #$HypotheticalContext.  Instances are microtheories that contain the assertions purporting to identify presupposed content of an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.")
(comment presuppositionHypothesisMtForPeg "<code>(#$presuppositionHypothesisMtForPeg MT PEG)</code> identifies <code>PEG</code> as the instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg for which <code>MT</code> is an instance of
 #$PresuppositionHypothesisMicrotheory -- I microtheory that purports to identify presuppositions in <code>PEG</code>.")
(comment presupposedContents-CycL "<code>(#$presupposedContents-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL expression <code>CYCL</code> is a correct representation of some of the presupposed content of <code>PEG</code>, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.  Compare with #$pegInterpretation-CycL.")
(comment candidatePresupposedContents-CycL "<code>(#$candidatePresupposedContents-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL expression <code>CYCL</code> is one of the CycL representations of presupposed content of the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that is/has been under consideration.  Compare with #$candidatePegInterpretations-CycL.")
(comment SulfurSpring "A specialization of #$Spring-WaterSource.  Each instance of #$SulfurSpring is a spring from which a small stream of sulphur ground water flows.")
(comment Ramp-Undersea "An instance of #$UnderseaTerrainFeatureType, and a specialization of both #$Ramp and #$Slope-LowGradient. Each instance of #$Ramp-Undersea is a gently sloping, undersea piece of terrain connecting areas of different elevation.")
(comment SatelliteFacility "An instance of #$ExistingObjectType and a specialization of #$Facility-Generic.  Each instance of #$SatelliteFacility is a facility for tracking and communicating with orbiting satellites (see the collection #$Satellite-Device).")
(comment DeterminingTheBestAnswerFromASetOfChoices "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$DeterminingTheBestAnswerFromASetOfChoices
is an event in which an agent determines the best answer from a set of choices.")
(comment termToSolveFor "(#$termToSolveFor Q-SPEC SENT TERM) means that in the context of the query Q-SPEC, the term to be solved for in inference is TERM, a term that occurs in SENT.  This predicate is intended to be used together with #$querySentenceOfQuery (q.v.).")
(comment PhysicsProblem-PotentialAndKineticEnergy "A specialization of #$PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem-PotentialAndKineticEnergy is a physics problem involving the concepts of potential energy and kinetic energy.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-WorkEnergyTheorem "A specialization of #$PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem-WorkEnergyTheorem is a physics problem involving the work-energy theorem (aka the work-energy principle), i.e. the principle that net work done on an object (in an event) equals the change in the kinetic energy of that object (in that event).")
(comment NewtonianPhysicsMt "#$NewtonianPhysicsMt is the context in which all of the laws of the scientific theory of mechanics developed originally by #$IsaacNewton are true.")
(comment PeopleDataMt "A #$DataMicrotheory for assertions about individual instances of #$Person who are relatively well-known, but don't belong in any of the more focused \"people data\" microtheories (e.g. #$HistoricalPeopleDataMt or #$LiteraryPeopleDataMt).  
<p>
Assertions about more obscure persons should generally be put in the proprietary specialization-mt, #$NonFamousPeopleDataMt (unless another, more focused microtheory is appropriate).
<p>
Alamanac-like information about whole <i>classes</i> of people can be found in #$PeopleMt.  The relationship between #$PeopleDataMt and #$PeopleMt is analogous to that between #$OrganizationDataMt and #$OrganizationMt.")
(comment extractedNounPhrases "A specialization of #$associatedPegPhrases. <code>(#$extractedNounPhrases CIS STRING)</code> means that the string <code>STRING</code> is a noun phrase that occurs in <code>CIS</code>, an instance of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure.")
(comment extractedNounCompounds "A specialization of #$associatedPegPhrases. <code>(#$extractedNounCompounds CIS STRING)</code> means that the string <code>STRING</code> is a noun compound that occurs in <code>CIS</code>, an instance of #$ContextualizedInformationStructure.")
(comment Watercourse "A specialization of #$FlowPath.  Each instance of #$Watercourse is either a natural channel produced by flowing water (for example, each instance of the collection #$Stream), or an artifact designed to carry flowing water (for example, some instances of #$Canal).")
(disjointWith BodyOfWater (CollectionDifferenceFn Watercourse BodyOfWater))
(comment GravitationalForceQuantity "A #$QuantityType and a sub-collection of #$ForceQuantity.  A #$GravitationalForceQuantity represents the magnitude component of a #$GravitationalForceVector (q.v.).")
(comment (QPQuantityFn GravitationalForceQuantity) "((#$QPQuantityFn #$GravitationalForceQuantity) OBJ1 OBJ2) denotes the quantity of gravitational force that OBJ1 is exerting on OBJ2.")
(comment (QPQuantityFn AngularDistance) "((#$QPQuantityFn #$AngularDistance) A B) denotes the <i>smallest</i> #$angleBetween A and B.")
(comment Waterworks "A specialization of #$Facility-Generic. Each instance of #$Waterworks is a facility whose primary function is to supply drinkable water to consumers by pumping water from some water source, and then applying some water purification equipment to make it potable.")
(comment Sill-TerrainFeature "A specialization of #$LandTopographicalFeature. Each instance of #$Sill-TerrainFeature is the low part of a saddle (see the collection #$Saddle-TerrainFeature) separating basins (see the collection #$Basin-Topographical).")
(comment ExperimentStation "A specialization of #$ResearchFacility. Each instance of #$ExperimentStation is a research facility in which experiments (see the collection #$Experimenting-Empirical) are carried out.")
(comment Degree-DMS "#$Degree-DMS is used in conjunction with #$Minute-DMS and #$Second-DMS to represent angular distances (see #$AngularDistance) in <i>degree-minute-second</i> format, as opposed to <i>decimal degree</i> format. In this format, an angular distance is expressed as <code>(#$Degree-DMS D (#$Minute-DMS M (#$Second-DMS S)))</code> where <code>D</code> is an integer, <code>M</code> is an integer between 0 and 59, and <code>S</code> is a real number between 0 and 60.")
(comment Minute-DMS "#$Minute-DMS is used in conjunction with #$Degree-DMS and #$Second-DMS to represent angular distances (see #$AngularDistance) in <i>degree-minute-second</i> format, as opposed to <i>decimal degree</i> format. In this format, an angular distance is expressed as <code>(#$Degree-DMS D (#$Minute-DMS M (#$Second-DMS S)))</code> where <code>D</code> is an integer, <code>M</code> is an integer between 0 and 59, and <code>S</code> is a real number between 0 and 60.")
(comment Second-DMS "#$Second-DMS is used in conjunction with #$Degree-DMS and #$Minute-DMS to represent angular distances (see #$AngularDistance) in <i>degree-minute-second</i> format, as opposed to <i>decimal degree</i> format. In this format, an angular distance is expressed as <code>(#$Degree-DMS D (#$Minute-DMS M (#$Second-DMS S)))</code> where <code>D</code> is an integer, <code>M</code> is an integer between 0 and 59, and <code>S</code> is a real number between 0 and 60.")
(comment InfiniteSetOrCollection "A specialization of #$NonEmptySetOrCollection.  A set or collection <code>CLASS</code> is an instance of #$InfiniteSetOrCollection if and only if it has a infinite number of elements.  That is, the #$cardinality of <code>CLASS</code> is some #$TransfiniteCardinal (q.v.).  
<p>
Cf. #$FiniteSetOrCollection.")
(comment ArcMinute "A specialization of #$AngularDistance. #$ArcMinutes are angular distances that are greater than or equal to (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0) and strictly less than (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 1). See in particular the function #$Minute-DMS which is used to denote instances of #$ArcMinute.")
(comment ArcSecond "A specialization of #$AngularDistance. #$ArcSeconds are angular distances that are greater than or equal to (#$Minute-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0) and strictly less than (#$Minute-UnitOfAngularMeasure 1). See in particular the function #$Second-DMS which is used to denote instances of #$ArcSecond.")
(comment Tongue-UnderseaTerrainFeature "A specialization of both #$LandTopographicalFeature and #$UnderwaterLocation. Each instance of #$Tongue-UnderseaTerrainFeature is a long and thin extension of a flat sea floor into a higher feature.")
(comment TalusSlope "A specialization of #$Slope-HighGradient.  Each instance of #$TalusSlope is a steep, concave slope formed by the accumulation of loose rock fragments at the base of a steep slope.")
(comment HistoricalSite "A specialization of both #$GeographicalThing and #$HistoricTemporalThing.  An #$HistoricalSite is a location of notable historical interest.")
(comment pcwIdentifies "(#$pcwIdentifies <code>PCW</code> <code>THING</code>) means
that <code>PCW</code> contains an identifying reference to
<code>THING</code>.  More precisely, it means that each
#$AbstractInformationStructure which expresses the full content of
<code>PCW</code> (see #$infoStructureOfCW) contains a component
structure which identifies <code>THING</code> -- i.e., either a
#$nameString or uniquely-denoting description which picks out
<code>THING</code>.")
(comment Cave "A specialization of #$Underground and #$CavityWithWalls.  Each instance of #$Cave is a natural, hollow passage under the surface of the Earth, typically with an opening to the surface.")
(comment CaveWithMultiplePortalsToSurface "A specialization of #$Cave.  Each instance of #$CaveWithMultiplePortalsToSurface is a cave with at least two openings to the surface.")
(comment InspectionFacility "A specialization of #$Facility-Generic.  Each instance of #$InspectionFacility is a facility where people, goods, or vehicles are inspected.")
(comment WhalingStation "A specialization of #$Facility-Construct.  Each instance of #$WhalingStation is a facility where whales are butchered, and their oil extracted.")
(comment IrrigationSystem "A specialization of #$PathArtifactSystem. Each instance of #$IrrigationSystem is a network of irrigation ditches, and also contains either a reservoir, canal, pump, well, or drain.")
(comment HangingValley "A specialization of #$Valley. Each instance of #$HangingValley is a valley whose lower end or floor is notably higher than the valley or shore to which it leads.")
(comment pastTense-Universal "(#$pastTense-Universal WORD STRING) means that STRING is the simple-aspect past-tense verb form of WORD for all person-number combinations.  The regular simple past is formed from the infinitive verb form with an `-ed' suffix.  Since regular forms are generated by the morphology component, verbs in the Cyc lexicon should have a #$pastTense-Universal entry only if they are irregular.  Regular example: `helped'. Irregular example: `ate'.")
(comment FreeTradeZone "A specialization of both #$Place-NonAgent and #$GeographicalThing. Each instance of #$FreeTradeZone is a place in or adjacent to a port of entry (for example, a seaport or airport) where foreign goods may be received and shipped without being subject to customs duty.")
(comment reflexiveOn "A binary #$MetaPredicate that relates a binary predicate to a collection.  <code>(#$reflexiveOn PRED COL)</code> means that <code>PRED</code> relates any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> to itself, i.e. that <code>(PRED INST INST)</code> is true.")
(comment Snowfield "A specialization of #$SnowCoveredArea. Each instance of #$Snowfield is an area of permanent snow and ice, and forms an accumulation area for a glacier.")
(comment MaritimeSchool "A specialization of #$SchoolInstitution. Each instance of #$MaritimeSchool is a school whose curriculum is focused on maritime topics such as navigation, maritime transportation, and marine ecology.")
(comment FinalizingKnowledgeAcquiredByTextLearner "The collection of knowledge-finalization events performed by the TextLearner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram).")
(comment FinalizingKnowledgeAcquiredByAProgram "The collection of events in which knowledge that has been acquired by a knowledge-acquisition program (e.g., #$UIA-CycProgram) is \"finalized\" -- i.e., vetted and refined and made a permanent part of the relevant knowledge repository.  Such events frequently have both manual and automatic steps.")
(comment sentenceAsserted "<code>(#$sentenceAsserted ASSERTING SENTENCE)</code> means that in the event <code>ASSERTING</code> (an instance of #$AssertingAnAssertionToACycKB), the sentence asserted, i.e., the sentence that is the input to the assertion event that yeilds a KB modifcation (typically an assertion), is <code>SENTENCE</code>.  Note that due to issues of canonicalization, the #$sentenceAsserted is not necessarily the #$assertionAsserted (the resultant instance of #$CycLAssertion).")
(comment AssertingTheResultsOfACycQuery "The collection of events consisting of one or more instances of #$AssertingASentenceToACycKB where the #$sentenceAsserted for each such event is a binding found by running a query, or a conjunction of such bindings.")
(comment FinalizingKnowledgeAboutConceptsExtractedByTextLearner "A specialization of 
#$FinalizingKnowledgeAcquiredByAProgram, each instance of which is performed by TextLearner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) and involves 1) identifying constants and re-reifying constants representing concepts extracted while reading text, and 2) finding and re-asserting (in an order amenable to wff-checking) all the assertions about those concepts (if there are any).")
(comment FinalizingKnowledgeAboutPCWContentExtractedByTextLearner "A specialization of 
#$FinalizingKnowledgeAcquiredByAProgram, each instance of which is performed by TextLearner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) and involves  finding and re-asserting (in an order amenable to wff-checking) all the assertions about the content of sentences read by TextLearner.")
(disjointWith Mountain Ramp)
(disjointWith Chimney Spillway)
(disjointWith Spillway Pipeline)
(disjointWith IrrigationSystem CustomarySystemOfLinks-Comm)
(disjointWith TranslationWithoutChangeOfOrientation Movement-Rotation)
(comment TranslationWithoutChangeOfOrientation "A specialization of #$Movement-TranslationEvent. Each instance of #$TranslationWithoutChangeOfOrientation is a movement event in which the orientation of the primary object moving does _not_ change.")
(comment VerbPhraseExpressionPeg "The collection of all instances of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that are used to represent verb phrases.")
(comment ChangingWeaponMode "A specialization of #$ChangingDeviceState. Each instance of #$ChangingWeaponMode is an event in which a weapon with multiple modes of use is switched from one mode to another. Instances of #$ChangingWeaponMode include changing a firearm from semi-automatic to fully automatic fire, and changing the orientation of a dagger from one suitable for slashing at an opponent to one suitable for throwing at an opponent.")
(comment pcwIdentifiesRolePlayer "(#$pcwIdentifiesRolePlayer
<code>PCW</code> <code>SIT</code> <code>ROLE</code>) means that
<code>PCW</code> identifies some item as playing <code>ROLE</code> in
<code>SIT</code>.  That is, <code>PCW</code> contains an identifying
reference to <code>ITEM</code> (in the sense of the predicate
#$pcwIdentifies, qv.), and also asserts the #$Proposition that
(<code>ROLE</code> <code>SIT</code> <code>ITEM</code>).  Note that
(#$pcwIdentifiesRolePlayer <code>PCW</code> <code>SIT</code>
<code>ROLE</code>) does not entail (<code>ROLE</code> <code>SIT</code>
<code>ITEM</code>), i.e., the identification could be mistaken.")
(comment DisambiguationStrategyFromPredAndRewardedArgsFn "A #$ReifiableFunction and #$IndividualDenotingFunction.  An expression of the form (#$DisambiguationStrategyFromPredAndRewardedArgsFn PRED SET-OF-INTGERS) denotes an instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategy that is based on the semantic relation expressed by the #$Predicate PRED.  Further, the scoring strategy will assign points to a #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory based on its hypothesized interpretation (call it, CYCL and see #$mtFocalContent and #$pegInterpretation-CycL) being able to enter into a provable formula that has PRED is its arg0 and CYCL as its Nth argument, where N is an element of SET-OF-INTEGERS.")
(comment ZoomingInOnSomething "A specialization of both #$WatchingSomething and #$AidedVisualPerception. Each instance of #$ZoomingInOnSomething is an event in which an agent is watching something through a device such a motion-picture or television camera. During an instance of  #$ZoomingInOnSomething, the agent focuses in on some object in the visual field afforded by this device, making that object larger while keeping it in focus.")
(disjointWith ZoomingInOnSomething ZoomingOutOnSomething)
(comment ZoomingOutOnSomething "A specialization of both #$WatchingSomething and #$AidedVisualPerception. Each instance of #$ZoomingOutOnSomething is an event in which an agent is watching something through a device such a motion-picture or television camera. During an instance of  #$ZoomingOutOnSomething, the agent focuses outward from the initial visual field afforded by this device, making that objects in that field smaller while keeping them in focus.")
(comment subjectExpressionOfVerbalPhraseExpression "<code>(#$subjectExpressionOfVerbalPhraseExpression NP-PEG VP-PEG)</code> identifies <code>NP</code> as the instance of #$NounPhraseExpressionPeg that is the subject of the instance <code>VP</code> of #$VerbPhraseExpressionPeg.")
(comment CopularVerbPhrase "The collection of all verb phrases that are constructed using the main copular verb of the language for that phrase.")
(comment cyclificationOfStringFromParser "A sentence of the form <code>(#$cyclificationOfStringFromParser STRING CYCL PARSER)</code> means that <code>CYCL</code> is one interpretation of <code>STRING</code> according to the Cyclifier and <code>PARSER</code>.")
(comment Person-SupportingObject "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible. Each instance of #$Person-SupportingObject is an object capable of supporting the weight of at least one ordinary human adult.")
(disjointWith Quicksand Person-SupportingObject)
(disjointWith Person-SupportingObject GaseousTangibleThing)
(comment ResettingAfterZoomingInOrOut "A specialization of both #$WatchingSomething and #$AidedVisualPerception. Each instance of #$ResettingAfterZoomingInOrOut is an event in which an agent returns the focus to his or her original field of vision after zooming in or zooming out from that field. See the related collections #$ZoomingInOnSomething and #$ZoomingOutOnSomething.")
(comment adjStrings-NonGraded "A specialization (see #$genlPreds) of #$adjStrings, <code>(#$adjStrings-NonGraded ADJECTIVE FORM)</code> identifies the string <code>FORM</code> as the non-graded (i.e., regular degree or non-gradable) form of the word <code>ADJECTIVE</code>.  For example <code>(#$adjStrings-NonGraded #$Atom-TheWord \"atomic\")</code> means that \"atomic\" is the non-graded adjectival form of #$Atom-TheWord.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-InclinedPlane "A specialization of #$PhysicsProblem..  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem-InclinedPlane is a physics problem involving an inclined plane.")
(comment objectStationary-Generalized "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a #$TemporalThing to a #$PartiallyTangible.  <code>(#$objectStationary-Generalized TT OBJ)</code> means that throughout <code>TT</code>, <code>OBJ</code>, does not move.  An important specialization is the #$ActorSlot #$objectStationary.")
(comment StrategyForPegSIHMRelationFn "An expression of the form (#$StrategyForPegSIHMRelationFn PRED) is used to denote an instance of #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy based on a particular #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory having a particular relationship to a #$LinguisticExpressionPeg.  Specifically, if (PRED PEG SIHM) holds for the #$BinaryPredicate and the #$LinguisticExpressionPeg PEG and the #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory SIHM, then SIHM will receive some score based on its satisfaction of the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy denoted by  (#$StrategyForPegSIHMRelationFn PRED).")
(comment strategyForPegSIHMRelation "The corresponding predicate of the function #$StrategyForPegSIHMRelationFn.  A sentence of the form (#$strategyForPegSIHMRelation STRATEGY PRED) means that the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy STRATEGY is based on the #$BinaryPredicate PRED holding between some instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg and some instance of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory.  That strategy will be used for evaluating the #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory.")
(comment strategyApplicableToHypothesis "A sentence of the form (#$strategyApplicableToHypothesis STRATEGY HYP) means that the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy STRATEGY is relevant to the evaluation of the #$HypotheticalContext HYP.")
(comment StrategyScoreForHypothesisFn "An #$EvaluatableFunction.  An expression of the form (#$StrategyScoreForHypothesisFn OPTIMIZED-FORM HYP-MT) returns a #$RealNumber that is the score assigned to the #$HypotheticalContext HYP-MT based on its satisfaction of the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy, whose optimized settings are represented in the #$Microtheory OPTIMIZED-FORM (see #$optimizedStrategyForm, the relationship between the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy and the #$Microtheory that contains its optimized settings).")
(comment SpaceTransportationEvent "The collection of all space transportation events.  For each instance TRAN of #$SpaceTransportationEvent, at least a part of the #$Trajectory of #$objectMoving in TRAN must be in the outer space.  Note that #$SpaceTransportationEvent is different from (#$TransportViaFn #$Spacecraft) because the former emphasizes where to find the #$Trajectory while the latter emphasizes what is used as a tool, not to mention instances of the former do not have to have parts on Earth or inside our atmosphere.")
(comment parserSpecificationParseCategory "<code>(#$parserSpecificationParseCategory SPECIFICATION SPEECH-PART)</code> means that in the parser specification <code>SPECIFICATION</code>, only parses that fall into the category <code>SPEECH-PART</code>, an instance of #$SpeechPart, are returned.  For example, if a parse of \"wet\" is performed according to <code>SPECIFICATION</code> such that <code>(#$parserSpecificationParseCategory SPECIFICATION #$Verb)</code> only verbal semantics (as opposed to adjectival, for example) for \"wet\" would be returned.")
(comment parserSpecificationPOSPredicate "<code>(#$parserSpecificationPOSPredicate SPECIFICATION SPEECH-PART-PRED)</code> means that in the parser specification <code>SPECIFICATION</code>, only parses that are consistent with <code>SPEECH-PART-PRED</code>, an instance of #$SpeechPartPredicate, are returned.  For example, if a parse of \"green\" is performed according to <code>SPECIFICATION</code> such that <code>(#$parserSpecificationPOSPredicate SPECIFICATION #$singular)</code>, only semantics licensed by a singular use of \"green\"(as opposed to those for #$regularDegree, for example) would be returned.")
(comment parserSpecificationLexicalMicrotheory "<code>(#$parserSpecificationLexicalMicrotheory SPECIFICATION MT)</code> means that when a parser is called according to the parsing specification <code>SPECIFICATION</code>, lexical information is accessed from <code>MT</code>, an instance of #$Microtheory. For example, if \"attest\" is parsed according to SPECIFICATION such that <code>(#$parserSpecificationLexicalMicrotheory SPECIFICATION #$GermanLexicalMt)</code>, only entries for #$Attest-TheWord found in that Mt (as opposed to #$EnglishMt, for example) will be considered.")
(comment programSpecificationMaxTime "<code>(#$programSpecificationMaxTime SPECIFICATION TIME)</code> means that when a program is called according to the specification <code>SPECIFICATION</code>, it is set to run for a length of time no longer than <code>TIME</code>, an instance of #$Time-Quantity.")
(comment Forever-Quantity "The instance of #$Time-Quantity that is, by definition, the longest in duration.  See also #$Always-TimeInterval, which is an instance of the collection of intervals of length #$Forever-Quantity.")
(comment TerroristAttackTacticType "A specialization of #$AttackType.  Each instance of this
collection is a collection of action types that are typically performed by terrorist agents as attacks.  For example, #$KillingPerson is not a specialization of #$TerroristAttacks, but it is typical of terrorist agents that their attacks are often instances of #$KillingPerson..")
(comment AngularDistanceBetweenTwoVectors "Each #$AngularDistanceBetweenTwoVectors is an #$AngularDistance that occurs as the third argument in a true #$angularDistanceBetweenVectors statement.")
(comment candidateExtractedContents-CycL "<code>(#$candidateExtractedContents-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL expression <code>CYCL</code> is one of the CycL representations of content, either presupposed or directly contained, of the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg that is/has been under consideration.  Compare with #$candidatePegInterpretations-CycL and #$candidatePresupposedContents-CycL.")
(comment consistentCandidateExtractedContents-CycL "<code>(#$consistentCandidateExtractedContents-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL semantics <code>CYCL</code> assigned to the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg as a candidate interpretation of its content has been validated through inference -- for example, #$True is returned if <code>(#$unknownSentence CYCL)</code> is asked and if <code>(#$unknownSentence (#$not CYCL))</code> is asked.")
(comment provedCandidateExtractedContents-CycL "<code>(#$provedCandidateExtractedContents-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL semantics <code>CYCL</code>, a candidate interpretation (a translation or a representation of its presupposed content) of the content (presupposed or  assigned to the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg, has been validated through inference -- i.e., <code>CYCL</code> has been asked in the content model (see #$contentModelFocalPeg) of <code>PEG</code> and #$True was returned.")
(comment contentHypothesisMtsForPeg "<code>(#$contentHypothesisMtsForPeg MT PEG)</code> means that <code>MT</code> is an instance of #$HypotheticalContext whose focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) is a hypothesis about the content of <code>PEG</code>, an instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance.")
(comment ConsistentHypothesisMicrotheory "The collection of instances of #$HypotheticalContext whose focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) has been deemed consistent by the Cyc inference engine.")
(comment HypothesisMicrotheory-ValidatedInInference "The collection of instances of #$HypotheticalContext whose focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) has been deemed true by the Cyc inference engine.")
(comment sihmSupportForPresupposedContent "<code>(#$sihmSupportForPresupposedContent MT PEG CYCL)</code> means that the CycL expression <code>CYCL</code> reflects possible presupposed content (see #$candidatePresupposedContents-CycL) of the instance <code>PEG</code> of
#$LinguisticExpressionPeg that was extracted due to focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) of the instance <code>MT</code> of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory.")
(comment prefersToRelnA "<code>(#$prefersToRelnA AGENT PREF-COL DISPREF-COL RELN)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> prefers to be in the relation <code>RELN</code> (expressed by a #$BinaryPredicate) to some element of the collection <code>PREF-COL</code> over some element of the collection <code>DISPREF-COL</code>.")
(comment playsRoleInSitTypeEnsuresRelnToA "<code>(#$playsRoleInSitTypeEnsuresRelnToA ROLE SIT-TYPE AGENT COL RELN)</code> means that when <code>AGENT</code> plays the role <code>ROLE</code> in an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, the upshot is that <code>AGENT</code> comes to bear the relation <code>RELN</code> (expressed by a #$BinaryPredicate) to an instance of <code>COL</code>.")
(comment offerInExchangeFor-PlaysRoleInSitType "(#$offerInExchangeFor-PlaysRoleInSitType <code>OFFER</code>
<code>OBJ</code> <code>SIT-TYPE</code> <code>ROLE</code>) means that
<code>OFFER</code> has a conditional form, in which something is offered to the #$recipientOfInfo-Intended in exchange for some action on her part.  More specifically, what is asked in
exchange is that the #$recipientOfInfo-Intended bring about some
instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> in which <code>OBJ</code> plays
<code>ROLE</code>. For example, if <code>AGT1</code> offers to wash
<code>AGT2</code> 's car in exchange for <code>AGT2</code>
mowing <code>AGT1</code>'s lawn, then
(#$offerInExchangeFor-PlaysRoleInSitType <code>OFFER</code>
<code>AGT2</code> #$MowingGrass #$performedBy)")
(comment offerInExchangeFor-NotRoleInSitType "(#$offerInExchangeFor-NotRoleInSitType <code>OFFER</code>
<code>OBJ</code> <code>SIT-TYPE</code> <code>ROLE</code>) means that
<code>OFFER</code> has a conditional form, in which something is
offered to the #$recipientOfInfo-Intended in exchange for her
refraining from an action of a certain sort.  More specifically, what
is asked in exchange is that the #$recipientOfInfo-Intended refrain
(for some period of time) from bringing about any instance of
<code>SIT-TYPE</code> in which <code>OBJ</code> plays
<code>ROLE</code>. For example, if <code>AGT1</code> offers to give
<code>AGT2</code> candy in exchange for <code>AGT2</code> ceasing to
complain, then (#$offerInExchangeFor-NotRoleInSitType
<code>OFFER</code> <code>AGT2</code> #$Complaining #$senderOfInfo)")
(comment communicationDeclined "(#$communicationDeclined <code>DECLINING</code>
<code>COMMUNICATION</code>) means that, in <code>DECLINING</code>,
the #$senderOfInfo declines or rejects the content of
<code>COMMUNICATION</code>. The types of communications that may be
declined in this sense include instances of
#$Offering-CommunicationAct, #$Proposing-CommunicationAct, and
#$Requesting-CommunicationAct.")
(disjointWith ConsistentHypothesisMicrotheory HypothesisMicrotheory-InvalidatedInInference)
(comment HypothesisMicrotheory-InvalidatedInInference "The collection of instances of #$HypotheticalContext whose focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) has been deemed false by the Cyc inference engine.")
(comment ContentHypothesisMicrotheory-ContentValidatedInInference "The collection of instances of #$ContentHypothesisMicrotheory that hypothesize <code>(#$pegExtractedContents-CycL PEG CYCL)</code> such that the CycL sentence <code>CYCL</code> has been proved True by the Cyc inference engine.")
(comment ContentHypothesisMicrotheory "The specialization of #$HypotheticalContext whose instances all hypothesize an interpretation or presupposed content of some contextualized information structure.  More precisely, every instance of this collection is such that its focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) hypothesize <code>(PRED STRUCTURE CYCL)</code> for some specialization (see #$genlPreds) <code>PRED</code> of #$pegExtractedContents-CycL.")
(comment sentenceTruthBonus "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategyParameter.")
(comment GeographicalThing "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized. This collection may be thought of as consisting of all the entities which are localizable within the context of a geography, in the sense that they might plausibly be represented on a map.  This includes both #$PartiallyTangible entities like #$GeographicalRegions, and also entities that may be wholly #$Intangible, like territorial borders and boundaries, #$LatitudeLines and #$LongitudeLines, trajectories of missiles and courses of ships, and the #$EarthsEquator.")
(comment hasReasonToPlayRole "(#$hasReasonToPlayRole <code>AGENT</code>
<code>SIT-TYPE</code> <code>ROLE</code>) means that <code>AGENT</code>
has a reason or motive to bring about an instance of
<code>SIT-TYPE</code> in which <code>AGENT</code> plays
<code>ROLE</code>.  To specify that a particular #$Situation or true
#$Proposition has this motivational force, use #$providesMotiveFor.")
(comment (QPQuantityFn EnergyQuantity) "A #$UnaryFunction that takes a #$PartiallyTangible and returns its #$EnergyQuantity.")
(comment mostSpecificSpeechPartPreds "A sentence of the form (#$mostSpecificSpeechPartPreds SPEECH-PART PRED) states that the #$SpeechPart SPEECH-PART bears the relation #$speechPartPreds to the #$SpeechPartPredicate PRED and that there is no other #$SpeechPartPredicate to which SPEECH-PART bears that relation and bears the relation #$genlPreds to PRED.")
(comment hasReasonForRoleInSitTypeWithRelationTo "(#$hasReasonForRoleInSitTypeWithRelationTo
<code>AGENT</code> <code>ROLE</code> <code>SIT-TYPE</code>
<code>REL</code> <code>THING</code>) means that <code>AGENT</code> has
a reason or motive to play <code>ROLE</code> in an instance of
<code>SIT-TYPE</code> which also stands in relation
<code>REL</code> to <code>THING</code>.  For example, if
<code>AGENT</code> has a reason to travel to New York City, then we
can say (#$hasReasonForRoleInSitTypeWithRelationTo <code>AGENT</code>
#$primaryObjectMoving #$Travel-TripEvent #$toLocation
#$CityOfNewYorkNY).")
(comment GeographicalSpaceRegion "A specialization of both #$SpaceRegion-Empirical and #$GeographicalThing-Intangible-FixedLocation (qq.v.).  Instances of #$GeographicalSpaceRegion are pieces of space that are relevant to geographical reasoning. They are intangible points, lines, surfaces or three-dimensional areas that have a fixed location with respect to the region descriped by a map.  Specializations of #$GeographicalSpaceRegion include #$GeographicalPlace-0D, #$GeographicalPlace-1D, #$GeographicalPlace-2D and #$GeographicalPlace-3D.")
(disjointWith (SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn Animal hasPhysicalPartTypes Eye) (CollectionDifferenceFn Animal (SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn Animal hasPhysicalPartTypes Eye)))
(comment vertexOfPolyhedron "A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a #$Polyhedron to its \"corners\". <code>(#$vertexOfPolyhedron POINT POLYHEDRON)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is a vertex #$Point of one of the #$LineStrings that constitute the boundary of a polygon that is a side of <code>POLYHEDRON</code>.")
(comment GeographicalPlace-3D "A specialization of #$GeographicalThing-Intangible, #$GeographicalSpaceRegion and #$ThreeDimensionalThing  (qq.v.).  This is the collection of three dimensional intangible geographical things.  Instances of #$GeographicalPlace-3D delineate space in a geographical context.  Examples include the intangible space occupied by the Earth's atmosphere and the spatial extent of an underground region to which a particular company has mineral rights. A specialization of #$GeographicalThing-Intangible, #$GeographicalSpaceRegion and #$ThreeDimensionalThing (qq.v.).")
(comment incidentInSpaceRegion "A binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and an (inverse) specialization of #$spatiallySubsumes and #$parts that relates space points to the space regions they are in.  <code>(#$incidentInSpaceRegion POINT REGION)</code> means that<code> POINT</code> is incident in <code>REGION</code>.  
<p>
#$incidentInSpaceRegion is introduced as a new primitive relation in an extension of #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory (where it is called <i>IN</i>), and is used there to help define #$connectedSpaceRegions and #$partOfSpaceRegion in terms of #$Point and #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion.  Thus, two regions connect to one another if and only if there is a point that is incident in both; and one region is part of another if and only if all points incident in the first are incident in the second.
<p>
See also the specializations #$incidentInInteriorOfSpaceRegion and #$incidentInBoundaryOfSpaceRegion.")
(comment vertexOfGeometricallyDescribableThing "The most general predicate relating a vertex to the thing on which that vertex is a point. <code>(#$vertexOfGeometricallyDescribableThing POINT THING)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is a vertex of <code>THING</code>.")
(comment MoviePredicate "A #$MoviePredicate is a predicate at least one of whose arguments is required to be a #$Movie-CW.")
(comment PointIn3DCoordinateSystemFn "A #$QuaternaryFunction that takes a #$ThreeDimensionalCoordinateSystem and three #$ScalarIntervals, and returns a #$Point.  <code>(#$PointIn3DCoordinateSystemFn SYSTEM X Y Z)</code> denotes the point in SYSTEM corresponding to the values X, Y, and Z along SYSTEM's x, y, and z axes, respectively.")
(comment pathTraversableFromToBy "An instance of #$QuaternaryPredicate. <code>(#$pathTraversableFromToBy PATH FROM TO TYPE)</code> implies that (i)the #$Path-Simple <code>PATH</code> connects <code>FROM</code> and <code>TO</code> (so that <code>(#$pathBetween PATH FROM TO)</code>) holds), and (ii) <code>PATH</code> is capable of serving as a pathway for instances of TYPE between <code>FROM</code> and <code>TO</code>. 
<p>
Note that this predicate is _not_ commutative in its second and third arguments, since a type of movement from one location to another might not be reversible. For example, one can get to the top of a stairway from the bottom, but not to the bottom from the top, by ascending that stairway (see the collection #$AscendingStairs).")
(comment ContentHypothesisMicrotheory-SentenceLevel "The specialization of #$HypotheticalContext whose instances all hypothesize an interpretation or presupposed content of some contextualized information structure.  More precisely, every instance of this collection is such that its focal content (see #$mtFocalContent) hypothesize <code>(PRED STRUCTURE CYCL)</code> for some specialization (see #$genlPreds) <code>PRED</code> of #$pegExtractedContents-CycL, such that <code>(#$quotedIsa CYCL #$CycLSentence)</code>. ")
(comment GettingFreeFromControl "A subcollection of
#$GainingFreedom.  In each instance of #$GettingFreeFromControl, the
agent who #$losesControl does not relinquish her previous
control voluntarily.  Examples include both cases in which the
#$objectOfControlTransfer escapes confinement or control on her own,
and cases in which she is liberated by a third party.")
(disjointWith TransferringCustodyOfAPerson GainingFreedom)
(comment TransferringControlOfAnAgent "#$TransferringControlOfAnAgent is the collection of
#$TransferOfControl events in which the #$objectOfControlTransfer is
an #$Agent-Generic.  The agent to whom control passes may be the
transferred agent herself (see #$GainingFreedom), or may be another
agent (see #$TransferringCustodyOfAPerson).")
(comment slotValueRangeWRTCollection "A ternary predicate that relates a #$BinaryPredicate to a #$Collection to a #$Set-Mathematical.  <code>(#$slotValueRangeWRTCollection PRED COL SET)</code> means that <code>SET</code> is the set of all (and only those) things to which some instance of <code>COL</code> is related by <code>PRED</code>.  That is, a thing <code>OBJ</code> is an element of <code>SET</code> if and only if there is at least one instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(PRED INST OBJ)</code> holds.  For example, the value-range of #$cityInState for #$USCity is the set of US states.
<p>
See also #$slotInverseValueRangeWRTCollection.")
(comment SlotValueRangeWRTCollectionFn "A binary function that takes a #$BinaryPredicate and a #$Collection to a #$Set-Mathematical.  (#$SlotValueRangeWRTCollectionFn <b>PRED COL SET</b>) is the set <b>SET</b> of all (and only those) things to which some instance of <b>COL</b> is related by <b>PRED</b>.  That is, a thing <b>OBJ</b> is an element of <b>SET</b> if and only if there is at least one instance <b>INST</b> of <b>COL</b> such that <b>(PRED INST OBJ)</b> holds.  Note that all instances of <b>COL</b> must be admitted-arguments for the first argument-position of <b>PRED</b>; otherwise #$SlotValueRangeWRTCollectionFn is undefined for <b>PRED</b> and <b>COL</b>.
<p>
For example, the value-range of #$cityInState for #$USCity is the set of US states.
<p>
See the corresponding predicate #$slotValueRangeWRTCollection.  Also see #$SlotInverseValueRangeWRTCollectionFn.")
(comment slotInverseValueRangeWRTCollection "A ternary predicate that relates a #$BinaryPredicate to a #$Collection to a #$Set-Mathematical.  <code>(#$slotInverseValueRangeWRTCollection PRED COL SET)</code> means that <code>SET</code> is the set of all (and only those) things that are related to some instance of <code>COL</code> by <code>PRED</code>.  That is, a thing <code>OBJ</code> is an element of <code>SET</code> if and only if there is at least one instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(PRED OBJ INST)</code> holds.  For example, the inverse value-range of #$cityInState for #$State-UnitedStates is the set of US cities.
<p>
See also #$slotValueRangeWRTCollection.")
(disjointWith PassingThroughAnInitiallyObstructedPortal PassingThroughAnInitiallyUnobstructedPortal)
(comment csqlToSQLTranslationFormat "This predicate is used by SKSI to translate between CSQL expressions and SQL expressions during query translation. <code>(#$csqlToSQLTranslationFormat CSQL-OPERATOR DB-SERVER FORMAT-STRING LIST)</code> means that the Common Lisp format string <code>FORMAT-STRING</code> is applied to the argument list <code>LIST</code> when translating a CSQL expression using the operator <code>CSQL-OPERATOR</code> into an SQL expression that conforms to the SQL flavor supported by <code>DB-SERVER</code>.")
(comment geographicallyOverlapsCartographicFeatureType "An instance of both #$BinaryPredicate and #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate. <code>(#$geographicallyOverlapsCartographicFeatureType GEO-THING FEATURE-TYPE)</code> means that there is some instance of <code>FEATURE-TYPE</code> that geographically overlaps (see the predicate #$geographicallyOverlaps) <code>GEO-THING</code>.")
(comment ExitingContainerFn "A #$UnaryFunction that takes a #$Container and returns the collection of instances of #$ExitingAContainer in which that container is the #$containerExited.")
(comment PassingThroughPortalFn "A #$UnaryFunction that takes a #$Portal and returns the collection of instances of #$PassingThroughPortal in which that portal is the #$portalPassedThrough.")
(comment RescuingACaptive "A subcollection of #$TransferringControlOfAnAgent.  In each
instance of #$RescuingACaptive, the #$captive in a #$HeldCaptive
situation is freed from the control of her #$captor through the actions of
a third party.  In such events, the erstwhile #$captor does not
relinquish control voluntarily (see also #$GettingFreeFromControl).
Note that the rescued agent may be released immediately (in which case
the event is also an instance of #$GainingFreedom), or she may briefly 
come under the control or custody of her rescuers (as is
sometimes the case with police or military rescues).")
(comment ObjectTypeWRTPredFn "A unary #$CollectionDenotingFunction that takes  a #$PartPredicate to the collection of all (and only those) collections that are object-like with respect to that part-predicate (see #$objectlikeWRTPred).  A collection <code>OBJECTTYPE</code> is an instance of <code>(#$ObjectTypeWRTPredFn PRED)</code> only if the following holds: if <code>OBJ</code> is an instance of <code>OBJECTTYPE</code> and <code>(PRED OBJ OBJPART)</code> holds, then <code>OBJPART</code> is <i>not</i> also an instance of <code>OBJECTTYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$ObjectTypeWRTPredFn #$properSubSituations) is the collection known more tersely as #$SituationalObjectType.")
(comment properTimeSlices "A #$TemporalPartPredicate and a specialization of both #$timeSlices and #$properParts (qq.v.) that relates a temporal thing to any of its \"pure\" proper temporal parts.  <code>(#$properTimeSlices TEMP SLICE)</code> means that <code>SLICE</code> is a proper part of <code>TEMP</code> that is distinguished from <code>TEMP</code> purely in terms of its #$temporalExtent, which is properly subsumed by the temporal extent of <code>TEMP</code> itself.  In other words, <code>SLICE</code> is \"everything there is to\" <code>TEMP</code> during the particular #$TimeInterval that <code>SLICE</code> occupies.  For example, today's noon hour has the interval from 12:00 noon to 12:30 p.m. today as one of its proper time-slices; the first act of a particular performance of \"Hamlet\" is a proper time-slice of that performance of the whole play.")
(comment penetratingObjectType "The predicate corresponding to #$PenetratingObjectFn (q.v.).")
(comment PenetratingObjectFn "A unary #$TermMacroFunction that takes a #$PartiallyTangible object and returns the collection of #$PenetratingSomethings in which that object is the #$objectPenetrated.
<p>
<code>(#$PenetratingObjectFn OBJ)</code> is shorthand for <code>(#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$PenetratingSomething #$objectPenetrated OBJ)</code>.
")
(comment exitingContainerType "This is the predicate corresponding to #$ExitingContainerFn (q.v.).")
(comment passingThroughPortal-Pred "This is the predicate corresponding to #$PassingThroughPortalFn (q.v.).")
(comment defaultParameterSpecificationForProgram "<code>(#$defaultParameterSpecificationForProgram PROGRAM SPEC)</code> means that the parameter specification for <code>PROGRAM</code> in which all parameters have their default values is SPEC.")
(comment DefaultParameterSpecificationFn "<code>(#$DefaultParameterSpecificationFn PROGRAM)</code> denotes the parameter specification for <code>PROGRAM</code> in which all parameters have their default values.")
(comment programSpecifications "<code>(#$programSpecifications PROGRAM SPEC)</code> means that <code>SPEC</code> is a specification for how the computer program <code>PROGRAM</code> (see #$ComputerProgram-CW) should behave.")
(comment EscapingCaptivity "A subcollection of #$GettingFreeFromControl.  In each
instance of #$EscapingCaptivity, the #$captive in a #$HeldCaptive
situation gets free from the control of her #$captor, at least partly through her own actions.")
(comment expressionPegAgreementPredicate "<code>(#$expressionPegAgreementPredicate PEG PRED)</code> means that the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg has the agreement properties of <code>PRED</code>, an instance of #$TermPhrasesConstraintPredicate.  For example, where <code>(#$originalPhrase PEG \"a democracy\")</code>, </code>(#$expressionPegAgreementPredicate PEG #$singular)</code> would hold, as \"a democracy\" is a singular NP.")
(comment ObjectType "The collection of all collections that are \"object-like\" in at least one respect. A collection <code>COL</code> is object-like just in case there is some sense of 'part' according to which any (or nearly any) given proper part of an instance of <code>COL</code> is <i>not</i> itself an instance of <code>COL</code>; when this happens <code>COL</code> is said to be object-like with respect to that sense of 'part'.
<p>
More precisely, for a collection <code>COL</code> to be an instance of #$ObjectType it is sufficient that there be some specialization <code>PARTPRED</code> of #$properParts such that, for any <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code>, if <code>(#$isa OBJ1 COL)</code> and <code>(PARTPRED OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> both hold, then <code>(#$isa OBJ2 COL)</code> does <i>not</i> hold.  (Also sufficient for <code>COL</code>'s being an object-type is that there be some specialization <code>INVPARTPRED</code> of the <i>inverse</i> of #$properParts (see #$genlInverse) such that <code>(INVPARTPRED OBJ2 OBJ1)</code>, with everything else remaining the same as above.)  Note that neither of the above sufficient conditions for <code>COL</code>'s being an object-type is strictly necessary: some exceptions are allowed.  Thus as long as either one of the above conditionals holds in <i>nearly</i> all cases, <code>COL</code> should be considered an instance of #$ObjectType.
<p>
Here are two examples.  Consider #$Automobile.  Take an instance of that, say my car.  Now consider one of the proper #$physicalParts of my car, say the steering wheel.  The steering wheel is <i>not</i> an instance of #$Automobile.  And the same would be true for any proper physical part of any car.  So #$Automobile is an #$ObjectType.  Consider #$CalendarYear. No proper #$timeSlices of a year is itself a year.  So #$CalendarYear is an #$ObjectType.  
<p>
See #$StuffType for the contrasting (though not disjoint) notion of being stuff-like.")
(comment (MakingFn Sauce) "The collection of all events in which somebody makes some #$Sauce.")
(comment AlfredoSauce "All instances of Alfredo Sauce.")
(comment PestoSauce "All instances of Pesto Sauce.")
(comment PastaDish "The collection of all #$FoodComposites in which #$Pasta is a substantial component.")
(comment FettuciniAlfredo "All instances of fettucini alfredo.")
(comment SpaghettiPesto "All instances of spaghetti pesto.")
(comment forwardReificationRule "An instance of #$InferenceSupportedPredicate. <code>(#$forwardReificationRule FUNCTION-TERM RULE-ASSERTION)</code> means that <code>RULE-ASSERTION</code> is a forward rule used by the system to forward-reify NATs constructed from the function term <code>FUNCTION-TERM</code>.")
(comment interestingRoleForSitType "A #$SummaryPredicate.  (#$interestingRoleForSitType SIT-TYPE ROLE) means that in instances of SIT-TYPE it is interesting (in the appropriate context) to know what stands in arg2 of ROLE.")
(comment RussiasOfferToEnrichUraniumForIran "An offer made by Russia to Iran, to enrich uranium for Iran.")
(comment IntelligenceAnalysisTask-Allotment "A specialization of #$Allotment that represents general intelligence analysis taskings.")
(comment controls "<code>(#$controls AGENT THING)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> has effective control over <code>THING</code>.  This does not mean that <code>AGENT</code> is at every moment directly affecting <code>THING</code>, but that <code>AGENT</code> can and will intervene when necessary.  However, it does mean that <code>AGENT</code> must exercise some form of influence over <code>THING</code> at some time.  For instance, in the military, a superior officer stands in this relation to a subordinate under his command.  The officer need not at every moment tell the soldier what to do, but will intervene to correct the subordinate's actions when needed.  However, the officer does at the very least affect the soldier psychologically, or the superior does not have effective control.  
<p>
Normally, when an agent is in control of something, the agent is held responsible for what that thing does, or what happens to it.  Note that #$controls refers to a standing relation between an agent and thing, perhaps as a result of stable social or causal facts.  Note also, however, that the thing controlled might be an <i>event</i> rather than an object, and in such cases the controller only #$controls the event while (i.e. some or all of the time during which) the event is taking place; thus it differs from an actor-slot like #$directingAgent, which continues to hold between a directing agent and the directed event even after that event is finished.  For the relation of controlling an object during a particular action, see the actor-slot #$objectControlled.")
(disjointWith Uranium-Enriched Uranium-Unprocessed)
(disjointWith Uranium-Depleted Uranium-Enriched)
(comment reasonsForAction "(#$reasonsForAction ACTION PRED AGENT THING) means that ACTION was performed by AGENT, and that a crucial reason for AGENT's
performing ACTION is the fact that (PRED AGENT THING), where PRED is an instance of #$CognitivePredicate. For example, if the Saudi Arabian air force were to conduct a  bombing raid (RAID01) against targets in Iran shortly after the occurrence of a terrorist attack (ATTACK01) directed against Saudi Arabia, and believed by the Saudis to have been sponsored by Iran, then it is reasonable to conclude that (#$reasonsForAction RAID01 #$beliefs #$SaudiArabia
(#$directingAgent ATTACK01 #$Iran)). Similarly, if Joe-Bob jumps (JUMP01) up
from his desk because he sees a spider (SPIDER01) on it, then it is true that
(#$reasonsForAction JUMP01 #$sees Joe-Bob SPIDER01). When arg2 is an instance of
PropositionalAttitudeSlot, arg4 will generally be an #$CycLSentence-Assertible. However, as the  example of Joe-Bob and the spider shows, it need not always be an #$CycLSentence-Assertible.
<p>
Note: #$reasonsForAction is not a spec-pred of #$inReactionTo, but in
some cases a given claim can be expressed using either of these predicates. For
example, the sentence above explaining Saudi Arabia's motivation for a
hypothetical attack on Iran might also be expressed as: (#$inReactionTo RAID01
ATTACK01). However, #$reasonsForAction is a more discriminating predicate, in
that it allows one to identify the precise attitude that motivated a given
action. For this reason, #$reasonsForAction is somewhat less versatile, since one must have more detailed information about the agent's mental state to use this predicate than to use #$inReactionTo; on the other hand, when this information is available, it can be quite useful to make it explicit. Finally, note that in some cases #$reasonsForAction is more versatile than #$inReactionTo for instance, #$inReactionTo does not apply to the example above involving Joe-Bob and the spider. See the comment on #$inReactionTo.")
(comment followingValue "An instance of both #$OrderingPredicate and #$ComparisonPredicate (qq.v.), and a specialization of both #$colinearQuantities and #$lessThan (qq.v.).  #$followingValue is simply the restriction of the very general #$lessThan relation to colinear #$Quantity[ies] (q.v.).  <code>(#$followingValue LO-QUANT HI-QUANT)</code> means that <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> are colinear quantities such that <code>HI-QUANT</code> is a higher or greater value than <code>LO-QUANT</code>.  It follows that there is some (unspecified) linear ordering (see #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType) to which both <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> belong.
<p>
Note that the above sentence would typically be asserted only if one or both of <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> are instances of #$GenericQuantity (or the more general #$NonNumericScalarQuantity), and -- as is usually the case -- they are not grounded in numerically-quantifiable units of measure (see #$NoteAboutGivingGenericValueFunctionsNumericValues).  If <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> <i>do</i> have numerical grounding, a #$followingValue assertion is unnecessary since #$numericallyEquals, #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, and #$greaterThan are automatically computable using arithmetic relations holding between the respective minimums and maximums (see #$minQuantValue and #$maxQuantValue) of <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code>.  For example, one might well use #$followingValue to state that (#$HighAmountFn #$Glamour) is more glamorous than (#$LowAmountFn #$Glamour); but it is unnecessary to make a #$followingValue assertion about (#$MilesPerHour 5) and (#$MilesPerHour 25 35), since #$greaterThan is automatically computable in that case.
<p>
See also #$GenericValueFunction and #$followingValueOnScale.")
(comment Heartbeat-Single "The collection of single hearbeats.  Instances, as well as series of instances done by (#$doneBy) the same heart (#$Heart) count as instances of the broader collection, #$Heartbeating.")
(comment nodesForConceptFilter "(#$nodesForConceptFilter NODE FILTER) means that NODE can be displayed (and its instances as well) in the the concept panel during tasks of the type correlated with FILTER.")
(comment SearchStringInappropriateFunction "Instances of <code>#$SearchStringInappropriateFunction</code> should be skipped when generating search strings for a web search.  For example, <code>#$TheSet</code> is a <code>#$SearchStringInappropriateFunction</code>, because <code>(#$TheSet A B C)</code> should not generate the string <code>\"{A,B,C}\"</code>, but rather the list of strings <code>(\"A\" \"B\" \"C\")</code>.  <code>#$MotherFn</code> on the other hand is <b>not</b> a <code>#$SearchStringInappropriateFunction</code>, because the search string <code>\"Chelsea Clinton's mother\"</code> would be an appropriate search string for <code>(#$MotherFn #$ChelseaClinton)</code>.")
(comment Balcony-BuildingPart "A specialization of both #$PartOfBuilding-AboveGroundFloor and #$Platform. Each instance of #$Balcony-BuildingPart is a (usually unroofed) platform that projects outward from a building, and is usually enclosed by a railing or short wall.")
(disjointWith PartOfBuilding-AboveGroundFloor FoundationOfABuilding)
(comment personSocialSecurityNumber "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates instances of #$Person to instances of #$SocialSecurityNumber. <code>(#$personSocialSecurityNumber PERSON SSN)</code> means that <code>PERSON</code>'s social security number is <code>SSN</code>.  Note that no person can have more than one social security number.")
(comment surfacePortals "A #$PhysicalPartPredicate.  #$surfacePortals is the #$predicateIntersection of #$containsPortals and #$surfaceParts.")
(comment TheSet "A variable-arity #$SetDenotingFunction that is used to specify a set by enumerating its elements.  For any finite sequence <code>THING1,...,THING<sub>N</sub></code> (<code>N</code> >= 1), (#$TheSet <code>THING1...THING<sub>N</sub>)</code> is the #$Set-Mathematical whose elements (see #$elementOf) are precisely <code>THING1,...,THING<sub>N</sub></code>.  For example, (#$TheSet #$SonnyBono #$Cher) is the set whose only elements are Sonny and Cher.  
<p>
Note that in the degenerate case where <code>N</code> is zero, (#$TheSet) is #$TheEmptySet.  
<p>
See also the related function #$TheSetOf, which is used to specify a set by giving a necessary and sufficient membership condition.  And see #$TheList and #$TheCollection.")
(comment actTypeRoleEffectiveForActTypeRole "A quintary (5-place) #$RuleMacroPredicate.  <code>(#$actTypeRoleEffectiveForActTypeRole ACT-TYPE-1 ROLE-1 ACT-TYPE-2 ROLE-2 THING)</code> is shorthand for the following rule: 
<pre>
(#$implies
  (#$and
    (#$subcollectionOfWithRelationTo ACT-TYPE-1 ROLE-1 THING ?SUBCOL-1)
    (#$subcollectionOfWithRelationTo ACT-TYPE-2 ROLE-2 THING ?SUBCOL-2))
  (#$effectiveActTypeForActType ?SUBCOL-1 ?SUBCOL-2))
</pre>
Thus, for example,
<pre>
(#$actTypeRoleEffectiveForActTypeRole #$PushingSomeone #$objectActedOn #$FallingDown #$primaryObjectMoving #$Bob)
</pre>
says, basically, that pushing Bob is a good way to make him fall down.")
(disjointWith Shoe SheetOfSomeStuff)
(disjointWith SomethingToWear Card)
(disjointWith SheetOfSomeStuff Pump-Generic)
(disjointWith MultiIndividualAgent Pump-Generic)
(comment sourceQueryForSimpleQuery "(#$sourceQueryForSimpleQuery SOURCE-QUERY OUTPUT-QUERY) means that SOURCE-QUERY was transformed into OUTPUT-QUERY via an application of #$pertinentQuerySentenceWithTerms.")
(comment VerySimpleCookingWorldTestCaseDataMt "This microtheory holds the data for the most basic Kautz-derived 'cooking world' test case.  The aim here is to see if Cyc can successfully identify candidate subsituations (see #$candidateProperSubSituations) for a putative instance of (#$PreparingFoodItemFn #$PastaDish).")
(comment agentActionsDataSentence "A binary #$SummaryPredicate and specialization of #$interestingSentence that relates an #$Agent-Generic to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible. <code>(#$agentActionsDataSentence AGENT SENTENCE)</code> means that <code>SENTENCE</code> expresses the fact that <code>AGENT</code> is a performer of some action.")
(comment agentAssetsDataSentence "A binary #$SummaryPredicate and specialization of #$interestingSentence that relates an #$Agent-Generic to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible. <code>(#$agentActionsDataSentence AGENT SENTENCE)</code> means that <code>SENTENCE</code> expresses the fact that <code>AGENT</code> is a performer of some action.")
(comment deviceDisarmed "An #$ActorSlot and specialization of #$objectOfStateChange that relates an instance of #$DisarmingADevice to a #$PhysicalDevice.  <code>(#$deviceDisarmed DISARMING DEVICE)</code> means that <code>DEVICE</code> is disarmed in <code>DISARMING</code>.")
(comment weaponDisarmed "An #$ActorSlot and (typed) specialization of #$deviceDisarmed that relates an instance of #$DisarmingAWeapon to something that is an instance of both #$Weapon and #$PhysicalDevice.  <code>(#$weaponDisarmed DISARMING WEAPON)</code> means that <code>WEAPON</code> is disarmed in <code>DISARMING</code>.")
(comment TriggerableDeviceType "A #$SecondOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$TriggerableDeviceType is a specialization of #$PhysicalDevice whose instances typically have a #$TriggeringMechanism as a proper physical part.  Notable instances include #$Device-Armed, #$Device-Disarmed, #$Gun, #$Bomb, #$AlarmDevice, #$TrapDevice.")
(comment TheSetOf "A binary #$SetDenotingFunction and #$ScopingRelation that takes a variable and an open sentence to a set, where the open sentence specifies a necessary and sufficient membership condition for the set.  If <code>SENT</code> is an open #$CycLSentence-Assertible in which <code>VAR</code> is the only variable occuring free, (#$TheSetOf <code>VAR SENT</code>) is the #$Set-Mathematical of exactly those things that satisfy <code>SENT</code>.  For example, (#$TheSetOf ?X (#$and (#$isa ?X #$Dog) (#$mainColorOfObject ?X #$BlackColor))) is the set of black dogs.
<p>
Note that if <code>VAR</code> does not occur free in <code>SENT</code>, or is not the only variable occuring free in <code>SENT</code>, the term <code>(#$TheSetOf VAR SENT)</code> will be itself be open (see #$CycLOpenNonAtomicTerm) and thus will not denote anything.  Given that a true sentence is satisfied by <i>everything</i>, we stipulate that <code>(#$TheSetOf VAR SENT)</code> is #$undefined whenever <code>SENT</code> is closed in order to avoid commitment to a \"universal set\". 
<p>
See also #$TheSet, which is used to specify a set by enumerating its elements.  And see #$TheCollectionOf.")
(comment memberOfLegislature "(#$memberOfLegislature ENTITY PERSON) means that PERSON is a member of a legislative body of ENTITY.")
(comment TriggerableDevice "An #$ExistingObjectType.  Each instance of #$TriggerableDevice is a #$PhysicalDevice that either has a #$TriggeringMechanism as a part, or was designed or created to be such.  Thus, for example, even if the triggering mechanism is removed from a gun, that gun is still an instance of #$TriggerableDevice.")
(comment Device-Armed "A #$DeviceTypeByState.  Each instance of #$Device-Armed is a #$TriggerableDevice that is in an \"armed\" state.  This means that the device is set to \"go off\" when triggered appropriately.  A necessary condition for this is that the device have a #$TriggeringMechanism as a part.
<p>
Positive examples: a loaded gun with the safety off, an enabled burglar alarm system, a \"set\" mousetrap.  Negative examples: an unloaded gun, a disabled burglar alarm system, an \"unset\" mousetrap.  Borderline case: a loaded gun with the safety button on.
<p>
See also #$Device-Disarmed.")
(comment Device-Disarmed "A #$DeviceTypeByState.  Each instance of #$Device-Disarmed is a #$TriggerableDevice that is in a \"disarmed\" state.  This means that the device is an instance of some #$TriggerableDeviceType (e.g. #$Gun, #$Bomb, #$AlarmDevice, #$TrapDevice), but has been deliberately acted on so as not to \"go off\" when triggered.
<p>
Positive examples: a gun from which all ammunition has been removed, a disabled burglar alarm system, an \"unset\" mousetrap.  Negative examples: a loaded gun with the safety off, an enabled burglar alarm system, a \"set\" mousetrap.  Borderline case: a loaded gun with the safety on.
<p>
See also #$Device-Armed.")
(disjointWith Device-Armed Device-Disarmed)
(disjointWith DirtyBomb Bomb-MolotovCocktail)
(disjointWith PipeBomb LetterBomb)
(disjointWith Bomb-MolotovCocktail LetterBomb)
(comment ppCompFrameForTypeAndWord "<code>(#$ppCompFrameForTypeAndWord FRAME FRAME-TYPE WORD)</code> identifies <code>FRAME</code> as the instance of #$PPCompFrame of type <code>FRAME-TYPE</code> that applies to the word (see #$LexicalWord) <code>WORD</code>.")
(disjointWith UserTriggeredWeapon ProximityTriggeredWeapon)
(comment semTransExtractedFromStructure "<code>(#$semTransExtractedFromStructure TEMPLATE STRUCTURE)</code> means that Cyc's knowledge of the semantic translation template <code>TEMPLATE</code> was arrived at through an analysis of <code>STRUCTURE</code>, an instance of #$AbstractStructure.")
(disjointWith Device-RemoteControlled (CollectionDifferenceFn Device-ProximatelyControlled Device-RemoteControlled))
(disjointWith Device-ProximatelyControlledOnly Device-RemoteControlled)
(comment Device-RemoteControlled "A collection. The instances of #$Device-RemoteControlled are those devices which can be controlled remotely when they are being used. For every instance of this collection there is an instance of #$RemoteControlDevice with which to do the controlling. Examples are some instances of #$TelevisionSet of which the channels, volume, etc. can be controlled remotely. Also many instances of #$TransportationDevice-Unmanned can be guided by remote control. This control need not happen throughout the event of using the device. An example of this are the laser-guided missiles that are only guided at the end by pointing with the laser to the intended target.
<p>
Note that a #$Device-RemoteControlled may also be a #$Device-ProximatelyControlled.  For devices that can only be controlled remotely, see #$Device-RemoteControlledOnly.")
(disjointWith Device-ProximatelyControlled (CollectionDifferenceFn Device-RemoteControlled Device-ProximatelyControlled))
(disjointWith Device-ProximatelyControlled Device-RemoteControlledOnly)
(comment Device-RemoteControlledOnly "#$Device-RemoteControlledOnly is a convenient #$rewriteOf (#$CollectionDifferenceFn #$Device-RemoteControlled #$Device-ProximatelyControlled).")
(comment inactiveIngredients "A #$PhysicalCompositionPredicate and specialization of #$ingredients that relates an #$Artifact to a #$PartiallyTangible.  <code>(#$inactiveIngredients ARTIFACT TANGIBLE)</code> means that <code>TANGIBLE</code> is an inactive ingredient in <code>ARTIFACT</code>--i.e., an ingredient in <code>ARTIFACT</code> that does not \"do the main work\" of <code>ARTIFACT</code>, but is included for other reasons.  (This is a vague but useful notion.)  For example, #$inactiveIngredients would relate a pill or a bomb to a substance that was added to that pill or bomb solely for weight, texture, or appearance.  See also #$activeIngredients.")
(comment activeIngredients "A #$PhysicalCompositionPredicate and specialization of #$ingredients that relates an #$Artifact to a #$PartiallyTangible.  <code>(#$activeIngredients ARTIFACT TANGIBLE)</code> means that <code>TANGIBLE</code> is an active ingredient in <code>ARTIFACT</code>--i.e., an ingredient that \"does the main work\" of <code>ARTIFACT</code>.  (This is a vague but useful notion.)  For example, #$activeIngredients would relate a pill to the drug substance(s) it contains, and a bomb to its explosive component(s).  See also #$inactiveIngredients.")
(comment RenderingADangerousThingSafe "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$PurposefulAction and #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent.  Each instance of #$RenderingADangerousThingSafe is an event in which an agent deliberately performs an action on a dangerous thing so as to render it reasonably safe.  (What qualifies as \"reasonably safe\" may vary widely by context.)
<p>
Positive examples: disarming a bomb, unloading a gun, tranquilizing a vicious wild animal.  Negative examples: putting on a bullet-proof vest, locking a loaded gun in a cabinet.  (These things do not bring about an intrinsic change in the dangerous thing, i.e. the gun.)")
(comment aggregateCNCScoreForRuleWithThresholds "(#$aggregateCNCScoreForRuleWithThresholds RULE COMPOUND-THRESHOLD COMPONENT-THRESHOLD SCORE SEARCH-ENGINE) means that SCORE (an instance of #$Real0-1) is the ratio candidate NC test cases passing COMPOUND-THRESHOLD to those passing COMPONENT-THRESHOLD for searches against the corpus covered by SEARCH-ENGINE.  The COMPOUND-THRESHOLD value represents a minimum number of hits which a compound-strings must have for candidate compounds to be regarded as sufficiently evident within a corpus to be of interest.  The COMPONENT-THRESHOLD value represents a minimum number of hits which the modifier- and head-strings for candiate compounds must have for candidates to count as vaild tests wrt. the corpus.")
(comment structureSubstringForNthStructureOfTypeAtOffsetHasInterpretationAndScore "<code>(#$structureSubstringForNthStructureOfTypeAtOffsetHasInterpretationAndScore  STRUCTURE STRING N TYPE OFFSET INTERPRETATION SCORE)</code> means that the <code>N</code>th occurrence of the structure-type <code>TYPE</code> in the abstract structure (#$AbstractStructure, q.v.) <code>STRUCTURE</code> contains (via #$subInfoStructures) the string <code>STRING</code> at offset <code>OFFSET</code>, such that this occurrence of <code>STRING</code> has been assigned a semantic interpretation <code>INTERPRETATION</code> with score <code>SCORE</code>.")
(comment USGSTopographicMapNamedFn "<code>(#$USGSTopographicMapNamedFn NAME)</code> denotes the #$USGSTopographicMap identified by <code>NAME</code>. See also the corresponding predicate #$nameStringForUSGSTopoMap.")
(comment nameStringForUSGSTopoMap "<code>(#$nameStringForUSGSTopoMap NAME USGS-MAP)</code> means that the #$USGSTopographicMap <code>USGS-MAP</code> is identified by the name string, <code>NAME</code>. The name is produced by using the name of an important feature in the depicted area, and appending the two-letter state or territory abbreviation at the end after a comma. See also the function corresponding to this predicate, #$USGSTopographicMapNamedFn.")
(comment USGSTopographicMap "Each #$USGSTopographicMap is a #$TopographicMap published by the #$USGeologicalSurvey and which depicts a portion of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica or one of its territories.  These maps range in scale from 1:20,000 to 1:250,000.   Each #$USGSTopographicMap is identified by a name string derived from the name of a prominent depicted feature appended with the two-letter abbreviation for the state or territory in which the depicted region lies, e.g. \"Chalk Mountain, CO\".")
(comment ExplosiveDeviceDisposalSpecialist "A #$PersonTypeByActivity.  Each instance of #$ExplosiveDeviceDisposalSpecialist is a #$Person who specializes in safely disposing of #$ExplosiveDevices such as #$Bombs.  Such a person may, but need not, be a #$MilitaryPerson.")
(comment agentTypeUsesType "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a specialization of #$IntelligentAgent to a #$FirstOrderCollection  <code>(#$agentTypeUsesType AGT-TYPE TYPE)</code> means that instances of <code>AGT-TYPE</code> frequently or typically use instances of <code>TYPE</code>.  For example:
<pre>
  (#$agentTypeUsesType #$ComputerProgrammer #$Computer)
  (#$agentTypeUsesType #$Carpenter #$Hammer)
  (#$agentTypeUsesType #$ExplosiveDeviceDisposalSpecialist #$BombSniffingDog)
</pre>  
See also the more specialized #$agentTypeUsesArtifactType.")
(comment agentTypeUsesArtifactType "A #$BinaryPredicate and (typed) specialization of #$agentTypeUsesType that relates a specialization of #$IntelligentAgent to a specialization of #$Artifact-Generic.  <code>(#$agentTypeUsesArtifactType AGT-TYPE ART-TYPE)</code> means that instances of <code>AGT-TYPE</code> frequently or typically use instances of <code>ART-TYPE</code>.  For example:
<pre>
  (#$agentTypeUsesArtifactType #$Dentist #$DentalDrill) ;
  (#$agentTypeUsesArtifactType #$ComputerProgrammer #$Computer) ;
  (#$agentTypeUsesArtifactType #$Carpenter #$Hammer) .")
(comment USBoardOnGeographicNames "The United States Board on Geographic Names (BGN) is the interagency board established by public law to standardize geographic name spellings for use in U.S. Government publications.")
(comment USBGNPlaceName "A specialization of #$LexicalWord. #$USBGNPlaceNames are complex lexical objects which describe a particular name assignment and spelling for a #$GeographicalPlace. They are determined and regulated by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames.")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameTypeByClassification "A partition of #$USBGNPlaceName into facets according to one of four classifications determined by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames: 'conventional,' 'native,' 'variant,' or 'not verified.'")
(comment USBGNPlaceName-Conventional "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceName. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceName-Conventional correspond to the name and spelling of a geographical place which are categorized as 'conventional' by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames.")
(comment USBGNPlaceName-Variant "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceName. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceName-Variant correspond to the name and spelling of a geographical place which are categorized as 'variant' by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames.")
(comment USBGNPlaceName-Native "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceName. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceName-Native correspond to the name and spelling of a geographical place which are categorized as 'native' by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames.")
(comment USBGNPlaceName-NotVerified "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceName. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceName-NotVerified correspond to the name and spelling of a geographical place which are categorized as 'not verified' by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames.")
(comment usbgnPlaceNameClassification "A typed spec pred of #$isa and the faceting predicate between #$USBGNPlaceName and #$USBGNPlaceNameTypeByClassification. <code>(#$usbgnPlaceNameClassification PLACE-NAME PLACE-NAME-TYPE)</code> means that the #$USBGNPlaceName <code>PLACE-NAME</code> is an instance of the #$USBGNPlaceNameTypeByClassification facet <code>PLACE-NAME-TYPE</code>.")
(comment TypicalityPredicate "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$TypicalityPredicate is a #$SetOrCollectionPredicate that expresses a typical (frequent, common, expected, standard, normal, significantly likely) relation between things, i.e. one that usually holds but does not hold universally or by definition.  Such predicates are typically used to state general (i.e. quantified), loose, defeasible or probabilistic principles in a terse way; for example, we may represent the fact that grass is typically green with the #$CycLSentence (#$typicalColorOfType #$Grass-Plant #$GreenColor).  #$TypicalityPredicates do not always have the word \"typical\" in their name, and may in certain \"degenerate\" cases express principles which do in fact hold universally, as in, for example, (#$typicalColorOfType GreenGrass #$GreenColor).")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameString "A specialization of #$ProperNameString. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceNameString are the name strings of geographical places that have been vetted and approved by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames for use in official US Government documents. #$USBGNPlaceNameString is covered by #$USBGNPlaceNameStringType, which facets #$USBGNPlaceNameString according to whether the name string is the full name of the geographical place, with or without diacritic characters, a generic name string, a short form, or a form to be used for rapid lexigraphic sorting.")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameStringType "A faceting covering of #$USBGNPlaceNameString. #$USBGNPlaceNameStringType facets #$USBGNPlaceNameString according to whether the name string is the full name of the geographical place, with or without diacritic characters, a generic name string, a short form, or a form to be used for rapid lexigraphic sorting.")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameString-Generic "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceNameString and an instance of #$USBGNPlaceNameStringType. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceNameString-Generic are the generic proper name strings of places, including any diacritic characters required to properly spell the name string in an extended roman alphabet.")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameString-ShortForm "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceNameString and an instance of #$USBGNPlaceNameStringType. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceNameString-ShortForm are the shortened proper name strings of places, including any diacritic characters required to properly spell the name string in an extended roman alphabet.")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameString-SortName "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceNameString and an instance of #$USBGNPlaceNameStringType. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceNameString-SortName are simplified versions of the name strings of places which have been specially chosen to optimize sorting the names lexigraphically.")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameString-FullName-ND "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceNameString and an instance of #$USBGNPlaceNameStringType. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceNameString-FullName-ND are the full proper name strings of places, in which any diacritic characters which would normally be required to properly spell the name string in an extended roman alphabet have been replaced with their simpler non-diacritic equivalents.")
(comment USBGNPlaceNameString-FullName "A specialization of #$USBGNPlaceNameString and an instance of #$USBGNPlaceNameStringType. Instances of #$USBGNPlaceNameString-FullName are the full proper name strings of places, including any diacritic characters required to properly spell the name string in an extended roman alphabet.")
(comment SettingAnObjectDown "A specialization of #$LoweringAnObject.  Each instance of #$SettingAnObjectDown is an event in which an agent gradually lowers an object that it is holding until it comes to rest on a surface.")
(disjointWith CSQLFunction CSQLLogicalOperator)
(disjointWith CSQLComparisonPredicate CSQLLogicalOperator)
(disjointWith CSQLLogicalConnective CSQLQuantifier)
(comment CATSContentTest "#$CATSContentTest is a collection of #$SKSIContentTests which test the content of the #$CATS-KS database.")
(comment MentallyCategorizingSomething "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$Categorizing.  Each instance of #$MentallyCategorizingSomething is a #$PurposefulMentalActivity in which an agent determines or decides that a certain thing belongs to a certain category or categories, i.e., is an instance of a certain collection of collections.  This does not include physically categorizing an item by, e.g., placing it into a bin (though such an event would likely be preceded by an instance of #$MentallyCategorizingSomething).")
(comment thingCategorizedAs "A #$TernaryPredicate that relates a #$Categorizing to a #$Thing to a #$Collection.  <code>(#$thingCategorizedAs CATEGORIZING THING TYPE)</code> means that in <code>CATEGORIZING</code>, it is determined or decided that <code>THING</code> is an instance of <code>TYPE</code>.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$thingCategorizedAs CATEGORIZING THING TYPE)</code> does not entail <code>(#$isa THING TYPE)</code>, since agents can categorize things incorrectly.")
(comment PlacingAVisibleIndicatorOnAnObject "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$PlacingAVisibleIndicatorOnAnObject is an event in which an agent marks, labels, or tags an object to indicate that it belongs to a certain category or has a certain feature.  Examples: placing a price tag on an item for sale, branding a cow.")
(comment PlacingAnIndicatorOnSomething "Each instance of #$PlacingAnIndicatorOnSomething is an event in which an agent marks, labels, or tags something in order to indicate that it is of a certain type.  The thing on which the indicator is placed need not be a #$PartiallyTangible; for example, it could be a piece of information in a database.")
(comment typeIndicated "A #$BinarySituationPredicate that relates an instance of #$PlacingAnIndicatorOnSomething to a #$Collection.  <code>(#$typeIndicated PLACING TYPE)</code> means that in <code>PLACING</code>, something is indicated to be an instance of <code>TYPE</code>.")
(comment CycDateParser "The parser used by default in Cyc-based programs to parse dates.")
(comment urlRankForStringWithEngine "An assertion of the form <code>(#$urlRankForStringWithEngine URL N STRING ENGINE)</code> means that the #$UniformResourceLocator <code>URL</code> was the <code>N</code>th result from a search using the #$SubLString <code>STRING</code> on the  #$SearchEngine <code>ENGINE</code>.  This is a temporally qualifiable relation.")
(comment PhysicallyCategorizingAnObject "A #$FirstOrderCollection and specialization of #$Categorizing and #$ActionOnObject.  Each instance of #$PhysicallyCategorizingAnObject is a #$PurposefulPhysicalAction in which an agent physically categorizes an object by performing an action on it -- for example, placing it into a bin or marking it with a label.")
(comment facesInDirection "A #$FunctionalSlot that relates a #$FrontAndBackSidedObject to a #$Direction.  (#$facesInDirection OBJ DIRECTION) means that the front side of OBJ faces in DIRECTION.")
(comment microtheoryParameterValueInSpecification "(#$microtheoryParameterValueInSpecification MT SPEC) states that, according to the #$ProgramSpecification SPEC, the #$SoftwareParameter #$InferenceMicrotheoryParameter has the value MT.  This is commonly used to specify the #$Microtheory in which to ask a #$CycLQuerySpecification.")
(comment sentenceParameterValueInSpecification "(#$sentenceParameterValueInSpecification QUOTED-SENTENCE SPEC) states that, according to the #$ProgramSpecification SPEC, the #$SoftwareParameter #$InferenceSentenceParameter has the value QUOTED-SENTENCE.  This is commonly used to specify the #$CycLSentence to be asked in a #$CycLQuerySpecification.")
(comment inferenceModeParameterValueInSpecification "(#$inferenceModeParameterValueInSpecification MODE SPEC) states that, according to the #$ProgramSpecification SPEC, the #$SoftwareParameter #$InferenceModeParameter has the value MODE.  This is commonly used to specify the inference mode with which to ask a #$CycLQuerySpecification.")
(comment RelativeAmbiguity-DisambiguationScoringStrategy "An instance of #$DisambiguationScoringStrategy.  This strategy assigns a value to an instance of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that takes a base value and divides it by the number of competing hypothesis-mts.")
(comment strategyApplicableToTypes "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  <code>(#$strategyApplicableToTypes STRATEGY TYPE)</code> means that the scoring strategy <code>STRATEGY</code> applies to every instance of the collection <code>TYPE</code>, or <code> (#$relationInstanceAll #$strategyApplicableToHypothesis STRATEGY TYPE)</code>.")
(disjointWith CleaningSubstance Card)
(disjointWith GoingToSleep Distilling)
(disjointWith HumanOccupationConstruct ElectricalComponent)
(comment EvaluatableHypothesisMicrotheory "The collection of instances of #$HypotheticalContext that represent hypotheses that the system is in a position to evaluate.  This class of microtheories represent claims, the determination of the truth-value of which can be said to be \"goals\" for Cyc, and so differs from those Mts generated to satisfy the antecedent conditions of a hypothetical query.")
(comment strategyAssignsBaseValue "<code>(#$strategyAssignsBaseValue STRATEGY VALUE)</code> means that the instance <code>STRATEGY</code> of #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy assigns a base value of <code>VALUE</code> to the objects to which it applies (see #$strategyApplicableToHypothesis).")
(comment strategyApplicableToHypotheses "<code>(#$strategyApplicableToHypotheses STRATEGY HYP1 HYP2)</code> means that the two instances of #$EvaluatableHypothesisMicrotheory <code>HYP1</code> and <code>HYP2</code> enter into the semantic relationship that the #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy <code>STRATEGTY</code> rewards.")
(comment usbgnPlaceNameForGeographicalPlace "A predicate which relates a place name (instance of #$USBGNPlaceName) assigned by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames (BGN) with the geographical place (instance of #$GeographicalPlace) to which it refers. <code>(#$usbgnPlaceNameForGeographicalPlace PLACE-NAME PLACE)</code> means that the BGN has determined <code>PLACE-NAME</code> to be one of the place names associated with <code>PLACE</code>.")
(comment usbgnPlaceNameStringOfType "A predicate which relates a place name (instance of #$USBGNPlaceName) assigned by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames (BGN) with a string representation of the name and the type of the name string, where the type is either full name, full name no diacritics, short form, generic, and sort. <code>(#$usbgnPlaceNameStringOfType PLACE-NAME STRING TYPE)</code> means that <code>STRING</code> is a string representation of <code>PLACE-NAME</code> of type <code>TYPE</code>.")
(comment usbgnPlaceNameLocalToGeopoliticalEntity "A predicate which relates a place name (instance of #$USBGNPlaceName) assigned by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames (BGN) to the geopolitical entity for which the name is locally used. <code>(#$usbgnPlaceNameLocalToGeopoliticalEntity PLACE-NAME ENTITY)</code> means that <code>PLACE-NAME</code> is the local name within <code>ENTITY</code> for the geographical place associated with <code<PLACE-NAME</code> via #$usbgnPlaceNameForGeographicalPlace.")
(comment numberOfOccurencesOfStringInCorpus "An assertion of the form (#$numberOfOccurencesOfStringInCorpus CORPUS STRING N) means that the string STRING occurs N times in the #$Corpus CORPUS.")
(comment contentModelWorkspace "<code>(#$contentModelWorkspace MODEL WORKSPACE)</code> means that <code>MODEL</code> is an instance of #$ContentModelMicrotheory that models one of N-many structures that are sub-structures of a structure modelled in <code>WORKSPACE</code>.")
(comment Translation-Complete "A collection of translational motion events; hence a subcollection of #$Movement-TranslationEvent.  In each instance of #$Translation-Complete, the entire moving object (see #$objectMoving) moves from the place of origin (see #$fromLocation) to the destination (see #$toLocation).  That is, the object completely leaves the origin and relocates to the destination.  The moving object may be either a #$NonFluidlikeTangibleThing (e.g. a baseball) or a #$FluidTangibleThing all of which moves from one place to another (e.g. the gasoline used to fill a gas tank).  Another example: a single molecule of water flowing from point A to point B in a river.  Non-examples: a river flowing from A to B  (the river itself is not relocated); a rubber band stretching.  A borderline case: a spider spins a web, leaving part of itself, in effect, extended out behind it; in most contexts that would still be considered a #$Translation-Complete.  Note that #$Translation-Complete is noncommittal as to whether net movement has occurred, so round-trip events qualify as complete translations (cf. #$Translation-LocationChange).")
(comment Locomotion-OuterSpaceRegion "The collection of all instances of #$LocomotionProcess in which the locomotor is moving though outer space (see #$OuterSpaceRegion).  An example of #$Locomotion-OuterSpaceRegion is interplanetary travel in a rocket ship.  A non-example is an astronaut on a space-walk.")
(disjointWith Locomotion-LiquidImmersed Locomotion-LiquidSurface)
(disjointWith Locomotion-LiquidImmersed Locomotion-SolidSurface)
(disjointWith Locomotion-LiquidImmersed Locomotion-Air)
(disjointWith Locomotion-OuterSpaceRegion Locomotion-LiquidImmersed)
(disjointWith Locomotion-OuterSpaceRegion Locomotion-LiquidSurface)
(disjointWith Locomotion-OuterSpaceRegion Locomotion-SolidSurface)
(disjointWith Locomotion-OuterSpaceRegion Locomotion-Air)
(disjointWith Locomotion-LiquidSurface Locomotion-Air)
(disjointWith Locomotion-LiquidSurface Locomotion-SolidSurface)
(comment TextLearnerDisambiguationResultsDistillationQuerySpecification "The collection of instances of #$CycLQuerySpecification that are run by the Text Learner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) to distill the results of disambiguating text, so as to keep a permanent record of what the TextLearner has done without maintaining the enormous infrastructure (parse trees, etc.) used to do it. Like all instances of the more general collection, #$TextLearnerFinalizationQuerySpecification, these queries should mention (#$TheFn #$LinguisticExpressionPeg) -- for which will be substituted the peg for the sentence just read -- and return #$ist sentences suitable to be asserted and transmitted.")
(comment structureSubstringForNthSentenceHasCandidatePresuppositionAndScore "<code>(#$structureSubstringForNthSentenceHasCandidatePresuppositionAndScore STRUCTURE STRING N INTERPRETATION SCORE)</code> means that the <code>N</code>th sentence of the abstract structure (#$AbstractStructure, q.v.) <code>STRUCTURE</code> with corresponding string <code>STRING</code> has been assigned a semantic interpretation <code>INTERPRETATION</code> of its presupposed content with score <code>SCORE</code>.  See also #$structureSubstringForNthStructureOfTypeAtOffsetHasInterpretationAndScore, which is used to identify the possible semantic interpretations (versus presupposed content) of contextualized string-occurrences (see #$ContextualizedInformationStructure) and their scores.")
(comment TextLearnerResultsDistillationConstant "A sub-collection of #$TextLearnerInfrastructureConstant. Each quoted instance of #$TextLearnerResultsDistillationConstant is a constant used to represent a summarized, or distilled version of the results of TextLearner processes.")
(comment testQueryFormula "(#$testQueryFormula FORMULA QUERY-SPEC) says that FORMULA is the #$CycLFormula to be used as the formula in the query, whose specification is determined by the #$CycLQuerySpecification QUERY-SPEC.")
(comment positionAlongXAxisInCoordinateSystem "A ternary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a #$SpatialThing to a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem to a #$ScalarInterval.  <code>(#$positionAlongXAxisInCoordinateSystem OBJECT SYSTEM POSITION)</code> means that the position of <code>OBJECT</code> along the x axis of <code>SYSTEM</code> (see #$xAxisPredicateOfSystem) is <code>POSITION</code>.")
(comment positionAlongZAxisInCoordinateSystem "A ternary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a #$SpatialThing to a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem to a #$ScalarInterval.  <code>(#$positionAlongZAxisInCoordinateSystem OBJECT SYSTEM POSITION)</code> means that the position of <code>OBJECT</code> along the z axis of <code>SYSTEM</code> (see #$zAxisPredicateOfSystem) is <code>POSITION</code>.")
(comment positionAlongYAxisInCoordinateSystem "A ternary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a #$SpatialThing to a #$CartesianCoordinateSystem to a #$ScalarInterval.  <code>(#$positionAlongYAxisInCoordinateSystem OBJECT SYSTEM POSITION)</code> means that the position of <code>OBJECT</code> along the y axis of <code>SYSTEM</code> (see #$yAxisPredicateOfSystem) is <code>POSITION</code>.")
(comment speedOfObjectInDirection "A #$TernaryPredicate that relates a #$PartiallyTangible to a #$Direction to a #$Speed.  <code>(#$speedOfObjectInDirection OBJ DIRECTION SPEED)</code> means the speed at which <code>OBJ</code> is moving in <code>DIRECTION</code> is <code>SPEED</code>.  This does not imply that the net movement of <code>OBJ</code> is in <code>DIRECTION</code>.  Rather, it means that if <code>OBJ</code> continues at the same velocity for <code>TIME</code>, and <code>SPEED = DISTANCE/TIME</code>, then <code>OBJ</code> will be located <code>DISTANCE</code> further in <code>DIRECTION</code> after <code>TIME</code>.
<p>
For example, suppose that the net velocity (see #$velocityOfObject) of Car001 is #$Northwest-Directly at 100 #$KilometersPerHour.  Then we have:
<pre>
(#$speedOfObjectInDirection Car001 #$Northwest-Directly (#$KilometersPerHour 100))
(#$speedOfObjectInDirection Car001 #$North-Directly (#$KilometersPerHour 70.71)
(#$speedOfObjectInDirection Car001 #$West-Directly (#$KilometersPerHour 70.71)
(#$speedOfObjectInDirection Car001 #$South-Directly (#$KilometersPerHour -70.71)
(#$speedOfObjectInDirection Car001 #$East-Directly (#$KilometersPerHour -70.71)
(#$speedOfObjectInDirection Car001 #$Southeast-Directly (#$KilometersPerHour -100))
(#$speedOfObjectInDirection Car001 #$Southwest-Directly (#$KilometersPerHour 0))
etc.
</pre>
That is, after one hour, Car001 will be located 100 km further Northwest, about 71 km further North, about -71 km further South, 0 km further Southwest, etc.")
(comment SIHM-NounCompoundRuleInterpretation "The collection of instances <code>SIHM</code> of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory that promote an interpretation based on some (possibly hypothesized) #$NounCompoundRule.")
(comment ncRuleAssignsPegInterpretation "<code>(#$ncRuleAssignsPegInterpretation RULE PEG CYCL)</code> means that the instance <code>RULE</code> of 
#$NounCompoundRule applies to the expression peg <code>PEG</code> so as to assign <code>CYCL</code> as CycL semantics for <code>PEG</code>.")
(comment ncRuleSpawnsHypotheses "<code>(#$ncRuleSpawnsHypotheses RULE HYPOTHESIS-MT)</code> means that the instance <code>RULE</code> of #$NounCompoundRule has applied to some noun compound-occurrence <code>NC</code> resulting in the reification of <code>HYPOTHESIS-MT</code>, a microtheory whose focal content (seee #$mtFocalContent) is a sentence of the form <code>(#$pegInterpretation-CycL NC CYCL)</code>, where <code>CYCL</code> is the CycL semantics assigned to <code>NC</code> by <code>RULE</code>.")
(comment StateCapitolBuilding "The collection of all buildings which house the legislature of a state or #$Country. Instances of #$StateCapitolBuilding usually are located within the territory of the #$capitalCity of the #$GeopoliticalEntity.")
(comment inconsistentMeanings "<code>(#$inconsistentMeanings NODE-1 MEANING-1 NODE-2 MEANING-2)</code> means that <i>at most</i>, either <code>MEANING-1</code> is the correct CycL interpretation of <code>NODE-1</code> or <code>MEANING-2</code> is the correct CycL interpretation of <code>NODE-2</code>, but it's not the case that both.")
(comment requestedQuantityOfWorkTime "<code>(#$requestedQuantityOfWorkTime REQUEST QUANT)</code> means that <code>QUANT</code> is the instance of #$Time-Quantity which is the amount of an agent's work time that is requested in  <code>REQUEST</code>, an instance of #$ResourceRequest.  This predicate represents the actual quantity of work time that is being reserved.  The related predicate, #$allotmentForInterval, is used to indicate the specific interval when the reservation holds.")
(disjointWith NonDangerousTangibleThing DangerousThing)
(comment NonDangerousTangibleThing "#$NonDangerousTangibleThing is a convenient #$rewriteOf:
<pre>
(#$CollectionDifferenceFn #$PartiallyTangible #$DangerousThing)
</pre>")
(disjointWith DangerousThing (CollectionDifferenceFn PartiallyTangible DangerousThing))
(comment MinCollectionsFn "An #$EvaluatableFunction (q.v.). <code>(#$MinCollectionsFn SET-OF-COLS)</code> denotes the subset of <code>SET-OF-COLS</code> which consists of only the collections in <code>SET-OF-COLS</code> that have no proper specializations among <code>SET-OF-COLS</code>.  For example,  (#$MinCollectionsFn (#$TheSet #$Thing #$Dog)) would evaluate to (#$TheSet #$Dog).")
(comment USBGNCharacterSet "A specialization of #$WritingSymbolCharacterSet, #$USBGNCharacterSet is the collection of specializations of #$WritingSymbolCharacter which are used by the #$USBoardOnGeographicNames to standardize the spelling of geographic place names in the native languages of the namers.")
(comment AmericasAndWesternEurope-USBGNCharacterSet "An instance of #$USBGNCharacterSet, #$AmericasAndWesternEurope-USBGNCharacterSet is the collection of #$WritingSymbolCharacters which are used to write the place names which are native to the languages of the Americas and Western Europe.")
(comment EasternEurope-USBGNCharacterSet "An instance of #$USBGNCharacterSet, #$EasternEurope-USBGNCharacterSet is the collection of #$WritingSymbolCharacters which are used to write the place names which are native to the languages of Eastern Europe.")
(comment AfricaAndTheMiddleEast-USBGNCharacterSet "An instance of #$USBGNCharacterSet, #$AfricaAndTheMiddleEast-USBGNCharacterSet is the collection of #$WritingSymbolCharacters which are used to write the place names which are native to the languages of Africa and the Middle East.")
(comment CentralAsiaAndRussia-USBGNCharacterSet "An instance of #$USBGNCharacterSet, #$CentralAsiaAndRussia-USBGNCharacterSet is the collection of #$WritingSymbolCharacters which are used to write the place names which are native to the languages of Central Asia and Russia.")
(comment AsiaAndThePacific-USBGNCharacterSet "An instance of #$USBGNCharacterSet, #$AsiaAndThePacific-USBGNCharacterSet is the collection of #$WritingSymbolCharacters which are used to write the place names which are native to the languages of Asiand the Pacific.")
(comment Vietnam-USBGNCharacterSet "An instance of #$USBGNCharacterSet, #$Vietnam-USBGNCharacterSet is the collection of #$WritingSymbolCharacters which are used to write the place names which are native to the languages of the Vietnam.")
(comment usbgnPlaceNameCharacterSet "<code>(#$usbgnPlaceNameCharacterSet PLACE-NAME CHARACTER-SET)</code> means that when the #$USBGNPlaceName, <code>PLACE-NAME</code>, is written or represented in writing symbol characters (see #$WritingSymbolCharacters), then <code>CHARACTER-SET</code> should be the#$USBGNCharacterSet used to display the characters of the place name.")
(comment UrbanCombatAgent "This is the guy, or robot, or whatever it is, that is running around in the Urban Combat environment.")
(comment UCTApartmentsMapCoordinateSystem "This is the three-dimensional #$CartesianCoordinateSystem with respect to which locations are stated in the markup of the Urban Combat \"UCT Apartments\" map (see #$UrbanCombatGame-UCTApartmentsMapMt).  A point within this framework can be specified using #$PointIn3DCoordinateSystemFn.  For example, the point specified by the markup text
<pre>
x=\"508.00\" y=\"2688.00\" z=\"-60.00\"
</pre>
can be specified in CycL
<pre>
(#$PointIn3DCoordinateSystemFn #$UCTApartmentsMapCoordinateSystem 508.00 2688.00 -60.00)
</pre>
")
(comment SpoiledFood "The collection of all former instances of fresh food (see #$FreshFoodOrDrink) that have undergone #$Spoiling and are no longer edible.")
(comment evincesGAF "<code>(#$evincesGAF OBTAINING-A-RESULT GAF)</code> means that in the instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult, <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, <code>GAF</code> is the #$CycLSentence-Assertible that is supported by the result returned as the fully-bound form of the #$gafGatheringTriggerSentence of the #$GAFGathering which is a super-event of <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>.")
(comment evincesVerifiedGAF "<code>(#$evincesVerifiedGAF OBTAINING-A-RESULT GAF)</code> means that in the instance of #$ObtainingAStringBasedSearchResult,
<code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>, <code>GAF</code> is the
#$CycLSentence-Assertible that is supported by the result returned as the fully-bound form of the #$gafGatheringTriggerSentence of the #$GAFGathering which is a super-event of <code>OBTAINING-A-RESULT</code>.")
(comment ILPMode-Orthogonal-SpecializationStrategyMt "#$ILPMode-Orthogonal-SpecializationStrategyMt is a microtheory containing rules that implement a specific strategy for getting the mode and type information for ILP. The strategy implemented in this microtheory is the leaf strategy (see #$ILPMode-LeafStrategyMt) along with a way of introducing a collection that links together argument positions that are constrained by orthogonal collections. This linkage is needed for ILP systems like ALEPH so that it understands that the same value can show up in both argument positions that Cyc constrains to orthogonal collections.
<p>
The specialization strategy introduces the #$CollectionIntersectionFn of the orthogonal collection argument constraints.
<p>
For instance, the first argument of #$rulerInDynasty is #$Monarch-HeadOfState and the second argument of #$father is #$MaleAnimal. This strategy introduces (#$CollectionIntersectionFn (#$TheSet #$MaleAnimal #$Monarch-HeadOfState)) as the collection which would link the two argument positions.")
(comment ngaImportanceOfGeographicalPlace "<code>(#$ngaImportanceOfGeographicalPlace PLACE IMPORTANCE)</code> means that the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US has determined that the #$GeographicalPlace <code>PLACE</code> has the importance level (see #$GeographicalPlaceByNGAImportanceLevel) <code>IMPORTANCE</code>.")
(comment reifiedMappingForSource "This predicate relates a structured knowledge source to the reified mappings (see #$ReifiedMapping) which are used to interpret the values of coded fields in the source's physical schema in order to arrive at well formed values of some logical field in the source's logical schema.")
(comment WorkWithNoAttributedDateOfPublication "A specialization of #$ConceptualWork.  Each instance of #$WorkWithNoAttributedDateOfPublication is a conceptual work entered by the FET that has no date of publication assertion.")
(comment WorkWithNoAttributedPublisher "A specialization of #$ConceptualWork.  Each instance of #$WorkWithNoAttributedPublisher is a conceptual work entered via the FET that has no #$issuerOfCW assertion.")
(comment WorkWithNoAssertedTitle "A specialization of #$ConceptualWork.  Each instance of #$WorkWithNoAssertedTitle is a conceptual work entered via the FET that has #$titleOfWork assertion.")
(comment NuclearPolicy "A specialization of #$Policy.  Each instance of #$NuclearPolicy is a policy for pursuing the development of nuclear technology, such as nuclear weapons or nuclear power.")
(comment officialPolicyOfTypeForAgent "(#$officialPolicyOfTypeForAgent AGENT POLICY-TYPE POLICY) means that the official POLICY-TYPE (e.g. #$NuclearPolicy) of AGENT is POLICY.")
(comment SKSIProfilingQuery "#$SKSIProfilingQuery is the collection of queries which correspond to the tests in #$SKSIProfilingTest.")
(comment SKSIProfilingQuery-SingleLiteral "#$SKSIProfilingQuery-SingleLiteral is the collection of queries which correspond to the tests in #$SKSIProfilingTest-SingleLiteral.")
(comment SKSIProfilingQuery-MultiLiteral "#$SKSIProfilingQuery-MultiLiteral is the collection of queries which correspond to the tests in #$SKSIProfilingTest-MultiLiteral.")
(comment SKSIProfilingQuery-SingleSource "#$SKSIProfilingQuery-SingleSource is the collection of queries which correspond to the tests in #$SKSIProfilingTest-SingleSource.")
(comment SKSIProfilingQuery-MultiSource "#$SKSIProfilingQuery-MultiSource is the collection of queries which correspond to the tests in #$SKSIProfilingTest-MultiSource.")
(comment facetBasedOnPredicate "The most general #$FacetBasisPredicate (q.v.), which is used to state of a #$FacetingCollectionType and a predicate that the former is \"based on\" the latter in one of several different ways.  <code>(#$facetBasedOnPredicate FACET-COL PRED)</code> means that at least one of the following holds:
<pre>
  (#$facetBasedOnBinaryPred FACET-COL PRED)  
  (#$facetBasedOnBinaryPredInverse FACET-COL PRED)  
  (#$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPred FACET-COL PRED)  
  (#$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverse FACET-COL PRED)  
  (#$facetBasedOnPredicateArg FACET-COL PRED N)
  (#$facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg FACET-COL PRED N)
</pre>
(where <code>N</code> is an integer >= 1).")
(comment RealNumberDatatype "The collection of all computer data types that are capable (only) of representing some subset of the real numbers.")
(comment 4ByteRealDatatype "The collection of all computer data types that employ exactly 4 bytes to represent some subset of the real numbers.")
(comment 8ByteRealDatatype "The collection of all computer data types that employ exactly 8 bytes to represent some subset of the real numbers.")
(comment StringOfLengthFn "(#$StringOfLengthFn N) denotes the collection of all strings with exactly N characters.  For example, the string <code>\"867-5309\"</code> is an element of (#$StringOfLengthFn 8) since it has 8 characters.  The string <code>\"yow     \"</code> is also an eight-character string, but it is padded with spaces.")
(disjointWith Biped Quadruped)
(comment TyrannosaurusRex "A specialization of #$Therapod (q.v.).  T. Rex was a large carnivorous dinosaur.")
(comment HondaMotorcycle "A #$MotorcycleTypeByBrand.  #$HondaMotorcycle is the collection of all #$Motorcycles manufactured by #$Honda-TheCompany.")
(comment ebmtTrainingExampleExtractedFromStructure "<code>(#$ebmtTrainingExampleExtractedFromStructure TEMPLATE STRUCTURE)</code> means that Cyc's knowledge of the EBMT template <code>TEMPLATE</code> was arrived at through an analysis of <code>STRUCTURE</code>, an instance of #$AbstractStructure.")
(comment Snowman "Sculptures  made of snow representing homo sapiens.  Typically abstract, with a ball representing the head, a larger ball representing the torso, and an even larger ball representing the legs.")
(comment suppressNATNodesWithPredInTaxonomy "(#$suppressNATNodesWithPredInTaxonomy BIN-PRED TAXONOMY) means that non-atomic terms created with BIN-PRED as an argument should be suppressed in TAXONOMY.")
(comment Biped "A specialization of #$Animal.  Each instance of #$Biped is an animal that is a member of a class of animals instances of which tend to be capable of locomoting on two legs, and tend to use two-legged locomotion (See #$Locomotion-TwoLegged) somewhat more regularly than other forms of leg-based locomotion.")
(comment Quadruped "A specialization of #$Animal.  Each instance of #$Quadruped is an animal that is a member of a class of animals instances of which tend to be capable of locomoting on four legs, and tend to use four-legged locomotion (see #$Locomotion-FourLegged) somewhat more regularly than other forms of leg-based locomotion.")
(comment Statue "A specialization of #$DurableGood and #$SculptureInTheRound (qq.v.).  Statues are usually figures of persons or animals carved in stone, wood, metal, etc.  Statues are relatively permanent compared to other sculptures.")
(comment SculptureInTheRound "A specialization of #$Sculpture (q.v.).  Instances of #$SculptureInTheRound are sculptures that are fully three-dimensional, in the sense that they are not attached to a background (the base of a statue does not count as a background, by convention), and so are, at least in principle, viewable from any direction.")
(comment Figurine "A collection of small carved or molded #$Sculptures.  These are usually #$Collectibles.   #$Figurines range in quality, but the more typical ones of high quality are made of #$Porcelain or crystal.")
(comment recordSetByObject "(#$recordSetByObject OBJ RECORD) means that OBJ set the record RECORD.")
(comment MostMajorLeagueHomeRunsInACareer "An instance of #$SportsRecord (q.v.).  #$MostMajorLeagueHomeRunsInACareer is the record set by the baseball player playing professional baseball in the major league who has hit the most home runs during their professional career.")
(comment RewardRelativeToNumberOfCompetitors-PresuppositionScoringStrategy "An instance of #$ScoringSoftwareStrategy.  This strategy assigns a value to an instance of #$PresuppositionHypothesisMicrotheory that takes a base value and divides it by the number of competing hypothesis-mts.")
(comment rulerInDynasty "<code>(#$rulerInDynasty MONARCH DYNASTY)</code> means that <code>MONARCH</code> (an instance of #$Monarch-HeadOfState) is a ruler in <code>DYNASTY</code> (an instance of #$DynasticFamily).")
(comment sihmPresuppositionSupportForHypothesisMt "<code>(#$sihmPresuppositionSupportForHypothesisMt SIHM HYP)</code> means that the instance <code>SIHM</code> of #$SemanticInterpretationHypothesisMicrotheory posits a CycL interpretation of some NL expression that supports they hypothesization of a presupposition of a subsuming NL expression reflected in the instance of #$PresuppositionHypothesisMicrotheory <code>HYP</code>.")
(comment hypothesesMutuallySupportive "An instance of #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate and #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.  <code>(#$hypothesesMutuallySupportive MT-1 MT-2)</code> means that the focal contents (see #$mtFocalContent) of the instances <code>MT-1</code> and <code>MT-2</code> of #$HypotheticalContext support one another, in the loose sense of each increasing the likelihood of the other's being true.")
(comment learnedWhileReading "<code>(#$learnedWhileReading ASSERTION STORE)</code> means that the CycL assertion <code>ASSERTION</code> was authored by Cyc or a Cyc-based program and was arrived at by reading <code>STORE</code>, an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore (typically an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure, #$ContextualizedInformationStructure, or #$PropositionalConceptualWork.")
(comment TextLearnerFinalizationConstant "A sub-collection of #$TextLearnerInfrastructureConstant. Each quoted instance of #$TextLearnerFinalizationConstant is a constant used to represent the results of TextLearner processes.")
(comment conceptExtractedWhileReading "<code>(#$conceptExtractedWhileReading THING STORE)</code> means that <code>THING</code>, constrained to be represented in CycL with a CycL Denotational Term, was made known to Cyc (i.e., its CycL denotational term was introduced to the CycL vocabulary) as a result of processing text contained in <code>STORE</code>, an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore.  See also #$learnedWhileReading, which relates a CycL assertion to an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore.")
(comment learnedOrLearnedOfWhileReading "The union (see #$predicateUnion) of #$learnedWhileReading and #$conceptExtractedWhileReading, <code>(#$learnedOrLearnedOfWhileReading THING STORE)</code> means that <code>THING</code> constrained to be a (quoted) instance of #$CycLExpression, is known by (in the case of an assertion) or known to (in the case of a denotational term) Cyc as a result of Cyc's having processed text contained in <code>STORE</code>, an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore.")
(comment SKSIProfilingTestType "#$SKSIProfilingTestType is a second order collection of specializations of #$SKSIProfilingTest which are intended to serve as test suites for profiling the performance of SKSI functionality under various perturbations of the Cyc architecture.")
(comment SKSIProfilingQueryType "#$SKSIProfilingQueryType is a second order collection of specializations of #$SKSIProfilingQuery which are intended to serve as query suites for profiling the performance of SKSI functionality under various perturbations of the Cyc architecture.")
(comment salientHypothesisTypes "A specialization (see #$genlPreds) of #$isa, <code>(#$salientHypothesisTypes MT TYPE)</code> identifies <code>TYPE</code> as one of the instances of #$EvaluatableHypothesisMicrotheory relevant for the evaluation of the hypothetical context <code>MT</code> in the context in which it holds.")
(comment kbOrthogonal "<code>(#$kbOrthogonal COL1 COL2)</code> means that in the current query context, the following are provable:<p>

1. <code>(#$unknownSentence (#$genls COL1 COL2))</code> <br>
2. <code>(#$unknownSentence (#$genls COL2 COL1))</code> <br> 
3. <code>(#$unknownSentence (#$disjointWith COL1 COL2))</code> <p>

In other words, in the current query context, it is not provable that either <code>COL1</code> subsumes <code>COL2</code> or that <code>COL2</code> subsumes <code>COL1</code>, and neither is it provable that <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code> are disjoint.<p>
Unlike other predicates related to orthogonality (such as #$orthogonalSetsOrCollections and #$orthogonalInMt (qq.v.)), #$kbOrthogonal is a #$KBDependentRelation and as such is not assertible.")
(comment linkGrammarLinkTypeTranslationPredicate "(#$linkGrammarLinkTypeTranslationPredicate LINK-TYPE PRED) means that in a  translation of sentence involving LINK-TYPE, the predicate PRED should hold between entities which are denoted by tokens which are linked by an instance of LINK-TYPE.")
(comment frameExtendedByPPCompFrameType "(#$subcatFramesForAlternation FRAME PPCOMPFRAMETYPE)
means that instances of PPCOMPFRAMETYPE may thought of as \"extensions\" of FRAME in that as complements subcategorized for by the former will resemble those of the latter, but will also include an additional PP complement.")
(comment linkGrammarLinkTypeWord "(#$linkGrammarLinkTypeTranslationPredicate LINK-TYPE PRED) means that in a  translation of sentence involving LINK-TYPE, the predicate PRED should hold between entities which are denoted by tokens which are linked by an instance of LINK-TYPE.")
(comment linkGrammarLinkTypeWord "(#$linkGrammarLinkTypeWord LINK-TYPE WORD) means that LINKTYPE is used to link to tokens of WORD.")
(comment Manuscript-Handwritten "A sub-collection of #$HandwrittenDocument. Each instance is a handwritten book or document.")
(comment TradingCardGameCard "A specialization of #$TradingCard (q.v.). #$TradingCardGameCard is the collection of trading cards used in #$TradingCardGames (q.v.).  Each trading card game card is one of a pack of cards used in some trading card game.")
(comment TradingCard "A specialization of #$Card (q.v.). #$TradingCard is the collection of cards with pictures or designs that are collected and traded, usually by children.  Specializations include #$SportsCard and  #$TradingCardGameCard.")
(comment SportsCard "This is the collection of #$TradingCards that depict #$Athletes (usually professionals in team sports such as baseball or football) and provide information about the depicted player's sports career.")
(comment BaseballCard "This is the collection of #$SportsCards that depict #$BaseballPlayers (usually professionals) and provide statistical information about their careers, such as batting averages, RBIs, ERAs, and so forth.")
(comment BasketballCard "This is the collection of #$SportsCards that depict #$BasketballPlayers (usually professionals) and provide statistical information about their careers.")
(comment AristotelianPredicate "An instance of #$PredicateType.  This is the collection of #$Predicates such that, when their names applied to the names of the sets or collections <b>S</b> and <b>P</b>, the resulting formulas express propositions (implicitly) having one of the following forms:
<pre>
  (A) All <b>S</b>s are <b>P</b>s
  (E) No <b>S</b>s are <b>P</b>s
  (I) Some <b>S</b>s are <b>P</b>s
  (O) Some <b>S</b>s are not <b>P</b>s
</pre>
(or the form of some conjunction of such forms).  For example, <code>(#$genls S P)</code> means that all <b>S</b>s are <b>P</b>s.  <code>(#$disjointWith S P)</code> means that no <b>S</b>s are <b>P</b>s.  <code>(#$orthogonalSetsOrCollections S P)</code> means that some <b>S</b>s are <b>P</b>s, some <b>S</b>s are not <b>P</b>s, and some <b>P</b>s are not <b>S</b>s.  
<p>
#$AristotelianPredicate gets its name from the fact that <code>(A),(E),(I)</code>, and <code>(O)</code> constitute the four corners of #$Aristotle's famous \"square of opposition\".")
(comment determinationArgIsaMinColls "<code>(#$determinationArgIsaMinColls DET SET-OF-MIN-COLLS)</code> relates a set of predicates that is the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination <code>DET</code> to the set of collections <code>SET</code>, where <code>SET</code> is determined by finding all of the #$argIsa collections on the predicates in <code>DET</code> and then reducing that set to the most specific collections (i.e., the collections that are not proper-subcollections of any elements of the original set).")
(comment orthogonalArgIsasLinkedByInDet-Specialization "<code>(#$orthogonalArgIsasLinkedByInDet-Specialization PRED1 PRED1-ARGN PRED2 PRED2-ARGN COL DET)</code> means <code>COL</code> is the collection intersection of two orthogonal collections -- namely, the constraining collections for the <code>PRED1-ARGN</code>th argument of <code>PRED1</code> and the <code>PRED2-ARGN</code>th argument of <code>PRED2</code>, when those constraining collections are considered relative to the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination <code>DET</code>. <code>COL</code> is constructed in the following way:
<p>
1. All of the #$argIsa assertions for the predicates in <code>DET</code> are compiled into a set <code>S</code>.<br>
2. <code>S</code> is then reduced to <code>S'</code>using the function #$MinCollectionsFn.<br> 
3. <code>COL</code> is composed of the two collections in <code>S'</code> that are sub-collections of the constraining collections for the relevant argument positions of <code>PRED1</code> and <code>PRED2</code>.")
(comment determinationGenlsCollections "An expression of the form (#$determinationGenlsCollections DET SET) relates an #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination to a set of collections.  The determination is a set of predicates.  SET is determined by finding all of the #$argGenl collections on the predicates of the determination and then reducing that set to the most specific collections (i.e., the collections that are not proper-subcollections of any elements of the original set).")
(comment totalPredicateListForDetermination "<code>(#$totalPredicateListForDetermination DET LIST-OF-PREDS)</code> means that for the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination <code>DET</code>, the list <code>LIST-OF-PREDS)</code> can be produced by joining the list of body predicates for the determination with the target predicate for the determination.")
(comment expressionPegSyntacticCategory "<code>(#$expressionPegSyntacticCategory PEG CATEGORY)</code> means that the instance <code>PEG</code> of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg is an occurrence of a sentential constituent of type <code>CATEGORY</code>.")
(comment bodyPredicateListForDetermination "<code>(#$bodyPredicateListForDetermination DET LIST-OF-PREDS)</code> means that for the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination <code>DET</code>, the list of body predicates is <code>LIST-OF-PREDS</code>.")
(comment PronounTypeByGender "The collection whose three instances are #$MasculinePronoun, #$FemininePronoun, and #$NeuterPronoun.")
(comment SingularPronoun "The collection of singular pronouns.")
(comment PronounTypeByNumber "The collection whose three instances are #$SingularPronoun, #$PluralPronoun, and #$DualPronoun.")
(comment PluralPronoun "The collection of plural pronouns.")
(comment DualPronoun "The collection of dual pronouns, which are used to refer to exactly two objects/subjects.  .")
(disjointWith ComputerProgrammer (CollectionDifferenceFn OntologicalEngineer ComputerProgrammer))
(disjointWith CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion War)
(disjointWith War BlockingTraffic)
(disjointWith HiringSomeoneToDoSomething War)
(disjointWith TakingCareOfSomething War)
(comment CoastGuardReserves "A specialization of #$Reserves-ArmedForces.  Each instance of #$CoastGuardReserves is a reserve military organization that is #$affiliatedWith a #$CoastGuardOrganization.")
(comment Jam-Foodstuff "The collection of all jams and jellies.  #$Jam-Foodstuff is a type of fruit preserve made by boiling fruit, sugar, and sometimes pectin.  If the fruit is strained out, it is more specifically an instance of #$Jelly-Foodstuff.")
(comment wineTypeOfWinery "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot that relates a #$Winery to the corresponding #$WineTypeByWinery.  <code>(#$wineTypeOfWinery WINERY WINETYPE)</code> means that <code>WINETYPE</code> is the collection of all #$Wine made by <code>WINERY</code>.  This is the predicate corresponding to #$WineOfWineryFn (q.v.).")
(comment targetPredicateForDetermination "<code>(#$targetPredicateForDetermination DET TARGET-PRED)</code> means that for the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination <code>DET</code>, the target predicate is <code>TARGET-PRED</code>.")
(comment semanticallyExcludedAntecedent "<code>(#$semanticallyExcludedAntecedent ANA ANTE)</code> means that the linguistic expression <code>ANTE</code> is excluded on semantic grounds from being a potential antecedent (see #$prospectiveAntecedent) for the anaphoric expression <code>ANA</code>.")
(comment CuttingSomethingOfTypeIntoPiecesFn "An instance of #$UnaryFunction, #$ReifiableFunction, and #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  (#$CuttingSomethingOfTypeIntoPiecesFn TYPE) returns the collection of all instances of #$CuttingInPieces in which the #$objectActedOn is an instance of TYPE, i.e., the collection of all #$Events in which one or more pieces are cut from an instance of TYPE.")
(comment PulverizingSomethingOfTypeFn "(#$PulverizingSomethingOfTypeFn TYPE) denotes the collection of all instances of #$PulverizationEvent in which the #$objectActedOn is an instance of TYPE.")
(comment GarlicClove "The part of the garlic plant that is most commonly used in cooking.  Each #$GarlicClove is technically a botanical bulb (see #$Bulb-ThePlantPart) from which a new garlic plant may be grown.")
(comment expressionAntecedent "A specialization of #$coreferentExpressions, <code>(#$expressionAntecedent ANA ANTE)</code> means that the linguistic expression peg (see #$LinguisticExpressionPeg) <code>ANTE</code> is the antecedent of the anaphoric expression peg <code>ANA</code>.")
(comment inconsistentWithMts "<code>(#$inconsistentWithMts SENTENCE MT-1 MT-2)</code> means that addition of <code>SENTENCE</code> to the union the microtheories <code>MT-1</code> and <code>MT-2</code> renders that union inconsistent.   #$inconsistentWithMts is thus a rule macro predicate, shorthand for <code>(#$inconsistentWithMt SENTENCE (#$MtUnionFn MT-1 MT-2))</code>.")
(comment semanticallyPossibleAntecedent "(#$nearestPossibleAntecedent DEP ANTE) means that the #$LinguisticObject ANTE has been identified as the, or one of the, \"nearest\" potential antecedent(s) for the referentially dependent expression DEP.  In general, this entails that no other potential antecedent for DEP has been used more recently than ANTE.")
(comment prospectiveAntecedent "A temporally qualifiable relation, <code>(#$prospectiveAntecedent ANE ANTE)</code> means that the linguistic expression <code>ANTE</code> is currently \"on the table\" as a candidate  antecedent for the anaphoric expression <code>ANA</code>.  As a discourse model develops and evidence about the grammatical and semantic properties of the two expressions are weighed, <code>(#$prospectiveAntecedent ANE ANTE)</code> can cease to hold.")
(comment queriesWithOperatorHaveDefaultInferenceMode "(#$queriesWithOperatorHaveDefaultInferenceMode TERM MODE) means that queries in which TERM is a constituent should have the inference mode parameter set to MODE.")
(comment May2006Tasks "The collection of tasks for which someone requested someone work on that task in May, 2006.  This is a temporary fix to make task related queries work faster for the projects meeting.  The permament solution is to automatically assert the appropriate month tag on assignments that had persons requested for a given month.")
(comment syntacticCategoryPresuppositionType "<code>(#$syntacticCategoryPresuppositionType CATEGORY TYPE)</code> means that the referents of expressions of syntactic category <code>CATEGORY</code> (see #$expressionPegSyntacticCategory) are typically presupposed to be instances of the collection <code>TYPE</code>.")
(comment Evaluating "A specialization of #$PurposefulAction and #$Thinking. Each instance of this collection is an event in which at least one agent evaluates some real-world thing or situation.  Notable specializations of #$Evaluating include #$MeasuringSomething, #$MedicalTesting, and #$Navigating. This collection differs from #$AnalyzingSomething in that the latter does not necessarily involve some extra-mental entity.")
(comment TextLearnerSupplementalLexiconMicrotheory "The collection of lexical microtheories used by the TextLearner (#$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) to generate a cyclifier lexicon (see #$CyclifierLexiconSpecification).")
(comment AssignmentHasRequestsForMonthFn "This function returns the collection of assignments that have had some work requested of them for the month that stands in arg1.  For example, (#$AssignmentHasRequestsForMonthFn (#$MonthFn May (#$YearFn 2006))) is the collection of assignments that someone has requested someone work on at some point in May, 2006.")
(comment assignmentHasRequestsForMonthColl "This is the corresponding predicate to the thatfunction returns the collection of assignments that have had some work requested of them for the month that stands in arg1.  For example, (#$AssignmentHasRequestsForMonthFn (#$MonthFn May (#$YearFn 2006))) is the collection of assignments that someone has requested someone work on at some point in May, 2006.")
(comment TextLearnerGarbageCollectionQuerySpecification "The collection of instances of #$CycLQuerySpecification that are run by the Text Learner (see #$TheCycTextLearnerProgram) to remove content that, though necessary for the processing of a particular sentence or paragraph, would potentially adversely affect the ability to process subsequent sentences or paragraphs.")
(comment VerbNetConstant-VN2_0 "The collection of constants used to represent #$VerbNet-Version2_0 in the #$CycKB.")
(comment FixedSizeComputerDatatypeType "The collection of all computer data types such that all program variables or database fields which are typed by a particular instance of this collection all have the same amount of memory allocated to store their values, whether or not the value could be stored in less space.  For example, the base 10 integer, 250, could be stored in a field or variable that is typed TINYINT, or #$1ByteIntegerDatatype.  In this case, one byte of storage is allocated to hold the value.  It can also be stored as a #$4ByteIntegerDatatype, and then 4 bytes of storage will be allocated, even though it could fit in a smaller space. ")
(comment DecimalRealNumberOfMaximumPrecisionFn "(#$DecimalRealNumberOfMaximumPrecisionFn PRECISION SCALE) denotes an instance of #$ComputerDatatypeType which is also a #$RealNumberDatatype, in that the values stored in variables or fields having this type are #$RealNumbers.  However, not every #$RealNumber can be stored in a variable or field of this type. PRECISION states the total maximum number of significant digits allowed in a value, and SCALE give the maximum number of digits to the right of the decimal point.  The amount of storage allocated to a variable or field of this type varies according to the PRECISION given.  A datatype denoted by (#$DecimalRealNumberOfMaximumPrecisionFn PRECISION SCALE) can store only real numbers which are precisely representable as decimal numbers within the PRECISION and SCALE limits provided.")
(comment CompositeDatatypeType "A specialization of #$ComputerDatatypeType. 
The collection of all data types that are compositional in nature, usually
consisting of several named data fields. Examples include structs in 
#$C-ProgrammingLanguage and objects in #$JavaProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment ObjectReferenceDatatypeType "A specialization of #$ComputerDatatypeType. The collection of data types whose elements reference or
\"point\" to another program object. In some programming languages (such as #$C-ProgrammingLanguage) 
object references may actually
be implemented as physical memory addresses.")
(comment VoidPointer "An instance of #$ObjectReferenceDatatypeType. The void pointer type references untyped physical memory. Such pointers
are typically returned by dynamic memory allocation routines.")
(comment PointerFn "A unary collection-denoting function. (#$PointerFn TYPE) denotes the data type of object references to
program representations that have type TYPE. For example, (#$PointerFn #$CharacterObject) denotes the data type
of character pointers in, say, the #$C-ProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment DefinedDatatypeType "A specialization of #$ComputerDatatypeType. 
The collection of all data types that are currently available for 
parameters in the current context of a #$ComputerCode-Source. Negative
examples for this collection would include abstract classes in 
#$JavaProgrammingLanguage, and underspecified data types like
(#$ProgramRepresentationFn #$RealNumber).")
(comment GeostationarySatellite "#$GeostationarySatellite is the collection of satellites
(#$Satellite-Device) which orbit the #$PlanetEarth in the equatorial
plane at an altitude of approximately (#$Mile 22300), which keeps them
stationary with respect to a fixed point on the #$EarthsTopographicSurface. Often
#$CommunicationsSatellites are put into geostationary orbits, since
their fixed position enables Earth bound users to point their
satellite receivers at a fixed position in the sky.")
(comment DecodeStringToDMSLatitudeFn "This function is used to convert the string representation of an angular distance that determines a latitude value in degree-minute-second format into a proper instance of #$AngularDistance, using the functions #$Degree-DMS, #$Minute-DMS, and #$Second-DMS. The function makes several assumptions about the format of the input strings:<p>
1. The strings are all of length seven.<p>
2. The first six characters of the string should all be numerals.<p>
3. The first two characters determine the value for the degree part of the angular distance, which should be between 0 and 90 inclusively.<p>
4. The third and fourth characters determine the value for the minute part of the angular distance, and the fifth and sixth characters determine the second part of the angular distance. Both should be nonnegative and strictly less than 60.<p>
5. The seventh character should be either 'N' or 'S'; this character indicates whether the latitude is above or below the equator; if the character is 'S', then the degree part of the angular distance is translated with a minus sign (-).<p>
Positive examples of valid input strings include '352106N' and '042154S'. Negative examples include '913254N' and '-27932S'.")
(comment EncodeLatitudeToDMSStringFn "This function is used to convert an angular distance that determines a latitude value into a fixed length string that encodes the angular distance in degree-minute-second format. The output string will be formatted to satisfy all of the following:<p>
1. The strings are all of length seven.<p>
2. The first six characters are all numerals.<p>
3. The first two characters determine the value for the degree part of the angular distance, and will be between 0 and 90 inclusively.<p>
4. The third and fourth characters determine the value for the minute part of the angular distance, and the fifth and sixth characters determine the second part of the angular distance. Both will be nonnegative and strictly less than 60.<p>
5. The seventh character will be either 'N' or 'S'; this character is determined by the sign of the angular distance. A negative angular distance corresponds to 'S' and positive to 'N'.")
(comment DecodeStringToDMSLongitudeFn "This function is used to convert the string representation of an angular distance that determines a longitude value in degree-minute-second format into a proper instance of #$AngularDistance, using the functions #$Degree-DMS, #$Minute-DMS, and #$Second-DMS. The function makes several assumptions about the format of the input strings:<p>
1. The strings are all of length eight.<p>
2. The first seven characters of the string should all be numerals. <p>
3. The first three characters determine the value for the degree part of the angular distance, which should be between 0 and 180 inclusively.<p>
4. The fourth and fifth characters determine the value for the minute part of the angular distance, and the sixth and seventh characters determine the second part of the angular distance. Both should be nonnegative and strictly less than 60.<p>
5. The eighth character should be either 'E' or 'W'; this character indicates whether the longitude is east or west of the prime meridian; if the character is 'W', then the degree part of the angular distance is translated with a minus sign (-). <p>
Positive examples of valid input strings include '1352106W' and '0042154E'. Negative examples include '1913254E' and '-027932W'. See also the corresponding function #$EncodeLongitudeToDMSStringFn.")
(comment EncodeLongitudeToDMSStringFn "This function is used to convert an angular distance that determines a longitude value into a fixed length string that represents the angular distance in degree-minute-second format. The output string will be formatted to satisfy all of the following:<p>
1. The strings are all of length eight.<p>
2. The first seven characters are all numerals.<p>
3. The first three characters determine the value for the degree part of the angular distance, and will be between 0 and 180 inclusively.<p>
4. The fourth and fifth characters determine the value for the minute part of the angular distance, and the sixth and seventh characters determine the second part of the angular distance. Both will be nonnegative and strictly less than 60.<p>
5. The eighth character will be either 'E' or 'W'; this character is determined by the sign of the angular distance. A negative angular distance corresponds to 'W' and positive to 'E'.<p>
See also the corresponding function #$DecodeStringToDMSLongitudeFn.")
(comment JDBCDatatypeDescribedByFn "(#$JDBCDatatypeDescribedByFn ID COLUMN-SIZE DIGITS TYPE-NAME) denotes a fully-specified #$ComputerDatatypeType described in the #$JDBCDatabaseProtocol's column metadata as having a base type identified by ID, an (optional) column size of COLUMN-SIZE (which gives the precision for numeric types and the string length or bit length for character or bit string types), an (optional) scale in the case of numeric types, and a DBMS-dependent type name of TYPE-NAME.  If values are not provided for the optional arguments, they are recorded as :NULL.")
(comment BitStringOfMaximumLengthTypeFn "(#$BitStringOfMaximumLengthTypeFn N) is a function denoting the collection of all bit strings up to length N.")
(comment Solubility "A specialization of #$DispositionalQuantity.  Each instance of #$Solubility describes how readily some tangible substance dissolves in some kind of liquid.  Different levels of #$Solubility may be represented with #$GenericValueFunctions or qualitatively (e.g., #$WillDissolve, #$Insoluble).")
(comment aggregateCNCResultForRuleWithThresholds "(#$aggregateCNCResultForRuleWithThresholds RULE COMPOUND-THRESHOLD COMPONENT-THRESHOLD RESULT SEARCH-ENGINE) means that RESULT--a list of various data used in evaluating RULE--was obtained by evaluating RULE wrt. COMPONENT-THRESHOLD and COMPOUND-THRESHOLD.  The COMPOUND-THRESHOLD value represents a minimum number of hits which a compound-strings must have for candidate compounds to be regarded as sufficiently evident within a corpus to be of interest.  The COMPONENT-THRESHOLD value represents a minimum number of hits which the modifier- and head-strings for candiate compounds must have for candidates to count as vaild tests wrt. the corpus.")
(comment SRIDForGeometryFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'srid' which is defined
in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL. Given a valid instance of #$OpenGISGeometry, the evaluation of this function by a compliant dbms should return the indentification number for the spatial reference system (#$CoordinateSystem) in which the geometry's coordinate values are represented. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction, #$CSQLSRIDForGeometryFn.")
(comment NounCompoundRuleVocabularyConstant "The collection of instances of #$CycLConstant that are used in the reprepresentation of and reasoning about noun compound rules (#$NounCompoundRule, q.v.).")
(comment ncRuleReificationRelationGeneralityCutoffs "<code>(#$ncRuleReificationRelationGeneralityCutoffs PREDICATE)</code> identifies <code>PREDICATE</code> as a generality limit on when a new NC rule should be constructed such that that rule has a #$semanticRelationForNCR constraint.  For example, <code>(#$ncRuleReificationRelationGeneralityCutoffs #$actors)</code> means that Cyc should not reify a new NC rule <code>RULE</code> such that <code>#$semanticRelationForNCR RULE PRED)</code> where <code>(#$genlPreds #$actors PRED)</code>.  As such, #$ncRuleReificationRelationGeneralityCutoffs is transitive via #$genlPreds in its argument.")
(comment AesopsFable "Stories thought to be authored by the Greek storyteller, Aesop.  Animals are the main characters.")
(comment CoordinateSystemForGeometryFn "This function returns the coordinate system in which a
geometry's coordinates are represented. While not evaluatable in
general, it is enabled when the geometry is stored in a PostGIS
spatial database accessible to Cyc via SKSI.")
(comment CSQLPostGISCoordinateTransformFn "A #$CSQLFunction corresponding to the
#$SKSISupportedFunction, #$PostGISCoordinateTransformFn, which is
translated by SKSI to the PostGIS SQL function 'transform'.")
(comment PostGISCoordinateTransformFn "This function is used by SKSI to transform the coordinate
system used to represent geometries stored in a PostGIS enhanced
database. <code>(#$PostGISCoordinateTransformFn GEOMETRY SRID)</code>
denotes <code>GEOMETRY</code> but with coordinates in the
#$CoordinateSystem with spatial reference id (see
#$sridForCoordinateSystem) <code>SRID</code>.")
(comment GeometryCoordinateTransformFn "This function is used to transform the coordinate system
with respect to which a given geometry is represented.
<code>(#$GeometryCoordinateTransformFn GEOMETRY SYSTEM)</code> denotes
<code>GEOMETRY</code> but with coordinates in the #$CoordinateSystem
<code>SYSTEM</code>. In practice, this function is just a wrapper
around #$PostGISCoordinateTransformFn, which is actually used by SKSI
to perform coordiante transformations on geometries stored in a
PostGIS enabled spatial database. Here, however, we reference the
target coordinate system directly, instead of its spatial reference id
(see #$sridForCoordinateSystem).")
(comment BitStringOfLengthTypeFn "(#$BitStringOfLengthTypeFn N) denotes a #$ComputerDatatypeType which holds strings of up to N bits in fixed size storage.  Some database manager implementations, such as #$PostgreSQL, require bit data to be exactly the length specified, while others may truncate or zero-pad as necessary to use exactly N bits.")
(comment BitData "The #$ComputerDatatypeType which can store a single bit as data.")
(comment UnlimitedSizeComputerDatatypeType "The collection of all computer data types such that program variables or database fields which are typed by a particular instance of this collection have a variable amount of storage, with no limit specified by the user, allocated to store their data.  The actual amount of storage used by an instance of such a type depends on the data stored there.  For example, the #$PostgreSQL TEXT datatype is a variable-size datatype of unlimited length. A field of this type may store character strings of length ten or ten thousand or more. Note that \"Unlimited\" in this context means \"not limited at field creation time;\" i.e., there is no parameter given to specify a maximum size for a TEXT field. In practice, though, a field typed by an instance of  #$UnlimitedSizeComputerDatatypeType will have SOME limit imposed by available disk, memory, or the DBMS implementation.")
(comment VariableLimitedSizeDatatypeType "The collection of all computer data types such that program variables or database fields which are typed by a particular instance of this collection have a variable amount of storage allocated to store their values, depending on the particular datum stored there.  Furthermore, at the time of field creation or variable definition, the user specifies the maximum amount of storage that can be used.   For example, fields typed by the SQL type VARCHAR(8), aka (#$StringOfMaximumLengthFn 8), will allow strings as long as eight characters to be stored, but no longer strings, and if a shorter string is stored in that field, the amount of storage used will be only what is required to hold the characters of the string.  The DBMS implementation will not allow strings longer than 8 characters to be stored in a VARCHAR(8) field.")
(disjointWith VariableLimitedSizeDatatypeType FixedSizeComputerDatatypeType)
(disjointWith UnlimitedSizeComputerDatatypeType VariableLimitedSizeDatatypeType)
(comment StringDatatype-Unlimited "A character string data type of unlimited length.  Database fields or program variables having this type store character strings that are not limited to a size specified at the time the variable or field was introduced.  In practice, however, the particular program implementation, operating system or available machine resources will impose SOME limit on the size of the strings that cab be stored in such a variable or field.")
(comment BooleanObject "An instance of #$ComputerDatatypeType. The collection of two-valued data
structures, typically used to represent #$True and #$False in computer
programs. Instances of this collection are expected to be of an explict boolean type. While many expressions may have a non-zero value, and hence \"evaluate to true\", they are not necessarily booleans")
(comment DoubleFloatObject "An instance of #$ComputerDatatypeType. The double
precision floating point number representation in, for example, the #$C-ProgrammingLanguage.")
(disjointWith UnlimitedSizeComputerDatatypeType FixedSizeComputerDatatypeType)
(comment BinaryObject "The #$ComputerDatatypeType such that instances store strings of binary bytes, aka octets.  Such strings are more permissive than character strings, which (depending on the character encoding standard used by the program implementation) disallow the presence of certain bytes, such as zero-bytes and non-printable bytes.  Program variables and database fields typed as #$BinaryObject or one of its specializations are intended to store \"raw bytes\", independent of a character encoding.")
(comment BinaryDatatype-Unlimited "A #$ComputerDatatypeType for storing binary data (strings of octets).  Program variables and database fields having this datatype are not limited in size at the time of their definition, but are of course limited in practice by OS, software implementation, or machine resources.   Any particular instantiation of a field or variable with the type #$BinaryDatatype-Unlimited will have an amount of storage allocated that is determined by the actual datum stored there.")
(comment BinaryStringOfMaximumLengthFn "(#$BinaryStringOfMaximumLengthFn N) denotes the #$BinaryObject datatype which causes up to N binary bytes (aka octets) of storage to be allocated for program variables or database fields of this type.  The actual amount of storage allocated in any instantiation of this type will depend on the datum stored there. N is the upper limit to the amount of storage that will be used; program implementations will signal an error or perform unpredictably if there is an attempt to store a larger datum in such a variable or field.")
(comment BinaryStringOfLengthFn "(#$BinaryStringOfLengthFn N) denotes the #$BinaryObject datatype which cause exactly N binary bytes (aka octets) to be allocated for any datum stored in a program variable or field having this type, irrespective of whether the datum would fit in a smaller space or not.")
(comment Viaduct "The collection of instances of #$Bridge that connect two regions of equal height.")
(comment WaterMain "The collection of networks of pipes that occur as part of a water-supply system (see #$PublicWaterSystem) and whose end points are the \"nodes\" of the system -- i.e., are points of delivery for the water being supplied.")
(disjointWith Pipe-GenericConduit ConvexTangibleObject)
(disjointWith HumanlyOccupiedSpatialObject PipingSystem-Generic)
(comment layers "<code>(#$layers OBJECT LAYER)</code> identifies <code>LAYER</code>, a tangible part of <code>OBJECT</code>, as one <code>OBJECT</code>'s layers.")
(comment ThatchedRoof "The collection of thatched roofs, roofs made from vegetation, such as straw or water reed.")
(comment Thatching "A specialization of #$Roofing.  Instances are events in which one builds or repairs a roof using vegetation (thatch -- #$Thatch-BuildingMaterial, q.v.).")
(comment RoofingActivityTypeByMaterialTypeUsed "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection that facets the collection of roofing events (#$Roofing, q.v.,) by the type of material used in the construction or repair of a roof.")
(comment Thatch-BuildingMaterial "A kind of building material formed by layering straw, water reed, or other suitable instances of vegetable matter (#$VegetableMatter, q.v.) used in the construction of roofs.  See also #$Thatching and #$ThatchedRoof.")
(disjointWith VegetableMatter Hide-Product)
(comment Tannery-Organization "A specializaton of #$ManufacturingOrganization.  Tanneries are organizations that produce leather (or instances of #$Hide-Product, generally) from animal skins.")
(comment FG1-KS "#$FG1-KS is the top-level structured knowledge source repesentation of a fused database created to encompass all information represented in both #$Usgs-KS and in #$NGA-KS. ")
(comment CSQLDirectionFromToFn "The #$CSQLFunction corresponding to #$DirectionFromToFn.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-DistanceTime "A specialization of #$PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem-DistanceTime is a description of a distance-time problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-1DKinematics "A specialization of #$PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem-1DKinematics is a description of a 1-D kinematics problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-ConstantAcceleration "A specialization of #$PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem-ConstantAcceleration is a description of a constant acceleration problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-DistanceVelocity "A specialization of #$PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of #$PhysicsProblem-DistanceTime is a description of a distance-velocity problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment argIsaWRTDeterminationArgIsaMinColls "(#$argIsaWRTDeterminationArgIsaMinColls PRED N COLLECTION DET) means that relative to the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination DET values for the Nth argument of the #$Predicate PRED are constrained to instances of the #$Collection COLL.")
(comment nearestCommonGenlsOfSet "<code>(#$nearestCommonGenlsOfSet SET-OF-COLS NEAR-GENL)</code> means that
<code>NEAR-GENL</code> is the nearest collection of which each element in <code>SET-OF-COLLS</code> is a specialization. 
<p>
For example, <code>(#$nearestCommonGenlsOfSet (#$TheSet #$Monarch-HeadOfState #$MaleAnimal #$FemaleAnimal) #$Animal)</code> means that #$Animal is the nearest collection of which #$Monarch-HeadOfState, #$MaleAnimal, and #$FemaleAnimal are each specializations.
<p>
Similarly, (#$nearestCommonGenlsOfSet (#$TheSet #$FemaleAnimal) #$FemaleAnimal) is trivially true.")
(comment maxSubsetConsistentWithElement "<code>(#$maxSubsetConsistentWithElement SET-OF-COLLS COLL SUBSET-OF-COLLS)</code> means that every element of <code>SET-OF-COLLS</code> that is not disjoint with <code>COLL</code> is an element of <code>SUBSET-OF-COLLS</code>, and that <code>SUBSET-OF-COLLS</code> is a subset of <code>SET-OF-COLLS</code>.
<p>
For example, 
<p>
<pre>
<code>(#$maxSubsetConsistentWithElement (#$TheSet #$Monarch-HeadOfState #$FemaleAnimal #$War) #$FemaleAnimal (#$TheSet #$FemaleAnimal #$Monarch-HeadOfState))</code>
</pre>
<p>
means that:<br>
1. (#$TheSet #$FemaleAnimal #$Monarch-HeadOfState) is a subset of (#$TheSet #$Monarch-HeadOfState #$FemaleAnimal #$War), and <br>
2. every element of (#$TheSet #$Monarch-HeadOfState #$FemaleAnimal) is is not disjoint with #$FemaleAnimal.")
(comment MaxSubsetConsistentWithElementFn "<code>(#$MaxSubsetConsistentWithElementFn SET-OF-COLLS COLL)</code> denotes 
<code>SUBSET-OF-COLLS</code>, where every element of <code>SET-OF-COLLS</code> that is not disjoint with <code>COLL</code> is an element of <code>SUBSET-OF-COLLS</code>, and <code>SUBSET-OF-COLLS</code> is a subset of <code>SET-OF-COLLS</code>.")
(comment collectionSalientSharedFeatureAssertions "This predicate relates a collection to an assertion that states a salient feature that all instances of that collection share.  For example:
<pre>
(#$collectionSalientSharedFeatureAssertions #$Insect (#$relationAllExistsCount #$anatomicalParts #$Insect #$Leg 6))
</pre>
")
(comment MaxCollectionsFn "An #$EvaluatableFunction (q.v.). <code>(#$MaxCollectionsFn SET-OF-COLS)</code> denotes the subset of <code>SET-OF-COLS</code> which consists of only the collections in <code>SET-OF-COLS</code> that have no proper generalizations among <code>SET-OF-COLS</code>.  For example,  (#$MaxCollectionsFn (#$TheSet #$Thing #$Dog)) would evaluate to (#$TheSet #$Thing).")
(comment argIsaWRTDeterminationArgIsaMaxColls "(#$argIsaWRTDeterminationArgIsaMaxColls PRED N COLLECTION DET) means that relative to the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination DET, and the maximumly general set of collections that is a subset of the asserted arg constraints on the predicates in DET, values for the Nth argument of the #$Predicate PRED are constrained to instances of the #$Collection COLL.")
(comment FacetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverseFn "A binary function that takes a collection and a type-level binary predicate to the #$FacetingCollectionType (if any) that facets the collection on the basis of the predicate.  (#$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverseFn <code>COL BINPRED</code>) is the faceting-collection-type <code>FACET</code> such that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverse FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverse.")
(comment expirationDate "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a #$TemporalThing to a #$Date.  (#$expirationDate THING DATE) means that DATE is the expiration date for THING.")
(comment determinationArgIsaMaxColls "<code>(#$determinationArgIsaMaxColls DET SET-OF-MAX-COLLS)</code> relates a set of predicates that is the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination <code>DET</code> to the set of collections <code>SET</code>, where <code>SET</code> is determined by finding all of the #$argIsa collections on the predicates in <code>DET</code> and then reducing that set to the most general collections (i.e., the collections that are just the supercollections of any elements of the original set).")
(comment determinationArgIsaColls "<code>(#$determinationArgIsaColls DET SET-OF-COLLS)</code> relates a set of predicates that is the #$InductiveLogicProgrammingDetermination <code>DET</code> to the set of collections <code>SET</code>, where <code>SET</code> is all of the asserted #$argIsa collections on the predicates in <code>DET</code>.  Cf. #$determinationArgIsaMinColls and #$determinationArgIsaMaxColls.")
(comment primarySupervisor "(#$primarySupervisor AGT1 AGT2) means that AGT2 is the default #$directingAgent in any work-related action in which AGT1 is a #$deliberateActors. One common override for this default relationship is when the action is a project or subevent of a project. In that case the #$projectLeader will be the directing agent in the event.")
(comment CycLDecimalDegreeTerm "The collection of all #$CycLClosedNonAtomicTerms of the form
<p>
<code>(#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure DD)</code><p>
<code>(#$Minute-UnitOfAngularMeasure DD)</code><p>
<code>(#$Second-UnitOfAngularMeasure DD)</code><p>
<p>
where <code>DD</code> is a #$ScalarInterval.")
(comment CycLDegreeMinuteSecondTerm "The collection of all #$CycLClosedNonAtomicTerms of the form
<p>
<code>(#$Degree-DMS D (#$Minute-DMS M (#$Second-DMS S)))</code><p>
<p>
for where <code>D</code>, is an integer <code>M</code>, is an integer between 0 and 59 inclusive, and <code>S</code> is a real number greater than or equal to 0 and strictly less than 60.")
(disjointWith CycLDecimalDegreeTerm CycLDegreeMinuteSecondTerm)
(comment FolderForCSTsAboutFn "(#$FolderForCSTsAboutFn THING) is the #$KEInteractionFolder in which to place #$CommonSenseTests about, or on the topic of, THING.  For example, a common sense test about artifacts should be placed in (#$FolderForCSTsAboutFn #$Artifact).")
(comment DecimalDegreeToDMSDegreeFn "A function which converts an #$AngularDistance expressed in 'decimal-degrees' format (see #$CycLDecimalDegreeTerm) into an equivalent #$AngularDistance expressed in 'degree-minute-second' format (see #$CycLDegreeMinuteSecondTerm).")
(disjointWith SportsTrack DrivingRange)
(comment SportsTrack "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea.  #$SportsTrack is the collection of all tracks used for sports events.  Specializations of this collection include #$RaceTrack (whose instances are used for head-to-head races) and #$BobsledRun (on whose instances athletes compete one at a time).")
(comment reviewedByAgentForTask "(reviewedByAgentForTask THING AGENT TASK) means that THING was reviewed by AGENT as part of TASK.")
(disjointWith TenderObject SomethingToWear)
(comment OGCGeometrySumFn "#$OGCGeometrySumFn is a spec function of #$SpatialSumFn and was introduced exclusively to be supported by SKSI so that queries using it can be translated directly into SQL queries using the function
<code>union</code> defined in the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL.")
(comment maximalConsistentSubsets "<code>(#$maximalConsistentSubsets SET SUBSET)</code> means that:
<p>
1. <code>SET</code>and <code>SUBSET</code> are sets of collections,<br>
2. <code>SUBSET</code> is a subset of <code>SET</code>,<br>
3. no element of <code>SUBSET</code> is disjoint with any other element of <code>SUBSET</code>,<br>
4. every element of the complement of <code>SUBSET</code> with respect to <code>SET</code> is disjoint with some element of <code>SUBSET</code>.<br>
<p>
This predicate is not functional in either argument position.")
(comment DecodeIntegerToDMSFn "This function is used to convert an integer into an angular distance expressed in 'degree-minute-second' format (see #$CycLDegreeMinuteSecondTerm). The input integer is interpreted such that:
<p>
1. The last two digits determine the arc seconds of the angular distance (rounded down);<p>
2. The two next to last two digits determine the arc minutes of the angular distance, and should be strictly less than 60;<p>
3. The remaining two or three digits determine the arc degrees of the angular distance.")
(comment EncodeDMSToIntegerFn "This function is used to convert an angular distance expressed in 'degree-minute-second' format (see #$CycLDegreeMinuteSecondTerm) into an integer. The integer is such that:
<p>
1. The last two digits determine the arc seconds of the angular distance (rounded down);<p>
2. The two next to last two digits determine the arc minutes of the angular distance, and should be strictly less than 60;<p>
3. The remaining two or three digits determine the arc degrees of the angular distance.")
(disjointWith BodyOfWater-Generic GaseousTangibleThing)
(comment ModifyingARecordInADatabase "#$ModifyingARecordInADatabase is the collection of all events in which an agent (human or computer) modifies (inserts, updates, or deletes) a record in a database.")
(comment DeletingARecordFromADatabase "#$DeletingARecordFromADatabase is the collection of all events in which an agent (human or computer) deletes a record from a database.")
(comment InsertingARecordIntoADatabase "#$InsertingARecordIntoADatabase is the collection of all events in which an agent (human or computer) inserts a record into a database.")
(comment UpdatingARecordInADatabase "#$UpdatingARecordInADatabase is the collection of all events in which an agent (human or computer) updates a record in a database.")
(comment EnforcingADatabaseReferentialIntegrityConstraint "#$EnforcingADatabaseReferentialIntegrityConstraint is the collection of all events in which a database management system enforces a referential integrity constraint that is imposed upon one or more fields in a database. It is faceted by #$DatabaseReferentialIntegrityConstraintType.")
(comment DatabaseReferentialIntegrityConstraintType "#$DatabaseReferentialIntegrityConstraintType is the collection of selected specializations of #$EnforcingADatabaseReferentialIntegrityConstraint which correspond to the types of referential integrity constraints that a database management system enforces between the primary key of a database table and references to that key from within the same, or other tables.")
(comment RestrictingADatabaseModification "#$RestrictingADatabaseModification is the collection of events in which some database management system restricts an agent from modifying a record in a database that is referenced by another record in the database. The restriction can either happen eagerly (see #$ImmediatelyRestrictingADatabaseModification) or lazily (see #$LazilyRestrictingADatabaseModification).")
(comment CascadingADatabaseModification "#$CascadingADatabaseModification is the collection of events in which some database management system cascades the modifications an agent makes to a record in a database to be reflected into all records that reference the modified record.")
(comment SettingToDefaultInADatabaseModification "#$SettingToDefaultInADatabaseModification is the collection of events in which some database management system sets to their default values all references to a record in a database that is modified by an agent.")
(comment SettingToNullInADatabaseModification "#$SettingToDefaultInADatabaseModification is the collection of events in which some database management system sets to null all references to a record in a database that is modified by an agent.")
(comment ImmediatelyRestrictingADatabaseModification "#$ImmediatelyRestrictingADatabaseModification is the collection of events in which some database management system immediately restricts an agent from modifying a record in a database that is referenced by another record in the database. The difference between these events and #$LazilyRestrictingADatabaseModification is that here the database management system checks for integrity violation at the time that the modification is requested within a larger transaction block, whereas in the latter case the database management system checks the constraint at the end of a transaction block.")
(comment LazilyRestrictingADatabaseModification "#$LazilyRestrictingADatabaseModification is the collection of events in which some database management system lazily restricts an agent from modifying a record in a database that is referenced by another record in the database. The difference between these events and #$ImmediatelyRestrictingADatabaseModification is that in the latter case the database management system checks for integrity violation at the time that the modification is requested within a larger transaction block, whereas in this case the database management system checks the constraint at the end of a transaction block")
(comment DatabaseModificationType "#$DatabaseModificationType is a partitioning (See #$facets-Partition) collection for #$ModifyingARecordInADatabase. Its three members are #$DeletingARecordFromADatabase, #$InsertingARecordIntoADatabase, and #$UpdatingARecordInADatabase.")
(comment foreignKeyConstraintWithActionType "<code>(#$foreignKeyConstraintWithActionType REFERENCED-SCHEMA REFERENCED-KEY REFERENCING-SCHEMA REFERENCING-FIELD-LIST MODIFY-TYPE RESTRICTION-TYPE)</code> means that: <p>
1. A foreign key reference (see #$foreignKeyMap) holds between <code>REFERENCED-SCHEMA</code>, <code>REFERENCED-KEY</code>, <code>REFERENCING-SCHEMA</code>, and <code>REFERENCING-FIELD-LIST</code>;<p>
2. Whenever an agent attempts to perform a database modification of type <code>MODIFY-TYPE</code> on a record in the table corresponding to <code>REFERENCED-SCHEMA</code> that will modify one or more of the values of the <code>REFERENCED-KEY</code> fields, then the database management system will enforce an integrity constraint of type <code>RESTRICTION-TYPE</code>.")
(comment borderBetweenPolities "#$borderBetweenPolities BORDER GEO-1 GEO-2) holds just in case BORDER is the border between GEO-1 and GEO-2.  This is the corresponding predicate to #$BorderBetweenPolitiesFn.")
(comment BorderBetweenPolitiesFn "(#$BorderBetweenPolitiesFn GEO-1 GEO-2) applied to two #$GeopoliticalEntitys returns the entire intangible Border (if any) that separates their territories.  The border returned by #$BorderBetweenPolitiesFn may be discontinuous (such as the border between France and Spain, interrupted by Andorra), or circular (such as the border between Italy and San Marino), or in abnormal cases it may even have multiple disconnected and nested cyclic fragments (the border between Belgium and Netherlands, due to the Baarle-Nassau and Baarle-Hartog enclaves), or move back and forth periodically (such as at the Isle des Faisans).  In any case, (#$BorderBetweenPolitiesFn GEO-1 GEO-2) must denote exactly one object (if it denotes at all) since #$BorderBetweenPolitiesFn is a function.  #$BorderBetweenPolitiesFn is undefined for pairs of  #$GeopoliticalEntitys whose territories do not actually share a border, such as #$Albania and #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica.  See also #$politiesBorderEachOther, assertions using that predicate express a necessary and sufficient condition on the entities involved returning a value with this function and #$BorderBetweenFn which defines the border between two #$GeographicalRegions.")
(comment CollectionSalientAttributePredicate "Each instance of this collection is a #$BinaryPredicate that relates a #$Collection COL to a #$Thing (usually a #$Predicate or #$Collection) that in some way correponds to a \"salient attribute\" with respect to COL.")
(comment SpatiallyDisjointRegionType "A collection of collections, and a specialization of #$RegionType.  Each instance of #$SpatiallyDisjointRegionType is a collection of geographical regions, where each of the regions in the collection is spatially disjoint with the other regions in the collection.  For example, the collection #$State-UnitedStates is an instance of #$SpatiallyDisjointRegionType, since the territories of U.S. states do not overlap.  Other instances of #$SpatiallyDisjointRegionType include #$CanadianProvince, #$IndependentCountry, #$City, and #$Colony-GeopoliticalEntity.  A non-example is #$EcologicalRegion, since ecological regions can overlap.")
(disjointWith SportsCourt AthleticField)
(disjointWith SportsTrack AthleticField)
(comment SportsCourse "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea.  Each instance of #$SportsCourse is an outdoor area used for a sport in which players proceed through or along a designated course and perform a number of tasks in a specified order.  This includes #$GolfCourses, obstacle courses used for competitions, and equestrian steeplechase courses.")
(disjointWith SkiSportsArea AthleticField)
(comment SkiSportsArea "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea and #$SkiArea.  Each instance of #$SkiSportsArea is an area on or over which people ski.  Specializations include #$SkiSlope and #$SkiJump.  
<p>
Note that certain types of #$SkiAreas (e.g. a ski resort), are not themselves instances of #$SkiSportsArea, though various proper subregions of them are ski sports areas.")
(comment TargetRange "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea.  Each instance of #$TargetRange is a sports area where players attempt to hit the center of a target with a projectile of some sort.  Examples include #$ArcheryRanges and shooting ranges.")
(comment GolfingArea "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea.  Each instance of #$GolfCourse is an grass-covered outdoor area used for playing #$Golf.")
(disjointWith SportsRing AthleticField)
(comment SportsRing "Where a #$BoxingSportsEvent occurs.")
(comment SportsRing "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea.  Each instance of #$SportsRing is is an area (usually square, with its perimeter roped off) designed for some type combative sports events.  Specializations include #$BoxingRing and #$WrestlingRing.")
(comment FieldSportArea "A specialization of #$SportsPlayingArea.  Each instance of #$FieldSportArea is an area designed for playing some particular type of #$FieldSportsEvent (q.v.).  Specializations include #$HighJumpArea and #$PolevaultArea.")
(comment GoalScoringRegion "Players of certain games e.g. football, hockey, basketball, golf have the objective of getting a ball, puck etc in (see #$spatiallySubsumes) a particular element of #$GoalScoringRegion.  Typically success in this objective is a significant event of the game in question and typically entails that a point or several points is scored for the player's team.  Positive exemplars include the tube of space which a basket ball net surrounds, the space between football goal posts, an end zone in football, or the cavity of a golf ball hole on a golf course.  Negative exemplars include a football goal post, or a soccer goal net.")
(disjointWith SpatiallyDisjointObjectType ExistingStuffType)
(comment usgsFeatureID "<code>(#$usgsFeatureID PLACE IDNUM)</code> states that the #$USGeologicalSurvey uses the #$Integer, <code>IDNUM</code>, as the unique identifier for the #$GeographicalPlace <code>PLACE</code>.  Currently, only geographic features in the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica or its territories are assigned a numer as a #$usgsFeatureID.")
(comment ngaUniqueFeatureIdentifier "<code>(#$ngaUniqueFeatureIdentifier PLACE IDNUM)</code> states that the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US uses the #$Integer, <code>IDNUM</code>, as the unique identifier for the #$GeographicalPlace <code>PLACE</code>.  IDNUM may be positive or negative.  Currently, only geographic features outside the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and its territories are assigned a number as a #$ngaUniqueFeatureIdentifier.")
(comment ebmtlMeaningOfStringFragment "<code>(#$ebmtlMeaningOfStringFragment STRING SUBSTRING-SET ASSERTION)</code> means that in the context of example-based machine translation to logic, the combination of strings in SUBSTRING-SET means ASSERTION or expresses the proposition given by ASSERTION, when this combination occurs in STRING.")
(comment ebmtlNLToCycLExpressionFromStringFragment "<code>(#$ebmtlNLToCycLExpressionFromStringFragment STRING SUBSTRING-SET QUOTE)</code> means that the #$CycLExpression QUOTE is an acceptable parse target for the combination of string fragments SUBSTRING-SET appearing in STRING, in the context of Cyc example-based machine translation to logic system.")
(comment normalWholeTypeForPartType "<code>(#$normalWholeTypeForPartType PART-TYPE WHOLE-TYPE)</code> means that
instances of <code>PART-TYPE</code> are normally parts of instances of <code>WHOLE-TYPE</code>. Usually, an instance of <code>PART-TYPE</code> will be incapable of serving its
normal function if it is not part of some instance of <code>WHOLE-TYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$normalWholeTypeForPartType #$DoorKnob #$DoorInABuilding)
is true because door knobs are normally parts of doors, and are incapable of serving their intended function when they are not parts of doors.
<p>
Similarly, (#$normalWholeTypeForPartType  #$AchillesTendon #$LowerLeg) is true because achilles tendons are normally parts of lower legs, and any achilles tendon is  incapable of performing its normal function if it is not part of a lower leg.")
(comment ngaUniqueNameIdentifier "<code>(#$ngaUniqueNameIdentifier NAME IDNUM)</code> states that the #$NationalGeospatialIntelligenceAgency-US uses the #$Integer, <code>IDNUM</code>, as the unique identifier for the #$USBGNPlaceName,  <code>NAME</code>.  IDNUM may be positive or negative.  Currently, only names given to geographic features outside the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and its territories are assigned a number as a #$ngaUniqueNameIdentifier.")
(disjointWith Indoors-IsolatedFromOutside StormAsObject)
(comment SpatiallyDisjointObjectType "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$ObjectType (q.v.). Each instance of #$SpatiallyDisjointObjectType is a collection C of spatial things (see #$SpatialThing), where each of the spatial things in C is spatially disjoint with the other spatial things in C.
<p>
For example, the collection #$Dog is an instance of #$SpatiallyDisjointObjectType, since dogs do not spatially overlap.  Even pregnant dogs do not spatially overlap the pups they bear; rather, pregnant dogs contain their unborn pups.  Other instances of #$SpatiallyDisjointObjectType include #$LatitudeLine, #$CompactDisc-Audio, and #$PlanetaryBody.  An important specialization of #$SpatiallyDisjointObjectType is #$SpatiallyDisjointRegionType (a collection of specializations of #$GeographicalRegion, such as #$City and #$TrueContinent).")
(comment SafeHouse-Refuge "A specialization of #$ModernHumanResidence.  Each instance of #$SafeHouse-Refuge is a residence that is used as a hiding place or secure refuge by individuals.  Users of a safe house are usually members of the same organization and the residence is usually provided or maintained by that organization.  Often, but not always, these organizations are criminal or covert in nature.")
(disjointWith SmallCollectionOfProperlyNamedEntities EmptyCollection)
(comment PhysicsCoreSolutionStepEventType "A specialization of #$SolutionStepEventType (q.v.).  #$PhysicsCoreSolutionStepEventType is the collection of types of events that are associated with the core solution steps used to generate a worked solution to a problem.  In any given physics worked spolution, these steps will play a role, and possibly other kinds of steps will, too.")
(disjointWith SoftDrink-Flavored Water)
(disjointWith SoftDrink-Flavored NoTaste)
(comment agentsGiveMutualSupport "(#$agentsGiveMutualSupport AGT1 AGT2) means that there is at least one support event where AGT1 gives support of some type to AGT2 and there is at least one support event where AGT2 gives support of some type to AGT1.  These events must happen within a certain timeframe -- e.g. they can't be decades or centuries apart.  But, the exact boundries of this timeframe are necessarily fuzzy and vary from context to context.")
(comment collectionSalientChoiceSet "Relates a #$Collection COL to a set of collections SET such that a salient question about a given instance of COL is which element(s) of SET it is an instance of.")
(comment nonNullFields "(#$nonNullFields PS PF) means that (i) PF is a physical
field of PS, and (ii) the values of PF are never null in any knowledge
source associated with PS via #$physicalSchemaSourceMap.")
(comment Caddy "An instance of #$PersonTypeByActivity, this is the collection of caddies -- people who advise and carry the golf clubs of a golfer during instances of (#$PlayingFn #$Golf).")
(comment Caddying "The collection of events in which someone caddies for (advises and carries the golf clubs of) a golfer (#$Golfer, q.v.) during rounds of golf (see #$Golf).")
(comment runningMates "<code>(#$runningMates AGENT-1 AGENT-2 ELECTION)</code> means that <code>AGENT-1</code> and <code>AGENT-2</code> was/are/will-be running mates in <code>ELECTION</code>, an instance of #$ElectionForOffice.")
(comment JohnEdwards "John Edwards, the former Senator from North Carolina who was John Kerry's running mate in the 2004 US Presidential election.")
(comment collectionSalientSharedAttributeNotes "A #$CollectionSalientAttributePredicate that relates a #$Collection to a #$SubLString pertaining to a salient attribute that all instances of the collection have, and that ought to be represented as such.
<p>
Cf. #$collectionSalientFacetingAttributeNotes and #$collectionSalientInstanceLevelAttributeNotes")
(comment collectionSalientInstanceLevelAttributeNotes "A #$CollectionSalientAttributePredicate that relates a #$Collection to a #$SubLString pertaining to an attribute that is salient for instances of the collection, but that would not be a worthwhile basis for a faceting of the collection.
<p>
Cf. #$collectionSalientFacetingAttributeNotes and #$collectionSalientSharedAttributeNotes")
(comment collectionSalientFacetingAttributeNotes "A #$CollectionSalientAttributePredicate that relates a #$Collection to a #$SubLString pertaining to an attribute that is salient for instances of the collection, and that could serve as the basis of a worthwhile faceting of the collection.
<p>
Cf. #$collectionSalientInstanceLevelAttributeNotes and #$collectionSalientSharedAttributeNotes")
(comment ConceptFilterSpecification "Instances of this collection are particular specifications of how a particular concept filter (i.e. bag of terms) is to be utilized by an application.")
(comment ConceptFilter "Instances of this collection are collections of terms suitable for display in the CAE.")
(comment ConceptOnlyFilterParameter "This filter parameter instructs an application to only display concepts that are explicitly in the concept filter.")
(comment ConceptAndInstancesFilterParameter "This filter parameter instructs an application to only display concepts that are either explicitly in the filter or are instances of something in the filter.")
(comment TriggerFromConcept "This filter parameter instructs an application to only trigger concept matches from the concept itself.")
(comment TriggerFromConceptOrSpec "This filter parameter instructs an application to trigger concept matches only from the concept itself or from its specializations.")
(comment TriggerFromConceptOrInstance "This filter parameter instructs an application to trigger concept matches only from the concept itself or from its instances")
(comment TriggerFromConceptSpecOrInstance "This filter parameter instructs an application to trigger concept matches from the concept itself, from its instances, or its specializations.")
(comment specificationForConceptFilter "(#$specificationForConceptFilter FILTER MODE TRIGGER SPECIFICATION) says that SPECIFICATION is the specification that results when FILTER is given MODE and TRIGGER as parameters.  This is the corresponding predicate to #$ConceptFilterSpecificationFn.")
(comment AestheticQuality "An instance of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType.  Each instance of #$AestheticQuality is a degree of aesthetic quality that something can have.  Important specializations are #$Beauty and #$Ugliness (qq.v.).  Generally, if something has a high degree of #$AestheticQuality it is either highly beautiful or highly ugly.")
(comment Beauty "An instance of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType.  Each instance of #$Beauty is a degree of beauty that something can have.")
(comment Ugliness "An instance of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType.  Each instance of #$AestheticQuality is a degree of ugliness that something can have.")
(comment PhysicalAttractiveness "An instance of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType.  Each instance of #$PhysicalAttractiveness is a degree of physical attractiveness that something can have.")
(comment DeterminingSpecificScalarOrVectorValuesFromContext "A specialization of #$SolvingAProblem. Each instance of #$DeterminingSpecificScalarOrVectorValuesFromContext 
is an event in which an agent determines the specific values of certain scalars or vectors based on contextual information.
<p>
An example would be determining that the speed at which Bob is travelling is 65 miles per hour based on the contextual information that Bob is travelling at the speed limit and the speed limit is 65 miles per hour.")
(comment conceptFilterSpecificationDefiningMt "An assertion of the form (#$conceptFilterSpecificationDefiningMt SPEC MT) means that the concept filter specification should only be operative in mts that that specializations of MT.")
(comment MaleFn "#$MaleFn is a #$CollectionDenotingFunction. <code>(#$MaleFn TYPE)</code> returns the subcollection of <code>TYPE</code> (itself a subcollection of #$Animal) that includes all and only the males of <code>TYPE</code>.  For example, (#$MaleFn #$HomoSapiens) and #$MaleHuman both denote the same collection, while (#$MaleFn #$Deer) denotes the collection of all bucks.")
(comment FemaleFn "#$FemaleFn is a #$CollectionDenotingFunction. <code>(#$FemaleFn TYPE)</code> returns the subcollection of <code>TYPE</code> (itself a subcollection of #$Animal) that includes all and only the females of <code>TYPE</code>.  For example, (#$FemaleFn #$HomoSapiens) and #$FemaleHuman denote the same collection, while (#$FemaleFn #$Deer) denotes the collection of all female members of #$Deer.")
(comment nonEmptyLogicalFields "<code>(#$nonEmptyLogicalFields LS LF)</code> means that <code>LF</code> is a logical field in the schema <code>LS</code> and the values for <code>LF</code> are constructed from physical fields that are known to never be null.")
(comment EMailServiceProvider "A collection of service-providing businesses. Each #$EMailServiceProvider provides to its clients with email acess.")
(comment EMailServiceProvider-MailServerBased "A collection of service-providing businesses. Each #$EMailServiceProvider-MailServerBased provides to its clients with email access via a centrally located server. Clients will usually need to provide their email client with the provider's incoming and outgoing server addesses to access and send emails.")
(comment EMailServiceProvider-WebBased "A collection of service-providing businesses. Each #$EMailServiceProvider-WebBased provides to its clients with email access through the World Wide Web. Clients can therefore send and receieve email from any #$Internet connection.")
(comment PayEmailServiceProvider "The collection of #$EmailServiceProvider-WebBased that offer  (FreeThingOfTypeFn EmailAccount).")
(comment RubeGoldbergDevice "An #$ArtifactTypeByGenericCategory.  Each instance of #$RubeGoldbergDevice is a #$MechanicalDevice that performs a relatively simple function in a needlessly (and often absurdly) complicated manner.")
(comment aestheticQualityOfThing "A #$NonAspectualQuantitySlot that relates a thing to its aesthetic quality (see #$AestheticQuality).  (#$aestheticQualityOfThing OBJECT AESTH) means that OBJECT has AESTH.")
(disjointWith AlcoholicBeverage Alkaloid)
(comment IncreaseInValueReturnedByFn "Where <code>FN</code> is a unary, scalar-denoting parametric function that returns a value for the admitted argument <code>ARG</code> that changes over time, <code>(#$IncreaseInValueReturnedByFn FN ARG)</code> returns the collection of all events in which the value returned by <code>FN</code> for <code>ARG</code> is greater at the end of the event than at the start.  E.g. where 
<p>
<pre>
(#$FunctionToArg 2
(#$Kappa (?X ?Y) (#$priceChargedFor ?X Petroleum-CrudeOil ?Y)))</pre></p> is
used to represent the function that denotes the price of oil in a
given region in a temporal context,

<p>
<pre>
(#$IncreaseInValueReturnedByFn                                                                                  
(#$FunctionToArg 2                                                                                  
(#$Kappa (?X ?Y) (#$priceChargedFor ?X Petroleum-CrudeOil ?Y)))                                    
#$Bahrain)                                                                            
</pre>
</p>

denotes the collection of all events in which the price of oil in Bahrain                            
is greater at the end of the event than it was at the start.")
(comment increaseInValueReturnedBy "The corresponding predicate (see <code>#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical</code>) for <code>#$IncreaseInValueReturnedByFn</code>.  Where FN is a unary, evaluatable function whose value changes over time for admitted argument <code>ARG</code>, <code>(#$increaseInValueReturnedBy FN ARG INCREASE-COL)</code> means that <code>COL</code> is the collection of all events in which the value returned by <code>FN</code> for <code>ARG</code> is greater at the end of the event than at the start.")
(comment DecreaseInValueReturnedByFn "Where <code>FN</code> is a unary, scalar-denoting parametric function that returns a value for the admitted argument <code>ARG</code> that changes over time, <code>(#$DecreaseInValueReturnedByFn FN ARG)</code> returns the collection of all events in which the value returned by <code>FN</code> for <code>ARG</code> is greater at the start of the event than at the end.   E.g. where 
<p>
<pre>
(#$FunctionToArg 2
(#$Kappa (?X ?Y) (#$priceChargedFor ?X Petroleum-CrudeOil ?Y)))</pre></p> is
used to represent the function that denotes the price of oil in a
given region in a temporal context,

<p>
<pre>
(#$DecreaseInValueReturnedByFn                                                                                  
(#$FunctionToArg 2                                                                                  
(#$Kappa (?X ?Y) (#$priceChargedFor ?X Petroleum-CrudeOil ?Y)))                                    
#$Bahrain)                                                                            
</pre>
</p>

denotes the collection of all events in which the price of oil in Bahrain                            
is less at the end of the event than it was at the start.")
(comment decreaseInValueReturnedBy "The corresponding predicate of <code>#$DecreaseInValueReturnedByFn</code> (cf. <code>#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical</code>).  Where <code>FN</code> is an <code>#$EvaluatableFunction</code> whose value for admitted argument <code>ARG</code> changes over time, <code>(#$decreaseInValueReturnedBy FN ARG COL)</code> means that <code>COL</code> is the collection of <code>#$DecreaseEvents</code> where the scalar value returned by <code>(FN ARG)</code> is strictly greater at the start of the event than at the end.")
(comment changeInValueReturnedBy "The <code>#$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical</code> of <code>#$ChangeInValueReturnedByFn</code>.  Where <code>FN</code> is a scalar-denoting function whose value on the <code>#$admittedArgument ARG</code> changes over time, <code>(#$changeInValueReturnedBy FN ARG COMPARE COL)</code> means that <code>COL</code> is the collection of all events such that the value given by <code>(FN ARG)</code> at <code>(#$EndFn COL)</code> has the relationship <code>COMPARE</code> to the value given by <code>(FN ARG)</code> at <code>(#$StartFn COL)</code>.")
(comment CommercialEnergyCompany "The collection of commerical businesses that are involved in the sale, transport, or trade of engegy or energy related things")
(comment RelationTuplesFn "(#$RelationTuplesFn ?PRED) :  A (#$ListOfTypeFn List) is returned, in which each element is a list of terms (a tuple) which are one set of bindings for the arguments of ?PRED.  The list returned contains such tuples for the entire extent of ?PRED.  In each tuple in the result, the order of the terms follows the argument order of ?PRED.  For example, (#$RelationTuplesFn #$capitalCity) would return a list whose elements include (#$TheList #$Japan #$CityOfTokyoJapan) and (#$TheList #$Italy #$CityOfRomeItaly).")
(comment OrderingResultFn "<code>(#$OrderingResultFn ORDERING)</code> denotes the list, expressed in CycL as an extensional list (a CycL expression of the form list <code>(#$TheList ITEM-1 ... ITEM-N)</code> (see #$List-Extensional), that is the result of applying the ordering relation (see #$orderingRelation) of the mathematical ordering <code>ORDERING</code> to <code>ORDERING</code>'s base set (#$baseSet, q.v.).")
(comment GrainProcessingOrganization "Local (one site) organizations that process grain.  This includes grain mills and also farms that process the grain from their fields on site.")
(comment OGCXCoordinateFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'X' which is defined in
the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL. (#$OGCXCoordinateFn
POINT) returns the value of the first coordinate of POINT, where the order of the
coordinates is determined by the data structure representing POINT in
the underlying database. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLXCoordinateFn")
(comment OGCYCoordinateFn "This function is introduced solely for the purposes of
translating it via SKSI into the SQL function 'Y' which is defined in
the #$OpenGISSimpleFeaturesSpecificationForSQL. (#$OGCYCoordinateFn
POINT) returns the value of the second coordinate of POINT, where the order of the
coordinates is determined by the data structure representing POINT in
the underlying database. See also the corresponding #$CSQLFunction,
#$CSQLYCoordinateFn")
(disjointWith Narrative CycKB)
(comment Sphenoid "The collection of all sphenoid bones.  A #$Sphenoid is a unique complexly convoluted bone at the base of the #$Skull which attaches to every other skull bone.  Holes in the sphenoid allow #$Nerves (e.g. #$OpticNerve), arteries, and #$Veins to pass through.  The #$PituitaryGland rests (is #$in-Rooted) in a cavity of the sphenoid bone called the sella turcica.")
(comment CommunicationTypeByStorageMediumUsed "A faceting collection on #$CommunicationAct-Single.  Each instance of #$CommunicationTypeByStorageMediumUsed. is a collection of instances of #$CommunicationAct-Single characterized by the fact that the #$presentationMedium used is an instance of some significant subclass of #$InformationStorageMedia, e.g. #$DVD-Disk.")
(comment CommunicatingByStorageMediumOfTypeFn "A #$CollectionDenotingFunction used to compositionally represent specializations of #$CommunicationAct-Single characterized by the fact that an instance of some common specialization of #$InformationStorageMedia is the #$presentationMedium of each instance.  I.e., (#$CommunicatingByStorageMediumOfTypeFn MEDIA-TYPE) denotes the collection of all instances of #$CommunicationAct-Single in which the #$presentationMedium is an instance of MEDIA-TYPE.")
(comment communicationTypeUsesStorageMediumOfType "The corresponding functional predicate of #$CommunicatingByStorageMediumOfTypeFn.  (#$communicationTypeUsesStorageMediumOfType COMM-TYPE MEDIUM-TYPE) means that every instance of COMM-TYPE features an instance of MEDIUM-TYPE as the #$presentationMedium.")
(comment HydroElectricEnergyCompany-Commercial "The collection of commerical businesses that are involved in the sale, transport, or trade of energy produced by hydro-electric plants.")
(comment FossilFuelBurningEnergyCompany-Commercial "The collection of commerical businesses that are involved in the sale, transport, or trade of energy produced by burning fossil fuels such as coal, natural gas, or oil as fuel for the production of steam.")
(comment AlternativeFuelEnergyCompany-Commercial "The collection of commerical businesses that are involved in the sale, transport, or trade of energy produced by means other than fossil fuels.  These alternative methods include wind power, burning of organic matter, solar power and fuel cells to name a few.")
(comment ElectricCompany "The collection of electricity providing utility companies")
(comment SalesOrServiceRelatedCompany "the collection of companies involved in sales (wholesale or retail) or service providing broadly construed.")
(comment typically "The intersection (see #$predicateIntersection) of #$most and #$some.  <code>(#$typically SET-OR-COL-1 SET-OR-COL-2)</code> means that the typical instance of <code>SET-OR-COL-1</code> is an instance of <code>SET-OR-COL-2</code>.")
(comment typicallyRelnTo "<code>(#$typicallyRelnTo PRED SET-OR-COL-1 SET-OR-COL-2)</code> means that typically (i.e., most and some) elements <code>EL-1</code> of <code>SET-OR-COL-1</code> are such that <code>(PRED EL-1 SET-OR-COL-2)</code>.")
(comment typicallyRelnFrom "<code>(#$typicallyRelnFrom PRED SET-OR-COL-2 SET-OR-COL-1)</code> means that typically (i.e., most and some) elements <code>EL-1</code> of <code>SET-OR-COL-1</code> are such that <code>(PRED SET-OR-COL-2 EL-1)</code>.")
(comment typicallyRelnToType "<code>(#$typicallyRelnToType PRED SET-OR-COL-1 SET-OR-COL-2)</code> means that typically (i.e., most and some) elements <code>EL-1</code> of <code>SET-OR-COL-1</code> are such that there exists an element <code>EL-2</code> of <code>SET-OR-COL-2</code> such that <code>(PRED EL-1 EL-2)</code>.")
(comment typicallyRelnFromType "<code>(#$typicallyRelnFromType PRED SET-OR-COL-2 SET-OR-COL-1)</code> means that typically (i.e., most and some) elements <code>EL-1</code> of <code>SET-OR-COL-1</code> are such that there exists an element <code>EL-2</code> of <code>SET-OR-COL-2</code> such that <code>(PRED EL-2 EL-1)</code>.")
(comment hypotheticalMicrotheoryOfTest "(#$microtheoryOfQuery T HMT) means that the test T is asked, and has its hypthetical preconditions (if any) reified in HMT.")
(comment workedSolutionMtForTestMt "(#$workedSolutionMtForTestMt T WS) means that WS is a worked solutiuon for T.")
(comment CAEUsableFETTemplateTopic "The collection of template topics that have been vetted for use with the CAE.")
(comment verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString "<code>(#$verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString SENTENCE RULE COMPOUND)</code> means that the CycL sentence <code>SENTENCE</code> is a logical sentence whose truth verifies that <code>RULE</code>, a rule for parsing noun compounds (see #$NounCompoundRule) is a good rule for parsing the particular noun compound <code>STRING</code>.  For example, <pre>
 (#$verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString
   (#$Quote
     (#$typicallyRelnTo #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation
      (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$Field-Land #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation
        (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant))  
      (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant))) #$PumpkinPatch-NCR \"corn fields\")</pre> means that the truth of the CycL sentence 
<pre>
 (#$typicallyRelnTo #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation
   (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$Field-Land #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation
     (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant)) 
   (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant))</pre> validates the semantics that #$PumpkinPatch-NCR produces for \"corn fields.\"  Thus a necessary condition for the truth of <code>(#$verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString SENTENCE RULE COMPOUND)</code> is the truth of <code>SENTENCE</code>.")
(comment resultQuotedIsa "A binary #$MetaRelation predicate that holds between functions and expression-types. #$resultQuotedIsa indicates that any value returned by a given #$Function-Denotational is a \"quoted instance\" of a given expression-type.   More correctly, <code>(#$resultQuotedIsa FUNC TYPE)</code> means that, for any term-sequence <code>ARGS</code>, if the expression <code>(FUNC . ARGS)</code> is an #$admittedNAT (q.v.), then it is an instance of the #$SubLExpressionType <code>TYPE</code>.  That is, the following holds:
<pre>
  (#$quotedIsa (FUNC . ARGS) TYPE)
</pre>
And, equivalently, so does:
<pre>
  (#$isa (#$Quote (FUNC . ARGS)) TYPE)
</pre>

To contrast this predicate with #$resultIsa: <code>(#$resultIsa FUNC COL)</code> holds just in case each #$defined term of the form <code>(FUNC . ARGS)</code> <i>denotes</i> an instance of <code>COL</code>, whereas <code>(#$resultQuotedIsa FUNC COL)</code> holds just in case each admitted (i.e. syntactically and semantically well-formed) term of the form <code>(FUNC . ARGS)</code> <i>is itself</i> an instance of <code>COL</code>.")
(comment ncRuleExceptionStrings "<code>(#$ncRuleExceptionStrings RULE COMPOUND)</code> means that parsing the noun compound string <code>COMPOUND</code> using the noun compound rule <code>RULE</code> will result in semantics that are an unlikely or even incorrrect interpretation of <code>COMPOUND</code>.")
(comment DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance "A specialiazation of #$Evaluating.  Each instance of
#$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance is an event in which an agent
determines that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of
#$CycLExpression) is either relevant or irrelevant with regard to the
performance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion of
an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(comment DeclaringAConceptIrrelevant "A specialiazation of #$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance.  Each instance of #$DeclaringAConceptIrrelevant is an event in which an agent declares that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of #$CycLExpression) is irrelevant with regard to the performance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion of an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(comment DeclaringAConceptRelevant "A specialiazation of #$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance.  Each instance of #$DeclaringAConceptRelevant is an event in which an agent declares that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of #$CycLExpression) is relevant with regard to the performance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion of an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(comment DeemingAConceptRelevant "A #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByHuman and #$DeclaringAConceptRelevant (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$DeemingAConceptRelevant an event in which a human evaluates that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of #$CycLExpression) is relevant with regard to the perormance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion of an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(comment DeemingAConceptIrrelevant "A #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByHuman and #$DeclaringAConceptIrrelevant (qq.v.). Each instance of #$DeemingAConceptIrrelevant an event in which some human evaluates that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of #$CycLExpression) is irrelevant with regard to the perormance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion of an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(disjointWith DeclaringAConceptIrrelevant DeclaringAConceptRelevant)
(comment DeemingAConceptIrrelevant-ByCyc "A specialization of #$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByCyc and #$DeclaringAConceptIrrelevant.  Each instance of #$DeemingAConceptIrrelevant-ByCyc is an event in which Cyc declares that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of #$CycLExpression) is relevant with regard to the performance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion of an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(disjointWith DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByHuman DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByCyc)
(comment DeemingAConceptRelevant-ByCyc "A specialization of #$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByCyc and #$DeclaringAConceptRelevant.  Each instance of #$DeemingAConceptRelevant-ByCyc is an event in which Cyc declares that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of #$CycLExpression) is relevant with regard to the performance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion of an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(comment DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByHuman "A specialization of #$Evaluating.  Each instance of
#$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByHuman is an event in which a person
declares that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of
#$CycLExpression) is either relevant or irrelevant with regard to the
performance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion
of an #$Assignment-Specification. Important specializations are #$DeemingAConceptRelevant and #$DeemingAConceptIrrelevant (qq.v.).")
(comment DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByCyc "A specialization of #$Evaluating.  Each instance of
#$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance-ByCyc is an event in which Cyc
declares that a #$Thing (typically, a quoted instance of
#$CycLExpression) is either relevant or irrelevant with regard to the
performance of a particular #$PurposefulAction, such as the completion
of an #$Assignment-Specification.")
(comment mostRecentImportantConceptEvent "<code>(#$mostRecentImportantConceptEvent THING EVENT)</code> means that #$DeclaringConceptRelevanceOrIrrelevance
<code>EVENT</code> is the most recent event that is important for assessing the relevance of the concept <code>THING</code> in the current context.
<p>
NOTE: This predicate should only be used in microtheories which specify the appropriate qualifications of assertions made with it: e.g., for whom the concept is pertinent, on what task, at what time and place, etc.")
(comment SecretServiceAgent "The collection of law-enforcement agents who work for the United States Secret Service. Though many are uniformed officers, and though the USSS is tasked primarily with combatting counterfeiting, secret service agents are usually associated with the personal protection that the agency provides the US President and Vice President and their families.")
(comment objectOfEvaluation "(#$objectOfEvaluation EVAL OBJ) means that in the instance of #$Evaluating, EVAL, information about OBJ is derived.  The performer (#$performedBy) of the evaluation must be #$awareOf OBJ.  If the information input to EVAL is OBJ (see #$evaluationInput), use the more specific predicate, #$evaluee-Direct.  For example, if a sailor examines tonight's sky to determine tomorrow's weather, tomorrow's weather is the #$objectOfEvaluation and tonight's sky is the #$evaluationInput.")
(comment AssignmentOfAllotmentOnDateFn "(#$AssignmentOfAllotmentOnDateFn ALLOT DATE) denotes the assignment of the #$Allotment ALLOT on DATE.")
(comment assignmentOfAllotmentOnDate "(#$assignmentOfAllotmentOnDate ALLOT DATE ASSIGN) means the assignment ASSIGN is of the #$Allotment ALLOT on DATE.")
(comment NavalShipyard "The collection of shipyards (see #$Shipyard) that are used by a navy for the repair of ships in their fleet.")
(comment Backpacking "A specialization of #$HikingEvent and #$CarryingWhileLocomoting. Instances are hiking events in which the performer (see #$performedBy) carries a backpack (see #$Backpack).")
(comment listCWItems "A predicate that relates a type of conceptual work to real-word things that it is about (in the sense of #$containsInformationAbout). <code>(#$listCWItems LIST ITEM)</code> means that in the work <code>LIST</code>, a conceptual work that has the structure of a list (see #$List-CW), one of the items on that list is <code>ITEM</code>.  This means that the information structure of <code>LIST</code> will contain an item -- to be precise, a contextualized information structure (see #$ContextualizedInformationStructure) -- that refers to (#$denotes) <code>ITEM</code>.")
(comment objectSecureToDegreeAgainstRoleInType "<code>(#$objectSecureToDegreeAgainstRoleInType OBJECT SECURITY-LEVEL SITUATION-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that the instance <code>OBJECT</code> of #$SomethingExisting is secure to degree <code>SECURITY-LEVEL</code> against situations of type <code>SITUATION-TYPE</code> in which <code>OBJECT</code> plays the role <code>ROLE</code>.")
(comment GenericSecurityLevel "An instance of #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType, #$GenericSecurityLevel is the collection of generic levels, or degrees of security, such as highly secure (see (#$HighToVeryHighAmountFn #$GenericSecurityLevel)) not secure (see (#$NoAmountFn #$GenericSecurityLevel)), etc., that an individual object (e.g., a facility, network, person, etc.) might have.  To \"have\" a generic security level is to be secure, or protected against, entering into a situation of a given type.  To specify assignments of generic security levels in this way, use #$objectSecureToDegreeAgainstRoleInType.")
(comment objectSecureToDegree "A highy context-sensitive predicate relating an instance of #$SomethingExisting to a level, or degree of security.  <code>(#$objectSecureToDegree OBJECT SECURITY-LEVEL),</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> (in the current context) is secure to the degree <code>SECURITY-LEVEL</code>.  To further specify the context (i.e., to specify what <code>OBJECT</code> is secure <i>against</i>, use #$objectSecureToDegreeAgainstRoleInType.")
(comment PushingAnObject "A specialization of #$Propelling and #$ApplyingAPressure (qq.v.) Each instance of #$PushingAnObject is an event throughout which an agent, using its own force, moves an object by applying a force directed away from itself.  Pushing a wheelbarrow would be an instance of this, even though the wheelbarrow doesn't get any farther away, because the pusher applies a force away and the wheelbarrow moves.  Pushing on a closed door, however, wouldn't be an instance of #$PushingAnObject if the door doesn't move -- rather, that would just be an instance of #$ApplyingAPressure.")
(comment PullingAnObjectAlong "A specialization of #$Propelling and #$PullingAnObject (qq.v.) Each instance of #$PullingAnObjectAlong is an event in whcih an agent uses a sudden or steady force directed toward itself to pull something towards itself. Contrast this collection with #$PushingAnObject.")
(comment CordlikeArtifact "A specialization of #$CordlikeObject (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CordlikeArtifact is both cordlike (i.e., it is relatively thin and has ends) and is an artifact.  An important specialization of #$CordlikeArtifact is #$Cord, which is the collection of artifacts that are not only cordlike, but which are actual cords.  For example, a chain (see #$Chain) is cordlike and an artifact (hence an instance of #$CordlikeArtifact) but chains are not actually cords.")
(comment Cord "A specialization of #$CordlikeArtifact.  #$Cord is the collection of all artifacts typically refered to as 'cords'.  Each instance of #$Cord must be long, thin, flexible, and have ends, whereas an instance of #$CordlikeObject (q.v.) must only be relatively thin and having ends.  For an example of something that is a #$CordlikeObject but not a #$Cord, see #$SpinalCord.")
(comment PhysicsOptionalSolutionStepEventType "A specialization of #$SolutionStepEventType (q.v.).  #$PhysicsCoreSolutionStepEventType is the collection of types of events that are associated with the core solution steps used to generate a worked solution to a problem.  In any given physics worked spolution, these steps will play a role, and possibly other kinds of steps will, too.")
(comment PhysicsSolutionStepEventType "A specialization of #$SolutionStepEventType (q.v.).  #$PhysicsCoreSolutionStepEventType is the collection of types of events that are associated with the core solution steps used to generate a worked solution to a problem.  In any given physics worked spolution, these steps will play a role, and possibly other kinds of steps will, too.")
(comment MakingNecessaryBackgroundAssumptions "A specialization of #$DecidingToDoSomething and #$AssumingSomethingToBeTrue.  Each instance of #$MakingNecessaryBackgroundAssumptions is an event in which an agent makes background assumptions that are necessary for solving a problem.")
(comment ExanimateObject "An important specialization of #$InanimateObject.  Each instance of #$ExanimateObject is an individual that is typically regarded as truly inanimate, or non-agentive in the sense that it is utterly incapable of any subjective or mental experience.")
(comment MummifiedRemains "A specialization of #$Artifact.  Instances of this collection are created through the process of mummifying (see #$Mummifying) the remains of a dead animal.")
(comment MummifiedRemains-Human "The intersection of #$MummifiedRemains and (#$DeadPhysicalRemainsFn #$HomoSapiens).")
(comment Embalming "The collection of actions in which an agent prepares the physical remains of an animal to guard against decomposition.")
(comment deadPhysicalRemainsOf "<code>(#$deadPhysicalRemainsOf REMAINS TEMPORAL-THING)</code> means that <code>REMAINS</code> constitute the dead physical remains of <code>TEMPORAL-THING</code>, an instance of #$TemporalThing, formerly an instance of #$Organism-Whole.  Note that <code>(#$deadPhysicalRemainsOf REMAINS TEMPORAL-THING)</code> entails <code>(#$temporallyDisjoint REMAINS TEMPORAL-THING)</code>.")
(comment ComputerDisk "A specialization of #$InformationStorageMedia, this is the collection of all disks used for storage of information.  This includes floppy disks as well as hard disks.")
(comment trajectory-Complete "<code>(#$trajectory-Complete MOVE TRAJ)</code> means that the #$Movement-TranslationEvent <code>MOVE</code> has the #$Trajectory <code>TRAJ</code> as its entire trajectory.  Suppose <code>OBJ</code> is the #$objectMoving in <code>TRAJ</code>.  <code>TRAJ</code> is a line or curve in space (technically a one-manifold) traced out by the centroid (see #$CenterFn) of  <code>OBJ</code>.  
<p>
Note that the trajectory of the motion may cross itself, zig-zag back and forth along the same path, repeat several cycles, etc., and the trajectory need not be confined to any pre-existing #$Path-Generics or #$Traversals of paths.
<p>
See also #$trajectory-Partial.  And cf. #$motionPathway-Complete and with #$traverses-Complete.")
(comment IdentifyingAWholeWithTheSumOfItsParts "A specialization of #$AnalyzingSomething. Each instance of #$IdentifyingAWholeWithTheSumOfItsParts is an event in which an agent explicitly equates or identifies a thing with the sum of (certain of) its parts.  An example would be the act of explicitly observing that the territory of the United States is the spatial sum of the territories of each of the fifty states.")
(comment DistanceTravelledInDirection "A specialization of #$Distance. #$DistanceTravelledInDirection is the collection of all distances that are translated by the primary objects moving in events in a particular direction. Each instance of #$DistanceTravelledInDirection is the #$Distance in a certain direction between an object's starting location and ending location over a time period.")
(comment valueOf "A #$BinaryPredicate and specialization of #$mathEquals.  <code>(#$valueOf X Y)</code> means that the term '<code>X</code>' is, in effect, a definite description that (contingently) picks out the #$ScalarOrVectorInterval that is directly denoted by '<code>Y</code>'.  
<p>
For example, <code>(#$valueOf ((#$QPQuantityFn #$Mass) #$EiffelTower) (#$MetricTon 10100))</code> is true.")
(comment StringsToPhraseFn "Given a #$List of #$SubLStrings, #$StringsToPhraseFn returns the concatenation of all the #$SubLStrings with a space inserted after each (except the last) individual string. For example, <code>(#$evaluate ?C (#$StringsToPhraseFn (#$TheList \"automatic\" \"teller\")))</code> will instantiate <code>?C</code> to \"automatic teller\".")
(comment NearestIsaRelevanceEventFn "(#$NearestIsaRelevanceEventFn DATE THING) denotes the event that occurred on DATE of Cyc determining that the nearest $isa for THING was relevant.")
(comment nearestIsaRelevanceEvent "(#$nearestIsaRelevanceEvent DATE THING EVT) means that EVT was the event of Cyc declaring, on DATE, that the nearest isa of THING was relevant.")
(disjointWith LandlineTelephone WirelessCommunicationDevice)
(disjointWith LandlineTelephone CordlessTelephone)
(comment TelephoneCord "A specialization of #$ElectricalConnector, this is the collection of telephone cords -- cords that connect phones to phone-jacks or, in the case of old-style \"landline\" telephones (see #$LandlineTelephone), connect telephone bodies (see #$TelephoneBody) to receivers (see #$TelephoneReceiver).")
(comment LandlineTelephone "The collection of old-style telephones (see #$Telephone) that are both hooked up to landline wiring (see #$TelephoneLandLine) and require that the receiver (see #$TelephoneReceiver) be connected to the body (see #$TelephoneBody) via a telephone cord (see #$TelephoneCord) that carries an electrical signal back and forth.")
(comment Harem "The collection of harems -- women who live as co-spouses to a single man.")
(disjointWith Situation Shadow)
(comment Silhouette-ShadedRegion "The collection of instances of #$Shadow whose shape closely corresponds to the the shape of its source (see #$castsShadow) so as to make the source recognizable to the perceiver (or at least the perceiver is capable of giving a fairly accurate description of the source (e.g., \"a paper cut-out of Lincoln's head\").")
(comment castsShadow "<code>(#$castsShadow OBJECT SHADOW)</code> means that partially tangible thing <code>OBJECT</code> is casting the shadow <code>SHADOW</code>.  Note that <code>OBJECT</code> could be a group of tangibles.")
(comment ExecutionByFiringSquad "A specialization of #$Execution-Judicial in which those executed are shot to death by a group of gun-weilding executioners.")
(comment FiringSquad "The collection of all firing squads, groups who carry out instances of #$ExecutionByFiringSquad.")
(comment endsAfterStartOfInSituationType "(#$endsAfterStartOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-ENDTYPE SUBSIT-STARTTYPE) means that where (#$isa SIT SIT-TYPE), it will be true of any #$properSubSituations of SITm SUBSIT-END and SUBSIT-START, that if SUBSIT-END is an instance of SUBSIT-ENDTYPE and SUBSIT-START isa an instance of SUBSIT-STARTTYPE, it will be the case that (#$endsAfterStartingOf SUBSIT-END SUBSIT-START.")
(comment Length "A specialization of #$Distance. #$Length is the collection of all distances that are lengths of objects (see #$lengthOfObject).")
(comment SolidTangibleThing "An instance of #$ExistingStuffType, and a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$SolidTangibleThing is a piece of stuff possessing many of the properties that matter in a solid state (see the constant #$Solid-StateOfMatter) exhibits (although not all instances of #$SolidTangibleThing are formally in a solid state).  Instances have a shape independent from their container, and, when deformed with sufficient force (which may be small for weak, brittle materials or high for materials that deform easily), they break.  Examples of #$SolidTangibleThings include: pieces of substances in a solid state of matter, such as ice cubes; solid mixtures like a quarter-dollar coin or a paper bag; and complex mixtures of biological origin that behave like solids, e.g., bone.  Note that some pieces of matter that are formally in a solid state (for example, pieces of clay) are not instances of #$SolidTangibleThing, since they do not readily break when deformed; for this reason, #$Solid-StateOfMatter is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$SolidTangibleThing.  Collections of the solid form of any type of stuff can be created using #$SolidFn (q.v.).")
(comment QuasiTemporalSubSituationTypesPredicate "Predicates in this collection are used to indicate that all of the instances of a given complex situation type are characterized by some quasi-temporal ordering obtaining between subsituations that are also characterized by type.")
(comment collectionSalientFacetingCollectionType "A #$CollectionSalientAttributePredicate that relates a given collection <code>COL</code> to a collection-type <code>COLTYPE</code> such that a salient question about a given instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> -- on which a worthwhile faceting of <code>COL</code> might be based -- is which instance(s) of <code>COLTYPE</code> is <code>INST</code> an instance of.  For example,
<pre>
  (#$collectionSalientFacetingCollectionType #$Organism-Whole #$BiologicalSpecies).")
(comment Bigfoot "A type of large hairy creature said to roam wilderness areas of North America.")
(comment IntelligentAgentActivity "A specialization of #$Action (q.v.).  Every instance of #$IntelligentAgentActivity is an action, each of whose \"doers\" (see #$doneBy and #$animateDoers) is either an #$IntelligentAgent or a group of such agents (i.e. a #$Group all of whose members are intelligent agents).  Specializations of this collection include #$HumanActivity and #$Reading.")
(comment kappaGenTemplateRuleForArity "<code>(#$kappaGenTemplateRuleForArity ARITY RULE)</code> means that <code>RULE</code> defines how to generate a #$genTemplate recipe for a #$Kappa predicate whose arity is <code>ARITY</code>.")
(comment BaggageHandler "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Instances are people who move traveller's bags and other cargo on and off of aircraft.")
(comment AircraftLandingAndTakeoffFacility "The collection of instances of #$Facility-Generic that are designed to be locations for the takeoff and landing of aircraft.")
(comment GroundCrewWorker "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Instances are people who work \"on the ground\" at an airport or other other aircraft transport facility, usually on the tarmac or in and around hangars.")
(comment ModuloFn "A binary function that takes a scalar and a comparable (non-null) point-value scalar to another comparable scalar, #$ModuloFn is a generalization of a basic notion of modular arithmetic, broadened to apply to #$ScalarIntervals of all sorts.
<p>
(#$ModuloFn <code>SCALAR BASE</code>) is the value of <code>SCALAR</code> modulo <code>BASE</code>, determined as follows.  (i) If <code>SCALAR</code> and <code>BASE</code> are both positive or both negative values, <code>SCALAR-MOD-BASE</code> is the remainder left when <code>BASE</code> is successively subtracted from <code>SCALAR</code> (zero or more times) until the result is a value that is closer to the comparable null-value (see #$ScalarNullValue) than <code>BASE</code> is.  (ii) If <code>SCALAR</code> and <code>BASE</code> differ in their numeric signs, <code>SCALAR-MOD-BASE</code> is the remainder left when <code>BASE</code> is successively <i>added</i> to <code>SCALAR</code> (zero or more times) until the result is a value <i>whose numeric sign agrees with that of</i> <code>BASE</code> or is null, and which is closer to the comparable null-value than <code>BASE</code> is.  (iii) If <code>SCALAR</code> is already a null-value, <code>SCALAR-MOD-BASE</code> is just <code>SCALAR</code> itself.  
<p>
For example, (#$ModuloFn 8 3) = 2, (#$ModuloFn (#$Meter 9) (#$Meter -4)) = (#$Meter -3), and (#$ModuloFn (#$Inch -12) (#$Inch -4)) = (#$Inch 0).
<p>
Note that in case (i) above, if the absolute value of <code>SCALAR</code> is any value #$lessThan the absolute value of <code>BASE</code>, <code>SCALAR-MOD-BASE</code> is equal to <code>SCALAR</code>.  In all cases, if <code>SCALAR</code> #$numericallyEquals <code>BASE</code>, <code>SCALAR-MOD-BASE</code> is the comparable null-value.  In all cases, the numeric sign of the returned value <code>SCALAR-MOD-BASE</code> agrees with that of <code>BASE</code> (unless the returned value is null).  Also note that <code>(#$ModuloFn SCALAR BASE)</code> is #$undefined if <code>SCALAR</code> and <code>BASE</code> are not #$numericallyComparable (q.v.), or if <code>BASE</code> is null. 
<p>
In practice, <code>BASE</code> will usually be a positive integral value, and both <code>SCALAR</code> and the returned value will often be integral as well (see #$ScalarIntegralValue).  Note that if <code>SCALAR</code> and <code>BASE</code> are given in <i>different</i> (but comparable) #$UnitOfMeasures (e.g. #$Meter and (#$Centi #$Meter)), the result returned by Cyc will be given in the same units as <code>BASE</code>.")
(comment SKSIHighlyProductiveQueryProfilingTest "#$SKSIHighlyProductiveQueryProfilingTest is a collection of #$SKSIContentTests which are known to generate tens and hundreds of thousands of answers. They are intended be used to profile and test Cyc's ability to handle (via pruning, efficient execution, etc.) highly productive queries in inference.")
(comment taskConceptForUser "(#$taskConceptForUser CONCEPT USER TASK) holds just in case CONCEPT is pertinent to USER working on TASK.  This is basically a dexcontextualized version of #$conceptPertinentToDegree with the degree argument existentially generalized.")
(comment PoliticalConvention "The collection of instances of #$Convention-Meeting where members of a political party meet, typically to decide issues of party leadership or to nominiate a member as the official party-backed candidate for election to some office.")
(comment NationalConvention "The collection of instances of #$Convention-Meeting that is organized by an instance of #$NationalOrganization, typically inviting all (and only) its members, and where matters of interest to the organization are discussed and sometimes resolved.")
(comment USPresidentialCandidateNominationConvention "A specialization of #$NationalConvention and #$PoliticalConvention.  Instances are conventions where members of US political parties meet in a Presidential election year to nominate Presidential (and Vice Presidential) candidates for that election.  The most well-known specializations of this collection are the Democratic National Convention (#$DemocraticNationalConvention) and the Republican National Convention (#$RepublicanNationalConvention).")
(comment PublicDebate "A specialization of #$Debate and #$Event-Organized.  Instances are debates between two or more parties in a public forum, where, unlike other debates, the participants do not necessarily intend to change the views of their opponents, but to sway (or at least present a more polished and well-argued case to) members of the audience.")
(comment PoliticalDebate "The collection of all political debates, defined broadly as debates about political issues (see #$PoliticalIssue).")
(comment politicalCampaignOf "<code>(#$politicalCampaignOf CAMPAIGN CANDIDATE)</code> means that <code>CAMPAIGN</code>, an instance of #$PoliticalElectionCampaign, is a campaign that promotes <code>CANDIDATE</code> for election to the relevant office in contest in the relevant election.")
(disjointWith USPresidentialDebate USVicePresidentialDebate)
(comment USPresidentialDebate "The collection of debates held between two or more of the final, party-endorsed candidates in a US Presidential election.")
(comment USVicePresidentialDebate "The collection of debates held between two or more of the final, party-endorsed candidates for Vice President in a US Presidential election.")
(comment FormalDebateBetweenCandidatesForAnElectedOffice "The collection of debates held between rival candidates hoping to fill an elected position.")
(comment RepublicanNationalConvention "The collection of national conventions, held every four years, by the Republican party (#$RepublicanParty-US) to select a candidate for that year's US Presidential election.")
(comment DemocraticNationalConvention "The collection of national conventions, held every four years, by the Democratic party (#$DemocraticParty-US) to select a candidate for that year's US Presidential election.")
(comment SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality "A specialization of #$SpatialThingTypeByShape; a collection of collections of spatial things.  Each instance of #$SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality is a subcollection of #$SpatialThing (q.v.)  whose own instances are all and only those spatial things that have a certain something in common as regards their dimensionality.  Examples include #$ThreeDimensionalThing and #$PositiveDimensionalThing.  
<p>
See also the specialization #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions.")
(comment nearestCommonGenls "A #$TaxonomicPredicateForCollections that relates two collections to their nearest common generalizations represented in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  <code>(#$nearestCommonGenls COL1 COL2 NEAREST-GENL)</code> means that (i) <code>NEAREST-GENL</code> in known in the Cyc Knowledge Base to subsume (via #$genls) both <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code> and (ii) there is no proper specialization of <code>NEAREST-GENL</code> that is known to subsume both <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code>.
<p>
Note that #$nearestCommonGenls is <i>not</i> functional with respect to its third argument, as a pair of collections might have several nearest common generalizations, all orthogonal to each other.  Note also that this predicate is #$notAssertible, since it must always be recomputed from the current state of the represented #$genls heirarchy.  
<p>
See also #$nearestCommonIsa and #$nearestCommonSpecs.")
(comment SiblingDisjointSetOrCollectionType "A #$KBDependentCollection of set-or-collections of set-or-collections; a specialization of #$SetOrCollectionType.  A sibling-disjoint set-or-collection type is such that its known (i.e. KB-represented) elements are set-or-collections that -- save for any that are related to each other by  #$subsetOf and any that are explicitly asserted to be exceptions (see  #$siblingDisjointExceptions) -- are disjoint from each other (see #$no). 
<p>
More precisely, each instance <code>SIB</code> of #$SiblingDisjointSetOrCollectionType is a #$SetOrCollection of #$SetOrCollections that has the following KB-dependent property: 
<p>
For any two known elements <code>SETORCOL1</code> and <code>SETORCOL2</code> of   <code>SIB</code>, at least one of the following is known to hold: 
<pre>
  (a) (#$subsetOf SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)
  (b) (#$subsetOf SETORCOL2 SETORCOL1)
  (c) (#$siblingDisjointExceptions SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)
  (d) (#$no SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)
</pre>  
Moreover, note that if <code>MT</code> is a context (see #$Microtheory) in which (i) both <code>(#$elementOf SETORCOL1 SIB)</code> and <code>(#$elementOf SETORCOL2 SIB)</code> hold and (ii) neither <code>(#$subsetOf SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)</code> nor <code>(#$subsetOf SETORCOL2 SETORCOL1)</code> nor <code>(#$siblingDisjointExceptions SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)</code> is known to hold (see #$knownSentence), then <code>(#$no SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)</code> holds by default in <code>MT</code>.
<p>
Cf. the stronger notion of #$DisjointSetOrCollectionType.")
(comment SiblingDisjointCollectionType "A #$KBDependentCollection of collections of  collections (and thus an instance of #$CollectionTypeType and a specialization of #$CollectionType).  A sibling-disjoint collection type is such that its known  (i.e. KB-represented) instances are collections that -- save for any that are related to each other by  #$genls and any that are explicitly asserted to be exceptions (see  #$siblingDisjointExceptions) -- are disjoint from each other. 
<p>
More precisely, each instance <code>SIB</code> of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType is a collection of collections that has the following KB-dependent property: 
<p>
For any two known instances <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code> of   <code>SIB</code>, at least one of the following is known to hold: 
<pre>
  (a) (#$genls COL1 COL2)
  (b) (#$genls COL2 COL1)
  (c) (#$siblingDisjointExceptions COL1 COL2)
  (d) (#$disjointWith COL1 COL2)
</pre>  
Moreover, note that if <code>MT</code> is a context (see #$Microtheory) in which (i) both <code>(#$isa COL1 SIB)</code> and <code>(#$isa COL2 SIB)</code> hold and (ii)  neither <code>(#$genls COL1 COL2)</code> nor <code>(#$genls COL2 COL1)</code> nor <code>(#$siblingDisjointExceptions COL1 COL2)</code> is known to hold (see #$knownSentence), then 
<code>(#$disjointWith COL1 COL2)</code> holds by default in <code>MT</code>.   
<p>
For example, in #$BiologyMt both #$Person and #$Dog are instances of the  sibling-disjoint collection type #$BiologicalSpecies, while neither  (#$genls #$Person #$Dog) nor (#$genls #$Dog #$Person) nor  (#$siblingDisjointExceptions #$Person #$Dog) is known to hold in that  context; consequently, (#$disjointWith #$Person #$Dog) holds by default  in #$BiologyMt.  Instances of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType include  #$BiologicalTaxon (and its specializations), #$OrganismPartType, and #$RelationshipTypeByArity.
<p>
See the generalization #$SiblingDisjointSetOrCollectionType.  Also cf. the stronger notion of #$DisjointCollectionType.")
(comment siblingDisjointExceptions "A predicate that is  used to make exceptions to constraints normally imposed by some instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType or #$SiblingDisjointSetOrCollectionType (qq.v.).  <code>(#$siblingDisjointExceptions SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)</code> means that the pair consisting of <code>SETORCOL1</code> and <code>SETORCOL2</code> is exempt from the disjointness constraint that would otherwise be imposed because those set-or-collections are both elements of some instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType or #$SiblingDisjointSetOrCollectionType.  (See these collections' comments for a full explanation of the constraints.)   
<p>
For example, #$SensoryNerve and #$CranialNerve are both instances of #$OrganismPartType, which in turn is an instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType.  Consequently, since #$SensoryNerve is not a specialization of #$CranialNerve (or vice versa), the two collections have a default disjointness constraint placed between them.  However, such a disjointness constraint should be blocked, since any instance of #$OpticNerve is an instance of both #$SensoryNerve and #$CranialNerve.  By asserting <code>(#$siblingDisjointExceptions #$SensoryNerve #$CranialNerve)</code>, we block the disjointness constraint between those two collections, without disturbing the constraints between each of those collections and all their other (sibling) instances of the parent collection #$OrganismPartType.")
(comment SemMarkDatabaseSaturationRules-UseCase0 "#$SemMarkDatabaseSaturationRules-UseCase0 contains the #$sksiSaturateRule queries that are used to populate the #$FG1-KS database from KB content, the #$NGA-KS database, and the #$Usgs-KS database.")
(comment PressingTheFlesh-Campaigning "The collection of (typically organized) events in which a candidate for some elected office introduces him or herself to (typically largish) groups of people, to create photo opportunities and impress upon some of those people a sense of likability.")
(comment PromotionalMeetAndGreetEvent "The collection of (typically organized) events in which a people, such as celebrities, executives, or politicians, introduce themselves to (typically largish) groups of people, primarily to generate interest in some thing (like a movie or book) or cause (such as a political campaign).")
(comment DeliveringAStumpSpeech "The collection of public speaking events performed by (#$performedBy, q.v.) someone running for public office, delivered as part of that person's campaign (see #$PoliticalElectionCampaign).")
(comment Backhoe "The collection of backhoes, a kind of mechanical arm used to dig large holes and trenches.  When deployed, they are attached to a front loader (see #$FrontLoader) or other platform to form an excavator (see #$Excavator-Vehicle).")
(comment DraglineExacavator "A kind of excavator (see #$Excavator-Vehicle) used in mining.  These excavators are notably larger (and taller) than other types of excavators, and have a distinctive boom (see #$Boom-MechanicalArm) from which a bucket is suspended, rather than a backhoe (see #$Backhoe).")
(comment discardedVerbNetVerbClassAssertion "(#$discardedVerbNetVerbClassAssertion SENTENCE) means SENTENCE expresses contetn which was imported from #$VerbNet-Version2_0 and has since been discarded.")
(comment SmallGroupOfPeople "The collection of small groups whose members (see #$groupMembers) are all people (#$Person, q.v.).  See the comment on #$SmallGroup for more information about what counts as a small group.")
(comment SmallTown "The collection of small towns.  \"Small\" here is defined relativistically, and in terms of population and geographical area.  That is, a small town is an instance of #$Town that has both a low population relative to most towns and a small area relative to most towns.  This conjunction is designed to prevent an unusually large town (one with a large population and area) from becoming, unintuitively, a small one due to a sudden plague outbreak that devistates the population, or due to an annexation or cecessation that results in a sudden drastic drop in size, with little drop in populaion.")
(comment SportsFranchiseTypeBySport "The collection of all specializations of #$SportsFranchise whose focal teams (see #$sportsFranchiseFocalTeam) play the same sport.")
(comment SportsFranchiseTypeBySportFn "A function that takes a sport (see #$Sport) and returns the collection of sports franchises (see #$SportsFranchise) built around a sports team for that sport. For example, <code>(#$SportsFranchiseTypeBySportFn #$Basketball-TheGame)</code> denotes the collection of basketball franchises, one instance of which, the Los Angeles Lakers (see #$LosAngelesLakers), is such that its focal sports team (see <code>(#$FocalTeamOfFranchiseFn #$LosAngelesLakers)</code> is a basketball team (see #$BasketballTeam).")
(comment sportsFranchiseTypeForSport "<code>(#$sportsFranchiseTypeForSport FRANCHISE-TYPE SPORT)</code> identifies <code>FRANCHISE-TYPE</code> as the instance of #$SportsFranchiseTypeBySport whose instances are sports franchises (see #$SportsFranchise) whose focal team (see #$sportsFranchiseFocalTeam) is a <code>SPORT</code> team (i.e., a team whose primary function is to compete in performances of the sport <code>SPORT</code>).  For example,  <code>(#$sportsFranchiseTypeForSport (#$SportsFranchiseTypeBySportFn #$Basketball-TheGame) #$Basketball-TheGame)</code> identifies the denotation of <code>(#$SportsFranchiseTypeBySportFn #$Basketball-TheGame)</code> as the collection of basketball franchises, one instance of which, the Los Angeles Lakers (see #$LosAngelesLakers), is such that the team that it is built around (see <code>(#$FocalTeamOfFranchiseFn #$LosAngelesLakers)</code> is a basketball team (see #$BasketballTeam).")
(comment sportsFranchiseFocalTeam "A specialization of (see #$genlPreds) #$subOrganizations.  <code>(#$sportsFranchiseFocalTeam FRANCHISE TEAM)</code> identifies <code>TEAM</code> as the sports team (see #$SportsTeam) that <code>FRANCHISE</code>, a sports franchise (see #$SportsFranchise) is built around.  Every member (see #$hasMembers) of <code>TEAM</code> is an employee (see #$employees) of <code>FRANCHISE</code>, but typically most members of <code>FRANCHISE</code> are not members of <code>TEAM</code>, but include coaches, scouts, trainers, sales and marketing staff, accountants, etc.")
(comment SportsFranchise "The collection of sports franchises, commercial organizations whose business revolves around promoting and improving a specific sports team.")
(comment teamTypeSport "<code>(#$teamTypeSport TEAM-TYPE SPORT)</code> identifies <code>TEAM-TYPE</code> as the collection of sports teams (see #$SportsTeam) that play the sport <code>SPORT</code>.")
(comment TeamFocusedSportsOrganization "The collection of instances of #$SportsOrganization whose main business is the maintenance, and possibly the promotion of, instances of #$SportsTeam.  Notable specializations include #$SportsFranchise, instances of which are businesses that revolve, primarily, around a single sports team, and #$AthleticDepartment, instances of which are sub-organizations of schools that focus on all of the sports teams sponsored by that school.")
(comment AthleticTrainer "The collection of athletic trainers, health professionals, typically hired by sports organizations to tend to the injuries of athletes employed by those organizations.")
(disjointWith Boathouse SubwayStation)
(comment Boathouse "The collection of boathouses, buildings whose primary function is to serve as storage for (and thus provide shelter to) boats. (see #$Boat).")
(disjointWith HeavyWind Breeze)
(comment Gale "A specialization of #$HeavyWind.  Gales are winds of at least 32 miles per hour.  Typically, such winds are not called \"gales\" unless they are over bodies of water.")
(comment ObjectDefiningSchema "Instances of #$ObjectDefiningSchema are logical  schemas that define entities, or objects which can be uniquely identified by the values of at least one of the table's implicit or explicit fields. In the language of entity-relationship data modeling, #$ObjectDefiningSchemas are 'entities' while #$RelationshipDefiningSchemas correspond to 'relationships'.")
(comment agentTypeBrokersSalesOfProductType "A specialization of #$agentTypeSellsProductType.  <code>(#$agentTypeBrokersSalesOfProductType AGENT-TYPE PRODUCT-TYPE)</code> means that means that instances of <code>AGENT-TYPE</code> typically broker sales (act with permission as an intermediary for the seller and buyer) of instances of <code>PRODUCT-TYPE</code>. ")
(comment InsuranceAgent "The collection of insurance agents, people licensed to broker sales of insurance plans (see #$InsurancePlan) from insurance companies to prospective policyholders.")
(comment MobileIceCreamVendor "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Instances are people who walk the streets carrying a cooler, or, more typically drive the streets in a vehicle that contains freezers, and sells ice cream.")
(comment RetailOutlet "The collection of places where a vendor sales his or her wares.")
(disjointWith MobileRetailOutlet RealEstate)
(disjointWith MobileRetailOutlet ConstructionArtifact)
(comment MobileRetailOutlet "The collection of instances of #$RetailOutlet that are not stationary, but move from place to place to generate business.  Notable examples include #$IceCreamTruck and #$WalkingConcessionStand.")
(comment IceCreamTruck "The collection of vehicles that are driven by ice-cream vendors (see #$MobileIceCreamVendor), often generating loud music to garner attention, from which (when parked) ice cream is sold.")
(comment ArborealModificationSpecialist "The collection of people who cut down or prune trees.")
(comment Arborist "The collection of people who tend to the needs of trees (or to the needs of those living close to them), primarily by trimming trees (e.g., to keep them away from power lines) or, occasionally, removing them when they become terminal or pose a safety hazard.")
(comment LoadingDock "The collection of loading docks, areas designed for the loading and unloading of trucks.")
(comment LoadingAVehicle "The collection of instances of #$Loading in which the #$toLocation is a vehicle.")
(comment DirectBinaryPredicate "A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and a specialization of  #$AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate (q.v.).  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$DirectBinaryPredicate only if it has the property that for any sequence of the form:
<p>
<pre>
(PRED X_1 X_2)
...
(PRED X_n X_n+1)
</pre>
<p>
where <code>n</code> is greater than or equal to 2, it is the case that <code>(#$not (PRED X_1 X_n+1))</code> holds.
<p>
This is a fairly common logical property of binary predicates.  Notable instances include #$biologicalFather and #$successor.")
(comment AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate "A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and a specialization of  #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate (q.v.).  A binary predicate PRED is an instance of  #$AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate only if it has this property: for any THING1, THING2, and THING3 such that both (PRED THING1 THING2) and (PRED THING2 THING3) hold, (#$not (PRED THING1 THING3)) holds.  Equivalently: for every X, Y, and Z that satisfy the (single-argument) argument-type constraints  (see #$admittedArgument) for the relevant argument positions of PRED,  (#$not (#$and (PRED X Y)(PRED Y Z)(PRED X Z))) holds.  Note that  PRED's two agument-positions must also be \"co-satisfiable\" with respect to their type constraints; see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement.
<p>
For example, #$divorcedFrom is an instance of #$AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate.
<p>
Note also that, while not strictly disjoint with #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate (q.v.), in practice few if any reified predicates should be instances of both of these collections. An important specialization of #$AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate is #$DirectBinaryPredicate (q.v.).")
(comment SetOfSetsUnionFn "An evaluatable macro expanded function that takes a set of sets (#$SetOfSets) and returns the union of its elements.")
(comment agentHasCreditors "<code>(#$agentHasCreditors AGENT CREDITOR)</code> identifies <code>CREDITOR</code> as one of the instances of #$IntelligentAgent to whom <code>AGENT</code>, a distinct instance of #$IntelligentAgent, owes a financial debt.")
(comment corporationStockType "<code>(#$corporationStockType CORPORATION STOCK-TYPE)</code> identifies <code>STOCK-TYPE</code> as the collection of shares of stock (see #$Stock) in the legal corporation (see #$LegalCorporation) <code>CORPORATION</code>.")
(comment stockIssuedBy "<code>(#$stockIssuedBy SHARE CORPORATION)</code> means that <code>SHARE</code> is a single share of stock in <code>CORPORATION</code>, an instance of #$LegalCorporation.  To talk about all such shares of stock, use #$corporationStockType.")
(comment SchemaIntegrationTargetSKSFn "<code>(#$SchemaIntegrationTargetSKSFn INTEGRATION)</code> denotes the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource that is the product of the source integration event <code>INTEGRATION</code>. It is the function corresponding to the predicate #$schemaIntegrationTargetSKS.")
(comment IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate "The collection of #$BinaryPredicates that are irreflexive.  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate only if, for every <code>THING</code> that satisfies the single-argument type-constraints (i.e. #$argIsa and #$argGenls; see #$admittedArgument) on both of <code>PRED</code>'s argument-places, <code>(#$not (PRED THING THING))</code> holds.
<p>
To rule out \"vacuous\" cases, there is an additional necessary condition for irreflexivity:  the constraints on <code>PRED</code>'s two argument-places must be co-satisfiable (see #$cosatisfiableInArgs).  Without this requirement, any binary predicate whose arguments were constrained in mutually-disjoint ways would trivially (and counterintuitively) count as irreflexive.  See #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement
<p>
Examples of irreflexive binary predicates include #$spouse, #$causes-ThingProp, #$northOf, and #$temporallyDisjoint.")
(comment DisembarkFromFn "(#$DisembarkFromFn TRANS-DEVICE) denotes the collection of instances of #$DisembarkingFromATransportationDevice that are disembarkings from TRANS-DEVICE.")
(comment disembarkingFromTransporter-Type "(#$disembarkingFromTransporter-Type TRANS-DEVICE COLLECTION) holds just in case COLLECTION is the collection of instances of #$DisembarkingFromATransportationDevice that are disembarkings from TRANS-DEVICE.")
(comment currentDurationOfStay "(#$currentDurationOfStay TRANSPORTER DURATION) holds just in case TRANPORTER is stopped either between transportation events it is involved in, or between legs of a single trip and the length of time before it starts its next trip or continues on the current trip is DURATION.  Note that if TRANSPORTER will no longer be the transporter in any future transportation event, then the duration of the stay will be the length of time remaining until either the transporter is moved from it's last destination or ceases to exist.  As time moves forward, DURATION decreases accordingly.  So, if this first became true on May 1, and the value for DURATION was 4 days, on May 3, the value for DURATION will be 2 days.")
(comment IntentionalMentalSituation "A specialization of #$MentalSituation-SingleSubject (q.v.).   Each instance of #$IntentionalMentalSituation is a mental state or event characterized by <i>intentionality</i>: it is \"of\" or \"about\" some thing external to itself.  Thoughts (see #$Thinking), acts of perception (see #$Perceiving), and propositional attitudes (e.g. #$BelievingThat and #$DesiringThat) are three common types of intentional mental situations.  Thus, a particular act of thinking about the Eiffel Tower, an event of seeing the Eiffel Tower, and a given subject's believing that the Eiffel Tower is in Paris are three intentional mental situations, all having (at least) the #$EiffelTower as their object.  
<p>
Any thing whatsoever can in principle be the object of an 
intentional mental situation: I can think about a given physical object, an event, a relation, an attribute, a proposition, a mental state (my own or another's), a set, a collection, or anything else.  (Note that the above mentioned belief about the Eiffel Tower and Paris is arguably not itself about the <i>proposition</i> that the Eiffel Tower is in Paris.  But now note that my belief in the content of the sentence preceding this one clearly <i>is</i>, in part, about that proposition.)  
<p>
Mental situations that are <i>not</i> intentional, and thus not in this collection, include moods, itches, and pain experiences.  
<p>
Note that, although they are indirectly related (see e.g. #$PurposefulMentalActivity), the sense of \"intentional\" described above is not to be confused with its other sense as an antonym of \"accidental\".  
<p>
See also #$IntentionalMentalEvent, #$IntentionalMentalState, #$objectOfMentalSituation, and #$thinksAbout.")
(comment hlPrototypicalInstance "(#$hlPrototypicalInstance TERM COL) means that TERM is a prototypical instance of COL at the HL.  It is assumed that TERM has only two asserted assertions on it: one #$hlPrototypicalInstance assertion and one assertion of the form (#$isa TERM COL).  The #$CycInferenceEngine makes use of the prototypical instance during assertions to cache the work done by forward inference.  It is used when asserting the first #$isa assertion on a term with no other assertions about it yet.  <b>Do not assert #$comments, #$cyclistNotes or any other assertions on prototypical instances.</b>  They are intentionally minimal.")
(comment arbitrageur "<code>(#$arbitrageur ARBITRAGE PERFORMER)</code> means that <code>PERFORMER</code> is the performer of the arbitrage event <code>ARBITRAGE</code>.")
(disjointWith RiskFreeArbitrage RiskArbitrage)
(comment sourceCMLSFieldType "(#$sourceCMLSFieldType SKS CMLS COLL) means that the #$StructuredKnowledgeSource, SKS, references an object that is an instance of COLL defined by the #$ReifiedMapping, CMLS.")
(comment HLPrototypicalTerm "The collection of all #$CycLTerms that are the #$hlPrototypicalInstance of some collection.  All #$HLPrototypicalTerms are #$IndeterminateTerms.")
(comment RadioTower "#$RadioTowers are #$CommunicationTowers which are used
primarily for the transmission of radio signals (see #$Broadcasting).")
(comment predicateTypicallyComparesToPredWRTSlot "The predicate that corresponds to the function #$TypicallyComparesToWRTSlotFn. <code>(#$predicateTypicallyComparesToPredWRTSlot PRED COMPARISION-PRED SLOT)</code> means that the binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is the predicate that serves to relate collections <code>COL1</code> <code>COL2</code> in the following way: <code>(PRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that, for most instances of <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code> if the numerical values of <code>SLOT</code> are sorted using <code>ORDERING</code>, <code>COL1</code> comes before <code>COL2</code>.")
(comment typeProvidesShelterDuringType "<code>(#$typeProvidesShelterDuringType SHELTER-TYPE EVENT-TYPE)</code> means that instances of <code>SHELTER-TYPE</code> are, by design, intended to provide shelter during instances of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code>.")
(disjointWith BodyOfWater-Intermittent-DryPhase (CollectionDifferenceFn BodyOfWater BodyOfWater-Intermittent-DryPhase))
(disjointWith BodyOfWater-Dry (CollectionDifferenceFn BodyOfWater-Generic BodyOfWater-Dry))
(comment BodyOfWater-Intermittent-DryPhase "A specialization of both #$BodyOfWater-Intermittent and #$BodyOfWater-Dry (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$BodyOfWater-Intermittent-DryPhase is a region that is often inundated with a relatively large volume of stationary or flowing water, but which has virtually no water present at the current time.")
(comment BodyOfWater-Dry "A specialization of #$BodyOfWater-Generic and #$ExistenceDependentIntermittentCollection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$BodyOfWater-Dry is a natural (or artificial) region that is often inundated (or designed to be inundated) with a mass of stationary or moving water, but is at present virtually empty of water.  Examples include lakes and streams that are seasonally dry (see #$BodyOfWater-Intermittent-DryPhase) and swimming pools that are drained for maintanence.")
(comment AnimalShelter "A specialization of #$ServiceOrganization, #$AnimalShelter is the collection of organizations that supply food, shelter, and some degree of health care to stray animals, typically unwanted or runaway pets.")
(comment AnimalCareOrganization "The collection of all instances of #$Organization that have as their mandate some combination of care/shelter/feeding/ for stray (typically ex-pet), non-human animals.")
(comment HomelessShelter "The collection of instances of #$Shelter-Organization that are organizations that provide (usually) temporary lodging to people who do not otherwise have lodging.")
(comment Shelter-Organization "A specialization of #$ServiceOrganization.  Instances are organizations who provide shelter and (sometimes) food and some degree of health care to animals (human or non-human) in need.")
(comment BoilerRoom "A specialization of #$RoomInAConstruction.  Typically found in the basement level of a building, instances are spaces that hold boilers (see #$Boiler-Device).")
(comment Arsenal "The collection of organizations whose main function is to build, repair, store, and issue weapons.")
(disjointWith BaggageCart ShoppingCart)
(comment BaggageCart "A type of cart used to transport luggage.")
(comment PushCart "A specialization of #$Device-UserPowered.  #$PushCart is the collection of carts that, by design, are small and light enough that they can be easily propelled by a single user pushing them.")
(disjointWith ShoppingCart BabyCarriage)
(disjointWith BaggageCart BabyCarriage)
(disjointWith PushCart AnimalDrawnCart)
(comment AnimalDrawnCart "The collection of carts that, by design, are intended to move by being pulled by a large animal, such as a horse or donkey.")
(comment Walkway "The most general collection of instances of #$PathArtifact designed primarily for instances of #$Person to walk along.")
(disjointWith CartPath SeaportFacility)
(disjointWith CartPath LaunchPad)
(disjointWith CartPath Heliport-Physical)
(disjointWith CartPath Roadway)
(comment CartPath "The collection of instances of #$PathForWheeledVehicles that are too narrow to serve as paths for automobiles or other similar-sized motorized vehicles.")
(comment collectionSalientAttribute-Unrepresented "A #$CollectionSalientAttributePredicate that relates a #$Collection to something (often a #$SubLString) describing or otherwise pertaining to a salient attribute of the collection that is neither explicitly represented in the CycL language nor readily representable by any CycL term constructable in the current CycL vocabulary.  Thus, anything in the extent of this predicate represents a gap in the Cyc ontology that we would do well to fill.
<p>
Specializations of this predicate are #$collectionSalientSharedAttributeNotes, #$collectionSalientFacetingAttributeNotes, and #$collectionSalientInstanceLevelAttributeNotes.
<p>
Cf. #$collectionSalientAttribute-Represented.")
(comment collectionSalientAttribute-Represented "A #$CollectionSalientAttributePredicate. <code>(#$collectionSalientAttribute-Represented COL ATTR)</code> means that <code>ATTR</code> is something (often a predicate, but possibly a function or collection-type) that is or corresponds to a salient attribute of <code>COL</code> that is either (i) explicitly represented in the CycL language by a reified term (see #$collectionSalientAttribute-ActuallyRepresented) or (ii) is readily representable by some reifiable CycL term constructable in the current CycL vocabulary (see #$collectionSalientAttribute-VirtuallyRepresented).
<p>
Cf. this predicate's sibling #$collectionSalientAttribute-Unrepresented.")
(comment openEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$openEntryFormatInArgs PRED N)</code> means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to <code>PRED</code> besides the <code>N</code>th, there might be any number (including zero) of things such that, if taken as the <code>N</code>th argument, <code>PRED</code> holds of those arguments.  For example, #$openEntryFormatInArgs holds of #$biologicalMother with respect to its first argument-place, since a given female animal might have any number of offspring.  
<p>
Unlike the other reified instances of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate, #$openEntryFormatInArgs actually places no restriction at all on what <code>PRED</code> might or might not hold of with respect to the specified argument-place.  But one should not infer from this that #$openEntryFormatInArgs assertions are pointless; for they forestall duplication of effort by serving notice that the entry-format of a given argument-place has previously been considered and #$openEntryFormatInArgs was deemed appropriate.  
<p>
#$openEntryFormatInArgs is the most commonly-encountered specific entry-format in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  Contrast with #$singleEntryFormatInArgs.")
(comment RoadwayInterchange "A specialization of #$ArrangementOfLikeObjects.  Each instance is either a simple two-junction path, where a #$HighwayRamp connects a road (see #$Roadway) to a highway (see #$Roadway), or an arrangment of two or more such simple interchanges, usually consisting of 1 or more on-ramp/off-ramp interchanges.")
(comment CloverleafInterchange "The collection of instances of #$RoadwayInterchange that connect two highways, such that (in right-side driving systems) highway changes from a relative North-to-West, South-to-East, West-to-South, and East-to-North direction involve exiting on the right onto a looped ramp (see #$HighwayRamp) and merging with traffic on one's left on the new highway.  The four looping ramps make a shape reminiscent of a four-leaf clover.")
(comment DiamondInterchange "The collection of instances of #$RoadwayInterchange that connect a highway to a second road (typically, not a highway), using a four-or-more ramp system that make a diamond-like shape.")
(comment RoadwayInterchangeTypeByStructure "A collection of collections that facets #$RoadwayInterchange by shared structural features.  Notable instances include #$CloverleafInterchange and #$DiamondInterchange.")
(comment CostumeFn "<code>(#$CostumeFn INDIVIDUAL)</code> denotes the collection of costumes (see #$Costume) that are intended to visually evoke images of <code>INDIVIDUAL</code>, an instance of #$Individual.  For example, <code>(#$CostumeFn #$Spock)</code> denotes the collection of Mr. Spock costumes,  <code>(#$CostumeFn #$EiffelTower)</code> denotes the collection of Eiffel Tower costumes, and so forth.  To talk about costumes for <i>types</i> rather than <i>individuals</i> (e.g., \"pirate costumes\"), use #$CostumeOfTypeFn.")
(disjointWith Nude (SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn Person wearsClothing Clothing-Generic))
(comment costumeTypeOfIndividual "<code>(#$costumeTypeOfIndividual COSTUME-TYPE INDIVIDUAL)</code> identifies <code>COSTUME-TYPE</code> as the collection of instances of #$Costume that are costumes of the individual <code>INDIVIDUAL</code>.  See also #$CostumeFn, for which #$costumeTypeOfIndividual is the corresponding predicate.")
(comment costumeOf "<code>(#$costumeOf COSTUME INDIVIDUAL)</code> means that <code>COSTUME</code> is a costume (see #$Costume) of the individual <code>INDIVIDUAL</code> -- i.e., <code>COSTUME</code> is such that its wearer is in some sense \"portraying\" <code>INDIVIDUAL</code> by wearing it.")
(disjointWith BroadcastMediaShow-CW Commercial-CW)
(comment BroadcastMediaShow-CW "The collection of radio and television programs.")
(comment logicalSchemaTypeMultiplicity "A predicate that relates a logical schema and one of its field types to the number of fields in the schema that are of that type. That is, <code>(#$logicalSchemaTypeMultiplicity LS TYPE MULT)</code> means that the #$LogicalSchema </code>LS</code> has exactly <code>MULT</code> fields (see #$logicalFields) of type <code>TYPE</code>. (See #$fieldIsa).")
(comment organizationName-Standard "(#$organizationName-Standard TERM STRING) means that the #$ProperNameString STRING is one of the standard names for the thing denoted by TERM. These will normally be either the official name of an organization, or a slight variation on that name.  Thus, \"IBM, Inc.\" might be a standard name for #$IBMInc, just as \"IBM, Incorporated\" might be one.  A company should be related to its official name with #$organizationName-Official.")
(comment organizationName-Official "(#$organizationName-Official TERM STRING) means that the #$ProperNameString STRING is the official legal name for the organization denoted by TERM.")
(comment RoundClosestToNthDecimalFn "A macro-function that takes a real number or a scalar quantity to the nearest amount expressible in up to some specified number of decimal places.  For example, (#$RoundClosestToNthDecimalFn (#$Meter 2.416) 2) is (#$Meter 2.42) and (#$RoundClosestToNthDecimalFn 3.14159 0) is 3.
<p>
See also #$RoundClosestFn.")
(comment Cannula "The collection of narrow tubes inserted into a vein, body cavity, etc., for the purpose of inserting instruments, administering medicine, etc.")
(comment logicalSchemaFieldOfTypeWithMultiplicity "A quaternary predicate relating a logical schema, logical field, collection, and integer. <code>(#$logicalSchemaFieldOfTypeWithMultiplicity SCHEMA FIELD TYPE N)</code> means that:
<p>
1. <code>FIELD</code> is a logical field of <code>SCHEMA</code> (See #$logicalFields);
<p>
2. <code>FIELD</code> is of type <code>TYPE</code> (See #$fieldIsa);
<p>
3. <code>FIELD</code> is the <code>N</code>th field in <code>SCHEMA</code> of type <code>TYPE<code>.<p>
See also the function corresponding to the second argument of this predicate, #$LogicalFieldFn.")
(comment logicalSchemaFieldIndexicalOfTypeWithMultiplicity "A quaternary predicate relating a logical schema, logical field, collection, and integer. <code>(#$logicalSchemaFieldIndexicalOfTypeWithMultiplicity SCHEMA INDEXICAL TYPE N)</code> means that:
<p>
1. <code>INDEXICAL</code> is a logical field indexical of <code>SCHEMA</code> (See #$logicalFieldIndexicals);
<p>
2. <code>INDEXICAL</code> is an indexical of a field of type <code>TYPE</code> (See #$indexicalForLogicalField and #$fieldIsa);
<p>
3. <code>INDEXICAL</code> is the <code>N</code>th indexical in <code>SCHEMA</code> corresponding to a field of type <code>TYPE<code>.
<p>
See also the function corresponding to the second argument of this predicate, #$TheLogicalFieldValueFn.")
(comment earningsPerShare-ForStockType "#$earningsPerShare-ForStockType denotes the relation between a stock and the amount of money per share of that stock earned by the issuing company. <code>(#$earningsPerShare-ForStockType STOCK VALUE)</code> means that <code>VALUE</code> is the monetary value of the earnings per share of <code>STOCK</code> of the company that issues shares of <code>STOCK</code>.")
(comment dilutedEarningsPerShare-ForStockType "<code>(#$dilutedEarningsPerShare-ForStockType STOCK VALUE)</code> means that <code>VALUE</code> is what the monetary value would be of the earnings per share of <code>STOCK</code> of the company that issues shares of <code>STOCK</code>, if all convertible securities of the company that issues <code>STOCK</code> were exercised.  In this case, \"convertible securities\" means outstanding convertible preferred shares, convertible debentures, stock options and warrants.
<p>
The diluted EPS will almost always be lower than the simple EPS (see #$earningsPerShare-ForStockType), except in the rare case in which the company has no additional potential shares outstanding.")
(comment assertedPredicateArgCardinality "A quaternary rule-macro predicate used for stating that there are a certain number of assertions of a particular form in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  <code>(#$assertedPredicateArgCardinality THING ARGNUM PRED N)</code> means that there are currently in the KB exactly <code>N</code> assertions of the form 
<pre>
  (PRED ...THING...)
</pre>
where (a CycL term denoting) <code>THING</code> appears in the <code>N</code>th argument-position.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$assertedPredicateArgCardinality #$PresidentFn 1 #$arg1Isa 1)</code> means that there is exactly one asserted #$arg1Isa constraint on #$PresidentFn.
<p>
Note that the above does <i>not</i> imply that no other sentence of the relevant form is derivable.  Indeed, given the assertion <code>(#$arg1Isa #$PresidentFn #$Organization)</code>, it follows by #$transitiveViaArg (q.v.) reasoning that <code>(#$arg1Isa #$PresidentFn #$Individual)</code> is also true.
<p>
See also #$assertedPredicateArg.")
(comment adjustedEarningsPerShare-ForStockType "<code>(#$adjustedEarningsPerShare-ForStockType STOCK VALUE)</code> means that <code>VALUE</code> is what the monetary value would be of the earnings per share of <code>STOCK</code> of the company that issues shares of <code>STOCK</code>, if all amortization and exceptional costs to the company that issues <code>STOCK</code> were added back to that companiy's profits.  This value is often called \"headline EPS\" because it is often the EPS reported by companies.  The exact nature of the adjustments made varies from company to company.  This value is also called \"adjusted EPS\".
<p>
See also #$earningsPerShare-ForStockType and #$dilutedEarningsPerShare-ForStockType.")
(comment FiscalPeriodOfIntervalFn "(#$FiscalPeriodOfIntervalFn INTERVAL) denotes the fiscal period that begins at the start of INTERVAL and ends at the culmination of INTERVAL.")
(comment agentFiscalYear "(#$agentFiscalYear AGENT INTEGER FISCAL-YEAR) means that a fiscal year of AGENT is the year FISCAL-YEAR, which ends during the year denoted by (#$YearFn INTEGER).")
(comment fiscalYearForAgent "(#$fiscalYearForAgent AGT START END) means that one of the fiscal years recognized by AGT runs from the starting date START to the ending date END.")
(comment FiscalPeriodTypeForAgentWithEndDateFn "(#$FiscalPeriodTypeForAgentWithEndDateFn AGENT FISCAL-PERIOD-TYPE DATE) is the fiscal period of type FISCAL-PERIOD-TYPE (either #$FiscalQuarter or #$FiscalYear) for AGENT that ends on DATE.")
(comment fiscalPeriodTypeForAgentWithEndDate "(#$fiscalPeriodTypeForAgentWithEndDate AGENT FISCAL-PERIOD-TYPE DATE FISCAL-PERIOD) means FISCAL-PERIOD is the fiscal period of type FISCAL-PERIOD-TYPE (either #$FiscalQuarter or #$FiscalYear) for AGENT that ends on DATE.")
(comment netIncomeForPeriod "(#$netIncomeForPeriod ORG MONEY TIME) means MONEY is the net income of ORG during TIME.")
(comment AccountingCOC "A specialization of #$CodeOfConduct (q.v.). Each instance of #$AccountingCOC is a code of conduct that dictates how accounting should be performed.")
(comment GenerallyAcceptedAccountingPrinciples "A specialization of #$FormalCOC and #$AccountingCOC (q.v.). Each instance of #$GenerallyAcceptedAccountingPrinciples is a formal code of conduct that dictates how accounting should be performed.")
(comment revenue "An instance of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation. (#$revenue ORG MONEY)<T> means MONEY is the amount earned as revenue (e.g net sales) by ORG during the time interval T, but not necessarily any time interval subsumed by T.")
(comment revenueForPeriodByAccountingCOC "(#$revenueForPeriodByAccountingCOC ORG MONEY TIME COC) means MONEY is the amount earned as revenue (e.g. net sales) by ORG during TIME based on the accounting principles allowed by the #$AccountingCOC COC.")
(comment netIncomeForPeriodByAccountingCOC "(#$netIncomeForPeriodByAccountingCOC ORG MONEY TIME COC) means MONEY is the net income of ORG during TIME based on the accounting principles allowed by the #$AccountingCOC COC.")
(comment nthFiscalQuarterOfFiscalYear "(#$nthFiscalQuarterOfFiscalYear N FISCAL-YEAR FISCAL-QUARTER) means that the Nth fiscal quarter of FISCAL-YEAR is FISCAL-QUARTER.")
(comment AccountingCodeOfTypeTypicallyUsedByAgentFn "(#$AccountingCodeOfTypeTypicallyUsedByAgentFn ACCT-COC-TYPE AGENT) is the accounting code of type ACCT-COC-TYPE typically used by AGENT.  For example, (#$AccountingCodeOfTypeTypicallyUsedByAgentFn #$GenerallyAcceptedAccountingPrinciples #$MicrosoftInc) is the GAAP used by Microsoft.")
(comment accountingCodeOfTypeTypicallyUsedByAgent "(#$accountingCodeOfTypeTypicallyUsedByAgentFn ACCT-COC-TYPE AGENT CODE) means CODE is the accounting code of type ACCT-COC-TYPE typically used by AGENT.  For example, <code>(#$accountingCodeOfTypeTypicallyUsedByAgent #$GenerallyAcceptedAccountingPrinciples #$MicrosoftInc CODE)</code>. means that CODE is the GAAP used by Microsoft.")
(comment FiscalQuarterFn "(#$FiscalQuarterFn N FISCAL-YEAR) denotes the  Nth fiscal quarter of FISCAL-YEAR. See also #$FiscalPeriodTypeForAgentWithEndDateFn.")
(comment FiscalYearFn "(#$FiscalYearFn AGENT INTEGER) denotes the fiscal year (see #$FiscalYear) for the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT that ends during the calendar year (#$YearFn INTEGER).  See also #$FiscalPeriodTypeForAgentWithEndDateFn.")
(comment ProFormaAccountingCOC "A specialization of #$NonGAAPAccountingCOC (q.v.). Each instance of #$ProFormaAccountingCOC is a code of conduct that dictates how accounting should be performed.  Typically when pro forma net income (see #$netIncomeForPeriodByAccountingCOC) is reported, it excludes what the reporting company determines to be unusual and nonrecurring expenses.")
(comment NonGAAPAccountingCOC "A specialization of #$AccountingCOC (q.v.). Each instance of #$NonGAAPAccountingCOC is a code of conduct that dictates how accounting should be performed, but which is distinct from the #$GenerallyAcceptedAccountingPrinciples that pertain to accounting in the relevant context.")
(comment operatingIncome "(#$operatingIncome BUSINESS VALUE)<T> holds just in case VALUE is the income of BUSINESS minus taxes and interest payments during T, but not necessarily any interval subsumed by T.  This is also known as \"Earnings before interest and taxes\", or \"EBIT\".")
(comment operatingIncomeForPeriod "(#$operatingIncomeForPeriod ORG MONEY TIME) means MONEY is the operating income (EBIT) of ORG during TIME.")
(comment operatingIncomeForPeriodByAccountingCOC "(#$operatingIncomeForPeriodByAccountingCOC ORG MONEY TIME COC) means MONEY is the operating income (EBIT) of ORG during TIME based on the accounting principles allowed by the #$AccountingCOC COC.")
(comment CorpseOfFn "A unary, reifiable, individual-denoting function.  Where ANIMAL is some #$Animal, (#$CorpseOfFn ANIMAL) denotes the physical remains persisting after the animal's death.")
(comment corpseOf "The corresponding predicate (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical) of #$CorpseOfFn.")
(comment tombOf "The corresponding predicate (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical) of #$TombOfFn.")
(comment Swamp-I2 "A specialization of #$Swamp. Each instance of #$Swamp-I2 is a #$WetlandRegion that is 25-50% covered with trees and woody bushes. The #$USArmyFieldManual5-33VegetationMapCode assigns Swamp-I2 a Vegetation Map Code of (<code>I2</code>).")
(comment Pi-CSQLConstant "#$Pi-CSQLConstant is the #$CSQLConstantFunction that corresponds to #$Pi-Number.")
(comment ArtilleryShell "A specialization of #$ProjectileShellAssembly and #$ExplosiveDevice.  Each instance of #$ArtilleryShell is a projectile containing explosive material and is constructed to be launched by artillery.")
(comment GeoRegionToGeoRegionPredicate "A #$PredicateTypeByArgumentType and specialization of #$GeographicalRegionSlot.  Each instance of #$GeoRegionToGeoRegionPredicate is a #$BinaryPredicate both of whose arguments are required (via #$argIsa) to be #$GeographicalRegions.")
(comment ConstructionBarrier "A specialization of #$Barrier-ConstructionArtifact (q.v).  Each
instance of #$ConstructionBarrier is a barrier (often portable, often
orange) that is set up to impede movement into an area undergoing
construction, such as a building or a road.")
(comment Interval-BoundedBelow "A specialization of #$ScalarInterval.  Each instance of #$Interval-BoundedBelow is an interval that has a lower bound.  Example: the interval comprising all integers greater than 10.")
(disjointWith PurposefulAction (CollectionDifferenceFn Action PurposefulAction))
(comment ConstructionWall "A specialization of #$ConstructionBarrier and #$Wall-Vertical (qq.v).
Each instance of #$ConstructionWall is a barrier (often portable,
often orange (when plastic), sometimes constructed of concrete) that is
set up to impede movement into an area undergoing construction, such
as a building or a road.")
(comment objectPassed "(#$objectPassed EVENT LOC) means that in the #$PassingBySomething event EVENT, the object being passed is LOC.  In the case that the #$objectPassed is also moving, the #$objectPassing approaches the #$objectPassed and eventually overtakes it.")
(comment LowConstructionWall "A specialization of #$ConstructionWall and #$HumanlyHurdlableObject
(qq.v).  Each instance of #$LowConstructionWall is a construction wall
that is overleapable by a typical human.")
(comment ClimbingDown "A specialization of #$DescendingAlongSurface and #$LocomotionProcess-Animal (qq.v.).  Each instance
of #$ClimbingDown is an event in which an agent climbs down the surface of an object.")
(disjointWith ClimbingOverSomething JumpingOverSomething)
(comment objectClimbedOver "(#$objectClimbedOver EVENT LOC) means that in the #$ClimbingOverSomething
event EVENT, the thing being climbed over is LOC.")
(comment LowChainLinkFence "A specialization of #$ChainLinkFence and #$HumanlyHurdlableObject
(qq.v).  Each instance of #$LowChainLinkFence is a chain link fence
that is overleapable by a typical human.")
(comment Foldable "An instance of #$PartiallyTangibleTypeByPhysicalStructuralFeature (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Foldable is a partially tangible object that is foldable in a flexible way, like towels or paper.  Note a distinction between #$Foldable objects and #$Collapsible objects: #$Collapsible objects are rigid objects collapsed by means of #$HingedJoints, while #$Foldable objects are flexible overall.")
(comment CollapsibleConstructionBarrier "A specialization of #$ConstructionBarrier and #$CollapsibleDevice
(qq.v).  Each instance of #$CollapsibleConstructionBarrier is a construction barrier that folds along a hinged joint at its top: when unfolded it makes an \"A\" shaped structure, much like a folding step ladder.")
(comment StepLadder "A specialization of #$Ladder and #$CollapsibleDevice.")
(comment TrafficCone "A specialization of #$Obstruction-ConstructionArtifact and #$HumanlyHurdlableObject
(qq.v).  Each instance of #$TrafficCone is a roughly cone-shaped and (typically plastic and orange) hollow cone designed to be placed on routes used by automobiles to redirect traffic along the those routes.")
(comment TrafficConeBarrier "A specialization of #$Barrier-ConstructionArtifact and #$HumanlyHurdlableObject
(qq.v).  Each instance of #$TrafficConeBarrier is a small barrier constructed by placing a bar across the top of two or more traffic cones (see #$TrafficCone).")
(comment Place "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized (q.v).  Each instance of #$Place is a spatial thing which has a relatively permanent location. Thus, in a given microtheory, each #$Place is stationary with respect to the frame of reference of that microtheory.")
(comment LowCollapsibleConstructionBarrier "A specialization of #$CollapsibleConstructionBarrier and #$HumanlyHurdlableObject
(qq.v).  Each instance of #$LowCollapsibleConstructionBarrier is a humanly overleapable construction barrier that folds along a hinged joint at its top.")
(comment HumanlyHurdlableObject "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized (q.v.).  Each instance
of #$HumanlyHurdlableObject is a tangible object over which a typical
#$HumanAdult can, unaided by technology, jump (see
#$JumpingOverSomething).")
(comment HumanlyScalableObject "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized (q.v.).  Each instance
of #$HumanlyScalableObject is a tangible object up which a typical
#$HumanAdult can, unaided by technology, climb (see
#$Climbing).")
(comment ObjectOverWhichAHumanCanClimb "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized (q.v.).  Each instance
of #$ObjectOverWhichAHumanCanClimb is a tangible object over which a
typical #$HumanAdult can, unaided by technology, climb (see
#$ClimbingOverSomething).")
(comment Configuration "A specialization of both #$StaticSituation and #$SpatialThing-Localized.  Each instance of #$Configuration is a static situation consisting of two or more #$PartiallyTangible things of certain types standing in a certain type of spatial relationship (or set of relationships).  This (set of) spatial relationship(s) \"characterizes\" the #$Configuration's <i>type</i> in the sense that any group of objects of the appropriate types standing in that relationship (or those relationships) correspond to a #$Configuration of that type; and each of these objects, in turn, is said to be \"configured\" (see #$configuredObjects) in the (individual) #$Configuration.  
<p>
For example, a knot configuration might correspond to a particular intertwining of end-segments of two #$CordlikeObjects, or to the intertwining of several segments of a single #$CordlikeObject.  For configurations like the latter, whose characterizing relationship(s) hold between different parts of a single salient object, see the specialization #$ConfigurationOfComplexObject.  Note that a static situation involving an #$Agent-PartiallyTangible might be a configuration, but only so long as it's just the agent's <i>spatial</i> relationship(s) to other partially tangibles that matters (see e.g. #$WearingSomething); static situations in which an agent's agency or sentience is crucial, on the other hand, are not configurations (cf. #$InterAgentSituation and #$InterAgentSituation-TwoAgent).")
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject HighChainLinkFence)
(comment HighChainLinkFence "A specialization of #$ChainLinkFence and #$ObjectOverWhichAHumanCanClimb (qq.v). Each instance of #$HighChainLinkFence is a chain link fence over which a typical human can climb but not jump.")
(comment ConstructionFence "A specialization of #$ConstructionBarrier and #$Fence (qq.v).  Each instance of #$ConstructionFence is a temporary fence erected to impede access to a construction site.")
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject HighConstructionFence)
(comment HighConstructionFence "A specialization of #$ConstructionFence and #$ObjectOverWhichAHumanCanClimb (qq.v). Each instance of #$HighConstructionFence is a construction fence over which a typical human can climb but not jump.")
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject ApartmentComplex)
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject ElectricPowerGrid)
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject Building)
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject BodyOfWater-Large)
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject HighTrafficBarrier)
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject HighInterlockingBarrier)
(comment HighInterlockingBarrier "A specialization of #$Barrier-ConstructionArtifact (q.v). Each instance of #$HighInterlockingBarrier is a construction artifact composed of large interlocking pieces of plastic.")
(disjointWith ObjectOverWhichAHumanCanClimb HumanlyInsurmountableObstruction)
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject HumanlyInsurmountableObstruction)
(disjointWith HumanlyInsurmountableObstruction ObjectUnderneathWhichAHumanCanMovePast)
(comment MovingPastSomethingUnderneathIt "A specialization of #$GoingUnderSomething and #$PassingBySomething (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$MovingPastSomethingUnderneathIt is an event in which an agent passes by an object by moving under it.")
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject HighConstructionBarrier)
(disjointWith RazorWire BarbedWire)
(comment FenceWithSharpWireOnTop "A specialization of #$Fence and #$HumanlyInsurmountableObstruction (qq.v). Each instance of #$FenceWithSharpWireOnTop is a fence with either #$BarbedWire or #$RazorWire on top.")
(comment SecurityFence "A specialization of #$Fence and #$HumanlyInsurmountableObstruction.  Each instance of #$SecurityFence is a high metal fence with additional security features (such as sharp metal) on the top.")
(disjointWith FenceWithSharpWireOnTop SecurityFence)
(disjointWith SecurityFence PicketFence)
(disjointWith PicketFence ChainLinkFence)
(disjointWith SecurityFence ChainLinkFence)
(comment HighConcreteSecurityWall "A specialization of #$Wall-Vertical and #$HumanlyInsurmountableObstruction.  Each instance of #$HighConcreteSecurityWall is a high concrete wall designed to be insurmountable by unaided humans.")
(comment PanelConstructionBarricade "A specialization of #$ConstructionBarrier (q.v). Each instance of #$PanelConstructionBarricade is a construction barricade construced of panels of wood.")
(disjointWith HumanlyHurdlableObject HighPanelConstructionBarricade)
(comment HighPanelConstructionBarricade "A specialization of #$PanelConstructionBarricade (q.v). Each instance of #$PanelConstructionBarricade is a construction barricade construced of panels of wood.  Unaided humans can climb over these, but cannot jump over them.")
(comment FenceWithRazorWireOnTop "A specialization of #$FenceWithSharpWireOnTop (qq.v). Each instance of #$FenceWithRazorWireOnTop is a fence with #$RazorWire on top.")
(comment ObjectUnderneathWhichAHumanCanMovePast "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized (q.v.).  Each instance
of #$ObjectUnderneathWhichAHumanCanMovePast is a tangible object underneath which a typical #$HumanAdult can, unaided by technology, move past by walking, crawling or slithering if need be (see #$MovingPastSomethingUnderneathIt).")
(comment YahooFinanceStockQuotePredicate "A small collection of predicates that correspond to the cells in the tabular data given on Yahoo! Finance stock quote pages.")
(comment perceivedInformation "A binary predicate that relates an act of #$Perceiving to any and all pieces of information (see #$AspatialInformationStore)  that the perceiver is made aware of through and during that perceiving.
<code>(#$perceivedInformation PERCEIVING INFO)</code> means that during <code>PERCEIVING</code> the subject acquires, or begins thinking about, some piece of information or informational thing <code>INFO</code>.  For example, 
<code>INFO</code> might be a concept, a proposition, an abstract structure, or a fact about the world.")
(comment subjectOfMentalSituation "A binary predicate that relates a #$MentalSituation-SingleSubject (q.v.) to its subject: the single agent who <i>is in</i>, <i>has</i>, <i>undergoes</i>, <i>experiences</i>, <i>entertains</i>, or <i>performs</i> the mental situation, as the case may be. <code>(#$subjectOfMentalSituation SIT AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is the subject of <code>SIT</code>.  If <code>SIT</code> is a #$MentalState (q.v.), <code>AGENT</code> might be said to \"have\" or be \"in\" <code>SIT</code> (e.g. \"Dreyfus has doubts about AI\" and \"Hubert's in a bad mood\").  If <code>SIT</code> is a #$PurposefulMentalActivity (q.v.), it is #$performedBy <code>AGENT</code>.  If <code>SIT</code> is a relatively passive mental event, such as an #$ExperiencingEmotion, <code>AGENT</code> might be said to \"experience\" or  \"undergo\" <code>SIT</code>, and the appropriate predicate to use would be #$doneBy or some specialization thereof.  
<p>
Cf. #$objectOfMentalSituation.")
